C \\):\n\n\\( C(S - C) = 5 + 10k \\) … (3e)\n\nSo each variable satisfies a quadratic equation in terms of \\( S \\). Let me rearrange each equation to standard quadratic form.\n\nStarting with Equation (1e):\n\n\\( A(S - A) = 8 + 4k \\)\n\nExpand left side:\n\n\\( AS - A^2 = 8 + 4k \\)\n\nBring all terms to one side:\n\n\\( -A^2 + AS - (8 + 4k) = 0 \\)\n\nMultiply both sides by -1:\n\n\\( A^2 - AS + (8 + 4k) = 0 \\) … (1f)\n\nSimilarly, Equation (2e):\n\n\\( B(S - B) = 9 + 6k \\)\n\nExpand:\n\n\\( BS - B^2 = 9 + 6k \\)\n\nBring all terms to one side:\n\n\\( -B^2 + BS - (9 + 6k) = 0 \\)\n\nMultiply by -1:\n\n\\( B^2 - BS + (9 + 6k) = 0 \\) … (2f)\n\nEquation (3e):\n\n\\( C(S - C) = 5 + 10k \\)\n\nExpand:\n\n\\( CS - C^2 = 5 + 10k \\)\n\nBring all terms to one side:\n\n\\( -C^2 + CS - (5 + 10k) = 0 \\)\n\nMultiply by -1:\n\n\\( C^2 - CS + (5 + 10k) = 0 \\) … (3f)\n\nSo now, each variable \\( A \\), \\( B \\), \\( C \\) is a root of a quadratic equation with the same coefficient for \\( X^2 \\) (which is 1), the same coefficient for \\( X \\) (which is \\( -S \\)), and different constants. Therefore, each variable satisfies a quadratic equation with roots related to the others?\n\nWait, perhaps we can consider that each quadratic equation has two roots, and the variables \\( A \\), \\( B \\), \\( C \\) are each one of the roots from their respective quadratics? Hmm, but each quadratic is specific to each variable. Alternatively, since all three quadratics have the same coefficient for \\( X^2 \\) and \\( X \\), but different constants, maybe there is some relationship between the roots?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider that \\( A \\), \\( B \\), \\( C \\) are all roots of the same quadratic equation? But that might not be the case, because each quadratic has a different constant term. Alternatively, maybe they are related in some way?\n\nAlternatively, let me consider that each quadratic equation can be written as \\( X^2 - SX + C_i = 0 \\), where \\( C_i \\)