e original equations and see if I can manipulate them differently.\n\nOriginal equations:\n\n1) \\( x^{\\log_2(yz)} = 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\)\n\n2) \\( y^{\\log_2(zx)} = 2^9 \\cdot 3^6 \\)\n\n3) \\( z^{\\log_2(xy)} = 2^5 \\cdot 3^{10} \\)\n\nPerhaps taking natural logarithm on both sides? Let me try that. Let me denote \\( \\ln \\) as the natural logarithm.\n\nTaking \\( \\ln \\) of both sides of Equation (1):\n\n\\( \\ln \\left( x^{\\log_2(y z)} \\right) = \\ln \\left( 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\right) \\)\n\nUsing logarithm power rule: \\( \\log_2(y z) \\cdot \\ln x = \\ln(2^8 \\cdot 3^4) \\)\n\nSimilarly, \\( \\log_2(y z) = \\frac{\\ln(y z)}{\\ln 2} \\), so substituting that in:\n\n\\( \\frac{\\ln(y z)}{\\ln 2} \\cdot \\ln x = \\ln(2^8 \\cdot 3^4) \\)\n\nSimilarly, \\( \\ln(y z) = \\ln y + \\ln z \\), so:\n\n\\( \\frac{(\\ln y + \\ln z)}{\\ln 2} \\cdot \\ln x = \\ln(2^8 \\cdot 3^4) \\)\n\nSimilarly, for Equation (2):\n\n\\( \\frac{(\\ln z + \\ln x)}{\\ln 2} \\cdot \\ln y = \\ln(2^9 \\cdot 3^6) \\)\n\nAnd Equation (3):\n\n\\( \\frac{(\\ln x + \\ln y)}{\\ln 2} \\cdot \\ln z = \\ln(2^5 \\cdot 3^{10}) \\)\n\nHmm, this might not necessarily be better, but perhaps we can let \\( a = \\ln x \\), \\( b = \\ln y \\), \\( c = \\ln z \\). Then \\( \\ln x = a \\), \\( \\ln y = b \\), \\( \\ln z = c \\), so \\( x = e^a \\), etc. Then, substituting into the equations:\n\nEquation (1):\n\n\\( \\frac{(b + c)}{\\ln 2} \\cdot a = \\ln(2^8 \\cdot 3^4) \\)\n\nSimilarly,\n\nEquation (2):\n\n\\( \\frac{(c + a)}{\\ln 2} \\cdot b = \\ln(2^9 \\cdot 3^6) \\)\n\nEquation (3):\n\n\\( \\frac{(a + b)}{\\ln 2} \\cdot c = \\ln(2^5 \\cdot 3^{10}) \\)\n\nHmm, but this still has \\( \\ln 2 \\) in the denominator, and constants on the right-hand side. Maybe this is similar to the previous approach but with natural logs instead of base 2 logs. Maybe we can write everything in terms of \\( k = \\log_2 3 \\), since \\( \\ln 3 = k \\ln 2 \\), so \\( \\ln(3^m) = m \\ln 3 = m k \\ln 2 \\).\n\nLet me try that substitution. Let me recall that \\( \\ln(2^m \\cdot 3^n) = m \\ln 2 + n \\ln 3 = m \\ln 2 + n k \\ln 2 = \\ln 2 (m + n k) \\). Therefore, the right-hand sides can be written as \\( \\ln 2 \\times \\text{something} \\).\n\nSo, for Equation (1):\n\nRight-hand side: \\( \\ln(2^8 \\cdot 3^4) = \\ln 2 \\cdot (8 + 4k) \\)\n\nSimilarly, Equation (2):\n\n\\( \\ln(2^9 \\cdot 3^6) = \\ln 2 \\cdot (9 + 6k) \\)\n\nEquation (3):\n\n\\( \\ln(2^5 \\cdot 3^{10}) = \\ln 2 \\cdot (5 + 10k) \\)\n\nTherefore, substituting into the equations with \\( a = \\ln x \\), \\( b = \\ln y \\), \\( c = \\ln z \\):\n\nEquation (1):\n\n\\( \\frac{(b + c)}{\\ln 2} \\cdot a = \\ln 2 \\cdot (8 + 4k) \\)\n\nMultiply both sides by \\( \\ln 2 \\):\n\n\\( (b + c) \\cdot a = (\\ln 2)^2 \\cdot (8 + 4k) \\)\n\nSimilarly, Equation (2):\n\n\\( (c + a) \\cdot b = (\\ln 2)^2 \\cdot (9 + 6k) \\)\n\nEquation (3):\n\n\\( (a + b) \\cdot c = (\\ln 2)^2 \\cdot (5 + 10k) \\)\n\nHmm, so this seems to complicate things further because now we have \\( (\\ln 2)^2 \\) factors, which are constants but not as nice as \\( k \\). Perhaps this isn't the best path.\n\nLet me revisit the earlier substitution with \\( A = \\log_2 x \\), \\( B = \\log_2 y \\), \\( C = \\log_2 z \\), so that \\( x = 2^A \\), etc. Then, the original equations can be written as:\n\nEquation (1): \\( (2^A)^{\\log_2(y z)} = 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\)\n\nBut \\( \\log_2(y z) = \\log_2 y + \\log_2 z = B + C \\), so:\n\nLeft side: \\( (2^A)^{B + C} = 2^{A(B + C)} \\)\n\nTherefore, Equation (1) becomes:\n\n\\( 2^{A(B + C)} = 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\)\n\nSimilarly, Equation (2):\n\n\\( y^{\\log_2(z x)} = (2^B)^{\\log_2(z x)} = 2^{B \\cdot \\log_2(z x)} \\)\n\nBut \\( \\log_2(z x) = \\log_2 z + \\log_2 x = C + A \\), so:\n\nLeft side: \\( 2^{B(C + A)} \\)\n\nEquation (2): \\( 2^{B(C + A)} = 2^9 \\cdot 3^6 \\)\n\nEquation (3):\n\n\\( z^{\\log_2(x y)} = (2^C)^{\\log_2(x y)} = 2^{C \\cdot \\log_2(x y)} \\)\n\n\\( \\log_2(x y) = A + B \\), so left side: \\( 2^{C(A + B)} \\)\n\nEquation (3): \\( 2^{C(A + B)} = 2^5 \\cdot 3^{10} \\)\n\nTherefore, now, all three equations have left-hand sides as powers of 2, and right-hand sides are products of powers of 2 and 3. Since the left-hand sides are pure powers of 2, but the right-hand sides have factors of 3, this suggests that perhaps there is a miscalculation here?\n\nWait a second! Hold on. Wait, this can't be. If I have \\( x^{\\log_2(y z)} \\), and \\( x = 2^A \\), then \\( x^{\\log_2(y z)} = (2^A)^{\\log_2(y z)} = 2^{A \\cdot \\log_2(y z)} \\), which is correct. However, \\( \\log_2(y z) \\) is just a real number, so exponentiating 2 to that power gives a power of 2. But the right-hand side of Equation (1) is \\( 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\), which is not a power of 2. Therefore, this suggests that my substitution might have an issue?\n\nWait, this is a contradiction. Wait, no, actually, hold on. Wait, the left-hand side is \\( 2^{A \\cdot \\log_2(y z)} \\), which is indeed a power of 2, but the right-hand side is \\( 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\), which is not a power of 2. Therefore, this suggests that my approach must be wrong?\n\nWait, no, actually, that can't be. Wait, let me check again.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, the original equation is \\( x^{\\log_2(y z)} = 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\). But if \\( x \\) is a power of 2, say \\( x = 2^A \\), then \\( x^{\\log_2(y z)} = (2^A)^{\\log_2(y z)} = 2^{A \\cdot \\log_2(y z)} \\), which is a power of 2. However, the right-hand side is \\( 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\), which is not a power of 2, so this is impossible unless \\( 3^4 = 1 \\), which it isn't. Therefore, my substitution must be flawed?\n\nWait a second, this is a problem. That suggests that my substitution \\( x = 2^A \\), \\( y = 2^B \\), \\( z = 2^C \\) is invalid because if \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) are arbitrary positive real numbers, they don't have to be powers of 2. Wait, but actually, in my substitution, I set \\( A = \\log_2 x \\), so \\( x = 2^A \\), so that's okay, but then when I plug back into the equation, the left-hand side becomes a power of 2, but the right-hand side isn't, which is a contradiction unless the exponent on 2 is such that the 3^4 is somehow incorporated?\n\nWait, this must mean that my substitution is okay, but then the equations can only hold if the exponents on the left-hand sides can account for the 3's on the right-hand sides. But how?\n\nWait, actually, no. Wait a second, perhaps I made a mistake in the substitution. Let me double-check.\n\nThe left-hand side of Equation (1): \\( x^{\\log_2(y z)} \\). If \\( x = 2^A \\), then yes, substituting gives \\( (2^A)^{\\log_2(y z)} = 2^{A \\cdot \\log_2(y z)} \\). But \\( y \\) and \\( z \\) are not necessarily powers of 2, so \\( y z \\) is not necessarily a power of 2, so \\( \\log_2(y z) \\) is just some real number, so \\( A \\cdot \\log_2(y z) \\) is a real number, so the entire left-hand side is a power of 2 raised to that real number, which is still a positive real number, but not necessarily an integer power of 2. Wait, but the right-hand side is \\( 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\), which is not a power of 2, so how can they be equal?\n\nAh! Wait a second, this is impossible unless \\( x^{\\log_2(y z)} \\) can somehow result in a number with factors of 3. But \\( x \\) is a positive real number, but it doesn't have to be a power of 2. Wait, but in my substitution, I set \\( x = 2^A \\), which forces \\( x \\) to be a power of 2, but that's not necessary. Wait, I see the problem now.\n\nI incorrectly assumed that \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) can be written as \\( 2^A \\), \\( 2^B \\), \\( 2^C \\), but actually, they can be any positive real numbers, not necessarily powers of 2. Therefore, my substitution was flawed because I forced them to be powers of 2, which introduced the inconsistency.\n\nOkay, so scratch that approach. Let me start over.\n\nLet me instead consider taking logarithms of both sides of the original equations with base 2, as I initially did, but not substituting variables yet.\n\nOriginal Equation (1):\n\n\\( x^{\\log_2(y z)} = 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\)\n\nTake \\( \\log_2 \\) of both sides:\n\n\\( \\log_2 \\left( x^{\\log_2(y z)} \\right) = \\log_2 \\left( 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\right) \\)\n\nLeft side: \\( \\log_2(y z) \\cdot \\log_2 x \\)\n\nRight side: \\( 8 + 4 \\log_2 3 \\), as before.\n\nSo that gives:\n\n\\( \\log_2(y z) \\cdot \\log_2 x = 8 + 4 \\log_2 3 \\) … (1a)\n\nSimilarly, Equation (2):\n\n\\( \\log_2(z x) \\cdot \\log_2 y = 9 + 6 \\log_2 3 \\) … (2a)\n\nEquation (3):\n\n\\( \\log_2(x y) \\cdot \\log_2 z = 5 + 10 \\log_2 3 \\) … (3a)\n\nSo, perhaps instead of substituting \\( A = \\log_2 x \\), etc., let me denote:\n\nLet me set \\( u = \\log_2 x \\), \\( v = \\log_2 y \\), \\( w = \\log_2 z \\). So then, \\( x = 2^u \\), \\( y = 2^v \\), \\( z = 2^w \\). Then, \\( \\log_2(y z) = \\log_2 y + \\log_2 z = v + w \\), etc.\n\nSo substituting into Equation (1a):\n\n\\( (v + w) \\cdot u = 8 + 4 \\log_2 3 \\) … (1b)\n\nSimilarly, Equation (2a):\n\n\\( (w + u) \\cdot v = 9 + 6 \\log_2 3 \\) … (2b)\n\nEquation (3a):\n\n\\( (u + v) \\cdot w = 5 + 10 \\log_2 3 \\) … (3b)\n\nAh! So this is the same as before when I set \\( A = u \\), \\( B = v \\), \\( C = w \\). So, the substitution is okay, but the problem arises when I tried to express the left-hand side as a power of 2, but actually, in the original equation, the left-hand side is \\( x^{\\log_2(y z)} \\), which is \\( (2^u)^{\\log_2(y z)} = 2^{u \\cdot \\log_2(y z)} \\). However, \\( \\log_2(y z) = v + w \\), so indeed, this is \\( 2^{u(v + w)} \\), which equals the right-hand side \\( 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\). Wait, but \\( 2^{u(v + w)} \\) is a power of 2, but the right-hand side is not a pure power of 2. Therefore, this suggests that \\( 3^4 \\) must somehow be incorporated into the exponent? That seems impossible unless the exponent is not an integer? Wait, but even so, \\( 2^{\\text{something}} \\times 3^4 \\) can’t be expressed as a single power of 2 unless the 3^4 is 1, which it isn’t. Therefore, my substitution is forcing the left-hand side to be a power of 2, but the right-hand side isn’t, which is a contradiction unless my substitution is invalid.\n\nWait, but how is that possible? The original equation is \\( x^{\\log_2(y z)} = 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\). If \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) are arbitrary positive real numbers, then \\( x^{\\log_2(y z)} \\) can indeed be any positive real number, not necessarily a power of 2. But when I set \\( x = 2^u \\), \\( y = 2^v \\), \\( z = 2^w \\), then \\( x^{\\log_2(y z)} = 2^{u(v + w)} \\), which is a power of 2. But the right-hand side is not a power of 2, so this substitution is only valid if the right-hand side is also a power of 2, which it isn't. Therefore, this substitution is invalid because it restricts \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) to be powers of 2, which is not necessary and causes inconsistency.\n\nTherefore, my mistake was assuming that \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) can be written as powers of 2. They can be any positive real numbers, so that approach is flawed.\n\nHmm, so perhaps I need a different substitution or method.\n\nAlternative approach: Let me consider taking logarithms of both sides with a different base, or perhaps take natural logs and then express in terms of variables.\n\nLet me try taking natural logarithm on both sides of each equation.\n\nStarting with Equation (1):\n\n\\( \\ln \\left( x^{\\log_2(y z)} \\right) = \\ln \\left( 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\right) \\)\n\nLeft side: \\( \\log_2(y z) \\cdot \\ln x \\)\n\nRight side: \\( 8 \\ln 2 + 4 \\ln 3 \\)\n\nSimilarly, \\( \\log_2(y z) = \\frac{\\ln(y z)}{\\ln 2} = \\frac{\\ln y + \\ln z}{\\ln 2} \\), so substituting:\n\nLeft side becomes:\n\n\\( \\frac{\\ln y + \\ln z}{\\ln 2} \\cdot \\ln x \\)\n\nTherefore, Equation (1) becomes:\n\n\\( \\frac{(\\ln y + \\ln z) \\cdot \\ln x}{\\ln 2} = 8 \\ln 2 + 4 \\ln 3 \\)\n\nSimilarly, for Equation (2):\n\n\\( \\frac{(\\ln z + \\ln x) \\cdot \\ln y}{\\ln 2} = 9 \\ln 2 + 6 \\ln 3 \\)\n\nEquation (3):\n\n\\( \\frac{(\\ln x + \\ln y) \\cdot \\ln z}{\\ln 2} = 5 \\ln 2 + 10 \\ln 3 \\)\n\nHmm, so now we have three equations with variables \\( \\ln x \\), \\( \\ln y \\), \\( \\ln z \\). Let me denote \\( a = \\ln x \\), \\( b = \\ln y \\), \\( c = \\ln z \\). Then, the equations become:\n\nEquation (1): \\( \\frac{(b + c) a}{\\ln 2} = 8 \\ln 2 + 4 \\ln 3 \\) … (1c)\n\nEquation (2): \\( \\frac{(c + a) b}{\\ln 2} = 9 \\ln 2 + 6 \\ln 3 \\) … (2c)\n\nEquation (3): \\( \\frac{(a + b) c}{\\ln 2} = 5 \\ln 2 + 10 \\ln 3 \\) … (3c)\n\nHmm, so now we have three equations with three variables \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\), and constants involving \\( \\ln 2 \\) and \\( \\ln 3 \\). Let me denote \\( k = \\ln 3 \\), and \\( m = \\ln 2 \\), so that the equations can be written as:\n\nEquation (1): \\( \\frac{(b + c) a}{m} = 8 m + 4 k \\) … (1d)\n\nEquation (2): \\( \\frac{(c + a) b}{m} = 9 m + 6 k \\) … (2d)\n\nEquation (3): \\( \\frac{(a + b) c}{m} = 5 m + 10 k \\) … (3d)\n\nMultiplying both sides of each equation by \\( m \\):\n\nEquation (1d): \\( (b + c) a = m(8 m + 4 k) = 8 m^2 + 4 m k \\) … (1e)\n\nEquation (2d): \\( (c + a) b = m(9 m + 6 k) = 9 m^2 + 6 m k \\) … (2e)\n\nEquation (3d): \\( (a + b) c = m(5 m + 10 k) = 5 m^2 + 10 m k \\) … (3e)\n\nHmm, so now, we have three equations:\n\n1. \\( a(b + c) = 8 m^2 + 4 m k \\)\n2. \\( b(c + a) = 9 m^2 + 6 m k \\)\n3. \\( c(a + b) = 5 m^2 + 10 m k \\)\n\nWhere \\( m = \\ln 2 \\), \\( k = \\ln 3 \\).\n\nThis still looks quite complicated, but maybe we can find ratios between these equations?\n\nLet me denote the right-hand sides as follows:\n\nLet me call \\( R_1 = 8 m^2 + 4 m k \\)\n\n\\( R_2 = 9 m^2 + 6 m k \\)\n\n\\( R_3 = 5 m^2 + 10 m k \\)\n\nSo:\n\n1. \\( a(b + c) = R_1 \\) … (1f)\n2. \\( b(c + a) = R_2 \\) … (2f)\n3. \\( c(a + b) = R_3 \\) … (3f)\n\nPerhaps if I divide Equation (2f) by Equation (1f):\n\n\\( \\frac{b(c + a)}{a(b + c)} = \\frac{R_2}{R_1} \\)\n\nSimilarly, Equation (3f) divided by Equation (2f):\n\n\\( \\frac{c(a + b)}{b(c + a)} = \\frac{R_3}{R_2} \\)\n\nAnd Equation (3f) divided by Equation (1f):\n\n\\( \\frac{c(a + b)}{a(b + c)} = \\frac{R_3}{R_1} \\)\n\nHmm, but this might not lead directly to something useful, but let me try with the first ratio.\n\nLet me compute \\( \\frac{R_2}{R_1} \\):\n\n\\( \\frac{R_2}{R_1} = \\frac{9 m^2 + 6 m k}{8 m^2 + 4 m k} \\)\n\nFactor numerator and denominator:\n\nNumerator: \\( 3m(3m + 2k) \\)\n\nDenominator: \\( 4m(2m + k) \\)\n\nWait:\n\nWait, 9m² + 6mk = 3m(3m + 2k)\n\n8m² + 4mk = 4m(2m + k)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( \\frac{R_2}{R_1} = \\frac{3m(3m + 2k)}{4m(2m + k)} = \\frac{3(3m + 2k)}{4(2m + k)} \\)\n\nSimilarly, \\( \\frac{R_3}{R_2} = \\frac{5 m^2 + 10 m k}{9 m^2 + 6 m k} = \\frac{5m(m + 2k)}{3m(3m + 2k)} = \\frac{5(m + 2k)}{3(3m + 2k)} \\)\n\nAnd \\( \\frac{R_3}{R_1} = \\frac{5 m^2 + 10 m k}{8 m^2 + 4 m k} = \\frac{5m(m + 2k)}{4m(2m + k)} = \\frac{5(m + 2k)}{4(2m + k)} \\)\n\nHmm, so the ratios involve \\( m \\) and \\( k \\), which are constants (\\( \\ln 2 \\) and \\( \\ln 3 \\)), so perhaps we can let \\( t = \\frac{k}{m} = \\frac{\\ln 3}{\\ln 2} \\), which is just \\( \\log_2 3 \\), which is the constant \\( k \\) from before. Since \\( k = \\log_2 3 \\), so \\( t = k \\). Therefore, \\( k = t m \\), since \\( m = \\ln 2 \\), \\( k = \\log_2 3 = \\frac{\\ln 3}{\\ln 2} = \\frac{k}{m} \\cdot m = t m \\), so \\( k = t m \\).\n\nTherefore, substituting \\( k = t m \\), then expressions become:\n\nLet me substitute \\( k = t m \\):\n\nFirst, \\( R_1 = 8 m^2 + 4 m k = 8 m^2 + 4 m (t m) = 8 m^2 + 4 t m^2 = m^2 (8 + 4 t) \\)\n\nSimilarly,\n\n\\( R_2 = 9 m^2 + 6 m k = 9 m^2 + 6 m (t m) = 9 m^2 + 6 t m^2 = m^2 (9 + 6 t) \\)\n\n\\( R_3 = 5 m^2 + 10 m k = 5 m^2 + 10 m (t m) = 5 m^2 + 10 t m^2 = m^2 (5 + 10 t) \\)\n\nTherefore, the ratios become:\n\n\\( \\frac{R_2}{R_1} = \\frac{m^2 (9 + 6 t)}{m^2 (8 + 4 t)} = \\frac{9 + 6 t}{8 + 4 t} \\)\n\nSimilarly,\n\n\\( \\frac{R_3}{R_2} = \\frac{m^2 (5 + 10 t)}{m^2 (9 + 6 t)} = \\frac{5 + 10 t}{9 + 6 t} \\)\n\n\\( \\frac{R_3}{R_1} = \\frac{m^2 (5 + 10 t)}{m^2 (8 + 4 t)} = \\frac{5 + 10 t}{8 + 4 t} \\)\n\nSo, now, the ratios are expressed purely in terms of \\( t = \\log_2 3 \\), which is a constant. Therefore, we can write the ratios as:\n\n\\( \\frac{R_2}{R_1} = \\frac{9 + 6 t}{8 + 4 t} \\)\n\n\\( \\frac{R_3}{R_2} = \\frac{5 + 10 t}{9 + 6 t} \\)\n\n\\( \\frac{R_3}{R_1} = \\frac{5 + 10 t}{8 + 4 t} \\)\n\nNow, going back to the earlier ratio of equations:\n\nFrom Equations (2f)/(1f):\n\n\\( \\frac{b(c + a)}{a(b + c)} = \\frac{R_2}{R_1} = \\frac{9 + 6 t}{8 + 4 t} \\)\n\nLet me denote this ratio as \\( \\frac{R_2}{R_1} \\), which we have above.\n\nLet me rearrange the left-hand side:\n\n\\( \\frac{b(c + a)}{a(b + c)} = \\frac{b}{a} \\cdot \\frac{c + a}{b + c} \\)\n\nHmm, not sure if that helps. Alternatively, let me set variables in terms of ratios. Let me define:\n\nLet \\( p = \\frac{a}{b} \\), \\( q = \\frac{b}{c} \\), \\( r = \\frac{c}{a} \\), but this might complicate things further.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider variables \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\), and set up the equations as a system.\n\nLet me write the three equations again:\n\n1. \\( a(b + c) = R_1 = m^2 (8 + 4 t) \\) … (1f)\n\n2. \\( b(c + a) = R_2 = m^2 (9 + 6 t) \\) … (2f)\n\n3. \\( c(a + b) = R_3 = m^2 (5 + 10 t) \\) … (3f)\n\nLet me denote \\( S = a + b + c \\). Then, note that \\( b + c = S - a \\), \\( c + a = S - b \\), \\( a + b = S - c \\). Therefore, substituting into the equations:\n\nEquation (1f): \\( a(S - a) = R_1 \\)\n\nEquation (2f): \\( b(S - b) = R_2 \\)\n\nEquation (3f): \\( c(S - c) = R_3 \\)\n\nSo now, similar to before, each variable satisfies a quadratic equation in terms of \\( S \\):\n\nFor \\( a \\): \\( a^2 - S a + R_1 = 0 \\)\n\nFor \\( b \\): \\( b^2 - S b + R_2 = 0 \\)\n\nFor \\( c \\): \\( c^2 - S c + R_3 = 0 \\)\n\nTherefore, each variable \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) is a root of its respective quadratic equation with coefficients depending on \\( S \\). Therefore, similar to before, each variable can be expressed via the quadratic formula:\n\n\\( a = \\frac{S \\pm \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_1}}{2} \\)\n\n\\( b = \\frac{S \\pm \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_2}}{2} \\)\n\n\\( c = \\frac{S \\pm \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_3}}{2} \\)\n\nNow, since \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are real numbers, the discriminants must be non-negative:\n\n\\( S^2 \\geq 4 R_1 \\)\n\n\\( S^2 \\geq 4 R_2 \\)\n\n\\( S^2 \\geq 4 R_3 \\)\n\nAlso, since \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are real and presumably positive (since \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) are positive reals, so their logarithms \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) can be any real numbers, but if we want \\( x y z \\) to be minimized, perhaps we can assume \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are positive? Not sure, but maybe.)\n\nAssuming \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are positive, then \\( S = a + b + c \\) is positive. So \\( S > 0 \\).\n\nAdditionally, since each quadratic equation for \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) has two roots, we need to choose the roots such that they are consistent with each other and sum to \\( S \\).\n\nThis seems quite involved, but perhaps we can assume that all variables take the same sign in their roots. For instance, perhaps all variables take the \"+\" root? Let me try that.\n\nAssume that:\n\n\\( a = \\frac{S + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_1}}{2} \\)\n\n\\( b = \\frac{S + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_2}}{2} \\)\n\n\\( c = \\frac{S + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_3}}{2} \\)\n\nThen, the sum \\( S = a + b + c \\) becomes:\n\n\\( S = \\frac{S + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_1}}{2} + \\frac{S + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_2}}{2} + \\frac{S + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_3}}{2} \\)\n\nMultiply both sides by 2:\n\n\\( 2S = [S + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_1}] + [S + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_2}] + [S + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_3}] \\)\n\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\n\\( 2S = 3S + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_1} + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_2} + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_3} \\)\n\nSubtract \\( 3S \\):\n\n\\( -S = \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_1} + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_2} + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_3} \\)\n\nHmm, similar to before, the left side is \\( -S \\), which is negative (since \\( S > 0 \\)), but the right side is a sum of square roots, which are non-negative. Therefore, this equation implies that a negative number equals a non-negative number, which is impossible. Therefore, this assumption is invalid.\n\nTherefore, perhaps some of the variables take the \"+\" root and others the \"-\" root? Let me consider that possibility.\n\nSuppose that two variables take the \"+\" root and one takes the \"-\" root. Let me try different combinations.\n\nCase 1: \\( a = \\frac{S + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_1}}{2} \\), \\( b = \\frac{S + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_2}}{2} \\), \\( c = \\frac{S - \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_3}}{2} \\)\n\nThen, the sum \\( S = a + b + c \\):\n\n\\( S = \\frac{S + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_1}}{2} + \\frac{S + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_2}}{2} + \\frac{S - \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_3}}{2} \\)\n\nMultiply by 2:\n\n\\( 2S = [S + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_1}] + [S + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_2}] + [S - \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_3}] \\)\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\( 2S = 3S + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_1} + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_2} - \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_3} \\)\n\nRearranged:\n\n\\( -S = \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_1} + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_2} - \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_3} \\)\n\nThis still has a mixture of positive and negative terms, but it's unclear if this can balance out to give \\( -S \\). This seems messy.\n\nAlternatively, maybe one variable takes the \"+\" root and the other two take the \"-\" roots? Let me try that.\n\nCase 2: \\( a = \\frac{S + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_1}}{2} \\), \\( b = \\frac{S - \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_2}}{2} \\), \\( c = \\frac{S - \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_3}}{2} \\)\n\nThen, sum \\( S = a + b + c \\):\n\n\\( S = \\frac{S + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_1}}{2} + \\frac{S - \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_2}}{2} + \\frac{S - \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_3}}{2} \\)\n\nMultiply by 2:\n\n\\( 2S = [S + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_1}] + [S - \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_2}] + [S - \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_3}] \\)\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\( 2S = 3S + \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_1} - \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_2} - \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_3} \\)\n\nRearranged:\n\n\\( -S = \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_1} - \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_2} - \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_3} \\)\n\nAgain, the left side is negative, and the right side is \\( \\sqrt{S^2 - 4 R_1} \\) minus the sum of two square roots. Depending on the values of \\( R_1 \\), \\( R_2 \\), \\( R_3 \\), this could potentially be negative, but it's not obvious.\n\nThis trial and error approach is not productive. Maybe there is a smarter way.\n\nLet me recall that in the original equations, the right-hand sides are multiples of \\( 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\), \\( 2^9 \\cdot 3^6 \\), \\( 2^5 \\cdot 3^{10} \\). Notice that the exponents of 2 and 3 in each equation have a relationship. For instance, in Equation (1), the exponent of 2 is 8 and of 3 is 4; in Equation (2), exponents are 9 and 6, which is 1.5 times as much; in Equation (3), exponents are 5 and 10, which is also double the exponent of 3 compared to Equation (1), but half the exponent of 2.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the exponents of 2 and 3 in each equation are proportional to some common factors?\n\nAlternatively, maybe taking the product of all three equations?\n\nLet me try multiplying all three original equations together:\n\nLeft-hand side:\n\n\\( x^{\\log_2(y z)} \\cdot y^{\\log_2(z x)} \\cdot z^{\\log_2(x y)} \\)\n\nRight-hand side:\n\n\\( (2^8 \\cdot 3^4) \\cdot (2^9 \\cdot 3^6) \\cdot (2^5 \\cdot 3^{10}) = 2^{8+9+5} \\cdot 3^{4+6+10} = 2^{22} \\cdot 3^{20} \\)\n\nSo, the product of the left-hand sides equals \\( 2^{22} \\cdot 3^{20} \\).\n\nNow, let me see if I can simplify the left-hand side:\n\nLeft-hand side:\n\n\\( x^{\\log_2(y z)} \\cdot y^{\\log_2(z x)} \\cdot z^{\\log_2(x y)} \\)\n\nLet me take the logarithm (base 2) of the left-hand side to see if that helps.\n\nLet me denote \\( L = \\log_2 \\left( x^{\\log_2(y z)} \\cdot y^{\\log_2(z x)} \\cdot z^{\\log_2(x y)} \\right) \\)\n\nThen,\n\n\\( L = \\log_2 \\left( x^{\\log_2(y z)} \\right) + \\log_2 \\left( y^{\\log_2(z x)} \\right) + \\log_2 \\left( z^{\\log_2(x y)} \\right) \\)\n\nUsing logarithm power rule:\n\n\\( L = \\log_2(y z) \\cdot \\log_2 x + \\log_2(z x) \\cdot \\log_2 y + \\log_2(x y) \\cdot \\log_2 z \\)\n\nWhich is exactly the left-hand sides of the equations (1a), (2a), (3a) summed together!\n\nFrom the original equations:\n\nEquation (1a): \\( \\log_2(y z) \\cdot \\log_2 x = 8 + 4 \\log_2 3 \\)\n\nEquation (2a): \\( \\log_2(z x) \\cdot \\log_2 y = 9 + 6 \\log_2 3 \\)\n\nEquation (3a): \\( \\log_2(x y) \\cdot \\log_2 z = 5 + 10 \\log_2 3 \\)\n\nTherefore, summing these:\n\n\\( L = (8 + 4 \\log_2 3) + (9 + 6 \\log_2 3) + (5 + 10 \\log_2 3) = (8 + 9 + 5) + (4 + 6 + 10)\\log_2 3 = 22 + 20 \\log_2 3 \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( L = \\log_2 (\\text{left-hand side}) = 22 + 20 \\log_2 3 \\)\n\nTherefore, the left-hand side of the product equation is:\n\n\\( 2^{22 + 20 \\log_2 3} \\)\n\nBut the right-hand side of the product equation is \\( 2^{22} \\cdot 3^{20} \\). Let me see if these are equal?\n\nCompute \\( 2^{22 + 20 \\log_2 3} = 2^{22} \\cdot 2^{20 \\log_2 3} = 2^{22} \\cdot (2^{\\log_2 3})^{20} = 2^{22} \\cdot 3^{20} \\)\n\nYes! Because \\( 2^{\\log_2 3} = 3 \\), so \\( 2^{20 \\log_2 3} = 3^{20} \\). Therefore, indeed, both sides are equal. So this doesn't give us new information; it's just an identity resulting from multiplying the three equations. Therefore, this approach doesn't help us find \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\).\n\nHmm. Back to the drawing board.\n\nLet me consider that in the original equations, each equation has a structure similar to \\( X^{\\log_2(YZ)} = \\text{something} \\). Maybe if I let \\( u = \\log_2 x \\), \\( v = \\log_2 y \\), \\( w = \\log_2 z \\), then we can write the equations in terms of \\( u \\), \\( v \\), \\( w \\), and maybe find a relationship between \\( u \\), \\( v \\), \\( w \\).\n\nWait, that's what I did earlier, but ran into the problem that the left-hand side became a power of 2, while the right-hand side wasn't. However, perhaps I need to keep in mind that \\( \\log_2(y z) \\) is not necessarily an integer, so the exponent on the left-hand side is a real number, so the left-hand side is \\( 2^{u \\cdot \\log_2(y z)} \\), but \\( y z = 2^{v + w} \\), so \\( \\log_2(y z) = v + w \\), so then the left-hand side is \\( 2^{u(v + w)} \\), which is \\( (2^u)^{v + w} = x^{v + w} \\), but the original left-hand side is \\( x^{\\log_2(y z)} = x^{v + w} \\), so that's consistent. Wait, but then the right-hand side is \\( 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\), so this is saying that \\( x^{v + w} = 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\). Similarly for the others.\n\nWait a second, perhaps this is the key. Let me write each equation in terms of the variables \\( u = \\log_2 x \\), \\( v = \\log_2 y \\), \\( w = \\log_2 z \\), then:\n\nEquation (1): \\( x^{v + w} = 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\)\n\nBut \\( x = 2^u \\), so substituting:\n\n\\( (2^u)^{v + w} = 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\)\n\nWhich simplifies to:\n\n\\( 2^{u(v + w)} = 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\)\n\nSimilarly, Equation (2): \\( y^{w + u} = 2^9 \\cdot 3^6 \\)\n\nSubstituting \\( y = 2^v \\):\n\n\\( (2^v)^{w + u} = 2^9 \\cdot 3^6 \\)\n\nWhich is:\n\n\\( 2^{v(w + u)} = 2^9 \\cdot 3^6 \\)\n\nEquation (3): \\( z^{u + v} = 2^5 \\cdot 3^{10} \\)\n\nSubstituting \\( z = 2^w \\):\n\n\\( (2^w)^{u + v} = 2^5 \\cdot 3^{10} \\)\n\nWhich becomes:\n\n\\( 2^{w(u + v)} = 2^5 \\cdot 3^{10} \\)\n\nTherefore, each equation is of the form:\n\n\\( 2^{(variable \\cdot expression)} = 2^{\\text{exponent}} \\cdot 3^{\\text{another exponent}} \\)\n\nBut the left-hand side is a power of 2, while the right-hand side has factors of 2 and 3. This can only be true if the exponent on the left-hand side accounts for both the power of 2 and the power of 3 on the right-hand side. But how?\n\nAh! Wait a second. The left-hand side is a power of 2, but the right-hand side has a factor of 3. Therefore, unless the exponent on the left-hand side is such that when expressed in terms of logarithms, it can incorporate the 3's? Wait, no. The left-hand side is strictly a power of 2, so the right-hand side must also be a power of 2. But it isn't. Therefore, this suggests that my substitution \\( x = 2^u \\), etc., is insufficient because it forces the left-hand side to be a power of 2, but the right-hand side isn't. Therefore, this substitution is invalid unless the 3's can be incorporated through the exponents?\n\nWait, but how?\n\nWait, actually, no. The substitution \\( x = 2^u \\), \\( y = 2^v \\), \\( z = 2^w \\) is just a way to express \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) in terms of logarithms with base 2, but it doesn't restrict \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) to be powers of 2. Wait, actually, it does! Because if you set \\( u = \\log_2 x \\), then by definition, \\( x = 2^u \\). So actually, this substitution does force \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) to be powers of 2, which is a restriction not present in the original problem. Therefore, this substitution is not valid for solving the problem because it imposes an unnecessary restriction on \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\).\n\nTherefore, I need to abandon this substitution and try a different approach.\n\nAlternative Idea:\n\nLet me consider taking the three original equations and express them in terms of exponents with base 2 and 3. Since the right-hand sides are products of powers of 2 and 3, perhaps I can write the left-hand sides similarly.\n\nLet me denote the original equations again:\n\n1) \\( x^{\\log_2(y z)} = 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\)\n\n2) \\( y^{\\log_2(z x)} = 2^9 \\cdot 3^6 \\)\n\n3) \\( z^{\\log_2(x y)} = 2^5 \\cdot 3^{10} \\)\n\nLet me take the logarithm of both sides of each equation with base 2 and base 3 separately? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, let me take the logarithm of both sides with base 2 and base 3 and see if I can form a system.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider taking the logarithm of both sides with base 2 and then base 3.\n\nWait, perhaps if I let \\( \\log_2 (x^{\\log_2(y z)}) = \\log_2 (2^8 \\cdot 3^4) \\), which we did before, but that led to an equation involving \\( \\log_2 x \\cdot \\log_2(y z) \\).\n\nAlternatively, let me define variables for the logs. Let me let:\n\nLet \\( a = \\log_2 x \\), \\( b = \\log_2 y \\), \\( c = \\log_2 z \\). Then, \\( \\log_2(y z) = b + c \\), as before.\n\nThen, Equation (1) becomes:\n\n\\( x^{b + c} = 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\)\n\nBut \\( x = 2^a \\), so substituting:\n\n\\( (2^a)^{b + c} = 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\)\n\nWhich is \\( 2^{a(b + c)} = 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\)\n\nSimilarly, Equation (2):\n\n\\( y^{c + a} = 2^9 \\cdot 3^6 \\)\n\n\\( (2^b)^{c + a} = 2^9 \\cdot 3^6 \\Rightarrow 2^{b(c + a)} = 2^9 \\cdot 3^6 \\)\n\nEquation (3):\n\n\\( z^{a + b} = 2^5 \\cdot 3^{10} \\)\n\n\\( (2^c)^{a + b} = 2^5 \\cdot 3^{10} \\Rightarrow 2^{c(a + b)} = 2^5 \\cdot 3^{10} \\)\n\nSo now, we have three equations:\n\n1. \\( 2^{a(b + c)} = 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\)\n\n2. \\( 2^{b(c + a)} = 2^9 \\cdot 3^6 \\)\n\n3. \\( 2^{c(a + b)} = 2^5 \\cdot 3^{10} \\)\n\nNow, since the left-hand sides are powers of 2 and the right-hand sides are not, this suggests that the exponents on the left must somehow account for the 3's on the right. This can only happen if the exponents are not integers, but the right-hand sides have integer exponents. Wait, but the left-hand sides are powers of 2, so their prime factorization only includes 2's. Therefore, the only way these equations can hold is if the exponents on the left-hand sides are such that when expressed in terms of exponents of 2 and 3, they match the right-hand sides. However, since the left-hand sides are pure powers of 2, this requires that the 3's on the right-hand sides must somehow be incorporated into the exponents, which is impossible unless the exponents on the left-hand sides are not integers, but the right-hand sides have integer exponents. Wait, but this seems contradictory.\n\nWait a second, this suggests that there is no solution unless the 3's can be expressed as powers of 2, which they can't. Therefore, this implies that my substitution is forcing the variables into a form that makes the equations inconsistent, which means that my substitution is invalid? But how?\n\nWait no, actually, the substitution is just expressing x, y, z in terms of exponents with base 2, but x, y, z themselves can have factors of 3 in them. Wait a second! Hold on. If x, y, z are allowed to have factors of 3, then taking \\( x = 2^a \\) is not the only possibility. In fact, x can be written as \\( x = 2^{a} \\cdot 3^{d} \\), where \\( a \\) and \\( d \\) are real numbers. Similarly for y and z. Therefore, my mistake was assuming that \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) are powers of 2 only. They can have factors of 3 as well.\n\nAh! This is the key. I need to represent x, y, z in terms of both base 2 and base 3 exponents. Let me try that.\n\nLet me redefine the substitution:\n\nLet me set:\n\n\\( x = 2^{a} \\cdot 3^{d} \\)\n\n\\( y = 2^{b} \\cdot 3^{e} \\)\n\n\\( z = 2^{c} \\cdot 3^{f} \\)\n\nWhere \\( a, b, c, d, e, f \\) are real numbers to be determined.\n\nThen, \\( \\log_2 x = a + d \\cdot \\log_2 3 \\), since \\( \\log_2 (2^{a} \\cdot 3^{d}) = a + d \\log_2 3 \\).\n\nSimilarly,\n\n\\( \\log_2 y = b + e \\log_2 3 \\)\n\n\\( \\log_2 z = c + f \\log_2 3 \\)\n\nLet me denote \\( k = \\log_2 3 \\), which is a constant (approximately 1.58496). Then,\n\n\\( \\log_2 x = a + k d \\)\n\n\\( \\log_2 y = b + k e \\)\n\n\\( \\log_2 z = c + k f \\)\n\nNow, let's rewrite the original equations in terms of these variables.\n\nFirst, Equation (1):\n\n\\( x^{\\log_2(y z)} = 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\)\n\nLet me compute \\( \\log_2(y z) \\):\n\n\\( \\log_2(y z) = \\log_2 y + \\log_2 z = [b + k e] + [c + k f] = b + c + k(e + f) \\)\n\nTherefore, the left-hand side of Equation (1) is:\n\n\\( x^{\\log_2(y z)} = \\left( 2^{a} \\cdot 3^{d} \\right)^{b + c + k(e + f)} \\)\n\nUsing exponentiation rules:\n\n\\( = 2^{a \\cdot (b + c + k(e + f))} \\cdot 3^{d \\cdot (b + c + k(e + f))} \\)\n\nThe right-hand side of Equation (1) is:\n\n\\( 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\)\n\nTherefore, equating the exponents of 2 and 3 on both sides:\n\nFor the exponent of 2:\n\n\\( a(b + c + k(e + f)) = 8 \\) … (1g)\n\nFor the exponent of 3:\n\n\\( d(b + c + k(e + f)) = 4 \\) … (1h)\n\nSimilarly, let's do the same for Equation (2):\n\n\\( y^{\\log_2(z x)} = 2^9 \\cdot 3^6 \\)\n\nFirst, compute \\( \\log_2(z x) \\):\n\n\\( \\log_2(z x) = \\log_2 z + \\log_2 x = [c + k f] + [a + k d] = a + c + k(d + f) \\)\n\nLeft-hand side:\n\n\\( y^{\\log_2(z x)} = \\left( 2^{b} \\cdot 3^{e} \\right)^{a + c + k(d + f)} \\)\n\nWhich is:\n\n\\( 2^{b \\cdot (a + c + k(d + f))} \\cdot 3^{e \\cdot (a + c + k(d + f))} \\)\n\nRight-hand side:\n\n\\( 2^9 \\cdot 3^6 \\)\n\nEquating exponents:\n\nFor 2:\n\n\\( b(a + c + k(d + f)) = 9 \\) … (2g)\n\nFor 3:\n\n\\( e(a + c + k(d + f)) = 6 \\) … (2h)\n\nSimilarly, Equation (3):\n\n\\( z^{\\log_2(x y)} = 2^5 \\cdot 3^{10} \\)\n\nCompute \\( \\log_2(x y) \\):\n\n\\( \\log_2(x y) = \\log_2 x + \\log_2 y = [a + k d] + [b + k e] = a + b + k(d + e) \\)\n\nLeft-hand side:\n\n\\( z^{\\log_2(x y)} = \\left( 2^{c} \\cdot 3^{f} \\right)^{a + b + k(d + e)} \\)\n\nWhich is:\n\n\\( 2^{c \\cdot (a + b + k(d + e))} \\cdot 3^{f \\cdot (a + b + k(d + e))} \\)\n\nRight-hand side:\n\n\\( 2^5 \\cdot 3^{10} \\)\n\nEquating exponents:\n\nFor 2:\n\n\\( c(a + b + k(d + e)) = 5 \\) … (3g)\n\nFor 3:\n\n\\( f(a + b + k(d + e)) = 10 \\) … (3h)\n\nSo now, we have six equations:\n\n(1g): \\( a(b + c + k(e + f)) = 8 \\)\n\n(1h): \\( d(b + c + k(e + f)) = 4 \\)\n\n(2g): \\( b(a + c + k(d + f)) = 9 \\)\n\n(2h): \\( e(a + c + k(d + f)) = 6 \\)\n\n(3g): \\( c(a + b + k(d + e)) = 5 \\)\n\n(3h): \\( f(a + b + k(d + e)) = 10 \\)\n\nThis seems even more complicated than before, but perhaps we can find ratios or relationships between the variables.\n\nNotice that equations (1g) and (1h) both involve the term \\( (b + c + k(e + f)) \\). Let me denote:\n\nLet me set:\n\nLet \\( M = b + c + k(e + f) \\)\n\nThen Equation (1g) becomes: \\( a M = 8 \\) ⇒ \\( a = 8 / M \\)\n\nEquation (1h): \\( d M = 4 \\) ⇒ \\( d = 4 / M \\)\n\nSimilarly, in Equation (2g) and (2h), let me define:\n\nLet \\( N = a + c + k(d + f) \\)\n\nThen Equation (2g): \\( b N = 9 \\) ⇒ \\( b = 9 / N \\)\n\nEquation (2h): \\( e N = 6 \\) ⇒ \\( e = 6 / N \\)\n\nSimilarly, in Equation (3g) and (3h):\n\nLet \\( P = a + b + k(d + e) \\)\n\nThen Equation (3g): \\( c P = 5 \\) ⇒ \\( c = 5 / P \\)\n\nEquation (3h): \\( f P = 10 \\) ⇒ \\( f = 10 / P \\)\n\nTherefore, now we have expressions for a, b, c, d, e, f in terms of M, N, P. Now, our goal is to find relationships between M, N, P.\n\nLet me write down all the expressions:\n\n\\( a = \\frac{8}{M} \\)\n\n\\( d = \\frac{4}{M} \\)\n\n\\( b = \\frac{9}{N} \\)\n\n\\( e = \\frac{6}{N} \\)\n\n\\( c = \\frac{5}{P} \\)\n\n\\( f = \\frac{10}{P} \\)\n\nNow, recall that M, N, P are defined as:\n\n\\( M = b + c + k(e + f) \\)\n\n\\( N = a + c + k(d + f) \\)\n\n\\( P = a + b + k(d + e) \\)\n\nNow, substitute the expressions for a, b, c, d, e, f in terms of M, N, P into these definitions.\n\nStarting with M:\n\n\\( M = b + c + k(e + f) \\)\n\nSubstitute b = 9/N, c = 5/P, e = 6/N, f = 10/P:\n\n\\( M = \\frac{9}{N} + \\frac{5}{P} + k\\left( \\frac{6}{N} + \\frac{10}{P} \\right) \\)\n\nSimilarly, N:\n\n\\( N = a + c + k(d + f) \\)\n\nSubstitute a = 8/M, c = 5/P, d = 4/M, f = 10/P:\n\n\\( N = \\frac{8}{M} + \\frac{5}{P} + k\\left( \\frac{4}{M} + \\frac{10}{P} \\right) \\)\n\nSimilarly, P:\n\n\\( P = a + b + k(d + e) \\)\n\nSubstitute a = 8/M, b = 9/N, d = 4/M, e = 6/N:\n\n\\( P = \\frac{8}{M} + \\frac{9}{N} + k\\left( \\frac{4}{M} + \\frac{6}{N} \\right) \\)\n\nNow, we have three equations involving M, N, P:\n\n1. \\( M = \\frac{9}{N} + \\frac{5}{P} + k\\left( \\frac{6}{N} + \\frac{10}{P} \\right) \\) … (M)\n\n2. \\( N = \\frac{8}{M} + \\frac{5}{P} + k\\left( \\frac{4}{M} + \\frac{10}{P} \\right) \\) … (N)\n\n3. \\( P = \\frac{8}{M} + \\frac{9}{N} + k\\left( \\frac{4}{M} + \\frac{6}{N} \\right) \\) … (P)\n\nThis system of equations is still quite complex, but perhaps we can find a substitution or assume some proportional relationships between M, N, and P.\n\nAlternatively, notice that the right-hand sides of the original equations have exponents that are multiples of each other. For example, Equation (1) has 8 and 4, which is a ratio of 2:1; Equation (2) has 9 and 6, which is 3:2; Equation (3) has 5 and 10, which is 1:2. Maybe there is a proportional relationship between the variables a, b, c, d, e, f that can be exploited?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps assume that the variables a, b, c, d, e, f are proportional to certain constants. For instance, maybe d = 2a, e = 3b, f = 5c or something like that? But this is just a guess.\n\nAlternatively, notice that in Equation (1h): d M = 4, and in Equation (1g): a M = 8. So, d = (4/M) and a = (8/M), so d = (1/2) a.\n\nSimilarly, in Equation (2h): e N = 6, and Equation (2g): b N = 9 ⇒ e = (6/N), b = (9/N), so e = (2/3) b.\n\nIn Equation (3h): f P = 10, and Equation (3g): c P = 5 ⇒ f = 2c.\n\nSo we have:\n\nd = (1/2) a\n\ne = (2/3) b\n\nf = 2 c\n\nThis is a useful relationship! Let me write these down:\n\n1. \\( d = \\frac{1}{2} a \\) … (D)\n\n2. \\( e = \\frac{2}{3} b \\) … (E)\n\n3. \\( f = 2 c \\) … (F)\n\nThis reduces the number of variables from six (a, b, c, d, e, f) to three (a, b, c), since d, e, f can be expressed in terms of a, b, c.\n\nNow, let's substitute these into the equations for M, N, P.\n\nFirst, recall that:\n\n\\( M = b + c + k(e + f) \\)\n\nSubstitute e = (2/3)b and f = 2c:\n\n\\( M = b + c + k\\left( \\frac{2}{3} b + 2 c \\right) = b + c + k \\left( \\frac{2}{3} b + 2 c \\right) \\)\n\nSimilarly,\n\n\\( N = a + c + k(d + f) \\)\n\nSubstitute d = (1/2)a and f = 2c:\n\n\\( N = a + c + k\\left( \\frac{1}{2}a + 2 c \\right) = a + c + k \\left( \\frac{1}{2}a + 2 c \\right) \\)\n\nAnd,\n\n\\( P = a + b + k(d + e) \\)\n\nSubstitute d = (1/2)a and e = (2/3)b:\n\n\\( P = a + b + k\\left( \\frac{1}{2}a + \\frac{2}{3}b \\right) \\)\n\nSo now, M, N, P are expressed in terms of a, b, c.\n\nAdditionally, recall that:\n\nFrom the expressions above,\n\n\\( a = 8 / M \\)\n\n\\( b = 9 / N \\)\n\n\\( c = 5 / P \\)\n\nTherefore, we can write:\n\n\\( a = 8 / M = 8 / [b + c + k( (2/3)b + 2c )] \\)\n\nSimilarly,\n\n\\( b = 9 / N = 9 / [a + c + k( (1/2)a + 2c )] \\)\n\n\\( c = 5 / P = 5 / [a + b + k( (1/2)a + (2/3)b )] \\)\n\nThis is still quite complex, but perhaps we can substitute recursively.\n\nLet me denote the expressions for M, N, P in terms of a, b, c as follows:\n\nLet me write:\n\nLet me express M in terms of b and c:\n\n\\( M = b + c + k \\left( \\frac{2}{3} b + 2 c \\right) \\)\n\nSimilarly,\n\n\\( N = a + c + k \\left( \\frac{1}{2} a + 2 c \\right) \\)\n\n\\( P = a + b + k \\left( \\frac{1}{2} a + \\frac{2}{3} b \\right) \\)\n\nNow, substituting a = 8/M, b = 9/N, c = 5/P into these expressions.\n\nWait, this seems like a loop. Alternatively, perhaps express everything in terms of a single variable.\n\nAlternatively, let me assume that there is a proportional relationship between a, b, c. For example, suppose that a, b, c are multiples of some common variable, say t.\n\nLet me suppose that:\n\nLet me set \\( a = m t \\), \\( b = n t \\), \\( c = p t \\), where m, n, p are constants to be determined, and t is a positive real number scaling factor.\n\nThen, d = (1/2) a = (m/2) t,\n\ne = (2/3) b = (2n/3) t,\n\nf = 2 c = 2 p t.\n\nNow, substituting into the expressions for M, N, P:\n\nFirst, M:\n\n\\( M = b + c + k(e + f) = n t + p t + k\\left( \\frac{2n}{3} t + 2 p t \\right) \\)\n\nFactor out t:\n\n\\( M = t \\left( n + p + k \\left( \\frac{2n}{3} + 2 p \\right) \\right) \\)\n\nSimilarly, N:\n\n\\( N = a + c + k(d + f) = m t + p t + k\\left( \\frac{m}{2} t + 2 p t \\right) \\)\n\nFactor out t:\n\n\\( N = t \\left( m + p + k \\left( \\frac{m}{2} + 2 p \\right) \\right) \\)\n\nAnd P:\n\n\\( P = a + b + k(d + e) = m t + n t + k\\left( \\frac{m}{2} t + \\frac{2n}{3} t \\right) \\)\n\nFactor out t:\n\n\\( P = t \\left( m + n + k \\left( \\frac{m}{2} + \\frac{2n}{3} \\right) \\right) \\)\n\nNow, recall that:\n\n\\( a = 8 / M \\Rightarrow m t = 8 / M \\)\n\nBut M = t * [ ... ], so:\n\n\\( m t = 8 / [ t ( ... ) ] \\Rightarrow m t^2 = 8 / ( ... ) \\)\n\nSimilarly for the other variables:\n\n\\( b = 9 / N \\Rightarrow n t = 9 / N \\Rightarrow n t^2 = 9 / ( ... ) \\)\n\n\\( c = 5 / P \\Rightarrow p t = 5 / P \\Rightarrow p t^2 = 5 / ( ... ) \\)\n\nThis suggests that:\n\n\\( m t^2 = \\frac{8}{n + p + k(\\frac{2n}{3} + 2p)} \\)\n\n\\( n t^2 = \\frac{9}{m + p + k(\\frac{m}{2} + 2p)} \\)\n\n\\( p t^2 = \\frac{5}{m + n + k(\\frac{m}{2} + \\frac{2n}{3})} \\)\n\nLet me denote the denominators as D1, D2, D3:\n\nLet:\n\n\\( D1 = n + p + k\\left( \\frac{2n}{3} + 2p \\right) \\)\n\n\\( D2 = m + p + k\\left( \\frac{m}{2} + 2p \\right) \\)\n\n\\( D3 = m + n + k\\left( \\frac{m}{2} + \\frac{2n}{3} \\right) \\)\n\nThen, the equations become:\n\n1. \\( m t^2 = \\frac{8}{D1} \\) … (A)\n\n2. \\( n t^2 = \\frac{9}{D2} \\) … (B)\n\n3. \\( p t^2 = \\frac{5}{D3} \\) … (C)\n\nNow, we can write t^2 from each equation:\n\nFrom (A): \\( t^2 = \\frac{8}{D1 m} \\)\n\nFrom (B): \\( t^2 = \\frac{9}{D2 n} \\)\n\nFrom (C): \\( t^2 = \\frac{5}{D3 p} \\)\n\nTherefore, setting these equal:\n\n\\( \\frac{8}{D1 m} = \\frac{9}{D2 n} = \\frac{5}{D3 p} \\)\n\nLet me denote this common value as \\( t^2 \\), so:\n\n\\( \\frac{8}{D1 m} = \\frac{9}{D2 n} \\)\n\nand\n\n\\( \\frac{9}{D2 n} = \\frac{5}{D3 p} \\)\n\nThese are two equations relating m, n, p, k.\n\nThis is still quite involved, but perhaps we can assume that m, n, p are constants to be solved for.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps assume that m, n, p are rational numbers or simple fractions, given the coefficients in the original problem.\n\nAlternatively, notice that the right-hand sides of the original equations have exponents that are multiples of each other. For instance, in Equation (1), the exponent of 3 is half that of 2 (4 vs 8). In Equation (2), the exponent of 3 is two-thirds that of 2 (6 vs 9). In Equation (3), the exponent of 3 is double that of 2 (10 vs 5). Perhaps the variables a, b, c are related to these ratios?\n\nAlternatively, let me consider that since we have d = (1/2)a, e = (2/3)b, f = 2c, then perhaps the terms involving k in M, N, P can be expressed in terms of a, b, c.\n\nLet me try substituting the relationships d, e, f into the expressions for M, N, P.\n\nRecall:\n\nM = b + c + k(e + f) = b + c + k( (2/3)b + 2c )\n\n= b + c + (2k/3)b + 2k c\n\n= b(1 + 2k/3) + c(1 + 2k)\n\nSimilarly,\n\nN = a + c + k(d + f) = a + c + k( (1/2)a + 2c )\n\n= a + c + (k/2)a + 2k c\n\n= a(1 + k/2) + c(1 + 2k)\n\nP = a + b + k(d + e) = a + b + k( (1/2)a + (2/3)b )\n\n= a + b + (k/2)a + (2k/3)b\n\n= a(1 + k/2) + b(1 + 2k/3)\n\nTherefore, M, N, P are linear combinations of a and b and c with coefficients involving k.\n\nLet me rewrite them:\n\nM = (1 + (2k)/3) b + (1 + 2k) c … (M1)\n\nN = (1 + k/2) a + (1 + 2k) c … (N1)\n\nP = (1 + k/2) a + (1 + (2k)/3) b … (P1)\n\nNow, recall that:\n\na = 8 / M … (from Equation 1g: a M = 8)\n\nb = 9 / N … (from Equation 2g: b N = 9)\n\nc = 5 / P … (from Equation 3g: c P = 5)\n\nSo substituting these into the expressions for M, N, P:\n\nStarting with M:\n\nM = (1 + (2k)/3) b + (1 + 2k) c\n\nBut b = 9/N and c = 5/P, so:\n\nM = (1 + (2k)/3)(9/N) + (1 + 2k)(5/P) … (M2)\n\nSimilarly, N = (1 + k/2)a + (1 + 2k)c\n\nSubstituting a = 8/M and c = 5/P:\n\nN = (1 + k/2)(8/M) + (1 + 2k)(5/P) … (N2)\n\nP = (1 + k/2)a + (1 + (2k)/3) b\n\nSubstituting a = 8/M and b = 9/N:\n\nP = (1 + k/2)(8/M) + (1 + (2k)/3)(9/N) … (P2)\n\nNow, we have three equations (M2), (N2), (P2) involving M, N, P, which are also related through the definitions of a, b, c.\n\nThis is quite recursive and complex, but perhaps we can substitute one equation into another.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider that this system might be solved by assuming that M, N, P are proportional to certain constants, or that they are all equal? Let me test the possibility that M = N = P. If that's the case, then perhaps the equations can be satisfied.\n\nAssume M = N = P = Q (some constant).\n\nThen, from Equation (M2):\n\nQ = (1 + (2k)/3)(9/Q) + (1 + 2k)(5/Q)\n\nMultiply both sides by Q:\n\nQ² = (1 + (2k)/3)(9) + (1 + 2k)(5)\n\nSimilarly, from Equation (N2):\n\nQ = (1 + k/2)(8/Q) + (1 + 2k)(5/Q)\n\nMultiply by Q:\n\nQ² = (1 + k/2)(8) + (1 + 2k)(5)\n\nAnd from Equation (P2):\n\nQ = (1 + k/2)(8/Q) + (1 + (2k)/3)(9/Q)\n\nMultiply by Q:\n\nQ² = (1 + k/2)(8) + (1 + (2k)/3)(9)\n\nTherefore, if M = N = P = Q, then all three expressions for Q² must be equal:\n\nFirst expression (from M2):\n\nQ² = [ (1 + (2k)/3)*9 + (1 + 2k)*5 ]\n\nSecond expression (from N2):\n\nQ² = [ (1 + k/2)*8 + (1 + 2k)*5 ]\n\nThird expression (from P2):\n\nQ² = [ (1 + k/2)*8 + (1 + (2k)/3)*9 ]\n\nLet me compute each of these expressions with k = log₂3 ≈ 1.58496, but perhaps we can keep it as k for now.\n\nFirst expression:\n\nTerm1 = (1 + (2k)/3)*9 + (1 + 2k)*5\n\n= 9 + 6k + 5 + 10k\n\n= 14 + 16k\n\nSecond expression:\n\nTerm2 = (1 + k/2)*8 + (1 + 2k)*5\n\n= 8 + 4k + 5 + 10k\n\n= 13 + 14k\n\nThird expression:\n\nTerm3 = (1 + k/2)*8 + (1 + (2k)/3)*9\n\n= 8 + 4k + 9 + 6k\n\n= 17 + 10k\n\nFor Q² to be equal in all three expressions, we must have:\n\n14 + 16k = 13 + 14k = 17 + 10k\n\nBut let's check if 14 + 16k equals 13 + 14k:\n\n14 + 16k = 13 + 14k ⇒ 14 - 13 = 14k - 16k ⇒ 1 = -2k ⇒ k = -0.5\n\nBut k = log₂3 is positive (~1.58496), so this is impossible.\n\nSimilarly, 13 + 14k vs 17 + 10k:\n\n13 + 14k = 17 + 10k ⇒ 13 -17 = 10k -14k ⇒ -4 = -4k ⇒ k = 1\n\nBut k = log₂3 ≈ 1.58496 ≠ 1, so this is also impossible.\n\nTherefore, the assumption that M = N = P is invalid.\n\nHmm. This is getting too convoluted. Perhaps there's a better way.\n\nLet me recall that we need to find the minimal value of \\( xyz \\). Since \\( x, y, z \\) are positive real numbers, by the AM-GM inequality, the product \\( xyz \\) is minimized when... Well, actually, AM-GM gives a lower bound for the sum given the product, but since we're dealing with a product to be minimized, perhaps under certain constraints? Wait, but we have three equations that constrain \\( x, y, z \\), so the minimal \\( xyz \\) would be the minimal value achievable under those constraints.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps express \\( xyz \\) in terms of a, b, c, d, e, f and then use the relationships we have.\n\nRecall that:\n\n\\( x = 2^a \\cdot 3^d \\)\n\n\\( y = 2^b \\cdot 3^e \\)\n\n\\( z = 2^c \\cdot 3^f \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( xyz = 2^{a + b + c} \\cdot 3^{d + e + f} \\)\n\nFrom earlier relationships:\n\n\\( d = \\frac{1}{2}a \\)\n\n\\( e = \\frac{2}{3}b \\)\n\n\\( f = 2c \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( d + e + f = \\frac{1}{2}a + \\frac{2}{3}b + 2c \\)\n\nThus,\n\n\\( xyz = 2^{a + b + c} \\cdot 3^{\\frac{1}{2}a + \\frac{2}{3}b + 2c} \\)\n\nLet me denote \\( S = a + b + c \\), and \\( T = \\frac{1}{2}a + \\frac{2}{3}b + 2c \\), so \\( xyz = 2^{S} \\cdot 3^{T} \\)\n\nOur goal is to minimize \\( 2^{S} \\cdot 3^{T} \\), given the constraints from the equations.\n\nBut we need to express S and T in terms of the variables or find relationships between S and T.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can find expressions for S and T in terms of M, N, P or other variables.\n\nAlternatively, notice that the exponents in the original equations relate to S and T somehow.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider taking the logarithm of \\( xyz \\), which is \\( \\log_2(xyz) = \\log_2 x + \\log_2 y + \\log_2 z = (a + k d) + (b + k e) + (c + k f) \\)\n\nSubstituting \\( d = \\frac{1}{2}a \\), \\( e = \\frac{2}{3}b \\), \\( f = 2c \\):\n\n\\( \\log_2(xyz) = a + k(\\frac{1}{2}a) + b + k(\\frac{2}{3}b) + c + k(2c) \\)\n\nSimplify:\n\n= \\( a(1 + \\frac{k}{2}) + b(1 + \\frac{2k}{3}) + c(1 + 2k) \\)\n\nHmm, this expression might be related to the expressions for M, N, P.\n\nRecall that:\n\nM = (1 + (2k)/3) b + (1 + 2k) c … (from earlier)\n\nN = (1 + k/2) a + (1 + 2k) c … (from earlier)\n\nP = (1 + k/2) a + (1 + (2k)/3) b … (from earlier)\n\nWait, the expression for \\( \\log_2(xyz) \\) is:\n\n\\( \\log_2(xyz) = (1 + \\frac{k}{2})a + (1 + \\frac{2k}{3})b + (1 + 2k)c \\)\n\nNotice that this is exactly the same as the expression for P + N + M?\n\nWait:\n\nWait, M = (1 + (2k)/3) b + (1 + 2k) c\n\nN = (1 + k/2) a + (1 + 2k) c\n\nP = (1 + k/2) a + (1 + (2k)/3) b\n\nTherefore, adding M + N + P:\n\nM + N + P = [ (1 + (2k)/3) b + (1 + 2k) c ] + [ (1 + k/2) a + (1 + 2k) c ] + [ (1 + k/2) a + (1 + (2k)/3) b ]\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nFor a terms: (1 + k/2) + (1 + k/2) = 2*(1 + k/2) = 2 + k\n\nFor b terms: (1 + (2k)/3) + (1 + (2k)/3) = 2*(1 + (2k)/3) = 2 + (4k)/3\n\nFor c terms: (1 + 2k) + (1 + 2k) = 2*(1 + 2k) = 2 + 4k\n\nTherefore:\n\nM + N + P = (2 + k)a + (2 + (4k)/3)b + (2 + 4k)c\n\nHmm, not quite the same as \\( \\log_2(xyz) \\), but close.\n\nWait, \\( \\log_2(xyz) = (1 + k/2)a + (1 + 2k/3)b + (1 + 2k)c \\)\n\nSo comparing coefficients:\n\nFor a: coefficient in log2(xyz): \\( 1 + k/2 \\)\n\nIn M + N + P: coefficient of a is \\( 2 + k \\)\n\nSimilarly for b:\n\nIn log2(xyz): \\( 1 + 2k/3 \\)\n\nIn M + N + P: \\( 2 + (4k)/3 \\)\n\nFor c:\n\nIn log2(xyz): \\( 1 + 2k \\)\n\nIn M + N + P: \\( 2 + 4k \\)\n\nHmm, so the coefficients in M + N + P are exactly double those in log2(xyz), except for the constants:\n\nWait:\n\nFor a:\n\nlog2(xyz) coefficient: \\( 1 + \\frac{k}{2} \\)\n\nM + N + P coefficient: \\( 2 + k = 2(1) + k = 2*(1) + k \\), while \\( 2*(1 + k/2) = 2 + k \\). So yes, exactly double.\n\nSimilarly for b:\n\nlog2(xyz): \\( 1 + \\frac{2k}{3} \\)\n\nM + N + P: \\( 2 + \\frac{4k}{3} = 2*(1 + \\frac{2k}{3}) \\)\n\nFor c:\n\nlog2(xyz): \\( 1 + 2k \\)\n\nM + N + P: \\( 2 + 4k = 2*(1 + 2k) \\)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n\\( M + N + P = 2 \\cdot \\log_2(xyz) \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( \\log_2(xyz) = \\frac{M + N + P}{2} \\)\n\nThus,\n\n\\( xyz = 2^{\\frac{M + N + P}{2}} \\)\n\nSo, to minimize \\( xyz \\), we need to minimize \\( 2^{\\frac{M + N + P}{2}} \\), which is equivalent to minimizing \\( M + N + P \\), since the exponential is a monotonically increasing function.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to finding the minimal value of \\( M + N + P \\), given the relationships between M, N, P defined by the earlier equations.\n\nThis seems more manageable. So now, our goal is to minimize \\( M + N + P \\), where M, N, P are defined by the system of equations:\n\n1. \\( M = \\frac{9}{N} + \\frac{5}{P} + k\\left( \\frac{6}{N} + \\frac{10}{P} \\right) \\)\n\n2. \\( N = \\frac{8}{M} + \\frac{5}{P} + k\\left( \\frac{4}{M} + \\frac{10}{P} \\right) \\)\n\n3. \\( P = \\frac{8}{M} + \\frac{9}{N} + k\\left( \\frac{4}{M} + \\frac{6}{N} \\right) \\)\n\nThis system is symmetric in a way, but still complex.\n\nPerhaps we can assume that M, N, P are proportional to the constants in the numerators of their definitions. For instance, notice that M involves 9 and 5, N involves 8 and 5, P involves 8 and 9. Maybe M : N : P = 9 : 8 : 5 or something similar? Let me try an assumption.\n\nLet me suppose that M = 9k', N = 8k', P = 5k' for some k' > 0. Then, substitute into the equations.\n\nLet me set M = 9k', N = 8k', P = 5k'\n\nThen, let's substitute into Equation 1:\n\nEquation 1: M = 9/N + 5/P + k*(6/N + 10/P)\n\nSubstituting:\n\n9k' = 9/(8k') + 5/(5k') + k*(6/(8k') + 10/(5k'))\n\nSimplify:\n\n9k' = (9)/(8k') + 1/k' + k*( (6)/(8k') + 2/k' )\n\nSimplify the terms inside the k*(...):\n\n6/(8k') + 10/(5k') = (3)/(4k') + 2/k' = (3/4 + 2)/k' = (11/4)/k'\n\nTherefore:\n\n9k' = [9/(8k') + 1/k'] + k*(11/(4k'))\n\nSimplify the terms without k:\n\n9/(8k') + 1/k' = (9 + 8)/8k' = 17/(8k')\n\nThe term with k:\n\nk*(11/(4k')) = 11k/(4k') = (11/4)(k/k')\n\nTherefore:\n\n9k' = 17/(8k') + (11/4)(k/k')\n\nMultiply both sides by 8k' to eliminate denominators:\n\n9k' * 8k' = 17 + (11/4)(k/k') * 8k'\n\nSimplify left side: 72 (k')²\n\nRight side: 17 + (11/4)*(8k) = 17 + 22k\n\nTherefore:\n\n72 (k')² = 17 + 22k\n\nSimilarly, let's substitute into Equation 2:\n\nEquation 2: N = 8/M + 5/P + k*(4/M + 10/P)\n\nSubstituting M = 9k', N = 8k', P = 5k':\n\n8k' = 8/(9k') + 5/(5k') + k*(4/(9k') + 10/(5k'))\n\nSimplify:\n\n8k' = 8/(9k') + 1/k' + k*(4/(9k') + 2/k')\n\nSimplify terms without k:\n\n8/(9k') + 1/k' = (8 + 9)/9k' = 17/(9k')\n\nTerms with k:\n\nk*(4/(9k') + 2/k') = k*( (4 + 18)/9k' ) = k*(22)/(9k') = (22k)/(9k')\n\nTherefore:\n\n8k' = 17/(9k') + (22k)/(9k')\n\nMultiply both sides by 9k':\n\n72 (k')² = 17 + 22k\n\nWait, this is the same equation as from Equation 1: 72 (k')² = 17 + 22k\n\nSo that's consistent so far.\n\nNow, check Equation 3:\n\nEquation 3: P = 8/M + 9/N + k*(4/M + 6/N)\n\nSubstituting M = 9k', N = 8k', P = 5k':\n\n5k' = 8/(9k') + 9/(8k') + k*(4/(9k') + 6/(8k'))\n\nSimplify:\n\n5k' = [8/(9k') + 9/(8k')] + k*(4/(9k') + 6/(8k'))\n\nCompute the terms without k:\n\n8/(9k') + 9/(8k') = (64 + 81)/(72k') = 145/(72k')\n\nTerms with k:\n\nk*(4/(9k') + 6/(8k')) = k*( (32 + 54)/(72k') ) = k*(86)/(72k') = (86k)/(72k') = (43k)/(36k')\n\nTherefore:\n\n5k' = 145/(72k') + (43k)/(36k')\n\nMultiply both sides by 72k':\n\n5k' * 72k' = 145 + (43k)/(36k') *72k'\n\nSimplify left side: 360 (k')²\n\nRight side: 145 + (43k * 72k')/(36k') = 145 + (43k * 2) = 145 + 86k\n\nTherefore:\n\n360 (k')² = 145 + 86k\n\nBut from Equation 1 and 2, we had:\n\n72 (k')² = 17 + 22k ⇒ (k')² = (17 + 22k)/72\n\nSubstitute this into the equation from Equation 3:\n\n360 * [ (17 + 22k)/72 ] = 145 + 86k\n\nSimplify left side:\n\n360/72 = 5, so 5*(17 + 22k) = 85 + 110k\n\nThus:\n\n85 + 110k = 145 + 86k\n\nSubtract 85 and 86k from both sides:\n\n24k = 60 ⇒ k = 60 / 24 = 2.5\n\nBut k = log₂3 ≈ 1.58496, which is not 2.5. Contradiction.\n\nTherefore, the assumption that M:N:P = 9:8:5 is invalid.\n\nHmm. So that assumption doesn't work. Maybe another proportion?\n\nAlternatively, suppose that M, N, P are proportional to the constants in the numerators of the original equations' right-hand sides? The original equations have constants 8+4k, 9+6k, 5+10k. Maybe M, N, P are proportional to those?\n\nLet me suppose that M = (8 + 4k) * m, N = (9 + 6k)*m, P = (5 + 10k)*m for some m > 0.\n\nLet me try this.\n\nSet M = (8 + 4k)m, N = (9 + 6k)m, P = (5 + 10k)m\n\nNow substitute into Equation 1:\n\nEquation 1: M = 9/N + 5/P + k*(6/N + 10/P)\n\nSubstitute:\n\n(8 + 4k)m = 9/[(9 + 6k)m] + 5/[(5 + 10k)m] + k*[6/[(9 + 6k)m] + 10/[(5 + 10k)m]]\n\nFactor out 1/m:\n\n(8 + 4k)m = [9/(9 + 6k) + 5/(5 + 10k) + k*(6/(9 + 6k) + 10/(5 + 10k))]/m\n\nMultiply both sides by m:\n\n(8 + 4k)m² = 9/(9 + 6k) + 5/(5 + 10k) + k*(6/(9 + 6k) + 10/(5 + 10k))\n\nLet me compute the right-hand side:\n\nFirst term: 9/(9 + 6k) = 9/[9(1 + (2/3)k)] = 1/(1 + (2/3)k)\n\nSecond term: 5/(5 + 10k) = 5/[5(1 + 2k)] = 1/(1 + 2k)\n\nThird term: k*[6/(9 + 6k) + 10/(5 + 10k)]\n\nCompute inside the brackets:\n\n6/(9 + 6k) = 6/[3(3 + 2k)] = 2/(3 + 2k)\n\n10/(5 + 10k) = 10/[5(1 + 2k)] = 2/(1 + 2k)\n\nTherefore, the third term is k*[2/(3 + 2k) + 2/(1 + 2k)] = 2k[1/(3 + 2k) + 1/(1 + 2k)]\n\nSo, the entire right-hand side is:\n\n1/(1 + (2/3)k) + 1/(1 + 2k) + 2k[1/(3 + 2k) + 1/(1 + 2k)]\n\nThis is quite complicated, but let's plug in k = log₂3 ≈ 1.58496 and see if we can solve for m².\n\nBut this might not be the best approach either.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the minimal value occurs when the variables are chosen such that the terms involving k balance out in some way.\n\nAlternatively, maybe set variables such that the expressions for M, N, P are equal? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, since this problem is likely designed to have an integer or nice fractional answer, perhaps we can assume that k = log₂3 is a variable to be kept as is, and look for a solution in terms of k, then plug in k = log₂3 at the end.\n\nAlternatively, notice that the problem asks for the minimal value of xyz, which is a positive real number, and given the symmetric nature of the equations, perhaps the minimal occurs when certain variables are equal?\n\nAlternatively, let me consider that the equations may be manipulated to express a, b, c in terms of k.\n\nRecall that:\n\nFrom earlier relationships:\n\nd = (1/2)a,\n\ne = (2/3)b,\n\nf = 2c,\n\nand we had:\n\nM = b + c + k(e + f) = b + c + k( (2/3)b + 2c )\n\nN = a + c + k(d + f) = a + c + k( (1/2)a + 2c )\n\nP = a + b + k(d + e) = a + b + k( (1/2)a + (2/3)b )\n\nAlso, we have:\n\na = 8/M,\n\nb = 9/N,\n\nc = 5/P.\n\nLet me substitute a = 8/M into the expression for N:\n\nN = (8/M) + c + k( (1/2)(8/M) + 2c )\n\nSimilarly, substitute b = 9/N into the expression for M:\n\nM = (9/N) + c + k( (2/3)(9/N) + 2c )\n\nAnd substitute c = 5/P into expressions for M, N, P.\n\nThis is still quite involved, but let me try to write everything in terms of M, N, P.\n\nStarting with M:\n\nM = (9/N) + c + k*( (6/N) + 2c )\n\nBut c = 5/P,\n\nso,\n\nM = 9/N + 5/P + k*(6/N + 10/P) … (M)\n\nSimilarly, N:\n\nN = (8/M) + c + k*(4/M + 2c )\n\nSubstitute c = 5/P,\n\nN = 8/M + 5/P + k*(4/M + 10/P) … (N)\n\nAnd P:\n\nP = a + b + k*( (1/2)a + (2/3)b )\n\nSubstitute a = 8/M and b = 9/N,\n\nP = 8/M + 9/N + k*(4/M + 6/N) … (P)\n\nNow, we have three equations:\n\n1. M = 9/N + 5/P + k*(6/N + 10/P)\n\n2. N = 8/M + 5/P + k*(4/M + 10/P)\n\n3. P = 8/M + 9/N + k*(4/M + 6/N)\n\nThis is a system of three equations with three variables M, N, P.\n\nPerhaps we can solve this system numerically. Since k is a constant (log₂3 ≈ 1.58496), we can plug in the numerical value and solve for M, N, P numerically.\n\nGiven that this is a competition problem, perhaps the values of M, N, P are integers or simple fractions when k is substituted numerically.\n\nLet me assign k ≈ 1.58496 and try to solve numerically.\n\nLet me denote k ≈ 1.58496.\n\nLet me rewrite the equations with this approximate value.\n\nEquation 1:\n\nM = 9/N + 5/P + 1.58496*(6/N + 10/P)\n\nEquation 2:\n\nN = 8/M + 5/P + 1.58496*(4/M + 10/P)\n\nEquation 3:\n\nP = 8/M + 9/N + 1.58496*(4/M + 6/N)\n\nThis is a system of nonlinear equations. To solve this numerically, we might need to use iterative methods or substitution.\n\nAlternatively, make an initial guess and iterate.\n\nLet me start with an initial guess for M, N, P.\n\nLooking at the original equations' right-hand sides:\n\nEquation 1's RHS constants: 8 and 4; Equation 2's: 9 and 6; Equation 3's: 5 and 10.\n\nPerhaps M, N, P are related to these numbers. Let me try M = 12, N = 15, P = 10 as a guess.\n\nLet me test M = 12, N = 15, P = 10.\n\nCompute RHS of Equation 1:\n\n9/15 + 5/10 + 1.58496*(6/15 + 10/10)\n\n= 0.6 + 0.5 + 1.58496*(0.4 + 1)\n\n= 1.1 + 1.58496*(1.4)\n\n≈ 1.1 + 2.21894 ≈ 3.31894\n\nBut LHS M is 12, which is much larger. So this guess is too low.\n\nMaybe M, N, P are larger.\n\nAlternatively, let's see what the RHS expressions look like.\n\nSuppose that M, N, P are all large numbers, then the terms like 9/N, 5/P, etc., become small, and the equations reduce to:\n\nM ≈ k*(6/N + 10/P)\n\nN ≈ k*(4/M + 10/P)\n\nP ≈ k*(4/M + 6/N)\n\nBut this is speculative.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider that if M, N, P are large, then terms like 9/N are negligible, so approximately:\n\nM ≈ k*(6/N + 10/P)\n\nN ≈ k*(4/M + 10/P)\n\nP ≈ k*(4/M + 6/N)\n\nThis still seems difficult, but perhaps assume that 10/P is negligible compared to 6/N in the first equation, etc., but this is not necessarily the case.\n\nAlternatively, let me try setting M = N = P, even though earlier it didn't work with the assumption of proportional constants, but let's see with the actual k value.\n\nLet M = N = P = Q.\n\nThen:\n\nEquation 1: Q = 9/Q + 5/Q + k*(6/Q + 10/Q) = (9 + 5 + 6k + 10k)/Q = (14 + 16k)/Q\n\nThus, Q² = 14 + 16k\n\nSimilarly, Equation 2:\n\nQ = 8/Q + 5/Q + k*(4/Q + 10/Q) = (8 + 5 + 4k + 10k)/Q = (13 + 14k)/Q ⇒ Q² = 13 + 14k\n\nEquation 3:\n\nQ = 8/Q + 9/Q + k*(4/Q + 6/Q) = (8 + 9 + 4k + 6k)/Q = (17 + 10k)/Q ⇒ Q² = 17 + 10k\n\nThus, for consistency:\n\n14 + 16k = 13 + 14k = 17 + 10k\n\nCompute 14 + 16k vs 13 + 14k:\n\n14 + 16k = 13 + 14k ⇒ 1 = -2k ⇒ k = -0.5, which is impossible since k = log₂3 >0.\n\nSimilarly, 13 + 14k vs 17 + 10k:\n\n13 +14k =17 +10k ⇒4k =4 ⇒k=1, but k ≈1.58496, so not equal.\n\nThus, M=N=P is impossible.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps set two variables equal.\n\nSuppose M = N.\n\nLet me assume M = N = Q, and P is different.\n\nThen:\n\nEquation 1: Q = 9/Q + 5/P + k*(6/Q + 10/P)\n\nEquation 2: Q = 8/Q + 5/P + k*(4/Q + 10/P)\n\nEquation 3: P = 8/Q + 9/Q + k*(4/Q + 6/Q) = (17/Q) + k*(10/Q) = (17 + 10k)/Q\n\nThus, P = (17 + 10k)/Q ⇒ Q = (17 + 10k)/P\n\nSubstitute Q = (17 + 10k)/P into Equations 1 and 2.\n\nEquation 1:\n\n(17 + 10k)/P = 9/Q + 5/P + k*(6/Q + 10/P)\n\nBut Q = (17 + 10k)/P, so 1/Q = P/(17 + 10k)\n\nSubstitute:\n\n(17 + 10k)/P = 9*(P/(17 + 10k)) + 5/P + k*(6*(P/(17 + 10k)) + 10/P )\n\nMultiply both sides by P*(17 + 10k) to eliminate denominators:\n\n(17 + 10k)^2 = 9P^2 + 5*(17 + 10k) + k*(6P^2 + 10*(17 + 10k))\n\nExpand:\n\nLeft side: (17 + 10k)^2\n\nRight side: 9P^2 + 5*(17 + 10k) + k*6P^2 + k*10*(17 + 10k)\n\nGroup terms with P² and constants:\n\nP² terms: (9 + 6k) P²\n\nConstants: 5*(17 + 10k) + 10k*(17 + 10k)\n\nThus:\n\n(17 + 10k)^2 = (9 + 6k) P² + 5*(17 + 10k) + 10k*(17 + 10k)\n\nBring all terms to left side:\n\n(17 + 10k)^2 - 5*(17 + 10k) - 10k*(17 + 10k) - (9 + 6k) P² = 0\n\nFactor out (17 + 10k):\n\n(17 + 10k)[(17 + 10k) - 5 - 10k] - (9 + 6k) P² = 0\n\nSimplify inside the brackets:\n\n(17 + 10k - 5 - 10k) = 12\n\nThus:\n\n(17 + 10k)*12 - (9 + 6k) P² = 0\n\nTherefore:\n\n(9 + 6k) P² = 12*(17 + 10k)\n\nThus,\n\nP² = [12*(17 + 10k)] / (9 + 6k)\n\nSimplify numerator and denominator:\n\nFactor numerator: 12*(17 + 10k)\n\nDenominator: 3*(3 + 2k)\n\nThus,\n\nP² = [12/3] * (17 + 10k)/(3 + 2k) = 4*(17 + 10k)/(3 + 2k)\n\nTherefore,\n\nP = 2*sqrt[ (17 + 10k)/(3 + 2k) ]\n\nSimilarly, since Q = (17 + 10k)/P,\n\nQ = (17 + 10k)/P = (17 + 10k) / [2*sqrt( (17 + 10k)/(3 + 2k) ) ]\n\nSimplify:\n\nQ = (17 + 10k) / [2 * sqrt( (17 + 10k)/(3 + 2k) ) ]\n\n= [sqrt(17 + 10k) * sqrt(17 + 10k) ] / [2 * sqrt( (17 + 10k)/(3 + 2k) ) ]\n\n= sqrt(17 + 10k) * sqrt( (3 + 2k)/(1) ) / 2\n\n= ( sqrt( (17 + 10k)(3 + 2k) ) ) / 2\n\nTherefore, Q = (1/2) * sqrt( (17 + 10k)(3 + 2k) )\n\nNow, let's compute this with k = log₂3 ≈ 1.58496\n\nFirst compute 17 + 10k:\n\n17 + 10*1.58496 ≈ 17 + 15.8496 ≈ 32.8496\n\n3 + 2k ≈ 3 + 3.16992 ≈ 6.16992\n\nMultiply them: 32.8496 * 6.16992 ≈ let's compute:\n\n32.8496 * 6 = 197.0976\n\n32.8496 * 0.16992 ≈ 32.8496 * 0.17 ≈ 5.5776\n\nTotal ≈ 197.0976 + 5.5776 ≈ 202.6752\n\nThus sqrt(202.6752) ≈ 14.234\n\nThen Q ≈ 14.234 / 2 ≈ 7.117\n\nSimilarly, P ≈ 2 * sqrt(32.8496 / 6.16992)\n\nFirst compute 32.8496 / 6.16992 ≈ 5.326\n\nsqrt(5.326) ≈ 2.308\n\nThen P ≈ 2 * 2.308 ≈ 4.616\n\nNow, check Equation 3:\n\nP = (17 + 10k)/Q ≈ 32.8496 / 7.117 ≈ 4.616, which matches.\n\nNow, check Equation 1 and 2 with these values:\n\nEquation 1:\n\nM = Q ≈ 7.117\n\nCompute RHS:\n\n9/Q + 5/P + k*(6/Q + 10/P)\n\nCompute each term:\n\n9/Q ≈ 9 / 7.117 ≈ 1.266\n\n5/P ≈ 5 / 4.616 ≈ 1.083\n\nk*(6/Q + 10/P) ≈ 1.58496*(6/7.117 + 10/4.616)\n\nCompute inside the brackets:\n\n6/7.117 ≈ 0.843\n\n10/4.616 ≈ 2.166\n\nSum ≈ 0.843 + 2.166 ≈ 3.009\n\nMultiply by k: 1.58496 * 3.009 ≈ 4.766\n\nTotal RHS ≈ 1.266 + 1.083 + 4.766 ≈ 7.115, which is approximately M ≈7.117. Close enough considering rounding errors.\n\nSimilarly, Equation 2:\n\nN = Q ≈7.117\n\nCompute RHS:\n\n8/Q + 5/P + k*(4/Q + 10/P)\n\n8/Q ≈ 8/7.117 ≈ 1.125\n\n5/P ≈1.083\n\nk*(4/Q + 10/P) ≈1.58496*(4/7.117 + 10/4.616)\n\nCompute inside the brackets:\n\n4/7.117 ≈0.562\n\n10/4.616≈2.166\n\nSum≈0.562+2.166≈2.728\n\nMultiply by k≈1.58496*2.728≈4.323\n\nTotal RHS≈1.125+1.083+4.323≈6.531\n\nBut N is supposed to be ≈7.117, so there's a discrepancy here. Hmm, so this suggests that the assumption M=N is slightly off.\n\nThe discrepancy might be due to the approximation of k as 1.58496. Let me use a more precise value for k.\n\nLet me compute k = log₂3:\n\nln3 / ln2 ≈ 1.098612289 / 0.693147181 ≈ 1.584962501\n\nSo more precisely, k ≈ 1.584962501\n\nRecalculate P and Q with this k.\n\nFirst, compute 17 + 10k:\n\n17 + 10*1.584962501 ≈ 17 + 15.84962501 ≈ 32.84962501\n\n3 + 2k ≈ 3 + 3.169925002 ≈ 6.169925002\n\nProduct: 32.84962501 * 6.169925002\n\nLet me compute this more accurately:\n\n32.84962501 * 6 = 197.09785006\n\n32.84962501 