hm of both sides of each equation with base 2, since the exponents are logarithms with base 2. Let me try that.\n\nLet me denote \\( \\log_2 \\) as log for simplicity.\n\nStarting with the first equation:\n\n\\( x^{\\log(y z)} = 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\).\n\nTake \\( \\log_2 \\) of both sides:\n\n\\( \\log_2 \\left( x^{\\log(y z)} \\right) = \\log_2 \\left( 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\right) \\).\n\nUsing the logarithm power rule: \\( \\log(a^b) = b \\log(a) \\), so the left side becomes:\n\n\\( \\log(y z) \\cdot \\log_2 x \\).\n\nAnd the right side: \\( \\log_2 (2^8 \\cdot 3^4) \\). Since \\( \\log_b (a \\cdot c) = \\log_b a + \\log_b c \\), so this is \\( \\log_2 (2^8) + \\log_2 (3^4) = 8 + 4 \\log_2 3 \\).\n\nTherefore, putting it all together, the first equation becomes:\n\n\\( \\log(y z) \\cdot \\log_2 x = 8 + 4 \\log_2 3 \\).  --- (1)\n\nSimilarly, let me do the same for the second equation:\n\n\\( y^{\\log(z x)} = 2^9 \\cdot 3^6 \\).\n\nTake \\( \\log_2 \\) of both sides:\n\n\\( \\log(z x) \\cdot \\log_2 y = \\log_2 (2^9 \\cdot 3^6) = 9 + 6 \\log_2 3 \\).  --- (2)\n\nAnd the third equation:\n\n\\( z^{\\log(x y)} = 2^5 \\cdot 3^{10} \\).\n\nTake \\( \\log_2 \\) of both sides:\n\n\\( \\log(x y) \\cdot \\log_2 z = \\log_2 (2^5 \\cdot 3^{10}) = 5 + 10 \\log_2 3 \\).  --- (3)\n\nAlright, so now equations (1), (2), and (3) are:\n\n(1) \\( \\log(y z) \\cdot \\log_2 x = 8 + 4 \\log_2 3 \\)\n\n(2) \\( \\log(z x) \\cdot \\log_2 y = 9 + 6 \\log_2 3 \\)\n\n(3) \\( \\log(x y) \\cdot \\log_2 z = 5 + 10 \\log_2 3 \\)\n\nHmm, okay. Now, let me see if I can express these equations in terms of logarithms of x, y, z, or perhaps take some substitutions.\n\nFirst, note that \\( \\log(y z) = \\log y + \\log z \\), since log of a product is the sum of logs. Similarly, \\( \\log(z x) = \\log z + \\log x \\), and \\( \\log(x y) = \\log x + \\log y \\). Also, the variables x, y, z are positive real numbers, so their logarithms are defined.\n\nLet me denote \\( a = \\log_2 x \\), \\( b = \\log_2 y \\), and \\( c = \\log_2 z \\). Since x, y, z are positive real numbers, a, b, c are real numbers.\n\nThen, since \\( \\log_2 x = a \\), so \\( x = 2^a \\), similarly \\( y = 2^b \\), \\( z = 2^c \\). Therefore, \\( \\log(y z) = \\log_2 (y z) = \\log_2 y + \\log_2 z = b + c \\), similarly \\( \\log(z x) = c + a \\), and \\( \\log(x y) = a + b \\).\n\nAlso, \\( \\log_2 x = a \\), \\( \\log_2 y = b \\), \\( \\log_2 z = c \\). So, substituting into equation (1):\n\n\\( (b + c) \\cdot a = 8 + 4 \\log_2 3 \\).  --- (1a)\n\nSimilarly, equation (2):\n\n\\( (c + a) \\cdot b = 9 + 6 \\log_2 3 \\).  --- (2a)\n\nEquation (3):\n\n\\( (a + b) \\cdot c = 5 + 10 \\log_2 3 \\).  --- (3a)\n\nSo now, we have a system of three equations with three variables a, b, c:\n\n(1a): \\( a(b + c) = 8 + 4 \\log_2 3 \\)\n\n(2a): \\( b(c + a) = 9 + 6 \\log_2 3 \\)\n\n(3a): \\( c(a + b) = 5 + 10 \\log_2 3 \\)\n\nHmm. So, this is a system of three equations with three variables, but the equations have this logarithmic term \\( \\log_2 3 \\). Maybe I can denote \\( k = \\log_2 3 \\), so that the equations become:\n\n(1b): \\( a(b + c) = 8 + 4k \\)\n\n(2b): \\( b(c + a) = 9 + 6k \\)\n\n(3b): \\( c(a + b) = 5 + 10k \\)\n\nNow, we can treat k as a constant, since \\( \\log_2 3 \\) is a constant. So, we have three equations with variables a, b, c and parameter k, which is fixed (since 3 is fixed). So, we can try to solve for a, b, c in terms of k, and then perhaps find relations between them?\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can manipulate the equations to eliminate k?\n\nAlternatively, maybe consider taking the three equations and multiplying them together?\n\nLet me see. Let me denote equations (1b), (2b), (3b):\n\nMultiply the left sides together: \\( a(b + c) \\cdot b(c + a) \\cdot c(a + b) \\)\n\nMultiply the right sides together: \\( (8 + 4k)(9 + 6k)(5 + 10k) \\)\n\nHmm, not sure if that would be helpful, but maybe? Let me see.\n\nAlternatively, let me see if I can express each equation in terms of variables and then relate them.\n\nLet me consider equations (1a), (2a), (3a):\n\nLet me write them again:\n\n1. \\( a(b + c) = 8 + 4k \\)\n\n2. \\( b(c + a) = 9 + 6k \\)\n\n3. \\( c(a + b) = 5 + 10k \\)\n\nLet me denote S = a + b + c. Maybe that could be useful? Hmm, but not sure yet.\n\nAlternatively, let me try to express each equation as:\n\nFrom equation (1a): \\( a b + a c = 8 + 4k \\)\n\nFrom equation (2a): \\( b c + a b = 9 + 6k \\)\n\nFrom equation (3a): \\( a c + b c = 5 + 10k \\)\n\nAh! Wait a second! That's a good point. Since, for example, \\( a(b + c) = a b + a c \\), and similarly for the others.\n\nSo, rewriting:\n\n(1c): \\( a b + a c = 8 + 4k \\)\n\n(2c): \\( b c + a b = 9 + 6k \\)\n\n(3c): \\( a c + b c = 5 + 10k \\)\n\nNow, notice that each equation is the sum of two products of variables. Let me write them again:\n\nEquation (1c): ab + ac = 8 + 4k\n\nEquation (2c): bc + ab = 9 + 6k\n\nEquation (3c): ac + bc = 5 + 10k\n\nHmm, so if I denote the three variables as a, b, c, then the equations are:\n\nEquation (1c): ab + ac = 8 + 4k\n\nEquation (2c): ab + bc = 9 + 6k\n\nEquation (3c): ac + bc = 5 + 10k\n\nHmm, so perhaps I can subtract equations from each other to eliminate some variables?\n\nLet me try subtracting equation (1c) from equation (2c):\n\nEquation (2c) - Equation (1c):\n\n(ab + bc) - (ab + ac) = (9 + 6k) - (8 + 4k)\n\nSimplify left side: ab + bc - ab - ac = bc - ac = c(b - a)\n\nRight side: (9 - 8) + (6k - 4k) = 1 + 2k\n\nTherefore, equation (4): c(b - a) = 1 + 2k\n\nSimilarly, let's subtract equation (2c) from equation (3c):\n\nEquation (3c) - Equation (2c):\n\n(ac + bc) - (ab + bc) = (5 + 10k) - (9 + 6k)\n\nLeft side: ac + bc - ab - bc = ac - ab = a(c - b)\n\nRight side: (5 - 9) + (10k - 6k) = -4 + 4k\n\nTherefore, equation (5): a(c - b) = -4 + 4k\n\nSimilarly, subtract equation (1c) from equation (3c):\n\nEquation (3c) - Equation (1c):\n\n(ac + bc) - (ab + ac) = (5 + 10k) - (8 + 4k)\n\nLeft side: ac + bc - ab - ac = bc - ab = b(c - a)\n\nRight side: (5 - 8) + (10k - 4k) = -3 + 6k\n\nTherefore, equation (6): b(c - a) = -3 + 6k\n\nHmm, so now we have three new equations:\n\n(4): c(b - a) = 1 + 2k\n\n(5): a(c - b) = -4 + 4k\n\n(6): b(c - a) = -3 + 6k\n\nHmm, okay, so equations (4), (5), (6) are three equations relating a, b, c with k. Let me see if I can express some variables in terms of others here.\n\nAlternatively, note that equations (4), (5), (6) can perhaps be combined in some way. Let me see.\n\nLet me write equations (4), (5), (6):\n\n(4): c(b - a) = 1 + 2k\n\n(5): a(c - b) = -4 + 4k\n\n(6): b(c - a) = -3 + 6k\n\nHmm, perhaps express (c - b) from equation (5), and (b - a) from equation (4), and (c - a) from equation (6)?\n\nAlternatively, maybe express (b - a), (c - b), (c - a) in terms of the right-hand sides divided by the coefficients.\n\nAlternatively, let me see if I can write equations (4), (5), (6) as:\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet me set:\n\nLet’s call equation (4): c(b - a) = 1 + 2k ⇒ (b - a) = (1 + 2k)/c\n\nEquation (5): a(c - b) = -4 + 4k ⇒ (c - b) = (-4 + 4k)/a\n\nEquation (6): b(c - a) = -3 + 6k ⇒ (c - a) = (-3 + 6k)/b\n\nHmm, so now we have expressions for (b - a), (c - b), and (c - a) in terms of a, b, c, and k. Perhaps we can use these to form a system?\n\nAlternatively, notice that (c - a) can also be written as (c - b) + (b - a). Let me see:\n\nIndeed, (c - a) = (c - b) + (b - a). Therefore,\n\nFrom above:\n\n(c - a) = [(-4 + 4k)/a] + [(1 + 2k)/c]\n\nBut according to equation (6), (c - a) = (-3 + 6k)/b\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n[(-4 + 4k)/a] + [(1 + 2k)/c] = (-3 + 6k)/b\n\nHmm, that's a bit messy, but maybe we can substitute variables or find another relation.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps express all variables in terms of one variable?\n\nAlternatively, let me see if I can find ratios between a, b, c.\n\nAlternatively, let me think of equations (4), (5), (6) as linear equations if we consider variables (b - a), (c - b), etc., but not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps introduce variables like p = a + b + c, q = ab + bc + ca, r = abc? Maybe that's overcomplicating, but let's see.\n\nWait, but in equations (1c), (2c), (3c), we have:\n\nEquation (1c): ab + ac = 8 + 4k ⇒ ab + ac = a(b + c) = 8 + 4k, which is the original equation (1a). But in terms of symmetric sums, if we let S = a + b + c, P = ab + bc + ca, and Q = abc, then:\n\nBut equation (1c): ab + ac = a(b + c) = a(S - a) = 8 + 4k\n\nSimilarly, equation (2c): bc + ab = b(c + a) = b(S - b) = 9 + 6k\n\nEquation (3c): ac + bc = c(a + b) = c(S - c) = 5 + 10k\n\nHmm, so each equation is of the form variable*(S - variable) = some constant. So, for example:\n\na(S - a) = 8 + 4k,\n\nb(S - b) = 9 + 6k,\n\nc(S - c) = 5 + 10k.\n\nHmm, that seems like a system where each variable is related to S through a quadratic equation.\n\nSpecifically, for variable a:\n\na(S - a) = 8 + 4k ⇒ -a² + S a - (8 + 4k) = 0,\n\nSimilarly for b:\n\n- b² + S b - (9 + 6k) = 0,\n\nAnd for c:\n\n- c² + S c - (5 + 10k) = 0.\n\nSo each of a, b, c is a root of a quadratic equation with the same coefficient for the linear term (which is S) and different constants.\n\nTherefore, the variables a, b, c are three different roots of the equation:\n\n- t² + S t - (C) = 0,\n\nwhere C is 8 + 4k, 9 + 6k, or 5 + 10k respectively for each variable.\n\nBut since a, b, c are three different roots, but a quadratic equation can have at most two distinct roots. Therefore, unless two of a, b, c are equal, but since they are roots of different quadratics (with different constants), so they can’t be the same root. Therefore, this seems problematic.\n\nWait, that suggests that perhaps my approach here is flawed? Because if a, b, c must each satisfy their own quadratic equation with the same S but different constants, then unless they are equal or something, but they can’t be equal because the constants differ. Hmm, so perhaps this approach is not helpful?\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a smarter substitution. Let me think again.\n\nWe have three equations:\n\n(1c): ab + ac = 8 + 4k,\n\n(2c): ab + bc = 9 + 6k,\n\n(3c): ac + bc = 5 + 10k.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet me call the left-hand sides as follows:\n\nLet’s let:\n\nEquation (1c): ab + ac = 8 + 4k ⇒ ab + ac = 8 + 4k,\n\nEquation (2c): ab + bc = 9 + 6k,\n\nEquation (3c): ac + bc = 5 + 10k.\n\nLet me denote the three left-hand sides as follows:\n\nLet me set:\n\nX = ab + ac,\n\nY = ab + bc,\n\nZ = ac + bc,\n\nThen, X = 8 + 4k,\n\nY = 9 + 6k,\n\nZ = 5 + 10k.\n\nAlso, note that X + Y + Z = 2(ab + bc + ca),\n\nsince X + Y + Z = (ab + ac) + (ab + bc) + (ac + bc) = 2ab + 2ac + 2bc = 2(ab + ac + bc).\n\nTherefore, X + Y + Z = 2(ab + bc + ca),\n\nSo, ab + bc + ca = (X + Y + Z)/2.\n\nLet me compute X + Y + Z:\n\nX + Y + Z = (8 + 4k) + (9 + 6k) + (5 + 10k) = (8 + 9 + 5) + (4k + 6k + 10k) = 22 + 20k.\n\nTherefore, ab + bc + ca = (22 + 20k)/2 = 11 + 10k.\n\nSo, we have that ab + bc + ca = 11 + 10k. --- (7)\n\nAdditionally, if we can find ab, bc, and ca individually, we might be able to find a, b, c?\n\nAlternatively, note that from equations (1c), (2c), (3c):\n\nWe can solve for each product:\n\nFrom (1c): ab + ac = 8 + 4k ⇒ ab = 8 + 4k - ac. Hmm, but that might not help directly.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider subtracting equations to get the products?\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nLet me write equations (1c), (2c), (3c):\n\n(1c): ab + ac = 8 + 4k,\n\n(2c): ab + bc = 9 + 6k,\n\n(3c): ac + bc = 5 + 10k,\n\nLet me try to solve for ab, bc, and ac.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet me call:\n\nLet’s set:\n\nLet’s let P = ab,\n\nQ = bc,\n\nR = ac,\n\nThen, equations become:\n\n(1c): P + R = 8 + 4k,\n\n(2c): P + Q = 9 + 6k,\n\n(3c): R + Q = 5 + 10k,\n\nSo now, we have a system of three equations with three variables P, Q, R:\n\n1. P + R = 8 + 4k,\n\n2. P + Q = 9 + 6k,\n\n3. Q + R = 5 + 10k.\n\nThis is a linear system in variables P, Q, R. Let me write it as:\n\nEquation 1: P + R = 8 + 4k,\n\nEquation 2: P + Q = 9 + 6k,\n\nEquation 3: Q + R = 5 + 10k.\n\nWe can solve this system for P, Q, R. Let me do that.\n\nLet me subtract equation 1 from equation 2:\n\n(Equation 2) - (Equation 1):\n\n(P + Q) - (P + R) = (9 + 6k) - (8 + 4k),\n\nWhich simplifies to:\n\nQ - R = 1 + 2k. --- (4d)\n\nSimilarly, subtract equation 3 from equation 2:\n\n(Equation 2) - (Equation 3):\n\n(P + Q) - (Q + R) = (9 + 6k) - (5 + 10k),\n\nSimplifies to:\n\nP - R = 4 - 4k. --- (5d)\n\nAlso, from equation 1: P + R = 8 + 4k.\n\nSo now, we have:\n\nFrom (4d): Q - R = 1 + 2k,\n\nFrom (5d): P - R = 4 - 4k,\n\nAnd from equation 1: P + R = 8 + 4k.\n\nSo, let me write equations (4d), (5d), and equation 1 as:\n\n(1): P + R = 8 + 4k,\n\n(5d): P - R = 4 - 4k,\n\n(4d): Q - R = 1 + 2k.\n\nLet me first solve equations (1) and (5d) for P and R.\n\nAdding equations (1) and (5d):\n\n(P + R) + (P - R) = (8 + 4k) + (4 - 4k),\n\nSimplifies to:\n\n2P = 12,\n\nTherefore, P = 6.\n\nThen, substituting back into equation (1): 6 + R = 8 + 4k ⇒ R = 8 + 4k - 6 = 2 + 4k.\n\nSimilarly, from equation (5d): P - R = 4 - 4k,\n\nSubstituting P = 6 and R = 2 + 4k,\n\n6 - (2 + 4k) = 4 - 4k,\n\nWhich simplifies to 4 - 4k = 4 - 4k, so that checks out.\n\nNow, from equation (4d): Q - R = 1 + 2k,\n\nWe have R = 2 + 4k, so:\n\nQ - (2 + 4k) = 1 + 2k ⇒ Q = 1 + 2k + 2 + 4k = 3 + 6k.\n\nTherefore, we have found:\n\nP = ab = 6,\n\nQ = bc = 3 + 6k,\n\nR = ac = 2 + 4k.\n\nSo, now we know ab, bc, and ac in terms of k. Now, can we find a, b, c?\n\nYes, perhaps. Let me see.\n\nWe have:\n\nab = 6,\n\nbc = 3 + 6k,\n\nac = 2 + 4k.\n\nWe can solve for a, b, c in terms of k.\n\nLet me note that if we have ab, bc, and ac, then we can write:\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet me write:\n\nFrom ab = 6 ⇒ a = 6/b,\n\nFrom bc = 3 + 6k ⇒ c = (3 + 6k)/b,\n\nFrom ac = 2 + 4k,\n\nBut a = 6/b and c = (3 + 6k)/b, so substituting into ac:\n\n(6/b) * [(3 + 6k)/b] = 2 + 4k,\n\nWhich is:\n\n6*(3 + 6k) / b² = 2 + 4k,\n\nTherefore,\n\nb² = [6*(3 + 6k)] / (2 + 4k),\n\nSimplify numerator and denominator:\n\nNumerator: 6*(3 + 6k) = 18 + 36k,\n\nDenominator: 2 + 4k = 2(1 + 2k),\n\nTherefore,\n\nb² = (18 + 36k) / [2(1 + 2k)] = [18(1 + 2k)] / [2(1 + 2k)] ) = 18/2 = 9,\n\nWait, really?\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nWait, numerator: 18 + 36k = 18(1 + 2k),\n\nDenominator: 2(1 + 2k),\n\nTherefore,\n\nb² = [18(1 + 2k)] / [2(1 + 2k)] ) = 18/2 = 9,\n\nProvided that 1 + 2k ≠ 0, which it isn't, because k = log_2 3, which is positive (since 3 > 1, so log_2 3 > 0), so 1 + 2k is definitely positive.\n\nTherefore, b² = 9 ⇒ b = 3 or b = -3.\n\nBut since b = log_2 y, and y is a positive real number, so log_2 y is real, but can it be negative? Well, if y is between 0 and 1, then log_2 y is negative, but the problem states that x, y, z are positive real numbers, but doesn't specify they have to be greater than 1. So technically, b could be negative, but let me see.\n\nWait, but let's check if b = 3 or b = -3 is possible.\n\nFirst, let me see if b = 3 is possible.\n\nCase 1: b = 3.\n\nThen, from ab = 6, we have a = 6 / b = 6 / 3 = 2.\n\nFrom bc = 3 + 6k ⇒ c = (3 + 6k)/b = (3 + 6k)/3 = 1 + 2k.\n\nFrom ac = 2 + 4k ⇒ Let's verify with a = 2 and c = 1 + 2k,\n\nac = 2*(1 + 2k) = 2 + 4k, which matches. Good.\n\nTherefore, in this case, with b = 3, we have a = 2 and c = 1 + 2k.\n\nNow, let's check the other case:\n\nCase 2: b = -3.\n\nThen, from ab = 6 ⇒ a = 6 / (-3) = -2.\n\nFrom bc = 3 + 6k ⇒ c = (3 + 6k)/(-3) = -1 - 2k.\n\nFrom ac = 2 + 4k ⇒ a * c = (-2)*(-1 - 2k) = 2 + 4k, which also matches.\n\nSo, in this case, a = -2, b = -3, c = -1 - 2k.\n\nBut now, recall that a = log_2 x, b = log_2 y, c = log_2 z.\n\nSince x, y, z are positive real numbers, their logarithms can be any real numbers (positive or negative). However, we need to ensure that when we compute x, y, z from a, b, c, they are positive, which they will be as long as a, b, c are real, which they are. However, we might have constraints from the original equations?\n\nWait, but the problem says \"positive real numbers\", so x, y, z just need to be positive, so a, b, c can be any real numbers. So both cases are mathematically possible? Hmm.\n\nHowever, we are to compute the smallest possible value of x y z. Since x y z = 2^a * 2^b * 2^c = 2^{a + b + c}. Therefore, x y z = 2^{S}, where S = a + b + c.\n\nTherefore, to minimize x y z, we need to minimize S = a + b + c.\n\nTherefore, in each case, we can compute S and see which one gives the smaller value.\n\nFirst, let's analyze Case 1: b = 3, a = 2, c = 1 + 2k.\n\nThen, S = a + b + c = 2 + 3 + (1 + 2k) = 6 + 2k.\n\nCase 2: b = -3, a = -2, c = -1 - 2k.\n\nThen, S = (-2) + (-3) + (-1 - 2k) = -6 - 2k.\n\nTherefore, in Case 1, S = 6 + 2k, and in Case 2, S = -6 - 2k.\n\nSince k = log_2 3, which is a positive number (since 3 > 1), so 2k is positive, so in Case 1, S is positive, and in Case 2, S is negative. Since x y z = 2^{S}, and 2^{S} is an exponential function which is decreasing when the exponent is negative and increasing when the exponent is positive. Since we want the smallest possible value of x y z, which would be the smaller of 2^{6 + 2k} and 2^{-6 - 2k}.\n\nSince 2^{-6 - 2k} is much smaller than 2^{6 + 2k}, because the exponent in the second case is negative, so 2 to a negative exponent is 1 over 2 to a positive exponent. Therefore, 2^{-6 - 2k} = 1 / 2^{6 + 2k}, which is smaller than 2^{6 + 2k}. Therefore, the minimal x y z would be in Case 2, where S = -6 - 2k, so x y z = 2^{-6 - 2k}.\n\nBut wait a minute, hold on. Wait, but we need to check if in Case 2, the values of a, b, c actually satisfy the original equations. Because even though algebraically we found solutions for a, b, c in terms of k, we need to ensure that when we plug them back into the original equations (1a), (2a), (3a), they hold, considering that k is log_2 3, which is a constant.\n\nWait, actually, k is a constant here (log_2 3), so in Case 2, we have expressions for a, b, c in terms of k, so we can check whether those expressions satisfy the original equations?\n\nWait, but actually, in the process above, when we solved for P, Q, R (which are ab, bc, ac), we used the equations (1c), (2c), (3c), which were derived from the original equations by taking log base 2 and substituting a, b, c = log_2 x, log_2 y, log_2 z. So as long as the substitutions are valid, then the solutions for P, Q, R should be valid, and hence the solutions for a, b, c should be valid as long as the steps are reversible. Since we didn't divide by anything that could be zero, and since we considered both possibilities for b (positive and negative roots), then both cases should be valid? Hmm.\n\nBut let me verify with the original equations. Let me pick Case 2 and plug back into the original equations to see if they hold.\n\nFirst, recall that k = log_2 3.\n\nCase 2: a = -2, b = -3, c = -1 - 2k.\n\nTherefore, x = 2^a = 2^{-2} = 1/4,\n\ny = 2^b = 2^{-3} = 1/8,\n\nz = 2^c = 2^{-1 - 2k} = 2^{-1} * 2^{-2k} = (1/2) * (2^{-2k}).\n\nBut let's see if these values satisfy the original equations.\n\nFirst original equation: x^{log_2(y z)} = 2^8 * 3^4.\n\nCompute log_2(y z):\n\nFirst, y z = (1/8) * z = (1/8) * [ (1/2) * 2^{-2k} ] = (1/16) * 2^{-2k}.\n\nTherefore, log_2(y z) = log_2( (1/16) * 2^{-2k} ) = log_2(1/16) + log_2(2^{-2k}) = -4 + (-2k) = -4 - 2k.\n\nThen, x^{log_2(y z)} = (1/4)^{-4 - 2k} = (4)^{4 + 2k} = (2^2)^{4 + 2k} = 2^{8 + 4k}.\n\nBut the right-hand side is supposed to be 2^8 * 3^4. However, 2^{8 + 4k} is 2^8 * 2^{4k} = 2^8 * (2^{k})^4.\n\nBut 2^k = 2^{log_2 3} = 3, since 2^{log_2 3} = 3.\n\nTherefore, 2^{4k} = (2^k)^4 = 3^4.\n\nTherefore, indeed, x^{log_2(y z)} = 2^8 * 3^4, which matches the first equation. Great.\n\nSimilarly, check the second equation: y^{log_2(z x)} = 2^9 * 3^6.\n\nCompute log_2(z x):\n\nz x = z * x = [ (1/2) * 2^{-2k} ] * (1/4) = (1/8) * 2^{-2k}.\n\nTherefore, log_2(z x) = log_2(1/8) + log_2(2^{-2k}) = -3 + (-2k) = -3 - 2k.\n\nThen, y^{log_2(z x)} = (1/8)^{-3 - 2k} = 8^{3 + 2k} = (2^3)^{3 + 2k} = 2^{9 + 6k}.\n\nAgain, 2^{6k} = (2^k)^6 = 3^6, so 2^{9 + 6k} = 2^9 * 3^6, which matches the second equation. Nice.\n\nThird equation: z^{log_2(x y)} = 2^5 * 3^{10}.\n\nCompute log_2(x y):\n\nx y = (1/4) * (1/8) = 1/32,\n\nSo log_2(x y) = log_2(1/32) = log_2(2^{-5}) = -5.\n\nTherefore, z^{log_2(x y)} = z^{-5} = [ (1/2) * 2^{-2k} ]^{-5} = [2^{-1} * 2^{-2k}]^{-5} = [2^{-1 - 2k}]^{-5} = 2^{5(1 + 2k)}.\n\nWait, hold on, let me compute that again step by step:\n\nz = (1/2) * 2^{-2k} = 2^{-1} * 2^{-2k} = 2^{-1 - 2k}.\n\nTherefore, z^{-5} = [2^{-1 - 2k}]^{-5} = 2^{5(1 + 2k)}.\n\nBut the right-hand side is supposed to be 2^5 * 3^{10}.\n\nHmm, so 2^{5(1 + 2k)} versus 2^5 * 3^{10}.\n\nWait, let me see:\n\nCompute exponent: 5(1 + 2k) = 5 + 10k.\n\nTherefore, z^{log_2(x y)} = 2^{5 + 10k}.\n\nBut the right-hand side is 2^5 * 3^{10}.\n\nSo, 2^{5 + 10k} vs. 2^5 * 3^{10}.\n\nAgain, 2^{10k} = (2^k)^{10} = 3^{10}, so 2^{5 + 10k} = 2^5 * 2^{10k} = 2^5 * 3^{10}, which matches the third equation. Perfect.\n\nSo Case 2 does satisfy all three original equations. Therefore, even though a, b, c are negative, the equations hold. So that case is valid.\n\nTherefore, in Case 2, we have S = a + b + c = -6 - 2k, so x y z = 2^{S} = 2^{-6 - 2k}.\n\nIn Case 1, we had S = 6 + 2k, so x y z = 2^{6 + 2k}, which is much larger than 2^{-6 - 2k}, so indeed the minimal x y z is 2^{-6 - 2k}.\n\nBut wait, before we conclude that, we need to ensure that there are no other solutions? For example, when we solved for b^2 = 9, we considered both roots b = 3 and b = -3, but are there any other possibilities?\n\nIn the step where we had:\n\nFrom the equations for P, Q, R (ab, bc, ac), we had a linear system which had a unique solution for P, Q, R, given k. Then, when solving for a, b, c, we ended up with a quadratic equation for b^2 = 9, so that gives two solutions for b, and then the rest follows uniquely. So, only two cases. Therefore, those are the only two possibilities. So, the minimal x y z is indeed 2^{-6 - 2k}.\n\nBut let me compute what that is in terms of numbers, since the problem asks for the numerical value of the smallest possible x y z. Wait, but they might want it in terms of exponents? Or as a number? Let me check.\n\nWait, the problem says \"compute the smallest possible value of x y z\" and says to put the final answer in a box. Since the expressions involve log_2 3, which is an irrational number, but perhaps the expression 2^{-6 - 2 log_2 3} can be simplified?\n\nYes, let me see:\n\nFirst, note that 2^{-6 - 2 log_2 3} can be rewritten using exponent rules:\n\n2^{-6} * 2^{-2 log_2 3}.\n\nNow, 2^{-2 log_2 3} can be rewritten as (2^{log_2 3})^{-2} = 3^{-2} = 1/9.\n\nBecause 2^{log_2 3} = 3, so raising that to the -2 gives 1/9.\n\nTherefore,\n\n2^{-6 - 2 log_2 3} = 2^{-6} * (1/9) = (1 / 64) * (1 / 9) = 1 / 576.\n\nWait, really?\n\nLet me verify:\n\nLet me compute exponent: -6 - 2 log_2 3.\n\nFirst, 2 log_2 3 = log_2 3^2 = log_2 9.\n\nTherefore, -6 - log_2 9.\n\nWait, but that might not help. Alternatively, let me see:\n\nWait, 2^{-2 log_2 3} is equal to (2^{log_2 3})^{-2} = 3^{-2} = 1/9, yes, that is correct.\n\nTherefore, 2^{-6 - 2 log_2 3} = 2^{-6} * 2^{-2 log_2 3} = (1/64) * (1/9) = 1/576.\n\nTherefore, the minimal x y z is 1/576.\n\nWait, so that's a numerical value? So, 1/576?\n\nBut let me confirm this step-by-step.\n\nFirst, k = log_2 3,\n\nSo,\n\nx y z = 2^{-6 - 2k} = 2^{-6} * 2^{-2k} = (1/2^6) * (2^{-2k}).\n\nBut 2^{-2k} = (2^k)^{-2} = (3)^{-2} = 1/9,\n\nYes, because 2^k = 2^{log_2 3} = 3,\n\nTherefore, 2^{-2k} = (2^{k})^{-2} = 3^{-2} = 1/9,\n\nTherefore,\n\nx y z = (1/64) * (1/9) = 1/(64*9) = 1/576.\n\nSo, 1/576 is the minimal value?\n\nBut let me confirm with Case 1 as well, just to be thorough.\n\nIn Case 1: a = 2, b = 3, c = 1 + 2k,\n\nTherefore, S = a + b + c = 2 + 3 + 1 + 2k = 6 + 2k,\n\nThus x y z = 2^{6 + 2k} = 2^6 * 2^{2k} = 64 * (2^{k})^2 = 64 * (3)^2 = 64 * 9 = 576.\n\nTherefore, in Case 1, x y z = 576,\n\nIn Case 2, x y z = 1/576,\n\nTherefore, the minimal possible value is 1/576.\n\nBut hold on, the problem says \"the smallest possible value of x y z\". Since 1/576 is much smaller than 576, that must be the answer. But let me just confirm once more that in Case 2, all variables x, y, z are positive real numbers, which they are:\n\nIn Case 2:\n\nx = 2^{-2} = 1/4 > 0,\n\ny = 2^{-3} = 1/8 > 0,\n\nz = 2^{-1 - 2k} = 2^{-1} * 2^{-2k} = (1/2) * (3^{-2}) since 2^{-2k} = 3^{-2} as above,\n\nWait, hold on, actually:\n\nWait, z = 2^{-1 - 2k} = 2^{-1} * 2^{-2k} = (1/2) * (2^{-2k}),\n\nBut 2^{-2k} = (2^{k})^{-2} = 3^{-2} = 1/9,\n\nSo z = (1/2) * (1/9) = 1/18,\n\nWait, hold on, so z = 1/18?\n\nWait, let me compute z step by step:\n\nGiven c = -1 - 2k,\n\nTherefore, z = 2^c = 2^{-1 - 2k} = 2^{-1} * 2^{-2k} = (1/2) * (2^{-2k}).\n\nBut 2^{-2k} = (2^k)^{-2} = 3^{-2} = 1/9,\n\nThus z = (1/2) * (1/9) = 1/18,\n\nTherefore, z = 1/18,\n\nTherefore, x = 1/4, y = 1/8, z = 1/18,\n\nTherefore, x y z = (1/4)(1/8)(1/18) = (1)/(4*8*18) = 1/(576),\n\nWhich is 1/576. So that checks out.\n\nTherefore, indeed, in Case 2, we have x y z = 1/576,\n\nand in Case 1, x y z = 576,\n\nTherefore, the minimal possible value is 1/576.\n\nWait, but hold on a second. The problem says \"the smallest possible value of x y z\". Since x, y, z are positive real numbers, and we have two solutions, one giving x y z = 576 and the other 1/576, then 1/576 is indeed smaller. So that's the answer?\n\nBut let me just think again: Is there any other case where maybe a, b, c could be complex? But no, since x, y, z are positive reals, so a, b, c must be real numbers, so we have considered all real solutions, and only two possibilities from the quadratic.\n\nTherefore, the minimal value is 1/576.\n\nWait, but just to make sure, is there any constraint that I missed which might invalidate Case 2?\n\nFor instance, in the original equations, when we took logs, we assumed that the arguments inside the logarithms are positive, which they are because x, y, z are positive. So log_2(y z) is defined because y z is positive, etc. So all steps are valid.\n\nTherefore, I think that 1/576 is indeed the minimal value.\n\nBut let me see if the problem expects an integer or a fraction? 1/576 is a fraction, but maybe it can be simplified? 1 and 576 share no common factors besides 1, so 1/576 is the simplest form.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made an error in the algebra when simplifying 2^{-6 - 2 log_2 3}?\n\nLet me verify that step again:\n\nWe had x y z = 2^{a + b + c} = 2^{-6 - 2k}, where k = log_2 3.\n\nSo exponent is -6 - 2k.\n\nExpressed as:\n\n2^{-6 - 2k} = 2^{-6} * 2^{-2k}\n\nNow, 2^{-2k} = (2^{k})^{-2} = (3)^{-2} because 2^{k} = 2^{log_2 3} = 3.\n\nTherefore, 2^{-2k} = 1/3^2 = 1/9,\n\nTherefore, 2^{-6} * 1/9 = (1/64) * (1/9) = 1/576.\n\nYes, that seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem expects the answer in terms of exponents with base 2 and 3? But 1/576 is 1/(24^2) or 1/(2^6 * 3^2), but I don't think that's necessary. Since 1/576 is a perfectly valid answer and it's a reduced fraction, so I think that's the answer.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made a mistake in the sign when computing S in Case 2?\n\nCase 2:\n\na = -2,\n\nb = -3,\n\nc = -1 - 2k,\n\nTherefore, S = a + b + c = (-2) + (-3) + (-1 - 2k) = -6 - 2k,\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nSo exponent is -6 - 2k,\n\nTherefore, 2^{-6 - 2k} = 1/(2^{6 + 2k}) = 1/(2^6 * 2^{2k}) = 1/(64 * (2^k)^2) = 1/(64 * 3^2) = 1/(64 * 9) = 1/576,\n\nYes, so that's correct.\n\nTherefore, the minimal value is 1/576.\n\nWait, but just to make sure, let me compute x y z in Case 2 numerically with actual numbers.\n\nGiven that k = log_2 3 ≈ 1.58496,\n\nTherefore, c = -1 - 2k ≈ -1 - 3.16992 ≈ -4.16992,\n\nTherefore, z = 2^{-4.16992} ≈ 2^{-4} * 2^{-0.16992} ≈ (1/16) * (0.877) ≈ 0.0548,\n\nSimilarly, x = 1/4 = 0.25,\n\ny = 1/8 = 0.125,\n\nTherefore, x y z ≈ 0.25 * 0.125 * 0.0548 ≈ 0.25 * 0.00685 ≈ 0.0017125,\n\nBut 1/576 ≈ 0.001736111...,\n\nHmm, so my approximate calculation gives about 0.0017125 versus 1/576 ≈ 0.0017361,\n\nThere's a slight discrepancy due to rounding errors in the approximation of k and hence c.\n\nBut that's okay, since my approximate calculation is rough.\n\nAlternatively, let me compute 1/576 exactly: 1 divided by 576 is approximately 0.001736111...\n\nWhereas with more precise calculation:\n\nLet me compute c = -1 - 2k,\n\nk = log_2 3 ≈ 1.5849625007211563,\n\nTherefore, 2k ≈ 3.1699250014423126,\n\nThus c ≈ -1 - 3.1699250014423126 ≈ -4.1699250014423126,\n\nTherefore, z = 2^{-4.1699250014423126} = e^{-4.1699250014423126 * ln 2}.\n\nCompute exponent: ln 2 ≈ 0.69314718056,\n\nThus exponent ≈ -4.1699250014423126 * 0.69314718056 ≈\n\nFirst compute 4.1699250014423126 * 0.69314718056,\n\nLet me compute 4 * 0.69314718056 ≈ 2.77258872224,\n\n0.1699250014423126 * 0.69314718056 ≈ approx 0.1699 * 0.6931 ≈ 0.118,\n\nTherefore total ≈ 2.77258872224 + 0.118 ≈ 2.8906,\n\nThus exponent ≈ -2.8906,\n\nTherefore z ≈ e^{-2.8906} ≈ e^{-2} * e^{-0.8906} ≈ (0.1353) * (0.411) ≈ 0.0557,\n\nTherefore, z ≈ 0.0557,\n\nx = 0.25,\n\ny = 0.125,\n\nTherefore, x y z ≈ 0.25 * 0.125 * 0.0557 ≈ 0.25 * 0.0069625 ≈ 0.0017656,\n\nWhich is closer to 1/576 ≈ 0.00173611..., so the difference is due to the approximation in c.\n\nTherefore, my exact calculation gives 1/576, and the approximate calculation is close, so that seems okay.\n\nTherefore, I think I can be confident that the minimal value is indeed 1/576.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{576}}.\n\nBut wait, hold on, the problem says \"compute the smallest possible value of x y z\". Is there a possibility that there are other solutions where x y z is even smaller?\n\nWait, in the process above, when we solved for b^2 = 9, we considered both roots, and found two solutions. Since the system of equations for a, b, c was linear after substituting P, Q, R, so once P, Q, R are fixed, then the equations for a, b, c lead to a quadratic in b, which only has two solutions. Therefore, there are only two solutions for (a, b, c), hence two solutions for (x, y, z), so those are the only two possibilities, so 1/576 is indeed the minimal.\n\nAlternatively, suppose that when we took logs, we might have introduced some restrictions? For example, when we took log_2 of both sides of the original equations, we assumed that the arguments were positive, which they are, since x, y, z are positive, so y z, z x, x y are all positive. So that's okay.\n\nAlternatively, is there a different approach that might get a different answer? Let me think.\n\nAlternatively, suppose we let’s consider taking the original three equations:\n\n1. \\( x^{\\log_2(y z)} = 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\)\n\n2. \\( y^{\\log_2(z x)} = 2^9 \\cdot 3^6 \\)\n\n3. \\( z^{\\log_2(x y)} = 2^5 \\cdot 3^{10} \\)\n\nLet me take the logarithm of each equation with base 2, as I did before, but perhaps instead of substituting a = log_2 x, etc., maybe take natural logs?\n\nAlternatively, take natural logarithm on both sides of each equation:\n\nLet me denote ln as the natural logarithm.\n\nThen, for the first equation:\n\nln(left side) = ln( x^{log_2(y z)} ) = log_2(y z) * ln x,\n\nAnd ln(right side) = ln(2^8 * 3^4) = 8 ln 2 + 4 ln 3.\n\nSimilarly, log_2(y z) = ln(y z)/ln 2,\n\nTherefore,\n\n[ ln(y z)/ln 2 ] * ln x = 8 ln 2 + 4 ln 3,\n\nMultiply both sides by ln 2:\n\nln(y z) * ln x = (8 ln 2 + 4 ln 3) * ln 2,\n\nSimilarly for the other equations.\n\nBut this seems more complicated. Alternatively, perhaps my first approach was better.\n\nAlternatively, let me think of variables as exponents.\n\nLet me suppose that x = 2^{a} 3^{b}, y = 2^{c} 3^{d}, z = 2^{e} 3^{f}, since the right-hand sides involve only primes 2 and 3, so maybe x, y, z can be expressed in terms of 2 and 3?\n\nLet me try that.\n\nLet me set:\n\nx = 2^{a} 3^{b},\n\ny = 2^{c} 3^{d},\n\nz = 2^{e} 3^{f},\n\nwhere a, b, c, d, e, f are real numbers.\n\nThen, since x, y, z are positive reals, this is a valid representation.\n\nNow, let me compute log_2(y z):\n\nlog_2(y z) = log_2( y ) + log_2( z ) = log_2( 2^{c} 3^{d} ) + log_2( 2^{e} 3^{f} ) = (c + d log_2 3) + (e + f log_2 3) = (c + e) + (d + f) log_2 3.\n\nSimilarly, x^{log_2(y z)} = [2^{a} 3^{b}]^{ [ (c + e) + (d + f) log_2 3 ] }.\n\nHmm, this seems messy, but let me see.\n\nWait, but the right-hand side of the first equation is 2^8 * 3^4.\n\nTherefore, equating exponents for prime 2 and 3:\n\nThe left-hand side, x^{log_2(y z)}, when expanded, would have exponents for prime 2 and 3 as follows:\n\nFor prime 2: a * log_2(y z),\n\nFor prime 3: b * log_2(y z),\n\nSimilarly, the right-hand side has exponents 8 for prime 2 and 4 for prime 3.\n\nTherefore, equating exponents:\n\nFor prime 2:\n\na * log_2(y z) = 8,\n\nFor prime 3:\n\nb * log_2(y z) = 4.\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation:\n\ny^{log_2(z x)} = 2^9 * 3^6,\n\nSimilarly, log_2(z x) = log_2(z) + log_2(x) = (e + f log_2 3) + (a + b log_2 3) = (e + a) + (f + b) log_2 3,\n\nThen, the exponent of 2 in y^{log_2(z x)} is c * log_2(z x),\n\nand exponent of 3 is d * log_2(z x),\n\nWhich must equal 9 and 6 respectively,\n\nThus:\n\nc * log_2(z x) = 9,\n\nd * log_2(z x) = 6.\n\nSimilarly, third equation:\n\nz^{log_2(x y)} = 2^5 * 3^{10},\n\nlog_2(x y) = log_2 x + log_2 y = (a + b log_2 3) + (c + d log_2 3) = (a + c) + (b + d) log_2 3,\n\nThen exponent of 2 in z^{log_2(x y)} is e * log_2(x y),\n\nExponent of 3 is f * log_2(x y),\n\nWhich must equal 5 and 10 respectively,\n\nTherefore:\n\ne * log_2(x y) = 5,\n\nf * log_2(x y) = 10.\n\nHmm, so now we have a system of equations involving a, b, c, d, e, f and log_2 3.\n\nThis seems more complicated, but perhaps we can find relations between variables.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet me let’s denote L1 = log_2(y z),\n\nL2 = log_2(z x),\n\nL3 = log_2(x y),\n\nThen, from the first set of equations:\n\na * L1 = 8,\n\nb * L1 = 4,\n\nSimilarly,\n\nFrom second equation:\n\nc * L2 = 9,\n\nd * L2 = 6,\n\nThird equation:\n\ne * L3 = 5,\n\nf * L3 = 10.\n\nAdditionally, note that L1, L2, L3 are related through the definitions:\n\nL1 = log_2(y z) = log_2 y + log_2 z = (c + e) + (d + f) log_2 3,\n\nL2 = log_2(z x) = log_2 z + log_2 x = (e + a) + (f + b) log_2 3,\n\nL3 = log_2(x y) = log_2 x + log_2 y = (a + c) + (b + d) log_2 3.\n\nHmm, this seems like a system with many variables but perhaps we can find ratios or express variables in terms of others.\n\nLet me see:\n\nFrom the first equation set (for x):\n\nWe have a * L1 = 8,\n\nand b * L1 = 4,\n\nTherefore, dividing the two equations: (a * L1)/(b * L1) = 8/4 ⇒ a/b = 2 ⇒ a = 2b.\n\nSimilarly, from the second equation set (for y):\n\nc * L2 = 9,\n\nd * L2 = 6,\n\nTherefore, c/d = 9/6 = 3/2 ⇒ c = (3/2) d.\n\nFrom the third equation set (for z):\n\ne * L3 = 5,\n\nf * L3 = 10,\n\nTherefore, e/f = 5/10 = 1/2 ⇒ e = (1/2) f.\n\nSo now, we have:\n\na = 2b,\n\nc = (3/2) d,\n\ne = (1/2) f.\n\nNow, let me express all variables a, c, e in terms of b, d, f.\n\nNow, let me also note that L1, L2, L3 can be expressed in terms of a, b, c, d, e, f as above, but since we have a, c, e in terms of b, d, f, maybe we can substitute those into the expressions for L1, L2, L3.\n\nLet me proceed step by step.\n\nFirst, let me write expressions for L1, L2, L3 in terms of b, d, f.\n\nFirst, recall:\n\nL1 = log_2(y z) = (c + e) + (d + f) log_2 3,\n\nBut c = (3/2) d,\n\ne = (1/2) f,\n\nTherefore,\n\nc + e = (3/2)d + (1/2)f,\n\nand d + f remains as is.\n\nThus,\n\nL1 = ( (3/2)d + (1/2)f ) + (d + f) log_2 3.\n\nSimilarly,\n\nL2 = log_2(z x) = (e + a) + (f + b) log_2 3,\n\nSubstituting e = (1/2)f and a = 2b,\n\ne + a = (1/2)f + 2b,\n\nand f + b remains,\n\nThus,\n\nL2 = ( (1/2)f + 2b ) + (f + b) log_2 3.\n\nSimilarly,\n\nL3 = log_2(x y) = (a + c) + (b + d) log_2 3,\n\nSubstituting a = 2b and c = (3/2)d,\n\na + c = 2b + (3/2)d,\n\nand b + d remains,\n\nThus,\n\nL3 = ( 2b + (3/2)d ) + (b + d) log_2 3.\n\nNow, recall that from the first equation set:\n\na * L1 = 8,\n\nBut a = 2b, so:\n\n2b * L1 = 8 ⇒ b * L1 = 4,\n\nBut wait, from the first equation set, we already had b * L1 = 4,\n\nWhich is consistent.\n\nSimilarly, from the second equation set:\n\nc * L2 = 9,\n\nBut c = (3/2)d,\n\nThus,\n\n(3/2)d * L2 = 9 ⇒ d * L2 = 6,\n\nWhich matches the second equation set: d * L2 = 6,\n\nSimilarly, third equation set:\n\ne * L3 = 5,\n\ne = (1/2)f,\n\nThus,\n\n(1/2)f * L3 = 5 ⇒ f * L3 = 10,\n\nWhich matches the third equation set: f * L3 = 10,\n\nSo, no new information there.\n\nTherefore, we still need to find relations between b, d, f.\n\nLet me see if I can express L1, L2, L3 in terms of b, d, f and then plug into the equations.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps set variables in terms of a single parameter?\n\nAlternatively, notice that from the first set, since a = 2b,\n\nand from the second set, c = (3/2)d,\n\nand from the third set, e = (1/2)f,\n\nPerhaps we can let’s express everything in terms of b, d, f, and then see if we can find relations between b, d, f using the expressions for L1, L2, L3.\n\nLet me write expressions for L1, L2, L3 again:\n\nL1 = ( (3/2)d + (1/2)f ) + (d + f) log_2 3,\n\nL2 = ( (1/2)f + 2b ) + (f + b) log_2 3,\n\nL3 = ( 2b + (3/2)d ) + (b + d) log_2 3.\n\nNow, from the first equation set:\n\nWe have b * L1 = 4,\n\nSimilarly, from the second equation set:\n\nd * L2 = 6,\n\nFrom the third equation set:\n\nf * L3 = 10,\n\nTherefore, we can write:\n\nEquation (A): b * L1 = 4,\n\nEquation (B): d * L2 = 6,\n\nEquation (C): f * L3 = 10.\n\nSo, substituting L1, L2, L3 into these equations gives three equations in variables b, d, f.\n\nLet me write Equation (A):\n\nb * [ ( (3/2)d + (1/2)f ) + (d + f) log_2 3 ] = 4,\n\nSimilarly, Equation (B):\n\nd * [ ( (1/2)f + 2b ) + (f + b) log_2 3 ] = 6,\n\nEquation (C):\n\nf * [ ( 2b + (3/2)d ) + (b + d) log_2 3 ] = 10.\n\nThis is a system of three equations with three variables: b, d, f.\n\nThis looks quite complicated, but perhaps we can find a substitution or ratio between variables.\n\nLet me see if we can assume that b, d, f are proportional to some constants?\n\nAlternatively, let me denote t = log_2 3, to simplify notation.\n\nLet me set t = log_2 3, so t is a constant (~1.58496).\n\nThen, the equations become:\n\nEquation (A):\n\nb * [ ( (3/2)d + (1/2)f ) + (d + f) t ] = 4,\n\nEquation (B):\n\nd * [ ( (1/2)f + 2b ) + (f + b) t ] = 6,\n\nEquation (C):\n\nf * [ ( 2b + (3/2)d ) + (b + d) t ] = 10.\n\nHmm, still complex, but maybe we can find ratios between b, d, f.\n\nLet me suppose that b, d, f are proportional to some constants. Let me assume that b = m * p,\n\nd = n * p,\n\nf = q * p,\n\nfor some constants m, n, q and scaling factor p.\n\nThen, substituting into the equations, perhaps the p will cancel out.\n\nLet me try this.\n\nLet me set:\n\nb = m p,\n\nd = n p,\n\nf = q p,\n\nThen, substituting into Equation (A):\n\n(m p) * [ ( (3/2)(n p) + (1/2)(q p) ) + (n p + q p) t ] = 4,\n\nFactor out p inside the brackets:\n\n= (m p) * [ p ( (3/2 n + 1/2 q ) + (n + q) t ) ] = 4,\n\nWhich is:\n\nm p^2 * [ (3/2 n + 1/2 q ) + (n + q) t ] = 4,\n\nSimilarly, Equation (B):\n\n(n p) * [ ( (1/2)(q p) + 2(m p) ) + (q p + m p) t ] = 6,\n\nFactor p inside the brackets:\n\n= (n p) * [ p ( (1/2 q + 2 m ) + (q + m) t ) ] = 6,\n\nWhich is:\n\nn p^2 * [ (1/2 q + 2 m ) + (q + m) t ] = 6,\n\nEquation (C):\n\n(q p) * [ ( 2(m p) + (3/2)(n p) ) + (m p + n p) t ] = 10,\n\nFactor p inside the brackets:\n\n= (q p) * [ p ( 2 m + (3/2 n ) + (m + n ) t ) ] = 10,\n\nWhich is:\n\nq p^2 * [ 2 m + (3/2 n ) + (m + n ) t ] = 10.\n\nNow, let me denote the coefficients inside the brackets as constants multiplied by p^2. Let me denote:\n\nLet me define:\n\nFor Equation (A): Let A = [ (3/2 n + 1/2 q ) + (n + q) t ]\n\nFor Equation (B): Let B = [ (1/2 q + 2 m ) + (q + m) t ]\n\nFor Equation (C): Let C = [ 2 m + (3/2 n ) + (m + n ) t ]\n\nThen, the equations become:\n\n(A) m p^2 A = 4,\n\n(B) n p^2 B = 6,\n\n(C) q p^2 C = 10.\n\nNow, if we can find constants m, n, q such that the ratios between the left-hand sides correspond to the ratios between the right-hand sides.\n\nLet me consider the ratios of the equations.\n\nFor instance, take Equation (B) divided by Equation (A):\n\n(n p^2 B) / (m p^2 A) ) = 6/4 =