s of logarithms of x, y, z? Let me see.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can take the logarithm (base 2) of both sides of each equation. Let me try that.\n\nStarting with the first equation: \\( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} = 2^{8} \\cdot 3^{4} \\).\n\nTake \\( \\log_{2} \\) of both sides:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}\\left( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} \\right) = \\log_{2}\\left( 2^{8} \\cdot 3^{4} \\right) \\).\n\nUsing logarithm power rule: \\( \\log_{2}(x^{\\log_{2}(y z)}) = \\log_{2}(y z) \\cdot \\log_{2}(x) \\).\n\nAnd on the right-hand side, using logarithm of a product: \\( \\log_{2}(2^{8} \\cdot 3^{4}) = \\log_{2}(2^{8}) + \\log_{2}(3^{4}) = 8 + 4 \\log_{2}(3) \\).\n\nTherefore, the first equation becomes:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}(y z) \\cdot \\log_{2}(x) = 8 + 4 \\log_{2}(3) \\).   [Equation 1']\n\nSimilarly, let me do the same for the second equation: \\( y^{\\log_{2}(z x)} = 2^{9} \\cdot 3^{6} \\).\n\nTake \\( \\log_{2} \\) of both sides:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}\\left( y^{\\log_{2}(z x)} \\right) = \\log_{2}\\left( 2^{9} \\cdot 3^{6} \\right) \\).\n\nAgain, power rule on the left: \\( \\log_{2}(z x) \\cdot \\log_{2}(y) \\).\n\nRight-hand side: \\( \\log_{2}(2^{9}) + \\log_{2}(3^{6}) = 9 + 6 \\log_{2}(3) \\).\n\nTherefore, the second equation becomes:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}(z x) \\cdot \\log_{2}(y) = 9 + 6 \\log_{2}(3) \\).   [Equation 2']\n\nThird equation: \\( z^{\\log_{2}(x y)} = 2^{5} \\cdot 3^{10} \\).\n\nTake \\( \\log_{2} \\) of both sides:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}\\left( z^{\\log_{2}(x y)} \\right) = \\log_{2}\\left( 2^{5} \\cdot 3^{10} \\right) \\).\n\nPower rule on the left: \\( \\log_{2}(x y) \\cdot \\log_{2}(z) \\).\n\nRight-hand side: \\( \\log_{2}(2^{5}) + \\log_{2}(3^{10}) = 5 + 10 \\log_{2}(3) \\).\n\nTherefore, the third equation becomes:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}(x y) \\cdot \\log_{2}(z) = 5 + 10 \\log_{2}(3) \\).   [Equation 3']\n\nOkay, so now I have three equations (Equation 1', Equation 2', Equation 3') each involving products of logarithms of x, y, z and logarithms of 2 and 3.\n\nLet me denote some variabl