3 and k≈0.827, the equation is satisfied.\n\nTherefore, this could be a valid solution.\n\nThus, we can take l=3, then k=3/(p +3),\n\nWith p=log₃2,\n\nTherefore,\n\nm = kp =3p/(p +3),\n\nn = lp=3p,\n\nThus,\n\nm =3p/(p +3),\n\nn=3p,\n\nNow, we can compute T from equation3:\n\nT=(5p +10)/(m +n)= (5p +10)/(3p/(p +3)+3p)= (5p +10)/[3p/(p +3) + 3p*(p +3)/(p +3) ]\n\n= (5p +10)/[ (3p +3p(p +3)) / (p +3) ]\n\n= (5p +10)*(p +3)/[3p +3p(p +3)]\n\nFactor numerator and denominator:\n\nNumerator: (5p +10)(p +3)=5(p +2)(p +3),\n\nDenominator:3p[1 + (p +3)]=3p(p +4),\n\nThus,\n\nT= [5(p +2)(p +3)] / [3p(p +4) ]\n\nTherefore,\n\nT= [5(p +2)(p +3)] / [3p(p +4) ]\n\nSince T = t²,\n\nThus,\n\nt= sqrt[5(p +2)(p +3)/(3p(p +4)) ]\n\nNow, recall that variables q = m t,\n\nr =n t=3p t,\n\ns =p t,\n\nThus,\n\nq= [3p/(p +3)] * t,\n\nr=3p * t,\n\ns=p * t,\n\nWe can now compute A = q + r + s,\n\nA= [3p/(p +3) +3p +p ] t = [3p/(p +3) +4p ] t,\n\n= [3p +4p(p +3)] / (p +3) * t,\n\n= [3p +4p² +12p]/(p +3) *t,\n\n= [4p² +15p]/(p +3) *t,\n\nNow, our goal is to compute xyz,\n\nRecall that x=3^q,\n\ny=3^r,\n\nz=3^s,\n\nThus,\n\nxyz=3^{q + r + s}=3^A,\n\nThus,\n\nxyz=3^A,\n\nSo we need to compute A.\n\nFirst, compute A in terms of p:\n\nA= [4p² +15p]/(p +3) * t,\n\nBut t= sqrt[5(p +2)(p +3)/(3p(p +4)) ],\n\nThus,\n\nA= [4p² +15p]/(p +3) * sqrt[5(p +2)(p +3)/(3p(p +4)) ],\n\nThis expression","num_tokens":32768,"correct":false,"formatted":false},{"predicted_answer":"576","full_generation":"Alright, so I have this problem here with three equations involving logarithms and exponents, and I need to find the smallest possible value of \\( xyz \\) where \\( x \\), \\( y \\), and \\( z \\) are positive real numbers. Let me write down the equations again to make sure I have them right:\n\n1. \\( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} = 2^{8} \\cdot 3^{4} \\)\n2. \\( y^{\\log_{2}(z x)} = 2^{9} \\cdot 3^{6} \\)\n3. \\( z^{\\log_{2}(x y)} = 2^{5} \\cdot 3^{10} \\)\n\nHmm, okay. So each equation has a variable raised to a logarithm (base 2) of the product of the other two variables, and the result is some power of 2 times some power of 3. The goal is to find the minimal value of \\( xyz \\).\n\nFirst, I need to recall some logarithm and exponent properties that might be helpful here. Let me jot down a few key properties:\n\n1. \\( a^{\\log_b c} = c^{\\log_b a} \\) — This is a useful identity that sometimes allows swapping bases and exponents in logarithmic/exponential expressions.\n\n2. \\( \\log_b a = \\frac{\\ln a}{\\ln b} \\) — Change of base formula.\n\n3. \\( \\log_b (a^c) = c \\log_b a \\) — Logarithm of a power.\n\n4. \\( \\log_b (a \\cdot c) = \\log_b a + \\log_b c \\) — Logarithm of a product.\n\n5. \\( \\log_b (a / c) = \\log_b a - \\log_b c \\) — Logarithm of a quotient.\n\n6. \\( b^{\\log_b a} = a \\) — This is the definition of logarithms and exponents.\n\nHmm, so maybe I can take the logarithm of both sides of each equation to bring down the exponents? Since the equations are of the form \\( \\text{something}^{\\text{logarithm}} = \\text{another thing} \\), taking logarithms might be helpful.\n\nLet me try taking the logarithm base 2 of both sides of each equation. Let me start with the first equation:\n\nEquation 1: \\( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} = 2^{8} \\cdot 3^{4} \\)\n\nTake log base 2 of both sides:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}\\left( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} \\right) = \\log_{2}\\left( 2^{8} \\cdot 3^{4} \\right) \\)\n\nUsing logarithm power rule on the left side: \\( \\log_{2}(x^{\\log_{2}(y z)}) = \\log_{2}(y z) \\cdot \\log_{2}(x) \\)\n\nWait, actually, hold on. Let me recall the power rule: \\( \\log_b (a^c) = c \\cdot \\log_b a \\). So here, the left side is \\( \\log_{2} \\left( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} \\right) \\), which would be \\( \\log_{2}(y z) \\cdot \\log_{2}(x) \\).\n\nOn the right side, \\( \\log_{2}\\left( 2^{8} \\cdot 3^{4} \\right) \\), I can use the logarithm of a product: \\( \\log_{2}(2^{8}) + \\log_{2}(3^{4}) \\).\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}(2^{8}) = 8 \\cdot \\log_{2}(2) = 8 \\cdot 1 = 8 \\)\n\n\\( \\log_{2}(3^{4}) = 4 \\cdot \\log_{2}(3) \\)\n\nTherefore, the right side simplifies to \\( 8 + 4 \\cdot \\log_{2}(3) \\).\n\nPutting it all together, after taking log base 2 of both sides of Equation 1, we get:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}(y z) \\cdot \\log_{2}(x) = 8 + 4 \\cdot \\log_{2}(3) \\)\n\nSimilarly, I can do the same process for Equations 2 and 3.\n\nLet me write that down for Equation 2:\n\nEquation 2: \\( y^{\\log_{2}(z x)} = 2^{9} \\cdot 3^{6} \\)\n\nTake log base 2 of both sides:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}\\left( y^{\\log_{2}(z x)} \\right) = \\log_{2}\\left( 2^{9} \\cdot 3^{6} \\right) \\)\n\nLeft side: \\( \\log_{2}(z x) \\cdot \\log_{2}(y) \\)\n\nRight side: \\( \\log_{2}(2^{9}) + \\log_{2}(3^{6}) = 9 \\cdot \\log_{2}(2) + 6 \\cdot \\log_{2}(3) = 9 + 6 \\cdot \\log_{2}(3) \\)\n\nTherefore, Equation 2 becomes:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}(z x) \\cdot \\log_{2}(y) = 9 + 6 \\cdot \\log_{2}(3) \\)\n\nSimilarly, Equation 3:\n\nEquation 3: \\( z^{\\log_{2}(x y)} = 2^{5} \\cdot 3^{10} \\)\n\nTake log base 2 of both sides:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}\\left( z^{\\log_{2}(x y)} \\right) = \\log_{2}\\left( 2^{5} \\cdot 3^{10} \\right) \\)\n\nLeft side: \\( \\log_{2}(x y) \\cdot \\log_{2}(z) \\)\n\nRight side: \\( \\log_{2}(2^{5}) + \\log_{2}(3^{10}) = 5 \\cdot \\log_{2}(2) + 10 \\cdot \\log_{2}(3) = 5 + 10 \\cdot \\log_{2}(3) \\)\n\nTherefore, Equation 3 becomes:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}(x y) \\cdot \\log_{2}(z) = 5 + 10 \\cdot \\log_{2}(3) \\)\n\nHmm, so now we have three equations:\n\n1. \\( \\log_{2}(y z) \\cdot \\log_{2}(x) = 8 + 4 \\cdot \\log_{2}(3) \\) — Let me denote \\( A = \\log_{2}(x) \\), \\( B = \\log_{2}(y) \\), \\( C = \\log_{2}(z) \\). Maybe this substitution will make things cleaner? Since we're dealing with logs base 2 of x, y, z, and products like y z, which can be written in terms of logarithms?\n\nAlternatively, maybe let me set variables for \\( \\log_{2}(x) \\), \\( \\log_{2}(y) \\), \\( \\log_{2}(z) \\), so that we can express the equations in terms of these variables. Let me try that.\n\nLet me define:\n\nLet \\( a = \\log_{2}(x) \\), so that \\( x = 2^{a} \\)\n\nLet \\( b = \\log_{2}(y) \\), so that \\( y = 2^{b} \\)\n\nLet \\( c = \\log_{2}(z) \\), so that \\( z = 2^{c} \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( \\log_{2}(y z) = \\log_{2}(y) + \\log_{2}(z) = b + c \\)\n\nSimilarly, \\( \\log_{2}(z x) = \\log_{2}(z) + \\log_{2}(x) = c + a \\)\n\nAnd \\( \\log_{2}(x y) = \\log_{2}(x) + \\log_{2}(y) = a + b \\)\n\nTherefore, substituting back into the equations, the three equations become:\n\n1. Original Equation 1: \\( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} = 2^{8} \\cdot 3^{4} \\)\n\nExpressed in terms of a, b, c:\n\nFirst, \\( x = 2^{a} \\), and \\( \\log_{2}(y z) = b + c \\), so the left side is \\( (2^{a})^{b + c} = 2^{a(b + c)} \\).\n\nThe right side is \\( 2^{8} \\cdot 3^{4} \\). Hmm, but this is a problem because the left side is a power of 2, but the right side has both 2 and 3 as prime factors. Wait, that can't be. Wait, hold on, this seems contradictory.\n\nWait, hold on a second. The left side \\( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} \\) is \\( (2^a)^{b + c} = 2^{a(b + c)} \\), which is a power of 2, but the right side is \\( 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\), which is not a power of 2; it has a factor of 3^4. That can't be possible unless 3^4 is equal to 1, which it isn't. So something is wrong here.\n\nWait a second, that suggests that my substitution or my approach might be flawed? Wait, but the problem states that \\( x \\), \\( y \\), and \\( z \\) are positive real numbers, so they can be any positive real numbers, not necessarily powers of 2. Therefore, when I set \\( x = 2^{a} \\), \\( y = 2^{b} \\), \\( z = 2^{c} \\), that would only account for x, y, z being powers of 2, but they can actually have other prime factors, like 3, so that complicates things.\n\nAh, right! My mistake. So I cannot assume that \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) are powers of 2. They can be any positive real numbers, so they can have other prime factors besides 2. Therefore, my substitution only considers the power of 2 component, but the equations also involve 3's. So perhaps I need a different substitution or a different approach.\n\nHmm, maybe I should instead express \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) in terms of their prime factorizations? Since the right-hand sides of the equations are powers of 2 and 3, perhaps \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) can be expressed as products of powers of 2 and 3? Let me try that.\n\nSuppose that:\n\nLet \\( x = 2^{a} \\cdot 3^{d} \\)\n\n\\( y = 2^{b} \\cdot 3^{e} \\)\n\n\\( z = 2^{c} \\cdot 3^{f} \\)\n\nWhere \\( a, b, c, d, e, f \\) are real numbers. Then, we can express each equation in terms of these exponents.\n\nBut this might get complicated with six variables, but perhaps manageable. Let me try.\n\nFirst, let me write down each equation with the substitutions.\n\nFirst Equation: \\( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} = 2^{8} \\cdot 3^{4} \\)\n\nFirst, compute \\( \\log_{2}(y z) \\).\n\nGiven that \\( y = 2^{b} \\cdot 3^{e} \\) and \\( z = 2^{c} \\cdot 3^{f} \\), then:\n\n\\( y z = (2^{b} \\cdot 3^{e}) \\cdot (2^{c} \\cdot 3^{f}) = 2^{b + c} \\cdot 3^{e + f} \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( \\log_{2}(y z) = \\log_{2}(2^{b + c} \\cdot 3^{e + f}) \\)\n\nUsing logarithm of a product: \\( \\log_{2}(2^{b + c}) + \\log_{2}(3^{e + f}) \\)\n\nWhich is \\( (b + c) \\cdot \\log_{2}(2) + (e + f) \\cdot \\log_{2}(3) \\)\n\nSince \\( \\log_{2}(2) = 1 \\), and \\( \\log_{2}(3) \\) is just a constant (let me denote \\( \\log_{2}(3) = k \\), where \\( k \\) is a constant approximately equal to 1.58496, but we can keep it as \\( k \\) for now). So:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}(y z) = (b + c) + k(e + f) \\)\n\nTherefore, the exponent in the first equation, \\( \\log_{2}(y z) \\), is \\( (b + c) + k(e + f) \\).\n\nNow, the left side of Equation 1 is \\( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} = [2^{a} \\cdot 3^{d}]^{\\log_{2}(y z)} \\)\n\nWhich is \\( 2^{a \\cdot \\log_{2}(y z)} \\cdot 3^{d \\cdot \\log_{2}(y z)} \\)\n\nBut \\( \\log_{2}(y z) = (b + c) + k(e + f) \\), so substituting:\n\nLeft side becomes \\( 2^{a[(b + c) + k(e + f)]} \\cdot 3^{d[(b + c) + k(e + f)]} \\)\n\nThe right side of Equation 1 is \\( 2^{8} \\cdot 3^{4} \\), so equating the exponents of the primes 2 and 3 on both sides, we get two equations:\n\nFor the exponent of 2:\n\n\\( a[(b + c) + k(e + f)] = 8 \\)\n\nFor the exponent of 3:\n\n\\( d[(b + c) + k(e + f)] = 4 \\)\n\nSimilarly, we can perform the same process for Equations 2 and 3.\n\nLet me write down the equations for the exponents of 2 and 3 for each equation.\n\nFirst, let me note that \\( k = \\log_{2}(3) \\), which is a constant. Let me keep that in mind.\n\nEquation 1 gives us two equations:\n\n1a. \\( a[(b + c) + k(e + f)] = 8 \\)\n\n1b. \\( d[(b + c) + k(e + f)] = 4 \\)\n\nNow, moving on to Equation 2: \\( y^{\\log_{2}(z x)} = 2^{9} \\cdot 3^{6} \\)\n\nFirst, compute \\( \\log_{2}(z x) \\):\n\n\\( z x = (2^{c} \\cdot 3^{f}) \\cdot (2^{a} \\cdot 3^{d}) = 2^{a + c} \\cdot 3^{d + f} \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( \\log_{2}(z x) = \\log_{2}(2^{a + c} \\cdot 3^{d + f}) = (a + c) + k(d + f) \\)\n\nThen, the left side of Equation 2 is \\( y^{\\log_{2}(z x)} = [2^{b} \\cdot 3^{e}]^{\\log_{2}(z x)} = 2^{b \\cdot \\log_{2}(z x)} \\cdot 3^{e \\cdot \\log_{2}(z x)} \\)\n\nPlugging in \\( \\log_{2}(z x) = (a + c) + k(d + f) \\):\n\nLeft side becomes \\( 2^{b[(a + c) + k(d + f)]} \\cdot 3^{e[(a + c) + k(d + f)]} \\)\n\nRight side is \\( 2^{9} \\cdot 3^{6} \\), so equating exponents:\n\n2a. \\( b[(a + c) + k(d + f)] = 9 \\)\n\n2b. \\( e[(a + c) + k(d + f)] = 6 \\)\n\nSimilarly, Equation 3: \\( z^{\\log_{2}(x y)} = 2^{5} \\cdot 3^{10} \\)\n\nFirst, compute \\( \\log_{2}(x y) \\):\n\n\\( x y = (2^{a} \\cdot 3^{d}) \\cdot (2^{b} \\cdot 3^{e}) = 2^{a + b} \\cdot 3^{d + e} \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( \\log_{2}(x y) = \\log_{2}(2^{a + b} \\cdot 3^{d + e}) = (a + b) + k(d + e) \\)\n\nThen, the left side of Equation 3 is \\( z^{\\log_{2}(x y)} = [2^{c} \\cdot 3^{f}]^{\\log_{2}(x y)} = 2^{c \\cdot \\log_{2}(x y)} \\cdot 3^{f \\cdot \\log_{2}(x y)} \\)\n\nSubstituting \\( \\log_{2}(x y) = (a + b) + k(d + e) \\):\n\nLeft side becomes \\( 2^{c[(a + b) + k(d + e)]} \\cdot 3^{f[(a + b) + k(d + e)]} \\)\n\nRight side is \\( 2^{5} \\cdot 3^{10} \\), so equating exponents:\n\n3a. \\( c[(a + b) + k(d + e)] = 5 \\)\n\n3b. \\( f[(a + b) + k(d + e)] = 10 \\)\n\nSo now, in total, we have six equations:\n\nFrom Equation 1:\n\n1a. \\( a[(b + c) + k(e + f)] = 8 \\)\n\n1b. \\( d[(b + c) + k(e + f)] = 4 \\)\n\nFrom Equation 2:\n\n2a. \\( b[(a + c) + k(d + f)] = 9 \\)\n\n2b. \\( e[(a + c) + k(d + f)] = 6 \\)\n\nFrom Equation 3:\n\n3a. \\( c[(a + b) + k(d + e)] = 5 \\)\n\n3b. \\( f[(a + b) + k(d + e)] = 10 \\)\n\nWhere \\( k = \\log_{2}(3) \\).\n\nHmm, okay, so this is a system of six equations with six variables: a, b, c, d, e, f. That seems daunting, but perhaps there is some symmetry or substitution that can be done here?\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can let \\( S = a + b + c + d + e + f \\) or something? Not sure yet.\n\nAlternatively, notice that in each of the equations, the terms like \\( (b + c) + k(e + f) \\), \\( (a + c) + k(d + f) \\), and \\( (a + b) + k(d + e) \\) appear as coefficients. Let me denote these terms as follows:\n\nLet me set:\n\nLet \\( P = (b + c) + k(e + f) \\)\n\n\\( Q = (a + c) + k(d + f) \\)\n\n\\( R = (a + b) + k(d + e) \\)\n\nThen, our equations become:\n\n1a. \\( a P = 8 \\)\n\n1b. \\( d P = 4 \\)\n\n2a. \\( b Q = 9 \\)\n\n2b. \\( e Q = 6 \\)\n\n3a. \\( c R = 5 \\)\n\n3b. \\( f R = 10 \\)\n\nAdditionally, we have definitions for P, Q, R in terms of a, b, c, d, e, f:\n\n\\( P = (b + c) + k(e + f) \\)\n\n\\( Q = (a + c) + k(d + f) \\)\n\n\\( R = (a + b) + k(d + e) \\)\n\nHmm, so now we have six equations (the three above plus the three expressions for P, Q, R) with variables a, b, c, d, e, f. Still six variables and six equations, but the equations are nonlinear because of the k terms (since k is a constant, but still, the products like aP, etc., involve variables multiplied by expressions involving variables). So, perhaps we can express some variables in terms of others?\n\nLet me see:\n\nFrom 1a: \\( a = 8 / P \\)\n\nFrom 1b: \\( d = 4 / P \\)\n\nSimilarly,\n\nFrom 2a: \\( b = 9 / Q \\)\n\nFrom 2b: \\( e = 6 / Q \\)\n\nFrom 3a: \\( c = 5 / R \\)\n\nFrom 3b: \\( f = 10 / R \\)\n\nSo, we can express a, d, b, e, c, f in terms of P, Q, R. Then, we can substitute these expressions into the definitions of P, Q, R, which are in terms of a, b, c, d, e, f. That might lead to equations only in P, Q, R, which can then perhaps be solved?\n\nLet me try that.\n\nFirst, let me write:\n\na = 8 / P,\n\nd = 4 / P,\n\nb = 9 / Q,\n\ne = 6 / Q,\n\nc = 5 / R,\n\nf = 10 / R.\n\nNow, substitute these into the definitions of P, Q, R.\n\nFirst, let's compute P:\n\n\\( P = (b + c) + k(e + f) \\)\n\nSubstituting b, c, e, f:\n\n\\( P = \\left( \\frac{9}{Q} + \\frac{5}{R} \\right) + k \\left( \\frac{6}{Q} + \\frac{10}{R} \\right) \\)\n\nSimilarly, compute Q:\n\n\\( Q = (a + c) + k(d + f) \\)\n\nSubstituting a, c, d, f:\n\n\\( Q = \\left( \\frac{8}{P} + \\frac{5}{R} \\right) + k \\left( \\frac{4}{P} + \\frac{10}{R} \\right) \\)\n\nSimilarly, compute R:\n\n\\( R = (a + b) + k(d + e) \\)\n\nSubstituting a, b, d, e:\n\n\\( R = \\left( \\frac{8}{P} + \\frac{9}{Q} \\right) + k \\left( \\frac{4}{P} + \\frac{6}{Q} \\right) \\)\n\nSo now, we have expressions for P, Q, R in terms of P, Q, R themselves. This seems recursive, but perhaps we can set up equations where we can solve for P, Q, R?\n\nLet me denote the expressions:\n\nEquation for P:\n\n\\( P = \\left( \\frac{9}{Q} + \\frac{5}{R} \\right) + k \\left( \\frac{6}{Q} + \\frac{10}{R} \\right) \\)\n\nEquation for Q:\n\n\\( Q = \\left( \\frac{8}{P} + \\frac{5}{R} \\right) + k \\left( \\frac{4}{P} + \\frac{10}{R} \\right) \\)\n\nEquation for R:\n\n\\( R = \\left( \\frac{8}{P} + \\frac{9}{Q} \\right) + k \\left( \\frac{4}{P} + \\frac{6}{Q} \\right) \\)\n\nThis is a system of three equations with three variables P, Q, R. However, these equations are highly nonlinear and involve k, which is \\( \\log_{2}(3) \\). This seems quite complicated. Maybe there is a better approach?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps assuming some proportionalities between the variables? Let me see.\n\nLooking back at the original equations, perhaps there is a way to relate the three equations together without diving into all these variables.\n\nLet me recall the original equations again:\n\n1. \\( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} = 2^{8} \\cdot 3^{4} \\)\n\n2. \\( y^{\\log_{2}(z x)} = 2^{9} \\cdot 3^{6} \\)\n\n3. \\( z^{\\log_{2}(x y)} = 2^{5} \\cdot 3^{10} \\)\n\nNotice that each equation has a similar structure: the left side is a variable raised to the log base 2 of the product of the other two variables, and the right side is a product of powers of 2 and 3.\n\nSuppose we take the product of all three equations together. Let me see what that would look like:\n\nLeft side: \\( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} \\cdot y^{\\log_{2}(z x)} \\cdot z^{\\log_{2}(x y)} \\)\n\nRight side: \\( (2^{8} \\cdot 3^{4}) \\cdot (2^{9} \\cdot 3^{6}) \\cdot (2^{5} \\cdot 3^{10}) = 2^{8+9+5} \\cdot 3^{4+6+10} = 2^{22} \\cdot 3^{20} \\)\n\nSo the product of the left sides equals \\( 2^{22} \\cdot 3^{20} \\). Let me see if I can simplify the left side.\n\nLet me write the left side as:\n\n\\( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} \\cdot y^{\\log_{2}(z x)} \\cdot z^{\\log_{2}(x y)} \\)\n\nHmm, perhaps I can manipulate this expression using properties of exponents and logarithms.\n\nFirst, note that exponents can be combined if the bases are the same, but here the bases are different. Alternatively, perhaps express each term using the same base?\n\nAlternatively, take the logarithm of the entire product to turn it into a sum. Let me try taking the logarithm (base 2) of the left side:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}\\left( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} \\cdot y^{\\log_{2}(z x)} \\cdot z^{\\log_{2}(x y)} \\right) \\)\n\nWhich is equal to:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}\\left( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} \\right) + \\log_{2}\\left( y^{\\log_{2}(z x)} \\right) + \\log_{2}\\left( z^{\\log_{2}(x y)} \\right) \\)\n\nWhich simplifies to:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}(y z) \\cdot \\log_{2}(x) + \\log_{2}(z x) \\cdot \\log_{2}(y) + \\log_{2}(x y) \\cdot \\log_{2}(z) \\)\n\nWait a second, this is exactly the same as the left-hand sides of the equations we had after taking log base 2 of each original equation and summing them up! Let me recall:\n\nEarlier, when I took log base 2 of each equation individually, I got three equations:\n\n1. \\( \\log_{2}(y z) \\cdot \\log_{2}(x) = 8 + 4 \\cdot \\log_{2}(3) \\)\n\n2. \\( \\log_{2}(z x) \\cdot \\log_{2}(y) = 9 + 6 \\cdot \\log_{2}(3) \\)\n\n3. \\( \\log_{2}(x y) \\cdot \\log_{2}(z) = 5 + 10 \\cdot \\log_{2}(3) \\)\n\nTherefore, adding these three equations together:\n\nLeft side: \\( \\log_{2}(y z) \\cdot \\log_{2}(x) + \\log_{2}(z x) \\cdot \\log_{2}(y) + \\log_{2}(x y) \\cdot \\log_{2}(z) \\)\n\nRight side: \\( (8 + 4k) + (9 + 6k) + (5 + 10k) = (8 + 9 + 5) + (4k + 6k + 10k) = 22 + 20k \\), where \\( k = \\log_{2}(3) \\).\n\nBut the left side, as we saw above, is exactly the logarithm (base 2) of the product of the left sides of the original equations, so:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}\\left( \\text{Left Side Product} \\right) = 22 + 20k \\)\n\nTherefore, exponentiating both sides with base 2:\n\n\\( \\text{Left Side Product} = 2^{22 + 20k} \\)\n\nBut we also know that the product of the left sides equals \\( 2^{22} \\cdot 3^{20} \\), as we computed earlier.\n\nTherefore:\n\n\\( 2^{22 + 20k} = 2^{22} \\cdot 3^{20} \\)\n\nHmm, let me see if that holds. Let's write \\( 2^{22 + 20k} = 2^{22} \\cdot 2^{20k} \\), so:\n\n\\( 2^{22} \\cdot 2^{20k} = 2^{22} \\cdot 3^{20} \\)\n\nDivide both sides by \\( 2^{22} \\):\n\n\\( 2^{20k} = 3^{20} \\)\n\nTake the 20th root of both sides:\n\n\\( 2^{k} = 3 \\)\n\nBut \\( k = \\log_{2}(3) \\), so \\( 2^{\\log_{2}(3)} = 3 \\), which is indeed true because \\( 2^{\\log_{2}(3)} = 3 \\). Therefore, this is a valid identity, so the equality holds. That checks out, but it doesn't give us new information. So this approach just confirms the consistency of the equations but doesn't help us solve for x, y, z.\n\nHmm, okay, so perhaps I need to think differently.\n\nLet me go back to the substitution idea with a, b, c, d, e, f but see if I can find some relations between them.\n\nRecall that:\n\nFrom Equations 1a and 1b, we had:\n\n1a. \\( a P = 8 \\)\n\n1b. \\( d P = 4 \\)\n\nTherefore, dividing 1a by 1b, we get:\n\n\\( \\frac{a}{d} = \\frac{8}{4} = 2 \\implies a = 2d \\)\n\nSimilarly, from Equations 2a and 2b:\n\n2a. \\( b Q = 9 \\)\n\n2b. \\( e Q = 6 \\)\n\nTherefore, dividing 2a by 2b:\n\n\\( \\frac{b}{e} = \\frac{9}{6} = \\frac{3}{2} \\implies b = \\frac{3}{2} e \\)\n\nSimilarly, from Equations 3a and 3b:\n\n3a. \\( c R = 5 \\)\n\n3b. \\( f R = 10 \\)\n\nDividing 3a by 3b:\n\n\\( \\frac{c}{f} = \\frac{5}{10} = \\frac{1}{2} \\implies c = \\frac{1}{2} f \\)\n\nSo now we have:\n\na = 2d,\n\nb = (3/2)e,\n\nc = (1/2)f.\n\nSo we can express a, b, c in terms of d, e, f. Let me note that.\n\nLet me set:\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet \\( d = d \\),\n\nthen \\( a = 2d \\),\n\nLet \\( e = e \\),\n\nthen \\( b = \\frac{3}{2} e \\),\n\nLet \\( f = f \\),\n\nthen \\( c = \\frac{1}{2} f \\).\n\nTherefore, all variables a, b, c can be expressed in terms of d, e, f. So now, we have three variables left: d, e, f, and the equations for P, Q, R can now be expressed in terms of d, e, f.\n\nLet me recall the definitions of P, Q, R in terms of a, b, c, d, e, f:\n\n\\( P = (b + c) + k(e + f) \\)\n\nBut substituting b and c in terms of e and f:\n\n\\( b = \\frac{3}{2} e \\), \\( c = \\frac{1}{2} f \\)\n\nTherefore:\n\n\\( P = \\left( \\frac{3}{2} e + \\frac{1}{2} f \\right) + k(e + f) \\)\n\nSimilarly, \\( Q = (a + c) + k(d + f) \\)\n\nSubstituting a and c:\n\n\\( a = 2d \\), \\( c = \\frac{1}{2} f \\)\n\nThus:\n\n\\( Q = \\left( 2d + \\frac{1}{2} f \\right) + k(d + f) \\)\n\nSimilarly, \\( R = (a + b) + k(d + e) \\)\n\nSubstituting a and b:\n\n\\( a = 2d \\), \\( b = \\frac{3}{2} e \\)\n\nThus:\n\n\\( R = \\left( 2d + \\frac{3}{2} e \\right) + k(d + e) \\)\n\nAdditionally, we have expressions for P, Q, R in terms of d, e, f. But also, we have the expressions for a, b, c, d, e, f in terms of P, Q, R from earlier:\n\nRecall that:\n\na = 8 / P,\n\nd = 4 / P,\n\nb = 9 / Q,\n\ne = 6 / Q,\n\nc = 5 / R,\n\nf = 10 / R.\n\nWait a second, so here we have a conflict: on one hand, we expressed a, b, c in terms of d, e, f (as above), and on the other hand, we have expressions for a, d, b, e, c, f in terms of P, Q, R. Therefore, we can set these equal.\n\nLet me write all variables in terms of d, e, f first, and then also in terms of P, Q, R, so that we can set up equations between d, e, f and P, Q, R, then perhaps substitute?\n\nAlternatively, since we have expressions for a, d, b, e, c, f in terms of P, Q, R, and also expressions for a, b, c in terms of d, e, f, we can substitute those into the equations for P, Q, R.\n\nLet me see.\n\nFirst, let me note that:\n\nFrom the expressions above:\n\na = 2d,\n\nbut also a = 8 / P,\n\nso 2d = 8 / P ⇒ d = 4 / P,\n\nwhich is consistent with our earlier result from Equation 1b: d = 4 / P.\n\nSimilarly,\n\nb = (3/2)e,\n\nbut also b = 9 / Q,\n\nso (3/2)e = 9 / Q ⇒ e = (9 / Q) * (2/3) = 6 / Q,\n\nwhich is consistent with Equation 2b: e = 6 / Q.\n\nSimilarly,\n\nc = (1/2)f,\n\nand c = 5 / R,\n\nso (1/2)f = 5 / R ⇒ f = 10 / R,\n\nconsistent with Equation 3b: f = 10 / R.\n\nTherefore, the substitutions are consistent. Therefore, we can proceed by expressing everything in terms of d, e, f, and then substitute into the expressions for P, Q, R, and then use the expressions for P, Q, R in terms of d, e, f to form equations in d, e, f.\n\nAlternatively, since we have expressions for P, Q, R in terms of d, e, f, and also P, Q, R are related to d, e, f through a, b, c, etc., but perhaps this is getting too convoluted. Maybe let's try to write P, Q, R in terms of d, e, f, and then also express P, Q, R in terms of themselves via the substitutions?\n\nAlternatively, let me see if I can write equations only in terms of d, e, f.\n\nFirst, let me recall:\n\nWe have expressions for P, Q, R in terms of d, e, f:\n\n\\( P = \\left( \\frac{3}{2} e + \\frac{1}{2} f \\right) + k(e + f) \\)\n\n\\( Q = \\left( 2d + \\frac{1}{2} f \\right) + k(d + f) \\)\n\n\\( R = \\left( 2d + \\frac{3}{2} e \\right) + k(d + e) \\)\n\nAdditionally, we have expressions for P, Q, R in terms of d, e, f via the definitions from the equations 1a, 1b, etc. Wait, actually, the variables P, Q, R are defined in terms of a, b, c, d, e, f, which are themselves expressed in terms of d, e, f (since a, b, c are expressed via d, e, f). Wait, perhaps we can substitute the expressions for a, b, c into the definitions of P, Q, R?\n\nWait, actually, the definitions of P, Q, R are already in terms of a, b, c, d, e, f, but we have already substituted a, b, c in terms of d, e, f, so the expressions for P, Q, R in terms of d, e, f are already given above. Therefore, we can consider P, Q, R as variables dependent on d, e, f, but we also have the expressions for P, Q, R in terms of a, b, c, d, e, f which have been substituted. Wait, perhaps this is going in circles.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I need to express P, Q, R in terms of d, e, f, and then also express d, e, f in terms of P, Q, R via the equations a = 8 / P, etc.\n\nWait, let me try this.\n\nWe have:\n\nFrom the expressions:\n\nd = 4 / P,\n\ne = 6 / Q,\n\nf = 10 / R,\n\nand also,\n\na = 2d = 2*(4 / P) = 8 / P,\n\nwhich matches the earlier a = 8 / P,\n\nsimilarly,\n\nb = (3/2)e = (3/2)*(6 / Q) = 9 / Q,\n\nwhich matches b = 9 / Q,\n\nand c = (1/2)f = (1/2)*(10 / R) = 5 / R,\n\nwhich matches c = 5 / R.\n\nTherefore, if we can write equations for P, Q, R in terms of d, e, f, and then substitute d = 4/P, e = 6/Q, f = 10/R into those equations, then we can get equations purely in terms of P, Q, R.\n\nLet me try that.\n\nStarting with the expression for P:\n\n\\( P = \\left( \\frac{3}{2} e + \\frac{1}{2} f \\right) + k(e + f) \\)\n\nSubstitute e = 6 / Q and f = 10 / R:\n\n\\( P = \\left( \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{6}{Q} + \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{10}{R} \\right) + k \\left( \\frac{6}{Q} + \\frac{10}{R} \\right) \\)\n\nSimplify the terms inside:\n\nFirst term inside the first parenthesis:\n\n\\( \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{6}{Q} = \\frac{18}{2Q} = \\frac{9}{Q} \\)\n\nSecond term inside the first parenthesis:\n\n\\( \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{10}{R} = \\frac{10}{2R} = \\frac{5}{R} \\)\n\nTherefore, first part: \\( \\frac{9}{Q} + \\frac{5}{R} \\)\n\nSecond part: \\( k \\left( \\frac{6}{Q} + \\frac{10}{R} \\right) \\)\n\nTherefore, overall:\n\n\\( P = \\frac{9}{Q} + \\frac{5}{R} + k \\left( \\frac{6}{Q} + \\frac{10}{R} \\right) \\)\n\nSimilarly, let's do the same substitution for Q and R.\n\nStarting with Q:\n\n\\( Q = \\left( 2d + \\frac{1}{2} f \\right) + k(d + f) \\)\n\nSubstitute d = 4 / P and f = 10 / R:\n\nFirst term inside the first parenthesis:\n\n\\( 2d = 2 \\cdot \\frac{4}{P} = \\frac{8}{P} \\)\n\nSecond term inside the first parenthesis:\n\n\\( \\frac{1}{2} f = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{10}{R} = \\frac{5}{R} \\)\n\nTherefore, first part: \\( \\frac{8}{P} + \\frac{5}{R} \\)\n\nSecond part: \\( k \\left( d + f \\right) = k \\left( \\frac{4}{P} + \\frac{10}{R} \\right) \\)\n\nThus, overall:\n\n\\( Q = \\frac{8}{P} + \\frac{5}{R} + k \\left( \\frac{4}{P} + \\frac{10}{R} \\right) \\)\n\nNow, moving on to R:\n\n\\( R = \\left( 2d + \\frac{3}{2} e \\right) + k(d + e) \\)\n\nSubstitute d = 4 / P and e = 6 / Q:\n\nFirst term inside the first parenthesis:\n\n\\( 2d = 2 \\cdot \\frac{4}{P} = \\frac{8}{P} \\)\n\nSecond term inside the first parenthesis:\n\n\\( \\frac{3}{2} e = \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{6}{Q} = \\frac{18}{2Q} = \\frac{9}{Q} \\)\n\nTherefore, first part: \\( \\frac{8}{P} + \\frac{9}{Q} \\)\n\nSecond part: \\( k(d + e) = k \\left( \\frac{4}{P} + \\frac{6}{Q} \\right) \\)\n\nTherefore, overall:\n\n\\( R = \\frac{8}{P} + \\frac{9}{Q} + k \\left( \\frac{4}{P} + \\frac{6}{Q} \\right) \\)\n\nSo now, we have expressions for P, Q, R in terms of P, Q, R themselves. Let me write them again:\n\n1. \\( P = \\frac{9}{Q} + \\frac{5}{R} + k \\left( \\frac{6}{Q} + \\frac{10}{R} \\right) \\)\n\n2. \\( Q = \\frac{8}{P} + \\frac{5}{R} + k \\left( \\frac{4}{P} + \\frac{10}{R} \\right) \\)\n\n3. \\( R = \\frac{8}{P} + \\frac{9}{Q} + k \\left( \\frac{4}{P} + \\frac{6}{Q} \\right) \\)\n\nThis is still a system of three equations with three variables P, Q, R, but they are highly nonlinear due to the terms like \\( \\frac{1}{Q} \\), \\( \\frac{1}{R} \\), etc., multiplied by constants. This seems very complex to solve algebraically. Maybe we can assume some relation between P, Q, R? For instance, perhaps they are proportional? Or perhaps set variables like \\( u = 1/P \\), \\( v = 1/Q \\), \\( w = 1/R \\), to linearize the equations?\n\nLet me try that substitution. Let me set:\n\nLet \\( u = 1/P \\),\n\n\\( v = 1/Q \\),\n\n\\( w = 1/R \\).\n\nThen, since P, Q, R are in denominators, this might help.\n\nThen, we can rewrite the equations in terms of u, v, w.\n\nFirst, Equation 1:\n\n\\( P = \\frac{9}{Q} + \\frac{5}{R} + k \\left( \\frac{6}{Q} + \\frac{10}{R} \\right) \\)\n\nExpressed in terms of u, v, w:\n\n\\( \\frac{1}{u} = 9 v + 5 w + k (6 v + 10 w) \\)\n\nSimilarly, Equation 2:\n\n\\( Q = \\frac{8}{P} + \\frac{5}{R} + k \\left( \\frac{4}{P} + \\frac{10}{R} \\right) \\)\n\nExpressed in terms of u, v, w:\n\n\\( \\frac{1}{v} = 8 u + 5 w + k (4 u + 10 w) \\)\n\nEquation 3:\n\n\\( R = \\frac{8}{P} + \\frac{9}{Q} + k \\left( \\frac{4}{P} + \\frac{6}{Q} \\right) \\)\n\nExpressed in terms of u, v, w:\n\n\\( \\frac{1}{w} = 8 u + 9 v + k (4 u + 6 v) \\)\n\nSo now, the system becomes:\n\n1. \\( \\frac{1}{u} = (9 + 6k)v + (5 + 10k)w \\)\n\n2. \\( \\frac{1}{v} = (8 + 4k)u + (5 + 10k)w \\)\n\n3. \\( \\frac{1}{w} = (8 + 4k)u + (9 + 6k)v \\)\n\nHmm, still nonlinear, but perhaps we can consider this as a linear system in terms of u, v, w if we treat \\( \\frac{1}{u} \\), \\( \\frac{1}{v} \\), \\( \\frac{1}{w} \\) as variables? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps assume that u, v, w are proportional to some constants? Let me see if there's a pattern in the coefficients.\n\nLooking at the coefficients in Equation 1:\n\nCoefficient of v: \\( 9 + 6k \\)\n\nCoefficient of w: \\( 5 + 10k \\)\n\nSimilarly, in Equation 2:\n\nCoefficient of u: \\( 8 + 4k \\)\n\nCoefficient of w: \\( 5 + 10k \\)\n\nIn Equation 3:\n\nCoefficient of u: \\( 8 + 4k \\)\n\nCoefficient of v: \\( 9 + 6k \\)\n\nNotice that the coefficients of u in Equations 2 and 3 are the same: \\( 8 + 4k \\), and coefficients of v in Equations 1 and 3 are the same: \\( 9 + 6k \\), while the coefficients of w in Equations 1 and 2 are the same: \\( 5 + 10k \\). This symmetry might suggest that u, v, w are related in some symmetric way.\n\nPerhaps we can assume that u = v = w? Let me test that.\n\nAssume u = v = w = m, where m is some constant.\n\nThen, substituting into Equation 1:\n\n\\( \\frac{1}{m} = (9 + 6k)m + (5 + 10k)m = [9 + 6k + 5 + 10k] m = (14 + 16k) m \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( \\frac{1}{m} = (14 + 16k) m \\implies (14 + 16k) m^2 = 1 \\implies m^2 = \\frac{1}{14 + 16k} \\implies m = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14 + 16k}} \\)\n\nSimilarly, substituting into Equation 2:\n\n\\( \\frac{1}{m} = (8 + 4k)m + (5 + 10k)m = [8 + 4k + 5 + 10k] m = (13 + 14k) m \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( \\frac{1}{m} = (13 + 14k) m \\implies (13 + 14k) m^2 = 1 \\implies m^2 = \\frac{1}{13 + 14k} \\)\n\nBut this contradicts the previous result where m^2 was \\( \\frac{1}{14 + 16k} \\). Since \\( 14 + 16k \\neq 13 + 14k \\), unless k has a specific value, which it doesn't (k is log₂3 ≈1.58496), so this assumption of u = v = w is invalid.\n\nHmm, okay, so that doesn't work. Maybe another symmetry?\n\nLooking at the coefficients, perhaps the variables u, v, w are proportional to some constants?\n\nSuppose that u = A * something, v = B * something, w = C * something, such that the equations can be satisfied. Alternatively, maybe set ratios between u, v, w?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps set variables like p = u / w, q = v / w, so that we can express equations in terms of p and q.\n\nLet me try that.\n\nLet me set:\n\nLet \\( p = \\frac{u}{w} \\),\n\n\\( q = \\frac{v}{w} \\).\n\nThen, u = p w,\n\nv = q w.\n\nTherefore, substituting into the equations:\n\nEquation 1:\n\n\\( \\frac{1}{u} = (9 + 6k)v + (5 + 10k)w \\)\n\nExpressed in terms of p and q:\n\n\\( \\frac{1}{p w} = (9 + 6k)(q w) + (5 + 10k)w \\)\n\nDivide both sides by w:\n\n\\( \\frac{1}{p w^2} = (9 + 6k) q + (5 + 10k) \\)\n\nSimilarly, Equation 2:\n\n\\( \\frac{1}{v} = (8 + 4k)u + (5 + 10k)w \\)\n\nExpressed in terms of p and q:\n\n\\( \\frac{1}{q w} = (8 + 4k)(p w) + (5 + 10k)w \\)\n\nDivide both sides by w:\n\n\\( \\frac{1}{q w^2} = (8 + 4k) p + (5 + 10k) \\)\n\nEquation 3:\n\n\\( \\frac{1}{w} = (8 + 4k)u + (9 + 6k)v \\)\n\nExpressed in terms of p and q:\n\n\\( \\frac{1}{w} = (8 + 4k)(p w) + (9 + 6k)(q w) \\)\n\nDivide both sides by w:\n\n\\( \\frac{1}{w^2} = (8 + 4k)p + (9 + 6k) q \\)\n\nNow, let me denote \\( \\frac{1}{w^2} = N \\), a new variable. Then:\n\nFrom Equation 1:\n\n\\( \\frac{1}{p} N = (9 + 6k) q + (5 + 10k) \\)\n\nFrom Equation 2:\n\n\\( \\frac{1}{q} N = (8 + 4k) p + (5 + 10k) \\)\n\nFrom Equation 3:\n\n\\( N = (8 + 4k)p + (9 + 6k) q \\)\n\nSo now, we have three equations with three variables p, q, N:\n\n1. \\( \\frac{N}{p} = (9 + 6k) q + (5 + 10k) \\)\n\n2. \\( \\frac{N}{q} = (8 + 4k) p + (5 + 10k) \\)\n\n3. \\( N = (8 + 4k)p + (9 + 6k) q \\)\n\nThis seems a bit more manageable. Let me see if I can express everything in terms of p and q, then substitute.\n\nFrom Equation 3, we can express N in terms of p and q:\n\n\\( N = (8 + 4k)p + (9 + 6k) q \\)\n\nThen, substitute this N into Equations 1 and 2.\n\nEquation 1 becomes:\n\n\\( \\frac{(8 + 4k)p + (9 + 6k) q}{p} = (9 + 6k) q + (5 + 10k) \\)\n\nSimplify left side:\n\n\\( (8 + 4k) + \\frac{(9 + 6k) q}{p} = (9 + 6k) q + (5 + 10k) \\)\n\nSimilarly, Equation 2 becomes:\n\n\\( \\frac{(8 + 4k)p + (9 + 6k) q}{q} = (8 + 4k) p + (5 + 10k) \\)\n\nSimplify left side:\n\n\\( \\frac{(8 + 4k)p}{q} + (9 + 6k) = (8 + 4k)p + (5 + 10k) \\)\n\nSo now, let me write the transformed equations:\n\nEquation 1 transformed:\n\n\\( (8 + 4k) + \\frac{(9 + 6k) q}{p} = (9 + 6k) q + (5 + 10k) \\)\n\nEquation 2 transformed:\n\n\\( \\frac{(8 + 4k)p}{q} + (9 + 6k) = (8 + 4k)p + (5 + 10k) \\)\n\nThese still look complex, but perhaps we can rearrange terms to group like variables.\n\nStarting with Equation 1 transformed:\n\nLet me move all terms to the left side:\n\n\\( (8 + 4k) - (5 + 10k) + \\frac{(9 + 6k) q}{p} - (9 + 6k) q = 0 \\)\n\nSimplify constants:\n\n\\( (8 + 4k - 5 - 10k) + \\left( \\frac{(9 + 6k) q}{p} - (9 + 6k) q \\right) = 0 \\)\n\nSimplify constants:\n\n\\( (3 - 6k) + (9 + 6k) q \\left( \\frac{1}{p} - 1 \\right) = 0 \\)\n\nSimilarly, for Equation 2 transformed:\n\nMove all terms to the left side:\n\n\\( \\frac{(8 + 4k)p}{q} - (8 + 4k)p + (9 + 6k) - (5 + 10k) = 0 \\)\n\nSimplify terms:\n\nFirst term: \\( \\frac{(8 + 4k)p}{q} - (8 + 4k)p = (8 + 4k)p \\left( \\frac{1}{q} - 1 \\right) \\)\n\nSecond part: \\( (9 + 6k - 5 - 10k) = (4 - 4k) \\)\n\nTherefore, Equation 2 transformed becomes:\n\n\\( (8 + 4k)p \\left( \\frac{1}{q} - 1 \\right) + (4 - 4k) = 0 \\)\n\nSo now, we have:\n\nEquation 1a:\n\n\\( (3 - 6k) + (9 + 6k) q \\left( \\frac{1}{p} - 1 \\right) = 0 \\)\n\nEquation 2a:\n\n\\( (8 + 4k)p \\left( \\frac{1}{q} - 1 \\right) + (4 - 4k) = 0 \\)\n\nLet me denote \\( \\frac{1}{p} = A \\), \\( \\frac{1}{q} = B \\), so that \\( A = 1/p \\), \\( B = 1/q \\). Then \\( p = 1/A \\), \\( q = 1/B \\).\n\nSubstituting into Equation 1a:\n\n\\( (3 - 6k) + (9 + 6k) \\cdot \\frac{1}{B} \\cdot \\left( A - 1 \\right) = 0 \\)\n\nBecause \\( q = 1/B \\), so \\( \\frac{1}{p} - 1 = A - 1 \\), and \\( q = 1/B \\), so:\n\n\\( (9 + 6k) \\cdot \\frac{1}{B} \\cdot (A - 1) \\)\n\nSimilarly, Equation 2a:\n\n\\( (8 + 4k) \\cdot \\frac{1}{A} \\cdot \\left( B - 1 \\right) + (4 - 4k) = 0 \\)\n\nBecause \\( p = 1/A \\), so \\( \\frac{1}{q} - 1 = B - 1 \\), and \\( p = 1/A \\).\n\nTherefore, the equations become:\n\nEquation 1b:\n\n\\( (3 - 6k) + \\frac{(9 + 6k)(A - 1)}{B} = 0 \\)\n\nEquation 2b:\n\n\\( \\frac{(8 + 4k)(B - 1)}{A} + (4 - 4k) = 0 \\)\n\nHmm, this substitution may or may not help. Let me see if I can express one variable in terms of the other from one equation and substitute into the other.\n\nLet me try Equation 2b first. Let me write it as:\n\n\\( \\frac{(8 + 4k)(B - 1)}{A} = - (4 - 4k) \\)\n\nThus,\n\n\\( \\frac{(B - 1)}{A} = - \\frac{(4 - 4k)}{(8 + 4k)} = - \\frac{4(1 - k)}{4(2 + k)} = - \\frac{(1 - k)}{(2 + k)} \\)\n\nSo,\n\n\\( \\frac{B - 1}{A} = - \\frac{(1 - k)}{(2 + k)} \\)\n\nLet me denote this as Equation 2c:\n\n\\( \\frac{B - 1}{A} = - \\frac{(1 - k)}{(2 + k)} \\)\n\nLet me solve for B in terms of A:\n\n\\( B - 1 = - \\frac{(1 - k)}{(2 + k)} A \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( B = 1 - \\frac{(1 - k)}{(2 + k)} A \\)\n\nLet me denote this expression for B as Equation 2d.\n\nNow, substitute this expression for B into Equation 1b.\n\nEquation 1b:\n\n\\( (3 - 6k) + \\frac{(9 + 6k)(A - 1)}{B} = 0 \\)\n\nSubstitute B from Equation 2d:\n\n\\( (3 - 6k) + \\frac{(9 + 6k)(A - 1)}{1 - \\frac{(1 - k)}{(2 + k)} A} = 0 \\)\n\nThis is getting really messy, but let me try to simplify step by step.\n\nFirst, let me denote the denominator in the second term:\n\nDenominator: \\( 1 - \\frac{(1 - k)}{(2 + k)} A \\)\n\nLet me write that as:\n\n\\( \\frac{(2 + k) - (1 - k) A}{(2 + k)} \\)\n\nTherefore, the entire fraction becomes:\n\n\\( \\frac{(9 + 6k)(A - 1)}{ \\frac{(2 + k) - (1 - k) A}{(2 + k)} } = \\frac{(9 + 6k)(A - 1)(2 + k)}{(2 + k) - (1 - k) A} \\)\n\nSo, Equation 1b becomes:\n\n\\( (3 - 6k) + \\frac{(9 + 6k)(A - 1)(2 + k)}{(2 + k) - (1 - k) A} = 0 \\)\n\nLet me denote the denominator as D = \\( (2 + k) - (1 - k) A \\), so:\n\n\\( (3 - 6k) + \\frac{(9 + 6k)(A - 1)(2 + k)}{D} = 0 \\)\n\nThis is still complicated, but perhaps cross-multiplying to eliminate the denominator:\n\nMultiply both sides by D:\n\n\\( (3 - 6k) D + (9 + 6k)(A - 1)(2 + k) = 0 \\)\n\nSubstitute D = \\( (2 + k) - (1 - k) A \\):\n\n\\( (3 - 6k)[(2 + k) - (1 - k) A] + (9 + 6k)(A - 1)(2 + k) = 0 \\)\n\nNow, let me expand each term step by step.\n\nFirst term: \\( (3 - 6k)[(2 + k) - (1 - k) A] \\)\n\nLet me distribute the (3 - 6k):\n\n= \\( (3 - 6k)(2 + k) - (3 - 6k)(1 - k) A \\)\n\nSecond term: \\( (9 + 6k)(A - 1)(2 + k) \\)\n\nLet me first expand (A - 1)(2 + k):\n\n= \\( A(2 + k) - 1*(2 + k) = (2 + k)A - (2 + k) \\)\n\nTherefore, the second term becomes:\n\n\\( (9 + 6k)[(2 + k)A - (2 + k)] = (9 + 6k)(2 + k) A - (9 + 6k)(2 + k) \\)\n\nPutting all together, the entire equation is:\n\nFirst part:\n\n\\( (3 - 6k)(2 + k) - (3 - 6k)(1 - k) A \\)\n\nPlus second part:\n\n\\( + (9 + 6k)(2 + k) A - (9 + 6k)(2 + k) \\)\n\nAll equals zero.\n\nLet me group the constant terms (without A) and the terms with A.\n\nConstant terms:\n\n\\( (3 - 6k)(2 + k) - (9 + 6k)(2 + k) \\)\n\nTerms with A:\n\n\\( - (3 - 6k)(1 - k) A + (9 + 6k)(2 + k) A \\)\n\nSo, let's compute the constants first:\n\nConstant terms:\n\nFactor out (2 + k):\n\n= \\( [ (3 - 6k) - (9 + 6k) ] (2 + k) \\)\n\n= \\( [ 3 - 6k - 9 - 6k ] (2 + k) \\)\n\n= \\( [ -6 - 12k ] (2 + k) \\)\n\n= -6(1 + 2k)(2 + k)\n\nWait, let me compute step by step:\n\n3 - 6k - 9 - 6k = (3 - 9) + (-6k -6k) = (-6) + (-12k) = -6 -12k = -6(1 + 2k)\n\nTherefore, constants: -6(1 + 2k)(2 + k)\n\nNow, terms with A:\n\nFactor out A:\n\n= [ - (3 - 6k)(1 - k) + (9 + 6k)(2 + k) ] A\n\nLet me compute the coefficient inside the brackets:\n\nFirst term: - (3 - 6k)(1 - k)\n\nSecond term: + (9 + 6k)(2 + k)\n\nLet me compute each product:\n\nFirst term:\n\n- (3 - 6k)(1 - k) = - [ 3(1 - k) - 6k(1 - k) ] = - [ 3 - 3k - 6k + 6k² ] = - [ 3 - 9k + 6k² ] = -3 + 9k - 6k²\n\nSecond term:\n\n(9 + 6k)(2 + k) = 9*2 + 9k + 6k*2 + 6k*k = 18 + 9k + 12k + 6k² = 18 + 21k + 6k²\n\nTherefore, adding the two terms:\n\n(-3 + 9k - 6k²) + (18 + 21k + 6k²) =\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nConstants: -3 + 18 = 15\n\nk terms: 9k + 21k = 30k\n\nk² terms: -6k² + 6k² = 0\n\nThus, total coefficient: 15 + 30k\n\nTherefore, terms with A: (15 + 30k) A\n\nPutting it all together, the entire equation becomes:\n\n-6(1 + 2k)(2 + k) + (15 + 30k) A = 0\n\nSolve for A:\n\n(15 + 30k) A = 6(1 + 2k)(2 + k)\n\nTherefore,\n\nA = [6(1 + 2k)(2 + k)] / (15 + 30k)\n\nSimplify numerator and denominator:\n\nFactor numerator:\n\n6(1 + 2k)(2 + k)\n\nDenominator: 15(1 + 2k)\n\nNotice that (1 + 2k) is a common factor in numerator and denominator, so we can cancel them out, provided that 1 + 2k ≠ 0, which it isn't since k = log₂3 ≈1.585, so 1 + 2k ≈ 4.17, which is positive.\n\nTherefore:\n\nA = [6(2 + k)] / 15 = [6/15]*(2 + k) = [2/5]*(2 + k)\n\nThus,\n\nA = (2/5)(2 + k)\n\nRecall that A = 1/p, so:\n\n1/p = (2/5)(2 + k)\n\nTherefore,\n\np = 5 / [2(2 + k)]\n\nOkay, so we have p in terms of k. Now, let's recall that we had an expression for B in terms of A from Equation 2d:\n\nB = 1 - [ (1 - k)/(2 + k) ] * A\n\nWe can substitute A = (2/5)(2 + k) into this:\n\nB = 1 - [ (1 - k)/(2 + k) ] * [ (2/5)(2 + k) ]\n\nSimplify:\n\nThe (2 + k) terms cancel:\n\nB = 1 - [ (1 - k) * (2/5) ] = 1 - (2/5)(1 - k) = 1 - 2/5 + (2/5)k = (3/5) + (2/5)k\n\nTherefore,\n\nB = (3 + 2k)/5\n\nSince B = 1/q,\n\nq = 1/B = 5/(3 + 2k)\n\nNow, we have expressions for p and q in terms of k. Recall that p = 1/A and q = 1/B, so now we can find u and v in terms of w, but perhaps we can proceed to find N?\n\nWait, recall that N = (8 + 4k)p + (9 + 6k) q, from Equation 3.\n\nWait, actually, we had N = (8 + 4k)p + (9 + 6k) q, yes.\n\nWe can compute N now that we have p and q in terms of k.\n\nLet me compute N:\n\nN = (8 + 4k) * p + (9 + 6k) * q\n\nWe have p = 5/[2(2 + k)], and q = 5/(3 + 2k)\n\nWait, hold on:\n\nWait, p = 5 / [2(2 + k)], yes,\n\nand q = 5/(3 + 2k), yes.\n\nTherefore,\n\nN = (8 + 4k) * [5 / (2(2 + k))] + (9 + 6k) * [5 / (3 + 2k)]\n\nLet me factor out the 5:\n\nN = 5 [ (8 + 4k)/(2(2 + k)) + (9 + 6k)/(3 + 2k) ]\n\nLet me simplify each term inside the brackets:\n\nFirst term: (8 + 4k)/(2(2 + k)) = [4*(2 + k)] / [2(2 + k)] = 4/2 = 2\n\nWait, let me check:\n\n8 + 4k = 4*(2 + k)\n\nTherefore,\n\n(8 + 4k)/(2(2 + k)) = [4*(2 + k)] / [2*(2 + k)] = 4/2 = 2\n\nYes, that's nice.\n\nSecond term: (9 + 6k)/(3 + 2k)\n\nNotice that 9 + 6k = 3*(3 + 2k)\n\nTherefore,\n\n(9 + 6k)/(3 + 2k) = 3*(3 + 2k)/(3 + 2k) = 3\n\nTherefore,\n\nN = 5 [ 2 + 3 ] = 5 * 5 = 25\n\nWow! So N = 25.\n\nRecall that N = 1/w², so:\n\n1/w² = 25 ⇒ w² = 1/25 ⇒ w = 1/5 or w = -1/5\n\nBut since w = 1/R and R is a logarithm (which can be positive or negative?), but since x, y, z are positive real numbers, and the original equations have logarithms which are defined only if their arguments are positive. Since y z, z x, x y are all positive (as products of positive numbers), so the logarithms are defined. Moreover, the exponents in the original equations are real numbers, so the logs are real. However, the variables a, b, c, d, e, f are exponents in the prime factorizations, so they can be any real numbers, but R, Q, P are defined in terms of those variables. However, since we took logarithms, which can be positive or negative depending on the values, but the variables P, Q, R are combinations of logs and such, but ultimately, since x, y, z are positive, the expressions inside the logs are positive, so the logs are real, but their combination could be positive or negative? Hmm.\n\nHowever, in our substitutions, we have w = 1/R, and R = log_{2}(x y) + k*(d + e). Since x and y are positive, log_{2}(x y) is real, and k is positive (since log₂3 is positive), and d and e are exponents in the prime factorization, which can be any real numbers. So R could be positive or negative, but since we have w = 1/R, and we squared it to get N = 1/w² = 25, so w can be ±1/5, but since we are dealing with real numbers and likely looking for positive values (since variables x, y, z are positive, leading to positive logs?), perhaps we can take w positive.\n\nTherefore, w = 1/5, so:\n\nw = 1/R = 1/5 ⇒ R = 5\n\nSimilarly, recall that:\n\nu = p w,\n\nv = q w,\n\nand we have p = 5/[2(2 + k)], q = 5/(3 + 2k), and w = 1/5.\n\nTherefore,\n\nu = p * w = [5 / (2(2 + k))] * (1/5) = 1 / [2(2 + k)]\n\nSimilarly,\n\nv = q * w = [5 / (3 + 2k)] * (1/5) = 1 / (3 + 2k)\n\nRecall that u = 1/P, v = 1/Q, w = 1/R,\n\nThus,\n\nP = 1/u = 2(2 + k),\n\nQ = 1/v = 3 + 2k,\n\nR = 1/w = 5.\n\nSo now, we have P, Q, R:\n\nP = 2(2 + k),\n\nQ = 3 + 2k,\n\nR = 5.\n\nRecall that k = log₂3.\n\nNow, with P, Q, R known, we can find a, b, c, d, e, f:\n\nFrom earlier expressions:\n\na = 2d,\n\nd = 4 / P,\n\nso d = 4 / [2(2 + k)] = 2 / (2 + k),\n\ntherefore a = 2d = 4 / (2 + k),\n\nSimilarly,\n\ne = 6 / Q,\n\nsince Q = 3 + 2k,\n\nso e = 6 / (3 + 2k),\n\nand b = (3/2)e = (3/2)*(6 / (3 + 2k)) = 9 / (3 + 2k),\n\nc = 5 / R = 5 / 5 = 1,\n\nf = 10 / R = 10 / 5 = 2,\n\nAdditionally,\n\na = 4 / (2 + k),\n\nd = 2 / (2 + k),\n\nb = 9 / (3 + 2k),\n\ne = 6 / (3 + 2k),\n\nc = 1,\n\nf = 2,\n\nNow, recall that x, y, z are expressed in terms of a, b, c, d, e, f as:\n\nx = 2^a * 3^d,\n\ny = 2^b * 3^e,\n\nz = 2^c * 3^f,\n\nWait, hold on, let me recall:\n\nEarlier, we set:\n\nx = 2^a * 3^d,\n\ny = 2^b * 3^e,\n\nz = 2^c * 3^f,\n\nYes, that was our substitution.\n\nTherefore, substituting the values:\n\nFirst, compute a, b, c, d, e, f:\n\nGiven k = log₂3, which is a constant, approximately 1.58496, but we can keep it as k for now.\n\nCompute a = 4 / (2 + k),\n\nd = 2 / (2 + k),\n\nb = 9 / (3 + 2k),\n\ne = 6 / (3 + 2k),\n\nc = 1,\n\nf = 2.\n\nTherefore, substituting into x, y, z:\n\nx = 2^{a} * 3^{d} = 2^{4/(2 + k)} * 3^{2/(2 + k)} = [2^4 * 3^2]^{1/(2 + k)} = [16 * 9]^{1/(2 + k)} = 144^{1/(2 + k)}.\n\nSimilarly,\n\ny = 2^{b} * 3^{e} = 2^{9/(3 + 2k)} * 3^{6/(3 + 2k)} = [2^9 * 3^6]^{1/(3 + 2k)} = [512 * 729]^{1/(3 + 2k)} = (512 * 729)^{1/(3 + 2k)}.\n\nWait, 2^9 is 512, and 3^6 is 729. So yes, that's correct.\n\nSimilarly,\n\nz = 2^{c} * 3^{f} = 2^{1} * 3^{2} = 2 * 9 = 18.\n\nWait, that's nice! z simplifies to 18, since c = 1 and f = 2.\n\nSo z = 2^1 * 3^2 = 2 * 9 = 18.\n\nNow, x and y are more complicated. Let me see if I can simplify x and y.\n\nFirst, let me compute x:\n\nx = 144^{1/(2 + k)}.\n\nSince k = log₂3,\n\n2 + k = 2 + log₂3 = log₂4 + log₂3 = log₂(4*3) = log₂12.\n\nWait, hold on: 2 + log₂3 is not log₂(4*3). Wait, actually, 2 is log₂4, so log₂4 + log₂3 = log₂(4*3) = log₂12, but here it's 2 + log₂3, which is not the same as log₂4 + log₂3 unless 2 = log₂4, which it is, but 2 + log₂3 is just a scalar, not a log. Hmm, perhaps better to leave it as is.\n\nAlternatively, note that 144 = 12^2, so 144^{1/(2 + k)} = (12^2)^{1/(2 + k)} = 12^{2/(2 + k)}.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps express in terms of base 2 or 3 exponents?\n\nAlternatively, let me see if I can write 144 as 2^4 * 3^2, so:\n\n144 = 2^4 * 3^2,\n\nTherefore,\n\nx = (2^4 * 3^2)^{1/(2 + k)} = 2^{4/(2 + k)} * 3^{2/(2 + k)}.\n\nSimilarly, y = [2^9 * 3^6]^{1/(3 + 2k)} = 2^{9/(3 + 2k)} * 3^{6/(3 + 2k)}.\n\nHmm, okay. So x and y are expressed in terms of exponents with denominators involving k = log₂3. Since k is log₂3, perhaps we can write these exponents in terms of log base 2 or something else?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can find numerical values for these exponents?\n\nSince k = log₂3 ≈ 1.58496,\n\nCompute denominator for x's exponent: 2 + k ≈ 2 + 1.58496 ≈ 3.58496,\n\nSimilarly, denominator for y's exponent: 3 + 2k ≈ 3 + 2*(1.58496) ≈ 3 + 3.16992 ≈ 6.16992.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's a way to write x and y in terms that can be multiplied together to find xyz?\n\nSince we need to compute xyz, and we already have z = 18, so xyz = x * y * 18.\n\nTherefore, if we can compute x * y, then multiply by 18 to get xyz.\n\nLet me compute x * y:\n\nx = 2^{4/(2 + k)} * 3^{2/(2 + k)}.\n\ny = 2^{9/(3 + 2k)} * 3^{6/(3 + 2k)}.\n\nTherefore,\n\nx * y = [2^{4/(2 + k)} * 3^{2/(2 + k)}] * [2^{9/(3 + 2k)} * 3^{6/(3 + 2k)}]\n\n= 2^{4/(2 + k) + 9/(3 + 2k)} * 3^{2/(2 + k) + 6/(3 + 2k)}.\n\nHmm, so we need to compute the exponents of 2 and 3 in x*y.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet me compute the exponent of 2:\n\nE2 = 4/(2 + k) + 9/(3 + 2k)\n\nSimilarly, exponent of 3:\n\nE3 = 2/(2 + k) + 6/(3 + 2k)\n\nTherefore, x*y = 2^{E2} * 3^{E3}\n\nThen, xyz = x*y*z = 2^{E2} * 3^{E3} * 18 = 2^{E2} * 3^{E3} * 2 * 3^2 = 2^{E2 + 1} * 3^{E3 + 2}\n\nTherefore, to compute xyz, we need to compute E2 and E3, then add 1 to E2 and 2 to E3, then write the result as a product of powers of 2 and 3. Maybe these exponents simplify nicely?\n\nLet me compute E2 and E3.\n\nFirst, let me compute E2:\n\nE2 = 4/(2 + k) + 9/(3 + 2k)\n\nSimilarly, E3 = 2/(2 + k) + 6/(3 + 2k)\n\nNotice that E3 is exactly (1/2) * E2, because:\n\nIf we take E2 and divide by 2:\n\nE2 / 2 = [4/(2 + k) + 9/(3 + 2k)] / 2 = 2/(2 + k) + 9/(2(3 + 2k))\n\nBut E3 is 2/(2 + k) + 6/(3 + 2k). Hmm, not exactly half, but close.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps factor something out?\n\nAlternatively, let me plug in the value of k = log₂3. Since k = log₂3, perhaps we can express denominators in terms of k?\n\nWait, let me see:\n\nLet me denote k = log₂3,\n\nTherefore, 2 + k = 2 + log₂3 = log₂4 + log₂3 = log₂(4*3) = log₂12,\n\nSimilarly, 3 + 2k = 3 + 2 log₂3 = log₂8 + log₂3^2 = log₂(8 * 9) = log₂72,\n\nWait, hold on:\n\nWait, 3 is log₂8, since 2^3 = 8,\n\nand 2k = 2 log₂3 = log₂3² = log₂9,\n\nTherefore, 3 + 2k = log₂8 + log₂9 = log₂(8*9) = log₂72,\n\nSimilarly, 2 + k = log₂4 + log₂3 = log₂12,\n\nSo,\n\nE2 = 4 / log₂12 + 9 / log₂72,\n\nSimilarly,\n\nE3 = 2 / log₂12 + 6 / log₂72,\n\nHmm, perhaps changing the base of the logarithm to natural logarithm to see if that helps?\n\nRecall that log_b a = (ln a)/(ln b),\n\nTherefore,\n\n1 / log₂12 = (ln 2)/(ln 12),\n\nSimilarly,\n\n1 / log₂72 = (ln 2)/(ln 72),\n\nTherefore,\n\nE2 = 4*(ln 2 / ln 12) + 9*(ln 2 / ln 72) = ln2 [4 / ln12 + 9 / ln72]\n\nSimilarly,\n\nE3 = ln2 [2 / ln12 + 6 / ln72]\n\nHmm, but this might not lead to a simplification. Alternatively, perhaps notice that 72 = 12 * 6, so ln72 = ln(12*6) = ln12 + ln6,\n\nBut not sure if that helps.\n\nAlternatively, let me compute numerical values.\n\nGiven that k ≈ 1.58496,\n\nCompute denominators:\n\nFirst denominator for E2 terms:\n\n2 + k ≈ 2 + 1.58496 ≈ 3.58496,\n\n3 + 2k ≈ 3 + 3.16992 ≈ 6.16992,\n\nTherefore,\n\nE2 ≈ 4 / 3.58496 + 9 / 6.16992 ≈\n\nCompute 4 / 3.58496 ≈ 1.116,\n\n9 / 6.16992 ≈ 1.463,\n\nThus, E2 ≈ 1.116 + 1.463 ≈ 2.579\n\nSimilarly,\n\nE3 ≈ 2 / 3.58496 + 6 / 6.16992 ≈\n\n2 / 3.58496 ≈ 0.557,\n\n6 / 6.16992 ≈ 0.973,\n\nThus, E3 ≈ 0.557 + 0.973 ≈ 1.530\n\nTherefore,\n\nxyz = 2^{E2 + 1} * 3^{E3 + 2} ≈ 2^{3.579} * 3^{3.530}\n\nHmm, but this is approximate. The problem asks for the smallest possible value of xyz, and likely expects an exact value, expressed in terms of powers of 2 and 3, perhaps as an integer?\n\nWait, given that z = 18, and x and y might be designed such that x*y*z is a nice number?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there is an error in my approach, because the problem says \"compute the smallest possible value of xyz\", implying that there might be multiple solutions and we need the minimal one, but in my approach, I might have assumed certain substitutions that led to a unique solution. Wait, let me check.\n\nWait, when I made substitutions with u, v, w, and ended up with equations that led to a unique solution for P, Q, R, hence for a, b, c, d, e, f, hence for x, y, z. Therefore, perhaps this is the only solution? But the problem says \"positive real numbers\", so maybe this is the only solution, hence the minimal value is this value?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made an assumption that constrained the solution to a single one, but maybe there are multiple solutions? Let me see.\n\nLooking back, when I set variables like u = 1/P, v = 1/Q, w = 1/R, and then proceeded through substitutions, I didn't assume any particular relationship except the equations derived from the problem, so that should lead to a unique solution. Hence, perhaps this is the only solution, so xyz is uniquely determined, so that's the minimal value (since there's only one value).\n\nTherefore, perhaps I need to compute xyz exactly, not approximately.\n\nLet me try to compute E2 and E3 exactly.\n\nFirst, note that k = log₂3,\n\nLet me see if I can express E2 and E3 in terms of k.\n\nE2 = 4/(2 + k) + 9/(3 + 2k),\n\nE3 = 2/(2 + k) + 6/(3 + 2k),\n\nLet me see if these expressions can be combined or simplified.\n\nLet me denote t = k for simplicity.\n\nSo,\n\nE2 = 4/(2 + t) + 9/(3 + 2t),\n\nE3 = 2/(2 + t) + 6/(3 + 2t),\n\nLet me see if there is a common denominator or something.\n\nLet me compute E2:\n\nLet me write E2 as:\n\nE2 = [4(3 + 2t) + 9(2 + t)] / [(2 + t)(3 + 2t)]\n\nCompute numerator:\n\n4*(3 + 2t) = 12 + 8t,\n\n9*(2 + t) = 18 + 9t,\n\nTotal numerator: 12 + 8t + 18 + 9t = 30 + 17t,\n\nDenominator: (2 + t)(3 + 2t) = 6 + 4t + 3t + 2t² = 6 + 7t + 2t²\n\nThus,\n\nE2 = (30 + 17t)/(2t² + 7t + 6)\n\nSimilarly, compute E3:\n\nE3 = [2*(3 + 2t) + 6*(2 + t)] / [(2 + t)(3 + 2t)]\n\nCompute numerator:\n\n2*(3 + 2t) = 6 + 4t,\n\n6*(2 + t) = 12 + 6t,\n\nTotal numerator: 6 + 4t + 12 + 6t = 18 + 10t,\n\nDenominator same as above: 2t² + 7t + 6,\n\nThus,\n\nE3 = (18 + 10t)/(2t² + 7t + 6)\n\nTherefore, E2 and E3 have the same denominator, which is 2t² + 7t + 6.\n\nNow, recall that t = k = log₂3,\n\nBut perhaps we can factor the denominator:\n\nDenominator: 2t² + 7t + 6.\n\nLet me see if this quadratic factors:\n\nLooking for factors of 2*6 = 12 that add up to 7: 3 and 4,\n\nYes: 2t² + 3t + 4t + 6 = t(2t + 3) + 2(2t + 3) = (t + 2)(2t + 3)\n\nWait,\n\nWait:\n\n2t² + 7t + 6 = (2t + 3)(t + 2)\n\nCheck:\n\n(2t + 3)(t + 2) = 2t*t + 2t*2 + 3*t + 3*2 = 2t² + 4t + 3t + 6 = 2t² + 7t + 6. Yes, correct.\n\nTherefore, denominator factors as (2t + 3)(t + 2).\n\nTherefore,\n\nE2 = (30 + 17t)/[(2t + 3)(t + 2)]\n\nE3 = (18 + 10t)/[(2t + 3)(t + 2)]\n\nHmm, perhaps we can relate numerators to the denominator's factors?\n\nLet me see:\n\nLet me see if 30 + 17t can be expressed in terms of (2t + 3) and (t + 2):\n\nSuppose 30 + 17t = A*(2t + 3) + B*(t + 2)\n\nLet me solve for A and B:\n\n30 + 17t = (2A + B)t + (3A + 2B)\n\nTherefore,\n\nEquating coefficients:\n\n2A + B = 17,\n\n3A + 2B = 30.\n\nSolve this system:\n\nFrom first equation: B = 17 - 2A,\n\nSubstitute into second equation:\n\n3A + 2*(17 - 2A) = 30,\n\n3A + 34 - 4A = 30,\n\n- A + 34 = 30,\n\n- A = -4 ⇒ A = 4,\n\nThen, B = 17 - 2*4 = 17 - 8 = 9.\n\nTherefore,\n\n30 + 17t = 4*(2t + 3) + 9*(t + 2)\n\nCheck:\n\n4*(2t + 3) = 8t + 12,\n\n9*(t + 2) = 9t + 18,\n\nTotal: 8t + 12 + 9t + 18 = 17t + 30. Correct.\n\nTherefore,\n\nE2 = [4*(2t + 3) + 9*(t + 2)] / [(2t + 3)(t + 2)] = 4/(t + 2) + 9/(2t + 3)\n\nSimilarly, let me see if the numerator of E3 can be expressed similarly:\n\n18 + 10t = C*(2t + 3) + D*(t + 2)\n\nSolve for C and D:\n\n18 + 10t = (2C + D)t + (3C + 2D)\n\nThus,\n\n2C + D = 10,\n\n3C + 2D = 18.\n\nFrom first equation: D = 10 - 2C,\n\nSubstitute into second equation:\n\n3C + 2*(10 - 2C) = 18,\n\n3C + 20 - 4C = 18,\n\n- C + 20 = 18 ⇒ -C = -2 ⇒ C = 2,\n\nThen, D = 10 - 2*2 = 6.\n\nTherefore,\n\n18 + 10t = 2*(2t + 3) + 6*(t + 2)\n\nCheck:\n\n2*(2t + 3) = 4t + 6,\n\n6*(t + 2) = 6t + 12,\n\nTotal: 4t + 6 + 6t + 12 = 10t + 18. Correct.\n\nTherefore,\n\nE3 = [2*(2t + 3) + 6*(t + 2)] / [(2t + 3)(t + 2)] = 2/(t + 2) + 6/(2t + 3)\n\nHmm, interesting. So,\n\nE2 = 4/(t + 2) + 9/(2t + 3)\n\nE3 = 2/(t + 2) + 6/(2t + 3)\n\nWait a second, notice that E3 is exactly half of E2?\n\nWait,\n\nE2 = 4/(t+2) + 9/(2t+3)\n\nE3 = 2/(t+2) + 6/(2t+3)\n\nYes! Indeed, E3 is exactly half of E2:\n\nE3 = (1/2) * E2,\n\nBecause:\n\n(1/2)*E2 = 2/(t + 2) + (9/2)/(2t + 3),\n\nWait, but E3 has 6/(2t + 3), not 9/2/(2t + 3). Hmm, so that doesn't hold. Wait, let me compute:\n\nWait, 9/2 is 4.5, but we have 6 in E3. So that idea is incorrect.\n\nWait, but:\n\nWait, let me see:\n\nWait, if I factor E3:\n\nE3 = 2/(t + 2) + 6/(2t + 3) = 2[1/(t + 2) + 3/(2t + 3)]\n\nBut E2 = 4/(t + 2) + 9/(2t + 3) = 4/(t + 2) + 9/(2t + 3)\n\nHmm, not a multiple.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's another relation?\n\nAlternatively, let me think of E2 and E3 in terms of t = log₂3.\n\nRecall that t = log₂3, so 2^t = 3,\n\nand 2t = log₂9,\n\nand 3t = log₂27,\n\nbut not sure if that helps.\n\nAlternatively, let me try to compute E2 + E3:\n\nE2 + E3 = [4/(t + 2) + 9/(2t + 3)] + [2/(t + 2) + 6/(2t + 3)] = 6/(t + 2) + 15/(2t + 3)\n\nHmm, not obviously helpful.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider that t = log₂3,\n\nTherefore,\n\nt + 2 = log₂4 + log₂3 = log₂12,\n\nand 2t + 3 = 2 log₂3 + log₂8 = log₂9 + log₂8 = log₂72,\n\nTherefore,\n\nE2 = 4 / log₂12 + 9 / log₂72,\n\nE3 = 2 / log₂12 + 6 / log₂72,\n\nRecall that log_b a = 1 / log_a b,\n\nTherefore,\n\n1 / log₂12 = log_{12}2,\n\nand 1 / log₂72 = log_{72}2,\n\nThus,\n\nE2 = 4 log_{12}2 + 9 log_{72}2,\n\nE3 = 2 log_{12}2 + 6 log_{72}2,\n\nHmm, perhaps expressing these logarithms in terms of common bases?\n\nNote that 72 = 12 * 6,\n\nSo log_{72}2 = log_{12*6}2 = [log_{12}2]/[log_{12}(12*6)] by change of base,\n\nWait,\n\nlog_{72}2 = log_{72}2 = (log_{12}2) / (log_{12}72),\n\nsince log_b a = log_c a / log_c b.\n\nTherefore,\n\nlog_{72}2 = log_{12}2 / log_{12}72,\n\nand log_{12}72 = log_{12}(12*6) = 1 + log_{12}6,\n\nHmm, but this might not help.\n\nAlternatively, let me compute log_{12}2 and log_{72}2 numerically?\n\nSince t = log₂3 ≈ 1.58496,\n\nTherefore,\n\nlog_{12}2 = 1 / log₂12 = 1 / (log₂(4*3)) = 1/(2 + log₂3) ≈ 1/(2 + 1.58496) ≈ 1/3.58496 ≈ 0.278,\n\nSimilarly,\n\nlog_{72}2 = 1 / log₂72 = 1 / log₂(8*9) = 1/(log₂8 + log₂9) = 1/(3 + 2 log₂3) ≈ 1/(3 + 3.16992) ≈ 1/6.16992 ≈ 0.162,\n\nTherefore,\n\nE2 ≈ 4*(0.278) + 9*(0.162) ≈ 1.112 + 1.458 ≈ 2.57,\n\nWhich matches our previous approximation.\n\nSimilarly,\n\nE3 ≈ 2*(0.278) + 6*(0.162) ≈ 0.556 + 0.972 ≈ 1.528,\n\nAlso matches.\n\nHmm, so xyz = 2^{E2 + 1} * 3^{E3 + 2} ≈ 2^{3.57} * 3^{3.528}\n\nBut the problem likely expects an exact form. Let me see if we can write E2 and E3 in terms that can be simplified with exponents.\n\nWait, note that:\n\nWe have:\n\nE2 = (30 + 17t)/(2t² + 7t + 6),\n\nand since t = log₂3,\n\nLet me see if numerator and denominator can be related through substitution.\n\nLet me denote s = 2t + 3,\n\nWait, maybe not. Alternatively, perhaps notice that:\n\nLet me recall that in our substitutions earlier, we had:\n\nP = 2(2 + t),\n\nQ = 3 + 2t,\n\nR = 5,\n\nand with these, we can express x and y in terms that might telescope when multiplied by z = 18.\n\nRecall that:\n\nx = 144^{1/(2 + t)} = (12^2)^{1/(2 + t)} = 12^{2/(2 + t)},\n\nSimilarly,\n\ny = (2^9 * 3^6)^{1/(3 + 2t)} = ( (2^3 * 3^2)^3 )^{1/(3 + 2t)} = ( (8*9)^3 )^{1/(3 + 2t)} = (72^3)^{1/(3 + 2t)} = 72^{3/(3 + 2t)}.\n\nWait, 2^9 * 3^6 = (2^3)^3 * (3^2)^3 = (8*9)^3 = 72^3, yes.\n\nTherefore, y = (72^3)^{1/(3 + 2t)} = 72^{3/(3 + 2t)}.\n\nSimilarly, x = 12^{2/(2 + t)}.\n\nTherefore, x * y = 12^{2/(2 + t)} * 72^{3/(3 + 2t)}.\n\nNote that 72 = 12 * 6,\n\nTherefore, 72 = 12 * 6,\n\nSo,\n\n72^{3/(3 + 2t)} = (12 * 6)^{3/(3 + 2t)} = 12^{3/(3 + 2t)} * 6^{3/(3 + 2t)}.\n\nTherefore,\n\nx * y = 12^{2/(2 + t)} * 12^{3/(3 + 2t)} * 6^{3/(3 + 2t)}.\n\nCombine the exponents on 12:\n\n= 12^{ [2/(2 + t) + 3/(3 + 2t)] } * 6^{3/(3 + 2t)}.\n\nHmm, the exponent on 12 is exactly E2,\n\nBecause:\n\nE2 = 4/(2 + t) + 9/(3 + 2t) divided by something? Wait, no:\n\nWait, actually, earlier we had:\n\nE2 = 4/(2 + t) + 9/(3 + 2t),\n\nWait, but in the exponent here, it's 2/(2 + t) + 3/(3 + 2t),\n\nWait, so not exactly E2. Hmm.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a miscalculation.\n\nWait, let me re-express:\n\nWait, x*y = 12^{2/(2 + t)} * 72^{3/(3 + 2t)}.\n\nBut 72 = 12 * 6,\n\nTherefore,\n\n72^{3/(3 + 2t)} = (12 * 6)^{3/(3 + 2t)} = 12^{3/(3 + 2t)} * 6^{3/(3 + 2t)}.\n\nThus,\n\nx*y = 12^{2/(2 + t)} * 12^{3/(3 + 2t)} * 6^{3/(3 + 2t)}.\n\nTherefore, combining the exponents on 12:\n\n= 12^{ [2/(2 + t) + 3/(3 + 2t)] } * 6^{3/(3 + 2t)}.\n\nTherefore, the exponent on 12 is [2/(2 + t) + 3/(3 + 2t)], which is different from E2.\n\nWait, but E2 was 4/(2 + t) + 9/(3 + 2t). Hmm, not directly related.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can write 6^{3/(3 + 2t)} as (12/2)^{3/(3 + 2t)} = 12^{3/(3 + 2t)} * 2^{-3/(3 + 2t)}.\n\nTherefore,\n\nx*y = 12^{ [2/(2 + t) + 3/(3 + 2t)] } * 12^{3/(3 + 2t)} * 2^{-3/(3 + 2t)}.\n\nWait, that might not help. Alternatively, perhaps this path is not fruitful.\n\nAlternatively, let me recall that z = 18 = 2 * 3^2,\n\nSo xyz = x*y*18 = x*y*2*3^2 = 2 * 3^2 * x*y,\n\nTherefore, if I can write x*y as 2^{E2} * 3^{E3}, then xyz = 2^{E2 + 1} * 3^{E3 + 2}.\n\nBut we need to find E2 and E3 exactly.\n\nWait, going back to the expressions for E2 and E3 in terms of t = log₂3:\n\nE2 = (30 + 17t)/(2t² + 7t + 6),\n\nE3 = (18 + 10t)/(2t² + 7t + 6),\n\nNotice that the denominator is the same for both.\n\nLet me see if numerator of E2 is related to numerator of E3 multiplied by something:\n\n30 + 17t = (18 + 10t) * (something) + ?\n\nLet me see:\n\nSuppose 30 + 17t = a*(18 + 10t) + b*(denominator term?),\n\nWait, perhaps not. Alternatively, let me compute E2 / E3:\n\n(E2)/(E3) = (30 + 17t)/(18 + 10t),\n\nBut not sure if that ratio simplifies.\n\nAlternatively, let me think of t as log₂3,\n\nLet me compute 2t² + 7t + 6,\n\nwith t = log₂3,\n\nLet me see:\n\nLet me compute 2t² + 7t + 6,\n\nFirst, t = log₂3,\n\nt² = (log₂3)^2,\n\nSo,\n\n2*(log₂3)^2 + 7*log₂3 + 6,\n\nThis expression doesn't seem to simplify nicely.\n\nHmm. Alternatively, perhaps there is a substitution that can be made here.\n\nLet me recall that in the problem, the right-hand sides of the original equations are 2^8 *3^4, 2^9 *3^6, 2^5 *3^10.\n\nNotice that the exponents of 2 and 3 in each equation are multiples:\n\nFirst equation: 8 and 4 → 8 = 2*4,\n\nSecond: 9 and 6 → 9 = 1.5*6,\n\nThird: 5 and 10 → 5 = 0.5*10,\n\nHmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, notice that the exponents of 2 and 3 in each equation are in the ratio:\n\nFirst equation: 8:4 = 2:1,\n\nSecond: 9:6 = 3:2,\n\nThird: 5:10 = 1:2,\n\nHmm, not sure.\n\nAlte