rithms with base 3? Not sure. Alternatively, perhaps taking logarithms with natural logs and then introducing variables?\n\nAlternatively, let me consider taking the product of all three original equations:\n\n\\( x^{\\log_2(y z)} \\cdot y^{\\log_2(z x)} \\cdot z^{\\log_2(x y)} = (2^8 \\cdot 3^4) \\cdot (2^9 \\cdot 3^6) \\cdot (2^5 \\cdot 3^{10}) \\)\n\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\nMultiply the 2 exponents: 8 + 9 + 5 = 22, so 2^{22}\n\nMultiply the 3 exponents: 4 + 6 + 10 = 20, so 3^{20}\n\nTherefore, the product is \\( 2^{22} \\cdot 3^{20} \\)\n\nNow, the left-hand side is the product of the three terms:\n\n\\( x^{\\log_2(y z)} \\cdot y^{\\log_2(z x)} \\cdot z^{\\log_2(x y)} \\)\n\nLet me see if I can manipulate the exponents.\n\nFirst, note that:\n\nLet me write each term's exponent as log_2 of something:\n\nThe exponent on x is log_2(y z),\n\nthe exponent on y is log_2(z x),\n\nthe exponent on z is log_2(x y).\n\nTherefore, the left-hand side can be written as:\n\n\\( x^{\\log_2(y z)} \\cdot y^{\\log_2(z x)} \\cdot z^{\\log_2(x y)} \\)\n\nHmm, perhaps we can express this product in terms of exponents with base 2?\n\nAlternatively, let me take the logarithm of the entire left-hand side. Let me take log base 2 of both sides:\n\nlog₂(LHS) = log₂(RHS) = log₂(2^{22} * 3^{20}) = 22 + 20 log₂ 3\n\nNow, log₂(LHS) = log₂ [x^{\\log_2(y z)} * y^{\\log_2(z x)} * z^{\\log_2(x y)} ]\n\nUsing logarithm properties: log₂(A * B * C) = log₂ A + log₂ B + log₂ C,\n\nand log₂(A^B) = B log₂ A,\n\nso:\n\nlog₂(LHS) = log₂(x^{\\log_2(y z)}) + log₂(y^{\\log_2(z x)}) + log₂(z^{\\log_2(x y)})\n\n= [log₂(y z) * log₂ x] + [log₂(z x) * log₂ y] + [log₂(x y) * log₂ z]\n\nBut this is exactly the same as the left-hand sides of equations (1), (2), (3) that we had earlier, before substituting variables a, b, c.\n\nWait, indeed! Let me recall:\n\nOriginally, we had:\n\nEquation (1): log₂(y z) * log₂ x = 8 + 4 log₂ 3,\n\nEquation (2): log₂(z x) * log₂ y = 9 + 6 log₂ 3,\n\nEquation (3): log₂(x y) * log₂ z = 5 + 10 log₂ 3,\n\nTherefore, the sum of the left-hand sides of these equations is:\n\n[log₂(y z) * log₂ x] + [log₂(z x) * log₂ y] + [log₂(x y) * log₂ z] = (8 + 4k) + (9 + 6k) + (5 + 10k) = (8 + 9 + 5) + (4k + 6k + 10k) = 22 + 20k,\n\nwhere k = log₂ 3.\n\nBut we also just found that log₂(LHS) = 22 + 20k,\n\nso that checks out. So this doesn't give us new information. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps if I consider the product of the three original equations, which we did, and then take logarithm base 2, but we already did that.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider ratios of the equations?\n\nLet me see. Let me denote the three original equations as Eq1, Eq2, Eq3.\n\nIf I take Eq1 / Eq2:\n\n\\( \\frac{x^{\\log_2(y z)}}{y^{\\log_2(z x)}} = \\frac{2^8 \\cdot 3^4}{2^9 \\cdot 3^6} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 3^{-2} \\)\n\nSimilarly, taking the ratio:\n\nLeft-hand side: \\( \\frac{x^{\\log_2(y z)}}{y^{\\log_2(z x)}} \\)\n\nLet me write this as \\( \\left( \\frac{x}{y} \\right)^{\\log_2(z x)} \\cdot x^{\\log_2(y z) - \\log_2(z x)} \\). Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, express exponents in terms of logs:\n\nLet me write the exponent of x as log₂(y z) and exponent of y as log₂(z x).\n\nAlternatively, take the logarithm of the ratio:\n\nlog₂(LHS / RHS) = log₂(LHS) - log₂(RHS) = [log₂(y z) * log₂ x - log₂(z x) * log₂ y] - [log₂(2^{-1} 3^{-2})]\n\nWait, this might get too convoluted. Maybe another approach.\n\nWait, going back to the variables a, b, c where a = log₂ x, b = log₂ y, c = log₂ z, and S = a + b + c.\n\nWe had the three equations:\n\n1. a(b + c) = 8 + 4k,\n\n2. b(c + a) = 9 + 6k,\n\n3. c(a + b) = 5 + 10k,\n\nand S = a + b + c.\n\nLet me denote P = ab + bc + ca.\n\nWait, perhaps if I can find expressions for ab + bc + ca and abc?\n\nAlternatively, let me consider adding the three equations:\n\nEquation1 + Equation2 + Equation3:\n\na(b + c) + b(c + a) + c(a + b) = (8 + 4k) + (9 + 6k) + (5 + 10k)\n\nLeft-hand side:\n\na(b + c) + b(c + a) + c(a + b) = a b + a c + b c + b a + c a + a b = 2(ab + bc + ca)\n\nWait, let me compute term by term:\n\nFirst term: a(b + c) = ab + ac\n\nSecond term: b(c + a) = bc + ba\n\nThird term: c(a + b) = ca + cb\n\nAdding all together:\n\n(ab + ac) + (bc + ba) + (ca + cb) =\n\nab + ac + bc + ba + ca + cb\n\nBut note that ab = ba, ac = ca, bc = cb, so this is:\n\n2(ab + ac + bc)\n\nTherefore, left-hand side is 2(ab + bc + ca)\n\nRight-hand side: (8 + 9 + 5) + (4k + 6k + 10k) = 22 + 20k\n\nTherefore:\n\n2(ab + bc + ca) = 22 + 20k ⇒ ab + bc + ca = 11 + 10k … (5)\n\nAlso, we know that S = a + b + c,\n\nand we can recall that (a + b + c)^2 = a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + 2(ab + bc + ca)\n\nTherefore, if we can find a^2 + b^2 + c^2, then we can get S^2.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can find a^2 + b^2 + c^2 from the individual quadratic equations.\n\nFrom equation (1c): a^2 = S a - (8 + 4k)\n\nSimilarly,\n\nFrom equation (2c): b^2 = S b - (9 + 6k)\n\nFrom equation (3c): c^2 = S c - (5 + 10k)\n\nTherefore,\n\na^2 + b^2 + c^2 = S(a + b + c) - [ (8 + 4k) + (9 + 6k) + (5 + 10k) ]\n\nBut a + b + c = S, so:\n\na^2 + b^2 + c^2 = S * S - [ (8 + 9 + 5) + (4k + 6k + 10k) ]\n\n= S² - [22 + 20k]\n\nTherefore,\n\n(a + b + c)^2 = a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + 2(ab + bc + ca)\n\nPlugging in the above expressions:\n\nS² = [S² - (22 + 20k)] + 2*(11 + 10k)\n\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\nS² - 22 - 20k + 22 + 20k = S²\n\nTherefore, S² = S², which is an identity. Hmm, so that doesn't give us new information.\n\nHmm. So that approach just leads to a tautology.\n\nHmm, maybe we can consider the product abc?\n\nAlternatively, let me see if I can write equations for ab, bc, ca?\n\nAlternatively, let me consider the three original equations again in terms of a, b, c.\n\nWe have:\n\n1. a(b + c) = 8 + 4k,\n\n2. b(c + a) = 9 + 6k,\n\n3. c(a + b) = 5 + 10k,\n\nAnd S = a + b + c,\n\nand we know ab + bc + ca = 11 + 10k.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet me call the right-hand sides as follows:\n\nLet’s set:\n\nM1 = 8 + 4k,\n\nM2 = 9 + 6k,\n\nM3 = 5 + 10k,\n\nSo equations:\n\n1. a(S - a) = M1,\n\n2. b(S - b) = M2,\n\n3. c(S - c) = M3,\n\nSo from equation 1: a(S - a) = M1 ⇒ aS - a² = M1,\n\nSimilarly,\n\nbS - b² = M2,\n\ncS - c² = M3,\n\nIf we add all three equations:\n\naS + bS + cS - (a² + b² + c²) = M1 + M2 + M3,\n\nWhich is S(a + b + c) - (a² + b² + c²) = M1 + M2 + M3,\n\nBut a + b + c = S, so left-hand side becomes S² - (a² + b² + c²) = M1 + M2 + M3,\n\nBut earlier, we found that a² + b² + c² = S² - (22 + 20k),\n\nTherefore,\n\nS² - [S² - (22 + 20k)] = M1 + M2 + M3,\n\nSimplify left-hand side: S² - S² + 22 + 20k = 22 + 20k,\n\nRight-hand side: M1 + M2 + M3 = (8 + 4k) + (9 + 6k) + (5 + 10k) = 22 + 20k,\n\nSo again, this gives 22 + 20k = 22 + 20k, which is just an identity. So no new info.\n\nHmm, so perhaps I need to consider another approach. Let me think.\n\nLet me suppose that S is the variable we need to solve for, and perhaps set up an equation in S.\n\nLet me recall that from the quadratic equations:\n\nFor variable a:\n\na = [S ± sqrt(S² - 4M1)] / 2,\n\nSimilarly for b and c.\n\nBut since we have S = a + b + c,\n\nperhaps if we consider that each of a, b, c is expressed in terms of S, and then substitute into S = a + b + c,\n\nbut this seems like the equation we had before, which is complicated.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps assume that a, b, c are proportional to some constants?\n\nLooking at the right-hand sides of the original equations:\n\nEquation 1: 2^8 * 3^4,\n\nEquation 2: 2^9 * 3^6,\n\nEquation 3: 2^5 * 3^{10}.\n\nNotice that the exponents of 2 and 3 in each equation are multiples of 4, 6, and 10? Wait, perhaps not exactly, but maybe there's a pattern.\n\nAlternatively, let me write the right-hand sides as:\n\nEquation 1: (2^2)^4 * (3)^4 = (4 * 3)^4 = 12^4? Wait no, 2^8 is (2^2)^4 = 4^4, so 4^4 * 3^4 = (4*3)^4 = 12^4,\n\nSimilarly, Equation 2: 2^9 * 3^6 = 2^(9) * (3^3)^2 = (2^3 * 3^3)^3 = (8 * 27)^3 = 216^3? Wait, 2^9 is 512, 3^6 is 729, so 512*729. Hmm, not sure if that helps.\n\nAlternatively, Equation 3: 2^5 * 3^{10