en the equations to eliminate t.\n\nLet me write the three equations:\n\n1. a(S - a) = 8 + 4t,\n\n2. b(S - b) = 9 + 6t,\n\n3. c(S - c) = 5 + 10t.\n\nLet me solve each equation for t:\n\nFrom equation 1: 4t = a(S - a) - 8 ⇒ t = [a(S - a) - 8]/4,\n\nFrom equation 2: 6t = b(S - b) - 9 ⇒ t = [b(S - b) - 9]/6,\n\nFrom equation 3: 10t = c(S - c) - 5 ⇒ t = [c(S - c) - 5]/10,\n\nTherefore, setting the expressions for t equal to each other:\n\n[a(S - a) - 8]/4 = [b(S - b) - 9]/6 = [c(S - c) - 5]/10\n\nSo, all three expressions equal to the same t.\n\nTherefore, we can set the first equal to the second:\n\n[a(S - a) - 8]/4 = [b(S - b) - 9]/6,\n\nand the second equal to the third:\n\n[b(S - b) - 9]/6 = [c(S - c) - 5]/10.\n\nThese give two equations that relate a, b, c, and S.\n\nAdditionally, we have S = a + b + c,\n\nand ab + bc + ca = 11 + 10t,\n\nbut t can be expressed in terms of a, b, c, S via any of the above expressions.\n\nThis seems quite involved, but perhaps plugging in S = a + b + c into the equations might help.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps assume that a, b, c are proportional to the coefficients in front of t in the equations?\n\nLooking back at the expressions for t:\n\nFrom equation 1: t = [a(S - a) - 8]/4,\n\nFrom equation 2: t = [b(S - b) - 9]/6,\n\nFrom equation 3: t = [c(S - c) - 5]/10,\n\nNotice that the coefficients of t in the original equations (the right-hand sides) are 4, 6, 10, which are 4, 6, 10. These might be related to the denominators here.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps set variables proportional to these coefficients?\n\nAlternatively, suppose that a, b, c are proportional to 4, 6, 10? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, notice that the coefficients of t in the original equations (the M1, M2, M3 terms):\n\nM1 = 8 + 4t,\n\nM2 = 9 + 6t,\n\nM3 = 5 + 10t,\n\nThese can be written as:\n\nM1 = 4*(2 + t),\n\nM2 = 3*(3 + 2t),\n\nM3 = 5*(1 + 2t),\n\nHmm, not sure if that helps.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider ratios between the equations.\n\nLet me take the ratio of equation1 to equation2:\n\n[a(S - a)] / [b(S - b)] = (8 + 4t)/(9 + 6t)\n\nSimilarly, equation2 / equation3:\n\n[b(S - b)] / [c(S - c)] = (9 + 6t)/(5 + 10t)\n\nAnd equation1 / equation3:\n\n[a(S - a)] / [c(S - c)] = (8 + 4t)/(5 + 10t)\n\nBut since we also have S = a + b + c,\n\nmaybe we can find ratios between a, b, c?\n\nAlternatively, suppose that a : b : c = p : q : r,\n\nthen express a = p k, b = q k, c = r k, for some k,\n\nthen S = (p + q + r)k,\n\nand then plug into the equations.\n\nBut this might be a shot in the dark, but let me try.\n\nLet me suppose that a : b : c = p : q : r,\n\nso let me set a = p * m,\n\nb = q * m,\n\nc = r * m,\n\nwhere m is a scaling factor.\n\nThen S = (p + q + r) m,\n\nand then plug into equation1:\n\na(S - a) = p m [ (p + q + r)m - p m ] = p m [ (q + r)m ] = p q m² + p r m²,\n\nWait, actually:\n\nWait, S - a = (p + q + r)m - p m = (q + r)m,\n\nTherefore, a(S - a) = p m * (q + r)m = p (q + r) m²,\n\nSimilarly, equation1 says this equals 8 + 4t,\n\nSimilarly, equation2: b(S - b) = q m * (p + r)m = q(p + r)m² = 9 + 6t,\n\nEquation3: c(S - c) = r m * (p + q)m = r(p + q)m² = 5 + 10t,\n\nSo now we have three equations:\n\n1. p(q + r) m² = 8 + 4t,\n\n2. q(p + r) m² = 9 + 6t,\n\n3. r(p + q) m² = 5 + 10t,\n\nLet me denote N = m²,\n\nthen:\n\n1. p(q + r) N = 8 + 4t,\n\n2. q(p + r) N = 9 + 6t,\n\n3. r(p + q) N = 5 + 10t,\n\nLet me divide equation1 by equation2:\n\n[ p(q + r) ] / [ q(p + r) ] = (8 + 4t)/(9 + 6t)\n\nSimilarly, equation2 / equation3:\n\n[ q(p + r) ] / [ r(p + q) ] = (9 + 6t)/(5 + 10t)\n\nAnd equation1 / equation3:\n\n[ p(q + r) ] / [ r(p + q) ] = (8 + 4t)/(5 + 10t)\n\nThis gives us three equations with variables p, q, r, N, t. However, t is a constant (log₂3), so perhaps we can treat t as known and solve for p, q, r, N?\n\nAlternatively, since t is a constant, we can consider these as equations that must hold for the ratios p:q:r.\n\nLet me denote the ratios p:q:r as some variables. Let me set variables such as let’s say p = α, q = β, r = γ, then the ratios are α:β:γ.\n\nAlternatively, let me assume that p, q, r are constants to be determined such that the above equations hold for some N and t.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can let’s see if there is a common factor or proportionality.\n\nLet me look at the right-hand sides of the divided equations.\n\nFirst ratio:\n\n[ p(q + r) ] / [ q(p + r) ] = (8 + 4t)/(9 + 6t) = [4(2 + t)] / [3(3 + 2t)]\n\nSecond ratio:\n\n[ q(p + r) ] / [ r(p + q) ] = (9 + 6t)/(5 + 10t) = [3(3 + 2t)] / [5(1 + 2t)]\n\nThird ratio:\n\n[ p(q + r) ] / [ r(p + q) ] = (8 + 4t)/(5 + 10t) = [4(2 + t)] / [5(1 + 2t)]\n\nHmm, interesting. Let me see if the first ratio can be expressed in terms of the second ratio or something.\n\nLet me note that in the first ratio, the numerator is p(q + r), denominator q(p + r),\n\nand in the second ratio, numerator q(p + r), denominator r(p + q).\n\nIf I denote the first ratio as R1 = [ p(q + r) ] / [ q(p + r) ] = [4(2 + t)] / [3(3 + 2t)]\n\nand the second ratio R2 = [ q(p + r) ] / [ r(p + q) ] = [3(3 + 2t)] / [5(1 + 2t)]\n\nThen, notice that R1 * R2 = [ p(q + r) / (q(p + r)) ] * [ q(p + r) / (r(p + q)) ] = [ p(q + r) ] / [ r(p + q) ] = R3,\n\nwhere R3 is the third ratio: [ p(q + r) ] / [ r(p + q) ] = [4(2 + t)] / [5(1 + 2t)]\n\nIndeed,\n\nR1 * R2 = [4(2 + t)/3(3 + 2t)] * [3(3 + 2t)/5(1 + 2t)] = [4(2 + t)/5(1 + 2t)] = R3,\n\nwhich matches. So the three ratios are consistent.\n\nTherefore, this doesn't give us new information, but shows that the ratios are consistent.\n\nPerhaps I can set variables such that p, q, r are chosen to make the left-hand sides of the equations proportional to the right-hand sides.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider that the numerators and denominators on the left-hand sides of the ratios must be proportional to the numerators and denominators on the right-hand sides.\n\nLet me suppose that:\n\np(q + r) / q(p + r) = [4(2 + t)] / [3(3 + 2t)]\n\nLet me write this as:\n\n(p/q) * [(q + r)/(p + r)] = [4(2 + t)] / [3(3 + 2t)]\n\nSimilarly for the other ratios.\n\nThis seems too vague. Maybe assign numerical values?\n\nAlternatively, since log₂3 is a constant approximately 1.58496, perhaps plugging in approximate values for t and then solving numerically?\n\nGiven that this is a competition problem, perhaps the minimal xyz is an integer or a nice fraction, so maybe S is a rational number?\n\nAlternatively, suppose that t = log₂3 is rational? But log₂3 is irrational, so that might not help.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there is a substitution where we let’s say u = 2^a, v = 2^b, w = 2^c, so that x = u, y = v, z = w. Then, since a = log₂x, etc.\n\nWait, but that’s essentially what we did before.\n\nAlternatively, let me think back to the original equations:\n\nEquation1: \\( x^{\\log_2(y z)} = 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\)\n\nTake both sides to the power of 1/(log₂(y z)):\n\nx = [2^8 * 3^4]^{1 / \\log_2(y z)}.\n\nBut log₂(y z) is in the exponent, which complicates things.\n\nAlternatively, take logarithm base 3 of both sides?\n\nLet me try that for the first equation:\n\nlog₃(x^{log₂(y z)}) = log₃(2^8 * 3^4)\n\nLeft side: log₂(y z) * log₃x,\n\nRight side: 8 log₃2 + 4.\n\nHmm, not sure if helpful.\n\nAlternatively, let me set variables:\n\nLet me let p = log₂x, q = log₂y, r = log₂z. So p = a, q = b, r = c.\n\nThen the original equations become:\n\nx^{log₂(y z)} = 2^8 * 3^4,\n\nBut x = 2^p, y z = 2^{q + r},\n\nSo log₂(y z) = q + r,\n\nTherefore, the left-hand side is (2^p)^{q + r} = 2^{p(q + r)}.\n\nThe right-hand side is 2^8 * 3^4.\n\nTherefore, equating exponents for base 2 and base 3:\n\nWait, but the left-hand side is 2^{p(q + r)}, which only has a base 2 component, but the right-hand side has both 2 and 3. Therefore, this suggests that my approach is missing something?\n\nWait a second, hold on. Wait, this is a problem. If I write x^{log₂(y z)} as (2^p)^{log₂(y z)}, then that is equal to 2^{p * log₂(y z)}. However, log₂(y z) is a real number, so exponentiating 2 to that power only gives a power of 2. But the right-hand side is 2^8 * 3^4, which has a factor of 3^4. That suggests that my initial approach is flawed?\n\nWait, hold on, this is a critical mistake!\n\nWait, no, actually, x, y, z are positive real numbers, so x^{log₂(y z)} is not necessarily a power of 2. Wait, but when I take log base 2 of both sides, I should get an expression involving logs, but perhaps I made a wrong assumption in breaking it into base 2 components.\n\nWait, let me re-express the original equation properly.\n\nOriginal first equation:\n\n\\( x^{\\log_2(y z)} = 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\)\n\nIf I take log base 2 of both sides, as I did before:\n\nlog₂(left) = log₂(right),\n\nlog₂(x^{log₂(y z)}) = log₂(2^8 * 3^4),\n\nWhich is:\n\nlog₂(y z) * log₂x = 8 + 4 log₂3,\n\nWhich is what I had before. So that is correct. The left side is log₂(y z) multiplied by log₂x, which can indeed result in a combination of logs that can produce terms with log₂3.\n\nTherefore, my previous steps are correct.\n\nSo, returning, perhaps I need to proceed numerically.\n\nGiven that k = log₂3 ≈ 1.58496,\n\nLet me compute the approximate numerical values for the right-hand sides of the quadratic equations:\n\nFor a:\n\nEquation (1c): a² - S a + (8 + 4k) = 0,\n\nSo the constant term is 8 + 4k ≈ 8 + 4*(1.58496) ≈ 8 + 6.33984 ≈ 14.33984,\n\nSimilarly, for b:\n\nEquation (2c): b² - S b + (9 + 6k) ≈ 9 + 6*1.58496 ≈ 9 + 9.50976 ≈ 18.50976,\n\nFor c:\n\nEquation (3c): c² - S c + (5 + 10k) ≈ 5 + 10*1.58496 ≈ 5 + 15.8496 ≈ 20.8496,\n\nNow, we need to find S such that there exist real numbers a, b, c satisfying the three quadratic equations and S = a + b + c,\n\nwith the constraints from the discriminants:\n\nS² ≥ 4*(8 + 4k) ≈ 4*14.33984 ≈ 57.35936,\n\nS² ≥ 4*(9 + 6k) ≈ 4*18.50976 ≈ 74.03904,\n\nS² ≥ 4*(5 + 10k) ≈ 4*20.8496 ≈ 83.3984,\n\nTherefore, S² must be at least approximately 83.3984, so |S| ≥ sqrt(83.3984) ≈ 9.13,\n\nSince we are to minimize xyz = 2^{S}, and since 2^S is minimized when S is as small as possible (since the exponential function is increasing), we need the minimal possible S. Since S can be negative or positive, but with |S| ≥ ~9.13,\n\nso the minimal S is approximately -9.13,\n\nbut we need to check if S can actually be negative.\n\nEarlier, we considered that if S is negative, then a, b, c must all be negative numbers greater than S (i.e., between S and 0),\n\nand the sum S = a + b + c must be negative.\n\nHowever, let's see if with S negative, we can satisfy the equations.\n\nLet me suppose that S is negative, say S = -N where N > 0,\n\nthen the quadratic equations become:\n\nFor a:\n\na² - (-N)a + 14.33984 = a² + N a + 14.33984 = 0,\n\nWait, no, wait: quadratic equation for a is a² - S a + C = 0,\n\nif S is negative, say S = -N,\n\nthen quadratic equation becomes a² - (-N)a + C = a² + N a + C = 0,\n\nSimilarly for b and c.\n\nSo discriminant for a would be N² - 4*C,\n\nwhere C is the constant term,\n\nso discriminant D_a = N² - 4*(14.33984),\n\nSimilarly for D_b = N² - 4*(18.50976),\n\nD_c = N² - 4*(20.8496).\n\nFor real roots, discriminants must be non-negative,\n\nso:\n\nN² ≥ 4*14.33984 ≈ 57.359,\n\nN² ≥ 4*18.50976 ≈ 74.039,\n\nN² ≥ 4*20.8496 ≈ 83.398,\n\nso same as before, N ≥ sqrt(83.398) ≈ 9.13,\n\nso S = -N would be ≤ -9.13,\n\nand then a, b, c would be roots of quadratics with negative coefficients?\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nFor a's quadratic: a² + N a + 14.33984 = 0,\n\nthe roots are [ -N ± sqrt(N² - 4*14.33984) ] / 2,\n\nsince N is positive (because S = -N is negative),\n\nthen the roots are:\n\n[ -N ± sqrt(N² - 57.359) ] / 2,\n\nsince N ≥ 9.13, sqrt(N² - 57.359) is real.\n\nThe roots will be:\n\nFirst root: [ -N + sqrt(N² - 57.359) ] / 2,\n\nSecond root: [ -N - sqrt(N² - 57.359) ] / 2,\n\nSince N is positive and sqrt(N² - 57.359) < N (because N² - 57.359 < N²),\n\nso the first root is negative (because -N + something less than N is negative),\n\nand the second root is more negative.\n\nSimilarly for the other quadratics.\n\nTherefore, both roots are negative, so a, b, c must be negative, as we thought earlier.\n\nAdditionally, since we required that a, b, c are greater than S (which is negative), so they must be between S and 0.\n\nTherefore, for each quadratic, the roots must lie in (S, 0).\n\nLet me check for the quadratic for a:\n\nThe quadratic equation for a: a² + N a + 14.33984 = 0,\n\nwith roots:\n\na = [ -N ± sqrt(N² - 57.359) ] / 2,\n\nWe need these roots to be greater than S = -N,\n\nso:\n\n[ -N + sqrt(N² - 57.359) ] / 2 > -N,\n\nMultiply both sides by 2:\n\n- N + sqrt(N² - 57.359) > -2N,\n\nAdd N to both sides:\n\nsqrt(N² - 57.359) > -N,\n\nBut sqrt(N² - 57.359) is positive, and -N is negative (since N positive),\n\nso this inequality is always true.\n\nThe other root:\n\n[ -N - sqrt(N² - 57.359) ] / 2 > -N ?\n\nMultiply by 2:\n\n- N - sqrt(N² - 57.359) > -2N,\n\nAdd N to both sides:\n\n- sqrt(N² - 57.359) > -N,\n\nMultiply both sides by -1 (inequality reverses):\n\nsqrt(N² - 57.359) < N,\n\nWhich is true because sqrt(N² - c) < N for c > 0,\n\nso both roots satisfy a > S.\n\nSimilarly for the other variables.\n\nTherefore, for any N ≥ sqrt(83.398) ≈9.13,\n\nthe roots a, b, c will lie in (S, 0).\n\nTherefore, S can be as low as -9.13 or lower,\n\nbut we are to find the minimal xyz = 2^{S},\n\nwhich is minimized when S is as small as possible (most negative),\n\nso the minimal S is -sqrt(83.3984),\n\nbut we need to check if this is achievable, i.e., if at S = -sqrt(83.3984),\n\nthe three quadratics have roots that add up to S.\n\nWait, but S is defined as a + b + c,\n\nso if we set S = -sqrt(83.3984),\n\nthen we can compute a, b, c from their quadratics,\n\nand check whether their sum is indeed S.\n\nBut this requires solving numerically.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the minimal S is achieved when all three discriminants are zero,\n\ni.e., when S² = 4*(5 + 10k),\n\nsince that was the largest discriminant condition,\n\nso S² = 4*(5 + 10k),\n\nso S = -2*sqrt(5 + 10k),\n\nsince we want the most negative S,\n\nso S = -2*sqrt(5 + 10k).\n\nLet me compute that:\n\nk = log₂3 ≈1.58496,\n\nso 5 + 10k ≈5 + 15.8496≈20.8496,\n\nso sqrt(20.8496)≈4.566,\n\nso S ≈ -2*4.566≈-9.132,\n\nwhich matches the earlier approximate value.\n\nTherefore, if we set S = -2*sqrt(5 + 10k),\n\nthen the discriminant for the c quadratic (the one with the largest constant term) becomes zero,\n\nmeaning that there is exactly one real root (a double root),\n\nand the other discriminants are larger,\n\nso they would have two real roots each.\n\nBut in order for the system to be consistent,\n\nmaybe the minimal S is exactly when the most restrictive discriminant is zero,\n\ni.e., when S² = 4*(5 + 10k),\n\nso S = -2*sqrt(5 + 10k),\n\nand in this case, the c quadratic has a double root,\n\nand the a and b quadratics have two roots each,\n\nand we can choose the roots such that a + b + c = S.\n\nThis might be the case where the minimal S is achieved,\n\nsince making S any more negative than that would violate the discriminant condition for c.\n\nTherefore, perhaps the minimal possible S is S_min = -2*sqrt(5 + 10k),\n\nand then xyz = 2^{S_min} = 2^{-2*sqrt(5 + 10k)}.\n\nBut this seems complicated and likely not the answer they want, as it's in terms of log₂3.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there is an exact solution where S is a rational number,\n\nand the terms involving log₂3 cancel out?\n\nLet me think again about the equations.\n\nRecall that we had:\n\nab + bc + ca = 11 + 10k,\n\nand we also have the three quadratic equations:\n\na(S - a) = M1,\n\nb(S - b) = M2,\n\nc(S - c) = M3,\n\nwhere M1 = 8 + 4k,\n\nM2 = 9 + 6k,\n\nM3 = 5 + 10k,\n\nLet me consider adding the three equations:\n\na(S - a) + b(S - b) + c(S - c) = M1 + M2 + M3,\n\nWhich we already did, and it gives S² - (a² + b² + c²) = 22 + 20k,\n\nBut also, we know that a² + b² + c² = S² - (ab + bc + ca),\n\nWait, no: (a + b + c)^2 = a² + b² + c² + 2(ab + bc + ca),\n\nso a² + b² + c² = S² - 2(ab + bc + ca),\n\nTherefore,\n\nS² - [S² - 2(ab + bc + ca)] = 2(ab + bc + ca) = 22 + 20k,\n\nWhich is consistent with our previous result that ab + bc + ca = 11 + 10k.\n\nHmm. So, no new info.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider the product (a)(b)(c).\n\nLet me denote P = abc.\n\nPerhaps I can find an expression for P in terms of S and k?\n\nAlternatively, let me consider the three quadratic equations:\n\na(S - a) = M1 ⇒ aS - a² = M1,\n\nSimilarly,\n\nbS - b² = M2,\n\ncS - c² = M3,\n\nIf I multiply all three equations together:\n\n(aS - a²)(bS - b²)(cS - c²) = M1 M2 M3,\n\nBut this seems very complex.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider that if we treat a, b, c as variables satisfying those quadratic equations,\n\nand S = a + b + c,\n\nthen maybe we can set up a system where we can express everything in terms of S and k,\n\nand solve for S.\n\nLet me try to write equations for a, b, c in terms of S and k.\n\nFrom equation for a:\n\na² - S a + (8 + 4k) = 0 ⇒ a = [S ± sqrt(S² - 4*(8 + 4k))]/2,\n\nSimilarly for b and c.\n\nBut since we need a + b + c = S,\n\nthis is a complicated equation in S.\n\nAlternatively, suppose that all three variables a, b, c take the smaller root of their respective quadratics,\n\nsince if we take the larger root, the sum might exceed S,\n\nwhereas taking the smaller roots might give a sum of S.\n\nRecall that in the case of S positive,\n\nboth roots of each quadratic are between 0 and S,\n\nso choosing either root would still allow the sum to be S,\n\nbut perhaps for S negative,\n\ntaking the \"smaller\" root (which is the one closer to S),\n\nmight be necessary.\n\nAssuming that we take the smaller root for each quadratic,\n\nthen:\n\na = [S - sqrt(S² - 4M1)] / 2,\n\nb = [S - sqrt(S² - 4M2)] / 2,\n\nc = [S - sqrt(S² - 4M3)] / 2,\n\nthen,\n\nS = a + b + c = [3S - (sqrt(S² - 4M1) + sqrt(S² - 4M2) + sqrt(S² - 4M3))]/2,\n\nthen,\n\n2S = 3S - [sqrt(S² - 4M1) + sqrt(S² - 4M2) + sqrt(S² - 4M3)],\n\nso,\n\nsqrt(S² - 4M1) + sqrt(S² - 4M2) + sqrt(S² - 4M3) = S,\n\nThis is the same equation I had before.\n\nLet me denote D1 = S² - 4M1,\n\nD2 = S² - 4M2,\n\nD3 = S² - 4M3,\n\nthen sqrt(D1) + sqrt(D2) + sqrt(D3) = S,\n\nwith D1 = S² - 4*(8 + 4k),\n\nD2 = S² - 4*(9 + 6k),\n\nD3 = S² - 4*(5 + 10k),\n\nand k = log₂3,\n\nLet me substitute numerical values for k to approximate S.\n\nLet me use k ≈1.58496,\n\nthen,\n\nM1 = 8 + 4k ≈8 + 6.33984 ≈14.33984,\n\nM2 =9 + 6k ≈9 + 9.50976 ≈18.50976,\n\nM3 =5 + 10k ≈5 +15.8496 ≈20.8496,\n\nSo,\n\nD1 = S² - 4*14.33984 ≈ S² - 57.35936,\n\nD2 = S² - 4*18.50976 ≈ S² - 74.03904,\n\nD3 = S² - 4*20.8496 ≈ S² - 83.3984,\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\nsqrt(S² - 57.35936) + sqrt(S² - 74.03904) + sqrt(S² - 83.3984) = S,\n\nLet me denote f(S) = sqrt(S² - 57.35936) + sqrt(S² - 74.03904) + sqrt(S² - 83.3984) - S,\n\nand we need to find S such that f(S) = 0.\n\nWe are looking for S ≤ -9.13 (since the minimal S is around -9.13),\n\nso let's try S = -10,\n\nthen,\n\nCompute each sqrt term:\n\nFirst term: sqrt(100 - 57.35936) = sqrt(42.64064) ≈6.532,\n\nSecond term: sqrt(100 - 74.03904) = sqrt(25.96096) ≈5.095,\n\nThird term: sqrt(100 - 83.3984) = sqrt(16.6016) ≈4.075,\n\nSum: 6.532 + 5.095 + 4.075 ≈15.702,\n\nThen f(-10) = 15.702 - (-10) = 15.702 +10 =25.702 >0,\n\nso f(-10)=25.7>0,\n\nWe need f(S)=0,\n\nso need to go to more negative S?\n\nWait, but S is already at -10,\n\nbut the sqrt terms are all real as long as S² ≥83.3984,\n\nso S <= -9.13 or S >=9.13.\n\nLet me try S = -9.13,\n\nthen S² ≈83.398,\n\nso,\n\nFirst term sqrt(83.398 -57.35936)=sqrt(26.03864)≈5.103,\n\nSecond term sqrt(83.398 -74.03904)=sqrt(9.35896)≈3.059,\n\nThird term sqrt(83.398 -83.3984)=sqrt(-0.0004),\n\nWait, that's imaginary!\n\nWait, S² - 83.3984 ≈83.398 -83.3984≈-0.0004,\n\nso the third sqrt becomes imaginary,\n\nso S must be less than or equal to -sqrt(83.3984)≈-9.13,\n\nso S must be less than -9.13,\n\nso let's pick S = -9.2,\n\nthen S² = 84.64,\n\nFirst term: sqrt(84.64 -57.35936)=sqrt(27.28064)≈5.223,\n\nSecond term: sqrt(84.64 -74.03904)=sqrt(10.60096)≈3.256,\n\nThird term: sqrt(84.64 -83.3984)=sqrt(1.2416)=1.114,\n\nSum: 5.223 + 3.256 +1.114≈9.593,\n\nThen f(S)=9.593 - (-9.2)=9.593+9.2≈18.793>0,\n\nStill positive.\n\nTry S = -12,\n\nS²=144,\n\nFirst term: sqrt(144-57.35936)=sqrt(86.64064)=~9.308,\n\nSecond term: sqrt(144-74.03904)=sqrt(69.96096)=~8.363,\n\nThird term: sqrt(144-83.3984)=sqrt(60.6016)=~7.785,\n\nSum:9.308+8.363+7.785≈25.456,\n\nf(S)=25.456 - (-12)=25.456+12≈37.456>0,\n\nHmm, getting larger.\n\nWait a second, as S becomes more negative, the left-hand side sqrt terms increase,\n\nand the right-hand side S is also becoming more negative,\n\nso f(S)= sum_sqrt - S,\n\nsince S is negative, subtracting a negative is adding its absolute value,\n\nso f(S) increases as S becomes more negative,\n\nso the function f(S) is increasing for S < -sqrt(83.3984),\n\nand at S = -sqrt(83.3984),\n\nthe third sqrt term becomes zero,\n\nso f(S) at that point would be sqrt(S² -57.35936) + sqrt(S² -74.03904) + 0 - S,\n\nwith S = -sqrt(83.3984)≈-9.13,\n\nthen,\n\nFirst term: sqrt(83.3984 -57.35936)=sqrt(26.039)≈5.103,\n\nSecond term: sqrt(83.3984 -74.03904)=sqrt(9.359)≈3.06,\n\nThird term:0,\n\nso total sum:5.103+3.06≈8.163,\n\nthen f(S)=8.163 - (-9.13)=8.163+9.13≈17.29>0,\n\nstill positive.\n\nWait, so f(S) is always positive for S ≤ -sqrt(83.3984),\n\nmeaning that there is no solution with S negative?\n\nHmm, that suggests that there is no solution with S negative,\n\nwhich would mean that S must be positive,\n\nand we need to find S>0 such that f(S)=0,\n\nwhere f(S)=sqrt(S² -57.35936)+sqrt(S² -74.03904)+sqrt(S² -83.3984) - S =0.\n\nLet me try S=10,\n\nthen,\n\nFirst term: sqrt(100 -57.35936)=sqrt(42.64064)=~6.532,\n\nSecond term: sqrt(100 -74.03904)=sqrt(25.96096)=~5.095,\n\nThird term: sqrt(100 -83.3984)=sqrt(16.6016)=~4.075,\n\nSum:6.532+5.095+4.075≈15.702,\n\nf(10)=15.702 -10≈5.702>0,\n\nStill positive.\n\nTry S=12,\n\nFirst term: sqrt(144-57.359)=sqrt(86.64)=~9.308,\n\nSecond term:sqrt(144-74.039)=sqrt(69.96)=~8.363,\n\nThird term:sqrt(144-83.398)=sqrt(60.602)=~7.785,\n\nSum≈9.308+8.363+7.785≈25.456,\n\nf(12)=25.456 -12≈13.456>0,\n\nStill positive.\n\nHmm. Hmm.\n\nWait, what happens as S approaches infinity?\n\nAs S→infty,\n\neach sqrt(S² - C) ~ S - C/(2S),\n\nso sqrt(S² - C) ≈ S - C/(2S),\n\nso the sum sqrt1 + sqrt2 + sqrt3 ≈ 3S - [C1 + C2 + C3]/(2S),\n\nwhere C1=4M1≈57.35936,\n\nC2=4M2≈74.03904,\n\nC3=4M3≈83.3984,\n\nso total constants:57.35936+74.03904+83.3984≈214.7968,\n\nso,\n\nsum ≈3S - 214.7968/(2S),\n\nthen f(S)=sum - S ≈2S - 214.7968/(2S),\n\nwhich for large S behaves like 2S, which tends to infinity,\n\nso f(S) → ∞ as S→infty,\n\nand f(S) is positive at S=10,12, etc.\n\nAt S= sqrt(83.3984)=~9.13,\n\nthen,\n\nFirst sqrt: sqrt(83.3984 -57.35936)=sqrt(26.039)=~5.103,\n\nSecond sqrt: sqrt(83.3984 -74.03904)=sqrt(9.359)=~3.06,\n\nThird sqrt: sqrt(83.3984 -83.3984)=0,\n\nso sum=5.103+3.06+0≈8.163,\n\nf(S)=8.163 -9.13≈-0.967,\n\nAh! Here we go. At S = sqrt(83.3984)≈9.13,\n\nf(S)≈-0.967,\n\nso f(S) is negative at S≈9.13,\n\nand positive at S=10,\n\nso by the Intermediate Value Theorem,\n\nthere is a root between S≈9.13 and S=10,\n\nwhere f(S)=0.\n\nTherefore, the minimal S is between 9.13 and 10,\n\nand we need to find this S numerically.\n\nLet me try S=9.5,\n\nthen,\n\nS²=90.25,\n\nFirst term: sqrt(90.25 -57.359)=sqrt(32.891)≈5.736,\n\nSecond term: sqrt(90.25 -74.039)=sqrt(16.211)≈4.026,\n\nThird term: sqrt(90.25 -83.398)=sqrt(6.852)≈2.618,\n\nSum≈5.736+4.026+2.618≈12.38,\n\nf(9.5)=12.38 -9.5≈2.88>0,\n\nHmm, still positive.\n\nAt S=9.13,\n\nf(S)=~ -0.967,\n\nat S=9.2,\n\nS²=84.64,\n\nFirst term: sqrt(84.64 -57.359)=sqrt(27.28)=~5.223,\n\nSecond term: sqrt(84.64-74.039)=sqrt(10.60)=~3.256,\n\nThird term: sqrt(84.64-83.398)=sqrt(1.24)=~1.113,\n\nSum≈5.223+3.256+1.113≈9.592,\n\nf(S)=9.592 -9.2≈0.392>0,\n\nSo between S=9.13 and S=9.2,\n\nf(S) crosses from negative to positive.\n\nLet me try S=9.15,\n\nS²=83.7225,\n\nFirst term: sqrt(83.7225 -57.359)=sqrt(26.3635)=~5.133,\n\nSecond term: sqrt(83.7225 -74.039)=sqrt(9.6835)=~3.112,\n\nThird term: sqrt(83.7225 -83.398)=sqrt(0.3245)=~0.569,\n\nSum≈5.133+3.112+0.569≈8.814,\n\nf(S)=8.814 -9.15≈-0.336<0,\n\nSo at S=9.15, f(S)=~ -0.336,\n\nat S=9.2, f(S)=+0.392,\n\nso the root is between 9.15 and 9.2.\n\nLet me try S=9.18,\n\nS²= (9.18)^2 = 84.2724,\n\nFirst term: sqrt(84.2724 -57.359)=sqrt(26.9134)=~5.187,\n\nSecond term: sqrt(84.2724 -74.039)=sqrt(10.2334)=~3.198,\n\nThird term: sqrt(84.2724 -83.398)=sqrt(0.8744)=~0.935,\n\nSum≈5.187+3.198+0.935≈9.32,\n\nf(S)=9.32 -9.18≈0.14>0,\n\nSo between 9.15 and 9.18,\n\nAt S=9.16,\n\nS²= (9.16)^2=83.9056,\n\nFirst term: sqrt(83.9056 -57.359)=sqrt(26.5466)=~5.144,\n\nSecond term: sqrt(83.9056 -74.039)=sqrt(9.8666)=~3.141,\n\nThird term: sqrt(83.9056 -83.398)=sqrt(0.5076)=~0.712,\n\nSum≈5.144+3.141+0.712≈8.997,\n\nf(S)=8.997 -9.16≈-0.163<0,\n\nAt S=9.17,\n\nS²=9.17²= approx 84.0889,\n\nFirst term: sqrt(84.0889 -57.359)=sqrt(26.7299)=~5.169,\n\nSecond term: sqrt(84.0889 -74.039)=sqrt(10.0499)=~3.17,\n\nThird term: sqrt(84.0889 -83.398)=sqrt(0.6909)=~0.831,\n\nSum≈5.169+3.17+0.831≈9.17,\n\nf(S)=9.17 -9.17=0,\n\nWait, exactly zero?\n\nWait, let me compute precisely:\n\nLet me compute S=9.17,\n\nFirst term: sqrt(S² -57.35936),\n\nS²=9.17^2= (9 +0.17)^2=81 + 2*9*0.17 +0.17²=81 +3.06 +0.0289=84.0889,\n\nSo S² -57.35936=84.0889-57.35936=26.72954,\n\nsqrt(26.72954)=5.169,\n\nSecond term: S² -74.03904=84.0889-74.03904=10.04986,\n\nsqrt(10.04986)=3.17,\n\nThird term: S² -83.3984=84.0889-83.3984=0.6905,\n\nsqrt(0.6905)=0.831,\n\nSum:5.169 +3.17 +0.831=5.169+3.17 is 8.339, plus 0.831 is 9.17,\n\nSo f(S)=9.17 -9.17=0,\n\nWow, so S=9.17 exactly satisfies the equation?\n\nWait, that seems too perfect. Let me verify the calculations with more precision.\n\nLet me compute each term with more decimal places.\n\nFirst, S =9.17,\n\nCompute S squared:\n\n9.17 * 9.17:\n\nFirst, 9 *9 =81,\n\n9 *0.17=1.53,\n\n0.17*9=1.53,\n\n0.17*0.17=0.0289,\n\nSo,\n\n(9 +0.17)^2 =9^2 + 2*9*0.17 +0.17^2 =81 + 3.06 +0.0289=84.0889,\n\nYes.\n\nFirst sqrt term:\n\nsqrt(S² -4*M1)=sqrt(84.0889 -4*14.33984),\n\nCompute 4*M1=4*(8 +4k)=4*14.33984=57.35936,\n\nThus,\n\nsqrt(84.0889 -57.35936)=sqrt(26.72954),\n\nCompute sqrt(26.72954):\n\n5.169^2 = (5 +0.169)^2 =25 + 2*5*0.169 +0.169²=25+1.69+0.028561≈26.718561,\n\nWhich is very close to 26.72954,\n\nso sqrt(26.72954)≈5.169 + (26.72954 -26.718561)/(2*5.169)\n\n≈5.169 + (0.010979)/(10.338)\n\n≈5.169 +0.00106≈5.17006,\n\nSecond sqrt term:\n\nsqrt(S² -4*M2)=sqrt(84.0889 -4*(9+6k)),\n\n4*M2=4*(9 +6k)=4*(9+9.50976)=4*(18.50976)=74.03904,\n\nThus,\n\nsqrt(84.0889 -74.03904)=sqrt(10.04986),\n\nsqrt(10.04986):\n\n3.17^2=10.0489,\n\nso sqrt(10.04986)=3.17 + (10.04986-10.0489)/(2*3.17)\n\n≈3.17 +0.00096/6.34≈3.17 +0.00015≈3.17015,\n\nThird sqrt term:\n\nsqrt(S² -4*M3)=sqrt(84.0889 -4*(5 +10k)),\n\n4*M3=4*(5 +10k)=4*(5+15.8496)=4*20.8496≈83.3984,\n\nThus,\n\nsqrt(84.0889 -83.3984)=sqrt(0.6905),\n\nsqrt(0.6905)=0.8308,\n\nSo total sum:\n\nFirst term:≈5.17006,\n\nSecond term:≈3.17015,\n\nThird term:≈0.8308,\n\nSum≈5.17006+3.17015=8.34021 +0.8308≈9.17101,\n\nThen f(S)=9.17101 -9.17≈0.00101,\n\nSo very close to zero, with a small positive error due to rounding.\n\nTherefore, S≈9.17 is a solution.\n\nTherefore, S is approximately 9.17,\n\nand since we need the minimal xyz = 2^S,\n\nso approximately 2^9.17,\n\ncompute 2^9 =512,\n\n2^0.17 ≈ e^{0.17 ln2} ≈ e^{0.17*0.6931}≈e^{0.1178}≈1.125,\n\nso 2^9.17≈512 *1.125≈576,\n\nHmm, 512 *1.125= 512*(9/8)=512*1.125=576,\n\nWait, 0.17 is approximately 1/6 (0.1667),\n\nso maybe the exact value is 576?\n\nWait, 2^9.17 is approximately 576,\n\nbut let me see if S is exactly 9.17,\n\nbut 9.17 is 9 + 0.17,\n\nbut perhaps S is exactly 9.17,\n\nWait, but in reality, we had S=9.17 gives f(S)=~0.001,\n\nvery close to zero,\n\nso perhaps S is exactly 9.17,\n\nbut 9.17 is 917/100,\n\nbut that's probably not exact.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the exact value is S=9.17,\n\nbut in fractional terms,\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's an exact solution where S=9.2,\n\nWait, but at S=9.17,\n\nthe approximate calculation gives f(S)=0.001,\n\nso it's very close,\n\nso likely the problem expects an exact answer,\n\nand my approach with assuming S=9.17 is approximate,\n\nbut perhaps there is an exact solution when the terms work out nicely.\n\nWait, let me consider that maybe k=log₂3 is involved in such a way that the terms simplify.\n\nLet me recall that in the equation f(S)=0,\n\nwe had:\n\nsqrt(S² -4M1) + sqrt(S² -4M2) + sqrt(S² -4M3) = S,\n\nwhere M1=8+4k,\n\nM2=9+6k,\n\nM3=5+10k,\n\nwith k=log₂3,\n\nLet me denote K = log₂3,\n\nso that,\n\nM1 =8 +4K,\n\nM2=9+6K,\n\nM3=5+10K,\n\nThen,\n\nthe equation is:\n\nsqrt(S² -4*(8 +4K)) + sqrt(S² -4*(9 +6K)) + sqrt(S² -4*(5 +10K)) = S,\n\nLet me factor the 4 inside the square roots:\n\nsqrt(S² - 16 -16K) + sqrt(S² -36 -24K) + sqrt(S² -20 -40K) = S,\n\nHmm, perhaps if we can find an S such that the terms inside the square roots are perfect squares,\n\nor relate to each other in a way that the sum simplifies to S.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps S is chosen such that each term inside the sqrt is a square,\n\nbut this might be a stretch.\n\nAlternatively, suppose that S is chosen such that S² -4M1 = (S - a)^2,\n\nbut this might not help.\n\nAlternatively, let me see if there's a relation between the coefficients.\n\nNotice that M3 =5+10K,\n\nM1=8+4K,\n\nM2=9+6K,\n\nIf I consider that M1, M2, M3 are in arithmetic progression?\n\nCheck the differences:\n\nM2 - M1 = (9+6K) - (8+4K) =1 +2K,\n\nM3 - M2 = (5+10K) - (9+6K)= -4 +4K,\n\nNot arithmetic progression.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the coefficients of K are multiples?\n\nM1 has 4K,\n\nM2 has 6K,\n\nM3 has 10K,\n\nwhich are 4,6,10, which are 2*2, 2*3, 2*5,\n\nbut not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps set S = something involving K,\n\nLet me suppose that S = a + bK,\n\nwhere a and b are constants to be determined,\n\nthen substitute into the equation and solve for a and b,\n\nbut this might be complicated.\n\nAlternatively, let me see if the terms can be expressed as multiples of S.\n\nSuppose that sqrt(S² -4M1) = p*S,\n\nsqrt(S² -4M2)= q*S,\n\nsqrt(S² -4M3)= r*S,\n\nthen p*S + q*S + r*S = S,\n\nso (p + q + r -1)S =0,\n\nso either S=0 or p+q+r=1,\n\nBut S can't be zero because then the left-hand side sqrt terms would involve sqrt(-4M1), which is imaginary,\n\nso p + q + r =1,\n\nAdditionally,\n\nsqrt(S² -4M1)=p S ⇒ S² -4M1 = p² S² ⇒ (1 - p²) S² =4M1,\n\nSimilarly,\n\n(1 - q²) S² =4M2,\n\n(1 - r²) S² =4M3,\n\nThus,\n\n(1 - p²):(1 - q²):(1 - r²) = 4M1 :4M2:4M3 = M1:M2:M3,\n\nSo,\n\n(1 - p²):(1 - q²):(1 - r²) = (8+4K):(9+6K):(5+10K),\n\nBut also, p + q + r =1,\n\nThis is a system of equations in variables p, q, r,\n\nwhich may or may not have a solution,\n\nbut it's getting too abstract.\n\nAlternatively, given that in the numerical solution S≈9.17,\n\nand 2^9.17 is approximately 576,\n\nand 576 is 2^6 * 3^2,\n\nWait, 2^6 is 64, 3^2 is 9, 64*9=576,\n\nHmm, interesting,\n\nAlternatively, maybe the exact value of S is 9.17,\n\nand 2^9.17 is exactly 576?\n\nWait, let me check 2^9.17,\n\nTake natural logs,\n\nln(2^9.17)=9.17*ln2≈9.17*0.6931≈6.365,\n\nExponentiate: e^6.365≈ e^6 * e^0.365≈403.4288 *1.44≈582.3,\n\nHmm, which is not 576,\n\nAlternatively, perhaps 2^9.17 is approximately 576,\n\nbut maybe the exact answer is 576,\n\nand the problem is set up so that S is 9.17,\n\nbut in exact terms,\n\nWait, let me see:\n\nSuppose that S is such that,\n\nsqrt(S² -4M1) + sqrt(S² -4M2) + sqrt(S² -4M3) = S,\n\nand when I plugged in S=9.17,\n\nit was very close to zero,\n\nso likely the problem is constructed such that S is exactly 9.2,\n\nbut then,\n\nWait, let me try S= 9.2,\n\nthen,\n\nFirst term sqrt(S² -4M1)=sqrt(84.64 -57.35936)=sqrt(27.28064)=5.223,\n\nSecond term sqrt(84.64 -74.03904)=sqrt(10.60096)=3.256,\n\nThird term sqrt(84.64 -83.3984)=sqrt(1.2416)=1.114,\n\nTotal sum≈5.223+3.256+1.114=9.593,\n\nthen f(S)=9.593 -9.2=0.393>0,\n\nso not zero,\n\nHmm,\n\nAlternatively, perhaps S= 9.18,\n\nthen,\n\nS=9.18,\n\nS²=84.2724,\n\nFirst term sqrt(84.2724-57.35936)=sqrt(26.913)=5.187,\n\nSecond term sqrt(84.2724-74.03904)=sqrt(10.233)=3.198,\n\nThird term sqrt(84.2724-83.3984)=sqrt(0.874)=0.935,\n\nTotal sum=5.187+3.198+0.935≈9.32,\n\nf(S)=9.32-9.18=0.14>0,\n\nHmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is designed so that when you multiply all three original equations, you can find xyz directly?\n\nLet me revisit that idea.\n\nOriginal three equations:\n\n1. \\( x^{\\log_2(yz)} = 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\)\n\n2. \\( y^{\\log_2(zx)} = 2^9 \\cdot 3^6 \\)\n\n3. \\( z^{\\log_2(xy)} = 2^5 \\cdot 3^{10} \\)\n\nLet me take the product of all three equations:\n\nLeft-hand side: \\( x^{\\log_2(yz)} \\cdot y^{\\log_2(zx)} \\cdot z^{\\log_2(xy)} \\)\n\nRight-hand side: \\( 2^{8+9+5} \\cdot 3^{4+6+10} = 2^{22} \\cdot 3^{20} \\)\n\nLet me work on the left-hand side.\n\nLet me write each term's exponent as a logarithm:\n\nFirst term exponent: \\( \\log_2(y z) \\),\n\nSecond term exponent: \\( \\log_2(z x) \\),\n\nThird term exponent: \\( \\log_2(x y) \\),\n\nTherefore,\n\nLeft-hand side = \\( x^{\\log_2(y z)} \\cdot y^{\\log_2(z x)} \\cdot z^{\\log_2(x y)} \\)\n\nLet me take the logarithm base 2 of the left-hand side:\n\nlog₂(LHS) = log₂(x^{log₂(y z)}) + log₂(y^{log₂(z x)}) + log₂(z^{log₂(x y)})\n\n= log₂(y z) * log₂x + log₂(z x) * log₂y + log₂(x y) * log₂z\n\nWhich is exactly what we had before, and we know this equals 22 + 20 log₂3,\n\nwhich is the same as log₂(RHS).\n\nBut this doesn't directly help us find xyz.\n\nAlternatively, let me express the left-hand side differently.\n\nLet me note that:\n\nLet me denote A = log₂x, B = log₂y, C = log₂z,\n\nso that x = 2^A, y=2^B, z=2^C,\n\nThen, log₂(y z) = B + C,\n\nlog₂(z x) = C + A,\n\nlog₂(x y) = A + B,\n\nTherefore, the left-hand side becomes:\n\n(2^A)^{B + C} * (2^B)^{C + A} * (2^C)^{A + B}\n\n= 2^{A(B + C)} * 2^{B(C + A)} * 2^{C(A + B)}\n\n= 2^{A(B + C) + B(C + A) + C(A + B)}.\n\nThe exponent is:\n\nA(B + C) + B(C + A) + C(A + B) = AB + AC + BC + BA + CA + CB = 2(AB + BC + CA),\n\nThus, LHS = 2^{2(AB + BC + CA)}.\n\nThe right-hand side is 2^{22} * 3^{20},\n\nSo,\n\n2^{2(AB + BC + CA)} = 2^{22} * 3^{20},\n\nBut this can't be directly equated because the left-hand side is a power of 2 and the right-hand side has a factor of 3^{20},\n\nwhich implies that this approach might not be the way to go,\n\nunless we consider that AB + BC + CA must somehow relate to the exponents of 3,\n\nbut this seems not straightforward.\n\nWait a second, perhaps I made a mistake in the substitution.\n\nWait, actually, when I substituted x = 2^A etc., the original equations are in terms of x, y, z raised to logarithmic exponents,\n\nbut the right-hand sides have factors of 3,\n\nwhich are not powers of 2,\n\nso when we take the logarithm base 2 of both sides,\n\nthe right-hand side has terms with log₂3,\n\nwhich is why we ended up with equations involving k = log₂3.\n\nTherefore, the LHS after substitution is 2^{2(AB + BC + CA)} = 2^{22} * 3^{20},\n\nwhich is not possible unless 3^{20} is also a power of 2,\n\nwhich it is not,\n\nso this suggests that my substitution missed something,\n\nWait, no: the left-hand side was computed as 2^{2(AB + BC + CA)}, but the right-hand side is 2^{22} * 3^{20},\n\nwhich is not a power of 2,\n\nso this indicates that my previous step must be wrong.\n\nWait, no, actually, no.\n\nWait, let me retrace.\n\nOriginal left-hand side after substitution:\n\nEach term is (2^A)^{log₂(y z)} = 2^{A * log₂(y z)}.\n\nBut log₂(y z) is log₂(2^B * 2^C) = log₂(2^{B+C}) = B + C,\n\nso exponent becomes A*(B + C),\n\ntherefore,\n\n(2^A)^{log₂(y z)} = 2^{A(B + C)}.\n\nSimilarly for the other terms,\n\nso the entire left-hand side is indeed 2^{A(B + C) + B(C + A) + C(A + B)} = 2^{2(AB + BC + CA)}.\n\nBut the right-hand side is 2^{22} * 3^{20},\n\nwhich is not a power of 2,\n\nso this is a contradiction unless 3^{20} is somehow incorporated,\n\nwhich it isn't,\n\nso this suggests that my approach is flawed?\n\nWait, no, actually, the problem is that when I took the logarithm base 2 of the left-hand side, I assumed it was equal to the logarithm of the right-hand side,\n\nbut the right-hand side has factors of 3,\n\nso log₂(RHS) = log₂(2^{22} * 3^{20}) = 22 + 20 log₂3,\n\nwhich is exactly what we had before,\n\nand the left-hand side's log₂ is 2(AB + BC + CA),\n\nso,\n\n2(AB + BC + CA) = 22 + 20 log₂3,\n\nThus,\n\nAB + BC + CA = 11 + 10 log₂3,\n\nwhich matches equation (5) that we had earlier,\n\nso this is consistent,\n\nbut this doesn't directly help us find xyz = 2^{A+B+C},\n\nsince we need to find A+B+C = S,\n\nand we have AB + BC + CA = 11 +10k,\n\nbut we also have the three quadratic equations.\n\nThis seems to loop back to the previous equations.\n\nGiven that in the numerical solution, S ≈9.17,\n\nand 2^9.17 ≈ 576,\n\nand 576 is 2^6 * 3^2,\n\nwhich is 64 * 9,\n\nand the exponents in the original equations have factors of 3^4, 3^6, 3^10,\n\nwhich are multiples of 4, 6, 10,\n\nand 4 +6 +10 =20,\n\nwhich is the exponent of 3 in the product,\n\nand 8+9+5=22 for the exponent of 2,\n\nso maybe the minimal xyz is 2^{9.17}=576,\n\nand since the problem asks for the smallest possible value,\n\nand 576 is a nice number,\n\nperhaps the answer is 576,\n\nand the slight discrepancy in the calculation is due to rounding,\n\nso maybe the exact value is 576,\n\nand S= log₂(576),\n\nsince xyz=576=2^{log₂576},\n\nso log₂576= log₂(2^6 *3^2)=6 + 2 log₂3,\n\nBut wait, S= A+B+C= log₂x + log₂y + log₂z= log₂(xyz),\n\nso xyz=2^S,\n\nso if S=6 + 2 log₂3,\n\nthen xyz=2^{6 + 2 log₂3}=2^6 * (2^{log₂3})^2=64 * 3^2=64*9=576,\n\nYes! That's brilliant.\n\nTherefore, if S=6 + 2 log₂3,\n\nthen xyz=2^{S}=2^{6 + 2 log₂3}=2^6 * 2^{2 log₂3}=64 * (2^{log₂3})^2=64*3^2=64*9=576,\n\nTherefore, the minimal value of xyz is 576,\n\nand this occurs when S=6 + 2 log₂3,\n\nand this must satisfy the earlier equations.\n\nWait, let me verify this.\n\nSuppose S =6 + 2 log₂3,\n\nthen we need to verify whether this S satisfies the equation:\n\nsqrt(S² -4M1) + sqrt(S² -4M2) + sqrt(S² -4M3) = S,\n\nwhere M1=8+4k, M2=9+6k, M3=5+10k, with k=log₂3.\n\nLet me compute each term.\n\nFirst, let me compute S =6 + 2k,\n\nsince k=log₂3,\n\nso S=6 + 2k,\n\nthen,\n\nCompute S² = (6 + 2k)^2 =36 +24k +4k²,\n\nCompute each term inside the square roots:\n\nFirst term: S² -4M1 = S² -4*(8 +4k)=36 +24k +4k² -32 -16k =4 +8k +4k²,\n\nSecond term: S² -4M2 = S² -4*(9 +6k)=36 +24k +4k² -36 -24k =4k²,\n\nThird term: S² -4M3 = S² -4*(5 +10k)=36 +24k +4k² -20 -40k =16 -16k +4k²,\n\nSo now,\n\nsqrt(S² -4M1)=sqrt(4 +8k +4k²)=sqrt(4(k² +2k +1))=sqrt(4(k+1)^2)=2(k+1),\n\nsince k is positive, so sqrt is positive.\n\nSecond term: sqrt(S² -4M2)=sqrt(4k²)=2k,\n\nThird term: sqrt(S² -4M3)=sqrt(4k² -16k +16)=sqrt{(2k -4)^2}=|2k -4|,\n\nSince k=log₂3≈1.58496,\n\n2k≈3.16992,\n\nso 2k -4≈-0.83008,\n\nso absolute value is 4 -2k,\n\nThus,\n\nThird sqrt term is 4 -2k,\n\nThus,\n\nTotal sum:\n\nFirst term: 2(k +1),\n\nSecond term: 2k,\n\nThird term:4 -2k,\n\nAdding them together:\n\n2(k +1) +2k + (4 -2k)=2k +2 +2k +4 -2k= (2k +2k -2k)+(2 +4)=2k +6,\n\nNow, set this equal to S:\n\n2k +6 = S,\n\nBut S=6 +2k,\n\nYes! Therefore,\n\n2k +6 =6 +2k,\n\nWhich is an identity.\n\nTherefore, this S=6 +2k satisfies the equation exactly!\n\nWow, so my initial numerical approach was leading me to S≈9.17,\n\nbut actually, the exact solution is S=6 +2k,\n\nwhere k=log₂3,\n\nand thus xyz=2^{S}=2^{6 + 2 log₂3}=2^6 * (2^{log₂3})^2=64 *3^2=64*9=576,\n\nTherefore, the minimal value of xyz is 576.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \\boxed{576}.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{576}\n</think>\n\nGiven the equations involving positive real numbers \\(x\\), \\(y\\), and \\(z\\):\n\n1. \\( x^{\\log_{2}(yz)} = 2^{8} \\cdot 3^{4} \\)\n2. \\( y^{\\log_{2}(zx)} = 2^{9} \\cdot 3^{6} \\)\n3. \\( z^{\\log_{2}(xy)} = 2^{5} \\cdot 3^{10} \\)\n\nWe aim to find the smallest possible value of \\(xyz\\).\n\nFirst, we take the logarithm base 2 of each equation:\n\n1. \\(\\log_{2}\\left(x^{\\log_{2}(yz)}\\right) = \\log_{2}(2^{8} \\cdot 3^{4})\\)\n   \\[\n   \\log_{2}(yz) \\cdot \\log_{2}(x) = 8 + 4 \\log_{2}(3)\n   \\]\n2. \\(\\log_{2}\\left(y^{\\log_{2}(zx)}\\right) = \\log_{2}(2^{9} \\cdot 3^{6})\\)\n   \\[\n   \\log_{2}(zx) \\cdot \\log_{2}(y) = 9 + 6 \\log_{2}(3)\n   \\]\n3. \\(\\log_{2}\\left(z^{\\log_{2}(xy)}\\right) = \\log_{2}(2^{5} \\cdot 3^{10})\\)\n   \\[\n   \\log_{2}(xy) \\cdot \\log_{2}(z) = 5 + 10 \\log_{2}(3)\n   \\]\n\nLet \\(a = \\log_{2}(x)\\), \\(b = \\log_{2}(y)\\), and \\(c = \\log_{2}(z)\\). Then the equations become:\n\n1. \\(a(b + c) = 8 + 4k\\)\n2. \\(b(c + a) = 9 + 6k\\)\n3. \\(c(a + b) = 5 + 10k\\)\n\nwhere \\(k = \\log_{2}(3)\\).\n\nWe also know that \\(S = a + b + c\\). Adding the three equations:\n\n\\[\na(b + c) + b(c + a) + c(a + b) = (8 + 4k) + (9 + 6k) + (5 + 10k)\n\\]\n\\[\n2(ab + bc + ca) = 22 + 20k \\implies ab + bc + ca = 11 + 10k\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\(S\\) such that the quadratic equations for \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) have real solutions. After detailed analysis and solving the quadratic equations, we find that the minimal \\(S\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\nS = 6 + 2k\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(k = \\log_{2}(3)\\). Therefore, the value of \\(xyz\\) is:\n\n\\[\nxyz = 2^{S} = 2^{6 + 2 \\log_{2}(3)} = 2^6 \\cdot (2^{\\log_{2}(3)})^2 = 64 \\cdot 3^2 = 64 \\cdot 9 = 576\n\\]\n\nThus, the smallest possible value of \\(xyz\\) is \\(\\boxed{576}\\).","num_tokens":25767,"correct":false,"formatted":true},{"predicted_answer":"\\dfrac{1}{576}","full_generation":"Okay, so I have this problem here with three equations involving logarithms and exponents, and I need to find the smallest possible value of the product \\( x y z \\) where \\( x \\), \\( y \\), and \\( z \\) are positive real numbers. The equations given are:\n\n1. \\( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} = 2^{8} \\cdot 3^{4} \\)\n2. \\( y^{\\log_{2}(z x)} = 2^{9} \\cdot 3^{6} \\)\n3. \\( z^{\\log_{2}(x y)} = 2^{5} \\cdot 3^{10} \\)\n\nHmm, all three equations have a similar structure. Each variable is raised to the log base 2 of the product of the other two variables, and the right-hand side is some power of 2 times some power of 3. Since they all involve logarithms with base 2 and exponents with base 2 implicitly (because the right-hand sides are powers of 2 and 3), maybe I can take logarithms base 2 of both sides to simplify these equations?\n\nLet me try that. Let me start with the first equation:\n\n\\( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} = 2^{8} \\cdot 3^{4} \\)\n\nIf I take the logarithm base 2 of both sides, that should help. Remember that \\( \\log_{b}(a^{c}) = c \\cdot \\log_{b}(a) \\). So applying that property:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}\\left( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} \\right) = \\log_{2}\\left( 2^{8} \\cdot 3^{4} \\right) \\)\n\nSimplify the left side first: \\( \\log_{2}\\left( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} \\right) = \\log_{2}(y z) \\cdot \\log_{2}(x) \\), because \\( \\log_b(a^c) = c \\cdot \\log_b(a) \\). Wait, actually, hold on. Wait, no, that's not quite right. Wait, actually, let me think again.\n\nWait, actually, the left-hand side is \\( \\log_{2}\\left( [x]^{\\log_{2}(y z)} \\right) \\). Let me denote \\( A = \\log_{2}(y z) \\), so then the expression becomes \\( \\log_{2}(x^{A}) \\), which is \\( A \\cdot \\log_{2}(x) \\), so substituting back in, that would be \\( \\log_{2}(y z) \\cdot \\log_{2}(x) \\). So yes, that is correct. So left side simplifies to \\( \\log_{2}(x) \\cdot \\log_{2}(y z) \\).\n\nNow, the right-hand side: \\( \\log_{2}\\left( 2^{8} \\cdot 3^{4} \\right) \\). Since logarithm of a product is the sum of logarithms, so:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}(2^{8}) + \\log_{2}(3^{4}) = 8 \\cdot \\log_{2}(2) + 4 \\cdot \\log_{2}(3) \\)\n\nAnd since \\( \\log_{2}(2) = 1 \\), this simplifies to \\( 8 + 4 \\cdot \\log_{2}(3) \\).\n\nTherefore, putting it all together, after taking log base 2 of both sides of the first equation, we have:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}(x) \\cdot \\log_{2}(y z) = 8 + 4 \\cdot \\log_{2}(3) \\). Let me write that as Equation (1):\n\n\\( \\log_{2}(x) \\cdot \\log_{2}(y z) = 8 + 4 \\log_{2} 3 \\) ... (1)\n\nSimilarly, let's do the same for the second equation:\n\n\\( y^{\\log_{2}(z x)} = 2^{9} \\cdot 3^{6} \\)\n\nTake log base 2 of both sides:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}\\left( y^{\\log_{2}(z x)} \\right) = \\log_{2}\\left( 2^{9} \\cdot 3^{6} \\right) \\)\n\nLeft side simplifies similarly to above: \\( \\log_{2}(z x) \\cdot \\log_{2}(y) \\).\n\nRight side simplifies as:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}(2^{9}) + \\log_{2}(3^{6}) = 9 + 6 \\cdot \\log_{2}(3) \\)\n\nThus, equation (2):\n\n\\( \\log_{2}(y) \\cdot \\log_{2}(z x) = 9 + 6 \\log_{2} 3 \\) ... (2)\n\nSimilarly, third equation:\n\n\\( z^{\\log_{2}(x y)} = 2^{5} \\cdot 3^{10} \\)\n\nTake log base 2 of both sides:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}\\left( z^{\\log_{2}(x y)} \\right) = \\log_{2}\\left( 2^{5} \\cdot 3^{10} \\right) \\)\n\nLeft side: \\( \\log_{2}(x y) \\cdot \\log_{2}(z) \\)\n\nRight side: \\( \\log_{2}(2^{5}) + \\log_{2}(3^{10}) = 5 + 10 \\cdot \\log_{2}(3) \\)\n\nThus, equation (3):\n\n\\( \\log_{2}(z) \\cdot \\log_{2}(x y) = 5 + 10 \\log_{2} 3 \\) ... (3)\n\nOkay, so now we have three equations, each of the form:\n\n\\( \\log_{2}(A) \\cdot \\log_{2}(B) = C \\), where A, B, and C are expressions in terms of the variables x, y, z and constants.\n\nLet me see if I can write these equations in terms of variables for the logarithms. Let me denote:\n\nLet \\( a = \\log_{2}(x) \\)\n\n\\( b = \\log_{2}(y) \\)\n\n\\( c = \\log_{2}(z) \\)\n\nThen, since \\( \\log_{2}(y z) = \\log_{2}(y) + \\log_{2}(z) = b + c \\)\n\nSimilarly, \\( \\log_{2}(z x) = \\log_{2}(z) + \\log_{2}(x) = c + a \\)\n\nAnd \\( \\log_{2}(x y) = \\log_{2}(x) + \\log_{2}(y) = a + b \\)\n\nTherefore, substituting into the equations:\n\nEquation (1): \\( a \\cdot (b + c) = 8 + 4 \\log_{2} 3 \\)\n\nEquation (2): \\( b \\cdot (c + a) = 9 + 6 \\log_{2} 3 \\)\n\nEquation (3): \\( c \\cdot (a + b) = 5 + 10 \\log_{2} 3 \\)\n\nSo now, we have a system of three equations with three variables: \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\). The goal is to solve for \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\), and then since \\( x = 2^{a} \\), \\( y = 2^{b} \\), \\( z = 2^{c} \\), so the product \\( x y z = 2^{a} \\cdot 2^{b} \\cdot 2^{c} = 2^{a + b + c} \\). Therefore, the minimal value of \\( x y z \\) is \\( 2^{a + b + c} \\), so we need to minimize \\( a + b + c \\), since the exponential function is increasing.\n\nWait, actually, since \\( 2^{a + b + c} \\) is an exponential function with base 2, which is increasing, so indeed the minimal value of \\( x y z \\) corresponds to the minimal value of \\( a + b + c \\). So, our task reduces to solving the system for \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\), and then finding \\( a + b + c \\).\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can work with the variables \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\), and express the equations in terms of them. Let me write the three equations again with substitutions:\n\nEquation 1: \\( a(b + c) = 8 + 4 \\log_{2} 3 \\) ... (1)\n\nEquation 2: \\( b(c + a) = 9 + 6 \\log_{2} 3 \\) ... (2)\n\nEquation 3: \\( c(a + b) = 5 + 10 \\log_{2} 3 \\) ... (3)\n\nHmm. Let me denote \\( S = a + b + c \\), the sum we need to find. Also, perhaps let me denote \\( P = ab + bc + ca \\), the sum of the products of the variables two at a time. Also, perhaps \\( Q = abc \\), the product of all three. But not sure yet. Let me see if I can manipulate these equations.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can write each equation as:\n\nEquation (1): \\( a b + a c = 8 + 4 \\log_{2} 3 \\)\n\nEquation (2): \\( b c + a b = 9 + 6 \\log_{2} 3 \\)\n\nEquation (3): \\( a c + a b = 5 + 10 \\log_{2} 3 \\)\n\nWait, let me check that.\n\nWait, equation (1): \\( a(b + c) = a b + a c = 8 + 4 \\log_{2} 3 \\)\n\nEquation (2): \\( b(c + a) = b c + a b = 9 + 6 \\log_{2} 3 \\)\n\nEquation (3): \\( c(a + b) = c a + c b = 5 + 10 \\log_{2} 3 \\)\n\nSo, indeed, each equation is the sum of two products of the variables, each pair. So, if I denote:\n\nLet me write:\n\nLet me call the left-hand sides:\n\nEquation (1): \\( ab + ac = RHS1 \\), where RHS1 is \\( 8 + 4 \\log_{2} 3 \\)\n\nEquation (2): \\( ab + bc = RHS2 \\), where RHS2 is \\( 9 + 6 \\log_{2} 3 \\)\n\nEquation (3): \\( ac + bc = RHS3 \\), where RHS3 is \\( 5 + 10 \\log_{2} 3 \\)\n\nSo, now, if I have three equations:\n\n1. \\( ab + ac = RHS1 \\)\n2. \\( ab + bc = RHS2 \\)\n3. \\( ac + bc = RHS3 \\)\n\nLet me write them as:\n\n(1): \\( ab + ac = RHS1 \\)\n\n(2): \\( ab + bc = RHS2 \\)\n\n(3): \\( ac + bc = RHS3 \\)\n\nSo, perhaps I can subtract equations from each other to eliminate variables?\n\nFor example, subtract equation (1) from equation (2):\n\n\\( (ab + bc) - (ab + ac) = RHS2 - RHS1 \\)\n\nSimplify left side: \\( bc - ac = RHS2 - RHS1 \\)\n\nFactor left side: \\( c(b - a) = RHS2 - RHS1 \\)\n\nSimilarly, subtract equation (2) from equation (3):\n\n\\( (ac + bc) - (ab + bc) = RHS3 - RHS2 \\)\n\nSimplify left side: \\( ac - ab = RHS3 - RHS2 \\)\n\nFactor left side: \\( a(c - b) = RHS3 - RHS2 \\)\n\nSimilarly, subtract equation (1) from equation (3):\n\nWait, maybe first let me note down the differences.\n\nLet me compute RHS2 - RHS1 and RHS3 - RHS2, etc.\n\nFirst, let's compute RHS1, RHS2, RHS3:\n\nGiven that \\( \\log_{2} 3 \\) is a constant, let me denote \\( k = \\log_{2} 3 \\), so that we can write:\n\nRHS1 = 8 + 4k\n\nRHS2 = 9 + 6k\n\nRHS3 = 5 + 10k\n\nTherefore, RHS2 - RHS1 = (9 + 6k) - (8 + 4k) = 1 + 2k\n\nSimilarly, RHS3 - RHS2 = (5 + 10k) - (9 + 6k) = -4 + 4k\n\nSimilarly, RHS3 - RHS1 = (5 + 10k) - (8 + 4k) = -3 + 6k\n\nBut let's get back to the differences from equations.\n\nFrom equation (2) - equation (1): \\( c(b - a) = 1 + 2k \\) ... (4)\n\nFrom equation (3) - equation (2): \\( a(c - b) = (RHS3 - RHS2) = -4 + 4k \\) ... (5)\n\nHmm, so equation (4): \\( c(b - a) = 1 + 2k \\)\n\nequation (5): \\( a(c - b) = -4 + 4k \\)\n\nHmm, note that \\( c - b = - (b - c) \\), so equation (5) can be written as \\( -a(b - c) = -4 + 4k \\), so \\( a(b - c) = 4 - 4k \\).\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can express variables in terms of each other.\n\nAlternatively, let me see if I can write equations (4) and (5) in terms of variables.\n\nLet me note that equation (4): \\( c(b - a) = 1 + 2k \\)\n\nequation (5): \\( a(c - b) = -4 + 4k \\)\n\nLet me denote \\( d = b - a \\), then equation (4) becomes \\( c d = 1 + 2k \\).\n\nBut then, \\( c - b = c - (a + d) = (c - a) - d \\). Hmm, maybe not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps express \\( b - a = \\frac{1 + 2k}{c} \\) from equation (4), so \\( b = a + \\frac{1 + 2k}{c} \\)\n\nSimilarly, from equation (5): \\( a(c - b) = -4 + 4k \\)\n\nBut \\( c - b = c - [a + \\frac{1 + 2k}{c}] = c - a - \\frac{1 + 2k}{c} \\)\n\nTherefore, substituting into equation (5):\n\n\\( a \\left[ c - a - \\frac{1 + 2k}{c} \\right] = -4 + 4k \\)\n\nHmm, this seems getting a bit messy. Maybe another approach.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider adding all three equations together.\n\nAdding equations (1), (2), (3):\n\nLeft side: (ab + ac) + (ab + bc) + (ac + bc) = 2ab + 2ac + 2bc = 2(ab + ac + bc)\n\nRight side: RHS1 + RHS2 + RHS3 = (8 + 4k) + (9 + 6k) + (5 + 10k) = (8 + 9 + 5) + (4k + 6k + 10k) = 22 + 20k\n\nTherefore, 2(ab + ac + bc) = 22 + 20k\n\nDivide both sides by 2: ab + ac + bc = 11 + 10k ... (6)\n\nSo, we have that \\( ab + ac + bc = 11 + 10k \\), where \\( k = \\log_{2} 3 \\).\n\nNow, if we can express each of the original equations in terms of this sum \\( ab + ac + bc \\), maybe we can find expressions for individual terms.\n\nFor instance, from equation (1): \\( ab + ac = RHS1 = 8 + 4k \\)\n\nBut \\( ab + ac = (ab + ac + bc) - bc = (11 + 10k) - bc = 8 + 4k \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( (11 + 10k) - bc = 8 + 4k \\)\n\nSolving for \\( bc \\):\n\n\\( bc = (11 + 10k) - (8 + 4k) = 3 + 6k \\) ... (7)\n\nSimilarly, from equation (2): \\( ab + bc = RHS2 = 9 + 6k \\)\n\nBut \\( ab + bc = (ab + ac + bc) - ac = (11 + 10k) - ac = 9 + 6k \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( (11 + 10k) - ac = 9 + 6k \\)\n\nSolving for \\( ac \\):\n\n\\( ac = (11 + 10k) - (9 + 6k) = 2 + 4k \\) ... (8)\n\nSimilarly, from equation (3): \\( ac + bc = RHS3 = 5 + 10k \\)\n\nBut \\( ac + bc = (ab + ac + bc) - ab = (11 + 10k) - ab = 5 + 10k \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( (11 + 10k) - ab = 5 + 10k \\)\n\nSolving for \\( ab \\):\n\n\\( ab = (11 + 10k) - (5 + 10k) = 6 \\) ... (9)\n\nSo, now we have:\n\n\\( ab = 6 \\) ... (9)\n\n\\( ac = 2 + 4k \\) ... (8)\n\n\\( bc = 3 + 6k \\) ... (7)\n\nAlso, we had from equation (6): \\( ab + ac + bc = 11 + 10k \\), which we can verify with the above:\n\nab + ac + bc = 6 + (2 + 4k) + (3 + 6k) = 6 + 2 + 3 + 4k + 6k = 11 + 10k. Yes, that checks out.\n\nSo now, we have expressions for \\( ab \\), \\( ac \\), and \\( bc \\). Let me note that \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are variables, so perhaps we can solve for each variable in terms of another.\n\nWe know that \\( ab = 6 \\), so \\( b = 6 / a \\).\n\nSimilarly, \\( ac = 2 + 4k \\), so \\( c = (2 + 4k)/a \\).\n\nAnd \\( bc = 3 + 6k \\), so substituting \\( b = 6/a \\) and \\( c = (2 + 4k)/a \\), we can write:\n\n\\( b c = (6/a) \\cdot ( (2 + 4k)/a ) = 6(2 + 4k)/a^2 \\)\n\nBut we also know that \\( bc = 3 + 6k \\), so:\n\n\\( 6(2 + 4k)/a^2 = 3 + 6k \\)\n\nSolving for \\( a^2 \\):\n\nMultiply both sides by \\( a^2 \\):\n\n\\( 6(2 + 4k) = (3 + 6k) a^2 \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( a^2 = \\frac{6(2 + 4k)}{3 + 6k} \\)\n\nSimplify numerator and denominator:\n\nNumerator: 6*(2 + 4k) = 12 + 24k\n\nDenominator: 3 + 6k = 3(1 + 2k)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( a^2 = \\frac{12 + 24k}{3(1 + 2k)} = \\frac{12(1 + 2k)}{3(1 + 2k)} = \\frac{12}{3} = 4 \\)\n\nWait, hold on! The \\( (1 + 2k) \\) terms cancel out? Let me check that.\n\nYes, numerator: 12 + 24k = 12(1 + 2k)\n\nDenominator: 3 + 6k = 3(1 + 2k)\n\nTherefore, \\( \\frac{12(1 + 2k)}{3(1 + 2k)} = \\frac{12}{3} = 4 \\), as long as \\( 1 + 2k \\neq 0 \\). Since \\( k = \\log_{2} 3 \\), which is positive (since 3 > 1 and log base 2 of 3 is about 1.58496), so \\( 1 + 2k \\) is definitely not zero. Therefore, yes, the \\( (1 + 2k) \\) terms cancel, so \\( a^2 = 4 \\), so \\( a = \\pm 2 \\). But since \\( a = \\log_{2} x \\), and \\( x \\) is a positive real number, \\( a \\) can be any real number, but \\( x = 2^{a} \\) is always positive regardless of \\( a \\). However, since we are looking for the minimal value of \\( x y z = 2^{a + b + c} \\), which is an exponential function, so to minimize \\( x y z \\), we need to minimize \\( a + b + c \\). Since exponentials are increasing functions, the smaller the exponent, the smaller the value.\n\nBut \\( a \\) can be positive or negative? Wait, but if \\( a \\) is negative, then \\( x = 2^{a} \\) would be between 0 and 1, but since \\( x \\) is just a positive real number, that's okay. However, we need to check whether the solutions for \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are real and satisfy the original equations.\n\nBut let's see, since we have \\( a^2 = 4 \\), so \\( a = 2 \\) or \\( a = -2 \\).\n\nLet me first consider \\( a = 2 \\):\n\nCase 1: \\( a = 2 \\)\n\nThen, from \\( ab = 6 \\), so \\( b = 6 / a = 6 / 2 = 3 \\).\n\nFrom \\( ac = 2 + 4k \\), so \\( c = (2 + 4k)/a = (2 + 4k)/2 = 1 + 2k \\).\n\nThen, let's check \\( bc \\):\n\n\\( b c = 3 \\times (1 + 2k) = 3 + 6k \\), which matches equation (7): \\( bc = 3 + 6k \\). Perfect, so that works.\n\nNow, let's compute \\( a + b + c \\):\n\n\\( a + b + c = 2 + 3 + (1 + 2k) = 6 + 1 + 2k = 7 + 2k \\)\n\nWait, hold on, \\( 2 + 3 is 5, plus (1 + 2k) is 6 + 2k \\). Wait, 2 + 3 is 5, plus 1 is 6, plus 2k is 6 + 2k. Yes, that's correct.\n\nCase 2: \\( a = -2 \\)\n\nThen, \\( a = -2 \\), so from \\( ab = 6 \\), we have \\( b = 6 / a = 6 / (-2) = -3 \\).\n\nFrom \\( ac = 2 + 4k \\), so \\( c = (2 + 4k)/a = (2 + 4k)/(-2) = - (2 + 4k)/2 = -1 - 2k \\).\n\nThen, check \\( bc \\):\n\n\\( b c = (-3) \\times (-1 - 2k) = 3(1 + 2k) = 3 + 6k \\), which again matches equation (7): \\( bc = 3 + 6k \\). So that also works.\n\nNow, compute \\( a + b + c \\):\n\n\\( a + b + c = (-2) + (-3) + (-1 - 2k) = -6 - 2k \\)\n\nHmm, so in this case, the sum \\( a + b + c = -6 - 2k \\), which is much smaller (more negative) than the previous case of \\( 6 + 2k \\). Therefore, since we want to minimize \\( x y z = 2^{a + b + c} \\), then if \\( a + b + c \\) can be made more negative, that would give a smaller \\( x y z \\). However, we need to check if the values of \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) in this case are valid, i.e., do they lead to positive real numbers \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\)?\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nIn Case 1: \\( a = 2 \\), \\( b = 3 \\), \\( c = 1 + 2k \\). Since \\( k = \\log_{2} 3 \\approx 1.58496 \\), so \\( c \\approx 1 + 2*(1.58496) \\approx 1 + 3.16992 \\approx 4.16992 \\). So all of \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are positive, so \\( x = 2^{2} = 4 \\), \\( y = 2^{3} = 8 \\), \\( z = 2^{c} \\approx 2^{4.16992} \\approx 2^{4} * 2^{0.16992} \\approx 16 * 1.13 \\approx 18.08 \\). So all positive, so that's okay.\n\nIn Case 2: \\( a = -2 \\), \\( b = -3 \\), \\( c = -1 - 2k \\approx -1 - 3.16992 \\approx -4.16992 \\). Then, \\( x = 2^{-2} = 1/4 \\), \\( y = 2^{-3} = 1/8 \\), \\( z = 2^{c} \\approx 2^{-4.16992} \\approx 1/(2^{4.16992}) \\approx 1/(16 * 2^{0.16992}) \\approx 1/(16 * 1.13) \\approx 1/18.08 \\approx 0.0553 \\). So all of \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) are positive real numbers, so that's okay too.\n\nTherefore, both cases are valid. So we have two possible values for \\( a + b + c \\): \\( 6 + 2k \\) and \\( -6 - 2k \\). Since we need the minimal \\( x y z = 2^{a + b + c} \\), then since \\( 2^{a + b + c} \\) is a decreasing function when the exponent is negative? Wait no, actually, the function \\( 2^t \\) is always increasing for all real \\( t \\). Therefore, to minimize \\( 2^{t} \\), we need to minimize \\( t \\), the exponent. Since \\( t = a + b + c \\), so the smaller the \\( t \\), the smaller the \\( 2^t \\). Therefore, between \\( t_1 = 6 + 2k \\) and \\( t_2 = -6 - 2k \\), since \\( t_2 < t_1 \\), then \\( 2^{t_2} < 2^{t_1} \\), so the minimal value is \\( 2^{-6 - 2k} \\).\n\nWait, but hold on. Wait, \\( k = \\log_{2} 3 \\), so let me compute \\( -6 - 2k \\).\n\nBut \\( k = \\log_{2} 3 \\approx 1.58496 \\), so \\( -6 - 2*(1.58496) \\approx -6 - 3.16992 \\approx -9.16992 \\).\n\nSo \\( 2^{-9.16992} \\approx \\) a very small number, like \\( 2^{-9} = 1/512 \\approx 0.00195 \\), and \\( 2^{-0.16992} \\approx 1 / 2^{0.16992} \\approx 1 / 1.13 \\approx 0.885 \\), so multiplying those together, \\( 0.00195 * 0.885 \\approx 0.00172 \\). So that's a very small number. However, the problem says \"compute the smallest possible value of \\( x y z \\)\", so is that really the case? But let me think again.\n\nWait a second, perhaps there are constraints I haven't considered? Because even though \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) can take on negative values leading to smaller exponents, but in the original equations, we have expressions like \\( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} \\). Since \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) are positive real numbers, but the exponents \\( \\log_{2}(y z) \\), etc., can be any real numbers, so the expressions are defined as long as the bases are positive, which they are, so that's okay. So technically, both solutions are valid, so the minimal \\( x y z \\) would be \\( 2^{-6 - 2k} \\).\n\nBut wait, but the problem says \"positive real numbers\", so that's okay, but maybe there's an error here because when I set up the equations, I assumed that taking logs was valid, but actually, in the original equations, the exponents are \\( \\log_{2}(y z) \\), which could be any real number, but since the base \\( x \\) is positive, it's okay. So perhaps both solutions are valid, so the minimal value is indeed \\( 2^{-6 - 2k} \\). But let me see if this is actually the case, or perhaps I made an error in assuming that both cases are valid?\n\nWait, but in the case of \\( a = -2 \\), \\( b = -3 \\), \\( c = -1 - 2k \\), then let's check if the original equations are satisfied.\n\nLet me verify with the first original equation:\n\n\\( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} = 2^{8} \\cdot 3^{4} \\)\n\nFirst, compute \\( x = 2^{-2} = 1/4 \\), \\( y = 2^{-3} = 1/8 \\), \\( z = 2^{c} = 2^{-1 - 2k} \\).\n\nFirst, compute \\( y z = (1/8) * 2^{-1 - 2k} = 2^{-3} * 2^{-1 - 2k} = 2^{-4 - 2k} \\).\n\nTherefore, \\( \\log_{2}(y z) = \\log_{2}(2^{-4 - 2k}) = -4 - 2k \\).\n\nThen, \\( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} = (2^{-2})^{-4 - 2k} = 2^{(-2)*(-4 - 2k)} = 2^{8 + 4k} \\).\n\nBut the right-hand side is supposed to be \\( 2^{8} \\cdot 3^{4} \\). Wait, but \\( 2^{8 + 4k} \\) is not equal to \\( 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\), unless \\( 4k = 4 \\log_{2} 3 \\), but \\( 4k = 4 \\log_{2} 3 \\), so \\( 2^{8 + 4 \\log_{2} 3} = 2^8 \\cdot 2^{4 \\log_{2} 3} = 2^8 \\cdot (2^{\\log_{2} 3})^4 = 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\). Oh! Wait, that actually does equal \\( 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\). Because \\( 2^{\\log_{2} 3} = 3 \\), so \\( 2^{4 \\log_{2} 3} = (2^{\\log_{2} 3})^4 = 3^4 \\). Therefore, \\( 2^{8 + 4 \\log_{2} 3} = 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\), which matches the right-hand side. So that works!\n\nSimilarly, let's check the second equation:\n\n\\( y^{\\log_{2}(z x)} = 2^{9} \\cdot 3^{6} \\)\n\nCompute \\( z x = 2^{-1 - 2k} * 2^{-2} = 2^{-3 - 2k} \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( \\log_{2}(z x) = -3 - 2k \\)\n\nThen, \\( y^{\\log_{2}(z x)} = (2^{-3})^{-3 - 2k} = 2^{(-3)*(-3 - 2k)} = 2^{9 + 6k} \\)\n\nWhich should equal \\( 2^9 \\cdot 3^6 \\). Let's see:\n\n\\( 2^{9 + 6k} = 2^9 \\cdot 2^{6k} \\). Now, \\( 2^{6k} = (2^{\\log_{2} 3})^{6} = 3^6 \\). Therefore, \\( 2^{9 + 6k} = 2^9 \\cdot 3^6 \\), which matches. Good.\n\nThird equation:\n\n\\( z^{\\log_{2}(x y)} = 2^{5} \\cdot 3^{10} \\)\n\nCompute \\( x y = 2^{-2} \\cdot 2^{-3} = 2^{-5} \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( \\log_{2}(x y) = \\log_{2}(2^{-5}) = -5 \\)\n\nThen, \\( z^{\\log_{2}(x y)} = (2^{-1 - 2k})^{-5} = 2^{(-1 - 2k)(-5)} = 2^{5 + 10k} \\)\n\nWhich should equal \\( 2^5 \\cdot 3^{10} \\). Let's see:\n\n\\( 2^{5 + 10k} = 2^5 \\cdot 2^{10k} \\). And \\( 2^{10k} = (2^{\\log_{2} 3})^{10} = 3^{10} \\). So indeed, \\( 2^{5 + 10k} = 2^5 \\cdot 3^{10} \\). Perfect.\n\nSo, the case with \\( a = -2 \\), \\( b = -3 \\), \\( c = -1 - 2k \\) does satisfy all three equations. Therefore, that is a valid solution, and the other case with \\( a = 2 \\), \\( b = 3 \\), \\( c = 1 + 2k \\) is also valid.\n\nTherefore, since we are to find the smallest possible value of \\( x y z \\), which is \\( 2^{a + b + c} \\), and since \\( a + b + c \\) can be either \\( 6 + 2k \\) or \\( -6 - 2k \\), then since \\( -6 - 2k < 6 + 2k \\), the minimal value is \\( 2^{-6 - 2k} \\).\n\nHowever, the problem says \"compute the smallest possible value of \\( x y z \\)\", so we need to express this in terms of numbers, not in terms of \\( k \\). Since \\( k = \\log_{2} 3 \\), so let me write that:\n\n\\( 2^{-6 - 2 \\log_{2} 3} \\). Let me see if we can simplify this expression.\n\nFirst, note that \\( -6 - 2 \\log_{2} 3 = - \\left( 6 + 2 \\log_{2} 3 \\right) \\), so the exponent is negative of that, so:\n\n\\( 2^{-6 - 2 \\log_{2} 3} = 2^{- (6 + 2 \\log_{2} 3)} = \\left( 2^{6 + 2 \\log_{2} 3} \\right)^{-1} \\)\n\nLet me compute \\( 2^{6 + 2 \\log_{2} 3} \\):\n\nFirst, \\( 2^6 = 64 \\), and \\( 2^{2 \\log_{2} 3} = (2^{\\log_{2} 3})^2 = 3^2 = 9 \\). Therefore,\n\n\\( 2^{6 + 2 \\log_{2} 3} = 2^6 \\cdot 2^{2 \\log_{2} 3} = 64 \\cdot 9 = 576 \\).\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( 2^{-6 - 2 \\log_{2} 3} = \\frac{1}{576} \\).\n\nWait, that's a nice number! So the minimal value is \\( \\frac{1}{576} \\).\n\nWait, let me confirm that step again:\n\n\\( 2^{6 + 2 \\log_{2} 3} = 2^6 \\times 2^{2 \\log_{2} 3} \\)\n\nSince \\( 2^{\\log_{2} 3} = 3 \\), so \\( 2^{2 \\log_{2} 3} = (2^{\\log_{2} 3})^2 = 3^2 = 9 \\), so that part is correct.\n\nTherefore, \\( 2^6 \\times 9 = 64 \\times 9 = 576 \\), so yes, so reciprocal is \\( 1/576 \\).\n\nTherefore, the minimal value is \\( \\frac{1}{576} \\).\n\nWait, but hold on, let me confirm once again with the case \\( a = -2 \\), \\( b = -3 \\), \\( c = -1 - 2 \\log_{2} 3 \\):\n\nThe product \\( x y z = 2^{a + b + c} = 2^{-2 - 3 + (-1 - 2 \\log_{2} 3)} = 2^{-6 - 2 \\log_{2} 3} = \\frac{1}{2^{6 + 2 \\log_{2} 3}} = \\frac{1}{576} \\), as above.\n\nAlternatively, if I compute \\( x y z \\) directly from the values:\n\n\\( x = 2^{-2} = 1/4 \\)\n\n\\( y = 2^{-3} = 1/8 \\)\n\n\\( z = 2^{c} = 2^{-1 - 2 \\log_{2} 3} = 2^{-1} \\times 2^{-2 \\log_{2} 3} = (1/2) \\times (2^{\\log_{2} 3})^{-2} = (1/2) \\times 3^{-2} = (1/2) \\times (1/9) = 1/18 \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( x y z = (1/4) \\times (1/8) \\times (1/18) = \\frac{1}{4 \\times 8 \\times 18} = \\frac{1}{576} \\). Perfect, that's consistent.\n\nTherefore, the minimal value is indeed \\( \\frac{1}{576} \\).\n\nBut wait, hold on a second. The problem says \"compute the smallest possible value of \\( x y z \\)\". But in the case where \\( a = 2 \\), \\( b = 3 \\), \\( c = 1 + 2k \\), then \\( x y z = 2^{a + b + c} = 2^{6 + 2k} \\), which is \\( 2^{6 + 2 \\log_{2} 3} = 2^6 \\times 2^{2 \\log_{2} 3} = 64 \\times 9 = 576 \\), so that's the other solution. So, the two possible values for \\( x y z \\) are \\( 576 \\) and \\( 1/576 \\), so the minimal is indeed \\( 1/576 \\).\n\nHowever, the problem didn't specify any constraints that would exclude the smaller solution? Wait, let me re-read the problem statement:\n\n\"Given that \\( x \\), \\( y \\), and \\( z \\) are positive real numbers such that [equations], compute the smallest possible value of \\( x y z \\).\"\n\nNo, there are no additional constraints. Since both solutions are valid, and \\( 1/576 \\) is smaller than \\( 576 \\), then that is the minimal value.\n\nWait, but in the problem statement, the right-hand sides of the equations are all positive, and since \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) are positive, the left-hand sides are also positive, so that's okay.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be \\( \\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{576}} \\).\n\nWait, but hold on. Let me check if there are more solutions. When I solved for \\( a \\), I got \\( a = \\pm 2 \\). But is that the only possibility?\n\nWait, when I set up the equation \\( a^2 = 4 \\), that came from the substitution where I expressed \\( bc \\) in terms of \\( a \\), and then substituted back. So that gave me \\( a^2 = 4 \\), so \\( a = \\pm 2 \\), so that's the only possibilities for \\( a \\). Therefore, only two solutions: one with \\( a = 2 \\), leading to \\( x y z = 576 \\), and one with \\( a = -2 \\), leading to \\( x y z = 1/576 \\). Therefore, those are the only two solutions? Hmm, but perhaps there are more solutions?\n\nWait, let me see. Let me recap the steps. We had three equations, and we reduced them to expressions for \\( ab \\), \\( ac \\), \\( bc \\), then we had:\n\n\\( ab = 6 \\)\n\n\\( ac = 2 + 4k \\)\n\n\\( bc = 3 + 6k \\)\n\nThen, we solved for \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) in terms of each other, and found that \\( a^2 = 4 \\). Therefore, that seems like the only solutions, given those three equations. Since we had three equations and three variables, so in principle, it's a system that should have a unique solution? But here we have two solutions because when we squared or something? Wait, actually, in this case, when we solved for \\( a \\), we didn't square both sides or anything; we just substituted and found that \\( a^2 = 4 \\), so that gives two possibilities for \\( a \\), each leading to a unique solution for \\( b \\) and \\( c \\), so in total two solutions. Therefore, the system has two solutions, and since both are valid, then the minimal is indeed \\( 1/576 \\).\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's an error in assuming that when we took logarithms, we might have introduced some constraints? Let me see. When we took logarithms of both sides, we assumed that the arguments were positive, which they are, since \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) are positive, so \\( y z \\), \\( z x \\), \\( x y \\) are positive, so the logarithms are defined, so that's okay.\n\nTherefore, the minimal value is indeed \\( 1/576 \\).\n\nWait, but just to be thorough, let me see if there are other possible solutions. Suppose that \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are complex numbers? But the problem states that \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) are positive real numbers, so \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) must be real numbers. Therefore, the only real solutions are the two we found. Therefore, yes, \\( 1/576 \\) is indeed the minimal value.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a different approach to solving the problem that could lead to another solution? Let me think.\n\nAlternatively, suppose we let \\( u = \\log_{2} x \\), \\( v = \\log_{2} y \\), \\( w = \\log_{2} z \\), which is essentially what I did with \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\). Then the equations become:\n\n\\( u(v + w) = 8 + 4k \\)\n\n\\( v(w + u) = 9 + 6k \\)\n\n\\( w(u + v) = 5 + 10k \\)\n\nWhich is exactly the same as equations (1), (2), (3) with \\( a = u \\), \\( b = v \\), \\( c = w \\). So that's the same system.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider the product \\( (x y z)^{\\log_{2}(x y z)} \\), but I don't see that immediately helpful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps taking the three original equations and multiplying them together?\n\nLet me try that:\n\nOriginal equations:\n\n1. \\( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} = 2^{8} \\cdot 3^{4} \\)\n\n2. \\( y^{\\log_{2}(z x)} = 2^{9} \\cdot 3^{6} \\)\n\n3. \\( z^{\\log_{2}(x y)} = 2^{5} \\cdot 3^{10} \\)\n\nMultiply all three left-hand sides and right-hand sides:\n\nLeft side: \\( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} \\cdot y^{\\log_{2}(z x)} \\cdot z^{\\log_{2}(x y)} \\)\n\nRight side: \\( (2^{8} \\cdot 3^{4}) \\cdot (2^{9} \\cdot 3^{6}) \\cdot (2^{5} \\cdot 3^{10}) = 2^{8+9+5} \\cdot 3^{4+6+10} = 2^{22} \\cdot 3^{20} \\)\n\nNow, let me see if I can simplify the left side.\n\nFirst, note that each term in the product is of the form \\( \\text{variable}^{\\log_{2}(\\text{product of the other two variables})} \\).\n\nLet me denote \\( A = \\log_{2}(y z) \\), \\( B = \\log_{2}(z x) \\), \\( C = \\log_{2}(x y) \\). Then the left side is \\( x^A y^B z^C \\).\n\nBut perhaps we can express \\( A \\), \\( B \\), \\( C \\) in terms of \\( u \\), \\( v \\), \\( w \\), but maybe that's not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, let me see if I can write the exponents in terms of sums.\n\nNote that \\( \\log_{2}(y z) = \\log_{2} y + \\log_{2} z = v + w \\), similarly \\( \\log_{2}(z x) = w + u \\), \\( \\log_{2}(x y) = u + v \\).\n\nTherefore, the left-hand side becomes:\n\n\\( x^{v + w} \\cdot y^{w + u} \\cdot z^{u + v} \\)\n\nBut \\( x = 2^{u} \\), \\( y = 2^{v} \\), \\( z = 2^{w} \\), so substituting:\n\n\\( (2^{u})^{v + w} \\cdot (2^{v})^{w + u} \\cdot (2^{w})^{u + v} \\)\n\nSimplify exponents:\n\nFirst term exponent: \\( u(v + w) \\)\n\nSecond term exponent: \\( v(w + u) \\)\n\nThird term exponent: \\( w(u + v) \\)\n\nTherefore, the entire left-hand side is:\n\n\\( 2^{u(v + w) + v(w + u) + w(u + v)} \\)\n\nLet me compute the exponent:\n\n\\( u(v + w) + v(w + u) + w(u + v) \\)\n\nExpand each term:\n\nFirst term: \\( uv + uw \\)\n\nSecond term: \\( vw + vu \\)\n\nThird term: \\( wu + wv \\)\n\nSo adding them together:\n\n\\( uv + uw + vw + vu + wu + wv \\)\n\nNotice that \\( uv = vu \\), \\( uw = wu \\), \\( vw = wv \\), so this is equal to:\n\n\\( 2uv + 2uw + 2vw = 2(uv + uw + vw) \\)\n\nTherefore, the left-hand side simplifies to \\( 2^{2(uv + uw + vw)} \\).\n\nTherefore, equating left and right sides:\n\n\\( 2^{2(uv + uw + vw)} = 2^{22} \\cdot 3^{20} \\)\n\nHmm, but the right-hand side has a factor of \\( 3^{20} \\), while the left-hand side is a power of 2. This suggests that unless \\( 3^{20} \\) is a power of 2, which it isn't, there might be an inconsistency? Wait, but this can't be, because we already found solutions that satisfy the original equations. Therefore, perhaps I made a mistake in the manipulation.\n\nWait, no, actually, the left-hand side is \\( 2^{2(uv + uw + vw)} \\), and the right-hand side is \\( 2^{22} \\cdot 3^{20} \\). Since the left-hand side is a power of 2 and the right-hand side has factors of 3, this seems contradictory. But this must mean that my approach here is flawed?\n\nWait, but we already have solutions that satisfy the original equations, so this suggests an error in my calculation.\n\nWait, let me check again the multiplication step.\n\nOriginal left-hand sides multiplied:\n\n\\( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} \\times y^{\\log_{2}(z x)} \\times z^{\\log_{2}(x y)} \\)\n\nExpressed in terms of exponents:\n\nYes, as above, that becomes \\( 2^{2(uv + uw + vw)} \\).\n\nBut the right-hand side when multiplied is \\( 2^{22} \\cdot 3^{20} \\), so unless \\( 3^{20} \\) is somehow incorporated into the exponent of 2, which it isn't, this seems like a problem.\n\nBut this must mean that my mistake is in assuming that the left-hand side can be expressed purely as a power of 2? But how is that possible?\n\nWait a second, actually, the left-hand side is \\( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} \\times y^{\\log_{2}(z x)} \\times z^{\\log_{2}(x y)} \\), which, when we expressed \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) as powers of 2, we ended up with only powers of 2, but in reality, the original equations have right-hand sides with factors of 3, so when we multiply them, we get factors of 3, but the left-hand side, when expressed in terms of \\( u \\), \\( v \\), \\( w \\), only has factors of 2? That can't be. Therefore, my mistake must be in the substitution step.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) are positive real numbers, but they don't have to be powers of 2. I assumed that \\( x = 2^u \\), \\( y = 2^v \\), \\( z = 2^w \\), but actually, \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) can have other prime factors besides 2. However, in my substitution, I set \\( a = \\log_{2} x \\), which would require \\( x = 2^a \\), but if \\( x \\) has other prime factors, then \\( \\log_{2} x \\) would not be defined in real numbers unless \\( x \\) is a power of 2. Wait a second, this is a critical mistake!\n\nOh no! I see now. My substitution \\( a = \\log_{2} x \\), \\( b = \\log_{2} y \\), \\( c = \\log_{2} z \\) is only valid if \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) are powers of 2. But in the problem, \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) are just positive real numbers, which may not be powers of 2. Therefore, my substitution is invalid because \\( \\log_{2} x \\), etc., are only real numbers if \\( x \\) is a positive real number, which they are, but if \\( x \\) is not a power of 2, then \\( \\log_{2} x \\) is just some real number, but expressing \\( x \\) as \\( 2^a \\) would still be okay, because any positive real number can be expressed as \\( 2^a \\) for some real number \\( a \\). So that substitution is actually valid.\n\nWait, but then why does the multiplication lead to a contradiction? Because when I multiplied the left-hand sides, I converted everything to base 2 exponents, but the right-hand sides have factors of 3, so unless the left-hand side also has factors of 3, which it shouldn't because I expressed everything in terms of powers of 2? Wait, but that can't be.\n\nWait, let me think again. If \\( x = 2^a \\), \\( y = 2^b \\), \\( z = 2^c \\), then \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) are all powers of 2, so their product \\( x y z \\) is also a power of 2, but in the problem, the right-hand sides of the original equations have factors of 3, which are not powers of 2. Therefore, this suggests that my substitution \\( x = 2^a \\), \\( y = 2^b \\), \\( z = 2^c \\) is invalid because if \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) are powers of 2, then the left-hand sides of the original equations would only have powers of 2, but the right-hand sides have factors of 3. Therefore, my substitution is wrong?\n\nWait, this is a contradiction. How come earlier, when I plugged in the solutions with \\( a = -2 \\), \\( b = -3 \\), \\( c = -1 - 2k \\), which corresponded to \\( x = 2^{-2} = 1/4 \\), \\( y = 2^{-3} = 1/8 \\), \\( z = 2^{-1 - 2k} \\), which is \\( 2^{-1} \\times 2^{-2k} = (1/2) \\times (2^{\\log_{2} 3})^{-2} = (1/2) \\times 3^{-2} = 1/18 \\), then plugging into the first equation:\n\n\\( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} = (1/4)^{\\log_{2}( (1/8)(1/18) )} \\)\n\nWait, compute \\( y z = (1/8)(1/18) = 1/(144) \\). Then \\( \\log_{2}(1/144) = \\log_{2}(1) - \\log_{2}(144) = 0 - \\log_{2}(144) \\). Since \\( 144 = 12^2 = (2^2 \\times 3)^2 = 2^4 \\times 3^2 \\), so \\( \\log_{2}(144) = \\log_{2}(2^4 \\times 3^2) = 4 + 2 \\log_{2} 3 \\). Therefore, \\( \\log_{2}(y z) = - (4 + 2 \\log_{2} 3) \\).\n\nTherefore, \\( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} = (1/4)^{ - (4 + 2 \\log_{2} 3) } = (4)^{4 + 2 \\log_{2} 3} = (2^2)^{4 + 2 \\log_{2} 3} = 2^{8 + 4 \\log_{2} 3} \\), which is equal to \\( 2^8 \\times 2^{4 \\log_{2} 3} = 2^8 \\times (2^{\\log_{2} 3})^4 = 2^8 \\times 3^4 \\), which matches the right-hand side. So even though \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) are powers of 2, their product \\( y z \\) is not a power of 2, so \\( \\log_{2}(y z) \\) is a real number, and raising a power of 2 to that power can give a number with factors of 3? Wait, but how?\n\nWait, actually, in this case, \\( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} \\), even though \\( x \\) is a power of 2, and \\( \\log_{2}(y z) \\) is some real number, the result can still have factors of 3 because the exponent can be chosen such that when you raise a power of 2 to that exponent, it can produce a multiple of 3. For example, in this case, \\( 2^{4 \\log_{2} 3} = (2^{\\log_{2} 3})^4 = 3^4 \\), so that's how the 3 comes in. Therefore, even though \\( x \\), \\( y \\), \\( z \\) are powers of 2, the exponents can still result in terms with factors of 3 because the exponent itself involves \\( \\log_{2} 3 \\). Therefore, my substitution is valid, and the contradiction I thought I saw when multiplying the equations is not actually a contradiction because the left-hand side, when expressed in terms of powers of 2, actually does have factors of 3 due to the exponents involving \\( \\log_{2} 3 \\).\n\nTherefore, going back to the multiplication of the three equations:\n\nLeft side simplifies to \\( 2^{2(uv + uw + vw)} \\), but the right-hand side is \\( 2^{22} \\cdot 3^{20} \\). Therefore, equating these:\n\n\\( 2^{2(uv + uw + vw)} = 2^{22} \\cdot 3^{20} \\)\n\nBut this can only hold if the exponents of 2 and 3 on both sides match. However, the left-hand side has only a power of 2, while the right-hand side has both 2 and 3. This seems impossible unless \\( 3^{20} = 1 \\), which it isn't, so there must be an error in my calculation.\n\nWait, but in reality, when I expressed the left-hand side in terms of \\( u \\), \\( v \\), \\( w \\), I might have made a mistake. Let me re-express the left-hand side step by step.\n\nOriginal left-hand side after substitution:\n\n\\( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} \\times y^{\\log_{2}(z x)} \\times z^{\\log_{2}(x y)} \\)\n\nExpressed with \\( x = 2^u \\), \\( y = 2^v \\), \\( z = 2^w \\):\n\nFirst term: \\( (2^u)^{\\log_{2}(2^v \\cdot 2^w)} = (2^u)^{\\log_{2}(2^{v + w})} = (2^u)^{v + w} = 2^{u(v + w)} \\)\n\nSecond term: \\( (2^v)^{\\log_{2}(2^w \\cdot 2^u)} = (2^v)^{\\log_{2}(2^{w + u})} = (2^v)^{w + u} = 2^{v(w + u)} \\)\n\nThird term: \\( (2^w)^{\\log_{2}(2^u \\cdot 2^v)} = (2^w)^{\\log_{2}(2^{u + v})} = (2^w)^{u + v} = 2^{w(u + v)} \\)\n\nTherefore, multiplying all three terms:\n\n\\( 2^{u(v + w)} \\times 2^{v(w + u)} \\times 2^{w(u + v)} = 2^{u(v + w) + v(w + u) + w(u + v)} \\)\n\nWhich is the same as before: exponent is \\( u(v + w) + v(w + u) + w(u + v) \\), which simplifies to \\( 2(uv + uw + vw) \\).\n\nTherefore, left-hand side is \\( 2^{2(uv + uw + vw)} \\).\n\nRight-hand side is \\( 2^{22} \\cdot 3^{20} \\).\n\nBut this is a problem because the left-hand side is a power of 2, and the right-hand side has a factor of 3^{20}, which is not a power of 2. Therefore, unless 3^{20} is equal to 1, which it isn't, this equality cannot hold. But this contradicts our earlier solution where we found that the equations are satisfied. Therefore, this suggests that there is an error in the process.\n\nWait a second, but in our solutions, when we plugged in the values, it worked out because the exponent on the left-hand side ended up having factors of 3 due to the exponents involving \\( \\log_{2} 3 \\). Wait, but in the substitution above, I expressed everything in terms of \\( u \\), \\( v \\), \\( w \\), which are real numbers, but when I computed the left-hand side as \\( 2^{2(uv + uw + vw)} \\), that seems to ignore the possibility that the exponents might involve logarithms that could introduce factors of 3?\n\nWait, no, actually, in the substitution \\( u = \\log_{2} x \\), \\( v = \\log_{2} y \\), \\( w = \\log_{2} z \\), so \\( x = 2^u \\), \\( y = 2^v \\), \\( z = 2^w \\), so all variables are expressed as powers of 2, so when you raise them to any exponent, they remain powers of 2. Therefore, the left-hand side should only have factors of 2, but the right-hand side has factors of 3, so how come in our specific solution it worked?\n\nWait, let me take the specific solution where \\( x = 1/4 \\), \\( y = 1/8 \\), \\( z = 1/18 \\).\n\nThen, compute the left-hand side of the product of the three original equations:\n\nFirst equation LHS: \\( x^{\\log_{2}(y z)} = (1/4)^{\\log_{2}( (1/8)(1/18) )} = (1/4)^{\\log_{2}(1/144)} = (1/4)^{-\\log_{2}(144)} = 4^{\\log_{2}(144)} \\)\n\nSimilarly, second equation LHS: \\( y^{\\log_{2}(z x)} = (1/8)^{\\log_{2}( (1/18)(1/4) )} = (1/8)^{\\log_{2}(1/72)} = (1/8)^{-\\log_{2}(72)} = 8^{\\log_{2}(72)} \\)\n\nThird equation LHS: \\( z^{\\log_{2}(x y)} = (1/18)^{\\log_{2}( (1/4)(1/8) )} = (1/18)^{\\log_{2}(1/32)} = (1/18)^{-\\log_{2}(32)} = 18^{\\log_{2}(32)} \\)\n\nTherefore, the product of the three LHS is:\n\n\\( 4^{\\log_{2}(144)} \\times 8^{\\log_{2}(72)} \\times 18^{\\log_{2}(32)} \\)\n\nLet me compute each term:\n\nFirst term: \\( 4^{\\log_{2}(144)} = (2^2)^{\\log_{2}(144)} = 2^{2 \\log_{2}(144)} = 2^{\\log_{2}(144^2)} = 144^2 \\)\n\nSecond term: \\( 8^{\\log_{2}(72)} = (2^3)^{\\log_{2}(72)} = 2^{3 \\log_{2}(72)} = 2^{\\log_{2}(72^3)} = 72^3 \\)\n\nThird term: \\( 18^{\\log_{2}(32)} = (2 \\times 9)^{\\log_{2}(32)} = 2^{\\log_{2}(32)} \\times 9^{\\log_{2}(32)} = 32 \\times (3^2)^{\