5 < 0 when j < -0.5. Since j is an integer, so j ≤ -1. Therefore, for all j ≤ -1, j + 0.5 ≤ -0.5, so negative, so 2025 divided by a negative number is negative, so floor of a negative number is a negative integer.\n\nTherefore, for j from -1000 to -1, the terms are negative integers, and for j from 0 to 1000, the terms are non-negative integers. So the entire sum is the sum over negative terms (from j = -1000 to -1) plus the term at j = 0 plus the sum over non-negative terms (from j = 1 to 1000).\n\nSo, let me write that as:\n\nSum_{j = -1000}^{1000} floor(2025/(j + 0.5)) = [Sum_{j = -1000}^{-1} floor(2025/(j + 0.5))] + floor(2025/(0 + 0.5)) + [Sum_{j = 1}^{1000} floor(2025/(j + 0.5))]\n\nNow, let me compute each part separately.\n\nFirst, the middle term at j = 0:\n\nfloor(2025 / (0 + 0.5)) = floor(2025 / 0.5) = floor(4050) = 4050.\n\nOkay, that's straightforward.\n\nNow, let's consider the sum from j = -1000 to -1. Let me make a substitution here. Let me let k = -j - 1. Wait, let me see. Let me let k = -j. Since j ranges from -1000 to -1, then k = -j ranges from 1 to 1000. Let me see if that helps.\n\nWait, so if j = -k, where k is from 1 to 1000, then substituting j = -k:\n\nfloor(2025 / (j + 0.5)) = floor(2025 / (-k + 0.5)) = floor(2025 / ( - (k - 0.5) )) = floor( -2025 / (k - 0.5) )\n\nSimilarly, as before, floor(-x) = -ceil(x). Therefore, this is equal to -ceil(2025 / (k - 0.5))\n\nTherefore, the sum over j from -1000 to -1 of floor(2025/(j + 0.5)) is equal to the sum over k = 1 to 1000 of [ -ceil(2025 / (k - 0.5)) ]\n\nTherefore, that sum is equal to - [ Sum_{k=1}^{1000} ceil(2025 / (k - 0.5)) ]\n\nSo now, the entire original sum becomes:\n\n- [ Sum_{k=1}^{1000} ceil(2025 / (k - 0.5)) ] + 4050 + [ Sum_{j=1}^{1000} floor(2025 / (j + 0.5)) ]\n\nHmm, so now if I can relate the sum over k of ceil(2025/(k - 0.5)) to the sum over j of floor(2025/(j + 0.5)), perhaps?\n\nLet me note that in the second sum, the index is j from 1 to 1000, and the term is floor(2025/(j + 0.5)), whereas in the first sum, the index is k from 1 to 1000, and the term is ceil(2025/(k - 0.5)).\n\nLet me make a substitution in the first sum. Let me let m = k - 1, so that k = m + 1, then when k = 1, m = 0, and when k = 1000, m = 999. So the sum becomes Sum_{m=0}^{999} ceil(2025/( (m + 1) - 0.5 )) = Sum_{m=0}^{999} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5))\n\nTherefore, the first sum (the negative one) is - [ Sum_{m=0}^{999} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5)) ]\n\nTherefore, the entire original sum is:\n\n- [ Sum_{m=0}^{999} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5)) ] + 4050 + [ Sum_{j=1}^{1000} floor(2025/(j + 0.5)) ]\n\nHmm, so now, if I can combine the two sums involving m and j. Let me note that the Sum_{j=1}^{1000} floor(2025/(j + 0.5)) is the same as Sum_{j=1}^{1000} floor(2025/(j + 0.5)), and the other sum is Sum_{m=0}^{999} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5)). So, perhaps I can adjust indices to see if these sums can be related?\n\nLet me write the first sum as Sum_{m=0}^{999} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5)), and the second sum as Sum_{j=1}^{1000} floor(2025/(j + 0.5)).\n\nLet me note that the terms in the first sum have denominators (m + 0.5) where m ranges from 0 to 999, so denominators from 0.5 to 999.5 in increments of 1. The terms in the second sum have denominators (j + 0.5) where j ranges from 1 to 1000, so denominators from 1.5 to 1000.5, also in increments of 1.\n\nTherefore, the denominators in the first sum are 0.5, 1.5, 2.5, ..., 999.5, and the denominators in the second sum are 1.5, 2.5, ..., 1000.5. So overlapping denominators from 1.5 to 999.5, but the first sum has an extra term at 0.5, and the second sum has an extra term at 1000.5.\n\nTherefore, let me write:\n\nSum_{m=0}^{999} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5)) = ceil(2025 / 0.5) + Sum_{m=1}^{999} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5))\n\nSimilarly, Sum_{j=1}^{1000} floor(2025/(j + 0.5)) = Sum_{j=1}^{999} floor(2025/(j + 0.5)) + floor(2025 / 1000.5)\n\nTherefore, substituting back into the original expression:\n\nOriginal Sum = - [ ceil(2025 / 0.5) + Sum_{m=1}^{999} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5)) ] + 4050 + [ Sum_{j=1}^{999} floor(2025/(j + 0.5)) + floor(2025 / 1000.5) ]\n\nLet me rearrange terms:\n\nOriginal Sum = - ceil(2025 / 0.5) - Sum_{m=1}^{999} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5)) + 4050 + Sum_{j=1}^{999} floor(2025/(j + 0.5)) + floor(2025 / 1000.5)\n\nNow, note that the terms Sum_{m=1}^{999} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5)) and Sum_{j=1}^{999} floor(2025/(j + 0.5)) can be combined if we consider that m and j are just dummy indices. Let me write them both with the same index variable, say n:\n\nLet me denote:\n\nSum_{m=1}^{999} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5)) = Sum_{n=1}^{999} ceil(2025/(n + 0.5))\n\nSum_{j=1}^{999} floor(2025/(j + 0.5)) = Sum_{n=1}^{999} floor(2025/(n + 0.5))\n\nTherefore, the expression becomes:\n\nOriginal Sum = - ceil(2025 / 0.5) - Sum_{n=1}^{999} ceil(2025/(n + 0.5)) + 4050 + Sum_{n=1}^{999} floor(2025/(n + 0.5)) + floor(2025 / 1000.5)\n\nNow, let's group the terms involving Sum_{n=1}^{999} [ -ceil(2025/(n + 0.5)) + floor(2025/(n + 0.5)) ]:\n\nWhich is equal to Sum_{n=1}^{999} [ floor(2025/(n + 0.5)) - ceil(2025/(n + 0.5)) ] + [ - ceil(2025 / 0.5) + 4050 + floor(2025 / 1000.5) ]\n\nHmm, so let me analyze the term inside the summation: floor(x) - ceil(x) for x = 2025/(n + 0.5). But for any real number x, floor(x) - ceil(x) = -1 if x is not an integer, and 0 if x is an integer. Because if x is an integer, then floor(x) = ceil(x) = x, so their difference is 0. If x is not an integer, then ceil(x) = floor(x) + 1, so floor(x) - ceil(x) = -1.\n\nTherefore, for each term in the summation over n from 1 to 999, we have floor(x_n) - ceil(x_n) = -1 if x_n is not integer, and 0 if x_n is integer, where x_n = 2025/(n + 0.5).\n\nTherefore, the sum Sum_{n=1}^{999} [ floor(x_n) - ceil(x_n) ] = Sum_{n=1}^{999} [ -1 if x_n is not integer, 0 if x_n is integer ]\n\nTherefore, this sum equals - (number of n in 1 to 999 for which x_n is not an integer )\n\nTherefore, putting that back into the expression:\n\nOriginal Sum = [ - (number of n in 1 to 999 where 2025/(n + 0.5) is not integer ) ] + [ - ceil(2025 / 0.5) + 4050 + floor(2025 / 1000.5) ]\n\nSo, now, let me compute each of the remaining terms:\n\nFirst, compute ceil(2025 / 0.5). Since 2025 divided by 0.5 is 4050, so ceil(4050) = 4050.\n\nSecond, compute floor(2025 / 1000.5). Let me compute 2025 divided by 1000.5.\n\nFirst, note that 1000.5 is equal to 2001/2. So, 2025 divided by (2001/2) is equal to 2025 * (2 / 2001) = (4050)/2001 ≈ let me compute that.\n\nCompute 4050 divided by 2001.\n\nWell, 2001 * 2 = 4002, so 4050 - 4002 = 48. Therefore, 4050 / 2001 = 2 + 48/2001. Simplify 48/2001: divide numerator and denominator by 3: 16/667. So, 4050 / 2001 = 2 + 16/667 ≈ 2.0239... So the floor of that is 2.\n\nTherefore, floor(2025 / 1000.5) = floor(4050/2001) = 2.\n\nTherefore, the bracketed term becomes:\n\n- ceil(2025 / 0.5) + 4050 + floor(2025 / 1000.5) = -4050 + 4050 + 2 = 2.\n\nSo, now, going back to the entire expression:\n\nOriginal Sum = [ - (number of n from 1 to 999 where 2025/(n + 0.5) is not integer ) ] + 2.\n\nTherefore, Original Sum = 2 - (number of n from 1 to 999 where 2025/(n + 0.5) is not an integer )\n\nHmm, so now, the problem reduces to computing the number of integers n between 1 and 999 inclusive such that 2025 divided by (n + 0.5) is not an integer. Then subtract that count from 2, and that gives the original sum.\n\nAlternatively, since we have Original Sum = 2 - N, where N is the number of integers n in [1, 999] with 2025/(n + 0.5) not integer. Alternatively, since the total number of terms in the summation over n from 1 to 999 is 999, then the number of terms where 2025/(n + 0.5) is an integer is equal to (999 - N), so then Original Sum = 2 - (999 - (number of integers n with 2025/(n + 0.5) integer )) ?\n\nWait, let me think again. Let me denote:\n\nLet me let A = number of integers n in [1, 999] such that 2025/(n + 0.5) is an integer.\n\nThen, the number of n in [1, 999] with 2025/(n + 0.5) not an integer is equal to 999 - A.\n\nTherefore, the Original Sum = 2 - (999 - A) = 2 - 999 + A = A - 997.\n\nTherefore, Original Sum = A - 997, where A is the number of integers n between 1 and 999 such that (n + 0.5) divides 2025 exactly, i.e., 2025 divided by (n + 0.5) is an integer.\n\nBut (n + 0.5) is a half-integer, since n is integer. So, (n + 0.5) = k/2, where k is an odd integer (since n is integer, so n + 0.5 is a half-integer, so k = 2n + 1, which is odd). Therefore, 2025 divided by (k/2) is equal to 2025 * 2 / k = 4050 / k. Therefore, the condition that 2025/(n + 0.5) is integer is equivalent to 4050/k being an integer, where k = 2n + 1, and k must be an odd integer between 2*1 + 1 = 3 and 2*999 + 1 = 1999.\n\nWait, more precisely, since n ranges from 1 to 999, then k = 2n + 1 ranges from 3 to 1999, stepping by 2 each time (since n increases by 1, so k increases by 2). Therefore, k is an odd integer from 3 to 1999 inclusive, and the condition is that k divides 4050. Therefore, the number A is equal to the number of odd integers k in [3, 1999] that divide 4050.\n\nTherefore, A = number of odd divisors of 4050 that are between 3 and 1999 inclusive.\n\nTherefore, to compute A, we need to find all the odd divisors of 4050, and count how many of them lie in the interval [3, 1999].\n\nFirst, let me factorize 4050 to find its divisors.\n\nFactorizing 4050:\n\n4050 ÷ 2 = 2025\n\n2025 ÷ 3 = 675\n\n675 ÷ 3 = 225\n\n225 ÷ 3 = 75\n\n75 ÷ 3 = 25\n\n25 ÷ 5 = 5\n\n5 ÷ 5 = 1\n\nSo prime factorization of 4050 is:\n\n4050 = 2 × 3^4 × 5^2\n\nTherefore, the divisors of 4050 are of the form 2^a × 3^b × 5^c, where a = 0 or 1, b = 0,1,2,3,4, c = 0,1,2.\n\nBut since we are interested in the odd divisors, we set a = 0. Therefore, the odd divisors are of the form 3^b × 5^c, with b ∈ {0,1,2,3,4} and c ∈ {0,1,2}.\n\nTherefore, the number of odd divisors is (5)(3) = 15. Let me list them all to be precise:\n\nFor b = 0:\n\nc = 0: 3^0 × 5^0 = 1\n\nc = 1: 3^0 × 5^1 = 5\n\nc = 2: 3^0 × 5^2 = 25\n\nFor b = 1:\n\nc = 0: 3^1 × 5^0 = 3\n\nc = 1: 3^1 × 5^1 = 15\n\nc = 2: 3^1 × 5^2 = 75\n\nFor b = 2:\n\nc = 0: 3^2 × 5^0 = 9\n\nc = 1: 3^2 × 5^1 = 45\n\nc = 2: 3^2 × 5^2 = 225\n\nFor b = 3:\n\nc = 0: 3^3 × 5^0 = 27\n\nc = 1: 3^3 × 5^1 = 135\n\nc = 2: 3^3 × 5^2 = 675\n\nFor b = 4:\n\nc = 0: 3^4 × 5^0 = 81\n\nc = 1: 3^4 × 5^1 = 405\n\nc = 2: 3^4 × 5^2 = 2025\n\nTherefore, the list of odd divisors of 4050 is:\n\n1, 3, 5, 9, 15, 25, 27, 45, 75, 81, 135, 225, 405, 675, 2025.\n\nSo there are 15 odd divisors. Now, we need to find how many of these divisors are in the interval [3, 1999].\n\nFirst, note that 1 is less than 3, so we exclude it. 2025 is greater than 1999, so we exclude it as well. Let me check the other divisors:\n\nList of odd divisors except 1 and 2025:\n\n3, 5, 9, 15, 25, 27, 45, 75, 81, 135, 225, 405, 675, 2025. Wait, no: 2025 is the last one, so excluding 1 and 2025, the remaining divisors are:\n\n3, 5, 9, 15, 25, 27, 45, 75, 81, 135, 225, 405, 675.\n\nWait, let me recount:\n\nOriginal list without 1 and 2025:\n\n3, 5, 9, 15, 25, 27, 45, 75, 81, 135, 225, 405, 675. That's 13 divisors.\n\nWait, let's count again:\n\nStarting from the list above:\n\nOriginal list of 15:\n\n1,\n\n3, 5, 9, 15, 25, 27, 45, 75, 81, 135, 225, 405, 675,\n\n2025.\n\nSo removing 1 and 2025, we have 13 divisors left. Each of these 13 divisors is between 3 and 1999 inclusive? Let me check the maximum: the largest one is 675. Wait, 675 is 675, which is less than 1999. Wait, 2025 is the only one above 1999, right? 2025 is over 1999, so all the others are less than or equal to 675? Wait, 675 is the second to last before 2025.\n\nWait, hold on, let's see:\n\nWait in the list:\n\nAfter 675 comes 2025. So 675 is the second largest odd divisor. So all the other divisors except 1 and 2025 are between 3 and 675. Since 675 is 675 < 1999, so all of them are within [3, 1999]. Therefore, all 13 divisors (excluding 1 and 2025) are within the desired interval [3, 1999].\n\nTherefore, A = 13.\n\nWait, hold on, let me confirm each of the divisors:\n\n3: yes, between 3 and 1999.\n\n5: yes.\n\n9: yes.\n\n15: yes.\n\n25: yes.\n\n27: yes.\n\n45: yes.\n\n75: yes.\n\n81: yes.\n\n135: yes.\n\n225: yes.\n\n405: yes.\n\n675: yes.\n\nAll of these are between 3 and 1999, inclusive. So A = 13.\n\nWait, but hold on, let me check 675: 675 is less than 1999, yes. 675 is 675, so that's fine.\n\nWait, but hang on, is 405 less than 1999? Yes, 405 is way less. 675 is 675. So all 13 divisors are in [3, 1999].\n\nTherefore, A = 13.\n\nTherefore, going back to the original expression:\n\nOriginal Sum = A - 997 = 13 - 997 = -984.\n\nWait, that can't be, because the original sum has positive and negative terms. Wait, but let me check my steps again because getting a negative number here might be an issue.\n\nWait, let me retrace:\n\nOriginal Sum = 2 - (number of n from 1 to 999 where 2025/(n + 0.5) is not integer )\n\nBut then I re-expressed that as Original Sum = 2 - (999 - A) = A - 997, where A is the number of n where it *is* integer.\n\nWait, so if A = 13, then Original Sum = 13 - 997 = -984. But let me see, in the small cases earlier, like N=2, the sum was 1, which was positive. So getting a negative number here seems odd, unless the majority of terms are negative.\n\nWait, perhaps my mistake is in the transformation steps. Let me double-check.\n\nStarting from the beginning:\n\nOriginal Sum = Sum_{j=-1000}^{1000} floor(2025/(j + 0.5))\n\nI split it into three parts: the negative j terms, the middle term at j=0, and the positive j terms.\n\nThen, for the negative j terms (j from -1000 to -1), I made substitution k = -j, leading to Sum_{k=1}^{1000} floor(2025/(-k + 0.5)) = Sum_{k=1}^{1000} floor( -2025/(k - 0.5) ) = - Sum_{k=1}^{1000} ceil(2025/(k - 0.5))\n\nThen, I reindexed the sum over k from 1 to 1000 as m from 0 to 999 by letting m = k -1, so that became - Sum_{m=0}^{999} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5))\n\nThen, the total sum became:\n\n- Sum_{m=0}^{999} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5)) + 4050 + Sum_{j=1}^{1000} floor(2025/(j + 0.5))\n\nThen, I split the Sum_{m=0}^{999} into m=0 term and m=1 to 999:\n\nSo, - [ ceil(2025/0.5) + Sum_{m=1}^{999} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5)) ] + 4050 + Sum_{j=1}^{1000} floor(2025/(j + 0.5))\n\nThen, the Sum_{j=1}^{1000} floor(...) can be split into j=1 to 999 and j=1000:\n\nSo, that became:\n\n- ceil(2025/0.5) - Sum_{m=1}^{999} ceil(...) + 4050 + Sum_{m=1}^{999} floor(...) + floor(2025/(1000.5))\n\nThen, grouped the Sum_{m=1}^{999} [ -ceil(...) + floor(...) ] which is Sum_{m=1}^{999} [ floor(x) - ceil(x) ] where x = 2025/(m + 0.5)\n\nWhich, as I said, for each term, floor(x) - ceil(x) = -1 if x is not integer, 0 if x is integer. Therefore, the sum over m=1 to 999 is equal to - (number of m in 1..999 where x is not integer). So that term becomes - (999 - A), where A is the number of m in 1..999 where x is integer.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, actually, in the expression:\n\nSum_{m=1}^{999} [ floor(x) - ceil(x) ] = Sum_{m=1}^{999} [ -1 if x not integer, 0 if x integer ]\n\nTherefore, the sum is equal to - (number of m where x not integer) + 0*(number where x is integer) = - (number of m where x not integer )\n\nBut the number of m where x not integer is equal to (999 - A), where A is the number of m where x is integer. Therefore, the sum is equal to - (999 - A) = A - 999.\n\nWait, hold on! Wait a second! I think I made a mistake here.\n\nEarlier, I said:\n\nOriginal Sum = [ - (number of n in 1 to 999 where x_n is not integer ) ] + [ - ceil(2025 / 0.5) + 4050 + floor(2025 / 1000.5) ]\n\nBut actually, in the grouping:\n\nThe term Sum_{m=1}^{999} [ floor(x) - ceil(x) ] is equal to - (number of non-integer x's in m=1..999). Let me call N_non_integer = number of m in [1,999] with x_m not integer. Then this sum is equal to - N_non_integer.\n\nBut N_non_integer = 999 - A, where A is the number of m with x_m integer. Therefore, this sum is equal to - (999 - A) = A - 999.\n\nTherefore, going back to the entire expression:\n\nOriginal Sum = [ A - 999 ] + [ - ceil(2025 / 0.5) + 4050 + floor(2025 / 1000.5) ]\n\nWait, so I had an er