oor of that is -3. Wait, let me compute that term:\n\nFor j = -1000: k = -999.5\n\n2025 / (-999.5) ≈ -2.025, so floor(-2.025) = -3.\n\nWait, so the terms in S2 start at -4050 (when j = -1) and increase (become less negative) as j decreases (goes more negative) until j = -1000, where it is -3.\n\nTherefore, the sum S2 is the sum of integers from -4050 up to -3, but not all integers in between, only specific ones. But the total sum S2 is going to be a large negative number, while S1 is a large positive number. The question is whether the positive sum S1 outweighs the negative sum S2 enough to give a positive result, or not.\n\nBut according to the formula, it's -984, which is negative. Hmm.\n\nWait, let me estimate the magnitudes.\n\nFirst, S1 is the sum from j=0 to 1000 of floor(2025/(j + 0.5)).\n\nThe terms in S1 start at j=0: floor(2025 / 0.5) = floor(4050) = 4050\n\nThen j=1: floor(2025 / 1.5) = floor(1350) = 1350\n\nj=2: floor(2025 / 2.5) = floor(810) = 810\n\nj=3: floor(2025 / 3.5) ≈ floor(578.571) = 578\n\nContinuing this way, the terms decrease, but they are all positive and decreasing. The sum S1 is going to be a large positive number.\n\nS2 is the sum from j=-1000 to -1 of floor(2025/(j + 0.5)), which as we saw starts at j=-1: -4050 and goes up to j=-1000: floor(-2025/999.5) ≈ floor(-2.025) = -3. So the terms in S2 go from -4050 up to -3, increasing by roughly 1 each time? Not exactly, but they are all negative integers.\n\nThe sum S2 is going to be roughly the sum of an arithmetic sequence from -4050 to -3, but with steps that might not be exactly 1. However, even if it were exactly an arithmetic sequence from -4050 to -3 with step 1, the number of terms would be 4050 - 3 + 1 = 4048 terms, but since it's only 1000 terms, that's not the case. Wait, no, actually the number of terms in S2 is 1000, as established before.\n\nWait, actually, in S2, the terms start at -4050 (j=-1) and go up to -3 (j=-1000). The exact values depend on the division, but they are all