s, in general, floor(-x) = -ceil(x). Therefore, if we let d = -k where k is a positive number, then floor(2025/d) = floor(-2025/k) = -ceil(2025/k).\n\nTherefore, if we denote for positive d, floor(2025/d) as some non-negative integer, and for negative d, floor(2025/d) is equal to -ceil(2025/|d|). Since |d| = -d when d is negative.\n\nTherefore, perhaps we can rewrite the entire sum as follows:\n\nSum_{m = -1000}^{1000} floor(2025/(m + 0.5)) = Sum_{m = -1000}^{1000} [if m + 0.5 > 0 then floor(2025/(m + 0.5)) else -ceil(2025/(-m - 0.5))]\n\nBut since m ranges from -1000 to 1000, so m + 0.5 ranges from -999.5 to 1000.5. Alternatively, perhaps it's better to split the sum into two parts: the sum over m from -1000 to -1 (so that d = m + 0.5 is negative) and the sum over m from 0 to 1000 (so that d = m + 0.5 is non-negative). Then, we can write the total sum as:\n\nSum_{m=-1000}^{-1} floor(2025/(m + 0.5)) + Sum_{m=0}^{1000} floor(2025/(m + 0.5))\n\nNow, in the first sum, where m ranges from -1000 to -1, let me make a substitution. Let me set n = -m - 1. Wait, perhaps better to let n = -m. Let me see:\n\nLet me set for the first sum, let n = -m - 1? Wait, let me think. Let me let n = -m. Then when m = -1000, n = 1000; when m = -1, n = 1. Wait, so if we let n = -m, then m = -n, so substituting into the first sum:\n\nSum_{m = -1000}^{-1} floor(2025/(m + 0.5)) = Sum_{n = 1}^{1000} floor(2025/(-n + 0.5))\n\nWait, hold on, let's do substitution step by step.\n\nLet me let n = -m. Then when m = -1000, n = 1000; when m = -1, n = 1; so the summation index n runs from 1000 down to 1 as m runs from -1000 up to -1. Since summation is just over integers, we can reverse the order:\n\nSum_{m = -1000}^{-1} floor(2025/(m + 0.5)) = Sum_{n = 1}^{1000} floor(2025/(-n + 0.5))\n\nWait, let me verify:\n\nIf m = -n, then m + 0.5 = -n + 0.5 = -(n - 0.5). Wait, but actually, m + 0.5 = (-n) + 0.5 = -n + 0.5 = -(n - 0.5). Hmm, but maybe better to write as:\n\nfloor(2025 / (m + 0.5)) where m = -n is floor(2025 / (-n + 0.5)).\n\nAlternatively, let me factor out the negative sign in the denominator:\n\nfloor(2025 / (m + 0.5)) = floor(2025 / ( - (n - 0.5) )) = floor( -2025 / (n - 0.5) )\n\nTherefore, the first sum becomes:\n\nSum_{n=1}^{1000} floor( -2025 / (n - 0.5) )\n\nNow, as I thought earlier, floor(-x) = -ceil(x). Therefore, floor(-2025/(n - 0.5)) = -ceil(2025/(n - 0.5))\n\nTherefore, the first sum is equal to:\n\nSum_{n=1}^{1000} [ -ceil(2025/(n - 0.5)) ] = - Sum_{n=1}^{1000} ceil(2025/(n - 0.5))\n\nSo, the total sum is:\n\nTotal = [ - Sum_{n=1}^{1000} ceil(2025/(n - 0.5)) ] + [ Sum_{m=0}^{1000} floor(2025/(m + 0.5)) ]\n\nNow, notice that in the second sum, m runs from 0 to 1000, so the terms are floor(2025/(m + 0.5)) for m from 0 to 1000. Let me make a substitution here as well. Let me let k = m + 0.5, but actually, let me see:\n\nWait, if we let in the second sum, let k = m, then m + 0.5 is just the denominator. Alternatively, let me shift the index in the second sum to align with the first sum's terms. Wait, perhaps it's better to consider the second sum as Sum_{m=0}^{1000} floor(2025/(m + 0.5)) = Sum_{k=0.5}^{1000.5} floor(2025/k) where k increments by 1 each time. But actually, since m is integer, m + 0.5 is half-integers starting at 0.5, 1.5, ..., up to 1000.5.\n\nAlternatively, let me set in the second sum, let p = m + 0.5, so p runs from 0.5 to 1000.5 in steps of 1.0. Therefore, p = 0.5, 1.5, 2.5, ..., 1000.5. Then, the second sum is Sum_{p=0.5, step 1.0}^{1000.5} floor(2025/p). Similarly, in the first sum, after substitution, we have terms involving ceil(2025/(n - 0.5)) where n runs from 1 to 1000. Let me see what n - 0.5 is. When n = 1, n - 0.5 = 0.5; when n = 2, n - 0.5 = 1.5; ... when n = 1000, n - 0.5 = 999.5. Therefore, the terms in the first sum's ceiling function are ceil(2025/(n - 0.5)) where n - 0.5 runs over the same set as p in the second sum, except that in the second sum, p runs from 0.5 to 1000.5, while in the first sum, n - 0.5 runs from 0.5 to 999.5. Wait, so actually, the terms in the first sum's ceiling function are over p from 0.5 to 999.5 in steps of 1.0, while the second sum's floor function is over p from 0.5 to 1000.5 in steps of 1.0. Therefore, the second sum includes an extra term at p = 1000.5, and the first sum's ceiling terms go up to p = 999.5. Therefore, the total sum can be written as:\n\nTotal = - [ Sum_{p=0.5}^{999.5} ceil(2025/p) ] + [ Sum_{p=0.5}^{1000.5} floor(2025/p) ]\n\nTherefore, combining the two sums:\n\nTotal = [ Sum_{p=0.5}^{1000.5} floor(2025/p) ] - [ Sum_{p=0.5}^{999.5} ceil(2025/p) ]\n\nThen, we can write this as:\n\nTotal = [ Sum_{p=0.5}^{1000.5} floor(2025/p) - Sum_{p=0.5}^{999.5} ceil(2025/p) ]\n\n= [ floor(2025/1000.5) + Sum_{p=0.5}^{999.5} floor(2025/p) - Sum_{p=0.5}^{999.5} ceil(2025/p) ]\n\nTherefore, the difference between the two sums reduces to just the last term of the first sum minus the difference between the sum of floor and ceil over the same terms. That is,\n\nTotal = floor(2025/1000.5) + Sum_{p=0.5}^{999.5} [ floor(2025/p) - ceil(2025/p) ]\n\nBut note that for any real number x, floor(x) - ceil(x) = -1 if x is not an integer, and 0 if x is an integer. Because if x is an integer, then floor(x) = ceil(x) = x, so their difference is 0. If x is not an integer, then ceil(x) = floor(x) + 1, so floor(x) - ceil(x) = -1.\n\nTherefore, the expression floor(x) - ceil(x) is equal to -1 when x is not an integer, and 0 when x is an integer. Therefore, in our case, for each p in the summation from p=0.5 to 999.5, the term [ floor(2025/p) - ceil(2025/p) ] is equal to -1 if 2025/p is not an integer, and 0 if 2025/p is an integer.\n\nTherefore, the entire sum becomes:\n\nTotal = floor(2025/1000.5) + Sum_{p=0.5}^{999.5} [ -1 if 2025/p is not an integer, else 0 ]\n\nTherefore, that can be written as:\n\nTotal = floor(2025/1000.5) - N,\n\nwhere N is the number of p in the interval [0.5, 999.5] (in steps of 1.0) for which 2025/p is not an integer. Alternatively, N = (number of p in [0.5, 999.5] with p ≠ 2025/k for some integer k).\n\nAlternatively, since N is the number of p in that interval where 2025/p is not an integer, so equivalently, N = (number of p in [0.5, 999.5]) minus (number of p in [0.5, 999.5] for which 2025/p is an integer).\n\nTherefore, if we let M be the number of p in [0.5, 999.5] with p = 2025/k for some integer k, then N = (Total terms in the sum) - M.\n\nFirst, let's compute the total number of terms in the sum from p = 0.5 to p = 999.5 with step 1.0. Since p starts at 0.5 and ends at 999.5, the number of terms is (999.5 - 0.5)/1 + 1 = (999)/1 + 1 = 999 + 1 = 1000. Wait, let me verify that formula:\n\nThe number of terms in an arithmetic sequence from a to b with step d is floor((b - a)/d) + 1. Here, a = 0.5, b = 999.5, d = 1.0. So (999.5 - 0.5)/1.0 = 999, so number of terms is 999 + 1 = 1000. Yes, so there are 1000 terms in the sum over p from 0.5 to 999.5.\n\nTherefore, N = 1000 - M, where M is the number of p in that interval where 2025/p is an integer. Therefore, substituting back into the expression for Total:\n\nTotal = floor(2025/1000.5) - (1000 - M) = floor(2025/1000.5) - 1000 + M.\n\nTherefore, we need to compute:\n\n1. floor(2025 / 1000.5)\n2. The value M, which is the number of p in [0.5, 999.5] (step 1.0) such that 2025/p is an integer.\n\nLet me compute floor(2025 / 1000.5) first.\n\nCompute 2025 divided by 1000.5. Let me note that 1000.5 = 1000 + 0.5 = (2001)/2. Therefore, 2025 divided by (2001/2) is equal to 2025 * (2 / 2001) = (4050)/2001 ≈ let's compute this.\n\nFirst, divide numerator and denominator by 3: 4050 ÷ 3 = 1350; 2001 ÷ 3 = 667. So 1350 / 667 ≈ Let's see, 667 * 2 = 1334, so 1350 - 1334 = 16, so 1350 / 667 ≈ 2 + 16/667 ≈ 2.0239. Therefore, 2025 / 1000.5 ≈ 2.0239, so the floor of that is 2.\n\nTherefore, floor(2025 / 1000.5) = 2.\n\nSo that term is 2.\n\nNow, we need to compute M: the number of p in [0.5, 999.5] (step 1.0) such that 2025/p is an integer.\n\nIn other words, p must be a divisor of 2025, but p is a half-integer in the interval [0.5, 999.5].\n\nWait, p is of the form k + 0.5 where k is an integer from 0 to 999. Because p starts at 0.5 (which is 0 + 0.5) and goes up to 999.5 (which is 999 + 0.5). Therefore, p = k + 0.5, where k is an integer from 0 to 999 inclusive. So p = k + 0.5, so 2025 divided by p must be an integer. Let me denote that integer as t, so that 2025 / p = t, where t is a positive integer (since p is positive in this interval). Therefore, p = 2025 / t, and since p must be equal to k + 0.5 for some integer k between 0 and 999, we have that 2025/t must equal k + 0.5, so 2025/t = k + 0.5 => t = 2025 / (k + 0.5) = 4050 / (2k + 1). Therefore, t must be an integer, so 4050 divided by (2k + 1) must be an integer. Therefore, (2k + 1) must be a positive divisor of 4050.\n\nTherefore, the condition is that (2k + 1) divides 4050, and since k is an integer between 0 and 999, 2k + 1 is an odd integer between 1 and 1999 (since when k=0, 2k+1=1; when k=999, 2k+1=1999). However, since p = k + 0.5 must be <= 999.5, then k <= 999, so 2k + 1 <= 1999, so that's okay.\n\nTherefore, the possible values of (2k + 1) are the positive odd divisors of 4050 that are between 1 and 1999. For each such divisor d = 2k + 1, we can solve for k = (d - 1)/2, which must be an integer between 0 and 999. Since d is odd, (d - 1) is even, so k is integer. Also, since d <= 1999, then k <= (1999 - 1)/2 = 999, which is okay.\n\nTherefore, the number M is equal to the number of positive odd divisors of 4050 that are less than or equal to 1999. Since 4050 is the number we're factoring, let me factorize 4050 first to find its divisors.\n\nFactorizing 4050:\n\n4050. Let's divide by 10 first: 4050 = 405 * 10 = (5 * 81) * (2 * 5) = 5 * 3^4 * 2 * 5 = 2 * 3^4 * 5^2. So prime factorization of 4050 is 2 × 3⁴ × 5².\n\nTherefore, the divisors of 4050 are of the form 2^a * 3^b * 5^c, where a ∈ {0,1}, b ∈ {0,1,2,3,4}, c ∈ {0,1,2}. However, we are interested only in the odd divisors of 4050, since d = 2k + 1 must be odd. Therefore, the odd divisors correspond to a = 0 in the prime factorization, so divisors of the form 3^b * 5^c, where b ∈ {0,1,2,3,4} and c ∈ {0,1,2}.\n\nTherefore, the number of odd divisors of 4050 is (4 + 1)(2 + 1) = 5 * 3 = 15. Wait, let me confirm:\n\nFor the exponent of 3: possible exponents are 0,1,2,3,4 (so 5 choices)\n\nFor the exponent of 5: possible exponents are 0,1,2 (so 3 choices)\n\nThus, total number of odd divisors is 5 * 3 = 15.\n\nBut we need the number of these odd divisors that are ≤ 1999. Since 4050 itself is 4050, which is greater than 1999, so the largest odd divisor less than or equal to 1999 would be 4050 divided by 2, but 4050 / 2 = 2025, which is still larger than 1999. Wait, perhaps I need to compute all the odd divisors of 4050 and see which ones are ≤ 1999.\n\nAlternatively, since all the odd divisors of 4050 are factors of 4050 that don't have a factor of 2. Since 4050 = 2 × 3⁴ × 5², so all odd divisors are divisors of 3⁴ × 5² = 81 × 25 = 2025. Wait, 3^4 is 81, 5^2 is 25, so 81 × 25 = 2025. Therefore, the odd part of 4050 is 2025. Therefore, the odd divisors of 4050 are exactly the divisors of 2025.\n\nTherefore, the odd divisors of 4050 are the same as the divisors of 2025, and since 2025 is 3^4 × 5^2, so its divisors are of the form 3^a × 5^b where a ∈ {0,1,2,3,4} and b ∈ {0,1,2}. Therefore, the number of divisors is (4+1)(2+1)=15, as above.\n\nTherefore, the odd divisors of 4050 are exactly the divisors of 2025, which are 15 in total.\n\nNow, we need to count how many of these 15 divisors are ≤ 1999.\n\nBut note that 2025 is itself a divisor of 2025 (when a=4, b=2), which is 2025. But 2025 is greater than 1999, so we need to exclude that one. Therefore, all the other 14 divisors are less than or equal to 2025, but since 2025 is the largest, and it's over 1999, so the number of odd divisors of 4050 that are ≤ 1999 is 14.\n\nWait, but hold on: Are there any other divisors of 2025 greater than 1999 besides 2025 itself? Let's see.\n\nThe divisors of 2025 can be listed as follows. Let me list them:\n\nStart with exponents for 3: 0 to 4, and exponents for 5: 0 to 2.\n\nList all combinations:\n\nFor a=0 (3^0):\n\n- 5^0: 1\n\n- 5^1: 5\n\n- 5^2: 25\n\nFor a=1 (3^1):\n\n- 5^0: 3\n\n- 5^1: 15\n\n- 5^2: 75\n\nFor a=2 (3^2):\n\n- 5^0: 9\n\n- 5^1: 45\n\n- 5^2: 225\n\nFor a=3 (3^3):\n\n- 5^0: 27\n\n- 5^1: 135\n\n- 5^2: 675\n\nFor a=4 (3^4):\n\n- 5^0: 81\n\n- 5^1: 405\n\n- 5^2: 2025\n\nSo compiling all divisors:\n\n1, 3, 5, 9, 15, 25, 27, 45, 75, 81, 135, 225, 405, 675, 2025.\n\nSo that's 15 divisors. Now, which of these are ≤ 1999? All except 2025. So that is 14 divisors. Therefore, M = 14.\n\nWait, but hold on: The divisors are as above. Let me list them numerically in order:\n\n1, 3, 5, 9, 15, 25, 27, 45, 75, 81, 135, 225, 405, 675, 2025.\n\nYes, so all except the last one, 2025, are ≤ 1999. Since 2025 is the only one exceeding 1999. Therefore, the number of odd divisors of 4050 (which are the same as the odd divisors of 2025) that are ≤ 1999 is 14. Therefore, M = 14.\n\nTherefore, going back to the expression for Total:\n\nTotal = floor(2025 / 1000.5) - 1000 + M = 2 - 1000 + 14 = (2 + 14) - 1000 = 16 - 1000 = -984.\n\nWait, that can't be right? Wait, hold on, let me double-check my steps.\n\nWait, let me recap:\n\nTotal = floor(2025 / 1000.5) - 1000 + M\n\nWe had floor(2025 / 1000.5) = 2,\n\nM = 14,\n\nso 2 - 1000 + 14 = (2 + 14) - 1000 = 16 - 1000 = -984.\n\nHmm. But this gives a negative total? Is that possible?\n\nWait, let me think about the original sum. The original sum is from j = -1000 to 1000 of floor(2025/(j + 0.5)). Let me consider some terms:\n\nFirst, when j = -1000, then denominator d = -999.5, so 2025 / (-999.5) ≈ -2.026, so floor of that is -3.\n\nSimilarly, when j = -999, d = -998.5, so 2025 / (-998.5) ≈ -2.028, floor is -3.\n\nContinuing down, for j = -1, d = -0.5, so 2025 / (-0.5) = -4050, so floor is -4050.\n\nThen, when j = 0, d = 0.5, so 2025 / 0.5 = 4050, so floor is 4050.\n\nSimilarly, when j = 1, d = 1.5, so 2025 / 1.5 = 1350, floor is 1350.\n\nAnd so on, up to j = 1000, d = 1000.5, so 2025 / 1000.5 ≈ 2.0239, floor is 2.\n\nSo, the terms when j is negative (from -1000 to -1) are all negative, and their floor values are negative integers. The terms when j is non-negative (from 0 to 1000) are non-negative integers. The positive terms when j is positive can be very large (like 4050, 1350, etc.), but the negative terms are all negative. Therefore, it's possible that the total sum is negative? Let me see.\n\nWait, but the problem is from j = -1000 to 1000, so there are 2001 terms. The negative terms (from j = -1000 to -1) are 1000 terms, each contributing a negative number, and the positive terms (from j=0 to 1000) are 1001 terms, contributing non-negative numbers.\n\nSo, if the negative terms sum to a large negative number and the positive terms sum to a large positive number, it's possible that the total is positive or negative depending on which is larger.\n\nBut according to my calculation above, I got Total = -984, but let's see if that's plausible.\n\nWait, but let me check my steps again, because getting a negative total seems surprising, but maybe it's correct?\n\nWait, first, let me retrace the steps where I broke down the sum into two parts:\n\nTotal = Sum_{m=-1000}^{-1} floor(2025/(m + 0.5)) + Sum_{m=0}^{1000} floor(2025/(m + 0.5))\n\nThen, I did substitution for the first sum, ending up with:\n\nTotal = - Sum_{n=1}^{1000} ceil(2025/(n - 0.5)) + Sum_{m=0}^{1000} floor(2025/(m + 0.5))\n\nThen, by reindexing, I expressed the total as:\n\nTotal = floor(2025/1000.5) - 1000 + M,\n\nwhere M = 14. Hmm, let me see if that reindexing was correct.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the reindexing steps. Let me go back.\n\nStarting again:\n\nTotal = [Sum_{m=-1000}^{-1} floor(2025/(m + 0.5))] + [Sum_{m=0}^{1000} floor(2025/(m + 0.5))]\n\nFirst sum: let m go from -1000 to -1. Let me set n = -m - 1? Wait, earlier I set n = -m, so let me see:\n\nWait, let me let n = -m. Then when m = -1000, n = 1000; when m = -1, n = 1. So m = -n. Then:\n\nFirst sum becomes Sum_{n=1}^{1000} floor(2025/( -n + 0.5 )) = Sum_{n=1}^{1000} floor(2025/( - (n - 0.5) )) = Sum_{n=1}^{1000} floor( -2025/(n - 0.5) )\n\nThen, as before, floor(-x) = -ceil(x), so this is equal to Sum_{n=1}^{1000} [ - ceil(2025/(n - 0.5)) ] = - Sum_{n=1}^{1000} ceil(2025/(n - 0.5))\n\nTherefore, the first sum is - Sum_{n=1}^{1000} ceil(2025/(n - 0.5))\n\nThen, the second sum is Sum_{m=0}^{1000} floor(2025/(m + 0.5))\n\nNow, if we let in the second sum, let p = m + 0.5, so p runs from 0.5 to 1000.5 in steps of 1.0. So the second sum is Sum_{p=0.5}^{1000.5} floor(2025/p), stepping by 1.0.\n\nSimilarly, the first sum's ceil terms are over n from 1 to 1000 of ceil(2025/(n - 0.5)), where n - 0.5 runs from 0.5 to 999.5 in steps of 1.0. So the first sum is over p from 0.5 to 999.5 of ceil(2025/p), then multiplied by -1.\n\nTherefore, total sum is:\n\nTotal = - Sum_{p=0.5}^{999.5} ceil(2025/p) + Sum_{p=0.5}^{1000.5} floor(2025/p)\n\n= [Sum_{p=0.5}^{1000.5} floor(2025/p) - Sum_{p=0.5}^{999.5} ceil(2025/p)]\n\n= floor(2025/1000.5) + Sum_{p=0.5}^{999.5} [floor(2025/p) - ceil(2025/p)]\n\nThen, as before, floor(x) - ceil(x) = -1 if x is not integer, 0 if x is integer. So:\n\nTotal = floor(2025/1000.5) - N, where N is the number of p in [0.5, 999.5] where 2025/p is not integer. And N = total terms - M, where M is the number of p where 2025/p is integer.\n\nTotal terms from p=0.5 to p=999.5 is 1000 terms (since (999.5 - 0.5)/1 +1 = 1000). Therefore,\n\nTotal = floor(2025/1000.5) - (1000 - M) = floor(2025/1000.5) - 1000 + M\n\nSo that part is correct.\n\nThen, floor(2025/1000.5) = floor(2025 / (2001/2)) = floor(4050/2001) ≈ floor(2.0239) = 2. Correct.\n\nM is the number of p in [0.5, 999.5] such that 2025/p is integer. And we found that M = 14, since the odd divisors of 4050 (which are the same as divisors of 2025) less tha