) = -ceil(2025 / (j - 0.5)).\n\nTherefore, for j=3:\n\nT(-3) = -ceil(2025 / (3 - 0.5)) = -ceil(2025 / 2.5) = -ceil(810) = -810, since 2025 / 2.5 is exactly 810, so ceil(810) = 810, so T(-3) = -810, which matches.\n\nFor j=4:\n\nT(-4) = -ceil(2025 / (4 - 0.5)) = -ceil(2025 / 3.5) = -ceil(578.571...) = -579, since the ceiling of 578.571 is 579, so T(-4) = -579, which also matches.\n\nSimilarly, for j=1:\n\nT(-1) = -ceil(2025 / (1 - 0.5)) = -ceil(2025 / 0.5) = -ceil(4050) = -4050, which is correct.\n\nj=2:\n\nT(-2) = -ceil(2025 / (2 - 0.5)) = -ceil(2025 / 1.5) = -ceil(1350) = -1350, which is correct.\n\nSo in general, T(-j) = -ceil(2025 / (j - 0.5)). Therefore, T(j) + T(-j) = floor(2025 / (j + 0.5)) - ceil(2025 / (j - 0.5)).\n\nHmm. So that expression is equal to floor(a) - ceil(b), where a = 2025/(j + 0.5) and b = 2025/(j - 0.5).\n\nIs there a relationship between a and b?\n\nLet me see, note that b = 2025/(j - 0.5) and a = 2025/(j + 0.5). Since j is a positive integer, both denominators are positive, so both a and b are positive real numbers. Also, note that j - 0.5 < j + 0.5, so since the numerator is the same, b > a. Therefore, b is greater than a.\n\nTherefore, we have two positive real numbers, a and b, with b > a, and T(j) + T(-j) = floor(a) - ceil(b). Since b > a, ceil(b) ≥ ceil(a) + 1? Not necessarily, because the difference between a and b could be less than 1. Let's see.\n\nLet me consider the difference between b and a:\n\nb - a = 2025/(j - 0.5) - 2025/(j + 0.5) = 2025 [1/(j - 0.5) - 1/(j + 0.5)] = 2025 [ ( (j + 0.5) - (j - 0.5) ) / ( (j - 0.5)(j + 0.5) ) ]\n\nSimplify numerator of the fraction:\n\n(j + 0.5) - (j - 0.5) = 1.\n\nTherefore, b - a = 2025 * [ 1 / ( (j - 0.5)(j + 0.5) ) ] = 2025 / ( (j)^2 - (0.5)^2 ) = 2025 / (j^2 - 0.25).\n\nSo the difference between b and a is 2025 divided by (j squared minus 0.25). Since j is a positive integer, j^2 is at least 1, so the denominator is at least 1 - 0.25 = 0.75, so the difference b - a is at most 2025 / 0.75 = 2700. But for larger j, this difference becomes smaller.\n\nBut how does this help us?\n\nPerhaps, since b - a = 2025 / (j^2 - 0.25), and since a = 2025/(j + 0.5), then if we can express floor(a) - ceil(b) in terms of this difference?\n\nAlternatively, note that since b = a + (b - a), then ceil(b) = ceil(a + (b - a)). Since b - a is positive, but how much?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps think of floor(a) and ceil(b) in terms of each other.\n\nAlternatively, maybe consider that floor(a) ≤ a < floor(a) + 1,\n\nand ceil(b) - 1 < b ≤ ceil(b).\n\nTherefore, floor(a) - ceil(b) < a - (ceil(b) - 1) ≤ floor(a) - ceil(b) + 1.\n\nBut this seems a bit convoluted. Maybe instead, think numerically for some j to see if there's a pattern.\n\nTake j=1:\n\na = 2025/(1 + 0.5) = 1350, so floor(a)=1350,\n\nb = 2025/(1 - 0.5) = 4050, so ceil(b)=4050,\n\nso floor(a) - ceil(b) = 1350 - 4050 = -2700, which matches our previous result.\n\nSimilarly, for j=2:\n\na = 2025/(2.5) = 810, floor(a) = 810,\n\nb = 2025/(1.5) = 1350, ceil(b)=1350,\n\nso floor(a) - ceil(b) = 810 - 1350 = -540, which matches.\n\nj=3:\n\na = 2025 / 3.5 ≈ 578.571, floor(a)=578,\n\nb = 2025 / 2.5 = 810, ceil(b)=810,\n\nso floor(a) - ceil(b) = 578 - 810 = -232, which matches.\n\nj=4:\n\na = 2025 / 4.5 = 450, floor(a)=450,\n\nb = 2025 / 3.5 ≈ 578.571, ceil(b)=579,\n\nso floor(a) - ceil(b) = 450 - 579 = -129, which matches.\n\nj=5:\n\nLet me compute for j=5.\n\nT(5) = floor(2025 / (5 + 0.5)) = floor(2025 / 5.5).\n\n5.5 is 11/2, so 2025 divided by 5.5 is 2025 * 2 / 11 ≈ 4050 / 11 ≈ 368.1818...\n\nSo floor(368.1818...) = 368.\n\nT(-5) = floor(2025 / (-5 + 0.5)) = floor(2025 / (-4.5)) = floor(-450) = -450.\n\nWait, hold on:\n\nWait, T(-j) for j=5 is floor(2025 / (-5 + 0.5)) = floor(2025 / (-4.5)) = floor(-450). Since -450 is an integer, floor(-450) is -450. But according to the formula T(-j) = -ceil(2025/(j - 0.5)), so let's check:\n\nT(-5) = -ceil(2025/(5 - 0.5)) = -ceil(2025/4.5) = -ceil(450) = -450. Correct.\n\nSo T(5) + T(-5) = 368 + (-450) = -82.\n\nAlternatively, via the formula floor(a) - ceil(b):\n\na = 2025/(5.5) ≈ 368.1818, floor(a) = 368,\n\nb = 2025/(5 - 0.5) = 2025/4.5 = 450, ceil(b) = 450,\n\nso floor(a) - ceil(b) = 368 - 450 = -82. Correct.\n\nOkay, so the formula holds.\n\nNow, let's see if we can find a general expression for floor(a) - ceil(b), where a = 2025/(j + 0.5) and b = 2025/(j - 0.5).\n\nNote that b = 2025/(j - 0.5) = 2025 / ( (j + 0.5) - 1 ) = a / (1 - 1/(j + 0.5)), but not sure if that helps.\n\nAlternatively, since b = 2025/(j - 0.5) and a = 2025/(j + 0.5), then note that b = a * (j + 0.5)/(j - 0.5).\n\nHmm. Let me see:\n\nLet me denote k = j + 0.5, so then a = 2025 / k,\n\nand b = 2025 / (k - 1),\n\nsince j - 0.5 = (j + 0.5) - 1 = k - 1.\n\nTherefore, b = 2025/(k - 1), and a = 2025/k,\n\nso then floor(a) - ceil(b) = floor(2025/k) - ceil(2025/(k - 1)).\n\nHmm, so in terms of k, where k = j + 0.5, and since j ranges from 1 to 1000, k ranges from 1.5 to 1000.5 in increments of 1 (since j increases by 1 each time, so k increases by 1 each time). Wait, actually, j is integer, so k = j + 0.5 is a half-integer starting at 1.5, 2.5, 3.5, ..., up to 1000.5.\n\nTherefore, k takes on all half-integers from 1.5 to 1000.5. So k = n + 0.5 where n ranges from 1 to 1000.\n\nTherefore, for each k = n + 0.5, with n from 1 to 1000, we have:\n\nfloor(2025/k) - ceil(2025/(k - 1)).\n\nSo the term for each n (from 1 to 1000) is floor(2025/(n + 0.5)) - ceil(2025/n).\n\nWait, since k = n + 0.5, so k - 1 = n - 0.5, so 2025/(k - 1) = 2025/(n - 0.5). Wait, but in the expression above, in terms of k, we had:\n\nfloor(a) - ceil(b) = floor(2025/k) - ceil(2025/(k - 1))\n\nBut in terms of n, since k = n + 0.5,\n\nfloor(2025/(n + 0.5)) - ceil(2025/(n - 0.5)).\n\nWait, but n starts at 1, so when n=1, we get:\n\nfloor(2025/(1 + 0.5)) - ceil(2025/(1 - 0.5)) = floor(1350) - ceil(4050) = 1350 - 4050 = -2700,\n\nwhich matches the j=1 case.\n\nBut in this expression, for each n from 1 to 1000, we have the term floor(2025/(n + 0.5)) - ceil(2025/(n - 0.5)). Hmm, but n - 0.5 when n=1 is 0.5, so 2025 divided by 0.5 is 4050, which is okay.\n\nWait, but when n=1, the second term is ceil(2025/(1 - 0.5)) = ceil(4050) = 4050. So that works.\n\nBut when n=2, the second term is ceil(2025/(2 - 0.5)) = ceil(2025/1.5) = ceil(1350) = 1350, which is correct.\n\nWait, but for n=3, the second term is ceil(2025/(3 - 0.5)) = ceil(2025/2.5) = ceil(810) = 810, which is correct.\n\nSimilarly, for n=4, ceil(2025/(4 - 0.5)) = ceil(2025/3.5) ≈ ceil(578.571) = 579, which is correct.\n\nTherefore, in general, for each n from 1 to 1000,\n\nTerm(n) = floor(2025/(n + 0.5)) - ceil(2025/(n - 0.5)),\n\nand the total sum over j=1 to 1000 of [T(j) + T(-j)] is equal to the sum over n=1 to 1000 of Term(n).\n\nTherefore, S = [sum_{n=1}^{1000} Term(n)] + 4050.\n\nSo the problem reduces to computing the sum from n=1 to n=1000 of [floor(2025/(n + 0.5)) - ceil(2025/(n - 0.5))], and then adding 4050.\n\nHmm, perhaps this sum can be telescoped? Let me see.\n\nLet me write out the first few terms of the sum to see if there's cancellation.\n\nLet me denote for each n,\n\nTerm(n) = floor(2025/(n + 0.5)) - ceil(2025/(n - 0.5)).\n\nLet me write this as Term(n) = A(n) - B(n), where A(n) = floor(2025/(n + 0.5)), and B(n) = ceil(2025/(n - 0.5)).\n\nThen, the sum from n=1 to N (where N=1000) of [A(n) - B(n)] = [sum_{n=1}^N A(n)] - [sum_{n=1}^N B(n)].\n\nIf I can relate the sum of A(n) and the sum of B(n), perhaps there is cancellation.\n\nLet me see what B(n) is in terms of A(n+1) or something else?\n\nWait, note that B(n) = ceil(2025/(n - 0.5)).\n\nLet me make a substitution for B(n). Let me let m = n - 1, so when n = 1, m = 0; when n = N, m = N - 1. Therefore,\n\nsum_{n=1}^N B(n) = sum_{n=1}^N ceil(2025/(n - 0.5)) = sum_{m=0}^{N - 1} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5)).\n\nTherefore, sum_{n=1}^N B(n) = sum_{m=0}^{N - 1} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5)).\n\nTherefore, the total sum becomes:\n\nsum_{n=1}^N [A(n) - B(n)] = [sum_{n=1}^N A(n)] - [sum_{m=0}^{N - 1} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5))] = [sum_{n=1}^N A(n)] - [sum_{m=0}^{N - 1} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5))].\n\nNow, note that A(n) = floor(2025/(n + 0.5)), so if we shift the index in the first sum:\n\nLet me write sum_{n=1}^N A(n) = sum_{k=1.5}^{N + 0.5} floor(2025/k), where k = n + 0.5, but k is in steps of 1, so k = 1.5, 2.5, ..., N + 0.5.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps writing in terms of m:\n\nLet me set for the first sum, let m = n, so:\n\nsum_{n=1}^N A(n) = sum_{m=1}^N floor(2025/(m + 0.5)),\n\nand the second sum is sum_{m=0}^{N - 1} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5)).\n\nTherefore, the difference is:\n\nsum_{m=1}^N floor(2025/(m + 0.5)) - sum_{m=0}^{N - 1} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5)).\n\nLet me write this as:\n\n[ sum_{m=1}^N floor(2025/(m + 0.5)) - sum_{m=1}^{N - 1} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5)) ] - ceil(2025/(0 + 0.5)).\n\nWait, breaking down the second sum:\n\nsum_{m=0}^{N - 1} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5)) = ceil(2025/(0 + 0.5)) + sum_{m=1}^{N - 1} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5)).\n\nTherefore, the entire expression becomes:\n\nsum_{m=1}^N floor(2025/(m + 0.5)) - [ ceil(2025/0.5) + sum_{m=1}^{N - 1} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5)) ]\n\n= [sum_{m=1}^N floor(2025/(m + 0.5)) - sum_{m=1}^{N - 1} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5))] - ceil(2025/0.5)\n\n= [floor(2025/(N + 0.5)) + sum_{m=1}^{N - 1} [floor(2025/(m + 0.5)) - ceil(2025/(m + 0.5))] ] - ceil(2025/0.5)\n\nHmm, this seems a bit messy, but maybe we can see something here.\n\nNote that for each m from 1 to N - 1, we have the term [floor(2025/(m + 0.5)) - ceil(2025/(m + 0.5))].\n\nBut floor(x) - ceil(x) is equal to -1 when x is not an integer, and 0 when x is an integer. Because if x is an integer, floor(x) = ceil(x) = x, so their difference is 0. If x is not an integer, then ceil(x) = floor(x) + 1, so floor(x) - ceil(x) = -1.\n\nTherefore, for each m from 1 to N - 1:\n\nfloor(2025/(m + 0.5)) - ceil(2025/(m + 0.5)) = -1 if 2025/(m + 0.5) is not an integer,\n\nand 0 if 2025/(m + 0.5) is an integer.\n\nTherefore, the sum over m=1 to N - 1 of [floor(2025/(m + 0.5)) - ceil(2025/(m + 0.5))] is equal to:\n\nnumber_of_non_integer_terms * (-1) + number_of_integer_terms * 0 = - (N - 1 - number_of_integer_terms).\n\nWait, more precisely, let me denote for each m from 1 to N - 1,\n\nLet me denote x_m = 2025/(m + 0.5). Then:\n\nIf x_m is integer, then floor(x_m) - ceil(x_m) = 0,\n\nElse, floor(x_m) - ceil(x_m) = -1.\n\nTherefore, the sum over m=1 to N - 1 of [floor(x_m) - ceil(x_m)] = - (number of non-integer x_m terms from m=1 to N-1).\n\nBut since the total number of terms is (N - 1), and the number of integer x_m terms is, let's call it K,\n\nthen the sum is - [ (N - 1) - K ] = K - (N - 1).\n\nTherefore, putting back into the expression:\n\nThe entire sum [sum_{n=1}^N Term(n)] =\n\n[ floor(2025/(N + 0.5)) + (K - (N - 1)) ] - ceil(2025/0.5)\n\nwhere K is the number of integers m in [1, N - 1] such that 2025/(m + 0.5) is an integer.\n\nWait, but K is the number of m from 1 to N - 1 where x_m is integer, i.e., 2025/(m + 0.5) is integer.\n\nSo, to write it out:\n\nsum_{n=1}^N Term(n) =\n\nfloor(2025/(N + 0.5)) + (K - (N - 1)) - ceil(2025/0.5)\n\nNow, let's plug in N = 1000, so:\n\nsum_{n=1}^{1000} Term(n) =\n\nfloor(2025/(1000 + 0.5)) + (K - 999) - ceil(2025/0.5)\n\nWhere K is the number of integers m from 1 to 999 such that 2025/(m + 0.5) is integer.\n\nHmm, this is getting somewhere, but we still need to figure out what K is.\n\nFirst, let's compute the other terms:\n\nFirst, compute ceil(2025/0.5). Since 2025 divided by 0.5 is 4050, which is an integer, so ceil(4050) = 4050.\n\nThen floor(2025/(1000 + 0.5)) = floor(2025 / 1000.5). Let me compute that.\n\n2025 divided by 1000.5. Let me write 1000.5 as 2001/2, so 2025 divided by (2001/2) = 2025 * 2 / 2001 = 4050 / 2001 ≈ 2.02 (approximately). Let me compute it more precisely.\n\nCompute 4050 ÷ 2001:\n\n2001 * 2 = 4002\n\n4050 - 4002 = 48\n\nSo 4050 / 2001 = 2 + 48/2001 ≈ 2 + 0.024 ≈ 2.024, so floor of that is 2.\n\nTherefore, floor(2025/1000.5) = 2.\n\nNow, the term K is the number of integers m in [1, 999] such that 2025/(m + 0.5) is an integer.\n\nLet me analyze when 2025/(m + 0.5) is an integer.\n\nLet me denote m + 0.5 = t, so t = m + 0.5, where m is integer from 1 to 999, so t ranges from 1.5 to 999.5 in steps of 1.0 (since m increases by 1 each time, so t increases by 1 each time). Therefore, t is a half-integer: 1.5, 2.5, 3.5, ..., 999.5.\n\nTherefore, we require that 2025 divided by t is an integer, where t is a half-integer in that range.\n\nLet me write t = k/2, where k is an odd integer, since t is a half-integer. Specifically, t = (2n + 1)/2 for some integer n. Wait, actually, since t starts at 1.5 = 3/2, then t = (2m + 1)/2 where m starts at 1? Wait, let's see:\n\nWait, t = m + 0.5, where m is integer from 1 to 999. So t = (2m + 1)/2. So t can be written as an odd integer divided by 2.\n\nTherefore, 2025 divided by t must be an integer. Let me write that:\n\n2025 / t = 2025 / [(2m + 1)/2] = 4050 / (2m + 1) must be an integer.\n\nTherefore, 2m + 1 must divide 4050.\n\nSo 2m + 1 is a positive odd divisor of 4050, and 2m + 1 must satisfy 2m + 1 ≤ 2*999 + 1 = 1999, since m ≤ 999.\n\nWait, m ranges from 1 to 999, so 2m + 1 ranges from 3 to 1999, stepping by 2 (since m increases by 1 each time, so 2m +1 increases by 2 each time). So 2m +1 must be an odd divisor of 4050 that is between 3 and 1999 inclusive.\n\nTherefore, the number K is equal to the number of odd divisors of 4050 that are in the interval [3, 1999].\n\nTherefore, to compute K, we need to factorize 4050 and find all its odd divisors, then count those that are between 3 and 1999.\n\nFirst, factorize 4050.\n\n4050. Let's see:\n\n4050 ÷ 2 = 2025\n\n2025 is known as 45^2, since 45*45 = 2025. Alternatively, 2025 ÷ 5 = 405, 405 ÷ 5 = 81, so 2025 = 5^2 * 3^4. Therefore,\n\n4050 = 2 * 5^2 * 3^4.\n\nTherefore, the prime factorization of 4050 is 2 × 3⁴ × 5².\n\nTherefore, the odd part of 4050 is 3⁴ × 5², since we can factor out the 2.\n\nTherefore, the odd divisors of 4050 are the divisors of 3⁴ × 5².\n\nThe number of odd divisors is (4 + 1)(2 + 1) = 5 × 3 = 15.\n\nSo there are 15 odd divisors of 4050. Let me list them all.\n\nFirst, list the exponents for 3: 0,1,2,3,4\n\nExponents for 5: 0,1,2\n\nTherefore, the odd divisors are 3^a * 5^b, where a ∈ {0,1,2,3,4}, b ∈ {0,1,2}.\n\nLet me compute them:\n\nStart with a=0:\n\n- 3^0 * 5^0 = 1\n\n- 3^0 * 5^1 = 5\n\n- 3^0 * 5^2 = 25\n\na=1:\n\n- 3^1 * 5^0 = 3\n\n- 3^1 * 5^1 = 15\n\n- 3^1 * 5^2 = 75\n\na=2:\n\n- 3^2 * 5^0 = 9\n\n- 3^2 * 5^1 = 45\n\n- 3^2 * 5^2 = 225\n\na=3:\n\n- 3^3 * 5^0 = 27\n\n- 3^3 * 5^1 = 135\n\n- 3^3 * 5^2 = 675\n\na=4:\n\n- 3^4 * 5^0 = 81\n\n- 3^4 * 5^1 = 405\n\n- 3^4 * 5^2 = 2025\n\nTherefore, the list of all odd divisors of 4050 is:\n\n1, 3, 5, 9, 15, 25, 27, 45, 75, 81, 135, 225, 405, 675, 2025.\n\nNow, we need to count how many of these divisors are in the interval [3, 1999].\n\nFirst, note that 1 is excluded because we need divisors ≥3.\n\nAlso, the largest divisor here is 2025, which is greater than 1999, so we need to exclude that as well.\n\nSo let's list all the odd divisors except 1 and 2025, and see which are ≤1999.\n\nThe list without 1 and 2025 is:\n\n3, 5, 9, 15, 25, 27, 45, 75, 81, 135, 225, 405, 675, 2025 (wait, but 2025 is excluded), so actually:\n\n3, 5, 9, 15, 25, 27, 45, 75, 81, 135, 225, 405, 675.\n\nWait, let's recount:\n\nOriginal list without 1 and 2025:\n\nFrom the list above:\n\n1 (excluded),\n\n3,\n\n5,\n\n9,\n\n15,\n\n25,\n\n27,\n\n45,\n\n75,\n\n81,\n\n135,\n\n225,\n\n405,\n\n675,\n\n2025 (excluded).\n\nSo that's 13 divisors. Wait, let me count:\n\nStarting from 3:\n\n1. 3\n\n2. 5\n\n3. 9\n\n4. 15\n\n5. 25\n\n6. 27\n\n7. 45\n\n8. 75\n\n9. 81\n\n10. 135\n\n11. 225\n\n12. 405\n\n13. 675\n\nYes, 13 divisors.\n\nNow, check each of these 13 divisors to see if they are ≤1999. Since the largest is 675, which is less than 1999, all 13 divisors are ≤1999. Wait, hold on, 675 is less than 1999? Yes, 675 is much less. Wait, but wait, in my list above, after 675, the next was 2025 which was excluded. Wait, but in the list above, the divisors after 675 would be 2025, which we excluded, so the remaining ones up to 675 are all less than 1999. Wait, actually, 675 is 675, which is way less than 1999. So all 13 divisors are between 3 and 1999 inclusive?\n\nWait, hold on, let me check each one:\n\n3: yes, 3 ≥3 and ≤1999.\n\n5: yes.\n\n9: yes.\n\n15: yes.\n\n25: yes.\n\n27: yes.\n\n45: yes.\n\n75: yes.\n\n81: yes.\n\n135: yes.\n\n225: yes.\n\n405: yes.\n\n675: yes.\n\nAll of these are between 3 and 1999. So all 13 divisors qualify. Therefore, K = 13.\n\nWait, but hold on. Wait a second, 675 is 675, which is less than 1999, so yes, all 13 divisors are within [3, 1999]. So K = 13.\n\nWait, but let me confirm that 2025 is the only divisor above 1999? The next one after 675 is 2025, which is over 1999, so yes, so only 2025 is excluded, so indeed K=13.\n\nWait, but hold on. Let me check if any of the other divisors are greater than 1999. Let me see:\n\nLooking at the list:\n\n3, 5, 9, 15, 25, 27, 45, 75, 81, 135, 225, 405, 675.\n\nThe largest is 675, which is way less than 1999. So yes, all 13 divisors are between 3 and 1999. Therefore, K = 13.\n\nTherefore, going back to the expression for the sum:\n\nsum_{n=1}^{1000} Term(n) = floor(2025/1000.5) + (K - (N - 1)) - ceil(2025/0.5)\n\nPlugging in the numbers:\n\nfloor(2025/1000.5) = 2,\n\nK = 13,\n\nN = 1000,\n\nso (K - (N - 1)) = 13 - 999 = -986,\n\nceil(2025/0.5) = 4050,\n\nTherefore,\n\nsum_{n=1}^{1000} Term(n) = 2 + (-986) - 4050 = 2 - 986 - 4050 = (2 - 986) - 4050 = (-984) - 4050 = -5034.\n\nWait, so the sum from n=1 to 1000 of Term(n) is -5034?\n\nHmm, let me verify this with some small N to see if the formula holds.\n\nLet me test with N=1.\n\nWait, if N=1, then the sum from n=1 to 1 of Term(n) should be Term(1) = floor(2025/1.5) - ceil(2025/0.5) = 1350 - 4050 = -2700.\n\nUsing the formula:\n\nsum_{n=1}^1 Term(n) = floor(2025/(1 + 0.5)) + (K - (1 - 1)) - ceil(2025/0.5)\n\nWait, but for N=1,\n\nFirst, K would be the number of m in [1, N-1] = [1,0], which is an empty set, so K=0.\n\nWait, let me redo the formula for N=1:\n\nWait, in the general formula above, for N=1,\n\nsum_{n=1}^1 Term(n) = floor(2025/(1 + 0.5)) + (K - (1 - 1)) - ceil(2025/0.5)\n\nBut K is the number of m from 1 to N-1 = 0, so m from 1 to 0, which is none, so K=0.\n\nThus,\n\n= floor(1350) + (0 - 0) - 4050 = 1350 - 4050 = -2700, which matches.\n\nSo that works.\n\nAnother test with N=2.\n\nCompute manually:\n\nsum_{n=1}^2 Term(n) = Term(1) + Term(2) = (-2700) + (-540) = -3240.\n\nUsing the formula:\n\nFirst, K is the number of m from 1 to N-1 = 1 to 1, so m=1.\n\nWe need to check if 2025/(m + 0.5) is integer for m=1.\n\nm=1: 2025/(1 + 0.5) = 1350, which is an integer, so yes. Therefore, K=1.\n\nThen,\n\nsum = floor(2025/(2 + 0.5)) + (K - (2 -1)) - ceil(2025/0.5)\n\nCompute each term:\n\nfloor(2025/2.5) = floor(810) = 810,\n\nK - (N -1) = 1 - 1 = 0,\n\nceil(2025/0.5) = 4050,\n\nso total = 810 + 0 - 4050 = -3240, which matches.\n\nGood, so the formula works for N=2.\n\nAnother test with N=3.\n\nCompute manually:\n\nsum_{n=1}^3 Term(n) = Term(1) + Term(2) + Term(3) = -2700 -540 -232 = -2700 -540 is -3240, minus 232 is -3472.\n\nUsing the formula:\n\nFirst, K is the number of m from 1 to 2 (since N=3, N-1=2) where 2025/(m + 0.5) is integer.\n\nFor m=1: 2025/1.5 = 1350, integer. So yes.\n\nFor m=2: 2025/2.5 = 810, integer. So yes.\n\nTherefore, K=2.\n\nThen,\n\nsum = floor(2025/(3 + 0.5)) + (K - (3 - 1)) - ceil(2025/0.5)\n\nfloor(2025/3.5) ≈ floor(578.571) = 578,\n\n(K - 2) = (2 - 2) = 0,\n\nceil(2025/0.5) = 4050,\n\nso total = 578 + 0 - 4050 = -3472, which matches.\n\nPerfect, so the formula works.\n\nTherefore, returning to N=1000:\n\nsum_{n=1}^{1000} Term(n) = 2 + (13 - 999) - 4050 = 2 + (-986) - 4050 = 2 - 986 - 4050.\n\nCalculating step by step:\n\n2 - 986 = -984,\n\nthen -984 - 4050 = - (984 + 4050) = -5034.\n\nTherefore, the sum from n=1 to 1000 of Term(n) is -5034.\n\nTherefore, going back to the original sum S:\n\nS = [sum_{n=1}^{1000} Term(n)] + T(0) = (-5034) + 4050 = -5034 + 4050.\n\nCompute that:\n\n-5034 + 4050 = -(5034 - 4050) = -984.\n\nWait, 5034 - 4050: 5034 - 4000 = 1034, then subtract 50 more: 1034 - 50 = 984. So yes, so -5034 + 4050 = -984.\n\nTherefore, the total sum S = -984?\n\nWait, but that seems surprisingly simple. Let me confirm once more.\n\nWait, let me recapitulate:\n\nTotal sum S = [sum_{j=-1000}^{1000} T(j)] = T(0) + sum_{j=1}^{1000} [T(j) + T(-j)] = 4050 + [sum_{n=1}^{1000} Term(n)] = 4050 + (-5034) = -984.\n\nHmm. But is this possible? Let me see with smaller N to see if the pattern holds.\n\nWait, let's take N=1 (i.e., sum from j=-1 to 1). Let's compute manually:\n\nCompute S for N=1 (i.e., j from -1 to 1):\n\nT(-1) + T(0) + T(1) = (-4050) + 4050 + 1350 = (-4050 + 4050) + 1350 = 0 + 1350 = 1350.\n\nUsing the formula:\n\nFor N=1, the total sum would be:\n\nsum_{j=-1}^1 T(j) = T(0) + sum_{n=1}^1 [T(n) + T(-n)] = 4050 + Term(1) = 4050 + (-2700) = 1350, which matches.\n\nUsing the formula we derived for general N:\n\nFor N=1, S = [sum_{n=1}^1 Term(n)] + T(0) = (-2700) + 4050 = 1350. Correct.\n\nSimilarly, for N=2:\n\nsum_{j=-2}^2 T(j) = [T(-2) + T(-1) + T(0) + T(1) + T(2)]\n\nCompute manually:\n\nT(-2) = floor(2025/(-2 + 0.5)) = floor(2025 / (-1.5)) = floor(-1350) = -1350,\n\nT(-1) = -4050,\n\nT(0) = 4050,\n\nT(1) = 1350,\n\nT(2) = 810,\n\nSo total sum: (-1350) + (-4050) + 4050 + 1350 + 810 =\n\nCompute step by step:\n\nStart with -1350 -4050 = -5400,\n\n-5400 + 4050 = -1350,\n\n-1350 + 1350 = 0,\n\n0 + 810 = 810.\n\nUsing the formula:\n\nsum_{j=-2}^2 T(j) = [sum_{n=1}^2 Term(n)] + T(0) = (-540 - 2700) + 4050? Wait, no.\n\nWait, for N=2, the formula gives:\n\nsum_{j=-2}^2 T(j) = [sum_{n=1}^2 Term(n)] + T(0).\n\nWe already calculated sum_{n=1}^2 Term(n) = -3240 (from earlier N=2 test). So then,\n\nsum = -3240 + 4050 = 810, which matches the manual calculation. So that works.\n\nTherefore, the formula seems consistent.\n\nThus, for N=1000, the total sum S = -984.\n\nBut wait, that seems low? Let me see if there's a miscalculation in the steps.\n\nWait, let's recapitulate the key steps:\n\nWe had:\n\nsum_{n=1}^{1000} Term(n) = floor(2025/(1000.5)) + (K - (1000 -1)) - ceil(2025/0.5)\n\n= floor(2025/1000.5) + (13 - 999) - 4050\n\n= 2 + (-986) - 4050\n\n= (2 - 986) = -984; then -984 - 4050 = -5034.\n\nThen adding T(0)=4050,\n\nTotal sum S = -5034 + 4050 = -984.\n\nHmm. Alternatively, let me think about the symmetry again.\n\nWait, for each pair j and -j (for j from 1 to 1000), their combined contribution is T(j) + T(-j) = Term(j). And we found that the sum over all Term(j) from j=1 to 1000 is -5034, then adding T(0)=4050 gives -984.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's an error in the calculation of K?\n\nWait, let me double-check K.\n\nWe had K = number of odd divisors of 4050 in [3, 1999].\n\nWe listed all odd divisors of 4050 except 1 and 2025, and got 13 divisors. Let me list them again:\n\n3, 5, 9, 15, 25, 27, 45, 75, 81, 135, 225, 405, 675.\n\nThat's 13 divisors, all between 3 and 1999. So K=13 is correct.\n\nWait, but let me confirm each of these divisors actually corresponds to an m in [1, 999].\n\nRecall that each such divisor d corresponds to m such that 2m + 1 = d, so m = (d - 1)/2.\n\nSince d must be an odd divisor of 4050 between 3 and 1999, then m = (d - 1)/2 must be an integer between 1 and 999.\n\nIndeed, for each d in our list:\n\nTake d=3: m=(3-1)/2=1, which is in [1,999].\n\nd=5: m=(5-1)/2=2, good.\n\nd=9: m=(9-1)/2=4,\n\nd=15: m=(15-1)/2=7,\n\nd=25: m=(25-1)/2=12,\n\nd=27: m=(27-1)/2=13,\n\nd=45: m=(45-1)/2=22,\n\nd=75: m=(75-1)/2=37,\n\nd=81: m=(81-1)/2=40,\n\nd=135: m=(135-1)/2=67,\n\nd=225: m=(225-1)/2=112,\n\nd=405: m=(405-1)/2=202,\n\nd=675: m=(675-1)/2=337.\n\nAll these m values are integers between 1 and 337, which are all within 1 to 999. So each of the 13 divisors corresponds to a valid m in [1, 999], so K=13 is correct.\n\nTherefore, the calculation of K is correct.\n\nTherefore, the steps are correct, so the total sum is indeed -984.\n\nWait, but let me do a sanity check with another value of N where I can compute manually.\n\nTake N=3, as before:\n\nsum_{j=-3}^3 T(j).\n\nCompute manually:\n\nCompute T(-3) = floor(2025 / (-3 + 0.5)) = floor(2025 / (-2.5)) = floor(-810) = -810,\n\nT(-2) = -1350,\n\nT(-1) = -4050,\n\nT(0) = 4050,\n\nT(1) = 1350,\n\nT(2) = 810,\n\nT(3) = 578.\n\nTherefore, sum all these:\n\nStart adding from the negatives:\n\n-810 + (-1350) + (-4050) = -810 -1350 = -2160; -2160 -4050 = -6210,\n\nThen add positives:\n\n+4050 + 1350 + 810 + 578.\n\nCompute positives:\n\n4050 + 1350 = 5400,\n\n5400 + 810 = 6210,\n\n6210 + 578 = 6788.\n\nTotal sum: (-6210) + 6788 = 578.\n\nUsing the formula:\n\nsum_{j=-3}^3 T(j) = [sum_{n=1}^3 Term(n)] + T(0).\n\nFirst, compute sum_{n=1}^3 Term(n):\n\nTerm(1) = -2700,\n\nTerm(2) = -540,\n\nTerm(3) = -232,\n\nso sum = -2700 -540 -232 = -3472.\n\nThen add T(0)=4050: -3472 + 4050 = 578. Which matches the manual calculation. So the formula works.\n\nTherefore, for N=1000, the answer is indeed -984?\n\nWait, but the problem is asking for the sum from j=-1000 to 1000 of floor(2025/(j + 0.5)). If the answer is negative, that seems possible because the negative terms dominate.\n\nWait, but let me check with N=4 to see:\n\nWait, let's do N=4 manually.\n\nCompute sum_{j=-4}^4 T(j).\n\nFirst, compute T(-4) = floor(2025/(-4 + 0.5)) = floor(2025/(-3.5)) = floor(-578.571...) = -579,\n\nT(-3) = -810,\n\nT(-2) = -1350,\n\nT(-1) = -4050,\n\nT(0)=4050,\n\nT(1)=1350,\n\nT(2)=810,\n\nT(3)=578,\n\nT(4)=floor(2025/4.5)=450.\n\nSo adding all these:\n\nStart with negatives:\n\n-579 -810 -1350 -4050 = let's compute step by step:\n\n-579 -810 = -1389,\n\n-1389 -1350 = -2739,\n\n-2739 -4050 = -6789.\n\nPositives:\n\n4050 + 1350 + 810 + 578 + 450.\n\nCompute step by step:\n\n4050 + 1350 = 5400,\n\n5400 + 810 = 6210,\n\n6210 + 578 = 6788,\n\n6788 + 450 = 7238.\n\nTotal sum: -6789 + 7238 = 449.\n\nUsing the formula:\n\nsum_{n=1}^4 Term(n) = ?\n\nFirst, compute K for N=4.\n\nK is the number of odd divisors of 4050 in [3, 3*2 +1 = 7? Wait no, for N=4, m ranges from 1 to N-1=3.\n\nWait, for N=4, the formula would be:\n\nsum_{n=1}^4 Term(n) = floor(2025/(4 + 0.5)) + (K - 3) - ceil(2025/0.5),\n\nwhere K is the number of m from 1 to 3 where 2025/(m + 0.5) is integer.\n\nCompute K:\n\nFor m=1: 2025/1.5 = 1350 integer,\n\nm=2: 2025/2.5 = 810 integer,\n\nm=3: 2025/3.5 ≈ 578.571 not integer.\n\nThus, K=2.\n\nTherefore,\n\nfloor(2025/4.5) = floor(450) = 450,\n\n(K - 3) = (2 - 3) = -1,\n\nceil(2025/0.5)=4050,\n\nso total sum_{n=1}^4 Term(n) = 450 + (-1) - 4050 = 449 - 4050 = -3601.\n\nThen total S = sum_{j=-4}^4 T(j) = (-3601) + 4050 = 449, which matches manual computation.\n\nTherefore, the formula is consistent. Thus, with confidence, for N=1000, the sum is -984.\n\nBut just to be thorough, let me check one more thing.\n\nThe final result is negative? That seems surprising because the positive terms (for positive j) are being outweighed by the negative terms (for negative j). Let me see:\n\nFor each positive j from 1 to 1000, the term T(j) is positive, and T(-j) is negative. For each j, T(j) + T(-j) is negative, as seen in the examples. Since there are 1000 such pairs, each contributing a negative amount, plus the central term T(0)=4050. So if the total of the pairs is -5034, then adding 4050 gives -984. So indeed, the negative contributions outweigh the positive.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is designed such that the answer is negative? The problem didn't specify, so maybe it is.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a miscalculation in the arithmetic?\n\nLet me recompute the key steps:\n\nsum_{n=1}^{1000} Term(n) = floor(2025/1000.5) + (K - (N - 1)) - ceil(2025/0.5)\n\nPlugging in numbers:\n\nfloor(2025 / 1000.5):\n\n2025 divided by 1000.5.\n\n1000.5 × 2 = 2001,\n\nso 2025 - 2001 = 24,\n\nso 2025 / 1000.5 = 2 + 24/1000.5 ≈ 2 + 0.0239 ≈ 2.0239,\n\nso floor is 2. Correct.\n\nK = 13,\n\nN - 1 = 999,\n\nso (K - (N - 1)) = 13 - 999 = -986,\n\nceil(2025/0.5) = ceil(4050) = 4050,\n\nso total sum_{n=1}^{1000} Term(n) = 2 + (-986) - 4050 = 2 - 986 - 4050.\n\nCompute 2 - 986 = -984,\n\nthen -984 - 4050 = - (984 + 4050) = -5034. Correct.\n\nThen S = -5034 + 4050 = -5034 + 4050.\n\nCompute 4050 - 5034:\n\n5034 - 4050 = 984,\n\nso 4050 - 5034 = -984. Correct.\n\nTherefore, the arithmetic is correct. Therefore, the answer should be \\boxed{-984}.\n\nBut wait, before I settle on this, let me think again: is there a possibility that when we considered K, the number of divisors, we missed something?\n\nWait, recall that K counts the number of m in [1, 999] such that 2025/(m + 0.5) is integer. Which translates to 2m + 1 divides 4050, as we did. And we found 13 such m's. Each such m gives a term where Term(n) has a 0 contribution instead of -1. So the formula accounts for that by adding back K - (N - 1). Since each non-integer term contributes -1, and there are (N - 1 - K) non-integer terms, so total contribution from those is -(N -1 - K) = K - (N -1). Then adding the floor(a) and subtracting the ceil(b), etc.\n\nWait, but perhaps I need to think of the entire expression again:\n\nWait, in the derivation, we had:\n\nsum_{n=1}^N [A(n) - B(n)] = [sum A(n)] - [sum B(n)]\n\nThen, we expressed sum B(n) as sum_{m=0}^{N-1} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5)),\n\nso the difference becomes [sum_{n=1}^N A(n)] - [sum_{m=0}^{N-1} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5))]\n\n= [sum_{n=1}^N floor(2025/(n + 0.5))] - [ceil(2025/0.5) + sum_{m=1}^{N - 1} ceil(2025/(m + 0.5))]\n\n= floor(2025/(N + 0.5)) + sum_{m=1}^{N - 1} [floor(2025/(m + 0.5)) - ceil(2025/(m + 0.5))] - ceil(2025/0.5)\n\nThen, as before, the sum over m=1 to N-1 of [floor(x) - ceil(x)] is equal to - (number of non-integer x terms), which is - ( (N - 1) - K ), so substituting,\n\n= floor(2025/(N + 0.5)) - ( (N - 1) - K ) - ceil(2025/0.5)\n\n= floor(2025/(N + 0.5)) + (K - (N - 1)) - ceil(2025/0.5)\n\nYes, that's exactly what we had. So the derivation is correct.\n\nTherefore, with all steps verified, I think the answer is indeed -984.\n\nBut just to be absolutely sure, let me consider that maybe I miscounted the number of odd divisors.\n\nLet me list all the odd divisors of 4050 again:\n\nPrime factorization of 4050 is 2 × 3⁴ × 5². The odd part is 3⁴ × 5².\n\nThe exponents for 3: 0,1,2,3,4\n\nExponents for 5: 0,1,2\n\nTherefore, the odd divisors are:\n\nFor 3^0:\n\n5^0: 1\n\n5^1: 5\n\n5^2: 25\n\nFor 3^1:\n\n5^0: 3\n\n5^1: 15\n\n5^2: 75\n\nFor 3^2:\n\n5^0: 9\n\n5^1: 45\n\n5^2: 225\n\nFor 3^3:\n\n5^0: 27\n\n5^1: 135\n\n5^2: 675\n\nFor 3^4:\n\n5^0: 81\n\n5^1: 405\n\n5^2: 2025\n\nSo that's 15 divisors. Removing 1 and 2025, we have 13 divisors left. Correct.\n\nEach of these 13 divisors corresponds to an m between 1 and 999, so K=13 is correct.\n\nTherefore, I think the answer is indeed -984.\n\nWait, but the problem is from j = -1000 to 1000, so 2001 terms. The central term is 4050, and the rest 2000 terms are paired into 1000 pairs each contributing a negative number. The total negative contribution from the pairs is -5034, so adding 4050 gives -984. Yes, that seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the question expects a positive answer? Maybe I have an off-by-one error?\n\nWait, let me check the value of K again. Wait, when m ranges from 1 to 999, then 2m + 1 ranges from 3 to 1999, as I had before. Each divisor d of 4050 in that interval corresponds to exactly one m. Since we listed all odd divisors of 4050 between 3 and 1999, which are 13, so K=13 is correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should have included d=1? But d=1 would correspond to m=(1-1)/2=0, but m starts at 1, so m=0 is not included. So no, d=1 is excluded.\n\nAlternatively, d=2025 would correspond to m=(2025 -1)/2 = 2024/2 = 1012, but m=1012 is beyond our upper limit of m=999, so indeed excluded.\n\nTherefore, all steps are correct.\n\nThus, I will conclude that the answer is \\boxed{-984}.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{-984}\n</think>\n\nTo compute the sum \\(\\sum_{j=-1000}^{1000} \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2025}{j + 0.5} \\right\\rfloor\\), we consider the symmetry of the terms and break the sum into parts. \n\nFirst, we note that the term at \\(j = 0\\) is \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{2025}{0.5} \\right\\rfloor = 4050\\).\n\nFor the terms \\(j\\) and \\(-j\\) (where \\(1 \\leq j \\leq 1000\\)), we use the relationship:\n\\[\nT(-j) = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2025}{-j + 0.5} \\right\\rfloor = -\\left\\lceil \\frac{2025}{j - 0.5} \\right\\rceil\n\\]\nThus, the sum of the pair \\(T(j) + T(-j)\\) is:\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{2025}{j + 0.5} \\right\\rfloor - \\left\\lceil \\frac{2025}{j - 0.5} \\right\\rceil\n\\]\n\nWe denote this as \\(\\text{Term}(n)\\) for \\(n = j\\). The total sum can be written as:\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=-1000}^{1000} \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2025}{j + 0.5} \\right\\rfloor = 4050 + \\sum_{n=1}^{1000} \\text{Term}(n)\n\\]\n\nTo compute \\(\\sum_{n=1}^{1000} \\text{Term}(n)\\), we analyze the expression:\n\\[\n\\text{Term}(n) = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2025}{n + 0.5} \\right\\rfloor - \\left\\lceil \\frac{2025}{n - 0.5} \\right\\rceil\n\\]\n\nWe find that the sum of these terms can be simplified using properties of floor and ceiling functions and the divisors of 4050. The key steps involve:\n1. Recognizing that \\(\\frac{2025}{n + 0.5}\\) must be an integer for certain \\(n\\).\n2. Counting the number of such \\(n\\) (denoted \\(K\\)), which are the divisors of 4050 in the range [3, 1999], resulting in \\(K = 13\\).\n3. Using these counts to compute the sum through telescoping series and divisor analysis.\n\nAfter detailed calculations, we find:\n\\[\n\\sum_{n=1}^{1000} \\text{Term}(n) = -5034\n\\]\n\nAdding the central term \\(4050\\), the final result is:\n\\[\n4050 + (-5034) = -984\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-984}\n\\]","correct":true,"formatted":true},{"dataset_tag":"hmmt25","dataset_path":"/gpfs/share/home/2501210611/opsd_analysis/OPSD_Analysis/data/HMMT25/test.parquet","category":"","problem_id":"5","problem":"Let $\\mathcal{S}$ be the set of all nonconstant monic polynomials $P$ with integer coefficients satisfying $P(\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{2})=$ $P(\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{2})$. If $Q$ is an element of $\\mathcal{S}$ with minimal degree, compute the only possible value of $Q(10)-Q(0)$.","ground_truth":"890","gen_n":8,"pass_at_k":{"1":true,"4":true,"8":true},"generations":[{"predicted_answer":"890","full_generation":"Okay, so I need to solve this problem about polynomials. Let me read it again and make sure I understand what it's asking.\n\nWe have a set 𝒮, which consists of all nonconstant monic polynomials P with integer coefficients such that P(√3 + √2) = P(√3 - √2). Then, Q is an element of 𝒮 with minimal degree, and we need to compute the only possible value of Q(10) - Q(0).\n\nHmm, okay. So first, let me parse the problem.\n\nWe are dealing with monic polynomials, so polynomials where the leading coefficient is 1. They must have integer coefficients. They are nonconstant, so degree at least 1. The key condition here is that when we evaluate the polynomial at √3 + √2, it gives the same result as when we evaluate it at √3 - √2. And we have to find the polynomial of minimal degree in this set 𝒮 and then compute Q(10) - Q(0).\n\nAlright, so first step: understand what the condition P(√3 + √2) = P(√3 - √2) tells us about the polynomial P.\n\nLet me denote α = √3 + √2 and β = √3 - √2. So the condition is that P(α) = P(β). Since α and β are conjugates in some sense, maybe? Let me see what α and β are numerically.\n\nFirst, compute α ≈ √3 ≈ 1.732, √2 ≈ 1.414, so α ≈ 1.732 + 1.414 ≈ 3.146. Similarly, β ≈ 1.732 - 1.414 ≈ 0.318. So they are distinct real numbers, but they are conjugates in the field extension Q(√2, √3) over Q, perhaps?\n\nWait, let me think about the field extensions here. The number α = √3 + √2 is an element of the field Q(√2, √3), which is a degree 4 extension over Q because [Q(√2, √3) : Q] = [Q(√2, √3) : Q(√2)] [Q(√2) : Q] = 2 * 2 = 4. Similarly, β = √3 - √2 is also in Q(√2, √3). Moreover, since √3 + √2 and √3 - √2 are both in this field, they satisfy certain minimal polynomial equations over Q.\n\nLet me recall that if we have an element like α = √a + √b, then its minimal polynomial over Q can be found by considering the conjugates. In this case, α = √3 + √2, so its conjugates would be √3 + √2, √3 - √2, -√3 + √2, and -√3 - √2. Wait, but actually, the minimal polynomial would have roots that are all the conjugates under the Galois group of the field extension.\n\nSince Q(√2, √3) is a Galois extension over Q with Galois group isomorphic to the Klein four-group, right? Because the Galois group of Q(√2, √3)/Q is isomorphic to C2 × C2, where the elements are determined by sending √2 to ±√2 and √3 to ±√3, independently. So the four automorphisms are:\n\n1. The identity: σ1(√2) = √2, σ1(√3) = √3,\n\n2. σ2: σ2(√2) = -√2, σ2(√3) = √3,\n\n3. σ3: σ3(√2) = √2, σ3(√3) = -√3,\n\n4. σ4: σ4(√2) = -√2, σ4(√3) = -√3.\n\nTherefore, the conjugates of α = √3 + √2 under this Galois group would be:\n\nσ1(α) = √3 + √2 = α,\n\nσ2(α) = -√3 + √2,\n\nσ3(α) = √3 - √2 = β,\n\nσ4(α) = -√3 - √2.\n\nTherefore, the minimal polynomial of α over Q is the product (x - α)(x - σ2(α))(x - σ3(α))(x - σ4(α)), which would be a degree 4 polynomial with integer coefficients. Let me compute that minimal polynomial.\n\nLet me let x = √3 + √2. Then, let me compute x^2: (√3 + √2)^2 = (√3)^2 + 2*√3*√2 + (√2)^2 = 3 + 2√6 + 2 = 5 + 2√6.\n\nThen x^2 - 5 = 2√6. Then square both sides: (x^2 - 5)^2 = (2√6)^2 = 4*6 = 24. So (x^2 - 5)^2 - 24 = 0.\n\nExpanding that: (x^2)^2 - 10x^2 + 25 - 24 = x^4 - 10x^2 + 1 = 0.\n\nTherefore, the minimal polynomial of α over Q is x^4 - 10x^2 + 1. Let me check that.\n\nLet me verify by plugging in α = √3 + √2 into this polynomial:\n\n( (√3 + √2)^4 ) - 10*( (√3 + √2)^2 ) + 1.\n\nWe already know that (√3 + √2)^2 = 5 + 2√6,\n\nso (√3 + √2)^4 = (5 + 2√6)^2 = 25 + 20√6 + (2√6)^2 = 25 + 20√6 + 4*6 = 25 + 20√6 + 24 = 49 + 20√6.\n\nThen, subtract 10*(5 + 2√6) = 50 + 20√6,\n\nSo, (49 + 20√6) - (50 + 20√6) + 1 = (49 - 50 + 1) + (20√6 - 20√6) = 0 + 0 = 0. Perfect, so indeed the minimal polynomial is x^4 - 10x^2 + 1.\n\nTherefore, α satisfies x^4 - 10x^2 + 1 = 0, and so does β = √3 - √2, since β is another conjugate of α. Similarly, the other conjugates also satisfy this equation.\n\nSo, in summary, the minimal polynomial of α over Q is f(x) = x^4 - 10x^2 + 1, which is a quartic (degree 4) monic polynomial with integer coefficients.\n\nNow, back to the problem. We have a polynomial P with integer coefficients, monic, nonconstant, such that P(α) = P(β). Since β is another conjugate of α, this condition is saying that P evaluated at α equals P evaluated at β. Since α and β are roots of the minimal polynomial f(x) above, perhaps this condition imposes that P commutes with the Galois group action? Or something like that?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps P must take the same value on α and β, so that P(α) - P(β) = 0. Since α and β are conjugates, maybe P must be invariant under the automorphism that swaps α and β? Wait, but actually, the Galois group acts on the roots, so perhaps P must be fixed by some subgroup?\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can think of it this way: since α and β are conjugates, if P has rational coefficients (which it does, being integer coefficients), then P(α) and P(β) are conjugates in some sense. But here, the problem states that they are equal. So, if P has rational coefficients, then P(α) and P(β) are conjugates over Q. But the problem says they are equal. So, in order for P(α) and P(β) to be equal, they must be fixed by the Galois group, or at least, the automorphism that sends α to β must fix P(α).\n\nWait, let me think. Let me consider the automorphism σ that swaps √2 and -√2, but keeps √3 fixed. Wait, actually, which automorphism sends α to β?\n\nRecall that α = √3 + √2, β = √3 - √2. So, if we have an automorphism that sends √2 to -√2, and leaves √3 fixed, then σ(α) = σ(√3 + √2) = √3 + (-√2) = √3 - √2 = β. Similarly, σ(β) = σ(√3 - √2) = √3 - (-√2) = √3 + √2 = α. So this automorphism σ swaps α and β.\n\nTherefore, if we apply σ to P(α), since P has rational coefficients, then σ(P(α)) = P(σ(α)) = P(β). But the problem states that P(α) = P(β), so σ(P(α)) = P(α). Therefore, σ fixes P(α). Since σ is an automorphism of the field Q(α), then for P(α) to be fixed by σ, it must lie in the fixed field of σ.\n\nWhat is the fixed field of σ? Since σ is an automorphism of the Galois group of Q(√2, √3)/Q, which is abelian (since it's a product of cyclic groups), and σ is an element of order 2 (since applying it twice gives the identity: σ^2(√2) = -(-√2) = √2, same with √3). The fixed field of σ is the set of elements x in Q(√2, √3) such that σ(x) = x.\n\nWhat elements are fixed by σ? Let's see, σ sends √2 to -√2 and √3 to √3. So, an element x in Q(√2, √3) is fixed by σ if x = a + b√2 + c√3 + d√6, then σ(x) = a - b√2 + c√3 - d√6. For this to equal x, we must have:\n\na - b√2 + c√3 - d√6 = a + b√2 + c√3 + d√6,\n\nwhich implies that -b√2 - d√6 = b√2 + d√6, so that:\n\n(-b - b)√2 + (-d - d)√6 = 0,\n\ni.e., -2b√2 - 2d√6 = 0,\n\nso, -2b√2 = 2d√6,\n\ndivide both sides by 2:\n\n- b√2 = d√6,\n\nthen square both sides:\n\nb² * 2 = d² * 6,\n\nso 2b² = 6d² ⇒ b² = 3d².\n\nSince we are working over Q, which has characteristic 0, this implies that b and d must be zero? Wait, because if b and d are rational numbers, then b² = 3d² implies that b/d = ±√3, but √3 is irrational, so unless b = d = 0, this can't hold. Therefore, the fixed field of σ consists of elements where the coefficients of √2 and √6 are zero. Therefore, fixed field is Q(√3). Because if you have an element in Q(√2, √3) that is fixed by σ, then it must have no √2 or √6 terms, so it must be of the form a + b√3, where a and b are in Q.\n\nTherefore, the fixed field of σ is Q(√3). Therefore, P(α) must lie in Q(√3). But since P has integer coefficients, then P(α) is an algebraic number, but specifically, since α is in Q(√2, √3), then P(α) is also in Q(√2, √3). However, for P(α) to be fixed by σ, it must lie in the fixed field Q(√3). Therefore, P(α) ∈ Q(√3).\n\nSimilarly, since P(β) = P(α), then P(β) is also in Q(√3). But β is in Q(√2, √3), so P(β) is also in Q(√2, √3), and being in Q(√3) is okay.\n\nSo, the condition P(α) = P(β) is equivalent to P(α) being fixed by the automorphism σ which swaps α and β, hence P(α) must lie in the fixed field Q(√3). Therefore, this gives us a condition on the polynomial P.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think of this condition in terms of the polynomial P. Since α and β are roots of the minimal polynomial f(x) = x^4 - 10x^2 + 1, then perhaps P must satisfy some divisibility condition with f(x)?\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can consider the polynomial P(x) - P(β), which has a root at x = α. But since β is another root, perhaps?\n\nWait, actually, since P(α) = P(β), then (x - α) and (x - β) might both divide P(x) - P(β), but since P(β) is a constant, so P(x) - P(β) has roots at α and β. But since the minimal polynomial of α is f(x) = x^4 - 10x^2 + 1, which has roots α, β, -α, -β. Wait, actually, let me confirm the roots of f(x):\n\nf(x) = x^4 - 10x^2 + 1. Let me set y = x^2, so f(x) becomes y^2 - 10y + 1 = 0, so y = [10 ± √(100 - 4)] / 2 = [10 ± √96]/2 = [10 ± 4√6]/2 = 5 ± 2√6. Therefore, the roots of f(x) are x = ±√(5 + 2√6) and x = ±√(5 - 2√6). Wait, but earlier I thought that α = √3 + √2 and β = √3 - √2. Let me see if √3 + √2 squared is 5 + 2√6?\n\nWait, (√3 + √2)^2 = 3 + 2√6 + 2 = 5 + 2√6. Yes, exactly. Therefore, √(5 + 2√6) is equal to √3 + √2, because (√3 + √2)^2 = 5 + 2√6, so √(5 + 2√6) = √3 + √2. Similarly, √(5 - 2√6) would be √3 - √2, because (√3 - √2)^2 = 3 - 2√6 + 2 = 5 - 2√6. Therefore, the roots of f(x) are indeed ±(√3 + √2) and ±(√3 - √2). Therefore, the roots are α, -α, β, -β where α = √3 + √2 and β = √3 - √2. Therefore, the minimal polynomial f(x) has roots α, -α, β, -β.\n\nTherefore, if P(α) = P(β), then P(α) - P(β) = 0. Since α and β are distinct roots of f(x), perhaps this implies that P(x) - P(β) is divisible by some factor related to f(x)?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model this condition as follows: since α and β are conjugate roots, and P takes the same value at both, then P must be constant on the orbit of α under the Galois group? Wait, but the orbit of α under the Galois group is {α, β, -α, -β}, right? Since applying the automorphisms can send α to β, to -α, etc.\n\nBut if P takes the same value on α and β, but maybe not necessarily on -α and -β? Hmm, the problem only requires that P(α) = P(β), not necessarily P(-α) = P(-β) or anything else. So perhaps the condition is weaker.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can think of P(x) as a polynomial that, when restricted to the field Q(√2, √3), is constant on the orbit of α under the subgroup that fixes β? Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think of the condition P(α) = P(β) as saying that P is invariant under the automorphism σ that swaps α and β. Wait, but actually, if we have an automorphism that sends α to β, then for P to satisfy P(α) = P(β), then applying that automorphism to P(α) gives P(β), so that would be consistent. But invariance would require that P(σ(x)) = P(x) for all x, but here we only require it for x = α. Hmm, maybe not exactly.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider that since σ(α) = β and σ(P(α)) = P(σ(α)) = P(β). Since we require that P(α) = P(β), then σ(P(α)) = P(α). Therefore, σ fixes P(α). So, as we thought before, P(α) must lie in the fixed field of σ, which is Q(√3). Since P has integer coefficients, P(α) is an algebraic integer in Q(√3). But perhaps this gives us a way to express P(x) in terms of polynomials that are invariant under the action of σ?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model this by considering that since α and β are roots of f(x), and we want P(α) = P(β), then P must satisfy P(α) - P(β) = 0. Since α and β are roots of f(x), maybe we can write P(x) as some polynomial that is symmetric in some way with respect to α and β?\n\nAlternatively, maybe construct a polynomial that has α and β as roots of some equation? Wait, but they are not roots of the same minimal polynomial in the sense of being roots of P(x), unless P(x) is a multiple of f(x), but P(x) is monic with integer coefficients, so if we take P(x) = f(x), then certainly P(α) = 0 and P(β) = 0, so P(α) = P(β) = 0. But f(x) is degree 4, but maybe there are lower degree polynomials?\n\nWait, but the problem says \"nonconstant monic polynomials with integer coefficients\", so maybe there are lower degree polynomials than 4 that satisfy the condition?\n\nThe question is asking for the minimal degree Q in 𝒮, so we need to find the smallest possible degree of such a polynomial.\n\nSo perhaps the minimal degree is 2? Let me see.\n\nSuppose that there exists a linear polynomial P(x) = x + c, but since it's monic, and nonconstant, so degree 1. Let's see if such a polynomial can satisfy P(α) = P(β).\n\nSo, P(α) = α + c, P(β) = β + c. So setting α + c = β + c ⇒ α = β. But α ≈ 3.146 and β ≈ 0.318, so they are not equal. Therefore, no linear polynomial can satisfy the condition. So degree 1 is impossible.\n\nHow about degree 2? Let me try to see if there exists a quadratic monic polynomial with integer coefficients such that P(α) = P(β).\n\nLet P(x) = x^2 + a x + b, with a, b integers. Then we require that P(α) = P(β).\n\nCompute P(α) - P(β) = (α^2 + a α + b) - (β^2 + a β + b) = α^2 - β^2 + a(α - β).\n\nFactor this as (α - β)(α + β) + a(α - β) = (α - β)(α + β + a).\n\nSet this equal to zero: (α - β)(α + β + a) = 0.\n\nSince α ≠ β, we must have α + β + a = 0.\n\nTherefore, a = - (α + β).\n\nBut α + β = (√3 + √2) + (√3 - √2) = 2√3. So a = -2√3. But a must be an integer. However, -2√3 is irrational, not an integer. Therefore, there is no such integer a. Therefore, no quadratic polynomial satisfies the condition.\n\nHmm, okay, so quadratic is impossible. Then moving on to degree 3.\n\nLet me try cubic. Let P(x) = x^3 + a x^2 + b x + c, with a, b, c integers. Then we require P(α) = P(β). Let me compute P(α) - P(β):\n\n= [α^3 + a α^2 + b α + c] - [β^3 + a β^2 + b β + c]\n\n= α^3 - β^3 + a(α^2 - β^2) + b(α - β)\n\nFactor terms:\n\nFirst, α^3 - β^3 = (α - β)(α^2 + α β + β^2)\n\nThen, a(α^2 - β^2) = a(α - β)(α + β)\n\nThen, b(α - β) = b(α - β)\n\nTherefore, overall:\n\nP(α) - P(β) = (α - β)[α^2 + α β + β^2 + a(α + β) + b]\n\nSet this equal to zero. Since α ≠ β, we can divide both sides by (α - β), so we have:\n\nα^2 + α β + β^2 + a(α + β) + b = 0.\n\nSo, we need to compute α^2 + α β + β^2 and α + β in terms that can be expressed with integers, perhaps?\n\nLet me compute α + β and α β first.\n\nWe know that α = √3 + √2, β = √3 - √2.\n\nTherefore:\n\nα + β = (√3 + √2) + (√3 - √2) = 2√3.\n\nα β = (√3 + √2)(√3 - √2) = (√3)^2 - (√2)^2 = 3 - 2 = 1.\n\nThat's nice, α β = 1.\n\nNow, let's compute α^2 + α β + β^2.\n\nFirst, note that α^2 + β^2 = (α + β)^2 - 2 α β.\n\nSo, α^2 + β^2 = (2√3)^2 - 2*(1) = 12 - 2 = 10.\n\nTherefore, α^2 + α β + β^2 = (α^2 + β^2) + α β = 10 + 1 = 11.\n\nSo, the expression becomes:\n\n11 + a*(2√3) + b = 0.\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n11 + 2√3 * a + b = 0.\n\nBut a and b are integers, and √3 is irrational. The only way this equation can hold is if the coefficient of √3 is zero and the constant term is zero. That is:\n\n2√3 * a = 0 ⇒ a = 0,\n\nand\n\n11 + b = 0 ⇒ b = -11.\n\nTherefore, if a = 0 and b = -11, then the equation is satisfied. So, in this case, with a = 0 and b = -11, then the cubic polynomial P(x) = x^3 + 0 x^2 - 11 x + c, where c is an integer. Wait, but in the cubic polynomial, the constant term is c, but in our equation above, the constant term canceled out. Let me check again.\n\nWait, let me recap. The expression we had was:\n\nα^2 + α β + β^2 + a(α + β) + b = 0,\n\nwhich simplified to 11 + a*(2√3) + b = 0.\n\nTherefore, the equation only constrains a and b, and the constant term c in the polynomial P(x) is free? Wait, actually, in the computation of P(α) - P(β), the constants c canceled out, so indeed c can be any integer. However, the problem states that the polynomial must be monic with integer coefficients and nonconstant. So, if we set a = 0, b = -11, then P(x) = x^3 - 11x + c, where c is any integer.\n\nHowever, we need P to be in 𝒮, which requires that P(α) = P(β). But in our derivation above, we already enforced that P(α) - P(β) = 0 by choosing a and b appropriately, regardless of c. Wait, but actually, when we computed P(α) - P(β), the constants c canceled, so indeed, for any c, the difference P(α) - P(β) is equal to that expression which we set to zero by choosing a and b. Therefore, as long as a = 0 and b = -11, then for any integer c, the polynomial P(x) = x^3 - 11x + c will satisfy P(α) = P(β). Therefore, such polynomials exist of degree 3. So, degree 3 is possible.\n\nBut the question is asking for the minimal degree. Since degree 1 and 2 are impossible, then the minimal degree would be 3? Wait, but hold on. Wait, is there a quadratic polynomial? Wait, we saw that quadratic requires a non-integer coefficient. So quadratic is impossible. Cubic is possible. So the minimal degree is 3?\n\nWait, but hold on a second. The problem says \"nonconstant monic polynomials with integer coefficients\". So, for example, take c = 0 for simplicity. Let me check with c = 0, so P(x) = x^3 - 11x. Let me compute P(α) and P(β).\n\nFirst, compute P(α) = α^3 - 11 α,\n\nand P(β) = β^3 - 11 β.\n\nWe need to check whether these are equal.\n\nLet me compute α^3 and β^3.\n\nFirst, recall that α = √3 + √2, so let me compute α^3:\n\nα^3 = (√3 + √2)^3.\n\nUsing the binomial theorem:\n\n( a + b )^3 = a^3 + 3a^2 b + 3a b^2 + b^3,\n\nso:\n\nα^3 = (√3)^3 + 3(√3)^2 (√2) + 3(√3)(√2)^2 + (√2)^3\n\nCompute each term:\n\n(√3)^3 = (√3)*(√3)^2 = √3 * 3 = 3√3,\n\n3(√3)^2 (√2) = 3*(3)*(√2) = 9√2,\n\n3(√3)(√2)^2 = 3*(√3)*(2) = 6√3,\n\n(√2)^3 = (√2)*(√2)^2 = √2 * 2 = 2√2.\n\nTherefore, adding all terms together:\n\n3√3 + 9√2 + 6√3 + 2√2 = (3√3 + 6√3) + (9√2 + 2√2) = 9√3 + 11√2.\n\nSo, α^3 = 9√3 + 11√2.\n\nSimilarly, compute β^3. Since β = √3 - √2,\n\nβ^3 = (√3 - √2)^3.\n\nAgain, using the binomial theorem:\n\n= (√3)^3 - 3(√3)^2 (√2) + 3(√3)(√2)^2 - (√2)^3\n\nCompute each term:\n\n(√3)^3 = 3√3,\n\n-3(√3)^2 (√2) = -3*(3)*(√2) = -9√2,\n\n3(√3)(√2)^2 = 3*(√3)*(2) = 6√3,\n\n- (√2)^3 = -2√2.\n\nAdding all terms:\n\n3√3 - 9√2 + 6√3 - 2√2 = (3√3 + 6√3) + (-9√2 - 2√2) = 9√3 - 11√2.\n\nTherefore, β^3 = 9√3 - 11√2.\n\nTherefore, compute P(α) = α^3 - 11 α = (9√3 + 11√2) - 11*(√3 + √2) = 9√3 + 11√2 - 11√3 - 11√2 = (9√3 - 11√3) + (11√2 - 11√2) = (-2√3) + 0 = -2√3.\n\nSimilarly, P(β) = β^3 - 11 β = (9√3 - 11√2) - 11*(√3 - √2) = 9√3 - 11√2 - 11√3 + 11√2 = (9√3 - 11√3) + (-11√2 + 11√2) = (-2√3) + 0 = -2√3.\n\nTherefore, indeed P(α) = P(β) = -2√3. So that works! So the polynomial P(x) = x^3 - 11x satisfies the condition, and it's monic, with integer coefficients, nonconstant, and of degree 3. Therefore, degree 3 is achievable.\n\nBut before we get too excited, the problem says \"the set 𝒮 of all nonconstant monic polynomials P with integer coefficients satisfying P(√3 + √2) = P(√3 - √2)\", and then asks for the element Q of 𝒮 with minimal degree, and compute Q(10) - Q(0).\n\nSo, if degree 3 is possible, then is there a polynomial of degree less than 3? We saw degree 1 and 2 are impossible, so 3 is the minimal degree. Therefore, the minimal degree is 3, and an example is P(x) = x^3 - 11x + c, for any integer c. Wait, but actually in the above, when we set c = 0, we had P(x) = x^3 - 11x, and that worked. But actually, in the earlier analysis, the constant term c could be any integer, because it cancels out in the difference P(α) - P(β). Therefore, actually, all such polynomials of the form x^3 - 11x + c will satisfy P(α) = P(β) for any integer c. Therefore, there are infinitely many such polynomials of degree 3, but they all have the same value of Q(10) - Q(0) minus the constant term? Wait, let me check.\n\nWait, let me compute Q(10) - Q(0) for a general Q(x) = x^3 - 11x + c.\n\nThen Q(10) = 10^3 - 11*10 + c = 1000 - 110 + c = 890 + c,\n\nand Q(0) = 0^3 - 11*0 + c = c,\n\nso Q(10) - Q(0) = (890 + c) - c = 890.\n\nSo, regardless of the constant term c, the difference Q(10) - Q(0) is always 890. Therefore, all such polynomials of degree 3 in 𝒮 will have Q(10) - Q(0) equal to 890. Therefore, the only possible value is 890. But the problem says \"the only possible value of Q(10) - Q(0)\", so that must be 890. But before I conclude, I need to make sure that there isn't a polynomial of lower degree that I missed, or that perhaps degree 3 isn't actually the minimal?\n\nWait, we saw that degree 1 and 2 are impossible, so degree 3 is indeed minimal. However, let me check if there might be another polynomial of degree 3 with a different form that perhaps gives a different Q(10) - Q(0). But according to the above, any monic cubic polynomial with integer coefficients satisfying P(α) = P(β) must be of the form x^3 + a x^2 + b x + c where a = 0, b = -11, and c is any integer, so indeed, all such polynomials are x^3 -11x + c, so their Q(10) - Q(0) is always 890. Therefore, 890 is the only possible value.\n\nWait, but hold on. Wait, in my analysis above for the cubic polynomial, I assumed that the polynomial is monic, so leading coefficient 1, and then we found that a (the coefficient of x^2) must be 0, and b (the coefficient of x) must be -11, and c is free. Therefore, the general form is x^3 - 11x + c. So that seems right.\n\nBut let me see if there are other cubic polynomials not of this form that might satisfy the condition. Suppose, for instance, that we take a cubic polynomial where a is not zero. Then, according to our earlier equation, we had:\n\n11 + 2√3 a + b = 0,\n\nso if a ≠ 0, then 2√3 a must be a rational number (since 11 and b are integers). But 2√3 is irrational, so the only way 2√3 a is rational is if a = 0, because otherwise, you'd have an irrational number times a non-zero integer, which is still irrational, and can't cancel out the 11 and b which are integers. Therefore, indeed, a must be zero, and then b must be -11. Therefore, there are no other cubic polynomials with a non-zero a that satisfy the condition. Therefore, all such polynomials must have a = 0, b = -11, and c arbitrary. Therefore, they are all of the form x^3 - 11x + c. Therefore, their Q(10) - Q(0) is always 890, regardless of c. Therefore, the only possible value is 890.\n\nBut hold on, the problem says \"the only possible value of Q(10) - Q(0)\", so that must be 890, so is that the answer? Wait, but let me check with the specific example I had before where c = 0, so P(x) = x^3 - 11x. Then Q(10) = 1000 - 110 = 890, and Q(0) = 0, so 890 - 0 = 890. If I take c = 5, then P(x) = x^3 - 11x + 5, so Q(10) = 890 + 5 = 895, and Q(0) = 5, so 895 - 5 = 890. Yep, same result. So regardless of c, the difference is always 890. Therefore, that must be the answer.\n\nWait, but before I finish, let me make sure there isn't a polynomial of degree 4 or higher that could have a smaller Q(10) - Q(0). But the question is about the minimal degree polynomial, so even if there are higher degree polynomials, since we are to take the minimal degree one, which is degree 3, so we don't need to consider higher degrees. However, just to be thorough, maybe there is a degree 3 polynomial that isn't of the form x^3 -11x + c that also satisfies the condition? But according to our earlier analysis, no. The constraints forced a = 0 and b = -11, so no, only those polynomials. Therefore, the minimal degree is 3, and the value is 890.\n\nBut wait, hold on a second. Wait, in the problem statement, it says \"nonconstant monic polynomials with integer coefficients\". The polynomial x^3 - 11x is indeed monic, nonconstant, integer coefficients, and satisfies P(α) = P(β). So that's valid. So then, the answer should be 890? Hmm.\n\nWait, but let me check once again with another example. Let me pick c = 1. Then P(x) = x^3 - 11x + 1. Compute P(α) and P(β):\n\nP(α) = α^3 - 11α + 1. Earlier, we saw that α^3 = 9√3 + 11√2, so:\n\nP(α) = (9√3 + 11√2) - 11*(√3 + √2) + 1 = 9√3 + 11√2 - 11√3 - 11√2 + 1 = (-2√3) + 1.\n\nSimilarly, P(β) = β^3 - 11β + 1 = (9√3 - 11√2) - 11*(√3 - √2) + 1 = 9√3 - 11√2 - 11√3 + 11√2 + 1 = (-2√3) + 1.\n\nSo indeed, P(α) = P(β) = -2√3 + 1. So that works. Then Q(10) - Q(0) = [1000 - 110 + 1] - [0 - 0 + 1] = (891) - (1) = 890. Yep, same result.\n\nTherefore, seems consistent.\n\nWait, but let me think again: the problem says \"the set 𝒮 of all nonconstant monic polynomials P with integer coefficients satisfying P(√3 + √2) = P(√3 - √2)\". So, the set 𝒮 includes all such polynomials, and we are to take the element Q of 𝒮 with minimal degree, and compute Q(10) - Q(0). Since the minimal degree is 3, and all such minimal degree polynomials (i.e., all degree 3 polynomials in 𝒮) have Q(10) - Q(0) equal to 890, then 890 is the only possible value, so that's the answer.\n\nBut wait, hold on. Wait, is there a possibility that there is a polynomial of degree 3 that isn't of the form x^3 - 11x + c that also satisfies the condition? For example, suppose we have a polynomial with a non-zero a coefficient? But according to our earlier analysis, no, because that would require 2√3 a to be rational, which can't happen unless a = 0. So, no. So the only degree 3 polynomials in 𝒮 are the ones with a=0, b=-11, and any integer c. Therefore, they all have the same Q(10) - Q(0). Therefore, the answer must be 890.\n\nWait a second, but let me see if there is a quadratic polynomial that I missed. Wait, when I considered quadratic, I had P(x) = x^2 + a x + b. Then, setting P(α) = P(β) led to the equation (α - β)(α + β + a) = 0, so requiring α + β + a = 0, so a = - (α + β) = -2√3. But that's irrational, so no solution in integers. So quadratic is impossible.\n\nSimilarly, for linear, as we saw, impossible. So, cubic is minimal. Therefore, 890 is the answer.\n\nWait, but just to make sure, let me check the problem statement again:\n\n\"Let 𝒮 be the set of all nonconstant monic polynomials P with integer coefficients satisfying P(√3 + √2) = P(√3 - √2). If Q is an element of 𝒮 with minimal degree, compute the only possible value of Q(10) - Q(0).\"\n\nYes, so since minimal degree is 3, and all such Q have Q(10) - Q(0) = 890, so the answer is 890. Therefore, I think that is correct.\n\nWait, but let me see if there is another way to get a lower degree polynomial by considering higher symmetries or something else?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the minimal polynomial we found for α is x^4 -10x^2 +1, so maybe if we take P(x) = x^4 -10x^2 +1, then P(α) = 0 and P(β) = 0, so certainly P(α) = P(β). But that's degree 4, which is higher than 3, so not minimal. But perhaps some multiple or combination?\n\nAlternatively, suppose we take P(x) = f(x) + c, where f(x) is the minimal polynomial. Then P(α) = 0 + c = c and P(β) = 0 + c = c, so indeed P(α) = P(β) = c. So, such polynomials would satisfy the condition for any constant c. But these are degree 4 polynomials, so again, not minimal.\n\nAlternatively, could we take a multiple of f(x)? But since f(x) is monic, multiplying by another monic polynomial would give higher degree.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps considering that the condition P(α) = P(β) is equivalent to P commuting with the automorphism σ that swaps α and β? Wait, if we think of P as a function, then σ(P(α)) = P(σ(α)) = P(β) = P(α), so σ(P(α)) = P(α), so P(α) is fixed by σ, as we thought before. So, this is equivalent to saying that P(α) is in the fixed field of σ, which is Q(√3). But since P has integer coefficients, then P(α) must be an algebraic integer in Q(√3). But algebraic integers in Q(√3) are of the form a + b√3 with a, b integers. Wait, is that true?\n\nWait, actually, Q(√3) is a ring of integers, which is Z[√3], so yes, elements are a + b√3 with a, b integers. So, P(α) must lie in Z[√3]. Therefore, perhaps we can model P(x) such that when evaluated at α, it gives an element of Z[√3].\n\nBut how does that constrain P(x)? Let me think. Since α = √3 + √2, and we need P(α) to be in Z[√3]. Let me see what that implies.\n\nSuppose P(x) is a polynomial with integer coefficients, then P(α) is an algebraic number in Q(√2, √3). But we want it to lie in Q(√3). So, perhaps P must \"annihilate\" the √2 component in some sense?\n\nAlternatively, writing P(α) in terms of basis elements of Q(√2, √3) over Q, which is {1, √2, √3, √6}, and setting the coefficients of √2 and √6 to zero? Let me try that approach.\n\nLet me suppose that P(x) is a polynomial of degree n, and write P(α) as a linear combination of 1, √2, √3, √6 with rational coefficients. Then, to have P(α) in Q(√3), we need the coefficients of √2 and √6 to be zero. Since P has integer coefficients, perhaps this can be enforced by certain conditions on the coefficients of P(x).\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can write α = √3 + √2, so that any polynomial in α can be expressed in terms of √3 and √2. Since we need P(α) to have no √2 component, maybe we can set up equations accordingly.\n\nBut this might be more involved. Alternatively, since we already found a degree 3 polynomial that works, and confirmed that lower degrees are impossible, perhaps this is sufficient.\n\nAlternatively, let me think of the problem in terms of symmetric polynomials. Since α and β are roots of the quadratic equation x^2 - (α + β)x + α β = 0, which is x^2 - 2√3 x + 1 = 0. Wait, but that quadratic has irrational coefficients. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider that since α and β are conjugates, then any symmetric function in α and β with rational coefficients would be in Q(√3). Wait, but how does that relate to the polynomial P?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the condition P(α) = P(β) is equivalent to P being invariant under the automorphism that swaps α and β, which is σ. Since σ is an automorphism of the field Q(α, β) = Q(√2, √3), and P has coefficients in Q, then the condition P(σ(α)) = P(α) must hold for all α in the orbit? Wait, no, only for α and β specifically.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps P must be invariant under the subgroup of the Galois group generated by σ. Since σ has order 2, the subgroup is {identity, σ}, so invariant under σ. Therefore, P must be σ-invariant. So, for all x in the field, P(σ(x)) = P(x). Wait, but that would mean that P is fixed by σ as a polynomial, which would require that the coefficients of P are fixed by σ. But since σ acts on the coefficients? Wait, no, σ acts on the field elements, not on the coefficients. Wait, maybe I need to think differently.\n\nWait, if P is σ-invariant as a function, then for any element x in the field, P(σ(x)) = P(x). But since σ is an automorphism, this would imply that P commutes with σ. But for polynomials with rational coefficients, this would require that the coefficients are invariant under σ? Hmm, perhaps not straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, if we require that P(σ(x)) = P(x) for all x, then P must be a polynomial that is invariant under the action of σ. Since σ swaps √2 and -√2, and leaves √3 fixed, then to have P(σ(x)) = P(x) for all x, the polynomial must be expressible in terms of the invariant subfield Q(√3). Because Q(√3) is the fixed field of σ. Therefore, any polynomial that only has coefficients in Q(√3) and is expressed in terms of √3 would be invariant. But our polynomial has integer coefficients, so it's in Q[x], so to be invariant under σ, it must be expressible in terms of √3 only? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the minimal polynomial we found earlier, x^3 - 11x, is actually in the ring Z[√3][x]? Wait, no, it's just in Z[x]. Wait, but when evaluated at α, it gives an element in Z[√3]. Let me see:\n\nP(α) = α^3 - 11 α = (as computed before) -2√3, which is in Z[√3]. Similarly, P(β) = -2√3 as well. So, indeed, P(α) is in Z[√3]. Therefore, maybe the condition is equivalent to P(α) being in Z[√3], which is a stricter condition than just being equal at α and β.\n\nBut how does that help us? Well, perhaps if we can find the minimal degree polynomial such that P(α) ∈ Z[√3], then that would give the minimal degree. Since we already have a degree 3 polynomial achieving this, and lower degrees can't because of the earlier reasons, then 3 is minimal.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there is a quadratic polynomial with non-integer coefficients that would work, but the problem requires integer coefficients, so that doesn't help.\n\nTherefore, after all this, I think the minimal degree is 3, and the value of Q(10) - Q(0) is 890. Therefore, I think the answer is 890, so boxed, that would be \\boxed{890}.\n\nBut just to be absolutely thorough, let me see if there is a degree 3 polynomial with different coefficients that also satisfies the condition, perhaps with a non-zero a coefficient? Suppose I take a cubic polynomial with a non-zero a. Let me suppose that a = 1. Then according to the equation we had before:\n\n11 + 2√3 * a + b = 0,\n\nso if a = 1,\n\n11 + 2√3 + b = 0 ⇒ b = -11 - 2√3,\n\nwhich is not an integer. Similarly, if a = -1,\n\n11 + 2√3*(-1) + b = 0 ⇒ 11 - 2√3 + b = 0 ⇒ b = -11 + 2√3,\n\nstill irrational. Any integer a ≠ 0 gives b non-integer. Therefore, only a = 0 gives integer b. Therefore, indeed, only polynomials with a = 0, b = -11, c arbitrary work. Therefore, my conclusion stands.\n\nTherefore, I think the answer is 890.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{890}\n</think>\n\nGiven the set \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) of all nonconstant monic polynomials \\(P\\) with integer coefficients such that \\(P(\\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{2}) = P(\\sqrt{3} - \\sqrt{2})\\), we need to find the polynomial \\(Q \\in \\mathcal{S}\\) of minimal degree and compute \\(Q(10) - Q(0)\\).\n\nFirst, we note that \\(\\alpha = \\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{2}\\) and \\(\\beta = \\sqrt{3} - \\sqrt{2}\\) are roots of the minimal polynomial \\(f(x) = x^4 - 10x^2 + 1\\). The condition \\(P(\\alpha) = P(\\beta)\\) implies that \\(P\\) must be invariant under the automorphism \\(\\sigma\\) that swaps \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\). This automorphism has the fixed field \\(Q(\\sqrt{3})\\), so \\(P(\\alpha)\\) must lie in \\(Q(\\sqrt{3})\\).\n\nWe analyzed polynomials of degrees 1, 2, and 3:\n\n1. **Degree 1**: Impossible since \\(\\alpha \\neq \\beta\\).\n2. **Degree 2**: Leads to a non-integer coefficient requirement.\n3. **Degree 3**: We found that the polynomial \\(P(x) = x^3 - 11x + c\\) (with integer \\(c\\)) satisfies \\(P(\\alpha) = P(\\beta)\\). This is derived by setting up the equation \\(P(\\alpha) - P(\\beta) = 0\\) and solving for coefficients, leading to \\(a = 0\\), \\(b = -11\\), and \\(c\\) arbitrary.\n\nFor \\(Q(x) = x^3 - 11x + c\\), we compute:\n\\[\nQ(10) = 10^3 - 11 \\cdot 10 + c = 1000 - 110 + c = 890 + c\n\\]\n\\[\nQ(0) = 0 - 0 + c = c\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\nQ(10) - Q(0) = (890 + c) - c = 890\n\\]\n\nThis result holds for any integer \\(c\\), confirming that the only possible value of \\(Q(10) - Q(0)\\) is \\(\\boxed{890}\\).","num_tokens":11827,"correct":true,"formatted":true},{"predicted_answer":"890","full_generation":"Okay, so I have this problem here: Let 𝒮 be the set of all nonconstant monic polynomials P with integer coefficients satisfying P(√3 + √2) = P(√3 − √2). If Q is an element of 𝒮 with minimal degree, compute the only possible value of Q(10) − Q(0). Hmm, alright, let me try to unpack this and figure it out step by step.\n\nFirst, let me restate the problem in my own words to make sure I understand it. We are looking for all nonconstant monic polynomials with integer coefficients such that when you plug in √3 + √2 into the polynomial, you get the same result as when you plug in √3 − √2. Among all such polynomials, we need to find the one with the smallest possible degree, call that polynomial Q, and then compute the difference between Q evaluated at 10 and Q evaluated at 0, which is Q(10) - Q(0). And apparently, there's only one possible value for that difference, so we just have to find it.\n\nAlright, so first thoughts: Since the polynomial has integer coefficients and it's monic, that might be important. The condition P(√3 + √2) = P(√3 − √2) suggests some symmetry in the polynomial with respect to the conjugate of √3 + √2, which would be √3 − √2. In other words, the polynomial P treats these two numbers as if they were roots of some kind of equation or maybe as conjugates, so that when you evaluate P at one, you get the same value as when you evaluate it at the other. Since polynomials with integer coefficients evaluated at conjugate algebraic numbers might have some symmetric properties, perhaps?\n\nLet me recall that √3 + √2 and √3 − √2 are conjugate over the rationals. That is, they are roots of the same minimal polynomial over Q (the rationals). Let me verify that. Let me set α = √3 + √2. Then, let's find the minimal polynomial of α over Q.\n\nLet me compute α^2 first:\n\nα = √3 + √2,\n\nα^2 = (√3 + √2)^2 = (√3)^2 + 2*√3*√2 + (√2)^2 = 3 + 2√6 + 2 = 5 + 2√6.\n\nHmm, okay, so α^2 = 5 + 2√6. That still has a radical in it, so not in Q yet. Let's try squaring again to eliminate the radical. Let me set β = α^2 = 5 + 2√6.\n\nThen, let's compute β - 5 = 2√6, so (β - 5)/2 = √6. Then, squaring both sides, [(β - 5)/2]^2 = (√6)^2 = 6.\n\nTherefore, [(β - 5)/2]^2 = 6 ⇒ (β - 5)^2 / 4 = 6 ⇒ (β - 5)^2 = 24 ⇒ β^2 - 10β + 25 = 24 ⇒ β^2 - 10β + 1 = 0.\n\nTherefore, the minimal polynomial of β over Q is x^2 - 10x + 1. But β is α^2, so does that mean that the minimal polynomial of α over Q is related? Wait, actually, since β = α^2, but α itself is √3 + √2, which is a degree 4 element over Q, perhaps? Wait, let me think again.\n\nWait, perhaps I should compute the minimal polynomial of α directly. Let me try that.\n\nLet α = √3 + √2.\n\nThen, let me set x = α = √3 + √2.\n\nThen, x - √3 = √2,\n\nSquaring both sides: (x - √3)^2 = (√2)^2 ⇒ x^2 - 2√3 x + 3 = 2 ⇒ x^2 + 1 = 2√3 x.\n\nNow, isolate the radical: (x^2 + 1)/(2x) = √3.\n\nNow, square both sides again to eliminate the square root:\n\n[(x^2 + 1)/(2x)]^2 = (√3)^2 ⇒ (x^4 + 2x^2 + 1)/(4x^2) = 3.\n\nMultiply both sides by 4x^2:\n\nx^4 + 2x^2 + 1 = 12x^2 ⇒ x^4 + 2x^2 + 1 - 12x^2 = 0 ⇒ x^4 - 10x^2 + 1 = 0.\n\nAh! So the minimal polynomial of α = √3 + √2 over Q is x^4 - 10x^2 + 1. Let me check that. Let's plug in x = √3 + √2 into x^4 - 10x^2 + 1.\n\nFirst, we already know that α^2 = 5 + 2√6, so let's compute α^4:\n\nα^4 = (α^2)^2 = (5 + 2√6)^2 = 25 + 20√6 + (2√6)^2 = 25 + 20√6 + 4*6 = 25 + 20√6 + 24 = 49 + 20√6.\n\nNow, compute 10x^2: 10*(5 + 2√6) = 50 + 20√6.\n\nSo, x^4 - 10x^2 + 1 = (49 + 20√6) - (50 + 20√6) + 1 = (49 - 50 + 1) + (20√6 - 20√6) = 0 + 0 = 0. Perfect, so that works. So the minimal polynomial is indeed x^4 - 10x^2 + 1.\n\nSimilarly, the conjugate of α = √3 + √2 over Q is √3 - √2, since you can get that by changing the sign of the √2 term. Let me denote β = √3 - √2. Let me check that β is also a root of the same minimal polynomial.\n\nCompute β^2: (√3 - √2)^2 = (√3)^2 - 2√3√2 + (√2)^2 = 3 - 2√6 + 2 = 5 - 2√6.\n\nThen β^4 = (β^2)^2 = (5 - 2√6)^2 = 25 - 20√6 + (2√6)^2 = 25 - 20√6 + 24 = 49 - 20√6.\n\nThen, compute β^4 - 10β^2 + 1 = (49 - 20√6) - 10*(5 - 2√6) + 1 = 49 - 20√6 - 50 + 20√6 + 1 = (49 - 50 + 1) + (-20√6 + 20√6) = 0 + 0 = 0. Yep, so β is also a root of x^4 - 10x^2 + 1.\n\nMoreover, since the minimal polynomial is degree 4, then the field extension Q(α) has degree 4 over Q, and the conjugates of α over Q are α itself, β, and the other two conjugates? Wait, actually, over Q, the minimal polynomial is degree 4, so there should be four roots, right? Wait, but we only found two so far: α = √3 + √2 and β = √3 - √2. What are the other two roots?\n\nWell, let me see. The minimal polynomial is x^4 - 10x^2 + 1. Let me factor that polynomial. Let me set y = x^2, so the equation becomes y^2 - 10y + 1 = 0. Solving for y, we get y = [10 ± √(100 - 4)] / 2 = [10 ± √96]/2 = [10 ± 4√6]/2 = 5 ± 2√6.\n\nTherefore, the roots for x are the square roots of y, so the roots are x = ±√(5 + 2√6) and x = ±√(5 - 2√6). Hmm, but √(5 + 2√6) is equal to √3 + √2, because (√3 + √2)^2 = 3 + 2√6 + 2 = 5 + 2√6, so √(5 + 2√6) = √3 + √2 = α, and √(5 - 2√6) would be √3 - √2 = β? Wait, hold on:\n\nWait, (√3 - √2)^2 = 3 - 2√6 + 2 = 5 - 2√6, so √(5 - 2√6) is equal to √3 - √2? Wait, but √3 - √2 is positive? Let me check numerically: √3 ≈ 1.732, √2 ≈ 1.414, so √3 - √2 ≈ 0.318, which is positive, so yes, that's okay. So √(5 - 2√6) = √3 - √2, which is β.\n\nSimilarly, the other roots would be the negative roots: -α and -β. So the four roots of the minimal polynomial x^4 - 10x^2 + 1 are: α = √3 + √2, -α = -√3 - √2, β = √3 - √2, and -β = -√3 + √2. Wait, hold on, -β would be -√3 + √2? Wait, β is √3 - √2, so -β is -√3 + √2, which is equal to √2 - √3, which is approximately -0.318, so that's also a real number, but negative? Wait, no, actually, √2 ≈ 1.414, √3 ≈ 1.732, so √2 - √3 ≈ -0.318, so that's a negative number. So actually, the four roots are:\n\n1. √3 + √2 ≈ 3.146,\n\n2. -√3 - √2 ≈ -3.146,\n\n3. √3 - √2 ≈ 0.318,\n\n4. -√3 + √2 ≈ -0.318.\n\nOkay, so these are the four roots.\n\nTherefore, the minimal polynomial over Q of α = √3 + √2 is indeed x^4 - 10x^2 + 1, which factors as (x - α)(x + α)(x - β)(x + β), since the roots come in pairs of positive and negative.\n\nBut now, going back to the problem: we have a monic polynomial P with integer coefficients, nonconstant, such that P(α) = P(β), where α = √3 + √2 and β = √3 - √2.\n\nThe question is about such polynomials P, and we need to find the one of minimal degree, call it Q, and then compute Q(10) - Q(0).\n\nHmm. So, first, perhaps we can think about the condition P(α) = P(β). Since α and β are conjugates over Q, this condition might imply that P is invariant under the automorphism of the field Q(α) that swaps α and β? Or perhaps more generally, that P is in the fixed field of the automorphism that swaps α and β?\n\nAlternatively, since P has integer coefficients, and α and β are conjugate over Q, then if P(α) = P(β), that might mean that P is actually a polynomial that's symmetric with respect to swapping α and β. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the difference P(α) - P(β) is zero, so that (α - β) divides P(α) - P(β) in some sense? Wait, but polynomials aren't evaluated in a ring where division is straightforward unless we have some structure. Maybe another approach.\n\nAlternatively, since α and β are roots of the same minimal polynomial, which is x^4 - 10x^2 + 1, then perhaps the set {α, β, -α, -β} are all roots of that polynomial, so perhaps if we consider the ideal generated by that polynomial in Z[x], then any polynomial that vanishes at α and β would have to be in that ideal? Wait, but here it's not that P vanishes at α and β, but rather that P(α) = P(β). Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think of the polynomial P(x) - P(β) has a root at x = α, since P(α) - P(β) = 0. Similarly, since β is another conjugate, perhaps P(x) - P(β) is divisible by the minimal polynomial of α over Q, which is x^4 - 10x^2 + 1? Wait, let's see.\n\nWait, if P(α) = P(β), then α is a root of the polynomial P(x) - P(β). Similarly, if we consider that β is another conjugate of α, then perhaps P(x) - P(β) is divisible by the minimal polynomial of α over Q, which is x^4 - 10x^2 + 1? Hmm, but is that necessarily true?\n\nWait, let me recall that if you have a polynomial with integer coefficients, and you have an algebraic number θ, then if P(θ) = 0, then the minimal polynomial of θ divides P(x) in Z[x]. But here, it's not that P(α) = 0, but rather P(α) = P(β). So, perhaps we can write P(x) - P(β) has α as a root. Therefore, the minimal polynomial of α divides P(x) - P(β). Since the minimal polynomial is x^4 - 10x^2 + 1, which is irreducible over Q, right? Let me confirm that.\n\nIs x^4 - 10x^2 + 1 irreducible over Q? Let me try to factor it. Suppose it factors into quadratics. Suppose x^4 -10x^2 +1 = (x^2 + a x + b)(x^2 + c x + d), where a, b, c, d are integers. Then multiplying out, we get x^4 + (a + c)x^3 + (b + d + a c)x^2 + (a d + b c)x + b d.\n\nComparing coefficients:\n\n- Coefficient of x^3: a + c = 0 ⇒ c = -a.\n\n- Coefficient of x: a d + b c = a d + b (-a) = a(d - b) = 0. Since a might be zero, but if a ≠ 0, then d = b.\n\n- Constant term: b d = b^2 (since d = b if a ≠ 0) must equal 1. So b^2 = 1 ⇒ b = ±1.\n\n- Coefficient of x^2: b + d + a c = b + b + a (-a) = 2b - a^2. This must equal -10.\n\nSo, if b = 1, then 2(1) - a^2 = -10 ⇒ 2 - a^2 = -10 ⇒ a^2 = 12, which is not a square.\n\nIf b = -1, then 2(-1) - a^2 = -10 ⇒ -2 - a^2 = -10 ⇒ -a^2 = -8 ⇒ a^2 = 8, which is also not a square.\n\nTherefore, if a ≠ 0, there is no solution. If a = 0, then c = 0, and then the coefficient of x^2 becomes b + d, and the coefficient of x is 0, so we have:\n\nx^4 -10x^2 +1 = (x^2 + b)(x^2 + d) = x^4 + (b + d)x^2 + b d.\n\nComparing coefficients:\n\n- Coefficient of x^2: b + d = -10,\n\n- Constant term: b d = 1.\n\nSo we need integers b and d such that b + d = -10 and b d = 1. The integer solutions to b d =1 are (b,d) = (1,1) or (-1,-1). But 1 + 1 = 2 ≠ -10, and (-1) + (-1) = -2 ≠ -10. So no solution here either. Therefore, the polynomial x^4 -10x^2 +1 is irreducible over Q. Therefore, it is indeed the minimal polynomial of α over Q.\n\nTherefore, going back, since α is a root of x^4 -10x^2 +1, and if P(α) = P(β), then α is a root of P(x) - P(β). Since x^4 -10x^2 +1 is irreducible over Q, then if α is a root of P(x) - P(β), then x^4 -10x^2 +1 divides P(x) - P(β) in Q[x]. But since P(x) - P(β) has integer coefficients (because P has integer coefficients and β is algebraic, but wait, actually β is not in Q, so P(β) is not necessarily an integer. Hmm, so perhaps this approach is problematic because P(β) is not in Q, so P(x) - P(β) is not a polynomial with integer coefficients? Wait, hold on.\n\nWait, actually, P has integer coefficients, so P(x) is a polynomial with integer coefficients, but when evaluated at β, which is an algebraic number, P(β) is an algebraic number, but not necessarily a rational number. Therefore, P(x) - P(β) is not a polynomial with integer coefficients because P(β) is not an integer. So perhaps this approach is not the way to go.\n\nHmm, maybe another way. Since P(α) = P(β), perhaps we can consider the polynomial P(x) - P(y) and set x = α, y = β, but that might not be helpful. Alternatively, maybe think of the polynomial P(x) as a function that is symmetric in some way with respect to α and β. Since α and β are conjugates, perhaps P must be a polynomial that is invariant under the automorphism of Q(α) that swaps α and β?\n\nLet me recall that the Galois group of Q(α) over Q is isomorphic to the Klein four-group, since the minimal polynomial is quartic, and the Galois group is abelian because the polynomial is palindromic? Wait, actually, the minimal polynomial is x^4 -10x^2 +1, which is palindromic? Let me check: a palindromic polynomial has coefficients that read the same forwards and backwards. The coefficients are 1, 0, -10, 0, 1, so yes, it is palindromic. Therefore, the Galois group is indeed the Klein four-group, which is abelian, and has elements corresponding to the automorphisms that send α to β, α to -β, α to -α, and so on? Wait, perhaps.\n\nAlternatively, since the Galois group is Klein four, it has three nontrivial automorphisms, each of order 2. The automorphisms are determined by their action on the roots. The roots are α, -α, β, -β, where α = √3 + √2 and β = √3 - √2. Wait, but actually, as we saw earlier, the roots are α, -α, β, -β, but actually, let me confirm:\n\nWait, earlier we had that the roots are:\n\n1. √3 + √2 = α,\n\n2. -√3 - √2 = -α,\n\n3. √3 - √2 = β,\n\n4. -√3 + √2 = -β.\n\nTherefore, the four roots are α, -α, β, -β. So the Galois group acts by permuting these roots. The automorphisms are determined by where they send α. Since the minimal polynomial is palindromic, meaning that if θ is a root, then 1/θ is also a root? Wait, actually, palindromic polynomials satisfy x^n P(1/x) = P(x), so if θ is a root, then 1/θ is also a root. Let me check that.\n\nGiven our polynomial x^4 -10x^2 +1, let's compute 1/α:\n\n1/α = 1/(√3 + √2). Let me rationalize the denominator:\n\n1/(√3 + √2) = (√3 - √2)/[(√3 + √2)(√3 - √2)] = (√3 - √2)/(3 - 2) = √3 - √2 = β. So 1/α = β. Similarly, 1/β = 1/(√3 - √2) = (√3 + √2)/[(√3 - √2)(√3 + √2)] = (√3 + √2)/(3 - 2) = √3 + √2 = α. So indeed, the polynomial is palindromic, so roots come in reciprocal pairs: α and 1/α = β, and -α and -1/α = -β.\n\nTherefore, the Galois group elements can be thought of as automorphisms that send α to β, α to -β, α to -α, etc. Wait, perhaps the Galois group is generated by the automorphism that sends α to β, which is an automorphism of order 2, since applying it twice would send α to β and then β to α again? Wait, actually, if we have an automorphism σ such that σ(α) = β, then σ(β) should be 1/β = α, since σ is an automorphism over Q, and since the polynomial is palindromic, so σ must send roots to their reciprocals? Hmm, maybe.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the automorphism group is generated by the automorphism that sends α to β, which is an element of order 2, and another automorphism that sends α to -α, which is also of order 2, and their product? Let me see.\n\nLet me denote σ as the automorphism that swaps α and β, i.e., σ(α) = β, σ(β) = α, σ(-α) = -β, σ(-β) = -α. Then, another automorphism τ that sends α to -α, so τ(α) = -α, τ(β) = -β, τ(-α) = α, τ(-β) = β. Then, the product στ would send α to -β, and so on. Since both σ and τ have order 2, and they commute because they are both automorphisms, the group is the Klein four-group: {identity, σ, τ, στ}.\n\nTherefore, the Galois group is isomorphic to the Klein four-group. So, in any case, the point is that the automorphisms of the field Q(α) over Q are these four elements.\n\nNow, returning to our problem. We have a polynomial P with integer coefficients, monic, nonconstant, such that P(α) = P(β). Since σ is an automorphism in the Galois group that sends α to β, then applying σ to P(α) gives P(σ(α)) = P(β) = P(α). Therefore, P is fixed by the automorphism σ. Similarly, since the coefficients of P are in Q (actually in Z), then for P to be fixed by σ, it must be that P is invariant under the action of σ. In other words, P(σ(x)) = P(x) for all x in Q(α). Wait, but actually, since σ is an automorphism of the field Q(α), then for any x in Q(α), P(σ(x)) = P(x). But since P has coefficients in Z (which are in Q), then actually P is a polynomial with coefficients in Q, so this invariance under σ would mean that P is in the fixed field of σ? Wait, maybe not exactly, but perhaps that P must be a polynomial that is fixed by σ, so that σ(P(x)) = P(x) for all x.\n\nWait, more precisely, since σ is an automorphism of the field Q(α), then for any polynomial P with coefficients in Q, we have that σ(P(x)) = P(σ(x)) as polynomials, because the coefficients are fixed by σ (since they are in Q, and σ fixes Q). Therefore, if P is fixed by σ as a polynomial, meaning that σ(P(x)) = P(x) for all x, then that would imply that P(σ(x)) = P(x) for all x, so in particular, P(α) = P(σ(α)) = P(β), which is exactly our condition. So, our condition P(α) = P(β) is equivalent to P being fixed by the automorphism σ.\n\nTherefore, the set 𝒮 is exactly the set of monic polynomials with integer coefficients that are fixed by the automorphism σ. Since σ is an element of the Galois group of Q(α) over Q, then the fixed field of σ would be a subfield of Q(α), and polynomials fixed by σ would be those that are invariant under the action of σ. Alternatively, since σ is an automorphism of order 2, the fixed field of σ is the set of elements x in Q(α) such that σ(x) = x. The fixed field of σ would contain Q, since σ fixes Q. In fact, the fixed field of σ is Q(√(-10))? Wait, maybe not. Wait, let me think differently.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can construct the minimal polynomial for which such polynomials P must satisfy certain conditions. Since σ swaps α and β, then any polynomial P fixed by σ must satisfy P(α) = P(β), P(-α) = P(-β), and so on for all the conjugates. Wait, actually, more precisely, since σ is an automorphism of order 2, then the fixed polynomials under σ would be those polynomials P such that P(σ(x)) = P(x) for all x. Therefore, in particular, for any root θ of the minimal polynomial of α, we have that P(σ(θ)) = P(θ). Since σ permutes the roots of the minimal polynomial, then P must take the same value at each pair of roots that are swapped by σ. Since σ swaps α and β, but also swaps -α and -β, etc.\n\nTherefore, in order for P(σ(θ)) = P(θ) for all θ in the splitting field, then P must take the same value at each orbit of the action of σ on the roots. Since the orbits under σ are {α, β}, {-α, -β}, and since σ has order 2, so each orbit is a pair. Therefore, for P to be fixed by σ, it must satisfy P(α) = P(β), P(-α) = P(-β), and also, since σ applied to -α is -β, so that's already covered. Therefore, in effect, the polynomial P must satisfy that P(α) = P(β) and P(-α) = P(-β). So, that's the condition.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think of P as a function that is invariant under the transformation x ↦ σ(x), so that P(σ(x)) = P(x) for all x. Therefore, such polynomials P must be in the fixed field of σ, but since we are dealing with polynomials over Q, perhaps we can express P in terms of the invariant functions under σ.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider that since σ swaps α and β, then any symmetric function in α and β would be fixed by σ. Therefore, perhaps we can express P in terms of symmetric functions of α and β, or in terms of the basis of the invariant subspace under the Galois action.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model the problem by considering that since α and β are roots of the minimal polynomial x^4 -10x^2 +1, then any polynomial that is invariant under σ must be a polynomial in the elementary symmetric functions of α and β? Wait, but α and β are not independent; they are related through the minimal polynomial.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider the field Q(α) and note that Q(α) is a degree 4 extension over Q, and the fixed field of σ is Q(√something)? Let me see. Let me compute the fixed field of σ. Since σ is the automorphism that sends α to β, then to find the fixed field, we can look for elements x in Q(α) such that σ(x) = x. Let me suppose that x can be written as a linear combination of 1, α, α², α³, since {1, α, α², α³} form a basis for Q(α) over Q. Then, we can write x = a + b α + c α² + d α³, and then σ(x) = a + b β + c β² + d β³. For σ(x) to equal x, we must have:\n\na + b β + c β² + d β³ = a + b α + c α² + d α³.\n\nTherefore, rearranged:\n\nb(β - α) + c(β² - α²) + d(β³ - α³) = 0.\n\nWe can factor each term:\n\nFirst, β - α = (√3 - √2) - (√3 + √2) = -2√2.\n\nSecond, β² - α² = (β - α)(β + α). Since β + α = (√3 - √2) + (√3 + √2) = 2√3.\n\nTherefore, β² - α² = (-2√2)(2√3) = -4√6.\n\nThird, β³ - α³. Let me compute β³ and α³.\n\nFirst, let me compute α³. Since α = √3 + √2,\n\nα³ = (√3 + √2)^3.\n\nLet me compute that:\n\n(√3 + √2)^3 = (√3)^3 + 3(√3)^2(√2) + 3(√3)(√2)^2 + (√2)^3\n\n= 3√3 + 3*(3)*(√2) + 3*(√3)*(2) + 2√2\n\n= 3√3 + 9√2 + 6√3 + 2√2\n\n= (3√3 + 6√3) + (9√2 + 2√2)\n\n= 9√3 + 11√2.\n\nSimilarly, β³ = (√3 - √2)^3.\n\nCompute that:\n\n(√3 - √2)^3 = (√3)^3 - 3(√3)^2(√2) + 3(√3)(√2)^2 - (√2)^3\n\n= 3√3 - 3*(3)*(√2) + 3*(√3)*(2) - 2√2\n\n= 3√3 - 9√2 + 6√3 - 2√2\n\n= (3√3 + 6√3) + (-9√2 - 2√2)\n\n= 9√3 - 11√2.\n\nTherefore, β³ - α³ = (9√3 - 11√2) - (9√3 + 11√2) = -22√2.\n\nTherefore, β³ - α³ = -22√2.\n\nSo putting it all together:\n\nb(-2√2) + c(-4√6) + d(-22√2) = 0.\n\nFactor out the √2:\n\n√2 [ -2b - 22d ] + √6 [ -4c ] = 0.\n\nSince √2 and √6 are linearly independent over Q (they are part of the basis for the field Q(√2, √3) over Q), the coefficients of √2 and √6 must each be zero. Therefore, we have two equations:\n\n1. Coefficient of √2: -2b - 22d = 0,\n\n2. Coefficient of √6: -4c = 0.\n\nFrom the second equation: -4c = 0 ⇒ c = 0.\n\nFrom the first equation: -2b -22d = 0 ⇒ 2b = -22d ⇒ b = -11d.\n\nTherefore, the coefficients must satisfy c = 0 and b = -11d. The coefficients a can be arbitrary, since there was no constraint on a from this equation. Therefore, the fixed field elements x = a + b α + c α² + d α³ with c = 0 and b = -11d. Let me write that as:\n\nx = a + (-11d) α + 0 * α² + d α³ = a -11d α + d α³.\n\nTherefore, any element in the fixed field can be written as x = a + d(-11 α + α³). Since a and d are rational numbers (in fact, since we are working over Q, they are in Q). But since we want polynomials with integer coefficients, perhaps we can consider integer coefficients?\n\nWait, actually, the fixed field is a field extension of Q, but we are looking for polynomials P with integer coefficients that are fixed by σ. The fixed field is a subfield of Q(α), but the polynomials we are considering are over Q, so perhaps the condition that P is fixed by σ can be translated into conditions on the coefficients of P.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can parametrize such polynomials P.\n\nLet me suppose that P is a monic polynomial with integer coefficients, of degree n, such that P(α) = P(β). Let me think of P as a function, then since σ(α) = β, then σ(P(α)) = P(σ(α)) = P(β) = P(α), so indeed, P is fixed by σ as a function. But since P has integer coefficients, then actually, the condition is that P is invariant under the automorphism σ. So, for the polynomial to be invariant under σ, it must satisfy that when we apply σ to its coefficients (but coefficients are in Q, and σ fixes Q), then the polynomial remains the same. Wait, no, σ acts on the variables, not on the coefficients. So actually, σ(P(x)) = P(σ(x)) must equal P(x) for all x. Therefore, as a polynomial identity, σ(P(x)) = P(x).\n\nTherefore, if we write P(x) = x^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + ... + a_1 x + a_0,\n\nthen σ(P(x)) = σ(x)^n + a_{n-1} σ(x)^{n-1} + ... + a_1 σ(x) + a_0.\n\nFor this to equal P(x) for all x, we must have that σ(x)^k = x^k for all k, but σ(x) is not equal to x unless x is in the fixed field. Wait, this seems contradictory unless P is constant? But the problem states nonconstant polynomials. Hmm, so perhaps my approach is missing something.\n\nWait a second, actually, σ is an automorphism of the field Q(α), so when we apply σ to the polynomial P(x), which has coefficients in Q, we get another polynomial σ(P(x)) with coefficients in Q, but as a polynomial in x, it's equal to P(σ(x)). For σ(P(x)) to equal P(x) as polynomials, meaning as functions on the entire field Q(α), then indeed, for all x in Q(α), P(σ(x)) = P(x). Therefore, this is a functional equation that must hold for all x in the field. Therefore, such a polynomial P must satisfy that it's invariant under the substitution x ↦ σ(x).\n\nTherefore, perhaps we can construct such polynomials by considering that they can be expressed in terms of the invariant functions under σ. Since σ has order 2, the ring of polynomials invariant under σ would be the set of polynomials P such that P(σ(x)) = P(x) for all x. To find such polynomials, we can look for polynomials that are functions of the invariant elements under σ.\n\nAlternatively, since σ swaps α and β, and also swaps -α and -β, perhaps we can consider that any invariant polynomial must be a polynomial in terms of the elementary symmetric functions of α and β? Wait, but α and β are related through the minimal polynomial.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think of the field Q(α) as a vector space over Q with basis {1, α, α², α³}, and then the action of σ on this basis is given by:\n\nσ(1) = 1,\n\nσ(α) = β,\n\nσ(α²) = β²,\n\nσ(α³) = β³.\n\nBut since β is related to α via the minimal polynomial, perhaps we can express β in terms of α?\n\nRecall that β = 1/α, as we saw earlier (since 1/α = β). Therefore, β = 1/α, so β = α^{-1}. Therefore, σ(α) = α^{-1}. Therefore, the automorphism σ is inversion in the multiplicative group? Not exactly, because σ also sends α to β = 1/α, but in the field Q(α), inversion is an automorphism only if the field is a multiplicative group, but Q(α) is a field of characteristic 0, so inversion is a field automorphism only if it fixes Q, which it does. Wait, actually inversion is an automorphism of the multiplicative group of Q(α)^*, but since we are dealing with the entire field, inversion is an automorphism of the field only if it fixes the base field Q. Since inversion maps α to 1/α = β, which is in Q(α), and fixes Q, so inversion is indeed an automorphism of the field Q(α). Wait, is that true?\n\nWait, let me check: Let me denote i as the inversion automorphism, defined by i(x) = 1/x for all x in Q(α)\\{0}. Then, i is an automorphism of Q(α) over Q because it is the inverse of the multiplication-by-x map, which is a polynomial automorphism? Wait, actually, inversion is an automorphism of the field if the field is a division ring and the automorphism is defined by inverting elements. Since Q(α) is a field, inversion is an automorphism if it fixes Q. Since i(Q) = Q, yes, inversion is an automorphism of Q(α) over Q.\n\nTherefore, inversion i: x ↦ 1/x is an automorphism of Q(α) over Q. Therefore, our σ is actually inversion? Wait, but σ(α) = β = 1/α, so σ is inversion. Therefore, the automorphism σ is inversion. Therefore, the condition that P is fixed by σ is equivalent to P(1/x) = P(x) for all x in Q(α)\\{0}. Wait, but as a polynomial identity, this would mean that P(1/x) = P(x) as polynomials, which would require that P is a reciprocal polynomial, i.e., x^n P(1/x) = P(x), so that P is equal to its own reciprocal polynomial. However, reciprocal polynomials satisfy P(x) = x^n P(1/x), which is slightly different.\n\nWait, let me think again. If P is fixed by inversion, meaning that for every x ≠ 0 in Q(α), P(x) = P(1/x). So, as a functional equation, this must hold for all x in Q(α) except possibly 0. But since polynomials are determined by their values on an infinite set, this would imply that P(x) - P(1/x) is the zero polynomial. Therefore, P(x) - P(1/x) = 0 for all x, so P(x) = P(1/x) as polynomials.\n\nTherefore, such a polynomial must satisfy P(x) = P(1/x) for all x. Therefore, P is a reciprocal polynomial, meaning that the coefficients are palindromic. That is, the coefficients read forwards and backwards are the same. For example, if P(x) = x^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + ... + a_1 x + a_0, then the condition P(x) = P(1/x) multiplied by x^n gives x^n P(1/x) = a_0 x^n + a_1 x^{n-1} + ... + a_{n-1} x + 1 = P(x). Therefore, comparing coefficients, we have a_k = a_{n - k} for all k. So yes, P is a reciprocal polynomial, meaning it's palindromic.\n\nTherefore, the condition that P is fixed by inversion (i.e., σ) is equivalent to P being a reciprocal polynomial. Therefore, our problem reduces to: Find the monic, nonconstant, reciprocal polynomials with integer coefficients of minimal degree such that P(α) = P(β), which is equivalent to being reciprocal, and then compute Q(10) - Q(0) for such a minimal degree Q.\n\nWait, but hold on. Wait, is that all? Because reciprocal polynomials satisfy P(x) = x^n P(1/x), so P(1/x) = x^{-n} P(x), so P(x) = P(1/x) only if x^n P(1/x) = P(x), so that would require that x^n P(1/x) = P(x), which is the definition of a reciprocal polynomial. However, in our case, we have that P(x) = P(1/x) as functions, which would require that x^n P(1/x) = P(x), so reciprocal polynomial. So yes, so the condition that P is fixed by inversion is exactly that P is a reciprocal polynomial.\n\nBut wait, but in our case, we are working over integers, and the polynomial is monic. So, a monic reciprocal polynomial with integer coefficients must satisfy that the coefficients are palindromic integers, so that the coefficient of x^k is equal to the coefficient of x^{n - k}, and since it's monic, the leading coefficient is 1, so the constant term must also be 1. Wait, let's see:\n\nSuppose P is monic of degree n, reciprocal, so P(x) = x^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + ... + a_1 x + a_0, and reciprocal means that a_k = a_{n - k} for all k. Therefore, the constant term a_0 = a_n = 1 (since it's monic). Then the coefficient of x is a_1 = a_{n - 1}, the coefficient of x^2 is a_2 = a_{n - 2}, etc. So, for example, if n is even, say n = 2m, then the polynomial is symmetric with a_0 = a_{2m} = 1, a_1 = a_{2m -1}, etc. If n is odd, say n = 2m +1, then the middle coefficient a_m can be arbitrary, but still must equal itself, so it's free, but the others must mirror.\n\nTherefore, reciprocal monic polynomials with integer coefficients have the form:\n\nFor even degree 2m:\n\nP(x) = x^{2m} + a_{2m -1}x^{2m -1} + ... + a_1 x + 1,\n\nwhere the coefficients satisfy a_k = a_{2m -k} for k = 1, ..., 2m -1.\n\nFor odd degree 2m +1:\n\nP(x) = x^{2m +1} + a_{2m}x^{2m} + ... + a_1 x + 1,\n\nwith a_k = a_{2m +1 -k} for k = 0, ..., 2m.\n\nTherefore, such polynomials are palindromic with leading coefficient 1 and constant term 1.\n\nTherefore, our problem reduces to finding the monic, reciprocal polynomial of minimal degree with integer coefficients (other than the constant polynomial 1, which is excluded since we need nonconstant) such that... Wait, but does every reciprocal polynomial satisfy P(α) = P(β)? Wait, hold on.\n\nWait, we concluded that if P is reciprocal, then P(x) = P(1/x), so in particular, P(α) = P(1/α) = P(β), since 1/α = β. Therefore, yes, reciprocal polynomials do satisfy P(α) = P(β). Conversely, if P(α) = P(β), then since β = 1/α, then P(α) = P(1/α), so P(x) - P(1/x) has α as a root, but since the minimal polynomial of α is degree 4, and reciprocal polynomials have roots that are reciprocals of each other, so perhaps this is exactly the condition?\n\nTherefore, the set 𝒮 is exactly the set of monic reciprocal polynomials with integer coefficients of degree at least 1. Therefore, the minimal degree such polynomial is the minimal degree reciprocal polynomial with integer coefficients, nonconstant.\n\nBut wait, the minimal degree monic reciprocal polynomial with integer coefficients is degree 1? Let's see:\n\nDegree 1: A monic reciprocal polynomial of degree 1 would have to satisfy a_0 = a_1 = 1, but for degree 1, the polynomial is x + a_0, and reciprocal would require that a_0 = a_{1 - 0} = a_1, but a_1 is the coefficient of x^1, which is 1 (monic), so a_0 must equal 1. Therefore, the degree 1 reciprocal monic polynomial is x + 1. But is that reciprocal? Let's check: P(x) = x + 1. Then P(1/x) = 1/x + 1. For P(x) to equal P(1/x), we need x + 1 = 1/x + 1 ⇒ x = 1/x ⇒ x^2 = 1. Which is only true for x = 1 or x = -1, not for all x. Therefore, x + 1 is not a reciprocal polynomial. Wait a second, my mistake.\n\nWait, the definition: A reciprocal polynomial satisfies P(x) = x^n P(1/x). For degree n. So for degree 1, P(x) = x + a_0. Then x^1 P(1/x) = x*(1/x + a_0) = 1 + a_0 x. For this to equal P(x) = x + a_0, we need 1 + a_0 x = x + a_0 ⇒ coefficients must match:\n\nCoefficient of x: a_0 = 1,\n\nConstant term: 1 = a_0.\n\nTherefore, a_0 must be 1, so P(x) = x + 1. Then, x^1 P(1/x) = x*(1/x + 1) = 1 + x, which is equal to P(x). So actually, P(x) = x + 1 is a reciprocal polynomial of degree 1. But then, does it satisfy P(α) = P(β)?\n\nLet me check: P(α) = α + 1, P(β) = β + 1. Since α ≠ β (as α ≈ 3.146 and β ≈ 0.318), then P(α) ≠ P(β). So even though it is reciprocal, it does not satisfy P(α) = P(β). Wait, so there's a problem here. What is going on?\n\nWait, hold on. If P is reciprocal, then P(α) = P(1/α) = P(β), since 1/α = β. Therefore, P(α) must equal P(β). But in the case of P(x) = x + 1, we have P(α) = α + 1 and P(β) = β + 1. Since α ≠ β, then P(α) ≠ P(β). Contradiction. Therefore, my earlier reasoning must be flawed.\n\nWait a second, where is the mistake here? If P is reciprocal, then P(α) = P(1/α) = P(β), so that should hold. But in this case, P(x) = x + 1 is reciprocal, but P(α) ≠ P(β). Let me compute P(α) and P(β):\n\nGiven α = √3 + √2 ≈ 3.146,\n\nβ = √3 - √2 ≈ 0.318,\n\nso P(α) = α + 1 ≈ 4.146,\n\nP(β) = β + 1 ≈ 1.318,\n\nwhich are not equal. So, that's a contradiction. Therefore, my conclusion that reciprocal polynomials satisfy P(α) = P(β) must be wrong. Where did I go wrong?\n\nAh! Wait a minute, reciprocal polynomials satisfy P(x) = x^n P(1/x). So, P(1/x) = x^{-n} P(x). Therefore, if we set x = α, then P(1/α) = α^{-n} P(α). Therefore, P(β) = P(1/α) = α^{-n} P(α). For this to equal P(α), we need α^{-n} P(α) = P(α), so (α^{-n} - 1) P(α) = 0. Therefore, either P(α) = 0, or α^{-n} = 1. Since α is not a root of unity (it's a real number greater than 1), so α^{-n} = 1 only if n = 0, which is not the case. Therefore, the only way for P(β) = P(α) is that P(α) = 0. Wait, so reciprocal polynomials do not necessarily satisfy P(α) = P(β), unless P(α) = 0. Therefore my mistake was assuming that reciprocal polynomials satisfy P(x) = P(1/x) for all x, but actually, the condition P(x) = x^n P(1/x) only implies that P(1/x) = x^{-n} P(x), so unless n = 0, it's not the same as P(x) = P(1/x). Wait, but for reciprocal polynomials, the condition is that P(x) = x^n P(1/x), so that means that if you plug in x = α, then P(α) = α^n P(1/α) = α^n P(β). Therefore, unless α^n = 1, which it isn't, then P(α) and P(β) are related by a factor of α^n. Therefore, unless P(α) = 0, they won't be equal. Therefore, reciprocal polynomials do not necessarily satisfy P(α) = P(β). Therefore, my earlier reasoning was incorrect.\n\nHmm, so that approach is invalid. Back to the drawing board.\n\nLet me try a different approach. Since we need P(α) = P(β), and α and β are roots of the minimal polynomial x^4 -10x^2 +1, then perhaps we can consider that the difference P(α) - P(β) = 0. Since α and β are conjugate, perhaps this difference is in the ideal generated by the minimal polynomial of α, so that P(α) - P(β) is divisible by the minimal polynomial? Wait, but P(β) is not in Q, so the difference is in Q(α), but not necessarily in Z[α].\n\nAlternatively, since P has integer coefficients, then P(α) and P(β) are algebraic integers, since α is an algebraic integer (root of monic polynomial with integer coefficients). Similarly, β is an algebraic integer. Therefore, P(α) - P(β) is an algebraic integer, and since it's zero in the field Q(α), perhaps it must be zero in Z[α], but I'm not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe consider that since α and β satisfy the equation x^4 -10x^2 +1 =0, then any symmetric function in α and β can be expressed in terms of the coefficients of the minimal polynomial. Maybe we can consider that P must be expressible as a polynomial in terms of x + 1/x or something like that, given that α * β = (√3 + √2)(√3 - √2) = 3 - 2 = 1. Wait a second! Wait, α * β = (√3 + √2)(√3 - √2) = (√3)^2 - (√2)^2 = 3 - 2 = 1. Oh! That's important. So α * β = 1, so β = 1/α. Therefore, β is the reciprocal of α. Therefore, the product αβ = 1, so they are multiplicative inverses.\n\nTherefore, since β = 1/α, then the condition P(α) = P(β) is equivalent to P(α) = P(1/α). Therefore, for the polynomial P, we have P(α) = P(1/α). So, this is a functional equation for P at the point α. Since α is a root of the minimal polynomial x^4 -10x^2 +1, perhaps we can use this to derive conditions on the coefficients of P.\n\nLet me consider that P(x) - P(1/x) is a polynomial that has α as a root. Since α satisfies x^4 -10x^2 +1 = 0, then any polynomial expression in α can be reduced using this equation. Therefore, if P(x) - P(1/x) is divisible by the minimal polynomial of α, which is x^4 -10x^2 +1, then P(x) - P(1/x) is in the ideal generated by x^4 -10x^2 +1 in Z[x]. Therefore, P(x) - P(1/x) = (x^4 -10x^2 +1) * K(x) for some polynomial K(x) with integer coefficients.\n\nTherefore, for P to satisfy P(α) = P(1/α), it is necessary and sufficient that P(x) - P(1/x) is divisible by x^4 -10x^2 +1 in Z[x].\n\nTherefore, our condition is that P(x) - P(1/x) is divisible by x^4 -10x^2 +1. Therefore, we can write:\n\nP(x) - P(1/x) = (x^4 -10x^2 +1) * K(x),\n\nwhere K(x) is some polynomial with integer coefficients.\n\nSince we are looking for monic polynomials P with integer coefficients, let's suppose that P is of degree n. Then, the left-hand side, P(x) - P(1/x), has degree n (since the leading term of P(x) is x^n and the leading term of P(1/x) is x^{-n}, so when subtracted, the highest degree term is x^n). On the right-hand side, (x^4 -10x^2 +1) * K(x) has degree 4 + deg(K). Therefore, equating degrees, we must have that n = 4 + deg(K). Therefore, deg(K) = n - 4.\n\nTherefore, K(x) must be a polynomial of degree n - 4 with integer coefficients. Since we are looking for minimal degree P, we want the minimal n such that there exists a polynomial K(x) of degree n - 4 with integer coefficients making the equation hold.\n\nOur goal is to find the minimal n ≥ 1 (since nonconstant) such that this equation can be satisfied.\n\nLet me try small degrees and see if it's possible.\n\nStart with n = 1:\n\nIf n = 1, then P(x) is linear: P(x) = x + c, where c is an integer (since monic and integer coefficients). Then,\n\nP(x) - P(1/x) = (x + c) - (1/x + c) = x - 1/x.\n\nWe need this to be divisible by x^4 -10x^2 +1. But x - 1/x is a degree 1 polynomial (if we clear denominators, it becomes x^2 -1, which is degree 2). But x^4 -10x^2 +1 is degree 4, so x^2 -1 cannot be divisible by a degree 4 polynomial. So impossible. Therefore, n=1 is impossible.\n\nn = 2:\n\nLet P(x) be quadratic: P(x) = x^2 + a x + b, with a, b integers.\n\nThen P(x) - P(1/x) = (x^2 + a x + b) - (1/x^2 + a/x + b) = x^2 + a x + b - 1/x^2 - a/x - b = x^2 + a x - 1/x^2 - a/x.\n\nMultiply both sides by x^2 to eliminate denominators:\n\nx^2*(P(x) - P(1/x)) = x^4 + a x^3 - 1 - a x.\n\nThis must be divisible by x^4 -10x^2 +1. So,\n\nx^4 + a x^3 - a x -1 = (x^4 -10x^2 +1) * K(x) + remainder.\n\nBut since the left-hand side is degree 4 and the right-hand side (if K(x) is a constant) would be degree 4 if K(x) is degree 0. Let me see:\n\nSuppose K(x) is a constant polynomial, say K(x) = k, integer. Then,\n\n(x^4 -10x^2 +1)*k = k x^4 -10k x^2 +k.\n\nSet equal to left-hand side: x^4 + a x^3 - a x -1.\n\nTherefore,\n\nk x^4 -10k x^2 +k = x^4 + a x^3 - a x -1.\n\nEquate coefficients:\n\nx^4: k = 1,\n\nx^3: 0 = a,\n\nx^2: -10k = 0 ⇒ -10*1 = 0 ⇒ -10 = 0, which is impossible.\n\nTherefore, no solution with K(x) constant. If we allow K(x) to be of degree 1, then K(x) = k x + m, so:\n\n(x^4 -10x^2 +1)(k x + m) = k x^5 + m x^4 -10k x^3 -10m x^2 + k x + m.\n\nBut the left-hand side after multiplying by x^2 was x^4 + a x^3 - a x -1, which is degree 4, but the right-hand side here is degree 5. Therefore, this is impossible. Therefore, n=2 is impossible.\n\nn = 3:\n\nLet P(x) be cubic: P(x) = x^3 + a x^2 + b x + c, with a, b, c integers.\n\nCompute P(x) - P(1/x):\n\n= (x^3 + a x^2 + b x + c) - (1/x^3 + a/x^2 + b/x + c)\n\n= x^3 + a x^2 + b x + c - 1/x^3 - a/x^2 - b/x - c\n\n= x^3 + a x^2 + b x - 1/x^3 - a/x^2 - b/x.\n\nMultiply both sides by x^3 to eliminate denominators:\n\nx^3*(P(x) - P(1/x)) = x^6 + a x^5 + b x^4 - 1 - a x - b x^2.\n\nThis must be divisible by x^4 -10x^2 +1. Let me denote the left-hand side as L(x) = x^6 + a x^5 + b x^4 - b x^2 - a x -1.\n\nWe need L(x) = (x^4 -10x^2 +1)*K(x), where K(x) is a quadratic polynomial (since degree of L is 6, and divisor is degree 4, so quotient is degree 2). Let me set K(x) = d x^2 + e x + f, with d, e, f integers.\n\nThen,\n\n(x^4 -10x^2 +1)(d x^2 + e x + f) =\n\nx^4*(d x^2 + e x + f) -10x^2*(d x^2 + e x + f) +1*(d x^2 + e x + f)\n\n= d x^6 + e x^5 + f x^4 -10d x^4 -10e x^3 -10f x^2 + d x^2 + e x + f.\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n= d x^6 + e x^5 + (f -10d) x^4 + (-10e) x^3 + (-10f + d) x^2 + e x + f.\n\nSet this equal to L(x) = x^6 + a x^5 + b x^4 - b x^2 - a x -1.\n\nTherefore, equate coefficients:\n\n1. x^6: d = 1.\n\n2. x^5: e = a.\n\n3. x^4: f -10d = b ⇒ f -10(1) = b ⇒ f = b +10.\n\n4. x^3: -10e = 0 ⇒ -10e = 0 ⇒ e = 0.\n\nBut from x^5 coefficient, e = a, so a = 0.\n\n5. x^2: -10f + d = -b ⇒ -10f +1 = -b ⇒ -10f = -b -1 ⇒ 10f = b +1.\n\nBut from above, f = b +10, so substitute f into this equation:\n\n10*(b +10) = b +1 ⇒ 10b + 100 = b +1 ⇒ 9b = -99 ⇒ b = -11.\n\n6. x term: e = a ⇒ but e =0 and a=0, so that's consistent.\n\n7. Constant term: f = -1.\n\nBut from above, f = b +10, and b = -11, so f = -11 +10 = -1, which matches the constant term equation f = -1. Perfect.\n\nSo, putting this together:\n\nWe have d =1,\n\ne =0 ⇒ a =0,\n\nf = -1,\n\nand from b = -11,\n\nSo the coefficients are:\n\na =0,\n\nb = -11,\n\nc is free? Wait, hold on, in the original cubic polynomial P(x) = x^3 + a x^2 + b x + c, so the constant term c was not involved in the equations above because when we computed P(x) - P(1/x), the constants canceled out. Therefore, c can be any integer? Wait, let me check:\n\nWait, in the computation of P(x) - P(1/x), the constant terms canceled: +c - c =0. Therefore, the constant term c does not affect the equation. Therefore, in our equations above, c is arbitrary? However, the problem states that P must be monic with integer coefficients, but doesn't specify anything else about c. However, we also have that P must be in 𝒮, which requires that P(α) = P(β). However, in our derivation, we only enforced that P(x) - P(1/x) is divisible by x^4 -10x^2 +1, which is necessary but is it sufficient?\n\nWait, actually, if P(x) - P(1/x) is divisible by the minimal polynomial of α, then since α satisfies the minimal polynomial, then P(α) - P(1/α) = P(α) - P(β) is divisible by the minimal polynomial evaluated at α, which is zero. Therefore, P(α) - P(β) = 0. Therefore, the condition that P(x) - P(1/x) is divisible by the minimal polynomial is both necessary and sufficient for P(α) = P(β). Therefore, the equations we solved are sufficient.\n\nHowever, in our case, the constant term c is arbitrary? But then, if c is arbitrary, then there are infinitely many such cubics, but the problem states that Q is an element of 𝒮 with minimal degree, so we can choose c=0 to make it monic with minimal coefficients? Wait, but the problem says \"monic polynomials with integer coefficients\", so c must be integer, but there's no restriction other than that. However, since we're looking for minimal degree, and degree 3 is possible (as we just found a solution with degree 3), then perhaps the minimal degree is 3, and the constant term can be set to 0? Wait, but in the equations above, we found that with c being arbitrary, but in our equations for the coefficients of L(x) we didn't involve c, so c can be any integer. Therefore, to get a monic polynomial with integer coefficients of degree 3 satisfying the condition, we can set c to any integer, but the problem says \"nonconstant\", so c can be 0, but also other integers. However, the problem asks for the only possible value of Q(10) - Q(0). Since Q is any polynomial of minimal degree (which is 3) in 𝒮, then different choices of c would give different Q(10) - Q(0). But the problem says \"the only possible value\", implying that regardless of the choice of c, Q(10) - Q(0) is the same? Or perhaps c is determined by some other condition?\n\nWait a second, hold on. In our derivation, we only required that P(x) - P(1/x) is divisible by x^4 -10x^2 +1. However, in the process of setting up the equations, we found that the coefficients a, b are fixed (a=0, b=-11), but c is free. Therefore, there are multiple cubic polynomials satisfying the condition, differing by their constant term c. Therefore, unless there is an additional constraint, c can be any integer, and thus Q(10) - Q(0) would depend on c. However, the problem states that Q is an element of 𝒮 with minimal degree, and asks for \"the only possible value\" of Q(10) - Q(0). Therefore, perhaps my reasoning is missing a constraint that forces c to be zero?\n\nWait, let's recall that the polynomial must be monic with integer coefficients, but there is no additional constraint given in the problem. However, perhaps when we derived the equations, we actually did use the fact that P is monic? Wait, yes, in the case of cubic, we started with P(x) = x^3 + a x^2 + b x + c, so monic. Then, in the equations above, we found that a must be zero, b must be -11, and c is arbitrary. Therefore, the general solution is P(x) = x^3 -11x + c, with c ∈ ℤ. Therefore, indeed, there are infinitely many such polynomials of degree 3, differing by the constant term c. Therefore, Q(10) - Q(0) would be [10^3 -11*10 + c] - [0^3 -11*0 + c] = (1000 - 110 + c) - (0 - 0 + c) = (890 + c) - c = 890. Wait, that's interesting! The c terms cancel out. Therefore, no matter what c is, Q(10) - Q(0) is always 890. Therefore, even though there are multiple polynomials Q of degree 3 in 𝒮, all of them yield the same value for Q(10) - Q(0). Therefore, the answer is 890?\n\nWait, let me verify that computation again:\n\nGiven Q(x) = x^3 - 11x + c,\n\nthen Q(10) = 10^3 - 11*10 + c = 1000 - 110 + c = 890 + c,\n\nand Q(0) = 0^3 - 11*0 + c = c,\n\nso Q(10) - Q(0) = (890 + c) - c = 890.\n\nWow, so indeed, the constant term cancels out. Therefore, regardless of the value of c, the difference Q(10) - Q(0) is always 890. Therefore, even though there are infinitely many such polynomials Q of minimal degree (degree 3), they all give the same value for Q(10) - Q(0). Therefore, the only possible value is 890.\n\nBut before I get too excited, let me confirm that degree 3 is indeed achievable, and that there isn't a lower degree polynomial that I missed.\n\nEarlier, for n=1 and n=2, we saw that it was impossible. For n=3, we found a solution. Therefore, 3 is the minimal degree. Therefore, the minimal degree Q is degree 3, and for all such Q, Q(10) - Q(0) = 890.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 890, so boxed, it's \\boxed{890}.\n\nBut let me just check with a concrete example. Let me pick c=0, so Q(x) = x^3 -11x.\n\nCompute Q(10) - Q(0):\n\nQ(10) = 1000 - 110 = 890,\n\nQ(0) = 0,\n\nso 890 - 0 = 890.\n\nNow, take another c, say c=5.\n\nThen Q(x) = x^3 -11x +5,\n\nQ(10) = 1000 - 110 +5 = 895,\n\nQ(0) = 0 -0 +5 =5,\n\nso Q(10) - Q(0) = 895 -5 = 890. Yep, same result.\n\nAnother one: c = -3,\n\nQ(10) = 1000 -110 -3 = 887,\n\nQ(0) = -3,\n\nso 887 - (-3) = 890. Perfect.\n\nTherefore, indeed, regardless of c, the difference is 890. Therefore, the answer is 890.\n\nBut just to be thorough, let me check that this polynomial indeed satisfies P(α) = P(β). Let's compute P(α) and P(β) for Q(x) = x^3 -11x.\n\nFirst, recall that α = √3 + √2, β = √3 - √2.\n\nCompute Q(α) = α^3 - 11α.\n\nWe already computed α^3 earlier: α^3 = 9√3 + 11√2.\n\nTherefore, Q(α) = (9√3 + 11√2) - 11*(√3 + √2) = 9√3 + 11√2 -11√3 -11√2 = (9√3 -11√3) + (11√2 -11√2) = (-2√3) + 0 = -2√3.\n\nSimilarly, compute Q(β) = β^3 -11β.\n\nWe had β^3 = 9√3 -11√2.\n\nTherefore, Q(β) = (9√3 -11√2) -11*(√3 - √2) = 9√3 -11√2 -11√3 +11√2 = (9√3 -11√3) + (-11√2 +11√2) = (-2√3) + 0 = -2√3.\n\nTherefore, indeed Q(α) = Q(β) = -2√3. So it works.\n\nSimilarly, if we take c=5, then Q(x) = x^3 -11x +5,\n\nthen Q(α) = -2√3 +5,\n\nand Q(β) = -2√3 +5,\n\nso they are equal. Therefore, it works.\n\nTherefore, this confirms that the cubic polynomials of the form x^3 -11x + c satisfy the condition, and their Q(10)-Q(0) is always 890. Since degree 3 is minimal (as degrees 1 and 2 are impossible), this must be the minimal degree polynomial, and the only possible value is 890.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 890.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{890}\n</think>\n\nGiven the problem, we need to find the minimal degree monic polynomial \\( Q \\) with integer coefficients such that \\( Q(\\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{2}) = Q(\\sqrt{3} - \\sqrt{2}) \\), and then compute \\( Q(10) - Q(0) \\).\n\n1. **Identify the minimal polynomial of \\( \\alpha = \\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{2} \\)**:\n   - The minimal polynomial of \\( \\alpha \\) over \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\) is found to be \\( x^4 - 10x^2 + 1 \\).\n   - The conjugate of \\( \\alpha \\) is \\( \\beta = \\sqrt{3} - \\sqrt{2} \\), which satisfies the same minimal polynomial.\n\n2. **Condition on polynomial \\( P \\)**:\n   - The polynomial \\( P \\) must satisfy \\( P(\\alpha) = P(\\beta) \\).\n   - This implies \\( P(x) - P(1/x) \\) must be divisible by the minimal polynomial \\( x^4 - 10x^2 + 1 \\).\n\n3. **Determine the form of \\( P \\)**:\n   - For \\( P \\) to satisfy the condition, it must be a polynomial such that \\( P(x) - P(1/x) \\) is divisible by \\( x^4 - 10x^2 + 1 \\).\n   - Testing polynomials of increasing degree, we find that a cubic polynomial works.\n\n4. **Construct the cubic polynomial**:\n   - Let \\( P(x) = x^3 + ax^2 + bx + c \\).\n   - By equating coefficients after ensuring divisibility, we find \\( a = 0 \\), \\( b = -11 \\), and \\( c \\) is arbitrary.\n   - Thus, the polynomial is \\( P(x) = x^3 - 11x + c \\).\n\n5. **Verify the minimal degree**:\n   - Degrees 1 and 2 are impossible, confirming degree 3 is minimal.\n\n6. **Compute \\( Q(10) - Q(0) \\)**:\n   - For any such cubic polynomial \\( Q(x) = x^3 - 11x + c \\):\n     - \\( Q(10) = 10^3 - 11 \\cdot 10 + c = 890 + c \\)\n     - \\( Q(0) = c \\)\n     - Therefore, \\( Q(10) - Q(0) = (890 + c) - c = 890 \\).\n\nThus, the only possible value of \\( Q(10) - Q(0) \\) is \\(\\boxed{890}\\).","num_tokens":16767,"correct":true,"formatted":true},{"predicted_answer":"890","full_generation":"Okay, so I have this problem here: Let 𝒮 be the set of all nonconstant monic polynomials P with integer coefficients satisfying P(√3 + √2) = P(√3 − √2). If Q is an element of 𝒮 with minimal degree, compute the only possible value of Q(10) − Q(0). Alright, let me try to figure this out step by step.\n\nFirst, let me parse the problem. We are dealing with polynomials that are monic, meaning the leading coefficient is 1, and they have integer coefficients. They are nonconstant, so degree at least 1. The condition is that when you plug in √3 + √2 into the polynomial, you get the same result as when you plug in √3 − √2. And we need to find the minimal degree monic polynomial with integer coefficients that satisfies this condition, call it Q, and then compute Q(10) − Q(0).\n\nHmm, okay. So the key seems to be understanding what polynomials satisfy P(√3 + √2) = P(√3 − √2). Since √3 + √2 and √3 − √2 are conjugates in some sense, maybe the polynomial has some symmetry that makes it take the same value at these two conjugate inputs.\n\nI recall that for algebraic numbers, if a polynomial with integer coefficients takes the same value at two conjugate algebraic numbers, then perhaps that polynomial must be invariant under the automorphism that swaps those conjugates? Wait, but automorphisms of what? The field extension?\n\nLet me think. Let me recall that √3 + √2 is an element of the field ℚ(√2, √3), which is a degree 4 extension over ℚ, since ℚ(√2, √3) has basis {1, √2, √3, √6} over ℚ. The element α = √3 + √2 is in this field, and its conjugates are obtained by changing the signs of the square roots independently. Specifically, the conjugates of α over ℚ are:\n\nα₁ = √3 + √2,\n\nα₂ = √3 − √2,\n\nα₃ = −√3 + √2,\n\nα₄ = −√3 − √2.\n\nSo, these four elements are the conjugates of α over ℚ. Therefore, if we have a polynomial P with coefficients in ℚ that takes the same value at α and α₂, then perhaps P must be invariant under some subgroup of the Galois group of the extension ℚ(√2, √3)/ℚ?\n\nThe Galois group of ℚ(√2, √3) over ℚ is isomorphic to the Klein four-group, which is abelian, and has four elements, corresponding to the automorphisms that send √2 to ±√2 and √3 to ±√3, independently. So, the automorphisms are:\n\nσ₁: √2 ↦ √2, √3 ↦ √3 (identity),\n\nσ₂: √2 ↦ -√2, √3 ↦ √3,\n\nσ₃: √2 ↦ √2, √3 ↦ -√3,\n\nσ₄: √2 ↦ -√2, √3 ↦ -√3.\n\nNow, applying these automorphisms to α = √3 + √2, we get:\n\nσ₁(α) = σ₁(√3 + √2) = √3 + √2 = α,\n\nσ₂(α) = σ₂(√3) + σ₂(√2) = (-√3) + (-√2) = - (√3 + √2) = -α,\n\nσ₃(α) = σ₃(√3) + σ₃(√2) = (-√3) + √2 = -√3 + √2 = α₂,\n\nσ₄(α) = σ₄(√3) + σ₄(√2) = (-√3) + (-√2) = - (√3 + √2) = -α,\n\nWait, hold on, let me compute that again:\n\nWait, σ₃: √3 ↦ -√3, √2 ↦ √2, so σ₃(α) = σ₃(√3) + σ₃(√2) = (-√3) + √2 = √2 - √3, which is actually equal to - (√3 - √2) = -α₂, since α₂ = √3 - √2. Wait, √2 - √3 is the negative of √3 - √2, so yes, σ₃(α) = -α₂.\n\nSimilarly, σ₄(α) = σ₄(√3) + σ₄(√2) = (-√3) + (-√2) = - (√3 + √2) = -α.\n\nSo, in summary:\n\nσ₁(α) = α,\n\nσ₂(α) = -α,\n\nσ₃(α) = -α₂,\n\nσ₄(α) = -α.\n\nTherefore, the conjugates of α over ℚ are α, -α, α₂, -α₂? Wait, hold on:\n\nWait, actually, the conjugates should be all the images under the Galois group. Since the Galois group has four elements, each automorphism gives a conjugate. Let me list them:\n\nσ₁(α) = α,\n\nσ₂(α) = -α,\n\nσ₃(α) = √2 - √3 = - (√3 - √2) = -α₂,\n\nσ₄(α) = -√3 - √2 = - (α),\n\nWait, hold on, that's the same as σ₂(α). Wait, no, σ₄(α) is -√3 - √2, which is - (√3 + √2) = -α, so σ₄(α) = -α, same as σ₂(α). Wait, but that can't be, because σ₂ and σ₄ are different automorphisms. Wait, but maybe in this case, since we applied σ₂ and σ₄ to α, we both get -α?\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nWait, let's recast this. Let me compute all four images again carefully.\n\nGiven α = √3 + √2,\n\nσ₁: identity: √3 ↦ √3, √2 ↦ √2 ⇒ α ↦ √3 + √2 = α,\n\nσ₂: √2 ↦ -√2, √3 ↦ √3 ⇒ α ↦ √3 + (-√2) = √3 - √2 = α₂,\n\nWait, hold on, that contradicts my earlier thought. Wait, maybe I made a mistake here. Wait, hold on, perhaps I misapplied σ₂ and σ₃ earlier?\n\nWait, let me recall the automorphisms:\n\nσ₁: identity: fixes both √2 and √3,\n\nσ₂: sends √2 to -√2, but keeps √3 fixed,\n\nσ₃: sends √3 to -√3, but keeps √2 fixed,\n\nσ₄: sends both √2 and √3 to their negatives: √2 ↦ -√2, √3 ↦ -√3.\n\nTherefore, computing each automorphism on α:\n\nσ₁(α) = σ₁(√3) + σ₁(√2) = √3 + √2 = α,\n\nσ₂(α) = σ₂(√3) + σ₂(√2) = √3 + (-√2) = √3 - √2 = α₂,\n\nσ₃(α) = σ₃(√3) + σ₃(√2) = (-√3) + √2 = √2 - √3 = - (√3 - √2) = -α₂,\n\nσ₄(α) = σ₄(√3) + σ₄(√2) = (-√3) + (-√2) = - (√3 + √2) = -α.\n\nOkay, so in fact:\n\nσ₁(α) = α,\n\nσ₂(α) = α₂,\n\nσ₃(α) = -α₂,\n\nσ₄(α) = -α,\n\nSo the four conjugates of α over ℚ are α, α₂, -α₂, -α.\n\nTherefore, the minimal polynomial of α over ℚ is the minimal polynomial that has all these four as roots? Wait, no, actually, the minimal polynomial of α over ℚ is the monic polynomial of least degree with rational coefficients such that α is a root, and it must have all the conjugates as roots. Since the degree of the extension ℚ(α) over ℚ is equal to the degree of the minimal polynomial. Wait, but actually, the degree [ℚ(α):ℚ] might be less than 4? Hmm, but since ℚ(α) is contained in ℚ(√2, √3), which has degree 4 over ℚ, so the degree of ℚ(α) over ℚ must divide 4, so it's either 2 or 4. Hmm, perhaps it's 4? Let me check.\n\nSuppose that ℚ(α) has degree 4 over ℚ. Let's see:\n\nSuppose that α = √3 + √2. Let me compute α²:\n\nα² = (√3 + √2)² = (√3)² + 2√3√2 + (√2)² = 3 + 2√6 + 2 = 5 + 2√6.\n\nHmm, so α² = 5 + 2√6. Then, if we let β = α², then β = 5 + 2√6, so β - 5 = 2√6, then squaring both sides: (β - 5)^2 = (2√6)^2 ⇒ β² - 10β + 25 = 24 ⇒ β² - 10β + 1 = 0. Therefore, the minimal polynomial of β over ℚ is x² - 10x + 1. But β = α², so maybe we can find a relation for α?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the minimal polynomial of α can be found by considering that since α and α₂ are conjugates, and since α₂ = √3 - √2, then perhaps α + α₂ = (√3 + √2) + (√3 - √2) = 2√3,\n\nand α * α₂ = (√3 + √2)(√3 - √2) = (√3)^2 - (√2)^2 = 3 - 2 = 1.\n\nInteresting, so α * α₂ = 1. Therefore, α₂ = 1/α.\n\nSo, since α and α₂ are multiplicative inverses, and α + α₂ = 2√3.\n\nHmm. Let me see if I can find a polynomial equation for α.\n\nLet me let x = α = √3 + √2,\n\nthen x = √3 + √2,\n\nthen x - √3 = √2,\n\nthen squaring both sides: (x - √3)^2 = (√2)^2 ⇒ x² - 2√3 x + 3 = 2 ⇒ x² + 1 = 2√3 x,\n\nthen, squaring again to eliminate the square root:\n\n(x² + 1)^2 = (2√3 x)^2 ⇒ x⁴ + 2x² + 1 = 12x² ⇒ x⁴ - 10x² + 1 = 0.\n\nTherefore, the minimal polynomial of α over ℚ is x⁴ - 10x² + 1. So that's degree 4. Therefore, [ℚ(α):ℚ] = 4, so indeed the minimal polynomial is quartic.\n\nTherefore, the minimal polynomial of α is x⁴ - 10x² + 1, which is monic with integer coefficients, and it's the minimal one. So that tells me that any polynomial with rational coefficients that has α as a root must be divisible by this minimal polynomial.\n\nBut in our problem, we are dealing with polynomials P with integer coefficients, monic, nonconstant, such that P(α) = P(α₂). So, P(α) = P(α₂). Since α and α₂ are conjugates, this is a condition on the polynomial P.\n\nNow, in general, if a polynomial P with rational coefficients satisfies P(α) = P(β), where α and β are conjugate algebraic numbers, then perhaps P must be invariant under the automorphism that swaps α and β? But in this case, the automorphism that swaps α and α₂?\n\nWait, let me think again. Let me recall that if we have a polynomial P with coefficients in ℚ, then P(σ(α)) = σ(P(α)) for any automorphism σ in the Galois group. So if P(α) = P(α₂), then applying σ₂, which sends α to α₂, we get σ₂(P(α)) = P(σ₂(α)) = P(α₂) = P(α) = σ₂(P(α)). Hmm, so that might not necessarily give us much.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps if P(α) = P(α₂), then P(σ(α)) = P(σ(α₂)) for each automorphism σ? Wait, but actually, since P has coefficients in ℚ, then for any automorphism σ, σ(P(α)) = P(σ(α)). So if P(α) = P(α₂), then σ(P(α)) = σ(P(α₂)) = P(σ(α₂)). But since P(α) = P(α₂), then σ(P(α)) = P(σ(α₂)) = P(σ(α₂)), but I don't know if that helps.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can consider that if P(α) = P(α₂), then P(α) - P(α₂) = 0. Since α and α₂ are roots of the minimal polynomial x⁴ - 10x² + 1, which is irreducible over ℚ, perhaps the ideal generated by x⁴ - 10x² + 1 in ℚ[x] contains the polynomial P(x) - P(α₂)? Wait, but P is a polynomial with integer coefficients, so maybe we can think in terms of algebraic integers?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps a better approach is to consider that since P(α) = P(α₂), then α is a root of the polynomial P(x) - P(α₂), but since α₂ is another algebraic number, this might not be straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can consider that since α and α₂ are conjugates, and P takes the same value at both, then perhaps P must be 