t has three subgroups: trivial, cyclic of order 2, and the whole group. Wait, actually, in the Klein four-group, every element is its own inverse, so the subgroups are:\n\n- {identity},\n\n- {identity, σ₂},\n\n- {identity, σ₃},\n\n- {identity, σ₄},\n\n- {identity, σ₂, σ₃, σ₄} (the whole group).\n\nSo, the subgroup generated by σ₂ and σ₃ is actually the whole Galois group, because σ₂ and σ₃ generate all four elements? Wait, actually, if you have σ₂ and σ₃, then since σ₂ and σ₃ are both elements of order 2, and they commute (since the group is abelian), so the subgroup generated by σ₂ and σ₃ is the entire group, because you can get σ₄ as σ₂σ₃, as we saw earlier, and then σ₄ is also in the group.\n\nTherefore, the subgroup generated by σ₂ and σ₃ is the entire Galois group. Therefore, the orbit of α under the entire Galois group is {α, α₂, -α, -α₂} as we saw before.\n\nTherefore, if we want a polynomial P with integer coefficients such that P(α) = P(α₂), then perhaps P must be invariant under the subgroup generated by σ₂? Wait, because σ₂ sends α to α₂, so if we want P(α) = P(σ₂(α)) = P(α₂), then perhaps P must be invariant under σ₂? Similarly, if we want P(α) = P(σ₃(α)), but σ₃(α) = -α₂, so that would be a different condition.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the condition P(α) = P(α₂) is equivalent to P being invariant under the automorphism σ₂? Because σ₂(α) = α₂, so if P(σ₂(α)) = P(α), then that would mean P(α₂) = P(α), which is exactly the condition given. So, if P is invariant under σ₂, then P(σ₂(α)) = σ₂(P(α))? Wait, no, hold on.\n\nWait, actually, if P has coefficients in ℚ, then for any automorphism σ, σ(P(α)) = P(σ(α)). So, if we want P(σ(α)) = P(α), that is, P(σ(α)) = P(α), then that would require that for all σ in some subgroup, P(σ(α)) = P(α). In our case, we only require that for σ = σ₂, that P(σ₂(α)) = P(α). Which is equivalent to saying that P(α₂) = P(α). So, that is exactly our condition.\n\nTherefore, the condition is that P(σ₂(α)) = P(α), for σ₂ being the automorphism that sends √2 to -√2 and leaves √3 fixed. Therefore, in terms of the polynomial, this condition can be translated into some equation on the coefficients of P.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider that the polynomial P must satisfy P(x) = P(-x) when restricted to the field ℚ(√2, √3)? Wait, not exactly. Wait, let's think about what σ₂ does. The automorphism σ₂ sends √2 to -√2 and leaves √3 fixed. Therefore, it corresponds to the substitution x ↦ x with √2 replaced by -√2 and √3 remains the same. Therefore, if we consider the polynomial P(x), then applying σ₂ to P(α) gives P(σ₂(α)) = P(α₂). But we want P(α) = P(α₂), so that is equivalent to P(σ₂(α)) = P(α). Since P has coefficients in ℚ, then this condition is equivalent to P being invariant under the automorphism σ₂? Wait, not exactly. Wait, if P is invariant under σ₂, then P(σ₂(α)) = σ₂(P(α))? Wait no, let me recall that if P has coefficients in ℚ, then σ₂(P(α)) = P(σ₂(α)). So, if we require that σ₂(P(α)) = P(α), then that would mean that P(σ₂(α)) = P(α). So that is exactly the condition we have. Therefore, the condition that P(σ₂(α)) = P(α) is equivalent to σ₂(P(α)) = P(σ₂(α)) = P(α), so that would require that σ₂(P(α)) = P(α). Since σ₂ is an automorphism, this would mean that P(α) is fixed by σ₂. However, P(α) is an algebraic number, and σ₂ acts on it, so unless P(α) is in the fixed field of σ₂, which is ℚ(√3), since σ₂ fixes ℚ(√3) and sends √2 to -√2. Therefore, if P(α) is in ℚ(√3), then it would be fixed by σ₂. But α = √3 + √2, so unless P(α) is in ℚ(√3), but P has integer coefficients, so P(α) is in ℚ(√3, √2) = ℚ(√2, √3), but perhaps it could be in ℚ(√3) if the √2 terms cancel out?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps instead of getting bogged down in algebraic number theory, I can approach this problem by considering that since P(√3 + √2) = P(√3 − √2), then the polynomial P(x) must satisfy P(x) = P( (something) ), but with x = √3 + √2, so perhaps we can set y = √3 + √2, and then note that √3 - √2 = 1/y, as we saw earlier because (√3 + √2)(√3 - √2) = 1. Therefore, √3 - √2 = 1/(√3 + √2) = 1/y. Therefore, the condition is that P(y) = P(1/y). So, the polynomial P(x) satisfies P(x) = P(1/x) when evaluated at x = y = √3 + √2. But does this have to hold for all x, or only at that particular x?\n\nHmm, that's a crucial point. The problem states that P(√3 + √2) = P(√3 - √2), which is P(y) = P(1/y). But does this condition force P(x) = P(1/x) for all x, or only at that specific x?\n\nProbably only at that specific x. Because otherwise, if it had to hold for all x, then P(x) - P(1/x) would be the zero polynomial, which would impose that all coefficients satisfy certain conditions. But since the problem only requires that it holds at that particular x, we can't conclude that P(x) = P(1/x) identically.\n\nTherefore, maybe we need to construct a polynomial P such that when we plug in x = y = √3 + √2, we get P(y) = P(1/y). How can we ensure that?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider that since y and 1/y are conjugates in some sense, and the polynomial must take equal values at conjugate points. To handle this, maybe we can consider the polynomial Q(x) = P(x) - P(1/x), and then set Q(y) = 0. Since y satisfies some equation, perhaps we can use that to factor Q(x)?\n\nAlternatively, let's think of P(x) as a polynomial with integer coefficients, and we want P(y) = P(1/y). Let me write P(x) = a_n x^n + a_{n-1} x^{n-1} + ... + a_1 x + a_0, with a_n = 1 since it's monic.\n\nThen, P(1/x) = a_n / x^n + a_{n-1}/x^{n-1} + ... + a_1 /x + a_0.\n\nIf we multiply both sides by x^n to eliminate denominators, we get:\n\nx^n P(1/x) = a_n + a_{n-1} x + ... + a_1 x^{n-1} + a_0 x^n.\n\nTherefore, the equation P(x) = P(1/x) would imply that P(x) - x^n P(1/x) = 0. But again, that's only if we require the equality to hold for all x ≠ 0, which is a stronger condition. Since in our case, the equality only needs to hold at x = y = √3 + √2, which is a specific algebraic number, we can't directly use that equation for all x.\n\nHmm. Maybe instead, let's consider that if P(y) = P(1/y), then (y - 1/y) divides P(y) - P(1/y). But since y ≠ 1/y (because y = √3 + √2 ≈ 3.146, and 1/y ≈ 0.318), so y - 1/y is not zero, so maybe we can write that P(y) - P(1/y) is divisible by (y - 1/y). But since we are working with polynomials over ℚ, perhaps we can use the minimal polynomial of y to express this condition?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider the field ℚ(y) and the relation that y satisfies, which is y⁴ - 10y² + 1 = 0, as we found earlier. Therefore, any polynomial in y can be reduced using this equation. Since we need P(y) = P(1/y), and since 1/y = y₂ = √3 - √2, which also satisfies the same minimal polynomial, because if we plug y₂ into x⁴ - 10x² + 1, we get (y₂)^4 - 10(y₂)^2 + 1 = (1/y)^4 - 10(1/y)^2 + 1. Let me compute that:\n\nLet me compute (1/y)^4 - 10(1/y)^2 + 1. Since y satisfies y⁴ -10y² +1 =0, then y⁴ = 10y² -1, so 1/y⁴ = 1/(10y² -1). Hmm, this might not be helpful. Alternatively, since 1/y = y₂, and y₂ also satisfies the same minimal polynomial: Let me check:\n\nLet me compute (y₂)^4 - 10(y₂)^2 + 1. Since y₂ = √3 - √2,\n\n(y₂)^2 = (√3 - √2)^2 = 3 - 2√6 + 2 = 5 - 2√6,\n\nthen (y₂)^4 = (5 - 2√6)^2 = 25 - 20√6 + 24 = 49 - 20√6,\n\nthen (y₂)^4 - 10(y₂)^2 + 1 = (49 - 20√6) - 10*(5 - 2√6) + 1 = 49 - 20√6 - 50 + 20√6 + 1 = (49 - 50 + 1) + (-20√6 + 20√6) = 0 + 0 = 0. So yes, y₂ also satisfies the same minimal polynomial x⁴ -10x² +1 =0. Therefore, both y and y₂ are roots of the same minimal polynomial.\n\nTherefore, if we have a polynomial P such that P(y) = P(y₂), then perhaps P is in the intersection of the kernels of the functionals that send P(y) to P(y₂) - P(y). Hmm, maybe this is too abstract.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider that since y and y₂ are roots of x⁴ -10x² +1, then any polynomial that takes equal values at y and y₂ must be in the ideal generated by (x⁴ -10x² +1) and (x - y₂) - (x - y)? Wait, not exactly.\n\nAlternatively, since y and y₂ are roots of the same minimal polynomial, then the difference P(y) - P(y₂) must be a multiple of the minimal polynomial? Not sure.\n\nWait, perhaps another approach: Let me suppose that Q is a monic polynomial with integer coefficients, minimal degree, such that Q(√3 + √2) = Q(√3 - √2). Let me try to construct such a polynomial.\n\nLet me denote α = √3 + √2, and β = √3 - √2 = 1/α as we saw before.\n\nWe need Q(α) = Q(β). Since Q is a polynomial with integer coefficients, and monic, and minimal degree.\n\nPerhaps the simplest such polynomials are those that are symmetric in some way with respect to α and β. Since β = 1/α, maybe Q(x) is a function that is symmetric in x and 1/x? But as above, that would require Q(x) = Q(1/x), but only at x = α, not necessarily everywhere.\n\nAlternatively, since α and β are roots of x⁴ -10x² +1, perhaps Q must be a function that is constant on the orbit {α, β, -α, -β}? Because if we have Q(α) = Q(β), and also perhaps Q(-α) = Q(-β), etc., then Q would be constant on the entire orbit. If that's the case, then Q must be a function that is invariant under the Galois group action, so that Q(σ(α)) = Q(α) for all σ in Gal(ℚ(α)/ℚ). Since the orbit of α under the Galois group is {α, β, -α, -β}, then if Q is invariant under the Galois group, then it must satisfy Q(σ(α)) = Q(α) for all σ, so in particular Q(β) = Q(α), Q(-α) = Q(α), Q(-β) = Q(α). Therefore, if Q is Galois-invariant, then it would satisfy the condition. However, the converse is not necessarily true: the condition Q(α) = Q(β) is weaker than being Galois-invariant, since it only requires invariance under σ₂, not under the entire Galois group.\n\nTherefore, the set 𝒮 includes all polynomials that are invariant under the subgroup generated by σ₂, which is the subgroup {identity, σ₂}, since σ₂ has order 2. The subgroup {identity, σ₂} corresponds to the automorphisms that send √2 to ±√2 and fix √3. Therefore, the fixed field of this subgroup is ℚ(√3), since it's the set of elements fixed by σ₂, which requires that the element is in ℚ(√3) (since σ₂ acts on √2 by flipping its sign, so to be fixed, the element must not depend on √2).\n\nTherefore, if Q is invariant under σ₂, then Q(σ₂(α)) = Q(α), which is exactly the condition we have. Therefore, the polynomials in 𝒮 are precisely the monic polynomials with integer coefficients that are invariant under the automorphism σ₂, i.e., σ₂(Q(x)) = Q(x) for all x. Wait, is that the case?\n\nWait, if Q is invariant under σ₂, then σ₂(Q(x)) = Q(σ₂(x)) = Q(x) for all x. Therefore, that would mean that Q is a polynomial with coefficients in the fixed field of σ₂, which is ℚ(√3). However, our polynomials Q are required to have integer coefficients. Therefore, if Q is invariant under σ₂ and has integer coefficients, then it must be that Q is actually in ℤ[√3][x], but with coefficients in ℤ, so that when we apply σ₂, which sends √2 to -√2 and √3 to √3, the polynomial remains the same. Therefore, the coefficients of Q must be such that any term involving √2 would have to be canceled out. Wait, but since Q has integer coefficients, it cannot have √2 in it. Wait, maybe I need to think of Q as a polynomial with integer coefficients such that when we apply σ₂ to it, we get the same polynomial. Since σ₂ acts on the variable x by replacing x with σ₂(x), which is σ₂(x) = x with √2 replaced by -√2. But since Q has integer coefficients, which are invariant under √2 ↦ -√2, then σ₂(Q(x)) = Q(σ₂(x)). For this to equal Q(x) for all x, we must have Q(σ₂(x)) = Q(x) for all x. Therefore, this is equivalent to Q being invariant under the substitution x ↦ x with √2 replaced by -√2. But since Q has integer coefficients, this substitution doesn't affect the coefficients, so actually, Q(x) is already invariant under that substitution? Wait, hold on.\n\nWait, let me clarify: If Q has integer coefficients, then replacing x by σ₂(x) in Q(x) is the same as replacing x by x with √2 replaced by -√2 in the expression of Q(x). However, since Q(x) is