with some other construction?\n\nWait, let me think differently. Suppose we let P(x) be any polynomial such that P(α) = P(β). Then, the difference P(x) - P(y) has roots at x = α and x = β when y is fixed? Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, consider that since α and β are roots of m(x) = x⁴ -10x² +1, then if we define the polynomial m(x) = x⁴ -10x² +1, then any polynomial that is a function of m(x) will satisfy P(α) = P(β) = P(0) if P is a function of m(x). Wait, but m(α) = 0, so if P is a polynomial in m(x), then P(α) = P(0), similarly P(β) = P(0). Therefore, such polynomials would satisfy P(α) = P(β). For example, P(x) = m(x) + 1 would give P(α) = 1 and P(β) = 1, so that works. But those are degree 4, which is higher than 3, so not minimal.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the minimal polynomial is indeed degree 3 as found.\n\nAlternatively, is there a quadratic polynomial that is not of the form x² + a x + b, but something else? Wait, quadratic polynomials must be of degree 2, so they have to be monic quadratic: x² + a x + b. We already saw that for that case, the coefficients lead to a contradiction unless a is non-integer, so no.\n\nAlternatively, maybe a cubic polynomial that's not of the form x³ -11x + c? But according to the earlier analysis, the only cubic polynomials that satisfy the condition are those with a=0, b=-11, and any c. So all such polynomials are of that form.\n\nTherefore, I think my conclusion is correct: the minimal degree is 3, and Q(10) - Q(0) = 890.\n\nWait, but hold on a moment. Let me check the problem statement again: It says \"nonconstant monic polynomials P with integer coefficients satisfying P(√3 + √2) = P(√3 - √2)\". So, in our solution, the polynomial x³ -11x is indeed nonconstant, monic, integer coefficients, and satisfies the condition. So that's valid.\n\nBut I just want to be 100% sure that there isn't a quadratic polynomial that I overlooked. Let me try to think differently. Suppose that instead of requiring P(α) = P(β), maybe there is a quadratic polynomial where P(α) - P(β) is a multiple of m(x), but since m(x) is quartic, that might not help.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider that since α and β satisfy α + β = 2√3 and αβ = 1, as we saw earlier.\n\nSuppose that we let P(x) be a quadratic polynomial such that P(α) = P(β). Let me denote P(x) = x² + a x + b.\n\nThen, as before, P(α) - P(β) = (α² - β²) + a(α - β) = (α - β)(α + β + a) = 0.\n\nSince α ≠ β, this requires α + β + a = 0 ⇒ a = - (α + β) = -2√3, which is not integer. Therefore, impossible. So quadratic is impossible.\n\nTherefore, yes, cubic is indeed minimal. So the answer is 890.\n\nWait, but hold on, the problem says \"the only possible value of Q(10) - Q(0)\". Since all minimal degree polynomials give the same value, then that's the only possible value. So 890 is the answer.\n\nHowever, just to be thorough, let me see if there is a monic polynomial of degree 3 with different coefficients that also satisfies the condition but gives a different Q(10) - Q(0). But according to our analysis above, the only possible cubic polynomials are of the form x³ -11x + c, so their difference Q(10) - Q(0) is always 890, regardless of c. Therefore, there is only one possible value, which is 890. So that must be the answer.\n\nBut just to be extra cautious, let me think if there could be another cubic polynomial not of that form. Suppose someone tried to set a ≠ 0. But according to the equation we had earlier for the cubic case:\n\nα² + αβ + β² + a(α + β) + b = 0,\n\nwe had α² + αβ + β² = 11,\n\nα + β = 2√3,\n\nso 11 + a*(2√3) + b = 0.\n\nThis equation requires that the irrational term 2√3 * a must cancel out, which can only happen if a = 0, because otherwise you can't get rid of the irrational part with integer a and b. Therefore, a must be zero, and then b must be -11. Therefore, there are no other solutions. Therefore, the only cubic polynomials are those with a=0, b=-11, and any c. Therefore, indeed, they all have the same Q(10) - Q(0).\n\nTherefore, I think I can be confident that the answer is 890.\n\nWait, but hold on, the problem says \"nonconstant monic polynomials P with integer coefficients\". The polynomial x³ - 11x is indeed nonconstant, monic, integer coefficients, and satisfies the condition. So that's in S, and it's degree 3, which we've shown is minimal. Therefore, Q is this polynomial (or any associate, but since we can choose c=0 for simplicity), and then Q(10) - Q(0) is 890. So the answer is 890.\n\nBut wait, hold on, just to make sure I didn't make an arithmetic error in calculating Q(10):\n\nQ(x) = x³ - 11x,\n\nso Q(10) = 10³ - 11*10 = 1000 - 110 = 890,\n\nQ(0) = 0³ - 11*0 = 0,\n\nso 890 - 0 = 890. That seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, if I take c = 5, then Q(x) = x³ -11x +5,\n\nthen Q(10) = 1000 - 110 +5 = 895,\n\nQ(0) = 5,\n\nso 895 - 5 = 890. Yep.\n\nOkay, so that seems consistent.\n\nTherefore, after all this, I think the answer is 890. But just to be 100% sure, let me see if there's another way someone might approach this problem and get a different answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the minimal polynomial is degree 4? For example, the minimal polynomial of α is quartic, so perhaps someone might think that any polynomial in S must be a multiple of the minimal polynomial? But no, because the condition is weaker: it doesn't require that P(α) = 0, just that P(α) = P(β). Since β is another root of the minimal polynomial, then if P is the minimal polynomial, then P(α)=P(β)=0, which satisfies the condition. But there are other polynomials that satisfy P(α)=P(β) without being the minimal polynomial.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is expecting the minimal polynomial of α, which is quartic, so degree 4, leading to a different answer? Let me see what would happen in that case.\n\nSuppose someone thought that the minimal polynomial is required, so Q(x) = x⁴ -10x² +1, then Q(10) - Q(0) would be (10⁴ -10*10² +1) - (0 - 0 +1) = (10000 - 1000 +1) -1 = (9001) -1 = 9000. But 9000 is different from 890, so that would be wrong, but maybe that's a trap if you misunderstood the question.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem requires that P(√3 +√2) = P(√3 -√2) = 0, but the problem doesn't say that. It just says they are equal, not necessarily zero. So the quartic polynomial would work, but it's not minimal.\n\nTherefore, given the problem as stated, the minimal degree is 3, so 890 is the answer.\n\nTherefore, after careful consideration and checking, I believe the answer is 890.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{890}\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem, we need to find the minimal degree monic polynomial \\( Q \\) with integer coefficients such that \\( Q(\\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{2}) = Q(\\sqrt{3} - \\sqrt{2}) \\), and then compute \\( Q(10) - Q(0) \\).\n\n1. **Identify the key elements**:\n   - Let \\( \\alpha = \\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{2} \\) and \\( \\beta = \\sqrt{3} - \\sqrt{2} \\).\n   - These are conjugates and roots of the minimal polynomial \\( m(x) = x^4 - 10x^2 + 1 \\).\n\n2. **Determine the condition for \\( P \\)**:\n   - \\( P \\) must satisfy \\( P(\\alpha) = P(\\beta) \\).\n   - This implies \\( P \\) is invariant under the automorphism \\( \\tau \\) that swaps \\( \\sqrt{2} \\) and \\( -\\sqrt{2} \\).\n\n3. **Construct polynomials of minimal degree**:\n   - **Degree 2**: Impossible because it requires non-integer coefficients.\n   - **Degree 3**: Consider \\( P(x) = x^3 + ax^2 + bx + c \\). Setting \\( P(\\alpha) = P(\\beta) \\) leads to the equation \\( 11 + 2a\\sqrt{3} + b = 0 \\). This requires \\( a = 0 \\) and \\( b = -11 \\), giving \\( P(x) = x^3 - 11x + c \\).\n\n4. **Verify the cubic polynomial**:\n   - For \\( P(x) = x^3 - 11x \\), compute \\( P(\\alpha) \\) and \\( P(\\beta) \\):\n     - \\( \\alpha^3 = 9\\sqrt{3} + 11\\sqrt{2} \\)\n     - \\( \\beta^3 = 9\\sqrt{3} - 11\\sqrt{2} \\)\n     - \\( P(\\alpha) = -2\\sqrt{3} \\) and \\( P(\\beta) = -2\\sqrt{3} \\), confirming \\( P(\\alpha) = P(\\beta) \\).\n\n5. **Calculate \\( Q(10) - Q(0) \\)**:\n   - For \\( Q(x) = x^3 - 11x \\):\n     - \\( Q(10) = 10^3 - 11 \\cdot 10 = 1000 - 110 = 890 \\)\n     - \\( Q(0) = 0 \\)\n     - Thus, \\( Q(10) - Q(0) = 890 - 0 = 890 \\).\n\n6. **Conclusion**:\n   - The minimal degree polynomial is cubic, and all such polynomials of this form yield the same result for \\( Q(10) - Q(0) \\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{890}\\).","num_tokens":16928,"correct":true,"formatted":true},{"predicted_answer":"890","full_generation":"Okay, so I have this problem here: Let S be the set of all nonconstant monic polynomials P with integer coefficients satisfying P(√3 + √2) = P(√3 - √2). If Q is an element of S with minimal degree, compute the only possible value of Q(10) - Q(0). Hmm, alright, let me try to unpack this and figure it out step by step.\n\nFirst, let me recall what a monic polynomial is. A monic polynomial is one where the leading coefficient (the coefficient of the highest power of x) is 1. So, for example, x² + 3x + 5 is monic, but 2x² + 3x + 4 is not. Since Q is in S, it's a monic polynomial with integer coefficients, nonconstant, and it satisfies that when you plug in √3 + √2 into Q, you get the same result as when you plug in √3 - √2 into Q. So, Q(√3 + √2) = Q(√3 - √2). And we need to find the minimal degree monic polynomial in S, call it Q, and then compute Q(10) - Q(0).\n\nAlright, so first, maybe I should think about what it means for Q(√3 + √2) to equal Q(√3 - √2). Since √3 + √2 and √3 - √2 are conjugates in some sense? Let me see. Let me denote α = √3 + √2 and β = √3 - √2. So, α and β are conjugates. In fact, α is a root of some quadratic equation, and β is the other root?\n\nLet me check: Let me compute α and β. Let α = √3 + √2 ≈ 1.732 + 1.414 ≈ 3.146, and β = √3 - √2 ≈ 1.732 - 1.414 ≈ 0.318. So, they are distinct real numbers. But they might be roots of a quadratic with integer coefficients. Let me try to find the minimal polynomial of α over the rationals.\n\nLet me set x = α = √3 + √2. Then, let me square both sides to eliminate the square roots. So, x = √3 + √2, so x² = (√3 + √2)² = (√3)² + 2*√3*√2 + (√2)² = 3 + 2√6 + 2 = 5 + 2√6.\n\nHmm, so x² = 5 + 2√6. Hmm, so then, if I want to eliminate the square root, I can isolate it and square again. Let me rearrange the equation:\n\nx² - 5 = 2√6.\n\nNow, square both sides again:\n\n(x² - 5)² = (2√6)²\n\nSo, x⁴ - 10x² + 25 = 4*6\n\nThus, x⁴ - 10x² + 25 = 24\n\nThen, x⁴ - 10x² + 1 = 0.\n\nSo, the minimal polynomial of α = √3 + √2 over the rationals is x⁴ - 10x² + 1. Let me verify that.\n\nLet me compute (√3 + √2)^4 - 10*(√3 + √2)^2 + 1. Wait, but actually, since we derived it through algebraic steps, assuming that we squared correctly and didn't make any errors, it should hold. Let me check with x = α:\n\nWe had x = √3 + √2, so x² = 5 + 2√6, then x⁴ = (x²)² = (5 + 2√6)^2 = 25 + 20√6 + 24 = 49 + 20√6.\n\nThen, x⁴ - 10x² + 1 = (49 + 20√6) - 10*(5 + 2√6) + 1 = 49 + 20√6 - 50 - 20√6 + 1 = (49 - 50 + 1) + (20√6 - 20√6) = 0 + 0 = 0. Perfect, that works. Similarly, if I plug in β = √3 - √2, let's see:\n\nLet me compute β²: (√3 - √2)^2 = (√3)^2 - 2*√3*√2 + (√2)^2 = 3 - 2√6 + 2 = 5 - 2√6.\n\nThen β⁴ = (β²)^2 = (5 - 2√6)^2 = 25 - 20√6 + 24 = 49 - 20√6.\n\nThen β⁴ - 10β² + 1 = (49 - 20√6) - 10*(5 - 2√6) + 1 = 49 - 20√6 - 50 + 20√6 + 1 = (49 - 50 + 1) + (-20√6 + 20√6) = 0 + 0 = 0. Perfect again. So, indeed, the minimal polynomial over ℚ of both α and β is x⁴ - 10x² + 1.\n\nTherefore, α and β are roots of this quartic polynomial, which is irreducible over ℚ? Wait, but let me check if that quartic is irreducible over ℚ. Since we derived it as the minimal polynomial, it should be irreducible, but let me confirm quickly.\n\nSuppose that f(x) = x⁴ - 10x² + 1 is reducible over ℚ. Then it would factor into polynomials of lower degree with integer coefficients. Since it's a quartic, it could factor into quadratics or into a cubic and a linear term. But since it has no roots in ℚ (by rational root theorem: possible roots would be ±1, but plugging in x=1: 1 - 10 + 1 = -8 ≠ 0; x=-1: 1 - 10 + 1 = -8 ≠ 0; so no linear factors). So, if it factors, it must factor into quadratics. Let me see if that's possible.\n\nSuppose f(x) = (x² + a x + b)(x² + c x + d), where a, b, c, d are integers. Then, multiplying out, we have:\n\nx⁴ + (a + c)x³ + (b + d + a c)x² + (a d + b c)x + b d.\n\nSet equal to x⁴ - 10x² + 1. So, equate coefficients:\n\n1. Coefficient of x⁴: 1 = 1, okay.\n\n2. Coefficient of x³: a + c = 0.\n\n3. Coefficient of x²: b + d + a c = -10.\n\n4. Coefficient of x: a d + b c = 0.\n\n5. Constant term: b d = 1.\n\nSo, let me see. From equation 5: b d = 1. Since b and d are integers, possible solutions are (b, d) = (1,1) or (b, d) = (-1, -1).\n\nCase 1: b = 1, d = 1.\n\nThen, from equation 2: a + c = 0 ⇒ c = -a.\n\nFrom equation 4: a d + b c = a*1 + 1*(-a) = a - a = 0. So equation 4 is satisfied automatically.\n\nFrom equation 3: b + d + a c = 1 + 1 + a*(-a) = 2 - a² = -10 ⇒ 2 - a² = -10 ⇒ -a² = -12 ⇒ a² = 12 ⇒ a = ±√12, which is not integer. So no solution here.\n\nCase 2: b = -1, d = -1.\n\nThen, equation 2: c = -a.\n\nEquation 4: a d + b c = a*(-1) + (-1)*(-a) = -a + a = 0. So equation 4 is satisfied.\n\nEquation 3: b + d + a c = (-1) + (-1) + a*(-a) = -2 - a² = -10 ⇒ -a² = -8 ⇒ a² = 8 ⇒ a = ±√8, which is not integer. So again, no solution.\n\nTherefore, f(x) is irreducible over ℚ. So, the minimal polynomial over ℚ for α = √3 + √2 is indeed quartic, degree 4. So, that's good to know.\n\nNow, going back to the problem. We have a polynomial Q in S, which is monic, integer coefficients, nonconstant, and satisfies Q(α) = Q(β), where α = √3 + √2 and β = √3 - √2. We need to find the minimal degree such polynomial Q, and then compute Q(10) - Q(0).\n\nHmm. So, the key idea here is probably that if a polynomial with integer coefficients takes the same value at α and β, then α and β must be related in some way with respect to the polynomial. Since Q has integer coefficients, and α and β are conjugates over ℚ, perhaps Q must be invariant under the automorphism that swaps α and β?\n\nWait, actually, in field theory terms, the field extension ℚ(α) over ℚ has degree 4, since the minimal polynomial is degree 4, as we saw. The Galois group of this extension is isomorphic to the Klein four-group, I believe. Let me recall: The splitting field of x⁴ -10x² + 1 over ℚ is ℚ(α, √6), since we have √3 and √2 in there? Wait, actually, let me think: α = √3 + √2, so if we have α, we can get √3 + √2, and then perhaps we can get √3 and √2 individually?\n\nWait, actually, let me see: Let me compute α + β = (√3 + √2) + (√3 - √2) = 2√3, and α - β = 2√2. So, if we know α and β, then we can get √3 and √2 as (α + β)/2 and (α - β)/2, respectively. Therefore, the field ℚ(α) actually contains √3 and √2, so ℚ(α) = ℚ(√3, √2). Since ℚ(√3, √2) has degree 4 over ℚ, because [ℚ(√3):ℚ] = 2, and then [ℚ(√3, √2):ℚ(√3)] = 2, since √2 is not in ℚ(√3), so the degree is 4. So, the Galois group of this extension is indeed the Klein four-group, since it's abelian and has four elements? Wait, the Galois group of ℚ(√3, √2) over ℚ is isomorphic to the Klein four-group because the automorphisms are determined by where they send √3 and √2.\n\nSpecifically, the automorphisms are:\n\n1. The identity: σ₁: √3 ↦ √3, √2 ↦ √2.\n\n2. σ₂: √3 ↦ -√3, √2 ↦ √2.\n\n3. σ₃: √3 ↦ √3, √2 ↦ -√2.\n\n4. σ₄: √3 ↦ -√3, √2 ↦ -√2.\n\nEach of these automorphisms is an automorphism of ℚ(√3, √2) that fixes ℚ, and they form a group under composition isomorphic to the Klein four-group, since each non-identity element has order 2.\n\nNow, in this context, since α = √3 + √2, then under the automorphisms:\n\nσ₁(α) = α,\n\nσ₂(α) = -√3 + √2,\n\nσ₃(α) = √3 - √2 = β,\n\nσ₄(α) = -√3 - √2.\n\nWait, so actually, the automorphism σ₃ sends α to β, and σ₄ sends α to -α? Let me check:\n\nσ₃(α) = σ₃(√3 + √2) = σ₃(√3) + σ₃(√2) = √3 + (-√2) = √3 - √2 = β. Yes, that's right.\n\nSimilarly, σ₄(α) = σ₄(√3) + σ₄(√2) = (-√3) + (-√2) = - (√3 + √2) = -α.\n\nSo, in particular, σ₃ swaps α and β, while σ₂ sends α to -√3 + √2, which is not β, but another element. Similarly, σ₄ sends α to -α, and so on.\n\nTherefore, the key automorphism here that swaps α and β is σ₃. So, if we have a polynomial Q with integer coefficients, then Q(α) must equal Q(β) if and only if Q is invariant under the automorphism σ₃. Because σ₃(α) = β, so if Q is invariant under σ₃, then Q(α) = Q(σ₃(α)) = Q(β). Conversely, if Q(α) = Q(β), then since σ₃ fixes ℚ, but swaps α and β, then Q must be invariant under σ₃. Wait, but does that hold?\n\nWait, actually, more precisely, if a polynomial Q with coefficients in ℚ (or in ℤ) satisfies Q(σ(α)) = Q(α) for an automorphism σ, then Q is said to be invariant under σ. But in our case, we have that Q(α) = Q(β) = Q(σ₃(α)), so that means that Q composed with σ₃ fixes α, but since σ₃ is an automorphism, if Q(σ₃(α)) = Q(α), then for all x in the field, Q(σ₃(x)) = Q(x), because polynomials with coefficients in the fixed field (here, ℚ) are invariant under the automorphism if and only if they satisfy Q(σ(x)) = Q(x) for all x in the field. Wait, is that the case?\n\nHmm, actually, more precisely, if a polynomial with coefficients in ℚ is invariant under an automorphism σ, meaning that for all x in the field, σ(Q(x)) = Q(σ(x)), but if we require that σ(Q(x)) = Q(x) for all x, then that would mean that Q is fixed by σ as a function. But here, we only know that Q(σ(α)) = Q(α), but not necessarily for all x. Hmm, so maybe my previous thought is not exactly precise.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the condition Q(α) = Q(β) is equivalent to Q being invariant under the automorphism σ₃, but only on the specific element α? Hmm, but since σ₃ is an automorphism of the entire field, if Q is a polynomial with coefficients in ℚ, then Q(σ₃(x)) = σ₃(Q(x)) only if Q has coefficients in the fixed field, which it does (since coefficients are in ℚ). Wait, actually, more precisely, for any polynomial Q with coefficients in ℚ, we have σ₃(Q(x)) = Q(σ₃(x)), because the coefficients are fixed by σ₃. Therefore, if σ₃(Q(α)) = Q(σ₃(α)) = Q(β), but we are given that Q(α) = Q(β), so that would mean that σ₃(Q(α)) = Q(α). Therefore, σ₃ fixes Q(α). But since α is in the field, and σ₃ is an automorphism, this would imply that Q(α) is fixed by σ₃. However, unless Q(α) is in ℚ, which it might not be, but even so, the point is that the condition Q(α) = Q(β) is equivalent to σ₃(Q(α)) = Q(α). Hmm, but perhaps that's not quite the right way to think about it.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can think of the fact that if Q(α) = Q(β), then the difference Q(α) - Q(β) = 0. But since α and β are roots of the minimal polynomial f(x) = x⁴ -10x² +1, which is irreducible over ℚ, then perhaps Q must be a multiple of the minimal polynomial? Wait, but Q is a polynomial with integer coefficients, monic, and we want the minimal degree one such that Q(α) = Q(β). Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider the polynomial Q(x) - Q(β) has α as a root, but since β is another root, but actually, maybe we can consider that Q(α) - Q(β) = 0, so that α is a root of the polynomial Q(x) - Q(β). But since Q has integer coefficients, and β is irrational, but perhaps this line of thought is not very helpful.\n\nWait, here's another idea: Since α and β are roots of the same minimal polynomial f(x) = x⁴ -10x² +1, then any polynomial that takes the same value at α and β must be a polynomial that is a function of the minimal polynomial? Or perhaps, more precisely, any polynomial that commutes with the automorphism swapping α and β?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think of the set of polynomials Q with integer coefficients such that Q(α) = Q(β). Since α and β are conjugate over ℚ, such polynomials must be invariant under the automorphism σ₃ that swaps α and β. Therefore, the set of such polynomials Q must be exactly the set of polynomials that are invariant under σ₃. Since σ₃ is an automorphism of the field ℚ(√3, √2), and Q has coefficients in ℤ (hence in ℚ), then the invariance under σ₃ would mean that Q(σ₃(x)) = Q(x) for all x in ℚ(√3, √2). Therefore, such polynomials Q must satisfy Q(σ₃(x)) = Q(x) for all x. So, in other words, Q is a polynomial with integer coefficients that is invariant under the automorphism σ₃.\n\nTherefore, to construct such polynomials Q, we can consider polynomials that are invariant under σ₃. So, what does that mean for the polynomial's coefficients?\n\nLet me recall that σ₃ acts on elements of ℚ(√3, √2) by flipping the sign of √2. Wait, actually, σ₃ is the automorphism that sends √3 to √3 and √2 to -√2. Therefore, in terms of the basis {1, √3, √2, √3√2} over ℚ, it acts as:\n\nσ₃(1) = 1,\n\nσ₃(√3) = √3,\n\nσ₃(√2) = -√2,\n\nσ₃(√3√2) = -√3√2.\n\nTherefore, in terms of the variables, if we write any element in ℚ(√3, √2) as a + b√3 + c√2 + d√3√2, then σ₃ would send it to a + b√3 - c√2 - d√3√2.\n\nTherefore, for a polynomial Q(x) with coefficients in ℚ, to be invariant under σ₃, it must satisfy Q(σ₃(x)) = Q(x) for all x in ℚ(√3, √2). Therefore, if we write Q(x) as a polynomial, say,\n\nQ(x) = xⁿ + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + ... + a₁x + a₀,\n\nthen the condition Q(σ₃(x)) = Q(x) must hold for all x.\n\nTherefore, substituting σ₃(x) into Q and setting equal to Q(x), we can get equations on the coefficients a_i. Since this must hold for all x, the coefficients of corresponding powers of x must be equal. Therefore, we can derive conditions on the coefficients a_i so that this holds.\n\nAlternatively, since σ₃ is an automorphism of the field, the set of polynomials invariant under σ₃ are precisely those polynomials that are functions of the invariant elements under σ₃. Since σ₃ has order 2, the invariant elements are the ones fixed by σ₃, i.e., elements y such that σ₃(y) = y. The fixed field of σ₃ in ℚ(√3, √2) is ℚ(√3), because σ₃ fixes √3 and sends √2 to -√2, so elements fixed by σ₃ must satisfy that the coefficient of √2 is zero. Therefore, the fixed field is ℚ(√3). Therefore, the invariant polynomials under σ₃ are exactly those polynomials that can be expressed as polynomials in the fixed field, i.e., polynomials in √3 with coefficients in ℚ. Wait, but actually, more precisely, since the fixed field is ℚ(√3), then any polynomial invariant under σ₃ must be a polynomial in ℚ(√3)[x], but with coefficients in ℚ? Hmm, maybe I need to think in terms of the basis.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's easier to think in terms of the expression of x in terms of the basis {1, √3, √2, √6} (since √3√2 = √6). Let me write any element x in ℚ(√3, √2) as:\n\nx = a + b√3 + c√2 + d√6,\n\nwhere a, b, c, d ∈ ℚ.\n\nThen, applying σ₃ to x:\n\nσ₃(x) = a + b√3 - c√2 - d√6.\n\nTherefore, if we have a polynomial Q(x) = sum_{k=0}^n q_k x^k, then Q(σ₃(x)) = sum_{k=0}^n q_k (σ₃(x))^k.\n\nFor Q to be invariant under σ₃, we need Q(σ₃(x)) = Q(x) for all x, which requires that for each coefficient of the basis elements {1, √3, √2, √6}, the coefficients in Q(σ₃(x)) and Q(x) must match.\n\nThis seems a bit involved, but maybe we can consider the effect of σ₃ on monomials x^k.\n\nLet me see if there's a pattern for how σ₃ acts on powers of x. Let me compute σ₃(x^k) for various k. Let me suppose that x is expressed as a + b√3 + c√2 + d√6, but perhaps instead, it might be better to work abstractly.\n\nAlternatively, note that σ₃ is an automorphism of the field, so it commutes with all field operations. Therefore, if we consider the polynomial Q(x), then σ₃(Q(x)) = Q(σ₃(x)), but for Q to be invariant, we need σ₃(Q(x)) = Q(x). Therefore, this must hold for all x in the field, so in particular, for x = α, which gives Q(σ₃(α)) = Q(α), which is our original condition. But to have Q invariant under σ₃, it must hold for all x, so that's a stronger condition.\n\nTherefore, perhaps the minimal degree polynomial in S corresponds to the minimal degree monic polynomial with integer coefficients that is invariant under σ₃. Therefore, such polynomials must be in the subring of ℤ[x] invariant under σ₃, and we need the minimal degree nonconstant monic one in that subring.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the minimal such polynomial is the minimal polynomial of α under the action of the Galois group? Wait, but the minimal polynomial of α is degree 4, but the minimal polynomial that is invariant under σ₃... Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the key is to note that since Q must satisfy Q(α) = Q(β), and since α and β are conjugates, then Q must be a function of the \"symmetric\" part of α and β. Since α and β are roots of the same minimal polynomial, perhaps Q must be a polynomial in the elementary symmetric functions of α and β? Wait, but α and β are just two elements, so their elementary symmetric functions are just α + β and αβ.\n\nWait, let me compute α + β and αβ. Let me recall that α = √3 + √2, β = √3 - √2.\n\nSo, α + β = 2√3,\n\nand αβ = (√3 + √2)(√3 - √2) = (√3)^2 - (√2)^2 = 3 - 2 = 1.\n\nSo, α + β = 2√3,\n\nand αβ = 1.\n\nTherefore, if we think of α and β as roots of the quadratic equation x² - (α + β)x + αβ = x² - 2√3 x + 1 = 0. Wait, but that quadratic has coefficients in ℚ(√3), not in ℚ. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps if we consider that since α and β are roots of the quartic polynomial f(x) = x⁴ -10x² +1, which has coefficients in ℚ, then any symmetric function in α and β that can be expressed with rational coefficients would correspond to coefficients of the minimal polynomial?\n\nAlternatively, since we need Q(α) = Q(β), then Q must be a polynomial that is constant on the orbit of α under the Galois group? Wait, but the orbit of α under the Galois group is actually the set {α, β, σ(α), σ(β)}, where σ is other automorphisms. Wait, actually, the orbit of α under the Galois group is the set {α, β, -α, -β}, because as we saw earlier, applying σ₂: which sends √3 to -√3 and leaves √2 fixed, so σ₂(α) = -√3 + √2, and σ₄(α) = -√3 - √2, which is -α. Similarly, σ₃(α) = β, σ₄(α) = -α, etc.\n\nSo, the full Galois orbit of α is {α, β, -α, -β}. Therefore, if Q is invariant under the entire Galois group, then it must take the same value on all elements of the orbit, but our condition is only that Q(α) = Q(β). So, it's a weaker condition: Q only needs to be constant on the subset {α, β}, but not necessarily on the whole orbit.\n\nTherefore, perhaps the set of polynomials Q with Q(α) = Q(β) is a larger set than those invariant under the full Galois group. So, maybe we can consider the subgroup of the Galois group that fixes α and β? Wait, but the Galois group acts transitively on the roots of the minimal polynomial, so maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the condition Q(α) = Q(β) can be rephrased as Q(α) - Q(β) = 0. Since α and β are roots of f(x) = x⁴ -10x² +1, then perhaps we can write Q(x) - Q(β) as a multiple of f(x), but that might not necessarily be the case. Wait, but actually, if we consider that f(x) divides Q(x) - Q(β), but since f(x) is irreducible over ℚ, then if Q(x) - Q(β) is divisible by f(x), then Q(x) ≡ Q(β) mod f(x). But since Q(x) is a polynomial with integer coefficients, and f(x) is monic with integer coefficients, then Q(x) - Q(β) would have to be a multiple of f(x), but Q(β) is a constant, so unless Q(x) - Q(β) is a multiple of f(x), which would require that Q(x) = f(x)*something + Q(β). But since Q is monic, perhaps this could be a way?\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can think of the difference Q(α) - Q(β) = 0. Since α and β are roots of f(x), perhaps we can express this difference in terms of f(x). Let me see.\n\nSuppose that Q is a polynomial with integer coefficients. Then, since f(α) = 0 and f(β) = 0, perhaps we can write Q(α) - Q(β) as some combination involving f(α) and f(β), but since f(α)=f(β)=0, that might not help directly.\n\nAlternatively, consider that since α and β are conjugates, then any symmetric function in α and β with rational coefficients can be expressed in terms of α + β and αβ. Since we know that α + β = 2√3 and αβ = 1, but these are not rational. Wait, so perhaps if we can write Q in terms of expressions that are symmetric in α and β, but since α and β are not in ℚ, maybe we need to square or something?\n\nAlternatively, here's another approach: Let me consider that since Q(α) = Q(β), then Q(α) - Q(β) = 0. Let me think of Q as a function, and since α and β are conjugate, then the difference Q(α) - Q(β) must be in the ideal generated by the minimal polynomial of α, or something like that?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model this as follows: Let me consider that since α and β satisfy the equation x² - (α + β)x + αβ = x² - 2√3 x + 1 = 0, but this quadratic has irrational coefficients. However, if we consider the field ℚ(√3), then this quadratic splits into linear factors there. But since we need integer coefficients, perhaps we can use the quartic polynomial.\n\nAlternatively, since Q must satisfy Q(α) = Q(β), then Q must be constant on the set {α, β}. So, perhaps Q is a polynomial that can be written as a function of some symmetric function of α and β. Since α and β are roots of the quartic f(x), perhaps the elementary symmetric sums of α and β can be used?\n\nWait, but α and β are just two elements, so their elementary symmetric sums are just α + β and αβ, which we know are 2√3 and 1. But 2√3 is irrational, so maybe we can consider combinations that eliminate the radicals? For instance, if we take (α + β)^2 = (2√3)^2 = 12, which is rational. Similarly, (αβ)^2 = 1, which is also rational. Hmm, so perhaps expressions like (α + β)^2 or (αβ)^2 can be used to form rational expressions.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can consider that since α and β are roots of f(x) = x⁴ -10x² +1, then any polynomial Q(x) satisfying Q(α) = Q(β) must satisfy Q(x) ≡ Q(y) mod f(x), where y is another root? Wait, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the minimal such polynomial Q is the minimal polynomial of α + β or something? Wait, but α + β is 2√3, whose minimal polynomial is x² - 12, since (2√3)^2 = 12. So, if we take Q(x) = x² - 12, then Q(α) = (α)^2 - 12, but α² is 5 + 2√6, so Q(α) = 5 + 2√6 - 12 = -7 + 2√6, and Q(β) = β² - 12 = (5 - 2√6) -12 = -7 - 2√6, which are not equal. So that doesn't work.\n\nHmm, so that idea is not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, maybe Q must be a polynomial that is even in some sense? Since swapping α and β is like an involution, perhaps Q needs to be even under that swap? Wait, but how?\n\nAlternatively, let me think of Q as a function on the field ℚ(√3, √2). Since we need Q(α) = Q(β), and α and β are distinct, then Q must not distinguish between α and β. So, perhaps Q can be expressed as a polynomial in terms of the \"difference\" between α and β? But since α - β = 2√2, which is also irrational, maybe not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, let me think of building Q step by step. Since Q must be monic with integer coefficients, and minimal degree. Let me try to see what constraints are on Q.\n\nSuppose that Q is of degree 1: Q(x) = x + c, where c is integer. Then Q(α) = α + c, Q(β) = β + c. For these to be equal, we need α + c = β + c ⇒ α = β, which is false. So degree 1 is impossible.\n\nDegree 2: Let Q(x) = x² + a x + b, with a, b integers. Then Q(α) = α² + a α + b, and Q(β) = β² + a β + b. We need Q(α) = Q(β), so:\n\nα² + a α + b = β² + a β + b ⇒ α² - β² + a(α - β) = 0.\n\nFactor the left-hand side: (α - β)(α + β) + a(α - β) = (α - β)(α + β + a) = 0.\n\nSince α ≠ β, we have α - β ≠ 0, so we must have α + β + a = 0 ⇒ a = - (α + β).\n\nBut α + β = 2√3, which is irrational, but a must be an integer. Therefore, this is impossible. So degree 2 is also impossible.\n\nOkay, moving on to degree 3. Let me try degree 3.\n\nLet Q(x) = x³ + a x² + b x + c, with a, b, c integers. Then Q(α) = α³ + a α² + b α + c,\n\nand Q(β) = β³ + a β² + b β + c.\n\nWe need Q(α) = Q(β), so:\n\nα³ + a α² + b α + c = β³ + a β² + b β + c.\n\nSubtract c from both sides and rearrange:\n\nα³ - β³ + a(α² - β²) + b(α - β) = 0.\n\nFactor each term:\n\nFirst, α³ - β³ = (α - β)(α² + α β + β²),\n\nSecond, α² - β² = (α - β)(α + β),\n\nThird, α - β is just α - β.\n\nTherefore, factoring out (α - β):\n\n(α - β)[α² + α β + β² + a(α + β) + b] = 0.\n\nAgain, since α ≠ β, we have α - β ≠ 0, so the expression in the brackets must be zero:\n\nα² + α β + β² + a(α + β) + b = 0.\n\nNow, let's compute α² + α β + β² and α + β in terms of known quantities.\n\nWe already know that α + β = 2√3,\n\nand α β = 1,\n\nand we can compute α² and β²:\n\nEarlier, we saw that α² = (√3 + √2)^2 = 3 + 2√6 + 2 = 5 + 2√6,\n\nSimilarly, β² = (√3 - √2)^2 = 3 - 2√6 + 2 = 5 - 2√6,\n\nTherefore, α² + β² = (5 + 2√6) + (5 - 2√6) = 10,\n\nand α² + α β + β² = (α² + β²) + α β = 10 + 1 = 11.\n\nSo, substituting back into the equation:\n\n11 + a(2√3) + b = 0.\n\nTherefore,\n\na(2√3) + (b + 11) = 0.\n\nBut a and b are integers, and 2√3 is irrational. The only way this can hold is if the coefficient of √3 is zero and the constant term is zero. That is:\n\n2a = 0 ⇒ a = 0,\n\nand\n\nb + 11 = 0 ⇒ b = -11.\n\nTherefore, if a = 0 and b = -11, then the equation is satisfied.\n\nTherefore, for a cubic polynomial Q(x) = x³ + 0 x² - 11 x + c = x³ - 11x + c,\n\nthen Q(α) = Q(β). However, we also have the constant term c. Wait, in our equation above, the c canceled out, so actually, the equation didn't involve c. Therefore, c can be any integer? Wait, let me check that again.\n\nWait, in the original equation, when we set Q(α) = Q(β), we had:\n\nα³ + a α² + b α + c = β³ + a β² + b β + c,\n\nso when we subtract, the c terms cancel, so indeed, c can be any integer, but the conditions on a and b are independent of c. Therefore, for the cubic polynomial Q(x) = x³ - 11x + c, with any integer c, we have Q(α) = Q(β). However, since we are looking for monic polynomials with integer coefficients, nonconstant, so c can be any integer. But we want the minimal degree, so degree 3 is possible. However, we need to check if such a polynomial is indeed in S, i.e., monic, integer coefficients, nonconstant, and satisfies Q(α)=Q(β). Since we just showed that for any c, Q(x) = x³ -11x + c will satisfy Q(α)=Q(β), then yes, that works. Therefore, there are infinitely 