n 2017, but not necessarily by 2017 again. Wait, actually, in \\( 2025! \\), the exponent of 2017 is exactly 1, since 2017 is a prime number and appears only once in the factorial. Therefore, \\( m = \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\) is an integer not divisible by 2017. So \\( \\gcd(2017, m) = 1 \\), because 2017 is prime and doesn't divide m. Therefore, 2017 and m are coprime.\n\nTherefore, since \\( n = 2017 \\times m \\), and \\( \\gcd(2017, m) = 1 \\), then by the Chinese Remainder Theorem, the ring \\( \\mathbb{Z}/n\\mathbb{Z} \\) is isomorphic to \\( \\mathbb{Z}/2017\\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{Z}/m\\mathbb{Z} \\). Therefore, to compute \\( 2017^{n} \\mod n \\), it suffices to compute it modulo 2017 and modulo m, then combine the results.\n\nFirst, compute \\( 2017^{n} \\mod 2017 \\). Since \\( 2017 \\equiv 0 \\mod 2017 \\), so \\( 2017^{n} \\equiv 0 \\mod 2017 \\), so \\( 2017^{n} - 1 \\equiv -1 \\mod 2017 \\).\n\nSecond, compute \\( 2017^{n} \\mod m \\). Since \\( \\gcd(2017, m) = 1 \\), because \\( m = \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\) and 2017 is prime and only appears once in the factorial, so m is not divisible by 2017. Therefore, by Euler's theorem, since \\( \\gcd(2017, m) = 1 \\), we have that \\( 2017^{\\phi(m)} \\equiv 1 \\mod m \\). Therefore, \\( 2017^{n} \\equiv 2017^{n \\mod \\phi(m)} \\mod m \\).\n\nTherefore, if we can compute \\( n \\mod \\phi(m) \\), then we can compute \\( 2017^{n} \\mod m \\).\n\nBut this seems recursive, since \\( \\phi(m) \\) depends on the prime factors of \\( m \\), which is \\( \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\). This might not be helpful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can note that \\( n = 2017 \\times m \\), so \\( n \\) is a multiple of \\( \\phi(m) \\) only if \\( \\phi(m) \\) divides \\( 2017 \\times m \\). But I don't see an immediate reason why that would be the case.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps \\( n \\) is a multiple of the Carmichael function of \\( m \\), but again, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \\( n \\) is a multiple of \\( \\phi(m) \