) = 1 \\).\n\nTherefore, perhaps we can write the expression \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\) modulo \\( 2025! \\), which is \\( 2017^{2017 \\times m} - 1 \\mod (2017 \\times m) \\). Since \\( 2017 \\times m = 2025! \\), so maybe we can use the Chinese Remainder Theorem by splitting the modulus into 2017 and m, since 2017 and m are coprime? Because m is \\( 2025! / 2017 \\), and since 2017 is prime and only appears once in the factorial, then m is an integer not divisible by 2017, so yes, \\( \\gcd(2017, m) = 1 \\). Therefore, by the Chinese Remainder Theorem, the ring \\( \\mathbb{Z}/(2017 \\times m)\\mathbb{Z} \\) is isomorphic to \\( \\mathbb{Z}/2017\\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{Z}/m\\mathbb{Z} \\).\n\nTherefore, to compute \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\mod (2017 \\times m) \\), it's equivalent to computing it modulo 2017 and modulo m, then combining the results. So let's compute \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\mod 2017 \\) and \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\mod m \\), then use the Chinese Remainder Theorem to find the unique residue modulo \\( 2017 \\times m \\), which is \\( 2025! \\).\n\nFirst, compute modulo 2017:\n\n\\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\mod 2017 \\).\n\nBut 2017 ≡ 0 mod 2017, so 2017^{anything} ≡ 0 mod 2017, so \\( 2017^{2025!} ≡ 0 \\mod 2017 \\), so \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 ≡ -1 \\mod 2017 \\). Therefore, the remainder modulo 2017 is 2016.\n\nSecond, compute \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\mod m \\).\n\nHere, m is \\( \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\), which is an integer coprime to 2017. Since m is coprime to 2017, perhaps we can apply Euler's theorem here? Since \\( \\gcd(2017, m) = 1 \\), because m is \\( \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\), and 2017 is prime and only divides the factorial once, so m is an integer not divisible by 2017, so they are coprime.\n\nTherefore, Euler's theorem tells us that \\( 2017^{\\phi(m)} ≡ 1 \\mod m \\), where \\( \\phi \\) is Euler's totient function. Therefore, if we can find the exponent \\( 2025! \\) modulo \\( \\phi(m) \\), then we can reduce the exponent.\n\nSo, let me denote \\( 