ble by 2017, so 2017 divides 2025!.\n\nTherefore, \\( 2017^{2025!} \\equiv 0 \\mod 2017 \\), as we saw before, so \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\equiv -1 \\mod 2017 \\). Therefore, \\( r \\equiv -1 \\mod 2017 \\). Since \\( r \\) is the remainder when divided by 2025!, which is a multiple of 2017, then \\( r = 2017 \\times t - 1 \\), for some integer \\( t \\). But since \\( r \\) must be non-negative and less than 2025!, then \\( t \\) must satisfy \\( 2017 \\times t - 1 < 2025! \\), so \\( t < \\frac{2025! + 1}{2017} \\), which is a huge number, but not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the key is to note that except for the modulus 2017, all other moduli from 1 to 2025 divide \\( 2025! \\), so perhaps the remainder \\( r \\) must satisfy \\( r \\equiv 0 \\mod m \\) for all \\( m \\) from 1 to 2025 except \\( m = 2017 \\), where \\( r \\equiv -1 \\mod 2017 \\). Then, using the Chinese Remainder Theorem, we can find a unique solution modulo \\( \\text{lcm}(1,2,...,2025) \\), which is \\( 2025! \\). Therefore, even though \\( r \\equiv -1 \\mod 2017 \\), and \\( r \\equiv 0 \\mod m \\) for all other \\( m \\leq 2025 \\), then such an \\( r \\) exists and is unique modulo \\( 2025! \\). Therefore, perhaps \\( r \\) is equal to \\( 2025! - 1 \\), since \\( 2025! - 1 \\equiv -1 \\mod 2017 \\) (because \\( 2025! \\equiv 0 \\mod 2017 \\), so \\( 2025! - 1 \\equiv -1 \\mod 2017 \\)), and \\( 2025! - 1 \\equiv -1 \\mod m \\) for other m? Wait, no, let me check:\n\nWait, let me think again. If we have a system of congruences:\n\nFor each \\( m \\) from 1 to 2025,\n\n- If \\( m \\neq 2017 \\), then \\( r \\equiv 0 \\mod m \\).\n\n- If \\( m = 2017 \\), then \\( r \\equiv -1 \\mod 2017 \\).\n\nThen, the solution \\( r \\) modulo \\( 2025! \\) is the unique number in \\( [0, 2025!) \\) satisfying these congruences.\n\nNow, the number \\( 2025! - 1 \\) satisfies:\n\n- For \\( m \\neq 2017 \\), since \\( 2025! \\equiv 0 \\mod m \\), so \\( 2025! - 1 \\equiv -1 \\mod m \\). Wait, but we