 the modulus is 2025!.\n\nBut in my small example, I set the exponent to be the same as the modulus, which was 5!.\n\nBut in the original problem, the modulus is 2025! and the exponent is also 2025!.\n\nHowever, in my small example, I set the modulus to 5! = 120, and the exponent was 120, so that was okay, but when I tried to compute k mod p, I used the wrong expression for k.\n\nWait, let's clarify:\n\nIn the original problem:\n\nLet me restate:\n\nLet m = 2025!.\n\nWe have to compute \\( a^{m} - 1 \\mod m \\), where a = 2017.\n\nGiven that a divides m (since a is prime and less than 2025), so a | m.\n\nTherefore, m = a × k, where k = m / a = 2025! / 2017.\n\nTherefore, to compute \\( a^m - 1 \\mod m \\), we can use the Chinese Remainder Theorem by splitting m into a and k, where a and k are coprime? Wait, but a and k: since m = a × k, and a is prime, then k = m / a, so gcd(a, k) = gcd(a, m/a) = gcd(a, m/a). Since a divides m, but m/a may or may not be divisible by a. Since m = 2025! and a = 2017, then m/a = 2025! / 2017, which still contains other factors but not 2017, since we divided out one factor of 2017. Therefore, gcd(a, k) = gcd(2017, k) = 1, since k has no factor of 2017.\n\nTherefore, m = a × k with gcd(a, k) = 1, so by Chinese Remainder Theorem, Z/mZ ≅ Z/aZ × Z/kZ.\n\nTherefore, to compute \\( a^m - 1 \\mod m \\), we can compute \\( a^m - 1 \\mod a \\) and \\( a^m - 1 \\mod k \\), then combine.\n\nModulo a: \\( a^m \\equiv 0 \\mod a \\), so \\( a^m - 1 \\equiv -1 \\mod a \\).\n\nModulo k: Since a and k are coprime, Euler's theorem applies: \\( a^{\\phi(k)} \\equiv 1 \\mod k \\). Since m = a × k is a multiple of φ(k) (assuming φ(k) divides m, which is likely, but not necessarily), then \\( a^m \\equiv (a^{\\phi(k)})^{m / \\phi(k)} \\equiv 1^{m / \\phi(k)} \\equiv 1 \\mod k \\). Therefore, \\( a^m - 1 \\equiv 0 \\mod k \\).\n\nTherefore, we need a number r such that:\n\nr ≡ -1 mod a,\n\nr ≡ 0 mod k.\n\nSince a and k are coprime, by Chinese Remainder Theorem, there is a unique solution modulo m = a × k.\n\nThe solution is r = k × t, where t ≡ -1 mod a. Since r < m = a × k, t must satisfy 0 ≤ t < a.\n\nTherefore, t = a × s - 1 for some s, but to get t in the range [0, a), we set s = 1, so t = a - 1. Wait, no:\n\nWait, solving t ≡ -1 mod a, with 0 ≤ t < a.\n\nThis congruence is equivalent to t ≡ a - 1 mod a, so the smallest non-negative solution is t = a - 1.\n\nTherefore, r = k × (a - 1).\n\nWait, hold on, if t ≡ -1 mod a, then t = a × n - 1 for integer n, but since t must be between 0 and a - 1, then n = 1 gives t = a - 1, and that's the only solution.\n\nTherefore, r = k × (a - 1).\n\nTherefore, r = k × (a - 1), and since m = a × k, then r/m = (a - 1)/a.\n\nWait, that can't be right, because in the small example, this would give r/m = (3 - 1)/3 = 2/3, which matched the small example. Wait, in the small example, a = 3, k = 40, so r = 40 × 2 = 80, and r/m = 80/120 = 2/3, which matches.\n\nTherefore, in the original problem, following this, r = k × (a - 1) = (2025! / 2017) × (2017 - 1) = (2025! / 2017) × 2016.\n\nTherefore, r / m = [ (2025! / 2017) × 2016 ] / (2025! ) = 2016 / 2017.\n\nWait, but that contradicts my previous result of 1311/2017.\n\nWait, so now I'm confused. Which is correct?\n\nWait, in the small example, this method gives the correct result, but in the original problem, according to this, it would be (a - 1)/a = 2016/2017, but my earlier detailed computation gave 1311/2017. There is a discrepancy here.\n\nTherefore, I must have made a mistake in the earlier detailed computation. Let me see.\n\nWait, in the small example, the method of solving t ≡ -1 mod a gives t = a - 1, so r = k × t = k × (a - 1), which worked. So in the original problem, following that, r = k × (a - 1) = (2025! / 2017) × 2016, so then r/m = (2016)/2017, which is simpler. So why did my previous detailed computation give 1311/2017?\n\nAh, because in my previous detailed computation, I incorrectly assumed that k ≡ 20 mod 2017, then set up the equation t × k ≡ -1 mod 2017, and solved for t, but in reality, according to this simpler method, r = k × (a - 1), so r/m = (a - 1)/a, so that is 2016/2017. So which is correct?\n\nWait, in the small example, this formula gives the correct answer, so perhaps in the original problem, the correct answer is 2016/2017.\n\nBut why did my detailed computation give a different answer? Let me see where I went wrong.\n\nIn the detailed computation, I considered:\n\nWe have r ≡ 0 mod k,\n\nand r ≡ -1 mod a,\n\nso r = t × k,\n\nand then t × k ≡ -1 mod a,\n\nso t ≡ (-1)/k mod a,\n\nbut since k and a are coprime (because k = m/a, and m = a × k, and a is prime, so k is not divisible by a),\n\nthen k has an inverse mod a,\n\nso t ≡ -k^{-1} mod a,\n\nthen t = a × s + (-k^{-1} mod a),\n\nthen since t must be in 0 ≤ t < a,\n\nt = (-k^{-1}) mod a,\n\nthen r = t × k,\n\nso r/m = t / a,\n\nsince m = a × k,\n\nso r/m = (t / a).\n\nWait, but in the small example:\n\na = 3, k = 40,\n\nthen k^{-1} mod 3 is the inverse of 40 mod 3.\n\nBut 40 mod 3 is 1, so inverse of 1 mod 3 is 1,\n\nso t ≡ -1 mod 3,\n\nso t = 2 mod 3,\n\nso t = 2,\n\nthen r = 2 × 40 = 80,\n\nso r/m = 80/120 = 2/3 = t / a = 2/3,\n\nwhich matches.\n\nIn the original problem, following this:\n\nt ≡ -k^{-1} mod a,\n\nso t = -k^{-1} mod a,\n\nthen r/m = t / a,\n\nso need to compute k^{-1} mod a,\n\nwhere a = 2017, k = 2025! / 2017,\n\nso k mod a is needed to compute the inverse.\n\nWait, but in the previous detailed computation, I computed k mod a (where a = 2017) and found k ≡ 20 mod 2017,\n\nthen k^{-1} mod 2017 is the inverse of 20 mod 2017,\n\nwhich we computed as 706,\n\nso then t ≡ -706 mod 2017,\n\nso t = 2017 - 706 = 1311,\n\nso r/m = 1311 / 2017,\n\nbut according to the simpler method, it should be (a - 1)/a = 2016/2017.\n\nTherefore, there is a contradiction. Therefore, one of the methods is wrong.\n\nWait, in the small example, the simple method gives the correct answer, but in the original problem, the two methods give different answers. Therefore, I must have made an error in the detailed computation.\n\nWait, in the simple method, r = k × (a - 1),\n\nso in the original problem, r = (2025! / 2017) × 2016,\n\nso r/m = (2025! / 2017 × 2016) / 2025! = 2016 / 2017,\n\nso that's straightforward.\n\nBut why did the detailed computation give 1311/2017?\n\nAh! Because in the detailed computation, I incorrectly assumed that r = t × k,\n\nand then tried to solve t × k ≡ -1 mod a,\n\nbut actually, according to the simple method, r = k × (a - 1),\n\nso r = k × (a - 1),\n\nso r ≡ k × (a - 1) mod a,\n\nbut k ≡ k mod a,\n\nso r ≡ k × (a - 1) mod a,\n\nbut k × (a - 1) mod a ≡ k × (-1) mod a,\n\nand since k × (-1) mod a should be ≡ -1 mod a,\n\nbecause r ≡ -1 mod a,\n\nso k × (-1) ≡ -1 mod a ⇒ k × (-1) +1 ≡ 0 mod a ⇒ (-k +1) ≡ 0 mod a ⇒ k ≡ 1 mod a,\n\nbut in our case, k mod a was computed as 20, not 1,\n\nso this is a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, the mistake must be in the computation of k mod a.\n\nWait, in the small example, k mod a = 40 mod 3 = 1,\n\nso k ≡ 1 mod 3,\n\nso k × (-1) ≡ -1 mod 3,\n\nwhich matches r ≡ -1 mod 3,\n\nso that works.\n\nIn the original problem, if k ≡ 1 mod a, then r = k*(a-1) ≡ (1)*(a-1) ≡ -1 mod a,\n\nwhich is correct,\n\nbut in our detailed computation, we found k ≡ 20 mod 2017,\n\nso unless 20 ≡ 1 mod 2017,\n\nwhich it isn't,\n\nthen there's a problem.\n\nTherefore, my computation of k mod a must have been wrong.\n\nSo where did I go wrong in computing k mod a?\n\nLet me recompute k mod a (where a = 2017):\n\nk = m! / a, with m = 2025,\n\nso k = (2025)! / 2017.\n\nWe need to compute k mod 2017.\n\nTo compute k mod 2017,\n\nnote that k = (2025)! / 2017,\n\nso (2025)! = k × 2017,\n\nso (2025)! ≡ 0 mod 2017,\n\nbut we need k mod 2017.\n\nTo compute k mod 2017,\n\nnote that k = (2025)! / 2017,\n\nso in modular arithmetic terms,\n\nk ≡ [(2025)! / 2017] mod 2017.\n\nBut division in modular arithmetic requires multiplying by the inverse.\n\nAlternatively, since (2025)! = 2017 × k,\n\nso (2025)! ≡ 0 mod 2017,\n\nbut we need k mod 2017.\n\nAlternatively, consider Wilson's theorem.\n\nWilson's theorem says that (p-1)! ≡ -1 mod p, where p is prime.\n\nHere, p = 2017,\n\nso (2017 - 1)! = 2016! ≡ -1 mod 2017.\n\nNow, (2025)! = 2017! × 2018 × 2019 × ... × 2025,\n\nbut 2017! includes 2017 as a factor,\n\nso (2025)! = 2017! × (2018 × 2019 × ... × 2025).\n\nBut we can write this as:\n\n(2025)! = [2017 × 2016!] × [2018 × ... × 2025].\n\nTherefore,\n\nk = (2025)! / 2017 = [2017 × 2016! × (2018 × ... × 2025)] / 2017 = 2016! × (2018 × ... × 2025).\n\nTherefore,\n\nk = 2016! × (2018 × ... × 2025).\n\nNow, compute k mod 2017.\n\nFirst, note that 2016! ≡ -1 mod 2017 by Wilson's theorem.\n\nNext, compute the product (2018 × ... × 2025) mod 2017.\n\nEach term in this product can be written as 2017 + j, where j ranges from 1 to 8 (since 2025 - 2017 = 8).\n\nTherefore,\n\n2018 ≡ 1 mod 2017,\n\n2019 ≡ 2 mod 2017,\n\n...\n\n2025 ≡ 8 mod 2017.\n\nTherefore,\n\n(2018 × ... × 2025) ≡ (1 × 2 × ... × 8) mod 2017 ≡ 8! mod 2017.\n\nCompute 8! = 40320.\n\nNow compute 40320 mod 2017.\n\nAs before, 2017 × 20 = 40340,\n\nso 40320 = 40340 - 20,\n\nso 40320 ≡ -20 mod 2017.\n\nTherefore,\n\n(2018 × ... × 2025) ≡ -20 mod 2017.\n\nTherefore,\n\nk ≡ [2016! × (2018 × ... × 2025)] ≡ [ -1 × (-20) ] mod 2017,\n\nbecause 2016! ≡ -1 mod 2017 and the other product is ≡ -20 mod 2017,\n\nso multiplying them:\n\nk ≡ (-1) × (-20) ≡ 20 mod 2017.\n\nAh! So that part was correct.\n\nTherefore, k ≡ 20 mod 2017.\n\nTherefore, k ≡ 20 mod 2017,\n\nso k ≡ 20 mod a,\n\nwhere a = 2017.\n\nTherefore, in the equation t × k ≡ -1 mod a,\n\nwe have t × 20 ≡ -1 mod 2017,\n\nso t ≡ (-1)/20 mod 2017,\n\nwhich is t ≡ -20^{-1} mod 2017,\n\nand since 20^{-1} mod 2017 is 706,\n\nso t ≡ -706 mod 2017,\n\nwhich is t = 2017 - 706 = 1311 mod 2017,\n\nso t = 1311,\n\nso r = t × k,\n\nand r/m = t / a = 1311 / 2017.\n\nBut according to the simple method, r/m should be (a - 1)/a = 2016/2017.\n\nThis c