om 2 to 2025 except 2017 is actually equal to \\( 2025! / 2017 \\), since LCM of numbers from 1 to n is n! only when n is 1? Wait no, actually, LCM(1, 2, ..., n) is not equal to n! in general. For example, LCM(1, 2, 3) = 6, which is 3!, but LCM(1, 2, 3, 4) = 12, whereas 4! = 24. So LCM(1, 2, ..., n) is actually much smaller than n!.\n\nWait, but in our case, we are taking LCM of numbers from 2 to 2025 except 2017. Hmm, but even so, this seems difficult to compute. Alternatively, perhaps the number \\( r \\) we are looking for is exactly \\( 2025! - 1 \\), but that can't be right, because \\( 2025! \\) is divisible by 2017, so \\( 2025! - 1 \\equiv -1 \\mod 2017 \\), which matches the condition for k=2017, but does it satisfy the other conditions?\n\nWait, if \\( r = 2025! - 1 \\), then for any \\( k \\) from 2 to 2025 except 2017, since \\( 2025! \\equiv 0 \\mod k \\), then \\( r = 2025! - 1 \\equiv -1 \\mod k \\). But we need \\( r \\equiv 0 \\mod k \\) for those k. So that doesn't work. So that's not the case.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps \\( r \\) is the LCM of all numbers from 2 to 2025 except 2017, multiplied by some integer, plus or minus something? Hmm, but I don't see an immediate way.\n\nWait a second, let me think again. The problem is that \\( r = 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\) must satisfy:\n\n1. For all \\( k \\in \\{2, 3, \\dots, 2016, 2018, \\dots, 2025\\} \\), \\( r \\equiv 0 \\mod k \\).\n2. \\( r \\equiv -1 \\mod 2017 \\).\n\nTherefore, \\( r \\) is a multiple of the LCM of all those numbers from 2 to 2025 except 2017, and also \\( r \\equiv -1 \\mod 2017 \\). Let me denote \\( L = \\text{LCM}(2, 3, \\dots, 2016, 2018, \\dots, 2025) \\). Then \\( r \\) must be a multiple of \\( L \\), so \\( r = L \\times t \\) for some integer \\( t \\), and also \\( L \\times t \\equiv -1 \\mod 2017 \\).\n\nTherefore, we need to solve for \\( t \\) in the congruence:\n\n\\( L \\times t \\equiv -1 \\mod 2017 \\).\n\nSo, if we can compute \\( L \\mod 2017 \\), then we can solve for \\( t \\).\n\nHowever, computing \\( L \\mod 2017 \\) might be feasible because \\( L \\) is the LCM of numbers from 2 to 2025 excluding 2017. Since 2017 is a prime number, and 2017 is within the range 2 to 2025, so we are excluding 2017 from the LCM. Therefore, \\( L \\) is the LCM of all numbers from 2 to 2025 except 2017.\n\nTo compute \\( L \\mod 2017 \\), note that since 2017 is prime, and none of the numbers in the LCM except possibly those congruent to 0 mod 2017 are excluded. But since we excluded 2017 itself, but all other multiples of 2017 in the range 2 to 2025 would be 2017 × 2 = 4034, which is greater than 2025. Therefore, there are no other multiples of 2017 in the range 2 to 2025 except 2017 itself, which we have excluded. Therefore, all numbers in the LCM from 2 to 2025 except 2017 are coprime to 2017, except for the number 2017 itself, which we excluded. Wait, actually, since 2017 is prime, and none of the other numbers in the LCM (from 2 to 2025 except 2017) are multiples of 2017, because the next multiple is 4034, which is beyond 2025. Therefore, all the numbers in the LCM are coprime to 2017. Therefore, the LCM \\( L \\) is coprime to 2017, since it's the LCM of numbers all of which are coprime to 2017.\n\nTherefore, \\( \\gcd(L, 2017) = 1 \\), so 2017 has an inverse modulo \\( L \\). Wait, actually, we need to compute \\( L \\mod 2017 \\), and then solve \\( L \\times t \\equiv -1 \\mod 2017 \\).\n\nSince \\( L \\) is the LCM of numbers from 2 to 2025 excluding 2017, and all those numbers are less than or equal to 2025, and 2017 is a prime number, then modulo 2017, each number in the LCM is just its own value modulo 2017, except that numbers greater than or equal to 2017 would wrap around. Wait, but actually, numbers from 2 to 2016 are less than 2017, so their values mod 2017 are themselves. Numbers from 2018 to 2025 are between 2018 and 2025, so modulo 2017, they are equal to (2018 - 2017) = 1, up to (2025 - 2017) = 8. So:\n\nNumbers from 2 to 2016: mod 2017, they are 2, 3, ..., 2016.\n\nNumbers from 2018 to 2025: mod 2017, they are 1, 2, ..., 8.\n\nTherefore, the LCM \\( L \\mod 2017 \\) is equal to the LCM of the numbers:\n\n- {2, 3, ..., 2016} mod 2017,\n\nand\n\n- {1, 2, ..., 8} mod 2017.\n\nBut since LCM is taken over the entire set, we can think of it as the LCM of all residues from 1 to 2016 and then 1 to 8, but since the LCM of numbers from 1 to 2016 is already a multiple of LCM(1 to 8). Therefore, LCM({2,...,2016} union {1,...,8}) = LCM({1,...,2016}), since the extra numbers {1,...,8} don't add anything new beyond what's already in {1,...,2016}. Wait, but actually, the LCM of numbers from 2 to 2016 and then 1 to 8 is the same as LCM of numbers from 1 to 2016, because the numbers 1 to 8 are already included in 2 to 2016? Wait, no, actually 1 is not included in the original LCM. Wait, hold on, the LCM we are taking is over the numbers from 2 to 2025 excluding 2017. So that includes numbers from 2 up to 2016, and then 2018 to 2025. The numbers from 2018 to 2025 modulo 2017 are 1 through 8, as above.\n\nTherefore, the LCM \\( L \\mod 2017 \\) is the LCM of:\n\n- The numbers 2 through 2016, and\n\n- The numbers 1 through 8 (from the 2018 to 2025 part modulo 2017).\n\nBut note that the LCM of numbers 2 through 2016 already includes the LCM of numbers 1 through 8, since 1 through 8 are all included in 2 through 2016 (except for 1, which is not included in 2 through 2016). Wait, actually, the original LCM is from 2 to 2016 and then 2018 to 2025 (which mod 2017 are 1 through 8). Therefore, the LCM needs to include the number 1 (from the 2018 mod 2017 = 1), but since 1 divides everything, it doesn't affect the LCM. Therefore, the LCM of all those numbers is the same as LCM of numbers from 2 to 2016 and numbers from 1 to 8. Since LCM(2 to 2016) already includes all the factors of numbers 2 through 2016, and numbers 1 through 8 are a subset of that except for 1, but 1 doesn't contribute anything. Therefore, the LCM \\( L \\mod 2017 \\) is equal to LCM(2, 3, ..., 2016) mod 2017.\n\nWait, but actually, when we take the LCM of numbers modulo 2017, does that correspond to taking the LCM of their residues modulo 2017? Not exactly, because LCM is taken over integers, but we are evaluating the LCM modulo 2017. Hmm, perhaps this is getting too convoluted.\n\nAlternatively, note that since all the numbers from 2 to 2016 are less than 2017 and coprime to 2017 (except for 2017 itself, which is excluded), so each of these numbers is invertible modulo 2017. Therefore, the LCM \\( L \\) of numbers from 2 to 2016 and 2018 to 2025 (which mod 2017 are 1 through 8) is congruent modulo 2017 to the LCM of all numbers from 1 to 2016 except 2017. Wait, but actually, numbers from 2 to 2016 and then 1 through 8 (from the upper end). Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, since L is the LCM of numbers from 2 to 2025 except 2017, and we can note that L is divisible by all numbers from 2 to 2025 except 2017, so in particular, L is divisible by all numbers from 2 to 2016 and from 2018 to 2025. Therefore, L is divisible by the LCM of those two ranges. Since LCM(a, b) where a and b are ranges of numbers... Well, the LCM of numbers from 2 to 2016 and from 2018 to 2025. Since 2018 to 2025 are just 8 numbers: 2018, 2019, ..., 2025, which modulo 2017 are 1, 2, ..., 8. So LCM(2018, ..., 2025) mod 2017 is LCM(1, 2, ..., 8) mod 2017. Since LCM(1,2,...,8) is 840. So LCM(1,2,...,8) = 840. Therefore, the LCM of the upper part (2018 to 2025) mod 2017 is 840 mod 2017.\n\nSimilarly, the LCM of numbers from 2 to 2016 mod 2017 is equal to LCM(2, 3, ..., 2016) mod 2017. Now, since 2017 is prime, the numbers from 2 to 2016 cover all residues modulo 2017 except 0 and 1. Wait, actually, residues from 1 to 2016. Since 2017 is prime, the multiplicative group modulo 2017 is cyclic of order 2016. Therefore, the LCM of the numbers 1 to 2016 modulo 2017 is actually the same as the LCM of all non-zero residues modulo 2017. But the LCM of all non-zero residues modulo a prime p is equal to p - 1? Wait, not sure.\n\nWait, actually, in the multiplicative group modulo a prime p, which is cyclic of order p-1, the LCM of all elements in the multiplicative group would be the exponent of the group, which is p-1. Wait, the exponent of a group is the least common multiple of the orders of all elements. In a cyclic group of order n, the exponent is n, so the LCM of all element orders is n. Therefore, in the multiplicative group modulo p (which is cyclic of order p-1), the exponent is p-1, so the LCM of all element orders is p-1. But does that mean the LCM of all elements in the multiplicative group is p-1? Not exactly, because the LCM of the elements themselves is different from the LCM of their orders.\n\nWait, perhaps this is a detour. Alternatively, note that the LCM of all numbers from 1 to p-1 (where p is prime) is known to be equal to the LCM of numbers from 1 to p-1. For example, for p=5, LCM(1,2,3,4)=12. For p=7, LCM(1,2,3,4,5,6)=60, etc. In general, it's a known value but not sure of a formula. However, modulo p, the LCM of numbers from 1 to p-1 mod p is the same as LCM(1,2,...,p-1) mod p. But since LCM(1,2,...,p-1) is less than p! and p is prime, so LCM(1,...,p-1) is less than p! but greater than (p-1)! / something? Not sure. But in any case, perhaps for our purposes, we can compute LCM(2,...,2016) mod 2017.\n\nWait, but LCM(2,...,2016) is the LCM of all numbers from 2 to 2016. Since 2017 is prime, none of these numbers are multiples of 2017, so each number from 2 to 2016 is coprime to 2017 or not? Actually, some of them may share factors with 2017, but since 2017 is prime, the only common factor would be 2017 itself, but none of the numbers from 2 to 2016 is 2017, so actually all numbers from 2 to 2016 are coprime to 2017? Wait, no, they can share other prime factors. For example, 4 and 2017 are coprime, but 4 and 3 are not coprime, but 3 is less than 2017. Wait, but we are considering LCM(2,...,2016) mod 2017. Since 2017 is prime, the modulus is prime, so when we take LCM of numbers from 2 to 2016, and then take that LCM modulo 2017, it's equivalent to the LCM of their residues modulo 2017, but since all residues from 2 to 2016 are already less than 2017, so LCM(2,...,2016) mod 2017 is just LCM(2,...,2016) mod 2017.\n\nBut LCM(2,...,2016) is a huge number, but modulo 2017, perhaps we can find its value by considering that LCM(2,...,2016) is divisible by all numbers from 2 to 2016, so in particular, it is divisible by 2016, which is one less than 2017. Since 2017 is prime, 2016 ≡ -1 mod 2017. Therefore, LCM(2,...,2016) is divisible by 2016, so LCM(2,...,2016) ≡ 0 mod 2016, but mod 2017, 2016 ≡ -1, so 2016 ≡ -1 mod 2017, so LCM(2,...,2016) is divisible by 2016, so LCM(2,...,2016) ≡ 0 mod 2016, but how does that help with mod 2017?\n\nAlternatively, since LCM(2,...,2016) is divisible by all numbers from 2 to 2016, so in particular, it is divisible by 2017 - 1 = 2016, but 2016 and 2017 are coprime? Wait, 2016 and 2017 are coprime because 2017 is prime and doesn't divide 2016. Therefore, LCM(2,...,2016) is divisible by 2016, but 2016 and 2017 are coprime, so LCM(2,...,2016) mod 2017 is congruent to something? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, since LCM(2,...,2016) is divisible by every number from 2 to 2016, then in particular, it is divisible by 2017 - 1 = 2016, but also by 2015, 2014, etc. So perhaps LCM(2,...,2016) is congruent to 0 mod every number from 2 to 2016, so modulo 2017, it's some multiple of those. But this seems not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps notice that LCM(2,...,2016) is a multiple of 2016, so LCM(2,...,2016) = 2016 × k for some integer k. Therefore, modulo 2017, LCM(2,...,2016) ≡ 2016 × k mod 2017. But unless we know k mod 2017, we can't proceed.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use Wilson's theorem, which states that (p-1)! ≡ -1 mod p for prime p. Here, p = 2017, so 2016! ≡ -1 mod 2017. Therefore, 2016! ≡ -1 mod 2017. Therefore, the LCM of numbers from 1 to 2016 is divisible by 2016!, so LCM(1,...,2016) is divisible by 2016!.\n\nBut LCM(1,...,2016) divides 2016! only if all the exponents in the prime factorization of LCM(1,...,2016) are less than or equal to those in 2016!. But actually, LCM(1,...,n) is equal to the product of the highest powers of primes less than or equal to n. So in fact, LCM(1,...,2016) is actually equal to the radical of 2016! (the product of distinct primes up to 2016), but no, actually no, LCM(1,...,n) is the product over primes p ≤ n of p^{floor(log_p n)}. So it's actually larger than 2016!? Wait no, actually LCM(1,...,n) is less than or equal to n! but in reality, LCM(1,...,n) is much smaller than n!.\n\nWait, for example, LCM(1,2,3) = 6 = 3!, but LCM(1,2,3,4) = 12 < 24 = 4!.\n\nWait, so LCM(1,...,n) is actually equal to the least common multiple, which is the product of the highest powers of primes less than or equal to n. For example, LCM(1,...,10) = 2520, which is less than 10!.\n\nBut in any case, LCM(1,...,2016) is equal to the product over primes p ≤ 2016 of p^{k_p}, where k_p is the exponent of p in 2016!.\n\nWait, actually, no. The exponent of a prime p in LCM(1,...,n) is equal to the exponent of p in n! divided by the gcd of the exponents in the numbers? No, actually, the exponent of a prime p in LCM(1,...,n) is equal to the largest exponent of p that appears in any number from 1 to n. Which is floor(log_p n). Wait, actually, the exponent of p in LCM(1,...,n) is equal to the largest integer k such that p^k ≤ n. Wait, no, that's not exactly right. For example, take p=2 and n=8. The exponents of 2 in the numbers 1 through 8 are: 0,1,1,2,1,2,1,3. So the maximum exponent is 3 (from 8=2^3). Therefore, the exponent of 2 in LCM(1,...,8) is 3, which is floor(log_2 8) = 3. Similarly, for p=3 and n=10: the exponents of 3 in numbers 1-10 are: 0,0,1,0,1,0,1,0,2,0. The maximum exponent is 2 (from 9=3^2). And floor(log_3 10) = 2, since 3^2=9 ≤10 < 3^3=27. So in general, the exponent of