le, it was (p - something)/p.\n\nAlternatively, maybe in the first example, because the exponent was n! where n=7,\n\nand p=5,\n\nand 7! is a multiple of φ(p)=4,\n\nsince φ(5)=4,\n\nand 7! is divisible by4,\n\nso the exponent was a multiple of φ(p),\n\nleading to the result?\n\nWait in the first example,\n\nsince p=5,\n\nφ(p)=4,\n\nand the exponent m=7! =5040,\n\nwhich is divisible by4,\n\nso 5^{m}≡1 mod5^1,\n\nbut we already knew that.\n\nHmm,\n\nAlternatively, perhaps in the first example,\n\nsince the exponent m is a multiple of the order of p modulo k,\n\nwhere k=m/p,\n\nbut the order of p modulo k is ?\n\nIn the first example,\n\nk=1008,\n\nand we needed the order of5 modulo1008,\n\nbut since 5 and1008 are coprime,\n\nthe order of5 modulo1008 divides φ(1008).\n\nφ(1008)=φ(16×63)=φ(16)×φ(63)=8×φ(7×9)=8×(6×6)=8×36=288,\n\nso order divides288,\n\nbut exponent m=5040,\n\nwhich is a multiple of288,\n\nsince 5040 ÷288=17.5,\n\nno, not integer,\n\nWait 288×17=4896,\n\n5040-4896=144,\n\nso no,\n\nso exponent m=5040 is not a multiple of the order,\n\nbut in the first example,\n\nwe had 5^{5040}≡1 mod1008,\n\nwhich required that the exponent m was a multiple of the order of5 modulo1008,\n\nbut if the order doesn't divide5040,\n\nthen this would not hold,\n\nbut it did hold.\n\nHmm,\n\nIn any case,\n\nperhaps the key insight from the first example is that the fraction r/m simplifies to (p - 2)/p,\n\nbut that only worked for the first example,\n\nand not the second,\n\nso maybe it's a coincidence.\n\nAlternatively,\n\nin the first example,\n\nthe fraction was 3/5,\n\nand 3 is the exponent's factorial divided by something?\n\nWait 5040 divided by 1680 is 3,\n\nbut not sure.\n\nAlternatively,\n\nsince in the first example,\n\nthe remainder r was equal to m - (m/p),\n\nsince 5040 -1008=4032,\n\nwhich is not 3024,\n\nso no.\n\nAlternatively,\n\nr = m - 2*(m/p),\n\nso 5040 - 2*1008=5040-2016=3024,\n\nwhich matches,\n\nso in first example,\n\nr= m -2*(m/p),\n\nso r/m=1 -2/p,\n\nso in first example,\n\n1 -2/5=3/5,\n\nwhich matches.\n\nIn the second example,\n\nif we tried this,\n\nr= m -2*(m/p)=3628800 -2*(3628800/3)=3628800 -2*(1209600)=3628800 -2419200=1209600,\n\nbut in reality r was1,030,400,\n\nso this doesn't match.\n\nHmm,\n\nAlternatively,\n\nin the first example,\n\nr/m= (p-2)/p,\n\nso 3/5,\n\nand in the second example,\n\nmaybe it's (p - something)/p,\n\nbut not sure.\n\nAlternatively,\n\nsince in the first example,\n\nthe solution required r ≡1 modk,\n\nand r ≡0 modp,\n\nso r= k*s +1,\n\nwith k*s +1 ≡0 modp,\n\nso k*s ≡-1 modp,\n\nthen s≡-k^{-1} modp,\n\nso s= -k^{-1} + p*t,\n\nthen r= k*(-k^{-1} +p*t)+1= -1 +k*p*t +1= k*p*t,\n\nso r= k*p*t,\n\nand since r must be less than m=k*p,\n\nthen t must be 0,\n\nbut that gives r=0,\n\nbut in reality,\n\nin the first example,\n\nr=3024,\n\nwhich is k*p*t with t= 3024/(k*p)=3024/5040=0.6,\n\nso t=0.6,\n\nbut t must be integer.\n\nHmm,\n\nWait,\n\nmaybe my mistake is in thinking that t must be an integer,\n\nbut in reality,\n\nthe solution is r =k*p*t + ...,\n\nbut the Chinese Remainder Theorem gives a unique solution modm,\n\nso r= k*p*t,\n\nbut t is an integer,\n\nand r must be in [0,m),\n\nso t can be fractional?\n\nNo,\n\nWait,\n\nNo,\n\nt must be an integer,\n\nbecause s= -k^{-1} +p*t,\n\nand s must be integer,\n\nsince r= k*s +1,\n\nand s must be integer,\n\nso t must be integer,\n\nso r= k*p*t must be a multiple of kp,\n\nbut then r=0 mod kp,\n\nbut kp=m,\n\nso r≡0 modm,\n\nwhich would make r=0,\n\nbut in the first example,\n\nr=3024≡3024 mod5040,\n\nwhich is not 0,\n\nso this approach is invalid.\n\nThis suggests that my initial approach is incorrect,\n\nand perhaps I need to think differently.\n\nAlternative approach:\n\nGiven that in the first example,\n\nthe remainder r was equal to (m/p)*(p-1),\n\nsince 5040/5*(5-1)=1008*4=4032,\n\nbut that's not 3024,\n\nso no.\n\nWait,\n\nAlternatively,\n\nin the first example,\n\nthe remainder r=3024= (m/p)*(3),\n\nand 3 is (p-2),\n\nso 3024=1008*(5-2)=3024,\n\nwhich works,\n\nso r= (m/p)*(p-2),\n\nthen r/m= (p-2)/p,\n\nwhich is exactly what happened.\n\nIn the second example,\n\nif we do the same,\n\nr= (m/p)*(p-2)= (3628800/3)*(3-2)=1209600*1=1,209,600,\n\nbut the actual remainder was1,030,400,\n\nso that doesn't match.\n\nHmm,\n\nAlternatively,\n\nmaybe in the first example,\n\nthe exponent n! was a multiple of the order of p modulo k,\n\nleading to the result,\n\nbut in the second example,\n\nthe exponent wasn't?\n\nWait,\n\nin the first example,\n\nthe exponent was7!,\n\nand k=1008=7!/5,\n\nand the order of5 modulo1008,\n\nif it divides7!,\n\nthen,\n\nthe order of5 modulo1008,\n\nsince 1008=16×63=16×7×9,\n\nso modulus factors are 16,7,9,\n\nand the order modulo each component,\n\nthen the overall order is LCM of orders modulo each component.\n\nCompute the order of5 modulo16,\n\nmod7,\n\nand mod9,\n\nthen take LCM.\n\nOrder modulo16:\n\nφ(16)=8,\n\nso order divides8,\n\nand 5^1=5 mod16,\n\n5^2=25≡9,\n\n5^4=81≡1 mod16,\n\nso order4 modulo16.\n\nOrder modulo7:\n\nφ(7)=6,\n\n5 mod7=5,\n\n5^1=5,\n\n5^2=25≡4,\n\n5^3=20≡6,\n\n5^4=30≡2,\n\n5^5=10,\n\n5^6=50≡1 mod7,\n\nso order6 modulo7.\n\nOrder modulo9:\n\nφ(9)=6,\n\n5 mod9=5,\n\n5^1=5,\n\n5^2=25≡7,\n\n5^3=35≡8,\n\n5^4=40≡4,\n\n5^5=20≡2,\n\n5^6=10≡1 mod9,\n\nso order6 modulo9.\n\nTherefore,\n\nthe order modulo1008 is LCM(4,6,6)=12.\n\nSo the order of5 modulo1008 is12,\n\nand since the exponent m=7!=5040 is a multiple of12,\n\nsince 5040/12=420,\n\nso yes,\n\nso 5^{5040}≡1 mod1008,\n\nwhich is why it worked out.\n\nTherefore,\n\nin the first example,\n\nbecause the exponent was a multiple of the order of p modulo k,\n\nthen p^{m}≡1 modk,\n\nand p^{m}≡0 modp,\n\nso the solution was straightforward.\n\nIn the second example,\n\nwith p=3,\n\nk=10!/3= 3628800/3=1209600,\n\nthen compute the order of3 modulo k=1209600.\n\nWait,\n\nbut 3 and k are not coprime,\n\nsince k=10!/3,\n\nand 10! includes multiple factors of3,\n\nso k is still divisible by3,\n\nsince10! has exponent of3 equal to floor(10/3)+floor(10/9)=3+1=4,\n\nso 10! has 3^4,\n\nso k=10!/3^4,\n\nso k is not divisible by3,\n\nbecause we divided out all factors of3.\n\nTherefore,\n\nk=10!/3^4,\n\nand since 3 andk are coprime,\n\nthe order of3 modulo k exists.\n\nWait,\n\nWait in the second example,\n\np=3,\n\nk=10!/3,\n\nbut 10! includes multiple factors of3,\n\nso k=10!/3 is still divisible by3^{3},\n\nso actually k is divisible by3,\n\nso 3 andk are not coprime,\n\nso the order is undefined,\n\nwhich complicates things.\n\nAh,\n\nhere is the issue:\n\nIn the second example,\n\np=3,\n\nand k=10!/3,\n\nbut since 10! contains multiple factors of3,\n\nk is still divisible by3,\n\nso gcd(p,k)=3>1,\n\nso the inverse of p modulo k does not exist,\n\nwhich complicates things,\n\nunlike in the first example,\n\nwhere p=5,\n\nand k=1008,\n\nwhich is coprime to5,\n\nsince1008 is divisible by2,3,7 but not5.\n\nTherefore,\n\nin the second example,\n\nthe approach fails because p andk are not coprime,\n\nwhereas in the first example,\n\nthey are coprime.\n\nTherefore,\n\nthe key difference is whether p andk are coprime.\n\nIn the original problem,\n\np=2017,\n\nand k=m/p=2025!/2017,\n\nsince2017 is a prime factor of2025!,\n\nand we've divided out one factor of2017,\n\nso k=2025!/2017 is an integer not divisible by2017,\n\nsince the exponent of2017 in2025! is1,\n\nso k is coprime to2017,\n\nbecause any prime factor ofk must be a prime less than or equal to2025 except2017,\n\nso gcd(2017,k)=1,\n\ntherefore,\n\nin the original problem,\n\np andk are coprime,\n\nso the inverse ofp modulo k exists,\n\nand thus,\n\nthe solution r= p*(p^{-1} modk),\n\nand thus,\n\nthe fraction r/m= [p*(p^{-1} modk)]/(p*k)= [ (p^{-1} modk) ]/k.\n\nTherefore,\n\nin the first example,\n\nthis gave us r/m= (p^{-1} modk)/k,\n\nwhere p^{-1} modk=605,\n\nk=1008,\n\nso 605/1008≈0.6,\n\nbut in reality,\n\nthe fraction was3/5=0.6,\n\nwhich is exactly equal to605/1008,\n\nsince605/1008= (605 ÷ GCD(605,1008))/ (1008 ÷ GCD(605,1008)),\n\nbut since GCD(605,1008)=1,\n\nit's already reduced,\n\nbut 605/1008 simplifies to?\n\nWait,\n\nWait 605 divided by5 is121,\n\n1008 divided by5 is201.6,\n\nso not integer,\n\nso it can't be reduced,\n\nbut 605/1008= (5×121)/(16×63)= (5×11²)/(16×7×9),\n\nwhich doesn't simplify to3/5.\n\nWait a second,\n\nbut 605/1008 is exactly equal to3024/5040,\n\nsince3024/5040= (3024 ÷ 1008)/(5040 ÷1008)=3/5,\n\nWait no,\n\nWait 3024 divided by1008 is3,\n\nand5040 divided by1008 is5,\n\nso yes,\n\n3024/5040=3/5,\n\nbut 605/1008 is not equal to3/5,\n\nbut in reality,\n\nr/m=3024/5040=3/5,\n\nwhich is different from (p^{-1} modk)/k=605/1008≈0.600198,\n\nwhich is approximately0.6 but not exactly.\n\nWait,\n\nWait what's going on here?\n\nIn the first example,\n\nr=3024,\n\nso r/m=3024/5040=3/5,\n\nbut according to the formula r= p*(p^{-1} modk)=5*605=3025,\n\nbut that was for p^{m} modm,\n\nnot p^{m} -1 modm,\n\nso in that case,\n\nfor p^{m} modm=3025,\n\nso p^{m}-1 modm=3024,\n\nso the remainder r=3024,\n\nso r/m=3/5,\n\nwhich is exactly (p - 2)/p,\n\nbut according to the formula,\n\nr/m= [p*(p^{-1} modk) -1]/m,\n\nWait no,\n\nWait,\n\nActually,\n\nthe remainder r is p^{m} -1 modm,\n\nwhich is equal to (p^{m} modm) -1 modm,\n\nso if p^{m} modm = p*(p^{-1} modk),\n\nthen r= [p*(p^{-1} modk)] -1 modm,\n\nbut since p*(p^{-1} modk) ≡1 modk,\n\nthen p*(p^{-1} modk)=1 + s*k,\n\nso r=1 +s*k -1= s*k modm,\n\nso r= s*k,\n\nand s*k <m,\n\nsince r is the remainder,\n\nso s*k <m,\n\nbut m=k*p,\n\nso s <p,\n\nso s is between0 andp-1,\n\nthus,\n\nr/m= s/k,\n\nwhere s= [p*(p^{-1} modk) -1]/k,\n\nHmm,\n\nThis is getting too convoluted.\n\nAlternatively,\n\nsince in the first example,\n\np=5,\n\nk=1008,\n\np^{-1} modk=605,\n\nthen p*(p^{-1} modk)=5*605=3025,\n\nwhich is congruent to1 modk,\n\nso3025 mod1008=1,\n\nso3025=1 +3*1008,\n\nthus,\n\np^{m}=3025=1 +3*1008,\n\ntherefore,\n\np^{m} -1=3*1008,\n\nso the remainder r=3*1008,\n\nso r/m=(3*1008)/5040=3/5,\n\nwhich is exactly the fraction.\n\nAh! Therefore,\n\nin general,\n\nif we have p^{m} ≡1 + s*k modm,\n\nthen p^{m} -1 ≡s*k modm,\n\nso the remainder r= s*k,\n\nand since s*k <m,\n\nthen s must be less than p,\n\nbecause m=k*p,\n\nso s*k <k*p ⇒s <p,\n\nand s is the coefficient such that p^{m} -1= s*k + multiple ofm,\n\nbut since we want the remainder,\n\nit's exactly s*k,\n\nso s= floor( (p^{m} -1)/k ),\n\nbut more importantly,\n\nsince p^{m} ≡1 modk,\n\nso p^{m} -1 ≡0 modk,\n\nso s*k is divisible byk,\n\nso s*k is the remainder,\n\nand since p^{m} -1 ≡0 modk,\n\nthen r= s*k,\n\nand r/m= s/k,\n\nbut s is the quotient when (p^{m}-1)/k,\n\nbut since p^{m} ≡1 modk,\n\nthen (p^{m} -1)/k is an integer,\n\nso s= (p^{m}-1)/k,\n\nso r/m= s/k= (p^{m}-1)/(k^2),\n\nHmm,\n\nWait,\n\nWait,\n\nWait,\n\nIf p^{m} -1 =s*k,\n\nthen r= s*k,\n\nso r/m= (s*k)/(k*p)= s/p,\n\nsince m=k*p,\n\nso r/m= s/p,\n\nwhere s= (p^{m}-1)/k,\n\ntherefore,\n\nr/m= ( (p^{m}-1)/k ) / p= (p^{m}-1)/(k*p)= (p^{m}-1)/m,\n\nso r/m= (p^{m} -1)/m,\n\nWait,\n\nWait,\n\nWait,\n\nWait,\n\nHold on,\n\nIf r= p^{m} -1 modm,\n\nthen r= p^{m} -1 - t*m for some integer t,\n\nbut the remainder r is the smallest non-negative integer such that r ≡p^{m} -1 modm,\n\nso r= (p^{m} -1) modm,\n\nwhich is equal to p^{m} -1 - floor( (p^{m} -1)/m )*m,\n\nbut this doesn't necessarily simplify to anything nice.\n\nHowever,\n\nfrom the first example,\n\nwe saw that r/m= (p^{m} -1)/m,\n\nbecause r= p^{m} -1 - t*m,\n\nso (p^{m} -1)/m= r/m +t,\n\nbut since r <m,\n\nthen (p^{m} -1)/m is between t and t+1,\n\nso it's not an integer.\n\nWait,\n\nWait in the first example,\n\n(p^{m} -1)/m= (5^{5040}-1)/5040,\n\nwhich is a gigantic number,\n\nbut the remainder r is 3024,\n\nso r/m=3024/5040=3/5,\n\nwhich is exactly (5^{5040} -1)/5040 mod1,\n\nbut not exactly the value,\n\nbut in this case,\n\nit so happened that (p^{m} -1)/m had fractional part 3/5,\n\nbut that's just a coincidence.\n\nAlternatively,\n\nin the first example,\n\nsince p^{m} ≡1 modk,\n\nand p^{m} ≡0 modp,\n\nso p^{m}=1 +a*k,\n\nso p^{m}-1=a*k,\n\nso r=a*k,\n\nand since r <m=k*p,\n\nthen a <p,\n\nso a is between0 andp-1,\n\nso a= floor( (p^{m}-1)/k ),\n\nbut since p^{m} ≡1 modk,\n\nthen (p^{m}-1)/k is integer,\n\nso a=(p^{m}-1)/k,\n\nand since p^{m} ≡0 modp,\n\nthen p^{m}=p*b,\n\nso p*b -1= a*k,\n\nso p*b -a*k=1,\n\nwhich is a linear Diophantine equation,\n\nwith solutions for b and a,\n\nbut I don't know if that helps.\n\nHowever,\n\nin the first example,\n\na=(5^{5040}-1)/1008,\n\nbut5^{5040}=3025 mod5040,\n\nso5^{5040}=5040*c +3025,\n\nso5^{5040}-1=5040*c +3024,\n\nso a=(5040*c +3024)/1008=5*c +3,\n\nso a=5*c +3,\n\nthen r= a*k= (5*c +3)*1008,\n\nbut r must be less than5040,\n\nso (5*c +3)*1008 <5040,\n\nso5*c +3 <5,\n\nso5*c <2,\n\nso c=0,\n\nthus,\n\na=3,\n\nso r=3*1008=3024,\n\nwhich matches,\n\nso in this case,\n\na=3,\n\nso r/m=3/5,\n\nsince a/p=3/5,\n\nbecause p=5,\n\nso r/m= a/p,\n\nwhere a= (p^{m}-1)/k,\n\nand since a <p,\n\nthen r/m= a/p,\n\nwhere a= (p^{m}-1)/k,\n\nbut since p^{m} ≡1 modk,\n\nthen (p^{m}-1)/k is integer,\n\nand since p^{m} ≡0 modp,\n\nthen p divides (p^{m}-1 -a*k),\n\nbut this is getting too tangled.\n\nHowever,\n\nin the first example,\n\nthe result was r/m= a/p,\n\nwhere a=3,\n\nand a= (p^{m}-1)/k,\n\nand since p^{m} ≡1 modk,\n\nthen (p^{m}-1)/k is integer,\n\nand in the first example,\n\nthis gave a=3,\n\nwhich is less than p=5,\n\nso r/m= a/p=3/5,\n\nSimilarly,\n\nin the original problem,\n\nif we let a= (2017^{2025!} -1)/k,\n\nwhere k=2025!/2017,\n\nthen since2017^{2025!} ≡1 modk,\n\nthen a is an integer,\n\nand since2017^{2025!} ≡0 mod2017,\n\nthen2017 divides (2017^{2025!} -1 -a*k),\n\nbut since2017 divides2017^{2025!},\n\nthen2017 divides ( -1 -a*k),\n\nso -1 -a*k ≡0 mod2017,\n\nso a*k ≡-1 mod2017,\n\nbut k=2025!/2017,\n\nand since2025! ≡0 mod2017,\n\nthen k=2025!/2017 is an integer,\n\nand since2017 is prime and doesn't divide k,\n\nbecause we've divided out all factors of2017,\n\nso k and2017 are coprime,\n\nso k has an inverse modulo2017,\n\nso a≡-k^{-1} mod2017,\n\nbut a must be less thanp=2017,\n\nsince r= a*k <m= k*p,\n\nso a <p,\n\nso a is the unique integer in0 ≤a <p such that a≡-k^{-1} mod2017,\n\nso a= ( -k^{-1} mod2017 ),\n\nthen,\n\nr/m= a/p,\n\nsince r= a*k,\n\nso r/m= (a*k)/(k*p)=a/p,\n\ntherefore,\n\nthe fraction r/m= a/p,\n\nwhere a is the unique integer between0 andp-1 such that a≡-k^{-1} modp.\n\nBut since a≡-k^{-1} modp,\n\nand a is in[0,p-1],\n\nthen a= (p -k^{-1} modp),\n\nso,\n\nr/m= [ (p -k^{-1} modp) ] /p.\n\nTherefore,\n\nthis is the general formula.\n\nTherefore,\n\nin the original problem,\n\nwe need to compute a= [ -k^{-1} modp ],\n\nwhere k=2025!/2017,\n\nand then r/m= a/p,\n\nwith a between0 andp-1.\n\nTherefore,\n\nto compute r/m,\n\nwe need to compute the inverse ofk modulo2017,\n\nthen negate it modulo2017,\n\nand then divide by2017.\n\nHowever,\n\ncomputing the inverse ofk modulo2017 is non-trivial,\n\nsincek=2025!/2017,\n\nwhich is a huge number,\n\nbut perhaps we can find a smarter way.\n\nNote thatk=2025!/2017,\n\nand we need to computek^{-1} mod2017.\n\nBut since2017 is prime,\n\nby Fermat's Little Theorem,\n\nfor any integer not divisible by2017,\n\nwe havek^{2016}≡1 mod2017,\n\nso inverses can be computed ask^{-1}≡k^{2015} mod2017.\n\nHowever,\n\ncomputingk mod2017 first,\n\nthen raising it to the2015th power mod2017,\n\nwould give the inverse.\n\nTherefore,\n\nfirst computek mod2017,\n\nwherek=2025!/2017,\n\nand then compute(k mod2017)^{2015} mod2017,\n\nwhich will be the inverse,\n\nthen multiply by-1 mod2017,\n\nthen divide by2017,\n\nbut since we're working modulo2017,\n\nthe final result a/p will be a fraction a/p,\n\nwitha being between0 and2016,\n\nso r/m= a/p,\n\nwitha= (-k^{-1} mod2017).\n\nTherefore,\n\nthe key steps are:\n\n1. Computek mod2017,\n\nwherek=2025!/2017.\n\n2. Compute the modular inverse ofk mod2017,\n\nwhich isk^{-1} mod2017.\n\n3. Compute a= (-k^{-1}) mod2017,\n\nso a= (2017 -k^{-1}) mod2017,\n\nifk^{-1}≠0,\n\nwhich it isn't sincek and2017 are coprime.\n\n4. Then the fraction r/m= a/2017.\n\nBut since the problem asks for r/m,\n\nand in the first example this worked out nicely as a simple fraction,\n\nperhaps in the original problem,\n\nthis fraction a/2017 simplifies to a small fraction,\n\npossibly even1/2017 or something,\n\nbut given the complexity,\n\nmaybe there's a trick.\n\nWait,\n\nnote thatk=2025!/2017,\n\nso k is divisible by all numbers from1 to2025 except2017,\n\nsince we divided out one factor of2017.\n\nBut modulo2017,\n\nwe can use Wilson's theorem,\n\nwhich says that (p-1)! ≡-1 modp,\n\nfor primep.\n\nHere,p=2017,\n\nso2016! ≡-1 mod2017.\n\nPerhaps we can expressk mod2017 in terms of factorials.\n\nLet me try that.\n\nFirst,\n\nk=2025!/2017,\n\nso let's write2025! as2025×2024×…×2018×2017×2016!.\n\nTherefore,\n\n2025! = (2017×2018×…×2025) ×2016!.\n\nTherefore,\n\nk=2025! /2017= (2018×2019×…×2025) ×2016!.\n\nTherefore,\n\nk= [2018×2019×…×2025] ×2016!.\n\nNow,\n\nwe need to computek mod2017.\n\nNote that2017 is a prime,\n\nso modulo2017,\n\nwe can reduce each term in the product modulo2017.\n\nFirst,\n\nnote that2018≡1 mod2017,\n\n2019≡2 mod2017,\n\n...\n\n2025≡2025-2017=8 mod2017.\n\nTherefore,\n\nthe product2018×2019×…×2025 mod2017 is equivalent to1×2×3×…×8 mod2017,\n\nsince2018≡1,\n\n2019≡2,\n\n...\n\n2025≡8 mod2017.\n\nTherefore,\n\nthe product from2018 to2025 mod2017 is equal to8! mod2017.\n\nSimilarly,\n\nthe other part is2016! mod2017.\n\nTherefore,\n\nk mod2017= [8! ×2016!] mod2017.\n\nBut2016! ≡-1 mod2017 by Wilson's theorem,\n\nsince2016! ≡-1 mod2017,\n\nso:\n\nk mod2017= [8! × (-1)] mod2017= -8! mod2017.\n\nTherefore,\n\nk ≡-40320 mod2017.\n\nCompute-40320 mod2017,\n\nfirst compute40320 divided by2017,\n\nto find40320 mod2017.\n\nLet me compute2017×20=40340,\n\nwhich is larger than40320,\n\nso2017×19=2017×20 -2017=40340-2017=38323,\n\nthen40320 -38323=1997,\n\nso40320=2017×19 +1997,\n\nso40320 mod2017=1997.\n\nTherefore,\n\nk mod2017≡-1997 mod2017,\n\nwhich is equivalent to2017 -1997=20 mod2017.\n\nTherefore,\n\nk ≡20 mod2017.\n\nTherefore,\n\nk mod2017=20.\n\nTherefore,\n\nwe need to compute the inverse of20 mod2017,\n\nthen multiply by-1 mod2017,\n\nthen divide by2017,\n\nbut let's proceed step by step.\n\nFirst,\n\ncomputek^{-1} mod2017,\n\nwherek≡20 mod2017,\n\nso we need the inverse of20 mod2017.\n\nFind an integerx such that20x≡1 mod2017.\n\nUse the extended Euclidean algorithm.\n\nCompute gcd(20,2017):\n\n2017 ÷20=100 with remainder17,\n\n20 ÷17=1 with remainder3,\n\n17 ÷3=5 with remainder2,\n\n3 ÷2=1 with remainder1,\n\n2 ÷1=2 with remainder0.\n\nSo gcd=1,\n\nbackwards:\n\n1=3 -1×2,\n\nbut2=17 -5×3,\n\nso1=3 -1×(17 -5×3)=6×3 -1×17,\n\nbut3=20 -1×17,\n\nso1=6×(20 -1×17) -1×17=6×20 -7×17,\n\nbut17=2017 -100×20,\n\nso1=6×20 -7×(2017 -100×20)=6×20 -7×2017 +700×20=706×20 -7×2017.\n\nTherefore,\n\n706×20 ≡1 mod2017,\n\nso the inverse of20 mod2017 is706.\n\nTherefore,\n\nk^{-1}≡706 mod2017.\n\nTherefore,\n\na≡-706 mod2017,\n\nwhich is2017 -706=1311 mod2017.\n\nTherefore,\n\na=1311.\n\nTherefore,\n\nthe fraction r/m= a/p=1311/2017.\n\nTherefore,\n\nthe answer is1311/2017,\n\nwhich cannot be simplified further since1311 and2017 are coprime?\n\nLet me check gcd(1311,2017).\n\nCompute gcd(2017,1311):\n\n2017 ÷1311=1 with remainder706,\n\n1311 ÷706=1 with remainder605,\n\n706 ÷605=1 with remainder101,\n\n605 ÷101=5×101=505, remainder100,\n\n101 ÷100=1 with remainder1,\n\n100 ÷1=100 with remainder0,\n\nso gcd is1.\n\nTherefore,\n\nthe fraction1311/2017 is reduced.\n\nTherefore,\n\nthe value of r/2025! is1311/2017,\n\nso the answer is\\boxed{\\dfrac{1311}{2017}}.\n\nBut let me verify this with the first example to see if the method holds.\n\nFirst example:\n\np=5,\n\nk=1008,\n\ncompute k mod5,\n\nsince5 is prime,\n\nk=1008 mod5=1008-201×5=1008-1005=3 mod5,\n\nso k≡3 mod5,\n\nthen compute inverse of3 mod5,\n\nwhich is2 since3×2=6≡1 mod5,\n\nso inverse is2,\n\nthen a≡-2 mod5=3 mod5,\n\nso a=3,\n\nthus r/m=3/5,\n\nwhich matches the first example.\n\nYes,\n\nthis method works!\n\nTherefore,\n\nin the original problem,\n\nthe fraction is1311/2017,\n\nso boxed answer is\\boxed{\\dfrac{1311}{2017}}.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{\\dfrac{1311}{2017}}\n</think>\n\nGiven the problem of finding the remainder \\( r \\) when \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\) is divided by \\( 2025! \\), and then computing \\( \\frac{r}{2025!} \\), we proceed as follows:\n\n1. **Understanding the Problem**:\n   - We need to compute \\( 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\mod 2025! \\).\n   - Let \\( m = 2025! \\). We need to find \\( r \\) such that \\( r \\equiv 2017^{2025!} - 1 \\mod m \\).\n\n2. **Prime Factor Considerations**:\n   - Since 2017 is a prime number less than 2025, it is a factor of \\( 2025! \\).\n   - We use the Chinese Remainder Theorem by breaking down \\( m = 2025! \\) into its prime power components.\n\n3. **Modulo Analysis**:\n   - For each prime power \\( q^e \\) dividing \\( m \\) (where \\( q \\neq 2017 \\)), we use Euler's theorem because \\( 2017 \\) and \\( q \\) are coprime.\n   - For the prime power \\( 2017^1 \\), \\( 2017^{2025!} \\equiv 0 \\mod 2017 \\).\n\n4. **Key Steps**:\n   - Compute \\( k = \\frac{2025!}{2017} \\).\n   - We need to find \\( 2017^{2025!} \\mod k \\) and combine results using the Chinese Remainder Theorem.\n   - Using Wilson's theorem and modular arithmetic properties, we find:\n     - \\( k \\equiv 20 \\mod 2017 \\).\n     - The modular inverse of 20 modulo 2017 is found to be 706.\n     - Thus, \\( a \\equiv -706 \\mod 2017 \\equiv 1311 \\mod 2017 \\).\n\n5. **Final Fraction**:\n   - The fraction \\( \\frac{r}{2025!} \\) simplifies to \\( \\frac{a}{2017} \\) where \\( a = 1311 \\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\dfrac{1311}{2017}}\n\\]","correct":true,"formatted":true},{"dataset_tag":"hmmt25","dataset_path":"/gpfs/share/home/2501210611/opsd_analysis/OPSD_Analysis/data/HMMT25/test.parquet","category":"","problem_id":"7","problem":"There exists a unique triple $(a, b, c)$ of positive real numbers that satisfies the equations\n$$\n2\\left(a^{2}+1\\right)=3\\left(b^{2}+1\\right)=4\\left(c^{2}+1\\right) \\quad \\text { and } \\quad a b+b c+c a=1\n$$\n\nCompute $a+b+c$.","ground_truth":"\\frac{9 \\sqrt{23}}{23}","gen_n":8,"pass_at_k":{"1":false,"4":true,"8":true},"generations":[{"predicted_answer":"[no boxed]","full_generation":"Okay, so I have this problem here: there's a unique triple (a, b, c) of positive real numbers that satisfies the equations\n\n$$\n2\\left(a^{2}+1\\right) = 3\\left(b^{2}+1\\right) = 4\\left(c^{2}+1\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nab + bc + ca = 1\n$$\n\nAnd I need to compute the value of a + b + c. Alright, let me try to unpack this step by step.\n\nFirst, the problem mentions that there exists a unique triple (a, b, c) of positive real numbers that satisfy those two equations. So, my goal is to find the values of a, b, and c that satisfy both equations, and then add them together to get a + b + c.\n\nLooking at the first set of equations: it says that 2(a² + 1) equals 3(b² + 1) and that equals 4(c² + 1). So, all three expressions are equal to each other. Let me denote this common value by some constant, say, k. So,\n\n$$\n2(a^{2} + 1) = k \\\\\n3(b^{2} + 1) = k \\\\\n4(c^{2} + 1) = k\n$$\n\nTherefore, I can write each variable squared in terms of k.\n\nStarting with the first equation:\n\n$$\n2(a^{2} + 1) = k \\\\\n\\Rightarrow a^{2} + 1 = \\frac{k}{2} \\\\\n\\Rightarrow a^{2} = \\frac{k}{2} - 1 \\\\\n\\Rightarrow a = \\sqrt{\\frac{k}{2} - 1}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, from the second equation:\n\n$$\n3(b^{2} + 1) = k \\\\\n\\Rightarrow b^{2} + 1 = \\frac{k}{3} \\\\\n\\Rightarrow b^{2} = \\frac{k}{3} - 1 \\\\\n\\Rightarrow b = \\sqrt{\\frac{k}{3} - 1}\n$$\n\nAnd from the third equation:\n\n$$\n4(c^{2} + 1) = k \\\\\n\\Rightarrow c^{2} + 1 = \\frac{k}{4} \\\\\n\\Rightarrow c^{2} = \\frac{k}{4} - 1 \\\\\n\\Rightarrow c = \\sqrt{\\frac{k}{4} - 1}\n$$\n\nSo, all three variables a, b, c are expressed in terms of k. Since a, b, c are positive real numbers, the expressions under the square roots must be positive. Therefore, we have constraints:\n\nFor a: \\(\\frac{k}{2} - 1 > 0 \\implies k > 2\\)\n\nFor b: \\(\\frac{k}{3} - 1 > 0 \\implies k > 3\\)\n\nFor c: \\(\\frac{k}{4} - 1 > 0 \\implies k > 4\\)\n\nTherefore, the most restrictive condition is k > 4. So, k must be greater than 4.\n\nOkay, so now I have expressions for a, b, c in terms of k. The second equation given is:\n\n$$\nab + bc + ca = 1\n$$\n\nSo, if I can express ab + bc + ca in terms of k, then I can set that equal to 1 and solve for k, and then backtrack to find a, b, c, and then compute a + b + c.\n\nAlright, so let me write expressions for a, b, c in terms of k first, as above:\n\nLet me denote:\n\n$$\na = \\sqrt{\\frac{k}{2} - 1} \\\\\nb = \\sqrt{\\frac{k}{3} - 1} \\\\\nc = \\sqrt{\\frac{k}{4} - 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, I need to compute the expression ab + bc + ca in terms of k and set it equal to 1. This seems a bit complicated because it will involve square roots and products of square roots. Maybe I can simplify this expression by squaring or something? Hmm, not sure yet. Let me try writing down the expression step by step.\n\nFirst, let me denote the terms under the square roots for clarity:\n\nLet me set:\n\n$$\na = \\sqrt{A}, \\quad b = \\sqrt{B}, \\quad c = \\sqrt{C}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nA = \\frac{k}{2} - 1 \\\\\nB = \\frac{k}{3} - 1 \\\\\nC = \\frac{k}{4} - 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second equation becomes:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{A} \\cdot \\sqrt{B} + \\sqrt{B} \\cdot \\sqrt{C} + \\sqrt{C} \\cdot \\sqrt{A} = 1\n$$\n\nWhich simplifies to:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{AB} + \\sqrt{BC} + \\sqrt{CA} = 1\n$$\n\nHmm, perhaps working with A, B, C might be easier? Let me see.\n\nAlternatively, maybe instead of substituting variables, I can just work with the expressions for a, b, c in terms of k directly. Let me try that.\n\nSo, ab is:\n\n$$\nab = \\sqrt{\\frac{k}{2} - 1} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{k}{3} - 1}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\nbc = \\sqrt{\\frac{k}