C = (k/4 -1)(k/2 -1) = (k²/8 -k/4 -k/2 +1) =k²/8 - (2k/8 +4k/8) +1 =k²/8 -6k/8 +1,\n\nTherefore, adding TermA + TermB + TermC:\n\nFirst, collect the k² terms:\n\nk²/6 + k²/12 + k²/8,\n\nConvert to 24 denominator:\n\n= (4k²)/24 + (2k²)/24 + (3k²)/24 = (9k²)/24 = (3k²)/8,\n\nNext, the linear terms:\n\n-5k/6 -7k/12 -6k/8,\n\nConvert to 24 denominator:\n\n- (20k)/24 - (14k)/24 - (18k)/24 = - (52k)/24 = -13k/6,\n\nConstant terms:\n\n1 +1 +1 =3,\n\nTherefore, TermA + TermB + TermC = (3k²)/8 - (13k)/6 +3.\n\nTherefore, the equation becomes:\n\n(3k²/8 -13k/6 +3) + 2 sqrt{ [(k/2 -1)(k/3 -1)(k/4 -1)] } * sqrt(13k/12 -1) =1.\n\nHmm. This is still very complicated, but perhaps we can denote Q = sqrt{ [(k/2 -1)(k/3 -1)(k/4 -1)] } * sqrt(13k/12 -1),\n\nthen the equation is:\n\n(3k²/8 -13k/6 +3) + 2Q =1 ⇒ 2Q =1 - (3k²/8 -13k/6 +3) = -3k²/8 +13k/6 -2,\n\nThus,\n\nQ = [ -3k²/8 +13k/6 -2 ] / 2.\n\nBut Q is also equal to sqrt{ [(k/2 -1)(k/3 -1)(k/4 -1)] } * sqrt(13k/12 -1),\n\nso,\n\nsqrt{ [(k/2 -1)(k/3 -1)(k/4 -1)] } * sqrt(13k/12 -1) = [ -3k²/8 +13k/6 -2 ] / 2.\n\nSquare both sides to eliminate the square roots:\n\nLeft side squared:\n\n[(k/2 -1)(k/3 -1)(k/4 -1)] * (13k/12 -1),\n\nRight side squared:\n\n[ (-3k²/8 +13k/6 -2)/2 ]^2 = [ (-3k²/8 +13k/6 -2) ]^2 /4.\n\nTherefore,\n\n[(k/2 -1)(k/3 -1)(k/4 -1)] * (13k/12 -1) = [ (-3k²/8 +13k/6 -2) ]^2 /4.\n\nThis equation is now in terms of k only, but it's a high-degree polynomial equation. Let me see if I can compute both sides.\n\nFirst, compute the left-hand side (LHS):\n\nFirst, compute (k/2 -1)(k/3 -1)(k/4 -1):\n\nLet me first compute (k/2 -1)(k/3 -1):\n\nLet me denote A = (k/2 -1)(k/3 -1),\n\nas before, which we expanded to k²/6 -5k/6 +1,\n\nthen multiply by (k/4 -1):\n\nA*(k/4 -1) = [k²/6 -5k/6 +1]*(k/4 -1)\n\nMultiply term by term:\n\nFirst term: k²/6 *k/4 = k³/24,\n\nSecond term: k²/6*(-1) = -k²/6,\n\nThird term: -5k/6 *k/4 = -5k²/24,\n\nFourth term: -5k/6*(-1) =5k/6,\n\nFifth term: 1*k/4 =k/4,\n\nSixth term:1*(-1) =-1,\n\nSo altogether:\n\nk³/24 -k²/6 -5k²/24 +5k/6 +k/4 -1,\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nk³/24,\n\nFor k² terms: -k²/6 -5k²/24 = (-4k²/24 -5k²/24)= -9k²/24 = -3k²/8,\n\nFor k terms:5k/6 +k/4 = (10k/12 +3k/12)=13k/12,\n\nConstants: -1,\n\nThus,\n\n(k/2 -1)(k/3 -1)(k/4 -1)=k³/24 -3k²/8 +13k/12 -1.\n\nTherefore, the LHS is:\n\n[ k³/24 -3k²/8 +13k/12 -1 ] * (13k/12 -1).\n\nNow, compute this product:\n\nLet me denote P = k³/24 -3k²/8 +13k/12 -1,\n\nand Q =13k/12 -1,\n\nthen LHS = P * Q.\n\nLet me compute P*Q term by term:\n\nFirst, multiply k³/24 by Q:\n\nk³/24 *13k/12 =13k⁴/(24*12)=13k⁴/288,\n\nk³/24*(-1)= -k³/24,\n\nNext, multiply -3k²/8 by Q:\n\n-3k²/8 *13k/12 = -39k³/(8*12)= -39k³/96 = -13k³/32,\n\n-3k²/8*(-1)=3k²/8,\n\nNext, multiply 13k/12 by Q:\n\n13k/12*13k/12=169k²/144,\n\n13k/12*(-1)= -13k/12,\n\nFinally, multiply -1 by Q:\n\n-1*(13k/12 -1)= -13k/12 +1,\n\nNow, combine all these terms:\n\n13k⁴/288,\n\n- k³/24 -13k³/32,\n\n+3k²/8 +169k²/144,\n\n-13k/12 -13k/12,\n\n+1,\n\nLet me compute each degree term separately:\n\n1) k⁴ term: 13k⁴/288,\n\n2) k³ terms:\n\n- k³/24 -13k³/32,\n\nTo combine, common denominator 96:\n\n- (4k³)/96 - (39k³)/96 = -43k³/96,\n\n3) k² terms:\n\n3k²/8 +169k²/144,\n\nConvert to common denominator 144:\n\n(54k²)/144 +169k²/144 =223k²/144,\n\n4) k terms:\n\n-13k/12 -13k/12 = -26k/12 = -13k/6,\n\n5) constants:\n\n+1,\n\nSo overall,\n\nLHS = (13/288)k⁴ - (43/96)k³ + (223/144)k² - (13/6)k +1.\n\nNow, compute the right-hand side (RHS):\n\n[ (-3k²/8 +13k/6 -2) ]^2 /4.\n\nFirst, compute the expression inside the square:\n\nLet me denote R = -3k²/8 +13k/6 -2,\n\nthen R = - (3/8)k² + (13/6)k -2,\n\nCompute R squared:\n\nR² = [ -3k²/8 +13k/6 -2 ]²,\n\nLet me expand this:\n\nLet me write R = A + B + C where,\n\nA = -3k²/8,\n\nB =13k/6,\n\nC = -2,\n\nThen,\n\nR² = (A + B + C)^2 = A² + B² + C² + 2AB + 2AC + 2BC,\n\nCompute each term:\n\nA² = (9k⁴)/64,\n\nB² = (169k²)/36,\n\nC² =4,\n\n2AB =2*(-3k²/8)*(13k/6)=2*(-39k³)/48= -39k³/24,\n\n2AC =2*(-3k²/8)*(-2)=2*(6k²/8)= (12k²)/8 = (3k²)/2,\n\n2BC =2*(13k/6)*(-2)=2*(-26k/6)= -52k/6 = -26k/3,\n\nThus,\n\nR² = (9k⁴)/64 + (169k²)/36 +4 - (39k³)/24 + (3k²)/2 - (26k)/3,\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nk⁴ term: 9k⁴/64,\n\nk³ term: -39k³/24 = -13k³/8,\n\nk² terms: 169k²/36 +3k²/2 = 169k²/36 +54k²/36 =223k²/36,\n\nk terms: -26k/3,\n\nconstants:4,\n\nTherefore,\n\nR² = (9/64)k⁴ - (13/8)k³ + (223/36)k² - (26/3)k +4,\n\nTherefore, RHS = R² /4 = [ (9/64)k⁴ - (13/8)k³ + (223/36)k² - (26/3)k +4 ] /4,\n\n= (9/256)k⁴ - (13/32)k³ + (223/144)k² - (13/6)k +1.\n\nTherefore, the equation is:\n\nLHS = RHS,\n\nwhich is,\n\n(13/288)k⁴ - (43/96)k³ + (223/144)k² - (13/6)k +1 = (9/256)k⁴ - (13/32)k³ + (223/144)k² - (13/6)k +1.\n\nSubtract RHS from both sides:\n\nLHS - RHS =0,\n\nThus,\n\n[13/288 -9/256]k⁴ + [ -43/96 +13/32 ]k³ + [223/144 -223/144]k² + [ -13/6 +13/6 ]k + [1 -1] =0.\n\nSimplify each coefficient:\n\nFirst term (k⁴):\n\n13/288 -9/256,\n\nFind common denominator, which is 288*256, but maybe simplify fractions first.\n\n13/288 -9/256,\n\nLet me compute decimal approximations:\n\n13/288≈0.0451389,\n\n9/256≈0.03515625,\n\nDifference≈0.0451389 -0.03515625≈0.00998265,\n\nBut let's do it exactly:\n\nConvert to common denominator, which is 288*256=73728,\n\n13/288 = (13*256)/73728 =3328/73728,\n\n9/256 = (9*288)/73728=2592/73728,\n\nThus,\n\n13/288 -9/256 = (3328 -2592)/73728=736/73728= divide numerator and denominator by 16: 46/4608=23/2304≈0.009990,\n\nSo first coefficient is 23/2304.\n\nSecond term (k³):\n\n-43/96 +13/32,\n\nConvert to common denominator 96:\n\n-43/96 + (13/32)*(3/3)= -43/96 +39/96= (-43 +39)/96= -4/96= -1/24.\n\nThird term (k²): 0,\n\nFourth term (k): 0,\n\nFifth term:0,\n\nThus, the equation reduces to:\n\n(23/2304)k⁴ - (1/24)k³ =0,\n\nFactor out k³:\n\nk³ [ (23/2304)k - 1/24 ] =0,\n\nThus, either k³=0 ⇒k=0, which is invalid since k>4,\n\nor,\n\n(23/2304)k -1/24 =0 ⇒ (23/2304)k =1/24 ⇒ k= (1/24)*(2304/23)= (2304)/(24*23)= (2304/24)/23= 96/23≈4.1739.\n\nAh! So, finally, we get k=96/23≈4.1739.\n\nSo, k=96/23,\n\nTherefore, now we can compute a + b + c = sqrt(13k/12 -1).\n\nLet me compute 13k/12 -1 with k=96/23:\n\nFirst, compute 13*(96/23)/12 -1,\n\n= (13*96)/(23*12) -1,\n\nSimplify:\n\n13*96 / (23*12) = (13*8)/23 = 104/23,\n\nsince 96/12=8,\n\nThus,\n\n13k/12 -1 =104/23 -1 = (104 -23)/23=81/23,\n\nTherefore,\n\n(a + b + c)^2 =81/23,\n\nso,\n\na + b + c = sqrt(81/23)=9/sqrt(23)= (9√23)/23.\n\nRationalizing the denominator,\n\nWait, but sqrt(81/23)=9/sqrt(23)= (9√23)/23.\n\nTherefore, the exact value of a + b + c is 9√23⁄23.\n\nBut let me verify this result with the value of k=96/23≈4.1739,\n\nLet me compute a, b, c:\n\nFirst, compute a = sqrt( (k/2) -1 ) = sqrt( (48/23) -1 )= sqrt( (48 -23)/23 )= sqrt(25/23)=5/sqrt(23)=5√23/23,\n\nSimilarly, b= sqrt( (k/3) -1 )= sqrt( (32/23) -1 )= sqrt( (32 -23)/23 )= sqrt(9/23)=3/sqrt(23)=3√23/23,\n\nc= sqrt( (k/4) -1 )= sqrt( (24/23) -1 )= sqrt( (24 -23)/23 )= sqrt(1/23)=1/sqrt(23)=√23/23,\n\nTherefore,\n\na=5√23/23,\n\nb=3√23/23,\n\nc=√23/23,\n\nThus, a + b + c= (5 +3 +1)√23/23=9√23/23,\n\nWhich matches the earlier result.\n\nNow, let me verify that ab + bc + ca=1,\n\nCompute ab:\n\na*b= (5√23/23)*(3√23/23)=15*(23)/ (23*23)=15/23,\n\nSimilarly, bc= (3√23/23)*(√23/23)=3*(23)/(23*23)=3/23,\n\nca= (√23/23)*(5√23/23)=5*(23)/(23*23)=5/23,\n\nTherefore,\n\nab + bc + ca=15/23 +3/23 +5/23=23/23=1,\n\nPerfect! It checks out.\n\nTherefore, the exact value of a + b + c is 9√23⁄23,\n\nwhich can be written as 9/√23, but rationalized is 9√23⁄23.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 9√23⁄23,\n\nSo, in boxed form, \\boxed{\\dfrac{9\\sqrt{23}}{23}}.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{\\dfrac{9\\sqrt{23}}{23}}\n</think>\n\nGiven the equations:\n\n\\[\n2(a^2 + 1) = 3(b^2 + 1) = 4(c^2 + 1) = k\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\nab + bc + ca = 1\n\\]\n\nwe start by expressing \\(a^2\\), \\(b^2\\), and \\(c^2\\) in terms of \\(k\\):\n\n\\[\na^2 = \\frac{k}{2} - 1, \\quad b^2 = \\frac{k}{3} - 1, \\quad c^2 = \\frac{k}{4} - 1\n\\]\n\nWe denote the common value \\(k\\) and express \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) as:\n\n\\[\na = \\sqrt{\\frac{k}{2} - 1}, \\quad b = \\sqrt{\\frac{k}{3} - 1}, \\quad c = \\sqrt{\\frac{k}{4} - 1}\n\\]\n\nWe then use the identity \\((a + b + c)^2 = a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + 2(ab + bc + ca)\\). Substituting the expressions for \\(a^2\\), \\(b^2\\), \\(c^2\\), and \\(ab + bc + ca = 1\\):\n\n\\[\na^2 + b^2 + c^2 = \\left(\\frac{k}{2} - 1\\right) + \\left(\\frac{k}{3} - 1\\right) + \\left(\\frac{k}{4} - 1\\right) = \\frac{13k}{12} - 3\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n(a + b + c)^2 = \\left(\\frac{13k}{12} - 3\\right) + 2 \\cdot 1 = \\frac{13k}{12} - 1\n\\]\n\nTo find \\(k\\), we solve the system of equations derived from the given conditions. After significant algebraic manipulation and solving the polynomial equation, we find \\(k = \\frac{96}{23}\\).\n\nSubstituting \\(k = \\frac{96}{23}\\) back into the expression for \\((a + b + c)^2\\):\n\n\\[\n(a + b + c)^2 = \\frac{13 \\cdot \\frac{96}{23}}{12} - 1 = \\frac{104}{23} - 1 = \\frac{81}{23}\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root, we get:\n\n\\[\na + b + c = \\sqrt{\\frac{81}{23}} = \\frac{9\\sqrt{23}}{23}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\dfrac{9\\sqrt{23}}{23}}\n\\]","num_tokens":21937,"correct":true,"formatted":true},{"predicted_answer":"\\dfrac{15}{8}","full_generation":"Okay, so I have this problem here: I need to find a unique triple (a, b, c) of positive real numbers that satisfies two equations:\n\nFirst, 2(a² + 1) = 3(b² + 1) = 4(c² + 1), and second, ab + bc + ca = 1. Then, I have to compute a + b + c and put the final answer in a box. Alright, let's see.\n\nHmm, the first part of the problem says that 2(a² + 1) equals 3(b² + 1) and also equals 4(c² + 1). Since they're all equal, I can set them equal to some common constant, right? Let me call that common value k. So, let me define:\n\nLet k = 2(a² + 1) = 3(b² + 1) = 4(c² + 1).\n\nSo, this gives me three separate equations:\n\n1) 2(a² + 1) = k\n\n2) 3(b² + 1) = k\n\n3) 4(c² + 1) = k\n\nTherefore, I can solve each of these equations for a², b², and c² respectively:\n\nFrom equation 1): 2a² + 2 = k ⇒ 2a² = k - 2 ⇒ a² = (k - 2)/2\n\nFrom equation 2): 3b² + 3 = k ⇒ 3b² = k - 3 ⇒ b² = (k - 3)/3\n\nFrom equation 3): 4c² + 4 = k ⇒ 4c² = k - 4 ⇒ c² = (k - 4)/4\n\nOkay, so now I have expressions for a², b², and c² in terms of k. Since a, b, c are positive real numbers, then a, b, c are positive square roots of those expressions, so:\n\na = √[(k - 2)/2]\n\nb = √[(k - 3)/3]\n\nc = √[(k - 4)/4]\n\nAlright, so now I have a, b, c expressed in terms of k. The next part of the problem is that these must satisfy the second equation: ab + bc + ca = 1.\n\nSo, I need to substitute these expressions into ab + bc + ca and set it equal to 1, then solve for k. Once I have k, I can then compute a, b, c, and then compute a + b + c.\n\nOkay, so let's try to do that step by step.\n\nFirst, let me write expressions for a, b, c again:\n\na = √[(k - 2)/2]\n\nb = √[(k - 3)/3]\n\nc = √[(k - 4)/4]\n\nHmm, so to compute ab + bc + ca, I need to compute the products of each pair and then add them up.\n\nLet me first compute each product individually.\n\nFirst, compute ab:\n\nab = √[(k - 2)/2] * √[(k - 3)/3]\n\nSimilarly, bc = √[(k - 3)/3] * √[(k - 4)/4]\n\nAnd ca = √[(k - 4)/4] * √[(k - 2)/2]\n\nHmm, so each of these products is the product of two square roots, which can be written as a single square root of the product of the insides. Remember that √x * √y = √(xy), provided that x and y are non-negative, which they are here because a, b, c are positive real numbers, so the expressions inside the square roots must be non-negative as well. Therefore, we must have that (k - 2)/2 ≥ 0 ⇒ k ≥ 2,\n\n(k - 3)/3 ≥ 0 ⇒ k ≥ 3,\n\n(k - 4)/4 ≥ 0 ⇒ k ≥ 4.\n\nSo, the most restrictive condition is k ≥ 4. Since all three expressions under the square roots must be non-negative, k must be at least 4. So, k is some real number greater than or equal to 4. Alright, so k ∈ [4, ∞).\n\nSo, proceeding with that in mind, let's write the products ab, bc, ca as:\n\nab = √[ ( (k - 2)/2 ) * ( (k - 3)/3 ) ] = √[ ( (k - 2)(k - 3) ) / (2*3) ] = √[ (k - 2)(k - 3)/6 ]\n\nSimilarly,\n\nbc = √[ ( (k - 3)/3 ) * ( (k - 4)/4 ) ] = √[ ( (k - 3)(k - 4) ) / (3*4) ] = √[ (k - 3)(k - 4)/12 ]\n\nAnd,\n\nca = √[ ( (k - 4)/4 ) * ( (k - 2)/2 ) ] = √[ ( (k - 4)(k - 2) ) / (4*2) ] = √[ (k - 4)(k - 2)/8 ]\n\nTherefore, ab + bc + ca = √[ (k - 2)(k - 3)/6 ] + √[ (k - 3)(k - 4)/12 ] + √[ (k - 4)(k - 2)/8 ]\n\nAnd this sum is equal to 1.\n\nSo, we have the equation:\n\n√[ (k - 2)(k - 3)/6 ] + √[ (k - 3)(k - 4)/12 ] + √[ (k - 4)(k - 2)/8 ] = 1\n\nHmm, this looks a bit complicated. It has three square roots, each involving terms with k. I wonder if there's a substitution that can simplify this equation?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can let t = k - something? Let me see.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can let x = k - 3? Since the terms (k - 2), (k - 3), and (k - 4) are consecutive terms decreasing by 1. Let me see:\n\nLet me set t = k - 3, so that k = t + 3. Then:\n\nk - 2 = (t + 3) - 2 = t + 1,\n\nk - 3 = t,\n\nk - 4 = (t + 3) - 4 = t - 1.\n\nTherefore, substituting into the expressions:\n\nab term: √[ (t + 1) * t / 6 ]\n\nbc term: √[ t * (t - 1) / 12 ]\n\nca term: √[ (t - 1) * (t + 1) / 8 ]\n\nTherefore, the equation becomes:\n\n√[ t(t + 1)/6 ] + √[ t(t - 1)/12 ] + √[ (t² - 1)/8 ] = 1\n\nHmm, not sure if that helps, but perhaps?\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can set another substitution. Let me think.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can let u = √[(k - 2)(k - 3)/6], v = √[(k - 3)(k - 4)/12], w = √[(k - 4)(k - 2)/8], so that u + v + w = 1. But I don't know if that helps. Maybe not directly.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can square both sides of the equation to eliminate some square roots, but squaring might complicate things because of cross terms. Let me see.\n\nLet me denote the left-hand side as S = √[ (k - 2)(k - 3)/6 ] + √[ (k - 3)(k - 4)/12 ] + √[ (k - 4)(k - 2)/8 ]\n\nWe have S = 1.\n\nIf I square both sides, then:\n\nS² = [√A + √B + √C]² = A + B + C + 2(√(AB) + √(AC) + √(BC)) = 1² = 1\n\nWhere I let A = (k - 2)(k - 3)/6,\n\nB = (k - 3)(k - 4)/12,\n\nC = (k - 4)(k - 2)/8.\n\nSo, then:\n\nA + B + C + 2(√(AB) + √(AC) + √(BC)) = 1\n\nHmm, okay, so now, if I can compute A + B + C, and then express the other terms in terms of k or in terms of variables related to k, maybe?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps this is getting too convoluted, and maybe it's better to instead consider that since the problem states that there is a unique triple (a, b, c) of positive real numbers satisfying these equations, so perhaps there's some symmetry or substitution that can be made here?\n\nAlternatively, maybe instead of introducing k, we can set variables in terms of ratios?\n\nWait, let me think again.\n\nWe have 2(a² + 1) = 3(b² + 1) = 4(c² + 1). Let me denote that common value as k as before, so each of those expressions equals k.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps set variables such that:\n\nLet’s denote:\n\n2(a² + 1) = k ⇒ a² = (k/2) - 1,\n\n3(b² + 1) = k ⇒ b² = (k/3) - 1,\n\n4(c² + 1) = k ⇒ c² = (k/4) - 1.\n\nWait, but actually, that's the same as before. Since 2(a² +1)=k ⇒ a² = (k/2) -1, etc.\n\nWait, but I just realized: in my previous steps, I had written:\n\nFrom equation 1): 2a² + 2 = k ⇒ a² = (k - 2)/2,\n\nSimilarly, equation 2): 3b² + 3 = k ⇒ b² = (k - 3)/3,\n\nEquation 3): 4c² + 4 = k ⇒ c² = (k - 4)/4,\n\nYes, that's correct. So, in terms of k, a², b², c² are as above.\n\nAlternatively, maybe instead of working with k, I can express a, b, c in terms of some other variable?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps set variables x = a², y = b², z = c². Then, we have:\n\n2(x + 1) = 3(y + 1) = 4(z + 1) = k,\n\nand also, the second equation is ab + bc + ca = 1.\n\nBut then, in terms of x, y, z, we can write a = √x, b = √y, c = √z, so ab + bc + ca = √(xy) + √(yz) + √(zx) = 1.\n\nHmm, so perhaps we can work with x, y, z?\n\nLet me try that approach.\n\nLet me set:\n\nLet’s let’s define:\n\n2(x + 1) = 3(y + 1) = 4(z + 1) = k,\n\nso then,\n\nx = (k/2) - 1,\n\ny = (k/3) - 1,\n\nz = (k/4) - 1,\n\nas before.\n\nThen, since a = √x, b = √y, c = √z,\n\nthen ab + bc + ca = √x √y + √y √z + √z √x = √(xy) + √(yz) + √(zx) = 1.\n\nSo, substituting x, y, z in terms of k:\n\n√[ ( (k/2 - 1) * (k/3 - 1) ) ] + √[ ( (k/3 - 1) * (k/4 - 1) ) ] + √[ ( (k/4 - 1) * (k/2 - 1) ) ] = 1\n\nHmm, this seems similar to what I had before. Let me check:\n\nWait, let me see:\n\nFirst term: √(xy) = √[ ( (k/2 - 1)(k/3 - 1) ) ]\n\nSecond term: √(yz) = √[ ( (k/3 - 1)(k/4 - 1) ) ]\n\nThird term: √(zx) = √[ ( (k/4 - 1)(k/2 - 1) ) ]\n\nYes, so that is the same as before. So, in terms of k, this is the equation we have to solve.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps instead of working with k, I can let t = k - something? Maybe set t = k - 4? Since k must be at least 4, so t ≥ 0.\n\nLet me try that. Let t = k - 4, so that k = t + 4, with t ≥ 0.\n\nThen:\n\nFirst, compute each term inside the square roots:\n\nFirst term inside the first square root: (k/2 - 1)(k/3 - 1)\n\nLet me compute k/2 - 1 when k = t + 4:\n\n(k/2 - 1) = ( (t + 4)/2 - 1 ) = (t/2 + 2 - 1) = t/2 + 1\n\nSimilarly, (k/3 - 1) = ( (t + 4)/3 - 1 ) = (t/3 + 4/3 - 1 ) = t/3 + 1/3\n\nSimilarly, (k/4 - 1) = ( (t + 4)/4 - 1 ) = (t/4 + 1 - 1) = t/4\n\nTherefore, substituting into the terms:\n\nFirst term: √[ (t/2 + 1)(t/3 + 1/3) ]\n\nSecond term: √[ (t/3 + 1/3)(t/4) ]\n\nThird term: √[ (t/4)(t/2 + 1) ]\n\nHmm, so the equation becomes:\n\n√[ (t/2 + 1)(t/3 + 1/3) ] + √[ (t/3 + 1/3)(t/4) ] + √[ (t/4)(t/2 + 1) ] = 1\n\nHmm, still complicated, but maybe this substitution makes it a bit more manageable? Let me see.\n\nLet me try to compute each term step by step.\n\nFirst term: √[ (t/2 + 1)(t/3 + 1/3) ]\n\nLet me factor out 1/3 from the second bracket: (t/3 + 1/3) = (1/3)(t + 1)\n\nSimilarly, first bracket: (t/2 + 1) = (1/2)(t + 2)\n\nTherefore, first term inside sqrt becomes:\n\n(1/2)(t + 2) * (1/3)(t + 1) = (1/6)(t + 2)(t + 1)\n\nTherefore, first term is √[ (1/6)(t + 1)(t + 2) ] = √( (t + 1)(t + 2) ) / √6\n\nSecond term: √[ (t/3 + 1/3)(t/4) ]\n\nSimilarly, (t/3 + 1/3) = (1/3)(t + 1), so:\n\nInside the sqrt: (1/3)(t + 1) * (t/4) = (t(t + 1))/(12)\n\nTherefore, second term is √[ t(t + 1)/12 ] = √[ t(t + 1) ] / √12 = √[ t(t + 1) ] / (2√3)\n\nThird term: √[ (t/4)(t/2 + 1) ]\n\nSimilarly, (t/2 + 1) = (1/2)(t + 2), so:\n\nInside the sqrt: (t/4) * (1/2)(t + 2) = t(t + 2)/8\n\nTherefore, third term is √[ t(t + 2)/8 ] = √[ t(t + 2) ] / √8 = √[ t(t + 2) ] / (2√2)\n\nTherefore, putting all three terms together:\n\nFirst term: √[ (t + 1)(t + 2) ] / √6\n\nSecond term: √[ t(t + 1) ] / (2√3)\n\nThird term: √[ t(t + 2) ] / (2√2)\n\nSo the equation is:\n\n[ √{(t + 1)(t + 2)} / √6 ] + [ √{t(t + 1)} / (2√3) ] + [ √{t(t + 2)} / (2√2) ] = 1\n\nHmm, this seems a bit better? Maybe not by much, but perhaps now I can let u = √t, or some other substitution?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps set s = √t, so that t = s², but then we have terms with √(t + 1), √(t + 2), etc. Maybe not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps make a substitution for the entire expression. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe let me denote some variables for the square roots:\n\nLet’s let’s set:\n\nLet’s denote:\n\nA = √[ t(t + 1) ]\n\nB = √[ t(t + 2) ]\n\nC = √[ (t + 1)(t + 2) ]\n\nThen, the equation becomes:\n\nC / √6 + A / (2√3) + B / (2√2) = 1\n\nHmm, but then, note that C can be expressed in terms of A and B?\n\nWait, let me see:\n\nC = √[ (t + 1)(t + 2) ] = √[ (t(t + 2) + (t + 2)) ] Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, note that (t + 1)(t + 2) = t² + 3t + 2,\n\nand t(t + 1) = t² + t,\n\nand t(t + 2) = t² + 2t,\n\nBut perhaps this isn’t helpful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps express C in terms of A and B:\n\nWait, note that:\n\nC² = (t + 1)(t + 2) = t² + 3t + 2,\n\nA² = t(t + 1) = t² + t,\n\nB² = t(t + 2) = t² + 2t,\n\nTherefore, C² = A² + 2t + 2,\n\nWait, let me check:\n\nA² + 2t + 2 = (t² + t) + 2t + 2 = t² + 3t + 2 = C²,\n\nYes, so C² = A² + 2t + 2,\n\nBut I don’t know if that helps.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps this is getting too abstract. Maybe I can instead consider that this equation in terms of t is a function of t, and since we know that k must be at least 4, so t = k - 4 ≥ 0, so t is non-negative. Therefore, the equation is defined for t ≥ 0, and we need to solve for t such that the sum equals 1.\n\nPerhaps, given that the problem states that there is a unique solution, maybe I can try plugging in some values of t to see if I can find a solution?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps t is a small number? Since when t = 0, let me see what happens.\n\nWhen t = 0, then k = 4,\n\nSo let me compute each term when t = 0:\n\nFirst term: √[ (0 + 1)(0 + 2) ] / √6 = √(1 * 2)/√6 = √2 / √6 = √(2/6) = √(1/3) ≈ 0.577\n\nSecond term: √[0*(0 + 1)] / (2√3) = 0 / (2√3) = 0\n\nThird term: √[0*(0 + 2)] / (2√2) = 0 / (2√2) = 0\n\nTherefore, total sum: ≈ 0.577 + 0 + 0 ≈ 0.577, which is less than 1. So, at t=0, the sum is about 0.577 < 1.\n\nWe need the sum to be 1, so we need a larger t? Let me try t = 1.\n\nLet me compute for t = 1:\n\nFirst term: √[ (1 + 1)(1 + 2) ] / √6 = √(2 * 3)/√6 = √6 / √6 = 1\n\nSecond term: √[1*(1 + 1)] / (2√3) = √(2) / (2√3) ≈ 1.414 / (3.464) ≈ 0.408\n\nThird term: √[1*(1 + 2)] / (2√2) = √3 / (2√2) ≈ 1.732 / 2.828 ≈ 0.612\n\nTherefore, total sum ≈ 1 + 0.408 + 0.612 ≈ 2.02, which is greater than 1.\n\nHmm, so at t=0, sum ≈ 0.577, at t=1, sum ≈ 2.02. Since the function is continuous (as t increases from 0 to infinity, the expressions under the square roots remain positive as long as t ≥ 0, so the function is continuous for t ≥ 0), and it goes from below 1 to above 1, so by the Intermediate Value Theorem, there must be some t between 0 and 1 where the sum equals 1.\n\nWait, but the problem states that there is a unique triple (a,b,c), so the equation should have a unique solution for t, which would correspond to a unique k, hence unique a,b,c.\n\nTherefore, perhaps I can try to use the Intermediate Value Theorem to approximate the value of t? But since the problem is likely constructed to have an exact answer, maybe there's a nice value of t that works? Let me try some fractions between 0 and 1.\n\nLet me try t = 0.5.\n\nSo t = 0.5:\n\nFirst term: √[ (0.5 + 1)(0.5 + 2) ] / √6 = √(1.5 * 2.5)/√6\n\nCompute 1.5 * 2.5 = 3.75,\n\n√3.75 ≈ 1.936,\n\ndivided by √6 ≈ 2.449,\n\nso ≈ 1.936 / 2.449 ≈ 0.790\n\nSecond term: √[0.5*(0.5 + 1)] / (2√3) = √[0.5 * 1.5] / (2√3) = √0.75 / (2√3) ≈ 0.866 / 3.464 ≈ 0.25\n\nThird term: √[0.5*(0.5 + 2)] / (2√2) = √[0.5 * 2.5] / (2√2) = √1.25 / (2.828) ≈ 1.118 / 2.828 ≈ 0.396\n\nTotal sum ≈ 0.790 + 0.25 + 0.396 ≈ 1.436, still above 1.\n\nHmm, so at t=0.5, sum ≈1.436, which is still above 1, so we need a smaller t? Wait, but at t=0, it was ≈0.577, so maybe between t=0 and t=0.5?\n\nWait, wait, at t=0, sum ≈0.577,\n\nt=0.25:\n\nLet me compute t = 0.25.\n\nFirst term: √[ (0.25 + 1)(0.25 + 2) ] / √6 = √[1.25 * 2.25]/√6\n\n1.25 * 2.25 = 2.8125,\n\n√2.8125 ≈ 1.677,\n\ndivided by √6 ≈2.449,\n\nso ≈ 1.677 / 2.449 ≈ 0.684\n\nSecond term: √[0.25*(0.25 + 1)] / (2√3) = √[0.25*1.25]/(2√3) = √0.3125 / (3.464) ≈ 0.559 / 3.464 ≈ 0.161\n\nThird term: √[0.25*(0.25 + 2)] / (2√2) = √[0.25*2.25]/(2.828) = √0.5625 / 2.828 ≈ 0.75 / 2.828 ≈ 0.265\n\nTotal sum ≈ 0.684 + 0.161 + 0.265 ≈ 1.11, still above 1.\n\nHmm, so at t=0.25, sum ≈1.11,\n\nt=0.1:\n\nFirst term: √[(0.1 + 1)(0.1 + 2)] / √6 = √[1.1 * 2.1]/√6 ≈ √2.31 / 2.449 ≈ 1.52 / 2.449 ≈ 0.62\n\nSecond term: √[0.1*(0.1 +1)] / (2√3) = √[0.1*1.1]/(3.464) ≈ √0.11 / 3.464 ≈ 0.331 / 3.464 ≈ 0.095\n\nThird term: √[0.1*(0.1 + 2)] / (2√2) = √[0.1*2.1]/(2.828) ≈ √0.21 / 2.828 ≈ 0.458 / 2.828 ≈ 0.162\n\nTotal sum ≈ 0.62 + 0.095 + 0.162 ≈ 0.877, which is below 1.\n\nSo at t=0.1, sum ≈0.877,\n\nt=0.2:\n\nFirst term: √[(0.2 +1)(0.2 + 2)] / √6 = √[1.2 * 2.2]/√6 ≈ √2.64 / 2.449 ≈ 1.624 / 2.449 ≈ 0.663\n\nSecond term: √[0.2*(0.2 +1)] / (2√3) = √[0.2*1.2]/(3.464) ≈ √0.24 / 3.464 ≈ 0.489 / 3.464 ≈ 0.141\n\nThird term: √[0.2*(0.2 + 2)] / (2√2) = √[0.2*2.2]/(2.828) ≈ √0.44 / 2.828 ≈ 0.663 / 2.828 ≈ 0.234\n\nTotal sum ≈ 0.663 + 0.141 + 0.234 ≈ 1.038, which is just above 1.\n\nHmm, so at t=0.2, sum ≈1.038,\n\nAt t=0.15:\n\nLet me compute t=0.15.\n\nFirst term: √[(0.15 +1)(0.15 +2)] / √6 = √[1.15 * 2.15]/√6 ≈ √(2.4725)/2.449 ≈ 1.572 / 2.449 ≈ 0.642\n\nSecond term: √[0.15*(0.15 +1)] / (2√3) = √[0.15*1.15]/(3.464) ≈ √0.1725 / 3.464 ≈ 0.415 / 3.464 ≈ 0.1197\n\nThird term: √[0.15*(0.15 + 2)] / (2√2) = √[0.15*2.15]/(2.828) ≈ √0.3225 / 2.828 ≈ 0.568 / 2.828 ≈ 0.201\n\nTotal sum ≈ 0.642 + 0.1197 + 0.201 ≈ 0.9627, which is below 1.\n\nSo between t=0.15 and t=0.2, the sum crosses from below 1 to above 1.\n\nAt t=0.15: ~0.963,\n\nt=0.175:\n\nLet me try t=0.175.\n\nFirst term: √[(0.175 +1)(0.175 +2)] / √6 = √[1.175 * 2.175]/√6\n\nCompute 1.175 * 2.175:\n\n1.175 * 2 = 2.35,\n\n1.175 * 0.175 = approx 0.2056,\n\nso total ≈ 2.35 + 0.2056 ≈ 2.5556,\n\nso sqrt(2.5556) ≈ 1.6,\n\ndivided by sqrt(6) ≈ 2.449,\n\nso approx 1.6 / 2.449 ≈ 0.653\n\nSecond term: √[0.175*(0.175 +1)] / (2√3) = √[0.175*1.175]/(3.464)\n\nCompute 0.175 * 1.175:\n\n0.1 * 1.175 = 0.1175,\n\n0.07 * 1.175 = 0.08225,\n\n0.005 *1.175 = 0.005875,\n\nTotal: 0.1175 + 0.08225 = 0.19975 + 0.005875 ≈ 0.205625,\n\nsqrt(0.205625) ≈ 0.4535,\n\ndivided by 3.464 ≈ 0.4535 / 3.464 ≈ 0.131\n\nThird term: √[0.175*(0.175 + 2)] / (2√2) = √[0.175*2.175]/(2.828)\n\nCompute 0.175 * 2.175:\n\n0.1 * 2.175 = 0.2175,\n\n0.07 * 2.175 = 0.15225,\n\n0.005 * 2.175 = 0.010875,\n\nTotal: 0.2175 + 0.15225 = 0.36975 + 0.010875 ≈ 0.380625,\n\nsqrt(0.380625) ≈ 0.617,\n\ndivided by 2.828 ≈ 0.617 / 2.828 ≈ 0.218\n\nTotal sum ≈ 0.653 + 0.131 + 0.218 ≈ 1.002\n\nWow, that's very close to 1! So at t ≈ 0.175, the sum is approximately 1.002, which is just over 1.\n\nSo, that's pretty close. Let me try t=0.174.\n\nCompute t=0.174:\n\nFirst term: √[(0.174 +1)(0.174 +2)] / √6 = √[1.174 * 2.174]/√6\n\nCompute 1.174 * 2.174:\n\nFirst compute 1.174 * 2 = 2.348,\n\n1.174 * 0.174 ≈ 1.174 * 0.1 = 0.1174; 1.174 * 0.07 = 0.08218; 1.174 * 0.004 = 0.004696,\n\nTotal: 0.1174 + 0.08218 = 0.19958 + 0.004696 ≈ 0.204276,\n\nSo total product: 2.348 + 0.204276 ≈ 2.552276,\n\nsqrt(2.552276) ≈ sqrt(2.55) ≈ 1.597,\n\ndivided by sqrt(6) ≈ 1.597 / 2.449 ≈ 0.652\n\nSecond term: √[0.174*(0.174 +1)] / (2√3) = √[0.174*1.174]/(3.464)\n\nCompute 0.174 * 1.174:\n\n0.1 * 1.174 = 0.1174,\n\n0.07 * 1.174 = 0.08218,\n\n0.004 * 1.174 = 0.004696,\n\nTotal: 0.1174 + 0.08218 = 0.19958 + 0.004696 ≈ 0.204276,\n\nsqrt(0.204276) ≈ 0.4519,\n\ndivided by 3.464 ≈ 0.4519 / 3.464 ≈ 0.1304\n\nThird term: √[0.174*(0.174 + 2)] / (2√2) = √[0.174*2.174]/(2.828)\n\nCompute 0.174 * 2.174:\n\n0.1 * 2.174 = 0.2174,\n\n0.07 * 2.174 = 0.15218,\n\n0.004 * 2.174 = 0.008696,\n\nTotal: 0.2174 + 0.15218 = 0.36958 + 0.008696 ≈ 0.378276,\n\nsqrt(0.378276) ≈ 0.615,\n\ndivided by 2.828 ≈ 0.615 / 2.828 ≈ 0.2173\n\nTotal sum ≈ 0.652 + 0.1304 + 0.2173 ≈ 1.00 (approximately 0.652 + 0.1304 = 0.7824; 0.7824 + 0.2173 ≈ 1.00)\n\nWow, so at t≈0.174, the sum is approximately 1. So that's very close.\n\nTherefore, t ≈ 0.174, so k = t + 4 ≈ 4.174.\n\nTherefore, k ≈ 4.174.\n\nTherefore, we can now compute a, b, c:\n\nRecall that:\n\na = √[(k - 2)/2] ≈ √[(4.174 - 2)/2] = √[2.174 / 2] = √[1.087] ≈ 1.0427\n\nb = √[(k - 3)/3] ≈ √[(4.174 - 3)/3] = √[1.174 / 3] ≈ √[0.3913] ≈ 0.6258\n\nc = √[(k - 4)/4] ≈ √[(4.174 - 4)/4] = √[0.174 / 4] ≈ √[0.0435] ≈ 0.2087\n\nThen, a + b + c ≈ 1.0427 + 0.6258 + 0.2087 ≈ 1.0427 + 0.6258 = 1.6685; 1.6685 + 0.2087 ≈ 1.8772\n\nHmm, so approximately 1.877. But the problem says to compute a + b + c, and it's likely an exact value. Since my approximation gives approximately 1.877, which is roughly √3.5 or something? Wait, √3 ≈1.732, √4=2, so maybe it's √(something). Alternatively, maybe it's 2? But 1.877 is less than 2.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's 1 + √2? Since √2 ≈1.414, so 1 + √2 ≈2.414, which is too big.\n\nAlternatively, maybe √(3) ≈1.732, but still less than my approximation.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the exact value is 2? Wait, but my approximate calculation gave ~1.877. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe my