.655\n\nSecond term: sqrt[0.18*(1.18)]/(2√3)\n\n0.18*1.18≈0.2124,\n\nsqrt≈0.461,\n\ndivided by 3.464≈0.461/3.464≈0.133\n\nThird term: sqrt[0.18*(2.18)]/(2√2)\n\n0.18*2.18≈0.3924,\n\nsqrt≈0.626,\n\ndivided by 2.828≈0.626/2.828≈0.221\n\nTotal≈0.655+0.133+0.221≈1.009\n\nAh, so at t=0.18, the sum is ≈1.009, which is slightly over 1.\n\nSo, between t=0.17 and t=0.18, the sum crosses 1.\n\nLet me try t=0.175 as before, which gave us approximately 1.002, and t=0.174 gave us approximately 1.000.\n\nWait, actually, when I did t=0.174, I got approximately 1.00, so maybe t≈0.174?\n\nWait, but in any case, since this is a competition-style problem, it's likely that the value of t is a rational number, perhaps t=1/5 or something? Let me try t=0.2, which we saw was 1.038,\n\nWait, t=1/5=0.2, which was over,\n\nt=0.17 (≈17/100) gave ≈0.993,\n\nHmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps t is 0.2, but that was over,\n\nAlternatively, maybe the exact value is t=0.2?\n\nWait, but at t=0.2, the sum was ≈1.038, which is over 1,\n\nAlternatively, maybe t= (sqrt(5)-2) or something? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps instead of approximating, I can set up the equation again in terms of k and try to manipulate it.\n\nLet me go back to the original equation in terms of k:\n\n√[ (k - 2)(k - 3)/6 ] + √[ (k - 3)(k - 4)/12 ] + √[ (k - 4)(k - 2)/8 ] = 1\n\nLet me denote the three terms as follows:\n\nLet’s let’s call the first term Term1 = √[ (k - 2)(k - 3)/6 ]\n\nSecond term Term2 = √[ (k - 3)(k - 4)/12 ]\n\nThird term Term3 = √[ (k - 4)(k - 2)/8 ]\n\nSo, Term1 + Term2 + Term3 = 1\n\nLet me see if I can write all terms with denominator 24 under the square roots to combine them? Let me see:\n\nTerm1: √[ (k - 2)(k - 3)/6 ] = √[4(k - 2)(k - 3)/24] = [2√{(k - 2)(k - 3)}]/√24\n\nWait, but maybe not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps square both sides, but this will be messy, but let me try.\n\nLet me denote S = Term1 + Term2 + Term3 = 1\n\nThen, S² = Term1² + Term2² + Term3² + 2(Term1 Term2 + Term1 Term3 + Term2 Term3) = 1\n\nCompute Term1², Term2², Term3²:\n\nTerm1² = (k - 2)(k - 3)/6,\n\nTerm2² = (k - 3)(k - 4)/12,\n\nTerm3² = (k - 4)(k - 2)/8.\n\nTherefore,\n\nTerm1² + Term2² + Term3² = [(k - 2)(k - 3)/6] + [(k - 3)(k - 4)/12] + [(k - 4)(k - 2)/8]\n\nLet me compute this sum:\n\nFirst, let me write all terms over a common denominator. The denominators are 6, 12, 8. The least common multiple of 6, 12, 8 is 24.\n\nConvert each term:\n\nFirst term: [(k - 2)(k - 3)/6] = 4(k - 2)(k - 3)/24,\n\nSecond term: [(k - 3)(k - 4)/12] = 2(k - 3)(k - 4)/24,\n\nThird term: [(k - 4)(k - 2)/8] = 3(k - 4)(k - 2)/24,\n\nTherefore, adding them together:\n\n[4(k - 2)(k - 3) + 2(k - 3)(k - 4) + 3(k - 4)(k - 2)] / 24\n\nLet me compute the numerator:\n\nLet me denote N = 4(k - 2)(k - 3) + 2(k - 3)(k - 4) + 3(k - 4)(k - 2)\n\nLet me expand each term:\n\nFirst term: 4(k - 2)(k - 3) = 4[ k² - 5k + 6 ] = 4k² - 20k + 24\n\nSecond term: 2(k - 3)(k - 4) = 2[ k² - 7k + 12 ] = 2k² - 14k + 24\n\nThird term: 3(k - 4)(k - 2) = 3[ k² - 6k + 8 ] = 3k² - 18k + 24\n\nNow, add them all together:\n\nFirst terms: 4k² + 2k² + 3k² = 9k²\n\nSecond terms: -20k -14k -18k = -52k\n\nThird terms: 24 +24 +24 = 72\n\nThus, numerator N = 9k² - 52k + 72\n\nTherefore, Term1² + Term2² + Term3² = (9k² - 52k + 72)/24\n\nNow, the other part of S² is 2(Term1 Term2 + Term1 Term3 + Term2 Term3). Let me compute each product Term1 Term2, Term1 Term3, Term2 Term3.\n\nFirst, Term1 * Term2:\n\nTerm1 * Term2 = √[ (k - 2)(k - 3)/6 ] * √[ (k - 3)(k - 4)/12 ]\n\n= √{ [ (k - 2)(k - 3)/6 ] * [ (k - 3)(k - 4)/12 ] }\n\n= √{ (k - 2)(k - 3)^2(k - 4) / (6*12) }\n\n= √{ (k - 2)(k - 3)^2(k - 4) / 72 }\n\nSimilarly, Term1 * Term3:\n\nTerm1 * Term3 = √[ (k - 2)(k - 3)/6 ] * √[ (k - 4)(k - 2)/8 ]\n\n= √{ [ (k - 2)(k - 3)/6 ] * [ (k - 4)(k - 2)/8 ] }\n\n= √{ (k - 2)^2(k - 3)(k - 4) / (6*8) }\n\n= √{ (k - 2)^2(k - 3)(k - 4) / 48 }\n\nSimilarly, Term2 * Term3:\n\nTerm2 * Term3 = √[ (k - 3)(k - 4)/12 ] * √[ (k - 4)(k - 2)/8 ]\n\n= √{ [ (k - 3)(k - 4)/12 ] * [ (k - 4)(k - 2)/8 ] }\n\n= √{ (k - 3)(k - 4)^2(k - 2) / (12*8) }\n\n= √{ (k - 3)(k - 4)^2(k - 2) / 96 }\n\nTherefore, the products are:\n\nTerm1 Term2 = √[ (k - 2)(k - 3)^2(k - 4) / 72 ] = (k - 3) * √[ (k - 2)(k - 4) / 72 ]\n\nSimilarly, Term1 Term3 = (k - 2) * √[ (k - 3)(k - 4) / 48 ]\n\nTerm2 Term3 = (k - 4) * √[ (k - 2)(k - 3) / 96 ]\n\nHmm, this seems quite complicated. Perhaps there is a better way?\n\nAlternatively, maybe let me denote some variables to simplify.\n\nLet me let’s set u = √[(k - 2)(k - 4)], v = √[(k - 3)^2] = |k - 3|. Since k ≥4, so k-3 ≥1, so v = k - 3.\n\nWait, that might help? Let me see.\n\nLet me note that:\n\nFirst, notice that (k - 2)(k - 4) = (k - 3)^2 -1,\n\nsince (k - 3)^2 = k² -6k +9,\n\nand (k - 2)(k -4) = k² -6k +8,\n\nso indeed, (k - 2)(k -4) = (k - 3)^2 -1.\n\nTherefore, √[(k -2)(k -4)] = √[ (k -3)^2 -1 ]\n\nHmm, perhaps this can be useful?\n\nLet me see:\n\nFirst, Term1 Term2:\n\nTerm1 Term2 = √[ (k - 2)(k - 3)^2(k - 4)/72 ]\n\n= (k - 3) * √[ (k - 2)(k -4)/72 ]\n\n= (k - 3) * √[ ( (k -3)^2 -1 ) /72 ]\n\nSimilarly, Term1 Term3:\n\nTerm1 Term3 = √[ (k - 2)^2(k - 3)(k - 4)/48 ]\n\n= (k - 2) * √[ (k -3)(k -4)/48 ]\n\nSimilarly, Term2 Term3:\n\nTerm2 Term3 = √[ (k - 3)(k -4)^2(k -2)/96 ]\n\n= (k -4) * √[ (k -3)(k -2)/96 ]\n\nHmm, still not obviously helpful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps let me define m = k - 3, so that m ≥ 1 (since k ≥4 ⇒ m =k-3 ≥1).\n\nLet me set m = k -3, so that k = m + 3.\n\nThen, substituting into expressions:\n\nFirst, let's rewrite all terms in terms of m:\n\nFirst, Term1² + Term2² + Term3² was (9k² -52k +72)/24,\n\nSubstituting k = m + 3,\n\nCompute numerator:\n\n9k² -52k +72 = 9(m+3)^2 -52(m+3) +72\n\nExpand:\n\n9(m² +6m +9) -52m -156 +72\n\n=9m² +54m +81 -52m -156 +72\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n9m² + (54m -52m) + (81 -156 +72)\n\n=9m² + 2m + (-3)\n\nTherefore numerator = 9m² + 2m - 3,\n\nThus Term1² + Term2² + Term3² = (9m² + 2m -3)/24\n\nSimilarly, need to express the cross terms: 2*(Term1 Term2 + Term1 Term