= s/(s -2),\n\nSo,\n\n(k -3)/(k -4) = (s -1)/(s -2),\n\nBut s =k -2,\n\nAlternatively, maybe this is getting too convoluted.\n\nAlternatively, let me see if I can express m, n, p in terms of r,\n\nFrom first division: r = m²/(2 n²) ⇒ m² =2 r n²,\n\nFrom second division: (k -3)/(k -4) = (3 m²)/(4 p²),\n\nBut (k-3)/(k-4) = [ (k-2) -1 ] / [ (k -2) -2 ] = [ s -1 ]/[s -2 ] where s=k-2,\n\nBut since r = s/(s -2),\n\nTherefore,\n\nLet me denote s =k -2,\n\nThen,\n\n(k -3)/(k -4) = (s -1)/(s -2),\n\nBut r = s/(s -2),\n\nThus,\n\n(k -3)/(k -4) = [ (s -1)/(s -2) ] = [ (s -2) +1 ]/(s -2) =1 + 1/(s -2),\n\nHmm,\n\nAlternatively, let me express everything in terms of r,\n\nSince r = s/(s -2) ⇒ s = r(s -2),\n\ns = r s -2r,\n\ns - r s = -2r,\n\ns(1 - r) = -2r,\n\ns= -2r/(1 - r)= 2r/(r -1),\n\nThus,\n\ns=2r/(r -1),\n\nTherefore,\n\n(k -3)/(k -4)= [s -1]/[s -2] = [ (2r/(r -1) ) -1 ] / [ (2r/(r -1) ) -2 ]\n\nCompute numerator:\n\n2r/(r-1) -1 = (2r - (r -1))/(r -1) )= (2r -r +1)/(r -1)= (r +1)/(r -1),\n\nDenominator:\n\n2r/(r -1) -2 = (2r -2(r -1))/(r -1)= (2r -2r +2)/(r -1)=2/(r -1),\n\nThus,\n\n(k -3)/(k -4)= [ (r +1)/(r -1) ] / [2/(r -1) ] = (r +1)/2,\n\nTherefore,\n\n(k -3)/(k -4)= (r +1)/2,\n\nBut from the second division equation,\n\n(k -3)/(k -4)= (3 m²)/(4 p²),\n\nThus,\n\n(r +1)/2 = (3 m²)/(4 p²),\n\nTherefore,\n\n(r +1)/2 = (3 m²)/(4 p²),\n\nBut from earlier, m²=2 r n²,\n\nSo,\n\n(r +1)/2 = (3 * 2 r n² )/(4 p² ) = (6 r n²)/(4 p² ) = (3 r n²)/(2 p²),\n\nThus,\n\n(r +1)/2 = (3 r n²)/(2 p²),\n\nMultiply both sides by 2:\n\nr +1 = (3 r n²)/p²,\n\nThus,\n\np² = (3 r n²)/(r +1),\n\nHmm,\n\nAlso, from equation 3: (k -2)(k -4)=8 p²,\n\nBut (k -2)(k -4)=s(s -2)= [2r/(r -1)] * [2r/(r -1) -2] = [2r/(r -1)] * [ (2r -2(r -1))/(r -1) ) ] = [2r/(r -1)] * [2/(r -1) ] =4r/(r -1)^2,\n\nThus,\n\n(k -2)(k -4)=4r/(r -1)^2 =8 p²,\n\nTherefore,\n\n4r/(r -1)^2 =8 p² ⇒ p²= r/(2 (r -1)^2 ),\n\nBut from above, p²= (3 r n²)/(r +1),\n\nThus,\n\n(3 r n²)/(r +1)= r/(2 (r -1)^2 ),\n\nCancel r from both sides (assuming r≠0),\n\n3 n²/(r +1)=1/(2 (r -1)^2 ),\n\nTherefore,\n\nn²= (r +1)/(6 (r -1)^2 ),\n\nHmm,\n\nAlso, from equation 2: (k -3)(k -4)=12 n²,\n\nBut (k -3)(k -4)= (k -4)(k -3)= [from above] (k -3)/(k -4)= (r +1)/2 ⇒ (k -3)= (r +1)/2 (k -4),\n\nThus,\n\n(k -3)(k -4)= (r +1)/2 (k -4)^2,\n\nBut also, from equation 2: (k -3)(k -4)=12 n²,\n\nTherefore,\n\n(r +1)/2 (k -4)^2 =12 n²,\n\nBut we can express (k -4) in terms of r,\n\nSince s =k -2=2r/(r -1),\n\nThus,\n\nk -4 = s -2= [2r/(r -1)] -2= [2r -2(r -1)]/(r -1)= [2r -2r +2]/(r -1)=2/(r -1),\n\nTherefore,\n\n(k -4)=2/(r -1),\n\nThus,\n\n(k -4)^2=4/(r -1)^2,\n\nTherefore,\n\n(r +1)/2 *4/(r -1)^2 =12 n²,\n\nThus,\n\n[ (r +1)/2 *4 ] / (r -1)^2 =12 n²,\n\nSimplify numerator: (r +1)*2,\n\nThus,\n\n2(r +1)/(r -1)^2 =12 n² ⇒\n\nn²= [2(r +1)/(r -1)^2 ] /12= (r +1)/(6 (r -1)^2 ),\n\nWhich matches the earlier result for n²,\n\nThus, this is consistent.\n\nNow, recall that m + n + p =1,\n\nWe have expressions for m², n², p² in terms of r,\n\nLet me write m, n, p in terms of r,\n\nFirst,\n\nm²=2 r n²,\n\nBut n²= (r +1)/(6 (r -1)^2 ),\n\nThus,\n\nm²=2 r * (r +1)/(6 (r -1)^2 )= (r(r +1))/(3 (r -1)^2 ),\n\nTherefore,\n\nm= sqrt[ r(r +1) ] / [ sqrt(3) (r -1) ],\n\nSimilarly,\n\nn= sqrt[ (r +1)/(6 (r -1)^2 ) ]= sqrt(r +1)/[ sqrt(6) (r -1) ],\n\np= sqrt[ r/(2 (r -1)^2 ) ]= sqrt(r)/[ sqrt(2) (r -1) ],\n\nTherefore, m + n + p =1,\n\nSubstituting,\n\nsqrt[ r(r +1) ] / [ sqrt(3) (r -1) ] + sqrt(r +1)/[ sqrt(6) (r -1) ] + sqrt(r)/[ sqrt(2) (r -1) ] =1,\n\nLet me factor out 1/(r -1) from all terms,\n\n[ sqrt(r(r +1))/sqrt(3) + sqrt(r +1)/sqrt(6) + sqrt(r)/sqrt(2) ] / (r -1) =1,\n\nMultiply both sides by (r -1),\n\nsqrt(r(r +1))/sqrt(3) + sqrt(r +1)/sqrt(6) + sqrt(r)/sqrt(2) = r -1,\n\nThis equation in terms of r seems very complicated, but perhaps we can make a substitution here.\n\nLet me set t = sqrt(r),\n\nLet me set t = sqrt(r) ⇒ r =t²,\n\nThen,\n\nsqrt(r(r +1))=sqrt(t²(t² +1))=t sqrt(t² +1),\n\nsqrt(r +1)=sqrt(t² +1),\n\nsqrt(r)=t,\n