ep.\n\nFrom Equation 1: 2a² = 3b² +1 ⇒ a² = (3b² +1)/2,\n\nFrom Equation 2: 3b² = 4c² +1 ⇒ b² = (4c² +1)/3,\n\nTherefore, substitute b² from Equation 2 into Equation 1:\n\na² = [ 3*( (4c² +1)/3 ) + 1 ] / 2,\n\nSimplify inside the brackets:\n\n3*( (4c² +1)/3 ) = 4c² +1,\n\nTherefore,\n\na² = (4c² +1 + 1)/2 = (4c² +2)/2 = 2c² +1,\n\nTherefore, a² = 2c² +1,\n\nSo now, we can express a in terms of c,\n\na = sqrt(2c² +1),\n\nAlso, from Equation 2, b² = (4c² +1)/3,\n\nThus, b = sqrt( (4c² +1)/3 ),\n\nNow, substitute these expressions for a and b in terms of c into Equation 3:\n\nab + bc + ca =1,\n\nLet me write each term:\n\nFirst term: ab = sqrt(2c² +1) * sqrt( (4c² +1)/3 ),\n\nSecond term: bc = sqrt( (4c² +1)/3 ) * c,\n\nThird term: ca = c * sqrt(2c² +1),\n\nTherefore, the equation becomes:\n\nsqrt(2c² +1) * sqrt( (4c² +1)/3 ) + c * sqrt( (4c² +1)/3 ) + c * sqrt(2c² +1) = 1,\n\nThis still looks complicated, but maybe we can factor out some terms?\n\nLet me denote sqrt( (4c² +1)/3 ) as term D,\n\nand sqrt(2c² +1) as term E,\n\nThen the equation is:\n\nE * D + c * D + c * E =1,\n\nFactor D and E:\n\nD(E + c) + c E =1,\n\nHmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, let me set t = c², so that c = sqrt(t),\n\nThen, expressions become:\n\nLet’s let t = c²,\n\nThen,\n\na² = 2t +1 ⇒ a = sqrt(2t +1),\n\nb² = (4t +1)/3 ⇒ b = sqrt( (4t +1)/3 ),\n\nc = sqrt(t),\n\nTherefore, the equation becomes:\n\nsqrt(2t +1) * sqrt( (4t +1)/3 ) + sqrt(t) * sqrt( (4t +1)/3 ) + sqrt(t) * sqrt(2t +1) =1,\n\nHmm, perhaps we can write this as:\n\nsqrt( (2t +1)(4t +1)/3 ) + sqrt( t(4t +1)/3 ) + sqrt( t(2t +1) ) =1,\n\nStill complicated, but maybe we can square both sides?\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet’s denote the three terms as follows:\n\nLet’s let’s set:\n\nTerm1 = sqrt( (2t +1)(4t +1)/3 ),\n\nTerm2 = sqrt( t(4t +1)/3 ),\n\nTerm3 = sqrt( t(2t +1) ),\n\nSo, Term1 + Term2 + Term3 =1,\n\nLet me square both sides:\n\n(Term1 + Term2 + Term3)^2 =1,\n\nWhich gives:\n\nTerm1² + Term2² + Term3² + 2(Term1 Term2 + Term1 Term3 + Term2 Term3) =1,\n\nCompute each term:\n\nTerm1² = (2t +1)(4t +1)/3,\n\nTerm2² = t(4t +1)/3,\n\nTerm3² = t(2t +1),\n\nTherefore,\n\nSum of squares:\n\n[(2t +1)(4t +1)/3] + [t(4t +1)/3] + [t(2t +1)].\n\nLet me compute each part:\n\nFirst term: [(2t+1)(4t+1)]/3,\n\nExpand (2t+1)(4t+1) = 8t² + 2t +4t +1 =8t² +6t +1,\n\nSo first term: (8t² +6t +1)/3,\n\nSecond term: [t(4t +1)]/3 = (4t² +t)/3,\n\nThird term: t(2t +1) = 2t² + t,\n\nTherefore, total sum of squares:\n\n(8t² +6t +1)/3 + (4t² + t)/3 + (2t² + t),\n\nCombine the first two fractions:\n\n[ (8t² +6t +1) + (4t² + t) ] /3 + (2t² + t),\n\n= [12t² +7t +1]/3 + 2t² + t,\n\nConvert the last terms to thirds:\n\n= [12t² +7t +1]/3 + [6t² + 3t]/3,\n\n= [12t² +7t +1 +6t² +3t]/3,\n\n= [18t² +10t +1]/3,\n\nOkay, so sum of squares is (18t² +10t +1)/3,\n\nNow, the cross terms:\n\n2*(Term1 Term2 + Term1 Term3 + Term2 Term3),\n\nLet me compute each product:\n\nFirst, Term1 Term2:\n\nsqrt( (2t +1)(4t +1)/3 ) * sqrt( t(4t +1)/3 )\n\n= sqrt{ [ (2t+1)(4t+1)/3 ] * [ t(4t+1)/3 ] }\n\n= sqrt{ [ t(2t+1)(4t+1)^2 ] / 9 }\n\n= [ (4t+1) / 3 ] * sqrt{ t(2t +1) }\n\nWait, let me see:\n\nWait, the expression inside the sqrt is:\n\n[ (2t+1)(4t+1)/3 ] * [ t(4t+1)/3 ] = [ t(2t+1)(4t+1)^2 ] / 9,\n\nTherefore,\n\nTerm1 Term2 = sqrt( [ t(2t+1)(4t+1)^2 ] / 9 ) = [ (4t+1)/3 ] * sqrt( t(2t+1) )\n\nSimilarly, Term1 Term3:\n\nsqrt( (2t+1)(4t+1)/3 ) * sqrt( t(2t+1) )\n\n= sqrt{ [ (2t+1)(4t+1)/3 ] * [ t(2t+1) ] }\n\n= sqrt{ t(2t+1)^2(4t+1)/3 }\n\n= (2t+1) sqrt{ t(4t+1)/3 }\n\nSimilarly, Term2 Term3:\n\nsqrt( t(4t+1)/3 ) * sqrt( t(2t+1) )\n\n= sqrt{ [ t(4t+1)/3 ] * [ t(2t+1) ] }\n\n= sqrt{ t²(4t+1)(2t+1)/3 }\n\n= t sqrt{ (4t+1)(2t+1)/3 }\n\nTherefore, putting it all together,\n\nTerm1 Term2 + Term1 Term3 + Term2 Term3 =\n\n[ (4t+1)/3 * sqrt(t(2t+1)) ] + [ (2t+1) sqrt( t(4t+1)/3 ) ] + [ t sqrt( (4t+1)(2t+1)/3 ) ]\n\nHmm, this is getting really complicated. Perhaps this approach is not the best.\n\nAlternatively, maybe assume that c is a simple fraction?\n\n