n 1/2.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the answer is 1/3, but my partial sums are higher than that.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's an error in my computations of the sine signs?\n\nLet me double-check a few of the earlier terms to ensure I didn't make a mistake there, because if there's an error in the sign of a term, that could throw off the partial sums.\n\nLet me re-examine the first few terms:\n\nTerm1: n=1, angle=2 radians. sin(2) is positive, since 2 radians is in the second quadrant (between π/2 and π), so sgn(sin(2))=+1. Correct.\n\nTerm2: n=2, angle=4 radians. 4 radians is greater than π (~3.1416), so between π and 3π/2 (~4.712). So in the third quadrant where sine is negative. So sgn(sin(4))=-1. Correct.\n\nTerm3: n=3, angle=8 radians. 8 radians minus 2π (~6.283) is 1.717 radians, which is in the second quadrant (between π/2 and π), so sine positive. Therefore, sgn=+1. Correct.\n\nTerm4: n=4, angle=16 radians. 16 radians divided by 2π is about 2.546, so subtract 2*(2π)=~12.566, giving 16-12.566≈3.434 radians. 3.434 radians is between π (~3.1416) and 3π/2 (~4.712), so third quadrant, sine negative. So sgn=-1. Correct.\n\nTerm5: n=5, angle=32 radians. 32 - 5*(2π) ≈32 - 31.415≈0.585 radians, first quadrant, sine positive. Correct.\n\nTerm6: n=6, angle=64 radians. 64 - 10*(2π)=64 - 62.831≈1.169 radians, first quadrant, sine positive. Correct.\n\nTerm7: n=7, angle=128 radians. 128 - 20*(2π)=128 - 125.663≈2.337 radians. 2.337 is between π/2 (~1.5708) and π (~3.1416), so second quadrant, sine positive. Correct.\n\nTerm8: n=8, angle=256 radians. 256 - 40*(2π)=256 - 251.327≈4.673 radians. 4.673 is between π and 3π/2? Wait, π≈3.1416, 3π/2≈4.7124. So 4.673 is less than 3π/2? Wait, 4.673 vs 4.7124: 4.673 is less than 4.7124, so it's still in the third quadrant? Wait, π to 3π/2 is the third quadrant. Since π≈3.1416, 3π/2≈4.7124. So 4.673 is between π and 3π/2, so third quadrant. So sine negative. Correct.\n\nTerm9: n=9, angle=512 radians. 512 - 81*(2π)=512 - 508.936≈3.064 r