h is very close to 1/π²? Wait, 1/π² ≈ 0.1013, no.\n\nWait, what is the value of ln(2)/π? ln(2) ≈ 0.6931, divided by π ≈ 0.220, no.\n\nWait, let me see if 0.359473903 is close to 1 - ln(2). 1 - ln(2) ≈ 0.30685, no.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's (π - 3)/2 ≈ (3.1416 - 3)/2 ≈ 0.0708, no.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's related to the integral of some function?\n\nAlternatively, since the problem involves doubling angles and signs of sine, perhaps there's a connection to the binary expansion of π or something?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps this sum is known in the literature? Since I can't recall, and given that in competition problems sometimes these sums telescope or have a clever trick.\n\nWait, going back to the original recurrence relation for θ_n:\n\nθ_n = 2θ_{n-1} mod 2π, with θ_1 = 2.\n\nSuppose we consider the sequence θ_n modulo π, since sine has period 2π but the sign depends on being in [0, π] or (π, 2π). Maybe that helps?\n\nLet me define φ_n = θ_n mod π.\n\nThen, since θ_n = 2θ_{n-1} mod 2π,\n\nφ_n = (2θ_{n-1} mod 2π) mod π = (2θ_{n-1} mod π) if 2θ_{n-1} mod 2π < π, else (2θ_{n-1} mod 2π) - π.\n\nAlternatively, φ_n = 2θ_{n-1} mod π.\n\nBecause:\n\nIf θ_{n-1} is in [0, π/2], then 2θ_{n-1} is in [0, π], so φ_n = 2θ_{n-1}\n\nIf θ_{n-1} is in [π/2, π], then 2θ_{n-1} is in [π, 2π], so φ_n = 2θ_{n-1} - π\n\nTherefore, in general, φ_n = 2θ_{n-1} - k_n π, where k_n is 0 or 1 depending on whether 2θ_{n-1} is less than π or not.\n\nTherefore, φ_n = 2θ_{n-1} mod π.\n\nTherefore, the angle φ_n is the double of the previous angle modulo π.\n\nTherefore, the sequence φ_n is defined by φ_n = 2φ_{n-1} mod π, with φ_1 = θ_1 mod π = 2 mod π ≈ 2 radians, since 2 < π (≈3.1416).\n\nWait, 2 mod π is just 2, since 2 < π.\n\nTherefore, φ_1 = 2.\n\nThen φ_2 = 2φ_1 mod π = 4 mod π ≈ 4 - π ≈ 0.8584 radians.\n\nφ_3 = 2φ_2 mod π ≈ 2*(0.8584) ≈ 1.7168 mod π ≈ 1.7168 (since 1.7168 < π).\n\nφ_4 = 2*1.7168 ≈ 3.4336 mod π ≈ 3.4336 - π ≈ 0.292 (since 3.4336 > π).\n\nφ_5 = 2*0.292 ≈ 0.584 mod π ≈ 0