ce.\n\nWait, but we already used that.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps express U in terms of itself:\n\nU = sum_{m=0}^infty [sgn(sinθ_m)/2^m] = sgn(sinθ_0) + sum_{m=1}^infty [sgn(sinθ_m)/2^m] = 1 + S\n\nBut also, using the recurrence relation θ_m = 2θ_{m-1} mod 2π,\n\nthen sinθ_m = sin(2θ_{m-1} mod 2π) = sin(2θ_{m-1}) if 2θ_{m-1} < π, else sin(2θ_{m-1} - 2π).\n\nBut sin(2θ_{m-1} - 2π) = sin(2θ_{m-1}) because sin is 2π-periodic.\n\nTherefore, sinθ_m = sin(2θ_{m-1}).\n\nTherefore, sgn(sinθ_m) = sgn(sin(2θ_{m-1})) = sgn(sinθ_{m}).\n\nWait, no, that's circular.\n\nAlternatively, sgn(sinθ_m) = sgn(sin(2θ_{m-1})).\n\nTherefore, the term sgn(sinθ_m)/2^m = sgn(sin2θ_{m-1}) / 2^m.\n\nTherefore, U = sum_{m=0}^infty [sgn(sinθ_m)/2^m] = sgn(sinθ_0) + sum_{m=1}^infty [sgn(sin2θ_{m-1}) / 2^m]\n\n= 1 + (1/2) sum_{m=1}^infty [sgn(sin2θ_{m-1}) / 2^{m-1}]\n\nBut the inner sum is sum_{k=0}^infty [sgn(sin2θ_k) / 2^k] where k = m-1,\n\nwhich is exactly U, because θ_k for k >=0 are the same angles as before but starting from θ_0 = 1.\n\nWait, no, because the angles θ_k in the inner sum are defined as θ_{m-1} for m from 1 to ∞, which is θ_0, θ_1, θ_2, ..., so the inner sum is sum_{k=0}^infty [sgn(sin2θ_k)/2^k]\n\nBut 2θ_k = θ_{k+1}, so sin2θ_k = sinθ_{k+1}, so sgn(sin2θ_k) = sgn(sinθ_{k+1}).\n\nTherefore, the inner sum is sum_{k=0}^infty [sgn(sinθ_{k+1}) / 2^k] = sum_{n=1}^infty [sgn(sinθ_n)/2^{n-1}] = 2 sum_{n=1}^infty [sgn(sinθ_n)/2^n] = 2S\n\nTherefore, U = 1 + (1/2)(2S) = 1 + S\n\nBut we already knew that U = 1 + S, so this again gives no new information.\n\nHmm.\n\nAt this point, after extensive exploration and not finding an exact form, and given that the problem is likely expecting an answer that can be expressed neatly, I might consider that the sum converges to 1/2 - something small, but my numerical calculation shows it's about 0.3595, which is close to 1/π but not exactly.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the answer is simply 1/2 multiplied by the sum_{n=1}^infty (-1)^{n} / 2^{n-1} }, but tha