erefore, the sequence of points {2^n mod 2π} is going to be dense in the circle [0, 2π), unless there is some special rational relationship, but since 2 is an integer and 2π is irrational with respect to integers (because π is irrational), so I think that {2^n mod 2π} is dense in [0, 2π). Therefore, the sequence sin(2^n) will take on both positive and negative values infinitely often, and sometimes zero? Well, sin(x) is zero when x is an integer multiple of π, so when does 2^n equal an integer multiple of π? Well, 2^n is an integer, so if 2^n = kπ for some integer k, then π would have to be rational, but π is irrational, so sin(2^n) is never zero? Wait, that seems important.\n\nWait, so sin(x) is zero when x is an integer multiple of π, so if 2^n = kπ for some integer k, then sin(2^n) would be zero. But since 2^n is an integer and π is irrational, then 2^n can never equal kπ for any integers n and k. Therefore, sin(2^n) is never zero. Therefore, sgn(sin(2^n)) is never zero. So, the sign function will only return either 1 or -1 for each term in the sum. Therefore, the series is actually a sum of terms that are either +1/(2^n) or -1/(2^n), depending on whether sin(2^n) is positive or negative. Therefore, the series is an alternating series, but not in the usual sense of alternating signs with fixed period or anything; the sign alternates in a more erratic fashion, depending on the value of sin(2^n).\n\nTherefore, to compute this infinite sum, I need to figure out, for each n ≥ 1, whether sin(2^n) is positive or negative, then assign the corresponding sign, and then sum those signed terms divided by 2^n. The problem is that since the sequence {2^n mod 2π} is dense and chaotic, it's not obvious how the signs will behave over the terms. So, perhaps there is some trick or theorem that can be used here?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the series converges to a particular value, and perhaps we can compute it by some clever manipulation or by recognizing a pattern?\n\nAlternatively,