/b) mod 2027, so that is exactly r. Wait, no, hold on.\n\nWait, actually, if c_{2025} = a/b, then c_{2025} mod 2027 is equal to (a mod 2027) * (b^{-1} mod 2027) mod 2027, which is exactly r. Therefore, if we compute c_{2025} mod 2027 as a number between 0 and 2026, that number is exactly r. Therefore, we can compute r by computing the sum over s=1 to 45 of Term_s mod 2027, where each Term_s is [ (S - s²) * (-1)^{s²} ] * [ ( (s² - 1)! (2027 - s²)! )^{-1} ] mod 2027.\n\nTherefore, to compute each Term_s mod 2027, we need to compute several components modulo 2027:\n\n1. Compute S mod 2027.\n\n2. For each s from 1 to 45, compute s² mod 2027, since we need s² and (-1)^{s²} mod 2027.\n\n3. Compute (s² - 1)! mod 2027.\n\n4. Compute (2027 - s²)! mod 2027.\n\n5. Compute the inverse of the product ( (s² - 1)! (2027 - s²)! ) mod 2027.\n\n6. Then compute the term as [ (S - s²) * (-1)^{s²} * inv_denom ] mod 2027, where inv_denom is the inverse from step 5.\n\nFinally, sum all those terms mod 2027 to get r.\n\nOkay, so let me tackle each of these components step by step.\n\nFirst, compute S mod 2027:\n\nS = (2027)(2028)/2.\n\nWe can compute this mod 2027. Since 2027 ≡ 0 mod 2027, so S ≡ 0 * (2028)/2 ≡ 0 mod 2027. Wait, hold on:\n\nWait, S = (2027)(2028)/2. Since 2027 is a factor in the numerator, so S is divisible by 2027, so S ≡ 0 mod 2027. Therefore, S mod 2027 = 0. That's convenient.\n\nTherefore, (S - s²) mod 2027 ≡ (-s²) mod 2027.\n\nTherefore, the term (S - s²) ≡ -s² mod 2027.\n\nSo, that simplifies part of the term: the (S - s²) part becomes -s² mod 2027.\n\nNext, (-1)^{s²} mod 2027. Since exponents mod 2027 can be simplified using Fermat's little theorem? Wait, but (-1)^{s²} is just either 1 or -1, because exponent is an integer. Since s² is an integer, and (-1) raised to any integer power is either 1 or -1. Specifically, (-1)^{s²} = 1 if s² is even, and -1 if s² is odd.\n\nTherefore, we can compute (-1)^{s²} mod 2027 by checking whether s² is even or odd.\n\nNote that s² is even if and only if s is even, since the square of an even number is even and the square of an odd number is odd. Therefore:\n\nIf s is even, then s² is even, so (-1)^{s²} = 1 mod 2027.\n\nIf s is odd, then s² is odd, so (-1)^{s²} = -1 mod 2027.\n\nTherefore, we can write:\n\n(-1)^{s²} ≡ 1 if s even,\n\n≡ -1 if s odd,\n\nmod 2027.\n\nSo that's straightforward.\n\nThird component: (s² - 1)! mod 2027.\n\nSimilarly, (2027 - s²)! mod 2027.\n\nNote that 2027 is prime, so Wilson's theorem tells us that (p - 1)! ≡ -1 mod p. But here, the factorials are of numbers less than 2027 except when s² is small?\n\nWait, s ranges from 1 to 45, so s² ranges from 1 to 2025. Therefore, (s² - 1)! is the factorial of a number from 0! up to 2024!.\n\nWait, when s=1, s² -1 = 0, so 0! = 1.\n\nFor s=2, s² -1 = 3, so 3!.\n\nFor s=45, s² -1 = 2025 - 1 = 2024, so 2024!.\n\nSimilarly, (2027 - s²)! is the factorial of a number from 2026 down to 2!.\n\nBecause when s=1, 2027 - 1 = 2026, so 2026!.\n\nWhen s=45, 2027 - 2025 = 2, so 2!.\n\nTherefore, both (s² -1)! and (2027 - s²)! are factorials of numbers less than 2027, so they are all integers not divisible by 2027 (since 2027 is prime and all factors in the factorial are less than 2027). Therefore, their inverses modulo 2027 exist.\n\nTherefore, to compute (s² - 1)! mod 2027 and (2027 - s²)! mod 2027, we can compute these factorials modulo 2027.\n\nHowever, computing factorials up to 2024! mod 2027 might be time-consuming, but perhaps we can find some patterns or use Wilson's theorem or properties of factorials modulo primes.\n\nRecall that for a prime p, (p - 1)! ≡ -1 mod p (Wilson's theorem). Also, for k from 1 to p-1, k! ≡ (-1)^{p - k} * (p - 1)! / (p - 1 - k)! )? Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, note that for k from 1 to p-1, k! mod p can be computed step by step, but doing this for each s from 1 to 45 might be tedious by hand. But perhaps there's a pattern or symmetry we can exploit?\n\nAlternatively, notice that (2027 - s²)! mod 2027 ca