lp.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there is a symmetry when s is replaced by (something). For instance, note that when s ranges from 1 to 45, s² ranges from 1 to 2025, and 2027 - s² ranges from 2026 down to 2. So, if we let t = 45 - s or something, but not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider that for each s, the term involves (s² -1)! and (2027 - s²)! which are factorials of numbers adding up to 2026. Because (s² -1) + (2027 - s²) = 2026. So, their product is the product of two numbers that add to 2026, but I don't know if that helps.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider that (s² -1)! * (2027 - s²)! = [ (s² -1)! ] * [ (2027 - s²)! ].\n\nBut 2027 - s² = (2027 - s²), so if we let k = s², then it's (k -1)! * (2027 - k)! where k = s², and k ranges over the squares from 1 to 2025.\n\nBut still, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps notice that for each term, the denominator is (k - 1)! * (2027 - k)! where k = s², and the numerator is -k * (-1)^k.\n\nWait, hold on, let me rewrite Term_s in terms of k = s²:\n\nTerm_s ≡ [ -k * (-1)^k ] / [ (k -1)! (2027 - k)! ] mod2027.\n\nSo, Term_s ≡ [ -k * (-1)^k ] / [ (k -1)! (2027 - k)! ] mod2027.\n\nLet me write this as:\n\nTerm_s ≡ [ -k * (-1)^k ] / [ (k -1)! (2027 - k)! ] mod2027.\n\nHmm, perhaps this can be rewritten as:\n\nTerm_s ≡ [ -k / (k -1)! ) ] * [ (-1)^k / (2027 - k)! ] mod2027.\n\nNote that k / (k -1)! = k / [ (k -1)! ] = k / [ (k -1)(k - 2)...1 ] = k / (k -1)! = 1 / ( (k - 2)! ) * [k / (k - 1) ].\n\nWait, k/(k-1)! = 1/( (k-2)! ) * [k/(k-1) ]\n\nHmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, k/(k -1)! = k / [ (k -1)! ] = [ (k -1) + 1 ] / (k -1)! = 1/( (k - 2)! ) + 1/( (k -1)! )\n\nHmm, maybe that's useful?\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nLet me consider that:\n\nTerm_s = [ -k * (-1)^k ] / [ (k -1)! (2027 - k)! ] mod2027.\n\nLet me write this as:\n\nTerm_s ≡ [ -k / (k -1)! ) ] * [ (-1)^k / (2027 - k)! ] mod2027.\n\nNow, note that k / (k - 1)! = k / [ (k - 1)! ] = [k / (k - 1)] * [1 / ( (k - 2)! ) ]\n\nWait, because (k - 1)! = (k - 1) * (k - 2)!,\n\nso,