erfect square, and 0 otherwise, so actually, the sum is over all perfect squares n between 1 and 2027, of L_n(x). So,\n\nf(x) = Σ_{n ∈ S} L_n(x),\n\nwhere S is the set of perfect squares in {1, 2, ..., 2027}.\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of x^{2025} in f(x) is equal to the sum over all n ∈ S of the coefficient of x^{2025} in L_n(x).\n\nSo, if we can compute the coefficient of x^{2025} in each Lagrange basis polynomial L_n(x), then sum them up over all n in S, that will give us the desired coefficient a/b (as a fraction), and then we can find r such that a - r b ≡ 0 mod 2027.\n\nOkay, so that seems like a plan, but computing the coefficient of x^{2025} in each Lagrange basis polynomial L_n(x) might be complicated. Let's see.\n\nFirst, note that each Lagrange basis polynomial L_n(x) is a polynomial of degree 2026, since it's constructed from the product over all m ≠ n of (x - m)/(n - m). So, each L_n(x) is degree 2026, and since we are summing over some of them (those where n is a perfect square), the resulting f(x) is also degree 2026.\n\nNow, to find the coefficient of x^{2025} in f(x), we need the coefficients of x^{2025} in each L_n(x) for n in S (the perfect squares between 1 and 2027), then add them together.\n\nBut how do we compute the coefficient of x^{2025} in L_n(x)?\n\nRecall that for a general monic polynomial of degree d, the coefficient of x^{d - k} is equal to (-1)^k times the k-th elementary symmetric sum of the roots. But Lagrange basis polynomials are not monic, so perhaps that complicates things.\n\nAlternatively, note that each Lagrange basis polynomial L_n(x) can be written as:\n\nL_n(x) = [Π_{m=1, m ≠ n}^{2027} (x - m)] / [Π_{m=1, m ≠ n}^{2027} (n - m)]\n\nLet me denote the numerator as N_n(x) = Π_{m=1, m ≠ n}^{2027} (x - m), and the denominator as D_n = Π_{m=1, m ≠ n}^{2027} (n - m).\n\nTherefore, L_n(x) = N_n(x) / D_n.\n\nNow, N_n(x) is a monic polynomial of degree 2026, since it's the product over all (x - m) for m from 1 to 2027 except m = 