ly, using polynomial division: if we divide P(x) by (x - n), the quotient is N_n(x), which is degree 2026, and the remainder is zero. So, by the division algorithm:\n\nP(x) = (x - n) * N_n(x).\n\nTherefore, expanding the right-hand side, the leading term is x * x^{2026} = x^{2027}, which matches the leading term of P(x). The next term is (x - n)*(coefficient of x^{2026} in N_n(x)) * x^{2026} plus ... Hmm, perhaps it's better to consider the coefficients via the relation.\n\nSuppose that N_n(x) = x^{2026} + a_{2025} x^{2025} + ... + a_0,\n\nthen multiplying by (x - n) gives:\n\nP(x) = (x - n)(x^{2026} + a_{2025} x^{2025} + ... + a_0)\n\n= x*(x^{2026} + a_{2025} x^{2025} + ... + a_0) - n*(x^{2026} + a_{2025} x^{2025} + ... + a_0)\n\n= x^{2027} + a_{2025} x^{2026} + ... + a_0 x\n\n- n x^{2026} - n a_{2025} x^{2025} - ... - n a_0\n\nTherefore, combining like terms:\n\nP(x) = x^{2027} + (a_{2025} - n) x^{2026} + (a_{2024} - n a_{2025}) x^{2025} + ... + (a_0 - n a_1) x - n a_0.\n\nBut we know that P(x) is the monic polynomial of degree 2027 with roots at 1, 2, ..., 2027, so its coefficients can be expressed in terms of the elementary symmetric sums. However, perhaps instead of dealing with all coefficients, we can just equate the coefficients of the highest degrees?\n\nSpecifically, since we are interested in the coefficient of x^{2025} in N_n(x), which is a_{2025}, we can look at the coefficient of x^{2025} in P(x) on the right-hand side.\n\nWait, in the expression above for P(x) after expansion:\n\nThe coefficient of x^{2027} is 1 (as expected).\n\nThe coefficient of x^{2026} is (a_{2025} - n).\n\nThe coefficient of x^{2025} is (a_{2024} - n a_{2025}).\n\nWait, but actually, in the expansion above, the coefficient of x^{2025} in P(x) is (a_{2024} - n a_{2025}).\n\nBut on the other hand, the coefficient of x^{2025} in P(x) is known, because P(x) is the monic polynomial with roots 1 through 2027, so its coefficients can be written in terms of the elementary symmetric sums