\nSum_{k ∈ S} [ k * (-1)^k * C(2026, k - 1) ] mod 2027,\n\nwhere S = {m² | m = 1, 2, ..., 45}.\n\nNow, this seems more manageable, though still non-trivial.\n\nLet me denote the sum as Sum_{m=1}^{45} [ (m²) * (-1)^{m²} * C(2026, m² - 1) ] mod 2027.\n\nHmm. Let me see if there is a generating function or combinatorial identity that can help here.\n\nConsider that C(2026, k - 1) is the binomial coefficient \"2026 choose (k - 1)\". The term k * (-1)^k * C(2026, k - 1) can be rewritten.\n\nLet me make a substitution: let j = k - 1, so that k = j + 1, then the term becomes:\n\n(j + 1) * (-1)^{j + 1} * C(2026, j).\n\nTherefore, the sum becomes:\n\nSum_{j ∈ J} [ (j + 1) * (-1)^{j + 1} * C(2026, j) ] mod 2027,\n\nwhere J is the set { m² - 1 | m = 1, 2, ..., 45 }.\n\nBut J is the set {0, 3, 8, ..., 2024}, since m² - 1 for m from 1 to 45:\n\nFor m=1: 1 - 1 = 0,\n\nm=2: 4 - 1 = 3,\n\nm=3: 9 - 1 = 8,\n\n...\n\nm=45: 45² - 1 = 2025 - 1 = 2024.\n\nTherefore, J = {0, 3, 8, 15, ..., 2024} where each term is m² - 1 for m from 1 to 45.\n\nTherefore, the sum is over j in J of (j + 1) * (-1)^{j + 1} * C(2026, j).\n\nHmm. Let me see if I can relate this to the derivative of a generating function or some generating function identity.\n\nRecall that the sum over j of C(n, j) x^j = (1 + x)^n.\n\nThe sum over j of j C(n, j) x^{j} = n x (1 + x)^{n - 1}.\n\nSimilarly, the sum over j of j(j - 1) C(n, j) x^j = n(n - 1) x^2 (1 + x)^{n - 2}, etc.\n\nBut in our case, we have terms like (j + 1) * (-1)^{j + 1} * C(2026, j).\n\nLet me write this as:\n\nSum_{j ∈ J} [ (j + 1) * (-1)^{j + 1} * C(2026, j) ] = - Sum_{j ∈ J} (j + 1) (-1)^j C(2026, j).\n\nLet me denote S = Sum_{j ∈ J} (j + 1) (-1)^j C(2026, j).\n\nTherefore, the desired sum is -S mod 2027.\n\nNow, let's consider expanding S:\n\nS = Sum_{j ∈ J} (j + 1) (-1)^j C(2026, j).\n\nThis can be split into two sums:\n\nS = Sum_{j ∈ J} j (-1)^j C(2026, j) + Sum_{j ∈ J} (-1)^j C(2026, j).\n\nLet me denote S1 = Sum_{j ∈ J} j (-1)^j C(2026, j),\n\nand S2 