he formula C(n, k) = n(n-1)...(n - k + 1)/k!.\n\nTherefore, modulo 2027, C(2026, j) ≡ [2026 * 2025 * ... * (2026 - j + 1)] / j! mod 2027.\n\nBut since 2026 ≡ -1 mod 2027, 2025 ≡ -2 mod 2027, ..., (2026 - j + 1) ≡ (-1 - j + 1) = -j mod 2027.\n\nWait, let me check:\n\nLet me compute C(2026, j) mod 2027:\n\nC(2026, j) = 2026! / [j! (2026 - j)! ] mod 2027.\n\nBut 2026! ≡ -1 mod 2027 by Wilson's theorem.\n\nTherefore,\n\nC(2026, j) ≡ [ -1 ] / [ j! (2026 - j)! ] mod 2027.\n\nHmm, so:\n\nC(2026, j) ≡ -1 / [ j! (2026 - j)! ] mod 2027.\n\nTherefore, this might be helpful.\n\nTherefore, let's rewrite S2 and S1 using this:\n\nFirst, S2 = Sum_{j ∈ J} (-1)^j C(2026, j) ≡ Sum_{j ∈ J} (-1)^j [ -1 / (j! (2026 - j)! ) ] mod 2027.\n\n= - Sum_{j ∈ J} [ (-1)^j / (j! (2026 - j)! ) ] mod 2027.\n\nSimilarly, S1 = 2026 Sum_{j ∈ J} (-1)^j C(2025, j - 1).\n\nFirst, let's compute C(2025, j - 1) mod 2027.\n\nAgain, using the same approach:\n\nC(2025, k) where k = j - 1,\n\nC(2025, k) = 2025! / [k! (2025 - k)! ] mod 2027.\n\nBut 2025! = 2026! / 2026 ≡ (-1) / (-1) ≡ 1 mod 2027? Wait:\n\nWait, 2025! = 2026! / 2026 ≡ (-1)/ (-1) mod 2027, since 2026 ≡ -1 mod 2027.\n\nTherefore, 2025! ≡ (-1)/(-1) = 1 mod 2027.\n\nTherefore, 2025! ≡ 1 mod 2027.\n\nTherefore,\n\nC(2025, k) ≡ 1 / [k! (2025 - k)! ] mod 2027.\n\nTherefore, C(2025, j - 1) ≡ 1 / [ (j - 1)! (2025 - (j - 1))! ] mod 2027.\n\n= 1 / [ (j - 1)! (2026 - j)! ] mod 2027.\n\nTherefore, S1 ≡ 2026 * Sum_{j ∈ J} (-1)^j * [ 1 / ( (j - 1)! (2026 - j)! ) ] mod 2027.\n\nBut 2026 ≡ -1 mod 2027, so S1 ≡ (-1) * Sum_{j ∈ J} (-1)^j / [ (j - 1)! (2026 - j)! ] mod 2027.\n\nTherefore, S1 ≡ - Sum_{j ∈ J} [ (-1)^j / ( (j - 1)! (2026 - j)! ) ] mod 2027.\n\nHmm. This is getting really involved, but perhaps we can relate this to the term in S2.\n\nLooking back at S2:\n\nS2 ≡ - Sum_{j ∈ J} [ (-1)^j / (j! (2026 - j)! ) ] mod 2027.\n\nLet me see if we can relate the terms in S1 and S2.\n\nLet me denote for a given j in J:\n\nTerm in S2: T2_j = [ (-1)^j / (j! (2026 - j)! )