agrange basis polynomial defined by\n\n\\[\nL_k(x) = \\prod_{\\substack{1 \\leq m \\leq 2027 \\\\ m \\neq k}} \\frac{x - m}{k - m}\n\\]\n\nSo, in this case, \\( f(k) \\) is 1 if \\( k \\) is a perfect square, else 0. Therefore, the polynomial simplifies to the sum over all perfect squares \\( k \\) in the range \\( 1 \\) to \\( 2027 \\), of \\( L_k(x) \\).\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\nf(x) = \\sum_{\\substack{k=1 \\\\ k \\text{ perfect square}}}^{2027} L_k(x)\n\\]\n\nSo, since \\( f(x) \\) is the sum over all perfect squares \\( k \\) from 1 to 2027 of the Lagrange basis polynomials \\( L_k(x) \\).\n\nNow, the coefficient of \\( x^{2025} \\) in \\( f(x) \\) would then be the sum over each of those Lagrange basis polynomials \\( L_k(x) \\) evaluated at their coefficient of \\( x^{2025} \\).\n\nSo, if we can compute the coefficient of \\( x^{2025} \\) in each \\( L_k(x) \\), then sum those coefficients together, we would get the coefficient of \\( x^{2025} \\) in \\( f(x) \\).\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to computing \\( \\sum_{k \\in S} c_{k,2025} \\), where \\( S \\) is the set of perfect squares between 1 and 2027, and \\( c_{k,2025} \\) is the coefficient of \\( x^{2025} \\) in \\( L_k(x) \\).\n\nSo, first, let me note that each Lagrange basis polynomial \\( L_k(x) \\) is a degree \\( 2026 \\) polynomial, since it's constructed as the product over all \\( m \\neq k \\), so there are \\( 2026 \\) factors, each linear in \\( x \\), so indeed degree \\( 2026 \\).\n\nTherefore, each \\( L_k(x) \\) has terms from \\( x^{2026} \\) down to \\( x^0 \\), so the coefficient of \\( x^{2025} \\) in each \\( L_k(x) \\) can be computed somehow.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can find a general formula for the coefficient of \\( x^{d-1} \\) in a Lagrange basis polynomial \\( L_k(x) \\), where \\( d = 2026 \\).\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can recall that the coefficient of \\( x^{n - 1} \\) in the Lagrange basis polynomial \\( L_k(x) \\) is \\( \\frac{(-1)^{n - 1}}{(k - k_1)(k - k_2)