1} (- (k - m)) = (-1)^{k - 1} \\prod_{j=1}^{k - 1} (k - m) \\). Wait, actually, let me substitute \\( j = k - m \\), but maybe better to note that \\( m - k = -(k - m) \\), so:\n\n\\( \\prod_{m=1}^{k - 1} (m - k) = \\prod_{m=1}^{k - 1} (- (k - m)) = (-1)^{k - 1} \\prod_{m=1}^{k - 1} (k - m) \\).\n\nBut \\( \\prod_{m=1}^{k - 1} (k - m) = \\prod_{i=1}^{k - 1} i \\), where \\( i = k - m \\). When \\( m = 1 \\), \\( i = k - 1 \\); when \\( m = k - 1 \\), \\( i = 1 \\). So it's just \\( (k - 1)! \\).\n\nSimilarly, the second part: \\( \\prod_{m=k + 1}^{2027} (m - k) = \\prod_{m=k + 1}^{2027} (m - k) \\). Let me let \\( j = m - k \\), so when \\( m = k + 1 \\), \\( j = 1 \\); when \\( m = 2027 \\), \\( j = 2027 - k \\). Therefore, this product is \\( \\prod_{j=1}^{2027 - k} j = (2027 - k)! \\).\n\nTherefore, putting it all together,\n\n\\( \\prod_{m=1, m \\neq k}^{2027} (m - k) = (-1)^{k - 1} (k - 1)! \\times (2027 - k)! \\).\n\nTherefore, the denominator of \\( L_k(x) \\) is \\( \\prod_{\\substack{m=1 \\\\ m \\neq k}}^{2027} (k - m) = (-1)^{2026} \\times \\prod_{m=1, m \\neq k}^{2027} (m - k) \\). Wait, hold on, let me recall:\n\nEarlier, I had:\n\nThe denominator of \\( L_k(x) \\) is \\( \\prod_{\\substack{m=1 \\\\ m \\neq k}}^{2027} (k - m) \\). Let me see:\n\nWait, \\( \\prod_{\\substack{m=1 \\\\ m \\neq k}}^{2027} (k - m) = \\prod_{m=1}^{k - 1} (k - m) \\times \\prod_{m=k + 1}^{2027} (k - m) \\).\n\nCompute each part:\n\nFirst part: \\( \\prod_{m=1}^{k - 1} (k - m) = \\prod_{j=1}^{k - 1} j = (k - 1)! \\), since \\( j = k - m \\), when \\( m = 1 \\), \\( j = k - 1 \\); when \\( m = k - 1 \\), \\( j = 1 \\). So that product is \\( (k - 1)! \\).\n\nSecond part: \\( \\prod_{m=k + 1}^{2027} (k - m) = \\prod_{m=k + 1}^{2027} - (m - k) = (-1)^{2027 - k} \\prod_{m=k + 1}^{2027} (m - k) \\).\n\nWait, \\( m - k \\) from \\( m = k + 1 \\) to \\( m = 2027 \\) is \\( (2027 - k) \\) terms. So \\( \\prod_{m=k + 1}^{2027} (k - m) = (-1)^{2027 - k} \\prod_{j=1}^{2027 - k} j = (-1)^{2027 - k} (2027 - k)! \\).\n\nTherefore, the entire denominator is:\n\n\\( (k - 1)! \\times (-1)^{2027 - k} (2027 - k)! \\).\n\nTherefore, the denominator of \\( L_k(x) \\) is \\( (-1)^{2027 - k} (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! \\).\n\nBut also, the denominator of \\( L_k(x) \\) was originally written as \\( \\prod_{m \\neq k} (k - m) \\), so that is equal to \\( (-1)^{2027 - k} (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! \\), as above.\n\nTherefore, the Lagrange basis polynomial \\( L_k(x) \\) is:\n\n\\[\nL_k(x) = \\frac{ \\prod_{\\substack{1 \\leq m \\leq 2027 \\\\ m \\neq k}} (x - m) }{ (-1)^{2027 - k} (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! } = \\frac{ (-1)^{2027 - k} }{ (-1)^{2027 - k} } \\times \\frac{ \\prod_{\\substack{1 \\leq m \\leq 2027 \\\\ m \\neq k}} (x - m) }{ (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! } \\times (-1)^{2027 - k} \\times \\frac{1}{ (-1)^{2027 - k} } }? Wait, maybe I confused myself.\n\nWait, actually, the denominator is \\( (-1)^{2027 - k} (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! \\). So,\n\n\\[\nL_k(x) = \\frac{ \\prod_{\\substack{m=1 \\\\ m \\neq k}}^{2027} (x - m) }{ (-1)^{2027 - k} (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! } = \\frac{ (-1)^{2027 - k} }{ (-1)^{2027 - k} } \\times \\frac{ \\prod_{\\substack{m=1 \\\\ m \\neq k}}^{2027} (x - m) }{ (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! } \\times (-1)^{2027 - k} / (-1)^{2027 - k} }? Hmm, perhaps better to just leave it as is.\n\nWait, actually, perhaps I can write:\n\nLet me note that:\n\nThe numerator is \\( \\prod_{\\substack{m=1 \\\\ m \\neq k}}^{2027} (x - m) = \\frac{ \\prod_{m=1}^{2027} (x - m) }{ (x - k) } \\).\n\nTherefore, \\( L_k(x) = \\frac{ \\prod_{m=1}^{2027} (x - m) / (x - k) }{ (-1)^{2027 - k} (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! } \\).\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\nL_k(x) = \\frac{ \\prod_{m=1}^{2027} (x - m) }{ (x - k) \\cdot (-1)^{2027 - k} (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! } \n\\]\n\nHmm. Alternatively, perhaps I can think of the numerator as a polynomial that is missing the \\( (x - k) \\) term. So, the numerator is the full polynomial \\( \\prod_{m=1}^{2027} (x - m) \\) divided by \\( (x - k) \\), which is a degree 2026 polynomial, as expected.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps this is getting too bogged down. Maybe there's another way to compute the coefficient of \\( x^{2025} \\) in \\( L_k(x) \\).\n\nRecall that in a polynomial \\( P(x) = a_{2026} x^{2026} + a_{2025} x^{2025} + \\dots + a_0 \\), the coefficient \\( a_{2025} \\) is equal to the negative of the sum of the roots taken one at a time, by Vieta's formula. Wait, Vieta's formula relates coefficients to sums of roots, products of roots, etc.\n\nBut in the case of Lagrange basis polynomials, which are monic? Wait, no, Lagrange basis polynomials are not monic. Wait, actually, let me recall Vieta's formula.\n\nFor a monic polynomial of degree \\( n \\), \\( P(x) = (x - r_1)(x - r_2)\\dots(x - r_n) \\), then the coefficient of \\( x^{n-1} \\) is \\( - (r_1 + r_2 + \\dots + r_n) \\), the coefficient of \\( x^{n - 2} \\) is \\( r_1 r_2 + r_1 r_3 + \\dots + r_{n-1} r_n \\), etc.\n\nBut in our case, the numerator of \\( L_k(x) \\), which is \\( \\prod_{\\substack{m=1 \\\\ m \\neq k}}^{2027} (x - m) \\), is a monic polynomial of degree \\( 2026 \\), since it's missing the \\( (x - k) \\) term from the full product \\( \\prod_{m=1}^{2027} (x - m) \\).\n\nTherefore, the numerator \\( N_k(x) = \\prod_{\\substack{m=1 \\\\ m \\neq k}}^{2027} (x - m) \\) is monic of degree 2026, so its coefficient of \\( x^{2025} \\) is \\( - \\sum_{\\substack{m=1 \\\\ m \\neq k}}^{2027} m \\).\n\nWait, yes! Because in the monic polynomial \\( N_k(x) = \\prod_{\\substack{m=1 \\\\ m \\neq k}}^{2027} (x - m) \\), the coefficient of \\( x^{2025} \\) is equal to \\( - \\sum_{\\substack{m=1 \\\\ m \\neq k}}^{2027} m \\).\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of \\( x^{2025} \\) in \\( N_k(x) \\) is \\( - S_k \\), where \\( S_k = \\sum_{\\substack{m=1 \\\\ m \\neq k}}^{2027} m \\).\n\nTherefore, since \\( L_k(x) = N_k(x) / D_k \\), where \\( D_k \\) is the denominator \\( (-1)^{2027 - k} (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! \\), then the coefficient of \\( x^{2025} \\) in \\( L_k(x) \\) is equal to \\( [ \\text{coefficient of } x^{2025} \\text{ in } N_k(x) ] / D_k \\), provided that \\( D_k \\neq 0 \\), which it isn't.\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\nc_{k,2025} = \\frac{ - S_k }{ D_k } = \\frac{ - S_k }{ (-1)^{2027 - k} (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! } \n\\]\n\nWhere \\( S_k = \\sum_{\\substack{m=1 \\\\ m \\neq k}}^{2027} m \\).\n\nTherefore, we need to compute \\( S_k \\).\n\nFirst, note that the sum \\( S_k = \\sum_{m=1}^{2027} m - k \\). Since the sum from 1 to 2027 is \\( \\frac{2027 \\times 2028}{2} \\), so:\n\n\\[\nS_k = \\frac{2027 \\times 2028}{2} - k\n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\nc_{k,2025} = \\frac{ - \\left( \\frac{2027 \\times 2028}{2} - k \\right) }{ (-1)^{2027 - k} (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! } \n\\]\n\nHmm, okay. Let me write this as:\n\nFirst, note that \\( (-1)^{2027 - k} = (-1)^{2027} (-1)^{-k} = (-1)^{2027} (-1)^k \\), since \\( (-1)^{-k} = (-1)^k \\).\n\nSince \\( 2027 \\) is odd, \\( (-1)^{2027} = -1 \\), so:\n\n\\( (-1)^{2027 - k} = - (-1)^k \\).\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\nc_{k,2025} = \\frac{ - \\left( \\frac{2027 \\times 2028}{2} - k \\right) }{ - (-1)^k (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! } \n\\]\n\nSimplify numerator and denominator:\n\nThe numerator has a negative sign, and the denominator has a negative sign from \\( - (-1)^k \\), so negatives cancel:\n\n\\[\nc_{k,2025} = \\frac{ \\left( \\frac{2027 \\times 2028}{2} - k \\right) }{ (-1)^k (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! } \n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\nc_{k,2025} = \\frac{ \\frac{2027 \\times 2028}{2} - k }{ (-1)^k (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! }\n\\]\n\nHmm, so that's the coefficient of \\( x^{2025} \\) in \\( L_k(x) \\).\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of \\( x^{2025} \\) in \\( f(x) \\), which is the sum over all perfect squares \\( k \\) from 1 to 2027 of \\( c_{k,2025} \\), so:\n\n\\[\na/b = \\sum_{\\substack{k=1 \\\\ k \\text{ is a perfect square} }^{2027} c_{k,2025} = \\sum_{\\substack{k=1 \\\\ k \\text{ is a perfect square} }^{2027} \\frac{ \\frac{2027 \\times 2028}{2} - k }{ (-1)^k (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! }\n\\]\n\nHmm, that seems complicated. Maybe there