\nFirst, let me rewrite the numerator of \\( c_{k,2025} \\):\n\n\\( \\frac{2027 \\times 2028}{2} - k \\).\n\nCompute this modulo 2027. Since 2027 ≡ 0 mod 2027, so 2027 × 2028 / 2 ≡ 0 × 2028 / 2 ≡ 0 mod 2027. Therefore, the entire numerator modulo 2027 is \\( 0 - k \\mod 2027 \\), so \\( -k \\mod 2027 \\).\n\nTherefore, the numerator modulo 2027 is \\( (-k) \\mod 2027 \\).\n\nNow, the denominator of \\( c_{k,2025} \\) is \\( (-1)^k (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! \\). Let me compute this denominator modulo 2027.\n\nFirst, note that \\( (2027 - k)! \\mod 2027 \\). Since 2027 is prime, and \\( 2027 - k \\) is between 2 and 2026 (since \\( k \\) is between 1 and 2025). Therefore, by Wilson's theorem, \\( (2026)! \\equiv -1 \\mod 2027 \\). But \\( (2027 - k)! = (2026 - (k - 1))! \\), so for example, when \\( k = 1 \\), \\( (2027 - 1)! = 2026! \\equiv -1 \\mod 2027 \\), and when \\( k = 2 \\), \\( (2027 - 2)! = 2025! \\equiv (2026)! / 2026 \\mod 2027 \\equiv (-1)/(-1) \\mod 2027 \\), since \\( 2026 \\equiv -1 \\mod 2027 \\), so \\( 2025! \\equiv (-1)/(-1) = 1 \\mod 2027 \\). Hmm, but perhaps we can find a general expression for \\( (2027 - k)! \\mod 2027 \\).\n\nWait, let me note that \\( (2027 - k)! = \\prod_{i=1}^{2027 - k} i \\mod 2027 \\). Since \\( 2027 - k \\leq 2026 \\), and since 2027 is prime, we can relate this to \\( (2026)! \\mod 2027 \\).\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use Wilson's theorem in a different way. Let me recall that for a prime \\( p \\), \\( (p - 1)! \\equiv -1 \\mod p \\), and for \\( n < p - 1 \\), \\( n! \\mod p \\) is just the regular factorial.\n\nAlternatively, note that \\( (2027 - k)! = \\prod_{i=1}^{2027 - k} i \\mod 2027 \\), and since 2027 is prime, this is just the usual factorial modulo 2027.\n\nTherefore, perhaps we can leave \\( (2027 - k)! \\) as is for now, but we can note that modulo 2027, inverses can be computed via Fermat's little theorem, since for any integer \\( a \\) not divisible by 2027, \\( a^{2026} \\equiv 1 \\mod 2027 \\), so