iginal problem but in the polynomial mod 2027, it's 0 because f(2027) = 0.\n\nWait, actually, in the polynomial \\( f(x) \\mod 2027 \\), the value at x=0 (which corresponds to n=2027) is 0, since f(2027)=0. The other values at x=1, 2, ..., 2026 correspond to n=1, ..., 2026, and at those points, f(x) is 1 if x is a quadratic residue and 0 otherwise. Therefore, the polynomial \\( f(x) \\mod 2027 \\) is exactly the indicator polynomial for the set of quadratic residues in \\( \\mathbb{F}_{2027} \\), including 0? Wait no, because at x=0 it's 0, and quadratic residues don't include 0 (since 0 is a separate case). So actually, it's the indicator polynomial for the non-zero quadratic residues.\n\nTherefore, in other words, \\( f(x) \\mod 2027 \\) is the unique polynomial of degree at most 2026 such that \\( f(x) = 1 \\) if \\( x \\) is a non-zero quadratic residue, and \\( f(x) = 0 \\) otherwise.\n\nTherefore, if we can find such a polynomial, then its coefficient of \\( x^{2025} \\) is exactly \\( r \\mod 2027 \\), which is the integer we need.\n\nNow, in finite field theory, the indicator polynomial for a subgroup can sometimes be expressed using characters or other means, but perhaps here we can use the fact that the set of quadratic residues (excluding 0) is a subgroup of the multiplicative group, which is cyclic. Since the multiplicative group is cyclic of order \\( p - 1 = 2026 \\), which is even, so the subgroup of quadratic residues has index 2.\n\nIn a cyclic group of even order, the subgroup of squares is the unique subgroup of index 2. Therefore, the quadratic residues form a subgroup of order \\( (p - 1)/2 = 1013 \\).\n\nTherefore, perhaps we can use the fact that in a cyclic group of order \\( m = 2026 \\), the indicator function for the subgroup can be expressed using the sum of characters or something similar, but I might be overcomplicating.\n\nAlternatively, recall that in the multiplicative group \\( G = \\mathbb{F}_{2027}^* \\), which is cyclic of order 