en as a product of linear factors unless it's a constant, which it isn't. So that approach might not help.\n\nHmm, perhaps returning to the Lagrange interpolation approach but working modulo 2027.\n\nRecall that we had:\n\nThe coefficient \\( c_{k,2025} = \\frac{ \\frac{2027 \\times 2028}{2} - k }{ (-1)^k (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! } \\mod 2027 \\).\n\nBut modulo 2027, as we noted earlier, the numerator simplifies to \\( -k \\mod 2027 \\), since \\( \\frac{2027 \\times 2028}{2} \\equiv 0 \\mod 2027 \\).\n\nTherefore, the numerator modulo 2027 is \\( -k \\mod 2027 \\).\n\nThe denominator modulo 2027 is \\( (-1)^k (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! \\mod 2027 \\).\n\nTherefore, \\( c_{k,2025} \\mod 2027 = \\frac{ -k }{ (-1)^k (k - 1)! (2027 - k)! } \\mod 2027 \\).\n\nBut note that \\( 2027 - k \\equiv -k \\mod 2027 \\), but actually, \\( 2027 - k \\) is just an integer between 2 and 2026, so perhaps we can write \\( (2027 - k)! \\equiv (-k)! \\mod 2027 \\)? Wait no, factorials are not linear in their arguments. Alternatively, perhaps use Wilson's theorem to relate \\( (2027 - k)! \\) to \\( (2026)! \\).\n\nWait, let's write \\( (2027 - k)! = (2026 - (k - 1))! \\). Let me set \\( m = k - 1 \\), so \\( (2027 - (m + 1))! = (2026 - m)! \\). Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, notice that \\( (2027 - k)! \\times k \\times (k - 1) \\times \\cdots \\times 1 = 2027! \\), but 2027! ≡ 0 mod 2027, so that doesn't help.\n\nWait, but modulo 2027, we have:\n\n\\( (2027 - k)! \\times (2027 - k + 1) \\times \\cdots \\times 2027 ≡ 0 \\mod 2027 \\), but that's trivial.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can write \\( (2027 - k)! \\equiv (-1)^{2027 - k} (k - 1)! \\mod 2027 \\), but I need to check.\n\nWait, let me recall that in general, for a prime \\( p \\), and \\( a \\) an integer between 1 and \\( p - 1 \\), we have:\n\n\\( (p - a)! \\equiv (-1)^a a! \\mod p \\).\n\nIs this true?\n\nLet me test with a small prime, say p = 5, and a = 1:\n\nThen, \\( (5 - 1)! = 4! = 24 ≡ 4 mod 5 \\).\n\nOn the other hand, (-1)^1