uation 3'.\n\nSubtract equation 3 from equation 4: (64a -27a) + (16b -9b) + (4c -3c) + (d - d)=1 -0 ⇒ 37a +7b +c =1. Let's call this equation 4'.\n\nNow we have three equations:\n\n2') 7a + 3b + c = 0,\n\n3') 19a +5b + c = -1,\n\n4') 37a +7b +c = 1.\n\nNow subtract equation 2' from equation 3': (19a -7a) + (5b -3b) + (c - c) = -1 -0 ⇒ 12a + 2b = -1 ⇒ 6a + b = -0.5. Let me call this equation 3''.\n\nSimilarly, subtract equation 3' from equation 4': (37a -19a) + (7b -5b) + (c - c)=1 - (-1) ⇒ 18a + 2b = 2 ⇒ 9a + b =1. Let's call this equation 4''.\n\nNow, equation 3'': 6a + b = -0.5,\n\nequation 4'': 9a + b =1.\n\nSubtract equation 3'' from equation 4'': (9a -6a) + (b - b) =1 - (-0.5) ⇒ 3a = 1.5 ⇒ a = 1.5 / 3 = 0.5.\n\nSo a = 1/2. Then plug into equation 4'': 9*(1/2) + b =1 ⇒ 9/2 + b =1 ⇒ b = 1 - 9/2 = -7/2.\n\nThen from equation 2': 7*(1/2) + 3*(-7/2) + c = 0 ⇒ 7/2 - 21/2 + c =0 ⇒ (-14/2) + c =0 ⇒ -7 + c =0 ⇒ c=7.\n\nThen from equation 1: a + b + c + d =1 ⇒ 1/2 + (-7/2) +7 + d =1 ⇒ (1 -7)/2 +7 + d =1 ⇒ (-6/2) +7 + d =1 ⇒ (-3) +7 + d =1 ⇒ 4 + d =1 ⇒ d = -3.\n\nTherefore, the polynomial is f(x)= (1/2)x³ - (7/2)x² +7x -3.\n\nHmm, so coefficients are fractions here. So indeed, the coefficients are not necessarily integers. So in this case, even though we interpolated integer points, the coefficients can be fractions. Therefore, in our problem, the polynomial f(x) may have fractional coefficients. Therefore, when the problem refers to the coefficient of x^{2025} being a/b with gcd(a,b)=1, that's just the reduced fraction form of that coefficient.\n\nAlright, so the problem is to find that coefficient, then reduce it to lowest terms, then compute r such that a - r*b ≡ 0 mod 2027, where r is between 0 and 2026 inclusive, and 2027 is prime.\n\nHmm, so maybe there is a theorem or method that relates the coefficients of such an interpolating polynomial to some combinatorial expressions or to evaluations at certain points?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use the concept of fini