is over 2027. So there are 45 perfect squares in the range 1 to 2027. However, in GF(2027), the number of quadratic residues is (2027 - 1)/2 = 1013 quadratic residues, since for a prime p, there are (p-1)/2 quadratic residues mod p. So that's a lot more squares mod 2027 than in the integers up to 2027.\n\nTherefore, the polynomial f(x) mod 2027 is a polynomial over GF(2027) which is 1 at 45 points (the squares in 1 to 2027) and 0 at the other 2027 - 45 = 1982 points? Wait, but GF(2027) has 2027 elements, so if we consider the polynomial over GF(2027), then f(x) mod 2027 would take the value 1 at the 45 squares in 1 to 2027, but what about the other elements of GF(2027)? The problem is that the original polynomial f(n) is only defined on n=1 to 2027, but when extended as a polynomial over all integers, then modulo 2027, the polynomial f(x) mod 2027 would take the value f(n) mod 2027 at each n in GF(2027), but for n not in 1 to 2027, it's not specified. Wait, actually, when considering polynomials over GF(2027), the polynomial is determined by its values on all elements of the field, so the values at n=1 to 2027 mod 2027 (i.e., n=1 to 2026, since n=2027 ≡ 0 mod 2027) are given, but the value at n=0 (i.e., x=0) is not given by the original problem. The original problem only specifies f(n) for n=1 to 2027, so when we reduce mod 2027, the value at x=0 is f(2027) mod 2027, but f(2027) is given as either 0 or 1? Wait, 2027 is a perfect square? Let me check: 2027, is that a perfect square?\n\nWell, sqrt(2027) is approximately 45.02, so 45² is 2025, 46² is 2116, so 2027 is not a perfect square. Therefore, f(2027)=0. Therefore, the value at x=0 mod 2027 (i.e., x=2027) is 0. Therefore, over GF(2027), the polynomial f(x) mod 2027 is a polynomial of degree at most 2026 which is 1 at the 45 points n = 1², 2², ..., 45² (since 45²=2025 < 2027, and 46²=2116 >2027) and 0 at all other points in GF(2027). Wait, but hold on: in GF(2027), the squares are all the quadratic residues mod 2027, which are 1013 elements, right? But in the original problem, f(n) is 1 only at the squares of integers from 1 to 45, since 45²=2025 <2027, and 46² is over. Therefore, in GF(2027), the polynomial f(x) mod 2027 is 1 at the 45 integer squares from 1² to 45², but 0 at all other 2027 - 45 = 1982 points? Wait, but actually, in GF(2027), the polynomial must be 1 at those 45 points and 0 at the other 2027 - 45 points? Wait, but actually, the polynomial f(x) mod 2027 is defined over all of GF(2027), but the original f(n) is only given for n from 1 to 2027, which correspond to all residues mod 2027 except 0 (since n=2027 ≡0 mod2027). However, since we know f(2027)=0, that gives the value at x=0. So actually, over GF(2027), the polynomial f(x) mod2027 is a polynomial of degree at most 2026 which is 1 at the 45 points n = 1², 2², ..., 45² and 0 at all the other 2027 - 45 points, including x=0.\n\nBut in GF(2027), a polynomial that is 1 at k points and 0 at the other (2027 -k) points must be the Lagrange interpolation polynomial for those k points. However, the problem is that Lagrange interpolation would require the polynomial to be of degree at most 2026, which it is, but constructing such a polynomial explicitly would be complicated. However, perhaps we can relate the coefficient of x^{2025} in f(x) mod2027 to some other expression?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use the concept of finite differences or generating functions?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there is a clever trick here. Since we need the coefficient of x^{2025} in f(x), and then we need to compute a - r*b mod2027 where a/b is that coefficient in reduced form. Since 2027 is prime, maybe we can compute the coefficient modulo 2027, then express it as a fraction a/b with denominator b, then since 2027 is prime, if b is not divisible by 2027, then we can write the coefficient as (a/b) mod2027, which would be equivalent to finding some integer r such that a - r*b ≡0 mod2027, so r ≡ a/b mod2027, but since a and b are