(since we exclude m=n), so exponent is2026.\n\nSince2026 is even, this is equal to product_{m≠n} (m -n).\n\nBut we already computed product_{m≠n} (m -n) = (-1)^{n-1} (n-1)! (2027 -n)!.\n\nWait, no, earlier we had:\n\nproduct_{m≠n} (m -n) = (-1)^{n-1} (n-1)! (2027 -n)!.\n\nWait, that was for product_{m≠n} (m -n). So then,\n\nproduct_{m≠n} (n -m) = product_{m≠n} (m -n) = (-1)^{n-1} (n-1)! (2027 -n)!.\n\nWait, no:\n\nWait, no, I think I confused myself:\n\nWait, product_{m≠n} (n -m) = (-1)^{2026} product_{m≠n} (m -n) = product_{m≠n} (m -n), because (-1)^{2026}=1.\n\nBut product_{m≠n} (m -n) = (-1)^{n-1} (n-1)! (2027 -n)! as previously computed.\n\nWait, no, actually:\n\nWait, product_{m≠n} (m -n) = [product_{m=1}^{n-1} (m -n)] * [product_{m=n+1}^{2027} (m -n)]\n\nFirst part: product_{m=1}^{n-1} (m -n) = product_{k=1}^{n-1} (- (n -k)) = (-1)^{n-1} (n-1)!.\n\nSecond part: product_{m=n+1}^{2027} (m -n) = product_{k=1}^{2027 -n} k = (2027 -n)!.\n\nThus,\n\nproduct_{m≠n} (m -n) = (-1)^{n-1} (n-1)! (2027 -n)!.\n\nTherefore,\n\nproduct_{m≠n} (n -m) = product_{m≠n} (m -n) = (-1)^{n-1} (n-1)! (2027 -n)!.\n\nWait, but product_{m≠n} (n -m) = product_{m≠n} (m -n) ?\n\nNo, product_{m≠n} (n -m) = product_{m≠n} [ - (m -n) ] = (-1)^{2026} product_{m≠n} (m -n) = product_{m≠n} (m -n) since (-1)^{2026}=1.\n\nThus,\n\nproduct_{m≠n} (n -m) = product_{m≠n} (m -n) = (-1)^{n-1} (n-1)! (2027 -n)!.\n\nTherefore,\n\nD_n = product_{m≠n} (n -m) = (-1)^{n-1} (n-1)! (2027 -n)!.\n\nTherefore, going back to Term_n mod2027,\n\nTerm_n ≡ [n / D_n ] mod2027,\n\nsince numerator was n mod2027,\n\nand denominator D_n = (-1)^{n-1} (n-1)! (2027 -n)! mod2027.\n\nThus,\n\nTerm_n ≡ n / [ (-1)^{n-1} (n-1)! (2027 -n)! ] mod2027.\n\nLet me write this as:\n\nTerm_n ≡ n * (-1)^{n} / [ (n-1)! (2027 -n)! ] mod2027,\n\nsince 1/(-1)^{n-1} = (-1)^{-(n-1)} = (-1)^{n} because (-1)^{-1} = -1.\n\nTherefore,\n\nTerm_n ≡ n*(-1)^n / [ (n-1)! (2027 -n)! ] mod2027.\n\nNow, note that:\n\n(n-1)! (2027 -n)! = (n-1)! (2027 -n)!,\n\nand