ent of a polynomial is equal to the d-th finite difference divided by d!, where d is the degree of the polynomial. Since our polynomial f is of degree at most 2026, but we don't know exactly what degree it is. Wait, but actually, since f(n) is 1 at the perfect squares and 0 at the non-perfect squares from n = 1 to 2027, and since there are floor(sqrt(2027)) perfect squares in that range. Let me compute how many perfect squares are there between 1 and 2027.\n\nSince sqrt(2027) is approximately sqrt(2025) = 45, since 45^2 = 2025, so sqrt(2027) is just a bit more than 45, so the perfect squares in {1,2,...,2027} are 1^2, 2^2, ..., 45^2, since 45^2 = 2025, and 46^2 = 2116 which is greater than 2027. Therefore, there are 45 perfect squares in the given range. Therefore, the function f(n) takes the value 1 at 45 points and 0 at the other 2027 - 45 = 1982 points.\n\nTherefore, f(x) is a polynomial of degree at most 2026 that interpolates these 2027 points. Since a non-zero polynomial of degree d can have at most d roots, so if f(n) = 0 for 1982 points, then unless f is the zero polynomial (which it isn't, since it's 1 at 45 points), then those 1982 roots would require that f has degree at least 1982, but since it's degree at most 2026, that's okay. So f is indeed a degree 2026 polynomial? Wait, actually, the number of roots is 1982, so if f(x) has 1982 roots, then it must be divisible by (x - n1)(x - n2)...(x - n_{1982}), but since it's degree 2026, there are 2026 - 1982 = 44 more factors? Wait, but actually, f(x) is not necessarily having roots only at the non-perfect squares, because f(n) = 0 at the non-perfect squares, but f(n) = 1 at the perfect squares. Therefore, f(x) - 1 has roots at all the perfect squares in 1 to 2027, and f(x) has roots at all the non-perfect squares in 1 to 2027. Wait, but actually, f(x) is a polynomial that takes the value 1 at the perfect squares and 0 at the non-perfect squares. Therefore, f(x) is equal to the interpolation polynomial that is