minator is a constant, so the leading coefficient of L_s(x) is 1 / [product_{t ≠ s} (s - t)].\n\nBut we need the coefficient of x^{2025} in L_s(x). Hmm, so perhaps we can use the formula for the coefficients of a polynomial given its roots? Wait, but L_s(x) is not a monic polynomial with roots at all the other points except s. Wait, actually, L_s(x) is a polynomial that is 1 at x = s and 0 at all other x_j, so its roots are exactly the other points x_j ≠ s, so L_s(x) = C * product_{t ≠ s} (x - t), where C is the constant such that L_s(s) = 1. Therefore, indeed, L_s(x) = [product_{t ≠ s} (x - t)] / [product_{t ≠ s} (s - t)].\n\nTherefore, the polynomial L_s(x) can be written as:\n\nL_s(x) = \\frac{1}{D_s} \\prod_{t \\neq s} (x - t)\n\nwhere D_s = \\prod_{t \\neq s} (s - t)\n\nTherefore, L_s(x) is a monic polynomial (leading coefficient 1) scaled by 1/D_s. Therefore, the leading term is (1/D_s) x^{2026}, and the coefficient of x^{2025} is (1/D_s) times the coefficient of x^{2025} in the product \\prod_{t \\neq s} (x - t).\n\nNow, the coefficient of x^{2025} in the product \\prod_{t \\neq s} (x - t) is equal to (-1)^{1} times the sum of all the roots of the product, right? Wait, more precisely, for a monic polynomial of degree n, the coefficient of x^{n-1} is equal to - (sum of the roots). Wait, yes, by Vieta's formula.\n\nWait, in general, for a monic polynomial P(x) = (x - r_1)(x - r_2)...(x - r_n), then the coefficient of x^{n - 1} is - (r_1 + r_2 + ... + r_n), and the coefficient of x^{n - k} is (-1)^k times the sum of all products of the roots taken k at a time.\n\nTherefore, in our case, the product \\prod_{t \\neq s} (x - t) is a monic polynomial of degree 2026 (since we are excluding one term, s, from the full set of 2027 points). Wait, actually, the full set has 2027 points, so the product over t ≠ s is over 2026 terms, so the degree is 2026. Therefore, the coefficient of x^{2025} in this product is - (sum_{t ≠ s} t). Therefore, the coefficient of x^{2025} in L_s(x) is [ - (sum_{t ≠ s} t) / D_s ].\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of x^{2025} in L_s(x) is equal to [ - (sum_{t ≠ s} t) ] divided by D_s, where D_s is the product over t ≠ s of (s - t).\n\nTherefore, to get the coefficient of x^{2025} in f(x), which is the sum over all s (the perfect squares) of the coefficients of x^{2025} in each L_s(x), so that would be:\n\nCoeff_{x^{2025}}[f(x)] = sum_{s \\text{ perfect square}} [ - (sum_{t ≠ s} t) / D_s ]\n\nSo, we can write this as:\n\nCoeff = - sum_{s \\in S} [ (sum_{t ≠ s} t) / D_s ]\n\nwhere S is the set of perfect squares between 1 and 2027, i.e., S = {1, 4, 9, ..., 45^2}.\n\nHmm, okay. So, if we can compute this sum, then we can get the coefficient. However, this seems very abstract and complicated because we have to compute this sum over all s in S of [ (sum_{t ≠ s} t) / D_s ], where D_s is the product over t ≠ s of (s - t). That seems really difficult, especially since s ranges over 45 different values, each with their own D_s.\n\nPerhaps there's a better way to approach this problem, especially since we are working modulo 2027, which is prime. Maybe we can use some properties from finite fields or modular arithmetic to simplify this?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps instead of working directly with the interpolating polynomial, we can use the concept of the discrete Fourier transform or generating functions? Hmm, not sure.\n\nWait, another idea: since we are dealing with a polynomial f(x) that takes the value 1 at the squares and 0 at the non-squares modulo 2027? Wait, actually, the problem is not modulo 2027; the polynomial is over the integers, but we are to consider coefficients modulo 2027? Wait, actually, no, the polynomial is over the rationals, but we need to find the coefficient a/b such that a and b are coprime, and then compute r such that a ≡ r b mod 2027. Since 2027 is prime, and we can think of this as a linear equation in r: a ≡ r b mod 2027, so r ≡ a b^{-1} mod 2027. Therefore, if we can compute the coefficient of x^{2025} modulo 2027, treating the fraction a/b as an element of the field GF(2027), then r would be that value mod 2027. Wait, but actually, since a and b are integers with gcd(a,b)=1, then a/b mod 2027 is equivalent to a * b^{-1} mod 2027, so that's exactly r. Therefore, if we can compute the coefficient of x^{2025} in f(x) modulo 2027, then that would give us r directly.\n\nTherefore, perhaps instead of computing the exact coefficient a/b and then reducing modulo 2027, we can compute the coefficient of x^{2025} in f(x) modulo 2027, which would be equivalent to (a/b) mod 2027, which is exactly r. Therefore, if we can compute [Coeff_{x^{2025}} f(x)] mod 2027, that would give us r. Therefore, maybe we can compute this coefficient modulo 2027 without having to compute the entire polynomial?\n\nSo, how can we compute the coefficient of x^{2025} in f(x) modulo 2027?\n\nFirst, note that f(x) is the unique interpolating polynomial of degree ≤ 2026 such that f(n) = 1 if n is a perfect square and 0 otherwise, for n = 1, 2, ..., 2027. Since we are working modulo 2027, perhaps we can consider the polynomial f(x) modulo 2027, and use properties of polynomials over finite fields?\n\nBut note that 2027 is prime, so GF(2027) is a field. However, the polynomial f(x) is defined over the integers, but we are to consider its coefficients modulo 2027. Since we need the coefficient of x^{2025} modulo 2027, perhaps we can use some interpolation in the field GF(2027)?\n\nWait, but f(x) is a polynomial that interpolates the function which is 1 at the squares and 0 at the non-squares in the integers from 1 to 2027, but when we reduce the coefficients modulo 2027, does that correspond to some function on the field GF(2027)? Hmm, perhaps not directly, because the original interpolation is over the integers, but the coefficients are being taken modulo 2027.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider the polynomial f(x) mod 2027, and note that for x in the set {1, 2, ..., 2026}, since 2