\)\n\nand\n\n\\( s ≈ [ -1 - 4.123 ] / 2 ≈ -5.123 / 2 ≈ -2.5615 \\)\n\nSo the six roots for \\( s \\) are approximately:\n\n-6, 9, 6.099, -4.099, 1.5615, -2.5615\n\nNow, we have to see which of these s-values correspond to solutions where a, b, c are distinct.\n\nWe already saw that s = -6 and s = 9 lead to triple roots, so they are invalid.\n\nNow, let's consider the other four roots.\n\nStarting with \\( s ≈ 6.099 \\):\n\nThis is close to 6. Let me see if this can lead to distinct a, b, c.\n\nSimilarly, \\( s ≈ -4.099 \\), \\( s ≈ 1.5615 \\), and \\( s ≈ -2.5615 \\).\n\nPerhaps all four of these lead to valid solutions with distinct a, b, c?\n\nBut the problem states there are two possible values for \\( a + b + c \\). Hmm. This is conflicting.\n\nWait, maybe when the problem says \"pairwise distinct complex numbers\", it allows for complex numbers, but perhaps some of these roots for \\( s \\) are complex? But no, all roots for \\( s \\) are real, as we saw earlier.\n\nWait, unless I made a mistake in assuming that. Let me confirm:\n\nThe quartic was \\( s^4 - s^3 -31 s^2 -17 s + 100 \\), which we factored into quadratics with real coefficients, so all roots are real. Therefore, all six roots for \\( s \\) are real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the four remaining roots (excluding -6 and 9) are real numbers. So why does the problem say \"two possible values\"?\n\nWait, perhaps there is an error in my earlier reasoning. Let me go back.\n\nWait, the problem says \"pairwise distinct complex numbers\", so even though all roots are real, perhaps for some of these s-values, the variables a, b, c are real and distinct, while for others, they might be complex? But if s is real and the coefficients of the cubic equation are real, then either all roots are real or one real and two complex conjugates. So, if the cubic equation for a given s has three real roots or one real and two complex conjugate roots.\n\nTherefore, for some s-values, the variables a, b, c could be real and distinct, and for others, they could be one real and two complex conjugates. Since the problem allows complex numbers, both cases are acceptable as long as they are pairwise distinct.\n\nHowever, the problem says \"compute the two possible values of a + b + c\". This suggests that despite there being four possible s-values (from the quartic), only two of them result in solutions where a, b, c are pairwise distinct. Perhaps two of the quartic roots lead to cubic equations with three distinct real roots, and the other two lead to cubic equations with one real and two complex conjugate roots, but in both cases, the variables are distinct (since complex conjugates are distinct unless they are real). So, perhaps all four roots are valid, but the problem might have a symmetry leading to two distinct sums?\n\nWait, but the problem says \"two possible values\", so maybe the two quadratics each give a value of s when considering the system's symmetry?\n\nWait, let me think: the original system is cyclic: a → b → c → a. The equations are symmetric under cyclic permutations. Therefore, perhaps the possible values of s = a + b + c come in pairs related by the symmetry?\n\nLooking at the approximate roots:\n\nThe quartic roots are approximately:\n\n6.099, -4.099, 1.5615, -2.5615\n\nNote that 6.099 and -4.099 are related by approximately s and -s - something? Let me see:\n\n6.099 + (-4.099) ≈ 2, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, note that 6.099 ≈ 1 + sqrt(26) ≈ 1 + 5.099 ≈ 6.099, and -4.099 ≈ 1 - sqrt(26) ≈ 1 - 5.099 ≈ -4.099.\n\nSimilarly, 1.5615 ≈ [ -1 + sqrt(17) ] / 2 ≈ [ -1 + 4.123 ] / 2 ≈ 1.5615,\n\nand -2.5615 ≈ [ -1 - sqrt(17) ] / 2 ≈ [ -1 -4.123 ] / 2 ≈ -2.5615.\n\nTherefore, the two quadratics give pairs of roots that are conjugate in some sense, but not exact negatives or anything.\n\nBut the problem says \"two possible values\", so maybe the question is expecting only the two real roots from the first quadratic (1 ± sqrt(26)), but since they are two distinct values, that would make two possible values?\n\nWait, but sqrt(26) is irrational, so 1 + sqrt(26) and 1 - sqrt(26) are two distinct real numbers, so they would be two different possible values for s. Similarly, the other quadratic gives two more distinct real numbers. So, that would give four possible values. But the problem states two possible values.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made a mistake in the earlier steps.\n\nWait, going back to the problem statement:\n\n\"Let \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\) be pairwise distinct complex numbers such that\n\n\\( a^{2} = b + 6 \\), \\( b^{2} = c + 6 \\), and \\( c^{2} = a + 6 \\).\n\nCompute the two possible values of \\( a + b + c \\).\"\n\nPerhaps there is a miscalculation in the polynomial derivation?\n\nAlternatively, maybe when we derived the equation for \\( s \\), we introduced extraneous roots, so only two of the roots are valid?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the two possible values are 3 and -3, but that doesn't align with my approximate roots.\n\nAlternatively, let me try to compute the cubic equations for one of the quadratic roots and see if they have three distinct roots.\n\nLet me pick \\( s = 1 + \\sqrt{26} \\). Let me denote \\( s = 1 + \\sqrt{26} \\approx 6.099 \\).\n\nFirst, compute \\( p = \\frac{s^2 - s - 18}{2} \\).\n\nLet me compute \\( s^2 \\):\n\n\\( s = 1 + \\sqrt{26} \\)\n\n\\( s^2 = (1)^2 + 2*1*\\sqrt{26} + (\\sqrt{26})^2 = 1 + 2\\sqrt{26} + 26 = 27 + 2\\sqrt{26} \\)\n\nThus,\n\n\\( s^2 - s - 18 = (27 + 2\\sqrt{26}) - (1 + \\sqrt{26}) - 18 = 27 - 1 - 18 + 2\\sqrt{26} - \\sqrt{26} = 8 + \\sqrt{26} \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( p = \\frac{8 + \\sqrt{26}}{2} = 4 + \\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2} \\)\n\nNow compute \\( q = \\frac{s^3 - 2 s^2 -43 s -18}{6} \\)\n\nFirst compute \\( s^3 \\):\n\n\\( s^3 = s * s^2 = (1 + \\sqrt{26})(27 + 2\\sqrt{26}) \\)\n\nMultiply this out:\n\n= 1*(27) + 1*(2\\sqrt{26}) + \\sqrt{26}*27 + \\sqrt{26}*2\\sqrt{26}\n\n= 27 + 2\\sqrt{26} + 27\\sqrt{26} + 2*(26)\n\n= 27 + 2\\sqrt{26} + 27\\sqrt{26} + 52\n\n= (27 + 52) + (2 + 27)\\sqrt{26}\n\n= 79 + 29\\sqrt{26}\n\nNow compute \\( 2 s^2 \\):\n\n\\( 2 s^2 = 2*(27 + 2\\sqrt{26}) = 54 + 4\\sqrt{26} \\)\n\nThen compute \\( 43 s \\):\n\n\\( 43 s = 43*(1 + \\sqrt{26}) = 43 + 43\\sqrt{26} \\)\n\nNow plug into the numerator:\n\n\\( s^3 - 2 s^2 -43 s -18 = [79 + 29\\sqrt{26}] - [54 + 4\\sqrt{26}] - [43 + 43\\sqrt{26}] - 18 \\)\n\nCompute term by term:\n\nFirst, 79 - 54 -43 -18:\n\n79 -54 = 25\n\n25 -43 = -18\n\n-18 -18 = -36\n\nNow the sqrt(26) terms:\n\n29√26 - 4√26 -43√26 = (29 - 4 -43)√26 = (-18)√26\n\nThus, the numerator is:\n\n-36 -18√26\n\nTherefore, \\( q = \\frac{ -36 -18\\sqrt{26} }{6} = -6 - 3\\sqrt{26} \\)\n\nThus, the cubic equation for \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) is:\n\n\\( x^3 - s x^2 + p x - q = 0 \\)\n\nPlugging in the values:\n\n\\( x^3 - (1 + \\sqrt{26})x^2 + \\left(4 + \\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2}\\right)x - (-6 - 3\\sqrt{26}) = 0 \\)\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\( x^3 - (1 + \\sqrt{26})x^2 + \\left(4 + \\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2}\\right)x +6 + 3\\sqrt{26} = 0 \\)\n\nThis is a cubic equation with irrational coefficients. To check if it has three distinct real roots or one real and two complex roots, we can analyze its discriminant or try to find rational roots, but this is quite involved.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps assume that since the problem states there are two possible values for \\( a + b + c \\), and given that the quartic had two quadratics each with two roots, but maybe the two quadratics correspond to two possible values when considering the system's symmetry?\n\nAlternatively, maybe there was an error in the problem interpretation. Wait, the problem says \"pairwise distinct complex numbers\", so even if variables are complex, as long as they are distinct, it's okay. However, the sum \\( s = a + b + c \\) is real in all cases because all roots for \\( s \\) are real. So all possible \\( s \\) are real numbers.\n\nGiven that the problem asks for two possible values, but we have four real roots for \\( s \\), excluding the two invalid ones (s = -6 and 9), which left us with four real roots, but the problem says two possible values, I must have made a mistake somewhere.\n\nWait a minute, let me go back to the start and see if I misapplied any step.\n\nWait, when I multiplied the three original equations:\n\n\\( a^2 b^2 c^2 = (b + 6)(c + 6)(a + 6) \\)\n\nBut actually, the left side is \\( (abc)^2 \\), which is correct.\n\nThen expanding the right side gave me terms leading to an equation involving \\( abc \\) and \\( a + b + c \\), which I then used in combination with other equations to get to the sixth-degree polynomial. That seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem has a solution where \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are roots of a cubic equation, and the sum \\( s \\) can only take two values because of some constraints?\n\nWait, let me consider that the system is cyclic and of order 3, so perhaps the values of \\( s \\) must satisfy a quadratic equation?\n\nWait, let me consider that if I let \\( s = a + b + c \\), and also note that from the original equations:\n\n\\( a^2 = b + 6 \\)\n\n\\( b^2 = c + 6 \\)\n\n\\( c^2 = a + 6 \\)\n\nThen, adding them, as before, gives \\( a^2 + b^2 + c^2 = s + 18 \\)\n\nAlso, we have \\( a^3 + b^3 + c^3 = ab + bc + ca + 6s \\), from earlier.\n\nAdditionally, we had expressions for \\( a^3 + b^3 + c^3 \\) in terms of \\( s \\), \\( p \\), and \\( q \\).\n\nBut maybe combining these equations can lead to a lower-degree equation for \\( s \\).\n\nLet me see:\n\nWe have:\n\n1. \\( a^2 + b^2 + c^2 = s^2 - 2p = s + 18 \\Rightarrow s^2 - 2p - s - 18 = 0 \\Rightarrow p = \\frac{s^2 - s - 18}{2} \\)\n\n2. \\( a^3 + b^3 + c^3 = s^3 - 3 s p + 3 q = p + 6s \\)\n\nSubstitute \\( p \\) from equation 1 into equation 2:\n\n\\( s^3 - 3 s \\left( \\frac{s^2 - s - 18}{2} \\right) + 3 q = \\frac{s^2 - s - 18}{2} + 6 s \\)\n\nMultiply through by 2 to eliminate denominators:\n\n\\( 2 s^3 - 3 s (s^2 - s - 18) + 6 q = (s^2 - s - 18) + 12 s \\)\n\nExpand the left-hand side:\n\nFirst term: \\( 2 s^3 \\)\n\nSecond term: \\( -3 s^3 + 3 s^2 + 54 s \\)\n\nThird term: \\( +6 q \\)\n\nSo combined:\n\n\\( 2 s^3 -3 s^3 + 3 s^2 + 54 s + 6 q = -s^3 + 3 s^2 + 54 s + 6 q \\)\n\nRight-hand side:\n\n\\( s^2 - s - 18 + 12 s = s^2 + 11 s - 18 \\)\n\nThus, equation becomes:\n\n\\( -s^3 + 3 s^2 + 54 s + 6 q = s^2 + 11 s - 18 \\)\n\nBring all terms to left-hand side:\n\n\\( -s^3 + 3 s^2 + 54 s + 6 q - s^2 - 11 s + 18 = 0 \\)\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\( -s^3 + 2 s^2 + 43 s + 18 + 6 q = 0 \\)\n\nWhich is the same equation I had earlier: \\( 6 q = s^3 - 2 s^2 - 43 s - 18 \\), so \\( q = \\frac{s^3 - 2 s^2 - 43 s - 18}{6} \\), which is consistent.\n\nThus, this didn't yield a lower-degree equation.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider the product \\( abc \\), which is \\( q \\), and relate it to \\( s \\).\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the system can be transformed into a cubic equation for \\( s \\).\n\nWait, let me think differently. Suppose we let \\( x = a \\), then from the first equation, \\( b = x^2 - 6 \\), then \\( c = (x^2 - 6)^2 - 6 \\), and then \\( a = c^2 - 6 \\). So, substituting back, we get \\( x = \\left[ \\left( x^2 - 6 \\right)^2 - 6 \\right]^2 - 6 \\), which is the eighth-degree equation we had before. But perhaps this can be reduced?\n\nAlternatively, maybe assuming that \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are roots of the equation \\( x^3 - s x^2 + p x - q = 0 \\), and using Vieta's formulas, but I think we already did that.\n\nAlternatively, since the problem specifies that \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are pairwise distinct, perhaps the valid solutions for \\( s \\) are exactly the roots of the quartic equation excluding the double roots at \\( s = -6 \\) and \\( s = 9 \\), but the problem says there are two possible values, so perhaps among those four roots of the quartic, two of them are actually the same when considering the system's symmetry?\n\nWait, looking back at the quartic roots:\n\nThe roots from the first quadratic \\( s^2 - 2 s -25 = 0 \\) are \\( 1 \\pm \\sqrt{26} \\), which are distinct.\n\nThe roots from the second quadratic \\( s^2 + s -4 = 0 \\) are \\( \\frac{ -1 \\pm \\sqrt{17} }{2} \\), which are also distinct.\n\nThus, these four roots are all distinct. Therefore, there should be four possible values for \\( a + b + c \\). But the problem says \"two possible values\". This inconsistency suggests I must have made a mistake in my process.\n\nWait, let me re-express the problem in another way.\n\nSuppose that \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) satisfy the cyclic equations:\n\n\\( a^2 = b + 6 \\)\n\n\\( b^2 = c + 6 \\)\n\n\\( c^2 = a + 6 \\)\n\nLet me consider that if we apply the function \\( f(x) = x^2 - 6 \\), then the sequence \\( a \\rightarrow b \\rightarrow c \\rightarrow a \\) is a cycle of period 3 under the function \\( f \\).\n\nTherefore, \\( f(f(f(a))) = a \\).\n\nThus, \\( f^3(a) = a \\), where \\( f^3 \\) denotes the third iterate.\n\nTherefore, the equation \\( f(f(f(a))) = a \\) must hold for \\( a \\).\n\nLet me compute \\( f(f(f(a))) \\):\n\nFirst, \\( f(a) = a^2 - 6 = b \\)\n\nThen, \\( f(b) = b^2 - 6 = c \\)\n\nThen, \\( f(c) = c^2 - 6 = a \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( f(f(f(a))) = a \\), so:\n\n\\( f(f(f(a))) - a = 0 \\)\n\nWhich is the equation we had before, leading to the eighth-degree polynomial.\n\nHowever, since this equation must hold for \\( a \\), and similarly for \\( b \\) and \\( c \\), but since they are cyclically related, the solutions for \\( a \\) must satisfy this equation, and similarly for \\( b \\) and \\( c \\).\n\nHowever, this equation is of degree 8, so there can be up to 8 solutions for \\( a \\), but due to the cyclic nature, each solution for \\( a \\) gives a solution for \\( b \\) and \\( c \\), so the total number of solutions is 8, but with some symmetries.\n\nHowever, the problem states that \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are pairwise distinct, so we must exclude solutions where \\( a = b \\), \\( b = c \\), or \\( c = a \\).\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the eighth-degree equation can be factored into lower-degree polynomials corresponding to the cycles.\n\nAlternatively, note that the system's symmetry might allow us to consider that \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are roots of a cubic equation, and the sum \\( s \\) is linked to that cubic's coefficients.\n\nWait, let me consider that if \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are roots of the cubic equation \\( x^3 - s x^2 + p x - q = 0 \\), then the equation \\( f(f(f(x))) = x \\) must be equivalent to this cubic equation, considering the cyclic relations.\n\nAlternatively, the equation \\( f(f(f(x))) = x \\) can be written as:\n\nLet me compute \\( f(f(f(x))) \\):\n\nFirst, \\( f(x) = x^2 - 6 \\)\n\nThen, \\( f(f(x)) = f(x^2 - 6) = (x^2 - 6)^2 - 6 = x^4 - 12x^2 + 30 \\)\n\nThen, \\( f(f(f(x))) = f(x^4 - 12x^2 + 30) = (x^4 - 12x^2 + 30)^2 - 6 \\)\n\nSet this equal to \\( x \\):\n\n\\( (x^4 - 12x^2 + 30)^2 - 6 = x \\)\n\nThus,\n\n\\( (x^4 - 12x^2 + 30)^2 - x - 6 = 0 \\)\n\nWhich is the same as the equation we obtained earlier when substituting back, leading to the eighth-degree polynomial.\n\nTherefore, the solutions for \\( a \\) are the roots of this eighth-degree equation, but since \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are related cyclically, each solution for \\( a \\) gives rise to a solution for \\( b \\) and \\( c \\), but we need to consider that \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) must be distinct.\n\nHowever, since the problem asks for the possible values of \\( a + b + c \\), which is \\( s \\), and we derived that \\( s \\) must satisfy the sixth-degree equation \\( P(s) = 0 \\), which factors into \\( (s +6)(s -9)(quartic) \\), and the quartic has four roots, so six roots total.\n\nThe problem states there are two possible values, so likely the quartic factors further into quadratics with roots that are conjugates or something, but when considering the sum \\( a + b + c \\), perhaps only two of the quartic's roots are possible because the other two lead to contradictions with distinctness.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the two quadratics each give one possible value for \\( s \\), but since they have two roots each, but they are actually the same when considering the system's symmetry?\n\nWait, looking at the roots:\n\nThe first quadratic: \\( s = 1 \\pm \\sqrt{26} \\)\n\nThe second quadratic: \\( s = \\frac{ -1 \\pm \\sqrt{17} }{2} \\)\n\nThese are all distinct real numbers, so four distinct values. But the problem states two possible values. This suggests I must have made an error in the process.\n\nWait, let me consider that when I set up the equation for \\( s \\), I might have introduced extraneous solutions.\n\nSpecifically, when I multiplied the three original equations, I assumed that \\( abc \\neq 0 \\), but even so, maybe some roots for \\( s \\) don't actually correspond to solutions of the original system.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the only valid solutions are those where the cubic equations for \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) have three distinct roots, which might eliminate two of the quartic roots.\n\nLet me consider the cubic equation for \\( s = 1 + \\sqrt{26} \\approx 6.099 \\):\n\nThe cubic equation was:\n\n\\( x^3 - (1 + \\sqrt{26})x^2 + \\left(4 + \\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2}\\right)x +6 + 3\\sqrt{26} = 0 \\)\n\nThis seems complicated, but maybe this cubic has three real roots?\n\nLet me consider that if \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are real and distinct, then this cubic must have three real roots. Similarly for the other roots.\n\nAlternatively, maybe two of the quartic roots lead to cubics with three real roots, and the other two lead to cubics with one real and two complex roots. In that case, only the first two would give valid solutions with three distinct real numbers, but the problem allows complex numbers as well.\n\nAlternatively, since the problem allows \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) to be complex, even if the cubic has one real and two complex roots, as long as they are distinct, it's acceptable.\n\nTherefore, perhaps all four roots of the quartic are valid, leading to four possible values of \\( s \\). But the problem says two, so I must have made a miscalculation.\n\nWait, let me check the original problem statement again:\n\n\"Let \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\) be pairwise distinct complex numbers such that\n\n\\( a^{2} = b + 6 \\), \\( b^{2} = c + 6 \\), and \\( c^{2} = a + 6 \\).\n\nCompute the two possible values of \\( a + b + c \\).\"\n\nWait a second—maybe when I derived the sixth-degree polynomial for \\( s \\), I might have introduced extraneous solutions because when I squared terms or multiplied equations, I might have introduced solutions that don't satisfy the original equations.\n\nSpecifically, when I multiplied the three original equations, I assumed that \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are non-zero? Or that \\( abc \\neq 0 \\)? Not necessarily, but squaring or multiplying can sometimes introduce extraneous roots.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the only valid solutions are those where the cubic equations for \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) have three distinct roots, which would require the discriminant of the cubic to be non-zero.\n\nLet me try calculating the discriminant for one of the quartic roots to see if it's positive (indicating three real roots) or negative (one real and two complex).\n\nTake \\( s = 1 + \\sqrt{26} \\approx 6.099 \\).\n\nThe cubic equation is:\n\n\\( x^3 - s x^2 + p x - q = 0 \\)\n\nWith \\( s = 1 + \\sqrt{26} \\), \\( p = 4 + \\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2} \\), and \\( q = -6 - 3\\sqrt{26} \\).\n\nThe discriminant \\( D \\) of a cubic equation \\( x^3 + ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) is given by:\n\n\\( D = 18abc - 4a^3c + a^2b^2 - 4b^3 - 27c^2 \\)\n\nWait, actually, the general formula is a bit more involved. Let me recall the discriminant formula for cubic \\( x^3 + px^2 + qx + r \\):\n\nThe discriminant is \\( D = 18pqr - 4p^3 r + p^2 q^2 - 4q^3 - 27r^2 \\).\n\nWait, actually, let me confirm:\n\nThe discriminant of cubic equation \\( ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0 \\) is:\n\n\\( D = 18abcd - 4b^3d + b^2c^2 - 4ac^3 - 27a^2d^2 \\)\n\nIn our case, the cubic is:\n\n\\( x^3 - s x^2 + p x - q = 0 \\), so coefficients are:\n\n\\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -s \\), \\( c = p \\), \\( d = -q \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( D = 18(1)(-s)(p)(-q) - 4(-s)^3(-q) + (-s)^2 p^2 - 4(1)p^3 - 27(1)^2 (-q)^2 \\)\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\n1. First term: \\( 18 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-s) \\cdot p \\cdot (-q) = 18 s p q \\)\n\n2. Second term: \\( -4 \\cdot (-s)^3 \\cdot (-q) = -4 \\cdot (-s^3) \\cdot (-q) = -4 s^3 q \\)\n\n3. Third term: \\( (-s)^2 \\cdot p^2 = s^2 p^2 \\)\n\n4. Fourth term: \\( -4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot p^3 = -4 p^3 \\)\n\n5. Fifth term: \\( -27 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-q)^2 = -27 q^2 \\)\n\nTherefore, putting it all together:\n\n\\( D = 18 s p q - 4 s^3 q + s^2 p^2 - 4 p^3 - 27 q^2 \\)\n\nThis is quite complex, but let's plug in the values for \\( s \\), \\( p \\), and \\( q \\) for \\( s = 1 + \\sqrt{26} \\).\n\nFirst, recall:\n\n\\( s = 1 + \\sqrt{26} \\)\n\n\\( p = 4 + \\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2} \\)\n\n\\( q = -6 - 3\\sqrt{26} \\)\n\nThis will be very tedious, but let me attempt to compute each term step by step.\n\nFirst, compute each variable numerically to approximate the discriminant:\n\nLet me approximate \\( \\sqrt{26} \\approx 5.099 \\).\n\nThus,\n\n\\( s ≈ 1 + 5.099 ≈ 6.099 \\)\n\n\\( p ≈ 4 + 5.099 / 2 ≈ 4 + 2.5495 ≈ 6.5495 \\)\n\n\\( q ≈ -6 - 3*5.099 ≈ -6 - 15.297 ≈ -21.297 \\)\n\nNow compute each term:\n\n1. \\( 18 s p q ≈ 18 * 6.099 * 6.5495 * (-21.297) \\)\n\nFirst compute 18 * 6.099 ≈ 18 * 6 = 108, 18 * 0.099 ≈ 1.782 → total ≈ 109.782\n\nThen multiply by 6.5495 ≈ 109.782 * 6.5 ≈ 714.243 + 109.782 * 0.0495 ≈ ~714.243 + 5.426 ≈ 719.669\n\nThen multiply by -21.297 ≈ 719.669 * (-21.297) ≈ -15,365 (approximate)\n\n2. \\( -4 s^3 q ≈ -4 * (6.099)^3 * (-21.297) \\)\n\nFirst compute \\( s^3 ≈ (6.099)^3 ≈ 6^3 + 3*6^2*0.099 + 3*6*(0.099)^2 + (0.099)^3 ≈ 216 + 3*36*0.099 + ... ≈ 216 + 10.572 + ... ≈ ~226.572 (exact calculation needed?)\n\nWait better to compute numerically:\n\n6.099^3 ≈ 6.099 * 6.099 * 6.099\n\nFirst compute 6.099 * 6.099:\n\n6 * 6 = 36, 6 * 0.099 = 0.594, 0.099 * 6 = 0.594, 0.099 * 0.099 ≈ 0.0098\n\nSo, (6 + 0.099)^2 ≈ 36 + 2*6*0.099 + 0.099^2 ≈ 36 + 1.188 + 0.0098 ≈ 37.1978\n\nThen multiply by 6.099:\n\n37.1978 * 6 ≈ 223.1868\n\n37.1978 * 0.099 ≈ 3.686\n\nTotal ≈ 223.1868 + 3.686 ≈ 226.8728\n\nThus, \\( s^3 ≈ 226.8728 \\)\n\nThus, term 2: -4 * 226.8728 * (-21.297) ≈ -4 * (-4,838) ≈ +19,352 (approx)\n\n3. \\( s^2 p^2 ≈ (6.099)^2 * (6.5495)^2 \\)\n\nCompute \\( s^2 ≈ 6.099^2 ≈ 37.197 \\)\n\n\\( p^2 ≈ (6.5495)^2 ≈ 42.9 (since 6.5^2=42.25, so ~42.9)\n\nThus, 37.197 * 42.9 ≈ 37 * 43 ≈ 1,591 + 0.197*43 ≈ 1,591 + 8.471 ≈ 1,599.471\n\n4. \\( -4 p^3 ≈ -4 * (6.5495)^3 \\)\n\nCompute \\( p^3 ≈ 6.5495^3 ≈ 6.5495 * 6.5495 * 6.5495 \\)\n\nFirst, 6.5495^2 ≈ 42.9 (from above)\n\nThen, 42.9 * 6.5495 ≈ 42.9*6 + 42.9*0.5495 ≈ 257.4 + 23.57 ≈ 280.97\n\nThus, \\( p^3 ≈ 280.97 \\), so term 4 ≈ -4 * 280.97 ≈ -1,123.88\n\n5. \\( -27 q^2 ≈ -27 * (-21.297)^2 ≈ -27 * 453.4 ≈ -27*450 = -12,150 -27*3.4 ≈ -12,150 -91.8 ≈ -12,241.8 \\)\n\nNow, summing all terms approximately:\n\n1. -15,365\n\n2. +19,352\n\n3. +1,599.471\n\n4. -1,123.88\n\n5. -12,241.8\n\nAdding them step by step:\n\nStart with term1 + term2: -15,365 + 19,352 = 3,987\n\nAdd term3: 3,987 + 1,599.471 ≈ 5,586.471\n\nAdd term4: 5,586.471 - 1,123.88 ≈ 4,462.591\n\nAdd term5: 4,462.591 - 12,241.8 ≈ -7,779.209\n\nThus, the discriminant \\( D ≈ -7,779 \\), which is negative. This indicates that the cubic has one real root and two complex conjugate roots. Therefore, for this value of \\( s \\approx 6.099 \\), the variables \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \\( c \\) are one real and two complex conjugates, which are distinct, so this is a valid solution.\n\nSimilarly, let's check another root, say \\( s = \\frac{ -1 - \\sqrt{17} }{2} ≈ -2.5615 \\).\n\nCompute the discriminant for this \\( s \\). This is going to be time-consuming, but let me try.\n\nFirst, approximate \\( \\sqrt{17} ≈ 4.123 \\), so \\( s ≈ (-1 -4.123)/2 ≈ -5.123/2 ≈ -2.5615 \\).\n\nCompute \\( p = \\frac{s^2 - s - 18}{2} \\):\n\nFirst compute \\( s^2 ≈ (-2.5615)^2 ≈ 6.561 \\)\n\nThen \\( s^2 - s -18 ≈ 6.561 - (-2.5615) - 18 ≈ 6.561 + 2.5615 - 18 ≈ 9.1225 - 18 ≈ -8.8775 \\)\n\nThus, \\( p ≈ -8.8775 / 2 ≈ -4.43875 \\)\n\nCompute \\( q = \\frac{s^3 - 2 s^2 -43 s -18}{6} \\)\n\nFirst compute \\( s^3 ≈ (-2.5615)^3 ≈ -16.78 \\)\n\n\\( -2 s^2 ≈ -2 * 6.561 ≈ -13.122 \\)\n\n\\( -43 s ≈ -43*(-2.5615) ≈ +110.11 \\)\n\nThus numerator ≈ -16.78 -13.122 +110.11 -18 ≈ (-16.78 -13.122 -18) + 110.11 ≈ (-47.902) + 110.11 ≈ 62.208\n\nThus, \\( q ≈ 62.208 / 6 ≈ 10.368 \\)\n\nNow, the cubic equation is:\n\n\\( x^3 - s x^2 + p x - q = 0 \\)\n\nPlugging in the approximate values:\n\n\\( x^3 - (-2.5615)x^2 + (-4.43875)x -10.368 ≈ x^3 +2.5615x^2 -4.43875x -10.368 = 0 \\)\n\nCompute the discriminant \\( D \\):\n\nUsing the formula again with approximate coefficients:\n\n\\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 2.5615 \\), \\( c = -4.43875 \\), \\( d = -10.368 \\)\n\nWait, no, the standard form is \\( x^3 + ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\), so here:\n\nThe equation is \\( x^3 + 2.5615x^2 -4.43875x -10.368 = 0 \\), so coefficients:\n\n\\( a = 2.5615 \\), \\( b = -4.43875 \\), \\( c = -10.368 \\)\n\nWait, no, the discriminant formula requires the cubic to be written as \\( x^3 + px^2 + qx + r = 0 \\), so:\n\nIn this case,\n\n\\( p = 2.5615 \\), \\( q = -4.43875 \\), \\( r = -10.368 \\)\n\nThus, discriminant \\( D = 18 p q r - 4 p^3 r + p^2 q^2 - 4 q^3 - 27 r^2 \\)\n\nCompute term by term:\n\n1. \\( 18 p q r = 18 * 2.5615 * (-4.43875) * (-10.368) \\)\n\nFirst compute 18 * 2.5615 ≈ 46.107\n\nThen multiply by -4.43875 ≈ 46.107 * -4.43875 ≈ -204.6\n\nThen multiply by -10.368 ≈ -204.6 * -10.368 ≈ 2,120\n\n2. \\( -4 p^3 r = -4*(2.5615)^3*(-10.368) \\)\n\nCompute \\( p^3 ≈ (2.5615)^3 ≈ 16.78 \\)\n\nThus, -4 * 16.78 * (-10.368) ≈ -4 * -173.7 ≈ +694.8\n\n3. \\( p^2 q^2 = (2.5615)^2 * (-4.43875)^2 ≈ (6.561) * (19.7) ≈ 12