is is getting too abstract.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can consider specific substitutions.\n\nLet me suppose that S is a solution, then perhaps the equation for S can be derived by assuming that a, b, c are roots of the cubic equation x³ - Sx² + Qx - P =0,\n\nand also each root satisfies x² = next root +6,\n\nso for each root x,\n\nx² - (next root) =6,\n\nso for root a,\n\na² - b =6,\n\nbut since b is another root,\n\nand in the cubic equation,\n\nthe sum of roots is S,\n\nsum of products Q,\n\nproduct P,\n\nso perhaps we can write equations based on that.\n\nLet me denote the roots as a, b, c.\n\nThen,\n\nFor root a: a² = b +6 ⇒ b = a² -6,\n\nFor root b: b² = c +6 ⇒ c = b² -6 = (a² -6)^2 -6,\n\nFor root c: c² = a +6 ⇒ a = c² -6 = [(a² -6)^2 -6]^2 -6,\n\nwhich is the same as before.\n\nAlternatively, since a, b, c are roots of the cubic,\n\nthen,\n\na + b + c = S,\n\nab + bc + ca = Q,\n\nabc = P,\n\nand also,\n\na² = b +6,\n\nb² = c +6,\n\nc² = a +6.\n\nLet me consider adding the three equations a² = b +6 etc.:\n\na² + b² + c² = S + 18,\n\nand we also know that a² + b² + c² = S² - 2Q,\n\nso,\n\nS² - 2Q = S + 18 ⇒ Q = (S² - S -18)/2,\n\nas before.\n\nAdditionally, multiplying the three equations:\n\na² b² c² = (b +6)(c +6)(a +6),\n\nso,\n\n(abc)^2 = (a +6)(b +6)(c +6),\n\nand expanding the right-hand side gives,\n\nabc + 6(ab + bc + ca) + 36(a + b + c) + 216,\n\nso,\n\nP² = P + 6Q + 36S + 216,\n\nso,\n\nP² - P -6Q -36S -216 =0,\n\nand substituting Q,\n\nP² - P -6*( (S² - S -18)/2 ) -36S -216 =0,\n\nwhich simplifies to,\n\nP² - P -3(S² - S -18) -36S -216 =0,\n\nso,\n\nP² - P -3S² +3S +54 -36S -216 =0,\n\nso,\n\nP² - P -3S² -33S -162=0,\n\nsame as before.\n\nNow, perhaps we can find another relation involving P and S.\n\nLet me consider the cubic equation whose roots are a, b, c:\n\nx³ - Sx² + Qx - P =0,\n\nand since each root satisfies x² = next root +6,\n\nfor example, for root a,\n\na² = b +6,\n\nbut since b is a root,\n\nb = S - a - c,\n\nso,\n\na² = (S - a - c) +6,\n\nsimilarly,\n\nc = S - a - b,\n\nso,\n\na² = S - a - (S - a - b) +6,\n\nWait, this might not be helpful.\n\nAlternatively, since a² = b +6,\n\nand b is a root,\n\nso b = S - a - c,\n\nso,\n\na² = S - a - c +6,\n\nbut also, from the third equation,\n\nc² = a +6,\n\nso,\n\nc = sqrt(a +6),\n\nbut c is also expressed in terms of b,\n\nthis seems to complicate.\n\nAlternatively, express everything in terms of a.\n\nWe have,\n\nb = a² -6,\n\nc = b² -6 = (a² -6)^2 -6,\n\nand then,\n\nsince a + b + c = S,\n\nS = a + (a² -6) + [(a² -6)^2 -6],\n\nwhich is the quartic we had before: S(a) = a^4 -11a² + a +24.\n\nAlso, since c² = a +6,\n\nthen c² - a =6,\n\nbut c = (a² -6)^2 -6,\n\nso,\n\n[(a² -6)^2 -6]^2 - a =6,\n\nwhich is the equation we had for a: [(a² -6)^2 -6]^2 -a -6=0,\n\nwhich is the same as f^3(a) - a -6 =0,\n\nbut earlier we had f^3(a) - a =0,\n\nwait, no:\n\nWait, let me re-derive that.\n\nWait, c = b² -6 = (a² -6)^2 -6,\n\nthen c² = [(a² -6)^2 -6]^2,\n\nand from the third equation, c² = a +6,\n\nso,\n\n[(a² -6)^2 -6]^2 = a +6,\n\nthus,\n\n[(a² -6)^2 -6]^2 - (a +6) =0,\n\nwhich is the equation we had earlier,\n\nso that is eighth degree,\n\nand S(a) is quartic in a,\n\nso S(a) = a^4 -11a² + a +24,\n\ntherefore, for each root a of the eighth-degree equation,\n\nwe get a corresponding S(a),\n\nbut since the eighth-degree equation has eight roots,\n\nthere are eight possible values of a,\n\nbut with the constraint that a, b, c are distinct,\n\nso some of these roots will correspond to a, b, c not being distinct,\n\nand the remaining ones will give distinct triples,\n\nand perhaps the sums S(a) for these remaining triples will only take on two distinct values,\n\nso the problem is asking for those two values.\n\nTherefore, perhaps the eighth-degree equation factors into two quartic equations,\n\neach corresponding to a value of S,\n\nso that the quartic equations in a would have solutions leading to the same S,\n\nso that the possible S values are the roots of a qu