-24x^6,\n\nThird term: 2*(x⁴)*(30) + (-12x²)^2 = 60x^4 + 144x^4 = 204x^4,\n\nFourth term: 2*(-12x²)*(30) = -720x²,\n\nFifth term: (30)^2 =900,\n\nSo altogether,\n\nx^8 -24x^6 +204x^4 -720x² +900,\n\nthen subtract 6,\n\nso f(f(f(x))) = x^8 -24x^6 +204x^4 -720x² +900 -6 = x^8 -24x^6 +204x^4 -720x² +894,\n\nthen f^3(x) -x = x^8 -24x^6 +204x^4 -720x² +894 -x,\n\nso,\n\nx^8 -24x^6 +204x^4 -720x² -x +894 =0,\n\nAh, so my initial expansion was correct, and in the current derivation I forgot to subtract the 6 from the final square. So that's where the discrepancy was.\n\nSo the correct eighth-degree equation is:\n\nx^8 -24x^6 +204x^4 -720x² -x +894 =0,\n\nwhereas in the other path, when I substituted into S(a), I had:\n\nS(a) = a^4 -11a² +a +24,\n\nand if we suppose that a satisfies the eighth-degree equation,\n\nthen perhaps we can write S(a) in terms of the equation?\n\nAlternatively, since S(a) is a quartic function of a,\n\nand a satisfies an eighth-degree equation,\n\nperhaps S(a) satisfies a quartic equation,\n\nbut the problem states there are only two possible values of S,\n\nso maybe S(a) can only take on two values despite a having eight roots,\n\ndue to the constraints of the problem (a, b, c distinct),\n\nso perhaps the eighth-degree equation factors into two quartic equations,\n\neach corresponding to a value of S,\n\nand each quartic equation in a would have four roots,\n\nbut only those roots which give distinct a, b, c,\n\nand the S values for those roots are the two we need.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the eighth-degree equation can be factored as (quadratic in S)(something),\n\nbut since S is a function of a,\n\nit's not straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the two possible values of S are roots of a quadratic equation derived from the system.\n\nLet me consider that since S = a + b + c,\n\nand from the original equations,\n\na² = b +6,\n\nb² = c +6,\n\nc² = a +6,\n\nwe can write:\n\na² - b =6,\n\nb² - c =6,\n\nc² - a =6.\n\nLet me add all three equations:\n\n(a² + b² + c²) - (a + b + c) = 18,\n\nwhich we already knew,\n\nand we also have:\n\na² + b² + c² = S² - 2Q,\n\nand S + 18 = S² - 2Q,\n\nso,\n\nS² - 2Q - S - 18 =0,\n\nand we also have Q = (S² - S -18)/2,\n\nso that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, subtract the equations pairwise.\n\nFor example,\n\n(a² - b) - (b² - c) =0,\n\nwhich gives a² - b - b² + c =0,\n\nso,\n\na² - b² - b + c =0,\n\nfactor a² - b² as (a - b)(a + b),\n\nso,\n\n(a - b)(a + b) - (b - c) =0,\n\nHmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider the differences between variables.\n\nLet me denote d = a - b,\n\ne = b - c,\n\nf = c - a,\n\nbut since a, b, c are distinct, d, e, f are non-zero,\n\nbut also d + e + f =0,\n\nsince (a - b) + (b - c) + (c - a) =0.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider the equations as:\n\na² - b =6,\n\nb² - c =6,\n\nc² - a =6,\n\nso each variable squared minus the next variable equals 6.\n\nLet me consider this as a cyclic system and try to find S = a + b + c.\n\nLet me suppose that S is a constant and try to find possible S.\n\nLet me consider that if we add the three equations:\n\na² + b² + c² - (a + b + c) = 18,\n\nand we also have:\n\n(a + b + c)^2 = a² + b² + c² + 2(ab + bc + ca),\n\nso,\n\nS² = (S + 18) + 2(ab + bc + ca),\n\nso,\n\nab + bc + ca = (S² - S - 18)/2,\n\nas before.\n\nAdditionally, multiplying the three original equations:\n\n(a²)(b²)(c²) = (b +6)(c +6)(a +6),\n\nso,\n\n(abc)^2 = (a +6)(b +6)(c +6),\n\nwhich expands to:\n\n(abc)^2 = abc +6(ab + bc + ca) +36(a + b + c) + 216,\n\nso,\n\n(abc)^2 - abc -6(ab + bc + ca) -36S -216 =0,\n\nsubstituting ab + bc + ca = (S² - S -18)/2,\n\nwe have:\n\n(abc)^2 - abc -6*( (S² - S -18)/2 ) -36S -216 =0,\n\nwhich simplifies to:\n\n(abc)^2 - abc -3(S² - S -18) -36S -216 =0,\n\nthen,\n\n(abc)^2 - abc -3S² +3S +54 -36S -216 =0,\n\nso,\n\n(abc)^2 - abc -3S² -33S -162 =0,\n\nso that's the same equation as before.\n\nNow, suppose we let T = abc,\n\nthen the equation becomes:\n\nT² - T -3S² -33S -162 =0,\n\nand we also have from before that T = P = (S³ -2S² -43S -18)/6,\n\nso substituting that into the equation,\n\n[(S³ -2S² -43S -18)/6]^2 - (S³ -2S² -43S -18)/6 -3S² -33S -162 =0,\n\nwhich is the equation we need to solve for S.\n\nThis is a sixth-degree equation in S,\n\nbut perhaps it can be factored.\n\nLet me denote N = S³ -2S² -43S -18,\n\nthen the equation becomes:\n\n(N/6)^2 - (N/6) -3S² -33S -162 =0,\n\nMultiply through by 36 to eliminate denominators:\n\nN² -6N -108S² -1188S -5832 =0,\n\nand since N = S³ -2S² -43S -18,\n\nwe substitute:\n\n(S³ -2S² -43S -18)^2 -6(S³ -2S² -43S -18) -108S² -1188S -5832 =0,\n\nThis is going to be a lengthy expansion, but perhaps there is a common factor or it can be simplified.\n\nAlternatively, maybe assume that S satisfies a quadratic equation and substitute S² = kS + m into the equation,\n\nbut this requires some guesswork.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps try to see if S = 3 + sqrt(3) or something,\n\nbut this is getting too vague.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is designed so that the two possible values of S are 0 and 6,\n\nbut when I tried S=0 and S=6, they didn't work,\n\nbut let me try S = 3 + 3 =6? No, tried that.\n\nAlternatively, maybe S = 3 - 3 =0? Tried that.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the two values are 3 and -3?\n\nWait, tried S=3 and S=-3,\n\nS=3 gave 264,\n\nS=-3 gave 20.\n\nHmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the two possible values are 3 and 6,\n\nbut both didn't work,\n\nor maybe  something else.\n\nAlternatively, maybe S = 1 + sqrt(13) or similar,\n\nbut without more insight,\n\nperhaps it's better to consider that this problem might have a trick or symmetry I'm missing.\n\nLet me try to consider that if we let x = a + b + c = S,\n\nand also note that from the three equations,\n\na² = b +6,\n\nb² = c +6,\n\nc² = a +6,\n\nthen adding them gives,\n\na² + b² + c² = S + 18,\n\nand also,\n\n(a + b + c)^2 = a² + b² + c² + 2(ab + bc + ca),\n\nso,\n\nS² = (S + 18) + 2(ab + bc + ca),\n\nso,\n\nab + bc + ca = (S² - S - 18)/2,\n\nNow, let me consider the product (a - b)(b - c)(c - a).\n\nThis expression is related to the differences between the variables,\n\nand since a, b, c are distinct,\n\nthis product is non-zero.\n\nBut I'm not sure if this helps.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider that:\n\nLet me consider the equations:\n\na² - b =6,\n\nb² - c =6,\n\nc² - a =6.\n\nLet me subtract the first equation from the second:\n\n(b² - c) - (a² - b) =0,\n\nso,\n\nb² - c -a² + b =0,\n\nb² -a² + b -c =0,\n\n(b -a)(b +a) + (b -c) =0,\n\nSimilarly, subtract the second from the third:\n\n(c² - a) - (b² - c) =0,\n\nc² - a -b² +c =0,\n\nc² -b² +c -a =0,\n\n(c -b)(c +b) + (c -a) =0,\n\nAnd subtract the third from the first:\n\n(a² - b) - (c² - a) =0,\n\na² -b -c² +a =0,\n\na² -c² +a -b =0,\n\n(a -c)(a +c) + (a -b) =0,\n\nHmm, so we have three equations:\n\n1. (b -a)(b +a) + (b -c) =0,\n\n2. (c -b)(c +b) + (c -a) =0,\n\n3. (a -c)(a +c) + (a -b) =0,\n\nLet me denote the differences:\n\nLet me set d = b - a,\n\ne = c - b,\n\nf = a - c,\n\nBut note that d + e + f =0,\n\nsince (b -a) + (c - b) + (a - c) =0.\n\nAlternatively, let me set x = b - a,\n\ny = c - b,\n\nthen f = a - c = - (x + y),\n\nSo,\n\nFrom the first equation:\n\n(b -a)(b +a) + (b -c) =0,\n\nwhich is,\n\nx*( (a + b) ) + (-y) =0,\n\nsince b - c = (b - a) - (c - b) =x - y? Wait, no:\n\nWait, b - c = b - (b + y) = -y,\n\nsince c = b + y,\n\nso b - c = -y,\n\nso the first equation becomes:\n\nx*(a + b) - y =0,\n\nSimilarly, the second equation:\n\n(c - b)(c + b) + (c - a) =0,\n\nwhich is,\n\ny*(c + b) + (c - a) =0,\n\nbut c - a = (c - b) + (b - a) = y + x,\n\nsince c - a = (c - b) + (b - a) = y + x,\n\nso,\n\ny*(c + b) + (x + y) =0,\n\nThird equation:\n\n(a - c)(a + c) + (a - b) =0,\n\na - c = - (c - a) = - (x + y),\n\na - b = -x,\n\nso,\n\n- (x + y)*(a + c) -x =0,\n\nHmm, so now we have three equations in variables x, y, a, b, c.\n\nThis might not be helpful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps express a + b in terms of x and other variables.\n\nFrom the first equation:\n\nx*(a + b) - y =0 ⇒ a + b = y / x,\n\nSimilarly, from the second equation:\n\ny*(c + b) + x + y =0 ⇒ y*(c + b) = - (x + y),\n\nso,\n\nc + b = - (x + y)/y = - (x/y +1),\n\nSimilarly, from the third equation:\n\n- (x + y)*(a + c) -x =0 ⇒ - (x + y)*(a + c) =x ⇒ (x + y)*(a + c) = -x,\n\nThus,\n\na + c = -x / (x + y),\n\nNow, we have expressions for a + b, c + b, and a + c in terms of x and y.\n\nLet me see if we can combine these.\n\nWe have:\n\na + b = y / x,\n\nc + b = - (x/y + 1),\n\na + c = -x / (x + y),\n\nLet me write these three equations:\n\n1. a + b = y/x,\n\n2. b + c = - (x/y + 1),\n\n3. a + c = -x/(x + y),\n\nLet me denote these as equations (1), (2), and (3).\n\nNow, let me try to solve for a, b, c in terms of x and y.\n\nFirst, from equation (1): a = y/x - b,\n\nFrom equation (2): c = - (x/y +1) - b,\n\nSubstitute these into equation (3):\n\na + c = (y/x - b) + (-x/y -1 - b) = y/x - x/y -1 -2b = -x/(x + y),\n\nTherefore,\n\ny/x - x/y -1 -2b = -x/(x + y),\n\nSolve for b:\n\n-2b = -x/(x + y) - y/x + x/y +1,\n\nMultiply both sides by -1/2:\n\nb = [x/(x + y) + y/x - x/y -1]/2,\n\nThis is getting very convoluted.\n\nPerhaps this approach is not fruitful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider specific numerical values.\n\nWait, perhaps the two possible values of S are 0 and 6,\n\nbut when I tried S=0, the equation didn't hold,\n\nbut maybe I made a mistake?\n\nWait, let me try S = 3 + 3 =6? No, tried S=6.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps S = sqrt( something ).\n\nAlternatively, let me try to assume that S is a solution to the equation S^2 - 3S - 12 =0,\n\nthen S = [3 ± sqrt(9 +48)]/2 = [3 ± sqrt(57)]/2,\n\nbut not sure.\n\nAlternatively, since the problem says \"compute the two possible values\",\n\nmaybe they are 0 and 6,\n\nbut when I plugged in S=0, the equation didn't hold,\n\nbut perhaps I made a mistake in calculation.\n\nWait, let me recheck S=0:\n\nIf S =0,\n\nthen Q = (0 -0 -18)/2 = -9,\n\nand P = (0 -0 -0 -18)/6 = -3,\n\nthen plugging into the equation P² - P -3S