-43*[ (-1 + sqrt(17))/2 ] -18,\n\nCompute term by term:\n\nFirst term: (-26 +10 sqrt(17))/4,\n\nSecond term: -2*(9 - sqrt(17))/2 = - (9 - sqrt(17)),\n\nThird term: -43*(-1 + sqrt(17))/2 = (43 -43 sqrt(17))/2,\n\nFourth term: -18,\n\nNow, combine all terms over a common denominator of 4:\n\nFirst term: (-26 +10 sqrt(17))/4,\n\nSecond term: - (9 - sqrt(17)) = -36/4 + (4 sqrt(17))/4,\n\nWait, alternatively,\n\nConvert all terms to quarters:\n\nSecond term: - (9 - sqrt(17)) = -9 + sqrt(17) = (-36/4 + (4 sqrt(17))/4),\n\nWait, actually, better to express each term as over 4:\n\nSecond term: - (9 - sqrt(17)) = -9 + sqrt(17) = (-36/4 + 4 sqrt(17)/4),\n\nThird term: (43 -43 sqrt(17))/2 = (86 -86 sqrt(17))/4,\n\nFourth term: -18 = -72/4,\n\nThus,\n\nTotal expression:\n\n[ (-26 +10 sqrt(17)) + (-36 +4 sqrt(17)) + (86 -86 sqrt(17)) + (-72) ] /4,\n\nCombine numerators:\n\nConstants:\n\n-26 -36 +86 -72 = (-26 -36) = -62; (-62 +86)=24; (24 -72)= -48,\n\nsqrt(17) terms:\n\n10 sqrt(17) +4 sqrt(17) -86 sqrt(17) = (14 sqrt(17) -86 sqrt(17)) = -72 sqrt(17),\n\nThus numerator: -48 -72 sqrt(17),\n\nTherefore,\n\nS³ -2S² -43S -18 = [ -48 -72 sqrt(17) ] /4 = -12 -18 sqrt(17),\n\nThus,\n\nP = [ -12 -18 sqrt(17) ] /6 = -2 -3 sqrt(17),\n\nNow, check the equation P² - P -3S² -33S -162 =0,\n\nFirst compute P²:\n\n(-2 -3 sqrt(17))² =4 +12 sqrt(17) +9*17 =4 +12 sqrt(17) +153=157 +12 sqrt(17),\n\n-P = 2 +3 sqrt(17),\n\n-3S²: recall S² = (9 - sqrt(17))/2,\n\nso -3*(9 - sqrt(17))/2 = (-27 +3 sqrt(17))/2,\n\n-33S: -33*( -1 + sqrt(17) )/2 = (33 -33 sqrt(17))/2,\n\n-162,\n\nSo putting all terms together:\n\nP² - P -3S² -33S -162 =\n\n[157 +12 sqrt(17)] + [2 +3 sqrt(17)] +\n\n[ (-27 +3 sqrt(17))/2 ] +\n\n[ (33 -33 sqrt(17))/2 ] +\n\n[ -162 ]\n\nConvert all terms to have denominator 2:\n\nFirst term: 157 +12 sqrt(17) = (314 +24 sqrt(17))/2,\n\nSecond term: 2 +3 sqrt(17) = (4 +6 sqrt(17))/2,\n\nThird term: (-27 +3 sqrt(17))/2,\n\nFourth term: (33 -33 sqrt(17))/2,\n\nFifth term: -162 = -324/2,\n\nCombine all terms over denominator 2:\n\nNumerator:\n\n314 +24 sqrt(17) +4 +6 sqrt(17) -27 +3 sqrt(17) +33 -33 sqrt(17) -324,\n\nCombine constants:\n\n314 +4 =318; 318 -27=291; 291 +33=324; 324 -324=0,\n\nCombine sqrt(17) terms:\n\n24 sqrt(17) +6 sqrt(17)=30 sqrt(17); 30 sqrt(17)+3 sqrt(17)=33 sqrt(17); 33 sqrt(17) -33 sqrt(17)=0,\n\nThus numerator is 0,\n\nso the entire expression is 0/2 =0,\n\nwhich satisfies the equation.\n\nTherefore, this S is indeed a solution.\n\nSimilarly, the other root S = [ -1 - sqrt(17) ] / 2 will also satisfy the equation.\n\nTherefore, the two possible values of a + b + c are:\n\nS = [ -1 + sqrt(17) ] / 2 and S = [ -1 - sqrt(17) ] / 2.\n\nThus, the answer is those two values.\n\nBut the problem says to list them in a comma-separated list of two valid \\LaTeX expressions.\n\nSo, in LaTeX, they would be written as:\n\n\\boxed{\\dfrac{-1 + \\sqrt{17}}{2}}, \\boxed{\\dfrac{-1 - \\sqrt{17}}{2}}\n\nWait, but the problem says \"compute the two possible values of a + b + c. In your answer, list the two values in a comma-separated list of two valid \\LaTeX expressions.\"\n\nSo likely they want both values inside a single box, separated by a comma.\n\nSo, like \\boxed{\\dfrac{-1 + \\sqrt{17}}{2}, \\dfrac{-1 - \\sqrt{17}}{2}}.\n\nBut let me check the instructions again:\n\n\"Compute the two possible values of a + b + c. In your answer, list the two values in a comma-separated list of two valid \\LaTeX expressions. Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\"\n\nHmm, so they want the final answer within a single box, as a comma-separated list of two LaTeX expressions.\n\nSo, the box should contain both values separated by a comma.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\boxed{\\dfrac{-1 + \\sqrt{17}}{2}, \\dfrac{-1 - \\sqrt{17}}{2}}\n\nYes, that seems to be the requirement.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{\\dfrac{-1 + \\sqrt{17}}{2}, \\dfrac{-1 - \\sqrt{17}}{2}}\n</think>\n\nGiven the equations for pairwise distinct complex numbers \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\):\n\n\\[\na^2 = b + 6, \\quad b^2 = c + 6, \\quad c^2 = a + 6\n\\]\n\nwe aim to find the two possible values of \\(a + b + c\\).\n\nFirst, we add all three equations:\n\n\\[\na^2 + b^2 + c^2 = (b + 6) + (c + 6) + (a + 6)\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the right-hand side, we get:\n\n\\[\na^2 + b^2 + c^2 = a + b + c + 18\n\\]\n\nLet \\(S = a + b + c\\). Using the identity \\(a^2 + b^2 + c^2 = S^2 - 2(ab + bc + ca)\\), we substitute:\n\n\\[\nS^2 - 2(ab + bc + ca) = S + 18\n\\]\n\nNext, we solve for \\(ab + bc + ca\\):\n\n\\[\nab + bc + ca = \\frac{S^2 - S - 18}{2}\n\\]\n\nWe then consider the product of the original equations:\n\n\\[\n(a^2)(b^2)(c^2) = (b + 6)(c + 6)(a + 6)\n\\]\n\nExpanding the right-hand side and using \\(abc = P\\), \\(ab + bc + ca = Q\\), and \\(a + b + c = S\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n(abc)^2 = abc + 6(ab + bc + ca) + 36(a + b + c) + 216\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(Q\\) and \\(S\\) into this equation, we derive:\n\n\\[\nP^2 - P - 3S^2 - 33S - 162 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe also express \\(P\\) in terms of \\(S\\):\n\n\\[\nP = \\frac{S^3 - 2S^2 - 43S - 18}{6}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(P\\) into the equation and simplifying, we find that \\(S\\) must satisfy the quadratic equation:\n\n\\[\nS^2 + S - 4 = 0\n\\]\n\nSolving this quadratic equation, we get the roots:\n\n\\[\nS = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{17}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the two possible values of \\(a + b + c\\) are:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\dfrac{-1 + \\sqrt{17}}{2}, \\dfrac{-1 - \\sqrt{17}}{2}}\n\\]","num_tokens":26360,"correct":true,"formatted":true},{"predicted_answer":"[no boxed]","full_generation":"Okay, so I have this problem here with three pairwise distinct complex numbers a, b, and c. The equations given are:\n\na² = b + 6,\n\nb² = c + 6,\n\nc² = a + 6.\n\nAnd I need to compute the two possible values of a + b + c. Hmm, interesting. Alright, let me see how to approach this. Since they are pairwise distinct complex numbers, but they satisfy these quadratic-like equations that chain together. It's a system of equations with three variables, but each variable is defined in terms of another variable squared minus 6. So it's a cyclic system, right? Like a → b → c → a, each squared minus 6 gives the next variable. Hmm.\n\nI think I need to find a relationship that connects a, b, and c together, perhaps in terms of their sum, or product, or something else. Since the equations are cyclic, maybe if I can express each variable in terms of the next one, and then substitute them all into a single equation involving only a, or only b, or only c, then perhaps I can solve for a, or for b, or for c, and then get the values of a + b + c from there.\n\nLet me try to write down the equations again:\n\n1) a² = b + 6,\n\n2) b² = c + 6,\n\n3) c² = a + 6.\n\nSo, if I can express b in terms of a from the first equation, then express c in terms of b (and hence in terms of a), and then express a in terms of c (and hence in terms of b and a), so maybe substitute all the way back?\n\nStarting with the first equation: b = a² - 6.\n\nThen substitute that into the second equation: b² = c + 6, so (a² - 6)² = c + 6, so c = (a² - 6)² - 6.\n\nThen substitute this expression for c into the third equation: c² = a + 6. So, [(a² - 6)² - 6]² = a + 6.\n\nWow, that seems like a quartic in terms of a², so actually an eighth-degree polynomial equation in terms of a? Let me see:\n\nLet me write this step by step. Let me let’s define each step.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nEquation 1: b = a² - 6.\n\nEquation 2: c = b² - 6 = (a² - 6)² - 6.\n\nEquation 3: Then c² = a + 6, so substituting c from above:\n\n[(a² - 6)² - 6]^2 = a + 6.\n\nYes, so this equation is in terms of a only. So, if I can expand the left-hand side and set it equal to the right-hand side, then move everything to one side to get a polynomial equation in a, and then solve for a. Then, once I have the possible a's, I can find b and c from the equations above, and then compute a + b + c for each case. Since they are pairwise distinct complex numbers, we have to ensure that the solutions we get result in a, b, c being distinct, but maybe some solutions will have a = b = c, which we have to exclude? Hmm, but the problem states that a, b, c are pairwise distinct, so we need to make sure that in our solutions, a ≠ b, b ≠ c, c ≠ a. So perhaps some of the roots of the polynomial equation will correspond to cases where a = b = c, but since they have to be distinct, we might need to exclude those? Hmm, interesting.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the system can be manipulated in a smarter way to avoid having to deal with an eighth-degree polynomial, which might be quite messy. Let me see if there's a better approach.\n\nLet me consider the sum a + b + c. Let me denote S = a + b + c. Maybe if I can find an equation involving S.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider multiplying all three equations together? Let's see.\n\nMultiplying the left sides: a² * b² * c² = (a b c)^2.\n\nMultiplying the right sides: (b + 6)(c + 6)(a + 6).\n\nSo, (a b c)^2 = (b + 6)(c + 6)(a + 6).\n\nHmm, not sure if that helps immediately, but maybe. Alternatively, perhaps express each variable in terms of the next, and then substitute cyclically.\n\nAlternatively, maybe subtract equations or manipulate them to find relationships between a, b, c.\n\nLet me see. Let me try to write each variable in terms of the next variable squared minus 6.\n\nSo, from equation 1: b = a² - 6.\n\nFrom equation 2: c = b² - 6 = (a² - 6)^2 - 6.\n\nFrom equation 3: a = c² - 6 = [(a² - 6)^2 - 6]^2 - 6.\n\nSo, as above, the equation in terms of a is a = [(a² - 6)^2 - 6]^2 - 6. Which is a rather high-degree equation, as I thought.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps let me consider the differences between the variables.\n\nAlternatively, let me suppose that S = a + b + c.\n\nThen, perhaps express each variable in terms of S.\n\nWait, but since each variable is defined in terms of another variable, maybe we can write equations for S?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider the equations:\n\nFrom equation 1: a² - b = 6,\n\nequation 2: b² - c = 6,\n\nequation 3: c² - a = 6.\n\nSo, we have three equations:\n\na² - b = 6, (1)\n\nb² - c = 6, (2)\n\nc² - a = 6. (3)\n\nSo, this looks like a system of equations where each equation relates the square of one variable minus another variable equals 6. Perhaps we can subtract these equations from one another or form a system that can be solved for the variables.\n\nAlternatively, maybe subtract equation (1) from equation (2):\n\n(b² - c) - (a² - b) = 6 - 6,\n\nso, b² - c - a² + b = 0,\n\nso, (b² - a²) + (b - c) = 0,\n\nFactor b² - a² as (b - a)(b + a), so:\n\n(b - a)(b + a) + (b - c) = 0,\n\nHmm, not sure if that helps.\n\nAlternatively, maybe subtract equation (2) from equation (3):\n\n(c² - a) - (b² - c) = 6 - 6,\n\nso, c² - a - b² + c = 0,\n\nso, (c² - b²) + (c - a) = 0,\n\nFactor c² - b² as (c - b)(c + b), so:\n\n(c - b)(c + b) + (c - a) = 0,\n\nHmm, again similar structure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps subtract equation (1) from equation (3):\n\n(c² - a) - (a² - b) = 6 - 6,\n\nso, c² - a - a² + b = 0,\n\nso, (c² - a²) + (b - a) = 0,\n\nFactor c² - a² as (c - a)(c + a), so:\n\n(c - a)(c + a) + (b - a) = 0,\n\nHmm, still similar structure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps think of the equations as:\n\na² = b + 6,\n\nb² = c + 6,\n\nc² = a + 6,\n\nand then substitute each into the next. Since it's cyclic, maybe composing the functions?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps define a function f(x) = x² - 6, then the equations become:\n\nb = f(a),\n\nc = f(b),\n\na = f(c).\n\nSo, composing f three times, we get:\n\na = f(f(f(a))).\n\nTherefore, a is a fixed point of the function f composed three times, i.e., f³(a) = a, where f³ is the third iterate of f.\n\nSimilarly, since b = f(a), c = f(b), so all three variables are in the same cycle of the function f.\n\nTherefore, the variables a, b, c form a 3-cycle of the function f(x) = x² - 6.\n\nTherefore, to solve for a, we need to find all points a such that f³(a) = a, but also that the cycle has length 3, meaning that applying f three times brings you back to a, but applying f once or twice doesn't bring you back to a or to another element of the cycle before the third application.\n\nHowever, in complex dynamics, fixed points of f³ include fixed points of f, so we have to be careful to exclude those where a is a fixed point of f, i.e., f(a) = a, because then the cycle would have length 1, not 3. Similarly, points where f²(a) = a would be 2-cycles, so we need to exclude those as well. Therefore, the solutions we want are the 3-cycles of f, which are fixed points of f³ but not fixed points of f or f².\n\nTherefore, perhaps the equation f³(a) = a is the key equation here, which would give us all possible a's (including fixed points and 2-cycles), and then we can subtract the fixed points and 2-cycles to get the 3-cycles. However, since we are dealing with complex numbers, there might be multiple roots, and we might have to consider all of them, but then check which ones give rise to distinct a, b, c.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps since we just need the possible values of a + b + c, maybe even if some of the solutions correspond to fixed points or 2-cycles, the value of a + b + c might still be the same or different? Not sure. Maybe it's better to just proceed with forming the equation f³(a) = a and see what happens.\n\nSo let me try to compute f³(a) step by step.\n\nFirst, f(x) = x² - 6.\n\nThen f(f(x)) = f(x² - 6) = (x² - 6)² - 6 = x⁴ - 12x² + 36 - 6 = x⁴ - 12x² + 30.\n\nThen f³(x) = f(f(f(x))) = f(x⁴ - 12x² + 30) = [ (x⁴ - 12x² + 30) ]² - 6.\n\nSo, f³(x) = (x⁴ - 12x² + 30)² - 6.\n\nTherefore, the equation f³(a) = a becomes:\n\n(x⁴ - 12x² + 30)² - 6 = x,\n\nwhere x is a. So, moving all terms to the left side:\n\n(x⁴ - 12x² + 30)² - 6 - x = 0.\n\nTherefore, expanding (x⁴ - 12x² + 30)² first.\n\nLet me compute (x⁴ - 12x² + 30)²:\n\nLet me denote u = x⁴, v = -12x², w = 30,\n\nthen (u + v + w)^2 = u² + v² + w² + 2uv + 2uw + 2vw.\n\nWait, actually, more straightforwardly:\n\n(x⁴ - 12x² + 30)² = (x⁴)² + (-12x²)^2 + (30)^2 + 2*(x⁴)(-12x²) + 2*(x⁴)(30) + 2*(-12x²)(30).\n\nCalculating term by term:\n\nFirst term: (x⁴)^2 = x^8.\n\nSecond term: (-12x²)^2 = 144x^4.\n\nThird term: 30^2 = 900.\n\nFourth term: 2*(x⁴)*(-12x²) = 2*(-12)x^{4+2} = -24x^6.\n\nFifth term: 2*(x⁴)*(30) = 60x^4.\n\nSixth term: 2*(-12x²)*(30) = 2*(-360)x² = -720x².\n\nTherefore, combining all terms:\n\nx^8 - 24x^6 + (144x^4 + 60x^4) + (-720x²) + 900.\n\nSimplify:\n\nx^8 - 24x^6 + (144 + 60)x^4 - 720x² + 900,\n\nso,\n\nx^8 - 24x^6 + 204x^4 - 720x² + 900.\n\nTherefore, the entire equation f³(a) = a becomes:\n\n(x^8 - 24x^6 + 204x^4 - 720x² + 900) - 6 - x = 0,\n\nso,\n\nx^8 - 24x^6 + 204x^4 - 720x² + 900 - 6 - x = 0,\n\nSimplify constants:\n\n900 - 6 = 894,\n\nso,\n\nx^8 - 24x^6 + 204x^4 - 720x² - x + 894 = 0.\n\nTherefore, the equation we need to solve is:\n\nx^8 - 24x^6 + 204x^4 - 720x² - x + 894 = 0.\n\nWhoa, that's an eighth-degree polynomial equation. That seems really daunting. Solving an eighth-degree polynomial is not straightforward. Maybe there's a factorization possible here?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the polynomial can be factored into lower-degree polynomials. Let me see if that is possible.\n\nFirst, let me write the polynomial again:\n\nP(x) = x^8 - 24x^6 + 204x^4 - 720x² - x + 894.\n\nHmm. Let me see if x= something simple is a root. Let me try plugging in x=1:\n\nP(1) = 1 - 24 + 204 - 720 - 1 + 894.\n\nCompute step by step:\n\n1 -24 = -23,\n\n-23 + 204 = 181,\n\n181 - 720 = -539,\n\n-539 -1 = -540,\n\n-540 + 894 = 354 ≠ 0.\n\nNot zero.\n\nx = -1:\n\nP(-1) = (-1)^8 -24*(-1)^6 +204*(-1)^4 -720*(-1)^2 - (-1) + 894.\n\nWhich is:\n\n1 -24 +204 -720 +1 +894.\n\nCompute step by step:\n\n1 -24 = -23,\n\n-23 + 204 = 181,\n\n181 -720 = -539,\n\n-539 +1 = -538,\n\n-538 + 894 = 356 ≠ 0.\n\nHmm, not zero.\n\nx = 2:\n\nP(2) = 256 - 24*(64) + 204*(16) - 720*(4) - 2 + 894.\n\nCompute each term:\n\nx^8 = 256,\n\n-24x^6 = -24*(64) = -1536,\n\n204x^4 = 204*16 = 3264,\n\n-720x² = -720*4 = -2880,\n\n-x = -2,\n\n+894.\n\nSo adding them up:\n\n256 - 1536 = -1280,\n\n-1280 + 3264 = 1984,\n\n1984 - 2880 = -896,\n\n-896 -2 = -898,\n\n-898 + 894 = -4 ≠ 0.\n\nHmm, close but not zero.\n\nx = 3:\n\nP(3) = 3^8 -24*3^6 +204*3^4 -720*3² -3 +894.\n\nCompute each term:\n\n3^8 = 6561,\n\n24*3^6: 3^6 = 729, so 24*729 = let's see, 24*700 = 16,800 and 24*29=696, so total 16,800 + 696 = 17,496,\n\nso -24*3^6 = -17,496,\n\n204*3^4: 3^4 = 81, so 204*81. Let's compute 200*81 = 16,200 and 4*81=324, so total 16,200 + 324 = 16,524,\n\n-720*3² = -720*9 = -6,480,\n\n-3,\n\n+894.\n\nNow, adding all together:\n\nStart with 6561 - 17,496 = (6561 - 17,496) = -10,935,\n\n-10,935 + 16,524 = 5,589,\n\n5,589 - 6,480 = -891,\n\n-891 -3 = -894,\n\n-894 + 894 = 0.\n\nOh! x = 3 is a root!\n\nGreat! So (x - 3) is a factor. Let me perform polynomial division or use synthetic division to factor this out.\n\nLet me try synthetic division with x = 3.\n\nBut since this is an eighth-degree polynomial, synthetic division might be tedious, but let me try.\n\nThe polynomial is:\n\nP(x) = x^8 - 24x^6 + 204x^4 - 720x² - x + 894.\n\nWait, note that the polynomial skips some degrees: it has x^8, x^6, x^4, x², x, and constant. So, the exponents are 8, 6, 4, 2, 1, 0. So missing x^7, x^5, x^3 terms. So in terms of descending exponents, coefficients:\n\nFor x^8: 1,\n\nx^7: 0,\n\nx^6: -24,\n\nx^5: 0,\n\nx^4: 204,\n\nx^3: 0,\n\nx^2: -720,\n\nx^1: -1,\n\nx^0: 894.\n\nSo the coefficients list is [1, 0, -24, 0, 204, 0, -720, -1, 894].\n\nWe are dividing by (x - 3), so using synthetic division with root 3.\n\nSet up the coefficients:\n\n1 (x^8), 0 (x^7), -24 (x^6), 0 (x^5), 204 (x^4), 0 (x^3), -720 (x^2), -1 (x), 894 (constant).\n\nWe write them down, and bring down the first coefficient (1).\n\nMultiply by 3: 1*3 = 3. Add to next coefficient: 0 + 3 = 3.\n\nMultiply by 3: 3*3 = 9. Add to next coefficient: -24 + 9 = -15.\n\nMultiply by 3: -15*3 = -45. Add to next coefficient: 0 + (-45) = -45.\n\nMultiply by 3: -45*3 = -135. Add to next coefficient: 204 + (-135) = 69.\n\nMultiply by 3: 69*3 = 207. Add to next coefficient: 0 + 207 = 207.\n\nMultiply by 3: 207*3 = 621. Add to next coefficient: -720 + 621 = -99.\n\nMultiply by 3: -99*3 = -297. Add to next coefficient: -1 + (-297) = -298.\n\nMultiply by 3: -298*3 = -894. Add to last coefficient: 894 + (-894) = 0. Perfect, so the remainder is 0 as expected.\n\nTherefore, the result of the division is the polynomial:\n\nx^7 + 3x^6 -15x^5 -45x^4 + 69x^3 + 207x^2 -99x -298.\n\nWait, let me check the coefficients step by step from the synthetic division:\n\nThe coefficients of the quotient polynomial are the numbers we got after each step, except the last one which is the remainder (which is zero). So starting from the first coefficient:\n\n1 (x^7),\n\nthen 3 (x^6),\n\nthen -15 (x^5),\n\nthen -45 (x^4),\n\nthen 69 (x^3),\n\nthen 207 (x^2),\n\nthen -99 (x^1),\n\nthen -298 (x^0),\n\nand the remainder 0.\n\nTherefore, the quotient polynomial is:\n\nQ(x) = x^7 + 3x^6 -15x^5 -45x^4 + 69x^3 + 207x^2 -99x -298.\n\nTherefore, we can write:\n\nP(x) = (x - 3)(x^7 + 3x^6 -15x^5 -45x^4 + 69x^3 + 207x^2 -99x -298).\n\nNow, perhaps we can factor this seventh-degree polynomial further? Maybe by trying another root. Let me test x=3 again on Q(x):\n\nQ(3) = 3^7 + 3*3^6 -15*3^5 -45*3^4 + 69*3^3 + 207*3^2 -99*3 -298.\n\nLet me compute step by step:\n\n3^7 = 2187,\n\n3*3^6 = 3*729 = 2187,\n\n-15*3^5 = -15*243 = -3645,\n\n-45*3^4 = -45*81 = -3645,\n\n69*3^3 = 69*27 = 1863,\n\n207*3^2 = 207*9 = 1863,\n\n-99*3 = -297,\n\n-298.\n\nNow add them all together:\n\nStart with 2187 + 2187 = 4374,\n\n4374 - 3645 = 729,\n\n729 - 3645 = -2916,\n\n-2916 + 1863 = -1053,\n\n-1053 + 1863 = 810,\n\n810 - 297 = 513,\n\n513 - 298 = 215 ≠ 0.\n\nSo x=3 is not a root of Q(x). How about x= -3?\n\nQ(-3) = (-3)^7 + 3*(-3)^6 -15*(-3)^5 -45*(-3)^4 + 69*(-3)^3 + 207*(-3)^2 -99*(-3) -298.\n\nCompute term by term:\n\n(-3)^7 = -2187,\n\n3*(-3)^6 = 3*(729) = 2187,\n\n-15*(-3)^5 = -15*(-243) = +3645,\n\n-45*(-3)^4 = -45*(81) = -3645,\n\n69*(-3)^3 = 69*(-27) = -1863,\n\n207*(-3)^2 = 207*9 = 1863,\n\n-99*(-3) = +297,\n\n-298.\n\nNow add them up:\n\nStart with -2187 + 2187 = 0,\n\n0 + 3645 = 3645,\n\n3645 - 3645 = 0,\n\n0 - 1863 = -1863,\n\n-1863 + 1863 = 0,\n\n0 + 297 = 297,\n\n297 - 298 = -1 ≠ 0.\n\nHmm, not zero. How about x = 2?\n\nQ(2) = 2^7 + 3*2^6 -15*2^5 -45*2^4 + 69*2^3 + 207*2^2 -99*2 -298.\n\nCompute each term:\n\n2^7 = 128,\n\n3*2^6 = 3*64 = 192,\n\n-15*2^5 = -15*32 = -480,\n\n-45*2^4 = -45*16 = -720,\n\n69*2^3 = 69*8 = 552,\n\n207*2^2 = 207*4 = 828,\n\n-99*2 = -198,\n\n-298.\n\nNow adding up:\n\n128 + 192 = 320,\n\n320 - 480 = -160,\n\n-160 -720 = -880,\n\n-880 + 552 = -328,\n\n-328 + 828 = 500,\n\n500 - 198 = 302,\n\n302 - 298 = 4 ≠ 0.\n\nHmm, close but not zero. How about x = 1?\n\nQ(1) = 1 + 3 -15 -45 + 69 + 207 -99 -298.\n\nCompute step by step:\n\n1 + 3 = 4,\n\n4 -15 = -11,\n\n-11 -45 = -56,\n\n-56 + 69 = 13,\n\n13 + 207 = 220,\n\n220 - 99 = 121,\n\n121 - 298 = -177 ≠ 0.\n\nx = -2?\n\nQ(-2) = (-2)^7 + 3*(-2)^6 -15*(-2)^5 -45*(-2)^4 + 69*(-2)^3 + 207*(-2)^2 -99*(-2) -298.\n\nCompute term by term:\n\n(-2)^7 = -128,\n\n3*(-2)^6 = 3*64 = 192,\n\n-15*(-2)^5 = -15*(-32) = +480,\n\n-45*(-2)^4 = -45*(16) = -720,\n\n69*(-2)^3 = 69*(-8) = -552,\n\n207*(-2)^2 = 207*4 = 828,\n\n-99*(-2) = +198,\n\n-298.\n\nAdding up:\n\nStart with -128 + 192 = 64,\n\n64 + 480 = 544,\n\n544 - 720 = -176,\n\n-176 -552 = -728,\n\n-728 + 828 = 100,\n\n100 + 198 = 298,\n\n298 - 298 = 0.\n\nHey! x = -2 is a root!\n\nSo Q(-2) = 0, so (x + 2) is a factor of Q(x). Let me perform synthetic division again on Q(x) with root x = -2.\n\nThe coefficients of Q(x) are [1, 3, -15, -45, 69, 207, -99, -298].\n\nWait, actually, Q(x) is a 7th-degree polynomial, so coefficients from x^7 down to x^0:\n\nQ(x) = x^7 + 3x^6 -15x^5 -45x^4 + 69x^3 + 207x^2 -99x -298.\n\nTherefore coefficients are:\n\n1 (x^7),\n\n3 (x^6),\n\n-15 (x^5),\n\n-45 (x^4),\n\n69 (x^3),\n\n207 (x^2),\n\n-99 (x^1),\n\n-298 (x^0).\n\nWe are dividing by (x + 2), so using synthetic division with root -2.\n\nSet up coefficients: 1, 3, -15, -45, 69, 207, -99, -298.\n\nBring down the first coefficient (1).\n\nMultiply by -2: 1*(-2) = -2. Add to next coefficient: 3 + (-2) = 1.\n\nMultiply by -2: 1*(-2) = -2. Add to next coefficient: -15 + (-2) = -17.\n\nMultiply by -2: -17*(-2) = 34. Add to next coefficient: -45 + 34 = -11.\n\nMultiply by -2: -11*(-2) = 22. Add to next coefficient: 69 + 22 = 91.\n\nMultiply by -2: 91*(-2) = -182. Add to next coefficient: 207 + (-182) = 25.\n\nMultiply by -2: 25*(-2) = -50. Add to next coefficient: -99 + (-50) = -149.\n\nMultiply by -2: -149*(-2) = 298. Add to last coefficient: -298 + 298 = 0. Perfect, so remainder is zero.\n\nTherefore, the quotient polynomial after dividing Q(x) by (x + 2) is:\n\nx^6 + 1x^5 -17x^4 -11x^3 + 91x^2 +25x -149.\n\nTherefore, now we have factored P(x) as:\n\nP(x) = (x - 3)(x + 2)(x^6 + x^5 -17x^4 -11x^3 +91x^2 +25x -149).\n\nNow, let's see if we can factor the sixth-degree polynomial further. Let me denote R(x) = x^6 + x^5 -17x^4 -11x^3 +91x^2 +25x -149.\n\nPerhaps try plugging in x=1:\n\nR(1) = 1 + 1 -17 -11 +91 +25 -149.\n\nCompute step by step:\n\n1 +1 = 2,\n\n2 -17 = -15,\n\n-15 -11 = -26,\n\n-26 +91 = 65,\n\n65 +25 = 90,\n\n90 -149 = -59 ≠ 0.\n\nx = -1:\n\nR(-1) = (-1)^6 + (-1)^5 -17*(-1)^4 -11*(-1)^3 +91*(-1)^2 +25*(-1) -149.\n\nCompute term by term:\n\n1 -1 -17*(1) -11*(-1) +91*(1) + (-25) -149,\n\nso,\n\n1 -1 = 0,\n\n0 -17 = -17,\n\n-17 +11 = -6,\n\n-6 +91 = 85,\n\n85 -25 = 60,\n\n60 -149 = -89 ≠ 0.\n\nx=2:\n\nR(2) = 64 + 32 -17*(16) -11*(8) +91*(4) +25*(2) -149.\n\nCompute each term:\n\n2^6 = 64,\n\n2^5 = 32,\n\n-17*(2^4) = -17*16 = -272,\n\n-11*(2^3) = -11*8 = -88,\n\n91*(2^2) = 91*4 = 364,\n\n25*2 = 50,\n\n-149.\n\nAdding up:\n\n64 + 32 = 96,\n\n96 -272 = -176,\n\n-176 -88 = -264,\n\n-264 + 364 = 100,\n\n100 +50 = 150,\n\n150 -149 = 1 ≠ 0.\n\nClose again, but not zero.\n\nx = -3:\n\nR(-3) = (-3)^6 + (-3)^5 -17*(-3)^4 -11*(-3)^3 +91*(-3)^2 +25*(-3) -149.\n\nCompute term by term:\n\n(-3)^6 = 729,\n\n(-3)^5 = -243,\n\n-17*(-3)^4 = -17*(81) = -1377,\n\n-11*(-3)^3 = -11*(-27) = +297,\n\n91*(-3)^2 = 91*9 = 819,\n\n25*(-3) = -75,\n\n-149.\n\nAdding up:\n\n729 -243 = 486,\n\n486 -1377 = -891,\n\n-891 +297 = -594,\n\n-594 +819 = 225,\n\n225 -75 = 150,\n\n150 -149 = 1 ≠ 0.\n\nHmm. x = 3?\n\nR(3) = 729 + 243 -17*(81) -11*(27) +91*(9) +25*3 -149.\n\nCompute each term:\n\n3^6 = 729,\n\n3^5 = 243,\n\n-17*(3^4) = -17*81 = -1377,\n\n-11*(3^3) = -11*27 = -297,\n\n91*(3^2) = 91*9 = 819,\n\n25*3 = 75,\n\n-149.\n\nAdding up:\n\n729 +243 = 972,\n\n972 -1377 = -405,\n\n-405 -297 = -702,\n\n-702 +819 = 117,\n\n117 +75 = 192,\n\n192 -149 = 43 ≠ 0.\n\nHmm, not zero.\n\nHow about x = 4?\n\nR(4) = 4^6 +4^5 -17*4^4 -11*4^3 +91*4^2 +25*4 -149.\n\nCompute:\n\n4^6 = 4096,\n\n4^5 = 1024,\n\n-17*4^4 = -17*256 = -4352,\n\n-11*4^3 = -11*64 = -704,\n\n91*16 = 1456,\n\n25*4 = 100,\n\n-149.\n\nAdding up:\n\n4096 +1024 = 5120,\n\n5120 -4352 = 768,\n\n768 -704 = 64,\n\n64 +1456 = 1520,\n\n1520 +100 = 1620,\n\n1620 -149 = 1471 ≠ 0.\n\nHmm. Maybe x = -4?\n\nR(-4) = (-4)^6 + (-4)^5 -17*(-4)^4 -11*(-4)^3 +91*(-4)^2 +25*(-4) -149.\n\nCompute term by term:\n\n(-4)^6 = 4096,\n\n(-4)^5 = -1024,\n\n-17*(-4)^4 = -17*(256) = -4352,\n\n-11*(-4)^3 = -11*(-64) = +704,\n\n91*(-4)^2 = 91*16 = 1456,\n\n25*(-4) = -100,\n\n-149.\n\nAdding up:\n\n4096 -1024 = 3072,\n\n3072 -4352 = -1280,\n\n-1280 +704 = -576,\n\n-576 +1456 = 880,\n\n880 -100 = 780,\n\n780 -149 = 631 ≠ 0.\n\nHmm. Maybe x = sqrt( something )? Not sure. Alternatively, maybe this sixth-degree polynomial factors into lower-degree polynomials, perhaps quadratics or cubics?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the original equation can be approached differently.\n\nWait a second, perhaps instead of going through all this, since we have that a, b, c satisfy a system of equations, and we need a + b + c, maybe we can find S = a + b + c and another equation involving S?\n\nLet me try that approach again.\n\nLet me denote S = a + b + c.\n\nAlso, let me denote the product P = a b c.\n\nPerhaps also consider the sum of the squares or something else.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider the equations:\n\na² = b + 6,\n\nb² = c + 6,\n\nc² = a + 6.\n\nIf I add all three equations together, I get:\n\na² + b² + c² = (b + 6) + (c + 6) + (a + 6) = a + b + c + 18.\n\nTherefore,\n\na² + b² + c² = S + 18. (Equation A)\n\nAlso, we know that (a + b + c)^2 = a² + b² + c² + 2(ab + bc + ca).\n\nSo, S² = (a² + b² + c²) + 2(ab + bc + ca).\n\nFrom Equation A, a² + b² + c² = S + 18, so substitute:\n\nS² = (S + 18) + 2(ab + bc + ca).\n\nTherefore,\n\nS² - S - 18 = 2(ab + bc + ca).\n\nSo,\n\nab + bc + ca = (S² - S - 18)/2. (Equation B)\n\nHmm, so now we have expressions for S and ab + bc + ca in terms of S. Maybe we can find another equation involving S and ab + bc + ca?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps multiply the original equations together.\n\nWe have:\n\na² b² c² = (b + 6)(c + 6)(a + 6).\n\nBut the left-hand side is (a b c)^2 = P², where P = a b c.\n\nThe right-hand side is (a + 6)(b + 6)(c + 6). Let me expand that.\n\nFirst, expand two terms first:\n\n(a + 6)(b + 6) = a b + 6a + 6b + 36.\n\nThen multiply by (c + 6):\n\n[ a b + 6a + 6b + 36 ] (c + 6) =\n\na b c + 6 a b + 6 a c + 36 a + 6 b c + 36 b + 36 c + 216.\n\nTherefore, altogether:\n\n(a + 6)(b + 6)(c + 6) = a b c + 6(a b + a c + b c) + 6(6a + 6b + 6c) + 216.\n\nWait, let me check term by term:\n\nWait, actually:\n\nWait, expanding step by step:\n\nFirst, (a + 6)(b + 6) = ab + 6a + 6b + 36,\n\nthen multiply by (c + 6):\n\nMultiply each term in the first product by c and by 6:\n\n(ab)(c) + (ab)(6) + (6a)(c) + (6a)(6) + (6b)(c) + (6b)(6) + (36)(c) + (36)(6).\n\nSo, that is:\n\na b c + 6 a b + 6 a c + 36 a + 6 b c + 36 b + 36 c + 216.\n\nTherefore, grouping like terms:\n\n= a b c + 6(a b + a c + b c) + 36(a + b + c) + 216.\n\nTherefore, indeed:\n\n(a + 6)(b + 6)(c + 6) = P + 6(ab + bc + ca) + 36 S + 216.\n\nTherefore, going back to the equation from multiplying the original three equations:\n\nP² = (a + 6)(b + 6)(c + 6) = P + 6(ab + bc + ca) + 36 S + 216.\n\nSo,\n\nP² = P + 6(ab + bc + ca) + 36 S + 216. (Equation C)\n\nNow, from Equation B, we have ab + bc + ca = (S² - S - 18)/2. Let me substitute that into Equation C:\n\nP² = P + 6*( (S² - S - 18)/2 ) + 36 S + 216.\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\n6*( (S² - S - 18)/2 ) = 3*(S² - S - 18) = 3 S² - 3 S - 54.\n\nTherefore, Equation C becomes:\n\nP² = P + 3 S² - 3 S - 54 + 36 S + 216.\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n-3 S + 36 S = 33 S,\n\n-54 + 216 = 162,\n\nTherefore:\n\nP² = P + 3 S² + 33 S + 162.\n\nSo,\n\nP² - P - 3 S² - 33 S - 162 = 0. (Equation D)\n\nHmm, so now we have Equation D involving P and S. But we need another equation relating P and S.\n\nPerhaps we can find another equation involving P and S?\n\nLet me see. Let me recall that we have expressions for a, b, c in terms of each other. For instance, from the original equations:\n\na² = b + 6,\n\nso, b = a² - 6,\n\nsimilarly, c = b² - 6,\n\nand a = c² - 6.\n\nTherefore, perhaps we can express P = a b c in terms of a, or in terms of S?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps express P in terms of a, using the substitutions.\n\nLet me try that.\n\nLet me express b and c in terms of a:\n\nb = a² - 6,\n\nc = (a² - 6)^2 - 6,\n\nso then,\n\nP = a * b * c = a * (a² - 6) * [ (a² - 6)^2 - 6 ].\n\nTherefore, P is expressed in terms of a. But since we also have S = a + b + c = a + (a² - 6) + [ (a² - 6)^2 - 6 ].\n\nTherefore, S can also be written in terms of a. Therefore, perhaps we can write everything in terms of a, then get an equation in terms of a and S, but this might not necessarily help unless we can eliminate a.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can find an expression for P in terms of S?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can find another relation between P and S?\n\nAlternatively, let me consider the product a b c.\n\nWait, let's see. Let me see if there is a way to get another equation involving P and S.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider the sum a^3 + b^3 + c^3 or something like that?\n\nAlternatively, let me see if I can find expressions for a^3, b^3, c^3 from the original equations.\n\nGiven that a² = b + 6,\n\nso, multiplying both sides by a: a³ = a b + 6a.\n\nSimilarly, b³ = b c + 6b,\n\nand c³ = c a + 6c.\n\nTherefore,\n\na³ + b³ + c³ = a b + b c + c a + 6(a + b + c).\n\nBut the left side is a³ + b³ + c³, and the right side is (ab + bc + ca) + 6 S.\n\nHmm, perhaps we can express a³ + b³ + c³ in another way?\n\nRecall that a³ + b³ + c³ - 3 a b c = (a + b + c)(a² + b² + c² - a b - b c - c a).\n\nTherefore,\n\na³ + b³ + c³ = 3 a b c + (a + b + c)(a² + b² + c² - a b - b c - c a).\n\nFrom earlier, we know that a² + b² + c² = S + 18,\n\nand ab + bc + ca = (S² - S - 18)/2,\n\nso,\n\na² + b² + c² - (ab + bc + ca) = (S + 18) - ( (S² - S - 18)/2 ).\n\nLet me compute that:\n\nLet me denote Q = a² + b² + c² - (ab + bc + ca) = (S + 18) - [ (S² - S - 18)/2 ].\n\nCompute:\n\n= [2(S + 18) - (S² - S - 18)] / 2\n\n= [2S + 36 - S² + S + 18] / 2\n\n= [ -S² + 3 S + 54 ] / 2.\n\nTherefore,\n\na³ + b³ + c³ = 3 P + S * [ (-S² + 3 S + 54)/2 ].\n\nBut also, from the other expression:\n\na³ + b³ + c³ = (ab + bc + ca) + 6 S = [ (S² - S - 18)/2 ] + 6 S.\n\nTherefore, setting equal the two expressions for a³ + b³ + c³:\n\n3 P + S * [ (-S² + 3 S + 54)/2 ] = [ (S² - S - 18)/2 ] + 6 S.\n\nLet me multiply both sides by 2 to eliminate denominators:\n\n2*(3 P) + S*(-S² + 3 S + 54) = (S² - S - 18) + 12 S.\n\nSimplify:\n\n6 P - S³ + 3 S² + 54 S = S² - S - 18 + 12 S.\n\nCombine like terms on the right side:\n\nS² + ( -S + 12 S ) + (-18) = S² + 11 S - 18.\n\nTherefore, equation becomes:\n\n6 P - S³ + 3 S² + 54 S = S² + 11 S - 18.\n\nBring all terms to the left side:\n\n6 P - S³ + 3 S² + 54 S - S² - 11 S + 18 = 0.\n\nSimplify terms:\n\n- S³ + (3 S² - S²) + (54 S - 11 S) + 18 + 6 P = 0,\n\nso,\n\n- S³ + 2 S² + 43 S + 18 + 6 P = 0.\n\nTherefore,\n\n6 P = S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18.\n\nThus,\n\nP = (S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)/6. (Equation E)\n\nNow, recall Equation D:\n\nP² - P - 3 S² - 33 S - 162 = 0.\n\nNow, substitute P from Equation E into Equation D.\n\nSo, let me write Equation D again:\n\nP² - P - 3 S² - 33 S - 162 = 0.\n\nSubstituting P = (S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)/6 into this equation.\n\nFirst, compute P²:\n\nP² = [ (S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)/6 ]².\n\nThen, - P = - [ (S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)/6 ].\n\nThen, the rest of the terms: -3 S² - 33 S - 162.\n\nSo, putting it all together:\n\n[ (S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)^2 ] / 36 - [ (S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)/6 ] - 3 S² - 33 S - 162 = 0.\n\nThis looks really complicated, but perhaps we can multiply both sides by 36 to eliminate denominators:\n\n(S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18)^2 - 6(S³ - 2 S² - 43 S - 18) - 36*(3 S² + 33 S + 162) = 0.\n\nWait, actually, let me verify term by term:\n\nMultiplying each term by 36:\n\nFirst term: [ (S³ - ...)^2 ] /36 *36 = (S³ - ...)^2.\n\nSecond term: - [ (S³ - ...)/6 ] *36 = -6*(S³ - ...).\n\nThird term: -3 S² *36 = -108 S²,\n\nFourth term: -33 S *36 = -1188 S,\n\nFifth term: -162 *36 = -5832.\n\nWait, actually, the original equation after substitution was:\n\n[ P² ] - [ P ] - [3 S²] - [33 S] - [162] = 0,\n\nso multiplying by 36:\n\n36 P² - 36 P - 36*(3 S²) - 36*(33 S) - 36*(162) = 0,\n\nso,\n\n36 P² - 36 P - 108 S² - 1188 S - 5832 = 0.\n\nBut since P is expressed in terms of S, and P² is [ (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6 ]²,\n\nthen 36 P² = [ (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)^2 ].\n\nSimilarly, -36 P = -36*(S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6 = -6*(S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18).\n\nTherefore, substituting back:\n\n(S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)^2 -6*(S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18) -108 S² -1188 S -5832 =0.\n\nThis is a very high-degree equation in S, but perhaps it can be simplified by letting’s say, let me denote T = S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18,\n\nthen the equation becomes:\n\nT² -6 T -108 S² -1188 S -5832 =0.\n\nBut T itself is a cubic in S, so substituting that in would lead to a 6th-degree equation in S, which is still very high. Hmm, this might not be helpful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there is a better way. Since we already have that P = (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6 from Equation E, and from Equation D:\n\nP² - P - 3 S² - 33 S - 162 =0,\n\nso substituting P into that equation gives an equation solely in terms of S, albeit a high-degree one. Perhaps we can attempt to solve this equation numerically or look for rational roots?\n\nAlternatively, since the problem is asking for the two possible values of a + b + c, which is S, so perhaps S is a root of some quadratic equation? The problem states there are two possible values, so likely S satisfies a quadratic equation, so maybe after all that substitution, the equation reduces to a quadratic in S?\n\nWait, that might be possible if all the higher-degree terms cancel out. Let me see.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can assume that S is a real number, or perhaps even an integer, given the problem's context? Let me see.\n\nSuppose that S is an integer. Let me try small integer values for S and see if they satisfy the equation.\n\nFirst, let me recall that we already found roots for a when we were solving for a earlier. We had x = 3 and x = -2 as roots of the original eighth-degree equation. Since S = a + b + c, then if a = 3, then what would S be?\n\nWait, if a = 3, then from the first equation: a² = 9 = b + 6 ⇒ b = 3.\n\nBut then b = 3, so from the second equation: b² = 9 = c + 6 ⇒ c = 3.\n\nThen c = 3, so from the third equation: c² = 9 = a + 6 ⇒ a = 3.\n\nTherefore, in this case, a = b = c = 3. But the problem states that a, b, c must be pairwise distinct. Therefore, this solution is invalid, so S = 3 + 3 + 3 = 9 is not acceptable.\n\nSimilarly, when we had x = -2 as a root for a, let's see what happens.\n\nSuppose a = -2,\n\nthen from first equation: a² = 4 = b + 6 ⇒ b = 4 - 6 = -2,\n\nthen b = -2,\n\nthen from second equation: b² = 4 = c + 6 ⇒ c = 4 - 6 = -2,\n\nthen c = -2,\n\nthen from third equation: c² = 4 = a + 6 ⇒ a = 4 - 6 = -2,\n\nso again, a = b = c = -2, which again is invalid because they must be pairwise distinct. So S = -6 is also invalid.\n\nTherefore, the roots a = 3 and a = -2 correspond to the fixed points of the function f(x) = x² -6, since f(3) = 9 -6 = 3, and f(-2) = 4 -6 = -2. Therefore, these are fixed points, which we already know correspond to the case where all three variables are equal, which is not allowed here. Therefore, we need to find other roots of the original equation where a, b, c are distinct.\n\nTherefore, perhaps S is different from 9 and -6, and there are two other possible values for S. Given that the problem says \"compute the two possible values of a + b + c\", so likely two real or complex numbers, and they want them in boxed notation.\n\nAlternatively, maybe despite the high-degree equation, S satisfies a quadratic equation? Let me see.\n\nAlternatively, maybe let me consider that when we had the eighth-degree equation, we factored out (x - 3)(x + 2), so the remaining sixth-degree polynomial might factor into quadratics or cubics with roots that come in pairs such that their sums S contribute to the possible values.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps note that if a, b, c form a 3-cycle, then applying the function f three times brings you back to a, but applying it once or twice doesn't. Therefore, the sum S = a + b + c might have some symmetric properties.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider that the three variables a, b, c satisfy the same equation, so they are roots of some cubic equation, and then we can use Vieta's formula on that cubic.\n\nWait, let me think. Suppose that a, b, c are roots of a cubic equation x³ + p x² + q x + r = 0. Then, we can relate the coefficients p, q, r to the sums S = a + b + c, ab + bc + ca, and abc.\n\nIndeed, if a, b, c are roots of x³ - S x² + (ab + bc + ca) x - abc = 0.\n\nSo, if we can find such a cubic equation that a, b, c satisfy, then we can find S from the coefficients.\n\nBut how do we know that a, b, c are roots of a cubic? Well, since they are linked cyclically via the equations a² = b + 6, etc., perhaps we can derive such a cubic equation.\n\nLet me try that.\n\nLet me suppose that a, b, c are roots of the cubic equation x³ + p x² + q x + r = 0.\n\nThen, we have:\n\na + b + c = -p,\n\nab + bc + ca = q,\n\nabc = -r.\n\nOur goal is to find S = a + b + c = -p, so if we can find p, then we can get S.\n\nFrom earlier, we have expressions for ab + bc + ca and abc in terms of S.\n\nFrom Equation B: ab + bc + ca = (S² - S - 18)/2,\n\nso q = (S² - S - 18)/2,\n\nand from Equation E: abc = P = (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6,\n\nso -r = (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6 ⇒ r = - (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6.\n\nNow, if a, b, c are roots of the cubic x³ - S x² + [(S² - S - 18)/2] x - [ (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6 ] = 0.\n\nBut since a, b, c also satisfy the original equations, perhaps we can find a relation that this cubic must satisfy?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps each of a, b, c satisfies the equation x² - y - 6 = 0 where y is the next variable, but since they are cyclic, maybe we can derive a polynomial equation that each of a, b, c satisfies?\n\nIndeed, starting from the original equations:\n\na² = b + 6,\n\nb² = c + 6,\n\nc² = a + 6.\n\nLet me try to eliminate b and c to get an equation in terms of a only.\n\nFrom the first equation: b = a² - 6.\n\nFrom the second equation: c = b² - 6 = (a² - 6)^2 - 6.\n\nFrom the third equation: a = c² - 6 = [ (a² - 6)^2 - 6 ]² - 6.\n\nWhich brings us back to the eighth-degree equation we had earlier: a = [ (a² - 6)^2 - 6 ]² - 6,\n\nso,\n\n[ (a² - 6)^2 - 6 ]² - 6 - a = 0,\n\nwhich is the same as P(x) = 0, the eighth-degree polynomial.\n\nTherefore, each of a, b, c are roots of this eighth-degree equation. However, since a, b, c are part of a 3-cycle, they are three distinct roots of this equation which are part of a 3-cycle, and the other roots are either fixed points (which we already saw are 3 and -2), or 2-cycles, or other cycles.\n\nGiven that, perhaps the cubic equation whose roots are a, b, c can be factored from the eighth-degree polynomial?\n\nIndeed, the eighth-degree polynomial P(x) factors as (x - 3)(x + 2)(sixth-degree poly). If the three roots a, b, c are roots of a cubic factor of the sixth-degree polynomial, then we can perhaps find that cubic.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the cubic equation I mentioned earlier, with coefficients in terms of S, is a factor of the sixth-degree polynomial?\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can consider that if a, b, c are roots of a cubic, then the cubic must divide the eighth-degree polynomial P(x). Since P(x) has factors (x - 3) and (x + 2), and the rest is the sixth-degree polynomial. So perhaps the cubic we are seeking is a factor of that sixth-degree polynomial.\n\nLet me denote the sixth-degree polynomial as R(x) = x^6 + x^5 -17x^4 -11x^3 +91x^2 +25x -149.\n\nSuppose that the cubic equation with roots a, b, c is a factor of R(x). Let me suppose that R(x) factors as (x³ + p x² + q x + r)(x³ + s x² + t x + u).\n\nThen, multiplying out:\n\n(x³ + p x² + q x + r)(x³ + s x² + t x + u) =\n\nx^6 + (p + s)x^5 + (ps + q + t)x^4 + (pt + qs + r + u)x^3 + (pu + qt + rs)x^2 + (qu + rt)x + ru.\n\nThis should equal R(x) = x^6 + x^5 -17x^4 -11x^3 +91x^2 +25x -149.\n\nTherefore, we can set up equations by equating coefficients:\n\n1. Coefficient of x^6: 1 = 1 (okay).\n\n2. Coefficient of x^5: p + s = 1.\n\n3. Coefficient of x^4: p s + q + t = -17.\n\n4. Coefficient of x^3: p t + q s + r + u = -11.\n\n5. Coefficient of x^2: p u + q t + r s = 91.\n\n6. Coefficient of x: q u + r t = 25.\n\n7. Constant term: r u = -149.\n\nHmm, this is a system of equations with variables p, q, r, s, t, u. That's six variables, but seven equations (including the first one which is trivial). Hmm, but maybe we can make some assumptions or find integer solutions?\n\nFirst, note that the constant term is r u = -149. Since 149 is a prime number (I believe 149 is prime; yes, 149 is a prime number), so the factors of -149 are limited. So possible integer pairs (r, u) are:\n\n(1, -149), (-1, 149), (149, -1), (-149, 1).\n\nLet me try these possibilities.\n\nFirst possibility: Let’s try r = 1, u = -149.\n\nThen, from equation 6: q u + r t = q*(-149) + 1*t = 25.\n\nSo, -149 q + t = 25 ⇒ t = 149 q + 25.\n\nFrom equation 7: already satisfied.\n\nNow, equation 5: p u + q t + r s = p*(-149) + q*t + 1*s = 91.\n\nBut t = 149 q + 25, so substituting:\n\n-149 p + q*(149 q + 25) + s = 91.\n\nEquation 4: p t + q s + r + u = p*(149 q +25) + q s + 1 + (-149) = p*(149 q +25) + q s - 148 = -11.\n\nEquation 3: p s + q + t = p s + q + (149 q +25) = p s + 150 q +25 = -17 ⇒ p s + 150 q = -42.\n\nEquation 2: p + s = 1 ⇒ s = 1 - p.\n\nThis is getting complicated, but let's try to substitute s = 1 - p into equation 3:\n\np*(1 - p) + 150 q = -42,\n\nso,\n\np - p² + 150 q = -42 ⇒ -p² + p + 150 q = -42 ⇒ p² - p -150 q = 42. (Equation A)\n\nFrom equation 4: p*(149 q +25) + q*(1 - p) -148 = -11,\n\nLet me expand:\n\np*(149 q) + p*25 + q - p q -148 = -11,\n\nGroup terms:\n\np*149 q - p q + p*25 + q -148 = -11,\n\nFactor p q terms:\n\np q (149 - 1) + 25 p + q -148 = -11,\n\nSo,\n\np q * 148 + 25 p + q -148 = -11,\n\nThus,\n\n148 p q +25 p + q = 137. (Equation B)\n\nFrom equation 5: -149 p + q*(149 q +25) + (1 - p) = 91,\n\nLet me expand:\n\n-149 p + 149 q² +25 q +1 - p = 91,\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n-150 p +149 q² +25 q +1 =91,\n\nThus,\n\n-150 p +149 q² +25 q = 90. (Equation C)\n\nNow, we have three equations: Equation A, B, and C.\n\nEquation A: p² - p -150 q =42,\n\nEquation B: 148 p q +25 p + q =137,\n\nEquation C: -150 p +149 q² +25 q =90.\n\nThis is a system of three equations with variables p and q (since s =1 - p and t=149 q +25, etc.). This is still complicated, but maybe we can guess integer values for q?\n\nGiven that q is likely to be small integer because of the coefficients.\n\nLet me suppose q is an integer. Let me see if I can find integer solutions.\n\nLet me try small integer values for q.\n\nStart with q=0:\n\nIf q=0,\n\nEquation A: p² - p =42 ⇒ p² - p -42=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 + 168=169 ⇒ p=(1±13)/2 ⇒ p=7 or p=-6.\n\nEquation B: 0 +25 p +0 =137 ⇒25 p =137 ⇒ p=137/25 not integer. So reject.\n\nq=1:\n\nEquation A: p² -p -150 =42 ⇒ p² -p -192=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 + 768=769, which is not a perfect square. No integer solution.\n\nq= -1:\n\nEquation A: p² - p -150*(-1)= p² -p +150 =42 ⇒ p² -p +108=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 - 432 negative. No real solution.\n\nq=2:\n\nEquation A: p² -p -300 =42 ⇒ p² - p -342=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 + 1368=1369=37² ⇒ p=(1 ±37)/2 ⇒ p=19 or p= -18.\n\nCheck Equation B with q=2 and p=19:\n\nEquation B: 148*19*2 +25*19 +2 = ?\n\n148*38 = let's see, 150*38=5700, minus 2*38=76 ⇒5700 -76=5624,\n\n25*19=475,\n\nso total: 5624 +475 +2=5624 +477=6101 ≠137. Not good.\n\nWith p=-18:\n\nEquation B: 148*(-18)*2 +25*(-18) +2 = 148*(-36) + (-450) +2 = -5328 -450 +2 = -5776 ≠137.\n\nq= -2:\n\nEquation A: p² - p -150*(-2)= p² - p +300=42 ⇒p² -p +258=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 - 1032 negative.\n\nq=3:\n\nEquation A: p² - p -450=42 ⇒ p² -p -492=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 + 1968=1969, not a square.\n\nq=4:\n\nEquation A: p² -p -600=42 ⇒ p² -p -642=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 + 2568=2569. Hmm, 2569 is 50.68 squared? 50²=2500, 51²=2601, so no.\n\nq=5:\n\nEquation A: p² -p -750=42 ⇒ p² -p -792=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 + 3168=3169. Hmm, sqrt(3169)=56.3, not integer.\n\nq= -3:\n\nEquation A: p² -p -150*(-3)= p² -p +450=42 ⇒ p² -p +408=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 - 1632 negative.\n\nq=6:\n\nEquation A: p² - p -900=42 ⇒ p² - p -942=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 + 3768=3769. Not square.\n\nHmm, this isn't working. Let me try q= -4:\n\nEquation A: p² - p -150*(-4)= p² - p +600=42 ⇒ p² - p +558=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 - 2232 negative.\n\nq= 7:\n\nEquation A: p² - p -1050=42 ⇒ p² - p -1092=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 + 4368=4369. Hmm, 66²=4356, 67²=4489, so no.\n\nq= -5:\n\nEquation A: p² - p -150*(-5)= p² -p +750=42 ⇒ p² -p +708=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 - 2832 negative.\n\nHmm, maybe q= something else?\n\nAlternatively, try q= -1/ something? But we are assuming integers.\n\nAlternatively, maybe my assumption that r and u are integers is wrong? Since 149 is prime, but perhaps r and u are fractions? This complicates things.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps try another pair for r and u.\n\nNext possibility: r = -1, u = 149.\n\nThen, equation 6: q u + r t = q*149 + (-1)*t =25 ⇒149 q - t =25 ⇒ t =149 q -25.\n\nEquation 7: r u = (-1)(149) = -149, which matches.\n\nEquation 5: p u + q t + r s = p*149 + q*(149 q -25) + (-1)*s = 91.\n\nEquation 4: p t + q s + r + u = p*(149 q -25) + q s + (-1) +149 = p*(149 q -25) + q s + 148 = -11 ⇒ p*(149 q -25) + q s = -159.\n\nEquation 3: p s + q + t = p s + q + (149 q -25) = p s + 150 q -25 = -17 ⇒ p s + 150 q = 8.\n\nEquation 2: p + s =1 ⇒ s =1 - p.\n\nEquation 1: already done.\n\nThis is again complicated, but let me see if with s =1 - p,\n\nEquation 3: p*(1 - p) + 150 q =8 ⇒ p - p² +150 q =8 ⇒ -p² +p +150 q =8 ⇒ p² -p -150 q =-8. (Equation A')\n\nEquation 4: p*(149 q -25) + q*(1 - p) = -159 ⇒ p*(149 q -25) + q - p q = -159 ⇒ p*(149 q -25 - q) + q = -159 ⇒ p*(148 q -25) + q = -159. (Equation B')\n\nEquation 5: p*149 + q*(149 q -25) - s = 91,\n\nbut s =1 - p,\n\nso,\n\n149 p +149 q² -25 q - (1 - p) =91 ⇒149 p +149 q² -25 q -1 +p =91 ⇒150 p +149 q² -25 q =92. (Equation C')\n\nNow, Equations A', B', and C' with variables p and q.\n\nAgain, let's try small integer q.\n\nStart with q=0:\n\nEquation A': p² -p = -8 ⇒ p² -p +8=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 -32 negative.\n\nq=1:\n\nEquation A': p² -p -150 = -8 ⇒ p² -p -142=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 + 568=569, not square.\n\nq= -1:\n\nEquation A': p² -p +150 = -8 ⇒ p² -p +158=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 -632 negative.\n\nq=2:\n\nEquation A': p² -p -300 = -8 ⇒ p² -p -292=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 + 1168=1169. Not square.\n\nq= -2:\n\nEquation A': p² -p +300 = -8 ⇒ p² -p +308=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 -1232 negative.\n\nq=3:\n\nEquation A': p² -p -450 = -8 ⇒ p² -p -442=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 + 1768=1769. Not square.\n\nq=4:\n\nEquation A': p² -p -600 = -8 ⇒ p² -p -592=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 + 2368=2369. Not square.\n\nq=5:\n\nEquation A': p² -p -750 = -8 ⇒ p² -p -742=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 + 2968=2969. Not square.\n\nq= -3:\n\nEquation A': p² -p +450 = -8 ⇒ p² -p +458=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 - 1832 negative.\n\nq=6:\n\nEquation A': p² -p -900 = -8 ⇒ p² -p -892=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 + 3568=3569. Hmm, sqrt(3569) is about 59.74, not integer.\n\nq=7:\n\nEquation A': p² -p -1050 = -8 ⇒ p² -p -1042=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 + 4168=4169. Not square.\n\nq= -4:\n\nEquation A': p² -p +600 = -8 ⇒ p² -p +608=0 ⇒ discriminant 1 - 2432 negative.\n\nHmm, not promising. Let's try the next possible pair for r and u.\n\nThird possibility: r = 149, u = -1.\n\nThen equation 7: r u =149*(-1) = -149, which matches.\n\nEquation 6: q u + r t = q*(-1) +149*t =25 ⇒ -q +149 t =25 ⇒149 t = q +25 ⇒ t = (q +25)/149.\n\nHmm, since we want integer coefficients, t must be rational, but unless q ≡ -25 mod 149, which might not be helpful.\n\nEquation 5: p u + q t + r s = p*(-1) + q t +149 s = -p + q t +149 s =91.\n\nEquation 4: p t + q s + r + u = p t + q s +149 + (-1) = p t + q s +148 = -11 ⇒ p t + q s = -159.\n\nEquation 3: p s + q + t = p s + q + t = -17.\n\nEquation 2: p + s =1 ⇒ s =1 - p.\n\nEquation 1: done.\n\nThis is getting too convoluted. Perhaps this trial and error is not the best approach.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the cubic equation that a, b, c satisfy has coefficients related to S in a way that allows us to form an equation for S.\n\nRecall that we had expressions for ab + bc + ca and abc in terms of S:\n\nab + bc + ca = (S² - S - 18)/2,\n\nabc = (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6.\n\nNow, if a, b, c are roots of the cubic equation x³ - S x² + [(S² - S -18)/2]x - [(S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6] = 0,\n\nthen each of a, b, c must satisfy this equation. Additionally, each of a, b, c satisfies the original equation, such as a² = b +6.\n\nPerhaps we can combine these two pieces of information.\n\nLet me take the cubic equation for a:\n\na³ - S a² + [(S² - S -18)/2]a - [(S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6] = 0. (Equation F)\n\nBut we also know from the original equation that a² = b +6,\n\nand from the cyclic relations, b = a² -6,\n\nand c = b² -6,\n\nand a = c² -6.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps express b in terms of a and substitute into the cubic equation?\n\nAlternatively, since b = a² -6,\n\nthen we can write the cubic equation in terms of a and S, but since S = a + b + c,\n\nand c = (b)^2 -6 = (a² -6)^2 -6,\n\nso S = a + (a² -6) + [(a² -6)^2 -6].\n\nTherefore, S is expressed in terms of a, so S is a function of a. Therefore, perhaps we can write Equation F in terms of S and a, but this might not help directly.\n\nAlternatively, note that in Equation F, we can replace S with the expression in terms of a, but this would result in a very complicated equation.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider that if a is a root of both the original eighth-degree equation and the cubic equation, then it must satisfy both. Therefore, the cubic equation must divide the eighth-degree polynomial.\n\nGiven that the eighth-degree polynomial factors as (x - 3)(x + 2) R(x), where R(x) is the sixth-degree polynomial, then if the cubic we are seeking divides R(x), then R(x) must be divisible by the cubic.\n\nTherefore, perhaps we can perform polynomial division of R(x) by the cubic equation we are trying to find, but without knowing the cubic, it's difficult.\n\nAlternatively, since the problem states there are two possible values of S = a + b + c, and given that the eighth-degree equation has roots at 3, -2, and the roots of R(x), and since the valid solutions must come from the roots of R(x) that form 3-cycles (i.e., distinct a, b, c not fixed points), then perhaps S takes on two distinct values corresponding to these two 3-cycles.\n\nGiven that the problem is from a competition, perhaps the two possible values of S are integers or simple fractions, and maybe we can find them by assuming S is a solution to a quadratic equation derived from the earlier equations.\n\nRecall that we had Equation D:\n\nP² - P - 3 S² - 33 S - 162 = 0,\n\nand Equation E:\n\nP = (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6.\n\nLet me substitute Equation E into Equation D.\n\nSo,\n\n[(S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6]^2 - [(S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6] - 3 S² - 33 S - 162 = 0.\n\nThis is an equation in terms of S only. Let me denote Q(S) = [(S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6]^2 - [(S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6] - 3 S² - 33 S - 162.\n\nWe need to solve Q(S) = 0.\n\nThis equation is of degree 6, since the first term is a square of a cubic, so degree 6, minus a cubic term, etc. So it's a sixth-degree equation, but maybe it factors?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there are some obvious roots?\n\nLet me try plugging in S = 6.\n\nCompute Q(6):\n\nFirst compute P = (6³ - 2*6² -43*6 -18)/6 = (216 - 72 -258 -18)/6 = (216 - 72 = 144; 144 -258 = -114; -114 -18 = -132)/6 = -132/6 = -22.\n\nThen,\n\nP² = (-22)^2 = 484,\n\n-P = 22,\n\n-3 S² = -3*36 = -108,\n\n-33 S = -198,\n\n-162.\n\nSo total:\n\n484 + 22 -108 -198 -162.\n\nCompute step by step:\n\n484 +22 = 506,\n\n506 -108 = 398,\n\n398 -198 = 200,\n\n200 -162 = 38 ≠ 0.\n\nNot zero.\n\nTry S = 0:\n\nP = (0 -0 -0 -18)/6 = -18/6 = -3,\n\nP² = 9,\n\n-P = 3,\n\n-3*0 =0,\n\n-33*0 =0,\n\n-162.\n\nTotal: 9 + 3 +0 +0 -162 = -150 ≠0.\n\nS = 1:\n\nP = (1 - 2 -43 -18)/6 = (1 -2= -1; -1 -43= -44; -44 -18= -62)/6 ≈ -10.333...\n\nNot integer, but let's compute Q(1):\n\nP = (1 -2 -43 -18)/6 = (-62)/6 = -31/3,\n\nP² = (961)/9,\n\n-P = 31/3,\n\n-3(1)^2 = -3,\n\n-33(1) = -33,\n\n-162.\n\nThus,\n\nQ(1) = 961/9 + 31/3 -3 -33 -162.\n\nConvert all to ninths:\n\n961/9 + (31/3)*(3/3) = 961/9 + 93/9,\n\n-3 = -27/9,\n\n-33 = -297/9,\n\n-162 = -1458/9,\n\nTotal:\n\n[961 + 93 -27 -297 -1458]/9 =\n\nCompute numerator:\n\n961 +93 = 1054,\n\n1054 -27 = 1027,\n\n1027 -297 = 730,\n\n730 -1458 = -728,\n\nSo Q(1) = -728/9 ≈ -80.888… ≠0.\n\nS = -3:\n\nP = [ (-27) - 2*(9) -43*(-3) -18 ] /6 = [ -27 -18 +129 -18 ] /6 = (-27 -18 = -45; -45 +129=84; 84 -18=66)/6 = 11.\n\nThen,\n\nP² = 121,\n\n-P = -11,\n\n-3*(-3)^2 = -27,\n\n-33*(-3) = +99,\n\n-162.\n\nSo total:\n\n121 -11 -27 +99 -162.\n\nCompute step by step:\n\n121 -11 = 110,\n\n110 -27 = 83,\n\n83 +99 = 182,\n\n182 -162 = 20 ≠0.\n\nS = -4:\n\nP = [ (-64) - 2*(16) -43*(-4) -18 ] /6 = [ -64 -32 +172 -18 ] /6 = (-64-32= -96; -96 +172=76; 76-18=58)/6 = 29/3 ≈9.666...\n\nQ(-4) = (29/3)^2 - (29/3) - 3*(16) -33*(-4) -162.\n\nCompute:\n\n(841/9) - (29/3) -48 + 132 -162.\n\nConvert to ninths:\n\n841/9 - 87/9 - (48*9)/9 + (132*9)/9 - (162*9)/9.\n\nWait, alternatively:\n\nFirst term: 841/9,\n\nSecond term: -29/3 = -87/9,\n\nThird term: -3*(16) = -48,\n\nFourth term: -33*(-4)= +132,\n\nFifth term: -162.\n\nSo,\n\n841/9 -87/9 = (841 -87)/9 = 754/9 ≈ 83.777...,\n\nThen, -48 +132 = 84,\n\n84 -162 = -78,\n\nThus total: 754/9 -78 ≈ 83.777 -78 ≈5.777 ≠0.\n\nHmm.\n\nS = 6 didn't work, S = -3 gave 20, S = -4 gave ~5.777.\n\nTry S = 4:\n\nP = (64 - 32 - 172 -18)/6 = (64-32=32; 32-172= -140; -140-18= -158)/6 = -158/6 = -79/3 ≈-26.333...\n\nThen,\n\nQ(4) = (-79/3)^2 - (-79/3) - 3*(16) -33*(4) -162.\n\nCompute:\n\n(6241/9) + 79/3 -48 -132 -162.\n\nConvert to ninths:\n\n6241/9 + 237/9 - (48*9)/9 - (132*9)/9 - (162*9)/9,\n\nWait, alternatively:\n\n6241/9 + 79/3 = 6241/9 + 237/9 = 6478/9,\n\nThen subtract 48 +132 +162 = 342,\n\nSo total is 6478/9 - 342,\n\nConvert 342 to ninths: 342 = 3078/9,\n\nThus,\n\n6478/9 - 3078/9 = 3400/9 ≈377.777… ≠0.\n\nHmm. Maybe S = 7?\n\nCompute P = (343 - 2*49 -43*7 -18)/6 = (343 -98 -301 -18)/6 = (343-98=245; 245-301= -56; -56-18= -74)/6 = -74/6 = -37/3 ≈-12.333...\n\nThen,\n\nQ(7) = (-37/3)^2 - (-37/3) - 3*(49) -33*7 -162.\n\nCompute:\n\n(1369/9) + 37/3 -147 -231 -162.\n\nConvert to ninths:\n\n1369/9 + 111/9 = 1480/9,\n\nThen subtract 147 +231 +162 = 540,\n\nSo total: 1480/9 -540 = 1480/9 - 4860/9 = -3380/9 ≈ -375.555… ≠0.\n\nHmm. This is not working.\n\nAlternatively, maybe S is a root of a quadratic equation derived from the original system?\n\nLet me think differently. Suppose that S = a + b + c, and let me consider the three equations:\n\na² = b +6,\n\nb² = c +6,\n\nc² = a +6.\n\nLet me try to express everything in terms of S.\n\nFrom the first equation: b = a² -6,\n\nFrom the second: c = b² -6 = (a² -6)^2 -6,\n\nFrom the third: a = c² -6 = [(a² -6)^2 -6]^2 -6.\n\nThus, as before, we get the eighth-degree equation. But perhaps if we consider that a, b, c are part of a 3-cycle, then applying the function f three times brings us back to a, so f³(a) = a, but f(a) ≠ a, f²(a) ≠ a.\n\nTherefore, the solutions for a are the roots of f³(x) -x =0 excluding the roots of f(x)-x=0 and f²(x)-x=0.\n\nWe already factored f³(x) -x as (x -3)(x +2) R(x), where R(x) is sixth degree.\n\nThe roots of f(x) -x =0 are x=3 and x=-2, as we saw.\n\nThe roots of f²(x) -x =0 would be the roots of f(f(x)) -x=0, which includes the fixed points and the 2-cycles.\n\nLet me compute f²(x) -x =0:\n\nf(f(x)) -x = [f(x)² -6] -x = (x² -6)^2 -6 -x = x⁴ -12x² +30 -x =x⁴ -12x² -x +30=0.\n\nTherefore, the equation f²(x) -x =0 is x⁴ -12x² -x +30 =0.\n\nThus, the roots of this quartic equation include the fixed points (x=3 and x=-2) and the 2-cycles.\n\nTherefore, to find the 3-cycles, we need the roots of f³(x) -x =0 that are not roots of f(x) -x=0 or f²(x) -x=0.\n\nTherefore, the sixth-degree polynomial R(x) = f³(x) -x divided by (x -3)(x +2) must have roots that are either 3-cycles or other cycles.\n\nTherefore, the roots of R(x) are either 3-cycles, 2-cycles, or other cycles of period dividing 3. Since we are looking for 3-cycles, which are primitive cycles of period 3, so their roots should not be roots of f(x)-x=0 or f²(x)-x=0.\n\nTherefore, the sixth-degree polynomial R(x) should factor into a product of a quadratic (for the two 3-cycles) and a quartic (for the 2-cycles and maybe fixed points?), but we already removed the fixed points.\n\nWait, but we divided out (x-3)(x+2), so R(x) is sixth-degree. The equation f²(x) -x=0 is a quartic, so it has four roots (counting multiplicity), two of which are the fixed points, so the other two are the 2-cycles. Therefore, the sixth-degree polynomial R(x) should factor into the product of two quadratics (each corresponding to a 3-cycle) and the quartic from the 2-cycles? Wait, no, since we already divided out the fixed points.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps R(x) factors into two cubics, each corresponding to a 3-cycle? If so, then each cubic would have three roots forming a 3-cycle, and the sum S would be the sum for each cubic, giving two possible values of S.\n\nTherefore, if R(x) factors into two cubics, each with roots a, b, c forming a 3-cycle, then the sum S for each cubic would be the sum of the roots, which by Vieta's formula would be - coefficient of x² divided by coefficient of x³.\n\nSince R(x) is monic (leading coefficient 1), then the sum S for each cubic factor would be the negative of the coefficient of x² in that cubic.\n\nTherefore, if we can factor R(x) into two cubics, then the two possible values of S would be the negatives of the coefficients of x² in each cubic.\n\nTherefore, perhaps we can attempt to factor R(x) into two cubics.\n\nGiven that R(x) = x^6 + x^5 -17x^4 -11x^3 +91x^2 +25x -149.\n\nSuppose it factors as (x^3 + a x^2 + b x + c)(x^3 + d x^2 + e x + f).\n\nMultiplying out:\n\nx^6 + (a + d)x^5 + (b + e + a d)x^4 + (c + f + a e + b d)x^3 + (a f + b e + c d)x^2 + (b f + c e)x + c f.\n\nSet equal to R(x):\n\nx^6 + x^5 -17x^4 -11x^3 +91x^2 +25x -149.\n\nThus, equate coefficients:\n\n1. Coefficient of x^6: 1 = 1 (okay).\n\n2. Coefficient of x^5: a + d = 1. (Equation 1)\n\n3. Coefficient of x^4: b + e + a d = -17. (Equation 2)\n\n4. Coefficient of x^3: c + f + a e + b d = -11. (Equation 3)\n\n5. Coefficient of x^2: a f + b e + c d = 91. (Equation 4)\n\n6. Coefficient of x: b f + c e = 25. (Equation 5)\n\n7. Constant term: c f = -149. (Equation 6)\n\nAgain, similar to before, but now with two cubics. Let's see if we can find integer solutions.\n\nFirst, from Equation 6: c f = -149. Since 149 is prime, the possible integer pairs (c, f) are:\n\n(1, -149), (-1, 149), (149, -1), (-149, 1).\n\nLet me try each possibility.\n\nFirst possibility: c = 1, f = -149.\n\nThen Equation 5: b*(-149) + 1*e = 25 ⇒ -149 b + e =25 ⇒ e =149 b +25.\n\nEquation 4: a*(-149) + b*e +1*d =91.\n\nBut e =149 b +25,\n\nso,\n\n-149 a + b*(149 b +25) + d =91.\n\nEquation 3:1 + (-149) + a e + b d = -11 ⇒ (1 -149) + a*(149 b +25) + b d = -11 ⇒ -148 + a*(149 b +25) + b d = -11 ⇒ a*(149 b +25) + b d = 137. (Equation 3a)\n\nEquation 2: b + e + a d = -17 ⇒ b + (149 b +25) + a d = -17 ⇒150 b +25 + a d = -17 ⇒ a d = -150 b -42. (Equation 2a)\n\nEquation 1: a + d =1 ⇒ d =1 - a. (Equation 1a)\n\nNow, substitute d =1 -a into Equation 2a:\n\na*(1 -a) = -150 b -42 ⇒ a -a² = -150 b -42 ⇒ a² -a -150 b =42. (Equation A)\n\nFrom Equation 3a: a*(149 b +25) + b*(1 - a) =137.\n\nExpand:\n\n149 a b +25 a + b -a b =137 ⇒ (149 a b -a b) +25 a +b =137 ⇒148 a b +25 a +b =137. (Equation B)\n\nFrom Equation A: a² -a -150 b =42 ⇒ a² -a =150 b +42 ⇒ b = (a² -a -42)/150. (Equation C)\n\nSubstitute b from Equation C into Equation B:\n\n148 a * [ (a² -a -42)/150 ] +25 a + [ (a² -a -42)/150 ] =137.\n\nThis is quite complex, but let's multiply through by 150 to eliminate denominators:\n\n148 a (a² -a -42) + 25 a *150 + (a² -a -42) =137*150.\n\nCompute each term:\n\nFirst term: 148a(a² -a -42),\n\nSecond term: 25a *150 = 3750 a,\n\nThird term: (a² -a -42),\n\nFourth term: 137*150 = 20550.\n\nSo,\n\n148a(a² -a -42) + 3750a + a² -a -42 =20550.\n\nExpand the first term:\n\n148a^3 -148a² -148*42 a +3750a +a² -a -42 =20550.\n\nCompute constants:\n\n148*42 = 6216,\n\nSo,\n\n148a^3 -148a² -6216a +3750a +a² -a -42 =20550,\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n148a^3,\n\n(-148a² +a²) = -147a²,\n\n(-6216a +3750a -a) = (-6216 +3750 -1)a = (-2467)a,\n\n-42,\n\nThus,\n\n148a^3 -147a² -2467a -42 =20550,\n\nBring all terms to left:\n\n148a^3 -147a² -2467a -42 -20550 =0 ⇒\n\n148a^3 -147a² -2467a -20592 =0.\n\nThis is a cubic in a with large coefficients, which is unlikely to have integer solutions. Therefore, this case likely does not yield integer solutions.\n\nSecond possibility: c = -1, f =149.\n\nThen Equation 6 is satisfied: (-1)(149) = -149.\n\nEquation 5: b*149 + (-1)*e =25 ⇒149 b - e =25 ⇒ e =149 b -25.\n\nEquation 4: a*149 + b*e + (-1)*d =91 ⇒149a + b*e -d =91.\n\nBut e =149 b -25,\n\nso,\n\n149a + b*(149 b -25) -d =91 ⇒149a +149 b² -25b -d =91. (Equation 4a)\n\nEquation 3: -1 +149 + a e + b d = -11 ⇒148 + a*(149 b -25) +b d = -11 ⇒ a*(149 b -25) +b d = -159. (Equation 3b)\n\nEquation 2: b + e + a d = -17 ⇒ b + (149 b -25) +a d = -17 ⇒150 b -25 +a d = -17 ⇒a d = -150 b +8. (Equation 2b)\n\nEquation 1: a + d =1 ⇒d =1 -a. (Equation 1b)\n\nSubstitute d =1 -a into Equation 2b:\n\na*(1 -a) = -150 b +8 ⇒a -a² = -150 b +8 ⇒a² -a +150 b = -8. (Equation A')\n\nFrom Equation 3b:\n\na*(149 b -25) +b*(1 -a) =-159 ⇒149 a b -25a +b -a b =-159 ⇒(149ab -ab) + (-25a) +b =-159 ⇒148ab -25a +b =-159. (Equation B')\n\nFrom Equation A': a² -a +150 b = -8 ⇒150 b = -a² +a -8 ⇒b = (-a² +a -8)/150. (Equation C')\n\nSubstitute b from Equation C' into Equation B':\n\n148a*[ (-a² +a -8)/150 ] -25a + [ (-a² +a -8)/150 ] = -159.\n\nMultiply through by 150 to eliminate denominators:\n\n148a*(-a² +a -8) -25a*150 + (-a² +a -8) = -159*150.\n\nCompute each term:\n\nFirst term: 148a*(-a² +a -8) = -148a³ +148a² -1184a,\n\nSecond term: -25a*150 = -3750a,\n\nThird term: (-a² +a -8),\n\nFourth term: -159*150 = -23,850.\n\nCombine all terms:\n\n-148a³ +148a² -1184a -3750a -a² +a -8 -23850 =0.\n\nSimplify:\n\n-148a³,\n\n+148a² -a² = +147a²,\n\n-1184a -3750a +a = (-1184 -3750 +1)a = (-4933)a,\n\n-8 -23850 = -23858.\n\nThus,\n\n-148a³ +147a² -4933a -23858 =0.\n\nThis is another cubic with large coefficients, likely no integer solutions.\n\nThird possibility: c =149, f =-1.\n\nEquation 6: 149*(-1) = -149, good.\n\nEquation 5: b*(-1) +149*e =25 ⇒ -b +149 e =25 ⇒149 e =b +25 ⇒e = (b +25)/149.\n\nEquation 4: a*(-1) +b*e +149*d =91 ⇒-a +b e +149 d =91.\n\nBut e = (b +25)/149,\n\nso,\n\n-a + b*(b +25)/149 +149 d =91. (Equation 4a)\n\nEquation 3:149 + (-1) + a e +b d = -11 ⇒148 + a e +b d = -11 ⇒a e +b d = -159. (Equation 3a)\n\nEquation 2: b + e + a d =-17 ⇒b + (b +25)/149 +a d =-17. (Equation 2a)\n\nEquation 1: a + d =1 ⇒d =1 -a. (Equation 1a)\n\nThis seems too fractional and messy to solve for integers.\n\nFourth possibility: c = -149, f =1.\n\nEquation 6: (-149)(1) = -149, good.\n\nEquation 5: b*1 + (-149)*e =25 ⇒b -149 e =25 ⇒b =149 e +25.\n\nEquation 4: a*1 +b*e + (-149)*d =91 ⇒a +b e -149 d =91.\n\nBut b =149 e +25,\n\nso,\n\na + (149 e +25)*e -149 d =91 ⇒a +149 e² +25 e -149 d =91. (Equation 4b)\n\nEquation 3: -149 +1 + a e +b d = -11 ⇒-148 +a e +b d = -11 ⇒a e +b d =137. (Equation 3b)\n\nEquation 2: b + e + a d =-17 ⇒ (149 e +25) + e +a d =-17 ⇒150 e +25 +a d =-17 ⇒a d = -150 e -42. (Equation 2b)\n\nEquation 1: a + d =1 ⇒d =1 -a. (Equation 1b)\n\nSubstitute d =1 -a into Equation 2b:\n\na*(1 -a) = -150 e -42 ⇒a -a² = -150 e -42 ⇒a² -a -150 e =42. (Equation A'')\n\nFrom Equation 3b:\n\na e +b d = a e + (149 e +25)*(1 -a) = a e +149 e (1 -a) +25(1 -a) = a e +149 e -149 a e +25 -25a.\n\nSet equal to 137:\n\na e +149 e -149 a e +25 -25a =137 ⇒\n\ne*(a +149 -149 a) + (-25a) +25 =137 ⇒\n\ne*(150 -148 a) -25a =112 ⇒\n\ne*(150 -148 a) =25a +112 ⇒\n\ne = (25a +112)/(150 -148a).\n\nFrom Equation A'': a² -a -150 e =42 ⇒150 e =a² -a -42 ⇒e=(a² -a -42)/150.\n\nSet equal the two expressions for e:\n\n(25a +112)/(150 -148a) = (a² -a -42)/150.\n\nCross-multiplying:\n\n150*(25a +112) = (a² -a -42)*(150 -148a).\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in a? Let me see.\n\nLeft side: 150*(25a +112) =3750 a + 16,800.\n\nRight side: (a² -a -42)*(150 -148a).\n\nExpand the right side:\n\nFirst, expand (a² -a -42)(150 -148a):\n\nMultiply term by term:\n\na²*150 = 150a²,\n\na²*(-148a) = -148a³,\n\n(-a)*150 = -150a,\n\n(-a)*(-148a) =148a²,\n\n(-42)*150 = -6,300,\n\n(-42)*(-148a) =6,216 a.\n\nSo altogether:\n\n-148a³ +150a² +148a² -150a +6,216a -6,300.\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n-148a³,\n\n(150a² +148a²)=298a²,\n\n(-150a +6,216a)=6,066a,\n\n-6,300.\n\nThus, right side is:\n\n-148a³ +298a² +6,066a -6,300.\n\nSet equal to left side:\n\n3750a +16,800 = -148a³ +298a² +6,066a -6,300.\n\nBring all terms to left side:\n\n148a³ -298a² + (3750a -6066a) + (16,800 +6,300) =0.\n\nCompute coefficients:\n\n148a³,\n\n-298a²,\n\n3750a -6066a = -2316a,\n\n16,800 +6,300 =23,100.\n\nThus:\n\n148a³ -298a² -2316a +23,100 =0.\n\nDivide all terms by 2 to simplify:\n\n74a³ -149a² -1158a +11,550 =0.\n\nHmm, still not promising for integer roots. Try rational root theorem: possible rational roots are factors of 11550 over factors of 74. Factors of 11550 include ±1, ±2, ±3, etc., but this is tedious.\n\nTry a=5:\n\n74*(125) -149*(25) -1158*5 +11550 =\n\n74*125 =9,250,\n\n149*25=3,725,\n\n1158*5=5,790,\n\nSo,\n\n9,250 -3,725 -5,790 +11,550 =\n\n9,250 -3,725 =5,525,\n\n5,525 -5,790= -265,\n\n-265 +11,550 =11,285 ≠0.\n\na=6:\n\n74*216 -149*36 -1158*6 +11550.\n\n74*216=16,  let's compute:\n\n70*216=15,120,\n\n4*216=864 ⇒ total 15,120+864=16, 15,120+864=16, 15,120+800=15,920, +64=15,984.\n\n149*36: 150*36=5,400 minus 1*36=5,364,\n\n1158*6=6,948,\n\nSo,\n\n16, 15,984 -5,364 -6,948 +11,550.\n\nWait, step by step:\n\n74a³ =74*(216)= 74*200 +74*16=14,800 +1,184=15,984,\n\n-149a² =-149*(36)= -5,364,\n\n-1158a =-1158*6= -6,948,\n\n+11,550.\n\nSo total:\n\n15,984 -5,364 =10,620,\n\n10,620 -6,948=3,672,\n\n3,672 +11,550=15,222 ≠0.\n\na=3:\n\n74*27 -149*9 -1158*3 +11550.\n\n74*27=1,998,\n\n149*9=1,341,\n\n1158*3=3,474,\n\nSo,\n\n1,998 -1,341 =657,\n\n657 -3,474 =-2,817,\n\n-2,817 +11,550=8,733 ≠0.\n\na=10:\n\n74*1000 -149*100 -11580 +11550 =\n\n74,000 -14,900 -11,580 +11,550 =\n\n74,000 -14,900=59,100,\n\n59,100 -11,580=47,520,\n\n47,520 +11,550=59,070 ≠0.\n\nHmm, this isn't working either.\n\nGiven the time I've spent and the lack of progress in factoring, perhaps I should consider that the problem likely expects us to recognize that the sum S satisfies a quadratic equation, and through the earlier relationships, we can derive that quadratic.\n\nRecall that we had:\n\nFrom Equation D and E, we had a complicated equation, but perhaps there is a smarter substitution.\n\nLet me recall that we have expressions for ab + bc + ca and abc in terms of S.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet me recall that in a cubic equation with roots a, b, c, we have:\n\nx³ - S x² + (ab + bc + ca)x - abc =0.\n\nIf we can show that this cubic equation must satisfy some condition, perhaps by substituting one of the original equations into it.\n\nFor example, since a² = b +6,\n\nwe can write b = a² -6,\n\nso substitute b into the cubic equation:\n\na³ - S a² + (ab + bc + ca)a - abc =0,\n\nbut this might not help.\n\nAlternatively, since a, b, c satisfy the cubic equation, then each of them must satisfy the original functional equations.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider that for each root a of the cubic, we have:\n\na³ = S a² - (ab + bc + ca)a + abc.\n\nBut also, from the original equation, a² = b +6,\n\nso, a³ = a*(a²) = a*(b +6) = a b +6a.\n\nThus,\n\na b +6a = S a² - (ab + bc + ca)a + abc.\n\nRearrange terms:\n\na b +6a - S a² + (ab + bc + ca)a - abc =0,\n\nFactor terms:\n\na b + (ab + bc + ca)a +6a - S a² - abc =0,\n\na b + a² b + a b c +6a - S a² - a b c =0,\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up in expanding.\n\nWait, let me do it step by step.\n\nStarting from:\n\na b +6a = S a² - (ab + bc + ca)a + abc.\n\nBring all terms to left side:\n\na b +6a - S a² + (ab + bc + ca)a - abc =0.\n\nNow, distribute the terms:\n\na b +6a - S a² + a*(ab + bc + ca) - a b c =0.\n\nExpand a*(ab + bc + ca):\n\na² b + a b c + a² c.\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\na b +6a - S a² + a² b + a b c + a² c - a b c =0.\n\nNotice that the a b c terms cancel:\n\n+a b c - a b c =0.\n\nSo remaining terms:\n\na b +6a - S a² + a² b + a² c =0.\n\nFactor terms with a²:\n\n- S a² + a² b + a² c + a b +6a =0,\n\nFactor a² from first three terms:\n\na²(-S + b + c) + a b +6a =0.\n\nFactor a from the last two terms:\n\na²(-S + b + c) + a(b +6) =0.\n\nBut note that from the original equation, b +6 =a²,\n\nso substitute that in:\n\na²(-S + b + c) + a*(a²) =0 ⇒\n\na²(-S + b + c) +a³ =0.\n\nFactor a²:\n\na² [ -S + b + c + a ] =0.\n\nSince a is a root of the cubic and presumably non-zero (since if a=0, then from a² = b +6, b= -6, then c = b² -6 = 36 -6=30, then a = c² -6 = 900 -6=894 ≠0. So a ≠0.\n\nThus, we can divide both sides by a²:\n\n- S + b + c + a =0 ⇒\n\n- S + (a + b + c) =0 ⇒\n\n- S + S =0 ⇒ 0=0.\n\nHmm, so this reduces to an identity, which gives no new information. So this approach didn't help.\n\nPerhaps I need to try another angle.\n\nLet me recall that we had expressions for ab + bc + ca and abc in terms of S:\n\nab + bc + ca = (S² - S - 18)/2,\n\nabc = (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6.\n\nNow, in the cubic equation with roots a, b, c, the product abc is the negative of the constant term, so:\n\nabc = - [constant term] ⇒\n\n(S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6 = - [constant term].\n\nBut in the cubic equation, the constant term is -abc, so that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider that in the cubic equation, the coefficients must satisfy certain relations based on the functional equations.\n\nAlternatively, maybe consider that since a, b, c are related cyclically, the cubic equation must be invariant under cyclic permutation of its roots, which might imply certain symmetric properties.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps assume that S satisfies a quadratic equation and use the expressions for ab + bc + ca and abc in terms of S to form such an equation.\n\nLet me suppose that S satisfies a quadratic equation: S² + p S + q =0.\n\nThen, perhaps we can express the higher-degree terms in terms of S using this equation.\n\nBut to find p and q, we need to find relations involving S².\n\nFrom earlier, we have:\n\nab + bc + ca = (S² - S -18)/2,\n\nand also, we might find another relation involving S².\n\nAlternatively, let me compute (a + b + c)^2 = S² = a² + b² + c² + 2(ab + bc + ca).\n\nBut we already used this to get S² = (S + 18) + 2*( (S² - S -18)/2 ),\n\nwhich simplifies to S² = S + 18 + S² - S -18 ⇒ S² = S², which is a tautology. So that doesn't help.\n\nHmm.\n\nAlternatively, compute a^3 + b^3 + c^3.\n\nEarlier, we had expressions for a^3 + b^3 + c^3 in two ways:\n\nFirst, using the identity:\n\na³ + b³ + c³ = 3abc + (a + b + c)(a² + b² + c² - ab - bc - ca),\n\nand we had expressions for all those terms in terms of S.\n\nSecond, from the original equations:\n\na³ = a*b +6a,\n\nb³ = b*c +6b,\n\nc³ = c*a +6c.\n\nTherefore,\n\na³ + b³ + c³ = a b +6a + b c +6b + c a +6c = (ab + bc + ca) +6(a + b + c) = (ab + bc + ca) +6S.\n\nTherefore,\n\na³ + b³ + c³ = (ab + bc + ca) +6S.\n\nBut also, from the other identity:\n\na³ + b³ + c³ = 3abc + S*(a² + b² + c² - ab - bc - ca).\n\nWe already know that a² + b² + c² = S + 18,\n\nand ab + bc + ca = (S² - S -18)/2,\n\nso,\n\na² + b² + c² - ab - bc - ca = (S + 18) - (S² - S -18)/2,\n\nwhich we computed earlier as (-S² + 3S + 54)/2.\n\nTherefore,\n\na³ + b³ + c³ = 3abc + S*(-S² + 3S +54)/2.\n\nBut we also have:\n\na³ + b³ + c³ = (ab + bc + ca) +6S = [(S² - S -18)/2] +6S.\n\nTherefore, setting equal the two expressions:\n\n3abc + S*(-S² + 3S +54)/2 = [(S² - S -18)/2] +6S.\n\nMultiply both sides by 2 to eliminate denominators:\n\n6abc + S*(-S² + 3S +54) = S² - S -18 +12S,\n\nSimplify the right side:\n\nS² +11S -18.\n\nLeft side:\n\n6abc -S³ +3S² +54S.\n\nThus,\n\n6abc -S³ +3S² +54S = S² +11S -18.\n\nBring all terms to left:\n\n6abc -S³ +3S² +54S -S² -11S +18 =0,\n\nSimplify:\n\n- S³ +2 S² +43 S +18 +6abc =0.\n\nBut we have an expression for abc in terms of S:\n\nabc = (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6.\n\nSubstitute into the equation:\n\n- S³ +2 S² +43 S +18 +6*(S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6 =0.\n\nThe 6 and 6 cancel:\n\n- S³ +2 S² +43 S +18 + (S³ -2 S² -43 S -18) =0.\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\n- S³ + S³ =0,\n\n2 S² -2 S² =0,\n\n43 S -43 S =0,\n\n18 -18 =0.\n\nThus, 0=0.\n\nAgain, an identity. So this approach also doesn't yield new information.\n\nHmm, so all these substitutions lead back to identities, meaning that the system is consistent but underdetermined for S? But the problem states there are two possible values for S, so there must be a way to derive a quadratic equation for S.\n\nPerhaps going back to the eighth-degree equation and considering that the sum S = a + b + c for the 3-cycle must satisfy a quadratic equation derived from the system.\n\nLet me consider that in a 3-cycle, applying the function three times brings you back, so f(f(f(a))) = a,\n\nwhich we already used to form the eighth-degree equation.\n\nBut perhaps for the sum S = a + b + c,\n\nif we consider that a, b, c are roots of the cubic equation we mentioned earlier,\n\nthen the sum S is the sum of the roots,\n\nand the product abc is known in terms of S,\n\nand the sum of products ab + bc + ca is also known in terms of S,\n\nso perhaps the cubic equation must satisfy some condition based on the functional equations.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider that each variable satisfies the equation x² - y -6 =0,\n\nwhere y is the next variable.\n\nSo for a: a² - b -6 =0,\n\nfor b: b² - c -6 =0,\n\nfor c: c² - a -6 =0.\n\nIf we treat these as a system,\n\nthen we can write:\n\na² - b =6,\n\nb² - c =6,\n\nc² - a =6.\n\nLet me try to write this system in matrix form or find a way to combine them.\n\nLet me denote the equations:\n\n1) a² - b =6,\n\n2) b² - c =6,\n\n3) c² - a =6.\n\nLet me try to express each variable in terms of the next and substitute cyclically.\n\nFrom equation 1: b = a² -6,\n\nFrom equation 2: c = b² -6 = (a² -6)^2 -6,\n\nFrom equation 3: a = c² -6 = [(a² -6)^2 -6]^2 -6.\n\nWhich brings us back to the eighth-degree equation.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider the differences between the equations.\n\nFor example, subtract equation 1 from equation 2:\n\n(b² - c) - (a² - b) =0,\n\nwhich gives b² - c -a² + b =0,\n\nas before.\n\nSimilarly, subtract equation 2 from equation 3:\n\n(c² - a) - (b² - c) =0 ⇒ c² -a -b² +c =0,\n\nand subtract equation 3 from equation 1:\n\n(a² - b) - (c² - a) =0 ⇒ a² -b -c² +a =0.\n\nThese three equations might be combined in some way.\n\nLet me write them again:\n\nEquation A: b² - a² + b - c =0,\n\nEquation B: c² - b² + c - a =0,\n\nEquation C: a² - c² + a - b =0.\n\nNotice that each equation resembles the previous one cyclically.\n\nIndeed, Equation A: (b² - a²) + (b - c) =0,\n\nEquation B: (c² - b²) + (c - a) =0,\n\nEquation C: (a² - c²) + (a - b) =0.\n\nEach can be factored:\n\nEquation A: (b - a)(b + a) + (b - c) =0,\n\nEquation B: (c - b)(c + b) + (c - a) =0,\n\nEquation C: (a - c)(a + c) + (a - b) =0.\n\nHmm, perhaps factor (b - a), (c - b), (a - c) terms.\n\nAlternatively, notice that if we add all three equations:\n\nEquation A + Equation B + Equation C:\n\n(b² -a² + b -c) + (c² -b² +c -a) + (a² -c² +a -b) =0,\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\nb² -a² +c² -b² +a² -c² =0,\n\nand b -c +c -a +a -b =0,\n\nso total sum is 0=0.\n\nThus, no new information.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps express each equation as:\n\nFrom Equation A: (b - a)(b + a) = c - b,\n\nFrom Equation B: (c - b)(c + b) = a - c,\n\nFrom Equation C: (a - c)(a + c) = b - a.\n\nNow, notice that each left-hand side is a product involving the difference of two variables and their sum, and the right-hand side is the negative of the next difference.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet me set D = b - a,\n\nE = c - b,\n\nF = a - c.\n\nNote that D + E + F = (b -a) + (c - b) + (a - c) =0.\n\nFrom Equation A: D*(b + a) = -E,\n\nFrom Equation B: E*(c + b) = -F,\n\nFrom Equation C: F*(a + c) = -D.\n\nSo we have:\n\nD*(a + b) = -E,\n\nE*(b + c) = -F,\n\nF*(a + c) = -D.\n\nMultiply all three equations together:\n\nD*E*F*(a + b)(b + c)(a + c) = (-E)*(-F)*(-D) = -D E F.\n\nThus,\n\nD E F (a + b)(b + c)(a + c) = - D E F.\n\nAssuming D E F ≠0 (which would be the case if a, b, c are distinct, which they are), we can divide both sides by D E F:\n\n(a + b)(b + c)(a + c) = -1.\n\nTherefore,\n\n(a + b)(b + c)(a + c) = -1.\n\nThis is a key relation!\n\nSo, we have derived that:\n\n(a + b)(b + c)(a + c) = -1. (Equation G)\n\nNow, recall that S = a + b + c.\n\nLet me express (a + b), (b + c), (a + c) in terms of S and the variables.\n\nNote that:\n\na + b = S - c,\n\nb + c = S - a,\n\na + c = S - b.\n\nTherefore,\n\n(a + b)(b + c)(a + c) = (S - c)(S - a)(S - b) = -1.\n\nThus,\n\n(S - a)(S - b)(S - c) = -1. (Equation H)\n\nBut the left-hand side is the value of the cubic polynomial P(x) = (x - a)(x - b)(x - c) evaluated at x = S,\n\nso,\n\nP(S) = (S - a)(S - b)(S - c) = -1.\n\nBut from the cubic equation that a, b, c satisfy,\n\nthe polynomial is x³ - S x² + [(S² - S - 18)/2]x - [(S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6] =0.\n\nThus, evaluating at x = S,\n\nP(S) = S³ - S * S² + [(S² - S - 18)/2] * S - [(S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6] =0.\n\nWait, but we also have P(S) = -1 from Equation H.\n\nTherefore,\n\nS³ - S³ + [(S² - S - 18)/2] * S - [(S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6] = -1.\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\nFirst term: S³ - S³ =0,\n\nSecond term: [(S² - S -18)/2] * S = [S³ - S² -18 S]/2,\n\nThird term: - [(S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6].\n\nThus,\n\n[ (S³ - S² -18 S)/2 ] - [ (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6 ] = -1.\n\nMultiply through by 6 to eliminate denominators:\n\n3(S³ - S² -18 S) - (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18) = -6.\n\nExpand:\n\n3S³ -3S² -54S -S³ +2S² +43S +18 = -6.\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n(3S³ - S³) + (-3S² +2S²) + (-54S +43S) +18 = -6,\n\nwhich is:\n\n2S³ - S² -11S +18 = -6.\n\nBring all terms to left side:\n\n2S³ - S² -11S +18 +6 =0 ⇒\n\n2S³ - S² -11S +24 =0.\n\nThus, we have a cubic equation for S:\n\n2S³ - S² -11S +24 =0. (Equation I)\n\nNow, this is a cubic equation, but the problem states there are two possible values for S. Hmm, perhaps one of the roots is extraneous, or maybe the cubic factors into a quadratic and a linear term, with the linear term corresponding to the fixed points?\n\nLet me try to factor Equation I: 2S³ - S² -11S +24 =0.\n\nTry possible rational roots using Rational Root Theorem. Possible roots are ±1, ±2, ±3, ±4, ±6, ±8, ±12, ±24, divided by factors of 2, so ±1/2, etc.\n\nTest S=2:\n\n2*(8) -4 -22 +24 =16 -4 -22 +24= (16+24)=40, ( -4-22)= -26, so 40 -26=14≠0.\n\nS=3:\n\n2*27 -9 -33 +24 =54 -9 -33 +24= (54+24)=78, (-9-33)= -42 ⇒78-42=36≠0.\n\nS=4:\n\n2*64 -16 -44 +24 =128 -16 -44 +24= (128+24)=152, (-16-44)= -60 ⇒152-60=92≠0.\n\nS= -2:\n\n2*(-8) -4 -(-22) +24 =-16 -4 +22 +24= (-20) +46=26≠0.\n\nS= 1:\n\n2 -1 -11 +24 =14≠0.\n\nS= -3:\n\n2*(-27) -9 -(-33) +24 =-54 -9 +33 +24= (-63) +57= -6≠0.\n\nS= 4/ something? Let's try S= 3/2:\n\n2*(27/8) - (9/4) -11*(3/2) +24.\n\nCompute:\n\n27/4 - 9/4 - 33/2 +24,\n\n= (27 -9)/4 - 33/2 +24,\n\n= 18/4 - 33/2 +24,\n\n= 9/2 - 33/2 +24,\n\n= (-24/2) +24,\n\n= -12 +24=12≠0.\n\nS= -4:\n\n2*(-64) -16 -(-44) +24= -128 -16 +44 +24= (-144) +68= -76≠0.\n\nS= 2/1 already tried.\n\nS= 3/2 tried.\n\nS= 4/ something else? Let me try S= 8/ something? Not likely.\n\nHmm. How about S= - 4/ something?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps S= 2 is a root but miscalculation?\n\nWait, S=2:\n\n2*(8) -4 -22 +24 =16 -4 -22 +24= (16+24)=40, ( -4-22)= -26 ⇒40-26=14, yes, not zero.\n\nHmm.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in deriving Equation I.\n\nLet me retrace the steps to ensure no error.\n\nWe had:\n\n(a + b)(b + c)(a + c) = -1,\n\nand expressed each term as S - variable,\n\nso (S - a)(S - b)(S - c) = -1,\n\nthen since a, b, c are roots of the cubic,\n\nthe polynomial evaluated at S is P(S) = (S - a)(S - b)(S - c) = -1,\n\nbut the cubic polynomial is x³ - S x² + [(S² - S -18)/2]x - [(S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6].\n\nTherefore,\n\nP(S) = S³ - S * S² + [(S² - S -18)/2] * S - [(S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6].\n\nYes.\n\nCompute each term:\n\nFirst term: S³ - S³ =0,\n\nSecond term: [(S² - S -18)/2] * S = (S³ - S² -18S)/2,\n\nThird term: - [(S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6].\n\nSo total expression:\n\n(S³ - S² -18S)/2 - (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6 = -1.\n\nMultiply both sides by 6:\n\n3(S³ - S² -18S) - (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18) = -6,\n\nExpand:\n\n3S³ -3S² -54S -S³ +2S² +43S +18 = -6,\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n(3S³ - S³) =2S³,\n\n(-3S² +2S²)= -S²,\n\n(-54S +43S)= -11S,\n\n+18,\n\nThus:\n\n2S³ - S² -11S +18 = -6,\n\nthen,\n\n2S³ - S² -11S +24 =0.\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nHmm, so the cubic is correct. Maybe it factors?\n\nLet me try to factor 2S³ - S² -11S +24.\n\nTry to factor by grouping:\n\nGroup terms as (2S³ - S²) + (-11S +24).\n\nFactor S² from first group: S²(2S -1),\n\nSecond group: -11S +24. Not obvious.\n\nAlternatively, group as (2S³ -11S) + (-S² +24).\n\nFactor S from first group: S(2S² -11),\n\nSecond group: -S² +24. Not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, try synthetic division for possible roots.\n\nPossible rational roots are factors of 24 over factors of 2: ±1, ±2, ±3, ±4, ±6, ±8, ±12, ±24, ±1/2, ±3/2, etc.\n\nTry S= 3:\n\n2*27 -9 -33 +24 =54 -9 -33 +24 =54+24=78; -9-33=-42; 78-42=36≠0.\n\nS= -3:\n\n2*(-27) -9 -(-33) +24 =-54 -9 +33 +24= (-63)+57= -6≠0.\n\nS= 4:\n\n2*64 -16 -44 +24=128-16=112; 112-44=68; 68+24=92≠0.\n\nS= -4:\n\n2*(-64) -16 -(-44)+24 =-128 -16 +44 +24= (-144)+68= -76≠0.\n\nS= 2:\n\n2*8 -4 -22 +24=16-4=12; 12-22=-10; -10+24=14≠0.\n\nS= 1.5 (3/2):\n\n2*(3.375) - (2.25) - 16.5 +24,\n\nWait, let's compute exactly:\n\nS= 3/2:\n\n2*(27/8) - (9/4) -11*(3/2) +24.\n\n= (27/4) - (9/4) - (33/2) +24,\n\n= (18/4) - (33/2) +24,\n\n= (9/2) - (33/2) +24,\n\n= (-24/2) +24,\n\n= -12 +24=12≠0.\n\nS= -2:\n\n2*(-8) -4 -(-22)+24= -16-4+22+24= (-20)+46=26≠0.\n\nS= 4/ something? Maybe S= 8/ something?\n\nAlternatively, S= 2.4 or something? Not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it factors as (2S^3 - S^2) - (11S -24).\n\nFactor S^2 from first two terms: S^2(2S -1) - (11S -24). Doesn't help.\n\nAlternatively, use the rational root theorem with S= 8/ something?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made an error in the derivation of Equation I?\n\nWait, let me retrace:\n\nWe had:\n\n(a + b)(b + c)(a + c) = -1,\n\nand then expressed each term as S - c, etc., so:\n\n(S - a)(S - b)(S - c) = -1,\n\nthen since a, b, c are roots of the cubic,\n\nthe cubic polynomial is P(x) = (x -a)(x -b)(x -c) = x³ - Sx² + [(S² - S -18)/2]x - [(S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6].\n\nTherefore, P(S) = (S -a)(S -b)(S -c) = -1,\n\nso substituting x = S into P(x):\n\nP(S) = S³ - S*S² + [(S² - S -18)/2]*S - [(S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6] = -1.\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nWait, but maybe there was an error in the expression for the cubic's coefficients?\n\nLet me re-derive the cubic equation coefficients:\n\nWe had earlier:\n\nFor the cubic with roots a, b, c:\n\nx³ - Sx² + (ab + bc + ca)x - abc =0.\n\nWe had expressions:\n\nab + bc + ca = (S² - S -18)/2,\n\nabc = (S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6.\n\nTherefore,\n\nthe cubic polynomial is:\n\nx³ - S x² + [(S² - S -18)/2] x - [(S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6] =0.\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nTherefore, substituting x = S,\n\nP(S) = S³ - S*S² + [(S² - S -18)/2] S - [(S³ - 2 S² -43 S -18)/6] = -1.\n\nYes, so that's correct.\n\nThus, the cubic equation for S is indeed 2S³ - S² -11S +24 =0.\n\nHmm. Since this cubic doesn't factor nicely with rational roots, perhaps it factors into a quadratic and a linear term with irrational roots?\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in the earlier steps leading to this cubic equation.\n\nWait, let me try to use the original functional equations to find possible S values numerically.\n\nSuppose that S = a + b + c.\n\nAssume that a, b, c are complex numbers forming a 3-cycle, so each is distinct and applying f three times brings you back.\n\nGiven that f(x) =x² -6,\n\nmaybe try to find such a cycle numerically.\n\nSuppose we start with a guess for a, then compute b = a² -6, c = b² -6, then a' = c² -6, and see if a' ≈a.\n\nLet me try with a = 2:\n\nThen b = 4 -6 = -2,\n\nc = (-2)^2 -6 =4-6 =-2,\n\na' = (-2)^2 -6 = -2 ≠2. So that gives a cycle of length 1 for a and b, but c also goes to -2, so not a 3-cycle.\n\nTry a =1:\n\nb =1 -6 =-5,\n\nc= (-5)^2 -6=25-6=19,\n\na' =19² -6=361-6=355≠1.\n\nNot a cycle.\n\nTry a=0:\n\nb= -6,\n\nc=36-6=30,\n\na'= 900 -6=894≠0.\n\na= -1:\n\nb=1-6=-5,\n\nc=25-6=19,\n\na'= same as above.\n\na=5:\n\nb=25-6=19,\n\nc=361-6=355,\n\na'= same as above.\n\nHmm, not helpful.\n\nTry a complex number.\n\nLet me try a = i (imaginary unit).\n\nThen b = (i)^2 -6 = -1 -6 = -7,\n\nc = (-7)^2 -6 =49-6=43,\n\na'=43² -6=1849-6=1843≠i.\n\nNot a cycle.\n\nTry a= sqrt(6):\n\nThen b =6 -6=0,\n\nc=0 -6=-6,\n\na'= (-6)^2 -6=36-6=30≠sqrt(6).\n\nHmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the values of S are roots of the cubic equation 2S³ - S² -11S +24 =0.\n\nLet me try to solve this cubic numerically.\n\nLet me denote f(S) =2S³ - S² -11S +24.\n\nCompute f(2)=2*8 -4 -22 +24=16-4-22+24=14,\n\nf(3)=2*27 -9 -33 +24=54-9-33+24=36,\n\nf(4)=2*64 -16 -44 +24=128-16-44+24=92,\n\nf(1)=2 -1 -11 +24=14,\n\nf(0)=0 -0 -0 +24=24,\n\nf(-1)= -2 -1 +11 +24=32,\n\nf( -2)= -16 -4 +22 +24=26,\n\nHmm, all positive at integer points. Let me try S= -3:\n\nf(-3)=2*(-27) -9 -(-33) +24= -54 -9 +33 +24= (-63)+57= -6.\n\nAh! f(-3)= -6.\n\nWait, that's interesting.\n\nWait, f(-3)=2*(-3)^3 - (-3)^2 -11*(-3) +24,\n\n=2*(-27) -9 +33 +24,\n\n= -54 -9 +33 +24,\n\n= (-63) +57= -6.\n\nSo f(-3)= -6,\n\nf(-4)=2*(-64) -16 -(-44) +24= -128 -16 +44 +24= (-144)+68= -76,\n\nf(-2.5):\n\nLet me try S= -2.5:\n\nf(-2.5)=2*(-15.625) - (6.25) -11*(-2.5) +24,\n\n= -31.25 -6.25 +27.5 +24,\n\n= (-37.5) +51.5=14.\n\nHmm,\n\nAt S= -3, f(S)= -6,\n\nAt S= -2.5, f(S)=14,\n\nSo between S= -3 and S= -2.5, the function crosses from -6 to14, so there is a root there.\n\nSimilarly, between S= -4 and S= -3, it goes from -76 to -6, so no crossing.\n\nBetween S= -3 and S= -2.5: root there.\n\nBetween S= -2.5 and S=0: f(S) goes from14 to24, no root.\n\nBetween S=0 and S=2: stays positive.\n\nBetween S=2 and S=3: increases to 36.\n\nHmm, so only one real root between -3 and -2.5?\n\nWait, but cubic equations have at least one real root. Since it's a cubic with leading coefficient positive, it goes from -infty to +infty, so there must be at least one real root.\n\nWait, but when S approaches +infty, f(S) approaches +infty,\n\nand as S approaches -infty, f(S) approaches -infty,\n\nso there must be at least one real root. But in the computations above, between S=-3 and S=-2.5, f(S) crosses from -6 to14, so one real root there,\n\nand between S=-infty to S=-3, it goes from -infty to f(-3)= -6,\n\nso another root?\n\nWait, let's check S=-4: f(-4)= -76,\n\nS=-5: f(-5)=2*(-125) -25 -(-55)+24= -250 -25 +55 +24= -250 -25= -275 +79= -196,\n\nstill decreasing?\n\nWait, derivative f’(S)=6S² -2S -11.\n\nAt S=-3, f’(-3)=6*9 -2*(-3) -11=54+6-11=49>0,\n\nso function is increasing at S=-3,\n\nso from S=-infty to the local maximum, then decreases to local minimum, then increases again.\n\nWait, compute critical points:\n\nSet f’(S)=0 ⇒6S² -2S -11=0,\n\nSolutions:\n\nS = [2 ± sqrt(4 + 264)] /12 = [2 ± sqrt(268)] /12 = [2 ± 2*sqrt(67)] /12 = [1 ± sqrt(67)]/6.\n\nApproximately sqrt(67)=8.185,\n\nso S≈ [1 +8.185]/6 ≈9.185/6≈1.53,\n\nand S≈ [1 -8.185]/6≈-7.185/6≈-1.197.\n\nTherefore, the function has a local maximum at S≈-1.197 and a local minimum at S≈1.53.\n\nCompute f(-1.197):\n\nApproximate,\n\nLet me take S≈-1.2:\n\nf(-1.2)=2*(-1.2)^3 - (-1.2)^2 -11*(-1.2)+24,\n\n=2*(-1.728) -1.44 +13.2 +24,\n\n= -3.456 -1.44 +13.2 +24,\n\n≈ (-4.896) +37.2≈32.304.\n\nHmm, so at S≈-1.197, local maximum around 32,\n\nand at S≈1.53,\n\ncompute f(1.53):\n\n≈2*(3.581) - (2.3409) -11*(1.53) +24,\n\n≈7.162 -2.3409 -16.83 +24,\n\n≈(7.162 -2.3409)=4.821,\n\n4.821 -16.83= -12.009,\n\n-12.009 +24≈11.991.\n\nSo local minimum around 12.\n\nThus, the function only crosses the x-axis once, between S=-3 and S=-2.5,\n\nsince at S=-3, f(S)=-6,\n\nand at S=-2.5, f(S)=14,\n\nso there's one real root there,\n\nand since after that, the function only goes up to local maximum at S≈-1.197 with f(S)=~32,\n\nthen decreases to local minimum at S≈1.53 with f(S)=~12,\n\nthen increases to infinity,\n\nso only one real root.\n\nTherefore, the cubic equation 2S³ - S² -11S +24 =0 has only one real root,\n\nand two complex conjugate roots.\n\nBut the problem states there are two possible values of a + b + c,\n\nso likely the two complex roots are the valid ones,\n\nand the real root corresponds to the fixed point cycles which are invalid.\n\nTherefore, the two possible values of S are the complex roots of the cubic equation,\n\nwhich are conjugates.\n\nTo find them, we can solve the cubic equation numerically,\n\nor perhaps factor it using the real root.\n\nSince we found that there is a real root between S=-3 and S=-2.5,\n\nlet me approximate it.\n\nLet me use the Newton-Raphson method.\n\nLet me denote f(S)=2S³ - S² -11S +24,\n\nf’(S)=6S² -2S -11.\n\nWe need to find root between S=-3 and S=-2.5,\n\nwhere f(-3)= -6,\n\nf(-2.5)=14.\n\nLet me start with S₀=-3,\n\nf(S₀)= -6,\n\nf’(S₀)=6*(9) -2*(-3) -11=54+6-11=49,\n\nNext iteration:\n\nS₁ = S₀ - f(S₀)/f’(S₀)= -3 - (-6)/49 ≈-3 + 0.1224≈-2.8776.\n\nCompute f(S₁):\n\nS₁≈-2.8776,\n\nf(S₁)=2*(-2.8776)^3 - (-2.8776)^2 -11*(-2.8776)+24.\n\nFirst compute (-2.8776)^3≈-23.7,\n\nso 2*(-23.7)= -47.4,\n\n(-2.8776)^2≈8.28,\n\n-(-2.8776)^2≈-8.28,\n\n-11*(-2.8776)=31.65,\n\nThus,\n\n-47.4 -8.28 +31.65 +24≈\n\n(-47.4 -8.28)= -55.68,\n\n+31.65= -24.03,\n\n+24≈-0.03.\n\nSo f(S₁)≈-0.03,\n\nvery close to zero.\n\nCompute f’(S₁)=6*(S₁)^2 -2*S₁ -11,\n\nS₁≈-2.8776,\n\nS₁²≈8.28,\n\nso 6*8.28≈49.68,\n\n-2*(-2.8776)=+5.755,\n\nso total f’(S₁)=49.68 +5.755 -11≈44.435.\n\nNext iteration:\n\nS₂ = S₁ - f(S₁)/f’(S₁) ≈-2.8776 - (-0.03)/44.435≈-2.8776 +0.000676≈-2.8769.\n\nCompute f(S₂):\n\nS₂≈-2.8769,\n\nf(S₂)=2*(-2.8769)^3 - (-2.8769)^2 -11*(-2.8769)+24.\n\nCalculate step by step:\n\nFirst term: 2*(-2.8769)^3,\n\nCompute (-2.8769)^3≈-2.8769*2.8769*2.8769.\n\nFirst compute 2.8769^2≈8.273,\n\nthen 2.8769*8.273≈23.84,\n\nso (-2.8769)^3≈-23.84,\n\nso 2*(-23.84)≈-47.68,\n\nSecond term: - ( (-2.8769)^2 )= -8.273,\n\nThird term: -11*(-2.8769)=+31.6459,\n\nFourth term:+24,\n\nSo total:\n\n-47.68 -8.273 +31.6459 +24 ≈\n\n(-47.68 -8.273)= -55.953,\n\n+31.6459= -24.307,\n\n+24≈-0.307.\n\nWait, that contradicts previous approximation. Maybe my manual calculations are too error-prone.\n\nAlternatively, since S₁≈-2.8776 gives f(S₁)≈-0.03