(a + b + c) = 18 \\)\n\nWhich is the same as:\n\n\\( a^2 + b^2 + c^2 = S + 18 \\), where \\( S = a + b + c \\).\n\nNow, as I had earlier, \\( a^2 + b^2 + c^2 = S^2 - 2(ab + bc + ca) \\).\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( S^2 - 2(ab + bc + ca) = S + 18 \\)\n\nSo,\n\n\\( S^2 - S - 18 = 2(ab + bc + ca) \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( ab + bc + ca = \\frac{S^2 - S - 18}{2} \\)\n\nHmm, so if I can find another equation involving \\( ab + bc + ca \\), then maybe I can solve for \\( S \\).\n\nAlternatively, perhaps multiplying the original equations together?\n\nOriginal equations:\n\n\\( a^2 = b + 6 \\)\n\n\\( b^2 = c + 6 \\)\n\n\\( c^2 = a + 6 \\)\n\nMultiply all three equations:\n\n\\( (a^2)(b^2)(c^2) = (b + 6)(c + 6)(a + 6) \\)\n\nLeft side: \\( (abc)^2 \\)\n\nRight side: Let's expand \\( (b + 6)(c + 6)(a + 6) \\).\n\nFirst, let me denote \\( (b + 6)(c + 6) = bc + 6b + 6c + 36 \\), then multiply by \\( (a + 6) \\):\n\n\\( [bc + 6b + 6c + 36] \\times (a + 6) \\)\n\nMultiply term by term:\n\n\\( bc \\times a + bc \\times 6 + 6b \\times a + 6b \\times 6 + 6c \\times a + 6c \\times 6 + 36 \\times a + 36 \\times 6 \\)\n\nSimplify each term:\n\n\\( abc + 6bc + 6ab + 36b + 6ac + 36c + 36a + 216 \\)\n\nSo, altogether:\n\n\\( abc + 6bc + 6ab + 36b + 6ac + 36c + 36a + 216 \\)\n\nTherefore, the right-hand side is:\n\n\\( abc + 6(ab + bc + ca) + 36(a + b + c) + 216 \\)\n\nTherefore, putting it all together, the equation from multiplying the three original equations is:\n\n\\( (abc)^2 = abc + 6(ab + bc + ca) + 36(a + b + c) + 216 \\)\n\nHmm. Let me denote \\( T = abc \\), and \\( S = a + b + c \\), and \\( Q = ab + bc + ca \\). Then, from above, we have:\n\nLeft side: \\( T^2 \\)\n\nRight side: \\( T + 6Q + 36S + 216 \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( T^2 = T + 6Q + 36S + 216 \\)\n\nBut we already have an expression for \\( Q \\) in terms of \\( S \\): from earlier,\n\n\\( Q = \\frac{S^2 - S - 18}{2} \\)\n\nSo substituting that into the above equation:\n\n\\( T^2 = T + 6 \\times \\left( \\frac{S^2 - S - 18}{2} 