3:12, x=4:16, x=5:20, x=6:24, x=7:28. So products with x=6 and x=7 exceed 20. Therefore, cannot be adjacent to 6 or 7. So adjacent to 1,2,3,5.\n\nNumber 5: 5*x: x=1:5, x=2:10, x=3:15, x=4:20, x=5:25, x=6:30, x=7:35. So products with x=5,6,7 exceed 20. Therefore, cannot be adjacent to 5,6,7. Wait, but 5 can be adjacent to 1,2,3,4? Let's see:\n\n5*1=5, okay; 5*2=10, okay; 5*3=15, okay; 5*4=20, okay; 5*5=25>20, so not allowed; 5*6=30>20, 5*7=35>20. So yes, 5 can be adjacent to 1,2,3,4 but not 5,6,7.\n\nNumber 6: 6*x: x=1:6, x=2:12, x=3:18, x=4:24, x=5:30, x=6:36, x=7:42. So products with x=4,5,6,7 exceed 20. So cannot be adjacent to 4,5,6,7. Therefore, adjacent only to 1,2,3.\n\nWait, 6*3=18 which is okay, 6*4=24 which is over, so yes, 6 can be adjacent to 1,2,3.\n\nNumber 7: 7*x: x=1:7, x=2:14, x=3:21, x=4:28, x=5:35, x=6:42, x=7:49. So 7 multiplied by any number except 1 and 2 gives a product over 20. Specifically, 7*3=21>20, so cannot be adjacent to 3,4,5,6,7. Since it can't be adjacent to itself, so 7 can only be adjacent to 1 and 2.\n\nWait, let me confirm:\n\n7*1=7 ≤20, okay.\n\n7*2=14 ≤20, okay.\n\n7*3=21>20, not okay.\n\n7*4=28>20, not okay.\n\nSimilarly, higher numbers also exceed, so yes, 7 can only be adjacent to 1 and 2.\n\nSo summarizing the adjacency lists:\n\n1: [2,3,4,5,6,7]\n\n2: [1,3,4,5,6,7]\n\n3: [1,2,4,5,6]\n\n4: [1,2,3,5]\n\n5: [1,2,3,4]\n\n6: [1,2,3]\n\n7: [1,2]\n\nOkay, so now we have the adjacency lists. Let me write them out again for clarity:\n\n- 1 is connected to everyone except itself: so neighbors: 2,3,4,5,6,7.\n\n- 2 is connected to everyone except itself: neighbors: 1,3,4,5,6,7.\n\n- 3 is connected to 1,2,4,5,6 (not 7).\n\n- 4 is connected to 1,2,3,5 (not 6,7).\n\n- 5 is connected to 1,2,3,4 (not 6,7).\n\n- 6 is connected to 1,2,3 (not 4,5,7).\n\n- 7 is connected to 1,2 (not 3,4,5,6).\n\nAlright, so now, with this adjacency list, we need to count the number of Hamiltonian cycles in this graph, then multiply by 2 (since each undirected c