mber ≤4 (since 5*4=20, 5*5=25>20, but again, same number not allowed). So numbers 2,3,4.\n\nCheck:\n\n5*2=10 ≤20: yes\n\n5*3=15 ≤20: yes\n\n5*4=20 ≤20: yes\n\n5*6=30>20: no\n\n5*7=35>20: no\n\nTherefore, 5 is connected to 2,3,4.\n\nNext, 6:\n\n6 can be adjacent to numbers where 6*number ≤20 => number ≤20/6 ≈3.333...\n\nThus, numbers 2 and 3 (since 6*3=18 ≤20, 6*4=24>20)\n\nCheck:\n\n6*2=12 ≤20: yes\n\n6*3=18 ≤20: yes\n\n6*4=24>20: no\n\n6*5=30>20: no\n\n6*7=42>20: no\n\nTherefore, 6 is connected to 2 and 3.\n\nFinally, 7:\n\n7 can be adjacent to numbers where 7*number ≤20 => number ≤20/7 ≈2.857...\n\nThus, only number 2 (since 7*2=14 ≤20; 7*3=21>20). Therefore, 7 is connected only to 2.\n\nLet me summarize the adjacency list:\n\n- 2: connected to 3,4,5,6,7\n\n- 3: connected to 2,4,5,6\n\n- 4: connected to 2,3,5\n\n- 5: connected to 2,3,4\n\n- 6: connected to 2,3\n\n- 7: connected to 2\n\nSo now, the graph looks like this:\n\nNodes: 2,3,4,5,6,7\n\nEdges:\n\nFrom 2: to 3,4,5,6,7\n\nFrom 3: to 2,4,5,6\n\nFrom 4: to 2,3,5\n\nFrom 5: to 2,3,4\n\nFrom 6: to 2,3\n\nFrom 7: to 2\n\nSo now, we need to count the number of Hamiltonian paths in this graph. A Hamiltonian path is a path that visits each node exactly once. Since we're dealing with permutations, the path is a sequence of all six nodes with each consecutive pair connected by an edge.\n\nSince the graph is undirected, each Hamiltonian path can be traversed in two directions, but since we are counting permutations (which are ordered sequences), each Hamiltonian path corresponds to two permutations (the forward and reverse directions), unless the path is a palindrome, which it can't be in a Hamiltonian path on distinct nodes. Wait, actually, in an undirected graph, a Hamiltonian path has a direction, but since the graph is undirected, the path can be traversed in either direction. However, when counting permutations, the direction matters because the permutation is an ordered list. So each undirected Hamiltonian path corresponds to two different permutations: one in each direction.\n\nBut in our case, since we need to count all Hamiltonian paths (i.e., all sequences where each consecutive pair is connected by an edge), regardless of direction, but since permutations are ordered, we need to count all possible orderings, so we have to consider all possible directions. Therefore, the number N is equal to twice the number of undirected Hamiltonian paths, except when the path is a palindrome, but in a Hamiltonian path on distinct nodes, it can't be a palindrome unless it's a single node, which it's not. Therefore, all Hamiltonian paths come in pairs (forwards and backwards), so the total number of directed Hamiltonian paths is 2 times the number of undirected Hamiltonian paths.\n\nHowever, in our problem, we need to count all permutations (i.e., all directed Hamiltonian paths) where the adjacency conditions are met. Therefore, N is exactly the number of directed Hamiltonian paths in the graph.\n\nTherefore, we need to compute the number of directed Hamiltonian paths in this graph. Since the graph is small (6 nodes), perhaps we can compute this using recursion or backtracking.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can use dynamic programming where the state is the current position and the set of visited nodes.\n\nLet me consider using recursion with memoization. Let me denote the nodes as numbers 2,3,4,5,6,7 for simplicity.\n\nLet me label them as follows for ease:\n\nLet’s assign labels:\n\nA = 2\n\nB = 3\n\nC = 4\n\nD = 5\n\nE = 6\n\nF = 7\n\nSo our nodes are A,B,C,D,E,F.\n\nEdges:\n\nFrom A: connected to B,C,D,E,F (since 2 is connected to all except none)\n\nWait, hold on:\n\nWait, in terms of the labels:\n\nOriginal nodes:\n\nA (2) connected to B(3), C(4), D(5), E(6), F(7)\n\nB(3) connected to A(2), C(4), D(5), E(6)\n\nC(4) connected to A(2), B(3), D(5)\n\nD(5) connected to A(2), B(3), C(4)\n\nE(6) connected to A(2), B(3)\n\nF(7) connected to A(2)\n\nSo in terms of the labels:\n\nEdges:\n\nA: