tion5 (b) and position6 (c): b×c ≤20,\n\n- Between position6 (c) and position7 (d): c×d ≤20,\n\n- Between position7 (d) and position1 (2): d×2 ≤20.\n\nAdditionally, the adjacency between position7 (d) and position6 (c) is already covered, and between position4 (a) and position3 (1) is okay.\n\nTherefore, the constraints are:\n\n1. a×b ≤20,\n\n2. b×c ≤20,\n\n3. c×d ≤20,\n\n4. d×2 ≤20.\n\nNote that the fourth constraint is new: the last element d must be adjacent to 2, so d×2 ≤20 ⇒ d ≤10, but since d is one of 3,4,5,6, all of which are ≤6, so d×2 is at most 12, which is ≤20. Therefore, the fourth constraint is automatically satisfied.\n\nTherefore, the only constraints are the same as in Case A: a×b ≤20, b×c ≤20, c×d ≤20, where [a,b,c,d] is a permutation of [3,4,5,6].\n\nWait a second! This is exactly the same problem as in Case A!\n\nTherefore, the number of valid permutations for Case B is also 4.\n\nTherefore, total arrangements for both cases (A and B) is 4 + 4 = 8.\n\nHowever, remember that in each case, the block [1,7,2] or [2,7,1] can be placed in the circle in different rotational positions, but we fixed the block's position to compute the permutations of the remaining numbers. Wait, no—actually in this approach, we fixed the block's position to specific positions (positions 1-3 for Case A and positions 1-3 for Case B), but since rotations are considered different arrangements, we need to account for all possible rotations.\n\nWait, hold on, this is a key point.\n\nIn this second approach, I fixed the block in specific positions (positions 1-3) to compute the permutations of the remaining numbers, but in reality, the block can be rotated to any position in the circle. Since rotations are considered different arrangements, we need to multiply by the number of positions the block can occupy.\n\nWait, but actually, in this method, by fixing the block's position, we are counting the number of arrangements where the block is in a specific set of three consecutive p