ted to 1,2,4,5. Similarly, node 4 is connected to 1,2,3,5, so it's connected to all except itself. Node 5 is connected to 1,2,3,4, so same. Nodes 1 and 2 are connected to everyone except themselves. Wait a second, actually in G', every node is connected to every other node except perhaps none? Wait, let's check:\n\nWait, node 3 in G' is connected to 1,2,4,5. So in the 5-node graph, node 3 is connected to all others except itself. Similarly, node 4 is connected to all except itself. Node 5 is connected to all except itself. Nodes 1 and 2 are also connected to everyone else. Therefore, G' is actually a complete graph on 5 nodes! Because every pair of distinct nodes is connected except... Wait, no, hold on:\n\nWait, in the original graph G (the full graph with 7 nodes), the subgraph G' (nodes 1-5) has edges as follows:\n\nWait, in G', node 3 is not connected to node 7, but node 7 isn't in G'. Within G', are there any missing edges?\n\nLooking back at the original adjacency lists for G':\n\nWithin G' (nodes 1,2,3,4,5):\n\n- Node 1 is connected to 2,3,4,5 ✔️ (all except none)\n\n- Node 2 is connected to 1,3,4,5 ✔️ (all except none)\n\n- Node 3 is connected to 1,2,4,5 ✔️ (all except none, since in G', node 3 isn't connected to node 6 or 7, but we're only considering G')\n\nWait, within G', node 3 is connected to all other nodes in G', right? Since in G', the edges are as per the original graph G, but excluding nodes 6 and 7. Since in G, node 3 was connected to 1,2,4,5,6, but in G', we exclude 6 and 7, so node 3 is connected to 1,2,4,5 in G', which are all the other nodes in G'. Therefore, node 3 is connected to all others in G'. Similarly, node 4 is connected to 1,2,3,5 in G', which are all except itself. Node 5 is connected to 1,2,3,4, which are all except itself. Nodes 1 and 2 are connected to everyone in G'.\n\nTherefore, in G', every node is connected to every other node except itself. Therefore, G' is indeed a complete graph K5.\n\nWait, really? That's a key insight! So the subgraph induced by nodes 1,2,3,4,5 is a complete graph K5. That simplifies things a lot!\n\nTherefore, in G', any permutation of the nodes 1-5 can be connected as a cycle, since it's a complete graph. Therefore, the number of Hamiltonian cycles in G' is (5-1)! / 2 = 12 (since in a complete graph K_n, the number of Hamiltonian cycles is (n-1)! / 2). Wait, let me recall: in a complete graph with n nodes, the number of distinct Hamiltonian cycles is (n-1)! / 2. Because you can arrange the nodes in (n-1)! ways in a cycle (fixing one node and permuting the rest), but since each cycle is counted twice (once in each direction), you divide by 2. So for K5, that would be (5-1)! / 2 = 24 / 2 = 12. So there are 12 undirected Hamiltonian cycles in G'.\n\nBut in our problem, we need to extend these cycles to include nodes 6 and 7, while respecting the edge constraints.\n\nGiven that, here's the plan:\n\nWe can take any Hamiltonian cycle in G' (K5), and then insert nodes 6 and 7 into the cycle such that:\n\n- Each insertion of 6 must be between two nodes that are both in {1,2,3}, since 6 can only be adjacent to 1,2,3.\n\n- Similarly, each insertion of 7 must be between two nodes that are both in {1,2,3}.\n\n- Additionally, 6 and 7 cannot be adjacent to each other, and cannot be inserted in a way that would require them to be adjacent to nodes outside {1,2,3}.\n\nMoreover, since we are inserting two nodes into the cycle, we need to choose two \"gaps\" in the original cycle where we can insert 6 and 7, each into a gap between two nodes in {1,2,3}.\n\nWait, but inserting a node into a cycle requires breaking the cycle at a point and inserting the node there. Since we have two nodes to insert (6 and 7), we need to choose two different gaps in the original cycle (in G') where we can insert 6 and 7 such that the insertion points meet the adjacency constraints.\n\nHowever, inserting a node into a cycle between two nodes u and v requires that the new node is connected to both u and v. Since we are inserting 6, it must be connected to both u and v, which must be in {1,2,3}. Similarly for 7.\n\nTherefore, to insert 6 into the cycle, we must choose an edge (u,v) in the original cycle (in G') where both u and v are in {1,2,3}, and insert 6 between them. Similarly for inserting 7.\n\nBut since we need to insert both 6 and 7, we need two such edges in the original cycle where we can insert them, and also ensure that after insertion, the new cycle includes all nodes and maintains the required connections.\n\nHowever, inserting 6 and 7 into two different edges of the original cycle would create a new cycle with the two insertions. However, we have to ensure that the insertions don't interfere with each other, i.e., the inserted nodes are not adjacent to each other unless allowed.\n\nBut since 6 and 7 cannot be adjacent (as they are not connected in G), we must insert them into non-adjacent edges of the original cycle.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to think of the process as follows:\n\nStart with a Hamiltonian cycle in G' (K5). Let's denote the nodes in the cycle as a sequence: v1, v2, v3, v4, v5, v1 (since it's a cycle). Since it's a cycle, we can break it at any point to make it a linear sequence.\n\nWe need to insert nodes 6 and 7 into this cycle such that:\n\n- Each insertion is between two nodes that are both in {1,2,3}.\n\n- The two insertions are not adjacent to each other (so that 6 and 7 are not next to each other).\n\n- After insertion, the new cycle includes all seven nodes.\n\nLet me consider the original cycle in G' (K5). Since it's a cycle of 5 nodes, all of which are connected to each other, we can represent it as any permutation of the 5 nodes arranged in a circle. Let's fix an arbitrary starting point and direction for simplicity, then account for symmetries later.\n\nBut since we are counting undirected cycles, let's fix the starting node as node 1 and consider the cycle in one direction, say clockwise. Then, the number of distinct cycles can be calculated accordingly.\n\nWait, perhaps instead of getting bogged down in fixing, let's think combinatorially.\n\nFirst, note that in the original K5 cycle, there are 5 edges. Each edge connects two nodes. For inserting a new node (say 6) between two nodes, we need to choose an edge in the original cycle where both endpoints are in {1,2,3}. Then, inserting 6 there would create a new edge from the first node to 6 and from 6 to the second node.\n\nSimilarly for inserting 7.\n\nTherefore, first, we need to know how many edges in the original K5 cycle have both endpoints in {1,2,3}. Since the original cycle is a Hamiltonian cycle in K5, which includes all 5 nodes, so the edges of the cycle will include some edges within {1,2,3} and some edges involving 4 or 5.\n\nWait, but in K5, any edge is allowed, but the specific edges in a given Hamiltonian cycle can vary. Since we're considering all possible Hamiltonian cycles in G', which is K5, each edge in any Hamiltonian cycle is a possible edge between any two nodes.\n\nWait, actually, no: in a specific Hamiltonian cycle, the edges are a subset of the complete graph's edges, forming a single cycle. However, different Hamiltonian cycles will have different edges.\n\nTherefore, to compute the number of ways to insert 6 and 7 into a given Hamiltonian cycle, we need to know, for that particular cycle, how many edges in it connect nodes within {1,2,3}, because those are the only edges where we can insert 6 or 7.\n\nBut since we are going to have to consider all possible Hamiltonian cycles in G', and for each cycle count the number of valid insertion points for 6 and 7, then sum over all cycles, this seems complicated. Perhaps there's a better way.\n\nAlternatively, maybe instead of thinking in terms of inserting into a cycle, we can think of building the entire cycle by considering the constraints on 6 and 7.\n\nLet me consider that in the full graph G (including nodes 6 and 7), the nodes 6 and 7 must each be placed between two nodes from {1,2,3}. So in the cycle, the nodes 6 and 7 are \"attached\" to the subgraph {1,2,3,4,5} via edges to nodes in {1,2,3}, and they cannot be adjacent to 4 or 5 or each other.\n\nLet me consider the possible neighbors of 6 and 7.\n\nSince 6 is connected only to 1,2,3, in the cycle, 6 must be between two nodes from {1,2,3}. Let's denote the two neighbors of 6 as a and b, where a, b ∈ {1,2,3}. Similarly, 7 must be between two nodes c and d, where c, d ∈ {1,2,3}, and also c and d are not adjacent to 6 in a way that would make 6 and 7 adjacent.\n\nWait, but since the cycle is a single loop, the positions of 6 and 7 are separate, so as long as their respective \"slots\" are not adjacent, they won't be next to each other.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model this as follows:\n\nThe cycle must include the five nodes 1-5 in some order forming a cycle (which can be any of the 12 Hamiltonian cycles in K5), and then insert 6 and 7 into the cycle such that each is between two nodes from {1,2,3}, and the insertions don't place 6 and 7 next to each other.\n\nFirst, let's note that in any Hamiltonian cycle of K5, the edges between the nodes 1,2,3 can vary. Let me consider the number of edges within {1,2,3} in a given Hamiltonian cycle.\n\nIn K5, a Hamiltonian cycle has 5 edges. The edges can be categorized based on their endpoints:\n\n- Edges within {1,2,3}: there are C(3,2)=3 possible such edges.\n\n- Edges involving 4 or 5: the remaining edges must connect to 4 or 5.\n\nIn any Hamiltonian cycle, the number of edges within {1,2,3} can vary. For example, a cycle could go 1-2-3-4-5-1, which has two edges within {1,2,3} (1-2 and 2-3), and the rest involve 4 and 5. Alternatively, a cycle could go 1-4-2-5-3-1, which has only one edge within {1,2,3} (1-4 is not within, 4-2 is not, 2-5 not, 5-3 not, 3-1 is within). Wait, 3-1 is within {1,2,3}, so that cycle has one edge within {1,2,3}.\n\nWait, actually, in any cycle that includes all five nodes, the number of edges within {1,2,3} can be 0, 1, 2, or maybe more?\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nSuppose we have a cycle that goes 1-2-4-5-3-1. Then the edges within {1,2,3} are 1-2 and 3-1, so two edges.\n\nAnother example: 1-3-5-4-2-1. The edges within {1,2,3} are 1-3 and 2-1 (the last edge 2-1 is within). So two edges.\n\nAnother example: 1-4-3-2-5-1. Edges within {1,2,3}: 1-4 (no), 4-3 (no), 3-2 (yes), 2-5 (no), 5-1 (no). So only one edge (3-2).\n\nAnother example: 1-5-2-4-3-1. Edges within {1,2,3}: 1-5 (no), 5-2 (no), 2-4 (no), 4-3 (no), 3-1 (yes). So one edge.\n\nAnother example: 1-2-3-5-4-1. Edges within {1,2,3}: 1-2, 2-3, and 4-1? No, 4-1 is not within {1,2,3}. So edges within: 1-2, 2-3. So two edges.\n\nWait, so in these examples, the number of edges within {1,2,3} in a Hamiltonian cycle can be 1 or 2. Can it be 0?\n\nSuppose we try to make a cycle with no edges within {1,2,3}. That would require that between any two nodes in {1,2,3}, the path in the cycle goes through 4 or 5. For example: 1-4-2-5-3-1. Wait, in that cycle:\n\nEdges: 1-4, 4-2, 2-5, 5-3, 3-1.\n\nEdges within {1,2,3}: only 3-1. So still one edge.\n\nAnother try: 1-5-2-4-3-1. Edges: 1-5, 5-2, 2-4, 4-3, 3-1. Within {1,2,3}: 3-1 and 5-2? No, 5-2 is not within {1,2,3}. Only 3-1. So still one edge.\n\nAlternatively, 1-4-5-2-3-1. Edges: 1-4, 4-5, 5-2, 2-3, 3-1. Within {1,2,3}: 2-3 and 3-1. So two edges.\n\nHmm, so seems like you can't have a Hamiltonian cycle in K5 with zero edges within {1,2,3}. Because to go from 1 to 2 without using an edge within {1,2,3}, you have to go through 4 or 5, but then to get back, you might still need an edge within {1,2,3}.\n\nWait, let's try: 1-4-2-5-3-1. That has one edge within {1,2,3} (the 3-1 edge). If I try to make a cycle that goes 1-4-5-3-2-1. Edges: 1-4, 4-5, 5-3, 3-2, 2-1. Within {1,2,3}: 2-1 and 3-2. So two edges.\n\nAlternatively, 1-5-4-2-3-1: edges 1-5,5-4,4-2,2-3,3-1. Within {1,2,3}: 2-3 and 3-1. Two edges.\n\nHmm, so maybe it's impossible to have a Hamiltonian cycle in K5 with zero edges within {1,2,3}. Let me see if that's the case.\n\nSuppose we try to make a cycle that goes through all five nodes without ever having two nodes from {1,2,3} adjacent. That would require that between any two nodes in {1,2,3}, there is at least one node from {4,5} in the cycle.\n\nBut since there are only two nodes in {4,5}, and there are three nodes in {1,2,3}, by the pigeonhole principle, at least two nodes from {1,2,3} must be adjacent. Because if you have three nodes (A,B,C) and two \"separators\" (D,E), then arranging A,B,C around the cycle with D and E as separators would require that at least two of A,B,C are next to each other.\n\nFor example, suppose you try to arrange the nodes as A-D-B-E-C-A. Then A and C are not adjacent, but B and E are adjacent? Wait, in this arrangement, the edges would be A-D, D-B, B-E, E-C, C-A. Here, C and A are adjacent, which are both in {1,2,3}. So you can't avoid having at least one edge within {1,2,3}.\n\nTherefore, every Hamiltonian cycle in K5 must have at least one edge within {1,2,3}, and in fact, at least two edges? Wait, in the example above, A-D-B-E-C-A has two edges within {1,2,3}: C-A and... Wait, no, in that example, the edges within {1,2,3} are only C-A. Wait, A and C are both in {1,2,3}, so that edge is within. Then B is connected to D and E, so no edges within {1,2,3} except C-A. So that cycle has one edge within {1,2,3}.\n\nWait, so actually, it's possible to have a Hamiltonian cycle with exactly one edge within {1,2,3}.\n\nTherefore, the number of edges within {1,2,3} in a Hamiltonian cycle can be 1 or 2.\n\nWait, can it be more than 2? Let's see.\n\nSuppose we have a cycle that goes 1-2-3-4-5-1. The edges within {1,2,3} are 1-2 and 2-3, so two edges. Another cycle: 1-2-4-3-5-1. Edges within {1,2,3}: 1-2 and 3-5? No, 3-5 is not within. Wait, edges: 1-2 (within), 2-4 (no), 4-3 (no), 3-5 (no), 5-1 (no). So only one edge within.\n\nAnother cycle: 1-3-2-4-5-1. Edges within {1,2,3}: 1-3 and 2-4? No, 2-4 is not within. Wait, edges: 1-3 (within), 3-2 (within), 2-4 (no), 4-5 (no), 5-1 (no). So two edges within {1,2,3}: 1-3 and 3-2.\n\nSo yes, you can have two edges within.\n\nIs three edges possible? Let's see.\n\nSuppose we have a cycle that goes 1-2-3-1-... Wait, no, can't repeat nodes.\n\nAlternatively, 1-2-3-4-5-2-... No, can't repeat 2.\n\nAlternatively, maybe 1-2-3-4-5-3-... No, can't repeat 3.\n\nHmm, seems difficult. Let me try: 1-2-3-4-5-1. That has two edges within {1,2,3}: 1-2 and 2-3.\n\nAlternatively, 1-2-3-5-4-1. Edges within: 1-2, 2-3, 4-1? No, 4-1 is not within. So two edges: 1-2 and 2-3.\n\nAnother try: 1-3-2-4-5-1. Edges within: 1-3, 3-2, and 5-1? No, 5-1 is not within. So two edges: 1-3 and 3-2.\n\nAlternatively, 1-2-4-3-5-1: edges within: 1-2 and 3-5? No, only 1-2.\n\nHmm, so maximum number of edges within {1,2,3} in a Hamiltonian cycle is 2. So possible numbers are 1 or 2.\n\nTherefore, for any Hamiltonian cycle in G', the number of edges within {1,2,3} is either 1 or 2.\n\nTherefore, when we want to insert nodes 6 and 7 into the cycle, we need to choose edges within {1,2,3} to insert them into. Let's consider the two cases separately: when the original cycle has 1 or 2 edges within {1,2,3}.\n\nCase 1: The original cycle in G' has exactly 1 edge within {1,2,3}.\n\nCase 2: The original cycle in G' has exactly 2 edges within {1,2,3}.\n\nWe can compute the number of Hamiltonian cycles in each case, then for each case, compute the number of ways to insert 6 and 7 into the cycle.\n\nFirst, let's compute the number of Hamiltonian cycles in K5 with exactly 1 edge within {1,2,3}, and those with exactly 2 edges within {1,2,3}.\n\nFirst, total number of Hamiltonian cycles in K5 is 12 (as (5-1)!/2 = 24/2 = 12).\n\nNow, to compute how many have exactly 1 edge within {1,2,3}, and how many have exactly 2 edges.\n\nLet me think about how to count them.\n\nFirst, let's fix the nodes {1,2,3} and consider the edges among them.\n\nSuppose we want a Hamiltonian cycle with exactly k edges within {1,2,3}. Let's denote the three nodes as A,B,C (for 1,2,3) and the other two as D and E (for 4 and 5).\n\nTo form a cycle with exactly k edges among A,B,C, we need to arrange the nodes such that exactly k of the edges between A,B,C are used, and the remaining edges go through D and E.\n\nLet me consider k=1 and k=2.\n\nFirst, for k=2:\n\nIf a cycle has two edges within {A,B,C}, then those two edges must form a path of two edges among A,B,C, and the remaining nodes D and E must be connected in such a way that the entire cycle is formed.\n\nFor example, suppose the two edges within {A,B,C} are A-B and B-C. Then the cycle could go A-B-C-D-E-A, but that uses only one edge within {A,B,C} (A-B and B-C are two edges). Wait, actually, in the cycle A-B-C-D-E-A, the edges within {A,B,C} are A-B and B-C, so that's two edges. Then the remaining edges are C-D, D-E, E-A. But E-A is not within {A,B,C}. So that cycle would have two edges within {A,B,C} and three edges outside.\n\nAlternatively, another cycle: A-B-D-C-E-A. Edges within {A,B,C}: A-B and C-E? No, C-E is not within. Wait, edges: A-B (within), B-D (outside), D-C (outside), C-E (outside), E-A (outside). So only one edge within.\n\nHmm, perhaps the count is non-trivial.\n\nAlternatively, let's model this as a graph and use combinatorial counting.\n\nFirst, consider the three nodes A,B,C and the two nodes D,E.\n\nWe need to form a Hamiltonian cycle that uses exactly m edges among A,B,C (m=1 or 2).\n\nLet me consider m=2 first.\n\nTo have two edges among A,B,C in the cycle, those two edges must form a path of two edges (i.e., three consecutive nodes in the cycle are A-B-C or B-A-C, etc.), and the remaining nodes D and E must be inserted into the cycle such that they don't create additional edges within {A,B,C}.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to use inclusion-exclusion or some other method.\n\nAlternatively, let me think of constructing such cycles.\n\nCase 1: Two edges within {A,B,C}.\n\nSuppose the two edges within {A,B,C} are between A-B and B-C. Then the three nodes A,B,C are connected in a path A-B-C. To form a cycle including D and E, we need to connect this path to D and E.\n\nThe cycle must continue from C to either D or E, then traverse the remaining nodes and return to A.\n\nFor example:\n\nA-B-C-D-E-A: This uses edges A-B, B-C (within), C-D, D-E, E-A. The edges within {A,B,C} are two, and the rest are outside. This is valid.\n\nAnother possibility: A-B-C-E-D-A: edges A-B, B-C (within), C-E, E-D, D-A. Also valid.\n\nAlternatively, starting with A-B-C, then going to D first, then E, then back to A: as above.\n\nAlternatively, the path could be in a different order, like A-C-B, but that's similar.\n\nWait, but the path A-B-C can be in any order as long as it's two edges within {A,B,C}. There are 3 possible paths of two edges among A,B,C: A-B-C, A-C-B, B-A-C, B-C-A, C-A-B, C-B-A. Wait, actually, the number of paths of two edges among three nodes is 3: since for three nodes, the number of paths of length 2 is 3 (each node can be the middle node). For example:\n\n- A-B-C\n\n- A-C-B\n\n- B-A-C\n\nWait, no, actually, the number of distinct paths of two edges among three nodes is 3. Each such path uses two edges and visits all three nodes.\n\nEach such path can be traversed in two directions, but in a cycle, the direction is determined by the cycle's orientation.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to think that for each pair of edges forming a path of two edges among A,B,C, there are 3 possible such paths (each corresponding to a permutation of the three nodes with two edges). For each such path, we can insert the D and E nodes into the cycle.\n\nSuppose we have a path P = [X-Y-Z], where X,Y,Z are A,B,C in some order, using two edges. Then, to form a cycle including D and E, we need to insert D and E into the remaining positions.\n\nThe cycle must start at X, go through Y, Z, then proceed to either D or E, then the other, and then back to X. However, since it's a cycle, we have to connect the ends appropriately.\n\nActually, perhaps it's easier to think of the cycle as starting at X, going through Y, Z, then inserting D and E in some order between Z and X.\n\nWait, for example, take the path A-B-C. To form a cycle with D and E, we can go A-B-C-D-E-A, or A-B-C-E-D-A, or A-B-C-D-A-E? Wait, no, that would repeat A. Hmm, need to ensure all nodes are visited once.\n\nWait, the full cycle must include all five nodes. If we have the path A-B-C, then we need to insert D and E into the cycle such that after C, we go to D or E, then to the other, then back to A.\n\nSo possible insertions:\n\n1. A-B-C-D-E-A\n\n2. A-B-C-E-D-A\n\nThese are two distinct cycles (since the order of D and E matters).\n\nAdditionally, the path could be in the reverse direction: C-B-A-D-E-C, but since we are considering undirected cycles, this is the same as A-B-C-D-E-A in reverse, which is considered the same cycle.\n\nWait, but in undirected cycles, the direction doesn't matter, so A-B-C-D-E-A is the same as A-E-D-C-B-A. Therefore, for each path, there are two distinct cycles depending on the insertion of D and E.\n\nWait, in the example above with path A-B-C, inserting D and E can be done in two different ways: D then E or E then D. Thus, two different cycles.\n\nSince the path A-B-C can be traversed in two directions (A-B-C or C-B-A), but since we are considering undirected cycles, those are the same cycle. Wait, no, if the path is part of the cycle, the direction is determined by the cycle's orientation.\n\nActually, since we are counting undirected cycles, the direction doesn't matter, so the two insertions (D-E or E-D) are different because they result in different edge sequences, but as undirected cycles, they are the same? Wait, no, in an undirected cycle, the cycle A-B-C-D-E-A is the same as A-E-D-C-B-A, but the sequence of nodes is different in direction. However, since we are counting undirected cycles, these are considered the same cycle. Therefore, inserting D and E in either order along the path would actually result in the same cycle when considering undirectedness.\n\nWait, this is confusing. Let me clarify:\n\nIn an undirected cycle, the cycle is a set of edges, not a sequence with direction. Therefore, the cycle A-B-C-D-E-A is the same as A-E-D-C-B-A because the edges are the same: {AB, BC, CD, DE, EA}, regardless of direction.\n\nHowever, when we insert D and E into the path A-B-C, the two different insertion orders (D then E vs E then D) result in different edge sets? Let's see:\n\nInserting D then E gives edges BC, CD, DE, EA (if we close the cycle back to A). Wait, no, in the cycle A-B-C-D-E-A, the edges are AB, BC, CD, DE, EA.\n\nAlternatively, if we insert E then D, we get A-B-C-E-D-A, which has edges AB, BC, CE, ED, DA. These are different edges: CE and ED vs CD and DE. Therefore, these are different cycles.\n\nAh, right! Because inserting D after C and then E would create edges CD and DE, whereas inserting E after C and then D would create CE and ED. Since CE and ED are different edges than CD and DE (unless CD=CE, which they aren't), these are different cycles.\n\nTherefore, for each path (like A-B-C), inserting D and E in either order gives two distinct cycles.\n\nSince the path A-B-C can be part of multiple cycles depending on where D and E are placed, but for each such path, there are two different cycles.\n\nHowever, the path A-B-C can be part of multiple cycles depending on how we route through D and E.\n\nWait, actually, for the path A-B-C, there are two choices for where to insert D and E: after C or somewhere else? Wait no, the path A-B-C is already three nodes, and we need to insert D and E into the remaining two spots to complete the cycle.\n\nActually, once you have the path A-B-C, the remaining nodes D and E must be placed in the two \"gaps\" between the existing path nodes and the start/end.\n\nWait, the cycle must include all five nodes, so starting at A, going through B, C, then we have to go to either D or E, then to the other, then back to A. So the two options are:\n\n1. A-B-C-D-E-A\n\n2. A-B-C-E-D-A\n\nThese are two distinct cycles because the edges between C and D/E are different.\n\nTherefore, for each such path (A-B-C, A-C-B, B-A-C, etc.), we get two cycles.\n\nBut how many distinct paths are there?\n\nThe number of distinct paths of two edges among A,B,C is 3, as follows:\n\n1. A-B-C\n\n2. A-C-B\n\n3. B-A-C (which is same as A-B-C in reverse)\n\nWait, no, in undirected paths, A-B-C is the same as C-B-A, but since we are considering the path as part of the cycle, the direction matters in terms of where you insert D and E.\n\nActually, maybe the number of distinct paths (regardless of direction) is 3: the three possible pairs of edges that form a path covering all three nodes.\n\nEach such path can be oriented in two directions, but since we are building an undirected cycle, perhaps each path corresponds to two different orientations, but when combined with the insertion of D and E, they give different cycles.\n\nAlternatively, let's think of the three possible paths:\n\n1. A-B-C\n\n2. A-C-B\n\n3. B-C-A (which is the reverse of the first)\n\nBut since in an undirected cycle, the direction doesn't matter, the path A-B-C and B-C-A are the same in terms of the cycle structure, but when inserting D and E, they can lead to different cycles.\n\nWait, if the path is considered as A-B-C, then inserting D and E gives two cycles as above. If the path is considered as B-C-A, then inserting D and E would give similar cycles but starting from B.\n\nHowever, since we are counting undirected cycles, these would be considered the same as the previous ones. Hmm, this is getting too tangled.\n\nPerhaps it's better to calculate the number of cycles with exactly two edges within {A,B,C} by another method.\n\nEach Hamiltonian cycle with two edges within {A,B,C} must contain a path of length two among A,B,C and then weave in D and E.\n\nT