th one edge within {A,B,C}, that adds to 12, which matches.\n\nYes: 7 + 5 = 12.\n\nTherefore, in K5 (G'), there are:\n\n- 7 undirected Hamiltonian cycles with two edges within {1,2,3}\n\n- 5 undirected Hamiltonian cycles with one edge within {1,2,3}\n\nNow, returning to our problem of inserting nodes 6 and 7 into these cycles.\n\nRecall that in order to insert nodes 6 and 7 into a cycle from G', we need to choose two edges within {1,2,3} in the cycle to insert them, such that:\n\n1. Each insertion is between two nodes in {1,2,3}, so we can insert 6 or 7 there.\n\n2. The two insertions are not adjacent to each other (so that 6 and 7 are not placed next to each other).\n\n3. Each insertion must be between nodes that are connected by an edge in the original cycle (so that the new edges 6-x and x-7 are valid).\n\nWait, actually, the insertion must be between two adjacent nodes u and v in the original cycle, and we insert the new node between them, so the new edges are u-new and new-v, which must be allowed by the original graph.\n\nBut since we are inserting 6 or 7, which can only connect to nodes in {1,2,3}, the nodes u and v must both be in {1,2,3}, and the new node (6 or 7) must be placed between them, so that the new node is connected to both u and v, which are in {1,2,3}, which is allowed.\n\nTherefore, for each original cycle in G', the number of ways to insert 6 and 7 is equal to the number of pairs of edges within {1,2,3} in the cycle, such that the two edges are not adjacent in the cycle.\n\nWait, because inserting 6 into one edge and 7 into another edge requires that those edges are not adjacent, so that 6 and 7 are not placed next to each other in the cycle.\n\nTherefore, for a given cycle in G', with m edges within {1,2,3}, the number of ways to insert 6 and 7 is equal to the number of ways to choose two non-adjacent edges among the m edges within {1,2,3}, and then assign 6 and 7 to those two edges (in order?).\n\nWait, let's formalize:\n\nSuppose in a given