hen each directed Hamiltonian cycle (i.e., a cyclic permutation with a specific direction) is unique and counted separately.\n\nIn graph theory terms, the number of directed Hamiltonian cycles in a graph is equal to the number of undirected Hamiltonian cycles multiplied by 2 (for direction) multiplied by n (the number of nodes) because you can start at any node and go in either direction. Wait, actually, no: for an undirected cycle, the number of directed cycles is 2n, since for each undirected cycle, you can traverse it in two directions, and for each direction, you can start at any of the n nodes. But in terms of counting distinct sequences, if you consider sequences where the starting point matters and the direction matters, then yes, each undirected cycle corresponds to 2n directed cycles. But in our problem, the arrangements are considered different if they are rotations or reflections, so starting point and direction matter. Therefore, the total number of arrangements is equal to the number of directed Hamiltonian cycles in the graph.\n\nHowever, in our case, since the graph is undirected, each edge is bidirectional, so a directed Hamiltonian cycle would correspond to choosing a starting point and a direction, so for each undirected cycle, there are 2n directed versions (n starting points and 2 directions). Therefore, the total number of directed Hamiltonian cycles is 2n times the number of undirected Hamiltonian cycles. Wait, actually, let me think again.\n\nSuppose there is one undirected Hamiltonian cycle. Then, how many directed Hamiltonian cycles does it correspond to? Well, in an undirected cycle, you can traverse it in two directions. For each direction, you can start at any of the n nodes. Therefore, for each undirected cycle, there are 2n directed Hamiltonian cycles. Therefore, if we let C be the number of undirected Hamiltonian cycles, then the number of directed Hamiltonian cycles is 2nC. However, in our problem, we are to count the number of circul