hbors besides 7, which are 1,3,4,5,6. But 1 is already connected to 7 and we need to form a cycle, so we can't go back to 1 yet. Therefore, from 2, the next node must be 3,4,5, or 6. Let's consider each possibility.\n\nCase 1: After 1-7-2, the next node is 3.\n\nSo the path so far is 1-7-2-3.\n\nNow, from 3, the next node must be adjacent to 3 and not yet visited. The neighbors of 3 are [1,2,4,5,6]. Already visited: 1,2,7. So available neighbors: 4,5,6.\n\nSo from 3, we can go to 4,5, or 6.\n\nSubcase 1a: 1-7-2-3-4\n\nFrom 4, neighbors are [1,2,3,5]. Already visited: 1,2,3,7. So available: 5.\n\nWait, 4 is connected to 1,2,3,5. 1,2,3 are already visited, so only 5 is available.\n\nSo next must go to 5.\n\nSubsubcase 1a1: 1-7-2-3-4-5\n\nFrom 5, neighbors are [1,2,3,4]. Already visited: 1,2,3,4,7. So available neighbors: none except maybe 5 itself? Wait, no. Wait, 5 is connected to 1,2,3,4. All those are already visited except... Wait, all neighbors of 5 are already in the path except perhaps none. Wait, 5's neighbors are 1,2,3,4, which are all already visited except that we haven't visited 6 yet. Wait, but 5 is not connected to 6. So from 5, there are no available nodes to go to except those already visited, which is a problem. So this path leads to a dead end. Therefore, this subsubcase is invalid.\n\nSubsubcase 1a2: Wait, no, actually, in Subcase 1a, after 1-7-2-3-4, the next node must be 5, as that's the only available neighbor. But then from 5, we can't proceed further because all neighbors are already visited except maybe 6, but 5 is not connected to 6. Therefore, this path is invalid.\n\nTherefore, Subcase 1a (going 3-4) leads to a dead end. So we must backtrack.\n\nSubcase 1b: From 3, go to 5 instead of 4.\n\nSo path: 1-7-2-3-5\n\nFrom 5, neighbors are [1,2,3,4]. Already visited: 1,2,3,7. So available: 4.\n\nTherefore, next must go to 4.\n\nSubsubcase 1b1: 1-7-2-3-5-4\n\nFrom 4, neighbors are [1,2,3,5]. Already visited: 1,2,3,5,7. So available: none except maybe 6? Wait, 4 is connected to 1,2,3,5. All those are visited except none. Wait, 4 is not connected to 6. So from 4, there are no unvisited neighbors. So this path also leads to a dead end.\n\nHmm, problem again. So 1-7-2-3-5-4 is stuck.\n\nSubcase 1c: From 3, go to 6.\n\nSo path: 1-7-2-3-6\n\nFrom 6, neighbors are [1,2,3]. All of these are already visited except... Wait, 6 is connected to 1,2,3. All those are already in the path, so from 6, there are no unvisited neighbors. Therefore, this path also dead-ends.\n\nTherefore, Subcase 1b and 1c also lead to dead ends. Therefore, Subcase 1 (after 1-7-2-3) all lead to dead ends. Therefore, the path starting with 1-7-2-3 is invalid.\n\nCase 2: After 1-7-2, the next node is 4.\n\nSo path: 1-7-2-4\n\nFrom 4, neighbors are [1,2,3,5]. Already visited: 1,2,7. So available: 3,5.\n\nSubcase 2a: From 4 go to 3.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-4-3\n\nFrom 3, neighbors are [1,2,4,5,6]. Already visited: 1,2,4,7. So available: 5,6.\n\nSubsubcase 2a1: From 3 go to 5.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-4-3-5\n\nFrom 5, neighbors [1,2,3,4]. Already visited: 1,2,3,4,7. Available: none except maybe 6? But 5 is not connected to 6. So stuck again.\n\nSubsubcase 2a2: From 3 go to 6.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-4-3-6\n\nFrom 6, neighbors [1,2,3]. All visited. So stuck.\n\nThus, Subcase 2a (going 4-3) leads to dead ends.\n\nSubcase 2b: From 4 go to 5.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-4-5\n\nFrom 5, neighbors [1,2,3,4]. Already visited: 1,2,4,7. Available: 3.\n\nSo next must go to 3.\n\nSubsubcase 2b1: 1-7-2-4-5-3\n\nFrom 3, neighbors [1,2,4,5,6]. Already visited: 1,2,4,5,7. Available: 6.\n\nSo go to 6.\n\nSubsubsubcase 2b1a: 1-7-2-4-5-3-6\n\nFrom 6, neighbors [1,2,3]. All visited except none. So stuck.\n\nWait, 6 is connected to 1,2,3. All those are already visited. So yes, stuck.\n\nAlternatively, from 3, after going to 5, then to 3, can we go somewhere else? Wait, no, after 5-3, the next from 3 must be 6 since 1,2,4,5 are already visited. So indeed stuck.\n\nTherefore, this path also dead-ends.\n\nTherefore, Subcase 2b also leads to a dead end.\n\nTherefore, Case 2 (starting with 1-7-2-4) also leads to dead ends.\n\nCase 3: After 1-7-2, the next node is 5.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-5\n\nFrom 5, neighbors [1,2,3,4]. Already visited: 1,2,7. So available: 3,4.\n\nSubcase 3a: From 5 go to 3.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-5-3\n\nFrom 3, neighbors [1,2,4,5,6]. Already visited: 1,2,5,7. Available: 4,6.\n\nSubsubcase 3a1: From 3 go to 4.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-5-3-4\n\nFrom 4, neighbors [1,2,3,5]. Already visited: 1,2,3,5,7. Available: none except maybe 6? But 4 isn't connected to 6. So stuck.\n\nSubsubcase 3a2: From 3 go to 6.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-5-3-6\n\nFrom 6, neighbors [1,2,3]. All visited. Stuck.\n\nThus, Subcase 3a dead ends.\n\nSubcase 3b: From 5 go to 4.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-5-4\n\nFrom 4, neighbors [1,2,3,5]. Already visited: 1,2,5,7. Available: 3.\n\nSubsubcase 3b1: From 4 go to 3.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-5-4-3\n\nFrom 3, neighbors [1,2,4,5,6]. Already visited: 1,2,4,5,7. Available: 6.\n\nGo to 6.\n\nSubsubsubcase 3b1a: 1-7-2-5-4-3-6\n\nFrom 6, neighbors [1,2,3]. All visited. Stuck.\n\nTherefore, this path also dead-ends.\n\nThus, Case 3 (starting with 1-7-2-5) also leads to dead ends.\n\nCase 4: After 1-7-2, the next node is 6.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-6\n\nFrom 6, neighbors [1,2,3]. Already visited: 1,2,7. So available: 3.\n\nSubcase 4a: From 6 go to 3.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-6-3\n\nFrom 3, neighbors [1,2,4,5,6]. Already visited: 1,2,6,7. Available: 4,5.\n\nSubsubcase 4a1: From 3 go to 4.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-6-3-4\n\nFrom 4, neighbors [1,2,3,5]. Already visited: 1,2,3,6,7. Available: 5.\n\nGo to 5.\n\nSubsubsubcase 4a1a: 1-7-2-6-3-4-5\n\nFrom 5, neighbors [1,2,3,4]. Already visited: 1,2,3,4,6,7. Available: none except maybe 5 is connected to 5? No, need to go to an unvisited node, but all are visited. Wait, 5 is connected to 1,2,3,4—all visited. So stuck.\n\nSubsubcase 4a2: From 3 go to 5.\n\nPath: 1-7-2-6-3-5\n\nFrom 5, neighbors [1,2,3,4]. Already visited: 1,2,3,6,7. Available: 4.\n\nGo to 4.\n\nSubsubsubcase 4a2a: 1-7-2-6-3-5-4\n\nFrom 4, neighbors [1,2,3,5]. All visited except none. So stuck.\n\nTherefore, both subsubcases lead to dead ends.\n\nSubcase 4b: From 6, no other options since only 3 is available. So only Subcase 4a, which leads to dead ends. So Case 4 also dead ends.\n\nHmm, so all cases where we start with 1-7-2 and then go to 3,4,5,6 all lead to dead ends? That can't be right. Maybe I made a mistake in my reasoning.\n\nWait a second, perhaps I need to consider that after some steps, we can return to a previously visited node but not yet completing the cycle? Wait, no, in a Hamiltonian cycle, we must visit each node exactly once except the start/end node which is visited twice. Wait, no, in a cycle, each node is visited exactly once except the starting/ending node which is visited twice. Wait, actually, in a cycle, every node is visited exactly once, and the cycle closes by connecting the last node back to the first. Therefore, in the path we're building, we cannot revisit any node until the very end when we connect back to the start.\n\nTherefore, in all the above cases, when we reached a node with no available unvisited neighbors, that meant the path couldn't be extended to include all nodes, hence those paths are invalid. So according to this, there are no Hamiltonian cycles starting with 1-7-2? That can't be, since the problem states that such arrangements exist, otherwise the answer would be zero, which is unlikely.\n\nWait, perhaps I made an error in the adjacency lists or in the logic.\n\nLet me double-check the adjacency lists:\n\nNumber 7: connected only to 1 and 2. Correct.\n\nNumber 6: connected to 1,2,3. Correct.\n\nNumber 5: connected to 1,2,3,4. Correct.\n\nNumber 4: connected to 1,2,3,5. Correct.\n\nNumber 3: connected to 1,2,4,5,6. Correct.\n\nNumber 2: connected to 1,3,4,5,6,7. Correct.\n\nNumber 1: connected to everyone except itself. Correct.\n\nOkay, adjacency lists are correct.\n\nHmm, so according to this, starting with 1-7-2, any continuation leads to a dead end