anything except maybe 7, but 7 isn't in H)\n\n3: connected to 1,2,4,5,6 (since 3×7 isn't in H, but 3's connections in H are those with products ≤20: 3×1=3, 3×2=6, 3×4=12, 3×5=15, 3×6=18—all ≤20, and 3×7 is excluded because 7 not in H. So yes, 3 connected to 1,2,4,5,6.\n\n4: connected to 1,2,3,5 (since 4×6=24 >20, so 4 not connected to 6 in H)\n\n5: connected to 1,2,3,4 (5×6=30>20, so 5 not connected to 6 in H)\n\n6: connected to 1,2,3 (6×4=24>20, 6×5=30>20, so 6 connected only to 1,2,3 in H)\n\nSo adjacency lists for H:\n\n1: [2,3,4,5,6]\n\n2: [1,3,4,5,6]\n\n3: [1,2,4,5,6]\n\n4: [1,2,3,5]\n\n5: [1,2,3,4]\n\n6: [1,2,3]\n\nWe need the number of Hamiltonian paths from 1 to 2 in this graph.\n\nThis is a smaller problem, perhaps manageable by hand.\n\nWe can use recursion with memoization or backtracking.\n\nLet me denote the nodes as 1,2,3,4,5,6, and we need paths starting at 1, ending at 2, visiting all nodes exactly once.\n\nLet me attempt to count them systematically.\n\nLet me consider the nodes in order, keeping track of the current node and the visited nodes.\n\nWe can represent the state as (current_node, visited_nodes), but with 6 nodes, there are 6! = 720 possible paths, but with constraints, it's manageable with pruning.\n\nAlternatively, since the graph is small, let's try to enumerate the paths step by step.\n\nStart at node 1.\n\nFrom 1, we can go to 2,3,4,5, or 6.\n\nBut since we need to end at 2, and we have to visit all nodes, let's consider each first step:\n\nCase 1: Start 1-2\n\nBut then we've already ended at 2, but we haven't visited all nodes. So this is invalid.\n\nCase 2: Start 1-3\n\nThen from 3, need to go to unvisited nodes: 2,4,5,6 (since 1 is visited).\n\nSubcase 2a: 1-3-2\n\nBut then we've reached 2 early, and have unvisited nodes 4,5,6. Can't end at 2 yet. So invalid.\n\nSubcase 2b: 1-3-4\n\nFrom 4, unvisited: 2,5,6.\n\nSubsubcase 2b1: 1-3-4-2\n\nThen we've reached 2 with unvisited nodes 5,6. Invalid.\n\nSubsubcase 2b2: 1-3-4-5\n\nFrom 5, 