r number, so all products are ≤7, which is ≤20. So all pairs with 1 are allowed.\n\nPairs involving 2: 2×3=6, 2×4=8, 2×5=10, 2×6=12, 2×7=14. All of these are ≤20. Wait, 2×7=14 which is okay. 2×6=12, okay, etc. So all pairs with 2 except... Wait, actually, all pairs with 2 are allowed, since the maximum product with 2 is 14 (with 7), which is still ≤20. So all pairs with 2 are okay.\n\nPairs involving 3: 3×4=12, 3×5=15, 3×6=18, 3×7=21. So 3×7=21 is over 20, so the pair (3,7) is not allowed. So pairs with 3: allowed with 1,2,4,5,6 but not with 7.\n\nPairs involving 4: 4×5=20, which is okay; 4×6=24, over; 4×7=28, over. So 4 can be paired with 1,2,3,5 but not with 6 or 7.\n\nPairs involving 5: 5×6=30, over; 5×7=35, over; 5×4=20, which is okay, so 5 can be paired with 1,2,3,4 but not with 6 or 7.\n\nPairs involving 6: 6× anything except 1? Wait, 6×1=6, which is okay, but 6×2=12, okay, 6×3=18, okay, 6×4=24, over; 6×5=30, over; 6×7=42, over. So 6 can be paired with 1,2,3 but not with 4,5,7.\n\nPairs involving 7: 7×1=7, okay; 7×2=14, okay; 7×3=21, over; 7×4=28, over; 7×5=35, over; 7×6=42, over. So 7 can only be paired with 1 and 2.\n\nOkay, so to summarize, the allowed adjacency pairs are as follows:\n\nFor each number, list its allowed neighbors:\n\n1: can be adjacent to anyone except... none, since 1×n = n ≤7 ≤20. So neighbors: 2,3,4,5,6,7.\n\nWait, hold on: 1 can be adjacent to all numbers 2 through 7, because 1×k = k, and since the maximum k is 7, which is ≤20. So yes, 1 is adjacent to everyone.\n\n2: can be adjacent to everyone except... no, as above, 2 can be adjacent to all numbers except... Wait, 2×7=14 which is allowed, so actually 2 can be adjacent to everyone. Wait, 2×7=14 is allowed, so 2 can be adjacent to all numbers except... none? Wait, 2× any number from 1 to 7, the products are 2, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, so all of those are ≤20. Therefore, 2 can be adjacent to everyone.\n\n3: can be adjacent to all except 7. So 3 can be adjacent to 1,2,4,5,6.\n\n4: can be adjacent to all except 6 and 7. So 4 can be adjacent to 1,2,3,5.\n\n5: can be adjacent to all except 6 and 7. So 5 can be adjacent to 1,2,3,4.\n\n6: can be adjacent to all except 4,5,7. So 6 can be adjacent to 1,2,3.\n\n7: can be adjacent to all except 3,4,5,6. So 7 can only be adjacent to 1 and 2.\n\nSo in other words, the adjacency restrictions are as above.\n\nTherefore, in constructing the circular arrangement, each number must be placed next to numbers that are in its allowed neighbor list.\n\nGiven that, this is a problem of counting the number of circular permutations of the numbers 1 through 7 where each number is adjacent only to numbers in its allowed neighbor list.\n\nThis seems similar to a graph theory problem where the numbers 1 through 7 are vertices, and edges connect numbers that can be adjacent in the arrangement. Then, we are to count the number of Hamiltonian circuits in this graph, considering that the circle is directed (since rotations and reflections are different, so direction matters? Wait, actually, in an undirected graph, a Hamiltonian circuit is a cycle that visits each vertex exactly once, and in an undirected graph, the cycle can be traversed in two directions. However, in our case, since rotations and reflections are considered different, does that mean that the direction matters?\n\nWait, in a circular arrangement, the direction (clockwise vs counterclockwise) would be considered different arrangements if reflections are considered different. Since reflections are considered different here, then direction does matter. So, in graph terms, if we model the problem as a directed graph where each edge from a to b is allowed only if b is in the allowed neighbors of a, then the number of Hamiltonian cycles would be the number of directed Hamiltonian cycles. But actually, in an undirected graph, a Hamiltonian cycle can be traversed in two directions, so if we consider the direction as important (since reflections are different), then each undirect