 end of the middle row, which is an edge center? Maybe my terminology is conflicting. \n\nWait, let's clarify:\n\nThe four edge centers are the pads that are on the edges but not corners. In a 3x3 grid, the edge centers are:\n\nTop edge: (1,2)\n\nBottom edge: (3,2)\n\nLeft edge: (2,1)\n\nRight edge: (2,3)\n\nYes, those are the four edge centers. So removing those four is one configuration.\n\nThen the middle row is three pads: (2,1), (2,2), (2,3). So when we remove the middle row plus one more, that's different. \n\nAdditionally, when we remove the two corners (1,3) and (3,1), plus the center (2,2), and one more pad, say (1,2) or (3,2), that's two configurations. \n\nEarlier, I also had the configuration where we removed (2,1), (2,3), (1,2), (3,2). Wait, is that the same as the four edge centers? Let me check:\n\n(2,1), (2,3), (1,2), (3,2):\n\n- (2,1) is the left edge center\n\n- (2,3) is the right edge center\n\n- (1,2) is the top edge center\n\n- (3,2) is the bottom edge center\n\nYes! So that is exactly the four edge centers. So that was actually the same as configuration 2 above. \n\nTherefore, that example was actually the four edge centers. \n\nAnother configuration was removing (1,3), (3,1), (2,2), and (1,2). Let me see what that looks like:\n\n(1,3) is the top-right corner,\n\n(3,1) is the bottom-left corner,\n\n(2,2) is the center,\n\nand (1,2) is the top edge center.\n\nSo this is removing two corners, the center, and one edge center. \n\nSimilarly, removing (1,3), (3,1), (2,2), and (3,2) is the other one. \n\nSo those are two distinct configurations. \n\nAdditionally, we had the case where we removed the middle row plus one edge center: four configurations. \n\nAre there any other configurations? \n\nLet me think of another possibility. Suppose we remove the two corners (1,3) and (3,1), plus the two edge centers (2,1) and (2,3). Let's see:\n\nRemoving (1,3), (3,1), (2,1), (2,3). \n\nRemaining pads:\n\nRow 1: (1,1), (1,2)\n\nRow 2: (2,2)\n\nRow 3: (3,2), (3,3)\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to (1,2) and (2,1) [removed], so only to (1,2).\n\n(1,2) connected to (1,1) and (2,2).\n\n(2,2) connected to (1,2), (2,1) [removed], (2,3) [removed], and (3,2).\n\n(3,2) connected to (2,2) and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to (3,2).\n\nSo the connected components:\n\nStarting at (1,1):\n\n(1,1) → (1,2) → (2,2) → (3,2) → (3,3). \n\nTherefore, (1,1) and (3,3) are still connected through this path. So that's not a valid configuration. \n\nHmm, so that doesn't work. \n\nWhat if we remove the two corners (1,3) and (3,1), plus the center (2,2), and the top edge center (1,2)? We already considered that, which was valid. \n\nAnother idea: remove the center (2,2) and the three adjacent edge centers: (1,2), (3,2), and either (2,1) or (2,3). Let's try removing (2,2), (1,2), (3,2), and (2,1):\n\nRemaining pads:\n\nRow 1: (1,1), (1,3)\n\nRow 2: (2,3)\n\nRow 3: (3,1), (3,2), (3,3)\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to (1,2) [removed] and (2,1) [removed], so isolated.\n\n(1,3) connected to (1,2) [removed] and (2,3).\n\n(2,3) connected to (1,3), (2,2) [removed], and (3,3).\n\n(3,1) connected to (2,1) [removed] and (3,2).\n\n(3,2) connected to (3,1), (3,3), and (2,2) [removed].\n\n(3,3) connected to (3,2) and (2,3).\n\nSo components:\n\n- { (1,1) }\n\n- { (1,3), (2,3), (3,3), (3,2) }\n\n- { (3,1) }\n\nThus, (1,1) is isolated, and (3,3) is in the big component with others, so disconnected from (1,1). So this is another valid configuration. \n\nSimilarly, removing (2,2), (1,2), (3,2), and (2,3) would be symmetrical on the other side:\n\nRemaining pads:\n\nRow 1: (1,1), (1,2) [but (1,2) is removed? Wait, no:\n\nWait, removing (2,2), (1,2), (3,2), (2,3):\n\nRemaining pads:\n\nRow 1: (1,1), (1,3) [since (1,2) is removed]\n\nRow 2: (2,1)\n\nRow 3: (3,1), (3,3) [since (3,2) is removed]\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to (1,2) [removed] and (2,1).\n\n(1,3) connected to (1,2) [removed] and (2,3) [removed], so isolated.\n\n(2,1) connected to (1,1), (2,2) [removed], and (3,1).\n\n(3,1) connected to (2,1) and (3,2) [removed], so connected to (2,1).\n\n(3,3) connected to (3,2) [removed] and (2,3) [removed], so isolated.\n\nThus components:\n\n- { (1,1), (2,1), (3,1) }\n\n- { (1,3) }\n\n- { (3,3) }\n\nTherefore, (1,1) and (3,3) are disconnected. So that's another valid configuration. \n\nTherefore, removing the center (2,2) plus three edge centers: specifically, either the top, bottom, and left, or top, bottom, and right. \n\nSo that gives two more configurations. \n\nSo now we have:\n\n1. Middle row plus one edge center: 4 configurations.\n\n2. Four edge centers: 1 configuration.\n\n3. Two corners + center + one edge center: 2 configurations.\n\n4. Center + three edge centers (left, right, and either top or bottom): 2 configurations.\n\nThat's 4 + 1 + 2 + 2 = 9 configurations so far. \n\nAre there more? Let's see.\n\nAnother possibility: remove the center (2,2) and the two corners (1,3) and (3,1), plus one more pad. Let's see:\n\nRemoving (2,2), (1,3), (3,1), and say (1,2). That was already counted in the third category. \n\nAlternatively, removing (2,2), (1,3), (3,1), and (3,2). That would be two corners, center, and bottom edge center. Let's see if that's valid:\n\nRemaining pads:\n\nRow 1: (1,1), (1,2)\n\nRow 2: (2,1), (2,3)\n\nRow 3: (3,3) [since (3,1) and (3,2) are removed? Wait:\n\nWait, removing (2,2), (1,3), (3,1), (3,2). So:\n\nRow 1: (1,1), (1,2) [since (1,3) removed]\n\nRow 2: (2,1), (2,3) [since (2,2) removed]\n\nRow 3: (3,3) [since (3,1) and (3,2) removed]\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to (1,2) and (2,1).\n\n(1,2) connected to (1,1) and (2,2) [removed].\n\n(2,1) connected to (1,1), (2,2) [removed], and (3,1) [removed].\n\n(2,3) connected to (1,3) [removed], (2,2) [removed], and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to (2,3) and (3,2) [removed].\n\nSo components:\n\n- { (1,1), (1,2), (2,1) }\n\n- { (2,3), (3,3) }\n\nThus, (1,1) and (3,3) are disconnected. So this is another configuration. \n\nWait, but this is similar to previous ones. Specifically, removing two corners (1,3) and (3,1), the center (2,2), and one edge center (3,2). So this is part of the third category: two corners + center + one edge center. Since we had two configurations there: one with (1,2) and one with (3,2). Wait, actually in this case, we removed (3,2), so that would be the second one in that category. Wait, but in the earlier third category, I had two configurations: one with adding (1,2) and one with adding (3,2). So this is the second one. \n\nSo actually, this is already counted in the third category. \n\nHmm. Let's see if there are other possibilities where we remove the center and two edge centers and another pad. \n\nSuppose we remove the center (2,2), plus the top edge (1,2), the bottom edge (3,2), and the left edge (2,1). That's four pads. \n\nWait, that's removing (2,2), (1,2), (3,2), (2,1). \n\nWhich is the same as one of the fourth category examples above. \n\nYes, that was one of the configurations in the fourth category (center plus three edge centers: left, top, bottom). \n\nAlternatively, removing center, top, bottom, and right edge: (2,2), (1,2), (3,2), (2,3). That's the other one in the fourth category. \n\nAnother idea: removing the center and the four edge centers? But that would be five pads, which is over the limit. \n\nAlternatively, removing the center and three non-adjacent edge centers? Not sure. \n\nWait, let's think of another configuration: removing the two middle edge centers and the two side edge centers? Not sure. \n\nAlternatively, let's think of removing the top row except (1,1). So removing (1,2) and (1,3), plus two more. Let's see:\n\nSuppose we remove (1,2), (1,3), (2,1), and (2,3). \n\nRemaining pads:\n\nRow 1: (1,1)\n\nRow 2: (2,2)\n\nRow 3: all three: (3,1), (3,2), (3,3)\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) is connected to (2,1) [removed] and (1,2) [removed], so isolated.\n\nRow 2: (2,2) connected to (2,1) [removed], (2,3) [removed], (1,2) [removed], and (3,2).\n\nRow 3: (