that the number might be growing, but I might be missing some or overcounting. Given that the total number of subsets is only 35, and we're getting up to 17, it's possible but tedious. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to calculate the total number using another method. \n\nTotal subsets S of size 5 containing (1,1) and (3,3): C(7,3) = 35.\n\nWe need to subtract the number of subsets where (1,1) and (3,3) are connected. \n\nTo compute the number of connected subsets, we can use the principle of inclusion or recursive counting, but it's complex. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can compute the number of subsets where (1,1) and (3,3) are connected, and subtract from 35. \n\nTo compute the number of connected subsets S containing (1,1) and (3,3) with |S|=5:\n\nThis is equivalent to counting all induced subgraphs on 5 vertices that include (1,1) and (3,3) and are connected. \n\nThis might be manageable by considering the different possible paths and ensuring connectivity. \n\nAlternatively, note that in the full grid, the number of paths from (1,1) to (3,3) is quite a few, but in a subset S, connectivity requires that there's at least one path within S. \n\nBut perhaps an easier way is to use the concept of \"cuts\". \n\nThe number of subsets S containing both (1,1) and (3,3) with |S|=5 where (1,1) and (3,3) are disconnected is equal to the total subsets minus subsets where they are connected. \n\nAlternatively, the number we want is equal to the number of 5-vertex induced subgraphs containing (1,1) and (3,3) that are disconnected between those two vertices. \n\nThis might be calculated using the principle of inclusion-exclusion over the possible cuts. \n\nAlternatively, recall that in the grid, the minimal vertex cut between (1,1) and (3,3) is 2 vertices, as we saw earlier. \n\nThus, any vertex cut of size 2, 3, or 4 can potentially disconnect them when combined with other vertices. \n\nBut since we are dealing with induced subgraphs of size 5, the vertex cuts must be subsets of those 5 vertices. \n\nWait, actually, in our case, the vertex cut must be a subset of the 5 remaining vertices. \n\nWait, no: the vertex cut is the set of removed vertices, but in our problem, the disconnectedness is in the remaining graph. \n\nWait, perhaps it's better to think in terms of the remaining graph S: we need that in S, (1,1) and (3,3) are in different connected components. \n\nTherefore, the set S must not contain any path from (1,1) to (3,3). \n\nTherefore, the complement of S (the removed set) must intersect every path from (1,1) to (3,3). \n\nThus, the removed set must be a vertex cover for all paths from (1,1) to (3,3). \n\nBut a vertex cover is a set of vertices that touches every edge, but here we need a set that intersects every path. \n\nThis is called a \"path cover\" or \"vertex cut\". \n\nSpecifically, the removed set must be a vertex cut between (1,1) and (3,3). \n\nThe minimal vertex cut size is 2, as we saw earlier. \n\nTherefore, any vertex cut of size 2, 3, or 4 can potentially disconnect (1,1) from (3,3) when those vertices are removed. \n\nHowever, in our problem, we are required to remove exactly 4 vertices, so the vertex cut must be of size 4, and the remaining set S (of size 5) must not include any path between (1,1) and (3,3). \n\nTherefore, the number we want is equal to the number of vertex cuts of size 4 between (1,1) and (3,3) in the original graph. \n\nBut wait, not exactly, because the vertex cut is the set of vertices whose removal disconnects the graph, but in our case, we are not removing the vertex cut; rather, we are removing a set of 4 vertices, and we want that set to be a vertex cut (i.e., its removal disconnects (1,1) from (3,3)), but the remaining set S must include (1,1) and (3,3). \n\nTherefore, the number we are seeking is exactly the number of vertex cuts of size 4 between (1,1) and (3,3) in the original grid graph. \n\nTherefore, if we can compute the number of vertex cuts of size 4 between (1,1) and (3,3), that would be our answer. \n\nNow, to compute the number of vertex cuts of size 4 between (1,1) and (3,3) in the 3x3 grid graph. \n\nVertex cuts of size 4 are sets of 4 vertices whose removal disconnects (1,1) from (3,3). \n\nWe can compute this by considering all possible 4-vertex subsets that include neither (1,1) nor (3,3) [since they must remain], and such that their removal disconnects (1,1) from (3,3). \n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to counting all 4-element subsets of the 7 non-start, non-home vertices that form a vertex cut between (1,1) and (3,3). \n\nWait, but actually, the vertex cut can include vertices from anywhere, but since we must leave (1,1) and (3,3) in the remaining set S, the vertex cut (the removed set) must not include (1,1) or (3,3). \n\nTherefore, the removed set is a subset of the 7 non-start, non-home vertices, of size 4, and it must be a vertex cut between (1,1) and (3,3). \n\nThus, the number we want is exactly the number of 4-element subsets of the 7 non-start, non-home vertices that form a vertex cut between (1,1) and (3,3). \n\nTherefore, we need to compute this number. \n\nTo compute this, perhaps we can use the following approach: \n\nFirst, list all 4-element subsets of the 7 non-start, non-home vertices and check whether their removal disconnects (1,1) from (3,3). \n\nThere are C(7,4) = 35 subsets. \n\nBut this is manageable, albeit tedious. \n\nThe non-start, non-home vertices are the 7 pads excluding (1,1) and (3,3). Let's label them for clarity:\n\nLet me list the 7 non-start, non-home pads with labels:\n\nLet me assign labels to the 7 non-start, non-home pads as follows:\n\nA: (1,2)\n\nB: (1,3)\n\nC: (2,1)\n\nD: (2,2)\n\nE: (2,3)\n\nF: (3,1)\n\nG: (3,2)\n\nSo the seven vertices are A, B, C, D, E, F, G. \n\nWe need to consider all 4-element subsets of these 7, and for each subset, check if removing them disconnects (1,1) from (3,3) in the remaining graph. \n\nThe total number is C(7,4) = 35, which is manageable with careful analysis. \n\nLet me proceed step by step. \n\nFirst, note that (1,1) is connected to A=(1,2) and C=(2,1). \n\n(3,3) is connected to E=(2,3) and G=(3,2). \n\nWe need to ensure that in the remaining graph (after removing the 4 vertices), there is no path from (1,1) to (3,3). \n\nA path from (1,1) to (3,3) must traverse through some sequence of adjacent pads. \n\nTo block all paths, the removed set must intersect every possible such path. \n\nLet me categorize the 7 non-start, non-home vertices and think of their roles:\n\n- A=(1,2): top edge center\n\n- B=(1,3): top-right corner\n\n- C=(2,1): left edge center\n\n- D=(2,2): center\n\n- E=(2,3): right edge center\n\n- F=(3,1): bottom-left corner\n\n- G=(3,2): bottom edge center\n\nNote that B and F are corners, A, C, E, G are edge centers, and D is the center.\n\nLet me consider different cases based on whether the center D is removed or not.\n\nCase 1: D is removed (i.e., D ∈ removed set).\n\nCase 2: D is not removed (D ∉ removed set).\n\nWe can handle these cases separately.\n\n**Case 1: D is removed.**\n\nWe need to choose 3 more vertices from the remaining 6 (A,B,C,E,F,G) to remove, totaling 4 vertices including D.\n\nNow, with D removed, the center is gone, which might help in disconnecting paths through the center.\n\nBut we still need to ensure that all paths from (1,1) to (3,3) pass through at least one of the removed vertices.\n\nLet me think about paths that go through the center D. Since D is removed, those paths are blocked. But there are other paths that avoid D.\n\nFor example:\n\nPath 1: (1,1) → A → B → E → (3,3)\n\nWait, let's map this:\n\n(1,1) to A=(1,2) to B=(1,3) to E=(2,3) to (3,3). Yes, that's a path of length 3.\n\nSimilarly:\n\nPath 2: (1,1) → C → F → G → (3,3)\n\n(1,1) to C=(2,1) to F=(3,1) to G=(3,2) to (3,3).\n\nPath 3: (1,1) → A → D → E → (3,3), but D is removed, so blocked.\n\nPath 4: (1,1) → C → D → G → (3,3), also blocked.\n\nPath 5: (1,1) → A → D → G → (3,3): blocked.\n\nPath 6: (1,1) → C → D → E → (3,3): blocked.\n\nOther paths that avoid D:\n\nPath 7: (1,1) → A → B → E → (3,3). \n\nPath 8: (1,1) → A → (2,2) [removed] → ... no, D is removed.\n\nWait, actually, without D, paths can go around:\n\nAnother path: (1,1) → A → B → E → (3,3) as above.\n\nOr (1,1) → A → (1,3) [B] → E → (3,3).\n\nOr (1,1) → C → F → G → (3,3).\n\nOr (1,1) → C → (2,1) → ... but from C=(2,1), can go up to (1,1) or down to F=(3,1), or right to D (removed), so only to F.\n\nThus, from C, you can go to F.\n\nFrom F=(3,1), you can go to G=(3,2).\n\nFrom G, you can go to (3,3).\n\nSo that's the path through C-F-G.\n\nAlternatively, from A=(1,2), can you go to E=(2,3)? No, because A is at (1,2), E is at (2,3); they are diagonally adjacent but not directly connected. So you have to go through B or D.\n\nSince D is removed, the only way from A to E is through B.\n\nSimilarly, from C=(2,1) to E=(2,3) would require going through D or other paths, but D is removed.\n\nTherefore, the only paths avoiding D are:\n\n1. (1,1) → A → B → E → (3,3)\n\n2. (1,1) → C → F → G → (3,3)\n\n3. Any longer paths? For example, (1,1) → A → B → (2,3) [E] → (3,3). That's the same as path 1.\n\nOr (1,1) → C → F → (3,2) [G] → (3,3). Same as path 2.\n\nAdditionally, could there be a path going through other nodes?\n\nFor instance, (1,1) → A → (1,2) can't go further right past B without going to E, which is through B.\n\nAlternatively, (1,1) → A → D is blocked, so no.\n\nAlternatively, (1,1) → C → (2,1) can go down to F=(3,1), then to G, etc.\n\nThus, only two fundamental paths when D is removed: the top path through B and the bottom path through F and G.\n\nTherefore, to block both paths, the removed set (including D) must intersect both paths.\n\nThat is, the removed set must include at least one vertex from the top path (A or B) and at least one vertex from the bottom path (F or G or E? Wait, the bottom path is C-F-G, so to block that path, you need to remove either C, F, or G.\n\nWait, the bottom p