raint that (3,1) and (1,3) are not removed is equal to the combination C(7,4). Because we have 7 lily pads to choose from to remove, and we need to choose 4 to remove, so the total number is C(7,4) = 35.\n\nBut not all of those 35 will satisfy the condition that Kevin cannot get home. So we have to find how many of those 35 configurations result in Kevin being unable to reach (1,3) from (3,1) on the remaining lily pads.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's easier to compute the total number of configurations where (3,1) and (1,3) are both present, and then subtract those configurations where Kevin still can get from (3,1) to (1,3) on the remaining lily pads. So, in other words, the answer would be C(7,4) minus the number of configurations where (3,1) and (1,3) are present, and Kevin can still reach from (3,1) to (1,3).\n\nTherefore, if I can compute the number of configurations where (3,1) and (1,3) are present, and Kevin can still reach from start to home, then subtract that number from the total number of such configurations (which is 35), then the result will be the number of configurations where Kevin cannot reach home.\n\nSo, let's see. Let me first note that the total number of configurations where we leave (3,1) and (1,3) in place and remove 4 of the other 7 lily pads is indeed C(7,4) = 35. So that's our total universe for the problem.\n\nNow, we need to compute how many of those 35 configurations still allow Kevin to get from (3,1) to (1,3). Let's denote this number as N. Then, the answer will be 35 - N.\n\nTherefore, the key task is to compute N, the number of ways to remove 4 lily pads (from the 7 non-start, non-home pads) such that there still exists a path from (3,1) to (1,3) on the remaining lily pads.\n\nAlternatively, equivalently, N is the number of subsets S of the 7 non-start/non-home pads with |S| = 4, such that in the graph induced by the remaining lily pads (i.e., the original grid minus the pads in S), there is a path from (3,1) to (1,3). Since we have