to compute N (the number of removals of 4 vertices that leave a path from (3,1) to (1,3)), we can think of it as all the subsets of 4 vertices (from the 7 non-start/non-home) except those that include a vertex cut of size 3? Wait, not exactly. Because even if you include a vertex cut of size 3, you might still have other paths around, unless the vertex cut is a minimal vertex cut.\n\nWait, actually, if you remove a vertex cut of size 3, then the graph is disconnected, so in that case, there is no path. But if you remove a different set of 4 vertices that includes a vertex cut of size 3 plus one more, then that might still leave the graph disconnected or not? Hmm, this is getting a bit confusing.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to think in terms of inclusion-exclusion or something else. Wait, perhaps another approach: instead of thinking about vertex cuts, let's think of the grid and the possible paths.\n\nGiven that the minimal path length is 4 (i.e., 4 edges, 5 vertices), so any path from (3,1) to (1,3) must traverse at least 4 edges. Therefore, if we can block all possible paths of length 4, then maybe longer paths can still exist? Wait, but in a 3x3 grid, the maximum distance between two nodes is 4 (from corner to opposite corner). For example, from (3,1) to (1,3), the Manhattan distance is |3-1| + |1-3| = 2 + 2 = 4, so the minimal path is 4 edges, and there are no longer paths because you can't loop around in a 3x3 grid without repeating edges or nodes. Wait, actually, in an unweighted grid graph, the shortest path is the Manhattan distance, and all paths have the same length or longer? Wait no, actually in a grid graph, you can have paths that are longer by taking detours, but in the case of a 3x3 grid, the maximum distance between two nodes is 4, and any path from (3,1) to (1,3) must have at least 4 steps, but can have more if it takes a detour. However, since the grid is small, any detour would require backtracking, but since we are dealing with simple paths (no repeated vertices), the maximum path length would be limited.\n\nWait, actually, in an unweighted grid graph, the number of paths can vary, but the minimal path length is fixed as the Manhattan distance. However, longer paths would require going around, but in a 3x3 grid, from (3,1) to (1,3), you can't really go around much because the grid is only 3x3. For example, if you go right to (3,2), then up to (2,2), then left to (2,1), then up to (1,1), then right to (1,2), then right to (1,3). That's a path of length 6. So that's a longer path. So there are paths of different lengths.\n\nTherefore, in order to block all paths from (3,1) to (1,3), we need to ensure that every possible path is blocked, i.e., for every possible path, at least one of its vertices is removed.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to: we need to remove 4 vertices (from the 7 non-start/non-home) such that every path from (3,1) to (1,3) includes at least one removed vertex. Then, the number of such removals is N', and the answer would be total configurations (35) minus N' (since N' is the number of removals that block all paths). Wait, no: actually, in our initial plan, N was the number of removals that leave at least one path, so the answer is 35 - N. But if N' is the number of removals that block all paths, then N = 35 - N', so the answer would be N' = 35 - N. Wait, maybe I need to clarify.\n\nWait, let me recap:\n\nTotal number of ways: C(7,4) = 35.\n\nWe want the number of these 35 where Kevin cannot get home, i.e., there is no path from (3,1) to (1,3) on the remaining graph. Let’s denote this number as X. Then, X is equal to the number of 4-vertex subsets S (from the 7 non-start/non-home) such that in the graph induced by the remaining vertices (which includes (3,1), (1,3), and the other 5 non-removed non-start/non-home vertices), there is no path from (3,1) to (1,3). Therefore, X is exactly the number we want, and the problem is asking for X.\n\nAlternatively, X = number of 4-vertex subsets S ⊆ {non-start, non-home} such that (3,1) and (1,3) are in separate connected components in the graph induced by the remaining vertices.\n\nAlternatively, to compute X, we can think of it as the number of 4-vertex subsets S whose removal disconnects (3,1) from (1,3). Since the minimal vertex cut is 3, then any subset S that includes a vertex cut of size 3 will disconnect the graph, provided that S contains exactly those 3 vertices plus one more. However, there might be subsets S of size 4 that don't include a vertex cut of size 3 but still disconnect the graph by some other means. Hmm, but since the minimal vertex cut is 3, any disconnecting set must have size at least 3. Therefore, all disconnecting sets have size ≥3, so subsets of size 4 that disconnect the graph must contain at least one vertex cut of size 3, but they might contain more.\n\nTherefore, to compute X, we can compute the number of 4-element subsets S that include a vertex cut of size 3, plus the number of 4-element subsets S that disconnect the graph without containing a vertex cut of size 3. But if the minimal vertex cut is 3, then any disconnecting set must contain a vertex cut of size 3, so perhaps all disconnecting sets of size 4 must contain a vertex cut of size 3. Is that true?\n\nSuppose that a set S of 4 vertices disconnects the graph. Then, the vertex cut must be a subset of S, and since the minimal vertex cut is 3, then S must contain at least one vertex cut of size 3. However, it's possible that S contains multiple vertex cuts or that the intersection with some other structure causes disconnection even without a minimal vertex cut. Hmm, but in graph theory, the vertex connectivity κ is the minimal size of a vertex cut. So, if the vertex connectivity is 3, then any vertex cut has size at least 3, so any disconnecting set must include a vertex cut of size at least 3. Therefore, in our case, since the vertex connectivity between (3,1) and (1,3) is 3, then any set of vertices whose removal disconnects (3,1) from (1,3) must include a vertex cut of size 3. Therefore, all disconnecting sets must contain a vertex cut of size 3. Therefore, to compute X, the number of disconnecting sets of size 4, we can compute for each minimal vertex cut of size 3, the number of 4-vertex subsets that include that cut, and then use inclusion-exclusion to avoid overcounting subsets that include multiple vertex cuts.\n\nTherefore, first, we need to find all minimal vertex cuts of size 3 between (3,1) and (1,3). Earlier, we saw that removing the entire middle row (positions (2,1), (2,2), (2,3)) is a vertex cut of size 3. Are there other minimal vertex cuts of size 3?\n\nLet me see. Let's try to find another set of 3 vertices whose removal disconnects (3,1) from (1,3).\n\nSuppose we remove the three corners: (1,1), (1,3), (3,1), but wait, we can't remove (3,1) or (1,3). So that's not allowed.\n\nAlternatively, remove three other vertices. Let's try removing the top row except the end: remove (1,2), (2,1), and (2,3). Let's see if that disconnects (3,1) from (1,3).\n\nAfter removing (1,2), (2,1), and (2,3):\n\n- (3,1) can go to (3,2) or (2,1) [removed], so only (3,2).\n\n- (3,2) can go to (3,1), (3,3), or (2,2).\n\n- (2,2) can go to (2,1) [removed], (2,3) [removed], (1,2) [removed], or (3,2).\n\n- (3,3) can go to (3,2) or (2,3) [removed].\n\n- (1,3) can go to (1,2) [removed] or (2,3) [removed], so it's isolated? Wait, (1,3) is only connected to (1,2) and (2,3), both of which are removed. So (1,3) is isolated. Meanwhile, (3,1) can reach (3,2), then to (2,2), but from (2,2), can you go anywhere? (2,2) is connected to (3,2) and that's it, since the other connections are removed. So (2,2) is connected only to (3,2). Therefore, (3,1) can reach (3,2) -> (2,2), but that doesn't lead anywhere else. From (3,2), you can go to (3,3), but (3,3) can't go anywhere except back. So the component containing (3,1) is {(3,1), (3,2), (2,2), (3,3)}. The component containing (1,3) is just {(1,3)}. So yes, they are disconnected. Therefore, removing (1,2), (2,1), and (2,3) also disconnects (3,1) from (1,3). So that's another minimal vertex cut of size 3.\n\nSimilarly, maybe there are more. Let's see another one.\n\nHow about removing the left column except the bottom: remove (1,1), (2,1), and (3,2). Wait, but (3,1) is still there, and (3,2) is being removed. Let's see:\n\nAfter removing (1,1), (2,1), (3,2):\n\n- (3,1) can go to (3,2) [removed] or (2,1) [removed], so (3,1) is isolated? Wait, that can't be. If (3,1) can't go anywhere, then it's isolated. But we need to see if (1,3) is also isolated or in another component.\n\nWait, (1,3) is still there. Let's see:\n\n- (1,3) can go to (1,2) or (2,3).\n\n- (1,2) can go to (1,1) [removed], (1,3), or (2,2).\n\n- (2,2) can go to (1,2), (2,1) [removed], (2,3), or (3,2) [removed].\n\n- (2,3) can go to (1,3), (2,2), or (3,3).\n\n- (3,3) can go to (2,3) or (3,2) [removed].\n\nSo (3,1) is isolated. (1,3) is connected to (1,2), which is connected to (2,2), which is connected to (2,3), which is connected to (3,3). So (1,3) is in a component with (1,2), (2,2), (2,3), (3,3). Therefore, (3,1) is in its own component, and (1,3) is in another component, so they are disconnected. So that's another vertex cut of size 3: removing (1,1), (2,1), (3,2).\n\nHmm, so there seem to be multiple minimal vertex cuts of size 3. Let me try to enumerate all possible minimal vertex cuts of size 3 that disconnect (3,1) from (1,3).\n\nFirst, the obvious one is the middle row: {(2,1), (2,2), (2,3)}.\n\nAnother one we found was {(1,2), (2,1), (2,3)}.\n\nAnother one was {(1,1), (2,1), (3,2)}.\n\nAre there others?\n\nLet me try another combination. How about {(1,1), (1,3), (2,2)}. Wait, but we can't remove (1,3) or (3,1). So (1,3) is not removable, so that set is invalid. Disregard.\n\nHow about {(1,2), (2,2), (3,2)}? Let's see if that disconnects.\n\nRemoving (1,2), (2,2), (3,2):\n\n- (3,1) can go to (3,2) [removed] or (2,1).\n\n- (2,1) can go to (1,1) or (3,1).\n\n- (1,1) can go to (1,2) [removed] or (2,1).\n\n- (1,3) can go to (1,2) [removed] or (2,3).\n\n- (2,3) can go to (1,3), (2,2) [removed], or (3,3).\n\n- (3,3) can go to (2,3) or (3,2) [removed].\n\nSo let's see connectivity:\n\nStarting from (3,1):\n\n(3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1). From (1,1), stuck. Can't get to (1,3). From (2,1), also can't go anywhere else except back. From (3,1), no other options. So (3,1) is in a component with (2,1) and (1,1). (1,3) is connected to (2,3) and (1,2) [removed]. (2,3) is connected to (1,3) and (3,3). (3,3) is connected to (2,3). So (1,3), (2,3), (3,3) form another component. So yes, disconnected. Therefore, removing {(1,2), (2,2), (3,2)} also disconnects the graph. So that's another minimal vertex cut.\n\nHmm, so that's a fourth one.\n\nSimilarly, let's see {(1,1), (2,2), (3,3)}. Removing these three:\n\n- (3,1) can go to (3,2) or (2,1).\n\n- (3,2) can go to (3,1), (3,3) [removed], or (2,2) [removed].\n\n- (2,1) can go to (1,1) [removed] or (3,1).\n\n- (1,1) is removed.\n\n- (1,3) can go to (1,2) or (2,3).\n\n- (1,2) can go to (1,1) [removed], (1,3), or (2,2) [removed].\n\n- (2,3) can go to (1,3), (2,2) [removed], or (3,3) [removed].\n\nSo let's see:\n\n(3,1) can go to (3,2) or (2,1). From (3,2), can't go anywhere except back to (3,1). From (2,1), can go to (3,1). So (3,1) is in a component with (3,2) and (2,1). (1,3) is connected to (1,2) and (2,3). (1,2) is connected only to (1,3). (2,3) is connected only to (1,3). So (1,3) is in a component with (1,2) and (2,3). Therefore, disconnected. So that's another vertex cut: {(1,1), (2,2), (3,3)}.\n\nHmm, so that's a fifth one.\n\nContinuing, let's see if we can find more.\n\nHow about {(1,1), (2,3), (3,2)}? Removing these three:\n\n- (3,1) can go to (3,2) [removed] or (2,1).\n\n- (2,1) can go to (1,1) [removed] or (3,1).\n\n- (1,3) can go to (1,2) or (2,3) [removed].\n\n- (1,2) can go to (1,1) [removed] or (1,3) or (2,2).\n\n- (2,2) can go to (1,2), (2,1) [connected? (2,1) is