[removed], or (2,2) [removed].\n\n- (2,1) can go to (1,1) or (3,1).\n\n- (1,1) can go to (1,2) or (2,1).\n\n- (1,2) can go to (1,1), (1,3), or (2,2) [removed].\n\n- (1,3) can go to (1,2) or (2,3) [removed].\n\n- (2,3) is removed.\n\nSo let's see components:\n\nStarting from (3,1):\n\n(3,1) -> (3,2) -> stuck (can't go to (3,3) or (2,2)). Alternatively, (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3). Wait, can we do that?\n\nFrom (3,1) to (2,1) to (1,1) to (1,2) to (1,3). That path exists! Because (1,2) is connected to (1,3), and (1,2) is connected to (1,1), which is connected to (2,1), which is connected to (3,1). So even though we removed (2,2), (2,3), and (3,3), the path from (3,1) to (1,3) still exists through the top row. Therefore, this set of removals does not disconnect the graph. So that set is not a vertex cut.\n\nSo that's not a minimal vertex cut. So scratch that.\n\nHow about {(1,1), (2,2), (3,1)}? But we can't remove (3,1). So no.\n\nHow about {(1,2), (3,2), (2,3)}? Let's see:\n\nRemoving {(1,2), (3,2), (2,3)}:\n\n- (3,1) can go to (3,2) [removed] or (2,1).\n\n- (2,1) can go to (1,1) or (3,1).\n\n- (1,1) can go to (1,2) [removed] or (2,1).\n\n- (1,3) can go to (1,2) [removed] or (2,3) [removed]. So (1,3) is isolated.\n\n- (2,3) is removed.\n\n- (3,3) can go to (3,2) [removed] or (2,3) [removed], so (3,3) is isolated.\n\nTherefore, components:\n\n- (3,1) connected to (2,1) and (1,1).\n\n- (1,3) isolated.\n\n- (3,3) isolated.\n\nTherefore, (3,1) is in a component with (2,1) and (1,1), while (1,3) is in its own component. So they are disconnected. Thus, this is another vertex cut: {(1,2), (3,2), (2,3)}.\n\nThat's a seventh one.\n\nHmm. This is getting a bit too tedious, but I can see that there are multiple minimal vertex cuts. Perhaps there is a pattern here.\n\nAlternatively, maybe instead of enumerating all minimal vertex cuts, I can compute the number of 4-vertex subsets that include any of the minimal vertex cuts, but being careful about overlaps.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can think in terms of the total number of minimal vertex cuts and then compute accordingly.\n\nAlternatively, maybe switch tactics and instead of going through vertex cuts, model the grid and compute the number of blocking sets of size 4.\n\nAlternatively, since the grid is small, maybe I can compute X directly by considering the different ways the frog can be blocked.\n\nFirst, note that to block all paths from (3,1) to (1,3), we need to ensure that every possible path is blocked by at least one removed pad.\n\nGiven that the minimal path length is 4, the longest path is 6, but in a 3x3 grid, actually the maximum path length between two nodes is 6? Let's see:\n\nFrom (3,1) to (1,3):\n\nOne possible long path: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) -> (2,3) -> (1,3) is length 4.\n\nAnother path: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3) is also length 4.\n\nA longer path would have to loop around:\n\nFor example: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3). That's 6 steps. So that's a path of length 6.\n\nAlternatively: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (2,2) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) -> (2,3) -> (1,3). Also length 6.\n\nTherefore, there are paths of length 4 and 6. To block all paths, you have to block all possible paths of length 4 and 6.\n\nTherefore, to block all paths, you need to ensure that every possible path of length 4 has at least one vertex removed, and every path of length 6 also has at least one vertex removed.\n\nAlternatively, since the minimal vertex cut is 3, any blocking set of size 4 must include at least one minimal vertex cut (as discussed earlier), but perhaps not necessarily.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's easier to think in terms of the complement: the number of subsets of 4 pads that do not block all paths is equal to the number of subsets that leave at least one path open. So X is the number we want (the blocking subsets), so:\n\nX = total subsets - subsets that leave at least one path open.\n\nBut that might not help unless we can compute the latter.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider all possible minimal vertex cuts and compute how many 4-vertex subsets include each minimal vertex cut, then use inclusion-exclusion to account for overlaps where a subset might include multiple minimal vertex cuts.\n\nFirst, let's try to count the number of minimal vertex cuts of size 3. Let's see how many there are.\n\nFrom the examples above, I can see at least 7 minimal vertex cuts, but I might have missed some or double-counted. Let me try to systematically list them.\n\nLet me consider that a minimal vertex cut must consist of 3 vertices such that their removal disconnects (3,1) from (1,3), and no proper subset of them does so.\n\nLet me consider all possible triplets of vertices (excluding (3,1) and (1,3)) and see which ones are minimal vertex cuts.\n\nThere are C(7,3) = 35 possible triplets. That's a lot, but maybe we can categorize them.\n\nFirst, the middle row: {(2,1), (2,2), (2,3)}. As established, this is a minimal vertex cut.\n\nSecond, the triplet {(1,2), (2,1), (2,3)}. This was another one.\n\nThird, {(1,1), (2,1), (3,2)}. Fourth, {(1,2), (2,2), (3,2)}. Fifth, {(1,1), (2,2), (3,3)}. Sixth, {(1,1), (2,3), (3,2)}. Seventh, {(1,2), (3,2), (2,3)}. That was the seventh one.\n\nNow, let's see if there are more.\n\nHow about {(1,1), (2,2), (3,1)}? No, can't remove (3,1).\n\nHow about {(1,2), (3,1), (2,3)}? Can't remove (3,1).\n\nHow about {(1,1), (3,2), (2,3)}? Let's check:\n\nRemoving {(1,1), (3,2), (2,3)}:\n\n- (3,1) can go to (3,2) [removed] or (2,1).\n\n- (2,1) can go to (1,1) [removed] or (3,1).\n\n- (1,3) can go to (1,2) or (2,3) [removed].\n\n- (1,2) can go to (1,1) [removed], (1,3), or (2,2).\n\n- (2,2) can go to (1,2), (2,1), (2,3) [removed], or (3,2) [removed].\n\n- (3,3) can go to (3,2) [removed] or (2,3) [removed], so (3,3) is isolated.\n\nSo components:\n\n- (3,1) connected to (2,1).\n\n- (2,1) connected to (3,1).\n\n- (1,3) connected to (1,2), which is connected to (2,2).\n\n- (2,2) connected to (1,2).\n\n- (1,3) is in a component with (1,2) and (2,2).\n\n- (3,3) is isolated.\n\nTherefore, (3,1) is in a separate component from (1,3). So this is another vertex cut: {(1,1), (3,2), (2,3)}. That's an eighth one.\n\nContinuing:\n\nHow about {(1,1), (2,2), (3,2)}? Let's check:\n\nRemoving {(1,1), (2,2), (3,2)}:\n\n- (3,1) can go to (3,2) [removed] or (2,1).\n\n- (2,1) can go to (1,1) [removed] or (3,1).\n\n- (1,3) can go to (1,2) or (2,3).\n\n- (1,2) can go to (1,1) [removed], (1,3), or (2,2) [removed].\n\n- (2,3) can go to (1,3), (2,2) [removed], or (3,3).\n\n- (3,3) can go to (2,3) or (3,2) [removed].\n\nSo components:\n\n- (3,1) connected to (2,1).\n\n- (1,3) connected to (1,2) and (2,3), which is connected to (3,3).\n\n- (2,3) connected to (3,3).\n\n- (1,2) connected to (1,3).\n\nTherefore, (3,1) is isolated from (1,3). So this is another vertex cut: {(1,1), (2,2), (3,2)}. Ninth one.\n\nHmm. This is getting extensive. Maybe there's a better way.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps notice that each minimal vertex cut corresponds to a \"middle row\" in some sense, either horizontal, vertical, or diagonal? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps note that in the 3x3 grid, the vertex connectivity between opposite corners is 3, and the minimal vertex cuts are the three \"central\" rows or columns or something.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to accept that enumerating all minimal vertex cuts is time-consuming and error-prone, and instead, perhaps compute the number of 4-vertex subsets that include any of the minimal vertex cuts, then adjust for overlaps.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps notice that each minimal vertex cut has 3 vertices, and we need to count the number of 4-vertex subsets that include any such cut, but then subtract overlaps where a subset includes two different minimal vertex cuts.\n\nBut to do this, I need to know how many minimal vertex cuts there are, how they overlap, etc.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps instead of that, let me think of the total number of 4-vertex subsets that include the middle row (the first minimal vertex cut we found: {(2,1), (2,2), (2,3)}). The number of 4-vertex subsets that include this middle row is equal to the number of ways to choose the 4th vertex from the remaining 4 vertices (since total non-start/non-home vertices are 7, and we've already chosen 3 in the middle row, so 7 - 3 = 4 remaining). So that would be C(4,1) = 4.\n\nSimilarly, for each minimal vertex cut of size 3, the number of 4-vertex subsets containing it is C(4,1) = 4, assuming that the minimal vertex cut doesn't share any vertices with itself (which it doesn't). However, if two minimal vertex cuts share some vertices, then subsets that include both would be overcounted.\n\nTherefore, if there are M minimal vertex cuts, each contributing 4 subsets, but with overlaps where some subsets include multiple minimal vertex cuts, then the total number of subsets that include at least one minimal vertex cut is at most 4*M, but we have to subtract the overlaps.\n\nTherefore, if we can find M, the number of minimal vertex cuts, and the number of subsets that include two minimal vertex cuts, etc., then we can apply inclusion-exclusion.\n\nBut since this is getting complex and I might not have time to compute all overlaps, perhaps I can estimate or find a pattern.\n\nAlternatively, let's suppose that there are, say, 8 minimal vertex cuts (from the ones I found above: 1 through 9, but some may not be minimal). Wait, actually in my above enumeration, I might have included some non-minimal vertex cuts. For instance, when I considered removing {(1,1), (2,2), (3,2)}, does that really disconnect the graph?\n\nWait, let me verify one of the later ones: {(1,1), (2,2), (3,2)}. Removing those three:\n\n- (3,1) can go to (3,2) [removed] or (2,1).\n\n- (2,1) can go to (1,1) [removed] or (3,1).\n\n- (1,3) can go to (1,2) or (2,3).\n\n- (1,2) can go to (1,1) [removed], (1,3), or (2,2) [removed].\n\n- (2,3) can go to (1,3), (2,2) [removed], or (3,3).\n\n- (3,3) can go to (2,3) or (3,2) [removed].\n\nSo components:\n\n- (3,1) connected to (2,1).\n\n- (1,3) connected to (1,2) and (2,3), which is connected to (3,3).\n\n- (2,3) connected to (3,3).\n\n- (1,2) connected to (1,3).\n\nTherefore, (3,1) is in a separate component from (1,3). So that is indeed a minimal vertex cut.\n\nBut how many are there?\n\nAlternatively, given that it's taking too long to enumerate, perhaps look for symmetry.\n\nThe grid is symmetric along the vertical and horizontal axes. The problem is symmetric with respect to reflection over the vertical axis and horizontal axis. Therefore, the number of minimal vertex cuts should be symmetric.\n\nGiven that, let's categorize the minimal vertex cuts based on their positions:\n\n1. **Middle row**: {(2,1), (2,2), (2,3)}. This is one distinct minimal vertex cut under symmetry.\n\n2. **Middle column**: {(1,2), (2,2), (3,2)}. Similarly, this is another distinct one under symmetry.\n\n3. **Diagonals or other lines**: For example, {(1,1), (2,2), (3,3)} and {(1,3), (2,2), (3,1)}, but we can't remove (1,3) or (3,1). So the diagonal {(1,1), (2,2), (3,3)} is a valid minimal vertex cut, and its counterpart {(1,3), (2,2), (3,1)} is invalid due to (3,1) and (1,3) being unremovable. So only one diagonal minimal vertex cut in this category.\n\nWait, but {(1,1), (2,2), (3,3)} is a minimal vertex cut as we saw earlier.\n\n4. **Corner-based cuts**: For example, cuts that involve two adjacent corners and a central square? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, cuts that form an \"L\" shape or something.\n\nLooking at the ones we found earlier:\n\n- Middle row: vertical middle.\n\n- Middle column: horizontal middle.\n\n- Diagonal: from (1,1) to (3,3) through (2,2).\n\n- Other cuts like {(1,2), (2,1), (2,3)}: this seems to block the middle row's left and right but also the top middle.\n\nWait, perhaps there are 8 minimal vertex cuts: the middle row, middle column, the two diagonals (but only one diagonal is possible because the other