ths from 7 to 3.\n\nA vertex cover for the paths from 7 to 3 would be a set of vertices such that every path from 7 to 3 includes at least one vertex from the set.\n\nWhat is the minimal size of such a vertex cover?\n\nIn graph theory terms, the vertex cover number for the path graph is known, but here the graph is not a simple path but a grid, so it's more complex.\n\nBut perhaps in our case, since we have multiple paths, the minimal vertex cover might be small.\n\nLet me see: suppose we try to find the smallest set of vertices that intersects every path from 7 to 3.\n\nLet me try to see if there is a vertex that is on all paths. Is there a node that is present in every path from 7 to 3?\n\nLooking at the four minimal paths:\n\nPath 1: 7-4-1-2-3\n\nPath 2: 7-4-5-6-3\n\nPath 3: 7-8-9-6-3\n\nPath 4: 7-8-5-2-3\n\nIs there a common node in all four paths?\n\nLooking at Path 1: nodes 7,4,1,2,3\n\nPath 2: 7,4,5,6,3\n\nPath 3: 7,8,9,6,3\n\nPath 4: 7,8,5,2,3\n\nThe common nodes among all four paths are only 7 and 3, which are the start and end. Since we cannot remove them, they are not in the hitting set. So there is no other node common to all paths. Therefore, the vertex cover must include at least one node from each \"branch\" of the paths.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can find a small vertex cover by selecting key nodes that are on many paths.\n\nFor example, node 4 is in paths 1 and 2.\n\nNode 5 is in paths 2 and 4.\n\nNode 6 is in paths 2 and 3.\n\nNode 8 is in paths 3 and 4.\n\nNode 2 is in paths 1 and 4.\n\nNode 1 is only in path 1.\n\nNode 9 is only in path 3.\n\nSo nodes 4,5,6,8,2 are each in two paths, while nodes 1 and 9 are only in one path each.\n\nTherefore, to cover all paths, we might need to pick nodes that cover the different branches.\n\nLet me attempt to find a minimal vertex cover:\n\nSuppose we pick node 4 and node 6. Then:\n\n- Path 1: includes 4, so covered.\n\n- Path 2: includes 4 and 6, so covered.\n\n- Path 3: includes 6, so covered.\n\n- Path 4: does path 4 go through 4 or 6? Path 4 is 7-8-5-2-3, so it doesn't go through 4 or 6. Therefore, path 4 is not covered by {4,6}. So we need another node to cover path 4, like node 8 or node 5 or node 2.\n\nSuppose we add node 8 to the cover: {4,6,8}\n\n- Path 1: covered by 4\n\n- Path 2: covered by 4 or 6\n\n- Path 3: covered by 6 or 8\n\n- Path 4: covered by 8\n\nSo {4,6,8} covers all four minimal paths. Is that a vertex cover for all paths? Let's see if there are any other paths that might not be covered.\n\nFor instance, the longer path I mentioned earlier: 7-4-5-8-9-6-3. This path goes through 4, 5, 8, 9, 6. Since we have 4 and 6 and 8 in the cover, this path is covered by 4, 6, or 8.\n\nAnother path: 7-8-5-2-1-4-5-6-3. This goes through 8,5,2,1,4,5,6. Since 8,4,6 are in the cover, it's covered.\n\nAnother path: 7-4-5-2-1-4-5-6-3. Again, includes 4 and 6, so covered.\n\nAlternatively, a path that goes 7-8-5-6-9-6-3? Wait, can't really loop, but maybe 7-8-5-6-9? No, 6 and 9 are not adjacent. Wait, 6 is middle-right, 9 is bottom-right, so they are adjacent vertically. So 6 is above 9. So path could be 7-8-5-6-9? Wait, no, from 6 you can go to 9? 6 is above 9? Wait in our labeling:\n\nPosition 6 is middle-right (row 2, column 3). Position 9 is bottom-right (row 3, column 3). So yes, they are vertically adjacent. So from 6 you can go down to 9. So a path could be 7-8-5-6-9-3? Wait, from 9 you can go up to 6 or left to 8. To get to 3, you need to go up from 6 to 3. So 7-8-5-6-9-6-3? But that's redundant. Alternatively, 7-8-5-6-9-6-3 is not a simple path, but in terms of just needing to have some path, you could go 7-8-5-6-9-6-3, but that's not a simple path, but in the induced subgraph, if 6 is present, then you can go from 9 to 6 to 3. So the path 7-8-5-6-9-6-3 is not simple, but in the induced subgraph, if both 6 and 9 are present, then you can go from 9 to 6 to 3. However, if we have removed 6, then you can't go from 9 to 3 that way, but if we didn't remove 6, then you can.\n\nBut in our hitting set {4,6,8}, we have not removed 6, so 6 is still there, so that path is covered because it goes through 6. Wait, actually, in the hitting set perspective, the hitting set is the set of nodes we are *removing*. Wait, hold on, I think I might have confused the direction.\n\nWait, actually, in our problem, the hitting set is the set of nodes we remove, such that every path from 7 to 3 includes at least one node from the hitting set (i.e., is removed). Therefore, the hitting set is the set of nodes we remove, and we need the hitting set to intersect every path.\n\nIn my previous example, if we choose to remove nodes 4,6,8, then we have a hitting set of size 3. Then, if we can also remove one more node (since we need to remove 4 nodes total), then we can have hitting sets of size 4 by adding any other node to {4,6,8}.\n\nBut first, let's see if {4,6,8} is a hitting set of size 3. Let's verify:\n\n- Path 1: 7-4-1-2-3: includes 4, so hit.\n\n- Path 2: 7-4-5-6-3: includes 4 and 6, so hit.\n\n- Path 3: 7-8-9-6-3: includes 8 and 6, so hit.\n\n- Path 4: 7-8-5-2-3: includes 8, so hit.\n\n- The longer path 7-4-5-8-9-6-3: includes 4,8,6, so hit.\n\n- Another longer path: 7-8-5-2-1-4-5-6-3: includes 4,6,8, so hit.\n\n- Path 7-4-5-2-1-4-5-6-3: includes 4 and 6, so hit.\n\n- Path 7-8-5-6-9-6-3: includes 8 and 6, so hit.\n\nSo it seems that {4,6,8} hits all these paths. Therefore, {4,6,8} is a hitting set of size 3. Therefore, any hitting set of size 4 that includes {4,6,8} plus any other node will also be a hitting set, but also, there might be other hitting sets of size 4 that don't include all three of 4,6,8 but still hit all paths.\n\nTherefore, to count all hitting sets of size 4, we need to consider:\n\n1. All subsets of size 4 that include {4,6,8} plus any one other node from the remaining nodes (since we can remove up to 4 nodes, and we already have 3, so one more). The remaining nodes we can remove are the other 4 nodes: since we are choosing 4 nodes to remove from the 7 non-start/non-home nodes, and we have already chosen 3 (4,6,8), so the fourth can be any of the remaining 4 nodes: nodes 1,2,5,9 (since nodes 4,6,8 are already in the hitting set, and the other nodes are 1,2,5,9,7,3 but we can't remove 7 or 3). Wait, the nodes available to remove are 1,2,4,5,6,8,9. We have already selected 4,6,8, so the remaining nodes we can choose to add to the hitting set are 1,2,5,9. So that's 4 choices. So that gives 4 subsets: {4,6,8,1}, {4,6,8,2}, {4,6,8,5}, {4,6,8,9}.\n\n2. Additionally, there may be other hitting sets of size 4 that do not include all three of 4,6,8 but still hit all paths. For example, maybe a hitting set like {4,5,6,8} or {4,5, something else}, but need to check if those also hit all paths.\n\nLet me check if {4,5,6} is a hitting set of size 3:\n\n- Path 1: includes 4, so hit.\n\n- Path 2: includes 4 and 6, so hit.\n\n- Path 3: includes 6, so hit.\n\n- Path 4: does path 4 go through 4,5, or 6? Path 4 is 7-8-5-2-3. It goes through 5, so yes, 5 is in the set. So path 4 is hit by 5.\n\n- The longer path 7-8-5-6-9-6-3: includes 5 and 6, so hit.\n\n- Path 7-4-5-8-9-6-3: includes 4,5,6, so hit.\n\n- Path 7-8-5-2-1-4-5-6-3: includes 4,5,6, so hit.\n\nTherefore, {4,5,6} is also a hitting set of size 3. So then, similar to the previous case, we can form hitting sets of size 4 by adding any one node from the remaining nodes (excluding 4,5,6). The remaining nodes to remove are 1,2,8,9 (since we already removed 4,5,6). Wait, the nodes we can remove are 1,2,4,5,6,8,9. If we have already chosen 4,5,6, then the remaining nodes we can add are 1,2,8,9. So four choices again: {4,5,6,1}, {4,5,6,2}, {4,5,6,8}, {4,5,6,9}.\n\nBut note that {4,5,6,8} is also included in the previous category (the ones that include 4,6,8 plus one more). Wait, actually, {4,5,6,8} is different from {4,6,8,5} which is the same set. So these are overlapping.\n\nWait, actually, the sets are the same. The set {4,5,6,8} is the same as {4,6,8,5}. So when we considered adding to {4,6,8}, we added 5, which gives {4,5,6,8}, and when we considered adding to {4,5,6}, we added 8, which also gives {4,5,6,8}. So those are duplicates.\n\nTherefore, to avoid double-counting, perhaps we need to consider all minimal hitting sets of size 3 and then see their supersets of size 4.\n\nSimilarly, are there other minimal hitting sets of size 3?\n\nLet's see another candidate: {4,8,6} we already considered.\n\nWhat about {4,8,5}? Let's check:\n\n- Path 1: includes 4, hit.\n\n- Path 2: includes 4 and 5? Path 2 is 7-4-5-6-3, so yes, 4 and 5 are in the set, so hit.\n\n- Path 3: includes 8 and 6? Wait, path 3 is 7-8-9-6-3, so includes 8 but not 5 or 6. Wait, 6 is not in {4,8,5}. So path 3 would go through 8 and 6, but 6 is not in the hitting set. Therefore, path 3 is not hit by {4,8,5}. So that's a problem.\n\nTherefore {4,8,5} is not a hitting set.\n\nHow about {6,8,5}? Let's see:\n\n- Path 1: 7-4-1-2-3: does it include 6,8, or 5? No. So path 1 is not hit. So no.\n\nHow about {4,2,6}?\n\n- Path 1: includes 4 and 2, so hit.\n\n- Path 2: includes 4 and 6, so hit.\n\n- Path 3: includes 6, so hit.\n\n- Path 4: includes 2, so hit.\n\n- The longer paths: let's see, 7-4-5-8-9-6-3: includes 4 and 6, so hit.\n\n- 7-8-5-2-1-4-5-6-3: includes 2,4,6, so hit.\n\n- 7-8-5-6-9-6-3: includes 6, so hit.\n\nSo {4,2,6} is also a hitting set of size 3.\n\nSo that's another minimal hitting set. Therefore, {4,2,6} is a hitting set.\n\nTherefore, then we can form hitting sets of size 4 by adding any one of the remaining nodes: the nodes not in {4,2,6} are 1,5,8,9. So adding each of those gives four more subsets: {4,2,6,1}, {4,2,6,5}, {4,2,6,8}, {4,2,6,9}.\n\nSimilarly, let's see if {4,2,8} is a hitting set:\n\n- Path 1: includes 4 and 2, so hit.\n\n- Path 2: includes 4, so hit.\n\n- Path 3: includes 8 and 6? Path 3 is 7-8-9-6-3. So includes 8 but not 6. So path 3 would go through 8, which is in the hitting set, so hit.\n\n- Path 4: includes 8 a