ies, all of which are hitting sets except when x is 6? Wait no, even with x=6, {4,8,6} is a hitting set?\n\nWait, {4,8,6}:\n\n- Path 1: hit by 4.\n\n- Path 2: hit by 4 and 6.\n\n- Path 3: hit by 8 and 6.\n\n- Path 4: hit by 8.\n\n- All longer paths: yes.\n\nSo {4,8,6} is also a hitting set of size 3. Wait, but earlier when I considered {4,6,8}, which is the same as {4,8,6}, so that was already counted.\n\nTherefore, {4,8,x} for x=1,2,5,6,9: but x=6 is already in the first category.\n\nWait, perhaps I need to systematically go through all combinations.\n\nThis is getting quite involved. Let me try to list all possible minimal hitting sets of size 3:\n\nStart with node 4:\n\n- If we include node 4, then we need to cover the remaining paths not covered by 4.\n\nPaths not covered by 4 are paths that don't go through 4. Looking at the four minimal paths, only path 3 (7-8-9-6-3) and path 4 (7-8-5-2-3) don't go through 4? Wait no:\n\nWait path 3: 7-8-9-6-3: does not go through 4.\n\nPath 4: 7-8-5-2-3: does not go through 4.\n\nThe other paths (1 and 2) go through 4.\n\nSo if we include 4, then we still need to cover paths 3 and 4.\n\nTo cover path 3 (7-8-9-6-3), we need to remove either 8,9,6, or 3 (but 3 can't be removed). So from nodes 8,9,6.\n\nTo cover path 4 (7-8-5-2-3), we need to remove either 8,5,2, or 3 (again, 3 can't be removed). So from nodes 8,5,2.\n\nTherefore, if we have node 4, then to cover paths 3 and 4, we need to choose nodes that cover both path 3 and path 4.\n\nThe intersection of the nodes needed for path 3 and path 4 is node 8.\n\nTherefore, if we include 4 and 8, then:\n\n- For path 3: 8 is included, so covered.\n\n- For path 4: 8 is included, so covered.\n\nAdditionally, paths 1 and 2 are already covered by 4.\n\nTherefore, {4,8} plus any other node not in paths 1,2,3,4? Wait no, actually, with {4,8}, do we need a third node?\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nWith just {4,8}:\n\n- Path 1: hit by 4.\n\n- Path 2: hit by 4.\n\n- Path 3: hit by 8.\n\n- Path 4: hit by 8.\n\n- Longer paths: Let's see a longer path like 7-4-5-8-9-6-3: includes 4 and 8, so hit.\n\n- Another path: 7-8-5-2-1-4-5-6-3: includes 4 and 8, so hit.\n\n- Path 7-8-5-6-9-6-3: includes 8, so hit.\n\n- Path 7-4-5-2-1-4-5-6-3: includes 4, so hit.\n\nSo actually, {4,8} alone might be a hitting set of size 2? Wait, is that true?\n\nWait, hold on! If we have just nodes 4 and 8 removed, does that disconnect 7 from 3?\n\nLet me see:\n\nIf we remove nodes 4 and 8, then the remaining graph includes:\n\nStart at 7 (bottom-left). From 7, you can go right to 8, but 8 is removed, so cannot go that way. From 7, the only neighbor is 4, which is also removed. Therefore, from 7, there are no edges left! Wait, that can't be.\n\nWait, hold on, if we remove nodes 4 and 8, then the node 7 (start) is only connected to node 4, which has been removed. Therefore, the start node 7 is isolated! Therefore, in the induced subgraph with nodes 7 and others except 4 and 8, the start node 7 has no edges, so it's disconnected from everything else, including home node 3. Therefore, {4,8} is actually a hitting set of size 2 that disconnects 7 from 3.\n\nWait a minute, this is a key point I missed earlier.\n\nIf we remove nodes 4 and 8, then the start node 7 has no neighbors left, since its only neighbor was 4, which is removed. Therefore, the start is isolated, so it cannot reach home. Therefore, {4,8} is indeed a hitting set of size 2 that disconnects 7 from 3.\n\nSimilarly, are there other size 2 hitting sets?\n\nFor example, removing nodes 6 and 8:\n\nIf we remove 6 and 8:\n\n- Start at 7. From 7, can go to 4 or 8. Both 4 and 8 are still present (unless we removed them). Wait, we only removed 6 and 8. So from 7, you can go to 4 or to 8 (but 8 is removed). So from 7, you can go to 4.\n\nFrom 4, you can go to 1, 5, or 7 (but 7 is start). From 4, going to 1 or 5.\n\nIf we removed 6 and 8, then node 5 is still there. From 4, going to 5.\n\nFrom 5, you can go to 2, 6 (removed), 8 (removed), or below to 8 (removed). So from 5, you can go to 2.\n\nFrom 2, you can go to 1, 3 (home), or 5.\n\nSo there is a path: 7-4-5-2-3. So even with removing 6 and 8, there is still a path from 7 to 3: 7-4-5-2-3. Therefore, {6,8} is not a hitting set.\n\nSimilarly, removing nodes 5 and 6:\n\n- Start at 7. Can go to 4 or 8.\n\nIf we remove 5 and 6:\n\n- From 4, can go to 1 or 7 or 5 (but 5 is removed), so to 1.\n\nFrom 1, can go to 2 or 4 (but 4 is still there). From 1 to 2.\n\nFrom 2, can go to 3 or 5 (removed) or 1.\n\nSo path: 7-4-1-2-3. So still connected. So {5,6} is not a hitting set.\n\nHow about removing nodes 2 and 6:\n\n- Start at 7. Can go to 4 or 8.\n\nIf we remove 2 and 6:\n\n- From 4, can go to 1,5,7.\n\nFrom 5, can go to 2 (removed), 4, 6 (removed), 8.\n\nFrom 8, can go to 7, 9, 5.\n\nFrom 9, can go to 6 (removed), 8.\n\nSo path possibilities:\n\n7-4-5-8-9-8-... but to get to 3, you need to go through 2 or 6. Since both are removed, can you get to 3?\n\nFrom 2 is removed, and 6 is removed. The only way to reach 3 is through node 2 or node 6. Since both are removed, there is no path from 7 to 3. Therefore, removing nodes 2 and 6 disconnects 7 from 3.\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nIf nodes 2 and 6 are removed, then:\n\n- Node 3 is still there, but to reach it, you need to go through node 2 or node 6, which are both removed. Therefore, node 3 is isolated from the rest of the graph except through node 2 or 6, which are gone. Therefore, home node 3 is also isolated? Wait no, node 3 is only connected to node 2 and node 6. If both are removed, then node 3 has no connections. So node 3 is also isolated. But the problem requires that node 3 must remain, but it's still present but disconnected from the start. Therefore, yes, {2,6} is a hitting set of size 2 that disconnects 7 from 3.\n\nSimilarly, removing nodes 1 and 6:\n\n- Start at 7 can go to 4 or 8.\n\n- From 4, can go to 1 (removed) or 5 or 7.\n\n- From 5, can go to 2,4,6 (removed),8.\n\n- From 8, can go to 7,5,9.\n\n- From 9, can go to 8 or 6 (removed).\n\n- To reach 3, need to go through 2 or 6. 6 is removed, so through 2.\n\nFrom 5 to 2, then to 3. So path: 7-4-5-2-3. So still connected. Therefore {1,6} is not a hitting set.\n\nHow about removing nodes 4 and 6:\n\n- Start at 7 can go to 4 (removed) or 8.\n\n- From 8, can go to 7,5,9.\n\n- From 5, can go to 2,4 (removed),6 (removed),8.\n\n- From 2, can go to 1,3,5.\n\n- From 1, can go to 2 or 4 (removed).\n\n- From 9, can go to 8 or 6 (removed).\n\nSo path: 7-8-5-2-3. So still connected. Therefore {4,6} is not a hitting set.\n\nHow about removing nodes 4 and 5:\n\n- Start at 7 can go to 4 (removed) or 8.\n\n- From 8, can go to 7,5 (removed),9.\n\n- From 9, can go to 8 or 6.\n\n- From 6, can go to 3 or 5 (removed).\n\nSo path: 7-8-9-6-3. So still connected. Therefore {4,5} not a hitting set.\n\nHow about removing nodes 8 and 6:\n\n- As above, there is a path 7-4-5-2-3. So not a hitting set.\n\nRemoving nodes 5 and 8:\n\n- Start at 7 can go to 4 or 8 (removed).\n\n- From 4, can go to 1,5 (removed),7.\n\n- From 1, can go to 2 or 4.\n\n- From 2, can go to 3 or 5 (removed) or 1.\n\n- From 7, can go to 4.\n\nSo path: 7-4-1-2-3. So still connected. Therefore {5,8} is not a hitting set.\n\nHow about removing nodes 2 and 8:\n\n- Start at 7 can go to 4 or 8 (removed).\n\n- From 4, can go to 1,5,7.\n\n- From 5, can go to 2 (removed),4,6,8 (removed).\n\n- From 6, can go to 3 or 5.\n\n- From 1, can go to 2 (removed) or 4.\n\nSo path: 7-4-5-6-3. So still connected. Therefore {2,8} is not a hitting set.\n\nHow about removing nodes 1 and 8:\n\n- Start at 7 can go to 4 or 8 (removed).\n\n- From 4, can go to 1 (removed),5,7.\n\n- From 5, can go to 2,4,6,8 (removed).\n\n- From 6, can go to 3 or 5.\n\n- From 2, can go to 3 or 5.\n\n- From 3, can't go anywhere except back.\n\nSo path: 7-4-5-6-3. Still connected. So {1,8} not a hitting set.\n\nHow about removing nodes 4 and 2:\n\n- Start at 7 can go to 4 (removed) or 8.\n\n- From 8, can go to 7,5,9.\n\n- From 5, can go to 2 (removed),4 (removed),6,8.\n\n- From 6, can go to 3 or 5.\n\n- From 9, can go to 8 or 6.\n\n- From 3, can go to 2 (removed) or 6.\n\nSo path: 7-8-5-6-3. Still connected. Therefore {4,2} not a hitting set.\n\nHow about removing nodes 4 and 5:\n\n- As above, still connected via 7-8-9-6-3.\n\nHow about removing nodes 6 and 8:\n\n- As above, still connected via 7-4-5-2-3.\n\nSo, the only size-2 hitting sets that disconnect 7 from 3 are:\n\n- {4,8}: which isolates 7.\n\n- {2,6}: which isolates both 7 and 3.\n\nWait, when we removed {2,6}, node 3 is also isolated because its only connections are to 2 and 6, which are removed. So node 3 is also isolated. But the problem states that we must keep node 3, but it's still present in the graph but disconnected. So that's acceptable for the problem's requirements: we just need that there's no path from start to home, regardless of other components.\n\nTherefore, {2,6} is also a hitting set of size 2.\n\nSimilarly, is there another size-2 hitting set?\n\nHow about {5, something}?\n\nWait, removing nodes 5 and 8:\n\n- As above, path 7-4-1-2-3 still exists.\n\nRemoving nodes 5 and 2:\n\n- Start at 7 can go to 4 or 8.\n\n- From 4, can go to 1,5 (removed),7.\n\n- From 1, can go to 2 (removed) or 4.\n\n- From 8, can go to 7,5 (removed),9.\n\n- From 9, can go to 8 or 6.\n\n- From 6, can go to 3 or 5 (removed).\n\n- From 3, can go to 2 (removed) or 6.\n\nSo path: 7-4-1-4-... no, from 1 you can't go back to 4? Wait, in the induced subgraph with nodes 5 and 2 removed:\n\n- Node 1 is connected to 4 and 2, but 2 is removed, so node 1 is connected only to 4.\n\n- Node 4 is connected to 1,7,5 (but 5 is removed), so only to 1 and 7.\n\n- From node 1, you can go to 4 or back to 7.\n\n- From node 7, you can go to 4 or 8.\n\n- From node 8, you can go to 7,9,5 (removed).\n\n- From node 9, you can go to 8 or 6.\n\n- From node 6, you can go to 3 or 5 (removed).\n\n- From node 3, you can go to 6 or 2 (removed).\n\nSo path from 7 to 3:\n\n7-8-9-6-3. That path still exists because nodes 8,9,6,3 are all present (only 5 and 2 are removed). Therefore, even with removing 5 and 2, there's still a path. So {5,2} is not a hitting set.\n\nHow about removing nodes 1 and 6:\n\n- As above, path 7-4-5-2-3 exists.\n\nRemoving nodes 1 and 8:\n\n- As above, path 7-4-5-6-3 exists.\n\nRemoving nodes 4 and 6:\n\n- As above, path 7-8-5-2-3 exists.\n\nRemoving nodes 4 and 5:\n\n- Path 7-8-9-6-3 exists.\n\nRemoving nodes 8 and 6:\n\n- Path 7-4-5-2-3 exists.\n\nTherefore, the only size-2 hitting sets are {4,8} and {2,6}.\n\nWait, what about removing nodes 5 and something else?\n\nWait, let me check removing nodes 5 and 2:\n\n- As above, path 7-4-1-2-3 is blocked because 2 is removed, but path 7-4-5-6-3 is also blocked because 5 is removed, but path 7-8-5-... is blocked by 5 being removed. Wait, actually, if we remove 5 and 2:\n\n- From node 7, can go to 4 or 8.\n\n- From 4: to 1 or 7 (since 5 is removed).\n\n- From 1: to 4 or 2 (removed).\n\n- From 8: to 7,9,5 (removed).\n\n- From 9: to 8 or 6.\n\n- From 6: to 3 or 5 (removed).\n\n- From 3: to 6 or 2 (removed).\n\nTherefore, the path from 7 to 3 would have to go through 6 or 2, but both are blocked via removals? Wait, node 6 is still present. From 9 to 6 is okay. From 6 to 3 is okay. But to get to 6 from 7:\n\n7-8-9-6-3. That path doesn't go through 5 or 2. So that path is still open. So {5,2} does not block all paths.\n\nSimilarly, removing nodes 5 and 8:\n\n- Path 7-4-1-2-3 still exists.\n\nTherefore, only the two size-2 hitting sets: {4,8} and {2,6}.\n\nTherefore, in summary, the hitting sets of size 2 are {4,8} and {2,6}. Then, for our problem, we need hitting sets of size 4. Therefore, we can build hitting sets of size 4 by adding two more nodes to these size-2 hitting sets, or by combining other minimal hitting sets of size 3 with additional nodes.\n\nBut this is getting extremely complex. Perhaps another approach is better.\n\nGiven the time I've spent and the potential for error in this enumeration, perhaps I should instead calculate the total number of subsets where 7 and 3 are disconnected by considering the two size-2 hitting sets and then building up.\n\nFirst, the two minimal hitting sets of size 2: {4,8} and {2,6}.\n\nAny hitting set of size 4 must include at least one of these two sets, or other combinations.\n\nBut perhaps instead, since we need subsets of size 4 that hit all paths, we can consider that the only way to disconnect 7 from 3 is to either:\n\n1. Remove nodes that isolate 7 (like removing 4 and 8, but that's size 2), or\n\n2. Remove nodes that isolate 3 (like removing 2 and 6, size 2), or\n\n3. Remove a combination that breaks all paths.\n\nBut since we need to remove exactly 4 nodes, perhaps the disconnected subsets are those that include either:\n\n- The size-2 hitting set {4,8} plus any 2 other nodes from the remaining 5 nodes (since total nodes to remove are 4: 2 already in the hitting set, plus 2 more). Wait, but we have to choose 4 nodes to remove from the 7 available (excluding 7 and 3). The hitting set {4,8} is part of that, so adding any 2 more nodes from the remaining 5 nodes (since 7 total nodes minus 2 in hitting set leaves 5). But need to ensure that adding those 2 more doesn't reconnect the graph.\n\nWait, no. Actually, if we have a hitting set of size 2 ({4,8}), then adding any other nodes might actually allow paths to reappear. For example, if we remove {4,8} plus nodes 1 and 2, then node 1 and 2 are removed, but node 5 and 6 are still there, allowing a path 7-4 (but 4 is removed) no, wait 4 is removed. Wait, if we have removed 4 and 8, then adding back nodes? No, we are removing nodes. Wait, sorry, in this context, the hitting set is the set of nodes we are removing. So if we choose to remove nodes 4,8, plus two others, say 1 and 2, then in the remaining graph, nodes 4 and 8 are gone, as well as 1 and 2. Then, is there still a path from 7 to 3?\n\nLet me see:\n\nAfter removing 4,8,1,2:\n\n- Start at 7. From 7, can go to 4 (removed) or 8 (removed). So 7 is isolated. Therefore, regardless of other nodes, 7 cannot reach anywhere, so it's disconnected. So actually, any subset that includes {4,8} plus any other nodes will still have 7 isolated, because 4 and 8 are removed, leaving 7 with no connections. Therefore, any subset that includes both 4 and 8 will automatically disconnect 7 from the rest of the graph, hence from 3. Similarly, any subset that includes both 2 and 6 will isolate node 3, since 2 and 6 are its only connections.\n\nTherefore, the disconnected subsets are exactly those that include either:\n\n- Both 4 and 8, plus any 2 other nodes from the remaining 5 nodes (since we have to remove 4 nodes total, and 2 are already chosen as 4 and 8).\n\nOR\n\n- Both 2 and 6, plus any 2 other nodes from the remaining 5 nodes.\n\nHowever, we have to be careful about overlap: subsets that include both {4,8} and {2,6} would be counted in both categories, so we have to subtract the overlap.\n\nFirst, compute the number of subsets that include {4,8} plus any 2 from the remaining 5 nodes (excluding 7 and 3, and excluding 4 and 8). The remaining nodes available to remove are: 1,2,5,6,9 (since we've already chosen to remove 4 and 8). So we need to choose 2 more from these 5: C(5,2) = 10.\n\nSimilarly, the number of subsets that include {2,6} plus any 2 from the remaining 5 nodes (excluding 7,3,2,6). The remaining nodes are: 1,4,5,8,9. So choosing 2 from these 5: C(5,2)=10.\n\nHowever, the overlap occurs when a subset includes both {4,8} and {2,6}, i.e., includes all four nodes: 4,8,2,6. Such a subset would be counted in both the first category and the second category. How many such subsets are there?\n\nIf a subset includes all four nodes: 4,8,2,6, then since we're removing exactly 4 nodes, this is exactly one subset: {2,4,6,8}. So there is 1 overlapping subset.\n\nTherefore, by inclusion-exclusion, the total number of disconnected subsets is:\n\nNumber with {4,8} + Number with {2,6} - Number with both {4,8} and {2,6}\n\n= 10 + 10 - 1 = 19.\n\nBut wait, let me confirm:\n\nFirst category: subsets containing {4,8} plus 2 others from {1,2,5,6,9}. The number is C(5,2)=10.\n\nSecond category: subsets containing {2,6} plus 2 others from {1,4,5,8,9}. The number is C(5,2)=10.\n\nOverlap: subsets that contain both {4,8} and {2,6}, which requires removing all four nodes: 4,8,2,6. Since we are removing exactly four nodes, this is exactly one subset: {2,4,6,8}. So overlap is 1.\n\nTherefore total disconnected subsets: 10 + 10 - 1 = 19.\n\nBut wait, is that all? Are there other ways to disconnect 7 from 3 with four removed nodes, not covered by these two cases?\n\nFor instance, suppose we remove nodes 4,5,6,8. This includes both {4,8} and {6}, but does this disconnect 7 from 3?\n\nLet's see:\n\nAfter removing 4,5,6,8:\n\n- Start at 7. From 7, can go to 4 (removed) or 8 (removed). So 7 is isolated. Therefore, yes, disconnected. This subset is included in the first category (since it includes 4 and 8) and also includes extra nodes, but it's already counted in the first 10.\n\nSimilarly, removing nodes 2,4,5,6: includes {2,6} and 4,5. This is in the second category (since it includes 2 and 6), and also includes extra nodes, but it's counted in the second 10.\n\nAnother example: removing nodes 4,8,1,2: this is in the first category (includes 4,8) and also includes 2, but it's still counted in the first 10.\n\nWhat about a subset that doesn't include both 4 and 8, nor both 2 and 6, but still disconnects 7 from 3?\n\nFor example, suppose we remove nodes 4,5,8,9. Let's see if this disconnects 7 from 3.\n\nAfter removing 4,5,8,9:\n\n- Start at 7. From 7, can go to 4 (removed) or 8 (removed). So 7 is isolated. Therefore, disconnected. But this subset includes 4 and 8, so it's already counted in the first category.\n\nAnother example: remove nodes 2,4,6,9. This includes 2 and 6 (so it's in the second category), and also includes 4 and 6. So it's counted in the second category.\n\nWhat about removing nodes 1,2,5,8? Let's see:\n\n- Remove 1,2,5,8.\n\n- Start at 7: can go to 4 or 8 (8 is removed), so can go to 4.\n\n- From 4: can go to 1 (removed),5 (removed),7.\n\n- So from 4, only back to 7.\n\n- From 7, stuck.\n\nSo 7 is isolated. Therefore disconnected. But this subset includes 8, but not 4. It includes 2 but not 6. Since it includes 8 and 2, but not both 4 and 8 or both 2 and 6, does this fall into any of the categories?\n\nWait, this subset is {1,2,5,8}. It includes 8 but not 4, and includes 2 but not 6. Therefore, it doesn't include both 4 and 8, nor both 2 and 6. However, it still disconnects 7 from 3 because 7 is isolated. Therefore, this subset should be counted as a disconnected subset but isn't included in the previous count of 19.\n\nAh! So my previous reasoning was incomplete. There are more subsets that disconnect 7 from 3 without including both 4 and 8 or both 2 and 6.\n\nIn this example, removing nodes 1,2,5,8 disconnects 7 because 7 can only go to 4 or 8, but both 4 is still present? Wait, no, in this subset, we removed nodes 1,2,5,8. Node 4 is still present unless we removed it. Wait, node 4 is not in the removed set {1,2,5,8}, so node 4 is still there.\n\nWait a second, in the example I just considered: removing nodes 1,2,5,8:\n\n- Node 4 is still present.\n\n- Start at 7: can go to 4 (since 4 is present) or to 8 (which is removed). So from 7, can go to 4.\n\n- From 4: can go to 1 (removed), 5 (removed), or 7.\n\n- So from 4, you can go back to 7 or to 5 (but 5 is removed). So from 4, you can only go back to 7.\n\n- Therefore, the only path from 7 is looping between 7 and 4.\n\n- To reach home (3), you have to go through node 2 or 6, which are both removed. Node 3 is connected only to 2 and 6, which are gone, so node 3 is also isolated. Therefore, both 7 and 3 are isolated, so there's no path between them.\n\nBut in this case, the subset {1,2,5,8} disconnects both 7 and 3, even though it doesn't include both 4 and 8 nor both 2 and 6. This shows that my previous approach missed some cases.\n\nTherefore, my initial assumption that only subsets containing {4,8} or {2,6} would disconnect the graph is incorrect. There are other subsets that also disconnect the graph by isolating 7 or isolating 3 through other means.\n\nThis complicates things significantly. So perhaps the only safe way is to consider that any subset that disconnects 7 from 3 must either:\n\n- Isolate 7 (i.e., remove all neighbors of 7), or\n\n- Isolate 3 (i.e., remove all neighbors of 3), or\n\n- Break all paths between them in some other way.\n\nThe neighbors of 7 are only node 4. Therefore, to isolate 7, you must remove node 4. But even if you remove node 4, 7 still can go to... nothing else, since its only neighbor is 4. Wait, if you remove node 4, then 7 has no neighbors, so it's isolated. Therefore, removing node 4 alone would isolate 7. But we have to remove 4 nodes. So any subset that includes node 4 and at least three other nodes will isolate 7, regardless of the other nodes removed.\n\nSimilarly, the neighbors of 3 are nodes 2 and 6. To isolate 3, you must remove both 2 and 6. Because if you remove only one of them, 3 still has a connection to the other. For example, if you remove node 2 but leave 6, then 3 is connected to 6, so there could still be a path through 6.\n\nTherefore, to isolate 3, you must remove both 2 and 6.\n\nAdditionally, there might be subsets that don't isolate either 7 or 3 but still block all paths between them. For example, the earlier case where we removed nodes 1,2,5,8: this doesn't isolate 7 or 3, but blocks all paths.\n\nTherefore, to accurately count all disconnected subsets, we need to consider three cases:\n\n1. Subsets that isolate 7 (i.e., remove node 4 and any three other nodes).\n\n2. Subsets that isolate 3 (i.e., remove nodes 2 and 6 and any two other nodes).\n\n3. Subsets that do neither but still block all paths between 7 and 3.\n\nCalculating case 1:\n\nIsolate 7 requires removing node 4. Then, we need to remove 3 more nodes from the remaining 6 nodes (since total to remove is 4, and we've already removed 4). The remaining nodes available to remove are: 1,2,5,6,8,9 (since 4 is already removed, and we can't remove 7 or 3). So choosing 3 from these 6: C(6,3) = 20.\n\nHowever, note that some of these subsets might also isolate 3 or do other things, but they are all counted here as they already isolate 7.\n\nCase 2:\n\nIsolate 3 requires removing nodes 2 and 6. Then, we need to remove 2 more nodes from the remaining 5 nodes: 1,4,5,8,9 (since we've already removed 2 and 6; the total nodes to remove are 4, so 2 more needed). The number of subsets here is C(5,2) = 10.\n\nCase 3:\n\nSubsets that don't isolate 7 or 3 but still block all paths. This is the tricky part.\n\nTo compute case 3, we need to find subsets that do not include node 4 (so 7 is not isolated) and do not include both nodes 2 and 6 (so 3 is not isolated), but still block all paths from 7 to 3.\n\nThis requires that in the remaining graph, there is no path from 7 to 3.\n\nTo count these, perhaps we can consider that such subsets must block all four minimal paths and any longer paths. Given the complexity, maybe we can calculate the total disconnected subsets as:\n\nTotal disconnected = Case1 + Case2 - Case3_overlap + Case3,\n\nWait, no, actually:\n\nTotal disconnected subsets = (Case1) + (Case2) - (Case1 ∩ Case2) + (Case3)\n\nBut actually, Case1 and Case2 may overlap in subsets that both isolate 7 and 3. Such subsets would have been counted in both Case1 and Case2, so we need to subtract the overlap.\n\nThe overlap between Case1 and Case2 is subsets that isolate both 7 and 3. To isolate both, we must remove node 4 (to isolate 7) and nodes 2 and 6 (to isolate 3). Therefore, such a subset must include nodes 4,2,6 and one more node. The number of such subsets is C(remaining nodes,1), where the remaining nodes are the ones not in {4,2,6,7,3}. The available nodes to remove beyond the three required (4,2,6) are: 1,5,8,9. So choosing 1 more node: C(4,1)=4.\n\nTherefore:\n\nTotal disconnected subsets = (Case1) + (Case2) - (Overlap) + (Case3)\n\nWait, no. Actually:\n\nTotal disconnected = Case1 + Case2 - Overlap + Case3.\n\nBut actually, Case3 is the subsets that are neither in Case1 nor in Case2 but still disconnect the graph. So:\n\nTotal disconnected = (Case1 + Case2 - Overlap) + Case3.\n\nBut we need to compute Case3.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to compute Total disconnected = (all subsets that isolate 7) + (all subsets that isolate 3 but don't isolate 7) + (all subsets that neither isolate 7 nor 3 but still disconnect).\n\nBut this is getting too convoluted. Perhaps instead, since we know the total number of subsets is 35, and if we can compute the number of connected subsets, then subtract from 35.\n\nBut computing connected subsets might be just as hard.\n\nAlternatively, let's try to compute Case3.\n\nCase3: subsets that do not isolate 7 or 3, but still disconnect them.\n\nSuch subsets must not include node 4 (to not isolate 7) and must not include both nodes 2 and 6 (to not isolate 3). So:\n\n- Node 4 is present in the remaining graph.\n\n- Nodes 2 and 6 are not both removed; at least one is present.\n\nBut in such subsets, every path from 7 to 3 must pass through at least one removed node.\n\nGiven that node 4 is present, the start node 7 can reach node 4. From node 4, it can go to node 1, 5, or 7 (but 7 is start). So from 4, paths can go to 1 or 5.\n\nSimilarly, to reach home (3), paths must go through node 2 or 6, or through other nodes.\n\nThis is quite complex, but maybe we can consider that such subsets must block all paths by removing key nodes on all possible paths.\n\nFor instance, to block all paths, you must ensure that every path from 7 to 3 passes through at least one removed node.\n\nGiven that node 4 is present, let's see:\n\nAny path from 7 to 3 must go through node 4 or not?\n\nNo, paths can go around node 4. For example, the path 7-8-5-2-3 doesn't go through node 4.\n\nSimilarly, path 7-8-9-6-3 also doesn't go through node 4.\n\nTherefore, to block all paths, the subset must block all paths that go through node 4 and all paths that don't go through node 4.\n\nFor paths that don't go through node 4 (like 7-8-5-2-3 or 7-8-9-6-3), we must block those by removing nodes along those paths.\n\nFor example, to block the path 7-8-5-2-3, we can remove one of 8,5,2.\n\nTo block the path 7-8-9-6-3, we can remove one of 8,9,6.\n\nAdditionally, paths that go through node 4 must be blocked by removing nodes along those paths (like 1,5, etc.).\n\nThis is very involved. Given time constraints, perhaps it's better to switch tactics and use the principle of inclusion-exclusion with the two main cases.\n\nRecall that:\n\nCase1: Subsets isolating 7: C(6,3) = 20.\n\nCase2: Subsets isolating 3: C(5,2) = 10.\n\nOverlap (subsets isolating both): C(4,1) = 4.\n\nTherefore, subsets that isolate either 7 or 3: 20 + 10 - 4 = 26.\n\nNow, the remaining subsets are 35 - 26 = 9 subsets that neither isolate 7 nor isolate 3. Among these 9 subsets, some may still disconnect 7 from 3 by blocking all paths without isolating either.\n\nSo we need to find how many of these 9 subsets disconnect 7 from 3.\n\nThe 9 subsets are those that:\n\n- Do not include node 4 (since Case1 requires including 4 to isolate 7).\n\n- Do not include both nodes 2 and 6 (since Case2 requires including both 2 and 6 to isolate 3).\n\nTherefore, in these 9 subsets:\n\n- Node 4 is present.\n\n- At most one of nodes 2 or 6 is removed.\n\n- They have exactly 4 nodes removed, none of which is 7 or 3, and they don't include both 2 and 6.\n\nLet me enumerate these 9 subsets.\n\nFirst, total subsets not in Case1 or Case2:\n\nTotal subsets: 35.\n\nSubsets in Case1: 20.\n\nSubsets in Case2: 10.\n\nSubsets in both Case1 and Case2: 4.\n\nTherefore, subsets not in Case1 or Case2: 35 - 20 -10 +4 = 9.\n\nNow, let's list all subsets of size 4 that don't include node 4 and don't include both nodes 2 and 6.\n\nWait, actually, the subsets not in Case1 are those that do NOT isolate 7, i.e., they do NOT include node 4. Because isolating 7 requires removing node 4.\n\nSimilarly, subsets not in Case2 are those that do NOT isolate 3, i.e., they do NOT include both nodes 2 and 6.\n\nTherefore, the subsets not in Case1 or Case2 are those that:\n\n- Do not include node 4 (so 7 is not isolated),\n\n- Do not include both nodes 2 and 6 (so 3 is not isolated),\n\n- And have size 4.\n\nTherefore, to count these 9 subsets:\n\nWe must choose 4 nodes to remove from the available nodes, excluding node 4, and excluding the requirement that both 2 and 6 are included.\n\nWait, more precisely:\n\nThe available nodes to remove are the 7 non-start/non-home nodes: 1,2,4,5,6,8,9.\n\nBut we are excluding subsets that include node 4 (to not be in Case1), and excluding subsets that include both 2 and 6 (to not be in Case2).\n\nTherefore, the subsets we are considering are those that:\n\n- Do not include node 4.\n\n- Do not include both nodes 2 and 6.\n\n- Have size 4.\n\nTherefore, we need to count the number of 4-element subsets of {1,2,5,6,8,9} (since we exclude node 4) that do not contain both 2 and 6.\n\nThe total number of subsets of size 4 from {1,2,5,6,8,9} is C(6,4)=15.\n\nFrom these, subtract the subsets that include both 2 and 6.\n\nHow many subsets of size 4 from {1,2,5,6,8,9} include both 2 and 6?\n\nIf both 2 and 6 are included, then we need to choose 2 more nodes from the remaining 4: {1,5,8,9}. So C(4,2)=6.\n\nTherefore, the number of subsets not including both 2 and 6 is 15 - 6 = 9, which matches our previous count.\n\nTherefore, these 9 subsets are exactly the ones we need to check for whether they disconnect 7 from 3.\n\nNow, to find how many of these 9 subsets disconnect 7 from 3 (i.e., have no path from 7 to 3 in the remaining graph).\n\nTo do this, we can analyze each of the 9 subsets:\n\nFirst, list all 9 subsets:\n\nThe set from which we are choosing is S = {1,2,5,6,8,9}.\n\nWe need all 4-element subsets that do not include both 2 and 6.\n\nSo the forbidden subsets are those that include both 2 and 6. The allowed subsets are all others.\n\nLet me list all 15 subsets of size 4 from S, then exclude those that have both 2 and 6, resulting in 9 subsets.\n\nBut this might take time, but perhaps manageable.\n\nFirst, list all C(6,4)=15 subsets:\n\n1. {1,2,5,6}\n\n2. {1,2,5,8}\n\n3. {1,2,5,9}\n\n4. {1,2,6,8}\n\n5. {1,2,6,9}\n\n6. {1,2,8,9}\n\n7. {1,5,6,8}\n\n8. {1,5,6,9}\n\n9. {1,5,8,9}\n\n10. {1,6,8,9}\n\n11. {2,5,6,8}\n\n12. {2,5,6,9}\n\n13. {2,5,8,9}\n\n14. {2,6,8,9}\n\n15. {5,6,8,9}\n\nNow, remove the subsets that include both 2 and 6:\n\nSubsets 1,4,5,6,10,11,12,13,14 contain both 2 and 6? Let's see:\n\n- Subset 1: {1,2,5,6} – has both 2 and 6: yes.\n\n- Subset 4: {1,2,6,8} – yes.\n\n- Subset 5: {1,2,6,9} – yes.\n\n- Subset 6: {1,2,8,9} – yes, has 2 but not 6? Wait no: subset 6 is {1,2,8,9}: does it include 6? No. Wait, subset 6 is {1,2,8,9}, which does not include 6. Wait, hold on, I think I made a mistake here.\n\nWait, let me re-express the subsets clearly:\n\nLet me renumber them properly:\n\nWait, when I listed them earlier, I might have miscounted which have both 2 and 6.\n\nLet me re-express:\n\nThe subsets are combinations of 4 elements from {1,2,5,6,8,9}. Let me list them systematically:\n\n1. {1,2,5,6}\n\n2. {1,2,5,8}\n\n3. {1,2,5,9}\n\n4. {1,2,6,8}\n\n5. {1,2,6,9}\n\n6. {1,2,8,9}\n\n7. {1,5,6,8}\n\n8. {1,5,6,9}\n\n9. {1,5,8,9}\n\n10. {1,6,8,9}\n\n11. {2,5,6,8}\n\n12. {2,5,6,9}\n\n13. {2,5,8,9}\n\n14. {2,6,8,9}\n\n15. {5,6,8,9}\n\nNow, which of these include both 2 and 6?\n\n- Subset 1: {1,2,5,6} – yes.\n\n- Subset 4: {1,2,6,8} – yes.\n\n- Subset 5: {1,2,6,9} – yes.\n\n- Subset 11: {2,5,6,8} – yes.\n\n- Subset 12: {2,5,6,9} – yes.\n\n- Subset 14: {2,6,8,9} – yes.\n\nSo subsets 1,4,5,11,12,14 contain both 2 and 6. That's 6 subs