,2) [removed] or (2,1). From (1,2): removed. From (3,2): removed. From (2,2): removed. From (3,3): can go to (2,3) or (3,2) [removed]. From (2,3): can go to (1,3) or (2,2) [removed] or (3,3). So path: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> can't proceed further except back. Alternatively, (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) is blocked. So no path to (1,3). Wait, but (3,1) can't go to (3,2) because it's removed, so only path is through (2,1), then to (1,1), then stuck. Alternatively, from (3,3) -> (2,3) -> (1,3). But how do you get to (3,3)? From (3,1) you can't go right past (3,2) which is removed, so you can't get to (3,3). So indeed, in this removal set {(2,2), (3,2), (1,2)}, the frog is stuck. So that's another three-vertex removal set.\n\nSo it seems that three vertices might be the minimal vertex cut? Let me see if two vertices can do it.\n\nSuppose we remove (2,2) and (1,3). Wait, but (1,3) is the home and must remain, so can't remove that.\n\nAlternatively, remove (2,2) and (1,1). As before, that didn't work because there was a path through (3,2) -> (3,3) -> (2,3) -> (1,3).\n\nAlternatively, remove (2,2) and (3,3). As before, there was a path through (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3). So that path still exists.\n\nAlternatively, remove (2,1) and (1,3). But (1,3) is home, can't remove.\n\nAlternatively, remove (2,3) and (3,2). Let's see:\n\nFrom S=(3,1): can go to (2,1) or (3,2) [removed]. So to (2,1). From (2,1): can go to (1,1) or (2,2). From (1,1): to (1,2) or (2,1). From (1,2): to (1,3) or (2,2). From (2,2): to (1,2), (2,1), (2,3) [removed], or (3,2) [removed]. So path: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3). So that path still exists. So removing (2,3) and (3,2) is not enough.\n\nHmm.\n\nHow about removing (1,2) and (2,2)? Let's see:\n\nFrom S=(3,1): can go to (2,1) or (3,2). From (2,1): to (1,1) or (2,2) [removed]. From (1,1): to (1,2) [removed] or (2,1). From (3,2): to (3,1) or (3,3) or (2,2) [removed]. From (3,3): to (2,3) or (3,2). From (2,3): to (1,3) or (2,2) [removed] or (3,3). So path: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) -> (2,3) -> (1,3). So that path exists. So still connected.\n\nHmm. Maybe three vertices are indeed minimal? Let me try to see if there is a two-vertex cut.\n\nSuppose we remove (2,2) and (1,2). As before, path through (3,1)->(3,2)->(3,3)->(2,3)->(1,3) still exists.\n\nAlternatively, remove (2,2) and (3,1). But (3,1) must remain.\n\nAlternatively, remove (2,1) and (2,3). As before, path through (3,1)->(3,2)->(2,2)->(1,2)->(1,3) still exists.\n\nAlternatively, remove (1,1) and (3,3). Let's see:\n\nFrom S=(3,1): can go to (2,1) or (3,2). From (2,1): can go to (1,1) [removed] or (2,2). From (2,2): can go to (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2). From (1,2): can go to (1,3) or (2,2). From (3,2): can go to (3,3) [removed] or (2,2). From (2,3): can go to (1,3) or (2,2). So path: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) -> (1,3). So that path exists. So removing (1,1) and (3,3) is not enough.\n\nHmm. Maybe three vertices are indeed the minimal vertex cut. Let's see if there exists a two-vertex cut.\n\nWait, perhaps removing (2,2) and (1,1). Wait, tried that before, but let me check again.\n\nFrom S=(3,1): can go to (2,1) or (3,2). From (2,1): can go to (1,1) [removed] or (2,2) [removed]. So from (2,1), you can't go anywhere except back to (3,1) or to (2,2) which is removed. From (3,2): can go to (3,1), (3,3), or (2,2) [removed]. From (3,3): can go to (2,3) or (3,2). From (2,3): can go to (1,3) or (2,2) [removed] or (3,3). So path: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) -> (2,3) -> (1,3). So that path exists. So even with removing (2,2) and (1,1), there's still a path through the bottom right.\n\nAlternatively, remove (2,2) and (3,3). As before, there's a path through the top.\n\nHmm.\n\nAlternatively, remove (2,1) and (3,3). Let's see:\n\nFrom S=(3,1): can go to (2,1) [removed] or (3,2). From (3,2): can go to (3,1), (3,3) [removed], or (2,2). From (2,2): can go to (1,2), (2,1) [removed], (2,3), or (3,2). From (1,2): can go to (1,1) or (1,3) or (2,2). From (2,3): can go to (1,3) or (2,2). From (1,3): is the home.\n\nSo path: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) -> (1,3). So that path exists. So removing (2,1) and (3,3) isn't enough.\n\nAlternatively, remove (2,3) and (3,2). Let me see:\n\nFrom S=(3,1): can go to (2,1) or (3,2) [removed]. So to (2,1). From (2,1): to (1,1) or (2,2). From (1,1): to (1,2). From (1,2): to (1,3) or (2,2). From (2,2): to (1,2), (2,1), (2,3) [removed], or (3,2) [removed]. So path: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3). That path still exists.\n\nHmm. It seems like every two-vertex removal I try still leaves a path. Maybe three vertices are indeed minimal. Let me confirm with another approach.\n\nIn graph theory, the vertex connectivity of a graph is the minimum number of vertices that need to be removed to disconnect the graph. For a grid graph, the vertex connectivity is known? Let me recall.\n\nFor a 3x3 grid graph, what is its vertex connectivity?\n\nLooking it up in my mind... For a grid graph, the vertex connectivity is equal to the minimum degree of the graph, by a theorem called Menger's theorem, which states that the vertex connectivity is equal to the minimum number of disjoint paths between two vertices.\n\nAlternatively, the vertex connectivity is at least the minimum degree, and in grid graphs, it's often equal to the minimum degree.\n\nLooking at the 3x3 grid graph, the minimum degree of any node:\n\nLooking at the corners: (1,1), (1,3), (3,1), (3,3) each have degree 2.\n\nThe nodes on the edges but not corners, like (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2) have degree 3.\n\nThe center node (2,2) has degree 4.\n\nSo the minimum degree is 2. However, vertex connectivity is not necessarily equal to the minimum degree unless the graph is 2-connected. Wait, but if the minimum degree is 2, then the vertex connectivity is at most 2. But in our case, we saw that removing two vertices doesn't disconnect S from H, so the vertex connectivity must be higher?\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, vertex connectivity is the minimum number of vertices whose removal disconnects the graph. If the minimum degree is 2, then the vertex connectivity is at most 2, but if the graph is not 2-connected, then it might be less. Wait, actually, Menger's theorem says that the vertex connectivity is equal to the number of disjoint paths between two nodes. So if between S and H there are two disjoint paths, then the vertex connectivity is at least 2. But if there's only one path, then it could be 1.\n\nWait, but in our case, are there two vertex-disjoint paths from S to H?\n\nLet me see. Let's see if there are two vertex-disjoint paths from (3,1) to (1,3).\n\nFirst path: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3).\n\nSecond path: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) -> (1,3).\n\nAre these two paths vertex-disjoint? Let's see the vertices used:\n\nFirst path uses: (3,1), (2,1), (1,1), (1,2), (1,3).\n\nSecond path uses: (3,1), (3,2), (2,2), (1,2), (1,3).\n\nThey share vertices (3,1), (1,2), (1,3). So they are not vertex-disjoint. So maybe another pair?\n\nHow about:\n\nFirst path: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (2,2) -> (2,3) -> (1,3).\n\nSecond path: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) -> (1,3).\n\nBut again, both paths go through (2,2), so not disjoint.\n\nAlternatively,\n\nFirst path: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3).\n\nSecond path: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) -> (2,3) -> (1,3).\n\nThese two paths:\n\nFirst path: (3,1), (2,1), (1,1), (1,2), (1,3).\n\nSecond path: (3,1), (3,2), (3,3), (2,3), (1,3).\n\nThese share only the start and end vertices, so they are vertex-disjoint except for the start and end. Since the start and end are required to remain, the paths are considered disjoint in terms of internal vertices. Therefore, there are two vertex-disjoint paths between S and H, which would imply that the vertex connectivity between S and H is at least 2. But we saw that removing two vertices (like (2,2) and something else) didn't disconnect them. Wait, but if there are two vertex-disjoint paths, then you need to remove at least two vertices to block all paths? Hmm, but in our trials above, removing two vertices didn't block all paths.\n\nWait, actually, in the two paths I just mentioned:\n\nFirst path: uses (2,1), (1,1), (1,2).\n\nSecond path: uses (3,2), (3,3), (2,3).\n\nSo if we remove the vertices that are in both paths? But they don't share any internal vertices. So to block both paths, you need to remove at least one vertex from each path. Since the paths are vertex-disjoint except for start and end, you can block each path by removing one vertex from each. So to disconnect S from H, you need to remove at least two vertices: one from each path. Therefore, the minimal vertex cut size is 2? But in my trials above, when I tried removing two vertices, I could still find a third path.\n\nWait, so maybe there are more than two vertex-disjoint paths? Let me see.\n\nThird path: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) -> (1,3).\n\nFourth path: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (2,2) -> (2,3) -> (1,3).\n\nSo, in fact, there are multiple paths. So the vertex connectivity between S and H is actually higher?\n\nWait, according to Menger's theorem, the vertex connectivity k between S and H is the maximum number of vertex-disjoint paths from S to H. So if there are two vertex-disjoint paths, then k >= 2. If there are three, then k >=3, etc.\n\nFrom above, we can find two vertex-disjoint paths (the first and second paths I mentioned), so k >=2.\n\nBut can we find three vertex-disjoint paths?\n\nLet me try to find three paths that don't share any internal vertices.\n\nFirst path: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3).\n\nSecond path: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) -> (2,3) -> (1,3).\n\nThird path: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (2,2) -> (2,3) -> (1,3).\n\nWait, but the third path shares (2,1) with the first path and (2,3) with the second path. So not disjoint.\n\nAlternatively,\n\nThird path: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) -> (1,3). But this shares (1,2) with the first path.\n\nAlternatively,\n\nThird path: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) -> (1,3). Same as above.\n\nAlternatively,\n\nThird path: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (2,2) -> (1,2) -> (1,3). Shares (2,1) and (1,2) with first path.\n\nHmm.\n\nAlternatively,\n\nThird path: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (2,2) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3). But this shares many vertices with the first path.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's impossible to have three vertex-disjoint paths. Let me see.\n\nSuppose we try to make a third path that doesn't share any vertices with the first two paths I mentioned:\n\nFirst path: uses (2,1), (1,1), (1,2).\n\nSecond path: uses (3,2), (3,3), (2,3).\n\nSo the third path would have to avoid those vertices. Let's see:\n\nStart at (3,1). From there, can go to (2,1) [used in first path] or (3,2) [used in second path]. So blocked at the first step. Therefore, no third path exists that avoids the first two paths' internal vertices. Therefore, only two vertex-disjoint paths exist. Therefore, by Menger's theorem, the vertex connectivity between S and H is 2. Therefore, the minimal vertex cut size is 2.\n\nBut earlier, when I tried removing two vertices, I could still find a path. For example, removing (2,2) and (1,1):\n\nWait, but in that case, the two vertex-disjoint paths were:\n\nFirst path: (3,1) -> (2,1) -> (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3). But if we remove (1,1), then that path is blocked. The second path was: (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3) -> (2,3) -> (1,3). If we remove (2,2) but not (3,2), then that second path is still intact. Wait, in the removal of (2,2) and (1,1), the second path doesn't go through (2,2), so it's still there. Therefore, removing those two vertices only blocks one of the two vertex-disjoint paths, leaving the other path open.\n\nAh! So to block all paths, you need to remove at least one vertex from each of the two vertex-disjoint paths. Since there are two vertex-disjoint paths, you need at least two vertices to block both. However, if the two paths share a common vertex, then removing that vertex would block both, but in this case, they don't share any internal vertices. Therefore, to block both paths, you need to remove at least one vertex from each path. Therefore, the minimal vertex cut size is 2. However, in my earlier attempt, when I removed two vertices, say (2,2) and (1,1), then the second path (the one going through (3,2), (3,3), (2,3)) wasn't affected by removing (2,2), so that path remains. Therefore, only one path was blocked, the other remained. Therefore, two vertices are not enough if they are not chosen properly.\n\nTherefore, to have a vertex cut of size 2, we must choose two vertices such that every path from S to H passes through at least one of them. So it's not sufficient to just remove any two vertices; they have to be a vertex cut.\n\nSo in graph theory terms, a vertex cut (or vertex separator) of size 2 between S and H would be a set of two vertices whose removal disconnects S from H. We need to find all such vertex cuts of size 2, and then all vertex cuts of size 3, etc., but since we are only removing 4 vertices, we can have cuts of size 2, 3, or 4.\n\nBut since we are to compute the number of subsets of 4 vertices (excluding S and H) that form a vertex cut (i.e., whose removal disconnects S from H), we need to count all such subsets of size 4 that are vertex cuts.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's easier to compute the total number of subsets of size 4 (that don't include S or H) and subtract those subsets that do NOT disconnect S from H. That is, the \"bad\" subsets are those where there exists some path from S to H in the remaining graph. So the desired number is total subsets minus bad subsets.\n\nTotal subsets: C(7,4) = 35.\n\nBad subsets: subsets of size 4 (not including S or H) such that there exists a path from S to H in the induced subgraph.\n\nTherefore, to compute the bad subsets, we need to count all subsets of 4 vertices (from the 7 non-S/H vertices) such that the remaining graph (which includes S and H and the 4 chosen vertices) still has a path from S to H.\n\nAlternatively, equivalently, the complement of the subset (the removed vertices) do not form a vertex cut. That is, the removed vertices do not intersect every path from S to H. Wait, actually, the subset we're considering is the removed vertices; the remaining graph is the original minus the removed. So the remaining graph has a path from S to H iff the removed set does not intersect every path from S to H. So the bad subsets (those we want to subtract) are those removed sets that are not hitting sets for all paths.\n\nTherefore, to compute the number of bad subsets: the number of subsets of size 4 (from the 7 non-S/H vertices) that do not hit all paths from S to H. In other words, for which there exists at least one path from S to H that doesn't use any removed vertex (i.e., all vertices on the path are in the remaining set).\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to counting the number of 4-vertex subsets (from the 7 non-S/H vertices) that do NOT form a vertex cut between S and H.\n\nThis seems a bit abstract, but maybe we can approach it by considering the two vertex-disjoint paths and using inclusion-exclusion or something.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's manageable to compute the total number of subsets (35) and subtract those subsets that are not hitting sets for all paths. To do this, we can compute the number of subsets that fail to hit all paths, i.e., subsets that leave at least one path intact.\n\nBut calculating that directly might be tricky due to overlapping paths. Maybe we can instead compute the number of vertex cuts of size 2, 3, and 4, and sum them up? Since a vertex cut of size k is a set of k vertices whose removal disconnects S from H. Then the number of vertex cuts of size 2, 3, 4 would be the \"good\" subsets we want. Wait no: actually, the \"good\" subsets are exactly the vertex cuts of size 4? No, not necessarily. A vertex cut can be of size 2, 3, or 4, but we are only considering subsets of size 4. So the \"good\" subsets are precisely the vertex cuts of size 4 (i.e., subsets of size 4 that are vertex cuts) plus any subsets of size 4 that are not vertex cuts but still disconnect S from H? Wait no: actually, a subset of size 4 is a vertex cut if and only if its removal disconnects S from H. So the \"good\" subsets we want are exactly the vertex cuts of size 4. Because we need the removal to disconnect S from H. Therefore, the number we want is the number of vertex cuts of size 4 in the graph (excluding S and H). Therefore, to compute the answer, we need to compute the number of vertex cuts of size 4 in the graph induced by the 7 non-S/H vertices.\n\nWait, but vertex cuts are defined as subsets of vertices whose removal disconnects the graph. However, in our case, we are only removing vertices from the 7 non-S/H nodes, and we require that after removal, S and H are disconnected. So yes, exactly the vertex cuts in the original graph between S and H, but only considering subsets of the non-S/H vertices. Since S and H must remain, we cannot remove them, so we are only removing from the other 7.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to: in the graph G, with vertex set V = {S, H, A}, where A is the 7 non-S/H nodes, find the number of subsets C ⊆ A with |C| = 4 such that C is a vertex cut between S and H in G.\n\nTherefore, to compute this, we can:\n\n1. Enumerate all subsets of the 7 non-S/H vertices of size 4.\n\n2. For each subset, check whether removing those 4 vertices disconnects S from H.\n\n3. Count how many such subsets satisfy the disconnection.\n\nBut since there are C(7,4) = 35 subsets, this is feasible by hand, although tedious.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can find a smarter way.\n\nFirst, let me label the 7 non-S/H vertices for clarity. Let me assign labels to them:\n\nLet me denote the nodes as follows:\n\nLet me use coordinates as before:\n\n(1,1): let's call this node A1\n\n(1,2): A2\n\n(1,3): H (home, cannot be removed)\n\n(2,1): B1\n\n(2,2): B2\n\n(2,3): B3\n\n(3,1): S (start,