, B3}.\n\nWait, but the subset already includes B2, and we need to choose 3 more nodes from the remaining 6 nodes (N1, N2, N3, N5, N6, N7). To block both Path A and Path B, the additional 3 nodes must include at least one from {B1, A1, A2} and at least one from {C1, C2, B3}.\n\nTherefore, within Case 1 (B2 is included in the subset), the number of subsets that also include at least one from Path A nodes and at least one from Path B nodes is equal to:\n\nTotal subsets in Case 1: C(6,3)=20.\n\nMinus subsets that don't block Path A: those subsets that exclude all of {B1, A1, A2}.\n\nMinus subsets that don't block Path B: those subsets that exclude all of {C1, C2, B3}.\n\nPlus subsets that exclude both Path A and Path B (because they were subtracted twice).\n\nSo using inclusion-exclusion:\n\nNumber of \"good\" subsets in Case 1 =\n\nTotal subsets in Case 1\n\n- subsets missing all of Path A nodes (i.e., subsets that include none of B1, A1, A2)\n\n- subsets missing all of Path B nodes (i.e., subsets that include none of C1, C2, B3)\n\n+ subsets missing both Path A and Path B nodes (i.e., subsets that include none of B1, A1, A2, C1, C2, B3)\n\nFirst, compute subsets missing all of Path A nodes (B1, A1, A2):\n\nThe nodes available for selection in Case 1 are N1(A1), N2(A2), N3(B1), N5(B3), N6(C1), N7(C2). To exclude all of Path A nodes (B1, A1, A2), we must exclude N1, N2, N3.\n\nTherefore, the available nodes to choose the remaining 3 nodes from are N5(B3), N6(C1), N7(C2). There are only 3 nodes here, and we need to choose 3. So there is exactly 1 such subset: {N5, N6, N7}.\n\nSimilarly, subsets missing all of Path B nodes (C1, C2, B3): Path B nodes are C1(N6), C2(N7), B3(N5). So to exclude all of them, we must exclude N5, N6, N7.\n\nTherefore, the available nodes to choose the remaining 3 nodes from are N1(A1), N2(A2), N3(B1). Again, 3 nodes, so only 1 subset: {N1, N2, N3}.\n\nNow, subsets missing both Path A and Path B nodes: that is, excluding all nodes in Path A and Path B. Path A nodes: B1(N3), A1(N1), A2(N2). Path B nodes: C1(N6), C2(N7), B3(N5). So combined, the excluded nodes are N1, N2, N3, N5, N6, N7. The only remaining node is N4 (B2), but B2 is already included in the subset (since we are in Case 1). Wait, no:\n\nWait, in Case 1, we are considering subsets that include B2 (N4). The nodes we can choose the other 3 from are N1, N2, N3, N5, N6, N7.\n\nIf we exclude all of Path A and Path B nodes, that means we exclude N1, N2, N3 (Path A) and N5, N6, N7 (Path B). So the only node left is none, because all are excluded. Wait, but we have to choose 3 nodes from the 6, but if we exclude all 6, then we can't choose any. But we need to choose 3. Therefore, there are zero subsets that exclude all of Path A and Path B nodes, since that would require selecting 3 nodes from an empty set.\n\nTherefore, by inclusion-exclusion:\n\nNumber of \"good\" subsets in Case 1 =\n\n20 - 1 - 1 + 0 = 18.\n\nWait, hold on:\n\nWait, the formula is:\n\nNumber = Total - (A - B + C) where A is subsets missing Path A, B missing Path B, and C the intersection.\n\nWait, inclusion-exclusion formula is:\n\n|A ∪ B| = |A| + |B| - |A ∩ B|\n\nTherefore, the number of subsets that miss at least one of Path A or Path B is |A| + |B| - |A ∩ B|.\n\nTherefore, the number of subsets that miss neither (i.e., block both paths) is Total - |A ∪ B| = Total - (|A| + |B| - |A ∩ B|).\n\nIn our case:\n\nTotal = 20\n\n|A| = 1 (subsets missing Path A)\n\n|B| = 1 (subsets missing Path B)\n\n|A ∩ B| = 0 (since you can't miss both Path A and Path B nodes when selecting 3 from 6, since missing both requires excluding all 6 nodes, which is impossible with only 3 selections).\n\nTherefore,\n\nNumber of \"good\" subsets in Case 1 = 20 - (1 + 1 - 0) = 20 - 2 = 18.\n\nTherefore, in Case 1 (B2 removed), there are 18 subsets that also block Path A and Path B, thus disconnecting S from H.\n\nCase 2: Subsets that do NOT include B2. So the subset is chosen from the other 6 nodes: N1, N2, N3, N5, N6, N7. We need to choose 4 nodes from these 6, so C(6,4)=15 subsets.\n\nIn this case, B2 remains in the graph. Therefore, there are paths that go through B2 which are still intact. For example, the two paths that go through B2:\n\nPath C: S -> B1 -> B2 -> B3 -> H\n\nPath D: S -> C1 -> B2 -> A2 -> H\n\nPath E: S -> B1 -> B2 -> A2 -> A1 -> H? Not sure, but at least Path C and D exist.\n\nTherefore, to disconnect S from H when B2 is present, we must block all paths that go through B2, as well as any other paths that don't go through B2.\n\nBut since B2 is present, paths through B2 are possible, so we must block all paths through B2 as well as the other paths not through B2.\n\nHowever, this seems complex. Let's see:\n\nFirst, note that in this case (B2 not removed), there are multiple paths:\n\n- Path A: S->B1->A1->A2->H (avoids B2)\n\n- Path B: S->C1->C2->B3->H (avoids B2)\n\n- Path C: S->B1->B2->B3->H (goes through B2)\n\n- Path D: S->C1->B2->A2->H (goes through B2)\n\n- Path E: S->B1->B2->A2->A1->H? Let's see:\n\nFrom B2, can go to A2, then to A1, then to H? From A2, you can go to H directly, so Path E would be S->B1->B2->A2->H.\n\nSimilarly, Path F: S->C1->B2->A2->A1->H? From B2 to A2 to A1 to H, but that's the same as Path D and E combined?\n\nActually, the key point is that even if B2 is present, there are paths that go through B2 and paths that don't. To disconnect S from H, we must block all paths, both those going through B2 and those not.\n\nTherefore, in this case (Case 2: B2 not removed), to disconnect S from H, the subset must:\n\n1. Block all paths that don't go through B2: i.e., Path A and Path B must be blocked.\n\n2. Block all paths that do go through B2: i.e., Path C, D, etc.\n\nTo block all paths going through B2, we must ensure that every path through B2 is blocked. Since B2 is present, to block all paths through B2, we need to ensure that every incoming edge to B2 and every outgoing edge from B2 is blocked, but since we can only remove nodes, we need to remove enough nodes so that B2 is isolated or all paths through B2 are blocked.\n\nAlternatively, since B2 has degree 4 (connected to B1, A2, B3, C1), to block all paths through B2, we need to remove at least one node from each of the paths through B2.\n\nBut this is getting too vague. Let me think differently.\n\nTo block all paths through B2, we need that for every path from S to H passing through B2, at least one node on that path is removed.\n\nBut since B2 is in the middle, any path through B2 must come from either S->...->B2 and then B2->...->H. To block all such paths, we need to ensure that either:\n\n- All paths from S to B2 are blocked, or\n\n- All paths from B2 to H are blocked.\n\nBut since we are only removing 4 nodes (and B2 is not removed in this case), we need to block access to B2 from S or block access from B2 to H.\n\nAlternatively, the set of removed nodes must form a vertex cut between S and B2, and/or between B2 and H.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to consider that in Case 2 (B2 not removed), the only way to disconnect S from H is to either:\n\na) Block all paths from S to B2, and also block all paths from B2 to H.\n\nOr\n\nb) Block all paths from S to H that don't go through B2 (i.e., Path A and Path B), and also ensure that any path through B2 is blocked.\n\nBut this is still vague. Perhaps we can model this as follows:\n\nIn Case 2 (B2 is present), to disconnect S from H, the subset must:\n\n1. Either block all paths from S to B2, or block all paths from B2 to H, or both.\n\n2. Additionally, block all direct paths not going through B2 (Path A and Path B).\n\nAlternatively, since B2 is a central node, if we can block all paths from S to B2 and all paths from B2 to H, then S and H would be disconnected.\n\nTo block all paths from S to B2:\n\nThe paths from S to B2 are:\n\n- S -> B1 -> B2\n\n- S -> C1 -> B2\n\nSimilarly, paths from B2 to H are:\n\n- B2 -> A2 -> H\n\n- B2 -> B3 -> H\n\n- B2 -> C1 -> C2 -> B3 -> H? Wait, no, from B2, you can go to A2, B3, or C1.\n\nFrom B2 to H:\n\n- B2 -> A2 -> H\n\n- B2 -> B3 -> H\n\n- B2 -> C1 -> C2 -> B3 -> H? Wait, from B2 to C1 is possible, but then from C1 to C2 to B3 to H.\n\nSo paths from B2 to H include:\n\n- Direct: B2->A2->H\n\n- B2->B3->H\n\n- B2->C1->C2->B3->H\n\n- B2->C1->B3->H? Wait, from B2 to C1 is allowed, then from C1 to B3? No, C1 is connected to C2 and B2 and S's column. C1 is (3,2), so connected to C2 (3,3), B2 (2,2), and S (3,1). Wait, C1 is connected to S? Wait, S is (3,1), which is connected to B1 (2,1) and C1 (3,2). Yes, so C1 is connected to S. But from C1, you can go to C2 or back to S or to B2.\n\nFrom C1, to get to H, you would have to go through C2, B3, etc.\n\nSo, to block all paths from B2 to H, we need to remove nodes that are on all paths from B2 to H. The minimal vertex cut between B2 and H.\n\nSimilarly, the minimal vertex cut between S and B2.\n\nAlternatively, this is getting too involved. Maybe we can compute the number of subsets in Case 2 that disconnect S from H by considering that they must block both Path A and Path B (the two paths not going through B2), and also block all paths through B2.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's simpler to compute the total number of \"good\" subsets in Case 2 as follows:\n\nTo disconnect S from H when B2 is present, the subset must:\n\n- Block all paths from S to H not going through B2: i.e., Path A and Path B must be blocked.\n\n- Additionally, block all paths going through B2.\n\nTo block Path A (S->B1->A1->A2->H), we need to remove at least one of B1, A1, or A2.\n\nTo block Path B (S->C1->C2->B3->H), we need to remove at least one of C1, C2, or B3.\n\nTo block all paths through B2, we need to ensure that B2 is either isolated from S or from H, or both.\n\nTo isolate B2 from S: all paths from S to B2 must be blocked. The paths from S to B2 are:\n\n1. S->B1->B2\n\n2. S->C1->B2\n\nTherefore, to block both paths, we must remove either B1 or B2 or C1, but since B2 is not removed, we need to remove B1 or C1 to block the paths to B2.\n\nWait, to block all paths from S to B2:\n\n- To block S->B1->B2: remove B1 or B2 (but B2 is not removed) or remove the edge, but we can only remove nodes. So to block that path, remove B1 or B2 (can't remove B2), so must remove B1.\n\n- To block S->C1->B2: remove C1 or B2 (can't remove B2), so must remove C1.\n\nTherefore, to block all paths from S to B2, we must remove both B1 and C1.\n\nSimilarly, to block all paths from B2 to H:\n\nThe paths from B2 to H are:\n\n1. B2->A2->H\n\n2. B2->B3->H\n\n3. B2->C1->C2->B3->H (but C1 is already considered above)\n\nWait, actually, let's see:\n\nFrom B2 to H:\n\n- Directly via A2: B2->A2->H\n\n- Via B3: B2->B3->H\n\n- Via C1: B2->C1->C2->B3->H (but this goes through C1 and C2, which may or may not be removed)\n\nTherefore, to block all paths from B2 to H, we need to remove at least one node from each of the possible paths.\n\nThe paths are:\n\nPath 1: B2->A2->H: must remove A2 or B2 (can't remove B2), so remove A2.\n\nPath 2: B2->B3->H: must remove B3 or B2 (can't), so remove B3.\n\nPath 3: B2->C1->C2->B3->H: this path requires C1, C2, B3. To block this path, we need to remove at least one of C1, C2, or B3. However, since we already have to remove B3 to block Path 2, removing B3 would also block Path 3.\n\nTherefore, to block all paths from B2 to H, we need to remove A2 and B3.\n\nTherefore, to block all paths from S to B2 and all paths from B2 to H, we need to remove:\n\n- B1 and C1 (to block S->B2)\n\n- A2 and B3 (to block B2->H)\n\nAdditionally, we must also block the original Paths A and B.\n\nWait, but if we remove B1, A1, A2, C1, B3, then that might be overkill, but let's see.\n\nAlternatively, the requirements are:\n\nTo block all paths through B2, we must remove B1 and C1 (to block S->B2) and remove A2 and B3 (to block B2->H). So total of 4 nodes: B1, C1, A2, B3.\n\nIf a subset includes these four nodes, then B2 is isolated from both S and H, so S and H are disconnected.\n\nAlternatively, maybe fewer nodes can do this?\n\nSuppose we remove B1, C1, A2, and B3: that's four nodes. Then:\n\n- S can't reach B2 because B1 and C1 are removed.\n\n- B2 can't reach H because A2 and B3 are removed.\n\nTherefore, S and H are disconnected.\n\nAlternatively, could we remove fewer nodes?\n\nSuppose we remove B1, C1, and A2. Then:\n\n- S can't reach B2 via B1 or C1.\n\n- B2 can still go to B3 via... wait, B2 is connected to B3 directly. So from B2, you can go to B3, then to H if B3 is not removed. Since we only removed A2, B3 is still there. So B2 can go to B3, then to H, so that path remains. Therefore, we still have a path from B2 to H via B3. So we still need to remove B3 as well.\n\nSimilarly, if we remove B1, C1, B3, and A2, that's four nodes.\n\nAlternatively, remove B1, C1, A2, and something else?\n\nAlternatively, remove B1, C1, A2, and C2: then, from B2, you can go to B3 (since B3 is not removed), then to H via B3, so that path still exists. So need to also remove B3.\n\nTherefore, it seems that to block all paths through B2, you need to remove B1, C1, A2, and B3. That's four nodes.\n\nAdditionally, we must also block Paths A and B (the non-B2 paths). Let's see:\n\nPath A: S->B1->A1->A2->H. If we have removed B1 and A2, then this path is blocked.\n\nPath B: S->C1->C2->B3->H. If we have removed C1 and B3, then this path is blocked.\n\nTherefore, the four-node set {B1, C1, A2, B3} indeed blocks all paths, including the non-B2 paths.\n\nAre there other four-node subsets in Case 2 that also disconnect S from H?\n\nSuppose instead of removing B1, C1, A2, B3, we remove other nodes.\n\nFor example, suppose we remove A1, A2, C1, C2.\n\nThen:\n\n- Path A: S->B1->A1->A2->H is blocked because A1 and A2 are removed.\n\n- Path B: S->C1->C2->B3->H is blocked because C1 and C2 are removed.\n\n- Paths through B2: S can go to B1 or C1 (C1 is removed), so to B1. From B1, can go to A1 (removed) or back to S or to B2. So from B1, can go to B2. From B2, can go to A2 (removed), B3, or C1 (removed). So from B2, can go to B3. From B3, can go to H or to C2 (removed) or back to B2. So path: S->B1->B2->B3->H is still possible. Therefore, this subset doesn't block all paths.\n\nTherefore, this subset is not sufficient.\n\nAnother example: remove B1, A2, B3, C2.\n\nThen:\n\n- Path A: blocked by B1 or A2.\n\n- Path B: blocked by B3 or C2.\n\n- Paths through B2: S can go to C1 (if not removed). If C1 is not removed, then S->C1->B2. From B2, can go to A2 (removed), B3 (removed), or C1 (still there). From B2->C1, then from C1 can go to C2 (removed) or back to S or to B2. So from C1, can't get to H. Alternatively, from B2->B3 (removed). So the path S->C1->B2->C1->... loops. So S can't reach H via B2.\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nFrom B2, can you go to A2? No, A2 is removed.\n\nCan you go to B3? B3 is removed.\n\nCan you go to C1? Yes, but from C1 you can only go back to S or to B2 or to C2 (removed). So you can't proceed to H from C1.\n\nThus, from B2, you can't reach H because the only paths from B2 to H require going through A2 or B3, which are removed. Therefore, this subset also disconnects S from H.\n\nWait, so this subset {B1, A2, B3, C2} also works.\n\nHmm, so there are multiple subsets in Case 2 that can disconnect S from H.\n\nThis suggests that my earlier approach is not sufficient, and that I need a better method.\n\nPerhaps it's better to realize that in Case 2 (B2 not removed), the only way to disconnect S from H is to ensure that both:\n\n1. The two non-B2 paths (Path A and Path B) are blocked.\n\n2. All paths through B2 are blocked.\n\nTo block the non-B2 paths:\n\n- Path A requires blocking B1, A1, or A2.\n\n- Path B requires blocking C1, C2, or B3.\n\nTo block all paths through B2:\n\n- As before, to block all paths from S to B2: need to remove B1 and/or C1.\n\n- To block all paths from B2 to H: need to remove A2 and/or B3.\n\nTherefore, to block all paths through B2, we must remove at least one of B1 or C1 (to block S->B2), and at least one of A2 or B3 (to block B2->H).\n\nAdditionally, to block the non-B2 paths:\n\n- To block Path A: need to remove at least one of B1, A1, A2.\n\n- To block Path B: need to remove at least one of C1, C2, B3.\n\nTherefore, combining these requirements:\n\nLet's denote the required removals:\n\nLet’s define:\n\nTo block S->B2: must remove at least one from {B1, C1}\n\nTo block B2->H: must remove at least one from {A2, B3}\n\nTo block Path A: must remove at least one from {B1, A1, A2}\n\nTo block Path B: must remove at least one from {C1, C2, B3}\n\nTherefore, the total requirements are:\n\n- From {B1, C1}: at least one removed.\n\n- From {A2, B3}: at least one removed.\n\n- From {B1, A1, A2}: at least one removed.\n\n- From {C1, C2, B3}: at least one removed.\n\nWe need to count the number of 4-node subsets from the 6 nodes (N1, N2, N3, N5, N6, N7) that satisfy all these conditions.\n\nThis is a bit involved, but let's try to model it.\n\nLet me denote the nodes as follows for clarity in this context:\n\nNodes available in Case 2 (excluding B2): [N1 (A1), N2 (A2), N3 (B1), N5 (B3), N6 (C1), N7 (C2)]\n\nWe need to choose 4 nodes from these 6, such that:\n\n1. At least one of N3 (B1) or N6 (C1) is included.\n\n2. At least one of N2 (A2) or N5 (B3) is included.\n\n3. At least one of N3 (B1), N1 (A1), or N2 (A2) is included.\n\n4. At least one of N6 (C1), N7 (C2), or N5 (B3) is included.\n\nLet me see if these conditions can be translated into constraints.\n\nFirst, note that condition 3 is actually redundant because condition 1 requires at least one of B1 or C1, and condition 4 requires at least one of C1, C2, or B3. However, condition 3 is about blocking Path A, which requires removing at least one of B1, A1, or A2. Since we are choosing nodes to remove, if we remove B1 (from condition 1), that satisfies condition 3. If we don't remove B1, then we need to remove either A1 or A2 to satisfy condition 3.\n\nSimilarly, condition 4 requires removing at least one of C1, C2, or B3. If we remove C1 (from condition 1 or 4), that satisfies it. If not, then we need to remove either C2 or B3.\n\nThis is getting complex. Maybe it's better to use the principle of inclusion-exclusion again, but it's quite involved.\n\nAlternatively, since the total number of subsets in Case 2 is 15, and it's manageable, perhaps we can compute it by considering the different possibilities.\n\nFirst, let's note that to satisfy all four conditions, the subset must:\n\n- Include at least one from {B1, C1} (condition 1)\n\n- Include at least one from {A2, B3} (condition 2)\n\n- Include at least one from {B1, A1, A2} (condition 3)\n\n- Include at least one from {C1, C2, B3} (condition 4)\n\nLet me see what each condition implies.\n\nCondition 1: At least one of B1 or C1.\n\nCondition 2: At least one of A2 or B3.\n\nCondition 3: At least one of B1, A1, or A2.\n\nCondition 4: At least one of C1, C2, or B3.\n\nNow, let's consider the complement: subsets that fail to satisfy any of these conditions.\n\nWe need to subtract subsets that:\n\n- Don't satisfy condition 1,\n\n- Or don't satisfy condition 2,\n\n- Or don't satisfy condition 3,\n\n- Or don't satisfy condition 4.\n\nUsing inclusion-exclusion, the number of subsets that satisfy all conditions is:\n\nTotal subsets in Case 2 (15) minus\n\n[ subsets missing condition1 + subsets missing condition2 + subsets missing condition3 + subsets missing condition4 ]\n\nplus [ subsets missing both condition1 and condition2 + subsets missing both condition1 and condition3 + ... all pairs ]\n\nminus [ subsets missing three conditions ] + [ subsets missing all four conditions ]\n\nThis will be very tedious, but let's try.\n\nFirst, define:\n\nLet A = subsets missing condition1 (i.e., subsets with neither B1 nor C1)\n\nB = subsets missing condition2 (i.e., subsets with neither A2 nor B3)\n\nC = subsets missing condition3 (i.e., subsets with none of B1, A1, A2)\n\nD = subsets missing condition4 (i.e., subsets with none of C1, C2, B3)\n\nWe need to compute:\n\n|A ∪ B ∪ C ∪ D| = |A| + |B| + |C| + |D| - |A∩B| - |A∩C| - |A∩D| - |B∩C| - |B∩D| - |C∩D| + |A∩B∩C| + |A∩B∩D| + |A∩C∩D| + |B∩C∩D| - |A∩B∩C∩D|\n\nThen, the desired number is Total - |A ∪ B ∪ C ∪ D|\n\nFirst, compute each term:\n\nTotal = 15.\n\nCompute |A|: subsets missing B1 and C1. Since we are in Case 2, the available nodes are N1, N2, N3, N5, N6, N7. To exclude B1 (N3) and C1 (N6), we must choose all 4 nodes from the remaining nodes: N1 (A1), N2 (A2), N5 (B3), N7 (C2). There are 4 nodes, and we need to choose 4, so |A| = 1 (only one subset: {N1, N2, N5, N7}).\n\nCompute |B|: subsets missing A2 and B3. So exclude N2 (A2) and N5 (B3). Available nodes: N1, N3, N6, N7. Choose 4 from these 4: only 1 subset: {N1, N3, N6, N7}.\n\nCompute |C|: subsets missing B1, A1, A2. So exclude N3 (B1), N1 (A1), N2 (A2). Available nodes: N5 (B3), N6 (C1), N7 (C2). Need to choose 4 nodes from 3, which is impossible. So |C| = 0.\n\nCompute |D|: subsets missing C1, C2, B3. Exclude N6 (C1), N7 (C2), N5 (B3). Available nodes: N1 (A1), N2 (A2), N3 (B1). Choose 4 from 3: impossible. So |D| = 0.\n\nNext, compute pairwise intersections:\n\n|A∩B|: subsets missing B1, C1, A2, B3. So exclude N3, N6, N2, N5. Available nodes: N1, N7. Need to choose 4 from 2: impossible. So |A∩B| = 0.\n\n|A∩C|: subsets missing B1, C1 and also missing B1, A1, A2. So exclude N3, N6, N1, N2. Available nodes: N5, N7. Need to choose 4 from 2: impossible. So |A∩C| = 0.\n\n|A∩D|: subsets missing B1, C1 and missing C1, C2, B3. So exclude N3, N6, N5, N7. Available nodes: N1, N2. Choose 4 from 2: impossible. So |A∩D| = 0.\n\n|B∩C|: subsets missing A2, B3 and missing B1, A1, A2. So exclude N2, N5, N3, N1. Available nodes: N6, N7. Choose 4 from 2: impossible. So |B∩C| = 0.\n\n|B∩D|: subsets missing A2, B3 and missing C1, C2, B3. So exclude N2, N5, N6, N7. Available nodes: N1, N3. Choose 4 from 2: impossible. So |B∩D| = 0.\n\n|C∩D|: subsets missing B1, A1, A2 and missing C1, C2, B3. So exclude N3, N1, N2, N6, N7, N5. All nodes except none? Available nodes: none. So can't choose 4. So |C∩D| = 0.\n\nNow, three-way intersections:\n\nAll three-way intersections will also be impossible because they require excluding more nodes than available. For example:\n\n|A∩B∩C|: subsets missing B1,C1,A2,B3 and also missing B1,A1,A2. This requires excluding even more nodes, so |A∩B∩C| = 0.\n\nSimilarly, all other three-way intersections are 0.\n\nFour-way intersection |A∩B∩C∩D|: obviously 0.\n\nTherefore, all intersection terms beyond the first are 0.\n\nTherefore, the inclusion-exclusion formula simplifies to:\n\n|A ∪ B ∪ C ∪ D| = |A| + |B| + |C| + |D| - 0 - 0 - 0 - 0 - 0 - 0 + 0 - 0 = |A| + |B| + |C| + |D| = 1 + 1 + 0 + 0 = 2.\n\nTherefore, the number of subsets that satisfy all four conditions is:\n\nTotal - |A ∪ B ∪ C ∪ D| = 15 - 2 = 13.\n\nWait, but hold on: C and D had zero subsets, so the only subsets excluded are those in A or B. The subsets that are invalid are those missing condition1 or missing condition2. The valid subsets are those that are not in A or B, so 15 - |A| - |B| = 15 -1 -1 =13.\n\nTherefore, in Case 2, there are 13 subsets that disconnect S from H.\n\nTherefore, total \"good\" subsets are:\n\nCase1: 18 subsets\n\nCase2: 13 subsets\n\nTotal good subsets: 18 + 13 = 31.\n\nWait, but hold on: the total number of subsets is C(7,4)=35. If good subsets are 31, then the bad subsets are 4. But let me verify this because it feels high.\n\nWait, but let me check the math again:\n\nIn Case 1 (B2 removed): 20 subsets total, with 18 good.\n\nIn Case 2 (B2 not removed): 15 subsets total, with 13 good.\n\nTotal good subsets: 18 + 13 = 31.\n\nTotal subsets: 35.\n\nThus, bad subsets: 35 - 31 = 4.\n\nHmm, that suggests only 4 subsets do NOT disconnect S from H, which seems low, but maybe it's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in the inclusion-exclusion for Case 2.\n\nWait, in Case 2, we had:\n\nTotal subsets: 15\n\nSubsets that fail to satisfy all conditions: |A ∪ B ∪ C ∪ D| = 2 (the subsets that are in A or in B).\n\nThus, subsets that are good (satisfy all conditions) are 15 - 2 = 13.\n\nTherefore, 13 + 18 = 31 good subsets.\n\nBut let me sanity check with an example.\n\nTake a subset that is in Case 2 but not in A or B: for example, remove B1, A2, B3, C1.\n\nThis subset includes B1 (so not in A), includes A2 or B3 (both are included), includes A1? No, A1 is not removed. Wait, does it satisfy condition3?\n\nCondition3 requires at least one of B1, A1, A2. Since B1 and A2 are removed, it satisfies condition3.\n\nCondition4 requires at least one of C1, C2, B3. C1 and B3 are removed, so satisfies.\n\nTherefore, this subset is good.\n\nAnother example: remove C1, B3, A2, C2.\n\nThis is in Case 2 (B2 not removed). It includes C1 (so not in A), includes B3 (so not in B), includes A2 (so satisfies condition3), includes C2 and B3 (satisfies condition4). So this is good.\n\nNow, a subset in A: which are subsets missing B1 and C1. For example, remove A1, A2, B3, C2. This subset is in Case 2, but in A (since it doesn't have B1 or C1). This subset does not satisfy condition1, so it's bad. Similarly, a subset in B: remove A1, A2, B3, C1. This subset is missing A2 and B3? No, it has B3? Wait, no: subset in B is missing A2 and B3. So for example, remove A1, B1, C1, C2. This subset is missing A2 and B3 (since A2 and B3 are not removed?), wait:\n\nWait, to be in B, a subset must not include A2 or B3. So for example, remove A1, B1, C1, C2. This subset does not include A2 or B3, so it's in B. This subset does not satisfy condition2 (needs at least one of A2 or B3, but neither is removed), so it's bad.\n\nTherefore, the two bad subsets in Case 2 are the two subsets that are entirely in A or entirely in B.\n\nThe two bad subsets in Case 2 are:\n\n1. The subset that excludes B1 and C1 (and is in Case 2), which is {N1, N2, N5, N7} = {A1, A2, B3, C2}\n\n2. The subset that excludes A2 and B3 (and is in Case 2), which is {N1, N3, N6, N7} = {A1, B1, C1, C2}\n\nTherefore, these two subsets are the only bad ones in Case 2.\n\nTherefore, in Case 2, 15 - 2 = 13 are good.\n\nIn Case 1, we had 18 good subsets out of 20.\n\nTherefore, total good subsets: 18 +13 = 31.\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to remove 4 lily pads so that Kelvin cannot get home is 31.\n\nBut wait, the problem states \"Compute the number of ways to remove 4 of the lily pads so that the bottom-left and top-right lily pads both remain, but Kelvin cannot get home.\"\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 31? But let me confirm once more.\n\nWait, but in Case 1, when we remove B2, we considered subsets that also block Path A and Path B. We found 18 subsets in Case 1.\n\nIn Case 2, we have 13 subsets that block all paths.\n\nTotal good subsets: 18 + 13 = 31.\n\nTotal subsets: 35.\n\nThus, the bad subsets are 4: the two in Case 2 and 2 more in Case 1?\n\nWait, no: in Case 1, total subsets are 20, of which 18 are good, so 2 are bad.\n\nIn Case 2, 15 subsets, of which 2 are bad, so 13 good.\n\nThus, total bad subsets: 2 (from Case 1) + 2 (from Case 2) = 4. So good subsets: 35 - 4 = 31.\n\nWait, yes, that's the same as before.\n\nBut let me think: are those two bad subsets in Case 1 actually bad?\n\nIn Case 1, the bad subsets are those that do NOT block both Path A and Path B. Since in Case 1 we had:\n\nNumber of good subsets in Case1 = 18, so bad subsets in Case1 = 20 - 18 = 2.\n\nWhat are those two bad subsets in Case1?\n\nCase1 subsets include B2 and three others from the remaining six nodes (N1, N2, N3, N5, N6, N7).\n\nThe bad subsets in Case1 are the ones that fail to block both Path A and Path B.\n\nFrom earlier analysis in Case1:\n\nThe subsets that fail to block Path A are those that exclude all of Path A nodes (B1, A1, A2). There was 1 such subset: {N5, N6, N7} (i.e., removing B3, C1, C2).\n\nSimilarly, subsets that fail to block Path B are those that exclude all of Path B nodes (C1, C2, B3). There was 1 such subset: {N1, N2, N3} (i.e., removing A1, A2, B1).\n\nAdditionally, subsets that exclude both Path A and Path B nodes would be the intersection, but that was zero.\n\nTherefore, the bad subsets in Case1 are:\n\n1. The subset that removes {B3, C1, C2} plus B2: so total removed nodes are B2, B3, C1, C2. This subset fails to block Path A because it doesn't remove B1 or A1 or A2. Path A is still open: S -> B1 -> A1 -> A2 -> H. Since B1, A1, A2 are not removed, this path remains.\n\n2. The subset that removes {A1, A2, B1} plus B2: total removed nodes are B2, A1, A2, B1. This subset fails to block Path B because it doesn't remove C1, C2, or B3. Path B: S -> C1 -> C2 -> B3 -> H remains open because those nodes are not removed.\n\nTherefore, these two subsets in Case1 are indeed bad, allowing a path from S to H.\n\nTherefore, total bad subsets: 2 (Case1) + 2 (Case2) = 4, so good subsets: 35 - 4 = 31.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 31?\n\nWait, but the problem says \"Compute the number of ways to remove 4 of the lily pads so that the bottom-left and top-right lily pads both remain, but Kelvin cannot get home.\"\n\nBut according to this calculation, it's 31. However, I need to make sure I didn't make a miscalculation in the inclusion-exclusion.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in the Case 1 calculation.\n\nIn Case1, when we calculated the number of good subsets as 18, which was Total (20) minus subsets missing Path A (1) minus subsets missing Path B (1) plus subsets missing both (0), giving 18.\n\nYes, that seems correct.\n\nTherefore, 18 +13=31.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider a small example.\n\nSuppose we remove the four nodes: B2, B3, C1, C2. This is in Case1 (since B2 is removed). Does this disconnect S from H?\n\nAfter removing B2, B3, C1, C2:\n\nRemaining nodes:\n\nS, H, and the non-removed non-S/H nodes: A1, A2, B1.\n\nCan S reach H?\n\nS is at (3,1). From S, can go to B1 or C1 (but C1 is removed). So only to B1.\n\nFrom B1, can go to A1 or back to S or to B2 (removed). So to A1.\n\nFrom A1, can go to A2 or back to B1.\n\nFrom A2, can go to H or to B2 (removed).\n\nSo path: S -> B1 -> A1 -> A2 -> H. This path exists because B1, A1, A2 are still present. Therefore, this subset is bad, which matches our earlier analysis: this subset was one of the two bad subsets in Case1 (specifically, the one that removes B3, C1, C2 and B2).\n\nAnother example: remove B2, A1, A2, B1. This is in Case1.\n\nAfter removal, remaining nodes:\n\nS, H, and non-removed non-S/H nodes: B3, C1, C2.\n\nCan S reach H?\n\nFrom S, can go to B1 (removed) or C1 (present). So to C1.\n\nFrom C1, can go to C2 or back to S or to B2 (removed). To C2.\n\nFrom C2, can go to B3 or back to C1.\n\nFrom B3, can go to H or to B2 (removed) or to C2.\n\nSo path: S -> C1 -> C2 -> B3 -> H. This path exists, so this subset is also bad (the other bad subset in Case1).\n\nTherefore, those two subsets in Case1 are indeed bad.\n\nIn Case2, the two bad subsets are:\n\n1. Removing A1, A2, B3, C2: which leaves B1, C1 present. Then path S->B1->A1->A2->H is blocked because A1 and A2 are removed, but path S->C1->C2->B3->H is blocked because C2 and B3 are removed. However, can they go through B2?\n\nWait, in this subset, B2 is not removed. So from C1, can go to B2. From B2, can go to A2 (removed), B3 (removed), or C1. So from B2, can't reach H. From B1, can go to B2. From B2, stuck. From C1, can go to B2, then stuck. From C1, can also go to S or to C2 (removed). So no path to H. Wait, hold on, this seems contradictory.\n\nWait, if we remove A1, A2, B3, C2, then the remaining non-S/H nodes are B1, C1, N5 (B3 is removed?), wait:\n\nWait, the subset is removing A1(N1), A2(N2), B3(N5), C2(N7). So remaining non-S/H nodes are B1(N3), C1(N6), and B3 is removed, C2 is removed. So remaining nodes besides S and H are B1 and C1.\n\nSo from S, can go to B1 or C1.\n\nFrom B1, can go to A1 (removed) or to B2 or back to S. So to B2.\n\nFrom B2, can go to A2 (removed), B3 (removed), or C1.\n\nFrom C1, can go to C2 (removed) or B2 or back to S.\n\nSo path from S to B2 to C1 to ...? From C1, can go to B2, but that loops. To get to H, need to go from B2 to A2 (removed) or B3 (removed) or C1. So no path to H. Therefore, this subset actually disconnects S from H? Wait, but we thought it was a bad subset.\n\nWait, confusion here. Earlier, in the analysis for Case2, we considered that subsets in A or B are bad. The subset removing A1, A2, B3, C2 is in A (since it excludes B1 and C1? No: it includes B1 and C1? Wait:\n\nWait, the subset in question is removing A1, A2, B3, C2. So the removed nodes are N1, N2, N5, N7. The remaining non-S/H nodes are N3 (B1), N6 (C1). So it does include B1 and C1. Therefore, it is not in set A (which requires excluding B1 and C1). It is in set B? Set B is subsets missing A2 and B3. Since A2 is removed (N2 is removed), and B3 is removed (N5 is removed), so yes, this subset is in set B (missing A2 and B3). Wait, but B3 is removed, so it is excluded, so the subset does not contain B3. Therefore, it is in set B.\n\nBut according to our earlier analysis, being in set B means it fails to satisfy condition2 (needs at least one of A2 or B3). Since it has removed A2 and B3, it does satisfy condition2? Wait, condition2 requires that in the removed set, at least one of A2 or B3 is removed. Wait no: condition2 in the inclusion-exclusion was about the subset failing to satisfy the condition that it includes at least one of A2 or B3. Wait, no:\n\nWait, let me re-express the conditions:\n\nIn Case2, the conditions for a subset to be good are:\n\n1. At least one of B1 or C1 (condition1)\n\n2. At least one of A2 or B3 (condition2)\n\n3. At least one of B1, A1, A2 (condition3)\n\n4. At least one of C1, C2, B3 (condition4)\n\nThe subset in question: removed nodes are A1, A2, B3, C2. So the removed nodes include:\n\n- B1? No, B1 is not removed.\n\n- C1? No, C1 is not removed.\n\nTherefore, for condition1: the subset does NOT have at least one of B1 or C1. Since neither is removed, it fails condition1. Wait, hold on, no:\n\nWait, condition1 requires that the subset includes at least one of B1 or C1 (because we are in the context of Case2 where B2 is not removed, and to block paths through B2, we need to remove at least one of B1 or C1). Wait, no, actually in the conditions for Case2, the conditions were about the subset needing to include certain nodes to block paths. Wait, I might have mixed up the direction.\n\nActually, the conditions were about the subset (the removed nodes) needing to cover certain nodes to block the paths.\n\nSpecifically:\n\nCondition1: to block S->B2, the subset must include at least one of B1 or C1 (since those are the nodes on the paths from S to B2).\n\nCondition2: to block B2->H, the subset must include at least one of A2 or B3 (nodes on the paths from B2 to H).\n\nCondition3: to block Path A (non-B2 path), the subset must include at least one of B1, A1, A2 (nodes on Path A).\n\nCondition4: to block Path B (non-B2 path), the subset must include at least one of C1, C2, B3 (nodes on Path B).\n\nTherefore, the subset that removes A1, A2, B3, C2:\n\n- For condition1: does it include at least one of B1 or C1? No, because B1 and C1 are not removed. So it fails condition1.\n\n- For condition2: does it include at least one of A2 or B3? Yes, it includes A2 and B3. So passes condition2.\n\n- For condition3: does it include at least one of B1, A1, A2? Yes, it includes A2. So passes condition3.\n\n- For condition4: does it include at least one of C1, C2, B3? Yes, it includes B3. So passes condition4.\n\nHowever, it fails condition1, so the subset does not satisfy all conditions and is thus bad.\n\nBut in reality, as we saw earlier, this subset actually does disconnect S from H because the only paths are blocked by the removal of A1, A2, B3, C2. Wait, but according to our earlier analysis, it should be bad because it fails condition1, but in reality, it might still be good?\n\nWait, let's re-examine the subset removing A1, A2, B3, C2.\n\nAfter removal:\n\nNodes present (excluding S and H):\n\nB1, C1.\n\nCan S reach H?\n\nFrom S=(3,1), can go to B1 or C1.\n\nFrom B1: can go to A1 (removed), B2, or back to S. So to B2.\n\nFrom B2: can go to A2 (removed), B3 (removed), or C1.\n\nFrom C1: can go to C2 (removed), B2, or back to S.\n\nFrom B2, can go to C1, but from C1 you can't proceed further towards H. From B2, you can't reach H because the only paths from B2 to H go through A2 or B3, which are removed. From C1, you can't reach H either. So S cannot reach H. Therefore, this subset is actually good, despite failing condition1?\n\nContradiction! This suggests an error in the earlier analysis.\n\nWait, what is condition1? Condition1 was: to block all paths through B2, you must remove at least one of B1 or C1 (to block S->B2). However, in this subset, we didn't remove B1 or C1, but we did remove B3 and A2, which might have blocked other paths.\n\nWait, actually, in this subset, even though we didn't remove B1 or C1, the path S->B1->B2 is still possible because B1 is present. From B1, you can go to B2, and from B2 you can go to C1, but from C1 you can't proceed to H because C2 is removed and B3 is removed. So the path S->B1->B2->C1->... can't reach H. Similarly, the path S->C1->B2->... also can't reach H. So even though we didn't remove B1 or C1, the other removals have blocked all paths through B2. So this subset is actually good, but according to our conditions, it failed condition1, so it was counted as bad, which is incorrect.\n\nThis means there was an error in the formulation of the conditions.\n\nThe error stems from the fact that condition1 was formulated as \"the subset must include at least one of B1 or C1\" to block S->B2, but actually, blocking S->B2 doesn't require removing B1 or C1; it could be blocked by other means, like removing B2 itself (but B2 is not removed in Case2) or by removing nodes further along the path.\n\nWait, no: the path S->B1->B2 can only be blocked by removing B1 or B2 or by making B1 or B2 unreachable. Since B2 is present, the only way to block that path is to remove B1.\n\nSimilarly, the path S->C1->B2 can only be blocked by removing C1 or B2 or making C1 unreachable. Since B2 is present, you must remove C1 to block that path.\n\nTherefore, to block all paths from S to B2, you must remove B1 and/or C1. If you don't remove either, then both paths S->B1->B2 and S->C1->B2 remain open, allowing access to B2.\n\nIn the subset that removes A1, A2, B3, C2:\n\n- B1 is still present, so S can go to B1, then to B2.\n\n- From B2, can go to C1 (since C1 is present), but from C1 you can't go to H because C2 is removed and B3 is removed. So the path S->B1->B2->C1->... cannot reach H. But the path S->B1->B2->... is still possible, but it doesn't lead to H because the continuation is blocked.\n\nTherefore, even though B2 is reachable from S, it cannot reach H, so effectively, S cannot reach H through B2. Therefore, the subset is good despite not removing B1 or C1.\n\nAh! So my earlier conditions were too strict. The requirement to block all paths from S to B2 is not just about preventing access to B2, but also ensuring that from B2, you can't reach H. Therefore, my conditions were incorrectly separating the blocking of access to B2 from the blocking of paths from B2 to H.\n\nTherefore, the earlier analysis was flawed because it treated the two aspects separately, but they are interdependent.\n\nThis means that the inclusion-exclusion approach I used for Case2 was incorrect because it didn't account for the fact that even if you can reach B2, you might still be unable to proceed to H, which could be blocked by other removals.\n\nThis complicates things significantly, and my previous count of 13 good subsets in Case2 is likely incorrect.\n\nGiven the time I've spent and the potential for error in manual calculations, perhaps I should consider an alternative approach or look for another way.\n\nAlternative approach:\n\nSince the grid is small, let's consider that the only way to block all paths is to ensure that either:\n\n1. The start and home are in separate components after removal.\n\nGiven the complexity, perhaps the answer is 31, but I need to verify with another method.\n\nAlternatively, note that in the original grid, the minimal vertex cut is size 2 (as per Menger's theorem with two vertex-disjoint paths). Therefore, any hitting set of size 2 can block all paths, but since we are required to use exactly 4 vertices, the number of subsets of size 4 that include at least one vertex from each of the two paths.\n\nWait, but this is not straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, the total number of vertex cuts of size 4 would be the answer, but counting vertex cuts is non-trivial.\n\nAlternatively, consider that the two vertex-disjoint paths are:\n\nPath 1: S-B1-A1-A2-H\n\nPath 2: S-C1-C2-B3-H\n\nTo block all paths, you must hit both paths. Since they are disjoint except for S and H, you need to hit at least one node from each path. Additionally, you must also block any other paths that go through B2.\n\nBut if we consider only these two paths, then the number of hitting sets of size 4 that hit both paths would be C(7,4) minus the subsets that miss Path1 or miss Path2.\n\nBut this is similar to inclusion-exclusion.\n\nLet me try:\n\nTotal subsets: C(7,4)=35.\n\nSubsets that miss Path1: subsets that contain none of B1, A1, A2. There are C(4,4)=1 subset (since the remaining nodes are C1, C2, B3, and the other non-path nodes? Wait:\n\nWait, Path1 nodes (excluding S and H): B1, A1, A2.\n\nPath2 nodes (excluding S and H): C1, C2, B3.\n\nThe other nodes: none, because all non-S/H nodes are in Path1 or Path2.\n\nWait, the non-S/H nodes are exactly the nodes in Path1 and Path2:\n\nPath1 nodes: B1, A1, A2\n\nPath2 nodes: C1, C2, B3\n\nSo total non-S/H nodes: 6 nodes, which matches earlier labeling.\n\nTherefore, the nodes not in Path1 are C1, C2, B3 (Path2 nodes).\n\nThe nodes not in Path2 are B1, A1, A2 (Path1 nodes).\n\nTherefore:\n\nSubsets that miss Path1: subsets that contain none of B1, A1, A2. Thus, they can only contain nodes from Path2: C1, C2, B3. To choose 4 nodes from 3 nodes: impossible. So |miss Path1| =0.\n\nSimilarly, subsets that miss Path2: subsets containing none of C1, C2, B3. Must choose 4 nodes from Path1 nodes: B1, A1, A2 (only 3 nodes), impossible. So |miss Path2|=0.\n\nTherefore, by inclusion-exclusion, the number of subsets that hit both Path1 and Path2 is Total - 0 - 0 + 0 = 35.\n\nBut this can't be, since clearly not all subsets hit both paths.\n\nWait, this suggests that every subset of 4 nodes must hit at least one node from Path1 and one from Path2, which is not true.\n\nFor example, the subset that removes all three Path1 nodes (B1, A1, A2) plus one Path2 node: say B3. This subset contains B3 (from Path2) but misses all of Path1, so it doesn't hit Path1.\n\nBut earlier, we saw that removing B1, A1, A2, and B3 would leave C1 and C2 present, allowing Path2: S->C1->C2->B3->H? Wait no, B3 is removed, so Path2 would be blocked.\n\nWait, in this subset: removing B1, A1, A2, B3.\n\nThen remaining non-S/H nodes: C1, C2.\n\nPath1 is blocked (nodes removed), Path2: S->C1->C2->B3 (but B3 is removed), so can't proceed to H. So actually, this subset does block all paths.\n\nWait, so even though it misses Path1 nodes entirely, the removal of B3 also blocks Path2.\n\nThis shows that the earlier assumption that subsets missing Path1 or Path2 necessarily leave a path open is incorrect because other nodes' removals can block the paths.\n\nTherefore, the inclusion-exclusion approach considering only the two paths is insufficient because the paths share no nodes but their removal can be interdependent with other nodes.\n\nGiven the time I've invested and the risk of calculation errors, I might have to go with the initial result of 31, but I'm not fully confident. Alternatively, perhaps the answer is 30 or something else.\n\nWait, let me think of the to