ount all vertex cuts of size exactly 4? Or any vertex cut of size ≤4? But since we are only removing exactly 4, so exactly 4, so we need all vertex cuts of size 4 between (1,1) and (3,3).\n\nTherefore, if we can compute the number of vertex cuts of size 4 between (1,1) and (3,3), then that would be our answer. However, vertex cut counting can be tricky.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it is feasible to compute the number of subsets of 4 lily pads (from the 7 non-(1,1), non-(3,3)) such that every path from (1,1) to (3,3) in the original grid passes through at least one of the removed lily pads. Wait, actually, that would be equivalent to saying that the removed lily pads form a vertex cut. Because a vertex cut is a set of vertices whose removal disconnects the graph. So yes, the removed set must be a vertex cut between (1,1) and (3,3).\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to counting the number of vertex cuts of size 4 between (1,1) and (3,3) in the 3x3 grid graph, where the vertex cut can only include vertices from the 7 non-(1,1), non-(3,3) lily pads. Since we cannot remove (1,1) or (3,3).\n\nTherefore, the question is: how many vertex cuts of size 4 are there between (1,1) and (3,3) in this graph, considering only the 7 non-(1,1), non-(3,3) vertices as candidates for the vertex cut.\n\nHmm. Alternatively, perhaps we can approach this by considering that in order for (1,1) and (3,3) to be disconnected in the remaining graph, the removed set must intersect every path from (1,1) to (3,3). Since we are removing exactly 4 vertices, we need that every path from (1,1) to (3,3) has at least one vertex in the removed set. Therefore, the removed set must be a hitting set for all paths from (1,1) to (3,3). The minimal hitting set would be the minimal vertex cut, and any hitting set of size 4 that includes a minimal hitting set would work, but actually, even non-minimal hitting sets of size 4 could work if they hit all paths.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to think in terms of 