ly, since we can only remove vertices from the 7 non-(1,1), non-(3,3) vertices, so we have to count all subsets of size 4 from those 7 that form a vertex cut.\n\nAlternatively, since the minimal vertex cut is 3, then any vertex cut of size 4 must contain a minimal vertex cut of size 3 plus one more vertex. But actually, not necessarily, because maybe there are vertex cuts of size 4 that don't include a minimal cut.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to think in terms of the total number of vertex cuts of size 4, regardless of whether they include minimal ones or not, as long as they disconnect (1,1) from (3,3).\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can compute the total number by considering all subsets of 4 vertices from the 7, and subtract those subsets that do not disconnect (1,1) from (3,3). Wait, but that might be difficult.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model this as a graph and use some combinatorial methods or recursion.\n\nAlternatively, since the total number of subsets is only 35, maybe it's feasible to compute it by considering cases.\n\nLet me try to proceed by considering the possible ways the remaining 5 lily pads can be arranged so that (1,1) and (3,3) are disconnected.\n\nFirst, note that to disconnect (1,1) from (3,3), there must be no path between them in the induced subgraph. Therefore, the induced subgraph must have (1,1) in one connected component and (3,3) in another.\n\nTherefore, the idea is that in the remaining 5 lily pads, the two corners are in different components.\n\nTo achieve this, we must have removed enough lily pads so that all paths from (1,1) to (3,3) in the original grid have at least one lily pad removed.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think of the grid and the necessary removals.\n\nLet me consider that in the original grid, there are several \"routes\" from (1,1) to (3,3). To block all routes, we need to remove at least one node from each route.\n\nGiven that we have to remove 4 nodes, and the minimal number of nodes to block