onnect them.\n\nHmm. What if we remove (2,2) and (3,1)? Let's see:\n\nFrom (1,1), can we go to (2,1), then to (3,1) but (3,1) is removed, so instead from (2,1), go to (2,2), but (2,2) is removed, so then from (2,1) you can only go back to (1,1) or to (3,1) which is removed. So, stuck? Wait:\n\nWait, from (1,1), you can go to (1,2) or (2,1). If we remove (2,2) and (3,1):\n\nFrom (1,1) -> (1,2). From (1,2), can go to (1,3) or (2,2). But (2,2) is removed, so only to (1,3). From (1,3), can go to (2,3) or back to (1,2). From (2,3), can go to (3,3) or (2,2) [removed] or (1,3). So, from (2,3) you can go to (3,3). So the path: (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (1,3) -> (2,3) -> (3,3). So that path still exists. Therefore, removing (2,2) and (3,1) does not disconnect (1,1) from (3,3).\n\nHmm. Maybe a different pair. Let me try removing (2,1) and (2,3). Let's see:\n\nFrom (1,1), can go to (1,2) or (2,1) [but (2,1) is removed]. So only (1,2). From (1,2), can go to (1,3) or (2,2). From (1,3), can go to (2,3) [which is removed] or back. From (2,2), can go to (2,1) [removed], (2,3) [removed], or (3,2). From (3,2), can go to (3,3) or (3,1) or (2,2). So, let's see:\n\nPath: (1,1) -> (1,2) -> (2,2) -> (3,2) -> (3,3). That works. So, even after removing (2,1) and (2,3), there's a path.\n\nHmm. Maybe a pair that does work? Let me try removing (1,2) and (2,3). Let's see:\n\nFrom (1,1), can go to (2,1). From (2,1), can go to (3,1) or (2,2). From (3,1), can go to (3,2). From (3,2), can go to (3,3) or (2,2). From (2,2), can go to (2,1), (2,3) [removed], or (3,2). So, path: (1,1) -> (2,1) -> (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3). That works. So, even with (1,2) and (2,3) removed, there's a path.\n\nHmm. Maybe removing (1,3) and (2,2). Let's see:\n\nFrom (1,1), go to (1,2) or (2,1). From (1,2), can go to (2,2) [removed] or (1,3) [removed], so only back to (1,1). From (2,1), go to (3,1) or (2,2) [removed]. From (3,1), go to (3,2). From (3,2), go to (3,3). So path: (1,1) -> (2,1) -> (3,1) -> (3,2) -> (3,3). So that works.