minimal cuts would be counted in both.\n\nTherefore, using inclusion-exclusion:\n\nNumber of supersets of the first minimal cut: C(5,2) = 10\n\nNumber of supersets of the second minimal cut: C(5,2) = 10\n\nNumber of subsets that include both minimal cuts: C(7 - 4, 4 - 4) = C(3,0) = 1? Wait, no. Wait, the intersection of the two minimal cuts: the two minimal cuts are {(1,2), (2,1)} and {(2,3), (3,2)}. The intersection of these two sets is empty, since they share no common vertices. Therefore, the subsets that include both minimal cuts are those that contain all four vertices: {(1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2)}. So there is exactly one such subset of size 4 that includes both minimal cuts.\n\nTherefore, by inclusion-exclusion, the total number of subsets of size 4 that contain at least one minimal cut is:\n\n10 + 10 - 1 = 19.\n\nTherefore, if our assumption is correct that all vertex cuts of size 4 are exactly the supersets of the two minimal cuts, then |A| = 19.\n\nHowever, this might not account for other vertex cuts of size 3 or 4 that aren't supersets of the minimal cuts.\n\nTherefore, this approach might undercount or overcount.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the only vertex cuts are the two minimal cuts and their supersets, so that the total number of vertex cuts of size 4 is indeed 19, and hence the answer is 35 - 19 = 16. But I'm not sure.\n\nAlternatively, let's think of it this way: if the only minimal vertex cuts are the two pairs we found, then any vertex cut of size 4 must contain at least one of these two pairs. Therefore, the total number of vertex cuts of size 4 is equal to the number of subsets that include at least one of the two minimal cuts, which we computed as 19 via inclusion-exclusion.\n\nHowever, this would mean that there are no vertex cuts of size 3 or 4 that don't include at least one minimal cut. Is that possible?\n\nSuppose there is a vertex cut of size 3 that doesn't include either of the two minimal cuts. For example, suppose we remove three verti