raph theory, a vertex cut is a set of vertices whose removal disconnects the graph. So any disconnecting set must contain a vertex cut. But the vertex cut doesn't have to be minimal. So, for instance, if you have a vertex cut of size 3, you could have a disconnecting set of size 4 by adding another node from the vertex cut or another node. But in our case, the minimal vertex cut is 3, so any disconnecting set must have at least 3 nodes, so removing 4 nodes can be thought of as choosing a vertex cut of size 3 and then one additional node (which could be inside or outside the vertex cut). However, this might complicate things.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can categorize the possible disconnecting sets by the structure of the removed nodes.\n\nAlternatively, let me try to think of the grid and see which nodes are on all possible paths from A to I. If we can identify nodes that are in every path, then removing those nodes would disconnect A from I. However, in a grid graph, typically there are no nodes that are on all paths unless it's a very constrained graph. In our case, since there are multiple paths, there might not be any single node common to all paths. For example, the center node E is on some paths but not all. For instance, the path going through the bottom row: A -> B -> C -> F -> I doesn't go through E. Similarly, the path A -> D -> G -> H -> I doesn't go through E. So E is not on all paths. Similarly, node B is only on paths that go through the bottom row. Node C is only on the bottom-right path. So no single node is on all paths. Therefore, there is no single node whose removal would disconnect A and I. Therefore, as we thought earlier, the minimum vertex cut is 3.\n\nTherefore, to disconnect A and I, we need to remove at least 3 nodes, and since we are removing 4, that gives us some flexibility.\n\nPerhaps the way to approach this is to consider all possible ways to remove 4 nodes from the 7 non-fixed nodes (B, C, D, E, F, G, H) such that in the remaining