removed. Therefore, yes, removing column 2 disconnects A from I. So removing column 2 (3 nodes) does the job, and then we can remove one more node anywhere else (since we need to remove 4 nodes total). But we have to be careful: when we remove column 2 (B, E, H), that's 3 nodes, and then we need to remove one more node from the remaining nodes: which are in column 1 (A, D, G), but A must remain, so D and G are candidates; column 3 (C, F, I), but I must remain, so C and F are candidates; and column 2 is already fully removed except we already removed B, E, H. Wait, actually, the remaining nodes after removing column 2 (B, E, H) are:\n\nOriginal non-fixed nodes: B, C, D, E, F, G, H.\n\nAfter removing B, E, H: remaining non-fixed nodes are C, D, F, G.\n\nPlus the fixed nodes A and I. So total nodes remaining: A, C, D, F, G, I. Wait, but we need to remove 4 nodes from the original 7 non-fixed, so if we remove 3 (B, E, H), we have to remove one more from the remaining 4 (C, D, F, G). So in this case, the fourth node to remove can be any of C, D, F, or G. However, we have to ensure that after removing that fourth node, the disconnection still holds. Wait, actually, if we remove an extra node from column 1 or 3, does that affect the disconnection?\n\nLet me see. Suppose we remove column 2 (B, E, H) and then remove, say, D (from column 1). Then the remaining nodes are: A, C, F, G, I. So let's see if A is still disconnected from I.\n\nFrom A (3,1): can go up to D, but D is removed. Can go right to B, which is removed. So A is only connected to itself? Wait, A is at (3,1). From A, you can only go up to D (which is removed) or right to B (removed). So A is isolated. Meanwhile, I is connected to F and C, etc. So yes, A is disconnected from I. Similarly, if we remove G instead of D: removing B, E, H, and G. Then remaining non-fixed nodes: C, D, F. So nodes: A, C, D, F, I. From A: can go up to D, then from D can go up to G (removed) or right to B (removed). So A is only connected to D. From D, can't go anywhere else except A. Meanwhile, I is connected to F and C. So still disconnected.\n\nIf we remove C instead: then remaining non-fixed nodes: D, F, G. Nodes: A, D, F, G, I. From A: can go to D, then D can go up to G. From G can go left? No, G is (1,1), so left is out, right is H (removed). So G is connected to D and A. From G, can't go to I. Meanwhile, I is connected to F and C (but C is removed). So I is connected to F, which is connected to... F is (2,3). From F, can go down to C (removed) or up to I or left to E (removed). So F is connected only to I. So the component with I is just I and F. The component with A is A, D, G. So still disconnected.\n\nSimilarly, if we remove F instead of C: then remaining non-fixed nodes: C, D, G. Nodes: A, C, D, G, I. From I, can go left to F (removed) or down to C? Wait, I is (1,3), so down is to F (removed), so I is only connected to itself? Wait, no, I is at (1,3). From I, you can go down to F (which is removed), or left to H (removed). So I is isolated? Wait, no: in the remaining nodes, after removing B, E, H, F, the nodes are A, C, D, G, I. From I (1,3), can you go down to F? F is removed, so no. Left to H? H is removed. So I is only connected to itself? Wait, but in the induced subgraph, edges only exist if both endpoints are present. Since I is present, but its neighbors (F and H) are removed, so I is isolated. Meanwhile, A is connected to D and G. So A is in a component with D and G, and I is isolated. So still disconnected from A. So in all cases, removing column 2 (3 nodes) plus one more node from the remaining (C, D, F, G) gives us 4-node removals that disconnect A and I. Therefore, this gives us 4 possibilities for the fourth node: C, D, F, G. So that's 4 subsets in this category.\n\nSimilarly, we could remove column 1 (nodes A, D, G) but A must remain, so we can't remove A. So column 1 has nodes D and G that can be removed. Wait, column 1 is column 1: nodes A (fixed), D, G. To remove column 1's non-fixed nodes (D and G), but we can't remove A. So if we wanted to remove column 1, we could remove D and G, but that's only 2 nodes, which isn't enough. To remove an entire column, we need to remove all non-fixed nodes in that column. For column 1, non-fixed nodes are D and G. Removing those two would only be part of disconnecting.\n\nAlternatively, what if we remove column 3's non-fixed nodes: C and F. Then, similar to column 2, removing column 3's non-fixed nodes (C, F) plus E and H? Wait, no, that might not be the right approach.\n\nAlternatively, let's consider removing column 1's non-fixed nodes (D and G) along with some others. Hmm, this might not be as straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there are other ways to disconnect A and I besides removing an entire column. For instance, removing the center node E and some others.\n\nSuppose we remove E, B, C, and F. Let's see if that disconnects A from I.\n\nAfter removing B, C, F, E: remaining non-fixed nodes are D, G, H. So the nodes present are A, D, G, H, I.\n\nFrom A (3,1): can go up to D (2,1). From D, can go up to G (1,1). From G, can go right to H (1,2). From H, can go right to I (1,3). So the path A -> D -> G -> H -> I still exists! So that doesn't disconnect them.\n\nHmm, so that removal doesn't work.\n\nAlternatively, remove E, B, D, F. Let's see:\n\nRemoved nodes: B, D, E, F. Remaining non-fixed nodes: C, G, H. Nodes present: A, C, G, H, I.\n\nFrom A: can go right to B (removed), so only up to D (removed). So A is isolated. From I: can go left to H, then to G, then down to D (removed) or to A (isolated). Wait, let's see:\n\nFrom I (1,3): can go left to H (1,2), then from H can go down to E (removed) or left to G (1,1). From G (1,1), can go down to D (removed) or right to H. So G is connected to H and I? Wait:\n\nWait, nodes present: A, C, G, H, I.\n\nEdges:\n\n- A is only connected to D (removed), so A is isolated.\n\n- C is (3,3). From C, can go up to F (removed) or left to B (removed) or down? No, it's the bottom row. So C is only connected to itself? Wait, C is (3,3). Its neighbors are B (removed), F (removed), and down? No, it's the bottom row, so only up and left/right. Since left is B (removed), up is F (removed). So C is isolated.\n\n- G is (1,1). Its neighbors: D (removed), H (1,2), and right to (1,2). Wait, G is (1,1), so right is H (1,2), and down is D (removed). So G is connected to H.\n\n- H is (1,2). Connected to G (1,1) and I (1,3). Since I is present, H is connected to I.\n\n- I is (1,3), connected to H.\n\nSo the connected components are:\n\n- A (isolated)\n\n- C (isolated)\n\n- G, H, I (connected together)\n\nTherefore, A is disconnected from I, since they're in separate components. So this removal (B, D, E, F) does disconnect them. So that's another valid removal.\n\nSo this is a different way of disconnecting, not by removing an entire column.\n\nTherefore, there are more disconnecting sets than just those that remove an entire column. Therefore, we need a better approach.\n\nPerhaps we can categorize the disconnecting sets based on which \"barriers\" they include.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's manageable to compute the total number by considering the different ways to block all paths.\n\nGiven that there are 35 total subsets, perhaps we can categorize the subsets based on which critical areas are blocked.\n\nAlternatively, since this might take a while, perhaps we can think in terms of the grid and possible blocking rows or columns or key nodes.\n\nFirst, let's note that to block all paths from A to I, we must ensure that every possible path from A to I in the original grid has at least one node removed from that path.\n\nSince all paths must be blocked, the removed nodes must form a hitting set for all paths from A to I.\n\nWe need a hitting set of size 4 (since we remove 4 nodes) that hits every path from A to I.\n\nThe minimal hitting set size is 3, as we saw, so we are looking for all hitting sets of size 4.\n\nThe number we want