other. Since (1,1) can reach (1,2) and (2,1), but those nodes can't reach back to (1,1) except through (1,1). Wait, actually, (1,2) is only connected to (1,1) and (2,2) (which is removed) and (1,3) (removed). So (1,2) is only connected to (1,1). Similarly, (2,1) is only connected to (1,1) and (3,1) (removed) and (2,2) (removed). So, the connected component of (1,1) includes (1,1), (1,2), and (2,1). But (1,2) and (2,1) can't reach (3,3) because their only connections are blocked. \n\nMeanwhile, what about (3,3)? Let's see, (3,3)'s neighbors are (3,2) and (2,3). Are those present? (3,2) is present? In this case, we removed (2,2), (1,3), (3,1). So (3,2) is still there, and (2,3) is still there? Let's see:\n\nOriginal pads: (3,3) has neighbors (3,2) and (2,3). Since we only removed (2,2), (1,3), (3,1). So (3,2) is still present, and (2,3) is still present. Therefore, (3,3) can go left to (3,2) or down to (2,3). \n\nFrom (3,2), he can go left to (3,1) which is removed, or up to (2,2) which is removed, or right to (3,3). So, (3,2) is only connected to (3,3). Similarly, (2,3) can go up to (1,3) which is removed, left to (2,2) which is removed, or down to (3,3). So (2,3) is only connected to (3,3). \n\nTherefore, the component containing (3,3) is just itself, (3,2), and (2,3). So, (3,3) is in a separate component from (1,1)'s component, which is {(1,1), (1,2), (2,1)}. Therefore, in this case, with three removed pads, we have disconnected (1,1) from (3,3). \n\nBut in our problem, we are required to remove four lily pads, so three is insufficient. But this shows that with three, you can disconnect them, but with four, you can also do so in multiple ways. \n\nBut perhaps the key is that to disconnect (1,1) from (3,3) with four removed pads, we need to ensure that all possible paths are blocked. Since there are multiple paths, we need to block all of them. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the four removed pads must form a \"barrier\" across the grid. \n\nAlternatively, let's try to think of the possible ways to remove four lily pads such that (1,1) and (3,3) are disconnected. \n\nFirst, note that in the grid, any path from (1,1) to (3,3) must traverse from the bottom-left to the top-right. The minimal paths are of length 4 (moving right, right, up, up or up, up, right, right, etc.), but there are also longer paths that wind around. \n\nTo block all paths, we need to ensure that every possible such path has at least one of its nodes removed. Since we can only remove four nodes, we have to choose four nodes that hit every possible path. \n\nAlternatively, since the grid is small, perhaps we can enumerate all possible subsets of four lily pads whose removal disconnects (1,1) from (3,3), given that (1,1) and (3,3) are kept. \n\nGiven that the total number of subsets to consider is C(7,3) = 35, which is manageable, perhaps we can categorize the subsets based on their structure. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider that in order to disconnect (1,1) from (3,3), the removed pads must form a cut that separates the start from the finish. Given the grid's symmetry, perhaps the cuts can be along the central row or column or something like that. \n\nLet me try to think of different cases:\n\nCase 1: The removed pads include the central pad (2,2). \n\nCase 2: The removed pads do not include the central pad (2,2). \n\nMaybe splitting into these two cases can help.\n\nFirst, Case 1: Removing (2,2). Then we have to remove 3 more lily pads from the remaining 7 - 1 = 6 lily pads (since (2,2) is one of the 7 non-required pads). Wait, actually, total non-required lily pads are 7, so if we are in Case 1, we remove (2,2) and three others. So the total removed pads are (2,2) plus three from the remaining six. \n\nBut we need the total removed to be four pads. \n\nIn this case, with (2,2) removed, does that help in disconnecting? Let's see. If we remove (2,2), then any path going through (2,2) is blocked. However, as we saw earlier, there are still paths that avoid (2,2). For example, the path going along the top row then down, or the path going along the left column then right. \n\nTherefore, just removing (2,2) is not enough. We need to remove additional pads to block those alternative paths. \n\nSimilarly, in Case 2, not removing (2,2), so we have to remove four pads from the other six (excluding (2,2)), but then we have to make sure that even without (2,2), the other paths are blocked. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the key is that to block all paths, we need to block all possible routes that go around the center. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the four removed pads must form a sort of \"cross\" or \"barrier\" around the center. \n\nAlternatively, let me try to think of specific examples. \n\nFirst, the example I had earlier where we removed (2,1), (2,3), (1,2), and (3,2). That set of four removed pads isolates (1,1) and (3,3) into separate components. \n\nAnother example: suppose we remove the entire middle row: (2,1), (2,2), (2,3). But that's three pads. If we remove those three plus one more, say (1,2), then we have four removed pads. Let's see what that does. \n\nRemoving (2,1), (2,2), (2,3), and (1,2):\n\nRemaining pads: (1,1), (1,3), (3,1), (3,2), (3,3), and (3,1) is present? Wait, let's list them:\n\nOriginal pads:\n\nRow 1: (1,1), (1,2), (1,3)\n\nRow 2: (2,1), (2,2), (2,3)\n\nRow 3: (3,1), (3,2), (3,3)\n\nRemoving (2,1), (2,2), (2,3), and (1,2). So remaining are:\n\nRow 1: (1,1), (1,3)\n\nRow 2: nothing, since all three are removed.\n\nRow 3: (3,1), (3,2), (3,3)\n\nSo the remaining pads are: (1,1), (1,3), (3,1), (3,2), (3,3). \n\nNow, let's see the connections:\n\n(1,1) is connected to (1,2) which is removed, and (2,1) which is removed. So (1,1) is isolated.\n\n(1,3) is connected to (1,2) which is removed and (2,3) which is removed, so (1,3) is isolated.\n\n(3,1) is connected to (2,1) which is removed and (3,2). So (3,1) is connected to (3,2).\n\n(3,2) is connected to (3,1), (3,3), and (2,2) which is removed. So (3,2) is connected to (3,1) and (3,3). \n\n(3,3) is connected to (3,2) and (2,3) which is removed. So (3,3) is connected to (3,2). \n\nTherefore, the connected components are:\n\n- { (1,1) }\n\n- { (1,3) }\n\n- { (3,1), (3,2), (3,3) }\n\nThus, (1,1) is in a separate component from (3,3), so this is another valid configuration. So that's another set of four removed pads: the entire middle row plus (1,2). \n\nSimilarly, we could remove the entire middle row plus (3,2) instead of (1,2). Let's see:\n\nRemove (2,1), (2,2), (2,3), and (3,2). Then remaining pads:\n\nRow 1: (1,1), (1,2), (1,3)\n\nRow 2: nothing\n\nRow 3: (3,1), (3,3)\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to (1,2) and (2,1) [removed], so connected to (1,2).\n\n(1,2) connected to (1,1), (1,3), and (2,2) [removed]. So (1,2) connected to (1,1) and (1,3).\n\n(1,3) connected to (1,2) and (2,3) [removed], so only to (1,2).\n\nRow 3: (3,1) connected to (2,1) [removed] and (3,2) [removed], so isolated.\n\n(3,3) connected to (3,2) [removed] and (2,3) [removed], so isolated.\n\nTherefore, components:\n\n- { (1,1), (1,2), (1,3) }\n\n- { (3,1) }\n\n- { (3,3) }\n\nThus, (1,1) and (3,3) are in different components. So that is another valid set. \n\nSimilarly, if we remove the entire middle row plus (1,3):\n\nRemove (2,1), (2,2), (2,3), and (1,3). Remaining pads:\n\nRow 1: (1,1), (1,2)\n\nRow 2: nothing\n\nRow 3: (3,1), (3,2), (3,3)\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to (1,2) and (2,1) [removed], so connected to (1,2).\n\n(1,2) connected to (1,1) and (1,3) [removed], so only to (1,1).\n\nRow 3: (3,1) connected to (2,1) [removed] and (3,2). \n\n(3,2) connected to (3,1), (3,3), and (2,2) [removed].\n\n(3,3) connected to (3,2) and (2,3) [removed].\n\nThus components:\n\n- { (1,1), (1,2) }\n\n- { (3,1), (3,2), (3,3) }\n\nTherefore, (1,1) and (3,3) are in different components. So that's another valid set. \n\nSimilarly, if we remove the entire middle row plus (3,1):\n\nRemove (2,1), (2,2), (2,3), and (3,1). Remaining pads:\n\nRow 1: (1,1), (1,2), (1,3)\n\nRow 2: nothing\n\nRow 3: (3,2), (3,3)\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to (1,2) and (2,1) [removed], so to (1,2).\n\n(1,2) connected to (1,1), (1,3), and (2,2) [removed].\n\n(1,3) connected to (1,2) and (2,3) [removed].\n\nRow 3: (3,2) connected to (3,1) [removed], (3,3), and (2,2) [removed]. So (3,2) connected to (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to (3,2).\n\nThus components:\n\n- { (1,1), (1,2), (1,3) }\n\n- { (3,2), (3,3) }\n\nTherefore, (1,1) and (3,3) are in different components. So that's another valid set. \n\nSo in total, by removing the entire middle row (three pads) plus one more pad from the top row or bottom row, we get four valid configurations. Specifically, removing the middle row plus one of the four corner pads? Wait, no: in the above examples, we removed the middle row plus (1,2), (3,2), (1,3), or (3,1). Those are edge pads, not corners. The corners are (1,1) and (3,3), which we can't remove, and the other corners are (1,3) and (3,1). Wait, actually (1,3) and (3,1) are the other two corners. \n\nWait, in the examples above:\n\n- Removing the middle row plus (1,2) [which is an edge, not a corner]\n\n- Removing the middle row plus (3,2) [edge]\n\n- Removing the middle row plus (1,3) [corner]\n\n- Removing the middle row plus (3,1) [corner]\n\nTherefore, there are four such configurations where we remove the entire middle row (three pads) plus one of the four edge pads adjacent to the middle row: (1,2), (3,2), (1,3), or (3,1). Wait, actually (1,3) and (3,1) are corners, but they are also adjacent to the middle row? (1,3) is above the middle row's (2,3), but not directly adjacent to the middle row's center. Wait, actually (1,3) is in row 1, column 3, so it's adjacent to (2,3) which is in the middle row. Similarly, (3,1) is in row 3, column 1, adjacent to (2,1) in the middle row. So those are edge pads adjacent to the middle row. \n\nTherefore, those four configurations are valid. \n\nAdditionally, earlier we had another configuration where we removed (2,1), (2,3), (1,2), and (3,2). That was removing the left and right ends of the middle row, plus the top and bottom edge centers. Wait, let me see:\n\nIn that case, removed pads were (2,1), (2,3), (1,2), (3,2). So that's removing the left and right ends of the middle row, plus the top center and bottom center. \n\nIs that another distinct configuration? Let's see if that can be categorized differently. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps there are more configurations where we don't remove the entire middle row. \n\nFor instance, suppose we remove the four corners except the start and home? Wait, the corners are (1,1) [start, can't remove], (1,3), (3,1), (3,3) [home, can't remove]. So removing the other two corners: (1,3) and (3,1). But that's only two pads. If we remove those two plus two others. \n\nSuppose we remove (1,3), (3,1), plus two more pads. Let's say we remove (2,2) as well, making three, and then one more. Let's see:\n\nSuppose we remove (1,3), (3,1), (2,2), and (1,2). Let's see if that disconnects (1,1) from (3,3).\n\nRemaining pads:\n\nRow 1: (1,1), (1,2) [since (1,3) is removed]\n\nRow 2: (2,1), (2,3) [since (2,2) is removed]\n\nRow 3: (3,2), (3,3) [since (3,1) is removed]\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) is connected to (1,2) and (2,1). \n\n(1,2) is connected to (1,1) and (2,2) [removed], so only to (1,1).\n\n(2,1) is connected to (1,1) and (2,2) [removed], so only to (1,1).\n\n(2,3) is connected to (1,3) [removed], (2,2) [removed], and (3,3). \n\n(3,3) is connected to (2,3) and (3,2). \n\n(3,2) is connected to (3,3) and (2,2) [removed], so only to (3,3).\n\nSo, components:\n\n- { (1,1), (1,2), (2,1) }\n\n- { (2,3), (3,3), (3,2) }\n\nTherefore, (1,1) and (3,3) are in separate components. So that's another valid configuration. \n\nSo that's a fifth configuration. \n\nSimilarly, if we remove (1,3), (3,1), (2,2), and (3,2). Let's see:\n\nRemaining pads:\n\nRow 1: (1,1), (1,2), (1,3) [but (1,3) is removed? Wait, no: we are removing (1,3), (3,1), (2,2), and (3,2). So:\n\nRow 1: (1,1), (1,2) [since (1,3) removed]\n\nRow 2: (2,1), (2,3) [since (2,2) removed]\n\nRow 3: (3,1) [removed], (3,2) [removed], (3,3) \n\nWait, (3,1) and (3,2) are removed, so Row 3 only has (3,3). \n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to (1,2) and (2,1).\n\n(1,2) connected to (1,1) and (2,2) [removed].\n\n(2,1) connected to (1,1) and (3,1) [removed], so only to (1,1).\n\nRow 2: (2,3) connected to (1,3) [removed], (2,2) [removed], and (3,3). \n\n(3,3) connected to (2,3) and (3,2) [removed].\n\nSo components:\n\n- { (1,1), (1,2), (2,1) }\n\n- { (2,3), (3,3) }\n\nThus, (1,1) and (3,3) are disconnected. So that's another valid configuration. \n\nContinuing this way, there are multiple configurations. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can categorize the valid subsets based on how they block the paths. \n\nLet me think of the possible ways to block all paths from (1,1) to (3,3). Since any path must go from the bottom-left to the top-right, they must move either right, up, or a combination. To block all paths, we need to ensure that every possible route is blocked by at least one removed pad. \n\nOne way is to block all paths that go through the center (2,2). To do that, we need to remove (2,2), but as we saw, that alone isn't enough. However, combined with other removed pads, it can help. \n\nAlternatively, think of the grid as having two main \"corridors\": the top row and the right column, and the left column and the bottom row. Wait, perhaps not. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the key is to separate the grid into two parts: one containing (1,1) and the other containing (3,3), with no connections between them. \n\nTo do that with four removed pads, we can consider different \"barriers\". For instance, a vertical or horizontal barrier in the middle, plus some other blocks. \n\nAlternatively, note that in the 3x3 grid, the minimal vertex cut between (1,1) and (3,3) is 2 vertices, as we saw earlier. For instance, removing (1,2) and (3,2) might not be sufficient, but perhaps with four vertices, we can have multiple such cuts. \n\nAlternatively, let me think of all the possible four-vertex subsets that include (2,2) or not, and see how they affect connectivity. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to try to count them systematically. \n\nFirst, note that in the earlier examples, we found several configurations:\n\n1. Remove the entire middle row (three pads: (2,1), (2,2), (2,3)) plus one more pad from the top or bottom row edges: (1,2), (3,2), (1,3), or (3,1). That gives four configurations.\n\n2. Remove the two \"side\" pads of the middle row (i.e., (2,1) and (2,3)) plus the two \"side\" pads of the top and bottom rows: (1,2) and (3,2). That was the first example I had: (2,1), (2,3), (1,2), (3,2). That's another configuration not covered in the previous case.\n\n3. Remove the two corners (1,3) and (3,1), plus the center (2,2) and one more pad, such as (1,2) or (3,2). As in the example above where we had (1,3), (3,1), (2,2), and (1,2). Similarly with (3,2). That gives two more configurations.\n\nWait, actually in the earlier example where I removed (1,3), (3,1), (2,2), and (1,2), that was one, and removing (1,3), (3,1), (2,2), and (3,2) was another. So that's two configurations there.\n\nAdditionally, are there others?\n\nSuppose we remove the two corners (1,3) and (3,1), plus the two edge centers (1,2) and (3,2). That would be four pads: (1,3), (3,1), (1,2), (3,2). Let's see if that disconnects (1,1) from (3,3).\n\nRemaining pads:\n\nRow 1: (1,1), (1,2) [since (1,3) removed]\n\nRow 2: (2,1), (2,2), (2,3) [all present? Wait, no: we only removed (1,3), (3,1), (1,2), (3,2). So Row 2 remains intact except none of its pads are removed. \n\nWait, no: the removed pads are only those four: (1,3), (3,1), (1,2), (3,2). So Row 2 is untouched: (2,1), (2,2), (2,3) are all present. \n\nRow 3: (3,1) is removed, (3,2) is removed, so only (3,3) remains. \n\nSo the remaining pads are:\n\nRow 1: (1,1), (1,2)\n\nRow 2: all three: (2,1), (2,2), (2,3)\n\nRow 3: (3,3)\n\nNow, connections:\n\n(1,1) is connected to (1,2) and (2,1).\n\n(1,2) is connected to (1,1) and (2,2).\n\n(2,1) is connected to (1,1), (2,2), and (3,1) [which is removed].\n\n(2,2) is connected to (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), and (3,2) [removed].\n\n(2,3) is connected to (2,2) and (1,3) [removed] and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) is connected to (2,3) and (3,2) [removed].\n\nSo let's see the connected components:\n\nStart at (1,1):\n\n(1,1) connected to (1,2) and (2,1).\n\nFrom (1,2), can go to (2,2).\n\nFrom (2,1), can go to (2,2).\n\nFrom (2,2), can go to (1,2), (2,1), (2,3).\n\nFrom (2,3), can go to (2,2) and (3,3).\n\nFrom (3,3), can only go back to (2,3).\n\nTherefore, all these pads are in one big connected component: { (1,1), (1,2), (2,1), (2,2), (2,3), (3,3) }.\n\nThus, (1,1) and (3,3) are still connected through this path: (1,1) → (2,1) → (2,2) → (2,3) → (3,3). \n\nTherefore, this configuration does NOT disconnect (1,1) from (3,3). So that is not a valid configuration. \n\nTherefore, removing the two corners and the two edge centers is insufficient. \n\nHmm. So that attempt failed. \n\nAnother idea: what if we remove the four edge centers? The edge centers are (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2). Let's see:\n\nRemoving those four pads: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2). \n\nThen remaining pads are:\n\nRow 1: (1,1), (1,3)\n\nRow 2: (2,2)\n\nRow 3: (3,1), (3,3)\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to (1,2) [removed] and (2,1) [removed], so isolated.\n\n(1,3) connected to (1,2) [removed] and (2,3) [removed], so isolated.\n\n(2,2) connected to (1,2) [removed], (2,1) [removed], (2,3) [removed], and (3,2) [removed], so isolated.\n\n(3,1) connected to (2,1) [removed] and (3,2) [removed], so isolated.\n\n(3,3) connected to (3,2) [removed] and (2,3) [removed], so isolated.\n\nTherefore, all remaining pads are isolated, so (1,1) and (3,3) are in separate components. Thus, this is another valid configuration. \n\nSo that's another one: removing the four edge centers. \n\nSo that's a fifth configuration: remove the four edge centers: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2). \n\nWait, but in my earlier count, when I removed the middle row plus one, that included some of these. For example, the middle row is (2,1), (2,2), (2,3). Removing the middle row plus (1,2) would remove (2,1), (2,2), (2,3), and (1,2). So that includes three of the four edge centers (the left, center, and right of the middle row) plus (1,2). The fourth edge center is (3,2), which is not removed in that case. \n\nWhereas the four edge centers removal is a separate case. \n\nSo far, I have identified the following distinct configurations:\n\n1. Middle row plus one edge center from top or bottom: 4 configurations (adding (1,2), (3,2), (1,3), or (3,1)).\n\nWait, actually when I removed the middle row plus (1,2), (3,2), (1,3), or (3,1), that's four different configurations. \n\n2. Removing the four edge centers: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2). That's one configuration.\n\n3. Removing the two corners (1,3) and (3,1), plus the center (2,2), plus one more edge center, either (1,2) or (3,2). That gives two configurations.\n\n4. The example with removing (2,1), (2,3), (1,2), (3,2) [the four edge centers? Wait, no, that was the four edge centers? Wait, (2,1) and (2,3) are part of the middle row, not edge centers. Wait, actually, (2,1) is the left end of the middle row, which is an edge center? Maybe my terminology is conflicting. \n\nWait, let's clarify:\n\nThe four edge centers are the pads that are on the edges but not corners. In a 3x3 grid, the edge centers are:\n\nTop edge: (1,2)\n\nBottom edge: (3,2)\n\nLeft edge: (2,1)\n\nRight edge: (2,3)\n\nYes, those are the four edge centers. So removing those four is one configuration.\n\nThen the middle row is three pads: (2,1), (2,2), (2,3). So when we remove the middle row plus one more, that's different. \n\nAdditionally, when we remove the two corners (1,3) and (3,1), plus the center (2,2), and one more pad, say (1,2) or (3,2), that's two configurations. \n\nEarlier, I also had the configuration where we removed (2,1), (2,3), (1,2), (3,2). Wait, is that the same as the four edge centers? Let me check:\n\n(2,1), (2,3), (1,2), (3,2):\n\n- (2,1) is the left edge center\n\n- (2,3) is the right edge center\n\n- (1,2) is the top edge center\n\n- (3,2) is the bottom edge center\n\nYes! So that is exactly the four edge centers. So that was actually the same as configuration 2 above. \n\nTherefore, that example was actually the four edge centers. \n\nAnother configuration was removing (1,3), (3,1), (2,2), and (1,2). Let me see what that looks like:\n\n(1,3) is the top-right corner,\n\n(3,1) is the bottom-left corner,\n\n(2,2) is the center,\n\nand (1,2) is the top edge center.\n\nSo this is removing two corners, the center, and one edge center. \n\nSimilarly, removing (1,3), (3,1), (2,2), and (3,2) is the other one. \n\nSo those are two distinct configurations. \n\nAdditionally, we had the case where we removed the middle row plus one edge center: four configurations. \n\nAre there any other configurations? \n\nLet me think of another possibility. Suppose we remove the two corners (1,3) and (3,1), plus the two edge centers (2,1) and (2,3). Let's see:\n\nRemoving (1,3), (3,1), (2,1), (2,3). \n\nRemaining pads:\n\nRow 1: (1,1), (1,2)\n\nRow 2: (2,2)\n\nRow 3: (3,2), (3,3)\n\nConnections:\n\n(1,1) connected to (1,2) and (2,1) [removed], so only to (1,2).\n\n(1,2) connected to (1,1) and (2,2).\n\n(2,2) connected to (1,2), (2,1) [removed], (2,3) [removed], and (3,2).\n\n(3,2) connected to (2,2) and (3,3).\n\n(3,3) connected to (3,2).\n\nSo the connected components:\n\nStarting at (1,1):\n\n(1,1) → (1,2) → (2,2) → (3,2) → (3,3). \n\nTherefore, (1,1) and (3,3) are still connected through this path. So that's not a valid configuration. \n\nHmm, so that doesn't work. \n\nWhat if we remove the two corners (1,3) and (3,1), plus the center (2,2), and the top edge center (1,2)? We already considered that, which was valid. \n\nAnother idea