hen we have two 2-blocks going into one B-box, is there an overcounting?\n\nWait, the two 2-blocks are distinguishable because they are in different positions on the path. For example, suppose the two 2-blocks are [1,2] and [5,6]. Putting both into the same box is just one way, since the box contains both sets, and the order within the box doesn't matter. So that should be 1 way.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the formula for the number of assignments is incorrect?\n\nWait, the number of ways to assign the 2-blocks to the B-boxes was calculated as (number of ways to partition into pairs) multiplied by (number of ways to assign to labeled boxes). For x=3, we have two 2-blocks and one B-box, so the number of ways should be 1 (since you have to put both into the one box). But according to the formula:\n\n(8 - 2x)! / 2^{4 - x} = 2! / 2^1 = 2 / 2 = 1, which is correct.\n\nTherefore, the assignment ways is 1, so the term should be T(x) * C(4,x) * x! * assignment_ways = 10 * 4 * 6 * 1 = 240.\n\nBut according to the algebraic simplification, it should be T(x) * [4! / ( (4 - x)! 2^{4 - x} ) ] = 10 * (24 / (1 * 2)) = 10 * 12 = 120.\n\nTherefore, there is a factor of 2 discrepancy. Where is this coming from?\n\nWait, looking back at the algebra:\n\nAfter all cancellations, the term became 4! / [ (4 - x)! 2^{4 - x} ].\n\nBut in reality, for x=3,\n\n4! / [ (4 - 3)! 2^{4 - 3} ] = 24 / (1 * 2) = 12,\n\nbut the actual term is 240 = 10 * 4 * 6 * 1 = 240,\n\nwhich is 10 * 24 / (1 * 2) * something?\n\nWait, 10 * (24 / (1 * 2)) = 10 * 12 = 120,\n\nbut actual is 240 = 10 * 24,\n\nso the discrepancy is that the formula is missing a factor of x!.\n\nWait, in the algebra, after all cancellations, we had:\n\nTerm_x = 4! / [ (4 - x)! 2^{4 - x} ]\n\nBut in reality, the term is:\n\nTerm_x = T(x) * C(4,x) * x! * [assignment_ways]\n\nBut in the algebra, we had substituted T(x) and everything, and ended up with Term_x = 4! / [ (4 - x)! 2^{4 - x} ]\n\nBut when we plug in x=3, we get 12, but actual is 2