ot allowed, so that's invalid—or two connected components each of size 2. Since 2 is the smallest allowed component size. So, each box must be either a single connected block of 4 vertices, or two disconnected blocks of 2 vertices each.\n\nWait, so each box must be either a single connected block (a path of 4 vertices) or two separate blocks of 2 vertices each (i.e., two separate edges). But in the original path graph, the connected blocks must themselves be intervals? Because in a path graph, connected components are intervals. For example, if you have vertices labeled 1 through 16, and you take some subset of them, then the connected components in that subset are intervals of consecutive vertices. Therefore, in order for a box to consist of two separate blocks of 2 vertices each, those two blocks must be non-adjacent? Because if they were adjacent, then they would form a single block of 4 vertices? Wait, no, if they are separated by at least one vertex, then they would be two separate blocks.\n\nWait, let's clarify:\n\nSuppose we have a box containing balls labeled a, b, c, d. For the induced subgraph on these balls to have minimum degree at least 1, each of these balls must have at least one neighbor also in the box. So, for ball a (if it's in the box), it must have a neighbor (either a-1 or a+1) also in the box. Similarly for ball b, etc. So, in terms of the path graph, if the box contains vertices {a, b, c, d}, then these vertices must form a set where each is adjacent to at least one other in the set. So, the induced subgraph cannot have any isolated vertices.\n\nTherefore, for a subset of 4 vertices in the path graph, the induced subgraph has no isolated vertices if and only if the subset is either:\n\n1. A single interval of 4 consecutive vertices (like {1,2,3,4}), or\n\n2. Two separate intervals of 2 consecutive vertices each, with at least one vertex between them (like {1,2,4,5}), but note that {1,2,3,5} would not be allowed because the vertex 5 is isolate