e B box's first block), so the next vertex could start the second block.\n\nWait, let's think carefully.\n\nSuppose we are building the tiling from left to right. At any point, the current position could be:\n\n- In the middle of placing a Type B box's first block: i.e., we've started a Type B box by placing the first domino (two vertices), but haven't placed the second domino yet. So, we have \"pending\" vertices that are part of an incomplete Type B box.\n\n- Or, we're in the middle of a Type A box: but since Type A boxes are solid blocks, once you start a Type A box, you have to complete it in the next three vertices.\n\nAlternatively, since Type A boxes are solid 4-blocks, whenever we start a Type A box at position i, it must occupy i, i+1, i+2, i+3. So, if we are at position i, and we decide to start a Type A box, then we must cover the next three positions as part of the same box.\n\nFor Type B boxes, since they consist of two separate dominoes with at least one space between them, starting a Type B box could mean starting the first domino at position i, then later placing the second domino at position j >= i+3.\n\nHowever, since we are moving left to right, once we place the first domino of a Type B box, we have to remember that we need to place another domino later, but also that between them there must be at least one space. This seems complex.\n\nPerhaps the states should encode the number of \"unmatched\" domino starts.\n\nLet me try this approach:\n\nDefine the states as follows:\n\n- State 0: No incomplete boxes. The last placed box is complete, and we are ready to start a new box.\n\n- State 1: We have started a Type B box's first domino (i.e., we've placed one vertex of a domino, but need to place another vertex for the first domino), and the next vertex must be the second vertex of this first domino to complete it, or we can start a new box.\n\nWait, maybe better:\n\nLet me think in terms of how many \"pending\" vertices we have from an incomplete Ty