o four blocks of four, without any restrictions. This is equal to the multinomial coefficient: 16! / (4!^4). However, this counts all possible partitions into four subsets of size four, without considering the adjacency requirements. We need only those partitions where each subset satisfies the neighbor condition.\n\nTherefore, we need to subtract those partitions where at least one subset has an isolated vertex. But inclusion-exclusion on this might be complicated because the subsets are interdependent.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model this as a recurrence where we build up the partition from left to right.\n\nLet me define f(n, k) as the number of ways to partition the first n vertices into k blocks (each of size 4) satisfying the neighbor condition. Our target is f(16, 4).\n\nTo define the recurrence, consider the last block (the k-th block). This block can be of Type A or Type B.\n\nCase 1: The last block is Type A. Then it occupies the last four vertices: positions n-3 to n. The number of ways to do this is f(n - 4, k - 1).\n\nCase 2: The last block is Type B. A Type B block consists of two pairs of consecutive vertices with at least one vertex between them. The last vertex of the Type B block must be at position n. Let's consider how such a block can end at position n.\n\nThe last block (Type B) must end at position n. Since it's a Type B block, it has two pairs: one ending at position n, and another pair somewhere before, separated by at least one vertex.\n\nLet me denote the second pair (the one ending at n) as positions [n-1, n]. The first pair of this Type B block must be somewhere before position n-2, say starting at position i, so the first pair is [i, i+1], with i ≤ n - 4 (since there needs to be at least one vertex between the two pairs). Then, the remaining vertices from 1 to i-1 must be partitioned into k - 1 blocks.\n\nHowever, the gap between the two pairs of the Type B block (positions i+2 to n-2) must be covered by other blocks. Wait, but t