Partition 2:\n\nGroup1: {1,2,3,4}\n\nGroup2: {5,6,7,8}\n\nGroup3: {9,10,11,12}\n\nGroup4: {13,14,15,16}\n\n3. Partition 3:\n\nGroup1: {1,2,3,4}\n\nGroup2: {5,6,9,10}\n\nGroup3: {7,8,11,12}\n\nGroup4: {13,14,15,16}\n\nYes, these are three distinct partitions. So the answer is 3?\n\nBut I have a nagging feeling that there might be more. Let me think if there's another way to partition the middle segment.\n\nConsider another possible Type B group in the middle:\n\nGroup A: {5,7,8,9} – but as before, 5 is isolated.\n\nNo.\n\nGroup A: {6,7,8,10} – 6-7-8 are consecutive, 10 is isolated (adjacent to 9 and 11). In Group A, 10 is isolated. Invalid.\n\nGroup A: {5,8,9,10} – 5 is isolated.\n\nGroup A: {6,9,10,11} – 6 is isolated (adjacent to 5 and 7), 9-10-11 are okay, but 6 is isolated.\n\nNo.\n\nAlternatively, Group A: {5,6,10,11} – 5-6 are adjacent, 10-11 are adjacent, with a gap between 6 and 10 (positions 7-9). So this is a valid Type B group.\n\nThen Group B would be {7,8,9,12} – original positions.\n\nCheck Group B: {7,8,9,12}\n\n- 7-8-9 are consecutive (Type A), and 12 is isolated (adjacent to 11 not in group). So 12 is isolated in Group B. Invalid.\n\nHmm.\n\nAnother try: Group A: {5,6,10,12} – edges 5-6 and 10-12? No, 10 and 12 are not adjacent. So induced subgraph has edges 5-6 and nothing else. So nodes 10 and 12 are isolated. Invalid.\n\nAlternatively, Group A: {5,7,10,11} – 5 is isolated, 10-11 are okay, but 7 is adjacent to 6 and 8 (not in group). 7 is isolated. Invalid.\n\nHmm, it seems challenging to find another valid partition. So likely only three.\n\nTherefore, the total number of ways is 3.\n\nBut wait, the problem says \"the order in which the balls are placed does not matter\", and the boxes are indistinct. So the three partitions I found are the only ones, so the answer should be 3.\n\nHowever, I recall that in the middle segment analysis, when we considered Case 2 Subcase 6, the two Type B groups were {5,6,9,10} and {7,8,11,12}, which is one partition