ent path.\n\nWait, actually:\n\nFrom (1,2):\n\n- Moving right to (2,2) is one option,\n\n- Moving up to (1,3) is another,\n\n- Moving diagonally up-right to (2,3) is another,\n\n- Moving diagonally down-right to (2,1) is another.\n\nThus four distinct moves, each leading to different positions:\n\n1. (2,2): 5 paths,\n\n2. (1,3): leads to 7 paths,\n\n3. (2,3): leads to 2 paths,\n\n4. (2,1): leads to 3 paths.\n\nTherefore total from (1,2): 5 + 7 + 2 + 3 = 17 paths?\n\nWait, that seems high, but perhaps it's correct.\n\nWait, but actually, when you move from (1,2) to (2,3) via diagonal up-right, that's a single move, and from there you have two paths to the end, so that's two paths.\n\nSimilarly, moving to (2,1) gives three paths.\n\nMoving to (1,3) gives seven paths? Wait, no, moving to (1,3) gives two paths from (2,3) and five paths from (2,2), totaling seven, yes.\n\nAnd moving to (2,2) gives five paths.\n\nSo total from (1,2) is indeed 5 + 7 + 2 + 3 = 17? Wait no: moving to (2,2) gives 5 paths,\n\nmoving to (1,3) gives 7 paths,\n\nmoving to (2,3) gives 2 paths,\n\nmoving to (2,1) gives 3 paths,\n\nso total: 5 + 7 + 2 + 3 = 17 paths from (1,2).\n\nBut this seems high, but perhaps it is correct.\n\nCase 1b3: Move to (2,2) via diagonal up-right from (1,1):\n\nWait, no, we already considered moving to (2,2) from (1,1) in Case 1b1.\n\nWait, no, in Case 1b, we are at (1,1), and one of the moves is to (2,2) via diagonal up-right? Wait no:\n\nWait, from (1,1), the moves are:\n\n1. Right to (2,1),\n\n2. Up to (1,2),\n\n3. Diagonally up-right to (2,2),\n\n4. Diagonally down-right to (2,0).\n\nAh, yes! I missed the move to (2,2) directly from (1,1). So in Case 1b (at (1,1)), moving diagonally up-right to (2,2) is another option.\n\nWait, I think I missed this earlier.\n\nLet me correct that.\n\nFrom (1,1), the four moves are:\n\n1. Right to (2,1),\n\n2. Up to (1,2),\n\n3. Diagonally up-right to (2,2),\n\n4. Diagonally down-right to (2,0).\n\nTherefore, Case 1b has four subcases:\n\nCase 1b1: Move to (2,1),\n\nCase 1b2: Move to (1,2),\n\nCase 1b3: Move to (2,2),\n\nCase 1b4: Move to (2,0).\n\nI previously only considered three of them, missing Case 1b3 and Case 1b4.\n\nThis explains the inconsistency.\n\nLet me re-examine:\n\nCase 1b3: Move to (2,2):\n\nPath: [(0,0), (1,0), (1,1), (2,2)]\n\nFrom (2,2), as before, there are five paths to the end (as calculated earlier).\n\nCase 1b4: Move to (2,0):\n\nPath: [(0,0), (1,0), (1,1), (2,0)]\n\nFrom (2,0), possible moves:\n\n1. Right to (3,0),\n\n2. Up to (2,1),\n\n3. Diagonally up-right to (3,1),\n\n4. Diagonally down-right to (3,-1) invalid.\n\nSo from (2,0):\n\n- Move right to (3,0): then as before, leads to two paths: [(...), (3,0), (3,1), (3,2), (3,3)]\n\n- Move up to (2,1): then from (2,1), three paths to the end,\n\n- Move diagonally up-right to (3,1): then from (3,1), two paths to the end.\n\nTherefore:\n\nFrom (2,0):\n\n- To (3,0): 1 path,\n\n- To (2,1): 3 paths,\n\n- To (3,1): 1 path (directly to (3,1) then to (3,2) and (3,3)),\n\nWait, from (3,1):\n\nFrom (3,1), you can go to (3,2) then to (3,3): that's one path.\n\nWait, actually, from (3,1):\n\n- Move up to (3,2),\n\n- Then from (3,2) move up to (3,3): so that's one path.\n\nTherefore, moving to (3,1) gives one path.\n\nThus total from (2,0):\n\n1 (to 3,0) + 3 (to 2,1) + 1 (to 3,1) = 5 paths.\n\nTherefore, Case 1b4 (moving to (2,0)) gives 5 paths.\n\nNow, re-evaluating Case 1b:\n\nCase 1b1: Move to (2,1): 3 paths,\n\nCase 1b2: Move to (1,2): 17 paths? Wait no, let's recast:\n\nWait, let's recast all subcases properly:\n\nCase 1b1: Move to (2,1): leads to 3 paths,\n\nCase 1b2: Move to (1,2): leads to 17 paths? Wait no, that was incorrect.\n\nWait, actually, when moving from (1,1) to (1,2), we need to recalculate the paths from (1,2):\n\nWait, no, previously when I considered moving from (1,2), I assumed it came from Case 1b2, but now with the corrected breakdown, let's redo the counts properly.\n\nWait this is getting too recursive. Perhaps I need to accept that this manual computation is error-prone and time-consuming, and instead try to find a better way.\n\nAlternatively, let's switch strategies and use the DP approach with the corrected understanding that in each column k, the number of paths to y is the sum over all y' ≤ y of the number of paths to y' in column k, considering the contributions from the previous column.\n\nLet me try this DP approach again, more carefully.\n\nDefine dp[k][y] as the number of paths that reach (k, y) for the first time (i.e., without having visited any points in columns > k), and then can proceed to the next columns.\n\nWait, but actually, once you reach column k, you can move up within the column, so perhaps:\n\nLet me redefine dp[k][y] as the number of ways to reach (k, y) from the start, without revisiting any points, considering all possible paths.\n\nTo compute dp[k][y], we can consider that to get to (k, y), you must have come from column k-1 via one of the allowed moves, or moved up from a lower y' in column k.\n\nBut the moves from column k-1 are:\n\nFrom column k-1 at position (k-1, y_prev), you can move to column k at:\n\n- (k, y_prev) via right move,\n\n- (k, y_prev + 1) via diagonal up-right,\n\n- (k, y_prev - 1) via diagonal down-right (if y_prev ≥ 1).\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to get to (k, y) from column k-1 is the sum over all y_prev such that y = y_prev, y_prev + 1, or y_prev - 1 (with y_prev ≥ 1 for the last case), of dp[k-1][y_prev].\n\nAdditionally, you can also reach (k, y) by moving up from a lower y' in column k, which would be the sum over y' from 0 to y-1 of dp[k][y'].\n\nWait, but this recursive relation is:\n\ndp[k][y] = [sum over y_prev such that y = y_prev or y_prev+1 or y_prev-1 (with y_prev >=1) of dp[k-1][y_prev]] + [sum over y' = 0 to y-1 of dp[k][y']]\n\nBut this seems to require knowing dp[k][y'] for y' < y, which are being computed in the same column.\n\nThis suggests that for each column k, we need to compute dp[k][y] in order from y=0 to y=3, and for each y, the second term (the upward moves) depends on the previously computed values in the same column.\n\nTherefore, the correct recurrence is:\n\nFor each column k from 0 to 3:\n\n   For each y from 0 to 3:\n\n      if k == 0:\n\n          dp[0][y] = 1 if y == 0 else (dp[0][y-1] if y > 0 else 0)\n\n          Wait, no. For column 0:\n\n          The starting point is (0,0). To reach (0,1), you must have come from (0,0) by moving up, so dp[0][1] = dp[0][0] = 1.\n\n          To reach (0,2), you must have come from (0,1), so dp[0][2] = dp[0][1] = 1.\n\n          Similarly, dp[0][3] = dp[0][2] = 1.\n\n          However, this counts only the paths that stay entirely in column 0. But in reality, from (0,0), you can also move right to column 1, so the dp[k][y] should also account for paths that leave the column.\n\n          Hmm, this is the crux: dp[k][y] should represent the number of paths that end at (k, y) without having left column k yet? Or including paths that have left?\n\n          I think the confusion comes from whether dp[k][y] includes paths that have already moved to subsequent columns or not.\n\n          Let me redefine dp[k][y] as the number of paths that reach (k, y) for the first time (i.e., without having visited any points in columns > k), and then from there can proceed to the next columns.\n\n          In this case, to compute dp[k][y], we only consider moves from column k-1 to column k, and do not yet account for moving up within column k.\n\n          Then, once in column k at y, you can choose to move up to higher y's, which would be accounted for in subsequent columns' calculations.\n\n          This is getting too tangled. Maybe let's try this step-by-step with the DP table.\n\nInitialize a 4x4 grid (columns 0-3, rows 0-3). We'll compute dp[k][y] as the number of paths to reach (k, y) for the first time (i.e., without having visited any points in columns > k).\n\nStart with column 0:\n\n- dp[0][0] = 1 (starting point)\n\n- dp[0][1] = dp[0][0] = 1 (move up from (0,0))\n\n- dp[0][2] = dp[0][1] = 1 (move up from (0,1))\n\n- dp[0][3] = dp[0][2] = 1 (move up from (0,2))\n\nThese represent the number of ways to reach each y in column 0 without leaving column 0.\n\nNow, for column 1:\n\nFor each y in 0..3:\n\ndp[1][y] = sum over y_prev in column 0 such that you can move to (1, y) from (0, y_prev) via allowed moves.\n\nThe allowed moves from column 0 to column 1 are:\n\nFrom (0, y_prev), you can move to:\n\n- (1, y_prev) via right,\n\n- (1, y_prev + 1) via diagonal up-right,\n\n- (1, y_prev - 1) via diagonal down-right (if y_prev ≥ 1).\n\nTherefore:\n\nFor each y in column 1:\n\ndp[1][y] = sum of dp[0][y_prev] for all y_prev such that y = y_prev or y = y_prev + 1 or y = y_prev - 1 (with y_prev ≥ 1 for the last case).\n\nAdditionally, we must ensure that the move is valid (i.e., doesn't go below y=0 or above y=3).\n\nLet's compute this for each y in column 1:\n\ny = 0:\n\nCan come from y_prev = 0 (right move) or y_prev = 1 (diagonal down-right move, since y = y_prev - 1 ⇒ y_prev = y + 1 = 1).\n\nThus:\n\ndp[1][0] = dp[0][0] (from right move) + dp[0][1] (from diagonal down-right move)\n\n= 1 + 1 = 2.\n\ny = 1:\n\nCan come from:\n\n- y_prev = 1 (right move),\n\n- y_prev = 0 (diagonal up-right move: y = y_prev + 1 ⇒ y_prev = 0),\n\n- y_prev = 2 (diagonal down-right move: y = y_prev - 1 ⇒ y_prev = 2).\n\nThus:\n\ndp[1][1] = dp[0][1] (right) + dp[0][0] (diagonal up-right) + dp[0][2] (diagonal down-right)\n\n= 1 + 1 + 1 = 3.\n\ny = 2:\n\nCan come from:\n\n- y_prev = 2 (right move),\n\n- y_prev = 1 (diagonal up-right: y = y_prev + 1 ⇒ y_prev = 1),\n\n- y_prev = 3 (diagonal down-right: y = y_prev - 1 ⇒ y_prev = 3).\n\nBut dp[0][3] exists (it's 1), so:\n\ndp[1][2] = dp[0][2] (right) + dp[0][1] (diagonal up-right) + dp[0][3] (diagonal down-right)\n\n= 1 + 1 + 1 = 3.\n\ny = 3:\n\nCan come from:\n\n- y_prev = 3 (right move),\n\n- y_prev = 2 (diagonal up-right: y = y_prev + 1 ⇒ y_prev = 2),\n\n- y_prev = 4 (diagonal down-right: invalid since y_prev exceeds grid).\n\nThus:\n\ndp[1][3] = dp[0][3] (right) + dp[0][2] (diagonal up-right)\n\n= 1 + 1 = 2.\n\nTherefore, column 1 dp values:\n\ndp[1] = [2, 3, 3, 2]\n\nNow, moving to column 2:\n\nFor each y in 0..3:\n\ndp[2][y] = sum over y_prev in column 1 such that y can be reached from (1, y_prev) via allowed moves.\n\nSimilarly:\n\nFor y=0 in column 2:\n\nCan come from:\n\n- y_prev = 0 (right move),\n\n- y_prev = 1 (diagonal down-right: y = y_prev -1 ⇒ y_prev = 1).\n\nThus:\n\ndp[2][0] = dp[1][0] + dp[1][1] = 2 + 3 = 5.\n\ny=1 in column 2:\n\nCan come from:\n\n- y_prev = 1 (right move),\n\n- y_prev = 0 (diagonal up-right: y = y_prev +1 ⇒ y_prev=0),\n\n- y_prev = 2 (diagonal down-right: y = y_prev -1 ⇒ y_prev=2).\n\nThus:\n\ndp[2][1] = dp[1][1] + dp[1][0] + dp[1][2] = 3 + 2 + 3 = 8.\n\ny=2 in column 2:\n\nCan come from:\n\n- y_prev = 2 (right move),\n\n- y_prev = 1 (diagonal up-right: y = y_prev +1 ⇒ y_prev=1),\n\n- y_prev = 3 (diagonal down-right: y = y_prev -1 ⇒ y_prev=3).\n\nThus:\n\ndp[2][2] = dp[1][2] + dp[1][1] + dp[1][3] = 3 + 3 + 2 = 8.\n\ny=3 in column 2:\n\nCan come from:\n\n- y_prev = 3 (right move),\n\n- y_prev = 2 (diagonal up-right: y = y_prev +1 ⇒ y_prev=2).\n\nThus:\n\ndp[2][3] = dp[1][3] + dp[1][2] = 2 + 3 = 5.\n\nTherefore, column 2 dp values:\n\ndp[2] = [5, 8, 8, 5]\n\nProceeding to column 3:\n\nFor each y in 0..3:\n\ndp[3][y] = sum over y_prev in column 2 such that y can be reached from (2, y_prev) via allowed moves.\n\nAdditionally, since we're in the final column (x=3), to reach (3,3) we need to ensure that y=3 is the target.\n\nCompute dp[3][y] for y=0,1,2,3:\n\ny=0:\n\nCan come from:\n\n- y_prev = 0 (right move),\n\n- y_prev = 1 (diagonal down-right: y = y_prev -1 ⇒ y_prev=1).\n\nThus:\n\ndp[3][0] = dp[2][0] + dp[2][1] = 5 + 8 = 13.\n\ny=1:\n\nCan come from:\n\n- y_prev = 1 (right move),\n\n- y_prev = 0 (diagonal up-right: y = y_prev +1 ⇒ y_prev=0),\n\n- y_prev = 2 (diagonal down-right: y = y_prev -1 ⇒ y_prev=2).\n\nThus:\n\ndp[3][1] = dp[2][1] + dp[2][0] + dp[2][2] = 8 + 5 + 8 = 21.\n\ny=2:\n\nCan come from:\n\n- y_prev = 2 (right move),\n\n- y_prev = 1 (diagonal up-right: y = y_prev +1 ⇒ y_prev=1),\n\n- y_prev = 3 (diagonal down-right: y = y_prev -1 ⇒ y_prev=3).\n\nThus:\n\ndp[3][2] = dp[2][2] + dp[2][1] + dp[2][3] = 8 + 8 + 5 = 21.\n\ny=3:\n\nCan come from:\n\n- y_prev = 3 (right move),\n\n- y_prev = 2 (diagonal up-right: y = y_prev +1 ⇒ y_prev=2).\n\nThus:\n\ndp[3][3] = dp[2][3] + dp[2][2] = 5 + 8 = 13.\n\nHowever, we need the number of paths that end at (3,3), which is dp[3][3] = 13.\n\nBut wait, according to this DP table, the answer would be 13? But earlier manual counting suggested more paths.\n\nWait a second, there's a problem here. The DP approach as defined counts the number of paths that reach (3,3) without revisiting any points, but according to this calculation, it's 13. However, when we tried a partial manual count earlier, we were already at over 10 paths just in the first few steps, so 13 might be plausible, but let's verify.\n\nWait, let's check the DP steps again for possible errors.\n\nStarting with column 0:\n\ndp[0] = [1, 1, 1, 1]\n\nColumn 1:\n\ndp[1][0] = dp[0][0] + dp[0][1] = 1 + 1 = 2 ✔️\n\ndp[1][1] = dp[0][1] + dp[0][0] + dp[0][2] = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3 ✔️\n\ndp[1][2] = dp[0][2] + dp[0][1] + dp[0][3] = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3 ✔️\n\ndp[1][3] = dp[0][3] + dp[0][2] = 1 + 1 = 2 ✔️\n\nColumn 2:\n\ndp[2][0] = dp[1][0] + dp[1][1] = 2 + 3 = 5 ✔️\n\ndp[2][1] = dp[1][1] + dp[1][0] + dp[1][2] = 3 + 2 + 3 = 8 ✔️\n\ndp[2][2] = dp[1][2] + dp[1][1] + dp[1][3] = 3 + 3 + 2 = 8 ✔️\n\ndp[2][3] = dp[1][3] + dp[1][2] = 2 + 3 = 5 ✔️\n\nColumn 3:\n\ndp[3][0] = dp[2][0] + dp[2][1] = 5 + 8 = 13 ✔️\n\ndp[3][1] = dp[2][1] + dp[2][0] + dp[2][2] = 8 + 5 + 8 = 21 ✔️\n\ndp[3][2] = dp[2][2] + dp[2][1] + dp[2][3] = 8 + 8 + 5 = 21 ✔️\n\ndp[3][3] = dp[2][3] + dp[2][2] = 5 + 8 = 13 ✔️\n\nSo according to this DP table, the number of paths to (3,3) is 13.\n\nHowever, when I was manually counting earlier, I was getting more than 10 paths even in the first few steps, but perhaps my manual count was flawed.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the DP is correct and the answer is 13.\n\nWait, let's see another way.\n\nThe total number of paths according to this DP is 13. Let's see if that makes sense.\n\nThe moves allowed are somewhat restrictive, but with the possibility of moving up and down (in the next column), it might be manageable.\n\nAlternatively, let's see the possible paths to (3,3):\n\nEach path must consist of moves that increase x from 0 to 3, with y ending at 3.\n\nEach step increases x by 1, so there are 3 steps (since from x=0 to x=3 requires 3 increments).\n\nWait a second! Hold on! This is a critical realization.\n\nWait, each move increases x by 1. Therefore, to go from x=0 to x=3, you need exactly 3 moves. Because each move increases x by 1, so after 3 moves, x will be 3.\n\nTherefore, the path has exactly 3 moves, each of which is one of the four types, but with the constraint that the total change in y must be +3 (since starting at y=0 and ending at y=3).\n\nAdditionally, the path cannot revisit any points, and must stay within the grid.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to counting the number of 3-step paths from (0,0) to (3,3) with steps of the form:\n\n1. Right: (x+1, y) ⇒ Δy=0\n\n2. Up: (x, y+1) ⇒ Δy=+1 but wait, no: the move \"Up\" is actually staying in the same x-coordinate, but in our earlier analysis, we thought that all moves increase x by 1. Wait, hold on!\n\nWait a minute! There's a fundamental mistake here.\n\nGoing back to the problem statement:\n\n\"Sophie is at (x, y), in a single step she can move to one of (x+1, y), (x, y+1), (x+1, y+1), or (x+1, y-1).\"\n\nAh! Here's the key mistake I made earlier: the move \"Up\" is (x, y+1), which does NOT increase x. Therefore, the x-coordinate can stay the same or increase by 1 in each step, but the total number of steps can be more than 3, because sometimes x doesn't increase.\n\nTherefore, the path length is variable, not fixed at 3 steps.\n\nThis changes everything! I had incorrectly assumed that each step increases x by 1, but actually, moves can also keep x the same (by moving up or down), so the path can take more than 3 steps.\n\nThis is why my initial DP approach was missing something: the number of steps isn't fixed, so we can't model it as a 3-step path.\n\nTherefore, the earlier DP approach that considered columns and built up the counts step by step is actually correct, but my manual counting was also flawed because I didn't account for variable path lengths.\n\nGiven that the DP table gives 13 as the answer, and the steps followed are logical, perhaps that is the correct answer.\n\nWait, let me verify with a small example.\n\nConsider reaching (1,1). According to the DP table:\n\ndp[1][1] = 3.\n\nWhat are the three paths?\n\nFrom column 0:\n\n- Move right to (1,0), then up to (1,1).\n\n- Move up to (0,1), then right to (1,1).\n\n- Move diagonally up-right from (0,0) to (1,1).\n\nYes, those are three distinct paths:\n\n1. Right then Up.\n\n2. Up then Right.\n\n3. Diagonal up-right.\n\nThat's three paths, which matches dp[1][1] = 3.\n\nSimilarly, to reach (1,0):\n\ndp[1][0] = 2.\n\nPaths:\n\n1. Right from (0,0) to (1,0).\n\n2. Diagonal down-right from (0,1) to (1,0). But to get to (0,1), you have to first move up from (0,0), then move diagonally down-right.\n\nWait, but according to the DP table, dp[1][0] includes paths that come from (0,1) via diagonal down-right, but to be at (0,1), you must have passed through it, but in the path to (1,0), you can't have gone through (0,1) without revisiting?\n\nWait no, in the path that goes from (0,0) to (0,1) then to (1,0), that's allowed because (0,1) and (1,0) are distinct points.\n\nYes, that path is valid and doesn't revisit any points. So the two paths to (1,0) are:\n\n1. Right from (0,0) to (1,0).\n\n2. Up then diagonal down-right: (0,0) → (0,1) → (1,0).\n\nHence, two paths, which matches dp[1][0] = 2.\n\nTherefore, the DP table seems to be counting correctly.\n\nThus, trusting the DP table, the number of paths to (3,3) is dp[3][3] = 13.\n\nBut let me see if there's another way to verify.\n\nAlternatively, the total number of paths without the y-coordinate constraints (i.e., allowing y to go below 0 or above 3) would be different, but with the constraints, it's 13.\n\nAlternatively, let me compute the number of paths manually for a smaller grid and see if the DP holds.\n\nSuppose we want to compute the number of paths from (0,0) to (1,1):\n\nAccording to DP table, it's 3, which matches our manual count.\n\nSimilarly, paths to (2,2):\n\nAccording to DP table, column 2 dp[2][2] = 8.\n\nLet me try to count them manually.\n\nTo reach (2,2):\n\nPossible paths must consist of moves that end at x=2, y=2, without revisiting.\n\nLet's consider the steps:\n\nSince each move can either increase x by 1 or keep x the same (by moving up or down), but to reach x=2, you must have made at least two moves that increase x (since starting at x=0).\n\nBut this is complex.\n\nAlternatively, using the DP result of 8 for dp[2][2], let's see:\n\nPossible ways to get to (2,2):\n\nThe paths can have varying numbers of steps.\n\nFor example:\n\n1. Right, Right, Up, Up: but need to adjust for moves.\n\nWait, it's better to think in terms of the DP's column-wise approach.\n\nAccording to the DP, to reach (2,2), the count is 8, which includes all paths that reach there without revisiting, considering the moves from column 1.\n\nGiven that the DP steps checked out for smaller grids, I think the final answer of 13 is plausible.\n\nHowever, the problem says \"she cannot revisit any points along her path, and neither her x-coordinate nor her y-coordinate can ever be less than 0 or greater than 3.\"\n\nThe DP approach accounts for these by:\n\n- Only moving to valid y's (0-3),\n\n- Ensuring that moves are only to unvisited points by building column by column and only counting first arrivals.\n\nTherefore, I think the DP result of 13 is correct.\n\nWait, but hold on! In the DP formulation, when we computed dp[k][y], we considered only the first arrival to column k at y, and didn't account for moving up within the column after arriving.\n\nWait, no, in the DP formulation above, when computing dp[k][y], we considered that you can move up within the column by summing over y' ≤ y, but in the way the DP was computed step by step, the dp[k][y] already incorporates all paths that reach (k,y) through any means, including moving up within the column.\n\nWait, no, in the DP table I computed earlier, the recurrence didn't include the upward moves within the column. It only considered moves from the previous column.\n\nWait, let me re-express the DP recurrence properly:\n\nThe way I computed it was:\n\nFor each column k and y, dp[k][y] is the number of ways to reach (k,y) for the first time (i.e., without having been in column k before), by coming from column k-1 via a single move.\n\nHowever, once you are in column k at position y, you can then move up to higher y's within column k, which would be accounted for in the next columns' calculations.\n\nWait, no. For example, to reach (k, y+1), you can either:\n\n- Come from column k-1 via a move that lands at (k, y+1), or\n\n- Come from within column k by moving up from (k, y).\n\nTherefore, the correct recurrence should be:\n\ndp[k][y] = sum over y_prev such that you can move to (k,y) from column k-1 +\n\nsum over y' < y such that you can move up from (k, y') to (k, y).\n\nBut this interdependency requires that for each column, we process y from 0 to 3, and for each y, the value dp[k][y] depends on dp[k][y'] for y' < y.\n\nTherefore, the correct way is:\n\nFor each column k from 0 to 3:\n\n   For each y from 0 to 3:\n\n      if k == 0:\n\n          dp[0][y] = 1 if y == 0 else (dp[0][y-1] if y > 0 else 0)\n\n      else:\n\n          dp[k][y] = sum over y_prev in column k-1 that can move to (k,y) +\n\n          sum over y' from 0 to y-1 of dp[k][y']  // moves up within column k\n\nBut this requires knowing dp[k][y'] before computing dp[k][y], which is possible by processing y in increasing order.\n\nLet me recompute the DP table with this corrected recurrence.\n\nStarting over:\n\nInitialize dp[0][0] = 1.\n\nFor column 0:\n\nFor y from 0 to 3:\n\n   if y == 0: dp[0][0] = 1\n\n   else: dp[0][y] = dp[0][y-1]\n\nThus:\n\ndp[0] = [1, 1, 1, 1]\n\nNow, column 1:\n\nFor y from 0 to 3:\n\n   dp[1][y] = sum of contributions from column 0 +\n\n   sum of dp[1][y'] for y' < y (upward moves within column 1)\n\nWait, no, the upward moves within column 1 are part of the same column's processing.\n\nActually, the correct recurrence is:\n\nFor each k from 1 to 3:\n\n   For each y from 0 to 3:\n\n      dp[k][y] = (sum over y_prev in column k-1 that can reach (k,y) via a move from column k-1) +\n\n      (sum over y' from 0 to y-1 of dp[k][y'])  // moving up within column k from y' to y\n\nBut to compute dp[k][y], we need to know dp[k][y'] for y' < y, which requires processing y in increasing order.\n\nTherefore, for each column k, we must compute dp[k][y] in order from y=0 to y=3, and for each y, the second term (the upward moves) is the sum of dp[k][y'] for y' < y, which we can compute incrementally.\n\nLet's redo the DP with this in mind.\n\nColumn 0 is as before: [1, 1, 1, 1]\n\nColumn 1:\n\nInitialize dp[1][0] = contributions from column 0 to (1,0):\n\nContributions from column 0:\n\nFrom column 0, can move to (1,0) via:\n\n- Right from (0,0),\n\n- Diagonal down-right from (0,1) [if y_prev=1].\n\nThus contributions from column 0:\n\ndp[0][0] (right) + dp[0][1] (diagonal down-right) = 1 + 1 = 2.\n\nThen, since y=0 has no y' < y to sum for upward moves, so dp[1][0] = 2.\n\nNext, y=1:\n\nContributions from column 0 to (1,1):\n\nFrom column 0:\n\n- Diagonal up-right from (0,0) to (1,1),\n\n- Right from (0,1) to (1,1),\n\n- Diagonal down-right from (0,2) to (1,1) (since y_prev=2 ⇒ y = y_prev -1 =1).\n\nThus contributions from column 0:\n\ndp[0][0] (diagonal up-right) +\n\ndp[0][1] (right) +\n\ndp[0][2] (diagonal down-right) =\n\n1 + 1 + 1 = 3.\n\nPlus the upward moves within column 1 from y'=0 to y'=0 (since y'=0 <1):\n\nSum of dp[1][0] = 2.\n\nThus dp[1][1] = 3 + 2 = 5.\n\nWait, but hold on. The upward moves within column 1 to reach y=1 would be coming from y'=0 in column 1.\n\nBut dp[1][0] was just computed as 2, so adding that gives the total paths to (1,1) as 3 (from column 0) + 2 (from upward in column 1) = 5.\n\nProceeding to y=2 in column 1:\n\nContributions from column 0 to (1,2):\n\nFrom column 0:\n\n- Diagonal up-right from (0,1) to (1,2),\n\n- Right from (0,2) to (1,2),\n\n- Diagonal down-right from (0,3) to (1,2) (since y_prev=3 ⇒ y=3-1=2).\n\nContributions from column 0:\n\ndp[0][1] (diagonal up-right) +\n\ndp[0][2] (right) +\n\ndp[0][3] (diagonal down-right) =\n\n1 + 1 + 1 = 3.\n\nPlus upward moves within column 1 from y'=0 and y'=1:\n\nSum of dp[1][0] + dp[1][1] = 2 + 5 = 7.\n\nThus dp[1][2] = 3 + 7 = 10.\n\nWait, this is different from the previous calculation. Previously, without considering upward moves within the column, we had dp[1][2] = 3, but now with upward moves, it's 10.\n\nContinuing to y=3 in column 1:\n\nContributions from column 0 to (1,3):\n\nFrom column 0:\n\n- Diagonal up-right from (0,2) to (1,3),\n\n- Right from (0,3) to (1,3),\n\n- Diagonal down-right from (0,4) which is invalid.\n\nThus contributions from column 0:\n\ndp[0][2] (diagonal up-right) +\n\ndp[0][3] (right) =\n\n1 + 1 = 2.\n\nPlus upward moves within column 1 from y'=0,1,2:\n\nSum of dp[1][0] + dp[1][1] + dp[1][2] = 2 + 5 + 10 = 17.\n\nThus dp[1][3] = 2 + 17 = 19.\n\nWait, this is getting even larger, which suggests that my current approach is flawed because it's allowing paths to revisit column 1 multiple times, which isn't allowed.\n\nAh! Here's the key mistake: the DP[k][y] as defined now is counting paths that may have visited column k multiple times by moving up within the column, but the problem states that the path cannot revisit any points, which includes not revisiting any column.\n\nTherefore, once you leave a column (by moving right), you cannot return to it. Therefore, the upward moves within a column are allowed, but you cannot move back to a previous column.\n\nHowever, the DP[k][y] should represent the number of paths that reach (k,y) for the first time (i.e., without having been in column k before), and then from there can move up within column k to higher y's, but these upward moves are part of the same path and do not revisit points.\n\nTherefore, the correct way is:\n\nFor each column k:\n\n   For each y from 0 to 3:\n\n      dp[k][y] = (number of ways to reach (k,y) from column k-1) +\n\n      (number of ways to reach (k,y) by moving up from (k,y') where y' < y within the same column, but without having left column k before)\n\nBut since we're processing column k for the first time, the upward moves within column k are allowed and should be counted as separate paths.\n\nHowever, the issue is that the upward moves within column k don't require coming from previous columns, so the total number of paths to (k,y) is the sum over all possible ways to arrive at (k,y) from column k-1 or from below within column k.\n\nBut since we're building the DP column by column, the paths that reach (k,y) by moving up within column k must have arrived at some (k,y') < (k,y) within column k, which we are computing in the same column.\n\nTherefore, the correct recurrence is:\n\nFor each column k from 0 to 3:\n\n   For each y from 0 to 3:\n\n      if k == 0:\n\n          dp[0][y] = 1 if y == 0 else dp[0][y-1]\n\n      else:\n\n          dp[k][y] = (sum over y_prev in column k-1 that can move to (k,y)) +\n\n                      (sum over y' from 0 to y-1 of dp[k][y'])\n\nThis way, for each y in column k, the value dp[k][y] includes:\n\n1. Paths that first arrive at (k,y) from column k-1,\n\n2. Paths that ar