rrive at (3,3), we add it to the mask, so when current is (3,3), the mask already includes it, so the base case is f(15, mask) = 1 if current is (3,3) and mask includes it.\n\nWait, actually, the function f(x, y, mask) represents the number of paths from (x,y) to (3,3) with the visited set mask (which includes all points visited so far, including (x,y)).\n\nThus, the base case is f(3,3, mask) = 1 if (3,3) is in mask, else 0. Wait, but when you are at (3,3), you are done, so if you are at (3,3) and it's in the mask, then it's valid, so f(3,3, mask) = 1 if (3,3) is in mask, but since you arrived there, you must have just added it to the mask.\n\nActually, the recursion is:\n\nf(x, y, mask) = sum over all next moves (nx, ny) not in mask and within grid,\n\nof f(nx, ny, mask | (1 << nx_pos)),\n\nwhere nx_pos is the position index of (nx, ny).\n\nThe base case is when (x, y) == (3,3), then return 1 if (3,3) is in the mask (which it must be, since we just arrived there).\n\nWait, actually, when you are at (x,y), the mask already includes (x,y). So to move to a next position (nx, ny), you must ensure that (nx, ny) is not in the mask, then the new mask is mask | (1 << nx_pos).\n\nThus, the base case is:\n\nIf (x, y) == (3,3), then return 1 if (3,3) is in the mask (which it is, since we arrived there), so return 1.\n\nOtherwise, for each valid next move (nx, ny) not in the current mask,\n\nsum f(nx, ny, mask | (1 << nx_pos)).\n\nThis is the correct recursive formula.\n\nGiven that, we can compute this using memoization.\n\nSince there are 16 positions and 2^16 masks, it's 16 * 65536 = 1,048,576 states, which is too many by hand, but perhaps we can compute it step by step, starting from the end.\n\nAlternatively, since the grid is small and the target is (3,3), we can compute f(x,y) as the number of paths from (x,y) to (3,3) without revisiting, assuming that the path so far hasn't visited any points except those along the path, but this is still not helpful.\n\nAlternatively,