at doesn't cause issues.\n\nFor instance, in the path R,R,U,U,U,R,R (but that's 7 moves and may not end correctly).\n\nWait let's try:\n\nPath: R,R,U,U,L+U,R,R.\n\nTracking:\n\n1. R (1,0)\n\n2. R (2,0)\n\n3. U (2,1)\n\n4. U (2,2)\n\n5. L+U (1,3)\n\n6. R (2,3)\n\n7. R (3,3)\n\nThis is valid! So this is another path.\n\nSimilarly, another path could be R,U,R,R,U,U,L+U.\n\nTracking:\n\n1. R (1,0)\n\n2. U (1,1)\n\n3. R (2,1)\n\n4. R (3,1)\n\n5. U (3,2)\n\n6. U (3,3) → already there, so instead of that, after move 5 (3,2), do L+U to (2,3), then move 6 R to (3,3).\n\nWait that's the same as previous.\n\nAlternatively:\n\nR,U,U,R,R,U,L+U.\n\nTracking:\n\n1. R (1,0)\n\n2. U (1,1)\n\n3. U (1,2)\n\n4. R (2,2)\n\n5. R (3,2)\n\n6. U (3,3) → already there, so need to adjust.\n\nHmm.\n\nAlternatively:\n\nR,U,R,U,R,U,L+U.\n\nTracking:\n\n1. R (1,0)\n\n2. U (1,1)\n\n3. R (2,1)\n\n4. U (2,2)\n\n5. R (3,2)\n\n6. U (3,3) → but then we can't do L+U after.\n\nAlternatively, after move 5 (3,2), do L+U to (2,3), then move 6 R to (3,3). That's the same as before.\n\nIt seems that for each pure R/U path that goes all the way right first then up, we can create two different paths by inserting the diagonal move at two different points.\n\nWait, in the all-right-then-up path R,R,R,U,U,U, we can insert the diagonal move after the third R (position (3,0)), leading to the path R,R,R,U,U,L+U,R as above. Alternatively, we could insert it after the fifth move (which would be the fifth U), but that might not work.\n\nWait, in the all-right-then-up path, the moves are R,R,R,U,U,U. To make a 7-move path, we can replace the last U with a L+U followed by an R:\n\nSo instead of the last U (which would take from (3,2) to (3,3)), we do L+U to (2,3), then R to (3,3). This replaces the final U with two moves: L+U and R. Thus, this is one way.\n\nAlternatively, we could replace an earlier U with a L+U followed by something else.\n\nFor example, replace the third U with L+U and then an R:\n\nOriginal moves: R,R,R,U,U,U.\n\nReplace the third U (which is the fourth move) with L+U and then an R:\n\nSo moves become R,R,R,L+U,R,U,U.\n\nTracking:\n\n1. R (1,0)\n\n2. R (2,0)\n\n3. R (3,0)\n\n4. L+U (2,1)\n\n5. R (3,1)\n\n6. U (3,2)\n\n7. U (3,3)\n\nThis is valid! So this is another path.\n\nThus, in this path, we inserted the diagonal move after the third R, leading to a different valid path.\n\nTherefore, for the all-right-then-up path, there are multiple ways to insert the diagonal move.\n\nSpecifically, the diagonal move can be inserted after any of the R's, provided that it doesn't cause an out-of-bound.\n\nIn the all-right-then-up path:\n\nAfter the first R: (1,0). Inserting L+U there would go to (0,1), then continue:\n\nPath: R,L+U,R,R,U,U,U.\n\nTracking:\n\n1. R (1,0)\n\n2. L+U (0,1)\n\n3. R (1,1)\n\n4. R (2,1)\n\n5. U (2,2)\n\n6. U (2,3)\n\n7. U (2,4) → invalid.\n\nNo good.\n\nAfter the second R: (2,0). Inserting L+U there:\n\nPath: R,R,L+U,R,U,U,U.\n\nTracking:\n\n1. R (1,0)\n\n2. R (2,0)\n\n3. L+U (1,1)\n\n4. R (2,1)\n\n5. U (2,2)\n\n6. U (2,3)\n\n7. U (2,4) invalid.\n\nNo good.\n\nAfter the third R: (3,0). Inserting L+U there gives:\n\nR,R,R,L+U,R,U,U → as before, which is valid.\n\nAfter the fourth move (first U): (3,1). Inserting L+U there:\n\nPath: R,R,R,U,L+U,R,U.\n\nTracking:\n\n1. R (1,0)\n\n2. R (2,0)\n\n3. R (3,0)\n\n4. U (3,1)\n\n5. L+U (2,2)\n\n6. R (3,2)\n\n7. U (3,3)\n\nValid!\n\nSo that's another valid path.\n\nSimilarly, after the fifth move (second U): (3,2). Inserting L+U there:\n\nPath: R,R,R,U,U,L+U,R.\n\nTracking:\n\n1. R (1,0)\n\n2. R (2,0)\n\n3. R (3,0)\n\n4. U (3,1)\n\n5. U (3,2)\n\n6. L+U (2,3)\n\n7. R (3,3)\n\nValid.\n\nAfter the sixth move (third U): (3,3). Can't insert after that.\n\nThus, in the all-right-then-up path, inserting the L+U move after the third R, fourth move (first U), fifth move (second U), or after the third R (position 3) gives valid paths.\n\nWait, inserting after the fourth move (first U) gives a valid path.\n\nWait, let's