oving left-up from (1,1) would be to (0,2). Then from (0,2), can you move right-up? Wait, moving right-up isn't a move; the moves are only right, up, left-up, or right-down. So from (0,2), moving right would be to (1,2), moving up would be to (0,3), moving left-up would be to (-1,3), which is invalid, and moving right-down would be to (1,1), but (1,1) has already been visited. So from (0,2), possible moves are right to (1,2) or up to (0,3). Suppose we go to (1,2), then from there, we might have to go somewhere, but this could take more steps. Let's see:\n\nStarting over:\n\nPath 1 (minimal 6 steps):\n\n(0,0) → (1,0) → (2,0) → (3,0) → (3,1) → (3,2) → (3,3). That's 6 steps.\n\nAnother path:\n\n(0,0) → (0,1) → (0,2) → (0,3) → (1,3) → (2,3) → (3,3). Also 6 steps.\n\nAnother path:\n\n(0,0) → (1,0) → (1,1) → (2,1) → (3,1) → (3,2) → (3,3). 6 steps.\n\nSimilarly, (0,0) → (0,1) → (1,1) → (2,1) → (3,1) → (3,2) → (3,3). 6 steps.\n\nAlternatively, can we have a path with more than 6 steps?\n\nSuppose we take a detour. For example:\n\nStart at (0,0) → (1,0) → (2,0) → (3,0) → (3,1) → (2,1) → (3,2) → (3,3). Wait, that's 7 steps. Let's check if that path is valid.\n\nStarting at (0,0):\n\n1. (1,0)\n\n2. (2,0)\n\n3. (3,0)\n\n4. (3,1)\n\n5. (2,1) – here, moving left from (3,1) to (2,1). Is that allowed? From (3,1), moving left would be to (2,1), which is a valid move (since left-up isn't required; moving left is part of the \"left\" component of the left-up move? Wait no: hold on, the moves are only the four specified: right, up, left-up, right-down. So from (3,1), can you move left? That would be (2,1), but that move isn't one of the allowed moves. The allowed moves from (3,1) are:\n\n- Right: would be (4,1), which is invalid (x=4 > 3).\n\n- Up: (3,2).\n\n- Left-up: (2,2).\n\n- Right-down: (4,0), which is invalid (x=4 and y=0).\n\nTherefore, from (3,1), the only allowed moves are up to (3,2) or left-up to (2,2). So moving left to (2,1) is not an allowed move. Because the move to (2,1) would require moving left without moving up or down, but the allowed moves are only the four specified. So moving left by itself isn't a move; the left move is only combined with an up move in the left-up move. Similarly, moving down is only combined with a right move in the right-down move.\n\nTherefore, from (3,1), you can't move left to (2,1); you can only move up or left-up. Therefore, the path I suggested above is invalid because the step from (3,1) to (2,1) is not allowed. So that path is not possible.\n\nHmm, so perhaps moving left or down can only be done in combination with moving up or right, respectively. Therefore, any left move must be accompanied by an up move (in the left-up move), and any down move must be accompanied by a right move (in the right-down move). Therefore, you can't move left or down by themselves; they have to be part of those diagonal moves. Therefore, this might constrain the path in a way that prevents certain detours.\n\nTherefore, maybe all paths must have exactly 6 steps? Let me see if that is possible.\n\nWait, another attempt at a 7-step path:\n\nStart at (0,0) → (1,0) → (2,0) → (3,0) → (3,1) → (3,2) → (2,2) → (3,3). Let's check validity:\n\n1. (1,0) – valid.\n\n2. (2,0) – valid.\n\n3. (3,0) – valid.\n\n4. (3,1) – valid.\n\n5. (3,2) – valid.\n\n6. (2,2) – from (3,2), moving left-up to (2,3) is possible, but moving left-up would be (2,3). Wait, from (3,2), moving left-up would be (2,3), not (2,2). To get to (2,2), you would have to move left without moving up? But that isn't a permitted move. From (3,2), allowed moves:\n\n- Right: invalid (x=4).\n\n- Up: (3,3).\n\n- Left-up: (2,3).\n\n- Right-down: (4,1), invalid.\n\nSo from (3,2), you can go to (3,3) or (2,3). So to get to (2,2) from (3,2), you can't do it in one move. Therefore, that path is invalid.\n\nAlternatively, maybe another path:\n\n(0,0) → (1,0) → (1,1) → (2,1) → (3,1) → (3,2) → (2,2) → (3,3). Let's see:\n\n1. (1,0)\n\n2. (1,1) – moving up from (1,0) is allowed.\n\n3. (2,1) – moving right from (1,1) is allowed.\n\n4. (3,1) – moving right from (2,1) is allowed.\n\n5. (3,2) – moving up from (3,1).\n\n6. (2,2) – from (3,2), moving left-up to (2,3) or up to (3,3). To get to (2,2), you would have to move left-down? But that's not a permitted move. From (3,2), you can't move left-down; the permitted moves are up, left-up, or right-down (which is invalid here). So moving to (2,2) from (3,2) is not possible in one move. Therefore, that path is invalid.\n\nHmm, tricky. Maybe it's actually impossible to have a path longer than 6 steps? Let me see.\n\nSuppose we try to go from (0,0) to (1,0) to (1,1) to (0,2) to (1,3) to (2,3) to (3,3). Let's check:\n\n1. (1,0)\n\n2. (1,1) – moving up from (1,0)\n\n3. (0,2) – moving left-up from (1,1). Wait, moving left-up from (1,1) is (0,2). That's allowed, since x=1 ≥1 and y+1=2 ≤3.\n\n4. From (0,2), can we go to (1,3)? Let's see: moving right-up? Wait, moving right from (0,2) would be (1,2), moving up from (0,2) is (0,3), moving left-up would be (-1,3) invalid, moving right-down would be (1,1) which is already visited. So from (0,2), possible moves: right to (1,2) or up to (0,3). To get to (1,3), you need to go from (0,2) to (1,3), but that would require a move of right and up, which isn't one of the allowed moves. The allowed moves from (0,2) are:\n\n- Right: (1,2)\n\n- Up: (0,3)\n\n- Left-up: invalid (x=-1)\n\n- Right-down: (1,1) which is visited.\n\nSo you can't go from (0,2) to (1,3) in one move. Therefore, that path is invalid.\n\nAlternatively, from (0,2), go to (0,3):\n\nThen path would be: (0,2) → (0,3), then from (0,3), possible moves:\n\n- Right: (1,3)\n\n- Up: invalid (y=4)\n\n- Left-up: invalid (x=-1)\n\n- Right-down: (1,2)\n\nSo from (0,3), can go to (1,3) or (1,2). Suppose we go to (1,3):\n\nThen from (1,3), can we go to (2,3) then (3,3). Let's see:\n\nPath so far:\n\n1. (1,0)\n\n2. (1,1)\n\n3. (0,2)\n\n4. (0,3)\n\n5. (1,3)\n\n6. (2,3)\n\n7. (3,3)\n\nLet's check each step:\n\n1. (1,0) – okay.\n\n2. (1,1) – okay.\n\n3. (0,2) – moving left-up from (1,1) to (0,2): valid.\n\n4. (0,3) – moving up from (0,2) to (0,3): valid.\n\n5. (1,3) – moving right from (0,3) to (1,3): valid.\n\n6. (2,3) – moving right from (1,3) to (2,3): valid.\n\n7. (3,3) – moving right from (2,3) to (3,3): valid.\n\nSo this is a 7-step path! So that works. Therefore, there exists a path with 7 steps. Therefore, the number of steps can be more than 6. So minimal is 6, but can be longer.\n\nSo, in this case, the path took a detour going up to (0,3) then over to (1,3), etc. So that added an extra step.\n\nTherefore, we must consider paths of varying lengths from 6 up to... what's the maximum possible?\n\nWell, the grid has 16 points, but since we start at (0,0) and end at (3,3), and can't revisit any points, the maximum number of steps is 15, but obviously, that's impossible because we have to end at (3,3). The maximum path length would be the total number of points minus 1, so 15 steps, but again, we have to end at (3,3). But given the movement constraints, probably the maximum path length is much less.\n\nBut in any case, enumerating all possible paths manually is going to be difficult. Maybe we can approach this with recursion, breaking down the problem by the current position and the number of steps taken, but keeping track of visited points.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use dynamic programming where the state is the current position (x,y) and the set of visited points, but as mentioned, the visited set is too big. However, maybe we can represent the visited set more cleverly, or note that certain symmetries exist.\n\nAlternatively, since the grid is small, maybe we can traverse the grid step by step, keeping track of possible positions at each step and the number of ways to reach them without revisiting.\n\nLet me try this approach.\n\nLet me denote by f(n, x, y) as the number of paths of length n ending at (x, y) without revisiting any points. Then, the total number of paths to (3,3) would be the sum over all n ≥ minimal steps (6) of f(n, 3, 3). But to compute f(n, x, y), we need to know the number of ways to get to (x, y) at step n without revisiting, which depends on the previous positions. However, because the path cannot revisit any points, the number of ways to get to (x, y) at step n depends on all the possible previous positions that can move to (x, y) in one step, and the number of paths to those previous positions at step n-1 that don't include (x, y) or any other points in the path so far.\n\nThis seems recursive but complicated. Alternatively, perhaps we can model this as a graph and compute the number of paths from (0,0) to (3,3) using adjacency matrices with path counts, but avoiding revisits. However, adjacency matrices typically count all paths without considering revisits, so that won't work.\n\nAlternatively, since the grid is small, perhaps we can perform a depth-first search (DFS) traversal, keeping track of visited nodes, and count the number of paths that reach (3,3). Since we're doing this manually, we can try to organize it by the current position and the path taken so far, but it might take a while.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can partition the grid into regions and compute the number of paths in each region.\n\nAlternatively, let's try to proceed step by step, keeping track of possible positions at each step, and the number of ways to get there.\n\nLet me try this.\n\nWe can think of the problem as starting at (0,0) at step 0.\n\nAt step 1, Sophie can move to either (1,0) or (0,1). So two possibilities.\n\nAt step 2, from each of those positions, we can go to new positions, making sure not to revisit (0,0).\n\nLet me create a table where each entry is (step, x, y, count), but since we need to track visited points, which complicates things, perhaps we can instead, for each step, keep a dictionary or list of current positions and the number of paths to each, along with the set of visited points. But since we can't track the entire visited set, perhaps we can instead use memoization with the current position and the previous positions, but that might not be feasible manually.\n\nAlternatively, since the grid is small, maybe we can proceed step by step, manually tracking possible positions and the number of ways to get there without revisiting.\n\nLet me attempt this step by step, keeping track of possible positions at each step and the number of paths to those positions.\n\nLet me denote:\n\n- Let S(n) be the set of positions reachable in n steps without revisiting any points, along with the number of paths to each position.\n\nBut actually, to compute S(n), we need to know for each position (x,y) in S(n), the number of paths to (x,y) at step n, which depends on the number of paths to each of its predecessors at step n-1, provided those predecessors didn't visit (x,y) before.\n\nBut since we can't track the entire path history, this is tricky. However, perhaps for small n, we can do it manually.\n\nLet me start:\n\nStep 0:\n\nOnly position: (0,0). Number of paths: 1.\n\nStep 1:\n\nFrom (0,0), can go to (1,0) or (0,1).\n\nSo positions at step 1: (1,0) and (0,1), each with 1 path.\n\nSo:\n\nS(1) = { (1,0): 1, (0,1): 1 }\n\nStep 2:\n\nFrom each position in S(1), we can move to new positions, provided they haven't been visited yet.\n\nFirst, from (1,0):\n\nPossible moves from (1,0):\n\n1. Right: (2,0)\n\n2. Up: (1,1)\n\n3. Left-up: (0,1) – but (0,1) was already visited at step 1, so cannot go there.\n\n4. Right-down: (2,-1) – invalid (y=-1).\n\nTherefore, from (1,0), can go to (2,0) or (1,1).\n\nSimilarly, from (0,1):\n\nPossible moves from (0,1):\n\n1. Right: (1,1)\n\n2. Up: (0,2)\n\n3. Left-up: (-1,2) – invalid (x=-1)\n\n4. Right-down: (1,0) – but (1,0) was visited at step 1, so cannot go there.\n\nTherefore, from (0,1), can go to (1,1) or (0,2).\n\nTherefore, the positions at step 2 are:\n\nFrom (1,0):\n\n- (2,0): 1 path (since only one path to (1,0) times the one move)\n\n- (1,1): 1 path\n\nFrom (0,1):\n\n- (1,1): 1 path\n\n- (0,2): 1 path\n\nTherefore, aggregating:\n\n(2,0): 1\n\n(1,1): 1 (from (1,0)) + 1 (from (0,1)) = 2\n\n(0,2): 1\n\nSo S(2) = { (2,0): 1, (1,1): 2, (0,2): 1 }\n\nStep 3:\n\nNow, from each position in S(2), we can move to new positions, again avoiding revisits.\n\nFirst, starting with (2,0):\n\nFrom (2,0):\n\nPossible moves:\n\n1. Right: (3,0)\n\n2. Up: (2,1)\n\n3. Left-up: (1,1) – but (1,1) was visited at step 2, so cannot go there.\n\n4. Right-down: (3,-1) – invalid.\n\nTherefore, from (2,0), can go to (3,0) or (2,1).\n\nFrom (1,1):\n\nFrom (1,1), possible moves:\n\n1. Right: (2,1)\n\n2. Up: (1,2)\n\n3. Left-up: (0,2) – but (0,2) was visited at step 2, so cannot go there.\n\n4. Right-down: (2,0) – (2,0) was visited at step 2, so cannot go there.\n\nTherefore, from (1,1), can go to (2,1) or (1,2).\n\nFrom (0,2):\n\nFrom (0,2):\n\nPossible moves:\n\n1. Right: (1,2)\n\n2. Up: (0,3)\n\n3. Left-up: (-1,3) – invalid\n\n4. Right-down: (1,1) – (1,1) was visited at step 2, so cannot go there.\n\nTherefore, from (0,2), can go to (1,2) or (0,3).\n\nNow, let's compute the contributions:\n\nStarting with (2,0):\n\n- To (3,0): 1 path (from (2,0)'s 1 path)\n\n- To (2,1): 1 path\n\nFrom (1,1):\n\n- To (2,1): 2 paths (since (1,1) has 2 paths)\n\n- To (1,2): 2 paths\n\nFrom (0,2):\n\n- To (1,2): 1 path (from (0,2)'s 1 path)\n\n- To (0,3): 1 path\n\nTherefore, aggregating:\n\n(3,0): 1\n\n(2,1): 1 (from (2,0)) + 2 (from (1,1)) = 3\n\n(1,2): 2 (from (1,1)) + 1 (from (0,2)) = 3\n\n(0,3): 1\n\nSo S(3) = { (3,0):1, (2,1):3, (1,2):3, (0,3):1 }\n\nStep 4:\n\nNow, proceeding to step 4. For each position in S(3), we'll compute their next moves.\n\nFirst, starting with (3,0):\n\nFrom (3,0):\n\nPossible moves:\n\n1. Right: invalid (x=4)\n\n2. Up: (3,1)\n\n3. Left-up: (2,1) – but (2,1) was visited at step 3, so cannot go there.\n\n4. Right-down: (4,-1) – invalid.\n\nTherefore, from (3,0), can only go to (3,1).\n\nNext, (2,1):\n\nFrom (2,1):\n\nPossible moves:\n\n1. Right: (3,1)\n\n2. Up: (2,2)\n\n3. Left-up: (1,2) – (1,2) was visited at step 3, so cannot go there.\n\n4. Right-down: (3,0) – (3,0) was visited at step 3, so cannot go there.\n\nTherefore, from (2,1), can go to (3,1) or (2,2).\n\nNext, (1,2):\n\nFrom (1,2):\n\nPossible moves:\n\n1. Right: (2,2)\n\n2. Up: (1,3)\n\n3. Left-up: (0,3) – (0,3) was visited at step 3, so cannot go there.\n\n4. Right-down: (2,1) – (2,1) was visited at step 3, so cannot go there.\n\nTherefore, from (1,2), can go to (2,2) or (1,3).\n\nNext, (0,3):\n\nFrom (0,3):\n\nPossible moves:\n\n1. Right: (1,3)\n\n2. Up: invalid (y=4)\n\n3. Left-up: invalid (x=-1)\n\n4. Right-down: (1,2) – (1,2) was visited at step 3, so cannot go there.\n\nTherefore, from (0,3), can go to (1,3).\n\nNow, let's compute contributions:\n\nStarting with (3,0):\n\n- To (3,1): 1 path\n\nFrom (2,1):\n\n- To (3,1): 3 paths (since (2,1) has 3 paths)\n\n- To (2,2): 3 paths\n\nFrom (1,2):\n\n- To (2,2): 3 paths (from (1,2)'s 3 paths)\n\n- To (1,3): 3 paths\n\nFrom (0,3):\n\n- To (1,3): 1 path\n\nTherefore, aggregating:\n\n(3,1): 1 (from (3,0)) + 3 (from (2,1)) = 4\n\n(2,2): 3 (from (2,1)) + 3 (from (1,2)) = 6\n\n(1,3): 3 (from (1,2)) + 1 (from (0,3)) = 4\n\nSo S(4) = { (3,1):4, (2,2):6, (1,3):4 }\n\nStep 5:\n\nProceeding to step 5, from each position in S(4):\n\nFirst, (3,1):\n\nFrom (3,1):\n\nPossible moves:\n\n1. Right: invalid (x=4)\n\n2. Up: (3,2)\n\n3. Left-up: (2,2) – (2,2) was visited at step 4, so cannot go there.\n\n4. Right-down: (4,0) – invalid.\n\nTherefore, from (3,1), can only go to (3,2).\n\nNext, (2,2):\n\nFrom (2,2):\n\nPossible moves:\n\n1. Right: (3,2)\n\n2. Up: (2,3)\n\n3. Left-up: (1,3) – (1,3) was visited at step 4, so cannot go there.\n\n4. Right-down: (3,1) – (3,1) was visited at step 4, so cannot go there.\n\nTherefore, from (2,2), can go to (3,2) or (2,3).\n\nNext, (1,3):\n\nFrom (1,3):\n\nPossible moves:\n\n1. Right: (2,3)\n\n2. Up: invalid (y=4)\n\n3. Left-up: (0,4) – invalid (y=4)\n\n4. Right-down: (2,2) – (2,2) was visited at step 4, so cannot go there.\n\nTherefore, from (1,3), can go to (2,3).\n\nNow, contributions:\n\nStarting with (3,1):\n\n- To (3,2): 4 paths\n\nFrom (2,2):\n\n- To (3,2): 6 paths\n\n- To (2,3): 6 paths\n\nFrom (1,3):\n\n- To (2,3): 4 paths\n\nTherefore, aggregating:\n\n(3,2): 4 (from (3,1)) + 6 (from (2,2)) = 10\n\n(2,3): 6 (from (2,2)) + 4 (from (1,3)) = 10\n\nThus, S(5) = { (3,2):10, (2,3):10 }\n\nStep 6:\n\nNow, step 6, starting from S(5) positions:\n\nFirst, (3,2):\n\nFrom (3,2):\n\nPossible moves:\n\n1. Right: invalid (x=4)\n\n2. Up: (3,3) – which is the destination!\n\n3. Left-up: (2,3) – (2,3) was visited at step 5, so cannot go there.\n\n4. Right-down: (4,1) – invalid.\n\nTherefore, from (3,2), can only go to (3,3).\n\nNext, (2,3):\n\nFrom (2,3):\n\nPossible moves:\n\n1. Right: (3,3)\n\n2. Up: invalid (y=4)\n\n3. Left-up: (1,4) – invalid (y=4)\n\n4. Right-down: (3,2) – (3,2) was visited at step 5, so cannot go there.\n\nTherefore, from (2,3), can only go to (3,3).\n\nTherefore, contributions:\n\nFrom (3,2): 10 paths to (3,3)\n\nFrom (2,3): 10 paths to (3,3)\n\nThus, total paths at step 6 to (3,3): 10 + 10 = 20.\n\nBut wait, is that all? Are there other paths that reach (3,3) in fewer than 6 steps? Earlier we saw that the minimal number of steps is 6, so step 6 is the first time we can reach (3,3). Therefore, the total number of paths is 20?\n\nBut hold on, let me verify this because when I was going through the steps, I might have made a miscalculation.\n\nLet me recap:\n\nAt step 6, the only way to reach (3,3) is from (3,2) or (2,3). The counts were:\n\nFrom (3,2): 10 paths,\n\nFrom (2,3): 10 paths,\n\nTotal 20 paths.\n\nBut let me check the earlier steps for possible errors.\n\nStarting from step 0 to step 1: straightforward, 2 paths.\n\nStep 2: positions (2,0):1, (1,1):2, (0,2):1. That seems okay.\n\nStep 3:\n\nFrom (2,0): can go to (3,0) and (2,1). So (3,0):1, (2,1):1\n\nFrom (1,1): can go to (2,1) and (1,