ch (3,3)?\n\nA move to (3,3) must come from:\n\n1. Moving right from (2,3): since moving right from (2,3) gives (3,3).\n\n2. Moving up from (3,2): moving up from (3,2) gives (3,3).\n\n3. Moving left-up from (4,4): which is invalid.\n\n4. Moving right-down from (2,2): moving right-down from (2,2) would be (3,1), which is not (3,3).\n\nWait, actually:\n\nWait, the move to (3,3) must be one of the four moves from some previous position (x,y):\n\n- If coming from the right: then previous position was (2,3), moving right to (3,3).\n\n- If coming from above: previous position was (3,2), moving up to (3,3).\n\n- If coming from left-up: previous position would have to be (4,4) moving left-up to (3,3), but (4,4) is invalid.\n\n- If coming from right-down: previous position would have to be (2,4) moving right-down to (3,3), but (2,4) is invalid.\n\nTherefore, only two possible predecessors: (2,3) and (3,2). Therefore, yes, only those two positions can reach (3,3) in one step, so our calculation is correct.\n\nTherefore, the total number is 20.\n\nBut wait, let me see if my step-by-step counts are accurate, because sometimes when doing manual counting, it's easy to make an off-by-one error or miscount the contributions.\n\nLet me retrace the counts for each step:\n\nStep 1:\n\n(1,0):1, (0,1):1 → total 2.\n\nStep 2:\n\nFrom (1,0):\n\n- (2,0):1\n\n- (1,1):1\n\nFrom (0,1):\n\n- (1,1):1\n\n- (0,2):1\n\nThus:\n\n(2,0):1\n\n(1,1):1+1=2\n\n(0,2):1\n\nTotal positions: 3, counts sum to 4? Wait, 1+2+1=4, but step 2 should have total paths equal to step1 total (2) multiplied by average branching factor, but since some moves are blocked, it's not straightforward. Anyway, the counts per position are as above.\n\nStep3:\n\nFrom (2,0):\n\n- (3,0):1\n\n- (2,1):1\n\nFrom (1,1):\n\n- (2,1):2\n\n- (1,2):2\n\nFrom (0,2):\n\n- (1,2):1\n\n- (0,3):1\n\nThus:\n\n(3,0):1\n\n(2,1):1+2=3\n\n(1,2):2+1=3\n\n(0,3):1\n\nTotal paths: 1+3+3+1=8. Since step2 had total 4 paths, step3 has 8, which is double, whi