ep5, so it's not present at step5.\n\nTherefore, to have a path that reaches (2,3) at step6, it must have been at some position at step5 that can move to (2,3) in one step. The only positions that can move to (2,3) are:\n\n- From (1,3): moving right to (2,3),\n\n- From (3,2): moving left-down? Wait, no, according to the moves:\n\nTo get to (2,3) from another position:\n\n- From (1,3): moving right (which is (2,3)),\n\n- From (3,2): moving left-down? But the moves from (3,2) are only to (3,3) or other invalid moves.\n\nWait, from (3,2), you can only move to (3,3).\n\nFrom (2,4): invalid.\n\nFrom (2,2): moving up to (2,3).\n\nWait, from (2,2), moving up would be (2,3). But (2,2) is in S(4), but at step5, (2,2) has already moved to either (3,2) or (2,3). Therefore, in S(5), (2,2) is no longer present.\n\nTherefore, the only way to reach (2,3) at step6 is from:\n\n- (1,3) at step5 moving right to (2,3),\n\nBut in S(5), there is no (1,3). The only positions in S(5) are (3,2) and (2,3).\n\nTherefore, you cannot reach (2,3) at step6 from any position in S(5), because S(5) doesn't include (1,3).\n\nTherefore, the path I described earlier is impossible? But that contradicts my manual path construction.\n\nWait, let me retrace that path step-by-step, and see where it goes wrong in terms of the DP.\n\nPath:\n\n1. (0,0) → (1,0) : step1\n\n2. (1,0) → (1,1) : step2\n\n3. (1,1) → (0,2) : step3\n\n4. (0,2) → (0,3) : step4\n\n5. (0,3) → (1,3) : step5\n\n6. (1,3) → (2,3) : step6\n\n7. (2,3) → (3,3) : step7\n\nWait a second, in step5, the position is (0,3) moving to (1,3). But according to the DP tables, at step4, the position (0,3) had 1 path. From (0,3) at step4, you can move to (1,3) at step5. Therefore, in step5, (1,3) should have 1 path from (0,3), but according to S(5), (1,3) is not present—it was moved to (2,3) or other places.\n\nWait, no: in step4, (0,3) has 1 path. From (0,3) at step4, you can move to (1,3) at step5. Therefore, (1,3) should have at least 1 path from (0,3). But in S(5), we have:\n\nFrom step4 positions:\n\n- (3,1):4 paths can go to (3,2)\n\n- (2,2):6 paths can go to (3,2) or (2,3)\n\n- (1,3):4 paths can go to (2,3)\n\nThus, the contribution to (1,3) at step5 is zero, because none of the step4 positions can move to (1,3). The only way to reach (1,3) at step5 is from a position that can move to (1,3) in one step. Which positions can move to (1,3)?\n\nLooking at the moves:\n\nTo reach (1,3):\n\n- From (0,3): moving right to (1,3).\n\n- From (1,2): moving up to (1,3).\n\n- From (2,4): invalid.\n\n- From (0,2): moving right-up? Not a valid move.\n\nWait, the moves are only the four specified. To get to (1,3):\n\n- From (0,3): moving right to (1,3).\n\n- From (1,2): moving up to (1,3).\n\n- From (2,3): moving left-down? But moving left-down isn't a move; the moves are only the four listed.\n\nWait, the moves from (2,3):\n\nFrom (2,3), you can move to (3,3) (right), or other moves? Let's see:\n\nFrom (2,3):\n\n- Right: (3,3)\n\n- Up: invalid\n\n- Left-up: (1,4) invalid\n\n- Right-down: (3,2)\n\nSo from (2,3), you can move to (3,3) or (3,2). Therefore, you cannot move to (1,3) from (2,3).\n\nFrom (1,2):\n\nFrom (1,2), you can move to (2,2) or (1,3). Ah! So from (1,2) at step4, you can move to (1,3) at step5.\n\nBut in step4, the position (1,2) had 3 paths. From (1,2), moving up to (1,3) is allowed. Therefore, those 3 paths from (1,2) at step4 can go to (1,3) at step5.\n\nHowever, in my earlier calculation for step5, I said that from (1,2) you can go to (2,2) or (1,3). Therefore, the contribution to (1,3) at step5 should be 3 paths from (1,2). But in my prior computation for step5, I said:\n\nFrom (1,2):\n\n- To (2,2):3 paths\n\n- To (1,3):3 paths\n\nTherefore, the total for (1,3) at step5 should be 3, not zero.\n\nWait a minute, this is the mistake! In my previous calculation for step5, I incorrectly stated that from (1,2) you can go to (2,2) or (1,3), so contributing 3 to (2,2) and 3 to (1,3). But in my earlier summary for S(5), I said:\n\n\"From (1,2):\n\n- To (2,2):3 paths\n\n- To (1,3):3 paths\"\n\nTherefore, (1,3) should have 3 paths from (1,2). But in my prior summary of S(5), I wrote:\n\n\"Thus:\n\n(3,2):4 +6=10\n\n(2,3):6 +4=10\"\n\nWait, where did the 4 and 6 come from?\n\nWait, let me recompute step5 properly:\n\nFrom step4 positions:\n\n1. From (3,1):4 paths can go to (3,2).\n\n2. From (2,2):6 paths can go to (3,2) or (2,3). So 6 to (3,2), 6 to (2,3).\n\n3. From (1,3):4 paths can go to (2,3). So 4 to (2,3).\n\nTherefore, contributions:\n\nTo (3,2):4 (from (3,1)) +6 (from (2,2)) = 10\n\nTo (2,3):6 (from (2,2)) +4 (from (1,3)) = 10\n\nBut what about contributions to (1,3)? From (1,2):\n\nWait, hold on, step4 positions are (3,1), (2,2), and (1,3). The position (1,2) is not in S(4). Wait, in step4, the positions are:\n\nS(4) = { (3,1):4, (2,2):6, (1,3):4 }\n\nTherefore, the position (1,2) is not present in S(4). Therefore, there are no paths in S(4) that can move to (1,3) at step5 except from (1,3) itself, which can't move to (1,3).\n\nWait, this is the critical error.\n\nIn step4, the position (1,2) is not present; it was in step3.\n\nAt step3, S(3) includes (1,2):3 paths.\n\nFrom step3's (1,2), which had 3 paths, moving to (2,2) or (1,3) at step4.\n\nTherefore, in step4, the position (1,3) receives 3 paths from (1,2) at step3.\n\nTherefore, in step4, the position (1,3) has 3 paths from (1,2), plus any other contributions?\n\nWait no: in step4, the positions are computed based on step3's positions.\n\nLet me recompute step4 properly:\n\nStep4:\n\nFrom S(3) positions:\n\nS(3) = { (3,0):1, (2,1):3, (1,2):3, (0,3):1 }\n\nFrom each of these:\n\n1. From (3,0): can go to (3,1)\n\n2. From (2,1): can go to (3,1) or (2,2)\n\n3. From (1,2): can go to (2,2) or (1,3)\n\n4. From (0,3): can go to (1,3)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- To (3,1):1 (from (3,0)) +3 (from (2,1)) =4\n\n- To (2,2):3 (from (2,1)) +3 (from (1,2)) =6\n\n- To (1,3):3 (from (1,2)) +1 (from (0,3)) =4\n\nTherefore, S(4) = { (3,1):4, (2,2):6, (1,3):4 }\n\nTherefore, the position (1,3) at step4 has 4 paths, which includes the 3 from (1,2) at step3 and 1 from (0,3) at step3.\n\nTherefore, in step4, (1,3) is present with 4 paths.\n\nThen, in step5, we must consider moves from S(4) positions:\n\nS(4) has (3,1):4, (2,2):6, (1,3):4.\n\nFrom (3,1):\n\n- To (3,2):4 paths\n\nFrom (2,2):\n\n- To (3,2):6 paths\n\n- To (2,3):6 paths\n\nFrom (1,3):\n\n- To (2,3):4 paths (moving right from (1,3) to (2,3))\n\nTherefore, contributions to step5:\n\n(3,2):4 +6 =10\n\n(2,3):6 +4 =10\n\nAdditionally, can any of these moves lead to (1,3) at step5?\n\nNo, because the only way to get to (1,3) at step5 would be from a position that can move to (1,3) in one step, but the only such positions are:\n\n- From (0,3): moving right to (1,3). But (0,3) is not in S(4); it was in S(3) but moved to (1,3) at step4.\n\n- From (1,2): moving up to (1,3). But (1,2) is not in S(4); it was in S(3) but moved to (2,2) or (1,3) at step4.\n\nTherefore, there are no positions in S(4) that can move to (1,3) at step5. Therefore, (1,3) is not in S(5).\n\nTherefore, the path that goes through (1,3) at step5 must have been at (1,3) at step4, then moved to (2,3) at step5. Therefore, such a path would have reached (2,3) at step5, not step6.\n\nTherefore, the path I described earlier that reaches (2,3) at step6 is actually impossible because to reach (2,3) at step6, you would need to be at a position that can move to (2,3) at step6, but by step5, all possible predecessors of (2,3) have already been accounted for and moved on.\n\nTherefore, my initial example path was flawed because it assumed you could stay at (1,3) at step5 and then move to (2,3) at step6, but in reality, once you are at (1,3) at step4, you have to move to either (2,2) or (1,3)'s other moves at step5. Wait, from (1,3) at step4, you can move to (2,3) at step5. Therefore, you can't stay at (1,3); you have to move from it.\n\nTherefore, the path I described is actually:\n\nStep1: (1,0)\n\nStep2: (1,1)\n\nStep3: (0,2)\n\nStep4: (0,3)\n\nStep5: (1,3)\n\nStep6: (2,3)\n\nStep7: (3,3)\n\nBut according to the DP, from (1,3) at step4, you can move to (2,3) at step5, so that would be:\n\nStep5: (2,3)\n\nThen step6: (3,3)\n\nTherefore, that path would actually be 6 steps:\n\n(0,0) → (1,0) → (1,1) → (0,2) → (0,3) → (1,3) → (2,3) → (3,3) is 7 steps, but according to the moves, from (1,3) at step4, you can move to (2,3) at step5, so the path would be:\n\nStep1: (1,0)\n\nStep2: (1,1)\n\nStep3: (0,2)\n\nStep4: (0,3)\n\nStep5: (1,3) → Wait no, from (0,3) at step4, you move to (1,3) at step5.\n\nThen from (1,3) at step5, you can move to (2,3) at step6.\n\nThen from (2,3) at step6, move to (3,3) at step7.\n\nBut according to the DP, at step5, (1,3) is not present; it was moved from at step4.\n\nWait, no: in step4, (1,3) has 4 paths. From (1,3) at step4, you can move to (2,3) at step5. Therefore, those 4 paths from (1,3) at step4 contribute to (2,3) at step5. Therefore, the path that goes through (1,3) at step5 doesn't exist because (1,3) is only present at step4.\n\nTherefore, the path must go through (1,3) at step4, then to (2,3) at step5, then to (3,3) at step6. So that's 6 steps total:\n\nStep1: (1,0)\n\nStep2: (1,1)\n\nStep3: (0,2)\n\nStep4: (0,3)\n\nStep5: (1,3)\n\nStep6: (2,3)\n\nStep7: (3,3)\n\nWait, that's still 7 steps. The confusion arises from whether step numbering starts at 0 or 1. In our DP, step0 is the starting point, so reaching (3,3) at step6 is 6 moves, i.e., 7 positions. Therefore, the path that goes through (1,3) at step4 (which is the fifth position) then to (2,3) at step5 (sixth position), then to (3,3) at step6 (seventh position) is indeed 6 steps (moves), resulting in 7 positions, but the step count in the DP is the number of moves. So step6 corresponds to 6 moves, ending at (3,3).\n\nHowever, according to the DP calculation, such a path would have been counted in the step5 contribution to (2,3), then step6 contribution to (3,3). Specifically:\n\nThe path's position at step4 is (0,3) with 1 path (from S(3)'s (0,3):1). Then from (0,3) at step4, it moves to (1,3) at step5, which is accounted for in the 4 paths to (1,3) at step4 moving to (2,3) or other places? Wait no:\n\nWait, from (0,3) at step4 (which has 1 path), you can move to (1,3) at step5. Therefore, that contributes 1 path to (1,3) at step5. But according to the DP computation for step5, (1,3) is not present because all paths from (1,3) at step4 moved to (2,3) at step5. Wait, no:\n\nHold on, let's track that specific path:\n\nStarting from (0,0):\n\nStep0: (0,0)\n\nStep1: (1,0)\n\nStep2: (1,1)\n\nStep3: (0,2)\n\nStep4: (0,3)\n\nStep5: (1,3)\n\nStep6: (2,3)\n\nStep7: (3,3)\n\nThis path's steps are:\n\nAfter 0 moves: (0,0)\n\nAfter 1 move: (1,0)\n\nAfter 2 moves: (1,1)\n\nAfter 3 moves: (0,2)\n\nAfter 4 moves: (0,3)\n\nAfter 5 moves: (1,3)\n\nAfter 6 moves: (2,3)\n\nAfter 7 moves: (3,3)\n\nTherefore, in terms of our DP steps (where step n is after n moves), this path reaches (3,3) at step7 (7 moves). But according to the DP, all paths that reach (2,3) must do so by step5 or earlier, and then reach (3,3) by step6 or earlier.\n\nHowever, in this path, (2,3) is reached at step6, which is after step5. Therefore, this path should contribute to the count of paths reaching (2,3) at step6, which in turn contribute to (3,3) at step7.\n\nBut according to our prior DP computation, we stopped at step6, and didn't account for step7.\n\nTherefore, the error was that we stopped the DP at step6 and assumed no further paths exist, but in reality, there are paths that reach (2,3) at step6 and then proceed to (3,3) at step7.\n\nTo correctly compute all paths, we must continue the DP beyond step6.\n\nLet me attempt to compute S(6) and S(7) properly.\n\nRecalling that S(n) is the set of positions reachable in n moves without having visited (3,3) before.\n\nWe had up to step5:\n\nS(5) = { (3,2):10, (2,3):10 }\n\nStep6:\n\nFrom S(5):\n\nFrom (3,2):\n\nCan only go to (3,3