tices C and I are odd, plus D and F are still odd (D connected to A, E, G → degree 3; F connected to C, E, I → degree 3). So now we have four odd vertices again.\n\nAlternatively, remove edges B-E and H-E and F-I and D-G. Wait, this is getting too convoluted.\n\nWait, perhaps in the 3×3 grid, it's indeed impossible to remove only two edges and keep the graph connected, meaning that the formula overestimates. However, according to the corrected formula for the 11×11 grid (which is analogous), maybe in larger grids the connectivity is better preserved.\n\nAlternatively, maybe in the 3×3 grid, the minimal number of edges to remove is actually 2 but requires a different pairing.\n\nWait, what if we remove edges B-F and D-H? Wait, those edges don't exist. B is connected to A, C, E. F is connected to C, E, I. D is connected to A, E, G. H is connected to G, I, E.\n\nAlternatively, remove edges B-E and F-H.\n\nThen:\n\n- B: degree 2 (A,C).\n\n- F: degree 2 (C,I).\n\n- H: degree 2 (G,I).\n\n- E: degree 3 (D, F, H → but F and H are removed? No, removing B-E and F-H:\n\n- Removing B-E: B loses connection to E.\n\n- Removing F-H: F loses connection to H.\n\nThus:\n\n- E is connected to D, F, H → still degree 3.\n\n- F is connected to C, E, I → degree 3.\n\n- H is connected to G, I, E → degree 3.\n\n- B is degree 2.\n\n- D is still connected to A, E, G → degree 3.\n\nSo now odd degrees at D, E, F, H. Worse.\n\nHmm.\n\nAlternatively, remove edges B-C and D-G.\n\n- B loses connection to C: degree 2 (A, E).\n\n- C loses connection to B: degree 2 (F).\n\n- D loses connection to G: degree 2 (A, E).\n\n- G loses connection to D: degree 2 (H).\n\nNow, check degrees:\n\n- B: 2.\n\n- C: 2 (connected to F).\n\n- D: 2.\n\n- G: 2.\n\n- The other vertices:\n\n- A: connected to B and D → degree 2.\n\n- E: connected to B, D, F, H → degree 4.\n\n- F: connected to C, E, I → degree 3.\n\n- H: connected to G, I, E → degree 3.\n\n- I: connected to F, H → degree 2.\n\nSo now odd degrees at F and H.\