ernatively, maybe we can construct such a set of 18 edges whose removal leaves the graph connected and with all even degrees.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the minimal number of edges to remove is indeed 18, and the graph remains connected, so 202 is achievable.\n\nBut maybe there is a different way of thinking about this problem?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps instead of trying to remove edges to make an Eulerian circuit, we can think directly about the trail.\n\nIn any graph, the longest trail (closed or open) is at most the total number of edges, but when there are odd-degree vertices, the longest trail can be at most total edges minus the number of odd-degree vertices over 2. Wait, actually, let me recall the theorem:\n\nIn any graph, the length of the longest trail is equal to the total number of edges minus the number of vertices with odd degree divided by 2. Wait, no, that doesn't sound right.\n\nWait, actually, in an open trail, the maximum length is equal to the total number of edges minus the number of odd-degree vertices divided by 2? Hmm, not sure.\n\nWait, let me recall: In any graph, the maximum length of an open trail (i.e., a trail that doesn't have to be closed) is equal to the total number of edges minus the number of \"defects\" required to make the graph Eulerian? Hmm, perhaps.\n\nWait, here's a better approach: According to a theorem by Euler, the number of edges in the longest trail in a graph is equal to the total number of edges minus the minimum number of edges that need to be removed to make the graph Eulerian. Since an Eulerian trail requires all vertices to have even degree, so to make the graph Eulerian, we need to remove edges to fix the odd-degree vertices. Since each edge removal can fix the parity of two vertices, as discussed earlier.\n\nTherefore, the minimal number of edges to remove is equal to the number of odd-degree vertices divided by 2, which in this case is 36 / 2 = 18. Therefore, the maximum trail length is total edges minus 18, so