f its edges removed. However, each edge is shared between two vertices, so removing an edge between two odd-degree vertices will decrease both of their degrees by 1, turning them from odd to even. But if we remove an edge between an odd-degree vertex and an even-degree vertex, then only the odd-degree vertex's degree is decreased by 1 (from 3 to 2, which is even), and the even-degree vertex's degree is decreased by 1 (from 4 to 3, which is odd). So that would actually create a new odd-degree vertex, which is bad because we wanted to eliminate all odd-degree vertices.\n\nTherefore, to avoid creating new odd-degree vertices, we must only remove edges that connect two odd-degree vertices. Because then, removing such an edge will decrease both vertices' degrees by 1, so if they were both odd (3), they become even (2). Perfect. So, if we can pair up the odd-degree vertices and remove an edge between each pair, then each removal will fix two odd-degree vertices, and since all the edges we remove are between two odd-degree vertices, we won't create any new odd-degree vertices.\n\nTherefore, the key is: can we pair up all 36 odd-degree vertices with edges that connect them to another odd-degree vertex, and remove those edges?\n\nSo, first, let's note that the odd-degree vertices are all along the edges of the grid, not at the corners. Each such vertex is on one of the four sides of the grid. Let me try to visualize the grid.\n\nIn an 11x11 grid, each side has 11 cells, but the corners are excluded from the odd-degree vertices, so each side has 9 odd-degree vertices (since 11 - 2 = 9). So, each side has 9 vertices of degree 3. Let me label the sides as top, bottom, left, right.\n\nTop side: the top edge, excluding the two corners, so 9 vertices.\n\nSimilarly, bottom side: 9 vertices.\n\nLeft side: 9 vertices (excluding the two corners on the left side, which are also corners of the grid).\n\nRight side: 9 vertices.\n\nSo, each of these sides has 9 vertices of degree 3.\n\nNow, each of these vertices is adjacent to two other vertices along the side, and one vertex inward into the grid. For example, take a vertex on the top side (not a corner): it is adjacent to the previous vertex on the top side, the next vertex on the top side, and the vertex directly below it in the grid (which is an inner vertex with degree 4). Similarly for the other sides.\n\nTherefore, each odd-degree vertex (on the edge) is connected to two other edge vertices (along the same side) and one inner vertex.\n\nTherefore, the edges between two odd-degree vertices are the edges along the sides of the grid. That is, the edges that form the perimeter of the grid, except for the corner edges. Wait, actually, the edges along the sides connect the odd-degree vertices to each other and to the inner vertices.\n\nWait, so on the top side, between two adjacent non-corner vertices on the top edge, there is an edge connecting them. Each such edge is between two odd-degree vertices (since both are on the top side, non-corner). Similarly for the other sides.\n\nTherefore, the edges along the perimeter (the outer edges of the grid) between two non-corner edge vertices are edges connecting two odd-degree vertices. The other edges from each odd-degree vertex go inward to the inner grid.\n\nTherefore, to pair up the odd-degree vertices, we can consider the edges along the perimeter. Each side has 9 odd-degree vertices, which form a path of 9 vertices connected by 8 edges along that side. Each of these edges is between two odd-degree vertices. So, on each side, there are 8 such edges. Since there are four sides, that gives 4 * 8 = 32 edges along the perimeter that connect two odd-degree vertices.\n\nHowever, each corner is a vertex where two sides meet, but the corners themselves are not odd-degree (they have degree 2). Therefore, the perimeter edges do not include the corner edges, but instead, the edges along each side between the non-corner vertices.\n\nWait, actually, each side has 11 vertices, including the two corners. So, the number of edges along each side is 10 (since 11 vertices make 10 edges). But the two edges at the corners connect to the corner vertices, which have degree 2. So, on each side, the edges can be divided into:\n\n- Two corner edges: each connects a corner (degree 2) to a non-corner (degree 3) vertex.\n\n- The remaining 10 - 2 = 8 edges along the side, connecting the non-corner vertices to each other.\n\nTherefore, along each side, there are 8 edges that connect two non-corner (odd-degree) vertices. Since there are four sides, that's 4 * 8 = 32 edges that connect two odd-degree vertices.\n\nAdditionally, each non-corner edge vertex is connected via one edge to an inner vertex (the one inward from the side). These edges go from an odd-degree vertex (degree 3) to an inner vertex (degree 4). These edges do not connect two odd-degree vertices, so they can't be used to pair up odd-degree vertices without creating a new odd-degree vertex.\n\nTherefore, the only edges that connect two odd-degree vertices are those 32 perimeter edges along the sides (the non-corner edges). So, there are 32 edges that can be used to pair up two odd-degree vertices. However, we have 36 odd-degree vertices, so we need 18 pairs, which would require 18 edges. But we only have 32 such edges available. Wait, 32 edges can provide 16 pairs (since each edge connects two vertices), so 16 pairs, which would fix 32 odd-degree vertices. But we have 36 odd-degree vertices, so we still have 4 left unpaired. Hmm, that's a problem.\n\nTherefore, my initial thought was wrong. There are only 32 edges along the perimeter that connect two odd-degree vertices, so we can only fix 32 of the 36 odd-degree vertices by removing those edges. The remaining 4 odd-degree vertices would still have odd degree, so we need another way to fix them.\n\nHmm, so perhaps my approach is flawed. Let me reassess.\n\nEach odd-degree vertex (there are 36) has degree 3. To make their degrees even, we need to reduce their degree by 1 (to 2) or increase by 1 (to 4). Since we can't add edges, only remove edges, so we must decrease their degree by 1. To decrease a vertex's degree by 1, we must remove one of its edges. However, if we remove an edge that connects it to another odd-degree vertex, then both vertices lose a degree, so both become even. If we remove an edge that connects it to an even-degree vertex (the inner ones), then only the odd-degree vertex loses a degree (to 2), and the even-degree vertex loses a degree (to 3, making it odd). So that would create a new odd-degree vertex, which is bad.\n\nTherefore, the only way to fix an odd-degree vertex without creating another is to pair it with another odd-degree vertex and remove the edge between them.\n\nBut as we saw, there are only 32 such edges (along the perimeter sides) connecting two odd-degree vertices. Each such edge can fix two odd-degree vertices. Therefore, with 32 edges, we can fix 64 vertex degrees? Wait, no. Each edge connects two vertices, so each edge removal fixes two vertices. Therefore, 32 edges can fix 64 vertex \"degrees\"? Wait, no. Each edge connects two vertices, so removing one edge reduces the degree of two vertices by 1, so each such removal fixes two odd-degree vertices (if they were both odd) or one and creates another, depending on the parities.\n\nWait, in our case, all the vertices connected by these perimeter edges are odd-degree (3). So, if we remove one of those perimeter edges, then both endpoints lose a degree, going from 3 to 2, which is even. So each such edge removal fixes two odd-degree vertices. Therefore, with 32 edges, we can fix 32 * 2 = 64 vertex degrees? Wait, no, each edge is between two vertices, so each edge removal fixes two vertices. Therefore, 32 edges can fix 32 * 2 = 64 vertex degrees? Wait, no, the number of vertex fixes is equal to the number of edges removed times 2, but since each vertex can only be fixed once, the total number of vertices that can be fixed is 2 * number of edges removed, but since each edge removal affects two vertices, the maximum number of vertices that can be fixed is 2 * E_removed, but only if all those vertices were odd to begin with. However, in this case, all the vertices on the perimeter sides (the 36) are odd, so we can use these 32 edges to fix 64 vertex degrees? Wait, no, each vertex is a single vertex. There are 36 vertices. Each edge connects two vertices, so if you have 32 edges, each can fix two vertices, so in total, you can fix up to 64 vertex \"oddness\", but since each vertex is only one, actually, you can fix 32 edges * 2 vertices per edge = 64 vertex \"degree changes\", but since each vertex can only be fixed once (if it was odd), you can fix at most 36 vertices. Wait, this is confusing.\n\nWait, perhaps better phrased: Each edge removal between two odd-degree vertices will turn both of those vertices from odd to even. Therefore, each such edge removal fixes two odd-degree vertices. Since we have 36 odd-degree vertices, we need to fix 36 of them, which requires 18 edge removals (since each edge removal fixes two vertices). However, as we saw, there are only 32 edges along the perimeter that connect two odd-degree vertices. So 32 edges can fix 32 * 2 = 64 vertex changes, but since we only have 36 vertices to fix, we can fix all 36 with only 18 edges (since 18 edges * 2 vertices per edge = 36 vertex fixes). Therefore, the problem is that there are not enough such edges along the perimeter.\n\nWait, hold on. Let me think again.\n\nSuppose I have 36 odd-degree vertices. Each edge that connects two odd-degree vertices can be used to fix two of them with one edge removal. So, to fix all 36, we need 18 such edges. However, how many edges actually connect two odd-degree vertices?\n\nAs we calculated before, on each side of the grid, there are 9 non-corner vertices (each of degree 3), connected along the side by 8 edges (since 9 vertices in a line have 8 edges between them). There are four sides, so 4 * 8 = 32 edges that connect two odd-degree vertices.\n\nTherefore, there are only 32 edges that can be used to fix two odd-degree vertices each. Since we need 18 edges to fix all 36 odd-degree vertices, but we only have 32 edges available, that's actually more than enough. Wait, 32 edges can fix 64 vertex degrees? Wait, no, each edge fixes two vertices, so 32 edges can fix 64 vertex degrees? But we only have 36 vertices to fix. Wait, no, each vertex is only one vertex. So actually, each edge can fix two vertices, so with 32 edges, you can fix up to 64 vertex degrees, but since we only have 36 vertices, each needing to be fixed once, then actually, 32 edges can fix all 36 vertices? Wait, no, because 32 edges can only fix 64 vertex degrees, but each vertex only needs one degree reduction (from 3 to 2). Therefore, each vertex only needs one edge removed adjacent to it. Therefore, the total number of edge removals needed is 36, since each of the 36 odd-degree vertices needs one edge removed from it. However, each edge removal can fix two vertices at once. Therefore, the minimal number of edge removals needed is ceiling(36 / 2) = 18. But we only have 32 edges available that can fix two vertices each. Therefore, since 18 ≤ 32, we can indeed choose 18 edges among the 32 perimeter edges to remove, each of which will fix two odd-degree vertices, thereby reducing the number of odd-degree vertices to zero. Wait, but hold on.\n\nWait, if we remove 18 edges from the 32 available perimeter edges (each connecting two odd-degree vertices), then each such removal will fix two odd-degree vertices, so 18 removals will fix 36 odd-degree vertices. Perfect! Because 18 edges * 2 vertices per edge = 36 vertices fixed. Therefore, we can do that. So, even though there are 32 edges available, we only need to remove 18 of them, and that will fix all 36 odd-degree vertices. The remaining 14 perimeter edges (32 - 18 = 14) can be left in place.\n\nHowever, we have to make sure that when we remove those 18 edges, the graph remains connected. Because if we remove too many edges, especially if we remove edges that are critical for connectivity, then the graph might become disconnected, and an Eulerian circuit wouldn't exist in the entire graph anymore.\n\nSo, the key question now is: can we remove 18 edges from the 32 available perimeter edges (connecting two odd-degree vertices) without disconnecting the grid graph?\n\nSince the grid graph is a highly connected graph, and the perimeter edges are just the outer edges, removing some of them shouldn't disconnect the graph, as long as we don't remove all edges from any one side or something.\n\nSpecifically, each side has 8 perimeter edges (connecting the 9 non-corner vertices on that side). If we remove edges from different sides, we can avoid disconnecting the graph.\n\nFor instance, suppose we decide to remove one edge from each of 18 different perimeter edges, each on a different side, but spread out so that no side loses too many edges. Since each side has 8 perimeter edges, removing up to, say, 4 edges from each side would still leave each side with 4 perimeter edges, so the side would still be connected (since 4 edges on a side with 9 vertices would still leave the side as a path of 9 vertices with 4 edges remaining, which is still connected). Wait, actually, if you remove edges from a side, the remaining edges on that side would still form a connected path? Let's see: suppose on a side with 9 vertices, originally connected by 8 edges. If you remove k edges from that side, the remaining edges would form a graph on those 9 vertices with 8 - k edges. As long as 8 - k ≥ 1, the vertices on that side would still be connected. Wait, actually, even if you remove k edges from a path of 9 vertices, as long as you don't remove all edges, the path remains connected. Because a path graph is minimally connected: you can't disconnect it by removing fewer than n-1 edges. Since we start with 8 edges on a side (for 9 vertices), removing any number less than 8 edges will still leave the path connected. So, even if we remove, say, 7 edges from one side, leaving 1 edge, the 9 vertices on that side would still be connected through that one edge? Wait, no. If you have 9 vertices in a line, connected by 8 edges. If you remove 7 edges, you still have 1 edge left. That one edge would connect two of the 9 vertices, but the rest would be isolated? Wait no, actually, if you remove 7 edges from a path of 8 edges, you're left with 1 edge. So, that one edge connects two adjacent vertices, and the other 7 vertices on that side would be disconnected from the rest? Wait, yes! Oh no, that's a problem.\n\nAh! So, if on a single side, you remove 7 edges, leaving only 1 edge, then the side is split into two components: the two vertices connected by the remaining edge, and the other 7 vertices, which are isolated. Therefore, the entire grid would become disconnected because that side's vertices are split into multiple components.\n\nTherefore, we must be careful not to remove too many edges from any single side. To keep each side connected, we must leave at least (number of vertices on the side - 1) edges on that side. Wait, the number of edges needed to keep a path of n vertices connected is n - 1. So, for a side with 9 vertices, we need at least 8 edges to keep it connected. But since we are removing edges from the perimeter edges (the 8 edges on each side), if we remove k edges from a side, we must have k ≤ 8 - (9 - 1) = 0? Wait, that can't be. Wait, actually, to keep the side connected as a path, you need at least (number of vertices - 1) edges. Since originally each side has 8 edges for 9 vertices, so to keep it connected, you can remove up to 0 edges? That can't be right.\n\nWait, actually, the side is part of the larger grid, so even if we remove some edges from the perimeter, the vertices on that side are still connected through the inner grid. Wait a second! I think I made a mistake here.\n\nThe sides are part of the entire grid. The vertices on the top side are connected not only along the top edge but also downward to the inner grid. Therefore, even if we remove some of the top side's perimeter edges (the ones along the top edge between the non-corner vertices), the vertices on the top side are still connected to the inner grid. Therefore, the entire grid's connectivity isn't solely dependent on the perimeter edges. So, perhaps removing some perimeter edges doesn't disconnect the graph, because the vertices can still reach each other through the inner edges.\n\nTherefore, my earlier worry about disconnecting the graph by removing perimeter edges might be unfounded because the vertices on a side can still connect through the inner grid.\n\nTherefore, perhaps we don't have to worry about disconnecting the graph as long as we don't remove all connections from a vertex to the inner grid? Wait, but the inner grid connections are separate edges. Each non-corner edge vertex is connected inward to an inner vertex. So, as long as those inward edges remain, the vertex can still reach the inner grid. However, if we remove the inward edge, then that vertex would be only connected along the perimeter.\n\nWait, actually, each non-corner edge vertex (on the top, bottom, left, or right side) has three edges: two along the side (perimeter edges) and one inward to the inner grid. Therefore, if we remove the inward edge, then that vertex loses one connection, but still has two perimeter edges, so it remains connected to the rest of the perimeter. However, if we remove both perimeter edges adjacent to that vertex, then it would only have the inward edge, but if we remove the inward edge as well, then it becomes disconnected.\n\nBut in our case, we are only removing perimeter edges (the ones between two odd-degree vertices). We are not removing the inward edges. The inward edges go from an odd-degree vertex to an inner vertex (which is even-degree). Therefore, if we remove a perimeter edge, we are only affecting the two adjacent perimeter vertices, but their inward edges are still present.\n\nTherefore, as long as for every perimeter edge we remove, we don't remove all connections from a vertex, the graph remains connected. Since each vertex on the perimeter has two perimeter edges and one inward edge, removing one perimeter edge still leaves it with one perimeter edge and the inward edge, so it's still connected to the rest of the grid. Even if we remove both perimeter edges from a vertex, it still has the inward edge, so it's connected to the inner grid. Therefore, as long as we don't remove all three edges from any vertex, the graph remains connected. But since we are only removing perimeter edges (the ones along the sides), each vertex can have at most two of its edges removed (the two perimeter edges), but even then, it still has the inward edge. Therefore, the graph remains connected.\n\nTherefore, as long as we don't remove all three edges from any vertex, which we aren't doing because we are only removing perimeter edges (each vertex has at most two perimeter edges), the graph remains connected. Therefore, even if we remove up to two perimeter edges from a single vertex (i.e., remove both perimeter edges adjacent to it), the vertex still has its inward edge, so it's connected to the inner grid, and the rest of the grid is connected through there. Therefore, the entire grid remains connected after any number of perimeter edge removals, as long as we don't remove all three edges from any vertex, which we aren't.\n\nTherefore, since we are only removing perimeter edges (each vertex on the perimeter has at most two perimeter edges), and we are only removing 18 edges in total, spread out over the 32 available perimeter edges, then the graph remains connected.\n\nTherefore, it is possible to remove 18 perimeter edges (each connecting two odd-degree vertices) such that the remaining graph has all even degrees (since all 36 odd-degree vertices have had one degree reduced by 1, turning them to even) and the graph remains connected. Therefore, the resulting graph has an Eulerian circuit, which is a closed trail that traverses every remaining edge exactly once.\n\nTherefore, the maximum number of doors Karthik can go through is the total number of edges minus the 18 edges we removed, so 220 - 18 = 202.\n\nBut wait, hold on a second. The problem says \"he cannot go through any door more than once (not even in opposite directions)\". So, in graph terms, that's exactly not repeating an edge, so an Eulerian trail is exactly that: a trail that doesn't repeat edges. So, an Eulerian circuit would be a closed trail that doesn't repeat edges. Therefore, if such a circuit exists in the remaining graph with 202 edges, then 202 is the answer.\n\nHowever, let me just verify with a smaller grid to see if this reasoning holds.\n\nLet me try a smaller grid, say a 3x3 grid, and see what the maximum closed trail without repeating edges would be, and see if the formula holds.\n\nIn a 3x3 grid, how many edges are there?\n\nUsing the formula: m = 3, n = 3.\n\nTotal edges = 2*3*3 - 3 - 3 = 18 - 6 = 12 edges? Wait, let me compute again:\n\nWait, horizontal edges: each row has 3 cells, so 2 horizontal edges per row, and 3 rows: 2*3 = 6 horizontal edges.\n\nVertical edges: each column has 3 cells, so 2 vertical edges per column, and 3 columns: 2*3 = 6 vertical edges.\n\nTotal edges: 6 + 6 = 12. Yes.\n\nNow, vertices: 9 cells.\n\nCorner vertices: 4, each with degree 2.\n\nEdge non-corner vertices: each side has 3 - 2 = 1 non-corner vertex (since 3 vertices per side, subtract 2 corners). There are four sides, so 4*1 = 4 edge non-corner vertices, each with degree 3.\n\nInner vertices: 1 center vertex with degree 4.\n\nSo total odd-degree vertices: the 4 edge non-corner vertices, each degree 3. So 4 odd-degree vertices.\n\nTherefore, to make the graph Eulerian, we need to remove edges to fix the odd degrees.\n\nNumber of odd-degree vertices: 4, so minimal edge removals: 4 / 2 = 2.\n\nTotal edges: 12. So maximum trail length would be 12 - 2 = 10.\n\nLet me see if that's achievable.\n\nIn a 3x3 grid, can we have an Eulerian circuit on 10 edges?\n\nFirst, let's try to find such a circuit.\n\nAlternatively, maybe in the 3x3 grid, the maximum closed trail without repeating edges is indeed 10?\n\nAlternatively, let me try to construct it.\n\nLabel the grid cells from (1,1) to (3,3). Let's consider the perimeter edges and inner edges.\n\nThe four edge non-corner vertices are (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2). Each has degree 3.\n\nThe center vertex (2,2) has degree 4.\n\nTotal odd-degree vertices: 4.\n\nTo make all degrees even, we need to remove two edges. Each edge removal can fix two odd-degree vertices.\n\nSo, let's pick two edges that are both between two odd-degree vertices. The odd-degree vertices are on the edges, so the edges connecting them are along the perimeter.\n\nFor example, the top edge between (1,1)-(1,2) and (1,2)-(1,3). But (1,1) and (1,3) are corners (degree 2), so the edges from (1,2) to (1,1) and (1,2) to (1,3) are edges connecting an odd-degree vertex to a corner (even-degree). Similarly for other sides.\n\nWait a second, in the 3x3 grid, the non-corner edge vertices (like (1,2)) are connected to two other edge vertices along the side and one inner vertex.\n\nWait, actually, in the 3x3 grid:\n\nTake vertex (1,2): it is on the top edge. Its neighbors are:\n\n- Left: (1,1) [corner, degree 2]\n\n- Right: (1,3) [corner, degree 2]\n\n- Below: (2,2) [center, degree 4]\n\nTherefore, the edges from (1,2) are to two corners and to the center. Therefore, the edges along the top edge from (1,2) to (1,1) and (1,2) to (1,3) are edges connecting an odd-degree vertex (3) to an even-degree vertex (2). The other edge from (1,2) goes inward to the center (even-degree).\n\nTherefore, in the 3x3 grid, the edges that connect two odd-degree vertices do not exist on the perimeter, except maybe... Wait, actually, between (1,2) and (2,2), that's an inward edge, connecting an odd-degree vertex to an even-degree vertex. The edges along the perimeter from (1,2) to the corners are also connecting to even-degree vertices. Therefore, in the 3x3 grid, there are actually NO edges that connect two odd-degree vertices! Because all the perimeter edges connect an odd-degree vertex to a corner (even-degree) or to another edge?\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, in the 3x3 grid, each side has only one non-corner vertex. So, for example, the top side has vertices (1,1), (1,2), (1,3). The non-corner vertex is (1,2). Similarly, the right side has (2,3), (3,3), so non-corner is (2,3), etc. Therefore, each side only has one non-corner vertex. Therefore, the perimeter edges on each side only have one non-corner vertex, so the edges along the perimeter sides only connect that one non-corner vertex to the corners. Therefore, there are no two odd-degree vertices connected by a perimeter edge. All perimeter edges connect an odd-degree vertex to an even-degree vertex (the corner). Therefore, in the 3x3 grid, there are zero edges that connect two odd-degree vertices. Therefore, my previous reasoning fails here because in the 3x3 grid, we cannot pair up the odd-degree vertices by removing edges between them, since there are no such edges.\n\nHmm, so in this case, how do we fix the odd-degree vertices?\n\nIn the 3x3 grid, we have four odd-degree vertices (each of degree 3). To make all degrees even, we need to reduce each of their degrees by 1, so total degree reductions needed: 4. Since each edge removal affects two vertices, we need to perform two edge removals, each of which will reduce two vertices' degrees by 1. However, in the 3x3 grid, every edge is either connecting an odd-degree vertex to an even-degree vertex or an even-degree vertex to another even-degree vertex (like the inner edges). Therefore, if we remove an edge that connects an odd-degree vertex to an even-degree vertex, we reduce the odd-degree vertex's degree by 1 (to 2, which is even) and the even-degree vertex's degree by 1 (to 3, which is odd). So that creates a new odd-degree vertex. Therefore, each such removal actually changes the number of odd-degree vertices by zero (since we remove one odd and add one even, and remove one even and add one odd: net change zero). Wait:\n\nLet me think: Suppose we have an edge between vertex A (odd) and vertex B (even). Removing that edge:\n\n- Vertex A's degree decreases by 1: from 3 to 2 (even).\n\n- Vertex B's degree decreases by 1: from 4 to 3 (odd).\n\nSo previously, we had one odd (A) and one even (B). After removal, we have one even (A) and one odd (B). So the total number of odd-degree vertices remains the same: still one odd-degree vertex (B). Therefore, this operation does not reduce the number of odd-degree vertices; it just moves the oddness from A to B.\n\nTherefore, in the 3x3 grid, every edge removal will preserve the number of odd-degree vertices modulo 2? Wait, actually, since we started with four odd-degree vertices, which is even, and each edge removal preserves the parity of the number of odd-degree vertices. Because each removal changes two vertices' degrees, so the total number of odd-degree vertices changes by -2, 0, or +2? Wait:\n\nWait, actually, when you remove an edge between two vertices:\n\n- If both were odd: then both become even, so the count decreases by 2.\n\n- If one was odd and one even: then the odd becomes even, and the even becomes odd, so the count remains the same.\n\n- If both were even: then both become odd, so the count increases by 2.\n\nIn the 3x3 grid, all edges are either between an odd and an even vertex or between two even vertices? Let's see:\n\nThe four odd-degree vertices are the four non-corner edge vertices: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2). Each of these is connected to two corners (even) and one inner vertex (the center, which is even). Therefore, every edge from an odd-degree vertex goes to an even-degree vertex. The edges between even-degree vertices are:\n\n- The center (2,2) connected to the four non-corner edge vertices (all odd).\n\n- The corners connected to their adjacent edge vertices (the non-corner ones, which are odd).\n\nWait, actually, the edges between even-degree vertices: for example, the center (2,2) is connected to four odd-degree vertices. The corners are connected only to edge vertices (odd) and one adjacent corner? Wait no, the corners are only connected to two edge vertices? Wait, no: each corner is part of only one side. For example, corner (1,1) is connected to (1,2) and (2,1), both of which are odd-degree vertices. So the corner (1,1) has degree 2, connected to two odd-degree vertices. Similarly, all corners are connected only to odd-degree vertices. Therefore, there are no edges between two even-degree vertices except... actually, the center (2,2) is connected to four odd-degree vertices, and the corners are only connected to odd-degree vertices. Therefore, in the 3x3 grid, all edges are either between an odd-degree vertex and an even-degree vertex or between an even-degree vertex and another even-degree vertex? Wait, but in this case, are there any edges between two even-degree vertices?\n\nWait, the center (2,2) is connected to four odd-degree vertices. The corners are only connected to odd-degree vertices. The edge non-corner vertices (the four odd ones) are each connected to two corners and the center. Wait, no, for example, (1,2) is connected to (1,1), (1,3), and (2,2). So, (1,2) is connected to two corners (even) and the center (even). Wait a second: the center is even, but (1,2) is connected to the center. So, (1,2) is connected to three even-degree vertices? Wait, hold on: corners are even-degree (degree 2), and the center is even-degree (degree 4). Therefore, (1,2) is connected to two corners (each degree 2) and the center (degree 4). Therefore, all neighbors of an odd-degree vertex (like (1,2)) are even-degree vertices. Therefore, every edge in the 3x3 grid is between an odd-degree vertex and an even-degree vertex. There are no edges between two even-degree vertices or two odd-degree vertices. Because the odd-degree vertices are only connected to even-degree vertices, and the even-degree vertices (corners and center) are only connected to odd-degree vertices or other even-degree vertices? Wait, the center is connected only to odd-degree vertices. The corners are connected only to odd-degree vertices. So actually, in the 3x3 grid, every edge is between an odd and an even vertex. Therefore, there are no edges between two even-degree vertices or two odd-degree vertices.\n\nTherefore, in the 3x3 grid, attempting to remove an edge between an odd and even vertex will result in:\n\n- The odd vertex's degree becomes even (good).\n\n- The even vertex's degree becomes odd (bad).\n\nTherefore, each such removal actually converts one odd to even and one even to odd, so the total number of odd-degree vertices remains the same. Since we started with four odd-degree vertices, after any number of edge removals, we still have four odd-degree vertices. Therefore, it's impossible to make all degrees even in the 3x3 grid by removing edges, because we can never reduce the number of odd-degree vertices below four. Since four is even, but we can't get it to zero. Wait, but four is even, so actually, maybe we can make it zero?\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, if we have four odd-degree vertices, and we can perform edge removals that flip two vertices' parities each time, but in this case, each edge removal only flips one odd to even and one even to odd, so the total number of odd-degree vertices remains the same. Therefore, you can never change the number of odd-degree vertices. Since we started with four (even number), we can never get to zero? Wait, but four is even, so if we could pair them up and remove edges between them, but there are no edges between them. So it's impossible.\n\nTherefore, in the 3x3 grid, it's impossible to make the graph Eulerian by removing edges. Therefore, the maximum trail cannot be an Eulerian circuit, but the next best thing is a trail that uses as many edges as possible without repeating any, forming a closed trail.\n\nIn such a case, what is the maximum number of edges in a closed trail in the 3x3 grid?\n\nLet me try to construct such a trail.\n\nStart at a corner, say (1,1). Let's try to make a closed trail.\n\nFrom (1,1), we can go right to (1,2) or down to (2,1). Let's go right to (1,2).\n\nFrom (1,2), we can go left back to (1,1), but that would repeat the door we just used. Not allowed. Or down to (2,2).\n\nGo down to (2,2).\n\nFrom (2,2), we can go left to (2,1), up to (1,2), right to (2,3), or down to (3,2). Let's go left to (2,1).\n\nFrom (2,1), we can go up to (1,1) or right to (3,1) or down to... Wait, (2,1) is on the left edge. Its neighbors are (1,1), (3,1), and (2,2). (3,1) is a corner, connected only to (2,1) and (3,2). Let's go down to (3,1).\n\nFrom (3,1), we can go right to (3,2) or up to (2,1). Let's go right to (3,2).\n\nFrom (3,2), we can go left to (3,1), up to (2,2), or right to (3,3). Let's go up to (2,2).\n\nBut we just came from (2,2) to (3,2), so going back to (2,2) would retrace that edge, which is not allowed. Alternatively, from (3,2), go right to (3,3).\n\nFrom (3,3), can go left to (3,2) or up to (2,3). Let's go up to (2,3).\n\nFrom (2,3), can go left to (2,2) or right to (3,3) or up to (1,3). Let's go left to (2,2).\n\nFrom (2,2), we've been here before. Hmm, this is getting convoluted.\n\nAlternatively, maybe a better approach: since we can't have an Eulerian circuit, the maximum trail will be limited by the number of edges minus the number of \"defects\".\n\nIn the 3x3 grid, with four odd-degree vertices, the maximum trail length is total edges minus the number of odd-degree vertices divided by 2? Wait, in general, for a graph with k vertices of odd degree, the maximum trail length is total edges - k/2, but since k must be even, so yes.\n\nWait, actually, according to some references, in any graph, the length of the longest trail is equal to the total number of edges minus the number of vertices with odd degree divided by 2. Wait, let me check.\n\nWait, according to the concept of the \"Eulerian trail\", which requires all but two vertices to have even degree. The longest trail in a graph with k vertices of odd degree is total edges - k/2. Because you have to start and end at two of the odd-degree vertices, but since we want a closed trail (circuit), which requires all vertices to have even degree, so if there are k vertices of odd degree, you can't have a circuit, but the longest trail would be total edges - k/2, because you have to leave two vertices with odd degrees unpaired? Wait, maybe not.\n\nWait, actually, according to the theory, the maximum length of a trail in a graph is equal to the total number of edges minus the number of odd-degree vertices divided by 2. Here's why: in any graph, the maximum trail can include at most all edges except for a matching that accounts for the odd-degree vertices. Since each time you have an odd-degree vertex, you need to \"start\" or \"end\" the trail there, but for a closed trail, you need all vertices to have even degree. Therefore, the deficiency is the number of odd-degree vertices, and each \"defect\" requires removing an edge or something.\n\nWait, here's a better way: In any graph, the maximum length of a trail is equal to the total number of edges minus the number of vertices with odd degree divided by 2. This is because each time you have an odd-degree vertex, you need to \"break\" the trail there, but since we want a closed trail, we need to have all vertices even. Therefore, the maximum trail length is total edges minus (number of odd vertices)/2.\n\nIn the 3x3 grid, that would be 12 edges - 4/2 = 12 - 2 = 10 edges.\n\nTherefore, the maximum closed trail should be 10 edges. Let me see if I can construct such a trail.\n\nLet me try again:\n\nStart at (1,1). Go right to (1,2). From (1,2), go down to (2,2). From (2,2), go left to (2,1). From (2,1), go down to (3,1). From (3,1), go right to (3,2). From (3,2), go up to (2,2). Wait, can't go back to (2,2). Alternatively, from (3,2), go right to (3,3). From (3,3), go up to (2,3). From (2,3), go left to (2,2). From (2,2), go up to (1,2). From (1,2), go left to (1,1). Wait, let's count the edges used:\n\n1. (1,1)-(1,2)\n\n2. (1,2)-(2,2)\n\n3. (2,2)-(2,1)\n\n4. (2,1)-(3,1)\n\n5. (3,1)-(3,2)\n\n6. (3,2)-(3,3)\n\n7. (3,3)-(2,3)\n\n8. (2,3)-(2,2)\n\n9. (2,2)-(1,2)\n\n10. (1,2)-(1,1)\n\nWait, that's 10 edges. Now, check if any edges are repeated:\n\nEdge 1: (1,1)-(1,2)\n\nEdge 2: (1,2)-(2,2)\n\nEdge 3: (2,2)-(2,1)\n\nEdge 4: (2,1)-(3,1)\n\nEdge 5: (3,1)-(3,2)\n\nEdge 6: (3,2)-(3,3)\n\nEdge 7: (3,3)-(2,3)\n\nEdge 8: (2,3)-(2,2)\n\nEdge 9: (2,2)-(1,2)\n\nEdge 10: (1,2)-(1,1)\n\nLooking at these edges, Edge 2 and Edge 9 both involve (1,2)-(2,2) and (2,2)-(1,2) respectively, but since edges are undirected, traversing in opposite directions is considered the same edge. Wait a minute! In an undirected graph, the trail cannot traverse the same edge in opposite directions, but since edges are undirected, going from A to B is the same as B to A. Therefore, in my above path, edge between (1,2) and (2,2) is traversed twice: once from (1,2) to (2,2) and then from (2,2) to (1,2). That's not allowed! So that path is invalid.\n\nAh! Right, in an undirected graph, a trail cannot traverse the same edge in either direction more than once. Therefore, my attempted path is invalid because it uses the edge between (1,2) and (2,2) twice, once in each direction.\n\nTherefore, that path doesn't work. Let me try again.\n\nAlternative path:\n\nStart at (1,1). Go right to (1,2). From (1,2), go down to (2,2). From (2,2), go right to (2,3). From (2,3), go up to (1,3). From (1,3), go left to (1,2). From (1,2), go down to (2,2). Wait, that's retracing. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively:\n\nStart at (1,1). Go down to (2,1). From (2,1), go right to (3,1). From (3,1), go up to (3,2). From (3,2), go left to (3,1) — no, that's backtracking. Not good.\n\nAlternatively:\n\nStart at (1,1). Go right to (1,2). From (1,2), go down to (2,2). From (2,2), go left to (2,1). From (2,1), go down to (3,1). From (3,1), go right to (3,2). From (3,2), go up to (2,2). From (2,2), go right to (2,3). From (2,3), go up to (1,3). From (1,3), go left to (1,2). From (1,2), go down to (2,2). Again, repeating edges.\n\nThis is tricky. Maybe a different approach: since the four odd-degree vertices must each have one degree reduced, but since we can't do that by removing edges, perhaps the maximum trail will have to start and end at two of the odd-degree vertices, using all edges except two. Wait, but we want a closed trail.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the maximum closed trail can only use 10 edges, but arranged so that it doesn't repeat any edge. Let me try again.\n\nStart at (1,1). Go right to (1,2). From (1,2), go down to (2,2). From (2,2), go left to (2,1). From (2,1), go down to (3,1). From (3,1), go right to (3,2). From (3,2), go up to (2,2). From (2,2), go right to (2,3). From (2,3), go up to (1,3). From (1,3), go left to (1,2). From (1,2), go down to (2,2). Wait, again, repeating the edge between (1,2) and (2,2).\n\nAlternatively, from (1,3), go left to (1,2), but then from (1,2), go down to (2,2), then from (2,2), go to (2,3), then up to (1,3)... no.\n\nAlternatively, start at (1,1):\n\n1. (1,1) -> (2,1) [down]\n\n2. (2,1) -> (3,1) [down]\n\n3. (3,1) -> (3,2) [right]\n\n4. (3,2) -> (2,2) [up]\n\n5. (2,2) -> (2,3) [right]\n\n6. (2,3) -> (1,3) [up]\n\n7. (1,3) -> (1,2) [left]\n\n8. (1,2) -> (2,2) [down]\n\n9. (2,2) -> (2,1) [left]\n\n10. (2,1) -> (1,1) [up]\n\nWait, let's list the edges:\n\n1. (1,1)-(2,1)\n\n2. (2,1)-(3,1)\n\n3. (3,1)-(3,2)\n\n4. (3,2)-(2,2)\n\n5. (2,2)-(2,3)\n\n6. (2,3)-(1,3)\n\n7. (1,3)-(1,2)\n\n8. (1,2)-(2,2)\n\n9. (2,2)-(2,1)\n\n10. (2,1)-(1,1)\n\nNow, check for repeated edges:\n\nEdge 1: (1,1)-(2,1)\n\nEdge 2: (2,1)-(3,1)\n\nEdge 3: (3,1)-(3,2)\n\nEdge 4: (3,2)-(2,2)\n\nEdge 5: (2,2)-(2,3)\n\nEdge 6: (2,3)-(1,3)\n\nEdge 7: (1,3)-(1,2)\n\nEdge 8: (1,2)-(2,2)\n\nEdge 9: (2,2)-(2,1)\n\nEdge 10: (2,1)-(1,1)\n\nEdge 9 is the same as Edge 1 but in reverse? Wait, Edge 1 is (1,1)-(2,1), and Edge 9 is (2,2)-(2,1). Wait no, Edge 9 is (2,2) to (2,1). That's a different edge than Edge 1. Edge 1 is vertical between (1,1) and (2,1); Edge 9 is horizontal between (2,1) and (2,2). So they are different edges. However, Edge 10 is (2,1) to (1,1), which is the reverse of Edge 1, but since it's undirected, that's the same edge. Therefore, Edge 10 is the same as Edge 1, so that's a repetition.\n\nAh! There we go. Edge 10 is retracing Edge 1 in the reverse direction, which is not allowed. So that's invalid.\n\nThis is really challenging. Maybe it's only possible to have a trail of 9 edges?\n\nLet me try a different path:\n\nStart at (1,1):\n\n1. (1,1) -> (1,2)\n\n2. (1,2) -> (2,2)\n\n3. (2,2) -> (2,3)\n\n4. (2,3) -> (1,3)\n\n5. (1,3) -> (1,2) ← can't, already been there.\n\nAlternatively:\n\n1. (1,1) -> (2,1)\n\n2. (2,1) -> (3,1)\n\n3. (3,1) -> (3,2)\n\n4. (3,2) -> (2,2)\n\n5. (2,2) -> (2,3)\n\n6. (2,3) -> (1,3)\n\n7. (1,3) -> (1,2)\n\n8. (1,2) -> (2,2)\n\n9. (2,2) -> (2,1)\n\n10. (2,1) -> (3,1) ← already used edge 2.\n\nHmm.\n\nAlternatively, omit some edges:\n\nMaybe a spiral:\n\nStart at (1,1):\n\n1. Right to (1,2)\n\n2. Down to (2,2)\n\n3. Right to (2,3)\n\n4. Up to (1,3)\n\n5. Left to (1,2) ← repeat.\n\nNo good.\n\nAlternatively:\n\n1. (1,1) -> (2,1)\n\n2. (2,1) -> (3,1)\n\n3. (3,1) -> (3,2)\n\n4. (3,2) -> (2,2)\n\n5. (2,2) -> (2,3)\n\n6. (2,3) -> (1,3)\n\n7. (1,3) -> (1,2)\n\n8. (1,2) -> (2,2)\n\n9. (2,2) -> (2,1)\n\n10. (2,1) -> (1,1)\n\nBut as before, edge 10 is same as edge 1.\n\nAlternatively, stop at 9 edges:\n\n1. (1,1) -> (2,1)\n\n2. (2,1) -> (3,1)\n\n3. (3,1) -> (3,2)\n\n4. (3,2) -> (2,2)\n\n5. (2,2) -> (2,3)\n\n6. (2,3) -> (1,3)\n\n7. (1,3) -> (1,2)\n\n8. (1,2) -> (2,2)\n\n9. (2,2) -> (2,1)\n\nNow, this is 9 edges. Let's check for repeats:\n\nEdges:\n\n1. (1,1)-(2,1)\n\n2. (2,1)-(3,1)\n\n3. (3,1)-(3,2)\n\n4. (3,2)-(2,2)\n\n5. (2,2)-(2,3)\n\n6. (2,3)-(1,3)\n\n7. (1,3)-(1,2)\n\n8. (1,2)-(2,2)\n\n9. (2,2)-(2,1)\n\nEdge 9: (2,2)-(2,1) is a new edge not used before except Edge 2 is (2,1)-(3,1), so Edge 9 is different. However, Edge 9 connects (2,2) and (2,1), which hasn't been used yet except maybe indirectly.\n\nWait, let's list all edges used:\n\nEdge 1: vertical between row1 and row2, column1.\n\nEdge 2: vertical between row2 and row3, column1.\n\nEdge 3: horizontal between column1 and column2, row3.\n\nEdge 4: horizontal between column2 and column3, row2? Wait no: (3,2) to (2,2) is vertical.\n\nWait, (3,2) to (2,2) is up one row, so vertical edge between row3 and row2, column2.\n\nEdge 4: vertical between (3,2) and (2,2).\n\nEdge 5: horizontal from (2,2) to (2,3).\n\nEdge 6: vertical from (2,3) to (1,3).\n\nEdge 7: horizontal from (1,3) to (1,2).\n\nEdge 8: vertical from (1,2) to (2,2).\n\nEdge 9: horizontal from (2,2) to (2,1).\n\nWait, Edge 9 is horizontal from (2,2) to (2,1), which is the reverse of Edge 1's direction but it's the same edge. Wait no: Edge 1 is (1,1) to (2,1), which is vertical. Edge 9 is (2,2) to (2,1), which is horizontal. So different edges.\n\nTherefore, these are all distinct edges. So this trail uses 9 edges without repeating any. Can we add one more edge?\n\nFrom the end at (2,1), can we go somewhere else?\n\nAfter Edge 9, we are at (2,1). From (2,1), we can go up to (1,1) or down to (3,1) or right to (2,2). But (2,2) is already connected via Edge 8 and Edge 9. Going to (1,1) would be Edge 1 reversed, which is the same edge. Going to (3,1) is Edge 2 reversed, same edge. Going to (2,2) is Edge 9 reversed, which is the same edge. So no available edges from (2,1) without repeating.\n\nAlternatively, if we rearrange the path to end at a 