9. (1,2) → (2,2)\n\n10. (2,2) → (2,3)\n\nBut this repeats edges 6 and 10.\n\nThis is really tough. Maybe 8 is the maximum for the 3x3 grid. If so, then according to the formula I thought of earlier: total edges (12) minus number of odd-degree vertices (4) divided by 2: 12 - 4/2 = 12 - 2 = 10, but we can only get 8? That discrepancy suggests my formula might be incorrect.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the formula is different. Maybe for a graph with k vertices of odd degree, the maximum trail length is total edges - k/2, but only if the graph is connected. In the 3x3 grid, it is connected, but we have k=4, so 12 - 2 = 10, but we can't find such a trail. Maybe it's not possible due to the specific structure?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the formula assumes that you can have an open trail, but for a closed trail, you need all degrees even, so if you can't achieve that, the maximum closed trail is less.\n\nIn the 3x3 grid, since we can't make all degrees even, the best we can do is have a closed trail that uses as many edges as possible, which would be total edges minus the number of \"defects\" needed to make it a trail. Since each time you have an odd-degree vertex, you have to \"exit\" the trail there, but for a closed trail, you can't have any defects. Therefore, the maximum closed trail is the total edges minus the minimal number of edges needed to remove to make it possible, which in this case is 2 edges (since you have 4 odd vertices, so need to remove 2 edges to fix two of them?), but I'm confused.\n\nAlternatively, in the 3x3 grid, since we have four odd-degree vertices, the maximum closed trail must exclude at least two edges (since each \"defect\" requires an edge to be excluded?), so 12 - 2 = 10, but we can't find it. Maybe it's actually 10 edges but requires a more clever path.\n\nWait, let me try once more:\n\nStart at (1,1):\n\n1. (1,1) → (1,2)\n\n2. (1,2) → (2,2)\n\n3. (2,2) → (2,3)\n\n4. (2,3) → (1,3)\n\n5. (1,3) → (1,2) ← can't.\n\nAlternatively,\n\n1. (1,1) → 