il, regardless of direction. So, if you traverse an edge from v0 to v1, that edge is incident to v0 and v1. If you later traverse an edge from v1 to v0, that's the same edge, so you can't do that. Therefore, in the trail, each edge is used only once, so the degree of a vertex in the trail is just the number of edges in the trail that are incident to it, and since the trail is a closed walk, the degrees must satisfy that all degrees are even? Wait, but in the above example where I tried to go A-B-C-A-D-A, which is invalid because you can't traverse the edge A-D twice, but if you instead make a valid closed trail, say A-B-C-A-D-C-B-A, but that would require traversing edge C-B twice? Wait, no, let me think of a valid example.\n\nTake a square: A-B-C-D-A. That's a cycle of length 4. Each vertex has degree 2 in the trail, which is even. Now, suppose we have a more complex graph. Let's say we have a figure-eight shape but in terms of edges. Suppose we have two triangles sharing a common vertex. Let's say vertices A-B-C-A and A-D-E-A. Then, can we have a closed trail that goes around both triangles without repeating edges? Starting at A, go A-B-C-A-D-E-A. That's a closed trail: edges A-B, B-C, C-A, A-D, D-E, E-A. Wait, but in this trail, the vertex A has degree 4 (edges A-B, C-A, A-D, E-A), which is even. The other vertices: B has degree 1 (A-B and then B-C?), wait no, vertex B is connected to A and C, so in the trail, it's only on edge A-B and then edge B-C? Wait, in the trail A-B-C-A-D-E-A, the path goes from A to B to C to A to D to E to A. Therefore, vertex B is only incident to edge A-B and edge B-C? Wait no, in the trail, after going from B to C, the next step is from C back to A, so vertex B is only in the first edge A-B and then the second edge B-C. So vertex B has degree 2 in the trail (edges A-B and B-C). Similarly, vertex C is in edges B-C and C-A, so degree 2. Vertex D is in A-D and D-E, so degree 2. Vertex E is in D-E and E-A, so degree 2. Vertex A is in A-B, C-A, A-D, E-A, so four edges, so degree 4. So all degrees are even. So even in this case, all vertices have even degrees in the trail's subgraph. Therefore, my previous reasoning was wrong because I considered a path that tried to go back to A prematurely, causing an odd degree, but if you actually form a closed trail properly, you can have all even degrees.\n\nTherefore, in any closed trail in an undirected graph, the subgraph formed by the trail's edges must have all vertices of even degree. Therefore, the closed trail's edge set forms an Eulerian circuit within that subgraph. Wait, not necessarily an Eulerian circuit of the entire graph, but an Eulerian circuit within the trail's subgraph. Since the trail's subgraph is connected? Wait, actually, the trail is a single path that is closed, so the subgraph must be connected. Because if it were disconnected, the trail couldn't go from one component to another without repeating edges or vertices? Hmm, actually, a trail is a path that doesn't repeat edges, so if the subgraph had multiple components, the trail would have to stay within one component. But since it's a closed trail, it must form a single cycle within its subgraph. Wait, actually, no. A closed trail can have branches? Wait, in an undirected graph, a trail is a path that doesn't repeat edges, so it can have vertices of degree higher than 2 in the trail's subgraph, but the trail itself is just a sequence of edges connected in a line, forming a single cycle? Wait, no, actually in an undirected graph, a trail can have vertices of degree higher than 2 in the trail's subgraph as long as the edges aren't repeated. For example, consider a star graph with a central hub connected to three leaves. If you start at the hub, go to leaf 1, back to hub, then to leaf 2, back to hub, then to leaf 3, and back to hub. That would be a closed trail: hub-leaf1-hub-leaf2-hub-leaf3-hub. But in this case, the hub has degree 6 in the trail's subgraph (since it's connected to leaf1, leaf2, leaf3 each twice?), Wait, no, in the trail described, the hub is connected to leaf1 via one edge, then back to hub via another edge? Wait, no, in an undirected graph, the edge between hub and leaf1 is a single edge. So you can't traverse it twice. Therefore, that trail isn't possible. Because once you go hub to leaf1, you can't go back along the same edge. Therefore, in a star graph with three leaves, the maximum closed trail would be hub-leaf1-hub-leaf2-hub-leaf3-hub, but you can't actually do that because after going hub-leaf1, you can't go back to hub without retracing the same edge. Therefore, in a star graph, the only closed trails are the single edge loops? Wait, no loops. In a star graph with center hub and leaves, the only closed trails possible are the trivial ones that don't go anywhere, or perhaps cycles that include two edges? Wait, no, since all edges from the hub to the leaves are distinct. So actually, in a star graph, you cannot have a closed trail of length greater than 2, because to make a closed trail, you have to return to the start without repeating edges. So starting at hub, go to leaf1, then you can't go anywhere else without repeating the hub-leaf1 edge, so the only closed trails are of length 0 (staying put) or 2? Wait, no, if you go hub to leaf1 and back? But you can't go back along the same edge. So actually, in a star graph with only one leaf, it's just an edge, so you can have a closed trail of length 2? Wait, no, if there's only one leaf, then it's just a single edge connecting hub and leaf. Then a closed trail would have to start at hub, go to leaf, and then... but there's no other edge from the leaf, so you can't get back. Therefore, the only closed trails are of length 0? That doesn't make sense.\n\nHmm, perhaps my understanding is off. Maybe in a star graph with three leaves, the only closed trails are the trivial ones? Or maybe you can't have a closed trail that isn't just the single hub? Wait, perhaps in a star graph, the only closed trail that doesn't repeat edges is just staying at the hub, which isn't moving. But that's not really a trail. Alternatively, maybe it's impossible to have a non-trivial closed trail in a star graph. Because once you leave the hub to a leaf, you can't return without retracing your steps. Therefore, in such a graph, the only closed trails are of length 0 or 2? Wait, no, length 2 would require going out and back, but you can't go back without repeating the edge. So actually, in a star graph, you cannot have any closed trail of length greater than 0 except maybe the trivial one. Therefore, perhaps my earlier thought that all vertices in the trail's subgraph must have even degree is correct, but in such a graph, the only way to have a closed trail with all even degrees is to have a subgraph where every vertex has even degree, which in the star graph would require that the hub has even degree in the subgraph, and the leaves have degree 0 or 2, but since leaves only have one edge, they can only have degree 0 or 1 in the trail. Therefore, the only way for all leaves to have even degree (0) is if they are not included in the trail, and the hub has degree 0, which is trivial. Therefore, in such a graph, the only closed trail is the trivial one at the hub. So that's a problem.\n\nTherefore, perhaps my earlier conclusion is still valid: in any closed trail, the subgraph must have all vertices of even degree. Therefore, in order to have a closed trail that uses as many edges as possible, we need to find a subgraph of the original grid graph which is connected, all vertices have even degree, and the number of edges in this subgraph is as large as possible.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to: In the 11x11 grid graph, find a subgraph with as many edges as possible such that every vertex has even degree, and the subgraph is connected (since a trail is a connected path). Then, the maximum number of doors (edges) Karthik can traverse is equal to the number of edges in such a subgraph.\n\nAlternatively, since the trail must be a single closed trail (i.e., a single cycle?), or can it be multiple cycles connected together? Wait, no, a trail is a path that doesn't repeat edges, so if the subgraph has multiple cycles, the trail would have to traverse all of them without lifting the pen, which would require that the subgraph is connected and the trail is a single cycle? Wait, no, actually, in an undirected graph, a trail can wind through multiple cycles as long as it doesn't repeat edges, but it has to be a single path. For example, in the figure-eight graph (two triangles sharing a common vertex), you could have a trail that goes around one triangle and then the other, but without repeating edges. However, such a trail would pass through the shared vertex twice, but since it's a single trail, it can do that. However, in terms of the subgraph, the trail's subgraph would consist of both cycles, but the trail itself is a single path that traverses both cycles. However, in such a case, the degrees of the vertices in the subgraph would still need to be even? Wait, in the figure-eight example with two triangles sharing a common vertex A, the subgraph consisting of both triangles has vertex A with degree 4 (connected to B, C, D, E?), Wait, let me define it properly.\n\nSuppose we have two triangles: Triangle 1: A-B-C-A, and Triangle 2: A-D-E-A. So vertex A is connected to B, C, D, E. Then the subgraph consisting of both triangles has vertex A with degree 4, and vertices B, C, D, E each with degree 2, and vertices B, C, D, E have degree 2. So all degrees are even, so such a subgraph is Eulerian, meaning there exists an Eulerian circuit which traverses every edge exactly once. Indeed, you can go A-B-C-A-D-E-A, which uses all edges: A-B, B-C, C-A, A-D, D-E, E-A. Wait, but that only uses 6 edges, but there are 6 edges in total (each triangle has 3 edges, so total 6). So that works. So in this case, the subgraph is connected, all degrees even, and the Eulerian circuit exists, and traverses all edges. So in this case, the maximum trail would be the entire subgraph.\n\nTherefore, returning to the original problem: in the 11x11 grid graph, we need to find a subgraph with as many edges as possible such that every vertex in the subgraph has even degree, and the subgraph is connected. Then, the number of edges in this subgraph would be the maximum number of doors Karthik can traverse in a closed walk without repeating any door. Because such a subgraph would allow an Eulerian circuit, which is a closed trail that uses every edge exactly once. Therefore, if such a subgraph exists with all even degrees and is connected, then the maximum number of doors is the total number of edges in the subgraph.\n\nHowever, in the grid graph, as we saw earlier, the original graph has 36 vertices of odd degree (the edge non-corner cells). To make a subgraph where all vertices have even degree, we need to remove a set of edges such that all vertices end up with even degrees. The minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even is equal to half the number of odd-degree vertices, because each edge removal can fix the parity of two vertices. Since we have 36 vertices of odd degree, we need to remove at least 18 edges to make all degrees even. However, the question is not about the minimal number of edges to remove, but rather the maximal number of edges we can retain such that the remaining graph has all even degrees and is connected. Therefore, perhaps the maximum such subgraph is the original graph minus a minimal edge set to make all degrees even, but also keeping it connected.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the maximum number of edges in such a subgraph is equal to the total number of edges in the original graph minus the minimal number of edges needed to make all degrees even, but also ensuring connectivity. Wait, but actually, the problem is not exactly about that. Let me think again.\n\nIn any graph, the maximum number of edges in a subgraph with all even degrees is equal to the total number of edges minus the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even. However, this is only if we don't care about connectivity. But we do care about connectivity, because the trail has to be a single connected path. So perhaps the maximum connected subgraph with all even degrees is what we need.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the maximum number of edges in a connected subgraph with all even degrees is equal to the total number of edges minus the number of edges in a minimal edge set whose removal makes all degrees even and the graph disconnected? Hmm, this is getting too vague.\n\nWait, let's recall that in any graph, the set of edges of any Eulerian subgraph (a subgraph where all vertices have even degree) must have an even number of edges incident to each vertex. To form the largest possible connected Eulerian subgraph, we can consider the concept of the \"Eulerian graph\" and the process of adding edges or removing edges to make it Eulerian.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model this as starting with the original graph and then removing the minimal number of edges such that all remaining vertices have even degree and the graph remains connected. The number of edges remaining would then be the total edges minus the minimal number of edges to remove. Since we want the maximum number of edges, we need to remove as few edges as possible.\n\nIn the original graph, there are 36 vertices of odd degree. To make all degrees even, we need to remove edges such that each removal changes the degree of two vertices by -1 (if we remove an edge between two vertices). So each edge removal can fix the parity of two vertices. Since we have 36 vertices of odd degree, we need to pair them up and remove edges between each pair. However, the minimal number of edges to remove would be 18, since each edge can fix two vertices. However, the problem is that the vertices are arranged in a grid, so we might not be able to pair all 36 odd-degree vertices with disjoint edges. Because edges are between adjacent cells, and the odd-degree vertices are the edge non-corner cells.\n\nWait, let me think of the grid. The odd-degree vertices are all the edge cells that are not corners. There are 36 of them. Each such vertex is on the border of the grid. Let me try to imagine pairing these 36 vertices with edges between them such that each edge connects two odd-degree vertices, thereby removing that edge would reduce their degrees by 1, making them even. But since we need to remove edges, not just consider them, we have to actually remove those edges from the graph. However, in order to make all degrees even, we need to remove a set of edges such that every odd-degree vertex is incident to an odd number of removed edges. Since each removed edge affects two vertices, the total number of \"odd degree\" corrections needed is 36, so we need to remove 18 edges, each contributing 2 to the total correction. So that part is okay.\n\nHowever, the problem is that the edges we remove have to be such that the remaining graph is still connected. Because if we remove too many edges, the graph might become disconnected, and then the Eulerian circuit (which requires the graph to be connected) wouldn't exist.\n\nTherefore, the challenge is to find a set of 18 edges to remove such that:\n\n1. Every odd-degree vertex is incident to an odd number of removed edges (so that their degrees become even).\n\n2. The remaining graph is still connected.\n\nThen, the maximum number of edges in the subgraph would be total edges minus 18.\n\nSo, first, let's compute the total number of edges in the original grid.\n\nIn an 11x11 grid of cells, the number of horizontal edges: each row has 11 cells, so 10 horizontal edges per row. There are 11 rows. So total horizontal edges: 11 * 10 = 110.\n\nSimilarly, the number of vertical edges: each column has 11 cells, so 10 vertical edges per column. There are 11 columns. So total vertical edges: 11 * 10 = 110.\n\nTherefore, total number of edges in the grid: 110 + 110 = 220.\n\nTherefore, the original graph has 220 edges. If we can remove 18 edges such that the remaining graph has all even degrees and is connected, then the maximum number of doors Karthik can traverse is 220 - 18 = 202. But is this possible? Can we remove 18 edges in such a way?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even and keep the graph connected is more than 18? Maybe due to the connectivity constraint.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can do it with 18 edges, but arranged in a way that doesn't disconnect the graph.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's a better way.\n\nWait, in graph theory, there's a concept called the \"Eulerian graph\" and the \"Eulerian trail\". But as we saw, since the original graph isn't Eulerian, we can't have an Eulerian circuit. But we can have a connected Eulerian subgraph by removing edges appropriately.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is analogous to the Chinese Postman Problem, where you have to find the minimal set of edges to duplicate to make the graph Eulerian, but here it's the opposite: we want to remove edges to make it Eulerian, but keeping it connected.\n\nWait, actually, in the Chinese Postman Problem, you want to find the minimal total length of edges to duplicate so that the graph becomes Eulerian. Here, we want to remove edges so that the graph becomes Eulerian, and remains connected. So perhaps similar ideas apply.\n\nIn the Chinese Postman Problem, for a connected graph with 2k odd-degree vertices, the minimal number of edges to duplicate is k, because each duplication of an edge reduces the number of odd-degree vertices by 2. But in our case, we have 36 odd-degree vertices, so to make them all even, we need to remove 18 edges, each removal affecting two vertices. However, in the Chinese Postman Problem, duplicating edges is different because duplicating an edge effectively adds two to the degree of its endpoints, but here, removing an edge subtracts one from each endpoint's degree. So the process is similar but in the opposite direction.\n\nHowever, the Chinese Postman Problem requires the graph to remain connected, but here we also need the resulting graph to remain connected. So perhaps the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even and keep the graph connected is indeed 18, but arranged in a way that doesn't disconnect the graph.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the grid graph has some structure that allows such a removal.\n\nLet me think of the grid as a planar graph. Since it's a grid, it's bipartite. The grid can be colored like a chessboard, with alternating black and white squares. In such a coloring, every edge connects a black square to a white square. Therefore, the graph is bipartite with two color classes.\n\nIn a bipartite graph, all cycles are of even length, which we already know. Also, in a bipartite graph, any Eulerian circuit must have even length, but that's inherent to bipartite graphs.\n\nNow, considering the degrees: in the original grid, the corners have degree 2 (even), the edges (non-corner) have degree 3 (odd), and internal cells have degree 4 (even). So the odd-degree vertices are all on the perimeter.\n\nPerhaps we can pair up the odd-degree vertices along the perimeter and remove edges between them in such a way that the graph remains connected.\n\nAlternatively, since all the odd-degree vertices are on the boundary of the grid, maybe we can \"fix\" their degrees by removing edges along the boundary.\n\nLet me try to imagine the grid's perimeter. The perimeter has 40 cells: 4 corners and 36 edge non-corner cells. Each of these 36 edge non-corner cells has degree 3, which is odd. The corners have degree 2, which is even.\n\nEach edge non-corner cell is on one side of the grid. For example, take the top edge (excluding corners): there are 9 cells (since 11 - 2 corners = 9). Each of these 9 cells on the top edge has neighbors: left, right, and below. Similarly for the bottom edge, left edge (excluding corners), and right edge (excluding corners). Each of these edge non-corner cells has three neighbors.\n\nTo make their degrees even, we need to remove one edge from each of these 36 cells. Since each edge is shared between two cells, removing an edge will reduce the degree of two cells by 1. Therefore, we need to pair up these 36 cells into 18 pairs, and for each pair, remove the edge that connects them. But are these cells adjacent to each other?\n\nWait, but the edge non-corner cells are not adjacent to each other along the perimeter. For example, on the top edge, the cells are arranged in a line from left to right. Each cell on the top edge (non-corner) is adjacent to its left and right neighbors on the top edge, and also to the cell below it. Therefore, the edges between two adjacent top-edge cells are horizontal edges on the top row. Similarly, the vertical edges go down from each top-edge cell to the cell below.\n\nTherefore, if we want to pair two adjacent top-edge cells and remove their shared horizontal edge, that would reduce each of their degrees by 1, making them even. However, if we do that for all adjacent pairs on the top edge, we would be removing all the horizontal edges on the top row, which would disconnect the top row from the rest of the grid. Similarly, if we do this for all edges around the perimeter, we might end up disconnecting the graph.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can remove every other edge along the perimeter to pair up the odd-degree vertices without disconnecting the graph.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps a better approach is to \"fix\" the degrees by removing edges in such a way that we don't break the connectivity.\n\nLet me consider that the perimeter has 36 odd-degree vertices. Each of these is on the boundary. Let's traverse the perimeter clockwise and label the cells. Starting at the top-left corner (which is even degree, so we can ignore it), then the first odd-degree cell is the next one to the right on the top edge. Let's call this Cell 1. Then Cell 2, ..., up to Cell 9 on the top edge (excluding corners). Then, moving to the rightmost top cell, then down the right edge to the bottom-right corner (which is even), so the next odd-degree cell would be the one just left of the bottom-right corner on the right edge, Cell 10, then continuing around the perimeter.\n\nAlternatively, since it's complicated to label them all, perhaps we can think of the perimeter as a cycle of 40 cells (including corners), but actually, the perimeter is a cycle of 40 edges? Wait, no. The perimeter of the grid is a cycle of 4*(11) - 4 = 40 cells, but the number of edges on the perimeter would be 40 as well? Wait, no. Each side of the grid has 11 cells, so the number of edges on each side is 10. Therefore, total perimeter edges: 4*10 = 40. But the perimeter cells are 40 as well. Each corner cell is part of two sides.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to think of the perimeter as a cycle graph with 40 edges and 40 vertices? No, because each corner is a vertex where two sides meet. Each side has 11 vertices, so the total perimeter vertices are 4*11 - 4 = 40 (since corners are counted twice). Therefore, the perimeter can be thought of as a cycle graph with 40 vertices, each connected to their two neighbors along the perimeter, plus the internal edges connecting each perimeter vertex to the next row inward.\n\nBut maybe this is overcomplicating. Let's try a different approach.\n\nSuppose we focus on the outer layer of the grid (the perimeter). Each of the 36 non-corner perimeter cells has degree 3. To make their degrees even, we need to remove one edge from each. Since each edge is shared between two cells, removing one edge will fix two cells' degrees. Therefore, we need to remove 18 edges in total from the perimeter to fix all 36 odd-degree cells.\n\nHowever, if we remove edges only from the perimeter, we might disconnect the perimeter from the interior. But perhaps we can remove edges in such a way that the perimeter remains connected and the interior is still connected to it.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can remove edges in pairs along the perimeter, forming \"breaks\" but keeping the perimeter as a single connected component.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can remove edges in such a way that we create a \"gap\" in the perimeter, but then connect that gap via an interior path.\n\nWait, actually, if we remove 18 edges from the perimeter, we are essentially creating 18 \"cuts\" along the perimeter, but since each cut is an edge removal, and the perimeter is a cycle, removing one edge from a cycle makes it a path. Removing two edges from a cycle can either keep it connected (if the two edges are not adjacent) or disconnect it (if they are adjacent). But since we need to keep the entire graph connected, we can't disconnect the perimeter from the rest of the grid.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps instead of only removing perimeter edges, we can also remove some internal edges to fix degrees while maintaining connectivity.\n\nWait, but the internal cells have even degrees (4), so if we remove an internal edge, we would be changing the degrees of two internal cells from 4 to 3, making them odd, which is bad. Therefore, we cannot remove any internal edges, because that would introduce new odd-degree vertices. Therefore, all edge removals must be on the perimeter edges, since those are the only edges whose removal doesn't create new odd-degree vertices.\n\nWait, let me confirm that. Suppose we have an internal edge, say between two internal cells. Each of those cells has degree 4. If we remove that edge, each of those cells now has degree 3, which is odd. So that's bad, because now we have two new odd-degree vertices. Therefore, we cannot remove any internal edges, because that would increase the number of odd-degree vertices by 2. Since we started with 36 odd-degree vertices, and we need to end up with 0, we cannot afford to create any new odd-degree vertices. Therefore, all edge removals must be on the perimeter edges, because those are the only edges whose removal doesn't create new odd-degree vertices.\n\nTherefore, we must remove edges only from the perimeter edges. There are 40 perimeter edges (as calculated earlier: 4 sides each with 10 edges). We need to remove 18 of them such that:\n\n1. Each removal reduces the degree of two perimeter vertices by 1, turning them from odd (3) to even (2). Since each perimeter edge is between two perimeter vertices (except for the corners? Wait, no: actually, each perimeter edge is either between two perimeter vertices or between a perimeter vertex and an internal vertex? Wait no, all perimeter edges are between two perimeter vertices? Wait no, actually, in the grid, each perimeter edge is either on the boundary between two perimeter cells or between a perimeter cell and an internal cell? Wait, no, actually, all edges on the perimeter are between two perimeter cells. Wait, let's think.\n\nTake a top-edge cell (not a corner). It has neighbors to the left, right, and below. The left and right neighbors are also top-edge cells (if not at the end), and the below neighbor is an internal cell (since it's one row down). Therefore, the edges between top-edge cells are horizontal edges along the top row, which are between two perimeter cells. The edges going down from the top-edge cells are vertical edges connecting to the cell below, which is an internal cell (since we're on the top row). Therefore, those vertical edges are between a perimeter cell and an internal cell.\n\nTherefore, the perimeter edges can be divided into two types:\n\n1. Horizontal edges along the top, bottom, left, and right sides, connecting adjacent perimeter cells.\n\n2. Vertical edges connecting each perimeter cell (except the corners) to the cell inward one step.\n\nTherefore, there are 4*(11 - 1) = 40 horizontal perimeter edges (since each side has 11 cells, so 10 edges per side), and 4*(11 - 1) = 40 vertical perimeter edges? Wait, no.\n\nWait, actually, for the vertical edges on the sides: each side (top, bottom, left, right) has vertical edges? Wait, no. Wait, actually, the vertical edges on the sides are the ones connecting the perimeter cells to the interior.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to compute the number of perimeter edges as follows:\n\nThe entire grid has 11 rows and 11 columns.\n\n- The top row has 11 cells. The horizontal edges in the top row: between the 11 cells, there are 10 horizontal edges. These are all perimeter edges (since they're on the top boundary).\n\n- The bottom row similarly has 10 horizontal perimeter edges.\n\n- The leftmost column has 11 cells. The vertical edges in the leftmost column: between the 11 cells, there are 10 vertical edges. These are all perimeter edges (left boundary).\n\n- The rightmost column similarly has 10 vertical perimeter edges.\n\nTherefore, total perimeter edges:\n\nHorizontal perimeter edges: top and bottom rows: 2 rows * 10 edges = 20.\n\nVertical perimeter edges: left and right columns: 2 columns * 10 edges = 20.\n\nTotal perimeter edges: 20 + 20 = 40, which matches the earlier count.\n\nAdditionally, the internal edges are the rest: total edges 220, so internal edges = 220 - 40 = 180.\n\nBut as we saw earlier, we cannot remove any internal edges because that would create new odd-degree vertices.\n\nTherefore, we are restricted to removing only the 40 perimeter edges, and we need to remove 18 of them such that:\n\n- Each removal reduces the degree of two perimeter vertices by 1, turning them from odd (3) to even (2).\n\n- The remaining graph (after removal) is still connected.\n\nSo, we need to remove 18 perimeter edges, each removal affects two perimeter vertices, turning them from odd to even, and we have to do this without disconnecting the graph.\n\nNow, the question becomes: can we remove 18 perimeter edges from the 40 perimeter edges such that the remaining graph is connected, and every perimeter vertex that was originally odd (all of them) now has even degree?\n\nSince every perimeter vertex (except corners) had degree 3 (odd), and we need to make all of them even. The corners had degree 2 (even), so they don't need any changes.\n\nEach perimeter vertex (non-corner) is part of two perimeter edges: for example, a top-edge non-corner cell is part of one horizontal edge (left-right) and one vertical edge (up-down, but actually down into the interior). Wait, actually, each non-corner perimeter vertex is part of two perimeter edges and one internal edge.\n\nWait, let's take a specific example: take a top-edge non-corner cell, say in the top row, not at the end. This cell has:\n\n- A left neighbor: connected by a horizontal perimeter edge (leftwards).\n\n- A right neighbor: connected by a horizontal perimeter edge (rightwards).\n\n- A downward neighbor: connected by a vertical edge that is NOT a perimeter edge, because that downward neighbor is an internal cell.\n\nTherefore, this cell has two perimeter edges (left and right) and one internal edge (down). Therefore, its degree is 3, as previously established.\n\nSimilarly, a non-corner edge cell on the rightmost column (but not the bottom-right corner) would have:\n\n- An upward neighbor connected by a vertical perimeter edge.\n\n- A downward neighbor connected by a vertical perimeter edge (if it's not the bottom row).\n\nWait, actually, let me clarify with coordinates.\n\nLet me assign coordinates to the grid cells. Let the top-left corner be (1,1), and the bottom-right corner be (11,11). Then:\n\n- A cell on the top edge (row 1) but not a corner has coordinates (1, j) where 2 ≤ j ≤ 10.\n\n- Its neighbors are:\n\n  - Left: (1, j-1) connected by a horizontal edge.\n\n  - Right: (1, j+1) connected by a horizontal edge.\n\n  - Down: (2, j) connected by a vertical edge.\n\nThe vertical edge to (2,j) is an internal edge because (2,j) is not on the perimeter (unless j is 1 or 11, but j is between 2 and 10).\n\nTherefore, the cell (1,j) has two perimeter edges (left and right) and one internal edge (down).\n\nSimilarly, a cell on the rightmost column (column 11) but not a corner has coordinates (i,11) where 2 ≤ i ≤ 10.\n\nIts neighbors are:\n\n  - Up: (i-1, 11) connected by a vertical perimeter edge.\n\n  - Down: (i+1, 11) connected by a vertical perimeter edge.\n\n  - Left: (i,10) connected by a horizontal internal edge.\n\nTherefore, this cell has two perimeter edges (up and down) and one internal edge (left).\n\nSimilarly, a cell on the bottom edge (row 11) not a corner has coordinates (11, j) where 2 ≤ j ≤ 10.\n\nIts neighbors are:\n\n  - Up: (10, j) connected by a vertical internal edge.\n\n  - Left: (11, j-1) connected by a horizontal perimeter edge.\n\n  - Right: (11, j+1) connected by a horizontal perimeter edge.\n\nTherefore, two perimeter edges (left and right) and one internal edge (up).\n\nSimilarly, a cell on the leftmost column (column 1) not a corner has coordinates (i,1) where 2 ≤ i ≤ 10.\n\nIts neighbors are:\n\n  - Right: (i,2) connected by a horizontal internal edge.\n\n  - Up: (i-1, 1) connected by a vertical perimeter edge.\n\n  - Down: (i+1, 1) connected by a vertical perimeter edge.\n\nTherefore, two perimeter edges (up and down) and one internal edge (right).\n\nTherefore, every non-corner perimeter cell has exactly two perimeter edges and one internal edge, totaling degree 3 (odd).\n\nThe corner cells have two perimeter edges and no internal edges, so degree 2 (even).\n\nTherefore, each non-corner perimeter cell is part of exactly two perimeter edges. Therefore, to reduce their degree from 3 to 2 (even), we need to remove one of their perimeter edges.\n\nEach perimeter edge is shared by two perimeter cells (except for the edges at the \"corners\" of the perimeter? Wait, no. Each perimeter edge is between two adjacent perimeter cells. For example, the horizontal edges on the top row connect (1,j) to (1,j+1), which are both perimeter cells. Similarly, the vertical edges on the rightmost column connect (i,11) to (i+1,11), which are both perimeter cells. The only perimeter edges that are not between two perimeter cells are the ones that connect a perimeter cell to an internal cell? Wait no, actually all perimeter edges are between two perimeter cells except for the ones that go inward? Wait no, no.\n\nWait, actually, all the perimeter edges are between two perimeter cells. Wait, let me think again.\n\nTake the vertical edge going downward from a top-edge cell (1,j) to (2,j). Is that edge a perimeter edge? No, because (2,j) is an internal cell (unless j is 1 or 11, but j is between 2 and 10). Therefore, that vertical edge is an internal edge. So the perimeter edges are only the ones along the outer boundary: the horizontal edges on the top and bottom rows, and the vertical edges on the leftmost and rightmost columns.\n\nTherefore, each perimeter edge is between two perimeter cells. For instance:\n\n- On the top row: horizontal edges between (1,j) and (1,j+1) for j from 1 to 10.\n\n- On the bottom row: horizontal edges between (11,j) and (11,j+1) for j from 1 to 10.\n\n- On the leftmost column: vertical edges between (i,1) and (i+1,1) for i from 1 to 10.\n\n- On the rightmost column: vertical edges between (i,11) and (i+1,11) for i from 1 to 10.\n\nTherefore, each perimeter edge is between two perimeter cells, so removing a perimeter edge will affect two perimeter cells, each of which will lose one degree (from 3 to 2), which is exactly what we want for those cells to become even.\n\nTherefore, if we remove 18 perimeter edges, each removal will fix two perimeter cells (from odd to even), and since there are 36 perimeter cells that need fixing, we need to remove 18 edges (since each edge fixes two cells). Therefore, in theory, it's possible if we can select 18 perimeter edges such that no two edges are adjacent in a way that would disconnect the perimeter.\n\nWait, but the perimeter is a cycle of 40 edges? Wait, actually, the perimeter is not a single cycle. Let me see:\n\nThe perimeter consists of four sides:\n\n1. Top side: 10 horizontal edges forming a path from (1,1) to (1,11).\n\n2. Right side: 10 vertical edges forming a path from (1,11) to (11,11).\n\n3. Bottom side: 10 horizontal edges forming a path from (11,11) to (11,1).\n\n4. Left side: 10 vertical edges forming a path from (11,1) to (1,1).\n\nTherefore, the entire perimeter is a cycle of 40 edges, forming a square. So it's a single cycle graph with 40 edges and 40 vertices (the perimeter cells). Wait, but the perimeter cells are 40 in number, as calculated earlier (4 sides * 11 cells per side - 4 corners counted twice = 40). So the perimeter is indeed a cycle of 40 edges and 40 vertices.\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is a cycle graph C₄₀. We need to remove 18 edges from this cycle such that:\n\n1. The resulting graph (the perimeter with 18 edges removed) is still connected.\n\n2. Every vertex in the perimeter (except the corners) has its degree reduced by exactly 1 (from 3 to 2). Wait, but in the cycle, each vertex has degree 2. Wait, hold on a second.\n\nWait a second, hold on! I think I made a mistake here.\n\nIn the perimeter cycle graph C₄₀, each vertex (perimeter cell) has degree 2. But in reality, the actual perimeter cells in the grid have degree 3, because each non-corner perimeter cell is also connected to an internal cell. Therefore, my mistake was considering the perimeter as just the cycle of outer edges, but in reality, the perimeter cells have additional edges going inward to the internal grid. Therefore, the perimeter cells are not just part of the outer cycle but also have another edge going inward. Therefore, the perimeter graph is not just a cycle, but each perimeter cell has an additional edge to the interior, so their degree is 3.\n\nTherefore, when we remove an outer edge (perimeter edge), we are only affecting the degree of the two perimeter cells that were connected by that edge, but those cells also have another edge going inward to the interior. Therefore, removing an outer edge does not affect the connectivity to the interior; the perimeter cell still has that inward edge, so the entire grid remains connected as long as the interior is intact.\n\nWait, but the key point is that when we remove an outer edge, we are only modifying the degrees of the two perimeter cells connected by that edge. The rest of the grid (the internal cells and the connections to them) remain intact. Therefore, as long as we don't disconnect any part of the grid by removing outer edges, the entire graph remains connected.\n\nHowever, removing edges from the perimeter cycle (the outer boundary) can potentially disconnect the perimeter itself from the rest of the grid? Wait no, because each perimeter cell is connected inward to the interior grid via a non-perimeter edge. Therefore, even if we remove all perimeter edges, each perimeter cell still has one edge going inward to the interior, so the entire grid remains connected. The only thing that could happen is that the perimeter cells form a disconnected cycle among themselves, but since they are still connected to the interior, the whole graph remains connected.\n\nWait, actually, if we remove edges from the perimeter cycle, the perimeter cells may form a disconnected subgraph among themselves, but since they are all connected to the interior, the entire graph remains connected. For example, suppose we remove all the horizontal edges on the top row. Then the top row's perimeter cells would form a path broken into segments, but each top perimeter cell still has a downward edge to the interior, so they can still reach the interior. Therefore, the entire graph remains connected.\n\nTherefore, actually, we can remove any subset of the perimeter edges we want, and the graph will remain connected, because each perimeter cell still has at least one edge (the inward one) to the interior. Therefore, the only constraint is that we need to remove exactly 18 perimeter edges to fix all the odd degrees, and since we can remove any 18 edges from the 40 perimeter edges without affecting connectivity, then it is possible.\n\nTherefore, the maximum number of doors Karthik can traverse is the total number of edges minus the 18 edges we removed: 220 - 18 = 202.\n\nWait, but hold on. Let me confirm this conclusion.\n\nIf we remove 18 perimeter edges, each removal reduces the degree of two perimeter cells from 3 to 2, turning them even. The internal edges remain untouched, so all internal cells still have degree 4 (even). The corners, which originally had degree 2, remain at degree 2. Therefore, all vertices now have even degrees. The graph is still connected because each perimeter cell is connected inward to the interior, so there's no disconnection. Therefore, this subgraph is Eulerian, so an Eulerian circuit exists, which is a closed trail that traverses every edge exactly once. Therefore, Karthik can traverse 220 - 18 = 202 doors.\n\nBut wait, hold on! In the problem statement, it says \"he cannot go through any door more than once (not even in opposite directions)\". So traversing an edge in one direction counts as using the door, and you can't go the other way. But in an Eulerian circuit, you traverse each edge exactly once in some direction, so that's fine. So an Eulerian circuit would indeed traverse each edge exactly once, so the number of doors traversed is equal to the number of edges in the Eulerian circuit, which is 202. Therefore, 202 would be the answer?\n\nBut I have a doubt here because I recall that in some grid graphs, the maximum Eulerian circuit might be less due to parity or other constraints, but according to this reasoning, it should be 220 - 18 = 202.\n\nWait, but let me sanity check with a smaller grid. Let's take a 2x2 grid. That's the smallest grid where the problem might make sense.\n\nIn a 2x2 grid, there are 4 cells. Each corner cell has degree 2 (connected to two adjacent cells), and there are no edge non-corner cells because in a 2x2 grid, all cells are corners? Wait, in a 2x2 grid, every cell is a corner. Wait, no, in a 2x2 grid, each cell is a corner? No, actually in a 2x2 grid, each cell is a corner of the larger grid, but each cell itself has two neighbors. Wait, in a 2x2 grid:\n\nCells: (1,1), (1,2), (2,1), (2,2).\n\nEach cell is connected to two others. For example, (1,1) is connected to (1,2) and (2,1). Similarly for others. Therefore, it's a cycle of 4 edges. Each vertex has degree 2. So all degrees are even. Therefore, it is Eulerian, and an Eulerian circuit exists that traverses all 4 edges. So the maximum number of doors would be 4. Indeed, starting at any cell, you can go around the square.\n\nBut according to the formula I used above: total edges in 2x2 grid: horizontal edges: 2 rows * 1 horizontal edge per row = 2. Vertical edges: 2 columns * 1 vertical edge per column = 2. Total edges: 4. Number of odd-degree vertices: 0, since all degrees are even. Therefore, no edges need to be removed, so maximum doors is 4. Which matches.\n\nAnother test: 3x3 grid.\n\n3x3 grid has 9 cells. Let's count edges:\n\nHorizontal edges: 3 rows, each with 2 edges: 3*2=6.\n\nVertical edges: 3 columns, each with 2 edges: 3*2=6.\n\nTotal edges: 12.\n\nNow, degrees of the cells:\n\nCorner cells (4 in total): each has degree 2.\n\nEdge non-corner cells: on each side, there is 1 non-corner cell. There are 4 sides, so 4 edge non-corner cells. Each has degree 3.\n\nInternal cell (center): degree 4.\n\nTherefore, total odd-degree vertices: 4 (the edge non-corner cells). So to make all degrees even, we need to remove 2 edges (since 4/2=2).\n\nNow, according to the formula, total edges: 12, remove 2 edges, so maximum doors: 10.\n\nIs this possible?\n\nLet's see: the 3x3 grid's perimeter is a cycle of 8 edges (the outer boundary). The internal edges are the 4 edges connecting the center to the middle of each side.\n\nWait, in a 3x3 grid:\n\nThe perimeter cells are the 8 surrounding the center. Each perimeter cell (non-corner) has degree 3: for example, the top-center cell is connected left, right, and down. The corner cells have degree 2. The center cell has degree 4.\n\nTo fix the 4 odd-degree vertices (the four edge non-corner cells), we need to remove two edges. Each removed edge will fix two odd-degree vertices.\n\nIf we remove two opposite perimeter edges, say the top-center horizontal edge and the bottom-center horizontal edge. Then, the top-center cell loses one degree (from 3 to 2), and the bottom-center cell similarly. The other two edge non-corner cells (left and right) still have degree 3. Hmm, that didn't fix all.\n\nAlternatively, remove two adjacent perimeter edges. Suppose we remove the top-left to top-center horizontal edge and the top-center to top-right horizontal edge. Then, the top-center cell loses two edges, going from degree 3 to 1? Wait no:\n\nWait, the top-center cell is connected to left, right, and down. If we remove the left and right horizontal edges on the top row, then the top-center cell loses those two edges, so its degree becomes 1 (only the downward edge remains), which is odd again. That's worse.\n\nAlternatively, remove one horizontal and one vertical perimeter edge.\n\nSuppose we remove the top-center to top-right horizontal edge and the right-center to bottom-right vertical edge.\n\nThen, the top-center cell loses one edge (to top-right), so its degree goes from 3 to 2. The right-center cell loses one edge (to bottom-right), so its degree goes from 3 to 2. The other two edge non-corner cells (bottom-center and left-center) still have degree 3. So that only fixed two out of four.\n\nHmm. Maybe we need to remove two edges that are both on the same side?\n\nFor example, remove both horizontal edges on the top row. That would affect the top-center cell (losing two edges, going from 3 to 1) and the top-left and top-right cells (each losing one edge: top-left loses the right edge, top-right loses the left edge). So top-left and top-right corners were originally degree 2, now degree 1 (odd), which is bad. So that's worse.\n\nAlternatively, remove one horizontal and one vertical edge from different sides such that each removal fixes two vertices without creating new odd vertices.\n\nSuppose we remove the top-center to top-right horizontal edge and the right-center to right-top vertical edge? Wait, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, let's think of the perimeter as a cycle of 8 edges. We need to remove two edges such that the remaining perimeter edges form a path that, when combined with the internal edges, keeps all degrees even.\n\nWait, perhaps it's easier to actually construct such a subgraph.\n\nIn the 3x3 grid, we need to remove two perimeter edges. Let's remove the top-middle horizontal edge and the middle-right vertical edge.\n\nWait, labeling the cells:\n\nLet me label the cells as follows:\n\n(1,1) (1,2) (1,3)\n\n(2,1) (2,2) (2,3)\n\n(3,1) (3,2) (3,3)\n\nThe perimeter edges are:\n\nTop row: (1,1)-(1,2), (1,2)-(1,3)\n\nRight column: (1,3)-(2,3), (2,3)-(3,3)\n\nBottom row: (3,3)-(3,2), (3,2)-(3,1)\n\nLeft column: (3,1)-(2,1), (2,1)-(1,1)\n\nInternal edges:\n\n(1,2)-(2,2), (2,2)-(1,1)? No, internal edges are the ones not on the perimeter.\n\nActually, internal edges are:\n\nBetween (1,2) and (2,2),\n\nBetween (2,1) and (2,2),\n\nBetween (2,2) and (3,2),\n\nBetween (2,3) and (2,2),\n\nWait, actually in a 3x3 grid, the internal edges are the ones not on the outer perimeter. So:\n\nHorizontal internal edges: between rows 2 and 1 (but only between columns 2?), no:\n\nWait, horizontal edges in row 2: between (2,1)-(2,2), (2,2)-(2,3)\n\nVertical internal edges: between columns 2 and 1 in column 2: (1,2)-(2,2), (2,2)-(3,2), etc.\n\nActually, in the 3x3 grid, the internal edges are:\n\nHorizontal:\n\nRow 2: (2,1)-(2,2), (2,2)-(2,3)\n\nVertical:\n\nColumn 2: (1,2)-(2,2), (2,2)-(3,2)\n\nAdditionally, column 1 has no internal vertical edges except between row 2 and row 1/3? Wait no.\n\nWait, all horizontal edges in rows 2 and vertical edges in column 2 are internal. The rest are perimeter edges.\n\nAnyway, focusing on perimeter edges, we have 8 perimeter edges as listed above.\n\nSuppose we remove two perimeter edges that are opposite each other, say the top-middle edge (1,2)-(1,3) and the bottom-middle edge (3,2)-(3,1). Wait, actually (3,2)-(3,1) is a horizontal edge on the bottom row.\n\nWait, let's pick two edges that are not adjacent. For example, remove the top-middle edge (1,2)-(1,3) and the right-middle edge (2,3)-(3,3).\n\nAfter removal:\n\n- The cell (1,2) loses one edge (to 1,3), so its degree goes from 3 (connected to (1,1), (1,3), and (2,2)) to 2.\n\n- The cell (1,3) loses one edge (to 1,2), so its degree goes from 3 (connected to (1,2), (2,3), and nothing else? Wait, (1,3) is a corner cell? No, (1,3) is a corner cell (top-right corner). Wait, in the 3x3 grid, (1,3) is a corner cell, so originally degree 2. If we remove the edge (1,2)-(1,3), then (1,3) loses one edge, so its degree becomes 1, which is odd. That's bad.\n\nAh, right, the corner cells have degree 2 originally, so if we remove an edge incident to a corner cell, we reduce its degree to 1, which is odd, which is not allowed.\n\nTherefore, we cannot remove any edges incident to corner cells, because that would create an odd degree at the corner.\n\nWait a minute! This is a critical mistake in my earlier reasoning.\n\nThe corner cells have degree 2 (even), so we must not remove any edges incident to them, because removing an edge from a corner cell would reduce its degree to 1, which is odd. Since we need all vertices to have even degrees, we cannot touch the corner cells' edges.\n\nTherefore, in the 3x3 grid example, the four corner cells have degree 2, so we cannot remove any of their incident edges. Therefore, the only edges we can remove are those that are not incident to the corners, i.e., the edges along the sides between the non-corner perimeter cells.\n\nIn the 3x3 grid, the non-corner perimeter cells are:\n\nTop-middle: (1,2)\n\nRight-middle: (2,3)\n\nBottom-middle: (3,2)\n\nLeft-middle: (2,1)\n\nEach of these four cells has degree 3, and each is connected to two other perimeter cells and one internal cell.\n\nThe edges incident to these non-corner perimeter cells that are not connected to corners are:\n\nFor (1,2): connected to (1,1) [corner], (1,3) [corner], and (2,2) [internal]. Wait, no: (1,2) is connected left to (1,1), right to (1,3), and down to (2,2). So its edges are two perimeter edges (left and right) and one internal edge (down). Therefore, the two perimeter edges of (1,2) are connected to the corners (1,1) and (1,3). Therefore, to reduce the degree of (1,2) without affecting the corners, we cannot remove its perimeter edges because those are connected to corners.\n\nWait, this is a problem.\n\nIn the 3x3 grid, the non-corner perimeter cells like (1,2) are connected via perimeter edges to corner cells. Therefore, removing the perimeter edge between (1,1) and (1,2) would reduce the degree of (1,2) by 1 (good) but also reduce the degree of (1,1) by 1 (bad, since (1,1) was degree 2, now 1). Similarly, removing the edge between (1,2) and (1,3) would affect (1,3)'s degree.\n\nTherefore, in the 3x3 grid, we cannot remove any perimeter edges that are adjacent to the corners, because that would reduce the degree of a corner cell to 1, which is odd and invalid.\n\nTherefore, in the 3x3 grid, the only perimeter edges we can remove are those between the non-corner perimeter cells, i.e., the edges along the sides between the non-corner cells.\n\nIn the top row, between (1,2) and (1,3) is an edge connected to a corner, so we can't remove that. But between (1,2) and (1,1) is also connected to a corner. Wait, actually in the top row, the edges are (1,1)-(1,2) and (1,2)-(1,3). Both edges are adjacent to corners. Similarly, on the right side, the vertical edges between (2,3) and (1,3) (a corner) and between (2,3) and (3,3) (a corner). So all perimeter edges on the sides are adjacent to corners, except perhaps the middle edges on the long sides?\n\nWait in the 3x3 grid, each side has only one non-corner cell. For example, the top side has (1,2) as the non-corner cell between the two corners (1,1) and (1,3). Therefore, the two perimeter edges on the top side are both adjacent to a corner and the non-corner cell. Therefore, there are no perimeter edges on the sides that connect two non-corner perimeter cells. Therefore, in the 3x3 grid, every perimeter edge is incident to at least one corner cell. Therefore, we cannot remove any perimeter edges without affecting a corner cell's degree.\n\nThis is a problem because we have 4 non-corner perimeter cells each needing one degree reduced, but we can't remove any edges without affecting a corner cell. Therefore, it's impossible to fix all the odd-degree vertices without making some corners have odd degree. Therefore, in the 3x3 grid, it's impossible to have an Eulerian circuit? But that can't be right.\n\nWait, let's count the number of odd-degree vertices in the 3x3 grid. The four non-corner perimeter cells each have degree 3 (odd), and the four corners have degree 2 (even). So total odd-degree vertices: 4. To make them all even, we need to remove 2 edges (since 4/2=2). However, every edge we remove will affect two vertices, but in the 3x3 grid, every perimeter edge is incident to at least one corner. Therefore, removing any perimeter edge will reduce the degree of a corner by 1 (from 2 to 1, odd) and the other vertex (a non-corner perimeter cell) by 1 (from 3 to 2, even). Therefore, each edge removal turns one corner into an odd degree and one non-corner into even. Since we have four odd-degree vertices (the non-corners), and four corners, if we remove two edges, each removal will fix one non-corner but mess up a corner. So after two removals:\n\n- We fixed two non-corner cells (from 3 to 2).\n\n- We turned two corner cells from even (2) to odd (1).\n\nTherefore, total odd-degree vertices: original 4 non-corners + 2 new corners = 6 odd-degree vertices. Which is worse.\n\nAlternatively, if we could remove an internal edge, but internal edges connect internal cells (which have degree 4) to perimeter cells. Removing an internal edge would reduce the degree of the internal cell by 1 (to 3, odd) and the perimeter cell by 1 (from 3 to 2, even). So that would create a new odd-degree vertex (the internal cell) and fix one non-corner perimeter cell. So that's a net increase of one odd-degree vertex. Not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, remove two internal edges: each would create one new odd-degree vertex (the internal cell) and fix one non-corner perimeter cell. So after two internal edge removals: fixed two non-corners, but created two new odd-degree internal cells. Total odd-degree vertices: 4 - 2 + 2 = 4. So same as before, but now the odd degrees are on internal cells instead of perimeter. Then we could try to fix those, but it's getting convoluted.\n\nAlternatively, in the 3x3 grid, it's impossible to have an Eulerian circuit because you can't fix the odd-degree vertices without creating new ones or leaving some odd. Therefore, the maximum closed trail would have to leave some edges unused.\n\nTherefore, in this case, the maximum number of doors Karthik can traverse would be less than the total edges.\n\nBut according to the problem's original question about an 11x11 grid, perhaps a similar issue arises where we cannot remove edges without affecting the corners, but in the 11x11 grid, the corners have degree 2, and the perimeter edges adjacent to corners are only connected to one corner and one non-corner perimeter cell.\n\nWait, let's analyze the 11x11 grid again.\n\nIn the 11x11 grid, the corner cells have degree 2. Each corner cell is connected to two edge non-corner cells. For example, the top-left corner (1,1) is connected to (1,2) (right) and (2,1) (down).\n\nThe edge non-corner cells adjacent to the corner are (1,2) and (2,1). Each of these has degree 3: for (1,2), it is connected to (1,1), (1,3), and (2,2). Similarly, (2,1) is connected to (1,1), (3,1), and (2,2).\n\nTherefore, the perimeter edges adjacent to the corner (1,1) are the edge between (1,1)-(1,2) and (1,1)-(2,1). These are the only perimeter edges incident to the corner.\n\nTherefore, if we want to remove an edge incident to a non-corner perimeter cell without affecting the corner, we can remove the edge that connects the non-corner perimeter cell to another non-corner perimeter cell, provided that such an edge exists.\n\nIn the 11x11 grid, along each side (top, bottom, left, right), between the corners, there are 9 non-corner edge cells. For example, on the top edge, from (1,1) to (1,11), the non-corner cells are (1,2) through (1,10). Each of these is connected to their immediate neighbors along the top edge. For instance, (1,2) is connected to (1,1) [corner] and (1,3) [non-corner], as well as down to (2,2). Similarly, (1,3) is connected to (1,2) and (1,4), etc.\n\nTherefore, along the top edge, the non-corner perimeter cells are connected in a line from (1,2) to (1,10), with each adjacent pair connected by a horizontal perimeter edge. Therefore, between (1,2) and (1,3) is a horizontal perimeter edge, between (1,3) and (1,4), etc., up to between (1,9) and (1,10). Similarly on the other sides.\n\nTherefore, on each side, the non-corner perimeter cells form a path of 9 cells, connected by 8 perimeter edges. The two ends of this path are adjacent to the corner cells.\n\nTherefore, on the top edge, the perimeter edges between the non-corner cells are from (1,2)-(1,3), (1,3)-(1,4), ..., (1,9)-(1,10). These are 8 edges, none of which are adjacent to the corner cells. The edges adjacent to the corner are (1,1)-(1,2) and (1,2)-(1,3)? Wait no, (1,1)-(1,2) is adjacent to the corner, but (1,2)-(1,3) is between two non-corner cells.\n\nTherefore, on each side, we have a segment of perimeter edges that are between two non-corner cells, and these edges can be removed without affecting the corner cells.\n\nSpecifically, on the top edge, the 8 edges between the non-corner cells can be removed without touching the corner cells. Similarly on the bottom, left, and right edges.\n\nTherefore, in the 11x11 grid, the perimeter edges can be divided into:\n\n- Edges adjacent to corners: 4 corners, each has two perimeter edges, but each such edge is shared between a corner and a non-corner cell. However, each corner is part of two perimeter edges, but each of those edges is unique to the corner and a non-corner cell. Therefore, there are 4 corners × 2 edges per corner = 8 perimeter edges adjacent to corners.\n\n- The remaining perimeter edges: total perimeter edges are 40. So 40 - 8 = 32 perimeter edges that are between two non-corner perimeter cells.\n\nTherefore, these 32 edges can be safely removed without affecting the corner cells' degrees. The 8 edges adjacent to corners connect a corner (degree 2) to a non-corner perimeter cell (degree 3). If we remove one of these edges, the corner's degree becomes 1 (odd), which is bad, and the non-corner cell's degree becomes 2 (even). Therefore, we must not remove any of the 8 edges adjacent to corners.\n\nTherefore, to fix the degrees of the 36 non-corner perimeter cells (each needing one degree reduction), we can only remove edges from the 32 non-corner-to-non-corner perimeter edges. Each such edge removal will fix two non-corner perimeter cells (each loses one degree). Since we have 36 non-corner perimeter cells that need to lose one degree each, and each removal fixes two, we need to remove 18 edges from the 32 available.\n\nBut 18 edges can be removed from the 32 without any problem, as long as we don't disconnect the graph. Since the graph remains connected even if we remove those edges (because each non-corner perimeter cell still has its inward edge to the interior), the only constraint is that we can indeed choose 18 edges from the 32 non-corner-to-non-corner perimeter edges such that every non-corner perimeter cell is incident to exactly one removed edge? Wait, no.\n\nWait, each non-corner perimeter cell is part of two perimeter edges: one towards the next cell along the perimeter and one towards the previous cell, except for those at the ends of the side segments. Wait, no, actually each non-corner perimeter cell on a side is between two other perimeter cells. For example, on the top edge, the non-corner cells (1,2) through (1,10) are each connected to their immediate neighbors along the top edge. Therefore, each non-corner perimeter cell on a side is part of two perimeter edges (except if they are at the very end near the corner? Wait no, because the ends are adjacent to the corner).\n\nWait, take cell (1,2) on the top edge: it is connected to (1,1) [corner] and (1,3) [non-corner], and also down to (2,2). So along the perimeter, it is connected to (1,1) and (1,3). Therefore, its two perimeter edges are to the corner and to the next non-corner cell. Therefore, the edge between (1,2) and (1,3) is a non-corner-to-non-corner perimeter edge, while the edge between (1,2) and (1,1) is a corner-to-non-corner perimeter edge.\n\nTherefore, each non-corner perimeter cell on a side is part of exactly one non-corner-to-non-corner perimeter edge and one corner-to-non-corner perimeter edge.\n\nTherefore, for each non-corner perimeter cell, one of its two perimeter edges is adjacent to a corner, and the other is between two non-corner cells.\n\nTherefore, to reduce the degree of a non-corner perimeter cell from 3 to 2, we need to remove its non-corner-to-non-corner perimeter edge. Because removing the corner-adjacent edge would ruin the corner's degree.\n\nTherefore, for each non-corner perimeter cell, we must remove its non-corner-to-non-corner perimeter edge to fix its degree. However, each such edge is shared between two non-corner perimeter cells. Therefore, removing that edge will fix both of those cells' degrees.\n\nTherefore, to fix all 36 non-corner perimeter cells, we need to remove 18 perimeter edges (since each removal fixes two cells), and these 18 edges must be chosen such that they form a matching on the non-corner-to-non-corner perimeter edges, i.e., no two edges share a common non-corner perimeter cell.\n\nWait, but each non-corner perimeter cell has exactly one non-corner-to-non-corner perimeter edge (the one not connected to the corner). Therefore, the non-corner-to-non-corner perimeter edges form a set of edges where each non-corner perimeter cell is in exactly one such edge. Therefore, the non-corner-to-non-corner perimeter edges form a collection of paths or cycles?\n\nActually, along each side of the grid (top, bottom, left, right), the non-corner-to-non-corner perimeter edges form a path. For example, on the top edge, the non-corner-to-non-corner edges are between (1,2)-(1,3), (1,3)-(1,4), ..., (1,9)-(1,10). This is a path of 8 edges connecting the non-corner cells from (1,2) to (1,10). Similarly on the bottom edge, right edge, and left edge.\n\nTherefore, each side's non-corner-to-non-corner perimeter edges form a path of length (number of edges) equal to (number of non-corner cells on that side minus 1). Since each side has 9 non-corner cells (on an 11x11 grid), the number of edges between them is 8 per side.\n\nTherefore, on each side, we have a path of 8 edges. To choose a set of edges to remove such that no two edges are adjacent (since each non-corner cell can only have its one edge removed), we need to pick a matching on each of these paths.\n\nA matching in a path graph is a set of edges with no two sharing a common vertex. The maximum matching in a path of n edges is floor(n/2). Wait, but in terms of vertices, a path with m vertices has m-1 edges. The maximum matching size is floor(m/2). Wait, let's clarify.\n\nFor a path with k vertices, the maximum matching has size floor(k/2). But here, the path consists of 9 non-corner cells on a side, connected by 8 edges. So the path has 9 vertices and 8 edges. The maximum matching in a path graph with 9 vertices is floor(9/2) = 4 edges. Because you can pair the first with the second, third with fourth, etc., but since it's a path, you can't have overlapping.\n\nWait, actually, in a path with n vertices, the maximum matching is indeed floor(n/2). So for 9 vertices, floor(9/2) = 4. So on each side's path of 9 non-corner cells (8 edges), the maximum matching is 4 edges. Therefore, on each side, we can remove up to 4 edges without overlapping, fixing 8 non-corner cells per side. Since each removed edge fixes two non-corner cells, 4 edges fix 8 cells.\n\nSince there are four sides, the total maximum number of edges we can remove from the non-corner-to-non-corner perimeter edges without overlapping is 4 sides × 4 edges/side = 16 edges. But we need to remove 18 edges to fix all 36 non-corner perimeter cells (since 36 / 2 = 18). Therefore, 16 is less than 18, so it's insufficient.\n\nAh, here's the problem. On each side, the maximum matching is 4 edges (removing 4 edges per side), which would fix 8 non-corner cells per side, totaling 4×8=32 non-corner cells fixed, but we have 36 to fix. Therefore, we are short by 4.\n\nThis suggests that it's impossible to fix all 36 non-corner perimeter cells by only removing edges from the non-corner-to-non-corner perimeter edges, because the maximum number of edges we can remove without overlapping is only 16, which fixes 32 cells, leaving 4 cells with odd degrees.\n\nTherefore, our initial assumption that we can remove 18 edges is flawed because the non-corner-to-non-corner perimeter edges form paths on each side, and the maximum matching across all four sides is only 16 edges, insufficient to fix all 36 non-corner cells.\n\nTherefore, we need another approach.\n\nPerhaps we can also remove some edges from the corner-adjacent perimeter edges, but carefully so as not to create new odd-degr