e 3, because they are in the perimeter cycle (so degree 2 from the perimeter) plus one inward edge. The corners have degree 2 (only perimeter edges). The inner vertices have degree 4.\n\nNow, going back to the problem of pairing the 36 odd-degree vertices (the non-corner edge vertices) to remove edges between them so that we can make their degrees even.\n\nEach of these 36 vertices has three edges: two along the perimeter and one inward. To make their degrees even, we need to remove one edge from each. However, if we remove an inward edge, then we are removing an edge that connects an odd-degree vertex (the non-corner edge vertex) to an inner vertex (which has degree 4, even). Removing that edge would decrease the inner vertex's degree by 1, making it odd, which is bad because we don't want to create new odd-degree vertices. Therefore, we cannot remove inward edges, because that would turn an inner vertex (which was even) into an odd vertex. Therefore, we must only remove perimeter edges between the non-corner edge vertices.\n\nTherefore, the only edges we can safely remove without introducing new odd-degree vertices are the perimeter edges that connect two non-corner edge vertices.\n\nTherefore, the 36 non-corner edge vertices form a cycle along the perimeter? Wait, actually, the perimeter is a cycle of 40 vertices (including corners). The non-corner edge vertices are 36 in number, arranged along this perimeter cycle. Since the corners are excluded, the non-corner edge vertices form a path along the perimeter cycle? Wait, no, actually, if you remove the four corners from the perimeter cycle, you get a cycle of 40 - 4 = 36 vertices? Wait, no: a cycle with 40 vertices, if you remove four vertices (the corners), you get a path of 36 vertices? Wait, no.\n\nWait, let me think again. The perimeter cycle is a closed loop of 40 vertices. If we remove the four corner vertices, then the remaining 36 vertices are still connected in a single cycle? Wait, no, because removing four vertices from a cycle breaks it into segments. Specifically, each corner removal would break the cycle into a path. Let me see:\n\nImagine the perimeter cycle as a square with 11 vertices on each side. Let's label the vertices going around the square:\n\nStart at the top-left corner (vertex A), then move right along the top edge to the top-right corner (vertex B), then down the right edge to the bottom-right corner (vertex C), then left along the bottom edge to the bottom-left corner (vertex D), then up the left edge back to vertex A.\n\nSo, the perimeter cycle is A-B-C-D-A, but with each side having 11 vertices. Wait, actually, each side has 11 vertices, but the corners are shared. So the entire perimeter has 4 sides × 11 vertices per side = 44 vertex positions, but subtract 4 duplicates for the corners, giving 40 unique vertices.\n\nIf we remove the four corner vertices (A, B, C, D), then the remaining 36 vertices are still connected in a single cycle? Wait, no. Because when you remove a vertex from a cycle, you break the cycle into a path. Each corner removal breaks the cycle into a path. However, since we are removing four vertices, each removal breaks the cycle, but since they are spaced apart, we end up with four separate paths? Wait, no, actually, let's think step by step.\n\nSuppose we have the cycle: A - v1 - v2 - ... - B - w1 - w2 - ... - C - u1 - u2 - ... - D - t1 - t2 - ... - A.\n\nWait, maybe it's better to consider the perimeter as a single cycle of 40 vertices. Let me number them from 1 to 40, going around the perimeter. Let's say vertex 1 is the top-left corner, vertex 2 is next along the top edge, ..., vertex 11 is the top-right corner (vertex B), then vertex 12 is the next down on the right edge, ..., vertex 22 is the bottom-right corner (vertex C), then vertex 23 is the next left on the bottom edge, ..., vertex 33 is the bottom-left corner (vertex D), then vertex 34 is the next up on the left edge, ..., vertex 40 is back to the top-left corner (vertex 1). Wait, actually, numbering might complicate, but the point is, the perimeter is a single cycle of 40 vertices.\n\nIf we remove the four corner vertices (vertices 1, 11, 22, 33, assuming that's how they are numbered), then the remaining 36 vertices are still connected in a single cycle? Wait, no. If you remove a vertex from a cycle, you break it into a path. So removing one vertex breaks the cycle into a path. Removing a second vertex from the path (not adjacent to the first removed vertex) would split it into two paths, etc. So, removing four vertices from the cycle, spaced apart, would result in four separate paths? Or how does it work?\n\nActually, in a cycle, if you remove k vertices, the number of connected components in the remaining graph is k if the vertices are removed in a way that they are not adjacent, but actually, it's more nuanced.\n\nWait, suppose you have a cycle of n vertices. If you remove one vertex, you get a path of n-1 vertices. If you remove two vertices, if they are adjacent, you get a path of n-2 vertices; if they are not adjacent, you get two paths. Similarly, removing three vertices, depending on their positions, you can get up to three paths, etc. So, in our case, if we remove four vertices (the corners) which are spaced equally around the cycle, then each removal is separated by 9 vertices (since total cycle length is 40, so between each corner, there are 40/4 = 10 edges, so 11 vertices? Wait, maybe my mental model is off.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to think that the four corners are each separated by 10 non-corner vertices along the perimeter. For example, between the top-left corner and the top-right corner along the top edge, there are 9 non-corner vertices (since the top edge has 11 vertices total, minus the two corners gives 9 non-corner vertices). Wait, actually, on each side of the square, there are 11 vertices. The corners are at each end, so between two corners on a side, there are 11 - 2 = 9 non-corner vertices. Therefore, along the entire perimeter, between two adjacent corners (moving around the square), there are 9 non-corner vertices, and then the next corner.\n\nTherefore, the four corners divide the perimeter into four segments, each consisting of 9 non-corner vertices between two corners. Therefore, if we remove the four corners, the remaining 36 non-corner edge vertices are partitioned into four separate paths, each of length 9 vertices (i.e., 9 edges). Wait, each segment between two corners has 9 non-corner vertices, so the path between two corners (without the corners) has 9 vertices, which would form a path of 9 vertices connected by 8 edges. Wait, no: the number of edges between two corners on a side is 10, since there are 11 vertices on a side, so 10 edges. But if we remove the two corners, then between them along that side, there are 9 vertices, connected by 8 edges? Wait, no:\n\nWait, on a single side of the square, say the top side from top-left corner (vertex A) to top-right corner (vertex B), there are 11 vertices: A, v1, v2, ..., v9, B. So, the edges along that side are A-v1, v1-v2, ..., v9-B. So, there are 10 edges on that side. If we remove the two corners A and B, then the remaining vertices on that side are v1 through v9, which form a path of 9 vertices connected by 8 edges. So, each side, after removing the corners, becomes a path of 9 vertices and 8 edges.\n\nTherefore, the entire perimeter, after removing the four corners, becomes four such paths, each of 8 edges, connecting the non-corner edge vertices along each side. Therefore, the remaining 36 non-corner edge vertices form four separate paths, each of 8 edges, and each path has 9 vertices? Wait, no, each path has 9 vertices (since between two corners on a side, there are 9 non-corner vertices), and each path has 8 edges. Therefore, the entire remaining graph (after removing the four corners) consists of four disconnected paths, each of length 9 vertices and 8 edges.\n\nTherefore, the non-corner edge vertices are not connected in a single cycle anymore; they're in four separate paths. Therefore, they form four separate paths. Therefore, the odd-degree vertices (the 36 non-corner edge vertices) are partitioned into four groups of 9 vertices each, arranged in four separate paths. Each of these paths has vertices of degree... Hmm, let's see.\n\nWait, each non-corner edge vertex is part of one of these four side segments. Take, for example, a vertex on the top side (between the two top corners). Originally, this vertex had degree 3: two along the top edge and one downward into the grid. But after removing the corners, the vertex is still on the top edge (but now the top edge is a path from v1 to v9 on the top side), so the two neighbors along the top edge are still there, and the downward edge is still present. Therefore, each non-corner edge vertex still has degree 3. However, in the remaining graph (after removing the four corners), the four paths (on each side) are still present, but the inward edges are still there. Wait, actually, we haven't removed any edges yet except the corners? Wait, no, we were just considering the perimeter.\n\nWait, actually, in our problem, we are trying to decide which edges to remove so that all degrees become even. Earlier, we thought we might need to remove edges along the perimeter between the non-corner edge vertices, but actually, the inward edges are still present. Wait, let me clarify.\n\nWait, in order to make the degrees of the non-corner edge vertices even, each of them currently has degree 3 (odd). To make it even, we need to remove one edge from each. However, as we considered earlier, if we remove an inward edge (the one going towards the center), then we are reducing the degree of an inner vertex (which was even) to odd, which is bad. Therefore, we cannot remove inward edges. Therefore, we must remove one of the two perimeter edges connected to the non-corner edge vertex. But each non-corner edge vertex has two perimeter edges (along the side it's on) and one inward edge.\n\nTherefore, to remove an edge from a non-corner edge vertex without affecting an inner vertex's degree, we must remove one of its two perimeter edges. However, each perimeter edge is shared between two adjacent non-corner edge vertices. For example, on the top side, between vertex v1 and v2, there is an edge. If we remove that edge, it affects both v1 and v2, each losing a degree. Since both v1 and v2 are non-corner edge vertices (so odd-degree), removing the edge between them would decrease each of their degrees by 1, making them even. Perfect! So, if we can pair up the non-corner edge vertices along the perimeter and remove the edges between them in such a way that every non-corner edge vertex has exactly one of its perimeter edges removed, then we can make all their degrees even, and also not affect any inner vertex's degree because we are only removing perimeter edges.\n\nTherefore, the key is to find a set of edges along the perimeter (between the non-corner edge vertices) such that every non-corner edge vertex has exactly one of its two perimeter edges removed. This is equivalent to selecting a perfect matching on each of the four side paths (the four segments between the corners) such that each vertex in the path is incident to exactly one removed edge.\n\nWait, each side path (after removing the corners) is a path graph with 9 vertices and 8 edges. We need to remove a set of edges from this path such that every vertex has exactly one incident edge removed. That is, we need a subset of edges to remove such that every vertex has exactly one neighbor removed. This is equivalent to selecting a perfect matching in the path graph? Wait, not exactly a perfect matching, because in a path with an odd number of vertices (9 vertices), a perfect matching isn't possible because perfect matchings require an even number of vertices. Since 9 is odd, we cannot have a perfect matching where every vertex is matched. However, we need each vertex to have exactly one edge removed incident to it. Hmm, so this is equivalent to a 1-factor or something else?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's a \"edge dominating set\" or something else. Wait, let me think differently. If we have a path of 9 vertices, labeled v1, v2, ..., v9. Each vertex except the endpoints has two neighbors along the path. We want to remove some edges so that every vertex has exactly one removed edge incident to it. Let me see.\n\nLet me consider a simple case: suppose we have a path of 3 vertices: A - B - C.\n\nWe need to remove edges so that each vertex has exactly one edge removed. Let's see:\n\n- If we remove the edge AB, then vertex A has one removed edge, vertex B has one removed edge (AB), but vertex C has zero. Not good.\n\n- If we remove edge BC, similar problem: C has one, B has one, A has none.\n\n- If we remove both AB and BC, then A has one, B has two, C has one. Not good.\n\n- If we remove only the middle edge, but in a 3-vertex path, there is no middle edge. Wait, in a 3-vertex path, edges are AB and BC. So, no, we can't remove just one edge to satisfy all.\n\nAlternatively, in a path of 3 vertices, it's impossible to have each vertex have exactly one edge removed. Because the endpoints can only have one edge each, so if we remove an edge at an endpoint, that endpoint is satisfied, but the other endpoint still needs to have an edge removed, but it only has one edge. So, you have to remove both edges, but then the middle vertex would have two edges removed, which is bad. So, impossible for a path of 3 vertices.\n\nTherefore, perhaps for paths of odd length, it's impossible? Wait, our side paths after removing corners have 9 vertices, so 8 edges. Let me see for a path of 5 vertices: v1 - v2 - v3 - v4 - v5.\n\nWe need to remove edges so that each vertex has exactly one removed edge.\n\nLet's try:\n\n- Remove edges v1-v2 and v3-v4 and v5-??? Wait, v5 only has one edge, so to satisfy v5, we have to remove its only edge, v4-v5. Then, v4 would have two removed edges: v3-v4 and v4-v5. Not allowed.\n\nAlternatively:\n\n- Remove edges v2-v3 and v4-v5.\n\nThen:\n\n- v1: has no removed edges.\n\n- v2: has one (v2-v3)\n\n- v3: has one (v2-v3) and one (v3-v4), so two? Wait, no, we only removed v2-v3 and v4-v5.\n\nWait, v3 is connected to v2 and v4. If we removed v2-v3, then v3 still has v3-v4, so v3 has one removed edge? No, v3 has one removed edge (v2-v3), and the other edge (v3-v4) is still there. So, v3 has one removed edge. Similarly, v4 has the edge v4-v5 removed, so v4 has one removed edge (v4-v5), and still has v3-v4. So, v4 has one removed edge. v5 has one removed edge (v4-v5). v1 has none, v2 has one (v2-v3), v3 has one (v2-v3), v4 has one (v4-v5), v5 has one (v4-v5). But v1 has none. So, v1 is problematic. To fix v1, we have to remove its only edge, v1-v2. Then:\n\n- Remove edges v1-v2, v2-v3, and v4-v5.\n\nNow:\n\n- v1: removed edge v1-v2: good.\n\n- v2: removed edges v1-v2 and v2-v3: two, bad.\n\nHmm, no good.\n\nAlternatively, remove edges v2-v3 and v3-v4:\n\nThen:\n\n- v1: no edges removed.\n\n- v2: removed edge v2-v3.\n\n- v3: removed edges v2-v3 and v3-v4: two, bad.\n\nHmm.\n\nAlternatively, remove edges v1-v2 and v3-v4 and v5-??? but v5 only has one edge.\n\nAlternatively, remove edges v2-v3 and v4-v5:\n\nAs before, but then v1 and v5 are okay? Wait, v1 has no edges removed, so that's bad. v5 has one removed edge. So only v1 is bad.\n\nAlternatively, remove edges v1-v2 and v4-v5, and also v3-v4? Then:\n\n- v1: removed edge v1-v2: good.\n\n- v2: removed edge v1-v2: good.\n\n- v3: removed edge v3-v4: good.\n\n- v4: removed edges v3-v4 and v4-v5: two, bad.\n\nHmm. Not working.\n\nAlternatively, remove edges v2-v3 and v4-v5 and also v1-v2? Then:\n\n- v1: removed edge v1-v2: good.\n\n- v2: removed edges v1-v2 and v2-v3: two, bad.\n\nThis is frustrating. Maybe it's impossible for a path of 5 vertices?\n\nWait, let's think differently. Suppose we model this as a graph where we need to select a subset of edges such that every vertex has exactly one incident edge selected. This is called a \"1-factor\" or \"perfect matching\", but in a path graph?\n\nWait, a perfect matching is a set of edges without common vertices, covering all vertices. But here, we want each vertex to have exactly one edge incident to it in the removed set. That is, the removed edges form a 1-regular graph (each vertex has degree 1 in the removed edge set). However, in a path graph, can we have a 1-regular subgraph?\n\nIn any graph, a 1-regular graph is a matching (set of edges with no shared vertices). So, if the path graph has a perfect matching (which requires even number of vertices), then we can have a 1-regular subgraph covering all vertices. If the number of vertices is odd, then it's impossible, since a perfect matching requires even number of vertices.\n\nTherefore, in our case, each side path has 9 vertices (odd), so it's impossible to have a 1-regular subgraph (i.e., a matching that covers all vertices) on a path with an odd number of vertices. Therefore, the best we can do is a matching that covers all but one vertex, leaving one vertex uncovered. But we need every vertex to have exactly one removed edge, so that's impossible for odd-length paths.\n\nTherefore, this suggests that on each side path (which has an odd number of vertices), it's impossible to remove edges such that every vertex has exactly one removed edge. Therefore, we cannot achieve the goal of making all non-corner edge vertices have even degree by only removing perimeter edges, because on each side path, we can't satisfy the condition for all vertices.\n\nHmm, so this complicates things. Maybe we need to also remove some inward edges? But earlier we thought that removing an inward edge would turn an inner vertex (which was even) into an odd vertex. However, perhaps if we remove an inward edge from a non-corner edge vertex, then we can also remove an inward edge from another non-corner edge vertex in such a way that the inner vertex's degree is adjusted appropriately?\n\nWait, let me think. Suppose we have two adjacent non-corner edge vertices, say v and w, connected by a perimeter edge. If we remove that perimeter edge between them, then both v and w have their degrees decreased by 1 (to even). Alternatively, if we instead remove the inward edges from both v and w, then each of v and w would have their degrees decreased by 1 (from 3 to 2, which is even), but then the inner vertices adjacent to v and w would each have their degrees decreased by 1 (from 4 to 3, which is odd). So now we have introduced two new odd-degree vertices where there were none before. So that's worse.\n\nAlternatively, maybe remove one inward edge and one perimeter edge? For example, remove the inward edge from v and the perimeter edge from w. Then v's degree goes from 3 to 2 (even), w's degree goes from 3 to 2 (even), but the inner vertex adjacent to v now has degree 3 (odd), and the inner vertex adjacent to w also has degree 3 (odd). So, we've fixed two odd-degree vertices (v and w) but created two new odd-degree vertices (the inner ones). So net zero change? Wait, actually, the original non-corner edge vertices were 36 odd-degree vertices. After this operation, we still have 36 odd-degree vertices, but now some are inner ones instead of the perimeter ones. Hmm, but the total number of odd-degree vertices remains 36, just moved around.\n\nBut this might not help us in the long run because we still have 36 odd-degree vertices. To make all degrees even, we need to eliminate all 36 odd-degree vertices, but each operation can only affect two vertices at a time (either two perimeter vertices or two inner vertices). Since we can't pair up the perimeter vertices because of the odd-length paths, perhaps we need to do some combination.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we need to remove some perimeter edges and some inward edges in such a way that the total number of odd-degree vertices is reduced by 36, but since each edge removal can fix two vertices, we need to remove 18 edges, but with the constraint that we might have to introduce some odd-degree vertices in the process, but then fix them later? This is getting too vague.\n\nMaybe another approach: in any graph, the minimum number of edges to remove to make all degrees even is equal to the number of odd-degree vertices divided by 2, but only if you can find a set of edges to remove that pairs up all the odd-degree vertices. Since in our case, the odd-degree vertices are the 36 non-corner edge vertices, and they form four separate paths of 9 vertices each (on the perimeter), which are odd-length paths, then perhaps we can pair them up within each path?\n\nWait, in each path of 9 vertices, which is an odd-length path, we can pair up 8 of the vertices into 4 pairs, and leave one unpaired. Then, for each pair, remove the edge between them. This would reduce the degree of each in the pair by 1, making them even, but the unpaired vertex remains odd. Then, for each path, we can do this, resulting in one leftover odd vertex per path. Since there are four paths, that gives four leftover odd vertices. Then, we still have 36 - 8*4 = 36 - 32 = 4 odd vertices left? Wait, no:\n\nWait, each path of 9 vertices: if we pair 8 vertices into 4 pairs, then we remove 4 edges, which affects 8 vertices (each pair loses one edge). The ninth vertex remains with degree 3 (since it wasn't paired). So, per path, after this operation, we have 8 vertices with even degree and 1 vertex with odd degree. Therefore, per path, we reduce the number of odd-degree vertices by 8 (from 9 to 1). So, for four paths, total odd-degree vertices go from 36 to 4. Then, we still have four odd-degree vertices left.\n\nNow, these four leftover odd-degree vertices are the unpaired vertices from each path. They are still on the perimeter, each in their own path. However, they are now isolated in their own paths? Wait, no, they are still part of the path but just not paired. Wait, actually, the paths are still connected, but we've removed some edges.\n\nWait, let me think of a single path of 9 vertices: v1 - v2 - v3 - v4 - v5 - v6 - v7 - v8 - v9.\n\nSuppose we pair v1-v2, v3-v4, v5-v6, v7-v8. Then we remove edges between these pairs: remove v1-v2, v3-v4, v5-v6, v7-v8. Then the remaining edges are v2-v3, v4-v5, v6-v7, v8-v9. So the path is now broken into segments:\n\nv9 is still connected to v8 via the edge v8-v9, but v8 was paired with v7, so we removed v7-v8. Wait no:\n\nWait, if we remove edges v1-v2, v3-v4, v5-v6, v7-v8, then the edges remaining on the path are:\n\nOriginal edges: v1-v2, v2-v3, v3-v4, v4-v5, v5-v6, v6-v7, v7-v8, v8-v9.\n\nAfter removals: removed v1-v2, v3-v4, v5-v6, v7-v8.\n\nSo remaining edges: v2-v3, v4-v5, v6-v7, v8-v9.\n\nTherefore, the path is now split into four separate segments:\n\n- v1 (isolated, since v1-v2 is removed)\n\n- v2-v3\n\n- v4-v5\n\n- v6-v7\n\n- v8-v9\n\nWait, actually, no:\n\nWait, v1 is only connected to v2, but v1-v2 is removed, so v1 is isolated.\n\nv2 is connected to v3 via the edge v2-v3.\n\nv3 is connected to v2 and v4, but v3-v4 was removed, so v3 is only connected to v2.\n\nWait, no: v3 is connected to v2 and v4, but we removed v3-v4, so v3 is only connected to v2.\n\nSimilarly, v4 is connected to v5 (since v4-v5 is still there), but v4 was connected to v3 (which is removed), so v4 is connected only to v5.\n\nv5 is connected to v4 and v6, but v5-v6 was removed, so v5 is only connected to v4.\n\nv6 is connected to v7 (since v6-v7 is still there), but v6-v5 was removed, so v6 is connected only to v7.\n\nv7 is connected to v6 and v8, but v7-v8 was removed, so v7 is only connected to v6.\n\nv8 is connected to v7 (removed) and v9, so v8 is connected only to v9.\n\nv9 is connected to v8.\n\nTherefore, the resulting graph after removals is:\n\n- Isolated vertex v1.\n\n- Edge v2-v3.\n\n- Edge v4-v5.\n\n- Edge v6-v7.\n\n- Edge v8-v9.\n\nSo, the path is broken