revious and next vertex along the top edge (so two perimeter edges, except for the very first and last non-corner vertices on that side, which are adjacent to a corner). Wait, no:\n\nWait, on the top side, the first non-corner vertex after the top-left corner (vertex A) is connected to vertex A and the next vertex. The last non-corner vertex before the top-right corner (vertex B) is connected to the previous vertex and vertex B. Therefore, actually, all non-corner edge vertices on a side have two perimeter edges except the ones adjacent to the corners, which have only one perimeter edge.\n\nWait, this is a crucial point. Let me take a specific example.\n\nConsider the top side of the grid. It has vertices: A (top-left corner), v1, v2, ..., v9, B (top-right corner). Each of v1 through v9 are non-corner edge vertices.\n\n- Vertex A (corner) has degree 2: connected to v1 and the vertex below it (if it's on the corner, actually, wait: in the grid, the corner vertex A is connected to v1 (to the right) and to the vertex below it (south). But in the perimeter path along the top edge, A is only connected to v1 along the perimeter. However, in the entire grid, the corner vertex A has two edges: one to the right (v1) and one downward (south). But since we are considering the non-corner edge vertices, v1 through v9, each of these has:\n\n- v1: connected to A (corner), v2, and downward (south).\n\n- Similarly, v9: connected to B (corner), v8, and downward (south).\n\n- The inner non-corner vertices, say v2 through v8: connected to their left and right neighbors along the top edge, and downward.\n\nTherefore, in the entire grid, each non-corner edge vertex has degree 3: two along the perimeter (except the ones at the ends of the side, which have one perimeter edge and one corner edge, but still two perimeter edges? Wait no:\n\nWait, for v1:\n\n- Left: connected to A (corner),\n\n- Right: connected to v2,\n\n- Down: connected inward.\n\nSo, three edges: yes, degree 3.\n\nSimilarly, v9:\n\n- Left: connected to v8,\n\n- Right: connected to B (corner),\n\n- Down: connected inward.\n\nAlso degree 3.\n\nv2 through v8:\n\n- Left: connected to previous vertex,\n\n- Right: connected to next vertex,\n\n- Down: connected inward.\n\nSo, three edges each. So, all non-corner edge vertices have degree 3 in the entire grid.\n\nHowever, along the perimeter path (just the top edge), the degrees are:\n\n- A: degree 1 (only connected to v1 along the perimeter),\n\n- v1: degree 2 (connected to A and v2 along the perimeter),\n\nWait no: along the perimeter path (the top edge), each vertex's degree within that path is:\n\n- A: connected only to v1,\n\n- v1: connected to A and v2,\n\n- v2: connected to v1 and v3,\n\n...\n\n- v9: connected to v8 and B,\n\n- B: connected only to v9.\n\nTherefore, within the perimeter path alone, the degrees are:\n\n- A and B have degree 1,\n\n- v1 through v9 have degree 2.\n\nBut in the entire grid, each non-corner edge vertex (v1 through v9) has an additional inward edge, so their total degree is 3.\n\nTherefore, when we consider the perimeter path as a separate graph (just the edges along the perimeter), the degrees are as above, but in the entire grid graph, they have an extra edge inward.\n\nTherefore, returning to the problem: when we remove edges from the perimeter path (the top edge, for example), we are affecting the degrees within the perimeter path, but the inward edges remain. So, for the purpose of making all degrees even in the entire grid graph, we must consider both the perimeter and inward edges.\n\nTherefore, if we remove an edge along the perimeter between two non-corner edge vertices, say between v1 and v2 on the top edge, then:\n\n- In the entire grid, v1 loses one edge (the perimeter edge to v2), so its degree becomes 2 (even).\n\n- Similarly, v2 loses one edge (the perimeter edge to v1), so its degree becomes 2 (even).\n\n- The inward edges of v1 and v2 remain, so their total degrees become 2 (from perimeter) + 1 (inward) = 3? Wait no:\n\nWait, no. Wait, the inward edges are separate. Each non-corner edge vertex has one inward edge. So, originally, their degree is 2 (from perimeter) + 1 (inward) = 3.\n\nIf we remove one perimeter edge from v1, then its perimeter degree becomes 1 (only connected to A and v2? Wait no: if we remove the edge between v1 and v2, then v1's perimeter connections are only to A, so perimeter degree 1, plus inward edge, so total degree 2 (even). Similarly, v2's perimeter connections are only to v3, so perimeter degree 1, plus inward edge, total degree 2 (even). Therefore, by removing the perimeter edge between v1 and v2, we reduce both v1 and v2's degrees from 3 to 2, which is even. Perfect! So that works.\n\nHowever, for the endpoints of the side, like v1 (adjacent to corner A):\n\nIf we remove the perimeter edge between v1 and A? Wait, but v1 is adjacent to A and v2. If we remove the edge between v1 and A, then:\n\n- v1's perimeter degree becomes 1 (only connected to v2), plus inward edge, so total degree 2 (even),\n\n- A's degree (which was 2: connected to v1 and the downward edge) becomes 1 (only connected to the downward edge), so A's degree becomes 1, which is odd. But A is a corner vertex, which originally had degree 2 (even). Now it's odd. That's bad, because we don't want to introduce new odd-degree vertices.\n\nTherefore, we cannot remove edges incident to the corners because that would turn the corner vertices (which were even) into odd-degree vertices.\n\nTherefore, on each side, we must not remove any edges incident to the corners. Therefore, on each side path (the non-corner edge vertices between two corners), we can only remove edges that are strictly between the non-corner vertices, not involving the corners.\n\nTherefore, on the top side, for example, the perimeter path from A to B consists of vertices A, v1, v2, ..., v9, B. The edges we can remove are the edges between v1-v2, v2-v3, ..., v8-v9. We cannot remove edges involving A or B.\n\nTherefore, the subgraph of the perimeter path excluding the corners is a path of 9 vertices: v1 through v9, connected as v1-v2-v3-...-v9. Each of these has degree 2 in this subgraph (except the endpoints v1 and v9, which have degree 1 in this subgraph). However, in the entire grid, their degrees are 3 (2 from perimeter, 1 inward). So, to make their total degrees even, we need to remove one edge from their perimeter connections.\n\nBut as we saw earlier, in this path of 9 vertices (v1 to v9), we can attempt to remove edges such that each vertex has exactly one edge removed from its perimeter connections.\n\nHowever, since this is a path of 9 vertices (odd length), it's impossible to have a perfect matching that covers all vertices, as perfect matchings require even numbers. The best we can do is a near-perfect matching that covers all but one vertex.\n\nFor example, in the path v1-v2-v3-...-v9, we can pair v1-v2, v3-v4, v5-v6, v7-v8, leaving v9 unpaired. Then, remove the edges between these pairs: remove v1-v2, v3-v4, v5-v6, v7-v8. This affects vertices v1 through v8, each losing one perimeter edge, so their total degrees become:\n\n- v1: perimeter degree 1 (only connected to v2, which is removed, so actually no—wait, removing v1-v2 means v1 is only connected to nothing on the perimeter? Wait no:\n\nWait, if we remove the edge v1-v2, then v1's perimeter connections are only to... nothing? Because v1 was only connected to v2 on the perimeter (since it's adjacent to the corner A, but we aren't removing that edge). Wait, no:\n\nHold on, v1 is connected to A (the corner) and to v2 along the perimeter. If we remove the edge v1-v2, then v1's perimeter connections are only to A, so its perimeter degree is 1, and inward edge remains, so total degree 2 (even). Similarly, v2's perimeter connections are to v1 (removed) and v3, so perimeter degree 1, plus inward edge, total degree 2 (even). Similarly for the other pairs:\n\n- v3: connected to v4 (removed), so perimeter degree 1 (from v2 and v4, but v3-v4 is removed, so only v2? Wait no:\n\nWait, let's detail it step by step.\n\nAfter removing edges:\n\n- Remove v1-v2: so v1's perimeter edges: only to A (degree 1), and v2's perimeter edges: only to v3 (degree 1).\n\n- Remove v3-v4: v3's perimeter edges: only to v2 (degree 1), and v4's perimeter edges: only to v5 (degree 1).\n\n- Remove v5-v6: v5's perimeter edges: only to v4 (degree 1), and v6's perimeter edges: only to v7 (degree 1).\n\n- Remove v7-v8: v7's perimeter edges: only to v6 (degree 1), and v8's perimeter edges: only to v9 (degree 1).\n\n- The edge v8-v9 remains, so v8 is connected to v9 (perimeter degree 1), and v9 is connected only to v8 (perimeter degree 1).\n\nTherefore, the remaining perimeter edges on the top side are:\n\n- A-v1,\n\n- v1 has no other perimeter edges (since v1-v2 is removed),\n\n- v2-v3 is removed, so v2 is only connected to A? No, v2 is connected to v3 via the removed edge? Wait no:\n\nWait, after removing v1-v2 and v3-v4, etc.:\n\nLet me list all remaining perimeter edges on the top side:\n\nOriginal edges (excluding corners):\n\nv1-v2, v2-v3, v3-v4, v4-v5, v5-v6, v6-v7, v7-v8, v8-v9.\n\nWe removed: v1-v2, v3-v4, v5-v6, v7-v8.\n\nSo remaining edges: v2-v3, v4-v5, v6-v7, v8-v9.\n\nTherefore, the perimeter path on the top side is now split into four separate edges:\n\n- A-v1 (but A is a corner, which we are not removing edges from),\n\nWait, actually, the perimeter path excluding corners is now split into:\n\n- The segment from A to v1: but A is a corner, and we didn't remove any edges involving A, so A-v1 is still there. However, we removed v1-v2, so v1 is only connected to A.\n\n- Then, the remaining edges are v2-v3, v4-v5, v6-v7, v8-v9.\n\nWait, but v2 is connected only to v3 via the remaining edge v2-v3? No, we removed v3-v4, but kept v2-v3. Wait, let's see:\n\nWait, after removals:\n\n- v1 is connected to A and has no other perimeter edges (since v1-v2 is removed).\n\n- v2 is connected to v3 (since v2-v3 is not removed),\n\n- v3 is connected to v2 and has no other perimeter edges (since v3-v4 was removed),\n\n- v4 is connected to v5 (since v4-v5 is not removed),\n\n- v5 is connected to v4 and has no other perimeter edges (since v5-v6 was removed),\n\n- v6 is connected to v7,\n\n- v7 is connected to v6 and has no other perimeter edges (since v7-v8 was removed),\n\n- v8 is connected to v9,\n\n- v9 is connected only to v8.\n\nTherefore, the remaining perimeter edges on the top side are:\n\n- A-v1,\n\n- v2-v3,\n\n- v4-v5,\n\n- v6-v7,\n\n- v8-v9.\n\nTherefore, the non-corner edge vertices now have the following degrees in the entire grid:\n\n- v1: connected to A (perimeter) and inward edge. So degree 2 (even).\n\n- v2: connected to v3 (perimeter) and inward edge. Degree 2 (even).\n\n- v3: connected to v2 (perimeter) and inward edge. Degree 2 (even).\n\n- v4: connected to v5 (perimeter) and inward edge. Degree 2 (even).\n\n- v5: connected to v4 (perimeter) and inward edge. Degree 2 (even).\n\n- v6: connected to v7 (perimeter) and inward edge. Degree 2 (even).\n\n- v7: connected to v6 (perimeter) and inward edge. Degree 2 (even).\n\n- v8: connected to v9 (perimeter) and inward edge. Degree 2 (even).\n\n- v9: connected to v8 (perimeter) and inward edge. Degree 2 (even).\n\nSo, all non-corner edge vertices on the top side now have even degree (2). The corners (A and B) still have their original degrees, which were 2 (even). The inward edges are untouched, so the inner vertices still have degree 4 (even). Therefore, by removing four edges on the top side (v1-v2, v3-v4, v5-v6, v7-v8), we have successfully made all degrees even on that side.\n\nWait a minute! That worked? Even though the path was of odd length? Because we didn't try to pair all vertices, but instead paired them in a way that covered all except the last one, but in this case, by leaving the last edge (v8-v9) intact, we still managed to cover all vertices except v9? Wait no, in the above, all vertices from v1 to v9 had their degrees reduced to 2, which is even. How?\n\nWait, let's recount:\n\n- v1: connected only to A and inward. So perimeter degree 1, inward degree 1: total degree 2.\n\n- v2: connected to v3 and inward: perimeter degree 1, inward degree 1: total 2.\n\n- v3: connected to v2 and inward: perimeter degree 1, inward degree 1: total 2.\n\n- Similarly, v4 through v8: each connected to one neighbor and inward: degree 2.\n\n- v9: connected to v8 and inward: perimeter degree 1, inward degree 1: total 2.\n\nWait, so actually, every non-corner edge vertex on the top side has perimeter degree 1 and inward degree 1, totaling 2. Therefore, all are even. So even though the path was of odd length (9 vertices), by removing edges in this specific way, we could make all degrees even. How?\n\nAh, because we didn't require that the removed edges form a matching covering all vertices, but rather that each vertex loses exactly one perimeter edge. In this case, by leaving the last edge (v8-v9) intact, the vertex v9 only loses no perimeter edges? Wait no:\n\nWait, v9 is connected to v8 via the edge v8-v9, which was not removed. So v9's perimeter connections are only to v8, so that's one edge. Since we didn't remove any edge incident to v9, its perimeter degree remains 1, and inward degree 1, so total degree 2. Therefore, v9 didn't lose any perimeter edges; it kept its only perimeter edge. But then, why did v8's degree become 2?\n\nv8 is connected to v9 (perimeter) and inward, so perimeter degree 1, inward degree 1: total 2. Similarly, v7 is connected to v6 (perimeter) and inward: total 2. So, actually, the strategy was to remove edges in such a way that every vertex except the last one loses one perimeter edge, and the last one keeps its only perimeter edge. But since the last one only had one perimeter edge to begin with, it didn't lose any, so it remained at degree 1 (perimeter) + 1 (inward) = 2. Therefore, actually, all vertices lost exactly one perimeter edge except the last one, which lost none, but since its perimeter degree was already 1, it remained at 2. Wait, but how did the vertex before the last lose an edge?\n\nLet me think of the sequence:\n\nWe removed edges between v1-v2, v3-v4, v5-v6, v7-v8. That's four edges. Each removal affects two vertices:\n\n- Removing v1-v2 affects v1 and v2: each loses one perimeter edge.\n\n- Removing v3-v4 affects v3 and v4: each loses one perimeter edge.\n\n- Removing v5-v6 affects v5 and v6: each loses one perimeter edge.\n\n- Removing v7-v8 affects v7 and v8: each loses one perimeter edge.\n\nTherefore, vertices v1, v2, v3, v4, v5, v6, v7, v8 each lost one perimeter edge, reducing their perimeter degree from 2 to 1, hence their total degree (including inward) becomes 1 + 1 = 2.\n\nVertex v9 was not affected by any removal, so its perimeter degree remains 1 (connected to v8), and inward degree 1, so total 2.\n\nTherefore, all nine vertices on the top side have even degree (2). So even though the path had an odd number of vertices, by strategically removing edges in this way, we could make all degrees even. So my earlier concern about odd-length paths was unfounded because we can adjust the removals to account for the endpoints.\n\nTherefore, this suggests that on any path of n vertices (regardless of parity), we can remove edges in a way that each vertex loses exactly one perimeter edge, except possibly the last one if n is odd? Wait, in this case with n=9 (odd), the last vertex didn't lose any, but since its original perimeter degree was 1, it ended up at 1 (perimeter) + 1 (inward) = 2. So actually, every vertex lost exactly one perimeter edge except the last one, which lost zero, but since its original perimeter degree was 1, it didn't need to lose any to become even. Therefore, in general, for a path of n vertices:\n\n- If n is even, we can pair them up and remove the edges between each pair, so every vertex loses one perimeter edge, resulting in all having perimeter degree 1 (if n even, the path is a straight line with two endpoints? Wait no.\n\nWait, let's think for a path of even length, say n=8 vertices:\n\nSuppose we have vertices v1 to v8.\n\nWe can pair them as (v1-v2), (v3-v4), (v5-v6), (v7-v8), remove those edges. Then each vertex except the last one in each pair loses an edge, but actually, each vertex in the pairs loses one edge. The last vertex v8 would have its edge v7-v8 removed, so v8 loses one perimeter edge. So all vertices lose one perimeter edge, so their perimeter degrees go from 2 (for internal vertices) to 1, and the endpoints (v1 and v8) originally had perimeter degree 1, so after losing the edge, they would have 0? Wait no:\n\nWait, in a path of even length, say 8 vertices:\n\nOriginal perimeter degrees:\n\n- v1: 1 (connected only to v2),\n\n- v2: 2,\n\n- v3: 2,\n\n- ...,\n\n- v8: 1.\n\nIf we remove edges v1-v2, v3-v4, v5-v6, v7-v8:\n\nThen:\n\n- v1: loses v1-v2, so perimeter degree 0,\n\n- v2: loses v1-v2, so perimeter degree 1 (connected to v3),\n\n- v3: loses v3-v4, so perimeter degree 1 (connected to v2 and v4, but v3-v4 is removed, so only v2),\n\n- v4: loses v3-v4, so perimeter degree 1 (connected to v5),\n\n- v5: loses v5-v6, so perimeter degree 1 (connected to v4 and v6, but v5-v6 removed, so only v4),\n\n- v6: loses v5-v6, so perimeter degree 1 (connected to v7),\n\n- v7: loses v7-v8, so perimeter degree 1 (connected to v6 and v8, but v7-v8 removed, so only v6),\n\n- v8: loses v7-v8, so perimeter degree 0.\n\nThen the total degrees:\n\n- v1: perimeter 0 + inward 1 = 1 (odd),\n\n- v2: perimeter 1 + inward 1 = 2 (even),\n\n- v3: perimeter 1 + inward 1 = 2,\n\n- v4: perimeter 1 + inward 1 = 2,\n\n- v5: perimeter 1 + inward 1 = 2,\n\n- v6: perimeter 1 + inward 1 = 2,\n\n- v7: perimeter 1 + inward 1 = 2,\n\n- v8: perimeter 0 + inward 1 = 1 (odd).\n\nSo, in this case, the endpoints v1 and v8 now have odd degrees, which is bad. Therefore, for even-length paths, this method leaves the two endpoints with odd degrees. Therefore, to fix that, we might need to do something else, like also remove an inward edge from one of them, but that would affect an inner vertex's degree.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps a different pairing strategy for even-length paths.\n\nAlternatively, for even-length paths, we can remove every other edge, but I need to think carefully.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps for any path, regardless of length, we can remove edges in such a way that every vertex loses exactly one perimeter edge, except possibly the endpoints, but then adjust for the endpoints by also removing an inward edge? But that would introduce new odd-degree vertices.\n\nHmm, this is getting too convoluted. Let's go back to the original problem with the 11x11 grid.\n\nWe have four side paths, each with 9 non-corner edge vertices. For each such path, we can perform the following:\n\n- Remove edges between vertices 1-2, 3-4, 5-6, 7-8 on the path. This leaves the last vertex (9th) untouched in terms of perimeter edges.\n\nAs we saw in the 9-vertex example, this results in all non-corner edge vertices on that side having even degree (2). The corners remain at even degree (2). The inward edges remain, so inner vertices stay at even degree (4). Therefore, this works for each side.\n\nTherefore, for each of the four sides, we can remove 4 edges (as in the top side example: removing 4 edges out of the 8 available on that side's perimeter path), thereby making all non-corner edge vertices on that side have even degree, without affecting the corners or inner vertices.\n\nTherefore, total edges removed per side: 4.\n\nSince there are four sides, total edges removed: 4 sides × 4 edges/side = 16 edges.\n\nWait, in the top side example, we had 9 vertices, and we removed 4 edges. The top side's perimeter path had 8 edges (since 9 vertices have 8 edges between them). Removing 4 edges leaves 4 edges remaining.\n\nSimilarly, each side has 9 non-corner edge vertices, connected by 8 perimeter edges. By removing 4 edges as described, we can make all their degrees even.\n\nTherefore, per side, we remove 4 edges, so 4 × 4 = 16 edges total removed from the perimeter.\n\nAdditionally, we must also consider the inward edges. Wait, no, the inward edges are not removed; we only removed perimeter edges. The inward edges remain intact, so the inner vertices still have degree 4, which is even. The non-corner edge vertices now have degree 2 (even), and the corners remain at degree 2 (even). Therefore, after removing these 16 perimeter edges, the entire graph now has all vertices of even degree.\n\nTherefore, the resulting graph has an Eulerian circuit! Because all degrees are even. Therefore, the maximum number of edges Karthik can traverse is equal to the number of edges remaining in this Eulerian subgraph.\n\nTotal edges in original grid: 220.\n\nEdges removed: 16.\n\nTherefore, edges remaining: 220 - 16 = 204.\n\nWait, but hold on: in the example of the top side, we removed 4 edges out of the 8 perimeter edges on that side, but each side's perimeter edges are part of the total 220 edges. Since there are four sides, each with 8 perimeter edges (since each side has 11 vertices, so 10 edges, but excluding the two corners, we have 9 non-corner vertices connected by 8 edges). Wait a second, actually, each side of the grid has 11 vertices, which include two corners. Therefore, the number of perimeter edges per side is 11 - 1 = 10 edges. However, when we exclude the corners, the non-corner edge vertices form a path of 9 vertices with 8 edges. Therefore, the total number of perimeter edges in the entire grid is 4 sides × 10 edges/side = 40 edges.\n\nWait, this is a critical mistake I made earlier.\n\nHold on, let's recast:\n\nEach side of the grid (top, right, bottom, left) has 11 vertices, connected by 10 edges (since n vertices have n-1 edges). Therefore, each side has 10 perimeter edges. Since there are four sides, total perimeter edges are 4 × 10 = 40 edges. The total edges in the grid are 11×10 (horizontal) + 10×11 (vertical) = 110 + 110 = 220 edges, which matches the earlier count.\n\nHowever, the non-corner edge vertices on each side are 9 in number (since 11 - 2 = 9), and they form a path of 9 vertices connected by 8 edges. Therefore, the perimeter edges not involving corners are 8 per side. The remaining perimeter edges on each side are the two edges adjacent to the corners: for the top side, the edge between the top-left corner and the first non-corner vertex (v1), and the edge between the last non-corner vertex (v9) and the top-right corner (B). These two edges per side involve the corner vertices and cannot be removed without affecting the corners' degrees.\n\nTherefore, on each side, we have 8 removable perimeter edges (the ones between non-corner vertices) and 2 non-removable edges (the ones adjacent to the corners). Therefore, in order to perform the operation on each side (removing 4 edges to make all non-corner edge vertices even), we must only remove edges among the 8 removable ones per side.\n\nTherefore, per side:\n\n- Total perimeter edges: 10\n\n- Removable edges (between non-corner vertices): 8\n\n- Non-removable edges (adjacent to corners): 2\n\nWe need to remove 4 edges from the 8 removable edges per side to make all non-corner edge vertices have even degree. As shown in the 9-vertex example, this is possible by removing every other edge starting from the first, resulting in all non-corner edge vertices having even degree.\n\nTherefore, per side, we remove 4 edges, so total over four sides: 4 sides × 4 edges/side = 16 edges removed from the perimeter.\n\nAdditionally, we must ensure that we do not remove any non-removable edges (the ones adjacent to corners), as that would affect the corners' degrees.\n\nTherefore, total edges removed from the entire grid: 16 (all perimeter edges, none inward).\n\nTherefore, the remaining edges in the graph are:\n\nTotal edges: 220\n\nMinus removed edges: 16\n\nThus, remaining edges: 220 - 16 = 204.\n\nSince after removing these 16 edges, all vertices have even degree, the remaining graph has an Eulerian circuit, which traverses every remaining edge exactly once. Therefore, Karthik can walk this Eulerian circuit, which has 204 edges, thus passing through 204 doors.\n\nBut wait, hold on: an Eulerian circuit in a graph with V vertices and E edges has exactly E edges, right? So if the remaining graph has 204 edges, then the Eulerian circuit would traverse all 204 edges, which would be the maximum possible, since you can't traverse more than all edges without repeating.\n\nTherefore, is 204 the answer? But let me confirm the calculations again.\n\nFirst, total edges in grid: 11x11 grid has 11 rows and 11 columns.\n\nNumber of horizontal edges: 11 rows × 10 columns between them = 110.\n\nNumber of vertical edges: 11 columns × 10 rows between them = 110.\n\nTotal edges: 110 + 110 = 220. Correct.\n\nPerimeter edges: Each side has 11 vertices, so 10 edges per side. Four sides: 40 perimeter edges. Therefore, non-perimeter edges (the inner edges) are total edges minus perimeter edges: 220 - 40 = 180.\n\nWait a second! Hold on, this is a key point.\n\nWait, the perimeter edges are the edges along the boundary of the grid. The total perimeter edges are 40, as calculated. The remaining edges (220 - 40 = 180) are the inner edges, connecting the inner cells.\n\nIn our earlier analysis, we were only removing perimeter edges (the 40), and specifically 16 of them. The inner edges remain untouched. Therefore, the remaining graph has:\n\n- Perimeter edges remaining: 40 - 16 = 24.\n\n- Inner edges remaining: 180.\n\nTotal edges remaining: 24 + 180 = 204. Correct.\n\nAnd all vertices have even degree:\n\n- Corner vertices: originally degree 2. We didn't remove any edges adjacent to corners (since we only removed edges between non-corner vertices), so corners still have degree 2 (even).\n\n- Non-corner edge vertices: each had degree 3 (2 perimeter, 1 inward). We removed one perimeter edge from each, so now their degree is 2 (1 perimeter, 1 inward) (even).\n\n- Inner vertices: each had degree 4 (all inner connections), and we didn't remove any of their edges, so still degree 4 (even).\n\nTherefore, yes, all degrees are even. Therefore, the remaining graph has an Eulerian circuit with 204 edges.\n\nTherefore, Karthik can traverse 204 doors by following this Eulerian circuit.\n\nBut wait, the question says \"he cannot go through any door more than once (not even in opposite directions)\". Since the Eulerian circuit traverses each edge exactly once, in one direction or the other, but since the graph is undirected, traversing an edge in either direction counts as using the door. Since we're not traversing any door more than once, this satisfies the condition. So 204 seems to be the answer.\n\nBut hold on, the problem is an 11x11 grid of cells, so 11 rows and 11 columns, which gives 121 cells. The perimeter edges are the ones on the border, totaling 40 as above, and inner edges 180. By removing 16 perimeter edges, we have 24 perimeter edges left and 180 inner edges, totaling 204.\n\nBut is there a way to remove fewer edges and still have all degrees even? Because if we can remove fewer edges, then the Eulerian circuit would have more edges. Wait, but we need to make all degrees even, so the minimal number of edges to remove is the minimal number such that all degrees become even, which we calculated as 16 in this case. If we can do it with fewer, then we could have a larger Eulerian circuit.\n\nBut in our analysis, we needed to remove 4 edges per side (total 16), because on each side's path of 9 vertices, we needed to remove 4 edges to make all degrees even. Is there a way to do it with fewer edge removals per side?\n\nSuppose on a single side with 9 non-corner edge vertices, can we remove fewer than 4 edges to make all degrees even?\n\nSuppose we try to remove only 3 edges on a side. Let's see:\n\nTake the path v1-v2-v3-...-v9.\n\nIf we remove edges v1-v2, v3-v4, v5-v6, that's three edges. Then:\n\n- v1 loses v1-v2: degree 1 (perimeter) + inward = 2 (even).\n\n- v2 loses v1-v2: perimeter degree 1 (connected to v3) + inward = 2 (even).\n\n- v3 loses v3-v4: perimeter degree 1 (connected to v2) + inward = 2 (even).\n\n- v4 loses v3-v4: perimeter degree 1 (connected to v5) + inward = 2 (even).\n\n- v5 loses v5-v6: perimeter degree 1 (connected to v4) + inward = 2 (even).\n\n- v6 loses v5-v6: perimeter degree 1 (connected to v7) + inward = 2 (even).\n\n- v7: still connected to v6 and v8. Perimeter degree 2 (since v6-v7 is still there and v7-v8 is still there). So perimeter degree 2 + inward = 3 (odd). Oops, problem.\n\n- v7: perimeter edges to v6 and v8. Since we didn't remove v7-v8, so v7's perimeter degree is 2. Thus, total degree 3 (odd). Not good.\n\n- v8: connected to v7 and v9. Perimeter degree 2 (if v7-v8 is still there). Wait, v8 is connected to v7 and v9. Since we didn't remove v7-v8, then v8's perimeter degree is 2 (connected to v7 and v9). Therefore, total degree 2 + inward = 3 (odd). Also bad.\n\n- v9: connected to v8 and inward. Perimeter degree 1 (only to v8) + inward = 2 (even).\n\nSo, v7 and v8 have odd degrees. Therefore, we still have two odd-degree vertices left. To fix them, we might need to remove another edge, say v7-v8, but that would require a fourth edge removal.\n\nTherefore, with three edge removals, we still have two odd-degree vertices left. Hence, we can't achieve all even degrees with only three removals on a side.\n\nSimilarly, trying to remove two edges:\n\nRemove v1-v2 and v3-v4.\n\nThen:\n\n- v1: perimeter degree 0 + inward = 1 (odd).\n\n- v2: perimeter degree 1 (to v3) + inward = 2 (even).\n\n- v3: perimeter degree 1 (to v2) + inward = 2 (even).\n\n- v4: perimeter degree 1 (to v5) + inward = 2 (even).\n\n- v5 through v9: perimeter degrees 2 or more, leading to odd degrees.\n\nThis is worse. So, it seems that on a side with 9 vertices, we must remove at least 4 edges to make all degrees even. Therefore, 4 per side is indeed minimal.\n\nTherefore, total edges to remove: 16.\n\nTherefore, the maximum number of doors Karthik can go through is 220 - 16 = 204.\n\nWait, but hold on a second. In the process above, we removed 16 perimeter edges, but is there a way to also remove some inner edges in a clever way to allow for a longer trail?\n\nWait, the key point is that the Eulerian circuit in the subgraph with all even degrees gives the maximum number of edges possible without repeating any edge. Because any closed trail cannot have more edges than the number of edges in the graph, and since we've constructed a subgraph with as many edges as possible (by removing the minimal number of edges to make all degrees even), then this must be the maximum.\n\nTherefore, 204 should be the answer.\n\nBut let me check for a smaller grid to see if this reasoning holds.\n\nLet's take a smaller grid, say 3x3 grid (3 rows and 3 columns), so 9 cells. Let's see what the answer would be there, and see if the same reasoning applies.\n\nIn a 3x3 grid:\n\nTotal cells: 9.\n\nTotal edges: horizontal: 3 rows × 2 columns between = 6 horizontal edges.\n\nVertical: 3 columns × 2 rows between = 6 vertical edges.\n\nTotal edges: 12.\n\nDegrees of vertices:\n\nCorners: 4 vertices, each has degree 2.\n\nEdge non-corner vertices: each side has 3 vertices, subtract 2 corners, so 1 non-corner per side. So 4 non-corner edge vertices, each has degree 3.\n\nInner vertices: 1 center vertex, degree 4.\n\nSo, total odd-degree vertices: 4 (the non-corner edge vertices), each of degree 3.\n\nFollowing the same method:\n\nPerimeter edges: each side has 3 vertices, so 2 edges per side. Total perimeter edges: 4 sides × 2 = 8.\n\nNon-perimeter edges: total edges 12 - 8 = 4.\n\nNon-corner edge vertices are the four edge centers: each on a side, between the two corners.\n\nEach side's non-corner edge vertex is connected to two perimeter edges and one inner edge.\n\nTo make all degrees even:\n\nFor each side, the non-corner edge vertex has degree 3. To make it even, need to remove one perimeter edge or one inner edge.\n\nBut if we remove a perimeter edge on a side, say between the corner and the non-corner vertex, but that would affect the corner's degree.\n\nAlternatively, remove an edge between the non-corner vertex and its neighbor along the perimeter.\n\nWait, in the 3x3 grid, each side's non-corner vertex is between two corners? No, in a 3x3 grid, each side has three vertices: corner, non-corner, corner. So the non-corner vertex is in the middle of the side.\n\nTherefore, the non-corner edge vertex on the top side is connected to the top-left corner, top-right corner, and the center vertex.\n\nWait, no: in a 3x3 grid, the top side has three vertices: (1,1) [top-left corner], (1,2) [top-center], (1,3) [top-right corner].\n\nThe vertex (1,2) is connected to (1,1), (1,3), and (2,2) [the center].\n\nTherefore, its degree is 3.\n\nSimilarly for other non-corner edge vertices.\n\nTo make its degree even, we can remove one of its perimeter edges or its inward edge.\n\nIf we remove a perimeter edge, say between (1,1) and (1,2), then the corner (1,1) loses a degree, going from 2 to 1 (odd), which is bad.\n\nAlternatively, remove the inward edge from (1,2) to (2,2). Then (1,2)'s degree becomes 2 (even), but the center vertex (2,2) loses a degree, going from 4 to 3 (odd), which is also bad.\n\nAlternatively, remove both a perimeter edge and an inward edge? But that would reduce degrees by 2 for the non-corner vertex and similarly for another vertex.\n\nWait, this is getting complex. Let's try applying our previous method.\n\nFor the 3x3 grid, each side has one non-corner edge vertex (the middle of the side). The perimeter path on each side is just two edges: between the two corners via the non-corner vertex.\n\nWait, the perimeter path for each side is just the two edges connecting the two corners through the non-corner vertex. So the path on the top side is (1,1)-(1,2)-(1,3). The non-corner vertex is (1,2).\n\nTo make the degree of (1,2) even, we can remove one of its perimeter edges. But removing either (1,1)-(1,2) or (1,2)-(1,3) would affect the corner vertices' degrees.\n\nAlternatively, remove the inward edge from (1,2) to (2,2). Then (1,2) has degree 2 (even), but (2,2) has degree 3 (odd). To fix (2,2), we might need to remove another edge from it, but this could propagate.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps in the 3x3 grid, the maximum closed trail without repeating edges is less than the total edges minus some.\n\nTotal edges: 12.\n\nOdd-degree vertices: 4 (the four non-corner edge vertices), each with degree 3.\n\nTo make all degrees even, need to remove edges such that each odd-degree vertex loses one edge. Since they are all connected via the perimeter and inward edges.\n\nIf we remove the four inward edges from the four non-corner edge vertices to the center, then:\n\n- Each non-corner edge vertex loses one inward edge, so their degrees become 2 (even).\n\n- The center vertex loses four edges, going from degree 4 to 0 (even).\n\nTherefore, all degrees are even. The remaining edges are the 12 - 4 = 8 perimeter edges.\n\nBut the perimeter edges form a cycle of the outer square: 8 edges (each side has two edges, four sides). Wait, the perimeter of the 3x3 grid has 12 perimeter edges? Wait no:\n\nWait, in a 3x3 grid, each side has 3 vertices, so 2 edges per side. Four sides: 8 perimeter edges. The inward edges are the four connecting the non-corner edge vertices to the center, plus the four edges connecting the center to the inner vertices? Wait no, in a 3x3 grid, the center vertex (2,2) is connected to four vertices: up, down, left, right. Wait, actually, in a 3x3 grid, the center cell (2,2) is connected to four cells: (1,2), (3,2), (2,1), (2,3). So four edges. Therefore, the inward edges from the non-corner edge vertices are those four edges to the center.\n\nTherefore, total inward edges: 4 (from the four non-corner edge vertices to the center), and the center has degree 4.\n\nTherefore, if we remove those four inward edges:\n\n- The four non-corner edge vertices now have degree 2 (their two perimeter edges).\n\n- The center has degree 0.\n\n- The corner vertices still have degree 2.\n\nTherefore, all degrees are even. The remaining edges are the 8 perimeter edges. Thus, the remaining graph is just the outer square, which is a cycle of 8 edges. Therefore, an Eulerian circuit exists on this cycle, which has 8 edges. So Karthik could walk 8 doors.\n\nAlternatively, is there a longer trail?\n\nSuppose instead of removing the inward edges, we remove some perimeter edges.\n\nFor example, on each side of the 3x3 grid, we have the two perimeter edges. If we remove one edge from each side's perimeter path, such that each non-corner vertex loses one perimeter edge.\n\nFor the top side: vertices (1,1), (1,2), (1,3). The non-corner vertex is (1,2). To make its degree even, we can remove one of its perimeter edges, say between (1,1)-(1,2). But then corner (1,1) loses a degree, going from 2 to 1 (odd), which is bad. Alternatively, remove the edge between (1,2)-(1,3), same problem with corner (1,3).\n\nAlternatively, remove both perimeter edges on a side? That would make the non-corner vertex have degree 1 (inward edge only) plus the inward edge? Wait no:\n\nIf we remove both perimeter edges on a side for the non-corner vertex, then the non-corner vertex would have only its inward edge, degree 1 (odd). Not good.\n\nAlternatively, this approach doesn't work. So the only way to make all degrees even without affecting corners is to remove the inward edges. Therefore, the maximum trail length is 8 edges.\n\nAlternatively, if we don't remove inward edges, but instead accept that some vertices have odd degrees and try to find the longest trail.\n\nIn the original 3x3 grid, with four odd-degree vertices (the four non-corner edge vertices), the longest closed trail would be the total edges minus the minimal number of edges needed to make all degrees even, which would be 12 - 4 = 8, same as above.\n\nTherefore, in the 3x3 grid, the maximum number of doors is 8.\n\nAlternatively, can we have a trail that uses more than 8 edges? Let's see.\n\nSuppose we try to make a trail that goes around the perimeter except one edge, then goes into the center and back.\n\nBut the center has degree 4, which is even. If we enter the center from one non-corner edge vertex, we have to exit from another, but since all non-corner edge vertices have odd degrees, we can't have a consistent trail.\n\nAlternatively, the maximum trail would indeed be the Eulerian circuit on the outer square, which is 8 edges.\n\nTherefore, in the 3x3 grid, the answer would be 8.\n\nApplying our formula: total edges 12, minus edges removed: 4 (the inward edges), giving 8, which matches.\n\nIn our original problem, following the same logic, we removed 16 perimeter edges, resulting in 204 edges remaining.\n\nBut in the 3x3 case, we had to remove inward edges, not perimeter edges, because removing perimeter edges would affect the corners.\n\nIn the 11x11 grid, we were able to remove only perimeter edges without affecting the corners, because we removed edges between non-corner vertices, not near the corners.\n\nTherefore, in the 11x11 grid, the analogous to the inward edges in the 3x3 grid are the perimeter edges between non-corner vertices. By removing those, we didn't affect the corner degrees, which stayed at 2.\n\nTherefore, the calculation seems consistent.\n\nTherefore, trusting the reasoning, the answer should be 204.\n\nWait, but let me confirm one more thing: in the 11x11 grid, when we remove 16 perimeter edges, the remaining perimeter edges form a graph where the non-corner edge vertices have degree 2 (from perimeter) + 1 (inward) = 3? Wait no, no:\n\nWait, no. Wait, in the 11x11 grid, each non-corner edge vertex originally had:\n\n- Two perimeter edges (except for those adjacent to corners, which have one perimeter edge and one corner edge). Wait no, actually, as established earlier, each non-corner edge vertex has two perimeter edges except those at the ends of the side, which have one perimeter edge and one corner edge. Wait, no, actually in the 11x11 grid, each non-corner edge vertex is between two other non-corner edge vertices or a corner.\n\nWait, let's take a specific example on the top side:\n\nVertex v1 is adjacent to the top-left corner (A) and to v2.\n\nVertex v2 is adjacent to v1 and v3, etc.\n\nVertex v9 is adjacent to v8 and the top-right corner (B).\n\nTherefore, the vertices v1 and v9 each have one perimeter edge adjacent to a corner and one perimeter edge to the next vertex.\n\nTherefore, their perimeter degree is 2: one to the corner and one to the next vertex.\n\nWait a second, this contradicts my earlier analysis.\n\nHold on, let's clarify:\n\nTake vertex v1 on the top side:\n\n- Connected to A (corner) and to v2.\n\nTherefore, two perimeter edges: A-v1 and v1-v2.\n\nSimilarly, vertex v9 on the top side:\n\n- Connected to v8 and to B (corner).\n\nTherefore, two perimeter edges: v8-v9 and v9-B.\n\nThe internal vertices on the top side, like v2 through v8:\n\n- Each connected to two perimeter vertices: e.g., v2 connected to v1 and v3.\n\nTherefore, each non-corner edge vertex on a side has two perimeter edges, except that the ones at the very ends of the side (v1 and v9) are connected to a corner and another non-corner vertex, so still two perimeter edges.\n\nWait a minute! This is different from my earlier analysis. I think I made a mistake earlier.\n\nEach non-corner edge vertex on a side actually has two perimeter edges, because even the ones next to the corners are connected to the corner and to the next non-corner vertex.\n\nTherefore, all non-corner edge vertices on a side have degree 3: two perimeter edges and one inward edge.\n\nTherefore, when we remove an edge between two non-corner edge vertices, say between v1 and v2, then:\n\n- v1 loses one perimeter edge (to v2), so its perimeter degree becomes 1 (only connected to A), and inward degree remains 1, so total degree 2 (even).\n\n- v2 loses one perimeter edge (to v1), so its perimeter degree becomes 1 (only connected to v3), and inward degree remains 1, so total degree 2 (even).\n\nWait, but v1 was originally connected to A and v2 (two perimeter edges), so removing the edge to v2 leaves it with one perimeter edge (to A) and inward edge, so total degree 2.\n\nSimilarly for v2: it loses the edge to v1, so now only connected to v3 and inward edge, total degree 2.\n\nThe corner vertices (A and B) remain connected to their respective non-corner vertices (v1 and v9), so their degrees remain 2 (even).\n\nTherefore, in this case, even the vertices adjacent to the corners lose one perimeter edge but still have their degree reduced to even without affecting the corners.\n\nTherefore, my initial analysis was correct: all non-corner edge vertices can be made even-degree by removing edges between them, without affecting the corners.\n\nTherefore, the earlier calculation holds: per side, removing 4 edges allows all non-corner edge vertices to have even degree, and the corners remain even.\n\nTherefore, total edges removed: 16, total edges remaining: 204.\n\nTherefore, the maximum number of doors Karthik can traverse is 204.\n\nBut hold on, the problem says \"he must end in the same cell he started in\", which is satisfied by an Eulerian circuit. And he cannot go through any door more than once, which is also satisfied because the Eulerian circuit traverses each edge exactly once.\n\nTherefore, 204 should be the answer.\n\nBut I recall that in some grid graphs, the maximum Eulerian trail might be less due to parity issues in the inner vertices, but in this case, since we've made all inner vertices have even degree (they were already even and we didn't touch them), so they remain even. The corners remain even. The non-corner edge vertices are made even by the removals. So everything is good.\n\nWait, but let me think if there's a different way to remove fewer edges.\n\nSuppose instead of removing edges only on the perimeter, we also remove some inward edges in a way that allows us to remove fewer perimeter edges.\n\nFor instance, suppose we remove an inward edge from a non-corner edge vertex and also remove a perimeter edge from its neighbor, thereby fixing two vertices with one edge removal each, but affecting another vertex's degree.\n\nBut this might not lead to a net decrease in the number of edges removed.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's a more efficient way, but given the earlier analysis that on each side we need to remove 4 edges, and there are four sides, leading to 16 total, I think that is minimal.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the four sides can be processed in a way that some edge removals affect two sides? Unlikely, since the sides are separate.\n\nTherefore, I think 16 is indeed the minimal number of edges to remove, leading to 204 doors.\n\nWait, but I just recalled that in an Eulerian circuit, the number of edges is equal to the sum of all degrees divided by 2. Since in the remaining graph, all degrees are even, so sum of degrees is even, and the number of edges is indeed (sum of degrees)/2.\n\nLet me verify with the 11x11 grid after removals:\n\nTotal vertices: 121.\n\nCorners: 4 vertices, each degree 2. Total degree from corners: 4×2 = 8.\n\nNon-corner edge vertices: 36 vertices, each degree 2. Total degree: 36×2 = 72.\n\nInner vertices: 81 vertices, each degree 4. Total degree: 81×4 = 324.\n\nTotal degree sum: 8 + 72 + 324 = 404.\n\nNumber of edges: 404 / 2 = 202.\n\nWait a minute! Hold on, this contradicts the earlier calculation of 204.\n\nWait, what's happening here?\n\nEarlier, I calculated total edges remaining as 220 - 16 = 204, but according to the degree sum, it should be 202.\n\nThere's a discrepancy here. I must have messed up somewhere.\n\nLet me recalculate:\n\nOriginal total edges: 220.\n\nEdges removed: 16 perimeter edges.\n\nTherefore, remaining edges: 220 - 16 = 204.\n\nBut according to the degree sum:\n\nSum of degrees in the remaining graph should equal twice the number of edges.\n\nLet me recalculate the degrees in the remaining graph:\n\nCorners: 4 vertices, each still degree 2. Total: 8.\n\nNon-corner edge vertices: 36 vertices. Originally, each had degree 3. We removed one perimeter edge from each, so each now has degree 2. Total: 36×2 = 72.\n\nInner vertices: 81 vertices. Each originally had degree 4, and none of their edges were removed (since we only removed perimeter edges), so still degree 4. Total: 81×4 = 324.\n\nTotal degree sum: 8 + 72 + 324 = 404.\n\nTherefore, number of edges: 404 / 2 = 202.\n\nBut 220 - 16 = 204. Therefore, there is a discrepancy of 2.\n\nThis suggests that my earlier assumption that we can remove 16 edges without affecting the inner vertices' degrees is wrong.\n\nWait, where is the mistake?\n\nAh! Wait a second: when we remove a perimeter edge between two non-corner edge vertices, we are only removing that one edge. However, each non-corner edge vertex has two perimeter edges and one inward edge.\n\nWhen we remove one perimeter edge from a non-corner edge vertex, its degree decreases by 1 (from 3 to 2). However, the adjacent non-corner edge vertex also loses one perimeter edge (so their degree also decreases by 1). Therefore, each perimeter edge removal affects two vertices, each losing one degree.\n\nTherefore, the total degree decrease per perimeter edge removed is 2 (one from each endpoint).\n\nOriginally, the total degree sum was:\n\nCorners: 4×2 = 8.\n\nNon-corner edge vertices: 36×3 = 108.\n\nInner vertices: 81×4 = 324.\n\nTotal: 8 + 108 + 324 = 440.\n\nAfter removing 16 edges, each removal reduces the total degree by 2, so total degree becomes 440 - 16×2 = 440 - 32 = 408.\n\nTherefore, the remaining edges should be 408 / 2 = 204, which matches the edge count 220 - 16 = 204.\n\nBut in my later calculation, I said that non-