\nYes, I think that is a theorem.\n\nYes, according to some sources, in any graph, the maximum length of a closed trail (circuit) is equal to the number of edges minus half the number of odd-degree vertices. This is because to make the graph Eulerian (all degrees even), you need to remove a set of edges equal to half the number of odd-degree vertices, and the remaining edges form the Eulerian circuit.\n\nTherefore, the formula is:\n\nMaximum closed trail length = E - (number of odd-degree vertices)/2\n\nIn our case:\n\nE = 220,\n\nnumber of odd-degree vertices = 36,\n\nso maximum closed trail length = 220 - 36/2 = 220 - 18 = 202.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 202.\n\nThis seems to align with the bipartite consideration as well, since in bipartite graphs, any cycle must have even length, and the formula still applies.\n\nTherefore, despite the complexity of the grid's structure, the formula gives 202.\n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is 202, so \\boxed{202}.\n\nBut then why did my earlier detailed analysis suggest 204? Because I forgot that the initial removal of 16 edges only reduced the number of odd-degree vertices by 16 (since each edge removal fixes two odd vertices). Wait, no:\n\nWait, each edge removal between two odd-degree vertices reduces the number of odd-degree vertices by 2. Therefore, to go from 36 odd-degree vertices to 0, you need to remove 18 edges, each removing two odd vertices. Hence, the formula E - 18 = 202.\n\nTherefore, my mistake earlier was in assuming that removing 16 edges could fix 32 odd vertices, but actually each edge removal can only fix two odd vertices. Therefore, to fix all 36 odd vertices, you need 18 edge removals, resulting in 220 - 18 = 202 edges remaining.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 202.\n\nThis aligns with the formula.\n\nTherefore, my initial detailed analysis was flawed because I tried to fix the degrees on each side independently, but didn't account for the fact that each edge removal can only fix two