 11 by 11 grid of cells. Each pair of edge-adjacent cells is connected by a door. Karthik wants to walk a path in this grid, starting in any cell, ending in the same cell he started in, without going through any door more than once—not even in opposite directions. The question is asking for the maximum number of doors he can go through in such a path. Hmm, interesting. I need to figure out the maximum number of doors possible in a closed loop path that doesn't repeat any doors, right? Since each door is between two adjacent cells, and if you traverse a door in one direction, you can't go back through it in the opposite direction. So essentially, this is like an Eulerian circuit? Wait, but an Eulerian circuit is a closed path that traverses every edge exactly once, right? But in this case, the grid is a graph where the vertices are the cells, and the edges are the doors between them. So, in graph theory terms, we have a grid graph of 11x11 vertices, with edges between each pair of adjacent vertices (horizontally and vertically adjacent). Then, an Eulerian circuit would require that every edge is traversed exactly once, and the path starts and ends at the same vertex. However, in order for an Eulerian circuit to exist in a graph, the graph must be connected and every vertex must have even degree. \n\nWait, but in this grid graph, each interior cell has four neighbors, so degree four, which is even. Each edge cell (on the border) has three neighbors, so degree three, which is odd. Therefore, the graph is not Eulerian because the vertices on the border have odd degree, so an Eulerian circuit is impossible. Therefore, Karthik cannot traverse every single door in the grid in such a closed path without repeating doors. So, the question is, then, what is the maximum number of doors he can traverse in such a closed path without repeating any door? That is, what's the longest possible closed walk that doesn't repeat any edge (door), starting and ending at the same vertex?\n\n