nt how many vertices of odd degree there are. \n\nFirst, the grid is 11x11, so there are 11 rows and 11 columns, so 121 vertices. The corner vertices: there are four corners, each with degree 2 (even). The edge vertices (but not corners): each side of the grid has 11 vertices, but subtract the two corners, so 9 edge vertices per side. There are four sides, so 4 * 9 = 36 edge vertices, each of degree 3 (odd). Then the remaining vertices are the interior ones, which are (11 - 2) rows and (11 - 2) columns, so 9x9 = 81 interior vertices, each of degree 4 (even). So total number of vertices with odd degree is 36. Since Eulerian trails require exactly zero or two vertices of odd degree, but here we have 36 vertices with odd degree, so we can't have an Eulerian circuit or trail. \n\nTherefore, the maximum closed trail would have to exclude some edges. But how do we maximize the number of edges in such a closed trail? \n\nI recall that in a graph, the maximum length of a closed trail (i.e., a closed walk without repeated edges) is equal to the number of edges minus the minimum number of edges that need to be removed to make the graph Eulerian. Wait, is that a thing? Let me recall... \n\nAlternatively, maybe it's related to the concept of an \"Eulerian trail\" and the concept of adding or removing edges to make a graph Eulerian. Since we have 36 vertices of odd degree, which is even in number (36 is even), so perhaps we can pair up the odd-degree vertices and add edges between them to make all degrees even, but in this case, we can't actually add edges; we can only remove edges. Hmm. Wait, but actually, in order to make a graph Eulerian, you can either add edges or remove vertices, but in this case, we can't add edges because the grid is fixed. Alternatively, perhaps the process is to remove a minimal set of edges so that all the remaining vertices have even degree, and then the remaining graph has an Eulerian circuit. Then the maximum closed trail would be the number of edges in the original graph minus the number of edges removed. \n\nYes, that might be a standard approach. Let me recall: To make a graph Eulerian, if you have k vertices of odd degree, you need to add (k - 2)/2 edges if k is even, but since you can't add edges here, you can instead remove edges to make the degrees even. Alternatively, each edge you remove can change the degrees of two vertices by -1, so it can turn two odd-degree vertices into even-degree vertices, or an even-degree vertex into odd, etc. Hmm, perhaps the process is: To fix the parity of the degrees, since each edge removal changes the degree of two vertices by -1, so if you have an odd degree vertex, you can pair up the odd-degree vertices and for each pair, remove an edge incident to both? Wait, but that might not be possible. \n\nAlternatively, the standard way to compute the maximum trail: if you have a graph with 2k odd-degree vertices, then you can find a closed trail that uses all edges except for k edges, by removing k edges appropriately. Wait, maybe? Let me check. \n\nSuppose you have a graph G with 2k vertices of odd degree. Then, according to the handshaking lemma, the number of odd-degree vertices must be even, which it is here (36). Then, the minimum number of edges you need to remove to make the graph Eulerian is k, since each edge you remove can fix two odd-degree vertices (by reducing their degrees by 1, turning them from odd to even or vice versa). Wait, actually, when you remove an edge, you decrease the degree of each of its two endpoints by 1. So if both endpoints were odd, then they become even. If one was odd and the other even, then the odd one becomes even and the even one becomes odd. Hmm, so actually, it's a bit more involved. \n\nAlternatively, the process is called the \"Eulerian trail augmentation\" or something else? Maybe I need to think in terms of the maximum trail. \n\nWait, here's a theorem: In any graph, the maximum length of a closed trail is equal to the total number of edges minus the minimum number of edges that need to be removed to make the graph Eulerian. Is that true?\n\nLet me see. Let me suppose that. So first, to compute the minimum number of edges to remove to make the graph Eulerian. Since Eulerian requires all degrees even. So starting with a graph with 36 vertices of odd degree, we can pair these up into 18 pairs. For each pair of odd-degree vertices, we can remove an edge that connects them? Wait, but they might not be connected. Alternatively, for each pair, we can remove an edge incident to each of them? Hmm, not sure. \n\nAlternatively, here's a better way. Let me recall that in any graph, the number of edges in the largest Eulerian subgraph (a subgraph that has an Eulerian circuit) is equal to the total number of edges minus the minimum number of edges to remove to make the graph Eulerian. \n\nTherefore, to find the maximum closed trail, which would correspond to the largest Eulerian subgraph, so yes, that would be total edges minus the minimum number of edges to remove to make it Eulerian. \n\nTherefore, if I can compute the minimum number of edges to remove so that all vertices have even degree, then subtract that number from the total number of edges, and that will give me the maximum number of edges in an Eulerian circuit in some subgraph, which would correspond to the maximum closed trail in the original graph. \n\nTherefore, first, let me compute the total number of edges in the 11x11 grid graph. \n\nIn a grid graph of m x n vertices, the number of edges is (m*(n-1) + n*(m-1)). Because horizontally, there are m rows, each with (n-1) horizontal edges per row, so m*(n-1) horizontal edges. Similarly, vertically, there are n columns, each with (m-1) vertical edges per column, so n*(m-1) vertical edges. Therefore, total edges = m*(n - 1) + n*(m - 1) = 2*m*n - m - n. \n\nIn our case, m = 11, n = 11. So total edges = 2*11*11 - 11 - 11 = 2*121 - 22 = 242 - 22 = 220. Wait, let me compute that again:\n\nWait, actually, hold on: The formula is m*(n - 1) for horizontal edges: each row has (n - 1) horizontal edges, and there are m rows. Similarly, vertical edges: each column has (m - 1) vertical edges, and there are n columns. Therefore, total edges = m*(n - 1) + n*(m - 1). \n\nSo plugging in m = n = 11:\n\nTotal edges = 11*(11 - 1) + 11*(11 - 1) = 11*10 + 11*10 = 110 + 110 = 220. Yes, that's correct. 220 edges. \n\nSo total edges: 220. \n\nNow, as we established before, there are 36 vertices of odd degree (the edge non-corner vertices). Since the four corners have degree 2 (even), the other edges (non-corner) have degree 3 (odd), so 36 vertices with odd degree. \n\nTo make the graph Eulerian, we need all vertices to have even degree. Since there are 36 vertices of odd degree, which is an even number, we can pair them up into 18 pairs. For each pair of odd-degree vertices, we can remove an edge incident to each of them to make their degrees even. However, the problem is that the edges we remove have to be chosen such that each edge removal affects two vertices. \n\nWait, actually, to fix the parity of two odd-degree vertices, we can remove an edge that connects them? But if they are not connected, we can't. Alternatively, we can remove an edge incident to one of them, which will decrease its degree by 1, turning it from odd to even, but then the other vertex in the pair still has odd degree. Hmm, perhaps this is not straightforward. \n\nAlternatively, here's a standard approach for this kind of problem: To make all degrees even, we can remove a set of edges such that for each vertex with odd degree, we remove an odd number of edges incident to it. Since each edge removal affects two vertices, the total number of edge removals must be equal to the number of vertices with odd degree divided by 2. Because each edge removal can fix two odd-degree vertices. \n\nWait, actually, let me think in terms of linear algebra over GF(2). Each vertex with an odd degree needs to have its degree reduced by an odd number (to make it even). Each edge removal affects two vertices, decreasing each by 1 (mod 2). So, the problem reduces to finding a set of edges such that the symmetric difference of their endpoints covers all the odd-degree vertices an odd number of times. Since each edge can cover two vertices, and we need to cover 36 vertices, each an odd number of times, but since 36 is even, this is possible? Wait, maybe.\n\nAlternatively, since the sum of the degrees mod 2 must be even (which it is, because the total degree is even), then it is possible to find such a set of edges. The minimal number of edges needed to remove would then be equal to the number of odd-degree vertices divided by 2, since each edge can fix two odd-degree vertices. Since there are 36 odd-degree vertices, so 18 pairs, so 18 edges need to be removed. \n\nWait, is that the case? Let me see. Suppose we have a graph with 2k odd-degree vertices. Then, the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even is k. Because each edge you remove can fix two odd-degree vertices. So in this case, since there are 36 odd-degree vertices, so k = 18, so we need to remove 18 edges. Therefore, the minimal number of edges to remove is 18, so the maximum number of edges in an Eulerian subgraph is 220 - 18 = 202. Therefore, the maximum closed trail would have 202 edges? \n\nBut wait, hold on. Is that actually correct? Because when you remove edges, you have to make sure that the remaining graph is connected? Because an Eulerian circuit also requires the graph to be connected. Hmm, so perhaps my previous reasoning is incomplete because even if you remove 18 edges to make all degrees even, the graph might become disconnected, so then there might not be an Eulerian circuit. \n\nTherefore, I need to ensure that after removing those 18 edges, the graph remains connected. So perhaps the minimal number of edges to remove is actually higher? Or maybe not necessarily, because maybe we can choose which edges to remove so that connectivity is preserved. \n\nHmm, this is getting a bit complicated. Let me think again. \n\nFirst, the problem is asking for the longest possible closed trail (i.e., a closed walk that does not repeat any edge) in the grid graph. In graph theory terms, this is equivalent to finding the longest possible closed trail in the graph. \n\nThere is a theorem that says that in any graph, the length of the longest closed trail is equal to the total number of edges minus the minimum number of edges that need to be removed to make the graph Eulerian, provided that the graph remains connected after the edge removals. \n\nAlternatively, if the graph becomes disconnected after removing those edges, then you might not have a single closed trail covering all those edges. \n\nTherefore, perhaps the key is that the minimal number of edges to remove to make the graph Eulerian while keeping it connected is indeed 18, so then the maximum closed trail would have 220 - 18 = 202 edges. But I need to verify this. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps another approach: in order to have a closed trail that uses as many edges as possible, we can start by considering that such a trail must form a single cycle, but in a grid graph, cycles can be of various lengths, but a single cycle would not use all edges. Alternatively, perhaps the maximum trail can be constructed by traversing the entire grid in a snake-like pattern, but since it has to be a closed trail, you can't just do a Hamiltonian cycle because the grid is 11x11 which is odd-sized, so maybe a Hamiltonian cycle exists? Wait, in a grid graph, does a Hamiltonian cycle exist?\n\nIn a grid graph of m x n vertices, a Hamiltonian cycle exists if and only if both m and n are even, or both are odd? Wait, actually, I think for a rectangular grid graph, a Hamiltonian cycle exists if and only if the graph is balanced, meaning that the number of vertices in each row is the same, but actually, more precisely, according to some the