n Eulerian circuit on the remaining graph. \n\nLet me try removing two edges that are not incident to the same vertex. For instance, remove the edge between (1,2) and (2,2), and the edge between (2,3) and (2,2). \n\nThen, the center vertex (2,2) loses two edges, so its degree goes from 4 to 2 (even). The vertex (1,2) now has degree 2 (since it loses the edge to center), and (2,3) now has degree 2 (loses the edge to center). The other vertices:\n\n- (1,1): still connected to (1,2) and (2,1), so degree 2.\n\n- (1,3): connected to (1,2) and (2,3), so degree 2.\n\n- (3,1): connected to (2,1) and (3,2), so degree 2.\n\n- (3,3): connected to (3,2) and (2,3), so degree 2.\n\n- (2,1): connected to (1,1), (3,1), and (2,2) [but (2,2) is still connected to (2,1)? Wait, no, we only removed edges from (2,2) to (1,2) and (2,3). So (2,1) is still connected to (2,2). Therefore, (2,1) still has degree 3: connected to (1,1), (3,1), and (2,2). Wait, so (2,1) still has degree 3, which is odd. Similarly, (3,2) is connected to (3,1), (3,3), and (2,2). Wait, (3,2) is connected to (3,1), (3,3), and (2,2). Since we didn't remove any edges from (3,2), so (3,2) still has degree 3. \n\nSo after removing those two edges, the vertices (2,1) and (3,2) still have degree 3 (odd), while (1,2) and (2,3) have degree 2 (even). The center (2,2) has degree 2 (even). The corners are all degree 2 (even). So now we still have two vertices of odd degree: (2,1) and (3,2). Therefore, we still need to remove one more edge to fix those. \n\nHmm, so in this case, removing two edges wasn't sufficient because we ended up with two other odd-degree vertices. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps another pair of edges to remove. Let's try removing the edge between (1,2) and (1,1), and the edge between (3,2) and (3,1). \n\nThen, vertex (1,1) loses one edge, going from 2 to 1 (odd), and vertex (3,1) loses one edge, going from 2 to 1 (odd). The vertices (1,2) now has degree 2 (since it lost the edge to (1,1)), and (3,2) has degree 