,1) is connected to (1,1), (3,1), and (2,2). Since we didn't remove any edges from (2,1), its degree remains 3 (odd). Similarly, (2,3) is connected to (1,3), (3,3), and (2,2). Its degree remains 3 (odd). \n\nSo after removing those two edges, we still have two vertices with odd degree: (2,1) and (2,3). So we need to remove one more edge. Let's remove the edge between (2,1) and (2,2). Then:\n\n- (2,1) loses an edge, so degree goes from 3 to 2 (even).\n\n- (2,2) loses another edge, so its degree goes from 2 to 1 (odd). Wait, that's bad because now (2,2) has odd degree again. \n\nHmm, alternatively, remove the edge between (2,1) and (1,1). Then:\n\n- (2,1) loses an edge, going from 3 to 2 (even).\n\n- (1,1) loses an edge, going from 2 to 1 (odd). \n\nNow, (1,1) is odd, and (2,2) is even (since we removed two edges from it earlier). Wait, let's track again:\n\nAfter first two edge removals (edges from center to (1,2) and (3,2)):\n\n- (2,2) has degree 2 (even).\n\n- (2,1) still has degree 3 (odd).\n\n- (2,3) still has degree 3 (odd).\n\nThen, removing edge (2,1)-(1,1):\n\n- (2,1): degree 2 (even).\n\n- (1,1): degree 1 (odd).\n\nNow, the odd-degree vertices are (1,1) and (2,3). So two vertices. Then, to fix those, we need to remove another edge connecting them? But they are not connected. (1,1) is connected to (1,2) and (2,1). (2,3) is connected to (1,3), (3,3), and (2,2). \n\nSo perhaps remove an edge from (1,1) to (1,2). Then:\n\n- (1,1) loses an edge, going from 1 to 0 (even, but degree 0).\n\n- (1,2) loses an edge, going from 2 to 1 (odd). \n\nNow, (1,2) is odd, and (2,3) is still odd. Hmm, this is getting too convoluted. Maybe in the 3x3 grid, the minimal number of edges to remove to make it Eulerian is actually 3? Because starting with four odd-degree vertices, each edge removal can fix two, but due to the graph's structure, you might need an extra one? \n\nAlternatively, perhaps in some cases, you can't pair up all the odd-degree vertices because of the graph's structure, so you have to remove an extra edge. \n\nIn any case, this suggests that in the 3x3 grid, it's not straightforward, and my initial assumption that it's k/2 where k is the number of odd-degree vertices might not hold because of connectivity issues. \n\nTherefore, perhaps for the original problem, the 11x11 grid, it's better to think that the maximum closed trail is 220 - 18 = 202, assuming that we can remove 18 edges in a way that doesn't disconnect the graph and leaves it connected with all even degrees. Given that the grid is so highly connected, it's plausible that such a set of 18 edges can be found. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the maximum number of doors Karthik can traverse is 220 - 2*(number of odd-degree vertices / 2). Wait, no. \n\nAlternatively, let me recall a different approach. In any graph, the longest closed trail is at most the number of edges minus the number of vertices with odd degree divided by 2. Wait, not sure. \n\nWait, actually, according to some references I recall, in a connected graph, the maximum length of a closed trail is equal to the number of edges minus the minimal number of edges needed to make the graph Eulerian. And that minimal number is indeed equal to the number of odd-degree vertices divided by 2, provided that the graph remains connected after the edge removals. \n\nSince in our case, the grid graph is connected, and we can choose which edges to remove (ideally, edges that are not critical for connectivity), then it should be possible to remove 18 edges such that the remaining graph is connected and Eulerian. Therefore, the maximum number of edges in such a trail would be 220 - 18 = 202. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the answer is 220 - 2*(number of odd-degree vertices / 2). Wait, but that's the same as 220 - 18. \n\nAlternatively, another way to think: Each time you have an odd-degree vertex, you need to \"pair it up\" with another one by removing an edge somewhere, but each such pairing requires removing one edge. Since there are 36 odd-degree vertices, you need 18 pairs, so 18 edges. \n\nTherefore, trusting this reasoning, the maximum number of doors is 220 - 18 = 202. \n\nHowever, just to be cautious, let me consider another angle. Suppose we model the grid as a graph and try to construct such a trail. \n\nIn a grid graph, a possible way to create a long closed trail is to snake through the grid, going back and forth, but since it's a closed trail, you have to return to the start without repeating edges. In an even by even grid, you can have a Hamiltonian cycle, but in an odd by odd grid, you can't. However, perhaps you can create a trail that covers almost all edges. \n\nAlternatively, note that in the grid graph, the number of edges is 220. If we can have a trail that uses 202 edges, that leaves 18 edges unused. If those 18 edges form a matching (i.e., a set of edges with no shared vertices), then maybe they can be the ones we removed? Wait, but we need to remove edges to make the degrees even. \n\nAlternatively, the 18 edges that need to be removed are precisely those that are \"left over\" to fix the degrees. Each removal affects two vertices, so 18 removals affect 36 vertices, which are exactly the ones with odd degree. Therefore, those 18 edges must be incident to the odd-degree vertices. \n\nBut since each odd-degree vertex has degree 3 (for the edge non-corner vertices) or 2 (for the corners), but wait the corners have degree 2, which is even, so actually, the odd-degree vertices are all the non-corner edge vertices, each of degree 3. \n\nEach of these 36 vertices has degree 3, which is odd. To make their degrees even, we need to reduce their degrees by 1. Since each edge removal that involves one of these vertices will reduce its degree by 1. Therefore, each such vertex needs to have an odd number of edges removed from it. Since all 36 vertices have odd degree, and each edge removal affects two vertices, the total number of edge removals must be 18, as 36 / 2 = 18. \n\nTherefore, in this case, the edges to be removed must form a matching in the subgraph induced by the odd-degree vertices? Wait, not necessarily a matching, but a set of edges such that every odd-degree vertex is incident to exactly one removed edge. Because each removed edge can \"fix\" two odd-degree vertices by reducing their degrees by 1 (thereby making them even). \n\nTherefore, if we can find a set of 18 edges such that every odd-degree vertex is incident to exactly one edge in this set, then removing those 18 edges will make all degrees even. Such a set is called an \"edge dominating set\" or more specifically, a \"matching\" if no two edges share a vertex, but actually, in this case, since each vertex needs to be incident to exactly one edge in the set, it is a perfect matching on the odd-degree vertices. \n\nHowever, the odd-degree vertices form a 36-vertex subgraph, each of degree 3, but arranged in the grid. Whether such a perfect matching exists is another question. \n\nIn graph theory, a necessary condition for a perfect matching is that the graph is bipartite and satisfies Hall's condition. But here, the subgraph induced by the odd-degree vertices might not be bipartite? Wait, actually, the entire grid is bipartite, so any subgraph is also bipartite. Let me see. \n\nThe grid graph is bipartite, with the two color classes being the black and white squares of a chessboard coloring. The odd-degree vertices are all the edge non-corner vertices. Let's see, in a chessboard coloring, the corners are all one color, say black, and the edge non-corner vertices are alternating colors. Wait, actually, in a chessboard coloring, adjacent vertices are different colors. \n\nLet me think: in the 11x11 grid, let's color the top-left corner (1,1) as black. Then (1,2) is white, (1,3) black, etc. The first row alternates starting with black. The second row starts with white, and so on. \n\nThe corner vertices (1,1), (1,11), (11,1), (11,11) are all black. The edge non-corner vertices: for example, (1,2) is white, (1,10) is black (since 1 + 10 is odd? Wait, coordinates: (1,2) is column 2, which is even, so if rows are alternating, then (1, even column) is white? Wait, perhaps better to think in terms of parity. \n\nThe color of a vertex (i,j) can be determined by (i + j) mod 2. If that's 0, it's one color; else, the other. So, for corner (1,1): 1+1=2 mod 2 = 0, so black. (1,11): 1+11=12 mod 2=0, black. (11,1): 11+1=12 mod 2=0, black. (11,11): 22 mod 2=0, black. So all corners are black. \n\nAn edge non-corner vertex, say (1,2): 1+2=3 mod 2=1, white. (1,3): 1+3=4 mod2=0, black. Wait, so along the top edge, the non-corner vertices alternate between white and black. Similarly, on the right edge (column 11), the non-corner vertices (rows 2 through 10) will have coordinates (i,11), so i + 11. Since 11 is odd, so i + 11 mod2 = i + 1 mod2. So for i from 2 to 10, which are even and odd rows:\n\nFor i even: (even + 1) mod2 = 1, so white.\n\nFor i odd: (odd +1) mod2 = 0, so black.\n\nTherefore, the edge non-corner vertices on the top and bottom edges alternate colors, as do the left and right edges. \n\nTherefore, the subgraph induced by the odd-degree vertices (the 36 edge non-corner vertices) is bipartite, since the entire grid is bipartite. Moreover, in this subgraph, each vertex has degree 3. Wait, but in the subgraph induced by the odd-degree vertices, what is the degree of each vertex? \n\nEach odd-degree vertex in the original graph has degree 3. However, in the subgraph induced by the odd-degree vertices, their degree might be less, because some of their edges go to vertices outside the subgraph (i.e., the corners and interior vertices). \n\nFor example, take a vertex like (1,2), which is an edge non-corner vertex (odd-degree). In the original graph, it is connected to (1,1) [a corner, which is even-degree], (1,3) [another edge non-corner vertex, which is odd-degree], and (2,2) [an interior vertex, which has degree 4, even]. Therefore, in the induced subgraph of the odd-degree vertices, (1,2) is only connected to (1,3), because the other two neighbors are not in the subgraph. So in the induced subgraph, (1,2) has degree 1. \n\nSimilarly, the vertex (1,3): connected to (1,2) [in the subgraph], (1,4) [another odd-degree vertex], and (2,3) [an interior vertex, even-degree]. So in the induced subgraph, (1,3) has degree 2. \n\nContinuing this way, the induced subgraph on the odd-degree vertices is not regular, and has varying degrees. Therefore, it may not be possible to find a perfect matching in this induced subgraph because of the degree constraints. \n\nTherefore, my earlier assumption that we can remove 18 edges, each incident to two odd-degree vertices, might not hold because the induced subgraph may not have a perfect matching. \n\nHmm, this complicates things. So perhaps the minimal number of edges to remove is actually more than 18? Or perhaps we need to remove some edges that are not between two odd-degree vertices, which would then affect two odd-degree vertices and one even-degree vertex? \n\nWait, if we remove an edge that connects an odd-degree vertex to an even-degree vertex, then that odd-degree vertex's degree becomes even, but the even-degree vertex's degree becomes odd. So, this would turn one odd into even, but one even into odd, so the total number of odd-degree vertices remains the same. Therefore, such a removal is not helpful for reducing the number of odd-degree vertices. \n\nTherefore, to reduce the number of odd-degree vertices by two, we must remove an edge between two odd-degree vertices. If we remove an edge between an odd and even-degree vertex, it just transfers the oddness to the even-degree vertex. Therefore, to fix the parity, we need to remove edges only between odd-degree vertices. \n\nTherefore, the set of edges we must remove must form a matching in the induced subgraph of the odd-degree vertices. However, if that induced subgraph does not have a perfect matching, then we cannot achieve the reduction in the number of odd-degree vertices by just removing edges between them. \n\nTherefore, in such a case, we might need to remove some edges that are not between odd-degree vertices, but then that would create new odd-degree vertices elsewhere, necessitating additional edge removals. \n\nThis seems like a problem. Therefore, perhaps the minimal number of edges to remove is actually higher than 18? \n\nAlternatively, perhaps in the 11x11 grid, the induced subgraph on the odd-degree vertices (the 36 edge non-corner vertices) does have a perfect matching? \n\nGiven that the induced subgraph is bipartite (since the entire grid is bipartite), and if it satisfies Hall's condition, then a perfect matching exists. \n\nLet me recall Hall's theorem: In a bipartite graph with partitions A and B, a perfect matching exists if and only if for every subset S of A, the number of neighbors of S is at least |S|. \n\nSo, let me consider the induced subgraph on the odd-degree vertices, which form a bipartite graph. Let me denote the two partitions as follows: since the entire grid is bipartite with partitions based on (i+j) mod 2, and the odd-degree vertices are all in one partition or the other? \n\nWait, in the original grid, the odd-degree vertices are the edge non-corner vertices. Let me see their colors. \n\nEarlier, we saw that the corner vertices are all black (assuming (1,1) is black). The edge non-corner vertices: let's take (1,2): (1+2)=3 mod2=1, so white. (1,3): 4 mod2=0, black. (1,4): 5 mod2=1, white, etc. So along the top edge, the non-corner edge vertices alternate between white and black. Similarly, on the right edge, non-corner vertices: (2,11): (2+11)=13 mod2=1, white; (3,11): 14 mod2=0, black; etc. \n\nTherefore, the edge non-corner vertices are distributed between both color partitions. Specifically, half of them are white and half are black? Since there are 36 of them, which is even, so 18 white and 18 black. \n\nTherefore, the induced subgraph on the odd-degree vertices is a bipartite graph with partitions of size 18 and 18. \n\nNow, to check if this bipartite graph has a perfect matching, we need to verify Hall's condition. \n\nHall's condition requires that for every subset S of one partition, the number of neighbors of S is at least |S|. \n\nGiven the complexity of the induced subgraph, it's hard to verify Hall's condition directly, but perhaps we can argue that since the graph is highly connected (being part of a grid), it likely satisfies Hall's condition. \n\nIn a grid-like structure, the induced subgraph on the edge non-corner vertices is likely to be connected and sufficiently dense to satisfy Hall's condition. \n\nAlternatively, since each vertex in the induced subgraph has degree at least 1 (since they are connected to other edge non-corner vertices along the edges of the grid), and given the grid's regularity, it's plausible that a perfect matching exists. \n\nAssuming that such a perfect matching exists, then we can remove 18 edges (the ones in the matching) between the odd-degree vertices, thereby reducing all their degrees by 1, turning them all to even. The center vertices and corners, which were even, remain even since we only removed edges between the odd-degree vertices. \n\nTherefore, the remaining graph would have all even degrees and be connected (since we only removed edges from the induced subgraph, which is part of the original connected graph). Therefore, an Eulerian circuit exists, and thus the maximum closed trail is 220 - 18 = 202. \n\nTherefore, under the assumption that such a perfect matching exists in the induced subgraph of the odd-degree vertices, the answer is 202. \n\nHowever, to be thorough, let me consider a smaller grid where I can manually verify. Let's take the 3x3 grid again. \n\nIn the 3x3 grid, the odd-degree vertices are the four edge non-corner vertices: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2). The induced subgraph on these four vertices: \n\n- (1,2) is connected to (1,3) [but (1,3) is a corner, so not in the subgraph] and (2,2) [interior, not in subgraph], and (1,1) [corner, not in subgraph]. Wait, no, in the induced subgraph, only the odd-degree vertices are included. Wait, no: the induced subgraph on the odd-degree vertices includes only those vertices, and edges between them. \n\nIn the 3x3 grid, the odd-degree vertices are (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2). \n\nEdges between them:\n\n- (1,2) is adjacent to (2,2) [not in subgraph] and (1,3) [corner, not in subgraph], and (2,1) [wait, (1,2) is adjacent to (2,2) which is interior, but also to (1,1) and (1,3), which are corners. Wait, actually in the induced subgraph, (1,2) is only connected to other odd-degree vertices if they are adjacent in the grid. \n\nLooking at coordinates:\n\n(1,2) is adjacent to (1,1) [corner], (1,3) [corner], and (2,2) [interior]. None of these are in the subgraph of odd-degree vertices except none. Therefore, in the induced subgraph, (1,2) has no edges! Similarly, (2,1) is adjacent to (1,1) [corner], (3,1) [corner], and (2,2) [interior]. So also no edges in the induced subgraph. \n\nWait a second! In the 3x3 grid, the induced subgraph on the four edge non-corner vertices has no edges between them? Because each of those vertices is only adjacent to corners or the center, which are not in the subgraph. Therefore, the induced subgraph is four isolated vertices. \n\nTherefore, in this case, there is no edge between any of the odd-degree vertices. Therefore, we cannot form any matching in this induced subgraph because there are no edges. Therefore, we cannot fix the degrees by removing edges between the odd-degree vertices. \n\nTherefore, in this case, we have to remove edges that connect an odd-degree vertex to an even-degree vertex. \n\nFor instance, take the vertex (1,2), which is odd-degree. It is connected to (1,1) [degree 2, even], (1,3) [degree 2, even], and (2,2) [degree 4, even]. To fix (1,2)'s degree, we can remove one of its three edges. Suppose we remove the edge between (1,2) and (2,2). Then, (1,2)'s degree becomes 2 (even), and (2,2)'s degree becomes 3 (odd). Now, (2,2) is now odd-degree. So we've turned one odd-degree vertex into even, but created a new odd-degree vertex. \n\nSimilarly, if we remove an edge from (1,2) to (1,1), then (1,2)'s degree becomes 2 (even), and (1,1)'s degree becomes 1 (odd). So again, creating a new odd-degree vertex. \n\nTherefore, in the 3x3 grid, each time we remove an edge incident to an odd-degree vertex, we either fix that vertex but create a new odd-degree vertex elsewhere. Therefore, the total number of odd-degree vertices remains the same. Hence, it's impossible to reduce the number of odd-degree vertices by just removing edges; we have to remove edges in pairs that affect two odd-degree vertices. But since in the 3x3 grid's induced subgraph of odd-degree vertices has no edges, we cannot do that. \n\nTherefore, in such a case, the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even is actually equal to the number of odd-degree vertices, because each removal can only fix one vertex at the cost of creating another. Wait, but that would mean it's impossible? \n\nWait, in the 3x3 grid, we have four odd-degree vertices. Each edge removal can fix one odd-degree vertex but create another, so the total number remains the same. Therefore, it's impossible to reduce the number of odd-degree vertices to zero? But that can't be, because the total degree is even. \n\nWait, the total degree is 4*3 (each of the four odd-degree vertices has degree 3) plus the other vertices' degrees. Let's compute total degree in 3x3 grid: \n\nCorners (4 vertices): degree 2 each, total 8.\n\nEdge non-corner (4 vertices): degree 3 each, total 12.\n\nCenter (1 vertex): degree 4, total 4.\n\nTotal degree: 8 + 12 + 4 = 24. Which is even, as required. \n\nIf we remove an edge, the total degree decreases by 2 (since each edge removal subtracts 1 from two vertices). Therefore, starting from 24, after removing k edges, total degree is 24 - 2k. To have all degrees even, we need total degree even, which it will be. However, the number of odd-degree vertices must be even. \n\nInitially, there are 4 odd-degree vertices. After removing an edge between two even-degree vertices, the number of odd-degree vertices remains 4. If we remove an edge between an odd and even-degree vertex, then we turn the odd into even and the even into odd, so the count remains 4. If we remove an edge between two odd-degree vertices, then both become even, so the count decreases by 2. \n\nBut in the 3x3 grid, there are no edges between two odd-degree vertices, as we saw. Therefore, we cannot perform the latter. Therefore, in the 3x3 grid, it's impossible to reduce the number of odd-degree vertices below 4 by edge removals. Therefore, the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even is actually impossible? But that can't be, since you can have a graph with all even degrees by removing edges appropriately. \n\nWait, but in the 3x3 grid, if you remove all edges incident to the center vertex, then the center vertex becomes degree 0 (even), and its neighbors lose edges. For example, if we remove all four edges from the center to the four edge non-corner vertices, then:\n\n- The center vertex has degree 0 (even).\n\n- Each of the four edge non-corner vertices now have degree 2 (since they lost one edge to the center). So their degrees go from 3 to 2 (even).\n\n- The corners remain at degree 2 (even).\n\nThus, after removing four edges (the four edges from the center to the edge non-corner vertices), all vertices have even degree. So in this case, we removed four edges to fix all degrees. \n\nTherefore, in this case, the minimal number of edges to remove is four, which is equal to the number of odd-degree vertices (four). \n\nTherefore, in cases where the odd-degree vertices are not connected to each other (like in the 3x3 grid), you have to remove edges from each odd-degree vertex t