lumn, so edge non-corner. (2,2) is interior. (3,2) is interior. \n\nTherefore, the vertex (2,2) is adjacent to:\n\n- North: (1,2) [edge non-corner]\n\n- South: (3,2) [interior]\n\n- West: (2,1) [edge non-corner]\n\n- East: (2,3) [interior]\n\nTherefore, (2,2) is adjacent to two edge non-corner vertices and two interior vertices. \n\nTherefore, the interior vertices adjacent to edge non-corner vertices are those that are directly below, above, left, or right of an edge non-corner vertex. \n\nHowever, each edge non-corner vertex is only adjacent to one interior vertex. Wait, let's take (1,2): it is adjacent to (2,2) [interior] and to (1,1) [corner] and (1,3) [edge non-corner]. So only one interior neighbor. Similarly, (2,1) is adjacent to (1,1) [corner], (3,1) [edge non-corner], and (2,2) [interior]. So only one interior neighbor. \n\nTherefore, each edge non-corner vertex has exactly one interior neighbor and two edge non-corner neighbors (except for the ones at the ends of the edge paths). Wait, actually, along the top edge path from (1,2) to (1,10), the endpoints (1,2) and (1,10) each have only one edge non-corner neighbor (since they are at the ends of the path), while the internal vertices on the top edge path (like (1,3), (1,4), etc.) have two edge non-corner neighbors. \n\nTherefore, the subgraph of the edge non-corner vertices consists of four paths (top, bottom, left, right), each of length 9 vertices (so 8 internal edges). Each of these paths has two endpoints (the ones at the \"corners\" of the path, i.e., closest to the grid corners) which have degree 2 within the path plus one connection to an interior vertex, so total degree 3. The internal vertices of the paths have degree 2 within the path plus two connections to interior vertices? Wait no:\n\nWait, take a middle vertex on the top edge path, say (1,5). It is connected to (1,4) and (1,6) along the top edge (so two edge non-corner neighbors), and to (2,5) (an interior vertex). Therefore, its degree withi