cent) + 28 (single connections) = 32. Therefore, 81 - 32 = 49 interior vertices are not connected to any edge non-corner vertices. \n\nTherefore, when we remove all 36 edges from edge non-corner vertices to their interior neighbors, we affect:\n\n- The four corner-adjacent interior vertices: each loses two edges, so their degree goes from 4 to 2 (even).\n\n- The 28 interior vertices connected to one edge non-corner vertex: each loses one edge, so their degree goes from 4 to 3 (odd). \n\n- The 49 interior vertices not connected to any edge non-corner vertices: their degrees remain 4 (even). \n\n- The edge non-corner vertices: each loses one edge (to their interior neighbor), so their degree goes from 3 to 2 (even). \n\nTherefore, after this removal:\n\n- All edge non-corner vertices are even degree.\n\n- The four corner-adjacent interior vertices are even degree.\n\n- The 28 interior vertices that were connected to one edge non-corner vertex are now odd degree (3).\n\n- The remaining 49 interior vertices remain even degree (4).\n\n- The four corner vertices remain even degree (2).\n\nTherefore, the total number of odd-degree vertices is 28. \n\nHmm, so by removing all 36 edges from edge non-corner vertices to their interior neighbors, we've reduced the number of odd-degree vertices from 36 to 28. Not enough. \n\nTo further reduce the number of odd-degree vertices, we need to perform additional edge removals. Each additional edge removal can affect two vertices. \n\nSuppose we now focus on the 28 odd-degree interior vertices. Each of these is connected to four neighbors, but one of those neighbors is an edge non-corner vertex (which is now even degree) or another interior vertex. \n\nWait, the 28 interior vertices that are now odd-degree (degree 3) are each connected to one edge non-corner vertex (which we've already removed the edge to) and three other interior vertices. \n\nFor example, take an interior vertex like (2,3), which was connected to (1,3) [edge non-corne