es such that each pair is connected by a single edge. But in the grid graph, the odd-degree vertices are the ones on the edges (but not corners). So, the 36 non-corner edge vertices each have degree 3 (odd), and the four corners have degree 2 (even). So, we have 36 odd-degree vertices. To fix all of them, we need to pair them up into 18 pairs, and for each pair, delete a set of edges such that the effect is to decrease the degree of both vertices in the pair by 1 (thereby making them even). However, the challenge is to do this with as few edge deletions as possible.\n\nBut if two odd-degree vertices are adjacent, then we can just delete the edge between them, which will decrease both degrees by 1, making them even. So, that's one edge deletion per pair if they are adjacent. However, if they are not adjacent, then we need a path between them, and deleting all edges along that path would decrease the degrees of all vertices along the path by 1, thereby flipping their parities. So, for a path of length k (i.e., k edges), you would flip the parity of k+1 vertices. But we need to flip the parity of exactly two vertices (the ones we are pairing) and leave the rest unchanged. Therefore, this complicates things because flipping an intermediate vertex's parity might mess up other pairs.\n\nTherefore, the minimal number of edge deletions required is actually equal to the number of odd-degree vertices divided by 2 only if all the odd-degree vertices can be paired with adjacent partners. But in the grid, the odd-degree vertices are the ones along the edges (but not corners). So, for instance, take a single edge of the grid (like the top edge), which has 11 vertices, but the two corners are degree 2, so the 9 non-corner edge vertices on that edge each have degree 3. Similarly for the other three edges.\n\nSo, on each side of the grid, there are 9 vertices with degree 3. Let's consider one side first: the top side. The 9 non-corner vertices on the top edge each have degree 3. Each of these is adjacent to two other vertices on the top edge (except the corners, but the corners are not included here) and one vertex below it (the cell just below on the next row). Similarly, the vertices on the bottom edge, left edge, and right edge each have similar structures.\n\nTherefore, perhaps we can pair up the odd-degree vertices along the same edge? For example, on the top edge, we have 9 vertices each of degree 3. If we can pair them up in adjacent pairs along the edge, then deleting the edge between each pair would fix two vertices at a time. However, since they are already on the edge, the edges between them are the horizontal edges along the top edge. So, for the top edge, which is a row of 11 cells, the horizontal edges between them are 10 in total. The non-corner vertices on the top edge are the 9 cells from position (1,2) to (1,10) if we consider the top-left corner as (1,1). Each of these has a left and right neighbor on the top edge except the very first and last (which are corners). Wait, actually, the non-corner vertices on the top edge are from column 2 to column 10 in row 1, so 9 vertices. Each of these has a left neighbor (except the leftmost one, which is column 2, which has a left neighbor at column 1, which is a corner, but the corner has degree 2). Wait, actually, each non-corner vertex on the top edge (say, at (1,j) where 2 ≤ j ≤ 10) has neighbors: left at (1, j-1), right at (1, j+1), and below at (2,j). Therefore, their degree is 3, as expected.\n\nTherefore, along the top edge, between the non-corner vertices, the horizontal edges are between (1,2)-(1,3), (1,3)-(1,4), ..., (1,9)-(1,10). So, 8 horizontal edges along the top edge between the non-corner vertices. Additionally, each non-corner vertex on the top edge also has a downward edge to the cell below.\n\nNow, if we want to pair up the 9 non-corner vertices on the top edge, we could pair them as (1,2) with (1,3), (1,4) with (1,5), ..., but since 9 is odd, we can't pair them all.