, actually, each edge deletion affects two vertices, so each deletion can be thought of as contributing a +1 to the count of flipped vertices for two vertices. Therefore, the problem reduces to covering all O odd-degree vertices with a set of \"flips\" where each flip (i.e., edge deletion) can cover two vertices, and we need to cover all O vertices with an odd number of flips for each? Wait, no, actually, each flip (edge deletion) affects two vertices, and we need each odd-degree vertex to be affected an odd number of times (to turn their degree from odd to even). Since the total number of flips (edge deletions) times 2 must equal the total number of \"flips needed\", which is O (since each of the O vertices needs to be flipped once). Wait, but O is even, so O/2 edge deletions would do it? Because each edge deletion flips two vertices, so O/2 edge deletions would flip O vertices total. But each vertex needs to be flipped exactly once. So, yes, in theory, if we can find a set of O/2 edge deletions such that each odd-degree vertex is incident to exactly one deleted edge, then that would work.\n\nBut in reality, in a grid graph, the odd-degree vertices are arranged in a way that might not allow such a perfect matching of their incident edges. Because each odd-degree vertex has degree 3, so three edges incident to it. So, for each odd-degree vertex, we need to choose one of its three edges to delete, such that for every pair, the edges chosen form a matching (i.e., no two edges share a common vertex). Wait, actually, no. Wait, actually, each edge deletion is an edge, and each edge deletion \"covers\" two vertices (the ones it's incident to). So, if we can select a set of edges such that every odd-degree vertex is incident to exactly one edge in the set, then that set would be a matching that covers all odd-degree vertices, and the number of edges in such a matching would be O/2, since each edge covers two vertices. Since O is even, this is possible only if such a matching exists.\n\nTherefore, the minimal number of edges to delete is O/2 = 36/2 = 18, provided that such a matching exists. So, if we can find a set of 18 edges in the grid graph such that every odd-degree vertex is incident to exactly one edge in the set, then deleting those 18 edges would make all degrees even, and thus the remaining graph would have an Eulerian circuit.\n\nTherefore, the question reduces to: does the grid graph have a matching that covers all 36 odd-degree vertices? Since each edge in the matching can cover two odd-degree vertices, and we have 36 odd-degree vertices, so we need 18 edges in the matching.\n\nBut in the grid graph, the odd-degree vertices are precisely the non-corner edge vertices, each of which has degree 3. Each such vertex has three edges: two along the edge of the grid (e.g., horizontal or vertical) and one going inward into the grid.\n\nWait, for example, take a non-corner vertex on the top edge: it has a left neighbor (another non-corner vertex on the top edge, unless it's the leftmost non-corner), a right neighbor (another non-corner vertex on the top edge, unless it's the rightmost non-corner), and a downward neighbor (a vertex in the second row). Similarly for other edges.\n\nSo, each non-corner edge vertex has two neighbors along the edge (unless it's near the corner, but even then, one neighbor is a corner which is even degree). Wait, actually, the very first non-corner vertex on the top edge (say, position (1,2)) has a left neighbor at the corner (1,1), which is a degree 2 vertex, and a right neighbor at (1,3), and a downward neighbor at (2,2). Similarly, the next one (1,3) has left at (1,2), right at (1,4), and downward at (2,3), etc., up to (1,10), which has left at (1,9), right at the corner (1,11), and downward at (2,10). So each non-corner edge vertex has two neighbors along the edge (one of which may be a corner) and one inward neighbor.\n\nTherefore, for each such vertex, we can choose to delete one of its three edges. The challenge is to choose edges such that no two chosen edges share a common vertex, and that all 36 odd-degree vertices are covered.\n\nThis seems similar to a matching problem in the graph induced by the odd-degree vertices and their incident edges, but it's a bit more complicated because the edges we can choose are the edges incident to the odd-degree vertices, but the vertices we are trying to cover are the odd-degree vertices themselves.\n\nWait, actually, each edge we choose to delete is incident to two vertices. If we delete an edge between two odd-degree vertices, then both of those vertices get their degrees reduced by 1 (to even), so that's good. But if we delete an edge between an odd-degree vertex and an even-degree vertex (like a corner), then the odd-degree vertex's degree becomes even (good), but the even-degree vertex's degree becomes odd (bad). So, we have to be careful not to delete edges that connect an odd-degree vertex to an even-degree vertex unless we can \"pair\" that in some way.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to only delete edges that connect two odd-degree vertices. Because if we delete an edge between two odd-degree vertices, then both become even, which is perfect. Whereas deleting an edge between an odd and an even vertex would ruin the even vertex's parity. Therefore, to avoid creating new odd vertices, we should only delete edges that are between two odd-degree vertices.\n\nTherefore, if we can find a matching consisting solely of edges between two odd-degree vertices, then that would be ideal, since each such edge deletion would fix two odd-degree vertices without introducing any new odd-degree vertices. Therefore, such a matching would be the best possible.\n\nTherefore, the question becomes: does the grid graph contain a matching that covers all 36 odd-degree vertices, using only edges that connect two odd-degree vertices?\n\nLet me see. Let's look at the structure of the grid.\n\nFirst, note that the odd-degree vertices are all the non-corner edge vertices. Let's consider the four sides of the grid. On each side, the non-corner vertices form a line of 9 vertices. For example, the top side has vertices from (1,2) to (1,10), each of which is an odd-degree vertex.\n\nNow, along the top side, the edges between these vertices are the horizontal edges along the top row. Each horizontal edge between two adjacent non-corner vertices on the top row connects two odd-degree vertices. Similarly, the vertical edges from each non-corner vertex on the top row down to the second row also connect an odd-degree vertex to an interior vertex (which is even-degree, since interior vertices have degree 4). So, the vertical edges from the top row's non-corner vertices go to interior vertices, which are even-degree.\n\nTherefore, on the top side, the edges that connect two odd-degree vertices are only the horizontal edges between the non-corner vertices on the top row. Similarly, on the bottom row, the non-corner vertices have horizontal edges among themselves and vertical edges going up to the second-to-last row, which are interior vertices (even-degree). Similarly for the left and right sides.\n\nTherefore, on each side of the grid, the horizontal edges between the non-corner vertices on that side connect two odd-degree vertices. The vertical edges on each side connect an odd-degree vertex to an even-degree vertex (interior vertex). Therefore, if we can form a matching using only the horizontal edges along each side, that might work.\n\nLet me consider the top side first. On the top side, there are 9 non-corner vertices, so 8 horizontal edges between them. These 8 edges connect pairs of odd-degree vertices. If we can select a matching on these 8 edges that covers as many as possible of the 9 vertices. Wait, but 9 is odd, so we can't cover all 9 with a matching on just the horizontal edges on the top side, because a matching can cover at most floor(9/2) = 4 pairs, covering 8 vertices, leaving one vertex uncovered.\n\nSimilarly, the same applies to each of the four sides. Each side has 9 non-corner vertices, so on each side, the horizontal edges (if we're on a horizontal side) can cover at most 4 pairs, leaving one vertex per side uncovered. Since there are four sides, that would leave 4 vertices uncovered. Then, we need to cover those remaining four vertices with edges from other sides or from vertical edges?\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can use the vertical edges as well, but vertical edges connect an odd-degree vertex to an even-degree vertex, which would ruin the even-degree vertex's parity. Therefore, perhaps it's better to avoid using vertical edges for deletions because they introduce new odd vertices.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use vertical edges in combination with horizontal edges on adjacent sides?\n\nWait, let's try to think of the entire perimeter of the grid. The perimeter consists of the outermost layer of cells. The non-corner edge vertices are all on the perimeter, except for the corners. Each side of the perimeter has 9 non-corner vertices. The perimeter itself forms a cycle of 11 cells on each side, but since the corners are counted in two sides, the total number of perimeter vertices is 11*4 - 4 = 44 (since each corner is counted in two sides), but actually, each side has 11 vertices including the corners, so total perimeter vertices: 4*11 - 4 = 44 (since the four corners are counted twice). Therefore, the perimeter has 44 vertices, of which 4 are corners (degree 2) and 40 are non-corner edge vertices (degree 3). Wait, but earlier we thought there were 36 non-corner edge vertices. Wait, perhaps miscalculation.\n\nWait, let's recast:\n\nIn an n x n grid, the number of perimeter vertices is 4n - 4. For n=11, that's 4*11 - 4 = 44. Of these, 4 are corners (each corner is a single vertex), so the remaining 40 are edge vertices (non-corner). But each edge has n vertices, including the two corners. So, for each side, the number of non-corner edge ve