e 4.\n\nTherefore, the number of non-corner edge vertices:\n\nOn each side, there are 12 vertices, minus 2 corners, so 10 non-corner edge vertices per side. Four sides, so 4*10 = 40 non-corner edge vertices. Each of these has degree 3 (odd).\n\nThe four corner vertices have degree 2 (even).\n\nThe remaining vertices are interior vertices: total vertices 144 - 4 (corners) - 40 (non-corner edges) = 100 interior vertices, each of degree 4 (even).\n\nTherefore, total odd-degree vertices: 40.\n\nWait, this is different from my initial calculation where I thought it was 36. That was my mistake earlier. So actually, there are 40 odd-degree vertices (the 40 non-corner edge vertices), each of degree 3.\n\nTherefore, O = 40, which is even, so it's possible to have an Eulerian circuit after deleting O/2 = 20 edges, provided we can find such a matching.\n\nTherefore, my previous calculation was incorrect because I miscalculated the number of non-corner edge vertices. So, now, with 40 odd-degree vertices, we need to delete 20 edges such that each odd-degree vertex is incident to exactly one deleted edge, and the deleted edges form a matching (no two deleted edges share a vertex). Wait, actually, in graph theory terms, a matching is a set of edges without common vertices. So, if we can find a matching that covers all 40 odd-degree vertices, meaning that every odd-degree vertex is incident to exactly one edge in the matching, then that would require 20 edges (since each edge covers two vertices), and that would be perfect.\n\nTherefore, the question is: does the grid graph contain a perfect matching that covers all the 40 non-corner edge vertices? Each non-corner edge vertex must be matched with another non-corner edge vertex via an edge that connects them.\n\nNow, the edges that connect two non-corner edge vertices are the horizontal edges on the sides and the vertical edges on the sides? Wait, no.\n\nWait, the edges that connect two non-corner edge vertices must be edges that ar