ween two non-corner edge vertices. Similarly, on the left side, the vertical edges between non-corner vertices are between two non-corner edge vertices.\n\nTherefore, each of these edges is between two odd-degree vertices (the non-corner edge vertices). Therefore, if we delete such an edge, both endpoints (which are non-corner edge vertices) will have their degrees reduced by 1, turning them from odd to even. Perfect! So, if we delete these 20 edges (5 per side, along the perimeter edges), then all 40 non-corner edge vertices will have their degrees reduced by 1 (from 3 to 2), becoming even. The corner vertices remain at degree 2 (even), and the interior vertices remain at degree 4 (even). Therefore, the entire graph after deleting these 20 edges will have all vertices of even degree, so it will have an Eulerian circuit.\n\nTherefore, the minimal number of edges to delete is 20, so the maximal closed trail Karthik can take is the total number of edges minus 20, which is 220 - 20 = 200.\n\nWait, but hold on. Let me confirm this again. If we delete 20 edges, each deletion removes one edge, so total edges remaining are 220 - 20 = 200. And the remaining graph has an Eulerian circuit, so an Eulerian circuit traverses each remaining edge exactly once, so the length of the trail is 200. Therefore, Karthik could walk 200 doors? But the problem says he must start and end at the same cell, without repeating any door. So that would be possible with an Eulerian circuit of 200 doors.\n\nBut wait, before I get too excited, let me verify this reasoning again because it's easy to make a mistake here.\n\nFirst, total edges: 220. If we can delete 20 edges such that the remaining graph is Eulerian (all degrees even), then an Eulerian circuit exists, which would traverse all 200 edges. Therefore, the maximum number of doors he can go through is 200. However, is this actually possible?\n\nWait, but in the process of deleting edges, we have to delete edges that form a perfect matching on each side's non-corner edge vertices. For each side, which has 10 non-corner edge vertices arranged in a path of 10 vertices, we can indeed find a perfect matching. For example, on a path of 10 vertices, you can match the first with the second, third with fourth, etc. But actually, a path graph with an even number of vertices does have a perfect matching. In fact, any path with an even number of vertices can be decomposed into a perfect matching by pairing consecutive vertices. Wait, actually, no. Wait, a path graph with 10 vertices can be matched by taking every other edge? Wait, perhaps I need to think of it as a line of vertices: v1 - v2 - v3 - ... - v10. A perfect matching would consist of edges (v1,v2), (v3,v4), ..., (v9,v10). That's 5 edges, which is a perfect matching. Alternatively, you could shift it: (v2,v3), (v4,v5), etc., but as long as you pick non-overlapping edges that cover all vertices.\n\nYes, so on each side's path of 10 vertices, there exists a perfect matching using the edges along that side. Therefore, we can perform such a matching on each side, resulting in 5 edges per side, 20 total edges, all of which are along the perimeter between non-corner edge vertices. Therefore, deleting those 20 edges would turn all non-corner edge vertices into degree 2 (since they originally had degree 3, minus 1 for the deleted edge), and leave the corners at degree 2 and interior vertices at degree 4. Therefore, all degrees even, so Eulerian circuit exists.\n\nTherefore, the remaining graph has 200 edges and is Eulerian, so Karthik can traverse all 200 edges in a closed trail, starting and ending at the same cell, without repeating any door. Therefore, the maximum number of doors he can go through is 200. Therefore, is 200 the answer?\n\nBut wait, hold on a second. There's a problem here. When we delete edges along the perimeter, we are removing edges that are part of the perimeter, but the Eulerian circuit in the remaining graph may not necessarily allow starting and ending at the same cell in the original grid? Wait, no, the Eulerian circuit is in the remaining graph, which is a subgraph of the original grid. The starting and ending cell just has to be a vertex in the remaining graph with even degree, which they all are. So, in the remaining graph, any vertex can be the start and end. So, as long as the remaining graph is connected, which it is, since we only removed edges, not vertices, then an Eulerian circuit exists, and can start anywhere.\n\nWait, but is the remaining graph connected? If we remove 20 edges from the grid, is it still connected? Well, the grid was originally connected. Removing edges can potentially disconnect the graph, but in this case, we are only removing edges along the perimeter, but not any edges that are necessary for connectivity. Since the interior of the grid is still fully connected via the interior edges, and the perimeter edges we removed are just a matching on each side, so each side still has a path through the interior to connect to other sides. Wait, actually, let's think: if we remove some edges along the perimeter, does that disconnect the graph?\n\nSuppose we remove a horizontal edge on the top side between two non-corner vertices. That just removes a single edge from the top perimeter; the rest of the top perimeter is still connected via other edges, and the interior is still connected. So, the entire graph should remain connected. Similarly for other sides. Since we are only removing a matching on each side, which doesn't disconnect the side itself (since it's a path and we're removing a matching, which doesn't break the path into disconnected components), and the interior remains fully connected. Therefore, the remaining graph is still connected.\n\nTherefore, the remaining graph is connected and Eulerian, so it has an Eulerian circuit. Therefore, an Eulerian circuit exists which traverses all 200 edges. Therefore, Karthik can walk such a circuit, starting and ending at the same cell, traversing 200 doors without repeating any.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 200? Hmm. But wait, let me see if there's a possible mistake here.\n\nWait, another way to think about it: in an Eulerian circuit, the number of edges is equal to the number of vertices visited? No, not necessarily. The number of edges in an Eulerian circuit is equal to the number of edges in the graph, since it traverses every edge exactly once. Wait, but in our case, we have an Eulerian circuit in the remaining graph with 200 edges, so the trail would have 200 edges, which is exactly the number of edges in that graph. So, yes, that seems right.\n\nBut let me think if there is a reason why this might not be possible. For example, in some graphs, even if you have an Eulerian circuit, you might not be able to have a single closed trail that covers all edges because of some other constraint? But no, an Eulerian circuit by definition is a single closed trail that covers every edge exactly once. So, as long as the graph is connected and Eulerian, such a trail exists.\n\nTherefore, if we can remove 20 edges to make the graph Eulerian, then the maximum trail is 220 - 20 = 200.\n\nBut let me check with a smaller grid to see if this reasoning holds.\n\nLet's take a smaller grid, say 2x2 grid of cells. So, 3x3 vertices. Let's see what happens there.\n\nIn a 2x2 grid of cells (so 3x3 vertices):\n\nTotal cells: 4.\n\nTotal edges: horizontal: 3 rows * 2 horizontal edges per row = 6 horizontal edges? Wait, no:\n\nWait, in a 2x2 grid of cells, there are 3 rows and 3 columns of vertices.\n\nNumber of horizontal edges: 3 rows * (3 - 1) columns = 3*2 = 6 horizontal edges.\n\nNumber of vertical edges: 3 columns * (3 - 1) rows = 3*2 = 6 vertical edges.\n\nTotal edges: 12.\n\nDegrees of vertices:\n\nCorners: 4 corners, each has degree 2.\n\nEdge non-corner vertices: on each side, there is 1 non-corner vertex (since 3 vertices per side, minus 2 corners). There are 4 sides, so 4 non-corner edge vertices, each of degree 3.\n\nInterior vertex: the center one, degree 4.\n\nTherefore, total odd-degree vertices: 4 (the non-corner edge vertices). So O = 4, so we need to delete 2 edges.\n\nFollowing the same logic: on each side of the 2x2 grid (which has 3 vertices per side), the non-corner edge vertices form a single vertex per side (since 3 - 2 = 1). So each side has 1 non-corner edge vertex. Since they are single vertices, we can't form a matching on each side because a matching requires at least two vertices. Hmm, problem here.\n\nWait, in the 2x2 grid case, each side has only one non-corner edge vertex, so we can't form a matching on the perimeter edges because there's only one vertex per side. Therefore, our previous method fails here.\n\nSo, in this case, how would we proceed?\n\nIn the 2x2 grid, we have four non-corner edge vertices, each on a side. Each of these has degree 3. To make all degrees even, we need to reduce each of their degrees by 1. Since each has degree 3, we can delete one edge from each. But each edge deletion affects two vertices. So, we need to pair up the four non-corner edge vertices and delete edges between them.\n\nBut in the 2x2 grid, the non-corner edge vertices are each adjacent only to one other non-corner edge vertex? Wait, let's look at the 2x2 grid:\n\nLabel the vertices as follows:\n\n(1,1) (1,2) (1,3)\n\n(2,1) (2,2) (2,3)\n\n(3,1) (3,2) (3,3)\n\nThe non-corner edge vertices are:\n\nTop side: (1,2)\n\nRight side: (2,3)\n\nBottom side: (3,2)\n\nLeft side: (2,1)\n\nEach of these four vertices has degree 3.\n\nNow, (1,2) is connected to (1,1) [corner, degree 2], (1,3) [corner], and (2,2) [interior, degree 4].\n\nSimilarly, (2,3) is connected to (1,3) [corner], (3,3) [corner], and (2,2) [interior].\n\n(3,2) is connected to (3,1) [corner], (3,3) [corner], and (2,2) [interior].\n\n(2,1) is connected to (1,1) [corner], (3,1) [corner], and (2,2) [interior].\n\nTherefore, each non-corner edge vertex is connected to two corners and the center vertex.\n\nTherefore, the only edges connecting two non-corner edge vertices would be... actually, none. Each non-corner edge vertex is only connected to corners and the center, not to other non-corner edge vertices.\n\nTherefore, in this case, there are no edges between the non-corner edge vertices. Therefore, we cannot form a matching among them using perimeter edges. Therefore, our previous method fails here.\n\nTherefore, in this case, how do we fix the degrees?\n\nWe have four odd-degree vertices (each degree 3). To make all degrees even, we need to reduce each by 1. Since we cannot pair them via perimeter edges (because there are none), we have to pair them with the center vertex? But the center vertex is even-degree (degree 4). If we delete an edge from a non-corner edge vertex to the center vertex, that would reduce the non-corner edge vertex's degree to 2 (even) but the center's degree to 3 (odd). So that's bad.\n\nAlternatively, if we delete two edges from the center vertex, but the center has degree 4. If we delete two edges incident to the center, then the center's degree becomes 2 (even), but the two vertices connected to those deleted edges would each have their degrees reduced by 1, turning them from 3 to 2 (even). However, we need to do this for all four non-corner edge vertices.\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nSuppose we delete two edges from the center vertex. Each deletion affects the center and one non-corner edge vertex. If we delete two edges from the center to, say, (1,2) and (3,2), then the center's degree becomes 4 - 2 = 2 (even), and the degrees of (1,2) and (3,2) become 3 - 1 = 2 (even). But the other two non-corner edge vertices, (2,1) and (2,3), still have degree 3 (odd). So we still have two odd-degree vertices left. Then we need to delete another two edges, perhaps from the center to (2,1) and (2,3). Then the center's degree becomes 2 - 2 = 0? Wait, no, we already deleted two edges from the center. If we delete two more edges from the center, its degree becomes 0, which is even, but then the four non-corner edge vertices each have their degrees reduced by 1, so all become 2 (even). However, deleting four edges from the center vertex: but the center only has four edges: to the four non-corner edge vertices. Therefore, if we delete all four edges from the center to the non-corner edge vertices, then the center's degree becomes 0, and each non-corner edge vertex's degree becomes 2 (even). Thus, all degrees are even. However, we have deleted four edges. Since O = 4, then O/2 = 2, but here we needed to delete four edges, which is more than O/2. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's a better way. Since we cannot pair the non-corner edge vertices with each other via edges (as there are none), we have to pair them with the center, but each such pairing would require deleting one edge from the center to a non-corner edge vertex, but that would create an odd degree at the center. To fix the center, we have to delete two edges from it, which would fix two non-corner vertices but create two new odd-degree vertices at the center (wait, no: deleting two edges from the center would reduce its degree by 2, so if it was 4, it becomes 2, which is even. Then the two non-corner vertices connected to those deleted edges would each lose one degree, going from 3 to 2 (even). The other two non-corner vertices remain at 3. Then we need to delete two more edges from the center to those remaining non-corner vertices, but that would require deleting a total of four edges from the center, making its degree zero, which is even, and all non-corner vertices at degree 2. So that works, but requires deleting four edges, which is O (4) edges, but O/2 is 2. Therefore, in this case, the minimal number of edges to delete is 4, not 2. Therefore, my previous assumption that it's O/2 is incorrect in this case.\n\nTherefore, my earlier reasoning may have a flaw when the graph doesn't have a perfect matching on the perimeter edges. In the 2x2 grid example, since the non-corner edge vertices are not connected to each other, we have to use the center vertex to \"pair\" them, but that requires more edge deletions.\n\nTherefore, perhaps in the 11x11 grid, even though we can form a perfect matching on each side's perimeter edges, in some grids where such matchings are not possible, you have to do something else. But in our 11x11 grid, since each side's non-corner edge vertices form a path of 10 vertices (even length), we can indeed form a perfect matching on each side using the edges along the side, so that we don't have to involve interior edges or other sides. Therefore, in the 11x11 grid, the minimal number of edges to delete is indeed 20, so the maximum trail is 200.\n\nBut in the 2x2 grid, since the non-corner edge vertices are not connected to each other, we have to delete more edges. So in that case, the minimal number of edges to delete is 4, resulting in an Eulerian circuit with 12 - 4 = 8 edges. Wait, original edges: 12, subtract 4, gives 8 edges. But in the 2x2 grid, after deleting all four edges from the center to the non-corner vertices, the remaining graph consists of the four corners, each connected to their adjacent corners via the perimeter edges. Wait, actually, if we delete all four edges from the center to the non-corner vertices, then the remaining edges are the perimeter edges.\n\nThe original perimeter edges: on each side of the 3x3 grid, there are 3 vertices, so 2 edges per side. Four sides, so 8 perimeter edges. Plus the four edges from the center to the non-corner vertices, totaling 12 edges. If we delete the four center-to-non-corner edges, we are left with the 8 perimeter edges. The degrees of the vertices in the remaining graph:\n\nCorners: each corner is connected to two perimeter edges and one center edge (which was deleted). Wait, no. Wait, each corner is connected to two perimeter edges and one center edge? Wait, in the original grid, each corner is connected to two adjacent perimeter edges and one interior edge? Wait no.\n\nWait, in the 3x3 grid of vertices:\n\nEach corner vertex, say (1,1), is connected to (1,2) [right] and (2,1) [down]. So two edges. So degree 2. The edges from (1,1) to the center (2,2) do not exist; the center is (2,2), and (1,1) is only connected to its immediate neighbors.\n\nWait a second, hold on. In the 3x3 grid of vertices (which represents a 2x2 grid of cells), each corner vertex is connected only to its two adjacent perimeter vertices. The center vertex (2,2) is connected to four edge non-corner vertices: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2). So, the center has degree 4. The edge non-corner vertices like (1,2) are connected to the two adjacent perimeter vertices (1,1) and (1,3), and to the center (2,2). So degree 3. The corners have degree 2.\n\nTherefore, if we delete the four edges from the center to the non-corner vertices, then:\n\n- The center vertex loses all four edges, so its degree becomes 0.\n\n- Each non-corner edge vertex loses one edge (the one to the center), so their degrees go from 3 to 2.\n\n- The corner vertices retain their degrees at 2.\n\nTherefore, the remaining graph consists of the 8 perimeter edges forming a cycle: the outer square. Each corner has degree 2, each non-corner edge vertex has degree 2 (since they were connected to two corners and lost the center edge). The center has degree 0.\n\nBut a graph with a disconnected component (the center isolated) and the outer cycle. However, the outer cycle is a connected component with all vertices of even degree (2), so it has an Eulerian circuit. The center is a separate component with a single vertex of degree 0, which is also Eulerian (trivially, since it has no edges). Therefore, the entire graph is Eulerian? Wait, but the center is a separate component. An Eulerian circuit requires the graph to be connected. So, if we consider the entire graph after deletion, it is disconnected (the outer cycle and the isolated center), so there is no Eulerian circuit for the entire graph. However, an Eulerian circuit must be in a connected graph. Therefore, in this case, the remaining graph is not connected, so we cannot have an Eulerian circuit for the entire graph. Therefore, my previous assumption was wrong: deleting edges can disconnect the graph, so even if each component is Eulerian, the whole graph isn't, so we can't have an Eulerian circuit for the whole graph.\n\nTherefore, in the 2x2 grid example, after deleting the four center edges, we have two connected components: the outer cycle (which is Eulerian) and the isolated center vertex (which is trivially Eulerian but has no edges). Therefore, if we want a closed trail that covers as many edges as possible, we could have an Eulerian circuit on the outer cycle, which has 8 edges, and ignore the center. However, the problem states that Karthik must start and end in the same cell, but he can choose any starting cell. If he starts on the outer cycle, he can traverse the entire outer cycle (8 edges) and return to the start. The center is a separate component, but he can't reach it from the outer cycle without traversing a deleted edge. Therefore, in this case, the maximum trail would be 8 edges.\n\nAlternatively, if we instead choose a different set of edges to delete, maybe we can have a connected graph with an Eulerian circuit covering more edges? Let's see.\n\nSuppose instead of deleting all four center edges, we delete two edges from the center. Let's say we delete the edges from the center to (1,2) and (3,2). Then:\n\n- The center's degree becomes 4 - 2 = 2.\n\n- The vertices (1,2) and (3,2) now have degree 3 - 1 = 2.\n\n- The other two non-corner vertices, (2,1) and (2,3), still have degree 3.\n\n- The corners remain at degree 2.\n\nSo now, the center has degree 2, (1,2) and (3,2) have degree 2, but (2,1) and (2,3) still have degree 3. So we still have two odd-degree vertices: (2,1) and (2,3). To fix those, we need to delete one more edge each, but again involving the center or other vertices.\n\nIf we delete the edges from the center to (2,1) and (2,3), then:\n\n- The center's degree becomes 2 - 2 = 0.\n\n- (2,1) and (2,3) now have degree 3 - 1 = 2.\n\nNow, all non-corner edge vertices have degree 2, the center has degree 0, corners have degree 2. So the graph is now:\n\n- The outer cycle (perimeter) with the four corners connected as before, but now the non-corner edge vertices are only connected to their adjacent corners, so the outer cycle remains intact (still 8 edges), and the center is isolated.\n\nSame as before, just the center is isolated. So we still can't have a connected Eulerian circuit covering more than 8 edges.\n\nAlternatively, if we delete different edges. Suppose we delete two adjacent perimeter edges. For example, delete the edge between (1,2) and (1,3) on the top side, and the edge between (1,2) and (1,1). Wait, but those are both on the top side. Wait, actually, on the top side, the edges are between (1,1)-(1,2), (1,2)-(1,3), and (1,3)-(1,4)... Wait, in the 3x3 grid, the top side has vertices (1,1), (1,2), (1,3). So the edges are (1,1)-(1,2), (1,2)-(1,3). Similarly for other sides.\n\nIf we delete the edge (1,2)-(1,3) and the edge (2,3)-(3,3), for example, then:\n\n- The vertex (1,2) loses one edge, going from degree 3 to 2.\n\n- The vertex (1,3) loses one edge, going from degree 3 (wait, (1,3) is a corner, degree 2 originally). Wait, (1,3) is a corner, so its degree is 2. So if we delete an edge incident to it, its degree becomes 1, which is odd. That's bad.\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Maybe in the 2x2 grid, the maximum trail is indeed 8 edges, which is the perimeter cycle, and that requires deleting the four center edges, but resulting in a disconnected graph, but since the problem allows starting and ending anywhere, as long as it's a closed trail, perhaps the trail can be confined to the connected component with the cycle. Since the trail just needs to start and end at the same cell, which can be on the cycle, so that's okay. Therefore, in that case, 8 edges is achievable.\n\nWhereas the total edges are 12, so 12 - 4 = 8. So in that case, it works. The key is that even though the graph is disconnected after deletion, as long as the trail is confined to one connected component that has an Eulerian circuit, then that's acceptable. The problem doesn't require the trail to traverse all edges of the original graph, just as many as possible without repeating doors, and returning to the start.\n\nTherefore, in the 2x2 grid, the maximum is 8, which is 12 - 4, and 4 is the minimal number of edges to delete to make the connected component (the outer cycle) Eulerian. So in that case, the formula still holds: total edges minus minimal edge deletions to make some connected component Eulerian.\n\nWait, but in this case, we had to delete edges not just to make the entire graph Eulerian, but to make a connected component Eulerian, and the rest can be ignored. Therefore, perhaps in the original problem with the 11x11 grid, we can do something similar: delete edges to make a single connected component Eulerian, and the rest can be ignored, but since we want the maximum trail, we want the largest possible connected component that is Eulerian, so we should delete as few edges as possible to make the entire graph Eulerian, but if that's not possible due to disconnection, perhaps we have to accept some disconnection but still have an Eulerian circuit in the largest component.\n\nBut in the 11x11 grid, when we delete the 20 edges along the perimeter as previously described, the graph remains connected, right? Because the interior is still fully connected via interior edges, and the perimeter edges we deleted are just along the perimeter but not disconnecting the interior from the perimeter. For example, even if we delete a horizontal edge on the top side between two non-corner vertices, the rest of the top perimeter is still connected via other edges, and the interior is connected to the top perimeter via the downward edges from the non-corner vertices to the second row. So, the entire graph remains connected. Therefore, in the 11x11 grid case, deleting those 20 edges results in a connected Eulerian graph, so an Eulerian circuit exists that traverses all 200 edges.\n\nIn contrast, in the 2x2 grid, if we try to make the entire graph Eulerian by deleting edges, we end up with a disconnected graph, but we can still have an Eulerian circuit in the connected component (the outer cycle) by deleting the four center edges, resulting in a connected component with an Eulerian circuit of 8 edges. Therefore, in that case, the formula still gives the correct result: total edges (12) minus minimal edge deletions to make a connected component Eulerian (4), resulting in 8 edges. So perhaps in general, the formula is total edges minus the minimal number of edges to delete to make some connected component Eulerian, but you have to choose the deletion set such that the remaining graph has a connected Eulerian subgraph with as many edges as possible.\n\nHowever, in the 11x11 grid, since we can make the entire graph Eulerian by deleting 20 edges without disconnecting it, then the entire graph becomes Eulerian and connected, so we can have an Eulerian circuit covering all remaining edges (200). Therefore, in that case, it's optimal.\n\nTherefore, returning to the 11x11 grid, with 40 odd-degree vertices, we can pair them up along the perimeter edges (since each side's non-corner edge vertices form a path of even length, allowing a perfect matching on each side), thereby deleting 20 edges, resulting in an Eulerian circuit of 200 edges. Therefore, the answer should be 200.\n\nBut let me check another small grid where the perimeter non-corner vertices can be matched.\n\nConsider a 3x3 grid of cells (4x4 vertices). Let's see:\n\nA 3x3 grid of cells has 4 rows and 4 columns of vertices, so 16 vertices.\n\nThe degrees:\n\nCorners: 4 corners, each degree 2.\n\nEdge non-corner vertices: each side has 4 - 2 = 2 non-corner vertices. So four sides * 2 = 8 non-corner edge vertices, each of degree 3.\n\nInterior vertices: 4x4 grid minus the perimeter: (4-2)*(4-2) = 4 interior vertices, each of degree 4.\n\nTherefore, total odd-degree vertices: 8, so O = 8, so need to delete 4 edges.\n\nEach side of the 3x3 grid (which has 4 vertices) has 2 non-corner edge vertices. For example, the top side has vertices (1,1), (1,2), (1,3), (1,4). The non-corner edge vertices are (1,2) and (1,3). These two form a path of length 2 vertices, connected by one edge. To form a perfect matching on this side, we can delete that one edge between them. Similarly for each side.\n\nTherefore, on each side, we can delete 1 edge (the one connecting the two non-corner edge vertices), so total edges to delete: 4 sides * 1 edge = 4 edges. Each such deletion fixes the two non-corner edge vertices on that side, turning their degrees from 3 to 2. The corners remain at degree 2, and the interior vertices remain at degree 4. The remaining graph is connected? Let's see.\n\nAfter deleting the four edges along the perimeter between the non-corner edge vertices:\n\nOn each side, the two non-corner edge vertices are no longer connected to each other via that perimeter edge, but they are still connected to the corners and the interior.\n\nFor example, on the top side, the vertices (1,2) and (1,3) were connected by an edge. If we delete that edge, then (1,2) is connected to (1,1) and (2,2), and (1,3) is connected to (1,4) and (2,3). The rest of the graph remains connected through the interior and other perimeter edges.\n\nTherefore, the remaining graph is still connected, and all vertices have even degree. Therefore, it has an Eulerian circuit. The total edges in the original grid: horizontal edges: 4 rows * 3 columns = 12 horizontal edges. Vertical edges: 4 columns * 3 rows = 12 vertical edges. Total edges: 24. After deleting 4 edges, we have 20 edges. So an Eulerian circuit of 20 edges.\n\nAlternatively, let's count manually:\n\nOriginal edges: 24.\n\nAfter deleting 4 perimeter edges (one per side), remaining edges: 20. Since all degrees are even, Eulerian circuit exists. So yes, that works.\n\nTherefore, in this 3x3 grid of cells (4x4 vertices), the maximum trail is 20 doors.\n\nTherefore, this method works here. Thus, returning to the original problem, the 11x11 grid of cells (12x12 vertices), by deleting 20 perimeter edges (5 per side, along the perimeter between non-corner edge vertices), we can achieve an Eulerian circuit of 200 edges, so the answer is 200.\n\nBut wait, just to be thorough, let me consider another aspect: in the grid graph, when we delete edges along the perimeter, are we sure that those edges are the only ones needed, and that no other edge deletions are required?\n\nIn the 11x11 grid, the non-corner edge vertices are only on the perimeter, and each has three edges: two along the perimeter (except near the corners) and one inward to the interior. By deleting one perimeter edge per non-corner edge vertex (paired appropriately), we can reduce their degree to 2, and the interior vertices connected to them still have degree 4 (since we only deleted edges that were on the perimeter, not affecting the interior edges). The corners remain at degree 2. Therefore, all degrees are even, so the graph is Eulerian. Thus, the reasoning holds.\n\nTherefore, I think the answer is 200. However, I recall that in some grid graphs, the maximum trail might be different, but given the above reasoning with smaller grids working out, and the structure of the 11x11 grid allowing a perfect matching on each side's perimeter edges, I think 200 is correct.\n\nWait, but hold on. Let me think about the actual path. An Eulerian circuit must traverse each edge exactly once. In the modified grid (after deleting 20 edges), the trail would have to go through all 200 edges. However, in the original grid, the doors (edges) are between cells. Th