ould be 4 × 9 = 36 edge cells (non-corner). Each of these has degree 3.\n\n- Interior cells: total cells are 11×11 = 121. Subtract the corner cells (4) and the edge cells (36), so interior cells are 121 - 4 - 36 = 81. Each interior cell has degree 4.\n\nTherefore, in the grid graph, there are four vertices of degree 2, 36 vertices of degree 3, and 81 vertices of degree 4.\n\nNow, Euler's theorem states that a connected graph has an Euler circuit if and only if every vertex has even degree. In this case, the degrees are 2, 3, and 4. So, the vertices with degree 2 are even, but the vertices with degree 3 are odd. The vertices with degree 4 are even. Therefore, since there are vertices with odd degree (specifically, 36 vertices of degree 3), the graph does not have an Euler circuit. Therefore, there is no closed trail that traverses every edge exactly once.\n\nTherefore, the maximum closed trail (which is what we want) cannot traverse all edges, because that would require an Euler circuit which doesn't exist here. So, we have to find a closed trail that uses as many edges as possible without repeating any edge, i.e., a trail that is as long as possible, but closed, so starting and ending at the same vertex.\n\nIn graph theory, the maximum length of a closed trail in a graph is equal to the number of edges in the graph minus the minimum number of edges that need to be removed to make the graph Eulerian (i.e., to make all vertices have even degree). This is because, if we remove a set of edges such that all vertices have even degree, then the remaining graph has an Euler circuit, and the closed trail would be that Euler circuit, which traverses all the remaining edges. Therefore, the maximum closed trail length is equal to (number of edges in the graph) minus (the number of edges in the minimum edge set to remove to make all degrees even).\n\nAlternatively, another way to think about it is: since the graph has some vertices with odd degree, each time you traverse an edge