n Euler circuit because you start at one vertex, traverse the edge to the other, and then you have to end there, but to make it a circuit you have to return, which would require traversing the edge again, which isn't allowed. So even a single edge cannot have an Euler circuit. Therefore, in a tree, which has exactly two vertices of odd degree, you cannot have an Euler circuit unless you remove all edges, which makes it trivial.\n\nTherefore, in general, for a graph that is a tree, you cannot have an Euler circuit unless you remove all edges (making all degrees zero, which is even), but that's trivial.\n\nTherefore, in non-tree graphs, perhaps you can achieve the t/2 edge removals while keeping the graph connected? The grid graph is certainly not a tree. A grid graph is a 2-connected graph? Let me recall: a grid graph is 2-connected if it's at least 2x2. For example, a 2x2 grid is 2-connected. A 3x3 grid is also 2-connected. In fact, any grid graph that's at least 2x2 is 2-connected, meaning it remains connected after removal of any single vertex. Therefore, being 2-connected might help in the sense that removing a small number of edges won't disconnect it, as long as we don't remove too many edges in a strategic way.\n\nTherefore, perhaps in a 2-connected graph, you can remove t/2 edges without disconnecting it? I need to think.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there is a different way to compute the maximum closed trail length in a grid graph.\n\nWait, another approach: in any graph, the maximum length of a closed trail is equal to the total number of edges minus the number of \"defects\", where defects are related to the number of vertices of odd degree. But in our case, since we can't have an Eulerian circuit, the maximum trail is limited by the number of odd-degree vertices.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the maximum closed trail can be found by considering that every time you enter a vertex of odd degree, you must leave it, but since you start and end at the same vertex, which will have its degree reduced by two (since you enter and leave it), but actually, the start/end vertex's degree in the trail is the number of times you enter and leave it. Wait, this is getting confusing.\n\nAlternatively, here's a different perspective: in a closed trail, every time you visit a vertex (except the start/end vertex), you must enter and exit, so the number of times you enter a vertex is equal to the number of times you leave it. For the start/end vertex, since it's the same, you have one more exit than entry? Wait no, in a closed trail, you start at the vertex, then each time you enter or leave, it's balanced except at the end. Wait, actually, in a closed trail, the number of times you enter a vertex equals the number of times you leave it, because you start and end at the same vertex. Therefore, for every vertex, the degree in the trail (i.e., the number of times you traverse edges incident to it) must be even. Except that the trail is a sequence of edges, so each time you traverse an edge into a vertex, you must traverse an edge out, except for the start/end vertex where the first and last edges are incident to it. Wait, actually, in a closed trail, every vertex has even degree in the trail, because each time you pass through a vertex (except the start/end), you enter and leave, and for the start/end vertex, you also enter and leave, except that the trail starts and ends there, but the first and last edges are both incident to the start/end vertex, so actually, the start/end vertex also has even degree in the trail. Wait a second, that's a key point!\n\nWait, in a closed trail, every vertex has even degree in the trail, because you enter and exit each time. Therefore, the trail itself forms a closed walk where every vertex has even degree. Therefore, the trail is actually an Eulerian circuit of the subgraph induced by the edges of the trail. Therefore, the trail's subgraph must be Eulerian, meaning all its vertices have even degree. Therefore, the trail is a closed Eulerian trail on some subgraph of the original grid graph. Therefore, the problem reduces to: find the largest possible subgraph of the grid graph that is Eulerian (i.e., all vertices have even degree) and connected, and then the number of edges in that subgraph is the answer.\n\nTherefore, to maximize the number of edges, we need the largest connected Eulerian subgraph of the original grid graph. The size of this subgraph will be equal to the total number of edges in the original graph minus the minimal number of edges that need to be removed to make the remaining graph Eulerian and connected. Since the original graph is connected, the question is: what's the minimal number of edges to remove so that the remaining graph is Eulerian (all degrees even) and connected?\n\nNow, in order to make the remaining graph Eulerian, we need to remove edges so that all vertices have even degree. As before, the number of vertices with odd degree in the original graph is t = 36. To make all degrees even, we need to remove edges such that each removal flips the parity of two vertices. Therefore, as before, we need to remove at least t/2 edges. However, as in the 1x3 grid example, we must also ensure that the graph remains connected. Therefore, the minimal number of edges to remove is the minimal number such that when you remove those edges, the graph remains connected and all degrees are even.\n\nIn the 1x3 grid, t = 2, so t/2 = 1, but removing 1 edge disconnects the graph, so we have to remove 2 edges (which is t edges) to make all degrees even and connected? Wait, no. If we remove both edges, then all degrees become zero, which is even, and the graph is disconnected (all isolated vertices), but the trail would have to be in a connected component, but there are none. Therefore, actually, the only connected Eulerian subgraph with all degrees even is the empty graph, which has zero edges. Therefore, in that case, the maximum is zero.\n\nTherefore, in general, the minimal number of edges to remove is not necessarily t/2, but could be higher, depending on the graph's structure and the requirement to stay connected.\n\nTherefore, in the case of the grid graph, which is 2-connected (since it's at least 2x2), perhaps we can always remove t/2 edges while keeping the graph connected? Since it's 2-connected, it remains connected after removal of any single vertex, but removing edges is different.\n\nWait, 2-connectedness means that you need to remove at least two vertices to disconnect the graph. But removing edges can disconnect it even if you remove fewer edges. For example, in a cycle graph (which is 2-connected), if you remove one edge, it becomes a path graph, which is still connected, but if you remove two edges, it might disconnect depending on which edges you remove.\n\nBut in our grid graph, which is much more than 2-connected? Actually, grid graphs are more than 2-connected. For example, a 3x3 grid is 4-connected? Not sure, but at least 2-connected. Anyway, perhaps in grid graphs, you can always find a set of t/2 edges whose removal will not disconnect the graph?\n\nAlternatively, maybe there is a different approach to compute the maximum closed trail in a grid.\n\nAnother idea: in a grid graph, which is a planar graph, the maximum closed trail is related to the number of edges minus the number of \"cuts\" or something. Alternatively, perhaps we can model this as an Eulerian trail problem with the grid's properties.\n\nAlternatively, note that in the grid graph, all the vertices except the corners and edges have even degrees, but the ones on the edges (non-corner) have odd degrees, and the corners have even degrees. Wait, no: corners have degree 2 (even), edge non-corner have degree 3 (odd), and interior have degree 4 (even). Therefore, the odd-degree vertices are exactly the 36 edge non-corner cells. So, t = 36.\n\nTherefore, to make all degrees even, we need to remove 18 edges, but in such a way that the graph remains connected.\n\nGiven that the grid is 2-connected, perhaps we can perform a \"pairing\" of the odd-degree vertices by removing a single path between each pair? But since there are 36 odd-degree vertices, that would require 18 paths, each consisting of at least one edge. But this might be too many edges to remove. Alternatively, perhaps there's a smarter way.\n\nAlternatively, since the grid is a bipartite graph? Let me recall that grid graphs are bipartite. Yes, because you can color the grid in a checkerboard pattern, with alternating black and white squares, and no two adjacent squares share the same color. Therefore, the grid graph is bipartite.\n\nIn a bipartite graph, all cycles are of even length. Also, in a bipartite graph, any Eulerian circuit must have even length, which it does, since all cycles are even. However, does this help us?\n\nWell, in a bipartite graph, the number of vertices with odd degree must be even, which they are (36), so that's consistent. But I'm not sure how this helps with the edge removal.\n\nWait, here's another thought. In a bipartite graph, the maximum closed trail (Eulerian circuit) can be found by considering the two partitions. Since the grid is bipartite, let's say the two partitions are A and B. In any closed trail, the number of edges must be even, which it will be since we're removing an even number of edges (18). Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps in the grid graph, since it's regular except for the boundary, we can find a way to pair up the odd-degree vertices along the boundary and remove edges in a systematic way.\n\nLet me think of the grid as having an outer boundary and an inner part. The odd-degree vertices are all along the edges (the non-corner edge cells). So, if I can pair up these edge cells and remove an edge between each pair, but since they are on the perimeter, perhaps we can do this in a way that doesn't disconnect the graph.\n\nAlternatively, consider that each \"defect\" (an odd-degree vertex) needs to have its degree reduced by 1 (to make it even). Since each edge removal affects two vertices, we can model this as a matching problem where we need to match up the 36 odd-degree vertices with 18 edges, each edge being a pair of vertices connected by an edge in the grid. However, not every pair of odd-degree vertices are adjacent. Therefore, we need to find a matching of the odd-degree vertices with edges in the graph such that each edge in the matching is an edge in the grid graph.\n\nBut since the grid graph is highly connected, perhaps such a matching exists? That is, can we find 18 edges in the grid graph that cover all 36 odd-degree vertices, with each edge covering two of them?\n\nWait, but the odd-degree vertices are all the edge non-corner cells. Each such cell is on the edge of the grid, so they are adjacent to other edge cells and interior cells. For example, take a non-corner edge cell on the top edge: it is adjacent to an interior cell below it and two other edge cells (left and right) along the top edge. Similarly for other edges.\n\nTherefore, the non-corner edge cells are adjacent to each other along the edges of the grid. For instance, on the top edge, the non-corner cells are adjacent to their immediate neighbors on the top edge. Therefore, along each edge of the grid, the non-corner edge cells form a path graph. For example, the top edge has 9 non-corner edge cells arranged in a line. Each of these is connected to its neighbor along the edge.\n\nTherefore, along each edge of the grid, the non-corner edge cells form a path of 9 vertices. In such a path, the two end vertices (which are actually corners of the grid?) Wait no, the non-corner edge cells on the top edge are between the two corners. For example, on the top edge of an 11x11 grid, the top-left corner is (1,1), then the next cell is (1,2), which is a non-corner edge cell, then (1,3), ..., up to (1,11), the top-right corner. So the non-corner edge cells on the top edge are (1,2) through (1,10), which is 9 cells. Each of these is connected to its immediate neighbor along the top edge. So the top edge's non-corner cells form a path graph of 9 vertices.\n\nSimilarly for the bottom edge, left edge, and right edge.\n\nTherefore, each edge of the grid has a path of 9 non-corner edge cells. Each of these paths has 9 vertices. In each such path, the degrees of the vertices are as follows:\n\n- The two endpoints of the path (which are adjacent to the grid's corners) have degree 3 in the original grid (since they are on the edge but not corners, except wait: actually, in the path along the top edge, the first cell (1,2) is adjacent to (1,1) [the corner], (1,3), and (2,2) [interior]. So its degree is 3. Similarly, the last cell on the top edge path, say (1,10), is adjacent to (1,9), (1,11) [the corner], and (2,10). So also degree 3. The middle cells on the path, like (1,5), are adjacent to (1,4), (1,6), and (2,5), so also degree 3. Wait a second, so actually all the non-corner edge cells on each edge have degree 3 in the original grid. Therefore, each of these 9 cells on the top edge path has degree 3 (odd), so they are all odd-degree vertices.\n\nTherefore, each of the four edges of the grid has a path of 9 odd-degree vertices. Therefore, the entire set of odd-degree vertices is partitioned into four disjoint paths, each of length 9 (i.e., 9 vertices). Each of these paths is a straight line along the grid's edge.\n\nNow, to fix the degrees, we need to pair up these 36 odd-degree vertices and remove an edge between each pair. Since the odd-degree vertices are arranged in these four paths, we can pair them within their own paths or across different paths.\n\nHowever, the edges available to remove are the edges along these paths and also the edges connecting the path vertices to the interior of the grid.\n\nWait, but each non-corner edge cell is connected to one interior cell (the one adjacent inward). For example, the cell (1,2) on the top edge is connected downward to (2,2). Similarly, (1,10) is connected downward to (2,10), etc. So each non-corner edge cell has one \"inward\" edge to the interior and two \"along the edge\" edges (except the endpoints, which have only one along-the-edge edge? Wait no: for the non-corner edge cells on the top edge, like (1,2), it is between (1,1) and (1,3), so it has two along-the-edge edges: left to (1,1) and right to (1,3). Wait, but (1,1) is a corner, which is a different type of cell. Wait, actually, (1,2) is adjacent to (1,1) [a corner], (1,3) [another non-corner edge cell], and (2,2) [an interior cell]. So indeed, it has three edges: left, right (along the top edge), and down into the interior.\n\nTherefore, each non-corner edge cell has three edges: two along the edge and one inward.\n\nTherefore, in order to pair up the odd-degree vertices, perhaps we can pair them along their own edge paths? For instance, in the top edge path of 9 vertices, we can pair them up as adjacent pairs and remove the edge between them? Wait, but if we remove an edge between two adjacent non-corner edge cells on the top edge, say between (1,2) and (1,3), then both (1,2) and (1,3) would have their degrees reduced by 1, turning their degrees from 3 to 2, which is even. That would fix their parities. However, doing this for all adjacent pairs would require removing 4 edges along the top edge path (since 9 vertices require 4 pairs to cover all, but 9 is odd). Wait, 9 vertices can be paired into 4 pairs and one leftover. Hmm, so that might not work.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can remove every other edge along the path? Let's see.\n\nTake the top edge path with 9 cells: let's label them T1, T2, ..., T9 from left to right, where T1 is (1,2), T2 is (1,3), ..., T9 is (1,10). Each Ti is connected to Ti-1 and Ti+1 along the edge (except T1 and T9, which are connected only to Ti+1 and Ti-1 respectively, but T1 is adjacent to the corner (1,1), which is a different vertex). Wait actually, T1 is (1,2), so its left neighbor is the corner (1,1), which is a separate vertex.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to think of the top edge path as starting from T1=(1,2) to T9=(1,10). The connections along the top edge are T1-T2, T2-T3, ..., T8-T9. Each Ti (for i=1 to 9) also has an inward connection to the cell below: let's call that cell Ii = (2,i).\n\nSo, for each Ti, the edges are: left (if applicable), right (if applicable), and down to Ii.\n\nNow, to fix the degrees of the Ti's, which are all odd (3), we can consider removing the inward edge from each Ti to Ii. Wait, but that would affect both Ti and Ii.\n\nIf we remove the edge Ti—Ii for each Ti, then each Ti loses one edge (so degree goes from 3 to 2, even), but each Ii (which is an interior cell) also loses one edge (so their degree goes from 4 to 3, which is odd). Therefore, that would actually create new odd-degree vertices at the Ii's, which were originally even. So that's bad.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps remove edges along the top edge path. For instance, if we remove every other edge along the top edge path, say remove T1-T2, T3-T4, T5-T6, T7-T8, leaving T9-T10? Wait, no, the top edge path only has 9 cells, so the edges along the path are 8 edges: between T1-T2, T2-T3, ..., T8-T9.\n\nIf we remove four edges, say alternating ones: remove T1-T2, T3-T4, T5-T6, T7-T8. Then, the remaining edges along the top path would be T2-T3, T4-T5, T6-T7, T8-T9. Wait, no, actually if you remove every other edge starting with the first, you remove T1-T2, T3-T4, etc., so the remaining edges would be T2-T3, T4-T5, T6-T7, T8-T9? Wait no, let's see:\n\nOriginal edges along the top path: E1=T1-T2, E2=T2-T3, E3=T3-T4, E4=T4-T5, E5=T5-T6, E6=T6-T7, E7=T7-T8, E8=T8-T9.\n\nIf we remove E1, E3, E5, E7: that is, every other edge starting with the first. Then the remaining edges are E2, E4, E6, E8. So the path is broken into four separate segments:\n\n- T1 isolated (since E1 is removed),\n\n- T2-T3 (connected via E2),\n\n- T4-T5 (connected via E4),\n\n- T6-T7 (connected via E6),\n\n- T8-T9 (connected via E8).\n\nWait, but T1 is now only connected to the corner (1,1) and inward to I1=(2,2). Its degree was originally 3 (connected to (1,1), (1,3)=T2, and (2,2)=I1). After removing E1=T1-T2, its degree becomes 2 (connected to (1,1) and I1). So that's even. Similarly, T2 is now only connected to T3 (since E1 is removed) and inward to I2=(2,2). Wait, no: T2 is connected to T1 (but E1 is removed), T3, and inward to I2=(2,2). So its degree is 2 (T3 and I2). So that's even. Similarly, T3 is connected to T2 and T4, and inward to I3=(2,3). So its degree is 3? Wait no: T3 is connected to T2, T4, and I3. Wait, no: if we didn't remove E2=T2-T3, then T3 is still connected to T2 and T4, and inward. So degree 3. Wait, but we only removed E1, E3, E5, E7.\n\nWait, hold on, let's re-examine:\n\nAfter removing E1 (T1-T2), E3 (T3-T4), E5 (T5-T6), E7 (T7-T8):\n\n- T1: connected to (1,1) and I1=(2,2). Degree 2.\n\n- T2: connected to T3 and I2=(2,2). Degree 2.\n\n- T3: connected to T2, T4, and I3=(2,3). Degree 3.\n\nWait, so T3 still has degree 3? Because we didn't remove E2=T2-T3. So T3 is still connected to T2 and T4, and inward. So degree 3. Hmm, so that didn't fix T3's degree.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Maybe this approach isn't the best.\n\nAlternatively, since all the non-corner edge cells are on the perimeter, perhaps we can \"pair off\" them by removing the inward edges from every other cell?\n\nFor example, for each non-corner edge cell, if we remove its inward edge to the interior, then that cell's degree decreases by 1 (from 3 to 2), but the interior cell it was connected to also loses a degree (from 4 to 3). However, as before, this creates new odd-degree vertices in the interior. Since there are many interior cells (81), this might not be a problem because we can then fix those by removing other edges.\n\nBut this seems like it could create a chain reaction where we have to remove many edges to fix all the degrees.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps a better approach is to note that in a grid graph, the maximum number of edges in an Eulerian subgraph is equal to the total number of edges minus the number of odd-degree vertices divided by 2, provided that such a subgraph can be formed without disconnecting the graph. Since the grid is highly connected, this is likely possible, so the answer is 220 - 18 = 202.\n\nGiven that in the 3x3 grid example, this formula worked when we could remove the edges without disconnecting the graph, and in the 1x3 grid it doesn't work because of its low connectivity, but the grid graph in the original problem is much more connected (2-connected), so maybe it's safe to assume that we can remove 18 edges without disconnecting it, hence the maximum trail length is 202.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the formula still holds because even though in some cases you might have to remove more edges to keep it connected, in the grid graph, because of its high connectivity, the minimal number of edges to remove is indeed t/2. Since the grid is 2-connected, you can always find a spanning tree, and maybe use that to route the necessary edge removals without disconnecting.\n\nAlternatively, here's another way to think about it: since the grid is bipartite, with partitions A and B. In a bipartite graph, any cycle is of even length. An Eulerian circuit must have even length, which it will be since we're removing an even number of edges (18). Furthermore, in a bipartite graph, the maximum closed trail can be constructed by ensuring that we don't get \"stuck\" in one partition.\n\nBut I might be overcomplicating again.\n\nGiven that in the 3x3 grid, the formula worked when we could actually perform the edge removals without disconnecting the graph, and in the 1x3 grid it fails due to its lower connectivity, but the 11x11 grid is highly connected (2-connected), so it's plausible that we can remove 18 edges without disconnecting it, thus allowing an Eulerian circuit on the remaining graph with 202 edges.\n\nTherefore, perhaps the answer is 202. However, to be cautious, let me see if I can find a different approach or verify with a smaller grid where I can actually compute the maximum closed trail.\n\nTake the 3x3 grid again. Total edges: 12. t = 4 odd-degree vertices (the four edge centers). So according to the formula, maximum trail length is 12 - 2 = 10.\n\nLet me try to construct such a trail.\n\nLabel the 3x3 grid cells as follows, with coordinates (row, column):\n\n(1,1) (1,2) (1,3)\n\n(2,1) (2,2) (2,3)\n\n(3,1) (3,2) (3,3)\n\nThe four odd-degree vertices are (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2). Each has degree 3.\n\nWe need to remove two edges to make all degrees even.\n\nAs before, let's remove the vertical edge between (1,2) and (2,2), and the horizontal edge between (2,1) and (2,2). Wait, but that removes two edges:\n\n1. Edge between (1,2) and (2,2): let's call this edge E1.\n\n2. Edge between (2,1) and (2,2): let's call this edge E2.\n\nAfter removing E1 and E2:\n\n- The degree of (1,2) becomes 2 (connected to (1,1) and (1,3)).\n\n- The degree of (2,1) becomes 2 (connected to (1,1) and (3,1)).\n\n- The degree of (2,3) remains 3? Wait, (2,3) is connected to (1,3), (3,3), and (2,2). But we didn't remove any edge incident to (2,3). Wait, hold on:\n\nWait, (2,3) is connected to (1,3), (3,3), and (2,2). We only removed E1 (between (1,2) and (2,2)) and E2 (between (2,1) and (2,2)). The edge between (2,3) and (2,2) is still present. So (2,3)'s degree is still 3. Similarly, (3,2) is connected to (3,1), (3,3), and (2,2). The edge between (3,2) and (2,2) is still there, so its degree is 3. Uh-oh, so actually, removing those two edges didn't fix the degrees of (2,3) and (3,2). They still have odd degrees.\n\nHmm, so my earlier assumption was wrong. Removing those two edges only fixed two of the four odd-degree vertices, leaving two still odd. Oops, that was a mistake.\n\nTherefore, my previous thought process had an error. Let's backtrack.\n\nIn the 3x3 grid, the four odd-degree vertices are the four edge centers: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2). Each has degree 3.\n\nTo fix all four, we need to remove two edges, each removal affecting two vertices. So we need to pair up the four vertices into two pairs and remove one edge from each pair.\n\nBut the pairs must be pairs of vertices connected by an edge.\n\nLooking at the 3x3 grid:\n\n- (1,2) is connected to (2,2), (1,1), and (1,3).\n\n- (2,1) is connected to (1,1), (3,1), and (2,2).\n\n- (2,3) is connected to (1,3), (3,3), and (2,2).\n\n- (3,2) is connected to (3,1), (3,3), and (2,2).\n\nTherefore, all four odd-degree vertices are connected to the center cell (2,2). Therefore, they are all adjacent to the center cell.\n\nTherefore, to pair them up, we can pair (1,2) with (3,2), and (2,1) with (2,3), and then remove the edges between each pair and the center? Wait, but they are not directly connected to each other except through the center.\n\nAlternatively, to pair (1,2) with (2,1), but they are not adjacent. Similarly, (1,2) and (2,3) are not adjacent.\n\nThe only edges between the odd-degree vertices are through the center. Since all four are connected to the center, but not to each other directly.\n\nTherefore, to fix their degrees, we can remove two edges that are incident to two of the odd-degree vertices each, but since they are all connected to the center, we can remove two edges from the center to each of two pairs.\n\nFor example, remove the edge from (2,2) to (1,2) and the edge from (2,2) to (3,2). Then:\n\n- (1,2) loses one edge, degree becomes 2.\n\n- (3,2) loses one edge, degree becomes 2.\n\n- (2,2) loses two edges, degree becomes 4 - 2 = 2 (since originally degree 4, minus 2).\n\n- The other two odd-degree vertices, (2,1) and (2,3), still have degree 3.\n\nSo that only fixed two of the four. Not enough.\n\nAlternatively, remove the edges from (2,2) to (1,2), (2,2) to (2,1), and (2,2) to (2,3), and (2,2) to (3,2). But that would remove four edges, fixing all four odd-degree vertices but also reducing the center's degree to zero, which is even. However, that requires removing four edges, which is more than t/2 = 2.\n\nWait, so in this case, the minimal number of edges to remove is actually 2, but we saw that it's not possible because the edges needed to remove don't exist between the odd-degree vertices. Therefore, we have to remove edges that are incident to them but also affect other vertices.\n\nAlternatively, to pair (1,2) with (2,1), even though they are not adjacent, we can remove a path between them? But that would require removing multiple edges.\n\nAlternatively, since they are both connected to (1,1), which is a corner (even degree), we can remove the edge between (1,1) and (1,2), and the edge between (1,1) and (2,1). Then:\n\n- (1,2) loses one edge (to (1,1)), degree becomes 2.\n\n- (2,1) loses one edge (to (1,1)), degree becomes 2.\n\n- (1,1) loses two edges, degree becomes 0 (even).\n\n- The other two odd-degree vertices, (2,3) and (3,2), remain at degree 3.\n\nSo now we've fixed two odd-degree vertices but created a new even-degree vertex at (1,1), but the other two remain odd. So we still need to fix (2,3) and (3,2). To fix those, we can do a similar thing: remove the edge between (1,3) and (2,3), and the edge between (3,3) and (3,2). Then:\n\n- (2,3) loses one edge (to (1,3)), degree becomes 2.\n\n- (3,2) loses one edge (to (3,3)), degree becomes 2.\n\n- (1,3) and (3,3) lose one edge each, becoming degree 1 and 2 respectively. Wait, (1,3) originally had degree 3 (connected to (1,2), (1,4) [but it's a 3x3 grid, so (1,3) is a corner? Wait no, in a 3x3 grid, (1,3) is a corner. Wait, in a 3x3 grid, the corners are (1,1), (1,3), (3,1), (3,3). So (1,3) is a corner with degree 2. So removing the edge between (1,3) and (2,3) would reduce (1,3)'s degree from 2 to 1, which is odd. That's bad.\n\nHmm, so that approach causes a new odd-degree vertex at (1,3). Not good.\n\nAlternatively, remove the edge between (2,3) and (3,3), and the edge between (3,2) and (3,3). Then:\n\n- (2,3) loses one edge (to (3,3)), degree becomes 2.\n\n- (3,2) loses one edge (to (3,3)), degree becomes 2.\n\n- (3,3) loses two edges, degree becomes 2 (from 3 to 1? Wait, (3,3) is a corner with degree 2. Removing two edges from it would make its degree 0, which is even. Wait:\n\nWait, (3,3) is connected to (3,2) and (2,3). So originally degree 2. If we remove both edges, then it has degree 0, which is even. So that works.\n\nTherefore, removing edges:\n\n1. (1,2)-(1,1)\n\n2. (2,1)-(1,1)\n\n3. (2,3)-(3,3)\n\n4. (3,2)-(3,3)\n\nWait, that's four edges removed. Then:\n\n- (1,2): degree 2 (from 3)\n\n- (2,1): degree 2 (from 3)\n\n- (2,3): degree 2 (from 3)\n\n- (3,2): degree 2 (from 3)\n\n- (1,1): degree 0 (from 2)\n\n- (3,3): degree 0 (from 2)\n\n- The center (2,2) still has degree 4 (since none of the removed edges were incident to it).\n\nSo all degrees are even now. The graph is still connected? Let's see.\n\nAfter removing those four edges:\n\n- The top row: (1,1) is isolated, (1,2) connected to (1,3) and (2,2). (1,3) is connected to (2,3). (2,3) is connected to (2,2) and (3,3). (3,3) is isolated.\n\n- The left side: (2,1) is connected to (3,1) and (2,2). (3,1) is connected to (3,2) and (2,1). (3,2) is connected to (3,3) [but (3,3) is isolated now] and (2,2).\n\n- The center (2,2) is connected to (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), and (3,2). Wait, but (2,3) is connected to (2,2) and (1,3). (1,3) is connected to (2,3). But (1,3) is only connected to (2,3) now? Wait, no:\n\nWait, let me detail all connections:\n\nAfter removals:\n\n- (1,1): isolated.\n\n- (1,2): connected to (1,3) [still there?] Wait, (1,2) was connected to (1,1) [removed], (1,3) [still connected?], and (2,2). So (1,2) is connected to (1,3) and (2,2).\n\n- (1,3): connected to (1,2) and (2,3).\n\n- (2,3): connected to (1,3), (2,2), and (3,3) [but edge to (3,3) was removed? Wait, no: we removed the edge between (2,3) and (3,3), so (2,3) is connected to (1,3) and (2,2).\n\n- (3,3): isolated.\n\n- (2,1): connected to (3,1) and (2,2).\n\n- (3,1): connected to (2,1) and (3,2).\n\n- (3,2): connected to (3,1) and (2,2).\n\n- (2,2): connected to (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), and (3,2).\n\nSo the connected components are:\n\n1. The component containing (1,2), (1,3), (2,3), (2,2), (3,2), (3,1), (2,1).\n\n2. (1,1) is isolated.\n\n3. (3,3) is isolated.\n\nSo the main component is the big one with most cells, and two isolated vertices. Therefore, the graph is disconnected. So this doesn't work because we need a single connected Eulerian circuit.\n\nTherefore, this approach of removing four edges also disconnects the graph. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, let's try a different set of edges to remove. Let's try removing two edges that are both incident to the center (2,2) and two of the odd-degree vertices.\n\nFor example, remove the edge between (2,2) and (1,2), and the edge between (2,2) and (3,2). Then:\n\n- (1,2) degree becomes 2.\n\n- (3,2) degree becomes 2.\n\n- (2,2) degree becomes 4 - 2 = 2.\n\n- The other two odd-degree vertices, (2,1) and (2,3), still have degree 3.\n\nTo fix those, we can remove the edge between (2,2) and (2,1), and the edge between (2,2) and (2,3). But that would require removing two more edges, totaling four, but let's see:\n\nIf we remove:\n\n1. (2,2)-(1,2)\n\n2. (2,2)-(3,2)\n\n3. (2,2)-(2,1)\n\n4. (2,2)-(2,3)\n\nThen:\n\n- (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2) all lose one edge, going from 3 to 2.\n\n- (2,2) loses four edges, going from 4 to 0.\n\n- All other vertices remain the same.\n\nNow, check degrees:\n\n- (1,2): 2 (connected to (1,1) and (1,3))\n\n- (2,1): 2 (connected to (1,1) and (3,1))\n\n- (2,3): 2 (connected to (1,3) and (3,3))\n\n- (3,2): 2 (connected to (3,1) and (3,3))\n\n- (2,2): 0\n\n- All corners (1,1), (1,3), (3,1), (3,3) have degree 2.\n\n- All other interior cells (like (2,2) is now 0, but others like (1,2) etc. are now degree 2.\n\nWait, actually, all non-corner, non-edge cells? In the 3x3 grid, the only interior cell is (2,2), which is now degree 0. The rest are either corners or edge centers, now degree 2. So all degrees are even. The graph is now disconnected: the center is isolated, and the rest form a sort of frame. So the connected components are the outer ring and the center. So the Eulerian circuit must be within a connected component. Therefore, the maximum closed trail would be in the outer ring, which has how many edges?\n\nThe outer ring of the 3x3 grid has the perimeter edges: the top row has two horizontal edges, the bottom row has two horizontal edges, the left and right columns each have one vertical edge (since corners are shared). Wait, actually, the perimeter edges form a cycle of length 8 (the outer square). Let me count:\n\nIn the 3x3 grid, the outer perimeter consists of:\n\n- Top edge: (1,1)-(1,2), (1,2)-(1,3) — 2 edges.\n\n- Right edge: (1,3)-(2,3), (2,3)-(3,3) — 2 edges.\n\n- Bottom edge: (3,3)-(3,2), (3,2)-(3,1) — 2 edges.\n\n- Left edge: (3,1)-(2,1), (2,1)-(1,1) — 2 edges.\n\nTotal perimeter edges: 8 edges.\n\nAdditionally, the inner edges? Wait no, after removing the four edges to the center, the remaining edges are the perimeter edges and the vertical/horizontal edges connecting the edge centers to the corners? Wait no, let's see:\n\nWait, after removing the four edges from the center to the four edge centers, the remaining edges are:\n\n- All perimeter edges (the outer square): 8 edges.\n\n- The vertical and horizontal edges between the edge centers and the corners? Wait no, for example, the edge center (1,2) is still connected to (1,1) and (1,3), which are corners. But those connections are still present unless we removed them. Wait in our removals above, we only removed edges incident to the center. The edges from the edge centers to the corners are still there.\n\nWait, let's list all remaining edges after removing the four edges from the center to the four edge centers:\n\nOriginal edges:\n\nHorizontal:\n\nRow 1: (1,1)-(1,2), (1,2)-(1,3)\n\nRow 2: (2,1)-(2,2), (2,2)-(2,3)\n\nRow 3: (3,1)-(3,2), (3,2)-(3,3)\n\nVertical:\n\nColumn 1: (1,1)-(2,1), (2,1)-(3,1)\n\nColumn 2: (1,2)-(2,2), (2,2)-(3,2)\n\nColumn 3: (1,3)-(2,3), (2,3)-(3,3)\n\nWe removed the four edges:\n\n(2,2)-(1,2), (2,2)-(3,2), (2,2)-(2,1), (2,2)-(2,3)\n\nSo the remaining edges are all except those four.\n\nTherefore, the remaining edges include:\n\n- All horizontal edges in rows 1 and 3: so row 1 has both edges, row 3 has both edges.\n\n- Row 2 has no horizontal edges, since (2,1)-(2,2) and (2,2)-(2,3) are removed.\n\n- Vertical edges:\n\nColumn 1: both edges (1,1)-(2,1) and (2,1)-(3,1) remain.\n\nColumn 2: the edge (1,2)-(2,2) is removed, and (2,2)-(3,2) is removed, so column 2 has no vertical edges remaining.\n\nColumn 3: both edges (1,3)-(2,3) and (2,3)-(3,3) remain.\n\nAdditionally, the vertical edges in column 2 are gone, and the horizontal edges in row 2 are gone.\n\nSo the remaining edges form:\n\n- The top row: (1,1)-(1,2), (1,2)-(1,3)\n\n- The bottom row: (3,1)-(3,2), (3,2)-(3,3)\n\n- The left column: (1,1)-(2,1), (2,1)-(3,1)\n\n- The right column: (1,3)-(2,3), (2,3)-(3,3)\n\n- The connections between the top and bottom through the sides? For example, (1,2) is connected to (1,1) and (1,3), but (1,2) is not connected downward anymore. Similarly, (3,2) is connected to (3,1) and (3,3), but not upward.\n\nTherefore, the graph is split into two separate components:\n\n1. The top part: cells (1,1), (1,2), (1,3), (2,1), connected via:\n\n   - (1,1) connected to (1,2) and (2,1)\n\n   - (1,2) connected to (1,1) and (1,3)\n\n   - (1,3) connected to (1,2) and (2,3)\n\n   - (2,1) connected to (1,1) and (3,1)\n\n   - (2,3) connected to (1,3) and (3,3)\n\n   Wait, actually, (2,3) is connected to (1,3) and (3,3), but (2,3) is in row 2, column 3. But row 2 has no horizontal edges remaining, so (2,3) is only connected vertically to (1,3) and (3,3). Similarly, (2,1) is only connected vertically to (1,1) and (3,1).\n\n   So the top component includes:\n\n   - The top row: (1,1), (1,2), (1,3)\n\n   - The left column below: (2,1), (3,1)\n\n   - The right column below: (2,3), (3,3)\n\n   But these are not all connected to each other. For example, (1,3) is connected to (1,2) and (2,3), but (2,3) is not connected to anything else except (1,3) and (3,3). Similarly, (3,3) is only connected to (2,3). So the top component is actually several disconnected parts:\n\n   - The top row forms a path: (1,1)-(1,2)-(1,3)\n\n   - The left side: (1,1)-(2,1)-(3,1)\n\n   - The right side: (1,3)-(2,3)-(3,3)\n\n   These three paths are disconnected from each other.\n\n   Similarly, the bottom row's left and right parts are disconnected from the top.\n\n   Therefore, the graph is actually split into multiple components. Hence, even after removing four edges, the graph is disconnected, so we can't have a single closed trail covering all remaining edges.\n\nThis is getting too convoluted. Maybe it's better to accept that in the 3x3 grid, the maximum closed trail has 10 edges, as per the formula, even if constructing it is non-trivial, and perhaps in the original 11x11 grid, the formula holds because the grid is sufficiently connected that we can remove t/2 edges without disconnecting it.\n\nGiven that in the 3x3 grid, even though my attempts to remove edges kept disconnecting it, perhaps there exists a way to remove only two edges without disconnecting the graph. Let me try again.\n\nThe four odd-degree vertices are (1,2), (2,1), (2,3), (3,2). Let's try to pair (1,2) with (2,3) and (2,1) with (3,2), even though they aren't directly connected. To fix their degrees, we can remove edges that are part of a path between them?\n\nAlternatively, remove the edge between (1,2) and (2,2), and the edge between (2,1) and (2,2). This affects:\n\n- (1,2): degree 2\n\n- (2,1): degree 2\n\n- (2,2): degree 4 - 2 = 2\n\nBut then (2,3) and (3,2) still have degree 3.\n\nTo fix those, remove the edge between (2,3) and (2,2), and the edge between (3,2) and (2,2). But that would require removing a total of four edges, which disconnects the graph as before.\n\nAlternatively, instead of pairing through the center, pair (1,2) with (2,1) by removing the edge between (1,1) and (1,2), and the edge between (1,1) and (2,1). Then:\n\n- (1,2): degree 2 (connected to (1,3) and (2,2))\n\n- (2,1): degree 2 (connected to (3,1) and (2,2))\n\n- (1,1): degree 0 (connected to no one)\n\n- The other two odd-degree vertices, (2,3) and (3,2), still have degree 3.\n\nThen, to fix those two, remove the edge between (2,3) and (3,3), and the edge between (3,2) and (3,3). This would make:\n\n- (2,3): degree 2 (connected to (1,3) and (2,2))\n\n- (3,2): degree 2 (connected to (3,1) and (2,2))\n\n- (3,3): degree 0 (connected to no one)\n\nNow, all degrees are even except (3,3) is 0, which is even, and (1,1) is 0. The center (2,2) still has degree 4 (since we didn't touch its edges). Let's see connectivity:\n\nAfter these removals:\n\n- (1,1) is isolated.\n\n- (3,3) is isolated.\n\n- The rest of the graph:\n\n  - (1,2) connected to (1,3) and (2,2).\n\n  - (1,3) connected to (1,2) and (2,3).\n\n  - (2,3) connected to (1,3) and (2,2).\n\n  - (2,2) connected to (1,2), (2,3), (3,2), and (2,1).\n\n  - (2,1) connected to (3,1) and (2,2).\n\n  - (3,1) connected to (2,1) and (3,2).\n\n  - (3,2) connected to (3,1) and (2,2).\n\nSo the main component includes all except (1,1) and (3,3). Is this connected?\n\nStarting from (1,2):\n\n- Can go to (1,3), then to (2,3), then to (2,2), then to (2,1), then to (3,1), then to (3,2), then back to (2,2). So yes, it's all connected.\n\nTherefore, the remaining graph is connected, with all degrees even. The number of edges removed is 4, but according to the formula, we should only need to remove 2 edges. Wait, but in this case, we had to remove 4 edges because we couldn't pair the odd-degree vertices without affecting other vertices' degrees.\n\nWait a second, in this case, t = 4, so t/2 = 2, but we had to remove 4 edges because the odd-degree vertices were not adjacent to each other, so we had to \"pair\" them through other vertices, which required more edge removals. This indicates that the minimal number of edges to remove can sometimes be higher than t/2 when the graph's structure doesn't allow for efficient pairing.\n\nIn the 3x3 grid, the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even while keeping the graph connected might actually be 4, which is greater than t/2 = 2. Therefore, the formula I recalled earlier might not apply when the graph's structure doesn't allow for efficient pairing of the odd-degree vertices with incident edges.\n\nThis complicates things. So perhaps in grids with high connectivity like the 11x11 grid, where the odd-degree vertices are arranged in long paths along the edges, we can pair them more efficiently.\n\nLet me think again about the 11x11 grid.\n\nThe odd-degree vertices are the 36 non-corner edge cells. Each edge of the grid has 9 non-corner edge cells arranged in a straight line (a path graph of length 9). Each such path has 9 vertices, all of which are odd-degree (degree 3).\n\nIn such a path of 9 vertices, each vertex (except the two endpoints) has two neighbors along the path and one inward edge. The two endpoints of the path have one neighbor along the path and two inward edges? Wait no:\n\nWait, in the path along the top edge of the grid, for example, the first non-corner cell (let's say (1,2)) is adjacent to the corner (1,1), the next cell (1,3), and the inward cell (2,2). So it has degree 3. Similarly, the last non-corner cell on the top edge, say (1,10), is adjacent to the corner (1,11), the previous cell (1,9), and the inward cell (2,10). So also degree 3. The middle cells, like (1,5), are adjacent to (1,4), (1,6), and (2,5), so degree 3.\n\nTherefore, each of these path's vertices has degree 3. To fix their degrees to 2 (even), we need to remove one edge from each.\n\nTo do this for a path of 9 vertices, we can remove every other edge along the path. For example, in the top edge path of 9 vertices, label them T1 through T9. The edges between them are E1 (T1-T2), E2 (T2-T3), ..., E8 (T8-T9).\n\nIf we remove edges E1, E3, E5, E7 (the odd-numbered edges), then:\n\n- The vertices T1 loses one edge (E1), so degree becomes 2.\n\n- T2 loses one edge (E1), so degree becomes 2.\n\n- T3 loses one edge (E2 is removed? Wait no: E2 is between T2-T3, which is not removed. Wait, let's clarify:\n\nIf we remove edges E1, E3, E5, E7:\n\n- T1 is connected to T2 (via E1, which is removed) and inward to I1. So degree 2.\n\n- T2 is connected to T3 (via E2, which remains) and inward to I2. Wait, no: T2's edges are to T1 (removed), T3 (still there), and inward to I2. So degree 2.\n\n- T3 is connected to T2 (still there), T4 (via E3, which is removed), and inward to I3. So degree 2.\n\n- T4 is connected to T3 (removed edge E3), T5 (still there via E4), and inward to I4. So degree 2.\n\n- T5 is connected to T4 (still there via E4), T6 (via E5, which is removed), and inward to I5. Degree 2.\n\n- T6 is connected to T5 (still there via E4), T7 (via E6, which remains), and inward to I6. Degree 3? Wait, no:\n\nWait T6's edges: T5 (still connected via E4?), T7 (via E6), and inward to I6.\n\nWait, let's step through each vertex:\n\nAfter removing E1, E3, E5, E7:\n\n- T1: edges to T2 (removed E1) and inward to I1 → degree 2.\n\n- T2: edges to T1 (removed E1) and T3 (still via E2) and inward to I2 → degree 2.\n\n- T3: edges to T2 (still there), T4 (via E3, removed), and inward to I3 → degree 2.\n\n- T4: edges to T3 (still there via E2?), no: T3-T4 is E3, which is removed. Wait, T4 is connected to T3 via E3 (which is removed), T5 via E4 (still there), and inward to I4 → degree 2.\n\n- T5: connected to T4 (still via E4?), no: T4-T5 is E4, which remains. So T5 has edges to T4 (E4), T6 (via E5, which is removed), and inward to I5 → degree 2.\n\n- T6: connected to T5 (still via E4?), no: T5-T6 is E5, which is removed. So T6 has edges to T7 (via E6), and inward to I6. Wait, T6's edges: left to T5 (removed E5), right to T7 (still via E6), and inward to I6. So degree 2.\n\n- T7: connected to T6 (still via E6), T8 (via E7, which is removed), and inward to I7 → degree 2.\n\n- T8: connected to T7 (still via E6?), no: T7-T8 is E7, which is removed. So T8 has edges to T9 (via E8), and inward to I8 → degree 2.\n\n- T9: connected to T8 (via E8), and inward to I9 → degree 2.\n\nWait a second, actually, all the vertices along the path have their degrees reduced to 2! Because for each Ti (i from 1 to 9), we've removed exactly one edge incident to it along the path, so their degree along the path is reduced by 1, and their inward edge remains, so total degree 2. The inward edges are untouched.\n\nTherefore, by removing four edges along the top edge path (every other edge), we've fixed all nine vertices' degrees to 2. However, the inward edges from each Ti to Ii (the interior cells) are still present, so the interior cells each lose one edge. For example, the inward cell I1 (which is (2,2)) was connected to T1, but now T1's edge to I1 is still there? Wait no:\n\nWait, each Ti is connected inward to Ii. The removal of the path edges doesn't affect the inward edges. So each Ti still has its inward edge to Ii. Therefore, the inward cells (like I1) lose one edge (the connection to Ti), so their degree decreases by 1.\n\nOriginally, each interior cell (like I1) has degree 4: connected to four adjacent cells. For example, I1=(2,2) is connected to T1=(1,2), T2=(1,3), I2=(3,2), and I0? No, in the grid, (2,2) is connected to (1,2), (3,2), (2,1), and (2,3). Wait, hold on:\n\nWait, in the grid, each interior cell (not on the edge) has four neighbors. For example, the cell (2,2) is connected to:\n\n- North: (1,2) [which is T1]\n\n- South: (3,2) [which is T8 in the top edge path? Wait no, T8 is (1,10). Wait, in the top edge path, the inward connections are to row 2.\n\nWait, actually, the inward connection from T1=(1,2) is to (2,2), which is I1.\n\nSimilarly, T2=(1,3) connects inward to (2,3)=I2.\n\nT3=(1,4) connects inward to (2,4)=I3.\n\nContinuing this way, T9=(1,10) connects inward to (2,10)=I9.\n\nTherefore, each Ti connects to Ii, where Ii is the cell directly below it in row 2.\n\nTherefore, each Ii (for i from 1 to 9) is connected to T_i (from the top edge), and also to other cells:\n\n- For I1=(2,2), its neighbors are:\n\n   - North: T1=(1,2)\n\n   - South: (3,2) [which is part of the bottom edge's non-corner cells? Wait, (3,2) is on the bottom edge? No, the bottom edge is row 11. Wait, in an 11x11 grid, the bottom edge is row 11, so (3,2) is an interior cell.\n\nWait, sorry, in the 11x11 grid, the bottom edge is row 11, so the non-corner edge cells on the bottom edge are (11,2) through (11,10). Therefore, (3,2) is an interior cell, not on the edge.\n\nTherefore, the cell I1=(2,2) is an interior cell (since it's not on the boundary), so it has four neighbors:\n\n- North: T1=(1,2)\n\n- South: (3,2)\n\n- West: (2,1) [which is part of the left edge's non-corner cells]\n\n- East: (2,3) [another interior cell]\n\nTherefore, originally, I1=(2,2) had degree 4.\n\nAfter removing the edge between T1 and I1? Wait no, we only removed the path edges along the top edge. The inward edges from the top edge non-corner cells to the interior are still present. Therefore, the inward edge from T1=(1,2) to I1=(2,2) is still there. So I1's degree is still 4? Wait no:\n\nWait, the inward edge from T1 to I1 is still present, so I1 still has that connection. However, we didn't remove any edges connected to I1 except possibly through other paths.\n\nWait, no, the only edges removed are the four along the top edge path. The inward edges from the top edge non-corner cells to the interior are untouched. Therefore, each Ii (the interior cells below the top edge non-corner cells) still have all their original connections except possibly to the T_i's? No, the T_i's are still connected to Ii's.\n\nWait, actually, each Ii is connected to T_i, and also to its own neighbors. Since we only removed edges along the top edge path, the connections from T_i to I_i remain. Therefore, each I_i still has degree 4 minus any edges removed incident to it. But the only edges removed are along the top edge path, which don't involve the I_i's. Therefore, each I_i still has degree 4.\n\nWait a minute, that can't be. Because the T_i's still have their inward edges to I_i's, so I_i's are still connected to T_i's. Therefore, the degrees of the I_i's remain 4. Therefore, by removing the four edges along the top edge path, we've fixed the degrees of all the top edge non-corner cells to 2, but the interior cells below them still have degree 4, which is even, so no problem. The other edges of the grid are untouched.\n\nWait, but what about the other edges? For example, the left edge's non-corner cells. Each of those also has degree 3, and similarly for the right and bottom edges.\n\nAh, right! I only fixed the top edge's non-corner cells. The other three edges also have their own paths of non-corner edge cells, each with 9 vertices of degree 3. So we have to do the same process for each of the four edges.\n\nTherefore, to fix all 36 odd-degree vertices, we need to perform this edge-removal process on each of the four edges' paths.\n\nFor each of the four edges (top, bottom, left, right), we have a path of 9 non-corner edge cells, each of degree 3. For each such path, we can remove 4 edges (every other edge along the path) to reduce the degree of each vertex in the path to 2, without affecting the interior cells' degrees (since the inward edges remain).\n\nTherefore, for each of the four edges, we need to remove 4 edges. Therefore, total edges to remove: 4 edges/edge * 4 edges = 16 edges.\n\nWait, but that can't be right, because 16 edges would leave us with 220 - 16 = 204 edges, but according to the earlier formula, it should be 220 - 18 = 202.\n\nHmm, discrepancy here. Let's see:\n\nEach of the four edges has a path of 9 vertices. For each path of 9 vertices, to reduce each vertex's degree by 1 (from 3 to 2), we need to remove one edge incident to each vertex. Since the vertices are arranged in a path, we can do this by removing every other edge along the path, which removes 4 edges per path (since 9 vertices require 4 edge removals to cover all vertices). Therefore, per edge's path: 4 edges removed.\n\nFour edges (top, bottom, left, right) each requiring 4 edge removals: 4 * 4 = 16 edges removed.\n\nHowever, the interior cells connected to these edge paths might have their degrees affected? Wait, no. The inward edges from the edge non-corner cells to the interior are still present, so the interior cells' degrees remain 4, which is even. The only vertices whose degrees are altered are the edge non-corner cells, which are reduced from 3 to 2.\n\nHowever, the corners of the grid: each corner cell has degree 2 (even), so they are unaffected.\n\nAdditionally, the other edge paths (the bottom, left, right) similarly have their own edge non-corner cells, each needing 4 edges removed per path.\n\nTherefore, total edges to remove: 4 paths * 4 edges per path = 16 edges.\n\nBut wait, the left and right edges also have their own paths of 9 non-corner edge cells. For example, the left edge's non-corner cells are in column 1, rows 2 through 10. Each of these has degree 3 (connected to the corner (1,1), the next cell along the left edge, and the interior cell to the right). Similarly for the right edge.\n\nTherefore, performing the same edge-removal on the left edge's path would require removing 4 edges along that path, and similarly for the bottom edge's path (which is a horizontal path on the bottom row, rows 11, columns 2 through 10).\n\nTherefore, total edge removals: 4 edges per path * 4 paths = 16 edges.\n\nThus, total edges remaining: 220 - 16 = 204.\n\nBut according to the formula earlier, we should be able to do it with 18 edge removals. There's a conflict here.\n\nWhere is the mistake?\n\nAh! Wait a second. When we remove edges along one edge's path, say the top edge, we are not affecting the other edge's paths. However, the interior cells connected to the top edge's non-corner cells are part of the interior grid, which is shared with other edge paths.\n\nWait, no. Each non-corner edge cell is only part of one edge path. For example, the cell (1,2) is on the top edge, not on the left or right or bottom edge. Similarly, a cell on the left edge, like (2,1), is only part of the left edge's path, not the top or bottom.\n\nTherefore, the four edge paths are entirely separate; their only shared cells are the corners, which are not part of the paths.\n\nTherefore, the removals on one edge's path don't affect the other edge's paths or the interior cells' degrees beyond what's local.\n\nHowever, the problem is that the formula I recalled earlier (t/2) assumes that you can pair up the odd-degree vertices with edges in the graph such that each edge removal fixes two vertices. In this case, by processing each edge path independently and removing 4 edges per path (which fixes all 9 vertices in the path), we are effectively pairing each vertex with its neighbor along the path and removing the edge between them, thereby fixing two vertices per edge removal. Since each path has 9 vertices, which is odd, we need to remove 4 edges to cover all 9 vertices (since each edge removal covers two vertices, but with an odd number, you need one more).\n\nWait, actually, in a path with n vertices, the number of edges is n-1. To reduce the degree of each vertex by 1, you need to remove a set of edges such that every vertex has exactly one edge removed incident to it. This is equivalent to selecting a matching that covers all vertices, but since it's a path, this is possible by selecting every other edge, as we did.\n\nIn a path with an odd number of vertices (9), removing every other edge will leave one vertex at the end with only one edge removed? Wait no, let's see:\n\nIn a path of 9 vertices labeled 1 through 9:\n\nEdges: 1-2, 2-3, ..., 8-9.\n\nIf we remove edges 1-2, 3-4, 5-6, 7-8, then:\n\n- Vertex 1: loses edge 1-2 → degree reduced by 1.\n\n- Vertex 2: loses edge 1-2 → degree reduced by 1.\n\n- Vertex 3: loses edge 3-4 → degree reduced by 1.\n\n- Vertex 4: loses edge 3-4 → degree reduced by 1.\n\n- Vertex 5: loses edge 5-6 → degree reduced by 1.\n\n- Vertex 6: loses edge 5-6 → degree reduced by 1.\n\n- Vertex 7: loses edge 7-8 → degree reduced by 1.\n\n- Vertex 8: loses edge 7-8 → degree reduced by 1.\n\n- Vertex 9: loses no edge → degree remains the same?\n\nWait, vertex 9 is only connected to vertex 8 via edge 8-9, which we didn't remove. So vertex 9's degree along the path is still 1 (connected to 8), and its inward edge is still there, so total degree remains 3 - 1 (if inward edge is still there?) Wait no:\n\nWait, in this case, the inward edge from vertex 9 (which is on the top edge) to the interior cell is still present. The degree along the path for vertex 9 is 1 (only connected to 8), but its total degree is 1 (path) + 1 (inward) = 2? Wait, no:\n\nWait, each non-corner edge cell has degree 3: two along the edge and one inward. If we remove one edge along the path, its degree along the path is reduced by 1, so total degree becomes 2 (even). For vertex 9, we didn't remove any edge along its path (since we stopped at edge 7-8), so its path degree remains 1 (connected to 8), and its inward edge is still there, so total degree is 2, which is even. So actually, all vertices are fixed.\n\nTherefore, in a path of 9 vertices, removing 4 edges (every other edge) fixes all 9 vertices' degrees to even. Each removed edge fixes two vertices, but since 9 is odd, the last vertex is fixed by the last removed edge? Wait, no, in the above, vertex 9 was only affected by the inward edge remaining, and its path degree was 1, but inward edge kept it at 2. Wait, actually, in the above example, vertex 9's path degree is 1 (connected to 8), and inward degree 1, totaling 2, which is even. So it works.\n\nTherefore, for each path of 9 vertices, we need to remove 4 edges to fix all 9 vertices' degrees. Since there are four such paths (top, bottom, left, right), each requiring 4 edge removals, the total number of edges to remove is 4 paths * 4 edges per path = 16 edges.\n\nHowever, this results in a total of 220 - 16 = 204 edges remaining.\n\nBut according to the earlier formula, it should be 220 - 18 = 202. There's a discrepancy of 2 edges.\n\nWhere is this coming from?\n\nAh! The four edge paths are not the only odd-degree vertices. Wait, no: the problem states that all non-corner edge cells are the odd-degree vertices. Each edge has 9 non-corner edge cells, and there are four edges, so 4*9=36, which matches t=36. So all odd-degree vertices are accounted for in these four paths.\n\nTherefore, processing each path by removing 4 edges should fix all 36 vertices. So why the discrepancy with the formula?\n\nBecause in the formula, we assumed that you can pair up the 36 odd-degree vertices with 18 edges (each edge removal fixes two vertices), but in reality, when the odd-degree vertices are arranged in paths, you need to remove more edges per path because of the path structure.\n\nSpecifically, in a path of 9 vertices, you need to remove 4 edges to fix all 9 vertices, which is more than 9/2 = 4.5, rounded up to 5? Wait, 4 edges can fix 8 vertices with one overlap? No, in the example above, 4 edges fixed all 9 vertices. Because each edge removal affects two vertices, but in a path, the end vertices are only affected by one edge removal.\n\nWait, in the path of 9 vertices, each internal vertex is between two edges, so removing an edge affects two vertices. The two end vertices are each affected by one edge removal. Therefore, with 4 edge removals in the path, you can cover 4*2 - overlap? Wait, actually:\n\nEach edge removal in the path affects two vertices. However, when you remove edge E1 between v1 and v2, it affects v1 and v2. Then removing edge E3 between v3 and v4 affects v3 and v4, etc. In the example above with 9 vertices and removing edges E1, E3, E5, E7:\n\n- E1 affects v1 and v2\n\n- E3 affects v3 and v4\n\n- E5 affects v5 and v6\n\n- E7 affects v7 and v8\n\nSo that's 8 vertices affected, but vertex v9 is not affected by any edge removal along the path. However, as we saw earlier, vertex v9 still has its inward edge remaining, so its total degree becomes 2 (1 from the path and 1 inward), which is even. Therefore, even though the path degree of v9 wasn't reduced, its total degree is still even because the inward edge remains. Thus, vertex v9's degree is adjusted by the inward edge's presence, but since we didn't remove that edge, its total degree remains 3 (path degree 1 + inward degree 1 = 2? Wait, no:\n\nWait, the non-corner edge cell on the end of the path (like v9) has:\n\n- Along the path: connected to v8 (since we didn't remove the last edge E8=v8-v9)\n\n- Inward: connected to the interior cell.\n\nTherefore, its path degree is 1, inward degree is 1, total degree 2, which is even. So even though we didn't remove an edge incident to v9 along the path, its total degree is even because the inward edge compensates.\n\nTherefore, in this case, all 9 vertices have their total degrees reduced to even by either removing an edge along the path or by having their inward edge remain. Thus, only 4 edges need to be removed per path to fix all 9 vertices.\n\nTherefore, total edges to remove: 4 paths * 4 edges = 16 edges.\n\nBut according to the formula, it should be t/2 = 18 edges. The difference is 2 edges. Where is this coming from?\n\nAh! The formula assumes that you can pair the odd-degree vertices with edges such that each edge removed fixes two vertices, but in reality, when the odd-degree vertices are arranged in paths, some of the edge removals needed to fix the vertices are along the path, which may not be directly between the odd-degree vertices but between them and other vertices (like the inward edges). Wait no, in our case, the inward edges are not part of the path edges; they're separate.\n\nWait, perhaps the formula is still correct, but my approach of processing each path independently is suboptimal, and there's a way to do it with fewer edge removals by strategically choosing edges that are shared between different paths or something?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the formula's assumption that you can remove t/2 edges is correct, and my path-based approach is overcounting because some edge removals can fix multiple paths simultaneously?\n\nWait, for example, an edge that is part of two different paths? But in the grid, the edge paths are on different edges of the grid, so their edges don't overlap. The only shared vertices between the paths are the corners, but the corners are not part of the paths.\n\nTherefore, the edge sets for the four paths are entirely separate. Therefore, the minimal number of edges to remove is indeed 16, which is more than t/2=18? Wait no, 16 is less than 18. Wait, 16 is less than 18. Wait, 4*4=16, which is less than 18. Wait, t=36, so t/2=18. But we only needed to remove 16 edges. This suggests that the formula's lower bound of t/2=18 is not tight in this case, and the actual minimal number is 16.\n\nHmm, this is confusing. Let me recast:\n\nEach edge removal can fix two odd-degree vertices. Since we have 36 odd-degree vertices, we need at least 18 edge removals to fix all of them (since each edge fixes two). However, in our path-based approach, we used 16 edge removals, which fixed all 36 vertices. This is possible because some edge removals are fixing two vertices from the same path, but since the paths are separate, those edge removals are independent. Wait, but 16 edge removals can only fix 32 vertices (16*2), but we have 36 vertices to fix. Therefore, this suggests an inconsistency.\n\nAh! Here's the mistake: in the path-based approach, each edge removal along a path fixes two vertices of that path. Since each path has 9 vertices, to fix all 9 vertices in a path, you need ceil(9/2)=5 edge removals per path? Wait no:\n\nWait in the path with 9 vertices, when we removed 4 edges, we fixed all 9 vertices. Each edge removal fixed two vertices, but because the path is linear, the end vertex is only affected by one edge removal. Therefore, the total number of vertex fixes per edge removal is two, except for the last one which fixes one more? Wait no:\n\nWait in the path of 9 vertices with edges E1 to E8:\n\nRemoving E1 fixes v1 and v2.\n\nRemoving E3 fixes v3 and v4.\n\nRemoving E5 fixes v5 and v6.\n\nRemoving E7 fixes v7 and v8.\n\nThis totals 4 edge removals, fixing 8 vertices (v1-v2, v3-v4, v5-v6, v7-v8). The ninth vertex, v9, is not directly fixed by an edge removal along the path, but its total degree becomes even because its inward edge remains. Therefore, the ninth vertex's degree is adjusted by the inward edge's presence, but since we didn't remove that edge, its total degree is still even. So effectively, the ninth vertex's odd degree was already \"fixed\" by not removing its inward edge, but actually, its path degree was 1 (odd) and inward degree 1 (odd), totaling 2 (even). Wait, no:\n\nWait, the non-corner edge cell's total degree is path degree (along the edge) plus inward degree. Originally, the path degree is 2 (for internal vertices) or 1 (for the endpoints). The inward degree is always 1 (connected to the interior cell). Therefore:\n\n- For an internal vertex in the path (not endpoint): path degree 2, inward degree 1 → total degree 3 (odd).\n\n- For an endpoint vertex: path degree 1, inward degree 1 → total degree 2 (even).\n\nWait a second! Hold on, this is a critical realization.\n\nEarlier, I thought that all non-corner edge cells have degree 3, but let's recalculate:\n\nTake a non-corner edge cell on the top edge, say (1,2):\n\n- Its neighbors are:\n\n   - Left: (1,1) [a corner, degree 2]\n\n   - Right: (1,3) [another non-corner edge cell on the top edge]\n\n   - Down: (2,2) [an interior cell]\n\nTherefore, its degree is 3 (correct).\n\nSimilarly, an internal non-corner edge cell on the top edge, say (1,5):\n\n- Left: (1,4)\n\n- Right: (1,6)\n\n- Down: (2,5)\n\nSo degree 3.\n\nHowever, the endpoint non-corner edge cell on the top edge, say (1,10):\n\n- Left: (1,9)\n\n- Right: (1,11) [a corner]\n\n- Down: (2,10)\n\nThus, its degree is 3 as well.\n\nWait, so all non-corner edge cells have degree 3, regardless of position. Therefore, even the endpoint non-corner edge cells have degree 3. Therefore, my previous analysis was wrong.\n\nTherefore, the endpoint non-corner edge cell (like (1,10)) has path degree 2 (connected to (1,9) and (1,11)), but (1,11) is a corner with degree 2. Wait no:\n\nWait, (1,10) is connected to (1,9) (left), (1,11) (right, which is a corner), and (2,10) (down). So three connections, so degree 3. The corner (1,11) is connected to (1,10) and (2,11), so it has degree 2.\n\nTherefore, all non-corner edge cells have degree 3, and the corner cells have degree 2.\n\nTherefore, in the path of the top edge's non-corner cells, each has degree 3, and we need to reduce each by 1 to make them 2.\n\nHowever, when we remove an edge along the path, say between (1,2) and (1,3), this affects both (1,2) and (1,3), reducing their degrees by 1.\n\nBut the endpoint non-corner cell, say (1,10), is connected to (1,9) and (1,11). If we remove the edge between (1,9) and (1,10), then (1,10)'s degree becomes 2 (connected to (1,9) is removed, so only (1,11) and (2,10)). Wait no:\n\nWait, if we remove the edge between (1,9) and (1,10), then (1,10) loses that connection, so its degree is:\n\n- Connected to (1,11) [corner], (2,10), and the removed edge to (1,9). So degree 2.\n\nSimilarly, (1,9) loses that connection, so its degree becomes 2 (connected to (1,8), (1,10) removed, and inward). Wait, no:\n\nWait, (1,9) is connected to (1,8), (1,10), and inward. If we remove the edge to (1,10), then (1,9)'s degree becomes 2.\n\nTherefore, even the endpoint non-corner cells can be fixed by removing an edge adjacent to them.\n\nTherefore, in the path of 9 vertices, to reduce each vertex's degree by 1, we need to remove 4 edges as before, and this works for all vertices, including the endpoints.\n\nTherefore, each path of 9 vertices requires 4 edge removals to fix all 9 vertices.\n\nSince there are four such paths, total edge removals needed are 4 * 4 = 16.\n\nHowever, according to the formula, it should be t/2 = 36/2 = 18.\n\nThis suggests that my path-based approach is undercounting the required edge removals, but actually, it's matching the required vertex fixes: 16 edge removals * 2 vertices per edge = 32 vertex fixes, but we have 36 vertices to fix. This inconsistency indicates an error.\n\nWait a second: each edge removal in a path fixes two vertices in that path. Since there are four paths, each needing 4 edge removals, that's 16 edge removals, each fixing two vertices, totaling 32 vertex fixes. But we have 36 vertices to fix. Therefore, this approach is insufficient by 4 vertices. Contradiction.\n\nAh! Here's the mistake: when we remove an edge along the path, we are not only fixing the two vertices on that edge but also potentially affecting other vertices through their inward connections? No, the inward connections are to interior cells, which are separate.\n\nWait, no. The inward connections are separate and not part of the path edges. The vertices in question are only the non-corner edge cells, each of which has three edges: two along the edge path and one inward. Therefore, when we remove an edge along the path, we only affect the two vertices connected by that path edge. The inward edge remains, so the inward degree is unaffected.\n\nTherefore, each edge removal in the path only affects two vertices (the ones on either side of the removed edge) in terms of their path degree. Their total degree is then path degree (reduced by 1) plus inward degree (unchanged), so total degree reduced by 1, making it even.\n\nTherefore, each edge removal in a path fixes two vertices. To fix all 9 vertices in a path, you need to perform 4 edge removals, which fix 8 vertices, but the ninth vertex is fixed by... Wait, no, as we saw in the example earlier, the ninth vertex still has its total degree even without an edge removal incident to it along the path. Because its path degree was 1 (connected to the eighth vertex) and inward degree 1, totaling 2. So even though we didn't remove an edge adjacent to it along the path, its total degree is already even.\n\nWait, but originally, its path degree was 2 (since it's an endpoint connected to the previous cell and the corner). Wait no:\n\nWait, take the last vertex on the top edge path, say (1,10):\n\n- It is connected to (1,9) (previous cell), (1,11) (corner), and (2,10) (inward).\n\nSo its path degree along the top edge is 2 (connected to (1,9) and (1,11)), and inward degree 1. Total degree 3 (odd).\n\nWhen we remove the edge between (1,9) and (1,10), then (1,10)'s path degree becomes 1 (only connected to (1,11)), so total degree 1 + 1 = 2 (even). Similarly, (1,9) loses one path connection, so its path degree is 1 (connected to (1,8) and (1,10) removed), but wait:\n\nWait, (1,9) is connected to (1,8), (1,10), and inward. If we remove the edge to (1,10), then (1,9)'s path degree is 1 (connected to (1,8) and (1,10) is removed), so path degree 1, inward degree 1, total degree 2.\n\nTherefore, the vertex (1,10) is fixed by the edge removal next to it, and vertex (1,9) is also fixed. The vertex before that, (1,8), if we removed the edge between (1,7)-(1,8), then (1,8) loses one path connection, etc.\n\nTherefore, in the path of 9 vertices, removing every other edge starting from the first will indeed fix all 9 vertices:\n\n- Removing edge 1-2 fixes vertices 1 and 2.\n\n- Removing edge 3-4 fixes vertices 3 and 4.\n\n- Removing edge 5-6 fixes vertices 5 and 6.\n\n- Removing edge 7-8 fixes vertices 7 and 8.\n\n- The ninth vertex (vertex 9) is fixed by the removal of edge 7-8? No, vertex 9 is not affected by that. Wait:\n\nWait, vertex 9 is connected to vertex 8 via edge 8-9. If we removed edge 7-8, then vertex 8's connection to 7 is removed, but vertex 8 is still connected to vertex 9. Therefore, vertex 9's connections are still to vertex 8 and the corner, so its path degree remains 2 until we remove edge 8-9.\n\nWait a minute! I think I made a mistake in the edge labeling.\n\nLet me re-express the path of 9 vertices as vertices V1 through V9 connected by edges E1 through E8:\n\n- E1: V1-V2\n\n- E2: V2-V3\n\n- E3: V3-V4\n\n- E4: V4-V5\n\n- E5: V5-V6\n\n- E6: V6-V7\n\n- E7: V7-V8\n\n- E8: V8-V9\n\nTherefore, to remove every other edge starting with E1:\n\nRemove E1, E3, E5, E7.\n\nThen:\n\n- V1 is connected to V2 (removed E1), so its path degree is 1 (only to V2 is removed, so V1 is only connected to V2's other side? Wait no:\n\nWait V1 is connected only to V2 via E1. If E1 is removed, V1 has no path connection left. Wait, no:\n\nWait V1 is at the end of the path, so its only path connections are to V2. If we remove E1 (V1-V2), then V1's path degree becomes 0, and its inward degree is 1, so total degree 1 (odd). Oh no! This is the mistake!\n\nEarlier, I incorrectly assumed that the endpoint vertices had path degree 1, but actually, the first vertex (V1) on the path has path degree 1 (connected only to V2), and the last vertex (V9) has path degree 1 (connected only to V8). The internal vertices have path degree 2.\n\nTherefore, removing E1 (the first edge) disconnects V1 from the path, so its path degree becomes 0, and total degree is inward degree 1, which is odd. That's a problem.\n\nAh! This is the critical error in the earlier analysis. Removing an edge at the end of the path causes the endpoint vertex to lose its only path connection, resulting in an odd total degree.\n\nTherefore, the strategy of removing every other edge starting from the first edge is flawed because it leaves the first vertex with an odd degree.\n\nSimilarly, removing E7 (the seventh edge, between V7 and V8) would leave V8 connected to V9 via E8, but V7 would lose its connection to V8, so V7's path degree becomes 1 (connected to V6), and inward degree 1, totaling 2 (even). Wait:\n\nLet me recast the removals with corrected endpoint considerations.\n\nSuppose we remove edges E2, E4, E6, E8 (the even-numbered edges):\n\n- E2: V2-V3\n\n- E4: V4-V5\n\n- E6: V6-V7\n\n- E8: V8-V9\n\nThen:\n\n- V1: connected only to V2 (still connected via E2 is removed? No, E2 is between V2 and V3. V1 is only connected to V2 via E1, which is not removed. So V1's path degree remains 1 (connected to V2), inward degree 1, total degree 2 (even).\n\n- V2: connected to V1 (E1) and V3 (E2 removed), so path degree 1, inward degree 1, total degree 2 (even).\n\n- V3: connected to V2 (E2 removed) and V4 (E3 still there), so path degree 1, inward degree 1, total degree 2 (even).\n\n- V4: connected to V3 (E3 still there) and V5 (E4 removed), path degree 1, inward degree 1, total degree 2.\n\n- V5: connected to V4 (E4 removed) and V6 (E5 still there), path degree 1, inward degree 1, total degree 2.\n\n- V6: connected to V5 (E5 still there) and V7 (E6 removed), path degree 1, inward degree 1, total degree 2.\n\n- V7: connected to V6 (E6 removed) and V8 (E7 still there), path degree 1, inward degree 1, total degree 2.\n\n- V8: connected to V7 (E7 still there) and V9 (E8 removed), path degree 1, inward degree 1, total degree 2.\n\n- V9: connected to V8 (E8 removed), so path degree 0, inward degree 1, total degree 1 (odd). Oh no, V9 now has odd degree.\n\nTherefore, this approach also fails because the last vertex's path degree becomes 0, leading to odd total degree.\n\nTherefore, to fix both endpoints, we need to ensure that each endpoint's path degree is reduced by 1, but they only have one path edge, so removing that edge would reduce their path degree to 0, but their inward degree is 1, totaling 1 (odd). Therefore, this is impossible; we cannot fix the endpoints by removing path edges because that would make their total degree odd.\n\nThis is a problem. Therefore, the only way to fix the endpoints is to not remove the edge adjacent to them, but then their path degree remains 1 (odd), so their total degree would be 1 (path) + 1 (inward) = 2 (even). Wait a minute:\n\nWait, for the endpoint V1:\n\n- Path degree: 1 (connected to V2)\n\n- Inward degree: 1 (connected to interior cell)\n\nTotal degree: 2 (even). So actually, the endpoint vertices are already even-degree!\n\nWait a second, this is the key realization I was missing earlier.\n\nThe non-corner edge cells on the ends of their respective paths (the endpoints of the edge's non-corner path) are actually degree 2 in the path (connected to one neighbor along the edge and the corner), plus inward degree 1, totaling 3 (odd). Wait no:\n\nWait, let's take the first non-corner edge cell on the top edge, V1 = (1,2):\n\n- Connected to:\n\n   - Left: (1,1) [a corner], which is degree 2.\n\n   - Right: (1,3) [next cell on the path].\n\n   - Down: (2,2) [interior cell].\n\nTherefore, its degree is 3 (correct).\n\nBut its path along the edge includes the corner? No, the path is only along the non-corner edge cells. The path we're considering is just the non-corner edge cells, which form a path from (1,2) to (1,10) on the top edge. The corner (1,1) is not part of this path.\n\nTherefore, the path degree for V1=(1,2) is 2: connected to (1,3) and the corner (1,1). Wait, but the corner is not part of the path's edges. The path's edges are only between the non-corner edge cells.\n\nTherefore, the path degree for V1 is 1 (connected to V2=(1,3)), and the other connection to the corner is not part of the path's edges.\n\nAh! This is where the confusion was.\n\nThe path we're considering for the top edge's non-corner cells is only the sequence of non-corner edge cells: V1=(1,2), V2=(1,3), ..., V9=(1,10). These are connected by edges between them: V1-V2, V2-V3, ..., V8-V9. The connections to the corners are not part of this path's edges.\n\nTherefore, the path degree for each vertex in the path is:\n\n- For internal vertices (not endpoints): 2 (connected to previous and next in the path).\n\n- For the two endpoints of the path (V1 and V9): 1 (connected to their single neighbor in the path).\n\nTheir total degree is path degree plus inward degree (1) plus any connections to corners? Wait no:\n\nWait, the corner connections are separate. For example, V1=(1,2) is connected to the corner (1,1), but that corner is not part of the path's edges. Therefore, the path's edges only account for connections between non-corner edge cells.\n\nTherefore, the total degree of V1=(1,2) is:\n\n- Path degree: 1 (connected to V2).\n\n- Connections to the corner (1,1): 1.\n\n- Inward connection: 1 (to (2,2)).\n\nTotal degree: 3 (odd).\n\nSimilarly, the last vertex V9=(1,10) has:\n\n- Path degree: 1 (connected to V8).\n\n- Connection to the corner (1,11): 1.\n\n- Inward connection: 1 (to (2,10)).\n\nTotal degree: 3 (odd).\n\nThe internal vertices, like V2=(1,3):\n\n- Path degree: 2 (connected to V1 and V3).\n\n- No corner connection (since it's not an endpoint).\n\n- Inward connection: 1.\n\nTotal degree: 3 (odd).\n\nTherefore, all vertices in the path have degree 3 (odd), and we need to reduce each by 1 to make them even.\n\nTo do this, we can remove edges along the path. Each edge removal affects two vertices in the path, reducing their path degrees by 1.\n\nFor the endpoints V1 and V9, their path degree is 1. If we remove the edge adjacent to them (e.g., remove V1-V2 and V8-V9), then:\n\n- V1's path degree becomes 0, so total degree: 0 (path) + 1 (corner) + 1 (inward) = 2 (even).\n\n- V9's path degree becomes 0, total degree: 0 + 1 (corner) + 1 (inward) = 2 (even).\n\n- The other vertices affected by the edge removals will have their path degrees reduced by 1.\n\nHowever, if we remove the edge V1-V2, then V1's path degree drops to 0, and V2's path degree drops to 1 (since it loses connection to V1 but still has connection to V3).\n\nSimilarly, removing V8-V9 causes V9's path degree to drop to 0 and V8's path degree to drop to 1.\n\nTherefore, to fix all vertices:\n\nLet's try removing edges V1-V2, V2-V3, V4-V5, V6-V7, V8-V9. That's five edges. But let's see:\n\nWait, let's plan carefully.\n\nGoal: reduce each vertex's path degree by 1.\n\nFor internal vertices (V2 to V8):\n\n- Each needs their path degree reduced by 1.\n\nFor the endpoints V1 and V9:\n\n- Need their path degree reduced by 1 (from 1 to 0).\n\nTo achieve this:\n\n- Remove V1-V2: reduces V1's path degree by 1 (to 0), and V2's path degree by 1 (to 1).\n\n- Remove V2-V3: reduces V2's path degree by 1 (to 0), and V3's path degree by 1 (to 1).\n\n- Remove V3-V4: reduces V3's path degree by 1 (to 0), and V4's path degree by 1 (to 1).\n\n- Remove V4-V5: reduces V4's path degree by 1 (to 0), and V5's path degree by 1 (to 1).\n\n- Remove V5-V6: reduces V5's path degree by 1 (to 0), and V6's path degree by 1 (to 1).\n\n- Remove V6-V7: reduces V6's path degree by 1 (to 0), and V7's path degree by 1 (to 1).\n\n- Remove V7-V8: reduces V7's path degree by 1 (to 0), and V8's path degree by 1 (to 1).\n\n- Remove V8-V9: reduces V8's path degree by 1 (to 0), and V9's path degree by 1 (to 0).\n\nThis requires removing all 8 edges of the path, which would fix all vertices' path degrees to 0 for endpoints and reduce internal vertices appropriately. But this results in removing all edges of the path, which is too many.\n\nAlternatively, to minimize the number of edge removals:\n\nWe can pair the vertices and remove edges between them:\n\n- Remove V1-V2 and V8-V9 (to fix the endpoints).\n\n- Remove V2-V3 and V7-V8 (to fix the next layer).\n\n- Remove V4-V5 and V6-V7 (to fix the middle).\n\nThis totals 6 edge removals.\n\nLet's see:\n\nAfter removing V1-V2 and V8-V9:\n\n- V1: path degree 0, total degr