erior cell's degree even:\n\nThe interior cell connected to (1,2) is (2,2), which has degree 4. If we remove the inward edge from (1,2) to (2,2), then (2,2)'s degree becomes 3 (odd), so we'd have to fix that as well.\n\nTherefore, in the 3x3 grid, to fix the four non-corner edge cells (each on their own edge path of length 1), we have to remove two edges per non-corner cell, but this complicates things. However, using the formula ceil(n/2) where n=1, we get ceil(1/2)=1 edge per path, so 4 paths *1 edge=4 edges, which matches the earlier attempt where we needed to remove four edges to fix all four odd-degree vertices while keeping the graph connected. This resulted in a disconnected graph, but if we instead remove edges smartly:\n\nFor each single-vertex path (a single non-corner edge cell):\n\nTo reduce its degree from 3 to 2, we must remove one of its edges. To also not affect other vertices' degrees:\n\n- Remove the inward edge. This reduces the cell's degree to 2 (path edges remain: connected to the corner and the adjacent cell if any). Wait, but for a single non-corner edge cell on a 3x3 grid, it's connected to the corner, the adjacent corner? No, it's connected to the corner and the inward cell and one other edge cell? No, in a 3x3 grid, the top edge's non-corner cell (1,2) is connected to (1,1) [corner], (1,3) [which is a corner], and (2,2) [interior]. Wait, no, in a 3x3 grid, the top edge has cells (1,1), (1,2), (1,3). The non-corner edge cell is (1,2), connected to (1,1), (1,3), and (2,2). So degree 3.\n\nTo reduce its degree to 2, we can remove one of its three edges:\n\n- Remove the edge to (1,1): then (1,2)'s degree becomes 2 (connected to (1,3) and (2,2)), but (1,1)'s degree drops to 1 (from 2), which is odd. We'd have to fix that.\n\n- Remove the edge to (1,3): similarly affects (1,3)'s degree.\n\n- Remove the inward edge to (2,2): then (2,2)'s degree drops to 3 (from 4), which is odd.\n\nTherefore, to fix only the non-corner edge cell, we must also fix