e odd-degree vertices, and for each pair, find a path between them, and then remove edges along that path in such a way that the degrees of the two endpoints are adjusted. Wait, but actually, perhaps more precisely, for each pair of odd-degree vertices, we can remove a single edge that connects them if they are adjacent, but if they are not adjacent, we might have to remove a path of edges between them, but in a way that each removal along the path affects the degrees appropriately.\n\nAlternatively, actually, according to the concept of an Eulerian trail, if a graph has 2k vertices of odd degree, then it is possible to add k-1 edges to make it Eulerian, but since we can't add edges, we need to remove edges instead. Each edge we remove can potentially reduce the number of odd-degree vertices by 0, 2, or even increase it, depending on which edges we remove.\n\nWait, actually, let me think step by step.\n\nSuppose we have a graph G with t vertices of odd degree. Let t be even (which it is here, t = 36). To make the graph Eulerian, we need all degrees even. Each time we remove an edge, we decrease the degree of its two endpoints by 1. Therefore, if both endpoints were odd, removing the edge will make them both even (since odd minus 1 is even). If one was odd and the other even, then the odd becomes even and the even becomes odd. If both were even, then both become odd. Therefore, in order to minimize the number of edges to remove to make all degrees even, we need to pair up the odd-degree vertices and for each pair, find a path between them and remove edges along that path in such a way that each removal affects the degrees appropriately.\n\nWait, actually, perhaps the minimal number of edges to remove is t/2, since each edge removal can fix two odd-degree vertices. However, this requires that we can pair up the odd-degree vertices and for each pair, there exists a path between them where we can remove edges in such a way that the removals only affect those two vertice