 and C are odd. Wait, that didn't help. Alternatively, remove the edge between B and C and also the edge between C and D? Then you have A-B (degree 1 for A, 1 for B), and C-D removed, so C and D have degree 0. Wait, that's not helpful. Hmm, maybe in a path graph with four vertices, you can't make all degrees even by removing edges? Wait, actually, if you remove the middle edge (B-C), then you have two edges: A-B and C-D. Then the degrees are A:1, B:1, C:1, D:1. Still all odd. Hmm, so that's bad.\n\nAlternatively, remove both the edge A-B and the edge C-D. Then you have only the edge B-C remaining. Then degrees: A:0, B:1, C:1, D:0. So A and D are even, but B and C are odd. Still two odd degrees. Hmm, so in the path graph with four vertices, you cannot make all degrees even by removing edges? Because you started with two odd degrees, and each edge removal can affect two vertices, but depending on which edges you remove, you might end up with more odd degrees.\n\nWait a second, so in this case, t=2, but you can't reduce it to zero? Because every time you remove an edge, you flip the parity of two vertices. So starting with two odd vertices (A and D), if you remove an edge incident to A, say A-B, then A becomes even (degree 0), and B becomes odd (degree 1). Now the odd degrees are B and D. Then, if you remove the edge D-C, then D becomes even (0), and C becomes odd (degree 1). Now the odd degrees are B and C. Then, if you remove the edge B-C, then B becomes even (0) and C becomes even (0). Wait, hold on, if you remove B-C, then B had degree 1 (from A-B removed) and C had degree 1 (from D-C removed). So removing B-C would make B's degree 0 and C's degree 0. So actually, by removing three edges: A-B, D-C, and B-C, you end up with all degrees even? Wait, but then you have removed three edges. Wait, but that's more than t/2 = 1. Hmm, so perhaps my initial thought was wrong?\n\nWait, let me recast:\n\nOriginal degrees: A:1, B:2, C:2, D:1 (so t=2, the two endpoints). If we remove edge A-B: then degrees become A:0, B:1, C:2, D:1. Now the odd degrees are B and D (t=2). Then remove edge D-C: degrees become A:0, B:1, C:1, D:0. Now odd degrees are B and C (t=2). Then remove edge B-C: degrees become A:0, B:0, C:0, D:0. Now all even. So, three edges removed. Hmm, so that's three edges, which is more than t/2 = 1. So my initial thought was wrong. Therefore, the minimal number of edges to remove might not be t/2. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe in some graphs it's possible to do better, but in others not? So perhaps the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even is at least t/2, but could be more depending on the graph structure?\n\nHmm, in the path graph with four vertices, we needed to remove three edges to make all degrees even, even though t=2. That seems bad. Wait, but actually, in that case, the graph is a path, so it's a tree. Maybe in trees, you have to remove a lot?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps in a connected graph, the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even is equal to t/2 if the graph is 2-connected or something? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps in our grid graph, since it's highly connected (each internal vertex has degree 4), we can pair up the odd-degree vertices and for each pair, find a path between them and remove edges along that path in such a way that each removal only affects those two vertices' parities? Hmm, maybe not, because the path between two vertices would pass through other vertices, whose degrees would be affected.\n\nAlternatively, maybe in the grid graph, since it's a planar graph with high connectivity, we can pair up the odd-degree vertices and for each pair, find a path between them and remove all edges along that path except for one? Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps think of the problem differently. Since we need a closed walk that doesn't repeat edges, the maximum such walk is equivalent to the size of the largest Eulerian trail in some subgraph of the grid graph where that subgraph is Eulerian. Therefore, the problem reduces to finding the largest subgraph H of the grid graph such that H is Eulerian (all vertices even degree), and then the maximum number of edges in such an H is the answer.\n\nTherefore, to maximize the number of edges in H, we need the largest possible Eulerian subgraph. Since the original grid graph has 36 vertices of odd degree, we need to remove a set of edges such that the remaining graph H has all even degrees, and we want H to have as many edges as possible. So, equivalently, we want to remove as few edges as possible while making all degrees even.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to: find the minimum number of edges to remove from the grid graph so that all remaining vertices have even degree, then subtract that number from the total number of edges in the grid graph to get the maximum number of edges in such a subgraph H, which would then be the maximum length of the closed trail.\n\nTherefore, we need to compute:\n\nMaximum number of edges in closed trail = Total edges in grid - Minimum edges to remove to make all degrees even.\n\nSo, first, let's compute the total number of edges in the 11x11 grid graph.\n\nIn an m x n grid graph, the number of edges is (m*(n-1) + n*(m-1)). Because, for each row, there are (n-1) horizontal edges, and there are m rows. Similarly, for each column, there are (m-1) vertical edges, and there are n columns. So total edges = m*(n-1) + n*(m-1).\n\nIn our case, m = 11, n = 11. So total edges = 11*(11 - 1) + 11*(11 - 1) = 11*10 + 11*10 = 110 + 110 = 220.\n\nSo total edges: 220.\n\nNow, we need to compute the minimum number of edges to remove so that all remaining vertices have even degree.\n\nAs above, the original grid graph has 36 vertices of odd degree (the non-corner edge vertices). Let me denote the number of odd-degree vertices as t = 36.\n\nIn graph theory, it is known that in any graph, the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even is equal to t/2, provided that the graph is connected. Wait, but in my earlier path graph example, that didn't hold. Wait, maybe in a connected graph?\n\nWait, in the path graph with four vertices (which is connected), t = 2, but the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even was 3, which is more than t/2 = 1. Hmm, so that contradicts that idea.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps in a connected graph, the minimal number is t/2, but only if t is even? Wait, but in that path graph example, t was even (2), but the minimal number was 3.\n\nAlternatively, maybe my calculation was wrong?\n\nWait, let me check again for the path graph with four vertices: A-B-C-D.\n\nOriginal degrees: A:1, B:2, C:2, D:1. So t = 2 (A and D are odd).\n\nIf I remove edge B-C, then degrees become: A:1, B:1, C:1, D:1. So t becomes 4, which is worse. So that's bad.\n\nIf I remove edges A-B and D-C, then degrees become: A:0, B:1, C:1, D:0. So now t = 2 (B and C). Hmm, still 2.\n\nIf I remove edges A-B, B-C, and D-C, then degrees: A:0, B:0, C:0, D:1. So t = 1, which is odd, which is impossible.\n\nWait, actually, you can't get all even degrees in that path graph? Wait, but in the path graph with four vertices, can you even get all even degrees?\n\nWait, let's see: suppose we remove all edges. Then all degrees are zero, which is even. So by removing all four edges, we get all degrees even. But that's removing four edges, which is more than t/2. Alternatively, maybe remove three edges as before: A-B, B-C, and D-C. Then degrees are A:0, B:0 (since A-B is removed, so B loses one edge; B-C is removed, so B loses another edge; so B had degree 2, now degree 0), C: degree from original 2, minus B-C (so -1) and minus D-C (another -1), so degree 0; D: degree 1 (original) minus D-C removed, so degree 0. So actually, all degrees are even (zero). So by removing three edges, we can get all degrees even. But that required removing three edges. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, if we remove edges A-B and C-D, then the graph becomes B-C, with degrees: A:0, B:1, C:1, D:0. So two odd degrees. Then remove edge B-C, resulting in all zeros. So that's three edges removed again.\n\nAlternatively, is there a way to do it with two edges? Let's see. Suppose we remove edges A-B and C-D. Then as above, degrees: A:0, B:1, C:1, D:0. So two odd degrees. Then, if we could remove an edge that affects both B and C, but the only edge between them is B-C. So if we remove B-C, that's the third edge. So indeed, it seems you have to remove three edges.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the formula is different? Maybe in a connected graph, the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even is t/2 if the graph is bipartite? Wait, the path graph is bipartite. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps in a bipartite graph, the minimal number is t/2? Wait, in the path graph with four vertices, t=2, so t/2=1, but we needed three edges. Hmm, so that doesn't fit.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps my confusion arises because in the path graph, you can't actually get an Eulerian graph by removing edges? Because an Eulerian graph must be connected? Wait, if you remove edges, you might disconnect the graph. In the path graph with four vertices, if you remove the middle edge B-C, you get two disconnected edges: A-B and C-D. Each of those components is just an edge, which is Eulerian (since both endpoints have degree 1, which is odd, so actually not Eulerian). Wait, Eulerian circuits require all vertices to have even degree AND the graph to be connected. So if you remove edges and disconnect the graph, then each connected component must individually have all even degrees to be Eulerian. Therefore, in the path graph example, if you remove the middle edge B-C, you have two disconnected edges, each of which is a single edge (so two components, each with two vertices of degree 1). Since each component has vertices of odd degree, they are not Eulerian. Therefore, in order to have an Eulerian graph, you have to have all components Eulerian, which requires each component to have all even degrees. Therefore, in order to have a connected Eulerian graph, you have to have all degrees even and connected.\n\nTherefore, in the path graph with four vertices, if you remove three edges, you end up with no edges, which is a graph with all degrees zero (even) and connected? Wait, no, if you remove all edges, the graph is disconnected (four isolated vertices). So, actually, the only connected Eulerian subgraph would be the empty graph (no edges), which has all degrees zero, but that's trivial.\n\nTherefore, in the path graph, there is no non-trivial connected Eulerian subgraph. Therefore, the minimal number of edges to remove to get a connected Eulerian graph is actually impossible? Because you can't have a connected graph with all even degrees unless you have at least some edges. Wait, but in the path graph, the only connected Eulerian subgraphs would have to be... Hmm, actually, a connected graph with all even degrees must have an Eulerian circuit. But in the path graph, the only connected subgraphs with all even degrees would have to have all degrees even. For example, if you take the entire path graph, it has two vertices of degree 1 (odd), so not Eulerian. If you take a subgraph with just the middle two vertices B and C connected by the edge B-C, then that's a single edge, so degrees 1 and 1 (both odd), so not Eulerian. If you take just the edge A-B, that's a single edge, degrees 1 and 0? Wait, no: in the subgraph with just A-B, the degrees are A:1, B:1. Still both odd. Similarly, any single edge has two odd-degree vertices. Therefore, in the path graph, there is no non-empty connected subgraph that is Eulerian. Therefore, the only connected Eulerian subgraph is the empty graph. Therefore, in such a case, the minimal number of edges to remove to get a connected Eulerian subgraph is the total number of edges, which is 3 edges in the path graph with four vertices? Wait, no, total edges in the path graph with four vertices is three edges. To get the empty graph, you have to remove all three edges. Therefore, the minimal number is three. But the total edges is three, so you have to remove all edges. Hmm.\n\nThis suggests that in some graphs, you might have to remove almost all edges to get an Eulerian subgraph, but in others, you can do it with fewer.\n\nTherefore, returning to our original grid graph problem, which is a connected graph with 36 vertices of odd degree, and we need to find the minimal number of edges to remove so that the remaining graph has all even degrees and is connected? Wait, but actually, the problem doesn't require the remaining graph to be connected, just that the closed trail is in the remaining graph. However, a closed trail must be a single connected component, right? Because a trail is a connected path. So, the subgraph H must be connected? Because otherwise, if it's disconnected, you can't have a single closed trail that covers all edges of H. Therefore, we actually need H to be connected and have all even degrees, so that it has an Eulerian circuit.\n\nTherefore, in order to have a connected Eulerian subgraph H, we need to remove edges from G such that H is connected and all degrees are even. Therefore, the minimal number of edges to remove is the minimal number such that the remaining graph is connected and all degrees even.\n\nTherefore, in the path graph example, since you can't have a connected Eulerian subgraph except the empty graph, the minimal number of edges to remove is 3 (all edges). In the grid graph, which is highly connected, perhaps we can do better?\n\nSo, in our grid graph, which is connected and has 36 vertices of odd degree, we need to find the minimal number of edges to remove so that the remaining graph is connected and all degrees even.\n\nThis seems related to the concept of an Eulerian subgraph in a connected graph. There might be some known results here, but I'm not sure. Alternatively, maybe we can think of it as pairing up the odd-degree vertices and connecting them through paths, removing edges along those paths to adjust the degrees.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps a better approach is to model the grid as a graph and try to compute the minimal edge set to remove.\n\nAlternatively, maybe think of the grid as a bipartite graph. Since it's bipartite, with the two partitions, say, black and white squares, as in a chessboard.\n\nIn a bipartite graph, any cycle must be of even length. Also, in a bipartite graph, the number of vertices with odd degree must be even, which it is (36). Moreover, in a bipartite graph, an Eulerian circuit requires that all vertices have even degree, which we can achieve by removing edges.\n\nBut perhaps in bipartite graphs, there's a more efficient way to pair the odd-degree vertices?\n\nAlternatively, here's another idea: since the grid is bipartite, with partitions A and B, and all edges go from A to B. Each edge removal affects one vertex in A and one in B. Since we have 36 vertices of odd degree, half of them must be in A and half in B? Wait, actually, in a bipartite graph, the number of vertices of odd degree in partition A must equal the number in partition B, because the sum of degrees in partition A equals the sum of degrees in partition B (since every edge goes from A to B). Therefore, the number of odd-degree vertices in A and B must be equal. Since total odd-degree vertices are 36, then 18 in A and 18 in B.\n\nTherefore, to make all degrees even, we need to pair each odd-degree vertex in A with an odd-degree vertex in B and connect them with a path, then remove edges along that path in such a way that the degrees are adjusted. Wait, but how exactly?\n\nAlternatively, here's a method from graph theory: to make a bipartite graph Eulerian, you can add edges (but we can't add edges), so instead, remove edges. Since in bipartite graphs, any Eulerian circuit must have all degrees even.\n\nAlternatively, the problem is similar to the Chinese Postman Problem, where you have to find the minimal route that traverses every edge, possibly repeating some edges, but here we want the maximal trail that doesn't repeat edges, so it's kind of the opposite.\n\nWait, in the Chinese Postman Problem, if the graph isn't Eulerian, you have to duplicate edges to make it Eulerian, so the minimal total length of duplicated edges is the minimal number of edges to add to make it Eulerian. But in our case, we can't add edges, so we have to remove edges. The question is then, what is the minimal number of edges to remove so that the remaining graph is Eulerian (connected, all degrees even). Then the maximum trail length is total edges minus that number.\n\nSo, perhaps in the Chinese Postman Problem terms, the minimal number of edges to remove is equal to the minimal number of edges to add to make it Eulerian in the original graph? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps in a connected bipartite graph with t vertices of odd degree, the minimal number of edges to remove to make it Eulerian is t/2. Since in bipartite graphs, the odd-degree vertices are equally divided between the two partitions, so pairing them up and for each pair, find a path between them and remove edges along that path? But how?\n\nAlternatively, here's a possible strategy:\n\n1. Since the grid is bipartite with partitions A and B, each with 61 and 60 vertices (since 11x11 grid has 121 vertices; 121 is odd, so one partition has (121 +1)/2 = 61, the other 60).\n\nWait, actually, in an odd by odd grid, the two partitions differ by one. Since 11x11 is odd, the number of black squares is (11*11 +1)/2 = 61, and white squares is 60.\n\nTherefore, partition A (black) has 61 vertices, partition B (white) has 60 vertices.\n\nNow, the odd-degree vertices: as we calculated before, there are 36 non-corner edge vertices, each of degree 3 (odd). The corner vertices have degree 2 (even). So, all the odd-degree vertices are among the edge vertices (non-corner). Since the grid is bipartite, the edge vertices are also divided between partitions A and B.\n\nLet me see: in the grid, the corner vertices are all in partition A or B? Let's suppose the top-left corner is in partition A. Then, moving right along the top row, the next cell is in B, then A, etc. Since it's an 11x11 grid, which is odd-sized, the corners will all be in the same partition. Specifically, the four corners: (1,1), (1,11), (11,1), (11,11). Since (1,1) is in A, then (1,11) would be in partition B if 11 is odd? Wait, let's think.\n\nIn a chessboard coloring, the color of a cell (i,j) is determined by (i+j) mod 2. So, if (1,1) is black (A), then (1,2) is white (B), (1,3) black, etc. So for the top row:\n\nRow 1: columns 1 to 11: colors A,B,A,B,...,A (since 11 is odd, ends on A). Therefore, the top-right corner (1,11) is A. Similarly, the bottom-left corner (11,1): (11 + 1) mod 2 = 12 mod 2 = 0, so white (B). Wait, no:\n\nWait, the color of (i,j) is (i + j) mod 2. So (1,1): 1+1=2 mod 2 = 0, which could be considered black or white depending on convention, but let's say partition A is 0, partition B is 1.\n\nSo (1,1): 0 (A)\n\n(1,2): 1 (B)\n\n(1,3): 0 (A)\n\n...\n\n(1,11): since 1 + 11 = 12, mod 2 is 0 (A). So top-right corner is A.\n\nSimilarly, (11,1): 11 + 1 = 12 mod 2 = 0 (A)? Wait, 11 + 1 = 12, yes, so (11,1) is also A? Wait, but that contradicts my earlier thought.\n\nWait, hold on: Let me compute (11,1): row 11, column 1.\n\n(11 + 1) mod 2 = 12 mod 2 = 0, so same as (1,1). So all four corners are in partition A? Because:\n\nTop-left: (1,1): 0\n\nTop-right: (1,11): (1 + 11)=12 mod2=0\n\nBottom-left: (11,1): (11 +1)=12 mod2=0\n\nBottom-right: (11,11): (11+11)=22 mod2=0\n\nSo all four corners are in partition A. Therefore, the four corner vertices are in partition A, and each has degree 2 (even). The non-corner edge vertices: as we said, there are 36 of them, each on the edges but not corners, and each has degree 3 (odd). Now, since the grid is bipartite, the non-corner edge vertices must be split between partitions A and B.\n\nLet me see: Take a non-corner edge vertex on the top edge (not a corner). For example, (1,2). Its coordinates: row 1, column 2. (1+2)=3 mod2=1, so it's in partition B. Similarly, (1,3): (1+3)=4 mod2=0, so in A. Wait, actually, along the top edge (row 1), the non-corner vertices alternate between B and A. Since the top row starts with A at (1,1), then (1,2) is B, (1,3) is A, ..., up to (1,11) which is A. Since there are 11 columns, the non-corner vertices on the top edge are columns 2 through 10 (since column 11 is the corner). Wait, columns 2 to 10 inclusive: that's 9 vertices. Starting from column 2 (B), alternating. Since 9 is odd, the last one (column 10) would be B? Let's see:\n\nColumns 2: B\n\n3: A\n\n4: B\n\n5: A\n\n6: B\n\n7: A\n\n8: B\n\n9: A\n\n10: B\n\nYes, column 10 is even column number, so (1,10): row 1 + column 10 = 11, which is odd, so partition B.\n\nTherefore, on the top edge, non-corner vertices: 9 vertices, starting with B and alternating, ending with B. So total B's: 5, A's: 4? Wait, let's count:\n\nFrom column 2 to 10: that's 9 columns.\n\nStarting at column 2 (B), then A, B, A,..., ending at column 10 (B). Since it's 9 elements, starting with B and alternating, the number of B's is ceil(9/2) = 5, and A's is 4.\n\nSimilarly, on the bottom edge (row 11), which also has non-corner vertices from column 2 to 10. Let's see:\n\nRow 11, column 2: (11 + 2) mod2 = 13 mod2=1 (B)\n\nRow 11, column 3: 14 mod2=0 (A)\n\nContinuing similarly, so same as top edge: 5 B's and 4 A's.\n\nOn the left edge (column 1), non-corner vertices are rows 2 through 10 (since row 1 and 11 are corners). Let's take row 2, column 1: (2+1)=3 mod2=1 (B). Row 3, column 1: 4 mod2=0 (A). So alternating starting with B. There are 9 vertices (rows 2-10). So again, 5 B's and 4 A's.\n\nOn the right edge (column 11), non-corner vertices are rows 2 through 10. Let's see row 2, column 11: (2 + 11)=13 mod2=1 (B). Row 3, column 11: 14 mod2=0 (A), etc. So same as the left edge: 5 B's and 4 A's.\n\nTherefore, total non-corner edge vertices:\n\nTop edge: 5 B, 4 A\n\nBottom edge: 5 B, 4 A\n\nLeft edge: 5 B, 4 A\n\nRight edge: 5 B, 4 A\n\nTotal B's: 5*4 = 20\n\nTotal A's: 4*4 = 16\n\nWait, hold on: each edge (top, bottom, left, right) has 5 B and 4 A non-corner edge vertices, so four edges: 4*(5 + 4) = 4*9=36 total non-corner edge vertices, which matches.\n\nTherefore, of the 36 non-corner edge vertices, 20 are in partition B and 16 are in partition A.\n\nWait, but earlier we said that in the entire grid, partition A has 61 vertices and partition B has 60. The four corners are all in partition A, so partition A has 61 - 4 = 57 internal vertices plus the 16 non-corner edge vertices? Wait, no, actually, the non-corner edge vertices are part of the perimeter.\n\nWait, let me clarify:\n\nTotal partition A vertices: 61.\n\nThese include:\n\n- The four corner vertices (all in A).\n\n- The non-corner edge vertices that are in A: as calculated above, on each edge, there are 4 A's (non-corner edge vertices) per edge, times 4 edges: 4*4=16 A non-corner edge vertices.\n\n- The internal vertices (not on any edge): total internal vertices are 81 (since total vertices 121 minus 40 perimeter vertices). Of these 81 internal vertices, how many are in partition A?\n\nSince the entire grid has 61 A's and 60 B's, and the perimeter has 40 vertices: of which 4 are corners (all A) and 36 are non-corner edges (16 A and 20 B). Therefore, the number of A internal vertices is total A's minus corner A's minus non-corner edge A's: 61 - 4 - 16 = 41. Similarly, B internal vertices: 60 - 20 = 40? Wait:\n\nWait total A internal vertices: total A vertices (61) minus corner A vertices (4) minus non-corner edge A vertices (16) = 61 - 4 -16 = 41.\n\nTotal B internal vertices: total B vertices (60) minus non-corner edge B vertices (20) minus corner B vertices (0, since all corners are A) = 60 - 20 = 40.\n\nTherefore, internal vertices: 41 A and 40 B.\n\nSo, going back, the odd-degree vertices are all the non-corner edge vertices: 16 A and 20 B, totaling 36. So in partition A, there are 16 odd-degree vertices, and in partition B, 20 odd-degree vertices.\n\nTo make all degrees even, we need to pair each odd-degree vertex in A with an odd-degree vertex in B and connect them with a path, then remove edges along that path in such a way that the degrees are adjusted appropriately.\n\nBut how exactly?\n\nIn bipartite graphs, any path between two vertices alternates partitions. So a path from an A-odd vertex to a B-odd vertex will have odd length? Because starting at A, then B, A,..., ending at B (since it's a path between A and B). The number of edges is equal to the number of vertices minus one. So if the path has k vertices, then it has k-1 edges. If it starts at A and ends at B, then the number of vertices is odd or even?\n\nWait, starting at A, then B, A,..., ending at B: the number of vertices must be odd, because starting at A and ending at B requires an odd number of vertices. For example: A-B (2 vertices, 1 edge: odd number of edges). A-B-A-B (4 vertices, 3 edges: odd number of edges). Wait, actually, the number of edges is one less than the number of vertices. So if the path has an odd number of vertices, then the number of edges is even; if even number of vertices, then odd number of edges.\n\nWait, let me see:\n\nPath length (number of edges) is equal to the number of vertices minus one.\n\nIf the path starts at A and ends at B, then the number of vertices must be odd (since you alternate partitions). So the number of vertices is odd, so the number of edges is even.\n\nTherefore, a path from an A-odd vertex to a B-odd vertex has an even number of edges.\n\nNow, if we remove all edges along such a path, then we would be removing an even number of edges. But how does that affect the degrees?\n\nEach time we remove an edge, we decrease the degree of its two endpoints by 1. So, if we remove all edges along a path from an A-odd vertex to a B-odd vertex, then:\n\n- The starting A-odd vertex loses degree equal to the number of edges incident to it along the path. But actually, each edge incident to it along the path is removed, so its degree is decreased by the number of edges along the path that are incident to it. Since the path starts at the A-vertex, it has exactly one edge incident to it along the path, so its degree is decreased by 1.\n\nSimilarly, the ending B-odd vertex also has its degree decreased by 1 (since the path ends there, so only one edge incident to it along the path is removed).\n\nThe internal vertices along the path (which are alternating A and B) each have two edges along the path incident to them (except the endpoints), so their degrees are decreased by 2. Since those internal vertices originally had odd or even degrees?\n\nWait, actually, the internal vertices along the path are non-endpoint vertices on the path. Since the path is between two odd-degree vertices, and we're removing edges along the path, the internal vertices (which are part of the grid's original graph) might have had even or odd degrees to begin with.\n\nWait, but in our case, the only vertices with odd degrees are the non-corner edge vertices. All internal vertices have even degrees (4), and the non-corner edge vertices have odd degrees (3). The corners have even degrees (2).\n\nTherefore, the internal vertices along the path (which are not endpoints) must be either internal vertices (degree 4, even) or non-corner edge vertices (degree 3, odd). Wait, but if we're taking a path between two non-corner edge vertices (the odd-degree ones), then the internal vertices of the path could include other non-corner edge vertices or internal vertices.\n\nBut if we take a path between two non-corner edge vertices, passing through other vertices, then those internal vertices on the path could be either internal vertices (with even degree) or other non-corner edge vertices (with odd degree). Hmm, complicating.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the idea is that by removing a path between two odd-degree vertices, we can adjust their parities without affecting other vertices' parities too much? Wait, but removing edges along the path will affect all the vertices along the path.\n\nSpecifically, for the two endpoints (the original odd-degree vertices), their degrees are reduced by 1 (from the removal of the edge incident to them on the path), turning them from odd to even.\n\nFor the internal vertices on the path:\n\n- If an internal vertex is an internal vertex (degree 4 originally), then removing two edges incident to it along the path (since it's in the middle of the path) will reduce its degree by 2, keeping it even (4-2=2).\n\n- If an internal vertex is a non-corner edge vertex (degree 3 originally), then removing two edges incident to it along the path will reduce its degree by 2, making it 1, which is odd. Wait, that's bad because we wanted to keep all degrees even.\n\nAlternatively, if an internal vertex is a non-corner edge vertex (which has degree 3), and we remove two edges from it along the path, then its degree becomes 1, which is still odd, so that's not helpful.\n\nHmm, this seems problematic. Therefore, perhaps this approach of removing a path between two odd-degree vertices doesn't work because it might create new odd-degree vertices along the path.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we need to take a different approach. Perhaps instead of pairing the odd-degree vertices and removing paths between them, we can instead find a set of edge-disjoint paths that connect pairs of odd-degree vertices, and then remove all edges in those paths. Each such path would connect two odd-degree vertices and adjust their degrees while affecting internal vertices appropriately.\n\nBut if the internal vertices along the paths have even degrees originally, then removing two edges from them (if they are internal) would keep their degrees even (since 4-2=2). However, if the internal vertices along the path are themselves non-corner edge vertices (which have odd degrees), then removing two edges from them would make their degrees odd minus 2, which could be odd or even?\n\nWait, for example, a non-corner edge vertex has degree 3. If we remove two edges incident to it along the path, its degree becomes 1, which is still odd. So that vertex remains odd, which is not desired.\n\nTherefore, this suggests that we cannot use paths that go through other odd-degree vertices, because that would not fix their degrees. Therefore, perhaps we must connect only through even-degree vertices (internal vertices), so that the internal vertices along the path have even degrees and thus their degrees remain even after edge removals.\n\nTherefore, to pair two odd-degree vertices, we need a path between them that only goes through even-degree vertices (internal vertices). Since non-corner edge vertices are the only odd-degree vertices, so such a path would have to go through internal vertices only.\n\nIn the grid graph, between any two non-corner edge vertices, can we find a path that goes only through internal vertices? Probably, since the grid is highly connected.\n\nFor instance, take two non-corner edge vertices on the same side of the grid. To connect them via internal vertices, you can go around the perimeter? Wait, but going along the perimeter would go through other non-corner edge vertices, which have odd degrees. Alternatively, go inward.\n\nSuppose we have two non-corner edge vertices on the top edge, say at positions (1,2) and (1,10). To connect them without going through other edge vertices, we could go down into the internal grid and then back up. For example, go from (1,2) down to (2,2), then right to (2,3), etc., but that might still require passing through other edge vertices? Wait, actually, if we go down from (1,2) to (2,2), then (2,2) is an internal vertex (since it's not on the top or bottom edge), so that's okay. Then proceed along internal vertices until we reach near (1,10), then go back up. This path would avoid other edge vertices except the endpoints.\n\nTherefore, such a path exists. Therefore, between any two non-corner edge vertices, we can find a path that uses only internal vertices (even-degree) as intermediaries.\n\nTherefore, if we can pair up the 36 odd-degree vertices (20 in B and 16 in A) into 18 pairs, and for each pair, find a path between them that only uses internal vertices (so that the internal vertices along the path have even degrees, so removing two edges from them keeps their degrees even), then removing all edges along those 18 paths would fix the degrees of the paired odd-degree vertices to even, while leaving all other vertices' degrees unchanged (except possibly the internal vertices along the paths, but since we remove two edges from each internal vertex along the path, their degrees decrease by 2, which preserves their evenness).\n\nTherefore, this would result in a graph where all odd-degree vertices have been paired and adjusted to even degrees, and all other vertices retain their even degrees (since internal vertices had even degrees and we only removed even numbers of edges from them). Thus, the resulting graph would be Eulerian.\n\nTherefore, if we can do this, then the minimal number of edges to remove is equal to the total number of edges along all these 18 paths.\n\nHowever, we need to minimize the number of edges removed, so we need to choose paths between the paired odd-degree vertices that are as short as possible.\n\nTherefore, to minimize the total number of edges removed, we should pair the odd-degree vertices as close as possible to each other and use the shortest possible paths between them.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to:\n\n- Pair the 36 odd-degree vertices (20 in B and 16 in A) into 18 pairs.\n\n- For each pair, find the shortest path between them that uses only internal vertices (i.e., not going through other odd-degree vertices).\n\n- Sum the lengths of all these shortest paths, which will be the minimal number of edges to remove.\n\nThen, the maximum number of doors Karthik can traverse is total edges (220) minus this minimal number.\n\nHowever, this seems quite involved to calculate, especially for an 11x11 grid. Maybe there is a pattern or formula for such a grid?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's a better way to compute the minimal number of edges to remove.\n\nWait, in any graph, the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even is equal to the number of odd-degree vertices divided by 2, but only if the graph is connected and you can pair the odd-degree vertices with paths that don't interfere. Wait, but in our case, with 36 odd-degree vertices, that would be 18 edges to remove, but that seems too low because each path between two odd-degree vertices might require multiple edges.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the minimal number of edges to remove is equal to the number of odd-degree vertices divided by 2, but in our case, since each edge removal can fix two odd-degree vertices, then theoretically, if you can find for each pair of odd-degree vertices a single edge that connects them, then you can fix both with one edge removal. However, in our grid graph, most non-corner edge vertices are not adjacent to each other.\n\nFor example, two non-corner edge vertices on the same side of the grid are not adjacent unless they are next to each other. For instance, on the top edge, the non-corner edge vertices are at columns 2 through 10. The ones next to each other, like (1,2) and (1,3), are adjacent, but (1,2) and (1,4) are not adjacent; they are separated by (1,3). So most non-corner edge vertices are not adjacent to each other.\n\nTherefore, most pairs of odd-degree vertices are not adjacent, so we can't fix them with a single edge removal. Therefore, we have to use paths between them, each path contributing multiple edges to be removed.\n\nTherefore, the minimal number of edges to remove is going to be more than 18.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's a different way to think about this problem.\n\nSince the grid is a planar graph, and we know that in planar graphs, there are certain bounds on the number of edges, but I don't know if that helps here.\n\nWait, another thought: In any graph, the size of the maximum Eulerian trail is equal to the total number of edges minus the number of vertices with odd degree divided by 2. Wait, is that a thing?\n\nWait, let me think. If a graph has t vertices of odd degree, then the minimal number of edges to remove to make it Eulerian is t/2, as per some sources I recall, but only if the graph is connected. Because each edge removal can fix two odd-degree vertices.\n\nWait, according to some references, in a connected graph, the minimum number of edges to remove to make all degrees even is indeed t/2, where t is the number of odd-degree vertices. However, this requires that the graph remains connected after the removal. Wait, but in our earlier path graph example, with t=2, the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even while keeping the graph connected would require removing only one edge? But in the path graph with four vertices, if we remove the middle edge, then the graph becomes two disconnected edges, which are not connected. So in order to keep the graph connected, you have to remove fewer edges?\n\nWait, no, in the path graph with four vertices, if we remove an edge that is not between the two odd-degree vertices, then the graph remains connected but the degrees of the odd-degree vertices might not be fixed.\n\nWait, let's try with the four-vertex path graph: A-B-C-D, with odd degrees at A and D.\n\nIf we remove edge B-C, then the graph becomes A-B and C-D, which are disconnected. Not connected.\n\nIf we remove edge A-B, then the graph becomes B-C-D, with degrees: B:1, C:2, D:1. So now the odd degrees are B and D. Still two, but now the graph is connected (B-C-D). So we still have two odd degrees, but the graph is connected. So we can continue: now remove edge D-C, resulting in B-C, which has degrees 1 and 1. Still two odd degrees. Then remove edge B-C, resulting in isolated vertices. So to make all degrees even while keeping the graph connected, you can't; the only connected Eulerian subgraph is the empty graph.\n\nTherefore, in this case, the minimal number of edges to remove to make the graph connected and Eulerian is 3, which is more than t/2 = 1.\n\nTherefore, that formula doesn't hold when connectivity is required.\n\nTherefore, perhaps in a connected graph, the minimal number of edges to remove to make it Eulerian is at least t/2, but can be more depending on the graph's structure.\n\nIn our grid graph, which is highly connected, maybe the number is close to t/2? Since the grid is so well-connected, perhaps we can find a set of t/2 edges whose removal fixes all the odd degrees without disconnecting the graph.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps in a grid graph, the minimal number of edges to remove is equal to the number of odd-degree vertices divided by 2, because of the high connectivity allowing us to pair up the odd-degree vertices with paths that don't require too many edge removals.\n\nAlternatively, let me think of a smaller grid and see what happens.\n\nLet's try a 3x3 grid. Maybe working through an example will help.\n\nConsider a 3x3 grid. Let's analyze it.\n\nFirst, total vertices: 9.\n\nTotal edges: horizontal: 3 rows, each with 2 edges, so 3*2=6 horizontal. Vertical: 3 columns, each with 2 edges, so 3*2=6 vertical. Total edges: 12.\n\nNow, degrees of the vertices:\n\nCorners: 4 corners, each has degree 2 (even).\n\nEdge non-corner vertices: each side of the 3x3 grid has 3 vertices, subtract 2 corners, so 1 non-corner edge vertex per side. There are 4 sides, so 4 non-corner edge vertices. Each of these has degree 3 (odd).\n\nInternal vertices: the center vertex, which has degree 4 (even).\n\nSo total odd-degree vertices: 4 (the non-corner edge vertices). So t=4.\n\nTherefore, in this 3x3 grid, we have 4 odd-degree vertices. So according to the formula t/2=2, we might expect that we can remove 2 edges to make all degrees even.\n\nLet's see.\n\nFirst, the non-corner edge vertices are the middle of each side. Let's label them as follows:\n\nTop edge non-corner: let's say (1,2)\n\nRight edge non-corner: (2,3)\n\nBottom edge non-corner: (3,2)\n\nLeft edge non-corner: (2,1)\n\nThese are the four odd-degree vertices.\n\nTo make all degrees even, we need to remove edges such that each of these four vertices loses one degree (from 3 to 2).\n\nLet me try to find two edges to remove that can fix two pairs.\n\nSuppose we remove the edge between (1,2) and (1,1) [the top-left corner], and the edge between (2,3) and (1,3) [the top-right corner].\n\nWait, but (1,2) is connected to (1,1), (1,3), and (2,2). Similarly, (2,3) is connected to (1,3), (3,3), and (2,2).\n\nIf we remove the edge (1,2)-(1,1), then (1,2)'s degree becomes 2 (even), and (1,1)'s degree becomes 1 (odd). Hmm, that's bad because we introduced a new odd-degree vertex at the corner.\n\nAlternatively, remove the edge (1,2)-(2,2) and the edge (3,2)-(2,2). Then:\n\n- (1,2) loses one edge to (2,2), so degree becomes 2 (even).\n\n- (3,2) loses one edge to (2,2), so degree becomes 2 (even).\n\n- (2,2) loses two edges (to (1,2) and (3,2)), so its degree becomes 4 - 2 = 2 (still even).\n\n- The other non-corner edge vertices: (2,3) and (2,1) are still at degree 3. So that didn't fix all.\n\nHmm. Alternatively, remove two edges that are on opposite sides.\n\nSuppose we remove the edge between (1,2) and (2,2), and the edge between (3,2) and (2,2). Then:\n\n- (1,2): degree 2 (even)\n\n- (3,2): degree 2 (even)\n\n- (2,2): degree 4 - 2 = 2 (even)\n\nBut the other two non-corner edge vertices, (2,1) and (2,3), still have degree 3.\n\nAlternatively, remove edges (2,1)-(1,1) and (2,3)-(3,3):\n\nThen:\n\n- (2,1): degree 3 -1 = 2 (even)\n\n- (2,3): degree 3 -1 = 2 (even)\n\n- The corners (1,1) and (3,3) now have degree 1 (odd). So now we have new odd-degree vertices at the corners.\n\nThis is problematic.\n\nAlternatively, remove edges that are on the same side. For example, on the top edge, remove the edge between (1,2) and (1,1), and between (1,2) and (1,3). But (1,2) is only connected to (1,1), (1,3), and (2,2). So removing both edges from (1,2) to its neighbors on the top row would leave (1,2) with degree 1 (odd), which is worse.\n\nAlternatively, remove two edges that are adjacent but on different sides.\n\nWait, maybe remove edges (1,2)-(2,2) and (2,1)-(2,2). Then:\n\n- (1,2) loses one edge: degree 2 (even)\n\n- (2,1) loses one edge: degree 2 (even)\n\n- (2,2) loses two edges: degree 4 - 2 = 2 (even)\n\n- The other two non-corner edge vertices: (2,3) and (3,2) still have degree 3.\n\nHmm, still two odd degrees left.\n\nAlternatively, remove edges (1,2)-(2,2), (2,3)-(2,2), (3,2)-(2,2), and (2,1)-(2,2). That's four edges. Then:\n\n- (1,2): degree 2\n\n- (2,1): degree 2\n\n- (2,3): degree 2\n\n- (3,2): degree 2\n\n- (2,2): degree 4 - 4 = 0 (even)\n\nSo all non-corner edge vertices are even, and (2,2) is 0, which is even. The corners remain at degree 2 (even). So that works, but required removing four edges. Which is t/2 * 2? Since t=4, t/2=2, but we had to remove four edges. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a better way?\n\nSuppose we remove the two edges that are diagonally opposite? For example, remove (1,2)-(2,2) and (3,2)-(2,2). Then, as before, that fixes (1,2) and (3,2) to degree 2, but leaves (2,1) and (2,3) at degree 3.\n\nAlternatively, remove edges (1,2)-(1,1) and (3,2)-(3,3). Then:\n\n- (1,2) degree 2 (even)\n\n- (3,2) degree 2 (even)\n\n- (1,1) degree 1 (odd)\n\n- (3,3) degree 1 (odd)\n\nSo now we have two new odd-degree vertices at the corners. Then, to fix those, we could remove edges incident to them, but that might require more edges.\n\nAlternatively, remove edges (1,2)-(1,1) and (3,2)-(3,3), then also remove edges (1,1)-(2,1) and (3,3)-(2,3). Then:\n\n- (1,1): degree 0 (even)\n\n- (3,3): degree 0 (even)\n\n- (2,1): degree 1 (odd)\n\n- (2,3): degree 1 (odd)\n\nNow we have two more odd degrees at (2,1) and (2,3). This is getting worse.\n\nHmm, this is tricky. Maybe in the 3x3 grid, the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even while keeping the graph connected is 4? Because if you remove the four edges around the center vertex (the four edges connecting to (2,2)), then:\n\n- The center vertex (2,2) would have degree 0 (even).\n\n- Each non-corner edge vertex is connected to the center vertex via one edge. So removing those four edges would reduce each non-corner edge vertex's degree by 1, from 3 to 2 (even). The corners remain at degree 2 (even). So this works! So by removing the four edges connecting the center to the non-corner edge vertices, we have:\n\n- Each non-corner edge vertex loses one edge (to the center), so their degrees go from 3 to 2.\n\n- The center loses all four edges, degree 0.\n\n- Corners remain at 2.\n\nTherefore, all degrees are even, and the graph remains connected? Wait, after removing those four edges, is the graph still connected?\n\nThe original grid without those four edges: the center is disconnected from the rest, but the rest of the grid is still connected through the outer edges. For example, you can still go around the perimeter. So the graph remains connected. Therefore, in this case, by removing four edges, we can make all degrees even and keep the graph connected. But four edges is equal to t (which was 4), not t/2=2.\n\nHmm, so in this case, it required removing as many edges as there were odd-degree vertices. Wait, but t=4, so removing four edges.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps in the 3x3 grid, the minimal number of edges to remove is equal to the number of odd-degree vertices, because each odd-degree vertex needs to lose one degree, and each edge removal can only affect two vertices. Since we have four odd-degree vertices, each needing to lose one degree, that requires four \"degree reductions\", and each edge removal can provide two degree reductions (one for each endpoint). Therefore, the minimal number of edges to remove is four / two = two edges. Wait, but in reality, we needed to remove four edges. So there's a contradiction here.\n\nWait, the degree reductions needed: each of the four odd-degree vertices needs to have their degree reduced by 1 (to make them even). Each edge removal can reduce the degree of two vertices by 1 each. Therefore, to achieve four total degree reductions, you need two edge removals (each providing two reductions). But in practice, in the 3x3 grid, we needed four edge removals. Why the discrepancy?\n\nBecause the edges we remove must be such that each removal targets two vertices that both need a degree reduction. However, in the 3x3 grid, the non-corner edge vertices are only adjacent to the center and one or two corners and another non-corner edge vertex? Wait, let's look at the non-corner edge vertices:\n\nTake (1,2): connected to (1,1), (1,3), and (2,2).\n\nSimilarly, (2,3): connected to (1,3), (3,3), and (2,2).\n\n(3,2): connected to (3,1), (3,3), and (2,2).\n\n(2,1): connected to (1,1), (3,1), and (2,2).\n\nSo each non-corner edge vertex is connected to the center and two other vertices: one corner and another non-corner edge vertex (except for (1,2) which is connected to (1,1) and (1,3), both corners? Wait no, (1,2) is connected to (1,1) [corner], (1,3) [corner], and (2,2) [center].\n\nWait, actually, each non-corner edge vertex on the side is connected to two corners and the center? Wait no:\n\nWait, let's take (1,2):\n\n- North: nothing (it's on the top edge)\n\n- South: (2,2)\n\n- East: (1,3)\n\n- West: (1,1)\n\nSo yes, it's connected to two corners and the center.\n\nSimilarly, (2,3):\n\n- North: (1,3)\n\n- South: (3,3)\n\n- East: nothing\n\n- West: (2,2)\n\nSo connected to two corners and the center.\n\nSame for (3,2) and (2,1).\n\nTherefore, each non-corner edge vertex is connected to two corners and the center.\n\nTherefore, the edges connecting non-corner edge vertices to corners are separate from their connections to the center.\n\nTherefore, if we want to reduce the degree of a non-corner edge vertex by 1, we can either remove its connection to a corner or to the center.\n\nHowever, the corners are already even-degree vertices (degree 2). If we remove an edge from a corner to a non-corner edge vertex, that would reduce the corner's degree by 1, making it odd. Which we don't want.\n\nTherefore, to reduce the degree of a non-corner edge vertex without affecting a corner's degree, we must remove the edge connecting it to the center.\n\nTherefore, each non-corner edge vertex's degree can only be reduced by removing its center-edge, because the other edges connect to corners which we cannot touch without creating new odd-degree vertices.\n\nTherefore, to reduce all four non-corner edge vertices' degrees by 1, we must remove all four edges connecting them to the center. Hence, four edge removals are necessary.\n\nThus, in this case, the minimal number of edges to remove is four, which is equal to the number of odd-degree vertices, not t/2.\n\nTherefore, in this case, it's t, but in the path graph example, it was also t, but in the path graph with four vertices, t=2, and we had to remove three edges, which was more than t.\n\nHmm, so perhaps in some graphs it's t, in others it's more?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps in bipartite graphs with high symmetry like the grid, the minimal number of edges to remove is equal to the number of odd-degree vertices?\n\nWait, in the 3x3 grid, we had t=4 odd-degree vertices and had to remove four edges. In the path graph with four vertices, t=2 but had to remove three edges, which is more than t. In the 11x11 grid, t=36, so perhaps we need to remove 36 edges? That seems too many, since total edges are only 220.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's a different way.\n\nWait, in the 3x3 grid example, the key was that the non-corner edge vertices were only connected to the center and two corners. Therefore, to reduce their degrees without affecting the corners, you had to remove all their connections to the center. Since each non-corner edge vertex has one connection to the center, you had to remove four edges.\n\nIn the 11x11 grid, perhaps a similar situation occurs where the odd-degree vertices (non-corner edge vertices) are only connected to the center region and corners, so to reduce their degrees without affecting the corners, you have to remove their connections to the interior, thereby requiring many edge removals.\n\nAlternatively, maybe in the 11x11 grid, each non-corner edge vertex has one connection to the interior (the next cell inward) and two connections to corners or other edge vertices.\n\nWait, let's consider a non-corner edge vertex on the top edge, say (1,2). Its neighbors are:\n\n- North: none (it's on the top edge)\n\n- South: (2,2)\n\n- East: (1,3)\n\n- West: (1,1)\n\nTherefore, similar to the 3x3 grid, this vertex is connected to two corners (if it's not near the end) and the cell below it (south). Wait, actually, (1,2)'s west neighbor is (1,1), which is a corner, and its east neighbor is (1,3), which is another non-corner edge vertex if we're not at the end.\n\nWait, in the 11x11 grid, a non-corner edge vertex on the top edge (row 1, column 2 to 10):\n\n- For column 2: neighbors are (1,1) [corner], (1,3) [non-corner edge], and (2,2) [internal].\n\n- For column 3: neighbors are (1,2) [non-corner edge], (1,4) [non-corner edge], and (2,3) [internal].\n\nContinuing this way, the non-corner edge vertices along the top edge are connected to two other non-corner edge vertices (except at the ends) and one internal vertex.\n\nWait, let's take a non-corner edge vertex not at the end of the edge. For example, on the top edge, column 5: (1,5). Its neighbors are:\n\n- West: (1,4) [non-corner edge]\n\n- East: (1,6) [non-corner edge]\n\n- South: (2,5) [internal]\n\nTherefore, this vertex has three neighbors: two non-corner edge vertices and one internal vertex.\n\nAh, so unlike the 3x3 grid, in the 11x11 grid, the non-corner edge vertices along the middle of the edge are connected to two other non-corner edge vertices and one internal vertex.\n\nTherefore, in this case, we can potentially remove edges between non-corner edge vertices to reduce their degrees without affecting the corners.\n\nFor example, take the non-corner edge vertex (1,5). It has edges to (1,4), (1,6), and (2,5). If we remove the edge between (1,5) and (1,4), then (1,5)'s degree becomes 2 (even), and (1,4)'s degree becomes 2 (since it previously had degree 3: connected to (1,3), (1,5), and (2,4)). Wait, (1,4) would then have degree 2, which is even. However, this affects (1,4)'s degree as well.\n\nTherefore, by removing an edge between two non-corner edge vertices, we can reduce both of their degrees by 1, turning them from odd to even, provided that they were both odd.\n\nSince all non-corner edge vertices are odd, this could be a way to fix two odd-degree vertices with one edge removal.\n\nAh! This is key. In the 11x11 grid, non-corner edge vertices along the same edge are connected to each other, so we can pair them up and remove the edge between them, thereby reducing both of their degrees by 1, turning them into even degrees, while not affecting the corners or internal vertices (except the one they're connected to internally, but wait, actually they are also connected to an internal vertex).\n\nWait, let's take an example:\n\nConsider two adjacent non-corner edge vertices on the top edge: (1,2) and (1,3). They are connected by an edge. If we remove that edge, then:\n\n- (1,2) loses one edge: degree goes from 3 to 2 (even).\n\n- (1,3) loses one edge: degree goes from 3 to 2 (even).\n\nAdditionally, both were connected to an internal vertex below them: (2,2) for (1,2), and (2,3) for (1,3). Removing the edge between (1,2)-(1,3) doesn't affect those internal vertices' degrees.\n\nTherefore, by removing this single edge, we fix two odd-degree vertices without affecting other vertices' parities.\n\nSimilarly, we can do this along the entire top edge: for the top edge's non-corner edge vertices, which are arranged in a line from (1,2) to (1,10), we can pair them up as (1,2)-(1,3), (1,4)-(1,5), ..., up to (1,8)-(1,9), leaving (1,10) unpaired? Wait, since there are 9 non-corner edge vertices on the top edge (columns 2 through 10), which is an odd number. So pairing them as adjacent pairs would leave one unpaired.\n\nSpecifically, 9 vertices: pairs would be (1,2)-(1,3), (1,4)-(1,5), (1,6)-(1,7), (1,8)-(1,9), and then (1,10) is left alone.\n\nTherefore, on the top edge, we can fix 8 non-corner edge vertices by removing 4 edges, leaving one vertex (1,10) still with degree 3 (odd).\n\nSimilarly, on the bottom edge (row 11, columns 2 through 10), same thing: 9 vertices, so after pairing, one left.\n\nOn the left edge (column 1, rows 2 through 10): 9 vertices, so after pairing, one left.\n\nOn the right edge (column 11, rows 2 through 10): 9 vertices, so after pairing, one left.\n\nTotal unpaired vertices: 4 edges (one per side), so 4 vertices left unpaired.\n\nAdditionally, the internal vertices along the paths we've removed edges might have their degrees affected, but wait, in the above example, when we removed the edge between (1,2) and (1,3), the internal vertices (2,2) and (2,3) are not affected because the removed edge is on the top row.\n\nWait, actually, the internal vertices adjacent to the top row's non-corner edge vertices are in the second row. The edge between (1,2) and (1,3) is on the top row; removing it doesn't affect any internal vertices' degrees because those internal vertices are one row down.\n\nTherefore, removing edges along the top row between adjacent non-corner edge vertices only affects the degrees of the non-corner edge vertices themselves, turning them from odd to even, without affecting any internal vertices' degrees.\n\nTherefore, this seems like a good strategy:\n\n1. On each edge of the grid (top, bottom, left, right), consider the non-corner edge vertices arranged in a line.\n\n2. Pair adjacent non-corner edge vertices and remove the edge between them. Each such removal fixes two odd-degree vertices.\n\n3. For each edge, since there are 9 non-corner edge vertices (an odd number), we can form 4 pairs and have 1 vertex left unpaired.\n\n4. Therefore, on each of the four edges, we have 1 unpaired non-corner edge vertex remaining with odd degree.\n\n5. Additionally, these unpaired vertices are still odd-degree vertices, so we need to fix them.\n\n6. For the unpaired vertices, since they are at the end of their respective edges, their only connections are to one corner and one internal vertex. For example, take the top-right non-corner edge vertex (1,10). Its neighbors are:\n\n   - West: (1,9) [which we might have paired or not? Wait, in the top edge, we paired up to (1,8)-(1,9), so (1,10) is the last one, unpaired.\n\n   - South: (2,10) [internal vertex]\n\n   - East: none (it's on the edge)\n\n   - North: none\n\n   So (1,10) is connected to (1,9) [which was paired and thus its edge to (1,9) was removed?] Wait, no: in our pairing on the top edge, we paired (1,2)-(1,3), (1,4)-(1,5), (1,6)-(1,7), (1,8)-(1,9), leaving (1,10) unpaired. Therefore, the edge between (1,9) and (1,10) remains intact. Therefore, (1,10) is still connected to (1,9), which is now even-degree (since (1,9) was paired and had its degree reduced by 1). Wait, no: (1,9) was part of the pair (1,8)-(1,9). When we removed the edge between (1,8) and (1,9), then (1,9)'s degree was reduced by 1, from 3 to 2 (even). So (1,9) is now even, but (1,10) is still connected to (1,9) via an edge. Therefore, (1,10) still has degree 3: connected to (1,9) [even], (2,10) [internal], and (1,11) [corner].\n\nWait, (1,10)'s neighbors are:\n\n- West: (1,9)\n\n- East: (1,11) [corner]\n\n- South: (2,10)\n\nSo three edges, so degree 3 (odd). To fix (1,10), we need to reduce its degree by 1. We can do this by removing one of its edges. However, removing the edge to the corner (1,11) would make the corner's degree 1 (odd), which we don't want. Removing the edge to (1,9) would make (1,9)'s degree 1 (since (1,9) was already even at 2, now becomes 1). Alternatively, removing the edge to (2,10), which is an internal vertex. That would reduce (1,10)'s degree to 2 (even), and (2,10)'s degree by 1 (from 4 to 3, which is odd). Oh no, that creates a new odd-degree vertex at (2,10).\n\nHmm, so removing the edge between (1,10) and (2,10) would fix (1,10) but introduce a new odd-degree vertex at (2,10). Not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, remove the edge between (1,10) and (1,9). Then:\n\n- (1,10)'s degree becomes 2 (even).\n\n- (1,9)'s degree becomes 1 (odd).\n\nBut now (1,9) is odd again. So we've just moved the problem to (1,9). Not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, remove the edge between (1,10) and (1,11) [the corner]. Then:\n\n- (1,10)'s degree becomes 2 (even).\n\n- (1,11)'s degree becomes 1 (odd).\n\nNow the corner is odd, which we don't want.\n\nSo it seems that the unpaired vertex on each edge cannot be fixed without creating another odd-degree vertex somewhere. Therefore, this approach leaves us with four unpaired vertices, each of which cannot be fixed without introducing a new odd-degree vertex. Therefore, we need to handle these four vertices differently.\n\nPerhaps pair them with vertices from other edges?\n\nFor instance, take the four unpaired vertices: let's name them:\n\n- Top-right: (1,10)\n\n- Bottom-left: (11,1) is a corner, so the unpaired on the bottom edge would be (11,2) maybe? Wait, let's clarify:\n\nWait, on the bottom edge (row 11), the non-corner edge vertices are columns 2 through 10. Following the same pairing as the top edge, the last unpaired vertex would be (11,10).\n\nSimilarly, on the left edge (column 1, rows 2 through 10), the unpaired vertex would be (10,1).\n\nOn the right edge (column 11, rows 2 through 10), the unpaired vertex would be (10,11).\n\nTherefore, the four unpaired vertices are:\n\n1. Top-right: (1,10)\n\n2. Bottom-right: (11,10)\n\n3. Bottom-left: (10,1)\n\n4. Top-left: (10,11)\n\nWait, actually, let me re-express:\n\nFor the left edge (column 1, rows 2-10):\n\nThe non-corner edge vertices are rows 2 through 10. Pairing adjacent rows: (2,1)-(3,1), (4,1)-(5,1), etc., leaving the last one as (10,1).\n\nSimilarly, on the right edge (column 11, rows 2-10):\n\nPairing rows 2-3, 4-5, etc., leaving (10,11).\n\nOn the bottom edge (row 11, columns 2-10):\n\nPairing columns 2-3, 4-5, etc., leaving (11,10).\n\nOn the top edge (row 1, columns 2-10):\n\nPairing columns 2-3, 4-5, etc., leaving (1,10).\n\nThus, the four unpaired vertices are:\n\n- Top: (1,10)\n\n- Bottom: (11,10)\n\n- Left: (10,1)\n\n- Right: (10,11)\n\nThese four vertices each have degree 3 (odd). To fix all four, we need to reduce each by one degree. However, each is connected to a corner and an internal vertex:\n\n- (1,10): connected to corner (1,11), non-corner edge vertex (1,9) [which is now even], and internal (2,10).\n\nWait, actually, after pairing on the top edge, (1,10) is connected to (1,9) [even] and (2,10) [internal], and the corner (1,11). So three edges, degree 3.\n\nTo reduce its degree by 1 without affecting the corner, we have to remove one of its other edges: either to (1,9) or to (2,10). But removing to (1,9) would make (1,9) odd again, and removing to (2,10) would make (2,10) odd.\n\nSimilarly for the other three unpaired vertices.\n\nTherefore, we have a problem with these four vertices. To fix all four, we might need to remove edges that affect two of them at once, or accept that we have to create additional odd-degree vertices and then fix those, but this could lead to an infinite regression.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can pair these four unpaired vertices among themselves through internal paths, and remove edges along those paths to adjust their degrees.\n\nFor example, take (1,10) and (11,10). These are on opposite sides of the grid, both on the right side (column 10). To connect them via an internal path that doesn't go through other edge vertices, we can go down from (1,10) to (2,10), then across the grid to (11,10). However, this path would pass through many internal vertices.\n\nAlternatively, since they are on the same column (column 10), but on opposite ends, the shortest path between them is through the internal grid. Let's see:\n\nFrom (1,10) to (11,10): moving straight down the column 10. However, column 10's cells from row 2 to row 10 are internal vertices (not on the top or bottom edge). So the path from (1,10) down to (11,10) would go through (2,10), (3,10), ..., (10,10), (11,10). But (2,10) to (11,10) is along column 10.\n\nHowever, each of these internal vertices (rows 2-10 in column 10) have degree 4 originally. If we remove the edges along this path from (1,10) to (11,10), that would be the edges:\n\n- (1,10)-(2,10)\n\n- (2,10)-(3,10)\n\n- ...\n\n- (10,10)-(11,10)\n\nThat's 10 edges (from row 1 to row 11 in column 10). Removing all these edges would reduce the degree of (1,10) by 1 (to 2), and (11,10) by 1 (to 2). All the internal vertices along the path (rows 2-10 in column 10) would have their degree reduced by 2 (since each is between two edges along the path), so their degrees go from 4 to 2, which is even. Therefore, this would fix both (1,10) and (11,10) without introducing any new odd-degree vertices.\n\nSimilarly, we can do the same for the other two unpaired vertices: (10,1) and (10,11).\n\nFor (10,1) and (10,11):\n\nThey are on the same row (row 10), columns 1 and 11. The path between them is along row 10 from column 1 to column 11.\n\nHowever, row 10 is an internal row (not the top or bottom edge). Therefore, the path from (10,1) to (10,11) would go through internal vertices in row 10.\n\nThe edges to remove would be:\n\n- (10,1)-(10,2)\n\n- (10,2)-(10,3)\n\n- ...\n\n- (10,9)-(10,10)\n\n- (10,10)-(10,11)\n\nThat's 10 edges (from column 1 to 11 in row 10). Removing these edges would reduce the degree of (10,1) by 1 (to 2), (10,11) by 1 (to 2), and each internal vertex in row 10 (columns 2-10) would have their degrees reduced by 2 (since each is between two edges along the path), so their degrees go from 4 to 2, which is even.\n\nTherefore, by doing this, we can fix the four unpaired vertices with two separate paths, each requiring 10 edge removals, totaling 20 edges.\n\nAdditionally, the edges we removed along the top/bottom/left/right edges to fix the paired vertices: on each of the four edges, we had to remove 4 edges per edge (since 9 vertices require 4 pairs, each pair removes 1 edge, so 4 edges per edge). Wait, no:\n\nWait, on each edge, there are 9 non-corner edge vertices. To pair them into 4 pairs, we need to remove 4 edges (one per pair). Therefore, per edge: 4 edges removed.\n\nThere are four edges, so 4 edges/edge * 4 edges = 16 edges removed.\n\nThen, the two paths for the unpaired vertices: 10 edges each, so 20 edges.\n\nTotal edges removed: 16 + 20 = 36 edges.\n\nTherefore, total edges in the grid: 220.\n\nThus, the maximum number of doors Karthik can traverse is 220 - 36 = 184.\n\nWait, but let me verify this step-by-step:\n\nFirst, on each of the four edges (top, bottom, left, right), we have 9 non-corner edge vertices. To pair them into 4 pairs and leave one unpaired:\n\n- Each pair requires removing one edge between them.\n\n- Therefore, per edge: 4 edges removed.\n\n- Four edges * 4 edges/edge = 16 edges removed.\n\nThen, for the four unpaired vertices:\n\n- Two pairs: (1,10) with (11,10), and (10,1) with (10,11). Each pair requires a path between them, removing edges along those paths.\n\n- The path between (1,10) and (11,10) is along column 10, from row 1 to row 11. That's 10 edges (between rows 1-2, 2-3, ..., 10-11).\n\n- Similarly, the path between (10,1) and (10,11) is along row 10, from column 1 to column 11. That's 10 edges (between columns 1-2, 2-3, ..., 10-11).\n\n- Total edges removed here: 10 + 10 = 20.\n\nTherefore, total edges removed: 16 + 20 = 36.\n\nTherefore, the remaining graph has all degrees even, is connected (since we only removed internal edges, not disconnecting the graph), so it's Eulerian. Therefore, Karthik can traverse all 220 - 36 = 184 edges in a single closed walk.\n\nBut wait, is the remaining graph connected? Let me check.\n\nAfter removing the edges along the four edges' paired vertices and the two long paths:\n\n- The top edge's non-corner vertices are now all even degrees, but the connections between them are partially removed. For example, on the top edge, after removing 4 edges to pair the vertices, the remaining edges are the unpaired vertices still connected to their neighbors except for the removed edges. Wait, actually, after pairing adjacent vertices and removing the connecting edge, the top edge's non-corner vertices form a path graph with the unpaired vertex at one end.\n\nWait, let's think of the top edge after processing:\n\nOriginal non-corner vertices on top edge: [ (1,2), (1,3), ..., (1,10) ]\n\nWe paired them as (1,2)-(1,3), (1,4)-(1,5), (1,6)-(1,7), (1,8)-(1,9), leaving (1,10) unpaired.\n\nBy removing the edges between each pair, the top edge's non-corner vertices become:\n\n- (1,2) is only connected to (1,1) [corner] and (2,2) [internal].\n\n- (1,3) is only connected to (2,3) [internal] and (1,4) [which is now disconnected? Wait, no:\n\nWait, when we remove the edge between (1,2)-(1,3), then (1,3) is only connected to (1,4) [if not removed], (2,3), and (1,2) [which is now disconnected from (1,3)].\n\nWait, actually, after removing the edges between the paired vertices, the top edge's non-corner vertices are no longer connected to each other along the top edge. So the top edge's non-corner vertices form four separate edges:\n\n- (1,2) connected to (1,1) and (2,2)\n\n- (1,3) connected to (2,3) and (1,4) [but (1,3)-(1,4) wasn't removed? Wait, no, (1,3) was paired with (1,2), so the edge between (1,3) and (1,4) remains?\n\nWait a second, I think I made a mistake here.\n\nWhen we pair (1,2) and (1,3), we remove the edge between them. The next pair is (1,4) and (1,5), so we remove the edge between (1,4)-(1,5). So the edges remaining on the top edge between the non-corner vertices are:\n\n- Between (1,2) and (1,1): still there.\n\n- Between (1,3) and (1,4): still there? No, (1,3) and (1,4) are not paired. The pairing was (1,2)-(1,3), so the edge between (1,3) and (1,4) is untouched. Wait, actually, the non-corner edge vertices are in a line: (1,2), (1,3), (1,4), ..., (1,10). When we pair (1,2)-(1,3), we remove that edge. Then pair (1,4)-(1,5), remove that edge, etc.\n\nTherefore, after these removals, the remaining edges on the top edge between non-corner vertices are:\n\n- Between (1,3) and (1,4): still present (since they weren't paired)\n\n- Between (1,5) and (1,6): still present (since they weren't paired)\n\n- Between (1,7) and (1,8): still present\n\n- Between (1,9) and (1,10): still present\n\nWait, no. Wait, the pairs were:\n\nFirst pair: (1,2)-(1,3): remove edge between them.\n\nSecond pair: (1,4)-(1,5): remove edge between them.\n\nThird pair: (1,6)-(1,7): remove edge between them.\n\nFourth pair: (1,8)-(1,9): remove edge between them.\n\nTherefore, the remaining edges between non-corner vertices on the top edge are:\n\n- Between (1,3) and (1,4): still there (not removed)\n\n- Between (1,5) and (1,6): still there\n\n- Between (1,7) and (1,8): still there\n\n- Between (1,9) and (1,10): still there\n\nWait, but actually, between each pair that was removed, there's a gap. For example, after removing (1,2)-(1,3), the next edge is (1,3)-(1,4), which remains. So the non-corner vertices on the top edge are now in segments:\n\n- (1,2) isolated from the rest except connected to (1,1) and (2,2)\n\n- (1,3) connected to (1,4)\n\n- (1,4) connected to (1,5) [but (1,4)-(1,5) was removed? Wait no, the second pair was (1,4)-(1,5), so that edge is removed.\n\nWait, no: the second pair was (1,4) and (1,5), so we remove the edge between them. Therefore, after the first removal, between (1,3) and (1,4) is still there, but between (1,4) and (1,5) is removed.\n\nTherefore, the top edge's non-corner vertices are fragmented into:\n\n- (1,2) connected to (1,1) and (2,2)\n\n- (1,3) connected to (1,4) [since edge (1,3)-(1,4) wasn't removed]\n\nWait, but (1,3) and (1,4) are adjacent and their edge wasn't removed, so they remain connected.\n\nWait, this is getting confusing. Maybe it's better to think that after removing the paired edges, the non-corner vertices on each edge are broken into separate components except for the unpaired one.\n\nBut in any case, the key point is that after removing the edges between the paired vertices on each edge, the non-corner vertices on that edge are now only connected to the corners and internal vertices, but their degrees have been reduced to even.\n\nHowever, the connectivity of the entire grid graph might be affected. For instance, on the top edge, after removing those edges, the non-corner vertices are no longer connected to each other along the top edge, but they are still connected via internal paths.\n\nFor example, (1,2) can go down to (2,2), then move right to (2,3), etc., to reach other non-corner vertices on the top edge. Therefore, the top edge's non-corner vertices are still connected to the rest of the grid through internal vertices, so the overall graph remains connected.\n\nSimilarly, after removing the edges along the long paths for the unpaired vertices, those paths are just removed, but the rest of the graph remains connected.\n\nTherefore, the resulting graph is connected and all degrees are even, so it has an Eulerian circuit. Therefore, Karthik can traverse all 220 - 36 = 184 edges in such a circuit.\n\nBut wait, let me verify the total edges removed:\n\n- On each of the four edges (top, bottom, left, right): 4 edge