ust leave some edges unused. The question is, how many edges can we include in such a circuit?\n\nI recall that in any graph, the maximum length of a closed trail (i.e., the longest cycle that is also a trail) is equal to the total number of edges minus the minimum number of edges needed to make the graph Eulerian. That is, if we can add the minimum number of edges to make the graph Eulerian, then the original graph has an Eulerian trail only if it's connected and has exactly zero or two vertices of odd degree. Since we have multiple vertices with odd degree, we need to pair them up and add a path between each pair to make them even. Each added edge reduces the number of odd-degree vertices by two. So, the minimum number of edges needed to make the graph Eulerian would be equal to half the number of odd-degree vertices.\n\nWait, let me recall the concept of Eulerian trails and the theorem by Hierholzer.\n\nFirst, in any graph, an Eulerian trail (a trail that visits every edge exactly once) exists if and only if the graph is connected and has exactly 0 or 2 vertices of odd degree. If there are 2 vertices of odd degree, then the Eulerian trail must start and end at those two vertices. If there are 0 vertices of odd degree, then the graph has an Eulerian circuit, which starts and ends at the same vertex.\n\nIn our case, the grid graph has 4 vertices of degree 3 (the four corners), and the rest? Let me count the number of vertices with odd degree.\n\nIn an m x n grid graph (vertices arranged in m rows and n columns), the corner vertices each have degree 2 (even). The vertices along the edges but not at the corners have degree 3 (odd). The interior vertices have degree 4 (even). So, how many vertices have odd degree?\n\nLet's consider the 11x11 grid. Each side of the grid has 11 vertices. The four corners each have degree 2 (even). Then, along each edge (there are four edges: top, bottom, left, right), the vertices on the edges excluding the corners: each edge has 11 - 2