ges, we can instead remove edges in a way that adjusts the degrees to even. Wait, actually, removing an edge between two vertices will decrease the degree of each of those two vertices by 1. So, if we remove an edge between two odd-degree vertices, their degrees become even, but if we remove an edge between an odd and an even vertex, the odd becomes even and the even becomes odd. Hmm, tricky.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's better to think of the problem as starting with the original graph and then considering that in order to have an Eulerian subgraph, we need to remove edges such that all degrees become even. The minimal number of edges to remove would be half the number of odd-degree vertices? Wait, no, because each edge removal affects two vertices.\n\nLet me think in terms of linear algebra over the field GF(2). The problem of making all degrees even is equivalent to solving a system of linear equations where for each vertex, the sum of the edges incident to it (mod 2) is 0. The variables are the edges, which can be 0 or 1 (present or removed). We want to minimize the number of edges removed (i.e., minimize the sum of the variables) while satisfying the equations. This is the minimum edge cut or something similar?\n\nWait, actually, this is exactly the problem of finding the minimum number of edges to remove to make all degrees even. The minimal number of edges to remove is equal to the number of odd-degree vertices divided by 2, because each edge removed can fix two vertices. Wait, but actually, each edge removal can affect two vertices, so each removal can fix at most two vertices (if they were both odd). So, if you have O vertices of odd degree, you need at least O/2 edge removals to make all degrees even. Since O is even, this is possible.\n\nTherefore, in our case, O = 36, so we need at least 18 edge removals to make all degrees even. However, in addition, the subgraph must remain connected. So, just removing 18 edges might disconnect the graph, so we have to make sure that after removing those edges, the graph remains connected.\n\nTherefore, the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even and keep the graph connected is... Hmm, perhaps 18 is the minimal number needed to make all degrees even, but we have to ensure connectivity. Since the original graph is connected, perhaps we can remove edges in a way that doesn't disconnect it. For example, if we can pair up the odd-degree vertices and remove a path between each pair, but not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the maximum Eulerian subgraph (in terms of number of edges) is equal to the total number of edges minus the minimal number of edges required to be removed to make all degrees even, while keeping the graph connected.\n\nBut this is getting complicated. Maybe there's a better way. Since the grid is bipartite, as I noted before, with the two color classes, say black and white in checkerboard pattern.\n\nIn a bipartite graph, any cycle must have even length, which we already knew. Also, in bipartite graphs, the maximum trail that is closed (i.e., a cycle) must alternate between the two partitions. Since it's closed, it must start and end on the same vertex, which must be in one partition, and since it alternates, the length must be even, as we said.\n\nBut how does that help us?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the maximum trail is equal to the total number of edges minus the number of edges in a minimal set of edges whose removal makes the graph Eulerian and connected. Since we need to remove edges to fix the degrees, but we have to keep it connected.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the maximum trail is 220 minus the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even and keep the graph connected. Since the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even is 18, but we have to ensure connectivity. If we can remove 18 edges without disconnecting the graph, then the maximum trail would be 220 - 18 = 202. But I don't know if it's possible to remove 18 edges and keep the graph connected. Alternatively, maybe more edges have to be removed.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the maximum trail can be found by considering that in any graph, the maximum length of a closed trail is equal to the total number of edges minus the number of odd-degree vertices over 2. Wait, in our case, that would be 220 - (36/2) = 220 - 18 = 202. Hmm, that seems plausible. Let me see if that formula is correct.\n\nWait, actually, in some references, I recall that in a graph, the maximum number of edges in an Eulerian subgraph (i.e., a subgraph with all even degrees and connected) is equal to the total number of edges minus the minimal number of edges that need to be removed to make all degrees even. Since the minimal number of edges to remove to make all degrees even is indeed the number of odd-degree vertices divided by 2, as each edge removal can fix two vertices. Therefore, if we can remove that many edges while keeping the graph connected, then the maximum Eulerian subgraph would have E - (O/2) edges, where O is the number of odd-degree vertices.\n\nBut is that formula valid? Let me see an example. Take a simple graph, say a single edge between two vertices. Then, each vertex has degree 1 (odd). So O=2. Then, according to the formula, maximum Eulerian subgraph would be 1 - (2/2) = 0. But actually, if you remove that single edge, you get two isolated vertices, each with degree 0 (even), but the subgraph is disconnected. So, the maximum connected Eulerian subgraph would actually be zero edges, since you can't have a connected graph with two vertices and no edges. Hmm, so in that case, the formula gives 0, which is correct, but only because the connectedness is required. Wait, but if we don't require connectedness, then the maximum Eulerian subgraph would be the empty graph, which trivially has all even degrees (zero), but that's not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the formula is for connected Eulerian subgraphs. So, in the case of the single edge, since you can't have a connected Eulerian subgraph with positive edges, the maximum is zero. So the formula works in that case. Another example: a triangle. A triangle has three vertices each of degree 2 (even). So O=0, so the formula would give 3 - 0 = 3, which is correct, since the entire triangle is Eulerian and connected.\n\nAnother example: a square with a diagonal, making a complete graph K4? Wait, no, a square with a diagonal is K4? No, a square with a diagonal is a cycle of length 4 with a chord, so each vertex has degree 3 except the two endpoints of the diagonal, which have degree 3 as well? Wait, no: in a square (4-cycle), each vertex has degree 2. If you add a diagonal, then two of the vertices now have degree 3, and the other two have degree 2. So O=2. Then, according to the formula, maximum Eulerian subgraph would be 5 (original edges) minus 1 (since O/2=1) = 4. Is that correct?\n\nLet me see: the original graph has 5 edges. The two vertices with degree 3 need to have their degrees reduced to even. If we remove one edge, say, the diagonal, then the two vertices that were connected by the diagonal go back to degree 2, and the other two vertices remain at degree 2. So the remaining graph is the 4-cycle, which is Eulerian (all degrees 2) and has 4 edges. So that works. Alternatively, if you remove another edge, you might not get an Eulerian subgraph. So yes, removing one edge (the minimal number) gives an Eulerian subgraph with 4 edges. So the formula works here.\n\nTherefore, perhaps in general, for any connected graph, the maximum number of edges in a connected Eulerian subgraph is equal to E - O/2, where O is the number of odd-degree vertices. But we have to make sure that such a subgraph exists, i.e., that you can remove O/2 edges to make all degrees even and keep the graph connected.\n\nIn our case, the grid graph is connected, and O = 36, so the formula suggests that the maximum number of edges in a connected Eulerian subgraph is 220 - 18 = 202. Therefore, the maximum closed trail would be 202 edges. But is this achievable? That is, can we actually remove 18 edges from the grid graph in such a way that all remaining vertices have even degree and the graph remains connected?\n\nIf so, then 202 would be the answer. But how can we be sure that such a removal is possible?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the grid graph is 2-connected or has high connectivity, so that we can remove edges without disconnecting it. Since grid graphs are highly connected, probably we can do this.\n\nAlternatively, here's a way to think about it: in a bipartite graph, the two partitions are, say, black and white squares. Each edge goes from black to white. Since it's bipartite, all cycles are even-length. To make all degrees even, we need to remove edges such that every vertex has even degree. Since the original degrees are mostly even except for the 36 odd-degree vertices (the edge non-corner vertices). Each such vertex is on the border of the grid and has degree 3 (so one more edge than needed for evenness). Therefore, to fix each such vertex, we need to remove one edge incident to it, but in a way that pairs up the removals so that each removal fixes two vertices.\n\nWait, actually, each vertex with odd degree needs to have an odd number of edges removed incident to it, right? Because if a vertex has degree d, which is odd, and we remove k edges incident to it, then its new degree is d - k. To make it even, we need d - k even, so k must have the same parity as d. Since d is odd, k must be odd. So each odd-degree vertex must have an odd number of its edges removed.\n\nTherefore, each edge removal affects two vertices, so each edge removal can contribute to fixing two vertices (by reducing their degrees by 1, which flips their parity). Therefore, to fix all 36 odd-degree vertices, which are each needing an odd number of edge removals incident to them, we need to have the total number of \"odd\" contributions be 36, and each edge removal contributes two to the total (since it affects two vertices). Since each edge removal changes the parity of two vertices, so if we remove an edge between two odd-degree vertices, then both of those vertices get their needed \"odd\" count satisfied by one removal each, so that's good. If we remove an edge between an odd and an even vertex, then the odd vertex gets its needed odd count (since it now has one less edge, so its parity flips from odd to even, which is bad because it was supposed to go from odd to even, but actually we need to go from odd to even by removing an odd number of edges. Wait, actually, if a vertex has an odd degree, and we remove one edge from it, then its new degree is even, which is what we want. So actually, each odd-degree vertex needs to have an odd number of edges removed from it. Therefore, each such vertex must have at least one edge removed. But since edges are shared between two vertices, we have to pair up the removals.\n\nTherefore, to fix all 36 odd-degree vertices, each requiring an odd number of edge removals, and each edge removal can fix two vertices (by removing one edge from each), we can pair up the odd-degree vertices and remove a single edge between each pair. However, we have 36 vertices to fix, so 18 pairs, so we need to remove 18 edges, each connecting a pair of odd-degree vertices. However, not all pairs of odd-degree vertices are connected by an edge. In the grid, the odd-degree vertices are all the non-corner edge vertices, each of which is on the border of the grid. So, for example, on the top edge, the 9 non-corner vertices each have three edges: up, left, right (except the top-left and top-right corners, which are corners and have degree 2). Wait, actually, on the top edge, each non-corner vertex on the top edge has edges: left, right, and down. Similarly, the bottom edge non-corner vertices have edges: left, right, and up. The left edge non-corner vertices have edges: up, down, and right. The right edge non-corner vertices have edges: up, down, and left. The other edges (the ones not on the border) are interior edges, connecting to other interior vertices, which have degree 4.\n\nTherefore, the odd-degree vertices (the 36 non-corner edge vertices) are all along the borders, each connected to two other border vertices and one interior vertex? Wait, let me think again.\n\nTake a vertex on the top edge, not a corner. It has neighbors: left (another top-edge vertex), right (another top-edge vertex), and down (an interior vertex). So that vertex has degree 3. Similarly, a vertex on the left edge (non-corner) has neighbors: up (another left-edge vertex), down (another left-edge vertex), and right (an interior vertex). So that's also degree 3. Similarly for the bottom and right edges.\n\nTherefore, each of these 36 odd-degree vertices is connected to two other odd-degree vertices (along the edge) and one interior vertex (which has degree 4, so even). Therefore, if we want to pair up the odd-degree vertices and remove an edge between them, we could do so along the edges. For example, on the top edge, between two adjacent non-corner vertices, there is an edge. If we remove that edge, then both of those vertices lose one degree, going from 3 to 2 (even), which is good. However, that edge is along the top edge between two non-corner vertices. Similarly, on each edge of the grid, we can remove edges between adjacent non-corner vertices.\n\nHowever, if we do that, we have to be careful not to disconnect the graph. For example, if we remove all the edges along one edge of the grid, say the top edge, between the non-corner vertices, then the top edge would become just the corners connected to the interior, but actually, if we remove the edges between the non-corner vertices on the top edge, then the top edge's non-corner vertices would only be connected to the corners and the interior? Wait, let's take an example.\n\nSuppose on the top edge, between vertex A (top-left corner) and vertex B (first non-corner on top edge), there is an edge. Then between B and C (next non-corner), etc., up to vertex Z (top-right corner). If we remove all the edges between the non-corner vertices on the top edge, then vertices B, C, ..., Y (the non-corner ones) would only be connected to the corners and the interior vertex below them. But each of these vertices still has degree 3: connected to the corner (A or Z), the interior vertex below, and the next vertex along the edge? Wait no, if we remove the edge between B and C, then vertex B is still connected to A, to the interior below, and to C? Wait no, if we remove the edge between B and C, then B is only connected to A, the interior below, and... wait, actually, on the top edge, each non-corner vertex is between two others. If we remove the edge between B and C, then B is connected to A and to the interior below, and also to... wait, no: along the top edge, each non-corner vertex is between two others except at the ends. Wait, actually, vertex B is between A (corner) and C (non-corner). So if we remove the edge between B and C, then B is only connected to A and the interior vertex below. So its degree becomes 2, which is even. Similarly, C is connected to B (but we removed that edge), so C is connected to D (next non-corner), Z (the top-right corner), and the interior below. Wait, no, C is between B and D? Wait, maybe my labeling is off.\n\nWait, let's label the top edge vertices as follows: starting from the top-left corner A, then the first non-corner is B, then C, D, ..., up to the top-right corner Z. Each non-corner vertex (B, C, D, ..., Y) is between two other vertices: for B, it's between A and C; for C, between B and D, etc. So if we remove the edge between B and C, then B is only connected to A and its interior neighbor, so degree 2. C is still connected to D and its interior neighbor, but also to B? No, we removed the edge between B and C, so C is connected to D, Z (if it's near the end?), no, actually C is not near Z unless it's the last non-corner. Wait, actually, the last non-corner before Z would be, say, Y, which is connected to Z. So C is connected to B (but we removed that edge), D, and its interior neighbor. Wait, no: each non-corner vertex on the top edge has neighbors: previous vertex on the top edge (if not the first), next vertex on the top edge (if not the last), and the interior vertex below. Therefore, if we remove the edge between B and C, then B's neighbors are A and the interior below; C's neighbors are D, the interior below, and Z? No, C is not adjacent to Z unless it's the last one. Wait, actually, each non-corner vertex on the top edge is only adjacent to its immediate neighbors along the top edge and the interior vertex below. Therefore, if we remove the edge between B and C, then B is only connected to A and the interior, and C is connected to D and the interior. So both B and C lose one degree, going from 3 to 2, which is even. So that works.\n\nBut if we do this for every pair of adjacent non-corner vertices along each edge, then on each edge of the grid, we can remove the edges between the non-corner vertices, thereby reducing each non-corner vertex's degree by 1 (from 3 to 2), thus making all their degrees even. However, if we do this for all four edges, then on each edge of the grid, we have a sequence of non-corner vertices connected by edges; by removing every other edge? Wait, no, if we remove all the edges between the non-corner vertices on each edge, then along each edge, the non-corner vertices would only be connected to the corners and their interior neighbors, but actually, if you remove all the edges between non-corner vertices on an edge, then the non-corner vertices on that edge would only be connected to the corners and their interior neighbors. Wait, but each non-corner vertex is between two other vertices on the edge. For example, on the top edge, the non-corner vertices are B, C, D, ..., Y. If we remove the edges BC, CD, DE, ..., YZ, then each non-corner vertex (B, C, D, ..., Y) would lose one edge (the one connecting to the next vertex on the edge). However, B is connected to A (the corner) and the interior below, so degree 2. C is connected to D (wait, no, we removed CD?), no: if we remove all edges between non-corner vertices, then between B and C, we remove BC; between C and D, remove CD; etc. So C is only connected to D? No, if we remove CD, then C is connected to B (but BC is removed), so actually C would only be connected to the interior below and to D? Wait no, if we remove CD, then C is connected to B? But BC was also removed. Wait a second, hold on. If we remove all the edges between the non-corner vertices along the top edge, that means we remove BC, CD, DE, ..., YZ. Therefore, each non-corner vertex (except the ones at the ends) would lose two edges: for example, vertex C is connected to B (removed), D (removed), and the interior below. Wait, no, vertex C is on the top edge, so its neighbors are B (left), D (right), and the interior vertex below. If we remove BC and CD, then C is only connected to the interior vertex below, so its degree becomes 1, which is odd! Oh no, that's a problem.\n\nAh, so my mistake earlier: if you remove all the edges between the non-corner vertices on a single edge of the grid, then the middle non-corner vertices lose two edges (the ones connecting to their left and right neighbors on the edge), thereby reducing their degree from 3 to 1, which is still odd. So that doesn't work. Therefore, we can't remove all the edges between the non-corner vertices on a single edge. Instead, we need to remove every other edge along the edge to pair up the vertices.\n\nFor example, on the top edge, which has 9 non-corner vertices (since total 11 vertices on the edge, minus 2 corners), so 9 vertices. To fix each of their degrees, we can remove every other edge along the top edge. Since there are 8 edges between the 9 non-corner vertices (and the two corners), actually, the number of edges on the top edge is 10: from A to B, B to C, ..., Y to Z. Of these, the edges between non-corner vertices are 8 (from B-C up to Y-Z). To fix the degrees, we need to remove edges such that each non-corner vertex loses exactly one edge. Since each non-corner vertex is part of two edges along the top edge (except the ones at the ends? Wait no: each non-corner vertex on the top edge is between two other vertices on the edge, so each is part of two edge connections along the top edge, except the ones at the very ends? Wait, no, actually, each non-corner vertex on the top edge is between two other vertices on the top edge. For example, vertex B is between A (corner) and C (non-corner). Vertex C is between B and D, etc., up to vertex Y, which is between X and Z (the top-right corner). Therefore, each non-corner vertex on the top edge is connected to two other vertices on the top edge (except the first one, B, which is connected to A and C, and the last one, Y, connected to X and Z). Wait, actually, all non-corner vertices on the top edge are between two other vertices on the top edge. Therefore, each non-corner vertex on the top edge has two neighbors along the top edge and one neighbor below (the interior vertex). Therefore, each has degree 3.\n\nTherefore, to reduce each non-corner vertex's degree by 1 (to make it 2), we need to remove one edge incident to each. However, each edge removal affects two vertices. Therefore, to remove one edge from each of the 9 non-corner vertices on the top edge, we need to remove 9/2 edges, but since 9 is odd, that's impossible. Therefore, we can only remove 4 edges, affecting 8 vertices, leaving one vertex with an odd degree. Hmm, that complicates things.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can do a pattern along the edge. For example, remove every other edge along the top edge. So starting from B-C, remove that edge, then skip C-D, remove D-E, etc. But since there are 8 edges between the 9 non-corner vertices, removing every other edge would result in 4 edges removed, affecting 8 vertices, leaving one vertex (the last one?) with an odd degree.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's better to pair up the non-corner vertices along the edge and remove the edge between each pair. Since there are 9 non-corner vertices on the top edge, we can pair them into 4 pairs and have one left over. The leftover would then need to be paired with someone else. Perhaps with a vertex from another edge?\n\nThis suggests that we can't fix all the odd-degree vertices on a single edge without leaving some unpaired. Therefore, we need to work across multiple edges.\n\nPerhaps the way to do this is to \"snake\" through the grid, pairing up the odd-degree vertices by removing edges between them in such a way that we don't disconnect the graph.\n\nAlternatively, here's another idea: since the grid is a planar graph, and planar graphs have certain properties, but I don't know if that helps here.\n\nAlternatively, let's consider that in order to make all degrees even, we need to remove a set of edges such that every odd-degree vertex loses an odd number of edges. Since each edge removal affects two vertices, we can model this as a graph where we need to select a set of edges to remove such that each odd-degree vertex is incident to an odd number of removed edges, and the total number of removed edges is minimized (which would be 18, as per O/2).\n\nThis is equivalent to finding a edge set S such that S is a subset of the edges with |S| = 18, and for each odd-degree vertex v, the degree of v in the original graph minus the degree of v in S is even. In other words, the characteristic vector of S must satisfy that for each odd-degree vertex v, the sum over edges incident to v in S is odd. This is a system of linear equations over GF(2). The question is whether such a set S exists that also keeps the graph connected.\n\nSince the original graph is connected and has an even number of odd-degree vertices, such a set S should exist by some theorem? Maybe.\n\nIn any case, assuming that such a set S of 18 edges exists that makes all degrees even and keeps the graph connected, then the maximum trail would be 220 - 18 = 202. Therefore, the answer would be 202.\n\nBut is this the correct approach? Let me see if I can think of a different way.\n\nAlternatively, in a bipartite graph, the maximum trail that is closed would be equal to the total number of edges minus the number of edges in a minimal edge cut that separates the two partitions? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can construct such a trail. Since the grid is a planar graph, perhaps we can construct a Hamiltonian circuit? But a Hamiltonian circuit in a grid graph? An 11x11 grid graph has 121 vertices. A Hamiltonian circuit would have 121 edges, but that's way less than 220. So that's not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can find a long cycle that winds through the grid, covering as many edges as possible without repeating any. Since we can't have an Eulerian circuit, but we can have a trail that covers almost all edges.\n\nAlternatively, note that in the original grid graph, the maximum trail without repeating edges is going to be just less than the total number of edges. Since we can't have an Eulerian circuit, but we can have a trail that's missing a set of edges that form a minimal set to make the degrees even. If that minimal set is 18 edges, then 220 - 18 = 202 is the maximum.\n\nAlternatively, let's think of it this way: if you have a graph with all even degrees, then it has an Eulerian circuit, so you can traverse all edges. But since our graph doesn't, we have to remove some edges to make it Eulerian, and the maximum number of edges we can keep is 220 - 18 = 202. So the answer is 202.\n\nAlternatively, maybe an example with a smaller grid can help verify.\n\nTake a 2x2 grid. That's a square with 4 vertices and 4 edges. Each vertex has degree 2 (even), so actually, it's already Eulerian. So the maximum trail is 4. That works: you can go around the square.\n\nTake a 3x3 grid. Let's see. A 3x3 grid of cells has 4x4=16 vertices? Wait no: wait, an n x n grid of cells has (n+1) x (n+1) vertices. So a 3x3 grid of cells has 4x4=16 vertices. The number of edges: horizontal edges: 4 rows, each with 3 edges, so 12 horizontal; vertical edges: 4 columns, each with 3 edges, so 12 vertical; total edges 24. The corner vertices have degree 2, the edge non-corner vertices have degree 3, and the interior vertices have degree 4. How many odd-degree vertices? The edge non-corner vertices: on each side of the square, there are 4 vertices per side (including corners), so non-corner edge vertices per side: 2, and 4 sides, so 8 vertices with degree 3. So O=8, so minimal edge removals needed: 4. So maximum Eulerian subgraph would be 24 - 4 = 20 edges.\n\nIs that achievable? Let's see. In a 3x3 grid, can we remove 4 edges such that all degrees become even and the graph remains connected?\n\nYes. For example, remove the four edges that form a \"square\" in the center. Wait, but let me think.\n\nAlternatively, pick one edge from each pair of edges that are on the same side. For example, on each side of the 3x3 grid (which has 4 vertices per side), remove one edge from each side between the non-corner vertices. For instance, on the top side, between the two non-corner vertices, remove that edge. Similarly on the bottom, left, and right sides. That would remove 4 edges, each on a different side, between the non-corner vertices. Then, each of those non-corner vertices loses one degree, going from 3 to 2 (even), and the corners remain at degree 2. The interior vertices: originally degree 4, but if we removed an edge on a side, does that affect the interior? Wait, no. The edges removed are on the perimeter, between the non-corner edge vertices. The interior edges remain intact. Therefore, the interior vertices still have degree 4. So all vertices now have even degrees, and the graph is still connected because we only removed perimeter edges, not disconnecting the interior. Therefore, the resulting graph has 24 - 4 = 20 edges and is Eulerian. Therefore, the maximum trail would be 20 edges.\n\nTherefore, in the 3x3 case, the formula works: O = 8, so 24 - 4 = 20. So that seems to check out.\n\nTherefore, returning to the 11x11 grid, with O = 36, so 220 - 18 = 202. So I think that is the answer.\n\nBut let me see if there is any reason to think it might be different.\n\nWait, in the 3x3 example, we could remove 4 edges (one from each side) and still have the graph connected. In the 11x11 grid, if we can similarly remove 18 edges, each from a different \"side\" or in some paired fashion without disconnecting the graph, then 202 is achievable.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the 18 edges can be removed in such a way: for each pair of odd-degree vertices that are adjacent along an edge on the border, remove the edge between them, thereby fixing both of their degrees. Since there are 36 odd-degree vertices, grouped into 18 pairs, each pair connected by an edge on the border.\n\nBut as we saw earlier, along each edge of the grid (there are four edges: top, bottom, left, right), there are 9 non-corner vertices, which is an odd number. Therefore, on each edge, you can pair up 8 of them into 4 pairs, leaving one unpaired. Therefore, on each edge, you can remove 4 edges, fixing 8 vertices, leaving one vertex with an odd degree. Since there are four edges, each contributing one leftover vertex, that gives 4 leftover vertices. Then, those four leftover vertices can be paired among the four edges, perhaps by connecting them through the interior? Wait, but they are on different edges.\n\nAlternatively, maybe instead of pairing along the same edge, we can pair vertices from different edges. For example, take a vertex from the top edge and a vertex from the right edge, and remove an edge between them if they are adjacent? But they might not be adjacent unless they are at a corner. The only vertices that are on two edges are the corners, which are already even-degree.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the leftover vertices can be paired with vertices from another edge by removing an interior edge? For instance, take a non-corner vertex on the top edge and a non-corner vertex on the right edge, and if they are connected by an interior edge? Wait, no, vertices on the top and right edges are only connected if they are at the corner, but the non-corner ones are not adjacent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we need to use some interior edges to pair up the leftover vertices.\n\nWait, this is getting too vague. Maybe another approach: since the grid is highly symmetric and 2-connected (in fact, much more), we can perform a systematic edge removal.\n\nLet me consider that on each of the four edges of the grid (top, bottom, left, right), there are 9 non-corner vertices. As we saw, on each edge, we can remove 4 edges between non-corner vertices, fixing 8 vertices, leaving one vertex with an odd degree. Therefore, after doing this on all four edges, we have 4 leftover vertices, each on a different edge, each with odd degree (1 instead of 3). Now, these four leftover vertices are each located near the corners but one step inward. For example, on the top edge, the last non-corner vertex before the top-right corner is one unit away from the corner. Similarly for the other edges.\n\nNow, these four leftover vertices are each adjacent to two interior vertices. For example, the top-left leftover vertex (on the top edge, one before the corner) is adjacent to the interior vertex below it and to the interior vertex to its right (if applicable). Wait, actually, let me define more precisely.\n\nTake the top edge's last non-corner vertex, let's call it T. T is adjacent to the top-right corner, the interior vertex below it (let's call it I1), and the previous vertex on the top edge (which we already fixed its degree by removing an edge). Wait, actually, after removing edges along the top edge between non-c