, that's the contrapositive. Let me formalize this:\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet A and B be two rectangles.\n\nDefine:\n\n- Separated along x-axis: x2 < x3 or x1 > x4 (i.e., no overlap in the x-projections)\n\n- Separated along y-axis: y2 < y3 or y1 > y4 (i.e., no overlap in the y-projections)\n\nThen, the rectangles A and B do NOT overlap in the plane if and only if they are separated along the x-axis OR separated along the y-axis.\n\nWait, but actually, if they are separated along the x-axis, then regardless of their y-projections, they don't overlap. Similarly, if they are separated along the y-axis, regardless of their x-projections, they don't overlap. Conversely, if they are not separated along the x-axis (i.e., their x-projections overlap) AND not separated along the y-axis (i.e., their y-projections overlap), then they do overlap in the plane. Therefore, the non-overlapping condition is equivalent to (separated along x OR separated along y). Therefore, the count of non-overlapping pairs is equal to the number of pairs separated along x plus the number of pairs separated along y minus the number of pairs separated along both x and y (because those would have been counted twice in the sum).\n\nTherefore, by inclusion-exclusion:\n\nNumber of non-overlapping pairs = (Number of pairs separated along x) + (Number of pairs separated along y) - (Number of pairs separated along both x and y)\n\nTherefore, if I can compute these three terms, I can get the desired count.\n\nHmm, this seems a bit more manageable. Let me try to compute each term.\n\nFirst, let me note that the total number of unordered pairs of rectangles is C(225, 2) as I thought earlier, but perhaps I can compute the terms needed via this inclusion-exclusion approach without referring to the total.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to compute each term separately.\n\nFirst, let me define:\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet R be the set of all rectangles in the 5x5 grid. |R| = 225.\n\nWe need to compute the numbe