ngles by their x-interval.\n\nSince there are 15 x-intervals, and for each x-interval X1 = [a,b], there are 15 rectangles with that x-interval (since each can have any of the 15 y-intervals). Therefore, the total over all R1 is:\n\nSum over all x-intervals X1 of [ (number of rectangles with x-interval X1) * (number of non-overlapping x-intervals for X1) * 15 ]\n\nWait, actually, let me clarify:\n\nFor each x-interval X1, which is [a,b], there are 15 rectangles with that x-interval (each paired with a different y-interval). For each such rectangle R1 with x-interval X1, the number of rectangles R2 with non-overlapping x-interval is [N(X1)] * 15, where N(X1) is the number of x-intervals not overlapping with X1, and then for each of those x-intervals, there are 15 y-intervals, so 15 per x-interval.\n\nWait, actually, no. Wait, if X2 is a non-overlapping x-interval with X1, then the number of rectangles with x-interval X2 is 15 (since each X2 can be combined with any y-interval). Therefore, for each R1 with x-interval X1, the number of R2 with non-overlapping x-interval is [N(X1) * 15].\n\nTherefore, for each x-interval X1, the contribution to the total ordered pairs is:\n\n(number of R1 with X1) * [N(X1) * 15] = 15 * [N(X1) * 15] = 15^2 * N(X1).\n\nTherefore, the total over all R1 is Sum_{X1 in X} [15^2 * N(X1)].\n\nSince there are 15 x-intervals, each contributing 15^2 * N(X1). Therefore, total ordered pairs with non-overlapping x-intervals is 15^2 * Sum_{X1 in X} N(X1).\n\nTherefore, first, we need to compute Sum_{X1 in X} N(X1), where N(X1) = C(a, 2) + C(6 - b, 2) for X1 = [a, b].\n\nTherefore, we need to compute, for each x-interval [a, b], the value C(a, 2) + C(6 - b, 2), then sum that over all 15 x-intervals.\n\nHmm, okay, so let me list all the x-intervals and compute N(X1) for each.\n\nFirst, note that the x-intervals are all pairs of integers (a, b) with 0 ≤ a < b ≤ 5.\n\nSo, let's list all possible x-intervals [a, b], compute a and b for each, then compute C(a, 2) + C(6 - b, 2), then sum them all.\n\nLet me make a table:\n\nFirst, list all x-intervals:\n\nThe x-coordinates are 0,1,2,3,4,5. So the possible a and b with a < b.\n\nLet me list them in order of increasing b:\n\nFor b = 1:\n\na can be 0: [0,1]\n\nFor b = 2:\n\na can be 0,1: [0,2], [1,2]\n\nFor b = 3:\n\na can be 0,1,2: [0,3], [1,3], [2,3]\n\nFor b = 4:\n\na can be 0,1,2,3: [0,4], [1,4], [2,4], [3,4]\n\nFor b = 5:\n\na can be 0,1,2,3,4: [0,5], [1,5], [2,5], [3,5], [4,5]\n\nSo total 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 = 15 intervals, as expected.\n\nNow, for each interval [a,b], compute C(a, 2) + C(6 - b, 2).\n\nFirst, note that C(n, 2) is n(n - 1)/2, which is 0 when n < 2.\n\nLet me compute for each interval:\n\n1. [0,1]:\n\na = 0, b = 1\n\nC(a, 2) = C(0, 2) = 0\n\nC(6 - b, 2) = C(6 - 1, 2) = C(5, 2) = 10\n\nSo N(X1) = 0 + 10 = 10\n\n2. [0,2]:\n\na = 0, b = 2\n\nC(0,2)=0\n\nC(6 - 2,2)=C(4,2)=6\n\nN(X1)=0 + 6 = 6\n\n3. [0,3]:\n\na=0, b=3\n\nC(0,2)=0\n\nC(6 - 3,2)=C(3,2)=3\n\nN(X1)=0 + 3 = 3\n\n4. [0,4]:\n\na=0, b=4\n\nC(0,2)=0\n\nC(6 - 4,2)=C(2,2)=1\n\nN(X1)=0 + 1 = 1\n\n5. [0,5]:\n\na=0, b=5\n\nC(0,2)=0\n\nC(6 - 5,2)=C(1,2)=0\n\nN(X1)=0 + 0 = 0\n\n6. [1,2]:\n\na=1, b=2\n\nC(1,2)=0 (since 1 < 2, can't choose 2 elements)\n\nC(6 - 2,2)=C(4,2)=6\n\nN(X1)=0 + 6 = 6\n\n7. [1,3]:\n\na=1, b=3\n\nC(1,2)=0\n\nC(6 - 3,2)=C(3,2)=3\n\nN(X1)=0 + 3 = 3\n\n8. [1,4]:\n\na=1, b=4\n\nC(1,2)=0\n\nC(6 - 4,2)=C(2,2)=1\n\nN(X1)=0 + 1 = 1\n\n9. [1,5]:\n\na=1, b=5\n\nC(1,2)=0\n\nC(6 - 5,2)=C(1,2)=0\n\nN(X1)=0 + 0 = 0\n\n10. [2,3]:\n\na=2, b=3\n\nC(2,2)=1\n\nC(6 - 3,2)=C(3,2)=3\n\nN(X1)=1 + 3 = 4\n\n11. [2,4]:\n\na=2, b=4\n\nC(2,2)=1\n\nC(6 - 4,2)=C(2,2)=1\n\nN(X1)=1 + 1 = 2\n\n12. [2,5]:\n\na=2, b=5\n\nC(2,2)=1\n\nC(6 - 5,2)=C(1,2)=0\n\nN(X1)=1 + 0 = 1\n\n13. [3,4]:\n\na=3, b=4\n\nC(3,2)=3\n\nC(6 - 4,2)=C(2,2)=1\n\nN(X1)=3 + 1 = 4\n\n14. [3,5]:\n\na=3, b=5\n\nC(3,2)=3\n\nC(6 - 5,2)=C(1,2)=0\n\nN(X1)=3 + 0 = 3\n\n15. [4,5]:\n\na=4, b=5\n\nC(4,2)=6\n\nC(6 - 5,2)=C(1,2)=0\n\nN(X1)=6 + 0 = 6\n\nOkay, let me list all these N(X1) values:\n\n1. 10\n\n2. 6\n\n3. 3\n\n4. 1\n\n5. 0\n\n6. 6\n\n7. 3\n\n8. 1\n\n9. 0\n\n10. 4\n\n11. 2\n\n12. 1\n\n13. 4\n\n14. 3\n\n15. 6\n\nNow, let me sum these up:\n\nStarting from the first:\n\n10 + 6 = 16\n\n16 + 3 = 19\n\n19 + 1 = 20\n\n20 + 0 = 20\n\n20 + 6 = 26\n\n26 + 3 = 29\n\n29 + 1 = 30\n\n30 + 0 = 30\n\n30 + 4 = 34\n\n34 + 2 = 36\n\n36 + 1 = 37\n\n37 + 4 = 41\n\n41 + 3 = 44\n\n44 + 6 = 50\n\nSo the total Sum_{X1 in X} N(X1) = 50.\n\nWait, let me verify the addition step by step to make sure I didn't make a mistake:\n\n1. 10\n\n2. +6 → 16\n\n3. +3 → 19\n\n4. +1 → 20\n\n5. +0 → 20\n\n6. +6 → 26\n\n7. +3 → 29\n\n8. +1 → 30\n\n9. +0 → 30\n\n10. +4 → 34\n\n11. +2 → 36\n\n12. +1 → 37\n\n13. +4 → 41\n\n14. +3 → 44\n\n15. +6 → 50\n\nYes, total Sum_{X1} N(X1) = 50.\n\nTherefore, going back, the total number of ordered pairs of rectangles with non-overlapping x-intervals is:\n\n15^2 * 50 = 225 * 50 = 11,250.\n\nWait, hold on: 15^2 is 225, and 225 * 50 is indeed 11,250. Hmm.\n\nBut wait, hold on. Wait a second, let me double-check the logic here.\n\nEarlier, I said that for each x-interval X1, the contribution to the total ordered pairs is 15^2 * N(X1). Wait, let me go back to that step.\n\nWe had:\n\nFor each x-interval X1, there are 15 rectangles with that x-interval (since each can have any of the 15 y-intervals). For each such rectangle R1 with x-interval X1, the number of rectangles R2 with non-overlapping x-interval is N(X1) * 15. Wait, why 15?\n\nBecause N(X1) is the number of x-intervals that don't overlap with X1. For each such x-interval X2, there are 15 rectangles with x-interval X2 (each combined with a y-interval). Therefore, for each R1, the number of R2 with non-overlapping x-interval is N(X1) * 15.\n\nTherefore, for each X1, the number of ordered pairs (R1, R2) where R1 has x-interval X1 and R2 has a non-overlapping x-interval is:\n\n( number of R1 with X1 ) * ( number of R2 with non-overlapping x-interval ) =\n\n15 * [N(X1) * 15] = 15^2 * N(X1).\n\nTherefore, since there are 15 x-intervals X1, the total over all X1 is:\n\nSum_{X1} [15^2 * N(X1)] = 15^2 * Sum_{X1} N(X1) = 225 * 50 = 11,250.\n\nSo that is the total number of ordered pairs (R1, R2) where the x-intervals do not overlap. Since we want unordered pairs, we need to divide by 2, so that would be 11,250 / 2 = 5,625.\n\nTherefore, A = 5,625.\n\nWait, but hold on. Wait a second. Is that correct?\n\nWait, actually, in the above calculation, when we compute for each X1, the number of R1 with X1 is 15, and for each such R1, the number of R2 with non-overlapping x-interval is N(X1)*15. So the total over all R1 is Sum_{R1} [N(X1(R1)) * 15], where X1(R1) is the x-interval of R1.\n\nBut since there are 15 R1 for each X1, then Sum_{R1} N(X1(R1)) * 15 = 15 * Sum_{X1} [N(X1) * 15] ?\n\nWait, perhaps I confused a step there. Let me rephrase.\n\nLet me think of it as:\n\nTotal ordered pairs with non-overlapping x-intervals = Sum_{R1} [number of R2 with non-overlapping x-interval with R1]\n\nFor each rectangle R1, let X1 be its x-interval. Then, the number of R2 with non-overlapping x-interval is N(X1) * 15, as before. Therefore, the total over all R1 is Sum_{R1} [N(X1(R1)) * 15].\n\nBut since there are 15 rectangles for each X1, and there are 15 X1s, the sum becomes:\n\nSum_{X1} [ (number of R1 with X1) * (N(X1) * 15) ] = Sum_{X1} [15 * N(X1) * 15] = 15^2 * Sum_{X1} N(X1) = 225 * 50 = 11,250, which is the same as before.\n\nTherefore, the total number of ordered pairs is 11,250, so unordered pairs is 11,250 / 2 = 5,625. So A = 5,625.\n\nSimilarly, since the problem is symmetric in x and y, the number of unordered pairs of rectangles with non-overlapping y-intervals is also 5,625. Let me denote this as B = 5,625.\n\nNow, we need to compute C, the number of unordered pairs of rectangles that have both x-intervals not overlapping AND y-intervals not overlapping.\n\nSo, C is the number of unordered pairs {R1, R2} such that both their x-intervals do not overlap AND their y-intervals do not overlap.\n\nTo compute C, we can use similar reasoning as above, but now considering both x and y separations.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can compute the total number of ordered pairs with both x and y non-overlapping, then divide by 2.\n\nLet me try that approach.\n\nFirst, compute the number of ordered pairs (R1, R2) where both x-intervals do not overlap AND y-intervals do not overlap.\n\nTo compute this, we can consider that for such a pair, the x-intervals must not overlap and the y-intervals must not overlap.\n\nTherefore, for each such pair, the x-interval of R1 and x-interval of R2 must not overlap, and similarly the y-interval of R1 and y-interval of R2 must not overlap.\n\nTherefore, similar to the previous computation, but now in two dimensions.\n\nPerhaps we can model this as:\n\nFirst, choose an x-interval X1 and an x-interval X2 that do not overlap, and a y-interval Y1 and a y-interval Y2 that do not overlap, then the number of rectangle pairs with these intervals is 1 * 1 (for the specific X1,X2,Y1,Y2) multiplied by... Wait, no, actually, for each such combination of X1,X2,Y1,Y2, the number of rectangle pairs is (number of rectangles with x-interval X1 and y-interval Y1) times (number of rectangles with x-interval X2 and y-interval Y2). Wait, but actually, each rectangle is determined by its own x and y interval. So, if we fix X1 and X2 as non-overlapping x-intervals, and Y1 and Y2 as non-overlapping y-intervals, then the number of rectangle pairs with x-intervals X1,X2 and y-intervals Y1,Y2 is 1 * 1 = 1? No, no, each rectangle with x-interval X1 can have any y-interval, so actually, if we fix X1 and X2 (non-overlapping x-intervals) and Y1 and Y2 (non-overlapping y-intervals), then the number of rectangle pairs where R1 has x-interval X1 and y-interval Y1, and R2 has x-interval X2 and y-interval Y2 is 1 (for each combination of Y1,Y2). Wait, no, actually, the count is:\n\nFor R1: must have x-interval X1 and any y-interval, but to have y-interval Y1 specifically? Wait, no, if we are considering pairs where R1 has x-interval X1 and R2 has x-interval X2 (non-overlapping), and also R1 has y-interval Y1 and R2 has y-interval Y2 (non-overlapping), then for each such quadruple (X1,X2,Y1,Y2), the number of such rectangle pairs is 1 * 1 = 1? No, actually, no, because R1 can have any y-interval Y1' and R2 can have any y-interval Y2' as long as Y1' and Y2' are non-overlapping? Wait, no, actually, in this approach, we are fixing Y1 and Y2 to be non-overlapping, but actually, the condition is that the y-intervals of R1 and R2 do not overlap, regardless of what Y1 and Y2 are. So perhaps this is getting too convoluted.\n\nAlternatively, let's think of it as follows:\n\nThe number of ordered pairs (R1, R2) where both x-intervals do not overlap and y-intervals do not overlap can be computed by:\n\nFor each rectangle R1, count the number of rectangles R2 such that:\n\n1. The x-interval of R2 does not overlap with that of R1,\n\nAND\n\n2. The y-interval of R2 does not overlap with that of R1.\n\nTherefore, for a given R1 with x-interval X1 and y-interval Y1, the number of R2 that satisfy both conditions is equal to:\n\n[Number of x-intervals X2 not overlapping with X1] * [Number of y-intervals Y2 not overlapping with Y1].\n\nBecause the x-interval of R2 must be one of the non-overlapping X2 with X1, and the y-interval of R2 must be one of the non-overlapping Y2 with Y1. Since the choices of X2 and Y2 are independent, the total number is N_x(X1) * N_y(Y1), where N_x(X1) is the number of x-intervals not overlapping with X1 (which we already computed earlier as part of N(X1)), and N_y(Y1) is the number of y-intervals not overlapping with Y1 (which, due to symmetry, will be the same as N(X1) for the corresponding x-interval, but since we are dealing with y-intervals, we can compute it similarly).\n\nWait, actually, since the grid is symmetric in x and y, for any y-interval Y1 = [c, d], the number of y-intervals not overlapping with Y1 is the same as the number of x-intervals not overlapping with a given x-interval X1. Therefore, for any y-interval Y1, N_y(Y1) = N_x(X1) where X1 is a generic x-interval? Wait, not exactly, but since the grid is symmetric, the computation for y-intervals will be the same as for x-intervals. Therefore, for any y-interval Y1 = [c, d], the number of y-intervals not overlapping with Y1 is equal to C(c, 2) + C(6 - d, 2), just like for x-intervals. Therefore, the value N(Y1) = C(c, 2) + C(6 - d, 2), which is the same as N(X1) for the corresponding x-interval [c, d].\n\nTherefore, for a given rectangle R1 with x-interval X1 = [a, b] and y-interval Y1 = [c, d], the number of R2 such that their x-intervals do not overlap and their y-intervals do not overlap is N(X1) * N(Y1).\n\nTherefore, the total number of ordered pairs (R1, R2) with both non-overlapping is:\n\nSum over all R1 of [ N(X1) * N(Y1) ]\n\nSince each R1 is determined by its x-interval X1 and y-interval Y1, and there are 15 x-intervals and 15 y-intervals, so 225 rectangles.\n\nTherefore, we can write this sum as:\n\nSum_{X1} Sum_{Y1} [ N(X1) * N(Y1) * (number of rectangles with X1 and Y1) ]\n\nWait, no, actually, for each R1, which is a specific pair (X1, Y1), the term is N(X1) * N(Y1). Since for each R1, regardless of its Y1, the number of R2 is N(X1) * N(Y1). Therefore, the total over all R1 is:\n\nSum_{X1, Y1} [ N(X1) * N(Y1) ]\n\nBecause for each (X1, Y1), there is one R1 with that x and y interval, and for each such R1, the number of R2 is N(X1) * N(Y1).\n\nTherefore, the total ordered pairs is Sum_{X1} Sum_{Y1} [ N(X1) * N(Y1) ].\n\nSince the x and y are independent, we can factor this sum as:\n\n[ Sum_{X1} N(X1) ] * [ Sum_{Y1} N(Y1) ].\n\nBecause Sum_{X1, Y1} N(X1) N(Y1) = [Sum_{X1} N(X1)] * [Sum_{Y1} N(Y1)].\n\nSince Sum_{X1} N(X1) = 50 (as computed earlier), and Sum_{Y1} N(Y1) is also 50 (by symmetry, since y-intervals are the same as x-intervals in terms of counting), so this becomes 50 * 50 = 2,500.\n\nTherefore, the total number of ordered pairs (R1, R2) where both x and y intervals do not overlap is 2,500.\n\nTherefore, the number of unordered pairs C is 2,500 / 2 = 1,250.\n\nTherefore, going back to the inclusion-exclusion formula:\n\nNumber of non-overlapping rectangle pairs = A + B - C = 5,625 + 5,625 - 1,250.\n\nCompute that:\n\n5,625 + 5,625 = 11,250\n\n11,250 - 1,250 = 10,000.\n\nWait, so according to this, the total number of non-overlapping pairs is 10,000?\n\nBut let me check the steps again to make sure.\n\nFirst, A = number of unordered pairs with non-overlapping x-intervals: 5,625\n\nB = number of unordered pairs with non-overlapping y-intervals: 5,625\n\nC = number of unordered pairs with both non-overlapping x and y intervals: 1,250\n\nTherefore, by inclusion-exclusion, the number of unordered pairs with either non-overlapping x OR non-overlapping y is A + B - C = 5,625 + 5,625 - 1,250 = 10,000.\n\nTherefore, the answer would be 10,000?\n\nBut wait, hold on a second. Wait, but the problem says \"the rectangles do not overlap at their interiors, edges, or vertices.\" So that is exactly the non-overlapping pairs as we have computed here? Because if two rectangles do not overlap at all (not even sharing a point), then they must satisfy that either their x-intervals don't overlap OR their y-intervals don't overlap. Wait, actually, no. Wait, actually, if two rectangles do not share any points, then they must be separated in at least one direction, so either x-intervals don't overlap OR y-intervals don't overlap. Wait, but actually, if they are separated in both x and y directions, then they still don't overlap, but that case is included in both A and B, so we have to subtract the overlap.\n\nWait, actually, the condition for non-overlapping is exactly that they are separated in x OR separated in y. Therefore, the total number is indeed A + B - C, which is 10,000.\n\nBut let me sanity check: total number of unordered pairs of rectangles is C(225, 2) = (225 * 224)/2 = let's compute that:\n\n225 * 224 = 225*(200 + 24) = 225*200 + 225*24 = 45,000 + 5,400 = 50,400\n\nThen 50,400 / 2 = 25,200.\n\nSo total unordered pairs: 25,200.\n\nIf our computed non-overlapping pairs are 10,000, then the overlapping pairs would be 25,200 - 10,000 = 15,200. That seems plausible? Not sure yet, but let's see.\n\nAlternatively, maybe my calculation is wrong. Let me see:\n\nWait, in the computation for A, we computed the number of unordered pairs where x-intervals do not overlap as 5,625. Similarly for B. Then C is the overlap where both x and y don't overlap, so 1,250. Then A + B - C = 5,625 + 5,625 - 1,250 = 10,000. Hmm.\n\nWait, but let me think of a small grid to see if this approach works.\n\nSuppose we have a 2x2 grid of squares, so a 3x3 grid of points. Then the number of rectangles in a 2x2 grid (of squares) would be C(3,2)*C(3,2) = 3*3 = 9 rectangles.\n\nThen, how many pairs of non-overlapping rectangles are there?\n\nLet me enumerate all rectangles in a 2x2 grid (so 3x3 grid points):\n\nThe rectangles can be:\n\n1x1: there are 4 of these, each single square.\n\n1x2: horizontal rectangles: 2 in each row, so 2 rows * 1 = 2? Wait, no.\n\nWait, in a 3x3 grid of points, the rectangles are determined by choosing two x-coordinates and two y-coordinates.\n\nSo x-coordinates: 0,1,2\n\ny-coordinates: 0,1,2\n\nTherefore, the number of rectangles is C(3,2)*C(3,2) = 3*3 = 9.\n\nThe rectangles are:\n\n1. [0,1]x[0,1] (top-left square)\n\n2. [0,1]x[1,2] (top-right square)\n\n3. [0,1]x[0,2] (top row)\n\n4. [1,2]x[0,1] (bottom-left square)\n\n5. [1,2]x[1,2] (bottom-right square)\n\n6. [1,2]x[0,2] (bottom row)\n\n7. [0,2]x[0,1] (left column)\n\n8. [0,2]x[1,2] (right column)\n\n9. [0,2]x[0,2] (the entire grid)\n\nNow, we need to count the number of pairs of these 9 rectangles that do not overlap at all (no shared points).\n\nLet me list all 9 rectangles and see which pairs don't overlap.\n\nFirst, list them with labels for clarity:\n\n1: [0,1]x[0,1] (top-left)\n\n2: [0,1]x[1,2] (top-right)\n\n3: [0,1]x[0,2] (top row)\n\n4: [1,2]x[0,1] (bottom-left)\n\n5: [1,2]x[1,2] (bottom-right)\n\n6: [1,2]x[0,2] (bottom row)\n\n7: [0,2]x[0,1] (left column)\n\n8: [0,2]x[1,2] (right column)\n\n9: [0,2]x[0,2] (entire grid)\n\nNow, let's consider pairs:\n\nFirst, pairs that include the entire grid (rectangle 9). Any pair with rectangle 9 and another rectangle will overlap, because the entire grid contains all other rectangles. So all pairs involving 9 are overlapping. So that's 8 pairs, all overlapping.\n\nNow, pairs not involving 9:\n\nTotal pairs: C(8,2) = 28. But we need to subtract the overlapping ones among these 8.\n\nWait, actually, let me do it step by step.\n\nFirst, list all pairs among the 8 non-entire grid rectangles and count the non-overlapping ones.\n\nFirst, rectangles 1 through 8.\n\nLet me see which pairs don't overlap.\n\nFirst, consider rectangle 1: [0,1]x[0,1]\n\nWhich other rectangles don't overlap with it?\n\nA rectangle doesn't overlap with 1 if it is to the right of x=1 or below y=1.\n\nSo:\n\nRectangles to the right of x=1: those with left x-coordinate >1, i.e., x in [1,2], so rectangles:\n\n4,5,6,7,8? Wait:\n\nWait, rectangles with x-interval starting after 1: so x-interval [1,2], so rectangles 4,5,6,7? Wait, no:\n\nWait, rectangle 7 is [0,2]x[0,1], which spans the entire x-axis but only up to y=1. So its x-interval is [0,2], so it includes x=1.\n\nWait, actually, to not overlap with rectangle 1 ([0,1]x[0,1]), a rectangle must either be entirely to the right of x=1, i.e., x-interval starting at x=2, or entirely below y=1, i.e., y-interval ending at y=0, but since y starts at 0, that can't happen. Or entirely above y=1, i.e., y-interval starting at y=1 or higher.\n\nWait, let me recall the condition:\n\nTwo rectangles do not overlap if their x-intervals do not overlap OR their y-intervals do not overlap.\n\nSo for rectangle 1 and another rectangle R:\n\nEither:\n\n- The x-interval of R is entirely to the right of [0,1], i.e., R's left x > 1,\n\nOR\n\n- The y-interval of R is entirely below [0,1], which would require R's top y < 0, which is impossible,\n\nOR\n\n- The y-interval of R is entirely above [0,1], i.e., R's bottom y > 1,\n\nOR\n\n- The x-interval of R is entirely to the left of [0,1], which would require R's right x < 0, impossible.\n\nTherefore, the only possibilities for non-overlapping with rectangle 1 are:\n\nEither R is entirely to the right of x=1, or entirely above y=1.\n\nRectangles entirely to the right of x=1: their x-interval must start at x=1 or x=2? Wait, no. To be entirely to the right of x=1, the left edge must be >1. Since x coordinates are integers from 0 to 2, the only possible x-interval entirely to the right of x=1 is [2,2], but that's a degenerate interval (zero width), which isn't a valid rectangle. Wait, in our grid, the x-intervals must have length at least 1? Wait, no: in a 3x3 grid of points, the x-intervals can be [0,1], [0,2], [1,2], etc. So the only x-interval entirely to the right of x=1 is [2,2], which is invalid. Wait, actually, there are no x-intervals entirely to the right of x=1 except those that start at x=2, but since the maximum x is 2, the only x-interval starting at x=2 is [2,2], which is invalid (since you need two distinct points). Therefore, there are no x-intervals entirely to the right of x=1 except maybe [1,2], but [1,2] starts at x=1, so it overlaps with [0,1] on x=1? Wait, no, the x-interval [1,2] starts at x=1, so the left edge is x=1, which is the same as the right edge of rectangle 1 (which is x=1). Therefore, they share the line x=1, so they share an edge. Therefore, they do overlap on the edge, which is not allowed.\n\nTherefore, rectangles entirely to the right of rectangle 1 would have to start at x=2, but since the grid only goes up to x=2, the only such interval is [2,2], which is invalid. Therefore, there are no rectangles entirely to the right of rectangle 1 in terms of x-coordinate.\n\nSimilarly, rectangles entirely above y=1: their bottom y must be >1. The possible y-intervals entirely above y=1 are those starting at y=2, but the maximum y is 2, so the only such interval is [2,2], which is invalid. Therefore, there are no rectangles entirely above y=1.\n\nWait, so does that mean rectangle 1 only overlaps with all other rectangles except itself? But that can't be.\n\nWait, rectangle 1 is [0,1]x[0,1]. Let's see rectangle 3: [0,1]x[0,2]. This rectangle includes rectangle 1, so they overlap.\n\nRectangle 7: [0,2]x[0,1]. This includes rectangle 1 in its lower part, so they overlap.\n\nRectangle 2: [0,1]x[1,2]. This is adjacent to rectangle 1 on the top edge. So they share the edge y=1 from x=0 to x=1. Therefore, they share an edge, so they overlap on the edge, which is not allowed.\n\nRectangle 4: [1,2]x[0,1]. This shares the vertical line x=1 from y=0 to y=1 with rectangle 1. So they share an edge, overlapping on that edge.\n\nRectangle 5: [1,2]x[1,2]. This is to the right and above rectangle 1, but does it share any points? The rectangle 1 is at [0,1]x[0,1], and rectangle 5 is [1,2]x[1,2]. They meet at the corner (1,1), so they share a vertex. Therefore, they overlap at the vertex (1,1), which is not allowed.\n\nRectangle 6: [1,2]x[0,2]. This rectangle includes rectangle 4 and 5, and overlaps with rectangle 1 along the line x=1 from y=0 to y=1 (the same as rectangle 4) and also overlaps with rectangle 1's bottom edge? Wait, rectangle 6 is from x=1 to x=2 and y=0 to y=2, so it includes the point (1,0) to (2,2). Rectangle 1 is from (0,0) to (1,1). So they share the point (1,0) to (1,1) along the edge x=1 from y=0 to y=1. So they share an edge, overlapping along that edge.\n\nRectangle 8: [0,2]x[1,2]. This rectangle spans the entire x-axis but from y=1 to y=2. It overlaps with rectangle 1 along the line y=1 from x=0 to x=1. So again, sharing an edge.\n\nTherefore, rectangle 1 only overlaps with all other rectangles, except perhaps none? Wait, so rectangle 1 cannot pair with any other rectangle without overlapping? That seems odd, but in this small grid, maybe that's the case.\n\nWait, rectangle 1 is the top-left square. The only rectangle that might not overlap with it is... Is there any?\n\nWait, rectangle 5 is [1,2]x[1,2], which is the bottom-right square. It shares the corner (1,1) with rectangle 1, so they share a vertex, which is not allowed.\n\nRectangle 7 is the left column, which includes rectangle 1's entire left side.\n\nRectangle 8 is the right column, which is to the right and above, but still shares edges or vertices.\n\nHmm, so in this 2x2 grid of squares (3x3 points), the only rectangle that doesn't overlap with rectangle 1 would have to be entirely to the right of x=1 and above y=1, but in this grid, such a rectangle would have to be [2,2]x[2,2], which doesn't exist.\n\nTherefore, rectangle 1 cannot form a non-overlapping pair with any other rectangle except itself, but since we are choosing two distinct rectangles, rectangle 1 cannot be in any non-overlapping pair.\n\nSimilarly, let's check rectangle 5: [1,2]x[1,2]. It is the bottom-right square. Similarly, it shares edges or vertices with all other rectangles except maybe none. Let's see:\n\nRectangle 5 and rectangle 2: [0,1]x[1,2]. They share the edge y=1 from x=0 to x=1? Wait, rectangle 2 is [0,1]x[1,2], so its bottom y is 1, top y is 2. Rectangle 5 is [1,2]x[1,2], so its bottom y is 1, top y is 2. So they share the edge y=1 from x=1 to x=2? Wait, no, rectangle 2 is from x=0 to x=1, so they meet at x=1. So they share the vertical line x=1 from y=1 to y=2. So they share an edge.\n\nRectangle 5 and rectangle 6: rectangle 6 is [1,2]x[0,2], so they share the entire rectangle 5's area? No, rectangle 6 includes rectangle 5. So they overlap entirely.\n\nRectangle 5 and rectangle 4: rectangle 4 is [1,2]x[0,1], so they share the edge y=1 from x=1 to x=2.\n\nRectangle 5 and rectangle 8: [0,2]x[1,2], which includes rectangle 5, so they overlap.\n\nRectangle 5 and rectangle 3: [0,1]x[0,2], which includes the left part, so overlaps.\n\nSo yes, rectangle 5 also cannot form a non-overlapping pair with any other rectangle.\n\nHow about rectangle 3: the top row [0,1]x[0,2]. Let's see:\n\nDoes rectangle 3 overlap with any rectangle? It includes rectangles 1, 2, and shares edges with rectangles 7 and 8? Wait, rectangle 3 is the entire top row: from x=0 to x=1, and y=0 to y=2. So it includes rectangles 1 and 2, and shares edges with rectangles 7 (left column) and 8 (right column)? Wait, rectangle 7 is the entire left column [0,2]x[0,1], so it overlaps with rectangle 3 in the region [0,1]x[0,1], which is rectangle 1. Similarly, rectangle 8 is [0,2]x[1,2], overlapping with rectangle 3 in [0,1]x[1,2], which is rectangle 2. So rectangle 3 overlaps with all other rectangles except maybe none.\n\nHmm, this is getting depressing. Maybe in this small grid, there are actually no non-overlapping pairs? But that can't be right.\n\nWait, let's consider rectangles 4 and 6.\n\nRectangle 4 is [1,2]x[0,1] (bottom-left square)\n\nRectangle 6 is [1,2]x[0,2] (bottom row)\n\nThey overlap entirely in the bottom row's left part? Wait, rectangle 4 is the left half of the bottom row, and rectangle 6 is the entire bottom row. So they overlap.\n\nHow about rectangle 4 and rectangle 8?\n\nRectangle 4: [1,2]x[0,1]\n\nRectangle 8: [0,2]x[1,2]\n\nThey share the edge at y=1 from x=1 to x=2? So overlapping on that edge.\n\nHmm, how about rectangle 4 and rectangle 5? They are adjacent horizontally, sharing an edge.\n\nWait, maybe the only possible non-overlapping pair is between rectangle 1 and rectangle 5? But they share a corner.\n\nWait, rectangle 1 is [0,1]x[0,1], and rectangle 5 is [1,2]x[1,2]. They share the point (1,1), so they overlap at a vertex, which is not allowed.\n\nSimilarly, rectangle 2 and rectangle 6: rectangle 2 is [0,1]x[1,2], rectangle 6 is [1,2]x[0,2]. They share the point (1,1) and (1,2). So they overlap at a vertex.\n\nHmm. Is there any pair of rectangles in this 2x2 grid that don't overlap at all?\n\nWait, what about rectangle 7 (left column) and rectangle 8 (right column)? They are both full-height columns, side by side. Their x-intervals are [0,2] for both, so their x-intervals do overlap entirely, but their y-intervals are [0,1] and [1,2]. The y-intervals: [0,1] and [1,2] do they overlap? The first ends at y=1, the second starts at y=1. So they share the line y=1 from x=0 to x=2. Therefore, they share an edge, so they overlap on that edge.\n\nSimilarly, rectangle 7 and rectangle 9 (the entire grid) obviously overlap.\n\nWait, how about rectangle 3 (the top row) and rectangle 6 (the bottom row). Their x-intervals are both [0,1] and [1,2]? Wait, no:\n\nWait, rectangle 3 is [0,1]x[0,2], so x-interval [0,1], y-interval [0,2].\n\nRectangle 6 is [1,2]x[0,2], x-interval [1,2], y-interval [0,2].\n\nSo their x-intervals are adjacent but not overlapping? The x-interval of 3 is [0,1], and of 6 is [1,2]. Since 1 is the endpoint, do they overlap?\n\nThe x-intervals [0,1] and [1,2] touch at x=1, but do not overlap in their interiors. Since the problem states that rectangles cannot overlap at edges or vertices, so even touching at a point (x=1) or along an edge (the line x=1 from y=0 to y=2) is considered overlapping.\n\nTherefore, rectangles 3 and 6 share the entire vertical line x=1 from y=0 to y=2, so they overlap along that edge.\n\nHmm, so in this 2x2 grid of squares, is there any pair of rectangles that do not overlap at all? It seems like every pair of distinct rectangles shares at least a point, edge, or vertex.\n\nWait, let's see rectangle 7 (left column [0,2]x[0,1]) and rectangle 8 (right column [0,2]x[1,2]). They share the horizontal line y=1 along the entire x-axis from 0 to 2. So that's a full edge overlap.\n\nHow about rectangle 4 ([1,2]x[0,1]) and rectangle 5 ([1,2]x[1,2]). They are stacked vertically in the bottom-right corner. They share the horizontal