se three ways: interior, edge, or vertex. So, two rectangles that share even a single point in their closure (i.e., including edges and vertices) are considered overlapping and thus invalid. Therefore, we need pairs of rectangles that are completely disjoint: neither their interiors, nor their edges, nor their vertices intersect.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to computing the number of unordered pairs of rectangles that are disjoint in the grid.\n\nTherefore, the total number of unordered pairs is C(225, 2), and then subtract the number of unordered pairs that do intersect (either in interior, edge, or vertex). But this might be tricky because computing the number of intersecting pairs directly might be complicated. Alternatively, maybe it's better to compute the number of disjoint pairs directly?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can compute the total number of unordered pairs of rectangles, and then subtract those pairs that intersect in some way. But intersections can be in various ways: overlapping in their interiors, overlapping on an edge, or overlapping at a vertex. However, note that if two rectangles share a common vertex, they also might share an edge, or not. For example, two rectangles can share just a vertex without sharing an edge. Similarly, two rectangles can share an edge without sharing a vertex? Wait, no—if two rectangles share an edge, then they must share infinitely many points along that edge, so in particular, they share all the points along that edge, which includes the vertices at the endpoints of the edge. So if two rectangles share an edge, they must share at least two vertices (the endpoints of that edge). Similarly, if two rectangles share a vertex, but not an edge, then they only share that single vertex. So overlapping at a vertex but not an edge is possible, but overlapping on an edge implies overlapping at vertices.\n\nTherefore, the set of overlapping pairs can be partitioned into three disjoint categories:\n\n1. Pairs that share at 