recall inclusion-exclusion formula:\n\nNumber of elements in the union of two sets A and B is |A ∪ B| = |A| + |B| - |A ∩ B|.\n\nTherefore, the number of pairs that are separated horizontally OR separated vertically is equal to:\n\nN_separated_horizontal + N_separated_vertical - N_separated_both.\n\nTherefore, the number of overlapping pairs is equal to:\n\nTotal pairs - [N_separated_horizontal + N_separated_vertical - N_separated_both].\n\nBut we want overlapping pairs, which are the pairs that are NOT separated in either direction, i.e., the complement of (separated horizontally OR separated vertically). So,\n\nNumber of overlapping pairs = Total pairs - N_separated_horizontal - N_separated_vertical + N_separated_both.\n\nYes, that's right. Because:\n\nNumber of overlapping pairs = Total pairs - (number separated horizontally + number separated vertically - number separated both)\n\n= Total pairs - N_separated_horizontal - N_separated_vertical + N_separated_both.\n\nTherefore, if I can compute N_separated_horizontal, N_separated_vertical, and N_separated_both, then I can compute the overlapping pairs.\n\nSo, let me define:\n\nN_separated_horizontal = number of unordered pairs of rectangles that are separated horizontally (i.e., one is entirely to the left of the other).\n\nSimilarly, N_separated_vertical = number of unordered pairs separated vertically.\n\nN_separated_both = number of unordered pairs that are separated both horizontally and vertically (i.e., one is to the left and above, left and below, right and above, or right and below the other; so they are separated in both directions).\n\nTherefore, if I can compute these three terms, then I can compute the overlapping pairs, and hence the disjoint pairs.\n\nSo, let's try to compute N_separated_horizontal first.\n\nN_separated_horizontal is the number of unordered pairs of rectangles that are separated horizontally, meaning one is entirely to the left of the other.\n\nTo compute N_separated_horizontal, we can