t are to the right of A (i.e., B's left edge is greater than A's right edge), and then sum over all A, then divide by 1? Wait, no. Wait, in ordered pairs, for each A, the number of B such that B is to the right of A is equal to the number of rectangles entirely to the right of A. Therefore, N_left = sum_{A} [number of B with b_A < a_B].\n\nTherefore, if I can compute for each rectangle A, the number of rectangles B that are to the right of A, then sum over all A, that will give N_left.\n\nSimilarly, N_separated_vertical = N_below = sum_{A} [number of B with d_A < c_B].\n\nThen, to compute N_separated_both, we need for each rectangle A, the number of rectangles B that are both to the right of A and above A, plus those to the right and below, plus those to the left and above, etc.— no, perhaps it's better to think that for each rectangle A, the number of rectangles B that are both to the right and above A, plus those to the right and below, plus those to the left and above, etc.— but this is getting too involved.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can compute N_separated_both as follows:\n\nFor an unordered pair {A,B} to be separated both horizontally and vertically, they must be in one of the four \"quadrant\" arrangements:\n\n1. A is to the left and below B,\n\n2. A is to the left and above B,\n\n3. A is to the right and below B,\n\n4. A is to the right and above B.\n\nBut since the pair is unordered, these four cases are actually two distinct cases: either one is to the left and above, left and below, right and above, or right and below. Wait, actually, no—each unordered pair has a unique separation in terms of relative positions. For any two distinct rectangles, they can be in one of four relative positions:\n\n- A is to the left and above B,\n\n- A is to the left and below B,\n\n- A is to the right and above B,\n\n- A is to the right and below B.\n\nBut since the pair is unordered, these four cases are actually two distinct cases: either the two rectangles are separate