ore, each such unordered pair is counted twice in the total sum over all A of (N_A + M_A + P_A + Q_A). Therefore, the total number of unordered pairs separated both horizontally and vertically is [sum_{A} (N_A + M_A + P_A + Q_A)] / 2.\n\nTherefore, if I can compute for each rectangle A, the values N_A, M_A, P_A, Q_A, then I can compute this sum.\n\nHowever, this still requires a lot of computation, as there are 225 rectangles, each requiring four counts. This seems impractical by hand, but maybe there is symmetry or a pattern we can exploit.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can compute the total number of separated_both pairs as follows:\n\nFirst, note that the total number of ordered pairs (A,B) where they are separated both horizontally and vertically is equal to:\n\nFor each rectangle A, the number of rectangles B that are either to the right and above, right and below, left and above, or left and below A. Then sum over all A.\n\nBut this is the same as sum_{A} [ (number of B to the right of A) * (number of B above A) ? No, that's not correct.\n\nWait, actually, the number of B that are both to the right and above A is equal to the number of rectangles B such that a_B > b_A and c_B > d_A.\n\nSimilarly, the number of B both to the right and below A is those with a_B > b_A and c_B < d_A.\n\nSimilarly, left and above: a_B < b_A and c_B > d_A,\n\nleft and below: a_B < b_A and c_B < d_A.\n\nTherefore, for each rectangle A, the four counts N_A, M_A, P_A, Q_A are:\n\nN_A = number of B with a_B > b_A and c_B > d_A,\n\nM_A = number of B with a_B > b_A and c_B < d_A,\n\nP_A = number of B with a_B < b_A and c_B > d_A,\n\nQ_A = number of B with a_B < b_A and c_B < d_A.\n\nTherefore, the total over all A of (N_A + M_A + P_A + Q_A) is equal to sum_{A} [ (number of B to the right of A) * (number of B above A) + (number of B to the right of A)*(number of B below A) + (number of B to the left of A)*(number of B above A) + (number of B to the left of A)*(number of B below A) ) ]? Wait