need to multiply by the number of vertical intervals J with that b_J.\n\nCount_I(b_J) is the number of vertical intervals with right endpoint b_J.\n\nFor b_J=0:0,\n\nb_J=1:1,\n\nb_J=2:2,\n\nb_J=3:3.\n\nTherefore,\n\nTotal ordered vertical separated pairs where J is left of I:\n\nSum over b_J=0 to 3 of [count_I(b_J) * count_J(b_J)].\n\nWait, no, actually, for each vertical interval J with right endpoint b_J, the number of I's to the right is count_J(b_J), so total is sum_{J} count_J(b_J).\n\nBut count_J(b_J) depends on J's b_J.\n\nAlternatively, the total is:\n\nFor b_J=0: there are 0 intervals with b_J=0, so 0.\n\nFor b_J=1: 1 interval J with b_J=1, each contributes count_J(b_J=1)=1, so total 1×1=1.\n\nFor b_J=2: 2 intervals J with b_J=2, each contributes count_J(b_J=2)=0, so 0.\n\nFor b_J=3: 3 intervals J with b_J=3, each contributes count_J(b_J=3)=0, so 0.\n\nThus total ordered vertical separated pairs where J is left of I:1.\n\nTherefore, total ordered vertical separated pairs (both directions):1 (I left of J) +1 (J left of I)=2.\n\nSimilarly, for horizontal separated pairs, it would also be 2.\n\nThus in the 4-line grid:\n\nNumber of ordered vertical separated pairs V=2,\n\nNumber of ordered horizontal separated pairs H=2,\n\nThus, ordered rectangle pairs separated both ways:2×2=4,\n\nThus unordered separated_both pairs:4 /2=2.\n\nBut earlier manual count found only 1 unordered pair.\n\nContradiction! So the formula overcounts.\n\nHmm, this indicates an error in the general approach.\n\nIn the 4-line grid:\n\nThe formula predicts 2 unordered separated_both pairs, but manual count shows 1.\n\nWhere is the mistake?\n\nLet me manually count all unordered pairs separated both horizontally and vertically in the 4-line grid (3x3 squares).\n\nVertical lines:0,1,2,3,\n\nHorizontal lines:0,1,2,3,\n\nRectangles are defined by vertical [a,b] and horizontal [c,d].\n\nA pair {A,B} is separated both horizontally and vertically if:\n\n- Their vertical intervals are separated (one left of the other),\n\nAND\n\n- Their horizontal intervals are separated (one below the other).\n\nLet's list all possible such pairs.\n\nFirst, list all vertical separated pairs (I,J) where I left of J:\n\nOnly one such pair: I=[0,1], J=[2,3].\n\nSimilarly, the reverse vertical pair (J left of I) is impossible since J can't be left of I if I is [0,1].\n\nWait, no: the vertical separated pairs in both directions are:\n\n1. I left of J: I=[0,1], J=[2,3]\n\n2. J left of I: I=[2,3], J=[0,1]\n\nBut in the vertical intervals, I and J are just labels; the unordered pair {I,J} is the same regardless of order.\n\nWait, but when considering rectangle pairs, the vertical separation can be in either direction, but the unordered pair {A,B} will have a specific vertical and horizontal separation.\n\nLet me consider all possible vertical separated interval pairs (unordered):\n\nVertical separated interval pairs (unordered):\n\n{ [0,1], [2,3] }\n\n{ [0,2], [2,3] } — are these separated? [0,2] and [2,3] are adjacent at x=2, so not separated.\n\n{ [0,1], [1,2] } — overlapping in x=1, not separated.\n\n{ [0,1], [1,3] } — overlapping in x=1, not separated.\n\n{ [0,1], [2,3] } — separated.\n\n{ [0,2], [1,3] } — overlapping in x=1 and x=2?\n\nWait, [0,2] and [1,3] overlap from x=1 to x=2.\n\nThus, not separated.\n\n{ [0,3], [1,2] } — overlapping.\n\n{ [1,2], [2,3] } — adjacent at x=2, not separated.\n\nThus, only one unordered vertical separated interval pair: { [0,1], [2,3] }.\n\nSimilarly for horizontal separated interval pairs: only one unordered pair: { [0,1], [2,3] }.\n\nTherefore, to form a rectangle pair separated both ways, we need to combine a vertical separated interval pair with a horizontal separated interval pair.\n\nEach vertical separated interval pair can be combined with each horizontal separated interval pair.\n\nFor the vertical pair { [0,1], [2,3] }, and the horizontal pair { [0,1], [2,3] }:\n\nThis gives two rectangle pairs:\n\n1. A with vertical [0,1] and horizontal [0,1], B with vertical [2,3] and horizontal [2,3].\n\n2. A with vertical [0,1] and horizontal [2,3], B with vertical [2,3] and horizontal [0,1].\n\nWait, but these are two different rectangle pairs:\n\nFirst pair: A is [0,1]x[0,1], B is [2,3]x[2,3].\n\nSecond pair: A is [0,1]x[2,3], B is [2,3]x[0,1].\n\nThese are two distinct unordered pairs because the rectangles are different.\n\nSimilarly, swapping the vertical and horizontal separations:\n\nWait, actually, for the vertical separated interval pair { [0,1], [2,3] } and horizontal separated interval pair { [0,1], [2,3] }, the combinations are:\n\n- Assign the left interval to A's vertical and the bottom interval to A's horizontal,\n\n resulting in A= [0,1]x[0,1], B= [2,3]x[2,3].\n\n- Assign the left interval to A's vertical and the top interval to A's horizontal,\n\n resulting in A= [0,1]x[2,3], B= [2,3]x[0,1].\n\n- Assign the right interval to A's vertical and the bottom interval to A's horizontal,\n\n resulting in A= [2,3]x[0,1], B= [0,1]x[2,3].\n\n- Assign the right interval to A's vertical and the top interval to A's horizontal,\n\n resulting in A= [2,3]x[2,3], B= [0,1]x[0,1].\n\nBut since we are considering unordered pairs {A,B}, these are actually two distinct unordered pairs:\n\n1. { [0,1]x[0,1], [2,3]x[2,3] }\n\n2. { [0,1]x[2,3], [2,3]x[0,1] }\n\nTherefore, there are two unordered pairs separated both ways in the 4-line grid, contradicting my earlier manual count where I only found one.\n\nAh, my mistake earlier was not considering the horizontal assignments properly.\n\nIndeed, there are two distinct unordered pairs:\n\n1. The pair where A is the lower-left small rectangle and B is the upper-right large rectangle.\n\n2. The pair where A is the upper-left small rectangle and B is the lower-right large rectangle.\n\nBoth are separated both horizontally and vertically.\n\nTherefore, the formula's prediction of 2 unordered separated_both pairs is correct.\n\nEarlier, I only counted one because I fixed th