ll possible combinations. So, actually, the total number of intersecting pairs would be equal to the number of pairs of x-intervals that overlap multiplied by the number of pairs of y-intervals that overlap? Wait, no, that can't be, because that would be the case only if for every overlapping pair of x-intervals and every overlapping pair of y-intervals, the combination forms an intersecting rectangle pair. But actually, the rectangles are formed by pairing an x-interval with a y-interval, so the overlaps have to be considered together.\n\nWait, actually, let me think of it as:\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet S be the set of all pairs of rectangles. Then |S| = C(225, 2).\n\nLet T be the set of intersecting rectangle pairs. We need |T|.\n\nEach element of T is a pair of rectangles (R1, R2) such that R1 and R2 intersect, which happens iff their x-intervals overlap and their y-intervals overlap.\n\nTherefore, T = { (R1, R2) | x-intervals of R1 and R2 overlap AND y-intervals of R1 and R2 overlap }.\n\nTherefore, the count |T| can be written as:\n\n|T| = Σ_{R1, R2} [ (x-intervals overlap) AND (y-intervals overlap) ]\n\nWhich can also be written as:\n\n|T| = Σ_{R1, R2} [ (x-intervals overlap) ] * [ (y-intervals overlap) ]\n\nWait, no, actually, that's not quite accurate because the events are not independent. The indicator function for the intersection is the product of the indicators for the x-overlap and y-overlap only if the x and y overlaps are independent, which they are not necessarily.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can express this as:\n\n|T| = Σ_{R1} Σ_{R2} [ (x-intervals overlap) AND (y-intervals overlap) ]\n\nWhich can be written as:\n\nΣ_{R1} Σ_{R2} [ (x-intervals overlap) * (y-intervals overlap) ]\n\nBut since the x and y overlaps are dependent on R1 and R2, perhaps we can separate variables?\n\nAlternatively, let me think of the entire space of pairs of rectangles as the Cartesian product of the set of all x-interval pairs and the set of all y-interval pairs? Wait, no,