{t=1}^5 C(r,t) = sum_{t=1}^5 (r * t) = r * sum_{t=1}^5 t = r * (15) since sum_{t=1}^5 t = 15.\n\nTherefore,\n\nsum_N_right = sum_{r=1}^5 [ N_right(r) * (15 * r) ]\n\nSimilarly, since N_right(r) = 15 * [ (4 - r)(5 - r)/2 ]\n\nTherefore,\n\nsum_N_right = sum_{r=1}^5 [ 15 * ( (4 - r)(5 - r)/2 ) * (15 * r) ]\n\nWait, let's write it step by step.\n\nFirst, compute for each r:\n\nTerm_r = N_right(r) * sum_{t=1}^5 C(r,t)\n\nBut N_right(r) = 15 * [ (4 - r)(5 - r)/2 ]\n\nsum_{t=1}^5 C(r,t) = sum_{t=1}^5 (r * t) = r * sum_{t=1}^5 t = r * (1+2+3+4+5) = r * 15\n\nTherefore,\n\nTerm_r = [15 * ( (4 - r)(5 - r)/2 )] * [15 * r] = 15 * 15 * r * ( (4 - r)(5 - r)/2 )\n\nThus,\n\nsum_N_right = sum_{r=1}^5 Term_r = 225 * sum_{r=1}^5 [ r * (4 - r)(5 - r)/2 ]\n\nSimilarly, we can compute this sum by plugging in r = 1 to 5:\n\nLet me compute each Term_r individually:\n\nFor r=1:\n\nTerm_1 = 225 * [1 * (4 - 1)(5 - 1)/2] = 225 * [1 * 3 * 4 / 2] = 225 * [12 / 2] = 225 * 6 = 1350\n\nWait, hold on, actually, wait, no, I think I might have miscalculated the factorization.\n\nWait, let's re-express:\n\nWait, actually, the 225 comes from 15 * 15, but actually, the Term_r is:\n\nTerm_r = [15 * ( (4 - r)(5 - r)/2 )] * [15 * r] = 15 * 15 * r * ( (4 - r)(5 - r)/2 ) = (225) * [ r * (4 - r)(5 - r)/2 ]\n\nTherefore, sum_N_right = sum_{r=1}^5 Term_r = 225 * sum_{r=1}^5 [ r*(4 - r)(5 - r)/2 ]\n\nWait, actually, perhaps better to compute each Term_r step by step without factoring out the 225 first.\n\nLet me do that to avoid confusion.\n\nFirst, compute for each r from 1 to 5:\n\nTerm_r = N_right(r) * sum_{t=1}^5 C(r,t)\n\nCompute N_right(r):\n\nFor r=1: N_right(1) = 15 * [ (4 -1)(5 -1)/2 ] = 15*(3*4)/2 = 15*6 = 90\n\nsum_{t=1}^5 C(r,t) when r=1: sum_{t=1}^5 (1*t) = 1+2+3+4+5 = 15\n\nTherefore, Term_1 = 90 * 15 = 1350\n\nFor r=2:\n\nN_right(2) = 15 * [ (4 -2)(5 -2)/2 ] = 15*(2*3)/2 = 15*3 = 45\n\nsum_{t=1}^5 C(r,t) when r=2: sum_{t=1}^5 (2*t) = 2*(1+2+3+4+5) = 2*15 = 30\n\nTerm_2 = 45 * 30 = 1350\n\nFor r=3