an be from 1 to 78 - 52 = 26. Exactly 26 possible starting positions. Therefore, if we set d = 26 for all letters, then each letter is assigned a unique starting position from 1 to 26, and their occurrences are at p, p + 26, p + 52. This is exactly the equally spaced block arrangement we considered earlier. In this case, the spacing d is 26 for all letters, and this allows exactly 26! arrangements, corresponding to permuting the starting positions.\n\nIf we tried a smaller spacing d, say d = 13, then the starting positions would need to be from 1 to 78 - 2*13 = 62, which gives 62 possible starting positions. Since we only need 26, that's okay, but then the problem is that the intervals between the occurrences of a single letter would be shorter, but the condition must still hold for all pairs of letters.\n\nHowever, if the spacings d_x for different letters are different, then the intervals between the occurrences of different letters could have different lengths, potentially violating the condition for some pairs.\n\nAlternatively, if all letters have the same spacing d, then the analysis similar to the block permutation case would apply, but with different block sizes? Wait, no. If all letters have spacing d, then the entire sequence would have a periodicity related to d, but unless d divides 78 appropriately, this might not work.\n\nActually, if all letters have the same spacing d, then the entire sequence would have a period of d, but since each letter's occurrences are spaced by d, the sequence must repeat every d positions? Not necessarily, because different letters can have different starting positions.\n\nHowever, for the condition to hold for all pairs of letters, it's likely that the only feasible uniform spacing is d = 26, leading to the block structure.\n\nAlternatively, suppose that all letters have spacing d = 13. Then each letter's three occurrences are 13 apart. Then the total length required for a single letter would be 1 + 2*13 = 27 positions, but 