Wait, no:\n\nWait, C1 has a term [b ∈ [a -25, a -1]] and C2 also has [b ∈ [a -25, a -1]], so they cancel out.\n\nWait, hold on:\n\nC1 = [b ∈ [a +1,26]] + [b ∈ [a -25, a -1]]\n\nC2 = [b ∈ [a +1, a +25]] + [b ∈ [a -25, a -1]]\n\nTherefore,\n\nC1 - C2 = [b ∈ [a +1,26]] - [b ∈ [a +1, a +25]] + [b ∈ [a -25, a -1]] - [b ∈ [a -25, a -1]] = 0 +0 =0.\n\nWait, so this suggests that C1 = C2 for all a and b?\n\nBut that can't be right because in the case when a=26, let's test it.\n\nLet me test a=26:\n\nFirst interval [27, 51].\n\nSecond interval [53, 77].\n\nWait, let's do it properly.\n\nFor a=26:\n\nFirst interval is [26 +1, 26 +25] = [27, 51].\n\nSecond interval is [26 +27, 26 +51] = [53, 77].\n\nNow, x₂'s positions:\n\n- b ∈ [1,26].\n\n- b +26 ∈ [27,52].\n\n- b +52 ∈ [53,78].\n\nC1: positions in [27,51].\n\nWhich can include:\n\n- b +26 if b +26 ∈ [27,51]. Since b +26 ranges from 27 to 52, so b +26 ∈ [27,51] ⇒ b ∈ [1,25].\n\n- b itself is in [1,26], so if b ∈ [27,51], but b ≤26, so no.\n\nThus,\n\nC1 = number of b ∈ [1,25].\n\nBecause for b from 1 to25, b +26 is in [27,51], so each such b contributes 1. For b=26:\n\n- b=26 is not in [27,51].\n\n- b +26=52, which is outside [27,51].\n\nThus, C1=25 (for b=1 to25) and 0 for b=26.\n\nC2: positions in [53,77].\n\nWhich can include:\n\n- b +52 if b +52 ∈ [53,77] ⇒ b ∈ [1,25] (since b +52 from 53 to 78, so b from1 to26, but [53,77] ⇒ b +52 ≤77 ⇒ b ≤25.\n\n- b itself is in [1,26], so can't be in [53,77].\n\nThus,\n\nC2 counts:\n\nFor b=1 to25: b +52 ∈ [53,77], so contributes 1 each.\n\nFor b=26: b +52=78, which is in [53,77]? 78 is outside, so no.\n\nThus, C2=25 (for b=1 to25) and 0 for b=26.\n\nTherefore, C1 and C2 are both 25 when b≠26 and 0 when b=26. Wait, but when b=26:\n\nC1: b=26 not in [27,51], and b +26=52 not in [27,51], so C1=0.\n\nC2: b=26 ⇒ b +52=78 not in [53,77], so C2=0.\n\nThus, for all b, C1=C2 when a=26.\n\nTherefore, in this case also, it holds.\n\nTherefore, by this general analysis, for any a and b, C1 = C2.\