us, so you can have multiple positions with the same value with positive probability, hence the expectation is less than N.\n\nTherefore, in the continuous case, the expected number of distinct values after t steps is N, because all positions have distinct values with probability 1? But that seems counterintuitive.\n\nWait, actually, in continuous space, for any finite set of continuous random variables, the probability that any two are equal is zero, so the probability that all are distinct is 1, hence the expected number of distinct values is N. But in our case, the values are not independent, because each value depends on its neighbors.\n\nWait, even though the original variables are independent, the new variables at each step are dependent because they are functions of the previous variables.\n\nHowever, even with dependencies, in the continuous case, the probability that any two positions have the same value after t steps is still zero, because the process is continuous. For example, in the N=4 case, after one step, the new values are maxima of triples, which are continuous functions of the original variables. Since the original variables are continuous and independent, the new variables are also continuous and the probability that any two are equal is zero. Therefore, the expected number of distinct values after any finite t should be N, because all values are almost surely distinct.\n\nBut this contradicts our earlier discrete examples where in N=4, after one step, you could have two distinct values with positive probability. But in the continuous case, those examples would have measure zero.\n\nWait a minute! The problem states that the initial numbers are uniformly and independently sampled from [0,1]. So it is a continuous setting. Therefore, in the continuous case, the probability that any two positions ever have the same value after any finite number of steps is zero. Therefore, the number of distinct values after any finite time is almost surely N, since all values are distinct with probability 1.\n\nBut that can't be right, because in the N=3 case, after one step, all values become the global maximum, so they are all equal. Wait, but in the continuous case, the probability that all three original numbers are equal is zero, so after one step, the three new values are all equal to the global maximum, which is unique almost surely. Wait, but the global maximum is a single value, so all three new values become equal to it, hence they are all equal. Therefore, in the continuous case, after one step for N=3, the three values are all equal, hence the number of distinct values is 1, which has probability 1. Similarly, for N=4, after one step, the four values are the maxima of their respective triplets. Since the original four numbers are continuous and independent, the probability that any two of these maxima are equal is zero, except perhaps in cases where the windows overlap in a way that forces equality?\n\nWait, no, let's think carefully.\n\nIn the continuous case, for N=3:\n\nAfter one step, all three values become the global maximum of the initial three. Since the initial three are independent uniform [0,1], the global maximum is a single value, which is unique with probability 1 (since the probability that two are equal is zero). Therefore, all three new values are equal to this global maximum, so after one step, all three are equal. Therefore, the number of distinct values is 1 with probability 1. So in the continuous case, for N=3, after one step, the number of distinct values is 1.\n\nSimilarly, for N=4:\n\nAfter one step, each value is the maximum of a triplet of the original four. The four maxima are functions of the original four variables. Since the original four are independent and continuous, the four maxima are also continuous variables. The probability that any two of these maxima are equal is zero. Therefore, the four maxima are all distinct with probability 1? Wait, but they might not be all distinct,