is at least as large as the original a_i. So, each value is non-decreasing over time. Since they are being replaced by a value that is at least as big as themselves.\n\nTherefore, for each individual position, the value at that position is non-decreasing over time. Therefore, once a value reaches a certain level, it can't go back down. So the values can only increase or stay the same over time. Therefore, in the three-element case, after the first step, all become the global maximum of the initial three, so they all jump to the same high value, and then stay there.\n\nBut in the case of more elements, like 2025, maybe the behavior is different? Let me try a small case with four elements, arranged in a circle. Let me consider four elements: a, b, c, d in a circle.\n\nAfter one step, each element is replaced by the max of itself and its two neighbors.\n\nSo, the new first element is max(a, b, d), because neighbors of a are b and d (since it's a circle: a is between d and b). Wait, actually, in a circle of four elements: positions 1, 2, 3, 4. Then for position 1, neighbors are position 4 and position 2. So, the new a₁ = max(a₄, a₁, a₂). Similarly, new a₂ = max(a₁, a₂, a₃), new a₃ = max(a₂, a₃, a₄), new a₄ = max(a₃, a₄, a₁).\n\nHmm, so in this case, each element is being replaced by the maximum of itself and its immediate left and right neighbors. Let's see if there's a similar global maximum thing here.\n\nSuppose we have four elements. Let me denote the initial values as a, b, c, d.\n\nAfter one step, each becomes:\n\nb₁ = max(a, b, d)\n\nb₂ = max(b, c, a)\n\nWait, hold on, hold on, for position 2, its neighbors are position 1 and position 3, so it's max(a, b, c). Wait, no: Wait, position 2's left neighbor is position 1 (which is a), and its right neighbor is position 3 (which is c). Therefore, new a₂ = max(a, b, c). Similarly, position 3's left neighbor is position 2 (b), and right neighbor is position 4 (d), so new a₃ = max(b, c, d). Position 4's left neighbor is position 3 (c), and right neighbor is position 1 (a), so new a₄ = max(c, d, a). Position 1's left neighbor is position 4 (d) and right neighbor is position 2 (b), so new a₁ = max(d, a, b).\n\nHmm, so in this case, each new value is the maximum of three consecutive elements in the original circle? Wait, no, actually, for each position i, the new value is the max of the element to the left, the element itself, and the element to the right. So in the four-element case, the new value at position i is the max of a_{i-1}, a_i, a_{i+1}. So, for each position, the new value is the max of three consecutive elements in the original configuration.\n\nWait, but in the four-element case, for instance, the new a₁ is max(a₄, a₁, a₂), which is the max of the last element, the first element, and the second element. Similarly, the new a₂ is max(a₁, a₂, a₃), which is the max of the first, second, and third elements, and so on.\n\nSo in this four-element case, after one step, each element becomes the maximum of three consecutive elements from the original circle. Then, after that, the next step would be replacing each element with the maximum of its new neighbors and itself, which are themselves maxima of three consecutive elements.\n\nHmm, this is getting a bit complicated. Let me think of an example with actual numbers.\n\nSuppose we have four numbers: let’s say a₁ = 0.1, a₂ = 0.3, a₃ = 0.2, a₄ = 0.4.\n\nThen, after the first step:\n\na₁_new = max(a₄, a₁, a₂) = max(0.4, 0.1, 0.3) = 0.4\n\na₂_new = max(a₁, a₂, a₃) = max(0.1, 0.3, 0.2) = 0.3\n\na₃_new = max(a₂, a₃, a₄) = max(0.3, 0.2, 0.4) = 0.4\n\na₄_new = max(a₃, a₄, a₁) = max(0.2, 0.4, 0.1) = 0.4\n\nSo after one step, the new configuration is [0.4, 0.3, 0.4, 0.4].\n\nNow, let's do the next step (second step):\n\na₁_new2 = max(a₄_new, a₁_new, a₂_new) = max(0.4, 0.4, 0.3) = 0.4\n\na₂_new2 = max(a₁_new, a₂_new, a₃_new) = max(0.4, 0.3, 0.4) = 0.4\n\na₃_new2 = max(a₂_new, a₃_new, a₄_new) = max(0.3, 0.4, 0.4) = 0.4\n\na₄_new2 = max(a₃_new, a₄_new, a₁_new) = max(0.4, 0.4, 0.4) = 0.4\n\nSo after the second step, all elements become 0.4, so from the second step onward, all elements stay at 0.4. So in this case, after two steps, all elements are equal to the global maximum of the initial configuration? Wait, the global maximum of the initial configuration was 0.4 (a₄), so indeed, after the first step, some elements became 0.4, and then in the second step, they all became 0.4. So similar to the three-element case, after two steps, all elements are equal to the global maximum of the initial configuration.\n\nWait, but in the first step, not all elements became the global maximum. In the example above, a₁ became 0.4 (which was the global max), a₂ became 0.3, a₃ became 0.4, and a₄ became 0.4. So only three of the four elements became the global max in the first step, and then in the second step, all of them did. Interesting.\n\nHmm, so in the three-element case, after one step, all elements become the global maximum. In the four-element case, after two steps, all elements become the global maximum. Maybe in general, for n elements arranged in a circle, after k steps, all elements become the global maximum? But that seems too fast?\n\nWait, let me check with another four-element example where the global maximum isn't in the same place.\n\nSuppose four elements: a₁=0.5, a₂=0.1, a₃=0.2, a₄=0.6.\n\nGlobal maximum is 0.6 (a₄).\n\nFirst step:\n\na₁_new = max(a₄, a₁, a₂) = max(0.6, 0.5, 0.1) = 0.6\n\na₂_new = max(a₁, a₂, a₃) = max(0.5, 0.1, 0.2) = 0.5\n\na₃_new = max(a₂, a₃, a₄) = max(0.1, 0.2, 0.6) = 0.6\n\na₄_new = max(a₃, a₄, a₁) = max(0.2, 0.6, 0.5) = 0.6\n\nSo after first step: [0.6, 0.5, 0.6, 0.6]\n\nSecond step:\n\na₁_new2 = max(a₄_new, a₁_new, a₂_new) = max(0.6, 0.6, 0.5) = 0.6\n\na₂_new2 = max(a₁_new, a₂_new, a₃_new) = max(0.6, 0.5, 0.6) = 0.6\n\na₃_new2 = max(a₂_new, a₃_new, a₄_new) = max(0.5, 0.6, 0.6) = 0.6\n\na₄_new2 = max(a₃_new, a₄_new, a₁_new) = max(0.6, 0.6, 0.6) = 0.6\n\nSo again, after two steps, all elements are 0.6, the global maximum. So in this case, two steps.\n\nHmm, so maybe in general, for n elements arranged in a circle, after log n steps or something? Wait, but in the three-element case, it took one step, in the four-element case, it took two steps. Hmm, so perhaps it takes n steps? Wait, but in the four-element case, n=4, and it took two steps. Hmm, not n steps.\n\nAlternatively, maybe after k steps, the maximum value propagates around the circle? Let me see.\n\nWait, in the three-element case, after one step, all elements become the global max, so it's fully propagated in one step. In the four-element case, in the first step, the global max is already present at one position, and then in the next step, it spreads to the adjacent positions? Let me see the first example with four elements: [0.1, 0.3, 0.2, 0.4].\n\nAfter the first step, the new values were [0.4, 0.3, 0.4, 0.4]. So the global maximum 0.4 was at position 4 originally, then in the first step, position 1, 3, and 4 became 0.4, but position 2 remained at 0.3. Then in the second step, position 2 also became 0.4 because it was the max of its neighbors (positions 1 and 3, which were 0.4 and 0.4) and itself (0.3). So the 0.4 value spread to position 2 in the second step. So the \"wave\" of the maximum value propagates one position to the left and right each step? Wait, in the first step, the maximum was already at position 4, then in the first step, positions 1, 3, and 4 became 0.4. Then in the second step, position 2 also became 0.4 because its neighbors were 0.4 and 0.4 (positions 1 and 3). So maybe the propagation is such that in each step, the maximum value can spread to adjacent positions?\n\nWait, in the first step, the original maximum was at position 4. Then, in the first step, position 1 (which is adjacent to position 4) became 0.4 because it was the max of position 4 (0.4), itself (0.1), and position 2 (0.3). So that's because position 1 was adjacent to the global max. Then position 3 became 0.4 because it was the max of position 2 (0.3), itself (0.2), and position 4 (0.4). So that's because position 3 was adjacent to the global max. Then position 4 itself became 0.4 because it's the max of itself (0.4), position 3 (0.2), and position 1 (0.1). Wait, actually, position 4 was already the global max, so its new value is still 0.4.\n\nSo after the first step, all positions adjacent to the original global max became 0.4 as well, and the global max position itself stayed at 0.4. Then, in the next step, the positions adjacent to those newly updated positions (positions 2 and 4) would also become 0.4? Wait, position 2 was adjacent to position 1 (now 0.4) and position 3 (now 0.4). So position 2's new value is the max of its neighbors (positions 1 and 3, both 0.4) and itself (0.3). So it becomes 0.4. Similarly, position 4's neighbors are position 3 (0.4) and position 1 (0.4), so position 4 remains 0.4. So in the second step, position 2 is also updated to 0.4. Then, in the third step, would position 3's other neighbor (position 4) already is 0.4, and position 2 is now 0.4, so position 3's neighbors are 0.4 and 0.4, so it stays at 0.4. Similarly, position 1's neighbors are position 4 (0.4) and position 2 (0.4), so it stays at 0.4. So indeed, after two steps, all positions are 0.4.\n\nTherefore, in this case, the time taken to propagate the maximum value around the entire circle is two steps for n=4. Since the circle has four positions, and the maximum can spread one step per side each second? Hmm, so in the first step, the original maximum and its immediate neighbors become the new maximum, and then in the next step, the neighbors of those neighbors become the new maximum, etc. So in general, for a circle of n elements, how many steps does it take for the maximum value to propagate to all positions?\n\nIt seems like in each step, the \"frontier\" of the maximum value expands by one position in each direction. So starting from the original position of the global maximum, after k steps, the maximum has spread to a block of size 2k + 1? Wait, but in the four-element example, starting at position 4, after one step, positions 1, 3, 4 are 0.4 (so a block of three positions). Then after two steps, all four positions are 0.4. Hmm, so in that case, starting from one position, after one step, you cover three positions, then after two steps, you cover all four. Wait, that seems inconsistent with the idea of expanding by one each step. Maybe because it's a circle?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the maximum spreads in both directions simultaneously each step. Since in each step, each position can be influenced by its neighbors, so if a position has a neighbor that is already the maximum, then that position will become the maximum in the next step.\n\nWait, let me formalize this a bit. Suppose that at some step t, the set of positions that have the global maximum value is some set S_t. Then, in the next step, t+1, a position i will have value max(a_{i-1}, a_i, a_{i+1}). If at least one of its neighbors (i-1 or i+1) is in S_t (i.e., has the global maximum value), then the maximum of the three will be the global maximum, so position i will be updated to the global maximum in the next step. Conversely, if neither neighbor is in S_t, then position i's value might not be the global maximum.\n\nWait, but actually, even if neither neighbor is in S_t, if position i itself was already the global maximum, then it stays. Otherwise, if position i was not the global maximum, but its neighbors are not either, then its new value could be lower or higher? Wait, but actually, since all values are non-decreasing over time, if position i was not the global maximum at step t, but its neighbors are not either, then its value at step t+1 is the max of its previous value and its two neighbors' previous values. Since none of the neighbors were the global maximum at step t, but they could have been lower, so the new value at position i could be higher than before but not necessarily the global maximum.\n\nHmm, so perhaps the set S_t where the global maximum is located can only grow over time? Because once a position becomes the global maximum, it stays that way forever. And the set S_t can only expand to include positions adjacent to S_{t-1}? Because only those positions adjacent to S_{t-1} can become the global maximum in the next step, since their neighbors in S_{t-1} would influence them.\n\nWait, let me think. Suppose that at step t, the set S_t is the set of positions that have the global maximum. Then, at step t+1, a position i will be in S_{t+1} if and only if either:\n\n1. It was already in S_t (so it stays as the global maximum), or\n\n2. At least one of its neighbors was in S_t at step t, so that the max(a_{i-1}, a_i, a_{i+1}) includes the global maximum from a neighbor, so the new value becomes the global maximum.\n\nTherefore, the set S_{t+1} is exactly the union of S_t and the neighbors of S_t. Because any position adjacent to S_t will have at least one neighbor in S_t, so their new value will be the global maximum, and positions not adjacent to S_t will not have any neighbors in S_t, so their new value is the max of their previous value and their two neighbors, which were not in S_t. But their previous value might not have been the global maximum, and their neighbors might not have been either, so their new value might still not be the global maximum.\n\nTherefore, the set S_t grows by including the neighbors of S_{t-1} at each step. So starting from the initial position(s) of the global maximum, each step the \"frontier\" of the maximum expands outward by one step in each direction along the circle.\n\nTherefore, the time it takes for the entire circle to be covered by S_t is equal to the time it takes for the frontier to reach all positions. Since the circle has n positions, and each step the frontier can expand by one position on each side, the number of steps needed for the entire circle to be covered would be the ceiling of n/2? Wait, let me think.\n\nWait, actually, since it's a circle, the maximum can expand in both directions simultaneously. Let me consider an unbroken circle. Suppose the initial global maximum is at a single position. Then, at each step, the set S_t will expand to include the immediate neighbors of the current S_{t-1}. So starting with one position, after one step, it includes that position and its two neighbors (total of three positions). After two steps, it includes those three plus the neighbors of those three, which would be the next two positions on either side, but since it's a circle, this might wrap around. Wait, actually, let me think in terms of distance.\n\nSuppose we have a circle of n positions, and the initial global maximum is at position 0. Let's number the positions 0 to n-1 for simplicity. Let’s define the distance from position 0 as the minimal number of steps needed to reach it along the circle in either direction. The frontier of the maximum will spread one step outward each time in both directions.\n\nWait, actually, in each step, the set S_t will include all positions within distance t from the initial position(s). Since in each step, the frontier moves one step outward in both directions. Therefore, the number of steps required for the entire circle to be covered is the maximum distance from the initial position to any other position, divided by 2 (since it expands in both directions). Since it's a circle, the maximum distance from any single point is floor(n/2). Therefore, to cover the entire circle, you need floor(n/2) steps? Wait, let me check with the examples.\n\nIn the three-element circle: n=3. The maximum distance from the initial position is 1 (since in a circle of three, the maximum distance between two points is 1 step in one direction or the other). So floor(3/2)=1. So that matches: after 1 step, the entire circle is covered.\n\nIn the four-element circle: n=4. The maximum distance from the initial position (say position 0) is 2 (going the other way around). Wait, but actually, in a circle of four, the distance from position 0 to position 2 is 2 steps in one direction or 2 steps in the other? Wait, no. On a circle of four, the distance from 0 to 1 is 1, 0 to 2 is 2 (if going the long way around, but actually minimal distance is min(2, 2) = 2. Wait, actually, the minimal distance between 0 and 2 on a 4-node circle is 2 steps in either direction? Wait, no: from 0 to 1 to 2 is two steps clockwise, or from 0 to 3 to 2 is two steps counter-clockwise. So the minimal distance is 2. So the maximum minimal distance from any point to the initial position is 2. Then floor(n/2)=2, so that would take 2 steps, which matches the four-element example above where after two steps, everything was covered.\n\nSimilarly, for n=5: maximum distance from any point to the initial position is 2 (since floor(5/2)=2). So after 2 steps, the entire circle would be covered? Let's test with n=5.\n\nSuppose n=5, with initial maximum at position 0. Let's say the initial values are [M, 0, 0, 0, 0], where M is the global maximum. Then:\n\nStep 1:\n\nEach position i becomes the max of its neighbors and itself.\n\nPosition 0: max(a4, a0, a1) = max(0, M, 0) = M\n\nPosition 1: max(a0, a1, a2) = max(M, 0, 0) = M\n\nPosition 2: max(a1, a2, a3) = max(0, 0, 0) = 0\n\nPosition 3: max(a2, a3, a4) = max(0, 0, 0) = 0\n\nPosition 4: max(a3, a4, a0) = max(0, 0, M) = M\n\nSo after step 1, the configuration is [M, M, 0, 0, M].\n\nStep 2:\n\nPosition 0: max(a4, a0, a1) = max(M, M, M) = M\n\nPosition 1: max(a0, a1, a2) = max(M, M, 0) = M\n\nPosition 2: max(a1, a2, a3) = max(M, 0, 0) = M\n\nPosition 3: max(a2, a3, a4) = max(0, 0, M) = M\n\nPosition 4: max(a3, a4, a0) = max(0, M, M) = M\n\nSo after step 2, all positions are M. So indeed, after two steps (which is floor(5/2)=2), the entire circle is covered.\n\nSo that seems to hold. So in general, for a circle of n elements, starting with a single global maximum at some position, the time required for all elements to become the global maximum is floor(n/2). Since floor(n/2) is the maximum minimal distance from the initial position to any other position.\n\nWait, actually, the maximum distance is floor(n/2) if n is even? Wait, for even n, the maximum distance from a point is n/2. For odd n, it's (n-1)/2. So in both cases, floor(n/2). So the number of steps required to cover the entire circle is floor(n/2). Therefore, in general, for n elements arranged in a circle, if you start with a single global maximum, then after floor(n/2) steps, all elements become the global maximum, and remain so forever after.\n\nBut in our problem, the initial configuration is random. The initial configuration has each element independently uniform on [0,1]. Therefore, the initial global maximum is a random variable, and the positions of the global maximum are not necessarily all at one location. Wait, actually, in the initial configuration, there could be multiple elements with the global maximum value. For example, two or more elements might have the same maximum value. However, since the values are continuous and independent, the probability that any two elements are exactly equal is zero. Therefore, almost surely, the initial configuration has a unique global maximum at a single position.\n\nTherefore, almost surely, the initial configuration has a single position with the maximum value, and all others are strictly less. Therefore, the process will be such that the set S_t, the set of positions holding the global maximum, starts as a singleton and grows as described above, expanding by one position on each side per step, until after floor(n/2) steps, it covers the entire circle.\n\nTherefore, after floor(n/2) steps, all positions will have the global maximum value, and remain so thereafter.\n\nTherefore, in our problem, n = 2025, so floor(n/2) = floor(2025/2) = floor(1012.5) = 1012. Therefore, after 1012 steps, all elements become the global maximum, and stay there forever. Since we are only performing 100 seconds (steps), which is much less than 1012, the number of distinct values remaining after 100 steps is not yet 1. Therefore, we need to compute the expected number of distinct values after 100 steps.\n\nHmm, okay. So perhaps instead of waiting until all elements become the global maximum, after 100 steps, the set S_t of positions that have the global maximum will have expanded outward from the initial position by 100 steps in each direction? Since each step expands the frontier by one position in each direction. Since the circle has 2025 positions, the distance from the initial position to the furthest point is floor(2025/2) = 1012. So after 100 steps, the set S_t would have covered a contiguous block of positions around the initial position, spanning 2*100 + 1 = 201 positions? Wait, let me think again.\n\nWait, starting from the initial position, at each step, the set S_t expands by one position on each side. So after t steps, the set S_t includes all positions within distance t from the initial position. Since it's a circle, the number of positions covered after t steps is 2*t + 1, but only if t is less than or equal to floor(n/2). If t exceeds floor(n/2), then it wraps around and covers the entire circle.\n\nWait, actually, the number of positions covered after t steps is min(2*t + 1, n). Because each step adds two positions (one on each side). However, since it's a circle, once you reach the end, you start wrapping around. Wait, actually, no, because in a circle, the expansion is symmetric from the initial position. So starting at position 0, after t steps, the covered positions are from position -t to +t modulo n, which would be a total of 2*t + 1 positions, but if 2*t + 1 exceeds n, then it wraps around and covers the entire circle.\n\nBut in our case, t = 100, and n = 2025. Then 2*100 + 1 = 201, which is much less than 2025, so the covered set is a contiguous block of 201 positions centered at the initial position. Wait, but actually, in a circle, the expansion would be symmetric in both directions, so the covered positions form a contiguous arc of length 2*t + 1, centered at the initial position.\n\nTherefore, after t steps, the set S_t consists of all positions within t steps clockwise or counter-clockwise from the initial maximum position. So the number of distinct values would depend on how many distinct values are present in the remaining parts of the circle not yet covered by S_t.\n\nBut actually, the values in S_t are all equal to the global maximum, right? Because once a position is in S_t, it has been updated to the global maximum and stays there. Whereas the positions not in S_t have been updated in some way, but their values might not yet be the global maximum.\n\nWait, but actually, the values in the non-S_t positions might have increased over time, but they are not necessarily equal to each other or to the global maximum. Therefore, the number of distinct values could be the number of distinct values among the non-S_t positions plus 1 (for the global maximum). But actually, the S_t positions all have the global maximum, so they contribute only one distinct value, and the non-S_t positions might have various values, which could be distinct among themselves or not.\n\nTherefore, the total number of distinct values after t steps is equal to 1 (for the global maximum) plus the number of distinct values among the non-S_t positions.\n\nBut this seems complicated because the non-S_t positions might have various values, some of which could be equal or not. To compute the expectation, perhaps we can model the expected number of distinct values in the non-S_t region?\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a smarter way. Let me think.\n\nFirst, note that all values are non-decreasing over time, so once a value increases, it can't decrease. Therefore, the final configuration after t steps is such that each element is at least as large as it was initially, and also, the global maximum has been \"propagated\" outward from its initial position to a certain extent.\n\nMoreover, the key observation is that the values in the covered region S_t are all equal to the global maximum, and the values in the uncovered region are each less than or equal to the global maximum, but their exact values depend on their neighbors.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think of the process as, after t steps, the system has \"filled in\" the values around the initial maximum up to distance t, and the rest are still influenced by their neighbors but haven't reached the maximum yet.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the expected number of distinct values can be approximated by considering that each position not in S_t has a certain probability of being equal to some other position's value, but this seems too vague.\n\nWait, perhaps another approach: Since all the values are non-decreasing, and the global maximum is eventually achieved everywhere after floor(n/2) steps, but we are only doing 100 steps, which is much smaller than 1012, so only a small portion of the circle has been \"infected\" with the global maximum. The rest of the circle still has their original values or values that have been updated but not yet reached the maximum.\n\nHowever, even the updated values in the non-S_t region are at least as large as their original values, but not necessarily equal to the global maximum. Moreover, their exact values depend on their neighbors in both the covered and uncovered regions.\n\nThis seems quite complex. Maybe we can model the expected number of distinct values by considering that in the covered region, all values are equal to the global maximum, so that contributes 1 to the count of distinct values. Then, in the uncovered region, each position has a value that is the maximum of its neighbors (which may or may not be in the covered region). Since the covered region has all values equal to the global maximum, any position adjacent to the covered region will have at least one neighbor equal to the global maximum, so their value will be the global maximum in the next step. Wait, but we are only at step t = 100, so the covered region is up to distance 100 from the initial position.\n\nWait, let me try to formalize this.\n\nLet me denote the initial position of the global maximum as position 0 (without loss of generality, due to rotational symmetry). Then, after t steps, the covered region S_t consists of all positions from -t to +t modulo n (i.e., a contiguous arc of length 2t + 1 centered at position 0). The remaining positions are the ones not in this arc, which form two arcs on either side of the covered region, each of length (n - (2t + 1))/2, assuming n is odd. Since n = 2025 is odd, then n - (2t + 1) is even, so each of the two uncovered arcs has length (2025 - (2*100 + 1))/2 = (2025 - 201)/2 = (1824)/2 = 912 positions.\n\nTherefore, after t = 100 steps, the covered region has 201 positions (from -100 to +100 around position 0), and the two uncovered regions each have 912 positions.\n\nNow, in the covered region S_t, all positions have the global maximum value, say M. In the uncovered regions, the values have been updated according to the process but are not yet M.\n\nNow, to compute the expected number of distinct values, we can note that:\n\n1. The global maximum M is present in the covered region, so that's one distinct value.\n\n2. The values in the uncovered regions may include other values, which could be distinct from each other and from M.\n\nHowever, the key point is that in the uncovered regions, the values are non-decreasing over time, but they are also constrained by their neighbors. Importantly, the boundary between the covered and uncovered regions (the positions at distance t = 100 from the initial position) will have neighbors in the covered region (which are M) and neighbors in the uncovered region. Therefore, the values at the boundary positions (distance exactly t from the center) will be the maximum of their neighbors, one of which is M and the other is in the uncovered region.\n\nTherefore, the boundary positions will have value M, because one of their neighbors is M. Wait a second! Hold on. Let me think.\n\nSuppose we are at the boundary of the covered region. Let's take a position at distance t from the center (position 0). Let's say position +t (in the clockwise direction). Its neighbors are position +t - 1 (which is in the covered region, since it's within distance t - 1 from the center) and position +t + 1 (which is in the uncovered region, since it's distance t + 1 from the center, which is beyond the coverage). Wait, actually, in a circle, the position at +t has neighbors at +t -1 and +t +1. The position +t -1 is in the covered region (since it's within t -1 of the center), and position +t +1 is in the uncovered region (distance t +1 from the center). Therefore, the value at position +t will be the maximum of its left neighbor (which is in the covered region and thus is M), its own previous value, and its right neighbor (which is in the uncovered region and thus has some value less than M). Therefore, the new value at position +t is the maximum of M, its previous value, and its right neighbor's previous value. Since M is the global maximum, the maximum of those three will be M. Therefore, actually, the boundary positions also become M in the next step?\n\nWait, but we are at step t, and the coverage is up to t steps. Wait, perhaps my earlier reasoning was flawed. Let me re-examine.\n\nWait, actually, in the process, at each step, the covered region expands by one in each direction. Therefore, at step t, the covered region is positions within distance t from the center. Therefore, the positions at distance exactly t from the center (the boundary) will have one neighbor in the covered region (distance t -1) and one neighbor outside (distance t +1). However, when we compute the new value for the boundary position at distance t, it will be the max of its left neighbor (which is in the covered region, so M), itself, and its right neighbor (which is outside, so not yet M). Therefore, the new value at the boundary position will be the maximum of M, its current value, and its right neighbor's current value. Since M is the global maximum, this new value will be M. Therefore, actually, the boundary positions also become M in the next step? Wait, but that would imply that the covered region actually expands by one each step, which it does.\n\nWait, perhaps my confusion arises from the fact that in step t, the covered region is defined as the set of positions that have been updated to M by step t. Therefore, when we compute step t+1, the boundary positions (distance t from center) will indeed get updated to M because their left neighbor is already M. Therefore, the covered region does indeed expand by one on each side each step.\n\nTherefore, at step t, the covered region is exactly the set of positions within distance t from the center. Therefore, at step t=100, the covered region is positions within distance 100 from the center (position 0), which is an arc of length 2*100 +1 = 201 positions. The remaining positions are those beyond 100 steps from the center in either direction.\n\nHowever, the key point is that in the uncovered regions (positions beyond distance 100 from the center), their values are not yet M, but they are influenced by their neighbors. Importantly, the values in the uncovered regions can only increase over time, but they are constrained by their neighbors.\n\nNow, to compute the expected number of distinct values after 100 steps, we can think of it as 1 (for the global maximum M) plus the expected number of distinct values in the uncovered regions.\n\nHowever, the uncovered regions are two separate arcs, each of length 912 positions (since total n=2025, covered is 201, so uncovered is 2025 - 201 = 1824, split into two arcs of 912 each). Each of these arcs is contiguous and separated from the covered region by a \"buffer\" of 100 positions on each side.\n\nNow, the values in the uncovered regions are non-decreasing over time, and they are each the maximum of their immediate neighbors and themselves. However, since their neighbors may be in the covered region (which are M) or in the other uncovered region, their values are influenced by the M values on one side and their own neighbors on the other.\n\nWait, let me consider a single position in the uncovered region. Let's take a position in the rightmost uncovered arc (the one that's clockwise from the covered region). Its left neighbor is a position at distance 100 from the center (which is in the covered region, so value M), and its right neighbor is another position in the uncovered arc (distance 101 from the center, so further out). Therefore, the value at this position at step t=100 is the maximum of its left neighbor (M), its own previous value, and its right neighbor's previous value. Since the left neighbor is M, the new value at this position will be the maximum of M, its previous value, and its right neighbor's previous value. But since M is the global maximum, this new value will be M. Wait, but that would imply that even positions just outside the covered region become M in the next step? But we are at step 100, so if the covered region is up to distance 100, then the positions at distance 101 would not have been covered yet.\n\nWait, no, confusion arises between the definition of the covered region and the step count.\n\nWait, let's clarify:\n\nAt step t=100, the covered region S_{100} consists of all positions within distance 100 from the initial maximum position. The positions at distance exactly 100 from the center are included in S_{100}. Therefore, their left neighbors are at distance 99 (covered) and their right neighbors are at distance 101 (uncovered). Therefore, when computing the value at a position at distance 100 (on the boundary), its left neighbor is M (since it's in S_{100}), its own value is whatever it was before step 100, and its right neighbor is in the uncovered region (distance 101). Therefore, the value at the boundary position at step 100 is the max of M, its own previous value, and its right neighbor's previous value. Since M is the global maximum, this max will be M, so actually, the boundary positions also become M at step 100? Wait, but that would mean that the covered region actually expands beyond distance 100 at step 100? That can't be.\n\nWait, no, perhaps my misunderstanding is in how the covered region is defined. The covered region S_t is the set of positions that have been set to M by step t. So, for a position at distance 100 from the center, during step t=100, its value is updated to the max of its left neighbor (which is in S_{99}, so already M), itself, and its right neighbor (which is not in S_{99}, so not yet M). Therefore, the new value at the boundary position (distance 100) will be the max of M, its own previous value, and its right neighbor's previous value. Since M is the global maximum, this new value is M, so indeed, the position at distance 100 becomes M at step 100. Therefore, the covered region S_{100} actually includes all positions up to distance 100, because the boundary positions at distance 100 become M at step 100.\n\nTherefore, the covered region after step 100 is indeed all positions within distance 100 from the center, including the boundary positions. Thus, the uncovered regions are positions beyond distance 100, i.e., at distance 101 and beyond.\n\nTherefore, the values in the uncovered regions (distance 101 and beyond) are still less than M, but they have been updated in the process.\n\nNow, for a position in the uncovered region (distance >100), its value at step 100 is the maximum of its left neighbor, its own value from step 99, and its right neighbor's value from step 99. Since its left neighbor is at distance (current distance -1), which for the first layer of uncovered regions (distance 101) has a left neighbor at distance 100 (which is now M), so the left neighbor is M. Therefore, the value at distance 101 will be the max of M, its own value from step 99, and its right neighbor's value from step 99. Since M is the global maximum, this new value will be M. Wait, hold on! That would mean that even the positions at distance 101 become M at step 100?\n\nWait, let me think carefully:\n\nSuppose we have a position at distance 101 from the center. Let's call this position X. Its left neighbor is at distance 100 (which is now M, as part of S_{100}), and its right neighbor is at distance 102 (which is further away, still uncovered). The value of position X at step 100 is computed as the max of:\n\n- Left neighbor: M (since distance 100 is covered),\n\n- Its own value from step 99,\n\n- Right neighbor: whatever value it had at step 99 (which is less than M, since it wasn't covered yet).\n\nTherefore, the new value at position X (distance 101) will be the maximum of M, its previous value, and its right neighbor's previous value. Since M is the global maximum, this new value is M. Therefore, position X becomes M at step 100.\n\nWait a second! Then that would mean that the covered region actually expands to distance 101 at step 100? But that contradicts my earlier reasoning.\n\nThis is confusing. Let me take a concrete example with a small n.\n\nLet’s take n = 5 again, with the initial maximum at position 0.\n\nAt step t=2, which is floor(n/2)=2, so after 2 steps, everything should be M.\n\nLet’s see:\n\nInitial: [M, 0, 0, 0, 0]\n\nAfter step 1:\n\nPosition 0: max(a4, a0, a1) = max(0, M, 0) = M\n\nPosition 1: max(a0, a1, a2) = max(M, 0, 0) = M\n\nPosition 2: max(a1, a2, a3) = max(0, 0, 0) = 0\n\nPosition 3: max(a2, a3, a4) = max(0, 0, 0) = 0\n\nPosition 4: max(a3, a4, a0) = max(0, 0, M) = M\n\nSo after step 1: [M, M, 0, 0, M]\n\nNow step 2:\n\nPosition 0: max(a4, a0, a1) = max(M, M, M) = M\n\nPosition 1: max(a0, a1, a2) = max(M, M, 0) = M\n\nPosition 2: max(a1, a2, a3) = max(M, 0, 0) = M\n\nPosition 3: max(a2, a3, a4) = max(0, 0, M) = M\n\nPosition 4: max(a3, a4, a0) = max(0, M, M) = M\n\nSo after step 2, all are M. So in this case, the positions at distance 2 from the center (position 2) became M in step 2 because their left neighbor (position 1) was already M. Wait, position 2 is distance 2 from the center (position 0). Its left neighbor is position 1 (distance 1), which was M at step 1, so in step 2, position 2's left neighbor is M, so it becomes M. Therefore, the distance from the center doesn't matter for the expansion; rather, the coverage propagates one step further each time because the neighbors of the previously covered positions become covered in the next step.\n\nTherefore, in the n=5 example, at step t=1, the covered region was positions 0,1,4 (distance 0,1, and 4 (which is distance 1 in the other direction)). Then at step t=2, the covered region expanded to include position 2 and 3 as well. So the expansion isn't strictly by distance from the center, but rather by the propagation from the existing covered region.\n\nTherefore, in the general case, after t steps, the covered region is all positions that are within t steps from the initial position along the circle, because each step allows the coverage to spread one step further in both directions.\n\nWait, in the n=5 example, after 1 step, the covered region was positions 0,1,4 (so distance 0, 1, and 1 from the center in both directions). Then after 2 steps, it covered everything. So in terms of distance from the center, after t steps, you can reach positions up to distance t from the center in either direction. Therefore, in n=5, after 2 steps, you can reach distance 2 from the center (position 2), which is the farthest point.\n\nTherefore, in general, after t steps, the covered region includes all positions within distance t from the initial position, where distance is the minimal number of steps along the circle. Therefore, the maximum distance from the initial position to any uncovered position after t steps is t+1? Wait, no.\n\nWait, in the n=5 example, after t=2 steps, the maximum distance from the initial position is 2 (position 2), which is exactly t. Therefore, the covered region after t steps includes all positions within distance t from the initial position. The uncovered positions are those at distance t+1 and beyond. Wait, but in n=5, after t=2 steps, there are no uncovered positions beyond distance 2, since the circle is fully covered.\n\nWait, so in general, after t steps, the covered region is all positions within distance t from the initial position, and the uncovered region consists of positions at distance t+1 and beyond. But since it's a circle, the maximum distance from the initial position is floor(n/2). Therefore, after t steps, if t < floor(n/2), then the uncovered region is the set of positions at distance t+1 to floor(n/2) from the initial position in both directions.\n\nTherefore, returning to the original problem with n=2025 and t=100:\n\nThe covered region after 100 steps includes all positions within distance 100 from the initial maximum position. The uncovered region consists of positions at distance 101 to floor(2025/2)=1012 from the initial position in both directions.\n\nNow, the key point is that in the uncovered regions, the values have not yet reached the global maximum M, but they are being updated each step based on their neighbors.\n\nHowever, for positions in the uncovered regions, their values are non-decreasing, and their updates depend on their neighbors. Importantly, any position adjacent to the covered region (i.e., at distance 100 from the center) will have one neighbor in the covered region (value M) and one neighbor in the uncovered region. Therefore, when updating such a boundary position (distance 100), its new value will be the max of M, its previous value, and its right neighbor's previous value. Since M is the global maximum, this new value will be M. Therefore, actually, the boundary positions at distance 100 become M at step 100, expanding the covered region to distance 100 in both directions.\n\nWait, but this seems to suggest that the covered region does expand by one step each time, so after t steps, the covered region is exactly the set of positions within distance t from the initial position.\n\nTherefore, the uncovered regions are those strictly beyond distance t from the initial position.\n\nTherefore, in our problem with t=100, the covered region is within 100 steps, and the uncovered regions are from 101 to 1012 steps away.\n\nNow, for the values in the uncovered regions: each of these positions is influenced by their neighbors. However, since their left neighbors (for the rightmost uncovered region) or right neighbors (for the leftmost uncovered region) are in the covered region (distance t), which have value M, so the value of a position at distance t+1 will be the maximum of M (from the covered neighbor), its own previous value, and its other neighbor (which is in the uncovered region). Since M is the global maximum, the new value will be M. Wait, that can't be, because that would mean all positions adjacent to the covered region become M in the next step, expanding the covered region further.\n\nWait, this is the same confusion as before. Let me think with the n=5 example again.\n\nAt step t=1, the covered region is positions 0,1,4 (distance 0,1, and 1 from center). The uncovered position is position 2 (distance 2 from center). Then, in step t=2, position 2 is updated based on its neighbors: position 1 (M) and position 3 (0). So its new value is max(M, previous value (0), position 3's previous value (0)) = M. Therefore, position 2 becomes M in step 2, thus expanding the covered region to include it. Therefore, the uncovered region after t=1 was position 2, but after step 2, it becomes covered.\n\nTherefore, in general, the covered region expands by one step each time, so after t steps, the covered region includes all positions within distance t from the initial position. Thus, the uncovered regions are positions strictly beyond distance t.\n\nTherefore, in our problem with t=100, the uncovered regions are positions at distance 101 and beyond from the initial position. However, those positions at distance 101 have a neighbor at distance 100 (covered, value M), so when updating their value at step 100, they would take the max of M, their previous value, and their other neighbor (which is at distance 102). Since M is the global maximum, their new value will be M, thereby expanding the covered region to distance 101 at step 100? Wait, but step 100 is the current step we're considering. Wait, perhaps the confusion is between the step count and the distance.\n\nWait, let's formalize:\n\nLet’s denote that at step k, the covered region is all positions within distance k from the initial position. Therefore, at step k, the covered region expands to include positions at distance k from the initial position. Therefore, the uncovered regions at step k are positions at distance k+1 and beyond.\n\nTherefore, when computing the value of a position at distance k+1 at step k, its left neighbor is at distance k (covered, value M), so its new value will be max(M, its previous value, its right neighbor's previous value). Since M is the global maximum, this new value is M, so it becomes covered at step k+1.\n\nTherefore, the covered region at step k includes all positions within distance k from the initial position, and the uncovered regions are those beyond distance k. Thus, after t=100 steps, the covered region includes positions within 100 steps, and the uncovered regions are those beyond 100 steps.\n\nTherefore, for the purposes of computing the expected number of distinct values at step 100, we can consider:\n\n- The covered region (within 100 steps) has all values equal to M.\n\n- The uncovered regions (beyond 100 steps) have values that are less than M, but have been updated over the steps.\n\nHowever, the values in the uncovered regions are not necessarily all equal to each other or to any other values. Each of these positions has been updated based on their neighbors, which may include some covered positions (which are M) and other uncovered positions.\n\nCrucially, for any position in the uncovered region at distance d > 100, its left neighbor is at distance d-1, and its right neighbor is at distance d+1 (mod n). The value of the position at distance d is the maximum of its left neighbor's value, its own previous value, and its right neighbor's previous value.\n\nSince the left neighbor at distance d-1 may be in the uncovered region or in the covered region. Wait, if d > 100, then d-1 could still be > 100 (if d > 101) or equal to 100 (if d = 101). For d=101, the left neighbor is at distance 100 (covered, value M). For d > 101, the left neighbor is at distance d-1 > 100, so also uncovered.\n\nTherefore, for a position at distance d=101:\n\n- Left neighbor is at distance 100 (M),\n\n- Right neighbor is at distance 102 (>100, uncovered),\n\n- Its own previous value (from step 99) is some value less than M.\n\nTherefore, its value at step 100 is the maximum of M, its previous value, and its right neighbor's previous value. Since M is the global maximum, this becomes M. Wait, so that position at d=101 becomes M at step 100?\n\nBut that would mean that the covered region expands to d=101 at step 100, contradicting the earlier assertion that the covered region at step t is exactly distance t.\n\nAh, here is the confusion: The step count and the distance are tied such that at step k, the covered region includes all positions up to distance k. However, the update rule causes the covered region to expand by one step each time, so after step k, the covered region is distance k. Therefore, when updating the position at distance k+1 at step k, its left neighbor is at distance k (covered, value M), so it becomes M in step k, thereby making it part of the covered region for step k+1.\n\nWait, perhaps the covered region at step k includes all positions that have been updated to M by step k. Therefore, the expansion happens during the step. So during step k, we are updating all positions, and as a result, some new positions (those adjacent to the previously covered region) become M in that step, thereby expanding the covered region for the next step.\n\nTherefore, after step k, the covered region includes all positions within distance k from the initial position. Therefore, when considering the state after step k, the covered region is exactly distance k, and the uncovered regions are beyond that.\n\nTherefore, in the example above with n=5 and k=1:\n\nAfter step 1, covered region is distance 1 (positions 0,1,4). The uncovered region is position 2 (distance 2). Then, during step 2, position 2 is updated to M because its left neighbor (position 1) is M. Thus, after step 2, covered region is distance 2 (all positions), as desired.\n\nTherefore, in general, after step k, the covered region is distance k, and the uncovered regions are beyond that. Therefore, for our problem with k=100, the covered region is distance 100, and the uncovered regions are distances 101 to 1012 (since floor(2025/2)=1012).\n\nTherefore, the values in the uncovered regions (distance 101 to 1012) have not yet reached M. Their values are non-decreasing, and their updates depend on their neighbors. Importantly, positions at distance exactly 101 have a left neighbor at distance 100 (M), so when updating them at step 100, their new value is the maximum of M, their previous value, and their right neighbor's previous value. Since M is the global maximum, this new value will be M. Wait, but that would mean they become M at step 100, contradicting that the covered region is only up to 100.\n\nHold on, this seems like a critical inconsistency. Let me parse this carefully.\n\nAt step 100, we are updating all positions simultaneously. The covered region before the step is distance 99 (since after step 99, the covered region is distance 99). Therefore, at step 100:\n\n- The covered region prior to the step is distance 99.\n\n- Therefore, during step 100, the positions at distance 100 from the initial position will have their left neighbor at distance 99 (covered, value M), and their right neighbor at distance 101 (uncovered, value something less than M).\n\n- The value of a position at distance 100 during step 100 is the max of its left neighbor (M), its own previous value (which was at distance 100, but prior to step 100, it was in the uncovered region, so its previous value was less than M), and its right neighbor (distance 101, previous value < M).\n\nTherefore, the new value for position at distance 100 is max(M, old_value, right_neighbor_old_value) = M.\n\nTherefore, during step 100, all positions at distance 100 become M. Thus, after step 100, the covered region is now distance 100 (since those positions were just updated to M). Therefore, the uncovered regions after step 100 are distances 101 to 1012.\n\nHowever, the positions at distance 101: during step 100, their left neighbor is at distance 100 (now M after the update), and their right neighbor is at distance 102 (uncovered). Their own previous value (before step 100) was some value less than M.\n\nTherefore, when updating the position at distance 101 during step 100, its new value is the max of its left neighbor (now M), its own previous value (before step 100), and its right neighbor's previous value (before step 100). Since the left neighbor is now M, the new value will be M. Therefore, the position at distance 101 also becomes M during step 100.\n\nWait a minute, this suggests that all positions at distance 100 and beyond become M during step 100, which would mean the entire circle becomes M after 100 steps, but that contradicts the earlier logic that it would take floor(n/2) = 1012 steps.\n\nClearly, there's a fundamental misunderstanding here. Let me try to resolve this confusion.\n\nPerhaps the key is that when updating positions simultaneously, the value of a position's neighbor that is being updated in the same step is not the updated value but the old value. Because all updates are done simultaneously based on the previous state.\n\nAh! That's the crucial point I was missing. When updating all positions simultaneously, each position's new value is computed based on the old values of its neighbors, not the updated values. Therefore, even though a position's left neighbor might be updated to M in the same step, the computation for the current position's new value uses the left neighbor's old value, not the new one.\n\nThis is a critical difference. Let me re-express:\n\nSuppose we have two adjacent positions, A and B. At step t, position A is being updated based on its left neighbor (which might be position Z) and its right neighbor (position B). Position B is also being updated based on its left neighbor (A) and its right neighbor (position Y). However, when computing the new value for position A, we use the old value of position B, and when computing the new value for position B, we use the old value of position A. Therefore, the updates are all based on the state at the beginning of the step, and the new values are computed in parallel, then applied simultaneously.\n\nTherefore, in the example with n=5 and step 2:\n\nAt the start of step 2, the configuration is [M, M, 0, 0, M].\n\nWhen computing the new value for position 2 (the uncovered position), its left neighbor is position 1 (which has the old value M), and its right neighbor is position 3 (old value 0). Therefore, the new value for position 2 is max(M, 0, 0) = M. Similarly, position 3's left neighbor is position 2 (old value 0) and its right neighbor is position 4 (old value M). Therefore, position 3's new value is max(0, 0, M) = M. Thus, after step 2, all positions become M.\n\nBut in this case, the position at distance 2 (position 2) was updated based on its left neighbor (position 1) which was already M in the previous step, so it could reach M in the same step.\n\nTherefore, in general, during step t, any position at distance t from the initial position will have a left neighbor at distance t-1 (which was already M in the previous step, since the covered region had expanded to t-1 by step t-1). Therefore, the left neighbor's old value is M, so the new value of the position at distance t will be the max of M, its own previous value, and its right neighbor's previous value. Since M is the global maximum, this new value will be M. Thus, during step t, all positions at distance t from the initial position become M, thereby expanding the covered region to t+1 for the next step? Wait, no.\n\nWait, actually, after step t, the covered region includes all positions that were at distance <= t before the step, plus the new positions at distance t that just became M. Therefore, after step t, the covered region is all positions within distance t from the initial position.\n\nTherefore, the expansion is that at each step t, the covered region expands by one step outward in both directions, and the positions at distance t become M during step t, hence the covered region after step t is distance t.\n\nTherefore, returning to the original problem with n=2025 and t=100:\n\nAfter 100 steps, the covered region includes all positions within distance 100 from the initial position. The positions at distance 100 become M during step 100, and the positions beyond distance 100 (distance 101 to 1012) remain uncovered and have values less than M.\n\nHowever, the values in the uncovered regions (distance >100) are non-decreasing over time and are influenced by their neighbors. Importantly, the leftmost uncovered position (distance 101) has a left neighbor at distance 100 (now M), so when computing its new value during step 100, it uses the left neighbor's old value (which was not M yet, because the left neighbor is at distance 100, which became M during step 100). Wait, no, confusion again.\n\nWait, let's clarify with the n=5 example at step 2:\n\nAt the start of step 2, the configuration is [M, M, 0, 0, M]. The position at distance 2 (position 2) has a left neighbor (position 1) which is M (from the previous step), and its right neighbor (position 3) is 0. Therefore, its new value becomes M. Similarly, position 3 has a left neighbor (position 2) which was 0 (old value) and a right neighbor (position 4) which is M. Therefore, its new value is M. Thus, both positions at distance 2 become M during step 2.\n\nTherefore, during step t, the positions at distance t from the initial position can become M because their left neighbor is at distance t-1 (which was already M in the previous step). Therefore, their new value will be M, since the left neighbor's old value is M.\n\nTherefore, in general, during step t, all positions at distance t from the initial position will become M, because their left neighbor (at distance t-1) is already M in the previous step's configuration. Therefore, their new value is the max of M, their own previous value (which is less than M), and their right neighbor's previous value (which is also less than M). Therefore, they become M.\n\nTherefore, after step t, the covered region is all positions within distance t from the initial position.\n\nTherefore, in our problem, after 100 steps, the covered region is distance 100, and the uncovered regions are distances 101 to 1012. The values in the uncovered regions have not yet reached M, and their values are non-decreasing but still less than M.\n\nNow, to compute the expected number of distinct values after 100 steps, we have:\n\n1. The global maximum M is present in the covered region (201 positions), contributing 1 distinct value.\n\n2. The values in the uncovered regions (the two arcs of length 912 each) can potentially take various values, but we need to find the expected number of distinct values among all these positions.\n\nHowever, the values in the uncovered regions are non-decreasing over time and are constrained by their neighbors. Importantly, the values in the uncovered regions are influenced by the M values on one side (for the first layer of uncovered regions) and by other uncovered regions on the other side.\n\nLet me consider a single position in the uncovered region. Let's focus on one of the two arcs. Due to symmetry, we can consider one arc and double the result if necessary, but since the arcs are separate, their values are independent.\n\nTake a position in the rightmost uncovered arc (distance 101 from the initial position). Its left neighbor is at distance 100 (covered, value M), and its right neighbor is at distance 102 (also uncovered). The value of this position at step 100 is the maximum of:\n\n- Left neighbor's old value (M),\n\n- Its own old value (from step 99),\n\n- Right neighbor's old value (from step 99).\n\nSince the left neighbor's old value is M (from the previous step), the new value will be M. Wait, but this position is in the uncovered region after step 100? Wait no, during step 100, we are updating all positions. The position at distance 101's left neighbor is at distance 100, which was already M at the start of step 100 (since the covered region at step 99 was distance 99, and during step 100, the distance 100 positions became M). Therefore, the left neighbor of the distance 101 position is M at the start of step 100. Therefore, the new value of the distance 101 position is the max of M, its own old value, and its right neighbor's old value. Since M is the global maximum, this new value will be M. Therefore, the distance 101 position becomes M during step 100, thereby expanding the covered region to distance 101.\n\nWait, this is the same problem as before. It seems that my earlier conclusion is incorrect because in reality, during step t, the positions at distance t become M, but their neighbors at distance t+1 also become M because their left neighbor is now M. This suggests that the covered region expands beyond distance t in the same step.\n\nBut in the n=5 example, step 2 caused the covered region to expand to distance 2 (the maximum possible). However, in our problem with n=2025 and t=100, which is much less than floor(n/2)=1012, this suggests that the covered region would expand to distance t in step t, but then in the next step, it would expand further.\n\nWait, let's think carefully about the n=5 example at step 1:\n\nAfter step 1, covered region is distance 1. Then in step 2, positions at distance 2 become M because their left neighbor (distance 1) was M at the start of step 2. Therefore, after step 2, covered region is distance 2.\n\nSimilarly, in step 3, positions at distance 3 would become M if n were larger, but in n=5, it's already covered.\n\nTherefore, in general, after step t, the covered region is distance t, and during step t+1, the positions at distance t+1 become M because their left neighbor (distance t) was M at the start of step t+1. Thus, the covered region expands by one each step.\n\nTherefore, in our problem with t=100 steps, the covered region is distance 100, and the positions at distance 101 will become M during step 101. However, we are only performing 100 steps, so those positions remain uncovered with values less than M.\n\nWait, now I see the confusion. The key is that during step t, the covered region expands to distance t, but this expansion only affects the positions at distance t. The positions beyond distance t (distance t+1 and beyond) are not yet covered and their values are not yet M.\n\nTherefore, after 100 steps:\n\n- Covered region: distance 0 to 100 (total 201 positions), all with value M.\n\n- Uncovered regions: distance 101 to 1012 (total 2025 - 201 = 1824 positions), which form two arcs of 912 positions each.\n\nThe values in the uncovered regions are non-decreasing over time and are influenced by their neighbors. Importantly, the leftmost uncovered position (distance 101)