\nDuring step 2 (t=2):\n\nThe positions at distance 2 (position 2) now have their left neighbor at distance 1 (which was M at the start of step 2). Therefore, position 2 becomes M during step 2, expanding the covered region to distance 2.\n\nThus, in general, during step t, the positions at distance t become M because their left neighbor (distance t-1) was M at the start of the step. Therefore, after step t, the covered region is distance t.\n\nTherefore, in our problem with n=2025 and t=100:\n\n- After step 100, the covered region is distance 100.\n\n- The positions at distance 101 will have their left neighbor at distance 100, which was M at the start of step 100, so during step 100, their new value is max(M, their previous value, their right neighbor's previous value) = M. Therefore, during step 100, the positions at distance 101 also become M.\n\nWait, this suggests that during step 100, not only the positions at distance 100 become M, but also positions at distance 101?\n\nNo, that can’t be, because their left neighbor is at distance 100, which was M at the start of step 100. Therefore, the distance 101 position's left neighbor is distance 100 (now M), so its new value is M. Therefore, during step 100, positions at distance 101 also become M.\n\nThis implies that during step t, all positions within distance t+1 become M? That can’t be.\n\nWait, let me formalize:\n\nAt the start of step t:\n\n- Covered region is distance t-1.\n\n- Positions at distance t-1 have value M.\n\n- Positions at distance t have value something < M.\n\nDuring step t:\n\n- For a position at distance d:\n\n  - If d < t: already M, stays M.\n\n  - If d = t: its left neighbor is at distance t-1 (M), so new value = max(M, previous value, right neighbor's previous value) = M.\n\n  - If d > t: its left neighbor is at distance d-1. If d-1 < t, then left neighbor is M, so new value = M.\n\nWait a second! If a position is at distance d > t, but its left neighbor is at distance d-1 < t, then that left neighbor is M, so the new value becomes M.\n\nTherefore, during step t, any position whose left neighbor is within distance t-1 (i.e., distance d-1 < t) will become M.\n\nTherefore, positions at distance d where d-1 < t ⇒ d ≤ t will become M. But also, positions with d-1 = t (i.e., d = t+1) will have their left neighbor at distance t, which was not yet M at the start of step t (since covered region was only up to t-1). Wait, no:\n\nAt the start of step t:\n\n- Covered region is distance t-1.\n\n- Therefore, positions at distance t have left neighbor at distance t-1 (M), but their own value is < M.\n\n- Positions at distance t+1 have left neighbor at distance t (which is < M).\n\nTherefore, during step t:\n\n- Positions at distance d ≤ t will have their left neighbor at distance d-1 ≤ t-1 (which is M if d-1 ≤ t-1), so their new value will be M.\n\n- Positions at distance d = t+1 will have their left neighbor at distance t (which was not M at the start of step t), so their new value is the max of their left neighbor's old value (which is < M), their own old value (< M), and their right neighbor's old value (< M). Therefore, their new value will be the max of three values all less than M, so it could be less than M.\n\nWait, this is key.\n\nLet me parse this again with care:\n\nAt the start of step t:\n\n- The covered region is distance t-1. So positions at distances 0 to t-1 are M.\n\n- Positions at distance t have left neighbor at distance t-1 (M), so during step t, their new value will be M (because max(M, their old value, right neighbor's old value) = M).\n\n- Positions at distance t+1 have left neighbor at distance t (which was not covered yet, so its value was < M at the start of step t). Their right neighbor is at distance t+2 (also < M). Therefore, their new value is the max of their left neighbor's old value (< M), their own old value (< M), and their right neighbor's old value (< M). So their new value could be anything less than M, but it's the maximum of three values each less than M, so it's still less than M.\n\nTherefore, during step t:\n\n- Positions at distance ≤ t become M.\n\n- Positions at distance > t remain < M.\n\nTherefore, after step t, the covered region is distance t.\n\nTherefore, in our problem with n=2025 and t=100:\n\n- After step 100, the covered region is distance 100.\n\n- Positions at distance 101 and beyond remain uncovered and have values < M.\n\nTherefore, the values in the uncovered regions (distance 101 to 1012) are still less than M, but they have been updated in previous steps.\n\nNow, to compute the expected number of distinct values after 100 steps, we have:\n\n1. The global maximum M is present in the covered region (201 positions).\n\n2. The values in the uncovered regions (1824 positions) are all less than M and form their own set of values. We need to find the expected number of distinct values among these 1824 positions, plus 1 for the global maximum.\n\nTherefore, the total expected number of distinct values is 1 + E[distinct values in uncovered regions].\n\nNow, the challenge is to compute E[distinct values in uncovered regions].\n\nThe uncovered regions consist of two arcs, each of length 912, separated by the covered region. Due to symmetry, the expected number of distinct values in each arc will be the same, so we can compute the expectation for one arc and double it, then add 1 for the global maximum.\n\nHowever, the two arcs are independent in terms of their values because they are separated by the covered region (which has value M), so the left arc's values don't influence the right arc's values and vice versa. Therefore, the expected number of distinct values in the entire uncovered region is twice the expected number in one arc.\n\nTherefore, let's focus on one uncovered arc of length L = 912.\n\nWe need to compute E[distinct values in an arc of length L], where each position in the arc is updated according to the rules, starting from independent uniform [0,1] initial