nce the updates happen simultaneously, all the new values are computed based on the old values from the previous second. So, in other words, in parallel, each \\( a_i \\) is updated to \\( \\max\\{a_{i-1}^{(t)}, a_i^{(t)}, a_{i+1}^{(t)}\\} \\), where \\( a^{(t)} \\) denotes the value at time \\( t \\).\n\nTherefore, the system is evolving over discrete time steps, with each time step updating all the values simultaneously based on their neighbors at the previous time step.\n\nThe question is, after 100 such time steps, what is the expected number of distinct values remaining in the circle?\n\nHmm. So, we need to compute \\( \\mathbb{E}[N_{100}] \\), where \\( N_{100} \\) is the number of distinct values in the circle at time 100, starting from an initial configuration with \\( a^{(0)}_i \\) uniformly random in [0,1].\n\nFirst, perhaps I should consider what happens to the values in the system over time. Since each update is taking the maximum of the three neighbors, intuitively, the values can only stay the same or increase over time, right? Because the new value \\( a_i^{(t+1)} = \\max\\{a_{i-1}^{(t)}, a_i^{(t)}, a_{i+1}^{(t)}\\} \\), so if you have a value at a position that was not the maximum of its neighbors before, then it will be updated to the maximum, which is at least as big as the previous value. So, the values can only increase or stay the same? Wait, actually, they can stay the same if the current value was already the maximum of its three neighbors. But they can also increase if one of the neighbors was larger than the current value.\n\nTherefore, the sequence of values at each position is non-decreasing over time. Since the system is deterministic once the initial values are set, but the initial values are random, so the expectation is over all possible initial configurations.\n\nMoreover, since all the updates are done simultaneously, there might be some interesting dynamics here.\n\nNow, the key question is: after 100 steps, how many distinct values are expected to remain?\n\nHmm. Since each value is non-decreasing, the set of values in the circle can only stay the same or increase over time, but they can also become constant if the maximum propagates around the circle or something.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the system will eventually converge to a steady state where all the values are equal? Or maybe not?\n\nWait, let me think about simpler cases first. Maybe start with smaller numbers of elements instead of 2025, like 3 or 4 elements, and see what happens, and then perhaps generalize?\n\nLet me try with 3 elements first. Let me consider a triangle of 3 numbers. Let me denote them as \\( a_1, a_2, a_3 \\), arranged in a circle, so \\( a_4 = a_1 \\), \\( a_0 = a_3 \\), etc. Then, at each time step, each \\( a_i \\) becomes the maximum of its two neighbors and itself. Wait, in the case of three elements, each \\( a_i \\) becomes the maximum of all three elements, right? Because each element has two neighbors and itself, so all three elements are involved in each new \\( a_i \\).\n\nWait, actually, hold on: for three elements, the neighbors of \\( a_1 \\) are \\( a_3 \\) and \\( a_2 \\), so \\( a_1^{(t+1)} = \\max\\{a_3^{(t)}, a_1^{(t)}, a_2^{(t)}\\} \\), which is the same as \\( \\max\\{a_1^{(t)}, a_2^{(t)}, a_3^{(t)}\\} \\). Similarly, \\( a_2^{(t+1)} = \\max\\{a_1^{(t)}, a_2^{(t)}, a_3^{(t)}\\} \\), and \\( a_3^{(t+1)} = \\max\\{a_2^{(t)}, a_3^{(t)}, a_1^{(t)}\\} \\). So, in the case of three elements, all three elements are being updated to the same value: the maximum of all three previous values. Therefore, in one step, all three elements become equal to the maximum of the initial three values. Then, in the next step, since all three are equal, say to \\( M \\), then the next step each element becomes \\( \\max\\{M, M, M\\} = M \\), so they stay the same. Therefore, for three elements, after the first step, all three elements become equal to the global maximum of the initial three values, and then they stay equal forever. Therefore, the number of distinct values at time \\( t \\geq 1 \\) is either 1 (if all three are equal) or... Wait, but actually, in the first step, all three become equal, so after the first step, there is only 1 distinct value, and it remains so forever. Therefore, for three elements, after any number of time steps \\( \\geq 1 \\), the number of distinct values is always 1, so the expected number is 1. But wait, in the problem, the initial values are random, so actually, even though after one step all three become equal, but depending on the initial values, maybe the maximum is achieved only once? Wait, no, in three elements, after one step, all three become the global maximum of the initial three, so they are all equal. So, regardless of the initial configuration, after one step, they all become equal. Therefore, the number of distinct values drops to 1 immediately after the first step, and stays there forever. So, for three elements, the expected number of distinct values after any number of steps \\( \\geq 1 \\) is 1. Hmm, that's interesting.\n\nBut in our problem, the number of elements is 2025, which is a multiple of 3? Wait, 2025 divided by 3 is 675, so yes, 2025 is divisible by 3. Hmm, but perhaps similar things happen?\n\nWait, let me try with four elements. Let me see what happens with four elements in a circle. Let's call them \\( a_1, a_2, a_3, a_4 \\), arranged in a circle, so \\( a_0 = a_4 \\), \\( a_5 = a_1 \\), etc. Then, each \\( a_i^{(t+1)} = \\max\\{a_{i-1}^{(t)}, a_i^{(t)}, a_{i+1}^{(t)}\\} \\).\n\nHmm, so for four elements, the update rule is a bit more involved. Let me see if I can analyze this.\n\nSuppose we have four numbers in a circle. Let me think of an example. Suppose initially all four are 0 except one, say \\( a_1 = 1 \\), and the rest are 0. Then, at the first time step:\n\n- \\( a_1^{(1)} = \\max\\{a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}\\} = \\max\\{0, 1, 0\\} = 1 \\)\n- \\( a_2^{(1)} = \\max\\{a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}\\} = \\max\\{1, 0, 0\\} = 1 \\)\n- \\( a_3^{(1)} = \\max\\{a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}\\} = \\max\\{0, 0, 0\\} = 0 \\)\n- \\( a_4^{(1)} = \\max\\{a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}\\} = \\max\\{0, 0, 1\\} = 1 \\)\n\nSo after one step, the values are [1, 1, 0, 1]. So now, the values are 1, 1, 0, 1. Then, at the next time step:\n\n- \\( a_1^{(2)} = \\max\\{a_4^{(1)}, a_1^{(1)}, a_2^{(1)}\\} = \\max\\{1, 1, 1\\} = 1 \\)\n- \\( a_2^{(2)} = \\max\\{a_1^{(1)}, a_2^{(1)}, a_3^{(1)}\\} = \\max\\{1, 1, 0\\} = 1 \\)\n- \\( a_3^{(2)} = \\max\\{a_2^{(1)}, a_3^{(1)}, a_4^{(1)}\\} = \\max\\{1, 0, 1\\} = 1 \\)\n- \\( a_4^{(2)} = \\max\\{a_3^{(1)}, a_4^{(1)}, a_1^{(1)}\\} = \\max\\{0, 1, 1\\} = 1 \\)\n\nSo now, after two steps, all four are 1. So in this case, starting with one 1 and three 0s, after two steps, everything becomes 1. Interesting. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, suppose we have a different initial configuration. Suppose all four elements are 0 except for \\( a_1 = 0.5 \\), and \\( a_2 = 0.5 \\). Then let's see:\n\nInitial: [0.5, 0.5, 0, 0]\n\nFirst step:\n\n- \\( a_1^{(1)} = \\max\\{a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}\\} = \\max\\{0, 0.5, 0.5\\} = 0.5 \\)\n- \\( a_2^{(1)} = \\max\\{a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}\\} = \\max\\{0.5, 0.5, 0\\} = 0.5 \\)\n- \\( a_3^{(1)} = \\max\\{a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}\\} = \\max\\{0.5, 0, 0\\} = 0.5 \\)\n- \\( a_4^{(1)} = \\max\\{a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}\\} = \\max\\{0, 0, 0.5\\} = 0.5 \\)\n\nSo after one step, all four become 0.5. Then in the next step, they stay 0.5. So in that case, after one step, all become equal.\n\nHmm. Another example: suppose we have alternating high and low values. Let's say \\( a_1 = 1 \\), \\( a_2 = 0 \\), \\( a_3 = 1 \\), \\( a_4 = 0 \\). Then let's see:\n\nFirst step:\n\n- \\( a_1^{(1)} = \\max\\{a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}\\} = \\max\\{0, 1, 0\\} = 1 \\)\n- \\( a_2^{(1)} = \\max\\{a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}\\} = \\max\\{1, 0, 1\\} = 1 \\)\n- \\( a_3^{(1)} = \\max\\{a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}\\} = \\max\\{0, 1, 0\\} = 1 \\)\n- \\( a_4^{(1)} = \\max\\{a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}\\} = \\max\\{1, 0, 1\\} = 1 \\)\n\nSo in one step, everything becomes 1. Hmm, so again, after one step, all values become 1.\n\nWait, but is that always the case? Let me think of a different initial configuration where maybe it doesn't become all equal so quickly.\n\nSuppose we have four elements where two are 1 and two are 0, but arranged such that they are opposite each other: say \\( a_1 = 1 \\), \\( a_2 = 0 \\), \\( a_3 = 1 \\), \\( a_4 = 0 \\). Wait, that's the same as the previous one, which turned into all 1s in one step. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, arrange them as \\( a_1 = 1 \\), \\( a_2 = 0.5 \\), \\( a_3 = 0 \\), \\( a_4 = 0.5 \\). Let me compute step by step.\n\nInitial: [1, 0.5, 0, 0.5]\n\nFirst step:\n\n- \\( a_1^{(1)} = \\max\\{a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}\\} = \\max\\{0.5, 1, 0.5\\} = 1 \\)\n- \\( a_2^{(1)} = \\max\\{a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}\\} = \\max\\{1, 0.5, 0\\} = 1 \\)\n- \\( a_3^{(1)} = \\max\\{a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}\\} = \\max\\{0.5, 0, 0.5\\} = 0.5 \\)\n- \\( a_4^{(1)} = \\max\\{a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}\\} = \\max\\{0, 0.5, 1\\} = 1 \\)\n\nSo after first step: [1, 1, 0.5, 1]\n\nSecond step:\n\n- \\( a_1^{(2)} = \\max\\{a_4^{(1)}, a_1^{(1)}, a_2^{(1)}\\} = \\max\\{1, 1, 1\\} = 1 \\)\n- \\( a_2^{(2)} = \\max\\{a_1^{(1)}, a_2^{(1)}, a_3^{(1)}\\} = \\max\\{1, 1, 0.5\\} = 1 \\)\n- \\( a_3^{(2)} = \\max\\{a_2^{(1)}, a_3^{(1)}, a_4^{(1)}\\} = \\max\\{1, 0.5, 1\\} = 1 \\)\n- \\( a_4^{(2)} = \\max\\{a_3^{(1)}, a_4^{(1)}, a_1^{(1)}\\} = \\max\\{0.5, 1, 1\\} = 1 \\)\n\nSo now, after two steps, everything is 1 again. Hmm.\n\nHmm, so in these examples with four elements, even if the initial configuration isn't all the same, after a couple steps, it seems to become all the same. Wait, is that always the case?\n\nWait, let's see another configuration. Suppose we have four elements: \\( a_1 = 1 \\), \\( a_2 = 0.9 \\), \\( a_3 = 0.1 \\), \\( a_4 = 0.2 \\). Let me compute step by step.\n\nFirst step:\n\n- \\( a_1^{(1)} = \\max\\{a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}\\} = \\max\\{0.2, 1, 0.9\\} = 1 \\)\n- \\( a_2^{(1)} = \\max\\{a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}\\} = \\max\\{1, 0.9, 0.1\\} = 1 \\)\n- \\( a_3^{(1)} = \\max\\{a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}\\} = \\max\\{0.9, 0.1, 0.2\\} = 0.9 \\)\n- \\( a_4^{(1)} = \\max\\{a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}\\} = \\max\\{0.1, 0.2, 1\\} = 1 \\)\n\nSo after first step: [1, 1, 0.9, 1]\n\nSecond step:\n\n- \\( a_1^{(2)} = \\max\\{a_4^{(1)}, a_1^{(1)}, a_2^{(1)}\\} = \\max\\{1, 1, 1\\} = 1 \\)\n- \\( a_2^{(2)} = \\max\\{a_1^{(1)}, a_2^{(1)}, a_3^{(1)}\\} = \\max\\{1, 1, 0.9\\} = 1 \\)\n- \\( a_3^{(2)} = \\max\\{a_2^{(1)}, a_3^{(1)}, a_4^{(1)}\\} = \\max\\{1, 0.9, 1\\} = 1 \\)\n- \\( a_4^{(2)} = \\max\\{a_3^{(1)}, a_4^{(1)}, a_1^{(1)}\\} = \\max\\{0.9, 1, 1\\} = 1 \\)\n\nSo again, after two steps, all become 1. Hmm, so maybe with four elements, regardless of initial configuration, after a few steps, all elements become equal to the global maximum of the initial configuration?\n\nWait, let's see: in the first step, the maximum value in the initial configuration is 1 (from \\( a_1 = 1 \\)), so after the first step, all elements are at least as large as the previous maximum? Wait, actually, in the first step, each element is updated to the maximum of its three neighbors (including itself). Since the global maximum is somewhere in the circle, its neighbors might not be as high, but the element at the global maximum will stay at the global maximum, because it is the maximum of itself and its neighbors. Wait, actually, in the first step, the element which was the global maximum will stay at the global maximum, because it's the max of its neighbors and itself. But other elements might increase to the global maximum if they are adjacent to the global maximum? Hmm.\n\nWait, in the first example with four elements where the initial configuration was [1, 0.5, 0, 0.5], the global maximum was 1, and after one step, the elements adjacent to the 1 (positions 1 and 2) became 1, and the element opposite (position 3) became 0.5, but then in the next step, that 0.5 was adjacent to 1s, so it got updated to 1. So, actually, the maximum propagates outward from the original maximums? Hmm, interesting.\n\nSo perhaps in general, for any initial configuration on a circle with N elements, after some number of steps, all the values will become equal to the global maximum of the initial configuration? If that is the case, then after that, they stay constant. Therefore, the number of distinct values would eventually become 1, once the maximum has propagated around the entire circle.\n\nTherefore, if that's the case, then after sufficient time, the system reaches the steady state where all values are equal to the initial global maximum. Then, the number of distinct values would be 1 for all times after that point. Therefore, if the time given (100 seconds) is larger than the \"diameter\" of the circle in terms of how long it takes for the maximum to propagate around the circle, then the number of distinct values would be 1. But if 100 is less than that diameter, then maybe the number is more than 1?\n\nTherefore, perhaps the key is to figure out, for a circle of N elements, how long it takes for the maximum value to propagate around the entire circle. Then, if 100 is greater than or equal to that time, then the number of distinct values would be 1, otherwise, it would be more?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the propagation time is logarithmic or something? Hmm, perhaps.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps in each step, the set of elements that have attained the global maximum can spread by one position each step? For instance, in the four-element example, starting with a single maximum, it took two steps to propagate to all elements. Wait, in the first step, the adjacent elements became the maximum, and then in the next step, the next adjacent ones, but in the four-element case, after one step, the two neighbors of the original maximum became the maximum, and the element opposite was still lower, but in the next step, that opposite element got updated to the maximum because it was between two maximums?\n\nWait, in the first example with four elements, starting with [1, 0, 0, 0], then after one step, it became [1, 1, 0, 1]. Wait, actually, the element opposite the original maximum (position 3) was between two 0s and a 1? Wait, no, in that case, the original maximum was at position 1, so neighbors were position 4 and position 2. Position 4 was 0, position 2 was 0, so position 1 stayed at 1. Then position 2, which was 0, had neighbors position 1 (1) and position 3 (0), so it became 1. Position 4 had neighbors position 3 (0) and position 1 (1), so it became 1. Position 3 had neighbors position 2 (0) and position 4 (0), so it stayed at 0. Then in the next step, position 3 is between two 1s, so it becomes 1. So, in effect, the maximum propagates one step further each time? So in step 1, the original maximum and its two neighbors are at maximum. In step 2, the next layer?\n\nWait, in the first step, from [1,0,0,0], you get [1,1,0,1]. So positions 1,2,4 are at maximum, and position 3 is still 0. Then in the next step, position 3 is between positions 2 and 4, which are both 1, so it becomes 1. So in two steps, all positions become 1. So the distance from the original maximum to the opposite side is two steps around the circle (since in four elements, the opposite is two steps away). So in two steps, the maximum has propagated to the opposite side.\n\nTherefore, in general, for a circle of N elements, the maximum will take at most floor(N/2) steps to propagate around the entire circle? Because in each step, the maximum can spread to the next neighbor in both directions? Wait, actually, in each step, the set of elements that have the maximum can expand by one in each direction, right?\n\nWait, suppose you have a circle with N elements. Suppose there is a single maximum value at some position. Then, in the first step, its two immediate neighbors will also become maximum (since they were adjacent to the original maximum and hence their new value is the max of their previous value and the maximum). Wait, actually, the neighbors of the original maximum will be updated to the maximum, because their previous value might have been less than the maximum, but they are adjacent to the maximum. So, in step 1, the two neighbors of the original maximum become maximum. Then, in step 2, the neighbors of those neighbors (which are two steps away from the original maximum) will also become maximum, because they are adjacent to a maximum. Similarly, in step t, the elements that are t steps away from the original maximum in either direction will become maximum. Therefore, the maximum will spread outward in both directions at a rate of one step per second. Therefore, the number of steps needed to cover the entire circle would be ceiling(N / 2). Because the circle has circumference N, so to go from the starting point to the opposite side, you need N/2 steps. Since it can spread in both directions, it can cover the circle in N/2 steps. Wait, actually, if you start at position 1, then at step 1, you cover positions 1, 2, N (since it's a circle). Wait, no, in a circle of N elements, each element has two neighbors. So starting at position 1, step 1: positions 1, 2, N become maximum? Wait, no, actually, in step 1, only the immediate neighbors of the original maximum (position 1) become maximum. The original maximum stays, and its two immediate neighbors (positions 2 and N) become maximum. So in step 1, positions 1, 2, N are maximum. Then in step 2, the neighbors of those positions (positions 3 and N-1) will become maximum, since they are adjacent to a maximum. So step 2: positions 1,2,3,N-1,N are maximum. Then step 3: positions 4 and N-2 become maximum, etc. So each step, the \"frontier\" of maximums expands by one in each direction. Therefore, after k steps, the maximum has spread to a contiguous block of length 2k + 1 (the original position and k steps in each direction). Wait, actually, starting at position 1, after step 1: positions 1,2,N (so three positions). Wait, actually, in a circle, when you have position 1, its neighbors are 2 and N. So after step 1, positions 1,2,N are maximum. Then in step 2, the neighbors of those positions: position 3 (neighbor of 2) and position N-1 (neighbor of N). So positions 3 and N-1 become maximum. So now, positions 1,2,3,N,N-1 are maximum. Then step 3: positions 4 and N-2 become maximum. So after k steps, the maximum has spread to positions from 1 - k to 1 + k modulo N? Wait, perhaps better to think in terms of distance from the original maximum.\n\nAlternatively, since it's a circle, the maximum will spread outward in both directions, and the number of elements that have the maximum after t steps is 2t + 1 (the original position plus t in each direction). Therefore, to cover the entire circle, we need 2t + 1 ≥ N, so t ≥ (N - 1)/2. Since t must be an integer, so t ≥ ceil((N - 1)/2). For even N, that would be N/2. For odd N, (N-1)/2.\n\nTherefore, in our problem, N = 2025, which is odd. Therefore, the time needed to cover the entire circle would be t = ceil((2025 - 1)/2) = ceil(2024/2) = ceil(1012) = 1012. So, after 1012 seconds, the entire circle would have the maximum value. Therefore, after 1012 seconds, all values are equal to the initial global maximum, and remain so forever. Therefore, if we run the process for 100 seconds, which is much less than 1012, then the maximum hasn't had time to propagate all the way around the circle. Therefore, the number of distinct values remaining after 100 seconds would be greater than 1.\n\nHmm, so now, we need to figure out, after t = 100 steps on a circle of N = 2025 elements, how many distinct values are present? Since the maximum hasn't yet spread all the way around, but has spread some distance from the original maxima.\n\nBut wait, in the initial configuration, there might be multiple maxima. Because the initial values are all independent uniform [0,1], so the global maximum could be anywhere, but actually, there is almost surely a unique global maximum, right? Because the probability that two or more elements are exactly equal to the maximum is zero in the continuous distribution. So, almost surely, there is a unique global maximum in the initial configuration. So, without loss of generality, we can assume that there is a single element which is the global maximum, and all others are less than it. Then, as we saw, the maximum propagates outward at one step per second in each direction. Therefore, after t seconds, the set of elements that have the global maximum is a contiguous arc of length 2t + 1 (since starting from the original maximum, expanding t steps in each direction). Wait, actually, in the four-element example, after t=1 step, we had three elements with the maximum (positions 1,2,N), which is 3 elements, which is 2*1 + 1. Then after t=2 steps, we had all four elements, which is 2*2 + 1 = 5? Wait, that doesn't fit. Wait, in the four-element case, after two steps, all four elements became maximum, but 2*2 + 1 = 5 > 4. Hmm, perhaps my formula is off.\n\nWait, in the four-element example, starting at position 1, after t=1 step, the maximum has spread to positions 1,2,4 (three positions). After t=2 steps, it's spread to all four positions. So the length of the arc covered is 4, which is 2t + something? Hmm, perhaps it's better to think in terms of the number of elements covered after t steps. Let me see.\n\nSuppose we have N elements in a circle. Let’s fix the position of the initial maximum at position 1. Then, after t steps, the maximum has spread to positions 1, 2, ..., t+1 and positions N, N-1, ..., N - t + 1. So, the covered positions form an interval of length 2t + 1 (from position N - t + 1 to position t + 1), but since it's a circle, if 2t + 1 exceeds N, then it wraps around. Wait, actually, the number of positions covered after t steps is 2t + 1, but when 2t + 1 exceeds N, it would have wrapped around the circle. However, since we are only going up to t = 100, and N = 2025, which is much larger than 2*100 + 1 = 201, so 201 < 2025, so the covered arc is still less than the whole circle. Therefore, after t steps, the set of positions with the global maximum is an interval of length 2t + 1 centered at the original maximum? Or starting at the original maximum and extending t steps in each direction?\n\nWait, actually, in the four-element example, after t=1 step, the covered positions are positions 1,2,4 (since position 4 is adjacent to 1). So that's three positions, which is 2*1 + 1. After t=2 steps, all four positions are covered, which is 2*2 + 0? Hmm, not sure. Alternatively, in the four-element case, the distance from the original maximum to the farthest point is two steps (since it's a circle of four). So in two steps, you can reach the farthest point.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps in general, the number of positions covered after t steps is 2t + 1, as long as 2t + 1 ≤ N. So in the four-element case, after t=1 step, 3 positions covered, which is 2*1 +1, and after t=2 steps, 2*2 +1=5, but since N=4, it's actually all four positions. So the formula 2t + 1 gives the number of positions covered until it wraps around the circle. Hmm. So in general, when 2t +1 ≤ N, the covered positions are exactly 2t +1. When 2t +1 > N, then it's the entire circle. But since in our problem, N=2025 and t=100, then 2*100 +1=201 < 2025, so the covered positions are exactly 201 positions, forming an arc of length 201 centered at the original maximum? Wait, actually, it's not an arc centered at the original maximum, but rather an arc that starts at the original maximum and extends t steps in each direction?\n\nWait, let me think again with the four-element example. Starting at position 1, after t=1 step, the covered positions are 1,2,4. So that's positions 1, then moving clockwise to 2, and counterclockwise to 4 (which is position N). So in terms of an arc, if we imagine the circle as a line from 1 to 4, then positions 1,2,4 are not a contiguous arc unless we wrap around. So actually, in a circle, positions 1,2,4 would form a contiguous arc if we go from 1 to 2 to 4, but since 4 is adjacent to 1, it's actually a contiguous arc covering three positions: 1,2,4? Wait no, in a circle of four elements, the positions 1,2,4 are not contiguous unless you go through position 4 back to 1. Wait, position 4 is adjacent to position 1, so positions 1,2,4 are actually three consecutive positions if you go 1-2-4-1? Wait, no, in a circle, the neighbors of position 4 are 3 and 1. So positions 1,2,4 are not all adjacent to each other. Position 1 is adjacent to 2 and 4. Position 2 is adjacent to 1 and 3. Position 4 is adjacent to 3 and 1. So the three positions 1,2,4 form a \"triangle\" but not a contiguous arc. Hmm, so maybe my previous assumption is incorrect.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the spread is such that after t steps, the set of positions with the maximum value is all positions within distance t of the original maximum, where distance is defined as the minimal number of steps along the circle. In that case, in the four-element example, after t=1 step, the positions at distance 0 (the original) and distance 1 (adjacent) are covered, so positions 1,2,4 (distance 0: 1; distance 1: 2 and 4). Then after t=2 steps, positions at distance 2 (the opposite side) are covered, so all four positions. That seems to make sense.\n\nTherefore, in general, for a circle of N elements, if you start with a single maximum at position 1, then after t steps, all positions within distance t of position 1 will have the maximum value, and positions further than t away will still be less than the maximum. The \"distance\" here is the minimal number of steps along the circle in either direction. So the number of positions covered after t steps is 2t + 1 (from position 1 - t to position 1 + t, modulo N). However, if 2t + 1 exceeds N, then it wraps around and covers the entire circle.\n\nTherefore, in our problem, with N = 2025 and t = 100, the number of positions covered is 2*100 + 1 = 201 positions. These 201 positions form an arc centered at the original maximum, extending 100 steps in each direction. The remaining 2025 - 201 = 1824 positions are still less than the global maximum.\n\nHowever, in the initial configuration, the other positions had random values less than the global maximum. But as time progresses, those values in the covered regions have been updated to the maximum, but the uncovered regions might have their own local maxima? Or perhaps their values just stay below the global maximum but can increase over time?\n\nWait, actually, the values in the uncovered regions can also increase over time, but they can never exceed the global maximum, since the global maximum is already present in the covered regions, and the update rule takes the maximum of the neighbors and itself. Therefore, any value in an uncovered region can only increase if one of its neighbors is increasing, but since the neighbors might be in the covered region (and thus stuck at the global maximum) or in another uncovered region.\n\nHmm, this is getting a bit complicated. Maybe we need to think in terms of the values in the uncovered regions. Let me try to formalize this.\n\nSuppose we have the circle divided into two parts: a \"covered\" arc of length L = 2t + 1 (positions within distance t of the original maximum), and an \"uncovered\" arc of length N - L = 2025 - 201 = 1824 positions. In the covered arc, all values are equal to the global maximum M (which was the initial value at the starting position). In the uncovered arc, the values can be anything less than M, but they might be increasing over time.\n\nHowever, in the uncovered arc, each position's value is updated each second based on its neighbors. Since the covered arc is adjacent to the uncovered arc, the first layer of the uncovered arc (immediately next to the covered arc) will have one neighbor in the covered arc (which is M) and the other neighbor in the uncovered arc. Therefore, those positions in the first layer of the uncovered arc will, in each step, have their value updated to the maximum of their previous value and their covered neighbor (which is M). Therefore, after the first step, those positions in the first layer will become M, right?\n\nWait a second, hold on. Wait, in the first step, the covered arc is already M. The first layer of the uncovered arc (positions adjacent to the covered arc) in the next step will have one neighbor that is M and the other neighbor that is in the uncovered arc. Therefore, their new value will be the maximum of their previous value (which was less than M) and M (from the covered neighbor) and their other neighbor (which is also less than M). Therefore, their new value becomes M. Therefore, in the next step, those positions in the first layer of the uncovered arc become M as well. Then, the second layer of the uncovered arc (two steps away from the original maximum) will have neighbors that are now in the first layer (which are now M) and the other neighbor which is further in the uncovered arc. Therefore, their new value will be the maximum of their previous value (less than M), the M from the first layer, and their other neighbor (still less than M). Therefore, they will also become M in the next step. Continuing this way, each layer of the uncovered arc will become M in the subsequent steps.\n\nWait, so actually, if we have an uncovered arc, the values in that arc will start increasing towards M, but in discrete steps, each step propagating the M value one layer further into the uncovered arc. Therefore, after t steps, the entire circle would have been covered? Wait, but that contradicts my earlier thought.\n\nWait, hold on. Let me think again with the four-element example. Suppose we have four elements, with the maximum at position 1. Then, after 1 step, positions 1,2,4 become M, and position 3 remains less than M. Then, in the next step, position 3 is adjacent to positions 2 and 4, both of which are now M, so position 3 becomes M. Therefore, after two steps, everything is M. So in that case, the uncovered arc (position 3) was only one position, and it took two steps to cover it, but according to the previous reasoning, it should take t steps where t is the distance from the original maximum. The distance from position 1 to position 3 is two steps (since it's a circle of four: 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 1). Wait, the minimal distance from 1 to 3 is two steps clockwise or two steps counterclockwise? Wait, minimal distance is the smaller of the two directions. From 1 to 3 clockwise is two steps (1->2->3), and counterclockwise is also two steps (1->4->3). So minimal distance is two. Therefore, to cover position 3, which is at distance two, it took two steps. So in general, to cover a position at distance d from the original maximum, it takes d steps to cover it? Because each step, the coverage expands by one in each direction.\n\nTherefore, in the four-element example, the position at distance two (the opposite side) is covered at step two, which matches. Similarly, in the general case, for a circle of N elements, a position at distance d from the original maximum will be covered at step d. Therefore, the maximum time needed to cover the entire circle is the maximal minimal distance from the original maximum to any other position, which is floor(N/2). Because the furthest any position can be from the original maximum is floor(N/2) steps in one direction (since beyond that, it's shorter the other way). For odd N, like 2025, the furthest distance is (N-1)/2 = 1012. So, indeed, as I thought before, to cover the entire circle, you need 1012 steps.\n\nBut in our problem, we only have 100 steps. Therefore, the covered arc after 100 steps is all positions within distance 100 from the original maximum. The number of positions in that arc is 2*100 + 1 = 201 (since from distance 0 to 100 in both directions). Therefore, the remaining positions are those at distance greater than 100 from the original maximum, which are 2025 - 201 = 1824 positions. These positions are all at distances from 101 to 1012 steps away from the original maximum.\n\nNow, the question is: what is the expected number of distinct values remaining after 100 seconds?\n\nFirst, note that in the covered arc (the 201 positions within distance 100), all the values are equal to the global maximum M, since they have been updated to M in the first 100 steps. Therefore, those 201 positions all have the same value, M.\n\nIn the uncovered arc (the remaining 1824 positions), what is happening? Let me consider an arbitrary position in the uncovered arc. Let me denote such a position as being at distance d from the original maximum, where d ranges from 101 to 1012. However, since the circle is symmetric, we can consider the values in the uncovered arc as being in different \"layers\" relative to the covered arc.\n\nSpecifically, the uncovered arc can be divided into layers based on their distance from the covered arc. The first layer of the uncovered arc is the positions adjacent to the covered arc (distance 101 from the original maximum), then the next layer is distance 102, etc., up to distance 1012.\n\nEach layer k (where k ranges from 1 to 1012) consists of positions at distance k + 100 from the original maximum (since the covered arc goes up to distance 100). Wait, actually, let me clarify.\n\nIf the covered arc includes all positions within distance 100 of the original maximum, then the first position outside the covered arc is at distance 101 from the original maximum. The distance from the covered arc to this first uncovered position is 1 (since it's adjacent to the covered arc). Similarly, the next layer is distance 2 from the covered arc, etc., up to distance 1012 - 100 = 912? Wait, maybe my terminology is confusing.\n\nAlternatively, let me think of the uncovered arc as starting at position S + 101 (where S is the original maximum position), and going around the circle for the remaining length. The distance from each position in the uncovered arc to the covered arc is from 1 to 1012 - 100 = 912? Hmm, not sure. Maybe better to think in terms of the distance from the original maximum.\n\nEach position in the uncovered arc is at a distance d from the original maximum, where d ranges from 101 to 1012. The minimal distance from the original maximum to any uncovered position is 101, and the maximum is 1012.\n\nNow, for a position at distance d from the original maximum (where d ≥ 101), how does its value evolve over time?\n\nLet me consider a single position P at distance d from the original maximum. Let's denote the original maximum position as O.\n\nAt time t = 0, the value at position P is some random variable uniformly distributed between 0 and 1, independent of everything else.\n\nAt each subsequent time step, the value at position P is updated to the maximum of its two neighbors and itself. However, the neighbors of P are at distances d-1 and d+1 from O, right? Because in a circle, moving clockwise or counterclockwise.\n\nWait, actually, if position P is at distance d from O, then its clockwise neighbor is at distance d - 1 (if d > 0) and its counterclockwise neighbor is at distance d + 1 (mod N). Wait, actually, depending on direction. Let me fix a direction, say clockwise.\n\nSuppose we define the distance as the minimal number of steps in the clockwise direction from O to P. Then, the clockwise neighbor of P is at distance d - 1 (if d > 0), and the counterclockwise neighbor is at distance d + 1 (mod N). However, since we are dealing with minimal distances, distances are defined as the minimal of the clockwise and counterclockwise distances. Hmm, this might complicate things.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to model the evolution of the value at position P in terms of the values of its neighbors over time.\n\nHowever, this seems complex because the neighbors of P are themselves evolving in a similar way, depending on their distances from O.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think of the values in the uncovered arc as approaching the global maximum M from below, but in discrete steps. Each step, the value at a position in the uncovered arc can increase if one of its neighbors has increased to a higher value.\n\nHowever, the neighbors of an uncovered position are either in the covered arc (and thus at M) or in the uncovered arc at a closer distance.\n\nWait, let me consider a position at distance d from O, where d > 100. Its two neighbors are at distances d - 1 and d + 1 (mod N). But since d > 100, the neighbor at distance d - 1 is still in the uncovered arc (since d - 1 ≥ 101 - 1 = 100? Wait, no: if d = 101, then d - 1 = 100, which is in the covered arc. Wait a second! Wait, hold on. If position P is at distance d = 101 from O, then its clockwise neighbor is at distance d - 1 = 100 (which is in the covered arc), and its counterclockwise neighbor is at distance d + 1 = 102 (still in the uncovered arc). Therefore, one neighbor is in the covered arc (value M), and the other is in the uncovered arc (distance 102).\n\nTherefore, at each time step, the value at position P (distance d = 101) is updated to the maximum of its two neighbors: one neighbor is M (from the covered arc) and the other neighbor is some value less than M (since it's in the uncovered arc). Therefore, at each time step, the value at position P (distance 101) will become the maximum of its current value and M. Wait, but since one neighbor is M and the other is less than M, then the new value is max(current value, M, other neighbor). But since the other neighbor is less than M, then the new value is max(current value, M). Therefore, since the current value is less than M (because it started less than M and has only been increasing), then the new value becomes M. Wait, so actually, after the first step, the value at distance 101 becomes M?\n\nWait, let me verify with the four-element example. In the four-element case, the position at distance 2 (the opposite side) took two steps to become M. But according to this reasoning, if a position is at distance d=2 from the original maximum, then its neighbors are at distance 1 and 3 (but distance 3 is equivalent to distance 1 in the other direction). Wait, in the four-element example, position 3 is at distance 2 from position 1. Its neighbors are position 2 (distance 1) and position 4 (distance 1). Wait, in the four-element circle, position 3's neighbors are position 2 and 4, both of which are at distance 1 from the original maximum (position 1). Therefore, in that case, the neighbors of position 3 (distance 2) are both in the covered arc (distance 1) at step t=1. Therefore, at step t=1, the value at position 3 is updated to the maximum of its two neighbors (both M) and itself (which was less than M). Therefore, it becomes M. Wait, but in the four-element example, we saw that after two steps, position 3 became M. Wait, that contradicts?\n\nWait, let me go back to the four-element example with initial configuration [1,0,0,0]. So original maximum at position 1 (M=1). At step t=1:\n\n- Position 1 remains 1.\n\n- Position 2 becomes max(1,0,0)=1.\n\n- Position 3 becomes max(0,0,0)=0.\n\n- Position 4 becomes max(0,0,1)=1.\n\nSo after step 1, positions 1,2,4 are 1, and position 3 is 0.\n\nThen at step t=2:\n\n- Position 1: max(1,1,1)=1.\n\n- Position 2: max(1,1,0)=1.\n\n- Position 3: max(0,1,1)=1.\n\n- Position 4: max(1,1,1)=1.\n\nSo position 3 becomes 1 at step t=2. Therefore, the position at distance 2 (position 3) becomes M at step t=2, which is one step after its neighbors (positions 2 and 4) became M at step t=1. Therefore, in that case, the distance from the original maximum to position 3 is 2, and it took 2 steps to become M. So the number of steps needed for a position at distance d is d steps? Because position 3 is at distance 2, and it took 2 steps. Similarly, in the general case, a position at distance d would take d steps to become M?\n\nWait, in the four-element example, the position at distance 1 (positions 2 and 4) became M at step 1, which is equal to their distance (1 step). The position at distance 2 became M at step 2 (2 steps). So yes, that seems to hold.\n\nTherefore, in general, for a position at distance d from the original maximum, it takes exactly d steps to become M. Therefore, in our problem with N=2025 and t=100, any position at distance ≤ 100 will have become M by time t=100. Positions at distance d > 100 will not have become M yet, and their value at time t=100 will be less than M.\n\nTherefore, in the uncovered arc (positions at distances 101 to 1012 from O), their values at time t=100 will be some values less than M, but they have been increasing over time.\n\nHowever, we need to compute the expected number of distinct values in the entire circle at time t=100. The covered arc (distance ≤100) all have the value M. The uncovered arc (distance >100) have values less than M, but possibly taking on various values.\n\nThe question is: how many distinct values are present in the uncovered arc, and how does that combine with the single value M in the covered arc?\n\nFirst, note that M is a single value, so if there are multiple values in the uncovered arc, the total number of distinct values will be 1 (for M) plus the number of distinct values in the uncovered arc.\n\nTherefore, to find the expected number of distinct values, we need E[1 + D] = 1 + E[D], where D is the number of distinct values in the uncovered arc at time t=100.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to computing E[D], the expected number of distinct values in the uncovered arc after 100 steps, and then adding 1.\n\nNow, the key question is: what is the distribution of the values in the uncovered arc at time t=100?\n\nEach position in the uncovered arc is at some distance d from the original maximum, where d ranges from 101 to 1012. Each such position's value at time t=100 depends on its own initial value and the evolution of its neighbors over time.\n\nHowever, this seems quite complex due to dependencies between positions. Maybe there is a smarter way to think about this.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps all the values in the uncovered arc at time t=100 are less than M and are independent? Probably not, since their values depend on their neighbors, which are also evolving.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps all the values in the uncovered arc have been updated in such a way that they have all increased to the maximum possible value given their distance from the original maximum?\n\nWait, let's think about how a position at distance d evolves. Let me consider a position P at distance d from O, where d > 100. Let me consider how its value changes over time.\n\nAt time t=0: value is X_{P,0} ~ Uniform[0,1], independent of others.\n\nAt each subsequent time step, the value at P is updated to the maximum of its two neighbors and itself. Let me denote the value at time t as X_P^{(t)}.\n\nLet me denote the two neighbors of P as N_{P} and N_{P}^c (clockwise and counterclockwise neighbors). Let me define the distance from O to N_{P} as d - 1 and to N_{P}^c as d + 1 (mod N). Since P is at distance d, and moving clockwise decreases the distance by 1, while moving counterclockwise increases it by 1. However, since we are considering positions in the uncovered arc (d > 100), then d - 1 might still be in the uncovered arc if d > 101, or in the covered arc if d = 101.\n\nWait, for a position P at distance d from O (d > 100), its clockwise neighbor N_{P} is at distance d - 1, and its counterclockwise neighbor N_{P}^c is at distance d + 1 (mod N). However, since we are dealing with distances up to 1012, and d + 1 could exceed N/2, but in any case, distances are modulo N, but minimal distance is considered.\n\nActually, this is getting too convoluted. Perhaps an alternative approach is needed.\n\nPerhaps instead of tracking individual positions, we can note that after t steps, the value at any position in the uncovered arc is the maximum of its initial value and the values propagated from the covered arc. But I'm not sure.\n\nAlternatively, consider that each position in the uncovered arc can only increase over time, approaching M from below. The rate at which it approaches M depends on its distance from the covered arc.\n\nSpecifically, a position at distance k from the covered arc (i.e., distance k+100 from O) will have its value updated each step based on its neighbors. The neighbor closer to the covered arc (distance k-1) will have a value that is increasing towards M, while the other neighbor is further away (distance k+1) and thus has a value that is also increasing but more slowly.\n\nHowever, this recursive relationship might be difficult to resolve exactly.\n\nAlternatively, maybe after t=100 steps, the value at any position in the uncovered arc is the maximum of its initial value and the value that would have propagated from the covered arc up to that point.\n\nWait, perhaps we can model the value at a position P at distance d from O (d > 100) as the maximum of its initial value and the value that would have been propagated from the covered arc in d - 100 steps? Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, notice that for a position at distance d from O, the value at time t=100 is equal to the maximum over all times s from 0 to 100 of the value that could have been propagated from the covered arc towards it.\n\nWait, this is vague. Maybe an inductive approach?\n\nLet me consider a position at distance d from O. Let’s denote its value at time t as X_{d}^{(t)}. We can think of X_{d}^{(t)} as being influenced by the values of its neighbors at time t-1.\n\nSpecifically:\n\nX_{d}^{(t)} = \\max\\left\\{ X_{d-1}^{(t-1)}, X_{d}^{(t-1)}, X_{d+1}^{(t-1)} \\right\\}\n\nWait, but the distance labels are a bit tricky here because moving clockwise or counterclockwise changes the distance. Let me instead think in terms of layers.\n\nLet me define the \"layer\" of a position as its distance from the covered arc. The covered arc is layer 0 (distance 0 from O), then the next layer is layer 1 (distance 1 from O), up to layer 100 (distance 100 from O), which is the inner edge of the uncovered arc. The uncovered arc then extends from layer 1 to layer 1012, but since we are only considering up to t=100, the values in layers beyond layer 100 haven't been influenced by the covered arc yet?\n\nWait, actually, no. Wait, the covered arc is all positions within distance 100 of O. Therefore, the first layer of the uncovered arc is layer 101 (distance 101 from O), which is adjacent to the covered arc (layer 100). The next layer is layer 102, etc.\n\nNow, consider a position in layer k (distance k from O, where k ≥ 101). Let me denote its value at time t as V_k(t). We can try to find a recurrence relation for V_k(t).\n\nAt time t=0, V_k(0) is uniform on [0,1], independent of other positions.\n\nAt time t=1, the value V_k(1) is the maximum of its two neighbors at time 0 and itself. The neighbors of a position in layer k are in layers k-1 and k+1 (assuming k > 101, so k-1 is still in the uncovered arc, except when k=101, in which case one neighbor is in layer 100 (covered)).\n\nWait, let me be precise. Let's take a position in layer k (distance k from O, where k ≥ 101). Its clockwise neighbor is in layer k - 1 (if k - 1 ≥ 101, i.e., k ≥ 102), or in layer 100 if k = 101. Similarly, its counterclockwise neighbor is in layer k + 1 (mod N), but since we are dealing with distances from O, actually, moving counterclockwise from O increases the distance, so the counterclockwise neighbor is in layer k + 1. However, since the total distance can't exceed N/2 (due to minimal distance), but since k can go up to 1012, which is more than N/2=1012.5, so actually, for k > N/2, the