next step (to t=101), the values in the uncovered arc will be updated based on their neighbors at t=100. Since one neighbor of an uncovered position is in the covered arc (which is M) and the other is in the uncovered arc (which is less than M). Therefore, at each subsequent step after t=100, the value of an uncovered position will increase to the maximum of its current value and M (from the covered neighbor) and the other neighbor (which is still less than M). Therefore, after t=100, every uncovered position will, in the next step (t=101), become M, because one of its neighbors is M and the other is less than M. Therefore, their new value will be M.\n\nWait a second! This is critical.\n\nAt time t=100, the covered arc is all M. The first layer of the uncovered arc (distance 101 from O) has neighbors: one neighbor is in the covered arc (M) and the other neighbor is in the second layer of the uncovered arc (distance 102). Therefore, at t=101, the value of the first layer position becomes max(current_value, M, other_neighbor). Since current_value < M and other_neighbor < M, then the new value is M. Therefore, at t=101, the first layer of the uncovered arc becomes M.\n\nSimilarly, at t=102, the second layer of the uncovered arc (distance 102 from O) has neighbors: one neighbor is now in the first layer (which is now M) and the other neighbor is in the third layer (distance 103). Therefore, the value at the second layer position will become max(current_value, M, other_neighbor), which is M. So at t=102, the second layer becomes M.\n\nContinuing this way, each layer k in the uncovered arc will become M at time t = 100 + k. Wait:\n\n- Layer 1 (distance 101 from O): becomes M at t=101 (100 + 1)\n\n- Layer 2 (distance 102): becomes M at t=102 (100 + 2)\n\n- ...\n\n- Layer d (distance 100 + d): becomes M at time t = 100 + d.\n\nTherefore, the last layer to become M is layer 1012 (distance 1012 from O), which would become M at time t = 100 + 1012 = 1112.\n\nBut our problem is only up to t=100. Therefore, at t=100, none of the uncovered arc positions have yet become M. Their values are still less than M, but have been increasing over the 100 steps.\n\nHowever, importantly, at t=100, the first layer of the uncovered arc (distance 101) has not yet reached M. Its value at t=100 is the result of 100 steps of updates, starting from its initial value and being updated based on neighbors that were themselves evolving.\n\nThis seems complicated, but perhaps we can find that the values in the uncovered arc at time t=100 are all independent and uniformly distributed? Probably not, but maybe they are all less than M and have some sort of uniform distribution?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps due to the symmetry and the random initial conditions, the expected number of distinct values in the uncovered arc can be approximated or calculated using linearity of expectation.\n\nRecall that the expected number of distinct values in a set of random variables can be calculated as the sum over all possible values of the probability that the value is present. However, since the values are continuous (uniform on [0,1]), the probability that any two values are exactly equal is zero. Therefore, the expected number of distinct values is equal to the sum over all positions of the probability that the value at that position is different from all others.\n\nBut that might not be helpful here. Alternatively, for continuous random variables, the expected number of distinct values in a collection of n independent random variables uniformly distributed on [0,1] is n - n + 1? Wait, no. Wait, actually, for continuous random variables, the probability that any two are equal is zero, so the expected number of distinct values is equal to the number of variables, because all are almost surely distinct. But in our case, the values are not independent, and they are evolving over time with dependencies.\n\nWait, in our problem, at time t=100, we have 2025 positions, each with a value i