o includes w, so the maximum of that triplet is also the global maximum, so the new value at a₂ will be the global maximum. Similarly, the triplet for a₄ (y,z,w) also includes w, so the new value at a₄ will be the global maximum. However, the triplet for a₃ (x,y,z) does not include w, so if the global maximum is only at w, then the maximum of (x,y,z) might be less than the global maximum. Therefore, the new value at a₃ (which is y's new value) would be the maximum of (x,y,z), which is less than the global maximum. Wait, but hold on.\n\nWait, actually, if the global maximum is only at position w, then the triplets that include w (positions a₁, a₂, a₄) will have their new values set to the global maximum. The triplet that does not include w is the triplet (x,y,z), which does not include w. Therefore, the new value at position a₃ (which corresponds to y) will be the max of (x,y,z). Since w is the global maximum and is not in this triplet, then the max of (x,y,z) could be less than the global maximum. Therefore, in this case, the new values would be:\n\na₁ = global_max,\n\na₂ = global_max,\n\na₃ = max(x,y,z) < global_max,\n\na₄ = global_max,\n\nWait, but hold on, a₄'s triplet is (y,z,w), which does include w, so a₄'s new value is the max of (y,z,w) = global_max,\n\nSimilarly, a₃'s triplet is (x,y,z), which does not include w, so a₃'s new value is max(x,y,z), which is less than global_max.\n\nTherefore, in this case, after one step, we have three elements at global_max and one element at some lower value.\n\nWait, so in this case, the number of distinct values after one step would be two: global_max and the lower value.\n\nWait, but let me make this concrete with numbers.\n\nSuppose the initial configuration is: w = 1 (global_max), x = 0, y = 0, z = 0.\n\nThen, the new values would be:\n\na₁ = max(z=0, w=1, x=0) = 1,\n\na₂ = max(w=1, x=0, y=0) = 1,\n\na₃ = max(x=0, y=0, z=0) = 0,\n\na₄ = max(y=0, z=0, w=1) = 1.\n\nTherefore, the new configuration is [1, 1, 0, 1]. So the values are 1, 1, 0, 1. So the distinct values are 0 and 1. So two distinct values after one step.\n\nThen, in the next step, we perform the same operation again.\n\nSo at time t=1, the configuration is [1, 1, 0, 1].\n\nNow, let's compute the next step, t=2.\n\nCompute each new value:\n\na₁^{(2)} = max(a₄^{(1)}, a₁^{(1)}, a₂^{(1)}) = max(1, 1, 1) = 1,\n\na₂^{(2)} = max(a₁^{(1)}, a₂^{(1)}, a₃^{(1)}) = max(1, 1, 0) = 1,\n\na₃^{(2)} = max(a₂^{(1)}, a₃^{(1)}, a₄^{(1)}) = max(1, 0, 1) = 1,\n\na₄^{(2)} = max(a₃^{(1)}, a₄^{(1)}, a₁^{(1)}) = max(0, 1, 1) = 1.\n\nTherefore, at time t=2, all values become 1. Then, from t=2 onward, they stay at 1.\n\nTherefore, in this example, after two steps, all values become 1. So in this case, the number of distinct values at t=1 was 2, then at t=2 it became 1, and stays 1.\n\nHmm. So in this case, even though after one step there were two distinct values, after two steps, they all became 1. So the number of distinct values can decrease over time? Wait, but in the 3-element case, once you hit the first step, you go from possibly 3 distinct values to 1, and stay there. In the 4-element case, you can go from possibly 4 distinct values (if all are different) to maybe 2 or 1 in the first step, then possibly to 1 in the next step.\n\nWait, let me try with all initial values distinct. Let me pick four numbers: 0.2, 0.5, 0.8, 0.3 arranged in a circle: w=0.2, x=0.5, y=0.8, z=0.3.\n\nThen, compute the first step:\n\na₁ = max(z=0.3, w=0.2, x=0.5) = 0.5,\n\na₂ = max(w=0.2, x=0.5, y=0.8) = 0.8,\n\na₃ = max(x=0.5, y=0.8, z=0.3) = 0.8,\n\na₄ = max(y=0.8, z=0.3, w=0.2) = 0.8.\n\nSo after the first step, the configuration is [0.5, 0.8, 0.8, 0.8]. So the distinct values are 0.5 and 0.8, so two distinct values.\n\nThen, the next step (t=2):\n\na₁ = max(a₄=0.8, a₁=0.5, a₂=0.8) = 0.8,\n\na₂ = max(a₁=0.5, a₂=0.8, a₃=0.8) = 0.8,\n\na₃ = max(a₂=0.8, a₃=0.8, a₄=0.8) = 0.8,\n\na₄ = max(a₃=0.8, a₄=0.8, a₁=0.5) = 0.8.\n\nSo all become 0.8 after the second step. Then, from t=2 onward, all are 0.8. So in this case, after two steps, only one distinct value remains.\n\nHmm. Interesting. So in the 4-element case, depending on the initial configuration, after one step, you might have two or maybe even more distinct values? Wait, let me see if I can get more than two.\n\nSuppose the initial configuration has three distinct values. Let me try: w=0.1, x=0.5, y=0.9, z=0.2.\n\nSo arranged in a circle: [0.1, 0.5, 0.9, 0.2].\n\nFirst step:\n\na₁ = max(z=0.2, w=0.1, x=0.5) = 0.5,\n\na₂ = max(w=0.1, x=0.5, y=0.9) = 0.9,\n\na₃ = max(x=0.5, y=0.9, z=0.2) = 0.9,\n\na₄ = max(y=0.9, z=0.2, w=0.1) = 0.9.\n\nSo after first step: [0.5, 0.9, 0.9, 0.9]. So two distinct values: 0.5 and 0.9.\n\nNext step (t=2):\n\na₁ = max(a₄=0.9, a₁=0.5, a₂=0.9) = 0.9,\n\na₂ = max(a₁=0.5, a₂=0.9, a₃=0.9) = 0.9,\n\na₃ = max(a₂=0.9, a₃=0.9, a₄=0.9) = 0.9,\n\na₄ = max(a₃=0.9, a₄=0.9, a₁=0.5) = 0.9.\n\nSo all become 0.9 at t=2.\n\nHmm. Another example with three distinct values in the initial configuration still collapses to two in one step, then to one in the next.\n\nWhat if the initial configuration has all four distinct? Let me pick [0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7].\n\nFirst step:\n\na₁ = max(z=0.7, w=0.1, x=0.3) = 0.7,\n\na₂ = max(w=0.1, x=0.3, y=0.5) = 0.5,\n\na₃ = max(x=0.3, y=0.5, z=0.7) = 0.7,\n\na₄ = max(y=0.5, z=0.7, w=0.1) = 0.7.\n\nSo after first step: [0.7, 0.5, 0.7, 0.7]. So distinct values: 0.5 and 0.7.\n\nSecond step:\n\na₁ = max(a₄=0.7, a₁=0.7, a₂=0.5) = 0.7,\n\na₂ = max(a₁=0.7, a₂=0.5, a₃=0.7) = 0.7,\n\na₃ = max(a₂=0.5, a₃=0.7, a₄=0.7) = 0.7,\n\na₄ = max(a₃=0.7, a₄=0.7, a₁=0.7) = 0.7.\n\nSo all become 0.7 at t=2.\n\nHmm. So even starting with all distinct values, after two steps, everything becomes the maximum of the initial configuration? Wait, in the first example with [0.1, 0.5, 0.9, 0.2], the maximum was 0.9, and indeed after two steps, everything became 0.9. In the [0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7] case, the maximum was 0.7, and after two steps, everything became 0.7. Wait a second, so actually, in the 4-element case, after two steps, does everything become the global maximum of the initial configuration?\n\nWait, in the first example where the initial maximum was at position w (0.1, 0.5, 0.8, 0.3) – wait, actually in that first example, the maximum was 0.8. Wait, in that example, the initial configuration was [0.2, 0.5, 0.8, 0.3], so the maximum was 0.8, and after two steps, everything became 0.8. Similarly, in the case where the initial maximum was 0.9, after two steps it became 0.9. So perhaps in the 4-element case, after two steps, all values become equal to the initial global maximum?\n\nWait, let's check the first example where the initial configuration was [0.1, 0.5, 0.9, 0.2]. The global maximum is 0.9. After two steps, all became 0.9. Similarly, in the case where initial was [0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7], the global maximum was 0.7, and after two steps, all became 0.7.\n\nWait, but in the first step of that last example, after the first step, we had [0.7, 0.5, 0.7, 0.7], so the maximum after the first step was still 0.7, so the next step it stays at 0.7. Wait, but actually, in the first step, even though the maximum of the initial configuration was 0.7, the first step already achieved that maximum in some positions? Wait, in that first example with [0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7], the first step resulted in [0.7, 0.5, 0.7, 0.7], so indeed, the maximum after the first step is 0.7, which is the same as the initial maximum. So in the first step, the maximum doesn't increase, because we are taking maxima over subsets of the initial values. Therefore, the maximum over any step cannot exceed the initial global maximum. Because each new value is the max of some subset of the previous values, so the maximum can stay the same or decrease? Wait, no, actually, since you're taking maxima, the maximum can't decrease. Wait, actually, the maximum over the entire configuration can only stay the same or increase? Wait, no, the maximum over the entire configuration can't increase because you are only taking maxima of subsets. The global maximum of the entire configuration at time t+1 is the maximum over all the new a_i^{(t+1)}, which are each the max of three elements from the previous configuration. Therefore, the global maximum at time t+1 is at least as large as the global maximum at time t, because the new values can't be smaller than the previous values. Wait, but actually, the new values are each the max of three elements from the previous step, so each new value is at least as large as each of those three elements. Therefore, the new configuration's elements are all ≥ the elements they replaced, but actually, more precisely, each new element a_i^{(t+1)} is ≥ a_{i-1}^{(t)}, a_i^{(t)}, and a_{i+1}^{(t)}. Therefore, each new element is at least as large as each of its immediate neighbors and itself from the previous step. Therefore, the new configuration's elements are all ≥ the previous elements in their respective positions, but also, the new configuration's elements could be larger than some non-adjacent elements?\n\nWait, but the key point is that the global maximum over the entire configuration at time t+1 must be at least as large as the global maximum at time t, since all the new elements are at least as large as the elements they are replacing? Not exactly, because an element might have been replaced by a new value that is larger than its previous value, but another element far away might have decreased? Wait, no, actually, every element's new value is the maximum of itself and its two neighbors. Therefore, each element's new value is at least as large as itself and its two neighbors. Therefore, the new value for each element is at least as large as the previous value. Wait, no: actually, the new value is the maximum of the previous element and its two neighbors. Therefore, it's possible that the new value is larger than the previous value if one of the neighbors was larger, but it could also stay the same or even decrease? Wait, no, hold on: the new value is the maximum of the previous element and its two neighbors. Since the previous element is one of the terms in the max, the new value is at least as large as the previous element. Because the maximum of (prev element, neighbor1, neighbor2) is at least the prev element. Therefore, each element's value is non-decreasing over time. Therefore, the entire configuration is non-decreasing in the sense that each coordinate is non-decreasing. Therefore, the global maximum over the entire configuration can only stay the same or increase? Wait, but how can it increase? Since each coordinate is non-decreasing, the global maximum can only stay the same or increase. But actually, if all coordinates are non-decreasing, then the global maximum can stay the same or increase. However, in our examples above, the global maximum remained the same. Because in the first step, we were taking maxima over subsets, so the global maximum didn't increase, it just got \"pulled up\" to the existing maximum.\n\nWait, actually, in the first step, the new values are each the maximum of three elements from the previous step. Therefore, the new global maximum is the maximum over all these new values, which are each the max of three elements. Since the original global maximum was present in at least one of those triplets, the new global maximum will be at least the original global maximum, but could it be higher? No, because all the elements are from the original configuration, so the maximum of any subset cannot exceed the global maximum of the original configuration. Therefore, the global maximum can only stay the same or... wait, it can't increase, because you can't get a value higher than the original maximum. Therefore, the global maximum is non-decreasing? Wait, no, actually, since each coordinate is non-decreasing, the global maximum must be non-decreasing. Because if all coordinates are non-decreasing, then the maximum among them can't decrease. So the global maximum is non-decreasing over time. However, since all the new values are computed from the previous values, the new global maximum is the maximum over all the new values, which are each maxima of triples from the previous configuration. Since the previous global maximum was in at least one of those triples, then the new global maximum is at least the previous global maximum. But since all the new values are bounded by the previous global maximum, the new global maximum is exactly the previous global maximum. Therefore, the global maximum is constant after the first step? Wait, no, actually, if the previous global maximum was achieved in at least one position, then in the next step, that position's new value will be the max of its neighbors and itself, which includes the global maximum, so it will remain the global maximum. However, other positions might have increased to the global maximum as well. But the global maximum can't exceed the original maximum because all the data is from the original set. Wait, perhaps the global maximum remains constant from the first step onward? Let me see.\n\nTake the 4-element example where initial configuration was [0.1, 0.5, 0.9, 0.2]. The global maximum was 0.9. After the first step, the configuration became [0.5, 0.9, 0.9, 0.9], so the global maximum is still 0.9. After the second step, it becomes [0.9, 0.9, 0.9, 0.9], so still 0.9. So the global maximum stays the same once it's reached in the first step.\n\nWait, but in another example: suppose the initial configuration has a global maximum that is not in any triplet? Wait, but in a 4-element circle, every element is in three triplets, but the global maximum is in at least one triplet. Wait, actually, the global maximum is in at least one triplet because it's part of the circle. Since the global maximum is at some position, say position w, then the triplet that includes w (and its two neighbors) will have the global maximum as its maximum. Therefore, the new value at the position opposite to w? Wait, no, actually, the position opposite to w in a 4-element circle would be two steps away, but in the triplet for that position, which is three consecutive elements, if the global maximum is at position w, then the triplet that includes w will have the global maximum, but the triplet that is opposite might not include w. Wait, but in the 4-element circle, each element is in three triplets, but the global maximum is in three triplets (the ones that include it). However, the other triplet (the one that doesn't include the global maximum) would have a maximum less than the global maximum. Therefore, the new value at that position would be less than the global maximum, but the other positions adjacent to the global maximum would have their new values set to the global maximum. Wait, in the first example where the global maximum was at w, the new value at position a₁ (which is w's new value) becomes the global maximum, and the new value at a₄ (the position before w) also becomes the global maximum because its triplet includes w. The new value at a₃ (the position opposite to w) becomes the maximum of the triplet that doesn't include w, so it's less than the global maximum. Therefore, after the first step, there is still one position with a value less than the global maximum, but in the next step, that position's new value is the maximum of its neighbors and itself. Let me see in the first example:\n\nInitial: [0.1, 0.5, 0.9, 0.2] (global max 0.9 at position 3, y).\n\nFirst step: [0.5, 0.9, 0.9, 0.9] (positions 1,2,3,4).\n\nSecond step: compute each new value:\n\na₁: max(a₄=0.9, a₁=0.5, a₂=0.9) = 0.9,\n\na₂: max(a₁=0.5, a₂=0.9, a₃=0.9) = 0.9,\n\na₃: max(a₂=0.9, a₃=0.9, a₄=0.9) = 0.9,\n\na₄: max(a₃=0.9, a₄=0.9, a₁=0.5) = 0.9.\n\nSo, all become 0.9. Wait, so in the second step, even the position that was 0.5 became 0.9 because its neighbors were 0.9 and 0.9. Therefore, in the next step, even the previously low value gets \"pulled up\" to the global maximum because its neighbors are now at the global maximum.\n\nAh! So in the second step, the previously low value (0.5) at position a₁ is now between two 0.9s, so its new value becomes the max of 0.9, 0.5, and 0.9, which is 0.9. Therefore, it gets promoted to the global maximum in the next step.\n\nTherefore, in the 4-element case, after two steps, even the last remaining non-global-maximum element gets promoted to the global maximum in the next step, because its neighbors have already become the global maximum.\n\nTherefore, in the 4-element case, after two steps, all elements become the global maximum of the initial configuration, and then stay there forever. Therefore, the number of distinct values after t ≥ 2 steps is 1, provided that the global maximum is achieved in the initial configuration (which it almost surely is, since the initial values are continuous, so the global maximum is achieved at some point, and with probability 1, it's not exactly 1, but still, the maximum is a specific value). Wait, but actually, in the continuous case, the global maximum is a single value (since all are continuous, the probability that two or more are equal is zero), so there is a unique global maximum, say M₀. Then, after two steps, all values become M₀, so the number of distinct values is 1 for t ≥ 2. However, at t=1, there might have been two distinct values: M₀ and some lower value. At t=0, there could have been up to 4 distinct values.\n\nTherefore, in the 4-element case, the expected number of distinct values after 100 seconds is 1, since 100 is much larger than 2.\n\nWait, but is that always the case? Let me see with another example.\n\nSuppose in the 4-element case, the initial configuration is such that the global maximum is achieved at two positions? Wait, but with continuous uniform variables, the probability that two or more are exactly equal is zero, so we can assume all initial values are distinct almost surely. Therefore, there is a unique global maximum M₀, and after two steps, all values become M₀, so the number of distinct values is 1 for t ≥ 2. Therefore, for the 4-element case, after any t ≥ 2, the number of distinct values is 1. Therefore, the expected number after 100 seconds is 1.\n\nSimilarly, in the 3-element case, after the first step, it becomes 1 distinct value, so after any t ≥ 1, it's 1.\n\nHmm, so maybe in general, for any n ≥ 3, after a sufficient number of steps, the number of distinct values becomes 1? But how many steps does it take?\n\nWait, in the 3-element case, it takes 1 step. In the 4-element case, it takes 2 steps. Maybe in general, for a circle of size n, it takes n-1 steps? Or something like that?\n\nWait, let me think of a 5-element circle.\n\nSuppose we have 5 elements arranged in a circle: w, x, y, z, v.\n\nSuppose the initial configuration has a global maximum at position w. Let's see what happens over time.\n\nAt time 0: [M₀, a, b, c, d], where M₀ is the global maximum, and a, b, c, d < M₀.\n\nTime 1:\n\nEach new value is the max of itself and its two neighbors.\n\nSo:\n\na₁ = max(v, w, x) = max(d, M₀, a). Since M₀ is the global maximum, this is M₀.\n\na₂ = max(w, x, y) = max(M₀, a, b) = M₀.\n\na₃ = max(x, y, z) = max(a, b, c).\n\na₄ = max(y, z, v) = max(b, c, d).\n\na₅ = max(z, v, w) = max(c, d, M₀) = M₀.\n\nTherefore, after the first step, the configuration is [M₀, M₀, max(a,b,c), max(b,c,d), M₀].\n\nSo the distinct values now are M₀ and the two maxima max(a,b,c) and max(b,c,d). Depending on the initial values, these could be equal or different.\n\nSuppose that in the initial configuration, the values a, b, c, d are such that max(a,b,c) ≠ max(b,c,d). For example, suppose a=0.1, b=0.2, c=0.3, d=0.4. Then:\n\nmax(a,b,c) = 0.3,\n\nmax(b,c,d) = 0.4.\n\nSo after the first step, the configuration is [M₀, M₀, 0.3, 0.4, M₀]. So the distinct values are M₀, 0.3, 0.4. Three distinct values.\n\nThen at time t=2:\n\nCompute each new value:\n\na₁ = max(a₅= M₀, a₁= M₀, a₂= M₀) = M₀,\n\na₂ = max(a₁= M₀, a₂= M₀, a₃= 0.3) = M₀,\n\na₃ = max(a₂= M₀, a₃= 0.3, a₄= 0.4) = M₀,\n\na₄ = max(a₃= 0.3, a₄= 0.4, a₅= M₀) = M₀,\n\na₅ = max(a₄= 0.4, a₅= M₀, a₁= M₀) = M₀.\n\nTherefore, at time t=2, all values become M₀. So after two steps, all become M₀.\n\nWait a minute! Even though at t=1 there were three distinct values, at t=2 they all became M₀. Interesting. So in the 5-element case, after two steps, everything becomes the global maximum.\n\nWait, let me check with another example where the intermediate maxima are different.\n\nSuppose initial configuration: [M₀, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4], so a=0.1, b=0.2, c=0.3, d=0.4.\n\nThen after first step: [M₀, M₀, 0.3, 0.4, M₀].\n\nThen at t=2:\n\na₁: max(M₀, M₀, M₀) = M₀,\n\na₂: max(M₀, M₀, 0.3) = M₀,\n\na₃: max(M₀, 0.3, 0.4) = M₀,\n\na₄: max(0.3, 0.4, M₀) = M₀,\n\na₅: max(0.4, M₀, M₀) = M₀.\n\nSo yes, all become M₀ at t=2. So even if there were intermediate distinct values, they get \"pulled up\" to M₀ in the next step because their neighbors are already M₀.\n\nWait, let me try a different initial configuration where the non-M₀ values are arranged such that their neighboring triplets don't immediately get promoted.\n\nSuppose the initial configuration is [M₀, 0.5, 0.1, 0.6, 0.2]. So a=0.5, b=0.1, c=0.6, d=0.2.\n\nThen, at time t=1:\n\na₁ = max(v=0.2, w=M₀, x=0.5) = M₀,\n\na₂ = max(w=M₀, x=0.5, y=0.1) = M₀,\n\na₃ = max(x=0.5, y=0.1, z=0.6) = 0.6,\n\na₄ = max(y=0.1, z=0.6, v=0.2) = 0.6,\n\na₅ = max(z=0.6, v=0.2, w=M₀) = M₀.\n\nTherefore, after first step: [M₀, M₀, 0.6, 0.6, M₀].\n\nSo distinct values: M₀ and 0.6.\n\nThen at t=2:\n\na₁ = max(a₅=M₀, a₁=M₀, a₂=M₀) = M₀,\n\na₂ = max(a₁=M₀, a₂=M₀, a₃=0.6) = M₀,\n\na₃ = max(a₂=M₀, a₃=0.6, a₄=0.6) = M₀,\n\na₄ = max(a₃=0.6, a₄=0.6, a₅=M₀) = M₀,\n\na₅ = max(a₄=0.6, a₅=M₀, a₁=M₀) = M₀.\n\nSo again, all become M₀ at t=2.\n\nHmm. So in the 5-element case, after two steps, everything becomes M₀. Wait, so maybe in general, for a circle of size n, after two steps, everything becomes the global maximum? But in the 3-element case, after one step, it becomes the global maximum. In the 4-element case, after two steps, it becomes the global maximum. In the 5-element case, after two steps, it becomes the global maximum. Hmm, so perhaps the number of steps needed is ceiling(n/2) or something? Wait, no, in the 3-element case, it was 1 step, which is floor(n/2). In the 4-element case, 2 steps, which is n/2. In the 5-element case, also 2 steps, which is less than n/2 (which would be 2.5). Hmm, not exactly.\n\nWait, in the 3-element case: after 1 step (which is n-2), becomes all M₀.\n\nIn the 4-element case: after 2 steps (n/2), becomes all M₀.\n\nIn the 5-element case: after 2 steps (still n/2 rounded down), becomes all M₀.\n\nWait, maybe it's always two steps? But in the 3-element case, it was one step. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe for any n ≥ 3, after two steps, all elements become the global maximum? But in the 3-element case, after one step, they all become the global maximum. So perhaps it's ceiling(log₂ n) or something? Not sure.\n\nWait, let me think more abstractly. Suppose we have a circle of n elements. Let M₀ be the global maximum in the initial configuration. Since all the values are non-decreasing over time, the value of each element can only increase or stay the same. Moreover, once an element reaches M₀, it will stay at M₀ forever, because the maximum of M₀ and its neighbors will be M₀.\n\nTherefore, the process can be thought of as the \"front\" of the M₀ spreading around the circle. Each step, the elements adjacent to the M₀ regions will also become M₀, because their new value is the max of themselves and their neighbors, which includes M₀.\n\nIn other words, the set of positions with value M₀ will grow by one element in each direction each step? Or something like that?\n\nWait, actually, in the 3-element case: starting with one M₀, after one step, all three elements become M₀. Because each element is the max of itself and its two neighbors, and since one of those neighbors is M₀, all three elements become M₀.\n\nIn the 4-element case: starting with one M₀, after the first step, the two neighbors of M₀ become M₀, but the element opposite remains non-M₀. Then, in the next step, that opposite element's new value is the max of its two neighbors (which are now M₀) and itself, so it becomes M₀.\n\nSimilarly, in the 5-element case: starting with one M₀, after first step, the two neighbors become M₀, and the element across from M₀ (two steps away) remains non-M₀. Then, in the next step, that element's neighbors are now M₀ (since the elements adjacent to it have become M₀ in the first step?), wait let me see.\n\nWait, in the 5-element example above: initial [M₀, a, b, c, d].\n\nAfter first step: [M₀, M₀, max(a,b), max(b,c), M₀].\n\nWait, actually, the element opposite to M₀ (position 3) has value max(b, c, d)? Wait, no, in the first step, position 3 (which is y) is the max of x, y, z. So in the initial configuration, x = a, y = b, z = c. Wait, hold on, in my earlier example, I had the initial as [M₀, a, b, c, d], so positions 1 to 5:\n\n1: M₀,\n\n2: a,\n\n3: b,\n\n4: c,\n\n5: d.\n\nTherefore, position 3 (b) is between a (position 2) and c (position 4). So the new value at position 3 is max(a, b, c). The new value at position 4 (c) is max(b, c, d). The new value at position 5 (d) is max(c, d, M₀) = M₀.\n\nTherefore, after the first step, positions 1,2,5 are M₀, position 3 is max(a,b,c), position 4 is max(b,c,d).\n\nThen, at step 2, let's compute position 3's new value: it is the max of position 2 (M₀), position 3 (max(a,b,c)), and position 4 (max(b,c,d)). Since position 2 is M₀, the new value at position 3 becomes M₀.\n\nSimilarly, position 4's new value is the max of position 3 (max(a,b,c)), position 4 (max(b,c,d)), and position 5 (M₀). Since position 5 is M₀, the new value at position 4 becomes M₀.\n\nTherefore, in two steps, all positions become M₀.\n\nSo in the 5-element case, even though the non-M₀ elements were separated by one element from M₀, they got \"caught up\" in the next step because their neighbors were already M₀ or their own value was surpassed by a neighbor.\n\nTherefore, perhaps in general, after two steps, all elements become M₀, regardless of the initial configuration (as long as there is a unique M₀). Since in the continuous case, the probability that two or more elements are equal is zero, so there is a unique M₀.\n\nWait, but in the 3-element case, it took only one step, but in the 4-element and 5-element cases, it took two steps. So maybe for n elements, the number of steps needed is the ceiling of log₂ n or something? Wait, no, that doesn't fit.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's always two steps except for n=3, which takes one step? But 3 is a small case. Wait, in the 3-element case, after the first step, all become M₀, so it's one step. For n ≥ 4, two steps?\n\nWait, let's check n=6.\n\nSuppose we have a 6-element circle with initial configuration: [M₀, a, b, c, d, e], where a,b,c,d,e < M₀.\n\nAt time 1:\n\nEach new value is the max of itself and its two neighbors.\n\nSo:\n\na₁ = max(e, M₀, a) = M₀,\n\na₂ = max(M₀, a, b) = M₀,\n\na₃ = max(a, b, c),\n\na₄ = max(b, c, d),\n\na₅ = max(c, d, e),\n\na₆ = max(d, e, M₀) = M₀.\n\nTherefore, after first step, the configuration is [M₀, M₀, max(a,b,c), max(b,c,d), max(c,d,e), M₀].\n\nSo the distinct values are M₀ and the three intermediate maxima: max(a,b,c), max(b,c,d), max(c,d,e). Depending on the initial values, these could be distinct or not, but assuming they're all different for simplicity, we have four distinct values: M₀, max(a,b,c), max(b,c,d), max(c,d,e).\n\nThen at time t=2:\n\nCompute each new value:\n\na₁ = max(a₆=M₀, a₁=M₀, a₂=M₀) = M₀,\n\na₂ = max(a₁=M₀, a₂=M₀, a₃=max(a,b,c)) = M₀,\n\na₃ = max(a₂=M₀, a₃=max(a,b,c), a₄=max(b,c,d)) = M₀,\n\na₄ = max(a₃=max(a,b,c), a₄=max(b,c,d), a₅=max(c,d,e)),\n\na₅ = max(a₄=max(b,c,d), a₅=max(c,d,e), a₆=M₀) = M₀,\n\na₆ = max(a₅=max(c,d,e), a₆=M₀, a₁=M₀) = M₀.\n\nNow, let's evaluate a₄:\n\na₄'s new value is the max of a₃ (which is max(a,b,c)), a₄ (which is max(b,c,d)), and a₅ (which is max(c,d,e)).\n\nSince all of these are less than M₀, but we need to see if they are all less than M₀ or if one of them is higher than the others.\n\nSuppose, for example, that the initial a,b,c,d,e are in increasing order: a < b < c < d < e < M₀. Then:\n\nmax(a,b,c) = c,\n\nmax(b,c,d) = d,\n\nmax(c,d,e) = e.\n\nTherefore, a₄'s new value is max(c, d, e) = e.\n\nBut e is less than M₀, so a₄ becomes e, which is still less than M₀.\n\nTherefore, after the second step, the configuration is:\n\n[M₀, M₀, M₀, e, M₀, M₀].\n\nTherefore, the distinct values are M₀ and e. So two distinct values.\n\nThen at time t=3:\n\nCompute a₄'s new value:\n\na₄ = max(a₃=M₀, a₄=e, a₅=M₀) = M₀.\n\nTherefore, at time t=3, all elements become M₀.\n\nTherefore, in the 6-element case, after three steps, everything becomes M₀. Hmm, so the number of steps required seems to be roughly n/2? For n=3, steps=1; n=4, steps=2; n=5, steps=2; n=6, steps=3.\n\nWait, so perhaps it's ceiling(n/2) steps?\n\nWait:\n\nn=3: ceiling(3/2)=2, but it only took 1 step.\n\nHmm, no. Maybe floor(n/2)?\n\nn=3: floor(3/2)=1, which matches.\n\nn=4: floor(4/2)=2, matches.\n\nn=5: floor(5/2)=2, matches.\n\nn=6: floor(6/2)=3, matches.\n\nAh, yes! So maybe in general, for a circle of n elements, it takes floor(n/2) steps for the entire circle to become M₀? Wait, but in the n=5 case, floor(5/2)=2, and indeed it took 2 steps. In n=6, floor(6/2)=3 steps. So that seems to fit.\n\nWait, but let me confirm with n=7.\n\nTake n=7 elements: [M₀, a, b, c, d, e, f].\n\nInitial configuration: positions 1 to 7: 1=M₀, 2=a, 3=b, 4=c, 5=d, 6=e, 7=f, all < M₀.\n\nAt time t=1:\n\na₁ = max(f, M₀, a) = M₀,\n\na₂ = max(M₀, a, b) = M₀,\n\na₃ = max(a, b, c),\n\na₄ = max(b, c, d),\n\na₅ = max(c, d, e),\n\na₆ = max(d, e, f),\n\na₇ = max(e, f, M₀) = M₀.\n\nSo after t=1, configuration is [M₀, M₀, max(a,b,c), max(b,c,d), max(c,d,e), max(d,e,f), M₀].\n\nDistinct values: M₀ and the four intermediate maxima. Let's assume they are all distinct for maximum distinctness.\n\nThen at t=2:\n\nCompute each new value:\n\na₁ = max(a₇=M₀, a₁=M₀, a₂=M₀) = M₀,\n\na₂ = max(a₁=M₀, a₂=M₀, a₃=max(a,b,c)) = M₀,\n\na₃ = max(a₂=M₀, a₃=max(a,b,c), a₄=max(b,c,d)) = M₀,\n\na₄ = max(a₃=max(a,b,c), a₄=max(b,c,d), a₅=max(c,d,e)),\n\na₅ = max(a₄=max(b,c,d), a₅=max(c,d,e), a₆=max(d,e,f)),\n\na₆ = max(a₅=max(c,d,e), a₆=max(d,e,f), a₇=M₀) = M₀,\n\na₇ = max(a₆=max(d,e,f), a₇=M₀, a₁=M₀) = M₀.\n\nNow, let's evaluate a₄ and a₅:\n\nAssume the initial a,b,c,d,e,f are in increasing order: a < b < c < d < e < f < M₀.\n\nThen,\n\nmax(a,b,c) = c,\n\nmax(b,c,d) = d,\n\nmax(c,d,e) = e,\n\nmax(d,e,f) = f.\n\nTherefore:\n\na₄ = max(c, d, e) = e,\n\na₅ = max(d, e, f) = f.\n\nTherefore, after t=2, the configuration is:\n\n[M₀, M₀, M₀, e, f, M₀, M₀].\n\nDistinct values: M₀, e, f.\n\nThen at t=3:\n\nCompute a₄ and a₅:\n\na₄ = max(a₃=M₀, a₄=e, a₅=f) = M₀,\n\na₅ = max(a₄=e, a₅=f, a₆=M₀) = M₀.\n\nTherefore, at t=3, all elements become M₀.\n\nSo for n=7, it took 3 steps, which is floor(7/2)=3. That fits the pattern.\n\nTherefore, in general, for a circle of n elements, it takes floor(n/2) steps for all elements to become M₀. Therefore, after floor(n/2) steps, the entire circle is M₀, and remains so forever.\n\nTherefore, in our problem, n=2025, which is an odd number. Therefore, floor(2025/2) = 1012.5 floored to 1012. Wait, but floor(2025/2) is 1012, since 2025 divided by 2 is 1012.5, and flooring it gives 1012. But in the examples above, for n=5 (odd), floor(5/2)=2 steps, which worked. For n=7, floor(7/2)=3 steps, which worked. For n=3, floor(3/2)=1 step, which worked. So for n=2025, floor(2025/2)=1012 steps would be required for all elements to become M₀.\n\nHowever, the problem asks for the expected number of distinct values after 100 seconds. Since 100 is much less than 1012, the process hasn't had time to propagate the M₀ all the way around the circle. Therefore, the number of distinct values after 100 seconds will be greater than 1, and we need to compute its expectation.\n\nHmm. So my previous line of thought suggests that for n elements, after k steps, the set of positions that have reached M₀ is expanding outward from the initial position of M₀. Each step, the \"front\" of M₀ propagates one step further in each direction? Wait, but in the examples above, in the first step, the neighbors of M₀ become M₀, then in the next step, their neighbors become M₀, etc. So it's like a wave moving around the circle at one step per iteration. Therefore, after t steps, the number of elements that have become M₀ is at most 2t + 1 (assuming the initial M₀ is at a single position). However, on a circle, the expansion wraps around, so actually, it's a bit different.\n\nWait, actually, in the first step, starting from one M₀, the two adjacent elements become M₀. So after 1 step, 3 elements are M₀? Wait no, in the 3-element case, starting with one M₀, after one step, all three become M₀. Wait, that's different.\n\nWait, perhaps on a circle with n elements, if you start with one M₀, then after each step, the number of M₀s increases by 2, until it wraps around the circle. But since it's a circle, once the wave has gone around half the circle, it starts overlapping.\n\nWait, let's think of it as a discrete process on a circle. Let's imagine the circle as a clock face with 2025 positions. Suppose the initial M₀ is at position 0. Then, at each step, any position that is adjacent to an existing M₀ will become M₀ in the next step. So the set of M₀s expands by one position in each direction each step.\n\nHowever, since it's a circle, the expansion can wrap around. The key is that the distance from the initial M₀ determines how many steps it takes to reach that position.\n\nTherefore, the time it takes for a position at distance d (in either direction) from the initial M₀ to become M₀ is d steps. Since the circle is symmetric, the maximum distance from the initial M₀ to any other position is floor(n/2). Because beyond that, the distance in the other direction is shorter.\n\nTherefore, the positions that have not become M₀ after t steps are those at distance greater than t from the initial M₀. However, since it's a circle, the number of positions not yet reached is the number of positions within distance greater than t from the initial M₀, but considering the wrap-around.\n\nWait, actually, the number of positions that have not yet become M₀ after t steps is equal to the number of positions that are more than t steps away from the initial M₀ in both clockwise and counter-clockwise directions? Hmm, perhaps not exactly.\n\nAlternatively, the set of positions that have not yet become M₀ after t steps form a contiguous arc of length 2t+1? Wait, perhaps not.\n\nWait, actually, in the first step (t=1), the two neighbors of the initial M₀ become M₀, so the non-M₀ region is the rest of the circle, which is n - 3 elements. But arranged how?\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to model the spread of M₀ as a \"front\" moving in both directions. Each step, the front extends by one in each direction. Therefore, after t steps, the M₀ region is an interval of length 2t + 1 (the initial position plus t in each direction). However, on a circle, this interval will wrap around when it exceeds n positions.\n\nTherefore, the number of positions that are still not M₀ after t steps is n - (2t + 1), but only if 2t + 1 ≤ n. When 2t + 1 > n, the M₀ region has wrapped around the circle multiple times, and the non-M₀ region becomes fragmented.\n\nHowever, since we are dealing with a finite number of steps (t=100), and n=2025 is quite large compared to 100, 2t + 1 = 201, which is much less than 2025, so the M₀ region is still a single contiguous block of 201 positions, and the non-M₀ region is a single contiguous block of 2025 - 201 = 1824 positions.\n\nWait, hold on: if the M₀ region expands by one in each direction each step, then after t steps, the M₀ region is an arc of length 2t + 1. Because starting from the initial position, after t steps, you've reached t positions clockwise and t positions counter-clockwise, so total of 2t + 1 positions.\n\nTherefore, the number of positions that have become M₀ after t steps is 2t + 1, assuming the initial M₀ was a single point. However, this is only true if the expansion hasn't wrapped around the circle. Since n=2025 and t=100, 2t + 1 = 201, which is much less than n, so indeed the M₀ region is a contiguous arc of 201 positions, and the non-M₀ region is the remaining 2025 - 201 = 1824 positions.\n\nHowever, this assumes that the initial M₀ was at a single position. But in reality, the initial configuration has a single global maximum M₀ (almost surely), which is at some position, say position 1 without loss of generality due to rotational symmetry.\n\nTherefore, the spread of M₀ will be symmetric around the initial position? Wait, actually, the spread is in both directions, but since the circle is symmetric, we can fix the initial M₀ at position 1, and then the M₀ region expands clockwise and counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, after t steps, the M₀ region consists of positions from (1 - t) mod n to (1 + t) mod n, but since it's a circle, we can think of it as an interval of length 2t + 1 centered at position 1.\n\nTherefore, the number of M₀ positions after t steps is 2t + 1, and the non-M₀ positions are the remaining n - (2t + 1) = 2025 - (2*100 + 1) = 2025 - 201 = 1824 positions.\n\nHowever, in the non-M₀ positions, the values are not necessarily all the same. In fact, each non-M₀ position has a value that is the maximum of its neighbors at the previous step. Wait, but actually, the non-M₀ positions are being updated each step based on their previous neighbors, some of which might already be M₀.\n\nWait, perhaps the non-M₀ positions form a contiguous block? Let me think.\n\nSuppose after t steps, the M₀ region is an arc of length 2t + 1, so the non-M₀ region is also an arc of length n - (2t + 1) = 1824. Within this non-M₀ arc, the values are being updated each step, but they can't exceed the current maximum in their neighborhood.\n\nHowever, since the non-M₀ arc is between two M₀ regions? Wait no, the non-M₀ region is between two ends of the M₀ arc? Wait, actually, on a circle, if the M₀ region is an arc of 201 positions, then the non-M₀ region is the complementary arc of 1824 positions, which is also a single contiguous arc. The two ends of the non-M₀ arc are adjacent to the M₀ region.\n\nTherefore, within the non-M₀ arc, each position's value is the maximum of itself and its two neighbors from the previous step. However, since the neighbors at the ends of the non-M₀ arc are part of the M₀ region, their values are M₀. Therefore, for the positions at the very ends of the non-M₀ arc (i.e., adjacent to the M₀ region), their new value in the next step will be the maximum of their previous value and M₀, so they will become M₀ in the next step.\n\nWait, but we're looking at the current step t=100, not t+1. So at step t=100, the non-M₀ region is an arc of 1824 positions, with the two ends adjacent to the M₀ region. The values in the non-M₀ region are not all the same. In fact, their values depend on the initial configuration and the propagation of the M₀ front.\n\nBut to compute the expected number of distinct values after 100 steps, we need to consider the distribution of the values in the non-M₀ region and how many distinct values exist there, plus the single value M₀.\n\nHowever, this seems complicated. Maybe there's a smarter way.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps all the non-M₀ values after t steps are equal? If that's the case, then the number of distinct values would be 2: M₀ and the value of the non-M₀ region. But in my earlier small examples, that wasn't the case.\n\nWait, in the n=5 case after 1 step, the non-M₀ region had two distinct values (max(a,b,c) and max(b,c,d)), but after the next step, they both got promoted to M₀. So in the intermediate step, they were different, but in the next step, they became the same (M₀). So perhaps at step t, the non-M₀ region has a certain number of distinct values, but at step t+1, those values get \"upgraded\" to M₀ or to some higher value, but not necessarily all to the same value?\n\nWait, actually in the n=5 case at t=1, the non-M₀ region had two distinct values, but at t=2, those values were replaced by M₀. So the distinct values at t=1 were two, but at t=2 it became one.\n\nSimilarly, in the n=6 case at t=2, the non-M₀ region had two distinct values (e and f in my example), but at t=3 they became M₀. So in the intermediate steps, the non-M₀ region can have multiple distinct values, but by the next step, those values get \"pulled up\" to M₀ or to some other value that then gets pulled up further.\n\nTherefore, perhaps at any step t < floor(n/2), the non-M₀ region consists of a set of values that are all less than M₀, and potentially some internal structure, but their exact values depend on the initial configuration.\n\nHowever, calculating the expectation of the number of distinct values in such a setup seems really challenging. Maybe we can find that the expected number of distinct values is 2? Or maybe it's related to the number of steps?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps all the non-M₀ values are equal in expectation? Due to some symmetry?\n\nWait, let's think about the initial configuration. Since all the initial values are independent uniform [0,1], the global maximum M₀ is a random variable, but given M₀ = m, the other values are uniform on [0, m] (since they have to be less than m almost surely). But actually, the other values are independent of each other and of M₀, except that they are all less than M₀ with probability 1.\n\nWait, actually, given that the maximum is M₀, the other values are independent uniform [0, M₀]. But since M₀ itself is a random variable, the joint distribution is a bit complicated.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can