₃, X₄ ≤ X₁ (because X₁ is the global_max), this requires that at least one of X₂, X₃, or X₄ equals X₁. But since all variables are continuous, the probability that any of X₂, X₃, or X₄ equals X₁ is zero. Therefore, in Case 2a and 2b, M1 ≠ M2 almost surely.\n\nTherefore, P(M1 = M2) = P(global_max ∈ {X₂, X₃}).\n\nSince the global_max is equally likely to be any of the four variables (due to symmetry), the probability that the global_max is in {X₂, X₃} is 2/4 = 1/2.\n\nTherefore, P(V = U | M₀ = m) = 1/2.\n\nTherefore, the expected number of distinct values in the non-M₀ region after 1 step is 2 - 1/2 = 3/2.\n\nTherefore, the total expected number of distinct values in the entire circle after 1 step is 1 (for M₀) + 3/2 (for the non-M₀ region) = 5/2 = 2.5.\n\nBut wait, in reality, in the n=5 case, after 1 step, the number of distinct values is either 1 or 2 or 3?\n\nWait, no, in our earlier example with n=5, after 1 step, the non-M₀ region had two distinct values (V and U), so total distinct values were 3: M₀, V, U. But if V=U, then it's 2 distinct values.\n\nTherefore, the expected number is indeed E[1 + indicator(V ≠ U) + indicator(V ≠ M₀) + indicator(U ≠ M₀)] but since V and U are both less than M₀, the indicators for V ≠ M₀ and U ≠ M₀ are always 1, so total distinct values are 1 + 2 - indicator(V=U). Wait, actually:\n\nThe distinct values are:\n\n- M₀,\n\n- V,\n\n- U,\n\nunless V = U, in which case it's just M₀ and V=U.\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct values is 2 if V=U, and 3 otherwise.\n\nTherefore, the expectation is 2 * P(V=U) + 3 * P(V ≠ U) = 3 - P(V=U).\n\nSince P(V=U) = 1/2, then expectation is 3 - 1/2 = 2.5, which matches the calculation above.\n\nTherefore, in the n=5 case, after 1 step, the expected number of distinct values is 2.5.\n\nSimilarly, in the n=4 case, let's see:\n\nAfter 1 step, in the 4-element case, the non-M₀ region has one element (position opposite the initial M₀). The value of that element is the max of its two neighbors from the init