herefore, if the global maximum of A,B,C,D is in B or C, then V1 = V2 = that global maximum.\n\nIf the global maximum is in A or D, then V1 ≠ V2.\n\nTherefore,\n\nP(V1 = V2) = P(global_max of A,B,C,D ∈ {B,C}).\n\nSince all variables are iid uniform, the probability that the global maximum is in any particular position is 1/4. Therefore,\n\nP(V1 = V2) = 2/4 = 1/2.\n\nSimilarly, by symmetry, P(V2 = V3) = 1/2,\n\nand P(V1 = V3) ?\n\nLet’s compute P(V1 = V3).\n\nV1 = max(A,B,C),\n\nV3 = max(C,D,E).\n\nThe overlap between the two triplets is only C.\n\nThe global maximum of A,B,C,D,E must be in the union of {A,B,C} and {C,D,E}, which is all variables except none, but actually the entire set.\n\nWait, the global maximum of all five variables must be in either {A,B,C} or {C,D,E} or both.\n\nWait, V1 = max(A,B,C),\n\nV3 = max(C,D,E),\n\nso V1 = V3 iff the global maximum of A,B,C,D,E is in both triplets or in one of them such that both maxima equal that global maximum.\n\nWait, actually, if the global maximum is in {A,B,C,D,E}, then:\n\nIf the global maximum is in {A,B,C}, then V1 = global_max, and V3 could be less than or equal to global_max, but since global_max is in A,B,C, then V3 = max(C,D,E) ≤ global_max (since global_max is in A,B,C which are separate from D,E unless overlapping at C).\n\nWait, actually, if the global maximum is in A,B,C, then V1 = global_max, and V3 is the max of C,D,E. Since D and E are independent of A,B,C (except for C), the maximum of C,D,E could be less than global_max or equal to C if C happens to be the global_max.\n\nThis is getting too convoluted. Maybe approximate that P(V1 = V3) is also 1/2 by symmetry?\n\nIf we assume symmetry, then P(V1 = V2) = P(V2 = V3) = P(V1 = V3) = 1/2,\n\nthen,\n\nE[number of distinct values] = 1 + [P(V1≠V2) + P(V2≠V3) + P(V1≠V3)] - 2 P(all equal)\n\nAssuming P(V1=V2=V3) is negligible or computable.\n\nBut this is getting too involved. Maybe for the purposes of a competition problem, there is a simpler answer.\n\nW