so a3 remains 0, but a4 becomes 1 because it looks at a3=0, a4=0, and a1=1. So a4_new is max(0,0,1)=1.\n\nSo now after one step: a1=1, a2=1, a3=0, a4=1.\n\nNow, let's do the next step (step 2):\n\nCompute a1_new = max(a4, a1, a2) = max(1,1,1)=1\n\na2_new = max(a1, a2, a3) = max(1,1,0)=1\n\na3_new = max(a2, a3, a4) = max(1,0,1)=1\n\na4_new = max(a3, a4, a1) = max(0,1,1)=1\n\nSo after step 2, all become 1. So in two steps, starting from one 1, we get all 1s in n=4.\n\nHmm, so in n=4, starting with one 1, it takes two steps to get all 1s.\n\nWait, so in n=3, one step; n=4, two steps; maybe in general, for a circle of size n, starting with a single 1 and the rest 0s, it takes ceiling(n/2) steps or something? Wait, let me check n=5.\n\nn=5: starting with a1=1, others 0.\n\nFirst step:\n\na1_new = max(a5, a1, a2) = max(0,1,0)=1\n\na2_new = max(a1, a2, a3) = max(1,0,0)=1\n\na3_new = max(a2, a3, a4) = max(0,0,0)=0\n\na4_new = max(a3, a4, a5) = max(0,0,0)=0\n\na5_new = max(a4, a5, a1) = max(0,0,1)=1\n\nSo after first step: [1,1,0,0,1]\n\nSecond step:\n\na1_new = max(a5, a1, a2) = max(1,1,1)=1\n\na2_new = max(a1, a2, a3) = max(1,1,0)=1\n\na3_new = max(a2, a3, a4) = max(1,0,0)=1\n\na4_new = max(a3, a4, a5) = max(0,0,1)=1\n\na5_new = max(a4, a5, a1) = max(0,1,1)=1\n\nSo after second step, all become 1. So for n=5, starting with one 1, it took two steps to get all 1s? Wait, but n=5 is odd. Wait, in step 1, we had [1,1,0,0,1], so the 0s were in positions 3 and 4. Then in step 2, the 0s got \"infect\" by their neighbors? Let me check a3_new in step 2:\n\na3_new is max(a2, a3, a4). At step 2, a2 was 1 (from step 1), a3 was 0, a4 was 0. So max(1,0,0)=1. Similarly, a4_new is max(a3, a4, a5). At step 2, a3 is 0 (from previous step), a4 is 0, a5 is 1 (from previous step). So max(0,0,1)=1. So yes, so in step 2, all become 1.\n\nHmm, so n=5 took two steps as well? Wait, n=3 took 1 step, n=4 took 2 steps, n=5 took 2 steps? Hmm, maybe the number of steps needed is floor(n/2) or ceiling(n/2