nd the rest are less than M_0.\n\nTherefore, the initial maximum is unique, occurring at a random position with probability uniform over the 2025 positions.\n\nTherefore, without loss of generality, we can assume that the initial maximum is at position 1 (due to rotational symmetry), and multiply by 1 (since all positions are symmetric).\n\nTherefore, we can fix the initial maximum at position 1, and analyze the process accordingly, then the expectation will be the same regardless of the initial position due to symmetry.\n\nTherefore, let's fix the initial maximum at position 1, with value M_0, and all other positions having values uniformly random in [0, M_0).\n\nWait, actually, since all variables are independent and uniform on [0,1], the value at position 1 is M_0, which is the maximum, so the other positions are uniformly distributed in [0, M_0), but conditioned on not exceeding M_0. However, since M_0 itself is a random variable, this complicates things.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can condition on M_0 = m, where m is in [0,1], and then integrate over m.\n\nLet me try this approach.\n\nLet’s denote:\n\nLet M_0 be the initial maximum value, which is a random variable with PDF f_{M_0}(m) = 2025 m^{2024} for 0 ≤ m ≤ 1.\n\nGiven M_0 = m, the initial position of the maximum is uniformly random among the 2025 positions, say position 1 without loss of generality.\n\nThen, the other 2024 positions are independent uniform [0, m) random variables.\n\nWe can then analyze the process given M_0 = m, and then integrate over m.\n\nGiven this, we can consider for a fixed m, the expected number of distinct values after t=100 steps.\n\nDue to the symmetry, we can fix the initial maximum at position 1, and analyze the spread of the maximum from there.\n\nAs previously established, the maximum will spread outward from position 1 at a rate of one position per step in each direction (clockwise and counter-clockwise).\n\nTherefore, after t steps, the maximum will have reached positions 1, 2, ..., t+1 in the clockwise direction, and positions 1, 2025, 2024, ..., 2025 - t in the counter-clockwise direction.\n\nTherefore, the set of positions that have been updated to M_0 after t steps is the union of the intervals [1 - t, 1 + t] modulo 2025.\n\nThe length of this interval is 2t + 1 positions (if t < 2025/2).\n\nThe remaining positions are those not in this interval, which are 2025 - (2t + 1) positions.\n\nHowever, these remaining positions will have values that are updated based on their neighbors, which may still be below M_0.\n\nHowever, their values will be the maximum of their current value and their neighbors. Since their neighbors might also be below M_0, their values could increase towards M_0 but won't reach it until the wavefront arrives.\n\nTherefore, the values in the un updated regions will form a sort of \"ring\" around the updated region, and their values will be updated in a way that propagates inward.\n\nHowever, tracking the exact number of distinct values in this setup is still complex.\n\nBut perhaps, after t steps, the number of distinct values is equal to the number of distinct values in the updated region (which is just M_0) plus the number of distinct values in the unupdated region.\n\nIn the unupdated region, the values are being updated in a way that depends on their neighbors, which are also in the unupdated region or on the edge.\n\nHowever, due to the randomness of the initial configuration, the values in the unupdated region are random variables that are less than M_0 and have been updated based on their neighbors.\n\nBut perhaps, after many steps, the unupdated region's values would form a random process that has some expected number of distinct values.\n\nAlternatively, maybe in the unupdated region, the values are still uniformly distributed or follow some distribution that allows us to compute the expected number of distinct values.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the key insight is that after t steps, the only distinc