 equal. Is that always the case?\n\nWait, let me try a different N=4 configuration where maybe it takes longer. Let me try with a₁=0.1, a₂=0.2, a₃=0.1, a₄=0.2.\n\nFirst step:\n\na₁' = max(a₄, a₁, a₂) = max(0.2, 0.1, 0.2) = 0.2\n\na₂' = max(a₁, a₂, a₃) = max(0.1, 0.2, 0.1) = 0.2\n\na₃' = max(a₂, a₃, a₄) = max(0.2, 0.1, 0.2) = 0.2\n\na₄' = max(a₃, a₄, a₁) = max(0.1, 0.2, 0.1) = 0.2\n\nSo after first step, all are 0.2. So that only took one step.\n\nHmm, so is there a configuration where it takes three steps?\n\nWait, let's see. Let me try to have a configuration where after step 1, it's not all equal, after step 2, still not all equal, and then step 3?\n\nSuppose we have a₁=0.1, a₂=0.3, a₃=0.2, a₄=0.4.\n\nFirst step:\n\na₁' = max(a₄, a₁, a₂) = max(0.4, 0.1, 0.3) = 0.4\n\na₂' = max(a₁, a₂, a₃) = max(0.1, 0.3, 0.2) = 0.3\n\na₃' = max(a₂, a₃, a₄) = max(0.3, 0.2, 0.4) = 0.4\n\na₄' = max(a₃, a₄, a₁) = max(0.2, 0.4, 0.1) = 0.4\n\nSo after step 1: [0.4, 0.3, 0.4, 0.4]\n\nStep 2:\n\na₁'' = max(a₄', a₁', a₂') = max(0.4, 0.4, 0.3) = 0.4\n\na₂'' = max(a₁', a₂', a₃') = max(0.4, 0.3, 0.4) = 0.4\n\na₃'' = max(a₂', a₃', a₄') = max(0.3, 0.4, 0.4) = 0.4\n\na₄'' = max(a₃', a₄', a₁') = max(0.4, 0.4, 0.4) = 0.4\n\nSo after step 2, all are 0.4. So again, two steps.\n\nHmm, so maybe for N=4, two steps is the maximum required to reach all equal. Let me try to see if there is a configuration where it takes three steps. Let me try:\n\nSuppose a₁=0.1, a₂=0.9, a₃=0.1, a₄=0.9.\n\nWait, that's similar to an earlier one. After first step, all become 0.9. So one step.\n\nAnother idea: let's have a₁=0.1, a₂=0.2, a₃=0.3, a₄=0.4. Wait, that was the first example, which took two steps.\n\nAlternatively, maybe starting with a₁=0.1, a₂=0.4, a₃=0.2, a₄=0.3.\n\nFirst step:\n\na₁' = max(a₄, a₁, a₂) = max(0.3, 0.1, 0.4) = 0.4\n\na₂' = max(a₁, a₂, a₃) = max(0.1, 0.4, 0.2) = 0.4\n\na₃' = max(a₂, a₃, a₄) = max(0.4, 0.2, 0.3) = 0.4\n\na₄' = max(a₃, a₄, a₁) = max(0.2, 0.3, 0.1) = 0.3\n\nSo after first step: [0.4, 0.4, 0.4, 0.3]\n\nSecond step:\n\na₁'' = max(a₄', a₁', a₂') = max(0.3, 0.4, 0.4) = 0.4\n\na₂'' = max(a₁', a₂', a₃') = max(0.4, 0.4, 0.4) = 0.4\n\na₃'' = max(a₂', a₃', a₄') = max(0.4, 0.4, 0.3) = 0.4\n\na₄'' = max(a₃', a₄', a₁') = max(0.4, 0.3, 0.4) = 0.4\n\nSo after step 2: all 0.4. So again, two steps.\n\nHmm, so in all my trials with N=4, it takes at most two steps to reach all equal. Therefore, perhaps for N=4, after two steps, all numbers are equal, so after any number of steps beyond that, they remain equal. Therefore, for N=4, after 100 seconds, the number of distinct values is 1. Wait, but is that always the case?\n\nWait, let me try a different configuration where maybe after two steps, it's not all equal? Let me try:\n\nLet me set a₁=0.1, a₂=0.3, a₃=0.5, a₄=0.2.\n\nFirst step:\n\na₁' = max(a₄, a₁, a₂) = max(0.2, 0.1, 0.3) = 0.3\n\na₂' = max(a₁, a₂, a₃) = max(0.1, 0.3, 0.5) = 0.5\n\na₃' = max(a₂, a₃, a₄) = max(0.3, 0.5, 0.2) = 0.5\n\na₄' = max(a₃, a₄, a₁) = max(0.5, 0.2, 0.1) = 0.5\n\nSo after first step: [0.3, 0.5, 0.5, 0.5]\n\nSecond step:\n\na₁'' = max(a₄', a₁', a₂') = max(0.5, 0.3, 0.5) = 0.5\n\na₂'' = max(a₁', a₂', a₃') = max(0.3, 0.5, 0.5) = 0.5\n\na₃'' = max(a₂', a₃', a₄') = max(0.5, 0.5, 0.5) = 0.5\n\na₄'' = max(a₃', a₄', a₁') = max(0.5, 0.5, 0.3) = 0.5\n\nSo again, after two steps, all are 0.5. Hmm.\n\nWait, so maybe for N=4, regardless of the starting configuration, after two steps, all numbers become equal? If so, then for N=4, after two steps, it's always 1. Then, for N=2025, perhaps after some logarithmic number of steps, all the numbers become equal? But 2025 is a large number, so maybe after O(log N) steps, they all become equal? But the problem is asking for after 100 seconds, which is a fixed number, so perhaps for N=2025, after 100 steps, the number of distinct values is still 1? But that seems too hasty.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the process converges to a single value very quickly, but for N=2025, maybe 100 steps is enough for all the values to become equal? But how can we know?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the number of distinct values decreases in some predictable way each step, so that after 100 steps, it's approaching 1, but perhaps not exactly 1? Wait, but in the N=4 case, it becomes 1 after 2 steps, so if N=2025, maybe it takes O(N) steps? But 100 is much smaller than 2025. Hmm.\n\nWait, perhaps my approach with small N is not sufficient. Maybe I need to think more generally.\n\nLet me consider the process in general for N elements arranged in a circle. Each step, every element is replaced by the maximum of itself and its two neighbors. So, in other words, each element is being updated to be at least as large as itself and its two neighbors. Wait, but actually, it's the maximum of itself and its two neighbors, so it's the maximum of three values: left neighbor, itself, right neighbor. Therefore, each element is set to the maximum of its three neighbors (including itself). Therefore, each element is non-decreasing over time, right? Because you're taking the maximum each time, so once an element becomes some value, in the next step, it can only stay the same or increase, since it's the max of its neighbors and itself. Therefore, all the values are non-decreasing over time. Moreover, since they are all non-decreasing, once a value reaches a certain level, it can't decrease afterward. Therefore, the process is monotonic in the sense that all values are non-decreasing over time.\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct values can only decrease or stay the same over time? Wait, actually, no. Wait, since the values are non-decreasing, but two adjacent values could potentially \"jump\" over each other? Hmm, not sure. Let me think.\n\nSuppose we have two adjacent elements, a and b. Suppose a < b. Then, in the next step, a will be replaced by the max of its left neighbor, itself, and its right neighbor. Since a is less than b, and the right neighbor is b, then the new a will be at least b, right? Because the right neighbor is b, so the max of (left neighbor, a, b) is at least b. Therefore, if a < b, then the new a will be at least b. Therefore, the new a will be ≥ b, and since b was already ≥ a, now a becomes ≥ b. So the new a is ≥ b. Therefore, after one step, the left neighbor of b (which was a) becomes at least as large as b. Therefore, in the next step, the value that was b will now have a left neighbor that is at least b, so when we update b, it will become the max of its left neighbor (which is now ≥ b), itself (b), and its right neighbor. Therefore, the new b will be at least the left neighbor, which is ≥ b, so the new b will be ≥ the old left neighbor, which was ≥ b. So it's non-decreasing as well.\n\nTherefore, in effect, any time a value is smaller than its neighbor, it will get \"pushed up\" to at least the value of its neighbor in the next step, and then the neighbor may also get pushed up further. Therefore, the effect is that high values tend to spread to their neighbors over time, while low values get \"elevated\" to at least the level of their neighbors.\n\nTherefore, over time, the maximum value in the entire circle will propagate to all positions. Because once the maximum value is present at some position, in the next step, its left and right neighbors will be updated to at least that maximum value, and then in the following steps, those neighbors will in turn spread the maximum value further. Since the process is simultaneous, the maximum value can spread to all neighbors in one step, and then to their neighbors in the next step, etc., like a wave.\n\nTherefore, the maximum value will eventually \"infect\" all positions in the circle. The question is, how many steps does it take for the maximum value to reach all positions?\n\nIn a circle of N positions, the maximum value can spread to adjacent nodes each step. So starting from the position where the maximum initially is, in step 1, the two neighbors of that position will have at least the maximum value, in step 2, the neighbors of those neighbors (i.e., two steps away from the original maximum) will also have the maximum value, and so on. Therefore, the maximum value will propagate outward at a rate of one position per step in each direction. Therefore, the time it takes for the maximum to reach all positions is roughly N/2 steps, since the circle has diameter N (the maximum distance between any two nodes is floor(N/2)). Wait, actually, in a circle of N nodes, the maximum distance between two nodes is floor(N/2). Therefore, to propagate the maximum value from one node to the opposite side of the circle would take floor(N/2) steps. Therefore, in general, the time required for all nodes to have the maximum value is O(N). Therefore, for N=2025, it would take about 1012.5 steps to propagate the maximum to all nodes, but since we are only doing 100 steps, which is much less than 1012.5, then after 100 steps, the maximum value has only spread to a certain portion of the circle, but not all.\n\nWait, but perhaps my reasoning is flawed here? Let me think again.\n\nSuppose we have the maximum value M located at position k. Then, in the first step, positions k-1 and k+1 (mod N) will be updated to at least M (since they are neighbors of k, and the value at k is M). Therefore, after step 1, positions k-1 and k+1 have at least M. Then, in step 2, the neighbors of positions k-1 and k+1 (i.e., positions k-2, k, and k+2) will be updated. Specifically, position k-2 will be updated to the max of its left neighbor (position k-3), itself, and its right neighbor (position k-1). But position k-1 is already at least M, so position k-2 will be at least M. Similarly, position k+2 will be updated to at least M. Position k itself is already M, so it stays M. Therefore, after step 2, positions k-2, k, k+2 are at least M. Similarly, step 3 would propagate M to k-3 and k+3, etc.\n\nTherefore, the spread of the maximum value is indeed one step per iteration in each direction. Therefore, after t steps, the maximum value has spread to all positions within distance t from the original maximum position. Since the circle has diameter floor(N/2), to cover the entire circle, you need t = floor(N/2) steps. Therefore, for N=2025, which is odd, the diameter is 1012 (since 2025/2 = 1012.5, so floor(2025/2)=1012). Therefore, to cover the entire circle, you need 1012 steps. Since we are only doing 100 steps, which is much less than 1012, the maximum value has only spread to a small portion of the circle. Therefore, after 100 steps, the maximum value is present in a contiguous block of 2*100 +1 positions? Wait, actually, let's think again.\n\nStarting from position k, after t steps, how many positions have the maximum value?\n\nAt step 0: only position k has M.\n\nAt step 1: positions k-1 and k+1 have M.\n\nAt step 2: positions k-2, k, k+2 have M.\n\nAt step t: positions from k - t to k + t (mod N) have M? Wait, actually, no. Because each step, the influence spreads one step outward in both directions. So after t steps, the maximum value has reached positions k - t, k - t +1, ..., k + t, but in a circular fashion. However, since it's a circle, if t exceeds N/2, then it wraps around. But for t less than N/2, it's a contiguous block of 2t +1 positions? Wait, actually, at step t, the number of positions with value M is 2t +1? Let me see with small N.\n\nTake N=5 (a pentagon). Suppose the maximum is at position 1.\n\nStep 0: [M, x, x, x, x]\n\nStep 1: [M, M, x, x, x] ? Wait no:\n\nWait, actually, let me do N=5 step by step.\n\nLet me label positions 1 through 5 in a circle.\n\nSuppose position 1 has M, and the rest are 0 for simplicity.\n\nInitial: [M, 0, 0, 0, 0]\n\nStep 1:\n\nPosition 1: max(a5, a1, a2) = max(0, M, 0) = M\n\nPosition 2: max(a1, a2, a3) = max(M, 0, 0) = M\n\nPosition 3: max(a2, a3, a4) = max(0, 0, 0) = 0\n\nPosition 4: max(a3, a4, a5) = max(0, 0, 0) = 0\n\nPosition 5: max(a4, a5, a1) = max(0, 0, M) = M\n\nSo after step 1: [M, M, 0, 0, M]\n\nSo positions 1,2,5 have M; position 3 and 4 are 0.\n\nStep 2:\n\nPosition 1: max(a5, a1, a2) = max(M, M, M) = M\n\nPosition 2: max(a1, a2, a3) = max(M, M, 0) = M\n\nPosition 3: max(a2, a3, a4) = max(M, 0, 0) = M\n\nPosition 4: max(a3, a4, a5) = max(0, 0, M) = M\n\nPosition 5: max(a4, a5, a1) = max(0, M, M) = M\n\nSo after step 2: all positions are M. So for N=5, starting with a single M, it takes 2 steps to spread to all positions.\n\nWait, so here N=5, diameter is 2 (since floor(5/2)=2), and it took 2 steps to cover all. So in this case, the number of steps needed is equal to the diameter. So in general, for a circle of N nodes, the maximum distance between any two nodes is floor(N/2), so to cover the entire circle, it would take floor(N/2) steps. So for even N, say N=4, the diameter is 2 (since floor(4/2)=2), and in my N=4 example, it took 2 steps to cover all. For N=5, floor(5/2)=2, and it took 2 steps. For N=3, diameter 1, and it took 1 step. For N=2, diameter 1, took 1 step. So that seems to hold.\n\nTherefore, in general, for a circle of N nodes, the maximum value will spread to the entire circle in floor(N/2) steps. Therefore, for N=2025, which is odd, floor(2025/2) = 1012. Therefore, it would take 1012 steps for the maximum value to propagate to all nodes. Since we are only doing 100 steps, which is much less than 1012, after 100 steps, the maximum value has only spread to a certain range around its original position.\n\nSpecifically, after t steps, the maximum value has spread to a contiguous arc of length 2t + 1 around the original maximum position. Wait, in the N=5 example, after 1 step, the maximum had spread to positions 1,2,5 (which is 3 positions, which is 2*1 +1). After 2 steps, it spread to all 5 positions. So yes, after t steps, the maximum has spread to an arc of length 2t +1. Therefore, in general, after t steps, the maximum value is present in an interval of 2t +1 consecutive nodes, centered at the original position of the maximum.\n\nTherefore, in our case, with N=2025 and t=100, the maximum value would have spread to an arc of length 2*100 +1 = 201 positions. Since 201 is much smaller than 2025, the majority of the circle still doesn't have the maximum value. Therefore, after 100 steps, there are still many positions that haven't reached the maximum value yet.\n\nHowever, note that the values are non-decreasing over time. So once a position has a value less than the maximum, it can only increase over time, but it won't exceed the maximum. Therefore, the values in the circle will eventually approach the maximum value, but they might not all reach it in finite steps unless the propagation has had enough time.\n\nTherefore, after 100 steps, the values in the circle will have increased, but only in a limited region near the original maximum. The rest of the circle may still be at their initial values or have increased slightly.\n\nBut how does this affect the number of distinct values?\n\nWell, since the values are non-decreasing, once a value is updated, it can only stay the same or increase. Therefore, the set of values present in the circle can only increase over time? Wait, not necessarily. Because even though individual values are non-decreasing, the set of values might decrease in cardinality if some values get \"absorbed\" into others. For example, if two values become equal, then the number of distinct values decreases.\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct values can both increase and decrease over time? Wait, no. Wait, actually, since values can only increase, the set of values present in the system can only stay the same or increase in terms of the minimum value? Wait, not exactly. Let me think.\n\nSuppose we have two values, a and b, with a < b. If a increases to a' ≥ b, then the two values become equal (if a' = b) or a' > b, so the set of values loses the distinction between a and b, or gains a new value a' > b. But since b is also non-decreasing, b can only stay the same or increase. Therefore, if a increases past b, then the value b may also increase, so the new values could be higher than before.\n\nAlternatively, if a increases to a' = b, then a and b become equal, so the number of distinct values might decrease. Therefore, the number of distinct values can both increase and decrease over time, depending on the updates.\n\nHowever, in the long run, as the maximum value propagates, eventually all values will become equal to the maximum, so the number of distinct values will decrease to 1. But we are only looking at 100 steps, which is before that happens for N=2025.\n\nTherefore, to compute the expected number of distinct values after 100 steps, we need to model how the values evolve and how the distinctness propagates.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there is a pattern or formula for the expected number of distinct values after t steps for a circular system like this.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps after each step, the number of distinct values decreases by a certain expectation, but I need to think more carefully.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can model this as a Markov chain, where the state is the set of distinct values, but that seems intractable for N=2025.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there is some symmetry or property we can exploit.\n\nWait, since all the initial values are independent uniform [0,1] random variables, maybe the process has some symmetry such that the expected number of distinct values only depends on the number of steps and the size N in a simple way?\n\nAlternatively, maybe after each step, the values become \"coarser\" in some sense, and the number of distinct values reduces by a factor each time? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, since each value is being replaced by the max of itself and its neighbors, which are random variables, perhaps the expected value at each position increases over time, but the variance decreases?\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can consider the expectation of the number of distinct values as the sum over all pairs of positions of the probability that those two positions have different values after 100 steps. Then, using linearity of expectation, even though the events are dependent, the expectation of the number of distinct values is equal to the sum over all pairs of positions of the probability that the two positions have different values.\n\nWait, actually, that is a standard technique. Let me recall:\n\nLet D be the number of distinct values after 100 steps. Then,\n\nD = sum_{i=1}^{2025} I_i,\n\nwhere I_i is an indicator that the value at position i is different from all other values. Wait, no, that's not exactly right. Alternatively, the standard formula for the number of distinct values is:\n\nD = 1 + sum_{1 ≤ i < j ≤ 2025} X_{i,j},\n\nwhere X_{i,j} is 1 if a_i ≠ a_j after 100 steps, and 0 otherwise. Wait, actually, that would count each pair once, and then add 1 for the fact that there is at least one value. Wait, actually, no, let me think again.\n\nActually, the number of distinct values D is equal to the sum over all subsets S of the positions of the indicator that all positions in S have the same value and no other position has that value. But that is complicated. Alternatively, the more straightforward approach is:\n\nThe number of distinct values D is equal to the sum over all positions i of the indicator that a_i is the minimum among all values, plus the indicator that a_i is the next minimum, etc., but that also seems complicated.\n\nHowever, there is a better way using indicator variables for pairs. Specifically, the expected number of distinct values can be written as:\n\nE[D] = sum_{i=1}^{N} E[I_i],\n\nwhere I_i is the indicator that the value at position i is unique in the sense that no other position has the same value. Wait, no, that is not correct because uniqueness is not required; distinct values just need to be different from each other. Actually, the standard formula is:\n\nE[D] = sum_{v} P(v is present),\n\nbut since the values are continuous, the probability that any particular value is present is 1 (since it's a continuous distribution, every value is present with probability 1, but actually no—wait, no, actually, the values are random variables, so the set of values present is a random subset of [0,1]. The expectation of the number of distinct values is the integral over [0,1] of the probability that a given value v is present, integrated over v from 0 to 1. But since the variables are continuous, the probability that any specific v is exactly equal to another is zero, but the presence of a value v in the system is certain if at least one position has that value. Wait, actually, since the initial values are continuous, the probability that any particular value v is exactly achieved by some position is 1, because for any v in [0,1], there's a positive probability that some initial a_i equals v, and since the process is deterministic, if v is achieved by some position at some step, it remains so or becomes higher. Wait, actually, no. Wait, actually, once a position reaches a value higher than v, it can never go back down, so if a position ever exceeds v, it will never be v again. But since the initial values are continuous, the probability that any particular position ever reaches exactly v is zero? Hmm, this is getting confusing.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use the fact that for continuous random variables, the probability that any two positions have exactly the same value after any finite number of steps is zero. Wait, but that can't be, because even though the initial values are continuous, after some deterministic operations, the values could become equal with positive probability? Wait, actually, no. Let me think carefully.\n\nSuppose we have two positions, say position 1 and position 2. The value at position 1 after t steps is a deterministic function of the initial values a₁, a₂, ..., a_N. Similarly for position 2. Since the initial values are continuous, the functions mapping the initial values to the final values are continuous functions. Therefore, the probability that two such functions output the same value is the probability that two continuous functions of independent continuous variables are equal, which is zero, because the set of initial configurations where they are equal is a lower-dimensional subset of the initial configuration space. Therefore, for any two positions, the probability that they have the same value after t steps is zero. Therefore, the number of distinct values after t steps is almost surely equal to N, since no two positions have the same value? But that contradicts our earlier examples where after one step in N=3, all three positions became equal. Wait, but in those examples, we started with specific numbers, but in the problem, the initial numbers are continuous uniform random variables. Wait a second, in my earlier examples with N=3, if I start with three independent uniform [0,1] variables, then after one step, all three become the maximum of the three initial values. Therefore, in that case, after one step, all three positions have the same value (the maximum of the three initial values). Therefore, in that case, the probability that all three are equal is 1? Wait, but in reality, the maximum of three independent uniform variables is a single value, so after one step, all three positions become that maximum value, so they are all equal. Therefore, in that case, the probability that all three are equal after one step is 1. So in that case, even though the initial values are continuous, after one step, they become equal with probability 1. Therefore, my previous reasoning about continuous functions must be flawed.\n\nAh! The key point is that although the initial values are continuous, the transformation is deterministic and can collapse multiple continuous variables into a single value. For example, in the N=3 case, after one step, all three positions become the maximum of the three initial values, so they are forced to be equal. Therefore, even though the initial values are continuous, the transformation can make them equal with probability 1. So in that case, the probability that two positions are equal after one step is 1, hence the number of distinct values is 1 with probability 1.\n\nTherefore, my earlier thought that the probability of two positions being equal is zero is incorrect in this context because the transformation can create dependencies that force equality with probability 1.\n\nTherefore, in some cases, even with continuous initial conditions, the transformed variables can become equal with probability 1. Therefore, we have to be careful.\n\nTherefore, in the N=3 case, after one step, the number of distinct values is 1 almost surely. Similarly, in the N=4 case, after two steps, the number of distinct values is 1 almost surely. Wait, but in my N=4 example with starting values [0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4], after two steps, all became 0.4, which is the maximum. Wait, but in that case, the maximum was 0.4 initially, so after step 1, the first position became 0.4, and then in step 2, everything became 0.4. Wait, but actually, in that example, the initial maximum was 0.4 (at position 4). Then in step 1, position 1 became the max of a₄ (0.4), a₁ (0.1), a₂ (0.2), so 0.4. Then position 4 in step 1 became the max of a₃ (0.3), a₄ (0.4), and a₁ (0.1), which is 0.4. So after step 1, positions 1 and 4 are 0.4, while positions 2 and 3 are 0.3 and 0.4? Wait, no, in my earlier calculation, after step 1 for that example, we had [0.4, 0.3, 0.4, 0.4]. So position 2 was 0.3. Then in step 2, position 2 became the max of a₁ (0.4), a₂ (0.3), a₃ (0.4), which is 0.4. So in step 2, all become 0.4. So in that case, after two steps, all are equal. But in that case, the initial maximum was 0.4, so after two steps, everything became the initial maximum.\n\nWait a second, in that case, actually, in step 1, the maximum value (0.4) spread to position 1 and position 4's neighbor? Wait, position 4's neighbors are position 3 and position 1. Position 4 was already 0.4, so its neighbors: position 3 was 0.3, so position 3's value after step 1 becomes the max of a₂ (0.2), a₃ (0.3), a₄ (0.4), which is 0.4. Similarly, position 1's value after step 1 is the max of a₄ (0.4), a₁ (0.1), a₂ (0.2), which is 0.4. So actually, in step 1, positions 1, 3, and 4 become 0.4. Position 2 remains at 0.3. Then in step 2, position 2 is updated to the max of a₁ (0.4), a₂ (0.3), a₃ (0.4), which is 0.4. So after step 2, all are 0.4. Therefore, the maximum value (0.4) was already present at position 4 initially, and after one step, it spread to positions 1, 3, and 4, then in the next step to position 2 as well. Therefore, the spreading happened in two steps because the maximum was initially at position 4, and it took two steps to reach position 2, which is two steps away in the circle (since in a 4-node circle, the distance from 4 to 2 is two steps: 4 -> 1 -> 2). Wait, actually, in a circle of 4, the distance from 4 to 2 is two steps clockwise (4 to 1 to 2) or two steps counterclockwise (4 to 3 to 2). So the maximum distance is two, hence two steps needed.\n\nBut in the case of N=3, the maximum distance is 1, so it takes one step to propagate to all nodes. So in general, for a circle of N nodes, the time needed for the maximum value to propagate to all nodes is equal to the maximum distance between any node and the original maximum node, which is floor(N/2). Therefore, for a general N, after floor(N/2) steps, all nodes will have the maximum value, hence the number of distinct values becomes 1.\n\nBut in our problem, N=2025, which is odd, so floor(N/2) = 1012. Therefore, after 1012 steps, all values become equal, so after 100 steps, which is much less than 1012, the values have not yet all converged to a single value. Therefore, we need to compute the expected number of distinct values after 100 steps.\n\nGiven that, perhaps we can model the expected number of distinct values by considering that each value is a non-decreasing process, and the distinctness is determined by whether neighboring values have \"caught up\" to each other.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the key insight is that after t steps, each position's value is the maximum of its original value and the values of its neighbors up to t steps away? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, since each step propagates the maximum value outward, maybe after t steps, the value at each position is the maximum of the original value at that position and the values within a certain distance t from it? Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think in terms of the maximum value propagation. Let me denote M_t as the maximum value present in the system at time t. Since all values are non-decreasing, M_t is non-decreasing over time, and eventually becomes constant once all values have reached the global maximum.\n\nAt each step, the set of positions that have the current maximum M_t can expand by one step in each direction each time. Therefore, after t steps, the number of positions that have the current maximum is at least 2t +1 (assuming it started from a single position). Wait, similar to the earlier example.\n\nHowever, since the initial maximum could be anywhere, and there might be multiple local maxima, this complicates things. But in expectation, perhaps we can assume that the initial maximum is a single point, and due to symmetry, the expected behavior is the same regardless of where the initial maximum is.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the expected number of distinct values after t steps is N - 2t, but that seems too simplistic and probably incorrect.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps after t steps, the number of distinct values is equal to the number of \"islands\" of values that have not yet been reached by the maximum propagation. But this is vague.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can think recursively. Let me consider smaller N and see if I can find a pattern.\n\nTake N=1: trivial, only one value, so the number of distinct values is always 1. But the problem states N=2025, so not helpful.\n\nN=2: After 1 step, both become the maximum of the two initial values. Therefore, after 1 step, they are equal, so the number of distinct values is 1 for any t ≥1. Since 100 ≥1, so E[D] =1.\n\nN=3: After 1 step, all become the maximum of the three initial values, so D=1 for any t ≥1. Therefore, E[D]=1.\n\nN=4: As we saw, after 2 steps, all become equal, so for t ≥2, D=1. Therefore, for t=100, which is ≥2, E[D]=1.\n\nWait a second, so for N=2,3,4, after a small number of steps (1,1,2 respectively), the number of distinct values becomes 1 and stays there. Therefore, for N=2025, maybe after some small number of steps (like up to 1012), the number of distinct values becomes 1, and for t ≥ that number, it's 1. Since 100 is much less than 1012, then for N=2025, after 100 steps, the number of distinct values is still greater than 1?\n\nWait, but in the N=4 case, after 2 steps it becomes 1, so for N=4 and t=100, it's 1. Similarly, for N=3, it's 1 for any t≥1. For N=2, it's 1 for t≥1.\n\nWait, but what about N=5? For N=5, as we saw, after 2 steps, it becomes all maximum. So for N=5, after 2 steps, D=1. Therefore, for any t≥2, D=1.\n\nWait, so in general, for any N, after floor(N/2) steps, the number of distinct values becomes 1. Therefore, for N=2025, after 1012 steps, it becomes 1. Since 100 < 1012, then after 100 steps, the number of distinct values is greater than 1.\n\nTherefore, the question reduces to: For a circle of N=2025 elements, each updated by taking the maximum of itself and its two neighbors every second, starting from independent uniform [0,1] initial values, what is the expected number of distinct values after 100 steps?\n\nHmm. So perhaps the key is that after t steps, the number of distinct values is equal to N minus the number of positions that have been \"covered\" by the maximum propagation? Not exactly, because multiple maxima can exist.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the expected number of distinct values after t steps is N minus 2t? But in N=4, after 2 steps, it's 1, which would be 4 - 2*2=0, which is not correct. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the expected number of distinct values decreases linearly with t? For N=4, after 0 steps, the expected number of distinct values is 4, since all are independent uniform variables, so the expected number of distinct values in N independent uniforms is known? Wait, actually, for continuous uniform variables, the probability that any two are equal is zero, so the expected number of distinct values is N? But that can't be, because in reality, for continuous variables, the probability that any two are equal is zero, so the number of distinct values is almost surely N, but in our case, after one step, for N=3, it becomes 1. Wait, but in the continuous case, the probability that all three become equal after one step is 1? Wait, no, in the continuous case, the initial values are continuous, so the probability that the maximum of three independent uniforms is equal to two others is actually zero? Wait, confusion arises again.\n\nWait, let's clarify with N=3. Suppose we have three independent uniform [0,1] variables a, b, c. Then after one step, each becomes the maximum of the three. Therefore, all three become the same value, which is the maximum of a, b, c. Therefore, in this case, the final value is a single value (the maximum), so the number of distinct values is 1 almost surely. Even though the initial values were continuous, the transformation forces them all to be equal. Therefore, in this case, the expected number of distinct values is 1.\n\nSimilarly, for N=4, after two steps, it becomes 1 almost surely, so the expected number is 1. Therefore, for any N, after floor(N/2) steps, it becomes 1 almost surely, so the expected number is 1.\n\nBut for t < floor(N/2), the number of distinct values is greater than 1. So for N=2025 and t=100, which is much less than floor(2025/2)=1012, we need to compute the expectation.\n\nPerhaps the process can be modeled such that after t steps, the number of distinct values is equal to the number of \"active\" regions that haven't yet been overtaken by the maximum propagation. Alternatively, since the maximum propagates outward at one step per iteration, after t steps, the number of distinct values would be N - 2t, but this seems too simplistic and likely incorrect because multiple maxima can emerge and interact.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps each step reduces the number of distinct values by a certain expected amount. For instance, in the first step, how does the number of distinct values change?\n\nLet me consider N=4 and t=1. After one step, what is the expected number of distinct values?\n\nStarting with four independent uniform [0,1] variables a, b, c, d.\n\nAfter one step:\n\na' = max(a, b, d)\n\nb' = max(a, b, c)\n\nc' = max(b, c, d)\n\nd' = max(c, d, a)\n\nWe need to compute the expected number of distinct values among a', b', c', d'.\n\nThis seems complex, but perhaps we can compute it for small N and then look for a pattern.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps for the general case, after t steps, the expected number of distinct values is N - 2t. But for N=4, at t=1, this would predict 4 - 2(1)=2, but let's see.\n\nTake N=4 and compute E[D] after 1 step.\n\nLet me denote the four initial variables as A, B, C, D ~ Uniform(0,1), independent.\n\nAfter one step:\n\nA' = max(A, B, D)\n\nB' = max(A, B, C)\n\nC' = max(B, C, D)\n\nD' = max(C, D, A)\n\nWe need E[D'], where D' is the number of distinct values among A', B', C', D'.\n\nThis seems complicated, but perhaps we can compute it by considering symmetry.\n\nFirst, note that due to the symmetry of the problem, the distribution of the four variables after one step is the same regardless of their initial arrangement, so we can consider the variables in a symmetric fashion.\n\nHowever, calculating the expectation directly might be difficult. Maybe we can compute the probability that any two of the new variables are equal, then use inclusion-exclusion?\n\nThe expected number of distinct values is equal to 4 - sum_{i < j} P(A'_i = A'_j) + sum_{i < j < k} P(A'_i = A'_j = A'_k) - ... + (-1)^{n+1} P(all equal)}.\n\nBut this is very complicated for N=4. Alternatively, perhaps approximate or find a pattern.\n\nAlternatively, consider that in the first step, each new value is the maximum of three of the original variables. Since the original variables are independent uniforms, the distribution of each new variable is the distribution of the maximum of three uniforms, which is a known distribution: the maximum of k independent uniform [0,1] variables has a probability density function f(x) = k x^{k-1} for 0 ≤ x ≤1.\n\nTherefore, each of A', B', C', D' is distributed as the maximum of three uniforms, but they are not independent, since they are all functions of the original variables.\n\nHowever, perhaps we can approximate the expectation?\n\nAlternatively, note that after one step, each position's value is the maximum of three original variables. Since the original variables are independent, the expected value of each A' is 3/4 (since E[max(X,Y,Z)] for X,Y,Z ~ U(0,1) is 3/4). However, the expected number of distinct values is not directly related to the expected values of the individual variables.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider that after each step, the number of distinct values can only decrease or stay the same? Wait, no. Since values can increase, but distinctness can both increase or decrease. For example, if two values become equal, the number of distinct values decreases, but if two values diverge, it could increase. However, in this process, since all values are non-decreasing, if two values were previously equal and then one increases while the other stays the same, then they become different, increasing the number of distinct values. Wait, but actually, if two values are equal at some step, and then in the next step, one of them increases while the other doesn't, then they become different, so the number of distinct values would increase by 1. Conversely, if two values are different and then both increase such that they become equal, then the number decreases by 1.\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct values can both increase and decrease over time, making it difficult to model directly.\n\nGiven the complexity, perhaps there's a different approach inspired by the problem's structure.\n\nNotice that in each step, every value is replaced by the maximum of itself and its two neighbors. This operation is similar to a cellular automaton with a specific rule. In such systems, sometimes the number of distinct values can be analyzed by considering that the values \"smooth out\" over time, but in this case, since it's a maximum operation, it's more like information propagating rather than smoothing.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think in terms of the minimum number of steps required for the system to stabilize, but we need the expected number after 100 steps.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the key is that after t steps, each position's value is the maximum of its original value and the values within a distance t of it in the circle. Therefore, the value at position i after t steps is the maximum of a_i and the values in the interval [i - t, i + t] (modulo N). If that's the case, then the value at each position is the maximum of a window of size 2t +1 centered at i.\n\nIf this is true, then after t steps, the value at each position is the maximum over a window of 2t+1 consecutive original values. Therefore, the entire system's values after t steps are determined by the maximum over windows of size 2t+1 in the original circle.\n\nIf this is the case, then the number of distinct values after t steps is equal to the number of distinct maximums over all such windows.\n\nHowever, the windows overlap, so the maximum of one window might be the same as another window's maximum.\n\nBut if this is the case, then the number of distinct values after t steps would be equal to the number of distinct local maxima in the original circle when considering windows of size 2t+1. However, since the original values are random, this might have a certain expectation.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the expected number of distinct values after t steps is equal to N - 2t, but this is just a guess.\n\nWait, let's see with N=4 and t=1. If the formula N - 2t were correct, then it would give 4 - 2 = 2. Let's see if that's plausible.\n\nSuppose we have four independent uniforms A,B,C,D. After one step, each new value is the maximum of three original values. Let me compute the expected number of distinct values among A', B', C', D'.\n\nFirst, note that each of A', B', C', D' is the maximum of three of the four original variables. Let me denote the original variables as X, Y, Z, W for simplicity.\n\nSo:\n\nA' = max(X, Y, W)\n\nB' = max(X, Y, Z)\n\nC' = max(Y, Z, W)\n\nD' = max(Z, W, X)\n\nDue to symmetry, the distribution of A', B', C', D' are all the same, so we can compute the probability that any two of them are equal and then use that to compute the expectation.\n\nLet me compute E[D'] = 4 - sum_{i < j} P(A'_i = A'_j) + sum_{i < j < k} P(A'_i = A'_j = A'_k) - ... + (-1)^{4+1} P(all equal)}.\n\nBut this is going to be complicated. Alternatively, approximate.\n\nFirst, compute P(A' = B'). Let's compute P(max(X,Y,W) = max(X,Y,Z)).\n\nNote that:\n\nA' = max(X,Y,W)\n\nB' = max(X,Y,Z)\n\nTherefore, A' = B' if and only if max(X,Y,W) = max(X,Y,Z), which occurs when the maximum of X,Y,W is equal to the maximum of X,Y,Z. Since Z and W are being compared in the two maxima, this requires that either Z ≥ W or W ≥ Z, but actually, more precisely, the maximum of X,Y,Z and the maximum of X,Y,W must be equal. That is, the larger of Z and W must be less than or equal to the maximum of X,Y, and the smaller of Z and W must be greater than or equal to the maximum of X,Y? Wait, no, perhaps better to think:\n\nLet M = max(X,Y). Then, A' = max(M, W) and B' = max(M, Z). Therefore, A' = B' if and only if max(M, W) = max(M, Z). This occurs when either:\n\n1. W ≥ Z and W ≤ M, so that A' = M and B' = M, so they are equal.\n\nOr\n\n2. Z ≥ W and Z ≤ M, so that A' = M and B' = M, so they are equal.\n\nOr\n\n3. W ≥ M and Z ≥ M, so that A' = W and B' = Z, so they are equal if W = Z.\n\nOr\n\n4. W < M and Z < M, so that A' = M and B' = M, so they are equal.\n\nWait, actually, breaking it down:\n\nA' = max(M, W)\n\nB' = max(M, Z)\n\nTherefore, A' = B' if and only if max(M, W) = max(M, Z).\n\nThis happens in the following cases:\n\nCase 1: W ≥ Z and W ≤ M: Then A' = M, B' = M ⇒ equal.\n\nCase 2: Z ≥ W and Z ≤ M: Similarly, A' = M, B' = M ⇒ equal.\n\nCase 3: W ≥ M and Z ≥ M: Then A' = W, B' = Z ⇒ equal iff W = Z.\n\nCase 4: W < M and Z < M: Then A' = M, B' = M ⇒ equal.\n\nTherefore, the probability that A' = B' is equal to P(Case 1 or Case 2 or Case 4) + P(Case 3 with W=Z).\n\nWait, but Case 3 requires W ≥ M and Z ≥ M, so W and Z are both ≥ M, but since M = max(X,Y), which is already the maximum of X and Y, so W and Z can be greater than or equal to M.\n\nThe probability that W ≥ M and Z ≥ M is equal to P(W ≥ M and Z ≥ M). Since M = max(X,Y), and X,Y,Z,W are independent uniforms.\n\nThis is getting very complicated. Maybe instead of computing exactly, we can approximate or notice a symmetry.\n\nAlternatively, note that in the original problem, we are to compute the expectation over all possible initial configurations. Due to the symmetry of the problem, the expected number of distinct values after t steps might have a simple form.\n\nWait a minute, here's an important observation: after t steps, every value in the system is at least as large as the original value at its position and at least as large as the original values in its neighborhood up to distance t. Therefore, the final value at each position is the maximum of a certain set of original values. Specifically, the value at position i after t steps is the maximum of the original values in the interval [i - t, i + t] (mod N). Therefore, the final value at each position is the maximum of a window of size 2t + 1 centered at i.\n\nTherefore, the entire configuration after t steps is determined by the maximums of all such windows in the original circle.\n\nNow, the key insight is that the number of distinct values after t steps is equal to the number of distinct maximums over all these windows. However, since the windows overlap, the maximums can coincide.\n\nHowever, due to the randomness of the original values, the expected number of distinct maximums can be analyzed.\n\nIn particular, for two adjacent windows, the probability that their maximums are equal can be computed, and then using linearity of expectation, we can compute the expected number of distinct values.\n\nWait, but even this is non-trivial. Let me think.\n\nSuppose we have the original circle of N positions, each with an independent uniform [0,1] value. After t steps, each position i has a value equal to the maximum of a window of size 2t+1 centered at i. The number of distinct values is the number of distinct such maxima across all windows.\n\nHowever, the windows overlap, so the maximum of one window is a superset of another window's values. Therefore, the maximum of a window containing another window will be at least as large as the maximum of the contained window. Therefore, the maximums form a non-decreasing sequence as the windows expand.\n\nWait, actually, the windows are centered at each position, so each window of size 2t+1 includes the previous window's values plus two new ones on each side. Wait, no—each window is centered at a different position, so the windows overlap with their neighbors.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the maximum over all windows will eventually become the global maximum, but we are only considering after t steps.\n\nAlternatively, think of it this way: after t steps, the value at each position is the maximum of a window of 2t+1 original values. Therefore, the set of values after t steps is exactly the set of all such window maxima. Therefore, the number of distinct values is the number of distinct maxima over all these windows.\n\nNow, since the original values are independent uniform [0,1], the distribution of each window's maximum is known: it's the maximum of 2t+1 independent uniforms, which has a distribution with PDF f(x) = (2t+1) x^{2t} for 0 ≤ x ≤1.\n\nHowever, the windows overlap, so the window maxima are not independent random variables. Therefore, the joint distribution is complicated.\n\nHowever, perhaps we can compute the expected number of distinct window maxima.\n\nThe expected number of distinct values is equal to the sum over all possible values v in [0,1] of the probability that v is the maximum of at least one window. Since the values are continuous, this is equivalent to integrating over v from 0 to 1 of the probability that v is the maximum of at least one window.\n\nBut this seems difficult.\n\nAlternatively, we can use the linearity of expectation in a different way: the expected number of distinct values is equal to the sum over all positions i of the probability that the value at position i after t steps is greater than all other positions' values after t steps. Wait, no, that would count each value only once if it's unique, but it's not straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, consider that for any two distinct positions i and j, the probability that their values after t steps are different. Then, the expected number of distinct values is approximately N minus the sum over all pairs of the probability that they are equal, but this is only an approximation because of dependencies.\n\nBut let's try this approach anyway, even though it's approximate.\n\nLet me denote by E[D] the expected number of distinct values after t steps. Then,\n\nE[D] = N - \\sum_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq N} P(a_i' = a_j'),\n\nwhere a_i' is the value at position i after t steps.\n\nIf we can compute this sum, we can find E[D].\n\nHowever, computi