 for an approximate answer?), the expected number of distinct values among K independent variables, each distributed as the maximum of 2t+1 uniforms, would be K * (1 - (1 - 1/(2t+2))^K) or something? Wait, no.\n\nActually, for a single such variable, the probability that it is equal to a specific value v is the PDF of the maximum, which is f(v) = (2t+1) v^{2t} for 0 ≤ v ≤1. Therefore, the probability that two such variables are equal is the integral over v of [f(v)]^2 dv, since for continuous variables, P(X=Y) = ∫ P(X=v, Y=v) dv = ∫ f(v)^2 dv.\n\nTherefore, for two independent variables, P(X=Y) = ∫₀¹ [(2t+1)v^{2t}]² dv = (2t+1)² ∫₀¹ v^{4t + 2} dv = (2t+1)² * [1/(4t + 3)].\n\nTherefore, for K independent variables, the expected number of distinct values is approximately K - C(K,2) * P(X=Y) + ... but again, this is an approximation because variables are not independent.\n\nHowever, if K is large and the variables are approximately independent (which they are not, but for large K and small t?), then the expected number of distinct values would be approximately K - (K choose 2) * P(X=Y).\n\nBut this is getting too hand-wavy.\n\nAlternatively, recall that for n independent uniform [0,1] random variables, the expected number of distinct values is n - n(n-1)/2 * P(X=Y) + ... but actually, the exact expectation is complicated, but for large n, it's approximately n - n log n / log(1/(1 - p)), where p is the probability that two variables are equal? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, for continuous variables, the probability that any two are exactly equal is zero, so the expected number of distinct values is exactly N if the variables are independent. But in our case, the outer regions' variables are not independent, but if they were, then the expected number of distinct values would be K, since each is a continuous variable, so the probability of any two being equal is zero, hence all K are distinct almost surely. But that can't be, because even for independent continuous variables, the probability that all are distinct is 1, so the expected number of distinct values is K.\n\nWait a minute! For continuous random variables, the probability that any two are equal is zero, so the number of distinct values is almost surely equal to the number of variables. Therefore, if the outer regions' values are continuous random variables (which they are), then the expected number of distinct values among them is K, since the probability that any two are equal is zero, hence all K are distinct with probability 1, so the expectation is K.\n\nBut this would mean that the total expected number of distinct values after t steps is 1 + K = 1 + (N - (2t + 1)) = N - 2t.\n\nHowever, this is under the assumption that the outer regions' values are continuous and independent enough that no two are equal with positive probability, which they are, since they are continuous. However, even though the outer regions' values are continuous, they might still be dependent, but dependency doesn't affect the expectation of the number of distinct values because the expectation is linear regardless of dependence.\n\nWait, actually, the key point is that for any set of continuous random variables, the probability that any two are equal is zero, so the number of distinct values is almost surely equal to the number of variables. Therefore, the expected number of distinct values is exactly equal to the number of variables, because the expectation of an indicator that two variables are equal is zero, so the expectation of the sum over all pairs of indicators that they are equal is zero, and thus the expectation of the number of distinct values is N - 0 = N? Wait, no, this is conflicting with previous thoughts.\n\nWait, let me clarify:\n\nLet me denote the outer regions' values as X₁, X₂, ..., X_K, each being continuous random variables. The number of distinct values among them is D_outer = sum_{i=1}^K I_i, where I_i is 1 if X_i is unique, but actually no. The standard formula is:\n\nD_outer = sum_{v} N_v,\n\nwhere N_v is the number of variables equal to v. But since v is continuous, this is not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, using the formula:\n\nD_outer = sum_{1 ≤ i < j ≤ K} I_{i,j},\n\nwhere I_{i,j} = 1 if X_i ≠ X_j, and 0 otherwise. Then,\n\nE[D_outer] = sum_{1 ≤ i < j ≤ K} E[I_{i,j}] = sum_{1 ≤ i < j ≤ K} P(X_i ≠ X_j).\n\nBut since the variables are continuous, P(X_i = X_j) = 0 for any i ≠ j, so P(X_i ≠ X_j) = 1. Therefore, E[D_outer] = sum_{1 ≤ i < j ≤ K} 1 = C(K, 2).\n\nBut this can't be right, because the number of distinct values can't exceed K. Wait, no, actually, the formula I used is incorrect.\n\nWait, actually, the number of distinct values D_outer is equal to 1 + sum_{i=2}^K (1 - P(all previous variables equal to X_i)), but this is complicated.\n\nAlternatively, the correct formula for the expectation of the number of distinct values among K continuous independent random variables is K, because each variable is almost surely distinct from the others. Wait, no, that's not true. For example, if you have two continuous random variables, the probability they are equal is zero, so the expected number of distinct values is 2. For three, it's 3, etc. Yes! For any finite set of continuous random variables, the probability that any two are equal is zero, so the number of distinct values is almost surely equal to the number of variables. Therefore, the expected number of distinct values is exactly equal to the number of variables.\n\nTherefore, in our case, the outer regions consist of K = N - (2t + 1) continuous random variables, each being the maximum of 2t+1 independent uniforms, but since these are continuous, the expected number of distinct values among them is K.\n\nAdding the inner block's single value M, which is a continuous random variable, but distinct from the outer regions' values with probability 1? Wait, no. M is the global maximum of all original variables, while the outer regions' values are the maximum of windows that don't includ