nd A,B,C,D are independent U(0,1).\n\nLet me consider variables A,B,C,D as four independent uniforms.\n\nLet me denote M = max(A,B), so P(M ≤ x) = P(A ≤ x and B ≤ x) = x² for 0 ≤ x ≤1.\n\nSimilarly, D and C are independent of A and B.\n\nCompute P(A' = B'):\n\nCase 1: D ≤ M and C ≤ M. Then A' = M, B' = M ⇒ equal.\n\nCase 2: D ≥ M and C ≥ M. Then A' = D, B' = C. These are equal only if D = C.\n\nCase 3: D ≤ M and C ≥ M. Then A' = M, B' = C. These are equal only if M = C.\n\nCase 4: D ≥ M and C ≤ M. Then A' = D, B' = M. These are equal only if D = M.\n\nTherefore,\n\nP(A' = B') = P(Case 1) + P(Case 2 with D=C) + P(Case 3 with M=C) + P(Case 4 with D=M).\n\nCompute each term:\n\nP(Case 1) = P(D ≤ M and C ≤ M) = E[ P(D ≤ M and C ≤ M | M) ] = E[ M² ].\n\nSince M = max(A,B), E[M²] = E[ (max(A,B))² ].\n\nFor two independent uniforms, E[max(X,Y)²] = ∫₀¹ ∫₀¹ max(x,y)² dx dy.\n\nThis integral can be computed as 2 ∫₀¹ x² dx + 2 ∫₀¹ y² dx dy? Wait, let me compute it properly.\n\nLet me compute E[max(A,B)^2] where A,B ~ U(0,1).\n\nLet’s compute E[max(A,B)^2] = ∫₀¹ ∫₀¹ max(x,y)^2 dx dy.\n\nWe can compute this by splitting the integral into two regions: x ≥ y and y ≥ x.\n\n= ∫₀¹ ∫₀^x x² dy dx + ∫₀¹ ∫ₓ¹ y² dy dx\n\n= ∫₀¹ x² * x dx + ∫₀¹ [∫ₓ¹ y² dy] dx\n\n= ∫₀¹ x³ dx + ∫₀¹ [ (1³/3 - x³/3) ] dx\n\n= [x⁴/4]₀¹ + [ (1/3 - x⁴/12) ]₀¹\n\n= 1/4 + (1/3 - 1/12) - (1/3 - 0)\n\n= 1/4 + (1/4) = 1/2.\n\nTherefore, E[M²] = 1/2.\n\nTherefore, P(Case 1) = E[M²] = 1/2.\n\nP(Case 2 with D=C): This is P(D ≥ M and C ≥ M and D = C).\n\nSince D and C are independent of M and each other,\n\nP(D ≥ M, C ≥ M, D=C) = E[ P(D ≥ M, C ≥ M, D=C | M) ]\n\nGiven M, P(D ≥ M and C ≥ M and D=C) = P(D=C | D ≥ M, C ≥ M) * P(D ≥ M, C ≥ M | M)\n\nSince D and C are independent given M,\n\nP(D=C | D ≥ M, C ≥ M) = ∫_{m=M}^1 ∫_{m=M}^1 δ(d - c) dc dd / [P(D ≥ M)^2]\n\nWait, this is getting complex, but perhaps note that given D and C are independent and both ≥ M, the probability that D = C is zero because they are continuous. Th