accurately, for each value x in [0,1], the probability that x is present in the sample is 1 - (1 - x)^n, but since it's continuous, the expectation is the integral over x from 0 to 1 of the probability that x is present, which is 1 - (1 - x)^n. Wait, but actually, the expectation of the number of distinct values is:\n\nE[D] = \\int_{0}^{1} \\mathbb{P}(x \\text{ is in the sample}) dx\n\nSince for each x, the indicator that x is present in the sample has expectation equal to the probability that at least one of the n variables is equal to x, but since they are continuous, the probability that any particular variable is exactly x is zero, so actually, the probability that x is present is the probability that at least one variable is in a neighborhood around x. Wait, no, in continuous distributions, the probability that any variable is exactly x is zero, so the probability that x is present in the sample is actually zero for each x. Wait, that can't be.\n\nWait, actually, the number of distinct values is a random variable that can take values from 1 to n, but in the continuous case, the probability that all n variables are exactly the same is zero, but the probability that all are distinct is also zero. The expectation is still well-defined as an integral over the possible values.\n\nWait, the correct formula for the expectation of the number of distinct values in n i.i.d. uniform [0,1] random variables is:\n\nE[D] = \\int_{0}^{1} \\mathbb{P}(x \\text{ is a value in the sample}) dx\n\nBut since for any specific x, the probability that at least one of the n variables is equal to x is zero (because it's a continuous distribution), but the expectation can still be computed by considering the probability that a value in some interval [x, x+dx] is present, then integrating over x.\n\nAlternatively, the expectation is:\n\nE[D] = \\sum_{k=1}^n \\mathbb{P}(\\text{the k-th order statistic is unique})\n\nWait, perhaps it's easier to recall the formula:\n\nFor n i.i.d. uniform [0,