iables are different, but that also seems off.\n\nWait, actually, let me look up the formula in my mind. For continuous uniform variables, the expectation of the number of distinct values is:\n\nE[D] = \\sum_{k=1}^n \\mathbb{P}(\\text{the k-th order statistic is unique})\n\nBut since all variables are continuous, the probability that any two are equal is zero, so all order statistics are unique almost surely. Therefore, the number of distinct values is exactly n almost surely? Wait, no, that can't be.\n\nWait a second, in continuous space, the probability that any two variables are exactly equal is zero, but the probability that all variables are distinct is also zero, because it's a countable intersection of measure zero events. However, the expectation can still be computed as an integral.\n\nWait, actually, the expectation of the number of distinct values is the same as the expectation of the cardinality of the support of the random vector, which in continuous case can be computed as:\n\nE[D] = \\int_{0}^{1} \\mathbb{P}(x \\text{ is in the support}) dx\n\nBut since the support is the set of points where at least one variable is in [x, x + dx], but integrating over all x, this is equivalent to the expected number of points where a variable changes value, which I might be confusing.\n\nAlternatively, here's a better way: For continuous random variables, the expected number of distinct values is equal to the integral over x from 0 to 1 of the probability density function of the minimum spacing between variables? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, I recall that for n i.i.d. uniform [0,1] random variables, the expected number of distinct values is:\n\nE[D] = n - \\sum_{k=1}^{n-1} \\frac{1}{k!} \\cdot something... Hmm, maybe not.\n\nWait, actually, let me think of it as follows. The probability that all n variables are in [0, x] is x^n. Therefore, the probability that the maximum is greater than x is 1 - x^n. Therefore, the expected number of distinct values can be related to