ens in those cases. Maybe we can spot a pattern or something.\n\nLet me try with N=3 first. Let's say we have three numbers in a circle: \\( a_1, a_2, a_3 \\), each initially uniform [0,1]. Then, each second, each number is replaced by the maximum of itself and its two neighbors. Since it's a circle of three, each number has two neighbors, so the update rule is:\n\nFor each \\( i = 1, 2, 3 \\), set \\( a_i' = \\max(a_{i-1}, a_i, a_{i+1}) \\), with indices modulo 3.\n\nLet me denote the state at time t as \\( \\{ a_{1}^{(t)}, a_{2}^{(t)}, a_{3}^{(t)} \\} \\).\n\nStarting at time 0: \\( \\{ a_{1}^{(0)}, a_{2}^{(0)}, a_{3}^{(0)} \\} \\), each independent uniform [0,1].\n\nThen at time 1: each new value is the max of the three original values. Wait a second, hold on. For a circle of three elements, each \\( a_i' = \\max(a_{i-1}, a_i, a_{i+1}) \\). Since it's a circle of three, \\( a_1' = \\max(a_3, a_1, a_2) \\), \\( a_2' = \\max(a_1, a_2, a_3) \\), and \\( a_3' = \\max(a_2, a_3, a_1) \\). So actually, all three new values are equal to the maximum of all three original values!\n\nWait, that's true? Because for each position, the maximum of the three neighbors (which are all the three elements since it's a triangle) so each new value is just the maximum of all three original values. Therefore, after the first step, all three numbers become equal to the maximum of the initial three numbers. So, after one step, all three numbers are equal to \\( \\max(a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}) \\). Therefore, at time t=1, all three numbers are the same, so the number of distinct values is 1.\n\nThen, at time t=2: Now, each number is replaced by the maximum of its two neighbors and itself. But since all three numbers are equal at time t=1, say they're all equal to M = \\( \\max(a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}) \\). Then, for each position, \\( a_i^{(2)} = \\max(a_{i-1}^{(1)}, a_i^{(1)}, a_{i+1}^{(1)}) \\). But since all \\( a_j^{(1)} = M \\), then each \\( a_i^{(2)} = \\max(M, M, M) = M \\). So, again, all numbers stay the same from time t=1 onward. So once you reach time t=1, you stay at 1 distinct value forever.\n\nTherefore, for N=3, after the first second, the number of distinct values drops to 1, and remains 1 thereafter. Therefore, after any number of seconds t ≥ 1, the number of distinct values is 1. Therefore, the expected number of distinct values after 100 seconds is just 1, since after the first second, it's already 1, and stays 1. So, for N=3, the answer would be 1.\n\nBut wait, that seems too straightforward. Let me verify again.\n\nSuppose N=3. Suppose we have three numbers: say [0.1, 0.3, 0.2]. Then, the maximum is 0.3, so after one step, all become 0.3, so yes, [0.3, 0.3, 0.3]. Then the next step, each is the max of its neighbors and itself, which are all 0.3, so again 0.3, so indeed stays constant. So in this case, the number of distinct values becomes 1 after the first step and stays there.\n\nAlternatively, suppose we have all three numbers initially the same: say [0.5, 0.5, 0.5]. Then, after one step, they stay the same, so the number of distinct values is still 1. So in all cases, after the first step, it's 1. Therefore, regardless of the initial configuration, after the first step, it becomes 1. Therefore, the expectation after 100 seconds is 1. So that's for N=3.\n\nHmm, okay, so maybe with N=3, it's trivial.\n\nHow about N=4? Let's try with N=4. Maybe this is more interesting.\n\nLet me consider N=4. Let's denote the numbers as \\( a_1, a_2, a_3, a_4 \\) arranged in a circle.\n\nThe update rule: each second, simultaneously, replace each \\( a_i \\) with \\( \\max(a_{i-1}, a_i, a_{i+1}) \\).\n\nSo, for N=4, each \\( a_i^{(t+1)} = \\max(a_{i-1}^{(t)}, a_i^{(t)}, a_{i+1}^{(t)}) \\).\n\nLet me see what happens over time. Let's start with an arbitrary initial configuration.\n\nFirst, at time t=0, four numbers, each uniform [0,1], independent.\n\nAt time t=1: Each number becomes the maximum of itself and its two neighbors. So, for example, \\( a_1^{(1)} = \\max(a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}) \\), \\( a_2^{(1)} = \\max(a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}) \\), \\( a_3^{(1)} = \\max(a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}) \\), and \\( a_4^{(1)} = \\max(a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}) \\).\n\nSo each new value is the maximum of three consecutive (in the circle) initial values.\n\nHmm, so after one step, the four numbers are each the maximum of three consecutive original numbers. So, the new configuration is determined by overlapping triplets of the original numbers.\n\nNow, what can we say about the number of distinct values after one step?\n\nWell, it's possible that some of these maxima are equal, but it's not clear. Let's consider an example.\n\nSuppose the initial numbers are all 0.5: [0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.5]. Then, after one step, each becomes the max of three 0.5's, so still 0.5, so all remain the same. The number of distinct values is still 1.\n\nAlternatively, suppose we have two adjacent numbers high and two low. Let's say [1, 1, 0, 0]. So, a1=1, a2=1, a3=0, a4=0.\n\nCompute the first step:\n\n\\( a_1^{(1)} = \\max(a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}) = \\max(0, 1, 1) = 1 \\)\n\n\\( a_2^{(1)} = \\max(a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}) = \\max(1, 1, 0) = 1 \\)\n\n\\( a_3^{(1)} = \\max(a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}) = \\max(1, 0, 0) = 1 \\)\n\n\\( a_4^{(1)} = \\max(a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}) = \\max(0, 0, 1) = 1 \\)\n\nSo again, all become 1. So the number of distinct values drops to 1 immediately? Wait, but that seems odd. Wait, in this case, even though there were 0s and 1s, after one step, everything becomes 1? Hmm, interesting.\n\nWait another example: let's say the initial numbers are [0, 0, 1, 1]. Then:\n\n\\( a_1^{(1)} = \\max(a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}) = \\max(1, 0, 0) = 1 \\)\n\n\\( a_2^{(1)} = \\max(a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}) = \\max(0, 0, 1) = 1 \\)\n\n\\( a_3^{(1)} = \\max(a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}) = \\max(0, 1, 1) = 1 \\)\n\n\\( a_4^{(1)} = \\max(a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}) = \\max(1, 1, 0) = 1 \\)\n\nSame result: all become 1. Hmm. Hmm, what if the initial numbers are not all the same?\n\nSuppose the initial numbers are [0, 1, 0, 1]. Let's see:\n\n\\( a_1^{(1)} = \\max(a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}) = \\max(1, 0, 1) = 1 \\)\n\n\\( a_2^{(1)} = \\max(a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}) = \\max(0, 1, 0) = 1 \\)\n\n\\( a_3^{(1)} = \\max(a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}) = \\max(1, 0, 1) = 1 \\)\n\n\\( a_4^{(1)} = \\max(a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}) = \\max(0, 1, 0) = 1 \\)\n\nAgain, all become 1. Hmm. Wait, so maybe in the N=4 case, after one step, regardless of the initial configuration, all the numbers become the maximum of the entire initial configuration?\n\nWait, hold on. Let me see. Let me think of that.\n\nSuppose the initial configuration has some maximum value M = \\( \\max_{i=1}^4 a_i^{(0)} \\). Then, is it true that in the next step, all the numbers become M?\n\nLet me check with a different example. Let me suppose the initial configuration is [0.5, 0.3, 0.7, 0.2]. Then:\n\nCompute \\( a_1^{(1)} = \\max(a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}) = \\max(0.2, 0.5, 0.3) = 0.5 \\)\n\n\\( a_2^{(1)} = \\max(a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}) = \\max(0.5, 0.3, 0.7) = 0.7 \\)\n\n\\( a_3^{(1)} = \\max(a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}) = \\max(0.3, 0.7, 0.2) = 0.7 \\)\n\n\\( a_4^{(1)} = \\max(a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}) = \\max(0.7, 0.2, 0.5) = 0.7 \\)\n\nSo the new configuration is [0.5, 0.7, 0.7, 0.7]. Therefore, the maximum value after one step is 0.7, which was the global maximum of the initial configuration. However, not all elements became the global maximum immediately. In this case, \\( a_1^{(1)} = 0.5 \\), which is less than the global maximum 0.7. But the others became 0.7. So the maximum value in the next step is the global maximum, but not all elements have become the global maximum yet.\n\nWait, so in this case, after one step, three of the four elements have become the global maximum, and one has not. Hmm. So in this case, the number of distinct values after one step is 2: 0.5 and 0.7. Wait, but actually, in the new configuration, we have [0.5, 0.7, 0.7, 0.7]. So there are two distinct values: 0.5 and 0.7. So the number of distinct values went from 4 (assuming all were different) to 2 in this case.\n\nWait, in my previous examples, when I had [0,1,0,1], all became 1, so only one distinct value, but in the case of [0.5, 0.3, 0.7, 0.2], it became [0.5, 0.7, 0.7, 0.7], so two distinct values.\n\nHmm, so maybe the number of distinct values can vary?\n\nWait, let's see another example. Suppose the initial configuration is [0, 0, 0, 1]. Then:\n\n\\( a_1^{(1)} = \\max(a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}) = \\max(1, 0, 0) = 1 \\)\n\n\\( a_2^{(1)} = \\max(a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}) = \\max(0, 0, 0) = 0 \\)\n\n\\( a_3^{(1)} = \\max(a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}) = \\max(0, 0, 1) = 1 \\)\n\n\\( a_4^{(1)} = \\max(a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}) = \\max(0, 1, 0) = 1 \\)\n\nTherefore, the new configuration is [1, 0, 1, 1]. So distinct values are 0 and 1, so two distinct values again.\n\nHmm, so in this case, two distinct values. Hmm.\n\nWait, so in the first example with [0.5, 0.3, 0.7, 0.2], after one step, we have two distinct values, but in the second example with [0,1,0,1], after one step, all become 1, so only one distinct value. Hmm, so it can vary between 1 and 2?\n\nWait, so perhaps the number of distinct values after one step can be either 1 or 2?\n\nWait, let's see if it can be more than 2.\n\nSuppose the initial configuration is [0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4]. Then, let's compute the next step:\n\n\\( a_1^{(1)} = \\max(a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}) = \\max(0.4, 0.1, 0.2) = 0.4 \\)\n\n\\( a_2^{(1)} = \\max(a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}) = \\max(0.1, 0.2, 0.3) = 0.3 \\)\n\n\\( a_3^{(1)} = \\max(a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}) = \\max(0.2, 0.3, 0.4) = 0.4 \\)\n\n\\( a_4^{(1)} = \\max(a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}) = \\max(0.3, 0.4, 0.1) = 0.4 \\)\n\nTherefore, the new configuration is [0.4, 0.3, 0.4, 0.4]. So distinct values are 0.3 and 0.4, so two distinct values again.\n\nHmm, so in this case, two. Wait, but if we have an initial configuration where the maxima of the triplets are all different?\n\nIs that possible? Let me see. Suppose the initial configuration is [a, b, c, d], with all four numbers distinct. Let me see if the new values can be all different.\n\nSuppose we have four numbers such that when taking the max of each triplet, the resulting four values are all different. Let me try to construct such a case.\n\nLet me suppose:\n\nLet’s set \\( a_1 = 0.1 \\), \\( a_2 = 0.2 \\), \\( a_3 = 0.3 \\), \\( a_4 = 0.4 \\). Wait, that's the previous example, and the new values were [0.4, 0.3, 0.4, 0.4], so not all distinct.\n\nHmm, how about another configuration. Let me try [0.1, 0.3, 0.2, 0.4].\n\nCompute the next step:\n\n\\( a_1^{(1)} = \\max(a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}) = \\max(0.4, 0.1, 0.3) = 0.4 \\)\n\n\\( a_2^{(1)} = \\max(a_1^{(0)}, a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}) = \\max(0.1, 0.3, 0.2) = 0.3 \\)\n\n\\( a_3^{(1)} = \\max(a_2^{(0)}, a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}) = \\max(0.3, 0.2, 0.4) = 0.4 \\)\n\n\\( a_4^{(1)} = \\max(a_3^{(0)}, a_4^{(0)}, a_1^{(0)}) = \\max(0.2, 0.4, 0.1) = 0.4 \\)\n\nAgain, [0.4, 0.3, 0.4, 0.4], so two distinct values.\n\nHmm, maybe it's difficult to get three distinct values? Let's see. Suppose I set the initial numbers such that each triplet's maximum is different?\n\nLet me try: Let me set \\( a_1 = 0.1 \\), \\( a_2 = 0.4 \\), \\( a_3 = 0.2 \\), \\( a_4 = 0.5 \\).\n\nCompute:\n\n\\( a_1^{(1)} = \\max(a_4, a_1, a_2) = \\max(0.5, 0.1, 0.4) = 0.5 \\)\n\n\\( a_2^{(1)} = \\max(a_1, a_2, a_3) = \\max(0.1, 0.4, 0.2) = 0.4 \\)\n\n\\( a_3^{(1)} = \\max(a_2, a_3, a_4) = \\max(0.4, 0.2, 0.5) = 0.5 \\)\n\n\\( a_4^{(1)} = \\max(a_3, a_4, a_1) = \\max(0.2, 0.5, 0.1) = 0.5 \\)\n\nThus, the new configuration is [0.5, 0.4, 0.5, 0.5], so distinct values are 0.4 and 0.5: two distinct values again.\n\nHmm, is there any initial configuration where after one step, you get three distinct values?\n\nSuppose I set \\( a_1 = 0.1 \\), \\( a_2 = 0.2 \\), \\( a_3 = 0.3 \\), \\( a_4 = 0.4 \\). Wait, we tried that earlier and got two distinct values. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, let me try \\( a_1 = 0.1 \\), \\( a_2 = 0.4 \\), \\( a_3 = 0.3 \\), \\( a_4 = 0.2 \\).\n\nCompute:\n\n\\( a_1^{(1)} = \\max(a_4, a_1, a_2) = \\max(0.2, 0.1, 0.4) = 0.4 \\)\n\n\\( a_2^{(1)} = \\max(a_1, a_2, a_3) = \\max(0.1, 0.4, 0.3) = 0.4 \\)\n\n\\( a_3^{(1)} = \\max(a_2, a_3, a_4) = \\max(0.4, 0.3, 0.2) = 0.4 \\)\n\n\\( a_4^{(1)} = \\max(a_3, a_4, a_1) = \\max(0.3, 0.2, 0.1) = 0.3 \\)\n\nThus, the new configuration is [0.4, 0.4, 0.4, 0.3]. So distinct values are 0.3 and 0.4: again two.\n\nHmm, maybe it's impossible to have three distinct values after one step in N=4?\n\nWait, let's see: suppose we have an initial configuration where the four numbers are such that each triplet has a different maximum?\n\nSuppose we have four numbers arranged such that:\n\nLet me set \\( a_1 = 0 \\), \\( a_2 = 1 \\), \\( a_3 = 0 \\), \\( a_4 = 1 \\). Wait, then:\n\n\\( a_1^{(1)} = \\max(a_4, a_1, a_2) = \\max(1, 0, 1) = 1 \\)\n\n\\( a_2^{(1)} = \\max(a_1, a_2, a_3) = \\max(0, 1, 0) = 1 \\)\n\n\\( a_3^{(1)} = \\max(a_2, a_3, a_4) = \\max(1, 0, 1) = 1 \\)\n\n\\( a_4^{(1)} = \\max(a_3, a_4, a_1) = \\max(0, 1, 0) = 1 \\)\n\nSo all become 1. Only one distinct value.\n\nHmm, what if I set \\( a_1 = 0.2 \\), \\( a_2 = 0.5 \\), \\( a_3 = 0.1 \\), \\( a_4 = 0.6 \\).\n\nThen:\n\n\\( a_1^{(1)} = \\max(a_4, a_1, a_2) = \\max(0.6, 0.2, 0.5) = 0.6 \\)\n\n\\( a_2^{(1)} = \\max(a_1, a_2, a_3) = \\max(0.2, 0.5, 0.1) = 0.5 \\)\n\n\\( a_3^{(1)} = \\max(a_2, a_3, a_4) = \\max(0.5, 0.1, 0.6) = 0.6 \\)\n\n\\( a_4^{(1)} = \\max(a_3, a_4, a_1) = \\max(0.1, 0.6, 0.2) = 0.6 \\)\n\nSo the new configuration is [0.6, 0.5, 0.6, 0.6], so distinct values 0.5 and 0.6: two again.\n\nHmm. Maybe in N=4, after one step, you can only have either 1 or 2 distinct values? Let me see.\n\nSuppose I try to have three distinct values. Suppose the initial configuration is such that when taking the maxima of the triplets, three different values emerge.\n\nSuppose:\n\nLet me set \\( a_1 = 0.1 \\), \\( a_2 = 0.6 \\), \\( a_3 = 0.4 \\), \\( a_4 = 0.5 \\).\n\nCompute:\n\n\\( a_1^{(1)} = \\max(a_4, a_1, a_2) = \\max(0.5, 0.1, 0.6) = 0.6 \\)\n\n\\( a_2^{(1)} = \\max(a_1, a_2, a_3) = \\max(0.1, 0.6, 0.4) = 0.6 \\)\n\n\\( a_3^{(1)} = \\max(a_2, a_3, a_4) = \\max(0.6, 0.4, 0.5) = 0.6 \\)\n\n\\( a_4^{(1)} = \\max(a_3, a_4, a_1) = \\max(0.4, 0.5, 0.1) = 0.5 \\)\n\nSo the new configuration is [0.6, 0.6, 0.6, 0.5]. So distinct values: 0.5 and 0.6. Still two.\n\nHmm. Maybe it's impossible to get three distinct values in N=4 after one step?\n\nWait, let's try another configuration. Let me set \\( a_1 = 0.2 \\), \\( a_2 = 0.7 \\), \\( a_3 = 0.1 \\), \\( a_4 = 0.8 \\).\n\nCompute:\n\n\\( a_1^{(1)} = \\max(a_4, a_1, a_2) = \\max(0.8, 0.2, 0.7) = 0.8 \\)\n\n\\( a_2^{(1)} = \\max(a_1, a_2, a_3) = \\max(0.2, 0.7, 0.1) = 0.7 \\)\n\n\\( a_3^{(1)} = \\max(a_2, a_3, a_4) = \\max(0.7, 0.1, 0.8) = 0.8 \\)\n\n\\( a_4^{(1)} = \\max(a_3, a_4, a_1) = \\max(0.1, 0.8, 0.2) = 0.8 \\)\n\nThus, the new configuration is [0.8, 0.7, 0.8, 0.8]. So distinct values are 0.7 and 0.8: two again.\n\nHmm, so maybe in N=4, after one step, the number of distinct values can only be 1 or 2?\n\nWait, let me try to see if there's a configuration where it's three.\n\nSuppose \\( a_1 = 0 \\), \\( a_2 = 1 \\), \\( a_3 = 0.5 \\), \\( a_4 = 0.5 \\).\n\nThen:\n\n\\( a_1^{(1)} = \\max(a_4, a_1, a_2) = \\max(0.5, 0, 1) = 1 \\)\n\n\\( a_2^{(1)} = \\max(a_1, a_2, a_3) = \\max(0, 1, 0.5) = 1 \\)\n\n\\( a_3^{(1)} = \\max(a_2, a_3, a_4) = \\max(1, 0.5, 0.5) = 1 \\)\n\n\\( a_4^{(1)} = \\max(a_3, a_4, a_1) = \\max(0.5, 0.5, 0) = 0.5 \\)\n\nThus, new configuration is [1, 1, 1, 0.5]. So distinct values: 1 and 0.5. Still two.\n\nHmm, so maybe in N=4, it's impossible to get three distinct values after one step?\n\nAlternatively, suppose all four original numbers are distinct and arranged such that each triplet's maximum is different. But in order for that to happen, the four original numbers must be arranged in a way that the maximum of each triplet is a different number. Let me see.\n\nSuppose we have four numbers: a, b, c, d, all distinct, and arranged so that:\n\n- The triplet (a, b, c) has maximum b,\n\n- The triplet (b, c, d) has maximum c,\n\n- The triplet (c, d, a) has maximum d,\n\n- The triplet (d, a, b) has maximum a?\n\nWait, but that would require a to be greater than d and b, which contradicts if a is the maximum of the last triplet.\n\nWait, maybe this is impossible? Because in a circle, each element is part of three triplets? Wait, actually in N=4, each element is part of three triplets? Wait no, in N=4, each element is part of three triplets? Wait, actually, in the update rule, each element is the center of a triplet? Wait, actually, each element is part of three triplets: for example, element \\( a_1 \\) is part of the triplet (a_4, a_1, a_2), the triplet (a_1, a_2, a_3), and the triplet (a_3, a_4, a_1)? Wait no, actually, each element is part of three triplets? Wait, no. Wait, actually, in the update rule for N=4, each element \\( a_i \\) is replaced by the max of its immediate left neighbor, itself, and immediate right neighbor. So each element is part of exactly one triplet for its own update? Wait no, actually, each triplet is centered at each position. So each element is the center of its own triplet, but also part of the triplets of its neighbors.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to think that each element is involved in three triplets: as the center, the left neighbor's triplet, and the right neighbor's triplet? Wait, actually, no. Let me clarify:\n\nEach element \\( a_i \\) is the center of the triplet \\( (a_{i-1}, a_i, a_{i+1}) \\), so each element is the center of exactly one triplet. However, each element is also part of two other triplets: the triplet centered at \\( a_{i-1} \\), which includes \\( a_i \\) as its right neighbor, and the triplet centered at \\( a_{i+1} \\), which includes \\( a_i \\) as its left neighbor.\n\nTherefore, each element is part of three triplets: its own triplet as the center, and two adjacent triplets as a neighbor. Therefore, each element's value is involved in three different max operations. However, when updating, only the center triplet's max is used to update the center element.\n\nTherefore, for the new value of \\( a_i \\), it's only the max of its own triplet, not considering the other triplets. Therefore, the new value at \\( a_i \\) is only dependent on the triplet centered at \\( i \\). Therefore, to have the new values all different, each of these triplets must have a different maximum.\n\nTherefore, in order to have three distinct values after one step, we would need three different maxima from the four triplets. But since there are four triplets, each with their own maximum, so potentially up to four different maxima? But in practice, maybe overlaps?\n\nWait, but in order to have three distinct values, you need three different maxima among the four triplets. But is that possible?\n\nSuppose we have four numbers arranged such that three of the triplets have different maxima, and the fourth triplet has one of those maxima again.\n\nWait, but in my attempts above, I couldn't find such a configuration. Maybe it's impossible?\n\nSuppose we try to construct such a configuration.\n\nLet me try to define four numbers such that three triplets have different maxima and one is a repeat.\n\nLet me suppose:\n\nLet’s set \\( a_1 = 0.1 \\), \\( a_2 = 0.4 \\), \\( a_3 = 0.6 \\), \\( a_4 = 0.5 \\).\n\nCompute the maxima:\n\n- Triplet 1 (centered at 1): \\( \\max(a_4, a_1, a_2) = \\max(0.5, 0.1, 0.4) = 0.5 \\)\n\n- Triplet 2 (centered at 2): \\( \\max(a_1, a_2, a_3) = \\max(0.1, 0.4, 0.6) = 0.6 \\)\n\n- Triplet 3 (centered at 3): \\( \\max(a_2, a_3, a_4) = \\max(0.4, 0.6, 0.5) = 0.6 \\)\n\n- Triplet 4 (centered at 4): \\( \\max(a_3, a_4, a_1) = \\max(0.6, 0.5, 0.1) = 0.6 \\)\n\nSo the new values are [0.5, 0.6, 0.6, 0.6]. So only two distinct values: 0.5 and 0.6.\n\nHmm, still two.\n\nAnother attempt: let me try \\( a_1 = 0.3 \\), \\( a_2 = 0.7 \\), \\( a_3 = 0.2 \\), \\( a_4 = 0.8 \\).\n\nCompute:\n\n- Triplet 1: \\( \\max(0.8, 0.3, 0.7) = 0.8 \\)\n\n- Triplet 2: \\( \\max(0.3, 0.7, 0.2) = 0.7 \\)\n\n- Triplet 3: \\( \\max(0.7, 0.2, 0.8) = 0.8 \\)\n\n- Triplet 4: \\( \\max(0.2, 0.8, 0.3) = 0.8 \\)\n\nThus, new configuration: [0.8, 0.7, 0.8, 0.8], so two distinct values.\n\nHmm. How about \\( a_1 = 0.25 \\), \\( a_2 = 0.75 \\), \\( a_3 = 0.35 \\), \\( a_4 = 0.65 \\).\n\nCompute:\n\n- Triplet 1: \\( \\max(0.65, 0.25, 0.75) = 0.75 \\)\n\n- Triplet 2: \\( \\max(0.25, 0.75, 0.35) = 0.75 \\)\n\n- Triplet 3: \\( \\max(0.75, 0.35, 0.65) = 0.75 \\)\n\n- Triplet 4: \\( \\max(0.35, 0.65, 0.25) = 0.65 \\)\n\nNew configuration: [0.75, 0.75, 0.75, 0.65]. Two distinct values again.\n\nHmm, is there a way to get three?\n\nWait, perhaps if the four original numbers are arranged such that two adjacent triplets have different maxima, and the other two have another?\n\nWait, let me try:\n\nLet me set \\( a_1 = 0.2 \\), \\( a_2 = 0.5 \\), \\( a_3 = 0.7 \\), \\( a_4 = 0.3 \\).\n\nCompute:\n\n- Triplet 1: \\( \\max(a_4, a_1, a_2) = \\max(0.3, 0.2, 0.5) = 0.5 \\)\n\n- Triplet 2: \\( \\max(a_1, a_2, a_3) = \\max(0.2, 0.5, 0.7) = 0.7 \\)\n\n- Triplet 3: \\( \\max(a_2, a_3, a_4) = \\max(0.5, 0.7, 0.3) = 0.7 \\)\n\n- Triplet 4: \\( \\max(a_3, a_4, a_1) = \\max(0.7, 0.3, 0.2) = 0.7 \\)\n\nThus, new configuration: [0.5, 0.7, 0.7, 0.7]. So two distinct values.\n\nHmm. Maybe it's impossible? Let's try one more.\n\nSuppose \\( a_1 = 0.1 \\), \\( a_2 = 0.6 \\), \\( a_3 = 0.4 \\), \\( a_4 = 0.5 \\).\n\nTriplet 1: \\( \\max(0.5, 0.1, 0.6) = 0.6 \\)\n\nTriplet 2: \\( \\max(0.1, 0.6, 0.4) = 0.6 \\)\n\nTriplet 3: \\( \\max(0.6, 0.4, 0.5) = 0.6 \\)\n\nTriplet 4: \\( \\max(0.4, 0.5, 0.1) = 0.5 \\)\n\nSo new configuration: [0.6, 0.6, 0.6, 0.5]. Again two.\n\nHmm. Maybe in N=4, after one step, you can only have either 1 or 2 distinct values?\n\nWait, in the first example where all were the same, you had 1 distinct value. In all other examples, you had two. Is there a configuration where after one step you have two distinct values, but not all the non-max values are the same?\n\nWait, in the first example I did with [0.5, 0.3, 0.7, 0.2], we had [0.5, 0.7, 0.7, 0.7], so one non-max and three max. So two distinct values. In another example, like [0,1,0,1] became all 1s, so only one distinct value. So maybe in N=4, after one step, the number of distinct values is either 1 or 2.\n\nTherefore, perhaps in general, for N=4, after one step, the number of distinct values is either 1 or 2, depending on whether the initial configuration had a global maximum that was \"dominant\" enough so that all triplets include it, or not?\n\nWait, in the case of [0.5, 0.3, 0.7, 0.2], the global maximum was 0.7, and the triplet centered at position 3 included the 0.7 and its neighbors 0.3 and 0.2, so that triplet's maximum was 0.7, but the triplet centered at position 1 was [0.2, 0.5, 0.3], which had a maximum of 0.5, which was less than the global maximum. Wait, but in that case, the center of that triplet was 0.5, but the maximum was 0.5? Wait no, the triplet centered at position 1 is \\( a_4, a_1, a_2 \\), which were 0.2, 0.5, 0.3, so maximum 0.5. So that was the maximum of that triplet. So the value at position 1 after the update is 0.5, which is less than the global maximum. So the global maximum wasn't necessarily in every triplet. Therefore, it's possible for some positions to not reach the global maximum in one step.\n\nTherefore, in N=4, after one step, the number of distinct values is 1 if the global maximum was present in all triplets (i.e., if every triplet included the global maximum), but if the global maximum was not in some triplet, then that position would have a lower value, so