bability that two different windows have the same maximum is zero, unless the windows are identical.\n\nWait, but the windows for different positions overlap, so their maximums could be the same even if the windows are different.\n\nHowever, due to the continuity of the distribution, the probability that two different windows have the same maximum is zero, because the maximum of a set of independent uniform variables is a continuous random variable, and the probability that two such maxima coincide is zero.\n\nWait, more precisely, the maximum of a set of independent uniform variables on [0,1] is a random variable that has a distribution that is continuous except at 0 and 1, but actually, the maximum is a continuous random variable on [0,1]. Therefore, the probability that two different maxima (from different windows) are equal is zero.\n\nTherefore, the expected number of distinct values after t steps is equal to the expected number of windows (which is N) minus the expected number of pairs of positions that have the same maximum. But since the probability that any two positions have the same value is zero, then the expectation of the number of distinct values is N multiplied by the probability that a given position's value is different from all others, but again, due to continuity, this is tricky.\n\nWait, but actually, in continuous space, the expected number of distinct values is equal to the expected number of positions where the value is a strict maximum in its neighborhood or something?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps in the continuous case, the expected number of distinct values after t steps is equal to N, because even though individual values may coincide with probability zero, the expected number is still N? But that can't be, because in our examples, after one step in N=4, the expected number of distinct values was 2, not 4.\n\nWait, but in the continuous case, the probability that two adjacent positions have the same value is zero, but the expected number of 