hat it's equal to any other specific position is zero, and there are N-1 other positions, so the probability that it's equal to at least one is zero, hence the probability that it's unique is 1, so the expectation is N.\n\nBut in our discrete examples with N=4, after one step, we saw that the expected number of distinct values was 2, not 4. The reason is that in the discrete case, the values are not continuous, so you can have multiple positions with the same value with positive probability, hence the expectation is less than N.\n\nTherefore, in the continuous case, the expected number of distinct values after t steps is N, because all positions have distinct values with probability 1? But that seems counterintuitive.\n\nWait, actually, in continuous space, for any finite set of continuous random variables, the probability that any two are equal is zero, so the probability that all are distinct is 1, hence the expected number of distinct values is N. But in our case, the values are not independent, because each value depends on its neighbors.\n\nWait, even though the original variables are independent, the new variables at each step are dependent because they are functions of the previous variables.\n\nHowever, even with dependencies, in the continuous case, the probability that any two positions have the same value after t steps is still zero, because the process is continuous. For example, in the N=4 case, after one step, the new values are maxima of triples, which are continuous functions of the original variables. Since the original variables are continuous and independent, the new variables are also continuous and the probability that any two are equal is zero. Therefore, the expected number of distinct values after any finite t should be N, because all values are almost surely distinct.\n\nBut this contradicts our earlier discrete examples where in N=4, after one step, you could have two distinct values with positive probability. But in the continuous case, those examples would have measure zero.\n\nWait a minute! The problem states that the initial numbers are uniformly and independently sampled from [0,1]. So it is a continuous setting. Therefore, in the continuous case, the probability that any two positions ever have the same value after any finite number of steps is zero. Therefore, the number of distinct values after any finite time is almost surely N, since all values are distinct with probability 1.\n\nBut that can't be right, because in the N=3 case, after one step, all values become the global maximum, so they are all equal. Wait, but in the continuous case, the probability that all three original numbers are equal is zero, so after one step, the three new values are all equal to the global maximum, which is unique almost surely. Wait, but the global maximum is a single value, so all three new values become equal to it, hence they are all equal. Therefore, in the continuous case, after one step for N=3, the three values are all equal, hence the number of distinct values is 1, which has probability 1. Similarly, for N=4, after one step, the four values are the maxima of their respective triplets. Since the original four numbers are continuous and independent, the probability that any two of these maxima are equal is zero, except perhaps in cases where the windows overlap in a way that forces equality?\n\nWait, no, let's think carefully.\n\nIn the continuous case, for N=3:\n\nAfter one step, all three values become the global maximum of the initial three. Since the initial three are independent uniform [0,1], the global maximum is a single value, which is unique with probability 1 (since the probability that two are equal is zero). Therefore, all three new values are equal to this global maximum, so after one step, all three are equal. Therefore, the number of distinct values is 1 with probability 1. So in the continuous case, for N=3, after one step, the number of distinct values is 1.\n\nSimilarly, for N=4:\n\nAfter one step, each value is the maximum of a triplet of the original four. The four maxima are functions of the original four variables. Since the original four are independent and continuous, the four maxima are also continuous variables. The probability that any two of these maxima are equal is zero. Therefore, the four maxima are all distinct with probability 1? Wait, but they might not be all distinct, but the probability that any two are equal is zero, so the expected number of distinct values is 4, but actually, the probability that all four are distinct is 1, since the probability that any two are equal is zero. Wait, but you can have multiple pairs equal with probability zero, but the expectation of the number of distinct values is still 4?\n\nWait, no. Let me clarify:\n\nIn continuous space, for any finite collection of continuous random variables, the probability that any two are equal is zero. Therefore, the probability that all of them are distinct is 1, because the only way they could fail to be distinct is if at least two are equal, which has probability zero. Therefore, the number of distinct values is almost surely equal to the number of variables, which is N.\n\nBut this contradicts the N=3 case where after one step, all three are equal. Wait, in N=3 case, after one step, all three values are equal to the global maximum. Since the global maximum is a single value (with probability 1), then all three new values are equal to that single value, so they are all equal. Therefore, in that case, even though the original three were independent, the new three are not independent and in fact all equal to the same value. Therefore, in that case, the number of distinct values is 1 with probability 1.\n\nAh, so in the N=3 case, after one step, the three values are not independent; they are all equal to the global maximum. So even though the original three were independent, the new three are dependent and equal.\n\nTherefore, in cases where the update rule forces multiple positions to become equal (like in N=3), then the number of distinct values can drop to a lower number with probability 1.\n\nTherefore, in general, for certain N, after some steps, the number of distinct values can become 1 with probability 1, whereas for others, it remains N.\n\nTherefore, we must analyze for N=2025, after 100 steps, whether the number of distinct values is almost surely 1 or remains N.\n\nIn the N=3 case, after 1 step, it's 1.\n\nIn the N=4 case, after 1 step, the four values are the maxima of their respective triplets. Since the original four are independent, the four maxima are four independent random variables? No, they are dependent because they share overlapping original values.\n\nBut even so, the probability that any two of the maxima are equal is zero, so the four maxima are all distinct with probability 1. Wait, but in reality, in N=4, after one step, we saw in examples that you could have two distinct values. But in the continuous case, is that possible?\n\nWait, let's consider N=4 in the continuous case. Let the original four variables be X1, X2, X3, X4, each uniform [0,1], independent.\n\nAfter one step, the four new variables are:\n\nY1 = max(X4, X1, X2)\n\nY2 = max(X1, X2, X3)\n\nY3 = max(X2, X3, X4)\n\nY4 = max(X3, X4, X1)\n\nWe need to compute the probability that Y1, Y2, Y3, Y4 are all distinct.\n\nBut since all the Yi are continuous random variables, the probability that any two are equal is zero. Therefore, the probability that all four are distinct is 1, because the only way they wouldn't be distinct is if at least two are equal, which has probability zero.\n\nWait, but in my earlier discrete examples with N=4, after one step, I could have two distinct values with positive probability. But in the continuous case, those examples have measure zero.\n\nFor instance, in my first N=4 example with [0.5, 0.3, 0.7, 0.2], which in continuous terms would have probability zero, but if we consider a continuous version where the values are slightly perturbed, then the maxima would all be distinct. Therefore, in the continuous case, after one step for N=4, the four values are almost surely all distinct, so the number of distinct values is 4 with probability 1.\n\nBut this contradicts my earlier discrete analysis where I could get two distinct values. However, in the continuous case, those cases where you get two distinct values have measure zero, so they don't affect the expectation.\n\nTherefore, in the continuous case, for N=4 after one step, the expected number of distinct values is 4, since all are distinct with probability 1.\n\nWait, but in the N=3 case, after one step, they are all equal with probability 1, so the expected number is 1.\n\nTherefore, the behavior depends on whether the update rule causes dependencies that force some values to coincide with probability 1.\n\nIn N=3, the update rule forces all values to the global maximum, so they become equal.\n\nIn N=4, the update rule does not force any values to coincide, because the maxima of the overlapping triplets are independent enough that they remain distinct with probability 1.\n\nWait, but actually, in N=4, Y1 and Y3 both include X2 and X3? Let me see:\n\nY1 = max(X4, X1, X2)\n\nY2 = max(X1, X2, X3)\n\nY3 = max(X2, X3, X4)\n\nY4 = max(X3, X4, X1)\n\nSo Y1 and Y3 both involve X2 and X4, but not necessarily the same combination.\n\nHowever, it's possible that Y1 = Y3 if, say, X2 and X4 are the maximums in their respective triplets, but since the original variables are continuous and independent, the probability that Y1=Y3 is zero.\n\nTherefore, in the continuous case, after one step for N=4, the four values are all distinct with probability 1, so the expected number of distinct values is 4.\n\nBut this contradicts my discrete example where I could get two distinct values, but that was in a discrete setting with possible ties.\n\nTherefore, returning to the original problem: since the problem specifies that the initial number