e directions, leading to probability 1/2.\n\nGiven the symmetry and the square case analogy, I'll settle on \\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{2}} as the answer.\n\nWait, but now I recall that in a regular hexagon, there are three pairs of opposite sides, and the central symmetry implies that for any line not through the center, there's a unique line through the center with the same slope. The set of lines intersecting opposite sides is symmetric with respect to the center, so maybe the measure is 1/2.\n\nAlternatively, here's a better way:\n\nThe hexagon can be divided into six equilateral triangles by drawing lines from the center to each vertex. Any line that intersects two opposite edges must pass through one of these triangles in such a way that it crosses through the center's opposite triangle. Due to symmetry, the probability might be 1/2.\n\nGiven that I can't resolve this definitively but the square case gives 1/2 and the hexagon's symmetry suggests a similar answer, I'll go with \\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{2}}.\n\nWait, but now I recall that in the problem statement, the points are selected uniformly at random inside the hexagon. In the square, if you choose random endpoints, the probability that the chord intersects a pair of opposite sides is indeed 1/2. For example, for a square, the probability that a random chord intersects the top and bottom sides is 1/2 because for any angle, the chord is equally likely to be on either side of the center.\n\nSimilarly, in the hexagon, due to its six-fold symmetry, the probability that a random chord intersects a specific pair of opposite edges is 1/3, so for any pair, it's 1/3, and since there are three pairs, the total probability is 1, which contradicts unless overlapping is considered. Wait, no, because a chord can only intersect one pair of opposite edges or none.\n\nWait, actually in the square, a chord can intersect either a pair of opposite sides or two adjacent sides. The probability of intersecting a pair of opposite sides is 1/2, 