ine y=0 intersects the hexagon at the two vertices (-1,0) and (1,0), but doesn't cross any edges except at those vertices. Wait, but vertices are points where two edges meet, so technically, the line y=0 is tangent to the hexagon at those vertices? Hmm, maybe. Anyway, perhaps my coordinate system is complicating things.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should think more abstractly. Let me recall that in a convex polygon, a line will intersect the polygon in at most two points, unless it's tangent or passes through a vertex. Since the hexagon is convex, any line will enter and exit the hexagon, unless it's parallel to an edge and lies on the \"outside\" side, in which case it might not intersect at all, or if it's exactly aligned with an edge, then it might coincide with an edge or be parallel and not intersect.\n\nBut in our case, since the points are selected inside the hexagon, the line connecting them is entirely inside the hexagon? Wait no, the line is infinite, but the segment between the two points is inside. The line itself extends beyond the segment, so parts of the line may lie outside the hexagon. Therefore, the question is: does the infinite line pass through at least one pair of opposite edges of the hexagon? That is, does the line intersect both edges of some pair of opposite edges?\n\nAlternatively, the problem says \"intersects a pair of opposite edges of the hexagon.\" So maybe it just needs to intersect at least one of the edges in the pair? Wait, but a pair is two edges. So, does it mean intersect both edges of some pair? Or just intersect one edge from some pair? Hmm, the wording is ambiguous. Wait, let me check again: \"Compute the probability that a line passing through both of the points intersects a pair of opposite edges of the hexagon.\" So, the line must intersect a pair, meaning that it intersects both edges of some pair? Because a pair is two edges. So, the line must intersect both edges of at least one of the three pairs of opposite edges.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"intersects a pair of opposite edges\" could mean that it intersects one edge from a pair and the other edge from another pair? But that seems less likely. The phrase \"a pair of opposite edges\" probably means a specific pair, so the line must intersect both edges of at least one such pair.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to: what is the probability that the line connecting two random points inside the regular hexagon intersects both edges of at least one of the three pairs of opposite edges?\n\nAlternatively, the complement event is that the line does not intersect any pair of opposite edges, meaning that for all three pairs of opposite edges, the line does not intersect both edges of that pair. So, for each pair of opposite edges, the line either doesn't intersect either edge of the pair or intersects exactly one edge of the pair. But since the edges are opposite and parallel, if the line intersects one edge of the pair, it must intersect the other edge as well? Wait, no, because the edges are finite segments. For example, take the horizontal pair: top edge (edge 1) at y = √3/2 and bottom edge (edge 4) at y = -√3/2. Suppose the line is nearly horizontal but slightly tilted, so it intersects the top edge near its left end and the bottom edge near its right end. Then, it only intersects one edge from each of the horizontal pair? Wait, actually, if it intersects the top edge, then since it's going downwards, it might also intersect the bottom edge? Wait, but the top edge is at the very top of the hexagon, so if a line goes from near the top edge on the left to somewhere else, it might not necessarily go all the way down to the bottom edge.\n\nWait, actually, in the hexagon, the horizontal edges are at the very top and very bottom. So, a line that starts near the top left corner and goes downward to the right might only intersect the top edge and then exit the hexagon through a non-horizontal edge, like the right side edge (edge 0). So in that case, it would intersect only the top edge (part of the horizontal pair) but not the bottom edge. Similarly, a line that starts near the top right and goes downward to the left might intersect the top edge and the left side edge, but not the bottom edge.\n\nTherefore, it is possible for a line to intersect only one edge of a pair of opposite edges. Hence, the complement event (the line does not intersect any pair of opposite edges) is the event that for every pair of opposite edges, the line either doesn't intersect either edge of the pair or intersects only one edge of the pair. But since the line is straight, if it intersects one edge of a pair, then depending on its slope and position, it might intersect the other edge or not. Hmm, this is getting a bit complicated. Maybe there's a better way.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's helpful to use symmetry. The hexagon is regular and symmetric, so perhaps we can exploit that symmetry to reduce the problem to a simpler case, maybe by considering coordinates or using some transformation.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's useful to consider the concept of a \"supporting line\" or something related to the convex hull. Wait, but the line is determined by two random points. Alternatively, maybe we can parameterize the problem.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model the hexagon as a coordinate system where we can use barycentric coordinates or something else, but that might not be straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can use the concept of the dual problem or consider the probability related to the slope of the line.\n\nWait, let me think: for the line to intersect a pair of opposite edges, it must cross from one side of the hexagon to the opposite side. Since the hexagon is convex, any line passing through two interior points will exit the hexagon through two edges, but those two edges might not be opposite. However, in order for the line to intersect a pair of opposite edges, those two exit points must be on opposite edges.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the key is that the line must cross from one side of the hexagon to the opposite side. Since the hexagon has three pairs of opposite sides, the line could potentially cross through one of those pairs. So the probability we want is the probability that the line crosses through at least one of the three opposite side pairs.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is analogous to a probability that a random chord in a circle crosses a diameter, but in a hexagon. But chords in a circle have different probability distributions than random points in a polygon.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use an area-based approach. If we fix one point, then the locus of points such that the line connecting them intersects a pair of opposite edges can be determined, and then integrate over all positions of the first point and the second point.\n\nBut this seems quite involved. Let me see if I can find some references or similar problems.\n\nWait, actually, I recall that in convex polygons, the probability that a random chord intersects a pair of opposite sides can sometimes be related to the polygon's properties. For a square, for instance, the probability that a random chord intersects a pair of opposite sides is 1/2, because a random chord is equally likely to be vertical or horizontal, but actually, in a square, the probability might be different. Wait, actually, in a square, the probability that a random chord crosses a pair of opposite sides might depend on the method of random selection. Since there are different ways to randomly select a chord: by selecting two random endpoints, by selecting a random midpoint, etc. In our case, it's two random points, so that's the \"random endpoints\" method.\n\nIn a square, if you pick two random points, the line through them will intersect two opposite sides if and only if the line is not parallel to any pair of sides and is not aligned in such a way that it only touches one side. Wait, actually, in a square, any line through two random points will cross two opposite sides unless it's parallel to one of the sides or coincident with a side. Since the points are selected uniformly inside the square, the probability that the line is parallel to a side is zero, because that would require the two points to lie on a line parallel to a side, which has measure zero. Therefore, in a square, the probability that a random chord crosses a pair of opposite sides is 1? But that can't be, because if you pick two points very close together near a corner, the line between them might not cross any opposite sides? Wait, no, even if they are close together near a corner, the line between them would still exit the square through two adjacent sides, not opposite sides. For example, if both points are near the top-right corner, the line between them would go from near the top edge to near the right edge, so it would exit through the top and right edges, which are adjacent, not opposite. Therefore, in a square, a random chord does not necessarily cross a pair of opposite sides; it can cross two adjacent sides instead.\n\nTherefore, in a square, the probability that a random chord crosses a pair of opposite sides is less than 1. Let me think: in a square, the pairs of opposite sides are top/bottom and left/right. A chord will cross a pair of opposite sides if it goes from left to right or top to bottom. The probability that a random chord crosses the left and right sides (i.e., is vertical) or crosses the top and bottom sides (i.e., is horizontal). But since the chord is determined by two random points, the probability that the chord is exactly vertical or horizontal is zero, so we have to consider the measure.\n\nAlternatively, the set of all lines through two random points in the square can be parameterized by their angle and distance from the center, but it's complicated.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps in the square, the probability that a random chord crosses a pair of opposite sides is 1/2. Wait, actually, I think in the \"random endpoints\" model for a square, the probability that the chord is longer than the side length is 1/3, but that's another question. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe in the square, the probability that a random chord crosses a pair of opposite sides is equal to the probability that the chord is not entirely contained within a semicircle or something? Not sure.\n\nBut in any case, returning to the hexagon problem. Maybe it's better to try to compute the probability by considering the dual problem or using coordinates.\n\nLet me try to set up coordinates for the regular hexagon. Let me consider the regular hexagon centered at the origin with vertices at (1,0), (0.5, √3/2), (-0.5, √3/2), (-1, 0), (-0.5, -√3/2), (0.5, -√3/2), as I did before. Let me consider that the hexagon can be divided into six equilateral triangles, each with side length 1, with the center at (0,0).\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to use a coordinate system where the hexagon is aligned with axes in a way that makes calculations easier. Maybe using axial coordinates for hexagons? Hmm, but maybe Cartesian coordinates are okay.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use a transformation to convert the hexagon into a square or another shape where the problem is easier, but I don't know.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use the concept of \"visibility\" or something else. Wait, let me think of the following: For the line connecting two random points to intersect a pair of opposite edges, the line must pass through the hexagon in such a way that it goes from one side to the opposite side. Since the hexagon is convex, any line through two interior points will exit the hexagon through two edges. The question is whether those two edges are a pair of opposite edges.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to: what is the probability that, given two random points in the hexagon, the line connecting them exits the hexagon through a pair of opposite edges?\n\nAlternatively, since the line exits through two edges, the probability that those two edges are opposite.\n\nTherefore, perhaps we can compute the probability by considering all possible exit edge pairs and the conditions under which the line between two random points causes the exit through a pair of opposite edges.\n\nAlternatively, note that in a regular hexagon, there are three pairs of opposite edges, so perhaps the probability is 1/3? But that seems too simplistic, and probably incorrect, since the distribution of exit edges isn't uniform.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's 1/2? Hmm. Alternatively, maybe 2/3? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model this using the concept of a random chord in a hexagon and compute the probability that it crosses a pair of opposite sides.\n\nWait, in a regular hexagon, due to its symmetry, maybe the probability can be calculated by considering the angle of the chord relative to the hexagon's axes.\n\nLet me consider that any line through the hexagon can be characterized by its angle θ with respect to the x-axis, and its distance from the center. Since the hexagon is regular and symmetric, perhaps we can compute the probability by integrating over all possible angles and distances, weighted by the likelihood of such a line being formed by two random points.\n\nBut this seems complicated. Alternatively, perhaps we can use a geometric probability approach by considering the set of all possible lines determined by two random points and computing the measure of those lines that intersect a pair of opposite edges.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use the fact that for a convex polygon, the probability that a random chord intersects a particular pair of opposite sides is equal to the ratio of the \"width\" in that direction over the total perimeter or something? Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, here's an idea: Let's fix one point, say point A, somewhere inside the hexagon. Then, the set of points B such that the line AB intersects a pair of opposite edges is some region inside the hexagon. Then, the probability we want is the average area of that region over all possible positions of A.\n\nSo, if we can, for a given point A, determine the area of points B such that line AB intersects a pair of opposite edges, then integrate that area over all A and divide by the area of the hexagon squared (since we're choosing two points independently and uniformly). This seems like a feasible approach, though computationally intensive.\n\nFirst, let's compute the area of the regular hexagon. Since it's regular with side length 1, the area is (3√3)/2. Wait, let me confirm that.\n\nThe area of a regular hexagon with side length s is given by (3√3 s²)/2. Since we set the side length s = 1, then the area is (3√3)/2 ≈ 2.598.\n\nAlternatively, if we considered the radius (distance from center to vertex) as 1, then the side length is also 1, so the area is the same. So, area = (3√3)/2.\n\nSo, if we can, for a given point A, compute the area of points B such that line AB intersects a pair of opposite edges, then the probability would be (1 / Area²) * ∫_{A} [Area_B(A)] dA, where Area_B(A) is the area of B's for which AB intersects a pair of opposite edges.\n\nBut integrating this over all A seems difficult, but perhaps we can exploit symmetry. Since the hexagon is regular, we can fix point A at a particular position due to rotational symmetry, and then multiply by the appropriate factor. For example, we can fix A at the center, and then compute the area_B(A) when A is at the center, then argue that due to symmetry, the average over all A is the same as the value at the center. Wait, is that true?\n\nWait, actually, if the problem is rotationally symmetric, then the probability should be the same regardless of where point A is, as long as we average over all positions. Wait, but actually, the distribution of point B relative to A might depend on A's position. Hmm, maybe not, due to the hexagon's symmetry.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the probability is the same regardless of where A is, so we can just compute it for a specific A, say the center, and that would be the average. Let me check that intuition.\n\nSuppose we pick point A uniformly at random, and then point B uniformly at random. Due to the symmetry of the hexagon, the probability should be the same as if we fix A at the center and compute the probability over B. Is that correct?\n\nWait, actually, no, because the position of A affects the distribution of the line AB. For instance, if A is near the edge, the set of B such that AB intersects an opposite edge might be different compared to if A is at the center. Therefore, the probability might depend on the location of A. Therefore, we cannot assume it's the same everywhere.\n\nHmm, so that complicates things. Maybe we can instead use a coordinate system where we fix one point and compute the conditional probability, then integrate over all positions of the first point.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can use a probabilistic argument based on the slope of the line.\n\nLet me consider that for the line connecting two random points to intersect a pair of opposite edges, it must have a slope that is such that it can reach from one side to the opposite side. Given the hexagon's geometry, perhaps we can characterize the slopes for which this is possible.\n\nAlternatively, note that in a regular hexagon, the opposite edges are separated by a certain distance, and the line must traverse that distance. The minimal distance between a pair of opposite edges is the distance between those two parallel lines. For instance, for the horizontal pair (top and bottom edges), the distance between them is the height of the hexagon, which is √3 (since from y = √3/2 to y = -√3/2 is a total distance of √3). For the other pairs of opposite edges (with slope ±√3), the distance between them can be computed as well.\n\nWait, let's compute the distance between two opposite edges. Let's take the pair with slope -√3: edges 0 and 3.\n\nEdge 0 is from (1, 0) to (0.5, √3/2). Let me find the equation of edge 0. The two points are (1, 0) and (0.5, √3/2). The slope is (√3/2 - 0)/(0.5 - 1) = (√3/2)/(-0.5) = -√3, as we had before.\n\nSo the equation of edge 0: using point-slope form from point (1, 0):\n\ny - 0 = -√3(x - 1)\n\nSo y = -√3 x + √3\n\nSimilarly, edge 3 is from (-1, 0) to (-0.5, -√3/2). Let's compute its equation.\n\nSlope is (-√3/2 - 0)/(-0.5 - (-1)) = (-√3/2)/(0.5) = -√3, same as edge 0.\n\nUsing point (-1, 0):\n\ny - 0 = -√3(x - (-1)) => y = -√3(x + 1) = -√3 x - √3\n\nTherefore, the two opposite edges (edge 0 and edge 3) have equations y = -√3 x + √3 and y = -√3 x - √3.\n\nWait, let me check edge 3's equation again. Wait, plugging in the point (-1, 0):\n\ny = -√3(x + 1). At x = -1, y = 0, which is correct. At x = -0.5, y = -√3(-0.5 + 1) = -√3(0.5) = -√3/2, which matches the vertex (-0.5, -√3/2). Okay, good.\n\nSimilarly, edge 0: at x = 0.5, y = -√3*(0.5) + √3 = (-√3/2) + √3 = √3/2, which matches the vertex (0.5, √3/2). Good.\n\nNow, to find the distance between these two lines. Since they are parallel (same slope), the distance between them can be computed by taking any point on one line and finding its distance to the other line.\n\nLet me take the point (1, 0) on edge 0. The distance from this point to edge 3's line (y = -√3 x - √3) is given by the formula for distance from a point (x0, y0) to line ax + by + c = 0:\n\nDistance = |a x0 + b y0 + c| / sqrt(a² + b²)\n\nFirst, write edge 3's equation in standard form:\n\ny = -√3 x - √3 => √3 x + y + √3 = 0\n\nSo a = √3, b = 1, c = √3\n\nPoint (1,0):\n\nDistance = |√3*1 + 1*0 + √3| / sqrt( (√3)^2 + 1^2 ) = |√3 + √3| / sqrt(3 + 1) = |2√3| / 2 = √3\n\nSo the distance between edge 0 and edge 3 is √3.\n\nSimilarly, the distance between the horizontal edges (edge 1 and edge 4):\n\nEdge 1 is y = √3/2, edge 4 is y = -√3/2. The distance between these two horizontal lines is √3/2 - (-√3/2) = √3.\n\nSimilarly, the distance between the other pair of edges with slope √3: edges 2 and 5.\n\nEdge 2: let's find its equation. Edge 2 is from vertex 2 (-0.5, √3/2) to vertex 3 (-1, 0). We already computed its slope as √3.\n\nUsing point (-1, 0):\n\nWait, actually, let me compute edge 2's equation properly. Points (-0.5, √3/2) and (-1, 0). The slope is √3 as before.\n\nUsing point (-1, 0):\n\ny - 0 = √3(x - (-1)) => y = √3(x + 1)\n\nSimilarly, edge 5 is from (0.5, -√3/2) to (1, 0). Let's compute its equation.\n\nSlope is √3, as before.\n\nUsing point (1, 0):\n\ny - 0 = √3(x - 1) => y = √3 x - √3\n\nTherefore, edge 5's equation is y = √3 x - √3.\n\nEdge 2's equation is y = √3(x + 1) = √3 x + √3\n\nSo the two lines for edges 2 and 5 are:\n\nEdge 2: y = √3 x + √3\n\nEdge 5: y = √3 x - √3\n\nAgain, these are parallel lines with slope √3. The distance between them can be calculated by taking a point on one line and finding its distance to the other line.\n\nTake the point (-1, 0) on edge 2. The distance from (-1, 0) to edge 5's line y = √3 x - √3.\n\nFirst, write edge 5's equation in standard form: √3 x - y - √3 = 0\n\nSo a = √3, b = -1, c = -√3\n\nDistance from (-1, 0):\n\n|√3*(-1) + (-1)*0 - √3| / sqrt( (√3)^2 + (-1)^2 ) = | -√3 - √3 | / sqrt(4) = | -2√3 | / 2 = √3\n\nSo the distance between edges 2 and 5 is also √3.\n\nTherefore, all three pairs of opposite edges are separated by a distance of √3. Interesting.\n\nNow, for a line to intersect a pair of opposite edges, it must cross from one side to the opposite side, traversing a distance of √3. The direction of the line must be such that it is \"long enough\" in that direction to span the distance between the opposite edges.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the key is to consider the angle of the line relative to the hexagon's axes.\n\nLet me consider that in order for a line to intersect a pair of opposite edges, the line must not be \"too aligned\" with the hexagon's sides. For instance, if the line is almost parallel to a pair of opposite edges, then it might not cross between them, but instead be parallel and miss both, or if it's not exactly parallel, it might cross only one edge?\n\nWait, but as we saw earlier, in the plane, a non-parallel line will intersect both edges of a pair of parallel lines, but in the context of the hexagon, those edges are finite segments. Therefore, even if the infinite line intersects the infinite line of the opposite edge, it might not intersect the actual edge segment.\n\nTherefore, the line must not only be non-parallel to the pair of opposite edges but also positioned such that it actually crosses between the two edges.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think in terms of the angle θ that the line makes with the horizontal axis. For each direction θ, we can compute the probability that a random chord in that direction intersects a pair of opposite edges.\n\nGiven the regular hexagon's symmetry, we can consider θ in the range [0, π/3), since the hexagon has six-fold symmetry, but angles differing by 60 degrees are equivalent.\n\nWait, the hexagon has angles every 60 degrees, so perhaps we can parameterize θ from 0 to π/3, and then multiply appropriately.\n\nAlternatively, here's a plan:\n\n1. For a given angle θ (the angle of the line with the x-axis), determine the set of lines with that angle that pass through the hexagon and intersect a pair of opposite edges.\n\n2. The \"density\" of such lines (i.e., the measure of θ where this occurs) can be integrated over all θ to find the probability.\n\nHowever, integrating over θ might require knowing the distribution of angles for random lines through two random points in the hexagon. That seems complicated, but maybe manageable.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use the fact that the probability is equal to the expected value over all lines through two random points of the indicator function that the line intersects a pair of opposite edges.\n\nSo, probability = E[ I(line intersects a pair of opposite edges) ]\n\nWhere the expectation is over all pairs of random points in the hexagon.\n\nTo compute this expectation, we can use the formula:\n\nProbability = (1 / Area^2) * ∫_{A} ∫_{B} I(line AB intersects a pair of opposite edges) dA dB\n\nWhere the double integral is over all points A and B in the hexagon, and I(...) is 1 if the line intersects a pair of opposite edges, else 0.\n\nThis integral seems difficult to compute directly, but perhaps we can switch the order of integration or use some geometric probability techniques.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use the concept of \"visibility\" or \"crossing\" probabilities. Alternatively, perhaps we can use a coordinate transformation to simplify the hexagon into a square or another shape.\n\nWait, another idea: since the hexagon is centrally symmetric, maybe we can use the concept of central symmetry to pair points A and B with points -A and -B, but I'm not sure if that helps.\n\nAlternatively, here's a different approach inspired by integral geometry:\n\nIn integral geometry, the probability that a random line intersects a given set of curves can be computed using Crofton's formula, which relates the probability to the length of the curves or something similar. However, I might need to recall the exact formulation.\n\nCrofton's formula states that the expected number of intersections of a random line with a curve is proportional to the length of the curve. But I'm not sure if that directly helps here, since we're dealing with a probability related to intersecting a pair of edges, not the expected number of intersections.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think in terms of the dual problem: for each pair of opposite edges, compute the probability that a random line intersects both edges of that pair, then use inclusion-exclusion to account for overlaps where a line might intersect two different pairs.\n\nLet me denote the three pairs of opposite edges as P1, P2, P3.\n\nLet A1 be the event that the line intersects both edges of P1,\n\nA2 the event for P2,\n\nA3 the event for P3.\n\nThen, the probability we want is P(A1 ∨ A2 ∨ A3) = P(A1) + P(A2) + P(A3) - P(A1 ∧ A2) - P(A1 ∧ A3) - P(A2 ∧ A3) + P(A1 ∧ A2 ∧ A3)\n\nDue to symmetry, P(A1) = P(A2) = P(A3), and similarly P(A1 ∧ A2) = P(A1 ∧ A3) = P(A2 ∧ A3), and P(A1 ∧ A2 ∧ A3) is the probability that the line intersects all three pairs of opposite edges. However, can a single line intersect all three pairs of opposite edges?\n\nWait, in a regular hexagon, can a single straight line intersect all three pairs of opposite edges?\n\nLet me visualize: each pair of opposite edges are separated by 60 degrees. If a line were to intersect all three pairs, it would have to intersect three different pairs, each separated by 60 degrees. But a straight line can only have a single direction, so it can intersect at most two pairs of opposite edges? Because each pair is separated by 60 degrees, so a line can be aligned such that it is at some angle that allows it to intersect one pair, but to intersect another pair would require a different angle. Since a line has a fixed angle, it can only intersect one pair of opposite edges? Wait, let me think again.\n\nSuppose we have three pairs of opposite edges, each pair separated by 60 degrees. A straight line has a fixed angle θ. For it to intersect a pair of opposite edges, the angle θ must be such that the line is \"pointing\" towards that pair. Since the hexagon is symmetric, the line can intersect at most one pair of opposite edges? Wait, actually, no. For instance, if the line is at 30 degrees from the horizontal, maybe it can intersect two different pairs?\n\nWait, let me consider the coordinate system again. Suppose we have the horizontal pair of edges (top and bottom). If a line is at a 30-degree angle from the horizontal, it might pass through the hexagon and intersect the top edge and the right edge (which is part of a different pair), but not necessarily both edges of the same pair.\n\nWait, actually, to intersect a pair of opposite edges, the line must cross from one edge of the pair to the other. Given that the edges are separated by a distance of √3, and the line must traverse that distance in the direction perpendicular to the edges.\n\nAlternatively, for a given pair of opposite edges, the set of lines that intersect both edges of that pair form a band around the hexagon. The probability that a random line falls into any of these bands is the desired probability.\n\nGiven the hexagon's symmetry, the three pairs of opposite edges are equally likely in terms of their orientation, so perhaps the probability for each pair is the same, and then we can compute the probability for one pair and multiply by three, adjusting for overlaps.\n\nBut this requires computing the probability for a single pair and then using inclusion-exclusion.\n\nLet me try to compute P(A1), the probability that a random line intersects both edges of pair P1 (say, the horizontal pair).\n\nTo compute P(A1), we can consider all possible lines through two random points in the hexagon and determine the probability that such a line intersects both the top edge and the bottom edge.\n\nWait, but as we saw earlier, a line can intersect the top and bottom edges only if it is not parallel to them and passes through the hexagon between them. Wait, actually, in the hexagon, the top and bottom edges are horizontal, so a line that is not horizontal will intersect them only if it passes through the hexagon between the top and bottom edges.\n\nWait, but actually, any non-horizontal line that passes through the hexagon will exit through two edges. If it's going from the left side to the right side, it might exit through the left and right edges (a different pair), or top and bottom if it's steep enough?\n\nWait, let me consider a specific example. Suppose we have a horizontal line: it will either be the top edge itself (probability zero) or the bottom edge, or lie between them. A horizontal line between them doesn't intersect any of the horizontal edges, but it will intersect the left and right edges? Wait, no, a horizontal line between y = -√3/2 and y = √3/2 would pass through the hexagon from left to right, intersecting the left and right edges (which are not horizontal). The left and right edges are the ones with slope -√3 and √3 respectively?\n\nWait, in our coordinate system, the left and right edges are actually edges 0 and 3? Wait no:\n\nWait, in our coordinate system, the edges with slope -√3 are edges 0 and 3. Edge 0 is on the right side (from (1,0) to (0.5, √3/2)), and edge 3 is on the left side (from (-1,0) to (-0.5, -√3/2)). So the left and right edges are edges 0 and 3, which are not horizontal. The horizontal edges are edges 1 (top) and 4 (bottom).\n\nTherefore, a horizontal line between the top and bottom edges would pass through the hexagon from left to right, intersecting the left and right edges (edges 0 and 3) which are not a pair of opposite edges. The opposite edges for the left and right edges are the ones with slope √3 and -√3?\n\nWait, actually, the left edge (edge 3) is part of the pair with slope -√3 (edges 0 and 3). The right edge (edge 0) is also part of that same pair. So, edges 0 and 3 are a pair of opposite edges with slope -√3. Therefore, a horizontal line passing through the hexagon would intersect edges 0 and 3, which are a pair of opposite edges. Wait a second! That's important.\n\nWait, so if I have a horizontal line cutting through the hexagon, it would intersect the left edge (edge 3) and the right edge (edge 0), which are a pair of opposite edges (since they are both slope -√3). Therefore, such a horizontal line would indeed intersect a pair of opposite edges.\n\nSimilarly, a vertical line (if possible) would intersect the top and bottom edges? Wait, but in our coordinate system, there are no vertical edges. The edges are at 0°, 60°, 120°, etc. So a vertical line (infinite slope) would pass through the hexagon and intersect the top edge (edge 1) and the bottom edge (edge 4), which are a pair of opposite edges (horizontal). Therefore, a vertical line would also intersect a pair of opposite edges.\n\nTherefore, in fact, any non-parallel line to the pairs of opposite edges will intersect a pair of opposite edges? Wait, let me see:\n\nSuppose we have a line with some arbitrary slope. If it's not parallel to any of the three pairs of opposite edges, then it must intersect exactly one pair of opposite edges? Or maybe two?\n\nWait, let's consider a line with slope 1 (45 degrees). Would such a line intersect a pair of opposite edges?\n\nIn our coordinate system, the hexagon's edges have slopes of 0, √3, -√3. A line with slope 1 is not parallel to any of these, so it should intersect the hexagon in two points. The question is, which edges does it intersect?\n\nDepending on the slope, the line might intersect two edges that are not a pair of opposites. For example, suppose we have a line with a shallow slope, say 30 degrees from the x-axis. It might enter the hexagon through the right side (edge 0) and exit through the top edge (edge 1), which are not opposite edges. Edge 0 is part of the pair with slope -√3, and edge 1 is part of the horizontal pair. Therefore, the line would intersect edges from two different pairs, but not both edges of a single pair.\n\nTherefore, such a line would not intersect a pair of opposite edges, only one edge from each of two different pairs. Therefore, the event A1 (intersecting a pair of opposite edges) is not the same as just intersecting any two edges.\n\nTherefore, to compute P(A1), the probability that a random line intersects both edges of a specific pair of opposite edges, say the horizontal pair (edges 1 and 4), we need to compute the probability that the line passes through the hexagon in such a way that it crosses from the top edge to the bottom edge.\n\nSimilarly, for the other pairs.\n\nGiven the symmetry, we can compute P(A1) for one pair and then multiply by 3, then subtract the overlaps where a line intersects two pairs, etc.\n\nSo let's try to compute P(A1), the probability that a random line intersects both the top edge (edge 1) and the bottom edge (edge 4).\n\nFirst, note that edges 1 and 4 are horizontal, at y = √3/2 and y = -√3/2, respectively.\n\nA line intersects both edges 1 and 4 if and only if it passes through the hexagon between y = -√3/2 and y = √3/2, and has a slope such that it actually crosses from the top to the bottom. Wait, actually, any non-horizontal line that passes through the hexagon between these two horizontal edges will cross from one side to the other, but will it necessarily cross both edges?\n\nWait, let me consider a line that is nearly horizontal but slightly tilted upwards. Such a line would enter the hexagon near the bottom edge and exit near the top edge, crossing both edges 1 and 4. Similarly, a line tilted downwards would do the same. A vertical line would also cross both edges 1 and 4. A line with a steep slope, say nearly vertical, would cross edges 1 and 4 as well? Wait, let's see.\n\nTake a vertical line x = c, where c is between -1 and 1. This line will intersect the top edge (edge 1) at (c, √3/2) if |c| ≤ 0.5 (since the top edge spans from x = -0.5 to x = 0.5 at y = √3/2). Wait a second! Hold on, actually, the top edge (edge 1) is from vertex 1 (0.5, √3/2) to vertex 2 (-0.5, √3/2). So the top edge is the horizontal line segment from x = -0.5 to x = 0.5 at y = √3/2. Similarly, the bottom edge (edge 4) is from x = -0.5 to x = 0.5 at y = -√3/2.\n\nTherefore, a vertical line at x = 0.6 would not intersect the top or bottom edges, since those edges only go from x = -0.5 to x = 0.5. Instead, such a vertical line would intersect the right side edges (edge 0 and edge 5? Wait, edge 0 is on the right side but at the bottom right? Wait, let me recall:\n\nEdge 0 is from (1,0) to (0.5, √3/2). So at x = 1, the rightmost vertex, but the vertical line x = 0.6 would intersect the right side edge (edge 0) somewhere?\n\nWait, let's parametrize edge 0: from (1, 0) to (0.5, √3/2). The parametric equations for edge 0 can be written as:\n\nx = 1 - 0.5*t\n\ny = 0 + (√3/2)*t\n\nfor t from 0 to 1.\n\nSimilarly, the vertical line x = 0.6 would intersect edge 0 when 1 - 0.5*t = 0.6 => 0.5*t = 0.4 => t = 0.8\n\nThen y = (√3/2)*0.8 = 0.4√3 ≈ 0.6928\n\nSimilarly, the vertical line x = 0.6 would also intersect the bottom right edge? Wait, the bottom right edge is edge 5: from (0.5, -√3/2) to (1, 0). Let me parametrize edge 5:\n\nx = 0.5 + 0.5*t\n\ny = -√3/2 + (√3/2)*t\n\nfor t from 0 to 1.\n\nSetting x = 0.6:\n\n0.5 + 0.5*t = 0.6 => 0.5*t = 0.1 => t = 0.2\n\nThen y = -√3/2 + (√3/2)(0.2) = -√3/2 + 0.1√3 = -0.9√3/2 ≈ -0.7794\n\nTherefore, the vertical line x = 0.6 intersects edge 0 at (0.6, ~0.6928) and edge 5 at (0.6, ~-0.7794). Therefore, it does not intersect the top or bottom edges (which are only between x = -0.5 and x = 0.5). Therefore, such a vertical line does not intersect the horizontal pair of edges (edges 1 and 4), but instead intersects the right-side edges (edges 0 and 5, which are a different pair).\n\nTherefore, to intersect the horizontal pair of edges (edges 1 and 4), a line must pass through the central part of the hexagon where x ranges from -0.5 to 0.5, and y between -√3/2 and √3/2. Wait, actually, the horizontal edges are exactly at y = ±√3/2 and x between -0.5 and 0.5. So, to intersect edge 1 (the top edge), the line must pass through the segment from (-0.5, √3/2) to (0.5, √3/2). Similarly for edge 4.\n\nTherefore, a line will intersect both edges 1 and 4 if and only if it crosses the hexagon from the top edge to the bottom edge, passing through the central part. That is, the line must enter the hexagon through the top edge and exit through the bottom edge, or vice versa.\n\nHowever, due to the hexagon's shape, not all lines passing through the central region will necessarily exit through the bottom edge. For instance, a line with a very steep slope might exit through a side edge instead.\n\nWait, let me consider a line that starts at the top edge at x = 0.5 (the right end of the top edge) and goes downward to the right. Such a line would exit the hexagon through the right side edge (edge 0), not the bottom edge. Therefore, such a line would not intersect both edges 1 and 4.\n\nTherefore, to intersect both edges 1 and 4, the line must pass through the central region between x = -0.5 and x = 0.5, and have a slope such that it goes from the top edge to the bottom edge without hitting the side edges first.\n\nThis is getting quite involved. Maybe we can model the hexagon as a coordinate system and use integration.\n\nLet me consider the following approach:\n\n1. Parameterize the hexagon in coordinates.\n\n2. For a given line, determine the conditions under which it intersects both edges of a specific pair.\n\n3. Compute the probability by integrating over all possible lines (or equivalently, over all pairs of points) that satisfy those conditions.\n\nGiven the complexity, perhaps we can use a probabilistic symmetry argument.\n\nNotice that the regular hexagon has three pairs of opposite edges, and due to its symmetry, the probability of a random line intersecting any specific pair should be the same. Therefore, the total probability is 3 times the probability for one pair, minus the probabilities of overlaps, etc.\n\nBut to compute this, we need to know the probability for one pair, say the horizontal pair, and then adjust for overlaps.\n\nAlternatively, here's a different idea inspired by the fact that in a centrally symmetric convex polygon, the probability that a random chord intersects a pair of opposite sides is equal to the ratio of the polygon's width in that direction to its diameter? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider that for a line to intersect a pair of opposite edges, it must pass through the \"middle\" of the hexagon in some sense.\n\nWait, another approach: for a line to intersect a pair of opposite edges, it must pass through the hexagon in such a way that it crosses from one side to the opposite side. Since the hexagon is convex, any chord will exit through two edges, and the question is whether those two edges are opposite.\n\nTherefore, the probability we want is equal to the probability that the two edges exited through are a pair of opposite edges.\n\nIn a regular hexagon, there are three pairs of opposite edges, and each pair is separated by 60 degrees. The total number of edge pairs is three, so if the exit edges are equally likely to be any of the three pairs, then the probability would be 1/3. But this is likely not the case because the distribution of exit edges isn't uniform.\n\nHowever, maybe due to the symmetry, the probability is indeed 1/3. Wait, but in the square example, the probability was not 1/2 (assuming pairs of opposite sides), so maybe in the hexagon it's different.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's 2/3? Or 1/2?\n\nAlternatively, let me think of the hexagon as composed of six equilateral triangles. If we pick two random points, the line connecting them is more likely to pass through regions where it can exit through opposite edges.\n\nAlternatively, here's a different plan: use the fact that the hexagon can be transformed into a coordinate system where calculations are easier, like a coordinate system aligned with one of the axes.\n\nLet me rotate the hexagon so that one of the pairs of opposite edges is horizontal. Wait, we already did that. Alternatively, use a coordinate system where one of the pairs of opposite edges is the x-axis.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider the following: in the regular hexagon, the three pairs of opposite edges are each separated by 60 degrees. For a random line, the direction θ of the line is uniformly distributed over [0, π), due to the symmetry of the hexagon. Wait, is the direction of random lines uniformly distributed?\n\nActually, in a convex body, the distribution of directions of random chords is uniform if the selection is done via two random points. Wait, according to some theorems in integral geometry, for a convex body, the distribution of chord directions is uniform. Wait, is that true?\n\nYes, according to Crofton's formula, the average number of intersections of a random line with a curve is proportional to the length of the curve, but in terms of probability, if we consider lines through a random point, the distribution might be uniform. Wait, but we are dealing with lines through two random points.\n\nActually, according to some sources, in a convex body, the distribution of directions of random chords (lines connecting two random points) is uniform with respect to the angle. That is, the probability density function for the angle θ of a random chord is constant over [0, π). Therefore, the probability that a random chord has direction θ is proportional to dθ, so uniform.\n\nIf this is the case, then we can model the direction θ as uniformly distributed over [0, π), and then for each direction θ, compute the probability that a chord in that direction intersects a pair of opposite edges, then integrate over θ and divide by π.\n\nWait, but actually, the distribution might not be exactly uniform because the \"length\" of chords in different directions varies, but in terms of the angle, it might be uniform. Let me check.\n\nAccording to the \"random chord paradox,\" in a circle, the probability that a random chord is longer than the side of an inscribed equilateral triangle depends on the method of random selection. However, in our case, the selection is two random points, which corresponds to the \"random endpoints\" method, and in that case, for a circle, the distribution of chord lengths is not uniform, but the distribution of angles might be uniform.\n\nWait, actually, in a circle, the angle θ of a random chord (as the angle between the chord and a fixed axis) is indeed uniformly distributed because of rotational symmetry. Because you can rotate the circle so that any direction is equally likely.\n\nSimilarly, in a regular hexagon, which is also rotationally symmetric, the direction θ of a random chord should also be uniformly distributed over [0, π). Because for any direction θ, you can rotate the hexagon so that θ becomes 0, and due to symmetry, all directions are equally likely.\n\nTherefore, assuming that the angle θ of a random chord is uniformly distributed over [0, π), we can compute the probability as follows:\n\nFor a given angle θ, compute the probability that a chord with that angle intersects a pair of opposite edges. Then integrate over θ from 0 to π, multiply by (1/π), and that would give the desired probability.\n\nSo, let me formalize this:\n\nLet θ be the angle that the chord makes with the x-axis, uniformly distributed over [0, π). For each θ, let p(θ) be the probability that a chord of direction θ intersects a pair of opposite edges. Then the total probability is (1/π) ∫₀^π p(θ) dθ.\n\nNow, we need to find p(θ) for each θ.\n\nTo compute p(θ), we need to determine, for a given angle θ, the set of chords with that angle that intersect a pair of opposite edges, divided by the set of all chords with that angle.\n\nWait, but actually, for a given direction θ, the set of chords with that direction form a family of parallel lines at angle θ, but shifted along the direction perpendicular to θ. However, in the hexagon, not all such lines will intersect the hexagon. Only those lines that pass through the hexagon will contribute.\n\nFor a given direction θ, the set of chords with that direction that lie within the hexagon form a \"strip\" of lines at angle θ that pass through the hexagon. The probability p(θ) would then be the probability that a randomly selected chord of direction θ intersects a pair of opposite edges.\n\nAlternatively, since all chords of direction θ are equally likely (due to uniformity in point selection), we can model this by considering the distribution of positions of such chords.\n\nFor a given direction θ, the set of all chords with direction θ can be parameterized by their midpoint or their distance from the center. However, this might be complex.\n\nAlternatively, for a given direction θ, the chords of that direction that pass through the hexagon form a region inside the hexagon. The \"density\" of these chords can be related to the length of the intersection of the hexagon with a line in direction θ.\n\nWait, according to integral geometry, the probability that a random line in direction θ intersects the hexagon is proportional to the length of the intersection of the hexagon with a line in direction θ. However, we need the probability that such a line intersects a pair of opposite edges.\n\nWait, this is getting too vague. Let me try to think with a specific example.\n\nLet me pick a specific angle θ and compute p(θ).\n\nLet's first consider θ = 0 (horizontal direction). As we saw earlier, horizontal lines can intersect the horizontal pair of edges (edges 1 and 4) only if they are between y = -√3/2 and y = √3/2, and pass through the central region where x is between -0.5 and 0.5. Wait, actually, for a horizontal line y = c, to intersect both edges 1 and 4, it must satisfy that c is between -√3/2 and √3/2, and the line y = c must pass through the hexagon between x = -0.5 and x = 0.5.\n\nWait, actually, a horizontal line y = c will intersect the hexagon if c is between -√3/2 and √3/2. For such a line, it will intersect the hexagon along the segment between x = -0.5 and x = 0.5 at y = c, provided that |c| < √3/2. Wait, actually, when c is between -√3/2 and √3/2, the horizontal line y = c will intersect the hexagon at two points: on the left and right sides of the hexagon. Wait, but in our coordinate system, the horizontal edges are only at y = ±√3/2 and between x = -0.5 and 0.5. For |c| < √3/2, the horizontal line y = c will pass through the hexagon's \"body,\" intersecting the left and right edges (edges 0 and 3, which are part of the pair with slope -√3). Therefore, a horizontal line at angle θ = 0 will intersect the pair of opposite edges with slope -√3 (edges 0 and 3), not the horizontal pair (edges 1 and 4).\n\nWait a minute! This contradicts my earlier thought. Let me clarify:\n\nIf we have a horizontal line y = c where |c| < √3/2, then it will intersect the hexagon at two points on the left and right sides (edges 0 and 3), which are a pair of opposite edges with slope -√3. Therefore, such a horizontal line does intersect a pair of opposite edges (the left and right edges), but not the top and bottom edges.\n\nOnly when the horizontal line is exactly at y = √3/2 or y = -√3/2 does it coincide with one of the horizontal edges, but those have measure zero.\n\nTherefore, for θ = 0 (horizontal), the probability p(0) that the chord intersects a pair of opposite edges is 1, because any horizontal chord (except those on the edges themselves) intersects the left and right edges (a pair of opposites). Wait, but actually, the horizontal line y = c with |c| < √3/2 does intersect a pair of opposite edges (left and right), so for θ = 0, p(0) = 1.\n\nWait, but the left and right edges are a pair of opposite edges (edges 0 and 3), so yes, intersecting them is an event we count. Therefore, for θ = 0, p(θ) = 1.\n\nSimilarly, for θ = π (which is the same as θ = 0 due to periodicity), same result.\n\nNow consider θ = π/3 (60 degrees). Let's see what happens here.\n\nA line at θ = π/3 (60 degrees) has a slope of tan(π/3) = √3. Such a line is parallel to one of the non-horizontal pairs of edges (the ones with slope √3). Specifically, edges 2 and 5 have slope √3. Therefore, a line with slope √3 is parallel to these edges.\n\nTherefore, a line with slope √3 will either be parallel and not intersect them, or coincide with them (which has probability zero). Therefore, such a line will not intersect the pair of edges with slope √3, but may intersect another pair.\n\nWait, let's consider a line with slope √3 that is not coincident with edges 2 or 5. Such a line will be parallel to edges 2 and 5, so it won't intersect them. However, it may intersect the other pairs of edges.\n\nFor example, take a line with slope √3 that is shifted upwards. It might intersect the top horizontal edge (edge 1) and the right edge (edge 0), which are not a pair of opposites. Alternatively, it might intersect the bottom horizontal edge and the left edge.\n\nWait, let me take a specific example. Let me write the equation of a line with slope √3: y = √3 x + c.\n\nWe need to see for what values of c this line intersects a pair of opposite edges.\n\nFirst, the line will intersect the hexagon if it passes through it. Let's find the range of c for which the line intersects the hexagon.\n\nThe hexagon's boundaries are given by its six edges. To find where the line y = √3 x + c intersects the hexagon, we can find intersections with each edge and see for which c the line passes through the hexagon.\n\nAlternatively, since the hexagon is convex, the line will enter and exit the hexagon, so there will be two intersection points unless it's tangent.\n\nBut we need to see whether those two intersection points are on opposite edges of a pair.\n\nAlternatively, since the line is parallel to edges 2 and 5 (slope √3), it cannot intersect those edges. Therefore, the intersections must be with the other pairs of edges: the horizontal pair (edges 1 and 4) and the pair with slope -√3 (edges 0 and 3).\n\nSuppose the line y = √3 x + c intersects edges 1 and 4 (the horizontal pair). For that to happen, the line must cross from the top edge to the bottom edge. Let's see when that occurs.\n\nThe top edge is y = √3/2 between x = -0.5 and x = 0.5.\n\nThe bottom edge is y = -√3/2 between x = -0.5 and x = 0.5.\n\nSo, to intersect both edges 1 and 4, the line y = √3 x + c must pass through the hexagon such that when x is between -0.5 and 0.5, y ranges from √3/2 to -√3/2. Wait, but the line y = √3 x + c is increasing, so it can only intersect the top edge at some x1 and the bottom edge at some x2 > x1. However, due to the hexagon's boundaries, the line might exit through a side edge before reaching the bottom edge.\n\nLet me compute the intersection points of y = √3 x + c with the hexagon's edges.\n\nFirst, check intersection with the top edge (edge 1): y = √3/2, x between -0.5 and 0.5.\n\nSet √3 x + c = √3/2 => x = (√3/2 - c)/√3 = 1/2 - c/√3.\n\nThis x must be between -0.5 and 0.5.\n\nSimilarly, intersection with the bottom edge (edge 4): y = -√3/2, x between -0.5 and 0.5.\n\nSet √3 x + c = -√3/2 => x = (-√3/2 - c)/√3 = -1/2 - c/√3.\n\nThis x must be between -0.5 and 0.5.\n\nAdditionally, the line might intersect other edges like edges 0, 3, 2, or 5.\n\nLet's also check intersection with edge 0: which is from (1, 0) to (0.5, √3/2). Its equation is y = -√3(x - 1).\n\nSet √3 x + c = -√3(x - 1)\n\n√3 x + c = -√3 x + √3\n\nBring terms together:\n\n√3 x + √3 x = √3 - c\n\n2√3 x = √3 - c\n\nx = (√3 - c)/(2√3) = 1/2 - c/(2√3)\n\nSimilarly, y-coordinate would be y = √3 x + c.\n\nWe need to see if this x is within the edge's x-range: from x=0.5 to x=1.\n\nSimilarly, for edge 3: from (-1,0) to (-0.5, -√3/2), equation y = -√3(x + 1)\n\nSet √3 x + c = -√3(x + 1)\n\n√3 x + c = -√3 x - √3\n\n√3 x + √3 x = -√3 - c\n\n2√3 x = -√3 - c\n\nx = (-√3 - c)/(2√3) = -1/2 - c/(2√3)\n\nThis x must be between -1 and -0.5.\n\nSimilarly, intersections with edges 2 and 5 (slope √3 edges) are parallel, so no intersection.\n\nNow, to determine whether the line y = √3 x + c intersects both edges 1 and 4 (the horizontal pair), we need that the x-values for intersections with edges 1 and 4 are within their respective x-ranges, and that the line doesn't exit earlier through another edge.\n\nThis is quite involved. Let's suppose that c is such that the line intersects both the top and bottom edges within their x-bounds. For that:\n\nFor the top edge intersection x1 = 1/2 - c/√3 must be between -0.5 and 0.5.\n\nSimilarly, the bottom edge intersection x2 = -1/2 - c/√3 must be between -0.5 and 0.5.\n\nSo:\n\nFor x1: -0.5 ≤ 1/2 - c/√3 ≤ 0.5\n\nLeft inequality: 1/2 - c/√3 ≥ -0.5 ⇒ -c/√3 ≥ -1 ⇒ c ≤ √3\n\nRight inequality: 1/2 - c/√3 ≤ 0.5 ⇒ -c/√3 ≤ 0 ⇒ c ≥ 0\n\nSimilarly, for x2: -0.5 ≤ -1/2 - c/√3 ≤ 0.5\n\nLeft inequality: -1/2 - c/√3 ≥ -0.5 ⇒ -c/√3 ≥ 0 ⇒ c ≤ 0\n\nRight inequality: -1/2 - c/√3 ≤ 0.5 ⇒ -c/√3 ≤ 1 ⇒ c ≥ -√3\n\nTherefore, combining the conditions for both edges:\n\nFrom x1: c must be between 0 and √3\n\nFrom x2: c must be between -√3 and 0\n\nTherefore, the only c that satisfies both is c = 0.\n\nWait, that's interesting. The only c where both intersections with edges 1 and 4 occur within their x-bounds is c = 0.\n\nTherefore, the line y = √3 x intersects both the top and bottom edges at their central points? Let me check:\n\nAt c = 0, the line is y = √3 x.\n\nIntersection with top edge y = √3/2:\n\n√3 x = √3/2 ⇒ x = 1/2, which is the right end of the top edge (x=0.5, y=√3/2). Similarly, intersection with bottom edge y = -√3/2:\n\n√3 x = -√3/2 ⇒ x = -1/2, which is the left end of the bottom edge (x=-0.5, y=-√3/2). Therefore, the line y = √3 x passes through the top-right corner (0.5, √3/2) and the bottom-left corner (-0.5, -√3/2). Therefore, this line is actually passing through two vertices, and thus technically intersects the edges at their endpoints. Since vertices are part of two edges, it's intersecting edges 1 and 4 at their endpoints, but also edges 0 and 3 at their endpoints.\n\nTherefore, this line intersects four edges? Wait, no, at each endpoint, it's part of two edges, but the line passes through the vertex, so it's intersecting two edges at that point. However, for our purpose, we are counting intersection with the edges as line segments. Since the line passes through the vertex, which is a corner where two edges meet, does that count as intersecting both edges? Probably not, since the vertex is a single point shared by two edges, but the line only touches that point, so it's not considered intersecting both edges in the sense of crossing from one to the other. So perhaps this case is excluded due to measure zero.\n\nTherefore, except for c = 0 (which is measure zero), there is no c for which the line y = √3 x + c intersects both the top and bottom edges within their segments. Therefore, for θ = π/3 (slope √3), the line will not intersect the horizontal pair of edges (edges 1 and 4), except possibly at the vertices.\n\nTherefore, for θ = π/3, the line will intersect another pair of opposite edges. Let's see which pair.\n\nSince the line is parallel to edges 2 and 5 (slope √3), it cannot intersect them. It might intersect the other pairs.\n\nFor example, take a line with slope √3 and c > 0. Let's say c = 0.5√3.\n\nThe line is y = √3 x + 0.5√3.\n\nLet me see where this line intersects the hexagon.\n\nFirst, check intersection with edge 0: from (1, 0) to (0.5, √3/2).\n\nEquation of edge 0: y = -√3(x - 1)\n\nSet equal to y = √3 x + 0.5√3:\n\n-√3(x - 1) = √3 x + 0.5√3\n\nDivide both sides by √3:\n\n-(x - 1) = x + 0.5\n\n- x + 1 = x + 0.5\n\n-2x = -0.5\n\nx = 0.25\n\nThen y = √3*(0.25) + 0.5√3 = (√3/4 + √3/2) = (3√3)/4 ≈ 1.299, but wait, the top edge is only at y = √3/2 ≈ 0.866. This suggests an error.\n\nWait, let me recalculate:\n\nWait, the line is y = √3 x + 0.5√3.\n\nAt x = 0.25,\n\ny = √3*(0.25) + 0.5√3 = (0.25 + 0.5)√3 = 0.75√3 ≈ 1.299, which is above the top edge's y-coordinate of √3/2 ≈ 0.866. Therefore, this intersection point is outside the edge 0's segment, which only goes up to y = √3/2 at x = 0.5.\n\nTherefore, the intersection with edge 0 is actually beyond the edge's endpoint. Let's check the intersection with edge 1 (top edge) instead.\n\nTop edge is y = √3/2 between x = -0.5 and 0.5.\n\nSet y = √3 x + 0.5√3 = √3/2\n\n√3 x + 0.5√3 = √3/2\n\nDivide both sides by √3:\n\nx + 0.5 = 0.5\n\nx = 0\n\nSo intersection at (0, √3/2), which is the midpoint of the top edge. So that's valid.\n\nNow, where else does the line intersect the hexagon?\n\nLet's check intersection with edge 5: which is from (0.5, -√3/2) to (1, 0). The equation of edge 5 is y = √3(x - 1).\n\nSet equal to y = √3 x + 0.5√3:\n\n√3(x - 1) = √3 x + 0.5√3\n\nDivide by √3:\n\nx - 1 = x + 0.5 ⇒ -1 = 0.5, which is impossible. So no intersection with edge 5.\n\nIntersection with edge 3: from (-1, 0) to (-0.5, -√3/2). Equation: y = -√3(x + 1)\n\nSet equal to y = √3 x + 0.5√3:\n\n-√3(x + 1) = √3 x + 0.5√3\n\nDivide by √3:\n\n-(x + 1) = x + 0.5 ⇒ -x -1 = x + 0.5 ⇒ -2x = 1.5 ⇒ x = -0.75\n\nThen y = √3*(-0.75) + 0.5√3 = (-0.75 + 0.5)√3 = (-0.25)√3 ≈ -0.433\n\nCheck if this x is within edge 3's x-range: from -1 to -0.5. x = -0.75 is within that range. So intersection at (-0.75, -0.433√3 ≈ -0.433*1.732 ≈ -0.75)\n\nWait, let me compute y-coordinate properly:\n\nWait, y = √3*(-0.75) + 0.5√3 = (-0.75 + 0.5)√3 = (-0.25)√3 ≈ -0.433√3 ≈ -0.75 (since √3 ≈ 1.732, so 0.25*1.732 ≈ 0.433, so negative of that is ≈ -0.433). Wait, but edge 3's bottom point is at y = -√3/2 ≈ -0.866. So y ≈ -0.433 is above that, so the intersection point is valid on edge 3.\n\nTherefore, the line y = √3 x + 0.5√3 intersects the top edge at (0, √3/2) and the left edge (edge 3) at (-0.75, -0.433√3). These are two edges: the top edge (part of the horizontal pair) and the left edge (part of the slope -√3 pair). Therefore, this line intersects edges from two different pairs, but not both edges of a single pair.\n\nTherefore, it does not intersect a pair of opposite edges. Therefore, for this c, the line does not intersect any pair of opposite edges? Wait, but it did intersect the top edge (part of horizontal pair) and the left edge (part of slope -√3 pair). Since it only intersects one edge from each of two different pairs, it doesn't count as intersecting a pair of opposite edges.\n\nTherefore, for θ = π/3, the lines parallel to the slope √3 do not intersect any pair of opposite edges except possibly at vertices, which have measure zero. Therefore, p(θ) = 0 for θ = π/3?\n\nWait, but let's consider another c. Let's take c = -0.5√3.\n\nThen the line is y = √3 x - 0.5√3.\n\nCheck intersections:\n\nWith the bottom edge (y = -√3/2):\n\n√3 x - 0.5√3 = -√3/2 ⇒ √3 x = -√3/2 + 0.5√3 = 0 ⇒ x = 0\n\nSo intersection at (0, -√3/2), the midpoint of the bottom edge.\n\nIntersection with another edge, say edge 5:\n\nEquation of edge 5: y = √3(x - 1)\n\nSet equal to y = √3 x - 0.5√3:\n\n√3(x - 1) = √3 x - 0.5√3 ⇒ √3 x - √3 = √3 x - 0.5√3 ⇒ -√3 = -0.5√3 ⇒ which is false.\n\nIntersection with edge 0:\n\nEdge 0: y = -√3(x - 1)\n\nSet equal to y = √3 x - 0.5√3:\n\n-√3(x - 1) = √3 x - 0.5√3 ⇒ -x + 1 = x - 0.5 ⇒ -2x = -1.5 ⇒ x = 0.75\n\nThen y = √3*(0.75) - 0.5√3 = 0.25√3 ≈ 0.433\n\nCheck if this is on edge 0: edge 0 goes from (1, 0) to (0.5, √3/2). At x = 0.75, which is between 0.5 and 1, so yes. The y-coordinate at x = 0.75 on edge 0 is y = -√3(0.75 - 1) = -√3(-0.25) = 0.25√3 ≈ 0.433, which matches. So intersection at (0.75, 0.433).\n\nTherefore, this line intersects the bottom edge at (0, -√3/2) and the right edge (edge 0) at (0.75, 0.433). These are edges from two different pairs: the bottom edge (horizontal pair) and the right edge (slope -√3 pair). Again, only one edge from each pair, so doesn't count.\n\nTherefore, it seems that for θ = π/3, the lines with slope √3 do not intersect both edges of any pair of opposite edges, except perhaps at vertices, which have measure zero. Therefore, p(θ) = 0 for θ = π/3.\n\nSimilarly, for θ = π/6 (30 degrees), which is the complement of π/3, perhaps similar things happen.\n\nThis suggests that for angles θ that are multiples of π/3, the probability p(θ) is either 1 or 0. For other angles, maybe it's something in between.\n\nGiven the complexity, perhaps it's better to consider that for each of the three pairs of opposite edges, the set of directions θ for which a line in direction θ intersects that pair of edges forms an interval of angles, and then compute the total measure.\n\nAlternatively, let's consider that for a line to intersect a particular pair of opposite edges, say the horizontal pair (edges 1 and 4), it must be that the line is not parallel to them and passes through the hexagon between their y-values.\n\nAs we saw earlier, for the horizontal pair, the lines that can intersect both edges must have a slope such that they cross from the top to the bottom within the central region.\n\nSpecifically, for a line to intersect both the top and bottom edges, its slope must be such that it can traverse from y = √3/2 to y = -√3/2 within the x-range of -0.5 to 0.5.\n\nThe slope required to go from (x1, √3/2) to (x2, -√3/2) with x1 and x2 within [-0.5, 0.5] must satisfy:\n\nΔy = -√3\n\nΔx = x2 - x1\n\nSo slope m = Δy / Δx = -√3 / (x2 - x1)\n\nSince x2 < x1 (because going down), the denominator is negative, so slope is positive.\n\nTo have the line pass through the central region, the slope must be between some minimum and maximum values.\n\nThe most \"shallow\" slope that can go from the top edge to the bottom edge within the central region would be when the line goes from the left end of the top edge (-0.5, √3/2) to the right end of the bottom edge (0.5, -√3/2). The slope here is:\n\nm = (-√3/2 - √3/2) / (0.5 - (-0.5)) = (-√3) / 1 = -√3\n\nWait, that slope is negative. Wait, let's compute it properly:\n\nStarting at (-0.5, √3/2) and ending at (0.5, -√3/2):\n\nΔy = -√3/2 - √3/2 = -√3\n\nΔx = 0.5 - (-0.5) = 1\n\nSo slope m = Δy / Δx = -√3 / 1 = -√3\n\nSimilarly, the steepest slope would be from the right end of the top edge (0.5, √3/2) to the left end of the bottom edge (-0.5, -√3/2):\n\nΔy = -√3/2 - √3/2 = -√3\n\nΔx = -0.5 - 0.5 = -1\n\nSlope = (-√3)/(-1) = √3\n\nTherefore, the lines that intersect both the top and bottom edges must have slopes between -√3 and √3, but wait, actually, the slope between those two points is -√3 and √3, so the possible slopes for lines that can connect a point on the top edge to a point on the bottom edge within the central region must have slopes between -√3 and √3?\n\nWait, actually, any line that goes from the top edge to the bottom edge must have a slope between -∞ and ∞, but constrained by the hexagon's boundaries.\n\nWait, perhaps the allowable slopes for lines that intersect both the top and bottom edges are those between -√3 and √3?\n\nWait, no, let's see. For a line to intersect both the top and bottom edges within the hexagon's central region (x between -0.5 and 0.5), the slope must be between -√3 and √3?\n\nWait, actually, if you consider the two extreme cases:\n\n- The shallowest slope (closest to horizontal) that can go from the top edge to the bottom edge within the central region is when the line goes from (-0.5, √3/2) to (0.5, -√3/2), which has slope -√3.\n\n- The steepest slope (closest to vertical) is when the line goes from (0.5, √3/2) to (-0.5, -√3/2), which has slope √3.\n\nWait, actually, slope of √3 is steeper than slope of -√3. So the allowable slopes for lines that can intersect both the top and bottom edges within the central region are between -√3 and √3.\n\nWait, but slope of √3 is steeper than slope of -√3? In terms of absolute value, yes, but in terms of the actual slope, positive and negative.\n\nActually, the lines that can intersect both top and bottom edges must have slopes between -√3 and √3. Because if the slope is greater than √3 in absolute value, the line would be too steep to reach from the top to the bottom within the central region.\n\nWait, let's consider a line with slope steeper than √3, say m = 2√3.\n\nSuch a line would have a very steep positive slope. Starting from the top edge at some point (x1, √3/2), going down to the bottom edge would require a large negative Δy, but with a positive slope, you would have to go to the left to get a large negative Δx, but within the central region's x-range of [-0.5, 0.5], you can't get enough Δx to achieve that slope.\n\nTherefore, the allowable slopes for lines intersecting both top and bottom edges are between -√3 and √3.\n\nTherefore, for a random line to intersect the horizontal pair of edges, its slope must be between -√3 and √3. Additionally, the line must pass through the central region where x is between -0.5 and 0.5.\n\nWait, but how does this translate into the angle θ?\n\nThe slope m = tan(θ), where θ is the angle from the x-axis.\n\nTherefore, the condition |tan(θ)| ≤ √3 corresponds to angles between -π/3 and π/3 (i.e., between -60 degrees and 60 degrees), but since θ is measured from 0 to π, we can say that angles between 0 and π/3 (30 degrees) and between 2π/3 and π (120 to 180 degrees) would have |tan(θ)| ≤ √3?\n\nWait, no. Let me think:\n\nThe slope tan(θ) = |Δy/Δx|. The condition |tan(θ)| ≤ √3 corresponds to angles where |θ| ≤ π/3 (i.e., between -60 and 60 degrees from the x-axis). But since θ is measured from 0 to π, the angles where |tan(θ)| ≤ √3 are θ in [0, π/3] and θ in [2π/3, π]. Wait, no.\n\nWait, tan(π/3) = √3, tan(2π/3) = tan(π - π/3) = -tan(π/3) = -√3. So the absolute value |tan(θ)| ≤ √3 occurs when θ is in [0, π/3] ∪ [2π/3, π]. Because between π/3 and 2π/3, the tangent has absolute value greater than √3.\n\nTherefore, the set of angles θ where |tan(θ)| ≤ √3 is two intervals of length π/3 each, totaling 2π/3.\n\nTherefore, the measure of angles where a line with that angle can potentially intersect the horizontal pair of edges is 2π/3. However, we also need the line to actually pass through the hexagon in such a way that it intersects both edges.\n\nBut earlier, we saw that for angles between 0 and π/3, and between 2π/3 and π, the lines with those angles can intersect the horizontal pair of edges, but only if they pass through the central region.\n\nWait, actually, for angles between 0 and π/3 (i.e., slopes between 0 and √3), the lines can intersect the horizontal pair of edges only if they pass through the central region. Similarly for angles between 2π/3 and π (slopes between -∞ and -√3), but since angles are modulo π, perhaps it's symmetric.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to note that for each direction θ where |tan(θ)| ≤ √3, the lines with that direction can potentially intersect the horizontal pair of edges, but we need to ensure that the line actually does so within the hexagon.\n\nGiven the hexagon's geometry, for a given angle θ where |tan(θ)| ≤ √3, the lines in that direction that pass through the hexagon will intersect the horizontal pair of edges if they pass through the central region between x = -0.5 and x = 0.5.\n\nThe set of such lines forms a band around the hexagon. The probability that a random chord in direction θ intersects the horizontal pair is equal to the proportion of lines in that direction that pass through the central region.\n\nThis is getting quite involved, but perhaps we can approximate that for each direction θ where |tan(θ)| ≤ √3, the probability p(θ) = 1, and for other directions p(θ) = 0. But we saw earlier that for θ = π/3, p(θ) = 0, which contradicts this.\n\nAlternatively, maybe p(θ) is 1 for angles in [0, π/3] ∪ [2π/3, π], and 0 otherwise. But when we considered θ = π/3, which is at the boundary, we found that p(θ) = 0, so perhaps it's open intervals.\n\nIf we assume that for θ in (0, π/3) ∪ (2π/3, π), the probability p(θ) = 1, and for θ in [π/3, 2π/3], p(θ) = 0, then the total measure would be 2*(π/3) = 2π/3, so the average probability would be (2π/3)/π = 2/3.\n\nTherefore, the probability would be 2/3.\n\nSimilarly, due to symmetry, the same probability would apply for the other two pairs of opposite edges. However, since we are considering the probability that the line intersects any of the three pairs, and if these events are mutually exclusive (which they aren't), we would have to adjust, but actually, a line can intersect two pairs at the same time?\n\nWait, can a single line intersect two different pairs of opposite edges?\n\nSuppose a line intersects both the horizontal pair and the slope -√3 pair. For example, a line going from the top edge to the right edge (part of the slope -√3 pair). Such a line would intersect one edge from the horizontal pair and one edge from the slope -√3 pair, so it doesn't intersect a full pair of opposites.\n\nTo intersect two pairs, the line would have to intersect both edges of two different pairs, which would require intersecting four edges, which is impossible for a straight line in a convex polygon.\n\nTherefore, a line can intersect at most one pair of opposite edges. Therefore, the events A1, A2, A3 are mutually exclusive. Therefore, the total probability is P(A1) + P(A2) + P(A3) = 3 * P(A1), since they're symmetric.\n\nIf P(A1) = 2/3, then total probability would be 2, which is impossible. Wait, no, if P(A1) is the probability for one pair, and the three pairs are mutually exclusive, then total probability is 3 * P(A1). But if P(A1) is 2/3, then total probability would exceed 1, which is impossible. Therefore, my assumption must be wrong.\n\nAh, right, the events are not mutually exclusive. A line can potentially intersect two different pairs of opposite edges if it's aligned in a special way? Wait, no, in a convex polygon, a line can intersect at most two edges, and those edges cannot be from two different pairs of opposite edges, because the pairs are separated by 60 degrees.\n\nWait, in a regular hexagon, any two pairs of opposite edges are separated by 60 degrees. A straight line can have a single direction, so it can only intersect edges that are aligned in a way consistent with that direction.\n\nWait, for instance, if a line intersects one edge from the horizontal pair and one edge from the slope -√3 pair, that's two edges from different pairs. But since the line can only intersect two edges total, it cannot intersect both edges from two different pairs. Because each pair requires two edges, but the line can only intersect two edges total, so it can only intersect one edge from each of two different pairs, but not both edges of a single pair.\n\nTherefore, the events A1, A2, A3 are mutually exclusive. Because to have A1, the line must intersect both edges of pair 1; to have A2, both edges of pair 2, etc. Since the line can only intersect two edges, it can only be in one of these events or none.\n\nTherefore, the total probability is P(A1) + P(A2) + P(A3) = 3 * P(A1), due to symmetry.\n\nEarlier, if we assumed that for each pair, the probability P(A1) is 2/3, then the total probability would be 2, which is impossible. So that can't be.\n\nAlternatively, if for each pair, the probability P(A1) is 1/3, then total probability would be 1, which is also impossible.\n\nHmm, this suggests my earlier approach was flawed.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the probability for one pair is 1/2, leading to total probability 3*(1/2) - overlaps, but this is unclear.\n\nWait, going back to the angle analysis. If for each direction θ, the probability that the line intersects a specific pair is p(θ), then due to symmetry, the total probability would be (1/π) * ∫₀^π [p₁(θ) + p₂(θ) + p₃(θ)] dθ, where p_i(θ) is 1 if the line in direction θ intersects pair i, else 0.\n\nDue to rotational symmetry, p₁(θ) = p₂(θ) = p₃(θ) = p(θ). Therefore, the total probability is 3 * (1/π) ∫₀^π p(θ) dθ.\n\nIf we can compute ∫₀^π p(θ) dθ, then multiply by 3/π.\n\nEarlier, I conjectured that p(θ) = 1 for θ in [0, π/3) ∪ (2π/3, π], and p(θ) = 0 otherwise. Then the integral would be:\n\n∫₀^{π/3} 1 dθ + ∫_{2π/3}^π 1 dθ = (π/3) + (π - 2π/3) = π/3 + π/3 = 2π/3.\n\nThus, total probability would be 3/π * (2π/3) = 2. Which is impossible, so this is wrong.\n\nTherefore, my assumption that p(θ) = 1 in those intervals is incorrect.\n\nWait, perhaps p(θ) is not 1 in those intervals, but less.\n\nActually, for angles θ where |tanθ| < √3, the line can potentially intersect the horizontal pair, but only if it's positioned correctly within the hexagon. However, for a random line in that direction, what fraction of them actually do intersect the horizontal pair?\n\nIn other words, for a given direction θ where |tanθ| < √3, the set of lines with that direction that intersect the horizontal pair forms a subset of all lines with that direction. The probability p(θ) is the measure of that subset divided by the total measure of lines with that direction.\n\nIn integral geometry, the measure of lines intersecting a convex set is proportional to the perimeter of the set or something? I might need to recall that the probability that a random line in direction θ intersects a convex body is proportional to the length of the intersection of the body with a line perpendicular to θ.\n\nWait, according to the Crofton formula, the average number of intersections of a random line with a convex body is proportional to its perimeter. But here we want the probability that a random line intersects a particular pair of edges, which is a specific subset.\n\nAlternatively, for a given direction θ, the set of lines with that direction that intersect the hexagon form a \"slice\" through the hexagon. The portion of this slice that intersects the specific pair of opposite edges would depend on the angle.\n\nAlternatively, consider that for a given direction θ, the set of lines with that direction that intersect the horizontal pair of edges corresponds to lines that pass through the central region between x = -0.5 and x = 0.5.\n\nThe \"width\" of the hexagon in the direction perpendicular to θ determines the measure.\n\nThis is getting too vague without concrete calculations.\n\nPerhaps an alternative approach inspired by the answer for the square:\n\nIn a square, the probability that a random chord intersects a pair of opposite sides is 1/2. Because for any chord not parallel to the sides, it will intersect two sides, which are either opposite or adjacent. Due to symmetry, half the time they are opposite, half the time adjacent? Wait, actually in a square, if you pick two random points, the line will intersect two adjacent sides or two opposite sides?\n\nWait, no. In a square, any chord that is not parallel to a side will exit through two adjacent sides. On