(parallel to the vertical pair) will intersect the horizontal pair (top and bottom sides).\n\n- A diagonal line (neither parallel to horizontal nor vertical) will exit through two adjacent sides.\n\nTherefore, all lines except those parallel to the sides intersect a pair of opposite sides. But lines parallel to the sides do intersect a pair of opposite sides as well (the non-parallel pair). The only lines that don't intersect a pair of opposite sides are those that are... actually, every line intersects two sides, which are either opposite or adjacent.\n\nWait, actually, every straight line passing through a convex polygon like a square will intersect exactly two sides, which are either adjacent or opposite. In a square:\n\n- If the line is not parallel to any sides, it will intersect two adjacent sides.\n\n- If the line is parallel to a pair of sides, it will intersect the other pair of opposite sides.\n\nTherefore, the set of lines that intersect a pair of opposite sides is precisely the set of lines not parallel to any side, plus the lines parallel to the sides (which intersect the other pair). Wait, but the lines parallel to the sides do intersect a pair of opposite sides (the ones they're not parallel to). Therefore, actually, every line intersects a pair of opposite sides.\n\nWait a minute, that can't be. For example, take a horizontal line in the square. It's parallel to the top and bottom sides, so it intersects the left and right sides, which are a pair of opposite sides. Similarly, a vertical line intersects the top and bottom, which are a pair. A diagonal line intersects two adjacent sides, which are not a pair of opposite sides. Wait, hold on, adjacent sides are not opposite.\n\nIn a square, each side has one opposite side and two adjacent sides. Therefore, a line intersecting two adjacent sides does not intersect a pair of opposite sides. Therefore, such lines do not count towards our desired event.\n\nTherefore, in the square, the probability that a ra