n this case, perhaps the probability is related to the angle.\n\nIf we consider a random chord, the angle it makes with the horizontal can determine whether it intersects a pair of opposite sides.\n\nAlternatively, here's a different approach inspired by integrating over all possible angles and positions:\n\nSuppose we fix point A somewhere in the hexagon, and then consider the distribution of point B relative to A.\n\nDue to the hexagon's symmetry, we can fix point A at a particular position, say the top vertex (0.5, √3/2 in the original coordinates, but in the rotated system, let's say in the rotated system, fix A at (0, √3/2), the midpoint of the top edge.\n\nWait, in the rotated system, the top edge is from (-0.5, √3/2) to (0.5, √3/2). Let me place point A at (0, √3/2), the center of the top edge.\n\nThen, the position of point B is uniformly random in the lower half of the hexagon (y' ≤ 0).\n\nWe can then compute the probability that the line from A(0, √3/2) to B(x, y) intersects the bottom edge y' = -√3/2.\n\nBecause if it does, then it intersects both top and bottom edges; if not, it doesn't.\n\nWait, but point A is on the top edge, so the line from A to B will always intersect the top edge at A, but to intersect the bottom edge, it must reach y' = -√3/2 within the hexagon.\n\nBut since B is in the lower half, the line from A to B will go from the top edge down to B, so it must cross the bottom edge only if B is below the bottom edge, which it isn't because B is inside the hexagon.\n\nWait, no. The bottom edge is part of the hexagon's boundary. So if the line from A to B exits the hexagon through the bottom edge, then it does intersect the bottom edge.\n\nBut since B is inside the hexagon, the line segment from A to B will not exit through the bottom edge unless B is on the other side.\n\nWait, this is confusing.\n\nAlternatively, if A is on the top edge, then the line from A to B (which is inside the lower half) will leave the hexagon at some edge other than the bottom edge, unless B is positioned such that the line goes straight down.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to consider coordinates.\n\nLet me define in the rotated system:\n\nLet’s fix point A at the top midpoint (0, √3/2).\n\nPoint B is uniformly random in the lower half of the hexagon (y' ≤ 0).\n\nWe need the probability that the line AB intersects the bottom edge y' = -√3/2 within the hexagon.\n\nWait, but since B is inside the hexagon, the line segment AB will not go beyond B, so it won't reach the bottom edge unless B is on the bottom edge itself.\n\nHmm, so this approach might not work.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we need to consider the entire line, not just the segment between A and B.\n\nThe problem states \"a line passing through both of the points intersects a pair of opposite edges\".\n\nSo it's the infinite line, not the segment between the points.\n\nAh! This is crucial. The line, not the segment. So even if the two points are both in the upper half, the infinite line might extend downward and intersect the bottom edge, even though the segment between them doesn't.\n\nTherefore, in this case, even if both points are on the same side of the x'-axis, the infinite line might still intersect both top and bottom edges.\n\nThis changes things.\n\nTherefore, the probability we're seeking is the probability that the infinite line through A and B intersects both the top and bottom edges of the hexagon.\n\nThis is different from the segment intersecting them.\n\nTherefore, even if both points are on the same side of the x'-axis, the line might still pass through the opposite side.\n\nTherefore, we need to compute for two random points in the hexagon, the probability that the infinite line through them intersects both the top and bottom edges (in the rotated system) or any pair of opposite edges.\n\nThis is more complex, but perhaps manageable.\n\nIn the rotated coordinate system, let's consider the hexagon H' with top edge y'=√3/2 and bottom edge y'=-√3/2.\n\nWe need the probability that a random line through two points in H' intersects both the top and bottom edges.\n\nTo compute this, note that a line intersects the top edge if it has a point on y'=√3/2 within the hexagon's horizontal extent (x' between -0.5 and 0.5), and similarly for the bottom edge.\n\nA line will intersect both edges if it crosses from above y'=√3/2 to below y'=-√3/2, or vice versa, within the hexagon's bounds.\n\nAlternatively, the line must have a slope such that it passes through the hexagon's vertical extent from top to bottom.\n\nLet me consider the general equation of a line in the rotated system: y' = m x' + c.\n\nThis line intersects the top edge y'=√3/2 when x' = (√3/2 - c)/m, provided m ≠ 0.\n\nSimilarly, it intersects the bottom edge y'=-√3/2 when x' = (-√3/2 - c)/m.\n\nFor the line to intersect both edges within the hexagon's horizontal extent (x' ∈ [-0.5, 0.5]), the corresponding x' values must lie within [-0.5, 0.5].\n\nAdditionally, the line must pass through the hexagon, so there must be points on the line within the hexagon.\n\nThis is getting very involved. Perhaps a better approach is to use the concept of the dual space or expected value.\n\nAlternatively, here's an idea inspired by the fact that in a centrally symmetric convex polygon, the probability that a random line intersects a pair of opposite sides is 1/2.\n\nWait, why 1/2?\n\nBecause for any line not passing through the center, it either intersects a pair of opposite sides or not, but due to symmetry, the probability might be 1/2.\n\nAlternatively, since the hexagon is centrally symmetric, for any line not passing through the center, there is a corresponding line through the center that is the reflection, and they would have symmetric probabilities.\n\nHmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, consider that for any line that doesn't pass through the center, it either intersects a pair of opposite sides or not, and due to symmetry, the probability might be 1/2.\n\nBut I need to think of a better argument.\n\nSuppose we have a line L. Either L intersects a pair of opposite sides, or it doesn't. Due to the central symmetry, for every line L that doesn't pass through the center and doesn't intersect a pair of opposite sides, there is a corresponding line L' which is the reflection through the center, which would intersect the opposite pair of sides. Since the distribution is uniform and symmetric, the measure of such lines would be equal, leading to probability 1/2.\n\nWait, this might be the case.\n\nLet me elaborate:\n\nConsider the central symmetry: for any point P in the hexagon, there is a point P' such that the center is the midpoint of P and P'. The set of all lines can be partitioned into those passing through the center and those not.\n\nFor lines not passing through the center:\n\n- If a line L does not intersect any pair of opposite sides, then its reflection L' through the center will also not intersect any pair of opposite sides.\n\nWait, no, reflection might flip the sides it intersects.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps if a line L doesn't intersect a pair of opposite sides, then its reflection L' will intersect the opposite pair.\n\nBecause reflecting across the center would map the sides it doesn't intersect to the opposite sides.\n\nWait, suppose a line L does not intersect the top and bottom edges (in the rotated system). Then its reflection across the center would map the top edge to the bottom edge and vice versa, so the reflected line L' would not intersect the original top and bottom edges but instead the reflected ones? Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, consider that the central symmetry implies that the probability of a line intersecting a particular pair of opposite sides is equal to the probability of intersecting the opposite pair. So, if we consider all three pairs, the total probability would be 3 * P(intersecting a specific pair).\n\nIf the events are mutually exclusive (a line can intersect at most one pair of opposite sides), then the total probability is 3 * P.\n\nBut due to symmetry, P is the same for each pair, so if we can compute P for one pair, multiply by 3.\n\nNow, if we can argue that P = 1/2 for each pair, then total probability is 3/2, which is impossible, so that can't be.\n\nAlternatively, the events are not mutually exclusive? Wait, a line can intersect more than one pair? No, in a convex polygon, a line can intersect at most two edges, which can be at most one pair of opposite edges.\n\nTherefore, mutually exclusive.\n\nThus, total probability is 3 * P.\n\nIf we can show that for any specific pair, the probability that a random line intersects that pair is 1/3, then total probability is 1, which can't be, since some lines don't intersect any pair.\n\nAlternatively, the probability for each pair is 1/3, so total probability is 1, implying every line intersects a pair, which is false.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the probability for each pair is 1/2, but that would give 3/2 > 1.\n\nHmm, so this approach is flawed.\n\nPerhaps the correct way is to note that for a centrally symmetric convex polygon, the probability that a random line intersects a pair of opposite sides is 1/2.\n\nHere's why: for any line not passing through the center, it either intersects a pair of opposite sides or it doesn't. Due to central symmetry, the set of lines that do intersect a pair is in one-to-one correspondence with those that don't, via central symmetry. Therefore, the probability is 1/2.\n\nYes, this seems plausible.\n\nBecause for any line L not passing through the center, if it does not intersect any pair of opposite sides, then its central reflection L' would also not intersect any pair of opposite sides. However, this doesn't directly give the correspondence.\n\nWait, perhaps better:\n\nSuppose we take a random line L. If it passes through the center, it will intersect two opposite edges (since the center is inside, and the line through the center must cross from one side to the opposite). Wait, no, a line through the center might be parallel to a pair of edges?\n\nWait, no, in a regular hexagon, a line through the center can be in any direction, but it will intersect two opposite edges only if it's not parallel to any pair.\n\nWait, actually, a line through the center will always intersect two opposite edges because of the hexagon's symmetry. For example, in the rotated system, a line through the center (0,0) with slope m will go through the hexagon and intersect the top and bottom edges if it's not too steep, or the left and right edges otherwise.\n\nWait, actually, in the rotated system, a line through the center can intersect either the top/bottom edges or the left/right edges depending on its slope.\n\nTherefore, lines through the center do intersect a pair of opposite edges (either top/bottom or left/right), so they do count.\n\nFor lines not through the center: For any such line, either it intersects a pair of opposite edges or it doesn't. Due to central symmetry, for every line that doesn't intersect any pair, there is a corresponding line (its central reflection) that also doesn't intersect any pair. However, this doesn't necessarily make the probability 1/2.\n\nAlternatively, consider that the set of all lines can be partitioned into those passing through the center and those not. The lines through the center all intersect a pair of opposite edges. For the non-central lines, the probability that they intersect a pair is equal to the probability that their reflection doesn't intersect the corresponding pair, leading to 1/2.\n\nAlternatively, here's a better symmetry argument:\n\nLet’s consider the central symmetry of the hexagon. For any line L not passing through the center, define L' as the line obtained by reflecting L through the center. The reflection of the hexagon through the center is itself, since it's centrally symmetric.\n\nThe line L intersects a pair of opposite edges if and only if L' intersects the reflected pair of opposite edges. However, since the hexagon is symmetric, the reflected pair is just another pair of opposite edges.\n\nTherefore, the property of intersecting a pair of opposite edges is invariant under central symmetry. Therefore, the probability that a random line intersects any specific pair is the same as the probability that its reflection intersects that pair. Since reflection is a bijection, the probability for each pair is the same.\n\nLet’s denote by P the probability that a random line intersects a specific pair of opposite edges, say the top and bottom edges in the rotated system. Due to symmetry, the probability of intersecting any other pair is also P.\n\nThe total probability that a random line intersects at least one pair of opposite edges is then 3P - (probabilities of overlapping events). But since a line can intersect at most one pair of opposite edges (as it can only cross two edges total), the events are mutually exclusive. Thus, the total probability is 3P.\n\nHowever, we also know that the total probability cannot exceed 1, so 3P ≤ 1 ⇒ P ≤ 1/3.\n\nBut also, the lines through the center all intersect a pair of opposite edges. The set of lines through the center has measure zero in the space of all lines, so they don't contribute to the probability.\n\nNow, consider that for any line not through the center, either it intersects a pair of opposite edges or it doesn't. Due to the central symmetry, the lines that do intersect a pair can be paired with lines that don't, except for those through the center. Since the center lines have measure zero, the probability that a random line intersects a pair is equal to the probability that a random line not through the center intersects a pair, which by symmetry should be 1/2 of the non-central lines. But since central lines are a set of measure zero, the total probability is 1/2.\n\nWait, this is getting somewhere.\n\nLet me formalize:\n\nLet Ω be the space of all lines in the plane. We can consider a uniform distribution over Ω, but it's tricky. However, we can consider a different measure, such as selecting a random point A and a random point B, then considering the line AB. This is a common way to define a \"random line\" in a region.\n\nIn this case, since the hexagon is compact and the selection is uniform, the probability we're seeking is the probability over all such lines AB that they intersect a pair of opposite edges.\n\nDue to central symmetry, for any line AB not passing through the center, there is a corresponding line A'B' (where A' and B' are the central reflections of A and B) such that the line A'B' intersects the opposite pair of edges if and only if line AB does not. Because reflecting the line AB through the center would map the edges it doesn't intersect to the opposite edges.\n\nTherefore, the set of lines AB that intersect a pair of opposite edges is in bijection with those that don't, except for lines through the center. Since lines through the center form a set of measure zero (as they require A and B to be reflections through the center, which is a lower-dimensional subset), the total probability is 1/2.\n\nTherefore, the probability that a random line through two random points in the hexagon intersects a pair of opposite edges is 1/2.\n\nWait, but this seems to contradict my earlier example where a line with slope 0.5 in the square didn't intersect opposite sides, but in the hexagon's case, due to higher symmetry, the probability might indeed be 1/2.\n\nBut let me verify with a simple case in the hexagon.\n\nTake a horizontal line in the rotated system (y' = c). If c is between -√3/2 and √3/2, then this line is horizontal and will intersect the left and right edges (which are slanted), not the top and bottom edges. Therefore, a horizontal line does not intersect the top and bottom edges, but it does intersect the left and right edges, which are another pair of opposite edges.\n\nSimilarly, a vertical line in the rotated system (x' = d) will intersect the top and bottom edges if d is between -0.5 and 0.5, otherwise it will intersect the left and right edges.\n\nTherefore, a random horizontal line has a certain probability of intersecting left/right edges, and a random vertical line has a probability of intersecting top/bottom edges.\n\nDue to the symmetry, the probability for any specific pair should be 1/3, leading to total probability 1, which is impossible.\n\nHmm, this suggests my symmetry argument might be flawed.\n\nAlternatively, the horizontal lines in the rotated system correspond to lines parallel to the left/right edges in the original system, so they are parallel to one pair of opposite edges, and thus don't intersect those, but intersect the other pair.\n\nWait, in the rotated system, horizontal lines are parallel to the top and bottom edges. Wait no, in the rotated system, the top and bottom edges are horizontal, so a horizontal line is parallel to them, so it won't intersect them unless it's the same line.\n\nTherefore, a horizontal line inside the hexagon (between y' = -√3/2 and y' = √3/2) will be parallel to the top and bottom edges and thus won't intersect them, but will intersect the left and right edges.\n\nSimilarly, a vertical line in the rotated system will intersect the top and bottom edges if it's within the horizontal span of the hexagon (x' between -0.5 and 0.5), otherwise it will intersect the left and right edges.\n\nTherefore, the probability that a random line intersects the top and bottom edges is the probability that the line is neither horizontal nor vertical and is positioned such that it crosses from left to right through the hexagon vertically.\n\nBut this is too vague.\n\nPerhaps the correct answer is 2/3, as per some standard results, but I need to think.\n\nAlternatively, here's a different approach inspired by the fact that in a regular hexagon, the probability might be 2/3 due to the three pairs and some symmetry.\n\nImagine that for any line, it either intersects 0, 1, or 2 pairs of opposite edges. But in a convex polygon, a line can intersect at most two edges, so at most one pair of opposite edges.\n\nTherefore, the probability we want is the probability that the line intersects exactly one pair of opposite edges.\n\nAssuming uniform distribution over all lines, the probability would be the sum over the three pairs of the probability that the line intersects that pair.\n\nIf the hexagon is regular and symmetric, each pair has the same probability, so we can compute the probability for one pair and multiply by three.\n\nSuppose we compute the probability that a random line intersects the top and bottom edges in the rotated system.\n\nTo compute this, consider that a line intersects the top and bottom edges if it is not parallel to any of the other pairs and is positioned such that it cuts through the hexagon vertically.\n\nThe set of lines intersecting the top and bottom edges form a region in the space of all lines.\n\nAlternatively, parameterize lines by their angle θ and distance d from the origin.\n\nIn the rotated system, consider polar coordinates for the lines: angle θ and distance from the center.\n\nA line at angle θ and distance d from the center will intersect the top and bottom edges if the distance d is such that the line passes through the hexagon vertically.\n\nThe regular hexagon in the rotated system has a width of √3 in the y'-direction (from -√3/2 to √3/2) and a horizontal width of 1 (from x' = -0.5 to 0.5).\n\nThe maximum distance from the center a line can be and still intersect the top and bottom edges is when the line is vertical, at x'=0, which passes through the center and intersects both top and bottom edges.\n\nWait, no, a vertical line at x'=0 passes through the center and intersects top and bottom edges at their midpoints.\n\nA line with angle θ will have a certain minimal and maximal distance from the center to still intersect the top and bottom edges.\n\nThis is getting too involved without a clear path.\n\nGiven the time I've spent and the symmetry arguments, I think the most plausible answer is 2/3 or 1/2.\n\nWait, in the square, the probability that a random chord intersects a pair of opposite sides is 1/2. Because for any chord, it either crosses a pair of opposite sides or not, and by symmetry, it's equally likely.\n\nBut in the hexagon, with more symmetry, maybe it's still 1/2.\n\nAlternatively, recall that in the rotated system, the hexagon has three pairs of opposite edges. For any line not through the center, it either intersects one pair or another, but due to symmetry over three axes, the probability might be 1/3 for each, totaling 1, which is impossible, so that can’t be.\n\nAlternatively, the correct answer is 2/3.\n\nWait, let's think of the hexagon as three pairs of opposite edges. For a random line, it must intersect two edges. These two edges could be a pair of opposites or not.\n\nIn a regular hexagon, how many of the 2-edge intersections are opposite pairs?\n\nTotal possible pairs of edges: C(6,2)=15, but each edge is part of one pair, so there are 3 opposite pairs and 12 non-opposite pairs.\n\nBut the line can only intersect two edges, and those two edges are either a pair of opposites or not.\n\nThe probability we want is the probability that the two edges intersected are a pair of opposites.\n\nIn a regular hexagon, for a random line, the probability that the two edges it intersects are opposite.\n\nDue to symmetry, the probability should be the same for any pair.\n\nThere are 3 opposite pairs and 12 non-opposite pairs, but the total number of edge pairs that can be intersected by a line is not uniform.\n\nAlternatively, each pair of opposite edges has the same probability of being intersected by a random line.\n\nAssuming that for any pair of opposite edges, the probability that a random line intersects both is p, then the total probability is 3p.\n\nIf we can compute p for one pair.\n\nIn the rotated system, focusing on the top and bottom edges.\n\nThe probability that a random line intersects both top and bottom edges.\n\nTo compute this, note that the hexagon in the rotated system is symmetric top-bottom and left-right.\n\nThe set of lines intersecting the top and bottom edges are those that cross from left to right through the hexagon vertically.\n\nThe total space of lines can be parameterized by angle θ and distance from the center.\n\nFor a line to intersect both top and bottom edges, its angle must be such that it's not too steep or shallow.\n\nAlternatively, the lines that intersect both top and bottom edges are those with angles between -60° and 60° in the rotated system (i.e., not too steep to hit the side edges first).\n\nIn the rotated system, the hexagon has a vertical extent of √3 and horizontal extent of 1.\n\nThe critical angle where a line just grazes a side edge is when the line is tangent to one of the side edges.\n\nFor example, the right side edge in the rotated system goes from (1, 0) to (0.5, √3/2). The slope of this edge is (√3/2 - 0)/(0.5 - 1) = (√3/2)/(-0.5) = -√3.\n\nSimilarly, the left side edge has a slope of +√3.\n\nTherefore, the angles of these edges are ±60° from the x-axis.\n\nThus, lines with angles between -60° and 60° relative to the x-axis will intersect the top and bottom edges, while lines with steeper angles will intersect the side edges.\n\nTherefore, the lines that intersect the top and bottom edges are those with angles between -60° and 60°, and lines outside this range intersect the side edges.\n\nThe total measure of angles is 120°, out of 360°, so the probability would be 1/3.\n\nBut since we have three such pairs (top-bottom, left-right, and the other pair), the total probability would be 3*(1/3) = 1, which can't be.\n\nWait, no, each pair corresponds to a 120° range of angles.\n\nWait, in the rotated system, the top and bottom edges are horizontal. The lines that intersect them must have angles between -60° and 60° from the vertical? Or from the horizontal?\n\nWait, the slope between -√3 and √3 corresponds to angles between -60° and 60° from the x-axis.\n\nWait, the side edges have slopes of ±√3, which correspond to angles of ±60° from the x-axis.\n\nTherefore, lines with angles between -60° and 60° from the x-axis will intersect the top and bottom edges, while lines with angles outside this range will intersect the side edges.\n\nThus, the probability that a random line intersects the top and bottom edges is the probability that its angle is between -60° and 60°, which is 120° out of 360°, so 1/3.\n\nSimilarly, the probability for the left-right edges is another 1/3, and the third pair also 1/3, totaling 1.\n\nTherefore, the probability that a random line intersects any pair of opposite edges is 1, which is impossible because some lines are parallel to the edges and don't intersect them.\n\nAh, right! Lines parallel to the edges won't intersect those edges. For example, a line parallel to the top and bottom edges (horizontal lines) won't intersect them unless it's one of the edges themselves. Similarly, lines parallel to the side edges won't intersect those edges.\n\nThe set of lines parallel to any pair of edges has measure zero in the space of all lines, so they don't affect the probability.\n\nTherefore, excluding those measure-zero cases, the probability is indeed 1. But this contradicts intuition because, for example, a horizontal line inside the hexagon doesn't intersect the top or bottom edges but does intersect the left and right edges.\n\nWait, but according to the angle argument, a horizontal line (angle 0°) is within the -60° to 60° range, so it should intersect the top and bottom edges. But in reality, a horizontal line inside the hexagon is parallel to the top and bottom edges and does not intersect them—it only intersects the left and right edges.\n\nThis inconsistency indicates an error in the angle analysis.\n\nAh, here's the mistake: In the rotated system, a horizontal line (angle 0°) is parallel to the top and bottom edges, so it won't intersect them unless it's one of those edges. Therefore, such lines should not be counted in the 120° range.\n\nThe angle between -60° and 60° from the x-axis includes angles where the lines are not only intersecting top and bottom edges but also those parallel to them.\n\nWait, no, the slope of ±√3 corresponds to angles of ±60°, so slopes between -√3 and √3 correspond to angles between -60° and 60°, which include horizontal lines (slope 0) and lines with slopes up to √3.\n\nHowever, a horizontal line has slope 0 and is parallel to the top and bottom edges, so it doesn't intersect them unless it's coincident, which it isn't inside the hexagon.\n\nTherefore, the earlier analysis was incorrect because it included lines parallel to the edges, which don't intersect them.\n\nTherefore, the valid range of angles for lines intersecting the top and bottom edges are those with slopes between -∞ and -√3, or between √3 and +∞? No, that can't be.\n\nWait, let me think again.\n\nThe top and bottom edges are horizontal in the rotated system. A line will intersect both the top and bottom edges if it is not horizontal and is not parallel to any side edges.\n\nWait, no. For a line to intersect both the top and bottom edges (which are horizontal), it must cross from above y'=√3/2 to below y'=-√3/2. To do that, the line must have a non-zero slope and pass through the hexagon vertically.\n\nThe critical slopes are those of the side edges, which are ±√3.\n\nLines with slopes between -√3 and √3 will intersect the top and bottom edges if they pass through the hexagon's vertical extent.\n\nWait, let's consider a line with a very gentle slope (close to 0), like nearly horizontal. Such a line, if it's not horizontal, will enter the hexagon from the left or right side, cross through the center, and exit on the opposite side, thus intersecting both top and bottom edges?\n\nWait, in the rotated system, take a nearly horizontal line just below the top edge. It would intersect the top edge and then exit through the right or left side edge.\n\nWait, let's take an example:\n\nConsider a line with a very small positive slope, starting from the left side of the hexagon near the bottom edge, going upwards to the right. It might intersect the bottom edge first, then the left side edge?\n\nThis is getting too confusing without a diagram.\n\nPerhaps the correct probability is indeed 2/3, as per some references I vaguely recall for regular polygons.\n\nAlternatively, since in the rotated system, the hexagon can be seen as a symmetric shape where the probability of a random line intersecting a pair of opposite edges is 2/3.\n\nGiven the time I've invested and the lack of progress, I'll hazard a guess that the probability is 2/3, so the final answer is \\boxed{\\dfrac{2}{3}}.\n\nBut wait, let me think one last time.\n\nSuppose we model the hexagon as three pairs of opposite sides. For a random line, the probability it intersects any specific pair is 1/3, so total probability is 1. But this can't be since some lines are parallel.\n\nAlternatively, the three pairs each have probability p, and due to symmetry, p is the same, so 3p = probability that the line intersects any pair.\n\nThe lines that don't intersect any pair are those parallel to a pair or something else.\n\nThe set of lines parallel to any pair has measure zero, so the probability that a random line intersects at least one pair is 1. But this contradicts with the horizontal line example.\n\nAh, but in the hexagon, every line that isn't parallel to a pair of edges will intersect exactly one pair of opposite edges.\n\nLines parallel to a pair of edges do not intersect those pairs but may intersect the other pairs.\n\nWait, a line parallel to the top and bottom edges (horizontal) will intersect the left and right edges, which are another pair.\n\nSimilarly, a line parallel to the left and right edges (which have slope ±√3 in original coordinates) will intersect the top and bottom edges.\n\nTherefore, every line either intersects one pair of opposite edges or is parallel to a pair and thus intersects the other pair.\n\nTherefore, every line intersects exactly one pair of opposite edges, except those parallel to a pair, which intersect the other pair.\n\nBut lines parallel to a pair have measure zero, so almost every line intersects exactly one pair of opposite edges.\n\nTherefore, the probability is 1.\n\nBut this contradicts the horizontal line example in the square where a horizontal line doesn't intersect opposite sides but intersects adjacent ones.\n\nWait, in the hexagon, a horizontal line (parallel to top/bottom) intersects the left and right edges, which are a pair of opposite edges.\n\nAh! In the hexagon, the left and right edges are also a pair of opposite edges.\n\nTherefore, every line either intersects one pair of opposite edges or is parallel to one pair and thus intersects the other pair.\n\nSince parallel lines form a measure zero set, the probability that a random line intersects a pair of opposite edges is 1.\n\nBut this can't be, since the problem states \"a pair of opposite edges\", and every line intersects exactly one pair (either directly or via being parallel to another pair).\n\nTherefore, the probability is 1.\n\nBut this contradicts the square example where a horizontal line doesn't intersect opposite sides but intersects adjacent ones, but in the hexagon, adjacent edges are not opposite.\n\nWait in the hexagon, left and right edges are opposite edges?\n\nIn the rotated system, the left and right edges are not opposite; the opposite edges are top/bottom, left/right? Wait no, in the rotated system, the opposite edges are top/bottom and the other two pairs are the left/right and the other two.\n\nWait, in the rotated system, after rotating so that top/bottom are horizontal, the other pairs are the left and right edges which are now slanted.\n\nWait, in the rotated coordinate system, the opposite edges