 intersecting another pair of adjacent edges. This symmetry implies that the measure of lines intersecting opposite edges is equal to the measure of lines intersecting adjacent edges.\n   - Therefore, the probability of a random chord intersecting any pair of opposite edges is equal to the probability of it intersecting any pair of adjacent edges. Since these are the only two possibilities, the total probability for intersecting any pair of opposite edges is 1/2.\n\n4. **Conclusion**:\n   - The probability that a line through two randomly selected points inside a regular hexagon intersects a pair of opposite edges is \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{2}}\\). This result holds due to the symmetry and convexity of the hexagon, ensuring that the measure of lines intersecting opposite edges is equal to those intersecting adjacent edges.","num_tokens":29844,"correct":false,"formatted":true},{"predicted_answer":"[no boxed]","full_generation":"Okay, so I have this problem here: \"Two points are selected independently and uniformly at random inside a regular hexagon. Compute the probability that a line passing through both of the points intersects a pair of opposite edges of the hexagon.\" Hmm, interesting. Alright, let me try to break this down and figure out how to approach it.\n\nFirst, let me make sure I understand the problem correctly. We have a regular hexagon, which is a six-sided polygon with all sides equal in length and all internal angles equal to 120 degrees. Points are selected independently and uniformly at random inside this hexagon. So, that means every point in the hexagon is equally likely to be chosen, right? And we pick two such points. Then, we consider the line that passes through both of these two points. The question is asking for the probability that this line intersects a pair of opposite edges of the hexagon.\n\nNow, by \"opposite edges\" of the hexagon, I assume they mean edges that are directly opposite each other in the hexagon. In a regular hexagon, each