is not horizontal will intersect each of them once. Therefore, such a line would intersect two edges, which are opposite (and hence parallel), so that would satisfy the condition. Whereas a line that is parallel to those two opposite edges would either not intersect them at all or be coincident with them, but since the hexagon is a finite figure, a line parallel to an edge but offset would not intersect that edge, unless it's passing through the hexagon in some way?\n\nWait, actually, in a regular hexagon, the opposite edges are separated by some distance. For example, in a regular hexagon centered at the origin with edges horizontal and vertical, the horizontal opposite edges would be the top and bottom edges, and the vertical opposite edges would be the left and right edges. Wait, actually, in a regular hexagon, the opposite edges are separated by three edges, and they are parallel, but depending on the orientation, they can be horizontal, vertical, or at 60-degree angles.\n\nBut regardless of the orientation, opposite edges are parallel. Therefore, a line that is not parallel to a pair of opposite edges will intersect both of them if it passes through the hexagon in such a way. Whereas a line parallel to a pair of opposite edges will either not intersect them (if it's outside the hexagon) or intersect them only if it coincides with them? Wait, but the edges are finite, so a line parallel to an edge but offset will not intersect that edge. For instance, in the horizontal direction, if you have a horizontal edge on top and a horizontal edge on the bottom, then a horizontal line above the top edge would not intersect the top edge, and a horizontal line below the bottom edge would not intersect the bottom edge. A horizontal line in between would intersect both top and bottom edges? Wait, no, actually, in a regular hexagon, the top and bottom edges are not directly opposite in the vertical direction; actually, in a regular hexagon, opposite edges are separated by three edges, so if one edge is horizontal at the top, the opposite edge would be the one three edges over, which is actually the bottom edge, but in a regular hexagon, the vertical distance between the top and bottom edges is the same as the distance between the left and right edges? Wait, no, actually, in a regular hexagon, all opposite edges are separated by the same distance, but depending on orientation.\n\nWait, let me perhaps consider coordinates to make this more precise. Maybe I should assign coordinates to the regular hexagon so that I can work with equations and such.\n\nLet me consider a regular hexagon centered at the origin with one vertex at (1, 0). Then the six vertices can be given in the complex plane as e^(i * 60° * k) for k = 0, 1, ..., 5. Converting to coordinates, the vertices are:\n\nVertex 0: (1, 0)\n\nVertex 1: (cos(60°), sin(60°)) = (0.5, √3/2)\n\nVertex 2: (-0.5, √3/2)\n\nVertex 3: (-1, 0)\n\nVertex 4: (-0.5, -√3/2)\n\nVertex 5: (0.5, -√3/2)\n\nSo, connecting these vertices in order gives the regular hexagon.\n\nNow, the edges are between these vertices. Let me label the edges as Edge 0 between Vertex 0 and Vertex 1,\n\nEdge 1 between Vertex 1 and Vertex 2,\n\nEdge 2 between Vertex 2 and Vertex 3,\n\nEdge 3 between Vertex 3 and Vertex 4,\n\nEdge 4 between Vertex 4 and Vertex 5,\n\nEdge 5 between Vertex 5 and Vertex 0.\n\nTherefore, as per the earlier labeling, Edge 0 is opposite Edge 3, since they are three apart (0 and 3), Edge 1 opposite Edge 4, Edge 2 opposite Edge 5.\n\nSo, Edge 0 is from (1, 0) to (0.5, √3/2). Edge 3 is from (-1, 0) to (-0.5, -√3/2). Are these edges parallel?\n\nLet me check the slopes.\n\nEdge 0: from (1, 0) to (0.5, √3/2). The slope is (√3/2 - 0)/(0.5 - 1) = (√3/2)/(-0.5) = -√3.\n\nEdge 3: from (-1, 0) to (-0.5, -√3/2). The slope is (-√3/2 - 0)/(-0.5 - (-1)) = (-√3/2)/(0.5) = -√3.\n\nSo yes, Edge 0 and Edge 3 both have slope -√3, so they are parallel. Similarly, Edge 1: from (0.5, √3/2) to (-0.5, √3/2). That's a horizontal line? Wait, the y-coordinate is √3/2 for both points, so yes, Edge 1 is horizontal. The slope is (√3/2 - √3/2)/(-0.5 - 0.5) = 0 / (-1) = 0, so slope 0. Then Edge 4 is from (-0.5, -√3/2) to (0.5, -√3/2), which is also horizontal, with slope 0. So Edge 1 and Edge 4 are both horizontal, hence parallel.\n\nEdge 2: from (-0.5, √3/2) to (-1, 0). Let's compute the slope: (0 - √3/2)/(-1 - (-0.5)) = (-√3/2)/(-0.5) = √3.\n\nEdge 5: from (0.5, -√3/2) to (1, 0). The slope is (0 - (-√3/2))/(1 - 0.5) = (√3/2)/0.5 = √3. So Edge 2 and Edge 5 both have slope √3, so they are parallel.\n\nTherefore, the three pairs of opposite edges are:\n\n- Edge 0 (slope -√3) and Edge 3 (slope -√3)\n\n- Edge 1 (slope 0) and Edge 4 (slope 0)\n\n- Edge 2 (slope √3) and Edge 5 (slope √3)\n\nSo, each pair of opposite edges are parallel, with slopes -√3, 0, and √3 respectively.\n\nTherefore, a line that is not parallel to any of these three directions will intersect each of the three pairs of opposite edges it crosses? Wait, no. Wait, a single line can only be parallel to at most one of these three directions, right? Because the three directions are different: -√3, 0, and √3. So, a line has a single slope, so it can be parallel to at most one of the three pairs of opposite edges.\n\nTherefore, for a given line, if it is not parallel to any of the three directions of the opposite edges, then it will intersect all three pairs of opposite edges? Wait, no, that can't be. Wait, actually, in the regular hexagon, a single straight line can only intersect two edges, as per convex polygon properties.\n\nWait, hold on, confusion here. Let me think again.\n\nSuppose I have a regular hexagon. Let me pick a line that is not parallel to any of the three directions of the opposite edges. For example, a line with a slope of 1, which is not one of -√3, 0, or √3. Such a line will pass through the hexagon, entering through one edge and exiting through another. Since the hexagon is convex, the line can only enter and exit once each, so it will intersect exactly two edges. These two edges may or may not be a pair of opposite edges.\n\nAlternatively, if the line is parallel to one of the three directions of the opposite edges, then it might be parallel to one pair, so it might not intersect that pair, but could intersect the other two pairs? Wait, but if it's parallel to a pair of opposite edges, then depending on where it is, it might not intersect any edges, or intersect edges from the other pairs?\n\nWait, let me consider an example. Let's take the horizontal direction, which is the slope 0. Suppose we have a horizontal line. If it's above the top edge (Edge 1), then it doesn't intersect any edges. If it's between the top edge (Edge 1) and the bottom edge (Edge 4), then it will intersect both Edge 1 and Edge 4, which are opposite edges (since Edge 1 and Edge 4 are a pair of opposite edges). Similarly, a horizontal line below the bottom edge would not intersect any edges. A horizontal line exactly at the height of Edge 1 would be coincident with Edge 1, so it would intersect Edge 1 along its entire length, but since we are considering lines passing through two random points inside the hexagon, such a line would have to pass through the interior, so maybe the line coinciding with an edge would only be counted if the two points are on that edge? But since the points are selected uniformly at random inside the hexagon, the probability that both points lie on the same edge is zero, since it's a one-dimensional subset in a two-dimensional area. So, we can ignore such cases as having measure zero.\n\nSimilarly, a line parallel to Edge 0 (slope -√3) but offset from it. If it's above Edge 0, then it might not intersect any edges, or if it's between Edge 0 and its opposite Edge 3, then it would intersect both Edge 0 and Edge 3. Wait, but Edge 0 and Edge 3 are opposite and parallel, so a line between them would intersect both.\n\nWait, so in general, if a line is not parallel to any of the three directions of the opposite edges, then it will cross two edges which are not a pair of opposite edges? Or could it cross a pair of opposite edges?\n\nWait, let's take a line with a random slope. Let me think of the regular hexagon. Let me imagine a line cutting through the hexagon from, say, the lower left to the upper right. Depending on the angle, it might cross two non-opposite edges or cross a pair of opposite edges?\n\nWait, for instance, let me consider a line that goes from the middle of the left side to the middle of the right side. Wait, in the regular hexagon, the left and right sides are actually edges? Wait, in my coordinate system above, the leftmost point is Vertex 3 at (-1, 0), and the rightmost point is Vertex 0 at (1, 0). So the left side is Edge 2 and Edge 3? Wait, no, Edge 2 is from Vertex 2 to Vertex 3, which is from (-0.5, √3/2) to (-1, 0), and Edge 3 is from Vertex 3 to Vertex 4, which is from (-1, 0) to (-0.5, -√3/2). So the left side is sort of a \"corner\" at Vertex 3, but the leftmost point is Vertex 3 at (-1, 0). Similarly, the rightmost point is Vertex 0 at (1, 0). So, the vertical line x = 0 would pass through the center, and would intersect the hexagon at two points: going up to (0, √3/2) and down to (0, -√3/2), right? Wait, actually, plugging x = 0 into the hexagon's boundaries.\n\nWait, actually, in the regular hexagon with vertices at (1,0), (0.5, √3/2), etc., the vertical line x = 0 would intersect the hexagon where?\n\nLooking at the edges:\n\nEdge 0: from (1,0) to (0.5, √3/2). Let's see if x=0 intersects Edge 0. Edge 0 is between x=1 and x=0.5, so x goes from 1 down to 0.5, so x=0 is to the left of that, so no intersection there.\n\nEdge 1: from (0.5, √3/2) to (-0.5, √3/2). This is a horizontal edge at y = √3/2 from x = -0.5 to x = 0.5. So x=0 is on this edge, at (0, √3/2). So that's an intersection point.\n\nEdge 2: from (-0.5, √3/2) to (-1, 0). This edge goes from x = -0.5 to x = -1, so x=0 is not in that interval.\n\nEdge 3: from (-1, 0) to (-0.5, -√3/2). Similarly, x from -1 to -0.5, so x=0 not there.\n\nEdge 4: from (-0.5, -√3/2) to (0.5, -√3/2). Horizontal edge at y = -√3/2, from x=-0.5 to x=0.5. So x=0 intersects here at (0, -√3/2).\n\nEdge 5: from (0.5, -√3/2) to (1, 0). x from 0.5 to 1, so x=0 is not there.\n\nTherefore, the vertical line x=0 intersects the hexagon at two points: (0, √3/2) and (0, -√3/2). These two points are on Edge 1 and Edge 4, respectively. Edge 1 and Edge 4 are a pair of opposite edges (since Edge 1 is opposite Edge 4). Therefore, the vertical line x=0 intersects a pair of opposite edges (Edge 1 and Edge 4). So in this case, the line intersects a pair of opposite edges.\n\nAlternatively, take a different line, say, a line that goes from the midpoint of Edge 0 to the midpoint of Edge 2. Wait, let me compute coordinates.\n\nMidpoint of Edge 0: Edge 0 is from (1, 0) to (0.5, √3/2). Midpoint is ((1 + 0.5)/2, (0 + √3/2)/2) = (0.75, √3/4).\n\nMidpoint of Edge 2: Edge 2 is from (-0.5, √3/2) to (-1, 0). Midpoint is ((-0.5 + (-1))/2, (√3/2 + 0)/2) = (-0.75, √3/4).\n\nSo the line connecting (0.75, √3/4) and (-0.75, √3/4) is a horizontal line at y = √3/4, going from x = -0.75 to x = 0.75. Wait, actually, that's just a horizontal line segment within the hexagon? Wait, no, the line itself, extended, would be the horizontal line y = √3/4. Let's see where this line intersects the hexagon's edges.\n\nThe horizontal line y = √3/4. Let's see which edges it intersects.\n\nEdge 0: from (1, 0) to (0.5, √3/2). The y-coordinate on Edge 0 goes from 0 to √3/2. So y = √3/4 is halfway up that edge. Let's find the x-coordinate at y = √3/4 on Edge 0.\n\nParametrize Edge 0: from (1, 0) to (0.5, √3/2). Let parameter t go from 0 to 1.\n\nx(t) = 1 - 0.5 t\n\ny(t) = 0 + (√3/2) t\n\nSet y(t) = √3/4, so (√3/2) t = √3/4 ⇒ t = 1/2\n\nThen x(t) = 1 - 0.5*(1/2) = 1 - 1/4 = 3/4. So the intersection point is (0.75, √3/4), which is the midpoint we already had.\n\nSimilarly, Edge 1 is from (0.5, √3/2) to (-0.5, √3/2), which is the top horizontal edge at y = √3/2. Since √3/4 is below that, so the line y = √3/4 does not intersect Edge 1.\n\nEdge 2: from (-0.5, √3/2) to (-1, 0). Let's see if y = √3/4 intersects Edge 2.\n\nParametrize Edge 2: from (-0.5, √3/2) to (-1, 0). Let parameter s go from 0 to 1.\n\nx(s) = -0.5 - 0.5 s\n\ny(s) = √3/2 - (√3/2) s\n\nSet y(s) = √3/4:\n\n√3/2 - (√3/2) s = √3/4 ⇒ subtract √3/2: - (√3/2) s = -√3/4 ⇒ s = ( -√3/4 ) / ( -√3/2 ) = (1/4)/(1/2) = 1/2\n\nThen x(s) = -0.5 - 0.5*(1/2) = -0.5 - 0.25 = -0.75, so the intersection point is (-0.75, √3/4), the other midpoint.\n\nEdge 3: from (-1, 0) to (-0.5, -√3/2). The y-coordinate on Edge 3 goes from 0 down to -√3/2. Since √3/4 is positive, this edge is below y=0, so no intersection.\n\nEdge 4: from (-0.5, -√3/2) to (0.5, -√3/2). This is the bottom horizontal edge at y = -√3/2, which is below y = √3/4, so no intersection.\n\nEdge 5: from (0.5, -√3/2) to (1, 0). Similarly, this edge is below y=0 except at the endpoint (1,0). Since √3/4 is above 0, no intersection here.\n\nTherefore, the line y = √3/4 intersects the hexagon at exactly two points: (0.75, √3/4) and (-0.75, √3/4), which are on Edge 0 and Edge 2, respectively. Edge 0 and Edge 2 are not opposite edges. Edge 0 is opposite Edge 3, and Edge 2 is opposite Edge 5. Therefore, this line intersects two edges that are not a pair of opposite edges.\n\nTherefore, in this case, the line does not intersect a pair of opposite edges. So, depending on the slope of the line, it can intersect a pair of opposite edges or not.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to: given two random points in the hexagon, what is the probability that the line through them intersects a pair of opposite edges?\n\nTo compute this probability, perhaps it's helpful to consider the dual problem: instead of looking at all possible lines through two random points, maybe we can characterize the set of lines that do intersect a pair of opposite edges, and then compute the probability that a random line (in some sense) is in that set.\n\nBut how do we define a \"random line\" through two random points in the hexagon? The distribution of lines through two random points in a convex set is a bit tricky because lines can be parameterized in multiple ways, and the probability measure isn't straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use symmetry and consider integrating over all possible pairs of points, and for each pair, determining whether the line through them intersects a pair of opposite edges, then dividing by the total number of pairs (or the area squared, since it's a continuous uniform distribution).\n\nGiven that the hexagon is regular and the problem is symmetric, perhaps we can exploit the symmetry to simplify the calculation.\n\nLet me recall that in problems involving random lines in a convex polygon, sometimes it's useful to use a coordinate system aligned with the polygon's symmetry axes, and then perform integration in that coordinate system.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use probabilistic symmetry by considering the hexagon's properties and transforming the problem into something more manageable.\n\nFirst, note that the regular hexagon can be divided into six equilateral triangles, each with a vertex at the center. So, if we consider the hexagon centered at the origin, each of the six \"slices\" between two adjacent vertices can be considered an equilateral triangle with the center.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use barycentric coordinates or something else, but I might need to think more carefully.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can parameterize the hexagon and perform a double integral over all possible points, then for each pair of points, determine whether the line between them intersects a pair of opposite edges, then integrate that indicator function over all pairs and divide by the area squared.\n\nHowever, that seems computationally intensive, but maybe manageable with some clever coordinate transformations.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use the concept of \"visibility\" or something related to whether the line passes through certain regions.\n\nWait, here's an idea: For a line to intersect a pair of opposite edges, it must cross from one side of the hexagon to the opposite side. Since opposite edges are on opposite sides, so crossing from one to the opposite would mean crossing through the hexagon in such a way that it goes from one side to the side directly opposite.\n\nAlternatively, since the hexagon is centrally symmetric, any line that passes through the center will intersect two opposite edges? Wait, is that true?\n\nWait, let's think of the vertical line through the center, which we saw earlier does intersect a pair of opposite edges (Edge 1 and Edge 4). Similarly, a horizontal line through the center would intersect Edge 0 and Edge 3? Wait, actually, the horizontal line through the center (y=0) in our coordinate system goes through Vertex 0 (1,0) and Vertex 3 (-1,0). Wait, actually, the horizontal line y=0 is the line connecting Vertex 0 and Vertex 3, which are opposite vertices. But in terms of edges, the horizontal line y=0 passes through Edge 0 (from Vertex 0 to Vertex 1) at Vertex 0 and Edge 3 (from Vertex 3 to Vertex 4) at Vertex 3. So, it passes through two vertices which are endpoints of opposite edges. But as a line, it coincides with the line connecting those two vertices, so it's passing through two vertices which are endpoints of opposite edges. But in terms of edges, does it intersect the edges themselves? Well, it passes through the vertices, which are the endpoints of the edges, so technically, it intersects those edges at their endpoints. But as before, the probability that a random line passes through a vertex is zero, since it would require the two points to be aligned with a vertex, which has measure zero.\n\nBut in any case, lines through the center: the vertical line through the center intersects two opposite edges (Edge 1 and Edge 4). The horizontal line through the center intersects the edges at the vertices (so maybe not considered as intersecting the edges in their interiors?), but in any case, lines through the center do intersect a pair of opposite edges.\n\nAlternatively, lines not through the center might still intersect a pair of opposite edges depending on their slope.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can characterize all lines that intersect a pair of opposite edges as those that are not parallel to any of the three directions of the opposite edges and are \"between\" the opposite edges?\n\nWait, actually, in the regular hexagon, for each pair of opposite edges, there is a region between them where lines crossing that region will intersect both of the opposite edges. Wait, but since the edges are on opposite sides, the \"region between\" them would actually be the entire hexagon except for the regions near the other edges? Hmm, perhaps not straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps for a given pair of opposite edges, the set of lines that intersect both of them can be considered as the set of lines that pass between those two opposite edges. Since the opposite edges are parallel, the region between them is a strip between the two parallel lines (the two opposite edges). But in the hexagon, the two opposite edges are separated by some distance, and the strip between them is a portion of the hexagon. Wait, actually, in the hexagon, between two opposite edges, the area between them is actually a band that goes around the hexagon? Wait, no, in a regular hexagon, each pair of opposite edges are separated by three edges, so the area between them would actually be the entire hexagon except for the regions adjacent to the other edges?\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to think in terms of the three axes of symmetry of the hexagon. Since the hexagon has six-fold symmetry, but also three lines of symmetry: one through opposite vertices, one through the midpoints of opposite edges, etc.\n\nWait, actually, the regular hexagon has six axes of symmetry: three that pass through opposite vertices, and three that pass through the midpoints of opposite edges. Each of these axes is a line of symmetry.\n\nTherefore, perhaps if we consider the three directions of the opposite edges (which are aligned with the axes through the midpoints of the edges), then a line that is not parallel to any of these three axes will cross two opposite edges if it is between the two opposite edges in some sense?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the key idea is that a line intersects a pair of opposite edges if and only if it is not parallel to any of the three pairs of opposite edges and lies within the \"strip\" between those opposite edges?\n\nWait, let me think again with the coordinate system.\n\nTake the pair of opposite edges with slope -√3 (Edges 0 and 3). These edges are on the \"left\" and \"right\" sides of the hexagon? Wait, in our coordinate system, Edge 0 is on the right side (from (1,0) to (0.5, √3/2)), and Edge 3 is on the left side (from (-1,0) to (-0.5, -√3/2)). So they are not directly left and right, but rather at 60 degrees.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the three directions of the opposite edges correspond to the three axes of symmetry of the hexagon. For instance, the horizontal axis (slope 0) is one axis, the axis with slope √3, and the axis with slope -√3. Each of these axes passes through the center and is aligned with a pair of opposite edges.\n\nTherefore, for a line that is not parallel to any of these three axes, it will cross two opposite edges if and only if it is \"between\" the two opposite edges along the direction perpendicular to those edges.\n\nWait, let me formalize this a bit.\n\nSuppose we pick a particular pair of opposite edges, say, the horizontal edges (Edges 1 and 4). These are the top and bottom edges of the hexagon in our coordinate system. A line that is not horizontal will intersect both the top and bottom edges if and only if it is not too steep or too shallow? Wait, actually, any non-horizontal line will intersect both the top and bottom edges unless it is parallel to them (i.e., horizontal) or unless it's so steep that it only intersects one? Wait, no.\n\nWait, in our coordinate system, the top edge is at y = √3/2 and the bottom edge is at y = -√3/2. A non-horizontal line will have some slope. If it's not horizontal, then as it goes from left to right, it will go from the bottom to the top or vice versa. Since the top and bottom edges are the highest and lowest points of the hexagon, a non-horizontal line will intersect both the top and bottom edges unless it is vertical? Wait, no, a vertical line does intersect both the top and bottom edges as we saw earlier (at x=0). Wait, actually, a vertical line does intersect both the top and bottom edges. Wait, but a vertical line is perpendicular to the horizontal edges.\n\nWait, actually, let me think of a line with a very steep slope, say, almost vertical. It would still intersect both the top and bottom edges, but very close to the sides. For example, a line with a slope of 1000 would go from the bottom left to the top right, but since the hexagon is bounded, it would still enter through one side and exit through the opposite side? Wait, actually, no. Wait, in a regular hexagon, the sides are at 60-degree angles. Let me try to think of a line that might not intersect both top and bottom edges.\n\nWait, suppose I have a line that is almost parallel to the top edge but slightly tilted. Since the top edge is horizontal, a line that's almost horizontal but slightly angled would still intersect both the top and bottom edges? Wait, no, actually, if it's slightly angled upwards to the right, then it might only intersect the top edge and one of the side edges?\n\nWait, let me take an example. Let me consider a line that starts at the bottom left corner (-1, 0) and goes upwards to the right with a very slight positive slope. Would that line intersect the top edge?\n\nStarting at (-1, 0), going up and to the right with a small positive slope. Since the top edge is at y = √3/2, which is about 0.866. So, if the slope is small enough, the line might reach the top edge before exiting the hexagon on the right side.\n\nWait, but in the regular hexagon, the right side is Edge 0, which goes from (1, 0) up to (0.5, √3/2). So, if the line starts at (-1,0) and goes up to the right, it would first intersect Edge 3 (the left edge?) Wait, no, starting at (-1,0), which is Vertex 3, so moving up and to the right along a line with a small positive slope, it would leave the hexagon immediately because the left side is already at that vertex. Wait, actually, the point (-1,0) is a vertex, so a line starting there would leave the hexagon along Edge 3 or Edge 2?\n\nWait, Edge 3 is from (-1,0) to (-0.5, -√3/2), which is downward, and Edge 2 is from (-0.5, √3/2) to (-1, 0), which is upward. So, from (-1,0), the edges going out are Edge 2 (upward to the left) and Edge 3 (downward to the right). Wait, actually, in terms of directions, from Vertex 3 (-1,0), the adjacent edges are Edge 2 (going to Vertex 2) which is up and left, and Edge 3 (going to Vertex 4) which is down and right.\n\nTherefore, a line starting at Vertex 3 (-1, 0) going up and to the right would go outside the hexagon immediately, because the hexagon's boundary there is Edge 2 going up and left. So, actually, such a line would leave the hexagon at the starting point. Hmm, perhaps this is getting too bogged down in specific examples.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider a line that is not passing through any vertices and is not parallel to any of the three axes of symmetry. Such a line will cross two edges of the hexagon. The question is, are those two edges a pair of opposite edges?\n\nSince the hexagon is centrally symmetric, if a line crosses two edges that are not opposite, then the line's mirror image through the center would cross the opposite pair of edges. Therefore, perhaps the probability that a random line crosses a pair of opposite edges is equal to the probability that it crosses any specific pair, multiplied by three (for the three pairs), but adjusted for overlap?\n\nWait, but lines can cross at most one pair of opposite edges, right? Because if a line crosses two different pairs of opposite edges, that would require it to intersect four edges, which is impossible in a convex polygon.\n\nTherefore, each line can intersect at most one pair of opposite edges, or none. Therefore, the events of intersecting different pairs of opposite edges are mutually exclusive. Therefore, the probability that a random line intersects any pair of opposite edges is equal to the sum over the three pairs of the probability that the line intersects that particular pair.\n\nTherefore, if I can compute the probability that a random line intersects a specific pair of opposite edges, say, the horizontal pair (Edges 1 and 4), then multiply that by 3 (for the three pairs), I would get the desired probability.\n\nHowever, we need to be careful here because the three pairs are not symmetric in terms of the distribution of lines. Wait, actually, due to the hexagon's symmetry, all three pairs should be symmetric, so the probability should be the same for each pair. Therefore, yes, we can compute the probability for one pair and multiply by three.\n\nTherefore, let's focus on one pair of opposite edges, say, the horizontal edges (Edges 1 and 4), which are the top and bottom edges of the hexagon in our coordinate system. Let me compute the probability that a random line through two random points in the hexagon intersects both of these edges.\n\nWait, but actually, the line might not necessarily pass through both edges unless it is \"between\" them in some sense. Wait, but in the hexagon, any non-horizontal line will pass through the hexagon from one side to another. Since the hexagon is convex and the top and bottom edges are the highest and lowest points, a non-horizontal line must go from below the bottom edge to above the top edge, but since it's inside the hexagon, it must enter through one edge and exit through another. Wait, but the top and bottom edges are the extreme top and bottom, so a line that goes from the bottom part to the top part must cross the top and bottom edges?\n\nWait, actually, no. Wait, the top edge is the highest horizontal line in the hexagon (y = √3/2), and the bottom edge is the lowest (y = -√3/2). So, if a line has a positive slope, it might go from the bottom left to the top right, crossing the bottom edge on the left side and the top edge on the right side. Similarly, a line with negative slope would cross the bottom edge on the right and the top edge on the left.\n\nHowever, a line that is nearly horizontal might only graze the top or bottom edge? But in reality, since the top and bottom edges are horizontal, a nearly horizontal line would intersect both the top and bottom edges only if it is between them vertically. Wait, actually, any non-horizontal line that goes from the lower part of the hexagon to the upper part must cross both the top and bottom edges? Wait, no. Wait, let me think of a horizontal line: it would be parallel and either above the top edge, between the top and bottom edges, or below the bottom edge. If it's between them, it intersects both top and bottom edges. If it's exactly at the top or bottom, it's along the edge. If it's outside, it doesn't intersect. But a non-horizontal line, say, with a very slight positive slope, starting from the lower left part of the hexagon, would it necessarily cross both the top and bottom edges?\n\nWait, let's take an example. Suppose we have a line that starts at the point (-1, 0) [a vertex], goes up and to the right with a very small positive slope. But as we saw earlier, starting at (-1, 0), which is a vertex, the line would immediately leave the hexagon along Edge 2 or Edge 3. But if we take a point just inside the hexagon near (-1, 0), say (-0.99, 0.01), and draw a line with a very slight positive slope, would that line intersect both the top and bottom edges?\n\nWait, let's parametrize such a line. Let me pick a point near the bottom left, say P1 = (-0.99, -0.01), and another point P2 = (0.99, 0.01), so that the line between them has a very slight positive slope. Then this line would go from near the bottom left to near the top right, and would likely intersect both the top edge (Edge 1) and the bottom edge (Edge 4). Wait, but actually, the bottom edge is at y = -√3/2 ≈ -0.866, so the point P1 is at y = -0.01, which is above the bottom edge. Similarly, the top edge is at y ≈ 0.866, so P2 is at y = 0.01, which is below the top edge. Therefore, this line would enter the hexagon from the left side (near the bottom) and exit through the right side (near the top), crossing the top and bottom edges? Wait, actually, in this case, the line is going from near the bottom left to near the top right, so it would cross the bottom edge on the left and the top edge on the right? Wait, but the bottom edge is on the left side? Wait no, in our coordinate system, the bottom edge is Edge 4, which is the lower horizontal edge from (-0.5, -√3/2) to (0.5, -√3/2). So the bottom edge is centered on the x-axis, spanning from x = -0.5 to x = 0.5 at the bottom. Similarly, the top edge is Edge 1, from (-0.5, √3/2) to (0.5, √3/2).\n\nTherefore, the bottom edge is actually in the middle horizontally, not on the left. So, the leftmost point of the hexagon is Vertex 3 at (-1, 0), but the bottom edge is centered at the origin, stretching from x = -0.5 to x = 0.5 at the bottom. Therefore, a line going from the bottom left corner (-1,0) upwards and to the right would first exit the hexagon through the bottom edge? Wait, no, because the bottom edge is from (-0.5, -√3/2) to (0.5, -√3/2), so to the left of x = -0.5, the bottom of the hexagon is actually the edge Edge 3 and Edge 4? Wait, no, actually, the left side of the hexagon below the x-axis is Edge 3 and Edge 4?\n\nWait, let me get back to the coordinate system.\n\nVertices:\n\n0: (1, 0)\n\n1: (0.5, √3/2) ≈ (0.5, 0.866)\n\n2: (-0.5, √3/2) ≈ (-0.5, 0.866)\n\n3: (-1, 0)\n\n4: (-0.5, -√3/2) ≈ (-0.5, -0.866)\n\n5: (0.5, -√3/2) ≈ (0.5, -0.866)\n\nEdges:\n\nEdge 0: 0 to 1\n\nEdge 1: 1 to 2\n\nEdge 2: 2 to 3\n\nEdge 3: 3 to 4\n\nEdge 4: 4 to 5\n\nEdge 5: 5 to 0\n\nTherefore, the bottom edge is Edge 4, which is from Vertex 4 (-0.5, -√3/2) to Vertex 5 (0.5, -√3/2). So it's the lower horizontal edge, centered on the origin, from x=-0.5 to x=0.5 at y = -√3/2.\n\nSimilarly, the top edge is Edge 1, from Vertex 1 (0.5, √3/2) to Vertex 2 (-0.5, √3/2), the upper horizontal edge, also centered on the origin.\n\nThe leftmost point is Vertex 3 at (-1, 0), and the rightmost is Vertex 0 at (1, 0).\n\nTherefore, the left side of the hexagon below the x-axis is actually Edge 3 (from Vertex 3 (-1,0) to Vertex 4 (-0.5, -√3/2)), which is a downward-sloping edge from (-1,0) to (-0.5, -√3/2). Similarly, the right side below the x-axis is Edge 5 (from Vertex 5 (0.5, -√3/2) to Vertex 0 (1,0)), which is an upward-sloping edge.\n\nTherefore, a line starting at the bottom left, say near Vertex 3 (-1,0), going upwards and to the right with a slight positive slope would first exit the hexagon through Edge 3 (the left edge below the x-axis) or through Edge 5 (the right edge below the x-axis)? Wait, no, if it starts inside the hexagon near (-1,0), moving up and to the right, it would first hit the edge Edge 3 or Edge 5?\n\nActually, from a point just inside the hexagon near (-1, 0), moving up and to the right, the immediate boundary is Edge 3 going downward to the right, but since we're moving upward, we'd actually be going away from Edge 3. Wait, maybe I need to draw this mentally better.\n\nAlternatively, let's take a concrete example. Let me pick a point P1 = (-0.9, -0.1), which is inside the hexagon. Let me see if this point is inside.\n\nTo check if (-0.9, -0.1) is inside the hexagon, we can see which edges it's near. The left side below the x-axis is Edge 3 from (-1,0) to (-0.5, -√3/2 ≈ -0.866). The point (-0.9, -0.1) has x = -0.9, which is between -1 and -0.5, and y = -0.1, which is above y = -0.866. So, yes, it's inside the hexagon.\n\nNow, let's draw a line from P1 = (-0.9, -0.1) to another point P2 = (0.9, 0.1), which is symmetric on the right side. This line has a slope of (0.1 - (-0.1))/(0.9 - (-0.9)) = 0.2 / 1.8 ≈ 0.111, a gentle positive slope.\n\nWe can parametrize this line as:\n\nx(t) = -0.9 + t*(1.8), where t from 0 to 1,\n\ny(t) = -0.1 + t*(0.2).\n\nWe can find where this line intersects the hexagon's edges.\n\nFirst, let's see if it intersects the bottom edge (Edge 4: y = -√3/2 ≈ -0.866 from x=-0.5 to 0.5). But our line starts at y = -0.1 which is above y = -0.866, so it doesn't go below that. Similarly, the top edge is at y ≈ 0.866, and our line ends at y=0.1, so it doesn't reach the top edge. Wait a second! So this line goes from (-0.9, -0.1) to (0.9, 0.1), but since both points are near the center, the line might not even reach the top or bottom edges?\n\nWait, hold on, the line from (-0.9, -0.1) to (0.9, 0.1) is a straight line passing through the origin? Let me check: when t = 0.5, x = -0.9 + 0.9 = 0, y = -0.1 + 0.1 = 0. Yes, it passes through the origin. So this line goes from the bottom left to the top right, passing through the center. Therefore, it should intersect the bottom edge and the top edge?\n\nWait, but the bottom edge is at y ≈ -0.866, but our line at x = -0.9 is at y = -0.1, which is above the bottom edge. To reach the bottom edge, the line would have to go further left, but it's starting at x = -0.9. Wait, actually, the bottom edge is only from x = -0.5 to x = 0.5 at y ≈ -0.866. So, the line starting at (-0.9, -0.1) is to the left of the bottom edge's x-range. Therefore, the line does not intersect the bottom edge because the bottom edge doesn't extend to x = -0.9. Instead, the line would exit the hexagon through the left side?\n\nWait, the left side below the x-axis is Edge 3, which is from (-1, 0) to (-0.5, -√3/2). Let me see where the line intersects Edge 3.\n\nEdge 3 can be parametrized as:\n\nFrom (-1, 0) to (-0.5, -√3/2 ≈ -0.866). Let parameter s go from 0 to 1.\n\nx(s) = -1 + 0.5 s\n\ny(s) = 0 - (√3/2) s\n\nSo, the line's parametrization is:\n\nx(t) = -0.9 + 1.8 t\n\ny(t) = -0.1 + 0.2 t\n\nWe need to find t and s such that:\n\n-0.9 + 1.8 t = -1 + 0.5 s\n\nand\n\n-0.1 + 0.2 t = - (√3/2) s\n\nThis is a system of two equations with two variables t and s.\n\nLet me write it as:\n\n1.8 t - 0.5 s = -0.1\n\n0.2 t + (√3/2) s = 0.1\n\nLet me write this in matrix form:\n\n[1.8   -0.5 ] [t]   = [ -0.1 ]\n\n[0.2   √3/2] [s]     [  0.1 ]\n\nLet me solve for t and s.\n\nFirst, let's solve the first equation for s:\n\nFrom equation 1: 1.8 t + 0.5 s = -0.1? Wait, no:\n\nWait, equation 1: 1.8 t - 0.5 s = -0.1\n\nSo rearranged: -0.5 s = -1.8 t - 0.1 ⇒ s = (1.8 t + 0.1)/0.5 = 3.6 t + 0.2\n\nNow plug into equation 2:\n\n0.2 t + (√3/2)(3.6 t + 0.2) = 0.1\n\nCompute term by term:\n\n0.2 t + (√3/2)(3.6 t) + (√3/2)(0.2) = 0.1\n\nCompute coefficients:\n\nFirst term: 0.2 t\n\nSecond term: (√3/2)(3.6 t) = (3.6 / 2) √3 t = 1.8 √3 t\n\nThird term: (√3/2)(0.2) = 0.1 √3\n\nSo altogether:\n\n0.2 t + 1.8 √3 t + 0.1 √3 = 0.1\n\nCombine the t terms:\n\nt (0.2 + 1.8 √3) + 0.1 √3 - 0.1 = 0\n\nSolve for t:\n\nt = [0.1 - 0.1 √3] / [0.2 + 1.8 √3]\n\nThis is getting messy, but let me approximate numerically.\n\nFirst, compute √3 ≈ 1.732.\n\nCompute numerator:\n\n0.1 - 0.1 * 1.732 ≈ 0.1 - 0.1732 ≈ -0.0732\n\nDenominator:\n\n0.2 + 1.8 * 1.732 ≈ 0.2 + 3.1176 ≈ 3.3176\n\nTherefore, t ≈ -0.0732 / 3.3176 ≈ -0.022\n\nHmm, t ≈ -0.022, which is less than 0, meaning that the intersection with Edge 3 occurs before the starting point of our line segment (which starts at t=0). Therefore, the line does not intersect Edge 3 within the segment from P1 to P2.\n\nSimilarly, check intersection with Edge 4 (the bottom edge from (-0.5, -0.866) to (0.5, -0.866)). The bottom edge is at y = -√3/2 ≈ -0.866. Our line's y(t) = -0.1 + 0.2 t. Set this equal to -0.866:\n\n-0.1 + 0.2 t = -0.866 ⇒ 0.2 t = -0.766 ⇒ t = -3.83\n\nWhich is way before t=0, so no intersection there either.\n\nNow check intersection with the right side, Edge 5: from Vertex 5 (0.5, -√3/2) to Vertex 0 (1,0). Let's parametrize Edge 5:\n\nx(s) = 0.5 + 0.5 s\n\ny(s) = -√3/2 + (√3/2) s, for s from 0 to 1.\n\nSo, set equal to the line's equations:\n\nx(t) = -0.9 + 1.8 t = 0.5 + 0.5 s\n\ny(t) = -0.1 + 0.2 t = -√3/2 + (√3/2) s\n\nAgain, two equations:\n\n1) -0.9 + 1.8 t = 0.5 + 0.5 s ⇒ 1.8 t - 0.5 s = 1.4\n\n2) -0.1 + 0.2 t = -√3/2 + (√3/2) s ⇒ 0.2 t - (√3/2) s = -√3/2 + 0.1\n\nThis is another system. Let me write it as:\n\n1.8 t - 0.5 s = 1.4\n\n0.2 t - (√3/2) s = -√3/2 + 0.1\n\nAgain, messy, but let's try plugging in approximate numbers.\n\nFirst equation:\n\n1.8 t - 0.5 s = 1.4\n\nSecond equation:\n\n0.2 t - 0.866 s ≈ -0.866 + 0.1 = -0.766\n\nLet me solve equation 1 for s:\n\nFrom equation 1: -0.5 s = 1.4 - 1.8 t ⇒ s = (1.8 t - 1.4)/0.5 = 3.6 t - 2.8\n\nPlug into equation 2:\n\n0.2 t - 0.866*(3.6 t - 2.8) ≈ -0.766\n\nCompute term by term:\n\n0.2 t - 0.866*3.6 t + 0.866*2.8 ≈ -0.766\n\nCalculate coefficients:\n\n0.2 t - 3.1176 t + 2.4248 ≈ -0.766\n\nCombine t terms:\n\n(0.2 - 3.1176) t + 2.4248 ≈ -0.766\n\n-2.9176 t ≈ -0.766 - 2.4248 ≈ -3.1908\n\nThus, t ≈ (-3.1908)/(-2.9176) ≈ 1.093\n\nSo t ≈ 1.093, which is greater than 1, meaning beyond the endpoint of our line segment (which ends at t=1). Therefore, the line does not intersect Edge 5 within the segment from P1 to P2.\n\nTherefore, the line from P1 to P2 does not intersect any of the horizontal edges (Edges 1 or 4) nor the other opposite edges? Wait, but where does it exit the hexagon then?\n\nIt must exit through one of the non-horizontal edges. Let's see, the line goes from (-0.9, -0.1) to (0.9, 0.1), passing through the origin. At the origin, it's inside the hexagon, but beyond that, when t > 1, it goes to (1.8, 0.2), which is outside the hexagon. The hexagon's rightmost point is at x=1, so at x=1.8, it's outside. Therefore, the line exits the hexagon at the right side, which is Edge 0 or Edge 5?\n\nWait, the right side above the x-axis is Edge 0 (from (1,0) to (0.5, √3/2)), and the right side below the x-axis is Edge 5 (from (0.5, -√3/2) to (1,0)).\n\nAt the point where x=1, the line has y-coordinate when t = (1 - (-0.9))/1.8 ≈ (1.9)/1.8 ≈ 1.055, but wait, parametrization was x(t) = -0.9 + 1.8 t, so to reach x=1:\n\n1 = -0.9 + 1.8 t ⇒ 1.8 t = 1.9 ⇒ t ≈ 1.055\n\nAt that t, y(t) = -0.1 + 0.2*(1.055) ≈ -0.1 + 0.211 ≈ 0.111\n\nTherefore, the exit point is at (1, 0.111), which is on the right side above the x-axis, so on Edge 0 or Edge 5?\n\nEdge 0 is from (1,0) to (0.5, √3/2). The point (1, 0.111) is just above (1,0), so it's on Edge 0? Wait, Edge 0 starts at (1,0) and goes up to (0.5, √3/2). So yes, the point (1, 0.111) is on Edge 0, since it's directly above Vertex 0 along the x-axis.\n\nWait, actually, Edge 0 is from (1,0) to (0.5, √3/2). So, parametrizing Edge 0:\n\nx(s) = 1 - 0.5 s\n\ny(s) = 0 + (√3/2) s, for s from 0 to 1.\n\nSo, to see if (1, 0.111) is on Edge 0, set x(s)=1:\n\n1 = 1 - 0.5 s ⇒ s=0. So that's the starting point (1,0). But y(s)=0.111 would require s = 0.111 / (√3/2) ≈ 0.111 / 0.866 ≈ 0.128. But then x(s) would be 1 - 0.5*0.128 ≈ 0.936, which is not 1. Therefore, the point (1, 0.111) is not on Edge 0; it's outside the hexagon beyond the starting point of Edge 0.\n\nWait, actually, the line exits the hexagon at x=1, y≈0.111, but since the hexagon's rightmost x is 1, and at x=1, the hexagon only exists at y=0 (the vertex) and above that on Edge 0. Since the point (1, 0.111) is to the right of the hexagon's boundary? Wait, no, x=1 is the rightmost point. The hexagon at x=1 only has the single point (1,0). Any point with x=1 and y≠0 is outside the hexagon. Therefore, the line exits the hexagon at the boundary between Edge 0 and the right side? Wait, actually, the rightmost point is (1,0), so moving along x=1 beyond that is outside. Therefore, the exit point is at (1,0), but that's a vertex. Wait, but our line passes through (1,0.111) which is outside, so the actual exit point is where the line crosses the boundary of the hexagon just before exiting.\n\nWait, this is getting too calculation-heavy. Maybe my approach is flawed. Perhaps instead of trying specific examples, I should think more geometrically.\n\nLet me recall that in a convex polygon, the set of lines that intersect a particular pair of opposite edges can be characterized as those lines that cross between those two opposite edges. Since the two opposite edges are parallel, the region between them is a strip, and lines that pass through this strip will intersect both edges. However, in the context of the hexagon, the strip between two opposite edges is actually the entire hexagon except for the regions adjacent to the other edges? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, since the hexagon is centrally symmetric, perhaps the probability we're seeking is 1/2? Because for any line, it either intersects a pair of opposite edges or it doesn't, and due to symmetry, the probability might be 1/2? But that seems too simple, and probably incorrect.\n\nWait, another thought: in a centrally symmetric convex body, the probability that a random chord intersects a pair of opposite sides might be related to the proportion of the area \"between\" those sides. But I need to formalize this.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use the concept of duality: mapping points to lines and lines to points, but I'm not sure.\n\nWait, here's another idea: instead of dealing with the hexagon directly, we can use a coordinate transformation to simplify the problem. Since the hexagon is regular and symmetric, we can use a coordinate system aligned with one of its axes, and then exploit symmetry to compute the probability for one case and multiply appropriately.\n\nLet me consider the horizontal pair of opposite edges (Edges 1 and 4). Due to the hexagon's symmetry, the probability that a random line intersects this pair is the same as for any other pair. Therefore, if I can compute this probability for one pair, say the horizontal pair, then multiply by 3 (for the three pairs) to get the total probability. However, as I thought earlier, this assumes that the events are mutually exclusive, which they are, since a line can't intersect two different pairs of opposite edges.\n\nTherefore, total probability P = 3 * P(intersecting a specific pair).\n\nSo, let's focus on computing P(intersecting the horizontal pair, Edges 1 and 4).\n\nTo compute this probability, we can consider all possible lines through two random points in the hexagon, and determine the proportion that intersect both Edges 1 and 4.\n\nHowever, integrating over all such lines is non-trivial. Perhaps we can use a probabilistic approach by considering the positions of the two points and determining the condition under which the line between them intersects both Edges 1 and 4.\n\nAlternatively, we can use the concept of \"visibility\": the line will intersect both Edges 1 and 4 if and only if the two points are on opposite \"sides\" with respect to the horizontal axis? Wait, not exactly, because the horizontal axis here is the x-axis, but the horizontal edges are Edges 1 (top) and 4 (bottom). So perhaps the line must cross from above the x-axis to below the x-axis or vice versa? But not necessarily, because the line could be entirely above or entirely below and still not intersect the horizontal edges.\n\nWait, actually, to intersect both the top and bottom edges (Edges 1 and 4), the line must pass from the upper part of the hexagon to the lower part, crossing the x-axis in between. Alternatively, if the line is horizontal, it might be between the top and bottom edges, thus intersecting both.\n\nWait, let me think: for a line to intersect both the top edge (Edge 1) and the bottom edge (Edge 4), it must cross from above the bottom edge to below the top edge, passing through the interior. Since the hexagon is convex, any line that enters the hexagon from one side must exit through another. To intersect both the top and bottom edges, the line must enter through one of the non-horizontal edges and exit through the opposite non-horizontal edge, but also pass through the top and bottom edges? Hmm, this is confusing.\n\nAlternatively, consider that the top edge is the highest horizontal line in the hexagon, and the bottom edge is the lowest. Therefore, a line that goes from left to right with a positive slope will intersect the bottom edge on the left side and the top edge on the right side. Similarly, a line with a negative slope will intersect the bottom edge on the right and the top edge on the left. A horizontal line between the top and bottom edges will intersect both edges. A nearly horizontal line will also intersect both edges if it's not too steep.\n\nConversely, a line that is almost vertical will intersect the left and right edges but not the top and bottom edges? Wait, no, a vertical line does intersect the top and bottom edges, as we saw earlier.\n\nWait, actually, in the regular hexagon, every straight line that is not parallel to one of the three pairs of opposite edges will intersect exactly two edges, which are either a pair of opposite edges or two adjacent edges? Hmm.\n\nWait, let's think of a line that is very close to being parallel to one of the non-horizontal edges. For example, a line nearly parallel to Edge 0 (which has slope -√3). Such a line would be almost parallel to the right edge, so it might only intersect one edge? Wait, no, in a convex polygon, a line can't be tangent to a vertex or edge without intersecting two edges at a vertex.\n\nWait, suppose a line is parallel to Edge 0 but offset slightly upwards. Since Edge 0 is on the right side of the hexagon, a line parallel to Edge 0 but above it would not intersect Edge 0