, no, if they are every third arc, then the number of arcs between two snakes is two? Let me think in terms of linear first.\n\nSuppose you have a straight line of 45 arcs. If you place a snake on every third arc starting at position 0, then the snakes are at positions 0, 3, 6, 9, ..., up to 42. Then between each snake, there are two empty arcs. For example, between snake at 0 and snake at 3, there are arcs 1 and 2 empty. So the separation between snakes is two arcs? But in a circle, the distance between the last snake and the first snake is also three arcs? Let me check. The last snake is at position 42. Then the next arc is 43, then 44, then back to 0. So from 42 to 0, going clockwise, there are arcs 43 and 44 in between, so that's two arcs between the snake at 42 and the snake at 0. So in the circular case, between any two adjacent snakes, there are two empty arcs. So each snake is separated by two empty arcs. So each snake is on an arc, and then there are two arcs between each snake and the next one in the clockwise direction. Okay, got it.\n\nNow, every second, each snake independently moves either one arc left or one arc right, each with probability 1/2. So each snake can move clockwise or counterclockwise by one arc each second, with equal probability. If two snakes ever touch, they merge to form a single snake occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes, and the merged snake moves as one snake. The question is to compute the expected number of seconds until there is only one snake left.\n\nHmm. Alright, so this seems like a process that starts with 15 snakes, each moving randomly, and when two snakes occupy the same arc or adjacent arcs? Wait, actually, when do they \"touch\"? The problem says: \"If two snakes ever touch, they merge to form a single snake occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes, and the merged snake moves as one snake.\"\n\nWait, so when two snakes \"touch\", they merge. What does it mean for two snakes to touch? Since each s