t gap's size, but the opposite gap would remain the same.\n\nWait, maybe an example will help. Suppose we have three snakes: A, B, C, with gaps g1, g2, g3 between them. Let's say gap g1 is between A and B, g2 between B and C, and g3 between C and A. Initially, all gaps are 2.\n\nSuppose snake B decides to move clockwise. It was originally between gap g1 (to its left) and gap g2 (to its right). If it moves clockwise, it moves into the gap g2, reducing g2 by 1, and the gap g1 remains the same? Wait, but then the total gaps would still sum to the same? Wait, no, because moving the snake into the gap g2 would actually shift the gaps.\n\nWait, perhaps when snake B moves clockwise, it is no longer between gap g1 and g2, but now is in the middle of gap g2. So the new gaps would be:\n\nThe gap to the left of snake B is now (g1 + 1), because the snake moved into the gap g2, so the left side of snake B now has one more empty arc from the previous gap g1?\n\nWait, this is getting confusing. Maybe a better way is to consider that moving a snake from between gaps g_prev and g_next to between gaps g_prev + 1 and g_next - 1. Therefore, moving clockwise decreases the next gap by 1 and increases the previous gap by 1.\n\nYes, that seems to make sense. So, if snake i is between gap g_{i-1} and gap g_i, then:\n\n- Moving clockwise: the snake moves into the gap g_i, thereby reducing gap g_i by 1 and increasing gap g_{i-1} by 1.\n\n- Moving counterclockwise: the snake moves into the gap g_{i-1}, thereby reducing gap g_{i-1} by 1 and increasing gap g_i by 1.\n\nTherefore, each move of a snake adjusts the two adjacent gaps by +1 and -1 in a way depending on the direction.\n\nTherefore, the gaps evolve according to the movements of the snakes. Importantly, when a gap reaches size 0, that means two snakes have merged, because the gap between them has been reduced to zero, so the two snakes are now adjacent and thus merge into a single snake. When this happens, the two snakes become a single snake, and the gap is eliminated. However, the merged snake now occupies two adjacent arcs, so the gaps adjacent to this merged snake will now be affected differently.\n\nWait, when two snakes merge into a single snake of length 2, occupying two adjacent arcs, how does this affect the gaps?\n\nSuppose originally, between snake A and snake B was a gap of size g, and between snake B and snake C was a gap of size h. Then, if snake B moves into the gap between A and B (or something?), leading to a merge.\n\nWait, actually, let's consider an example where two adjacent gaps become zero.\n\nSuppose we have two adjacent gaps, g_i and g_{i+1}, both being 1. If snake i moves into gap g_i (decreasing it to 0) and simultaneously snake i+1 moves into gap g_{i+1} (also decreasing it to 0), then the two gaps would both reach zero, causing the two snakes to merge? Wait, but actually, if a gap reaches zero, that means the two snakes on either side of that gap are now adjacent, so they merge. So if gap g_i becomes zero, then snakes i and i+1 merge. Similarly, if gap g_{i+1} becomes zero, snakes i+1 and i+2 merge.\n\nBut if two adjacent gaps both reach zero in the same step, that would mean three snakes are merging? For example, if gap g_i and g_{i+1} both reach zero in the same time step, then snakes i and i+1 merge, and snakes i+1 and i+2 merge, resulting in all three snakes merging into one? But actually, if snakes i and i+1 merge, then the merged snake is now between snake i+2 and the next snake, but this might complicate things.\n\nActually, perhaps when a gap reaches zero, the two snakes on either side of that gap merge, forming a single snake, and the gap is removed. The new snake then has its own gaps on both sides, which are the previous gaps adjacent to the merged snake, but adjusted because the merged snake now occupies two arcs.\n\nWait, let's formalize:\n\nSuppose we have snakes S_{i-1}, S_i, S_{i+1}, ..., with gaps g_{i-1}, g_i, g_{i+1}, etc.\n\nIf the gap g_i (between 