ons \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\". If two snakes are on adjacent arcs, then they don't occupy the same arcs, so merging them would require occupying both arcs. So, perhaps \"touching\" is defined as being on the same arc? Then overlapping is considered touching. But if they are on adjacent arcs, they are not touching, so they don't merge. That might make more sense.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"touching\" means that their paths crossed during the movement? But since they move simultaneously, it's hard to say. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is using \"touch\" to mean that after moving, their positions are adjacent, so that they are now next to each other, and hence considered to have touched. Then, in that case, merging would occur when two snakes are on adjacent arcs after the movement.\n\nGiven that the problem says \"if two snakes ever touch, they merge to form a single snake occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\", so if two snakes are on adjacent arcs, then the merged snake would occupy both arcs, so that merged snake is now of length 2. So, in that case, merging occurs when two snakes end up on adjacent arcs after moving.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they have to be on the same arc to merge? Because if they are on adjacent arcs, then the merged snake would occupy two arcs, but the problem says \"the arcs of both of the previous snakes\", so if they were on adjacent arcs, then the merged snake would occupy both arcs. But if they were on the same arc, then the merged snake would just occupy that one arc? That seems contradictory.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is such that when two snakes are on the same arc, they merge into a single snake on that arc, but that would mean they just stay on that arc? But then they wouldn't have \"merged\" into a longer snake. Hmm, this is confusing.\n\nWait, the problem says \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\". So if two snakes were on arcs A and B, then the merged sna