o the first merger will take an expected time of 84 seconds. After that merger, we have 14 snakes left. Now, what is the expected time to go from 14 to 13?\n\nAssuming that after merging two snakes into one, the new snake is now at a position that is adjacent to its neighbors, but the distances between the other snakes might have changed.\n\nActually, when two snakes merge, they form a snake of length 2. This merged snake can now potentially merge with other snakes more easily or less easily depending on their movements.\n\nHowever, due to the symmetry of the initial configuration and the random movements, perhaps the expected time between each merger is the same, so each merger takes 84 seconds on average, leading to a total of 14 * 84 = 1176 seconds.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the expected time between each merger is proportional to the current number of snakes? For example, if with N snakes, the expected time until the next merger is proportional to N^2, then the total time would be the sum_{k=2}^{15} k^2, but I need to think.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the expected time until all snakes have merged is 15^2 = 225, but this is a guess.\n\nWait, but in the two-snake case, we computed E(d=3)=84, which is much larger than 2²=4, so that can't be.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's related to the total number of steps needed for all snakes to meet at a single point, but that would be different.\n\nAlternatively, since each snake has to cover a certain distance to meet others, but with random walks, the expected time might be related to the variance or something.\n\nAlternatively, let me think of the entire system as 15 independent random walks on the circle, and we want the expected time until all walks have been \"absorbed\" into a single position through mergers when they meet.\n\nIn such a system, the coalescence time might be similar to the meeting time of multiple random walks.\n\nIn the literature, for the meeting time of two random walks on a circle, the expected time unt