/4)E(1) + (1/2)E(3) + (1/4)E(4)\n\n=> E(3) - (1/2)E(3) = 1 + (1/4)E(1) + (1/4)E(4)\n\n=> (1/2)E(3) = 1 + (1/4)(E(1) + E(4))\n\n=> E(3) = 2 + (1/2)(E(1) + E(4)) --- (a)\n\nFor d=4:\n\nE(4) = 1 + (1/4)E(2) + (1/2)E(4) + (1/4)E(6)\n\nBut E(6)=E(9-6)=E(3)\n\nThus,\n\nE(4) = 1 + (1/4)E(2) + (1/2)E(4) + (1/4)E(3)\n\n=> E(4) - (1/2)E(4) = 1 + (1/4)E(2) + (1/4)E(3)\n\n=> (1/2)E(4) = 1 + (1/4)(E(2) + E(3))\n\n=> E(4) = 2 + (1/2)(E(2) + E(3)) --- (b)\n\nNow, we have equations (a) and (b):\n\nFrom (a):\n\nE(3) = 2 + (1/2)E(1) + (1/2)E(4)\n\nFrom (b):\n\nE(4) = 2 + (1/2)E(2) + (1/2)E(3)\n\nFrom d=2:\n\nE(2) = 2 + (1/2)E(4)\n\nFrom d=1:\n\nE(1) = 2 + (1/2)E(3)\n\nNow, let's solve these equations step by step.\n\nFirst, express E(2) and E(1) in terms of E(4) and E(3):\n\nE(2) = 2 + (1/2)E(4) --- (c)\n\nE(1) = 2 + (1/2)E(3) --- (d)\n\nSubstitute E(2) and E(1) into equation (a):\n\nE(3) = 2 + (1/2)(2 + (1/2)E(4)) + (1/2)E(4)\n\nSimplify:\n\nE(3) = 2 + 1 + (1/4)E(4) + (1/2)E(4)\n\n= 3 + (3/4)E(4) --- (e)\n\nFrom equation (b):\n\nE(4) = 2 + (1/2)E(2) +","num_tokens":32768,"correct":false,"formatted":false},{"predicted_answer":"[no boxed]","full_generation":"Okay, so I have this problem here about snakes on a circle divided into 45 arcs, each of length 1. There are initially 15 snakes, each of length 1, and they occupy every third arc. So, that means the snakes are spaced three arcs apart? Let me visualize this. Since the circumference is 45 arcs, and there are 15 snakes, each taking up one arc, so the spacing between snakes is 45 / 15 = 3 arcs apart, right? So, like, if we number the arcs from 0 to 44, then the snakes are on arcs 0, 3, 6, 9, ..., 42, right? Because 15 times 3 is 45, so starting at 0, every third arc. So, positions 0, 3, 6, ..., 42. Each of those positions has a snake, each snake is length 1, so each snake occupies exactly one arc? Wait, but the problem says \"each of length 1\", so each snake occupies one arc? But then the circumference is 45 arcs, and there are 15 s