nk. The problem says \"If two snakes ever touch, they merge...\", so the merging is triggered by the event that two snakes are in a state where they are touching. Since the snakes are moving on a circular track divided into discrete arcs, the most natural interpretation is that two snakes \"touch\" if they are on the same arc. Because if they are on adjacent arcs, they are just next to each other but not overlapping. However, in some contexts, \"touching\" might mean adjacent, but in others, it might mean overlapping. Since the problem mentions that after merging, the merged snake occupies \"the arcs of both of the previous snakes\", so if they were on adjacent arcs, then the merged snake would occupy both arcs, making it a snake of length 2? Whereas if they were on the same arc, the merged snake would occupy just that one arc? Wait, but that would be inconsistent. If two snakes are on the same arc, then merging them would just result in a snake that is still on that one arc, right? Because both were on the same arc. Whereas if they were on adjacent arcs, then merging them would result in a snake occupying both adjacent arcs. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"touching\" means that their paths cross? Wait, no, they are moving on discrete arcs. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps \"touching\" means that during their movement, their paths cross? But since they move one arc at a time, perhaps if two snakes are moving towards each other and would end up on the same arc in the next step, then they collide and merge? Hmm. Alternatively, perhaps the movement is simultaneous, so if two snakes move towards each other and end up on the same arc, then they merge. Whereas if they move away from each other, they just pass by without merging? Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe we need to look at the exact wording again: \"If two snakes ever touch, they merge to form a single snake occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes, and the merged snake moves as one snake.\"\n\nSo, the merging happens when two snakes \"ever touch\", which is presumably during their movement. Since they move simultaneously, perhaps if two snakes move into the same arc, then they merge? Because that would be the only way two snakes could end up on the same arc in the same time step, thereby merging. Alternatively, if they are on adjacent arcs and move towards each other, then they would end up on the same arc? Wait, no, moving towards each other would mean one moves left and the other moves right, so they would cross over? Wait, let me think with an example.\n\nSuppose we have two snakes on arcs 1 and 3. The snake on arc 1 can move left to 0 or right to 2. The snake on arc 3 can move left to 2 or right to 4. So, if the left snake moves right to 2, and the right snake moves left to 2, then both would end up on arc 2. So that's a collision at arc 2, so they merge there. Alternatively, if the left snake moves left to 0 and the right snake moves right to 4, then they just move apart. If the left snake stays on 1 (but they can't stay; they have to move either left or right) so actually, they both have to move. So in this case, moving towards each other can lead to collision on the middle arc. Alternatively, moving away from each other would not cause collision. So in this case, \"touching\" might refer to ending up on the same arc as a result of their movement.\n\nAlternatively, if two snakes are on adjacent arcs, say arcs 1 and 2. Then, in the next move, each can move left or right. The snake on 1 can go to 0 or 2; the snake on 2 can go to 1 or 3. So, if the snake on 1 moves right to 2 and the snake on 2 moves left to 1, then they would swap places, ending up on arcs 2 and 1, respectively, so they are still on adjacent arcs but swapped. So, they didn't merge? Because they didn't end up on the same arc. Alternatively, if both the snake on 1 moves right to 2 and the snake on 2 stays put? But they can't stay put; they have to move. So actually, the snake on 2 can move left to 1 or right to 3. So if snake 1 moves right to 2, and snake 2 moves left to 1, then they end up on arcs 2 and 1, which are adjacent but not overlapping. So they didn't merge. Alternatively, if snake 1 moves left to 0 and snake 2 moves right to 3, then they just move apart. Alternatively, if snake 1 moves right to 2 and snake 2 moves right to 3, then snake 1 is now on 2 and snake 2 is on 3, so still adjacent but not overlapping. Similarly, if snake 1 moves left to 0 and snake 2 moves left to 1, then snake 1 is on 0 and snake 2 is on 1, again adjacent but not overlapping. So in this case, starting from adjacent arcs, they can move in such a way that they remain adjacent but not overlapping, or they can move apart, but they never end up on the same arc? Wait, unless one of them moves into the other's starting position? Wait, in the first case, if snake 1 is on 1 and snake 2 is on 2, then snake 1 moving right to 2 and snake 2 moving left to 1 would result in them swapping positions, so they are still on adjacent arcs, just swapped. So they don't end up on the same arc.\n\nTherefore, in order for two snakes to end up on the same arc in a single time step, they must have moved towards each other across an arc? Wait, in the previous example with snakes on 1 and 3, if the snake on 1 moves right to 2 and the snake on 3 moves left to 2, then both are moving into arc 2, so they end up on the same arc, hence merging.\n\nTherefore, perhaps the only way two snakes can merge in a single time step is if they move towards each other and into the same arc. So, in general, two snakes on arcs i and j will merge in the next step if and only if one is moving towards the other across the arc between them? Hmm. Wait, actually, more precisely, if they are two arcs apart, say, with one snake on arc k and another on arc k+2, then if the snake on k moves right to k+1, and the snake on k+2 moves left to k+1, then they both end up on k+1, hence merging. Alternatively, if they are one arc apart, say on k and k+1, then as in the earlier example, they can't both move into the same arc unless they both move towards the same intermediate arc? Wait, but if they are on adjacent arcs, say k and k+1, then the snake on k can move to k-1 or k+1, and the snake on k+1 can move to k or k+2. So if the snake on k moves to k+1 and the snake on k+1 moves to k, then they swap places, so they end up on k and k+1 again, just swapped. They don't end up on the same arc. Therefore, in order for two adjacent snakes to merge, they would have to both move into the same arc, but since they are on adjacent arcs, moving towards each other would just swap their positions, not cause a collision. Therefore, adjacent snakes cannot merge in a single time step? Because they can only swap positions or move apart, but not collide?\n\nWait, but if they are on the same arc, then they are already overlapping. So perhaps \"touching\" is defined as being on the same arc? So initially, all the snakes are on every third arc, so they are not adjacent or overlapping. Then, during movement, two snakes can only merge if they end up on the same arc in the next time step. Therefore, merging occurs only when two snakes move into the same arc. So, in that case, \"touching\" would mean occupying the same arc. So, perhaps that's the key: two snakes \"touch\" if they occupy the same arc after moving. Therefore, merging occurs when two snakes end up on the same arc after moving, and then they merge into one snake occupying that single arc? Wait, but the problem says \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\". If they were on different arcs before, then merging them would require occupying both arcs? Wait, now I'm confused again.\n\nWait, if two snakes are moving to the same arc, then they were on different arcs before, so the merged snake should occupy both of their previous arcs? But they are moving to the same arc, so the previous arcs are different, but the merged snake is now on that single arc? That seems contradictory. Alternatively, maybe the merged snake occupies the union of the arcs that the two snakes were previously on? But if they moved to the same arc, then their previous positions were different, so the merged snake would occupy both previous positions? But that might not make sense because they are now at the same new position.\n\nHmm, this is a problem. The problem statement is a bit ambiguous. Let me see if I can find another way.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"touching\" is defined as being on adjacent arcs, and when they merge, the merged snake occupies both arcs. So if two snakes are on adjacent arcs, they merge into a snake of length 2 occupying both arcs. Then, in the next move, that snake can move as a single unit, either left or right, meaning the entire block of two arcs moves one arc left or right? So, for example, if a snake of length 2 is on arcs 3 and 4, then moving left would take it to arcs 2 and 3, and moving right would take it to arcs 4 and 5? Wait, but that would mean that moving left from arcs 3-4 would go to arcs 2-3? But then arc 3 is now part of the snake's new position, overlapping with the previous position? Hmm, perhaps that's okay.\n\nAlternatively, maybe when a snake of length L moves, it shifts its entire body by one arc in the chosen direction. So, for a length-1 snake, moving left or right just shifts its single arc by one. For a length-2 snake, moving left would shift both of its arcs to the left by one, so if it was on arcs i and i+1, moving left would put it on arcs i-1 and i, and moving right would put it on arcs i+1 and i+2. So that seems consistent.\n\nIn that case, when two snakes merge, they form a snake of length equal to the sum of the lengths of the two snakes? Wait, but initially, all snakes are length 1, so merging two would give a snake of length 2. Then merging another snake of length 1 with that would give length 3, etc. So the merged snake's length is the sum of the lengths of the snakes that merged. So in that case, \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\" would mean that the merged snake occupies all the arcs that the previous snakes occupied. Since each previous snake was length 1, the merged snake is length 2, occupying two arcs.\n\nTherefore, with that interpretation, \"touching\" must mean that two snakes are on adjacent arcs, so that when they merge, they form a snake of length 2 covering both arcs. But wait, in that case, how do two snakes ever get to be on the same arc? Because if they are on adjacent arcs and move towards each other, they just swap places, as in the earlier example, so they remain on adjacent arcs but swapped, so they don't merge? Wait, that seems like a problem. Because if two snakes start on adjacent arcs, they can only swap places or move apart, but never end up on the same arc? Therefore, under this interpretation, adjacent snakes cannot merge? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, if two snakes are on the same arc, then they are already \"touching\" and should merge immediately? But initially, they are not on the same arc; they are on every third arc. So, the first time two snakes could possibly be on the same arc is if, during their movement, they both move into the same arc. So, for example, if two snakes are three arcs apart, then they could move towards each other and end up on the same arc? Wait, let me think of an example.\n\nSuppose we have a snake on arc 0 and another on arc 3. Then, the snake on 0 can move to 1 or 44 (assuming the circle is 0-44). The snake on 3 can move to 2 or 4. So, if the snake on 0 moves right to 1, and the snake on 3 moves left to 2, then they are now on 1 and 2, which are adjacent, so maybe that's considered touching? Or if they move towards each other across the gap? Wait, if the snake on 0 moves right to 1, and the snake on 3 moves left to 2, then they are now on 1 and 2, which are adjacent. Then, according to the merging rule, if \"touching\" is defined as adjacent, then they would merge into a snake of length 2 on