 as a Markov chain where the state is the configuration of the snakes, and we need to compute the expected time until the state reduces to a single snake. But with 15 snakes initially, and the state space being enormous (since each snake can be on any of 45 positions, but with constraints), this seems intractable.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there is some symmetry or structure we can exploit. The initial configuration has snakes every three arcs. Since the circle has 45 arcs, which is divisible by 3, 5, 9, 15, etc. The initial spacing is 3 arcs between each snake. Since all snakes are identical and the circle is symmetric, perhaps we can model the problem in terms of the distances between the snakes, or perhaps consider the circle as a one-dimensional lattice with periodic boundary conditions, and the snakes are particles moving on the lattice with certain rules.\n\nWait, actually, this problem resembles the \"voter model\" or \"coalescing random walks\" on a circle. In the voter model, particles move and when two meet, they merge their opinions, but here it's similar in that when two snakes meet (collide), they merge into one. So it's a type of coalescent process on a circle with movement rules.\n\nIn the standard coalescent processes, like in the linear case or on a circle, the expected time to coalesce can sometimes be computed using known results, but here the movement is random with equal probability left or right each step, which might complicate things.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can think of each snake as performing a random walk on the circle, and the merging happens when two random walks meet at the same site. Then, the process continues until all snakes have merged into one. The question is the expected time until all have merged.\n\nHowever, in standard coalescing random walks, typically the merging happens when two walks meet, and then they coalesce into a single walk that continues, but in this case, the merged snake moves as one, so it's a different dynamics.\n\nAlternatively, since all snakes start equally spaced, maybe we can consider the problem in terms of the gaps between the snakes. Initially, all gaps are 3 arcs, but as snakes move, the gaps can change. The process ends when there's only one snake, meaning all gaps have been closed except one? Wait, no, actually, when there's only one snake, all the other snakes have merged into it, so all gaps would have been closed by the merging process.\n\nAlternatively, since the circle is symmetric, perhaps we can \"unfold\" the circle into a line by cutting it at a point and considering the snakes as particles on a line with periodic boundary conditions, but I don't know if that helps.\n\nAlternatively, note that the problem has rotational symmetry, so perhaps we can fix the position of one snake and consider the relative positions of the others. Since all snakes start 3 apart, their initial configuration is rotationally symmetric. So perhaps we can fix one snake at position 0, and then the others are at positions 3, 6, ..., 42. Then, we can model the movement of the other snakes relative to this fixed snake. But since all snakes are moving simultaneously, this might not reduce the complexity.\n\nAlternatively, since all snakes move independently, maybe the expected time until all have merged can be related to the expected time for a single snake to meet all others? But that seems too vague.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model this as a Markov chain where the state is the number of snakes remaining, and compute the expected time to go from 15 to 1. However, the transition probabilities between states (number of snakes) might not be straightforward because the merging can happen in various ways depending on the current configuration.\n\nWait, maybe we can approximate or find some linearity of expectation argument? Maybe the expected time until all snakes have merged can be expressed as the sum over all pairs of the expected time until those two snakes merge? But I don't think that's correct because mergers can happen in overlapping events and dependencies.\n\nAlternatively, maybe think of each pair of snakes and compute the probability that they eventually merge, and then use some formula? Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, since all snakes start equally spaced, maybe the problem has some periodicity or symmetry that can be exploited.\n\nWait, let me think of a simpler case. Suppose instead of 15 snakes on 45 arcs, suppose we have 3 snakes on 9 arcs, each starting every 3 arcs. Maybe compute the expected time for that smaller case, and see if a pattern emerges.\n\nLet me try that. Let's consider a circle of 9 arcs, with 3 snakes, each of length 1, starting at positions 0, 3, 6. Let's see what happens in that case.\n\nEach second, each snake moves left or right with probability 1/2. When two snakes move into the same arc, they merge into one snake occupying that arc (or both arcs? Wait, in the smaller case, if two snakes merge, do they occupy two arcs or one? Wait, in the original problem, merging two snakes would occupy both of their previous arcs. Wait, but in the 3-snake, 9-arc example, if two snakes are on arcs 0 and 3, and they move towards each other, say the snake at 0 moves right to 1, and the snake at 3 moves left to 2, then they are now on 1 and 2, which are adjacent. If \"touching\" means adjacent, then they would merge into a snake of length 2 on arcs 1 and 2. Then, in the next step, that snake can move as a block. Alternatively, if merging requires being on the same arc, then in this case, they didn't merge.\n\nHmm, so perhaps in the smaller case, we need to define the merging rule precisely.\n\nAlternatively, let me suppose that merging occurs only when two snakes end up on the same arc. So in the 9-arc, 3-snake example, starting at 0, 3, 6.\n\nSuppose in the first step, snake 0 moves right to 1, snake 3 moves left to 2, and snake 6 moves... say, let's suppose all snakes move in some direction. Let me pick a specific scenario:\n\nSuppose all snakes move clockwise. So each snake moves right. Then:\n\n- Snake at 0 moves to 1\n\n- Snake at 3 moves to 4\n\n- Snake at 6 moves to 7\n\nNow, all snakes are at 1, 4, 7. Still spaced 3 apart. So no merging occurs.\n\nAlternatively, suppose snake 0 moves right to 1, snake 3 moves left to 2, snake 6 moves left to 5.\n\nThen, the positions are 1, 2, 5. Now, snake 1 and 2 are adjacent. If merging occurs when adjacent, then they would merge into a snake on 1-2. Then the snakes would be at 1-2 and 5. Then, in the next step, the merged snake (length 2) can move left or right. If it moves left, it would go to 0 and 1; moving right would go to 2 and 3. The snake at 5 can move left to 4 or right to 6. Depending on their movements, maybe they can merge again.\n\nAlternatively, if merging only occurs when two snakes are on the same arc, then in the above case, snakes at 1 and 2 are not on the same arc, so they don't merge. So only when two snakes land on the exact same arc do they merge.\n\nIn that case, in the first step, to have a collision, two snakes must move to the same arc. For example, suppose snake 0 and snake 3 both move left: snake 0 to 44 (if 0-based, moving left from 0 would be to 44 in a 9-arc circle? Wait, hold on, in a 9-arc circle labeled 0 to 8, moving left from 0 would be to 8. So, in that case:\n\nSnake 0 moves left to 8, snake 3 moves left to 2, snake 6 moves left to 5.\n\nThen positions: 8, 2, 5. No collisions.\n\nAlternatively, snake 0 moves right to 1, snake 3 moves right to 4, snake 6 moves right to 7: same as before.\n\nAlternatively, suppose snake 0 moves left to 8, snake 3 moves right to 4, snake 6 moves left to 5.\n\nThen positions: 8, 4, 5. Then snake 4 and 5 are adjacent? If adjacent counts as touching, then they might merge? But if only same arc counts, then no.\n\nAlternatively, if two snakes move towards each other and cross paths onto the same arc. For instance, suppose snake 0 moves right to 1, and snake 3 moves left to 2, and snake 6 moves left to 5. Then, no collision. Alternatively, snake 0 moves right to 1, snake 3 moves left to 2, and snake 6 moves right to 7: still no collision.\n\nAlternatively, snake 0 and snake 3 both move towards each other across the gap between them. The gap between snake 0 and snake 3 is 3 arcs (from 0 to 3). To collide, they need to move towards each other such that their paths cross. For example, snake 0 moves right towards 1, 2, 3, and snake 3 moves left towards 2, 1, 0. So, if snake 0 moves right twice and snake 3 moves left twice in two steps, but in one step, can they meet?\n\nIn one step, snake 0 can move to 1 or 8 (if on 0). Snake 3 can move to 2 or 4. The only way they can end up on the same arc in one step is if snake 0 moves to 2? Wait, no. Wait, in one step, each snake only moves one arc. So snake 0 can only go to 1 or 8, snake 3 can go to 2 or 4. So they can't reach the same arc in one step. Similarly, the distance between them is 3, so to meet, they would need to cover that distance in one step? Which is impossible since they can only move 1 arc each. So in this case, with 3 snakes on a 9-arc circle, initially spaced 3 apart, no two snakes can ever collide in a single step, because they are too far apart? Wait, that can't be.\n\nWait, if they start at 0, 3, 6 on a 9-arc circle. The distance between snake 0 and snake 3 is 3 arcs clockwise or 6 arcs counter-clockwise. Since it's a circle, the minimal distance is 3. To meet, they would need to close that distance. But each can only move 1 arc per step. So in one step, the minimal distance can decrease by at most 2 (if they move towards each other). For example, snake 0 moves right to 1, snake 3 moves left to 2: then the distance between them becomes 1 (from 1 to 2 is 1 arc). Then in the next step, they can potentially meet.\n\nAh, right! So in the first step, they can close the distance, but not necessarily collide. So in the first step, the distance between snake 0 and snake 3 can go from 3 to 1 or 5 (if they move away). Then in the next step, if they are now 1 apart, they can move towards each other and meet in the next step.\n\nSo in the 9-arc, 3-snake example:\n\nStart: positions 0, 3, 6.\n\nStep 1:\n\nSnake 0 can go to 1 or 8.\n\nSnake 3 can go to 2 or 4.\n\nSnake 6 can go to 5 or 7.\n\nSuppose we track the positions of snakes 0 and 3 first.\n\nCase 1: Snake 0 moves right to 1, snake 3 moves left to 2.\n\nThen new positions: 1, 2, 6 (assuming snake 6 didn't move towards them). The distance between snake 0 and 3 is now 1 (between 1 and 2). Then in the next step, they could potentially move towards each other and collide.\n\nCase 2: Snake 0 moves left to 8, snake 3 moves left to 2.\n\nThen positions: 8, 2, 6. Distance between 0 and 3 is now 6 (from 8 to 2 going the short way is 4? Wait, on a 9-arc circle, the distance from 8 to 2 is min(|8-2|, 9 - |8-2|) = min(6, 3) = 3? Wait, actually, the distance between 8 and 2: moving clockwise from 8 to 2 is 3 steps (8→0→1→2), and counter-clockwise is 6 steps (8→7→6→5→4→3→2). So minimal distance is 3. So actually, moving snake 0 left and snake 3 left keeps their minimal distance the same? Hmm, interesting.\n\nWait, actually, when snake 0 moves left to 8 and snake 3 moves left to 2, the new positions are 8, 2, 6. The distance between 8 and 2 is 3 (clockwise), same as before. The distance between 2 and 6 is 4? From 2 to 6 clockwise is 4, counter-clockwise is 5. So minimal is 4. Hmm.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Maybe it's better to try to see if in the 9-arc, 3-snake example, the expected time until all snakes have merged can be calculated, and perhaps see a pattern.\n\nAlternatively, maybe in the original problem, since the circle is divided into 45 arcs with 15 snakes, each initially 3 apart, and since 45 is divisible by 15 and 3, maybe the system has some periodicity or symmetry that allows for an exact answer.\n\nAlternatively, notice that all snakes start equally spaced, so the problem is symmetric with respect to rotation. Therefore, we can fix one snake's position and consider the relative positions of the others. Since the circle is symmetric, the expected time should not depend on the absolute positions, only on the relative spacing.\n\nGiven that all snakes are identical and start at the same distance apart, perhaps we can model the problem as a Markov chain where the state is the set of gaps between consecutive snakes. Initially, all gaps are 3. When two snakes merge, the gaps adjacent to the merged snake will be affected. However, since the circle is symmetric, the state can be represented by the multiset of gaps between snakes, sorted in some order.\n\nIn such a model, the process would involve the gaps changing as snakes move and merge. The merging reduces the number of gaps? Wait, actually, when two snakes merge, they form a single snake, which effectively reduces the number of snakes by one, but also affects the gaps.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps think of the circle as being partitioned into intervals between snakes, and each interval has a certain length (number of arcs between two snakes). When a snake moves, it can either decrease or increase the length of the gaps it's adjacent to, depending on direction. When two snakes merge, they combine two adjacent intervals into a single interval? Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, since all movements are random, perhaps the expected time until all snakes have merged can be related to the expected time for a single snake to traverse the entire circle? But that seems different.\n\nWait, another thought: in such a coalescing random walk on a circle, the expected time until all particles have merged might be related to the number of particles and the size of the circle. Maybe it's similar to the \"coupon collector\" problem but in reverse?\n\nAlternatively, consider that each snake performs a random walk on the circle, and the merging happens when two walks meet. The process ends when all walks have merged into