ecreases by 1 each time with some constant probability, which is likely not the case.\n\nAlternatively, note that in the symmetric initial configuration, all gaps between snakes are equal (3 arcs). When a snake moves, it can either increase or decrease the gap on either side. For instance, if a snake at position x moves left, then the gap to its left increases by 1, and the gap to its right decreases by 1. Wait, actually, let me think carefully.\n\nSuppose we have two adjacent snakes, but in our initial configuration, they are not adjacent. Wait, initially, all snakes are spaced 3 apart. Let me consider the gaps between consecutive snakes. Since it's a circle with 15 snakes, there are 15 gaps, each of length 3 arcs. So each gap is 3 arcs between two snakes. Now, when a snake moves, say, to the right, it will move into the gap to its right, thereby reducing that gap by 1 and increasing the gap to its left by 1? Wait, no. Let me formalize this.\n\nImagine the circle divided into 45 arcs, with snakes at positions 0, 3, 6, ..., 42. The gap between the snake at position 0 and the next snake at position 3 is 3 arcs (positions 1, 2). Similarly, between 3 and 6 is positions 4, 5, etc. So each gap is 3 arcs long.\n\nNow, if the snake at position 0 moves right to position 1, then the gap between the previous snake (now at 1) and the next snake (still at 3) is now positions 2, so a gap of 2 arcs. Meanwhile, the gap to the left of snake 0 (which was at position 44) now has an additional arc: the snake at 0 moved into the gap between 44 and 0, so the gap between 44 and 0's new position (1) is now from 44 to 1, which is 2 arcs (positions 44 and 0, but since it's a circle, maybe need to think differently).\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to model the gaps between consecutive snakes. Let me label the snakes S_0, S_1, ..., S_{14}, arranged clockwise around the circle, each initially separated by 3 arcs. The gap between S_i and S_{i+1} (with S_{15}=S_0) is 3 arcs. Each snake can move left or right, which affects the gaps adjacent to it.\n\nSuppose snake S_i moves right. Then, it will enter the gap to its right (the gap between S_i and S_{i+1}), reducing that gap by 1, and the gap to its left (between S_{i-1} and S_i) will increase by 1. Similarly, if snake S_i moves left, it will enter the gap to its left (between S_{i-1} and S_i), reducing that gap by 1, and increasing the gap to its right by 1.\n\nTherefore, each snake's movement affects the two gaps adjacent to it: decreasing one and increasing the other by 1.\n\nTherefore, the gaps between snakes evolve according to the movement of the snakes. Importantly, the total sum of all gaps remains constant, since each move just redistributes the gap lengths.\n\nInitially, each of the 15 gaps is 3, so total gap length is 15*3 = 45, which matches the total number of arcs (since each arc is part of exactly one gap). So the total gap length is conserved.\n\nNow, the process ends when there is only one snake left, which would mean that all gaps have been merged into a single gap of length 45? Wait, no. If there's only one snake, then there are no gaps, because all snakes have merged into one. Wait, actually, with one snake, there are no gaps between snakes, since there's only one snake. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, when two snakes merge, they form a snake of length 2, which occupies two adjacent arcs. Then, the gaps adjacent to that merged snake would be adjusted accordingly. Wait, this is getting more complicated because the gaps are defined between snakes, so if two snakes merge into one snake, then the number of gaps reduces by 1.\n\nWait, perhaps the key idea is to model the system in terms of the gaps between snakes, where each gap is an integer length (number of arcs between two snakes). The process is that in each time step, each snake independently decides to move left or right, which affects the two gaps adjacent to it: decreasing one gap by 1 and increasing the other by 1. When two gaps become zero (or