y are on adjacent arcs, they are not touching? Hmm, but in common language, if two objects are on adjacent positions, they might be considered adjacent, but not necessarily \"touching\".\n\nWait, actually, the problem says \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\". So, if two snakes are on adjacent arcs, then when they merge, the merged snake occupies both arcs? So, maybe \"touching\" means being on adjacent arcs? So, if two snakes are on arcs that are next to each other (i.e., separated by one arc), then they are considered touching and can merge? But then merging would result in a snake occupying both arcs?\n\nAlternatively, if two snakes are on the same arc, then they are on top of each other, so they would be touching as well? Hmm. The problem says \"each of length 1\", so they can't occupy the same arc because they are each length 1, so if two snakes are on the same arc, they would overlap? But the problem doesn't specify what happens if two snakes are on the same arc. It just says \"if two snakes ever touch, they merge\". Hmm. Maybe \"touch\" is defined as being on adjacent arcs? Because if they are on the same arc, that would be a collision, but the problem doesn't mention collision, only \"touching\".\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"touching\" is when they are on the same arc? But then the problem says \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\". If they were on the same arc, then merging would just be occupying that one arc? But that doesn't make sense. Wait, the wording is a bit ambiguous here.\n\nWait, the problem says: \"If two snakes ever touch, they merge to form a single snake occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\". So, \"the arcs of both of the previous snakes\"—so, if two snakes were on arcs A and B, and they touch, then the merged snake occupies both arcs A and B. Therefore, that suggests that when two snakes touch, they must have been on adjacent arcs? Because if they were on the same arc, then \"both\" of the previous