h snake (regardless of length) chooses to move left or right with probability 1/2 each.\n\n   b. When moving, the entire snake shifts its block of arcs in the chosen direction by one arc. So a snake of length L moving left will have its leftmost arc move to the previous arc, and the rightmost arc will shift left by one, wrapping around if necessary. Similarly for moving right.\n\n   c. After all snakes have moved, check for any overlaps between snakes. If two or more snakes occupy the same arc (i.e., their blocks overlap on at least one arc), then they merge into a single snake that occupies the union of all arcs covered by the overlapping snakes. The merged snake's length is the sum of the lengths of the merged snakes. The merged snake then moves as a single entity in the next step.\n\nWait, but in this case, if two snakes of length 1 move towards each other, say one moves right and the other moves left, then their blocks would swap places but not overlap, so they wouldn't merge. But if they move in the same direction, then they might end up adjacent or overlapping?\n\nWait, actually, if two length-1 snakes are on adjacent arcs, say arc i and arc i+1. If both move to the right, then the snake on i moves to i+1, and the snake on i+1 moves to i+2. Then, after moving, they are on arcs i+1 and i+2, so they are now separated by one arc. If they both move left, then the snake on i moves to i-1, and the snake on i+1 moves to i, so they would both be on arc i? Wait, no: the snake on i moving left goes to i-1, and the snake on i+1 moving left goes to i. So after moving, the snake from i is on i-1, and the snake from i+1 is on i. So they are now on adjacent arcs again, just swapped.\n\nBut if the snake on i moves right to i+1, and the snake on i+1 moves left to i, then after moving, the snake from i is on i+1, and the snake from i+1 is on i, so again they are on adjacent arcs but swapped.\n\nHowever, if two snakes are on the same arc initially? Well, they can't be initially because they start on every third arc, so they are all separated by at least two arcs. But once merging occurs, they can be on the same arc.\n\nWait, perhaps if two snakes move into the same arc, then they merge. For example, suppose two snakes are on arcs i and i+2. If the snake on i moves right to i+1, and the snake on i+2 moves left to i+1, then both end up on arc i+1, so they merge into a single snake of length 2 on arc i+1? But the problem says \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\"—so if they were on arcs i and i+2, then merging would result in a snake on arcs i, i+1, i+2? That seems like a stretch.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps when two snakes move into the same arc, they merge into a single snake on that arc, but their previous positions are forgotten? So the merged snake is just on the target arc, so length 1? But then why mention \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\"?\n\nWait, the problem says: \"If two snakes ever touch, they merge to form a single snake occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\".\n\nSo \"touching\" must mean that the two snakes were adjacent before merging? Or that their movement caused them to come into contact?\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"touching\" just means occupying the same arc after movement. So if two snakes move to the same arc, then they are considered to have touched, and thus merge into a single snake that occupies that arc (so length 1?), but the problem says \"the arcs of both of the previous snakes\"—which were the arcs they were on before moving?\n\nWait, this is really ambiguous. Maybe we need to look for another way.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\" means that the merged snake now occupies the union of the arcs that the two snakes were on before they merged. So if two snakes were on arcs A and B, then after merging, the new snake is on arcs A and B, regardless of where they moved to? But that doesn't make sense because they moved during that time step.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the merging happens before the movement? No, the problem says \"every second, each snake independently moves... If two snakes ever touch, they merge...\". So the movement happens first, then the merging.\n\nWait, perhaps the problem is similar to the following: each snake is a particle on a circular track with 45 positions. Each particle moves left or right with 50% chance. When two particles end up on the same position, they annihilate and form a single particle at that position? But the problem says \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\", so maybe they form a particle that occupies both previous positions? Which would be two positions? Hmm, but annihilation usually means they disappear.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps when two snakes collide (move to the same position), they merge into a single snake that occupies both previous positions? So like, if two snakes were at positions A and B, and they move to position C, then the merged snake is at position C but also remembers positions A and B? That seems odd.\n\nAlternatively, maybe when two snakes collide (move to the same position), the merged snake occupies the position they collided at, and the length of the snake is increased by 1? So each snake is a point particle, but when two meet, they become a snake of length 2? Then moving as a snake of length 2 would require moving both ends? But the problem says \"the merged snake moves as one snake\".\n\nThis is really ambiguous. Maybe I need to make an assumption and see if it leads to a solvable problem, or if there's a standard way these problems are interpreted.\n\nIn many cellular automaton or particle collision problems on a circle, when two particles collide, they might annihilate or merge. In this case, since the problem says they merge into a single snake occupying both previous arcs, I think the most plausible interpretation is:\n\n- When two snakes move to the same arc, they merge into a single snake that occupies that single arc (so length 1), but the problem says \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\", so maybe the merged snake is considered to occupy both arcs that the two original snakes were on before moving? But that seems odd because they moved to a new position.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps \"touching\" means being on adjacent arcs, and when two snakes are on adjacent arcs, they merge into a snake of length 2 occupying both arcs. Then, in the next step, that snake can move as a single entity. In that case, the merging occurs whenever two snakes are adjacent, and they merge into a length-2 snake, which then can move left or right as a block.\n\nBut the problem says \"if two snakes ever touch, they merge...\", which might mean that the touching (being adjacent) causes them to merge at the next time step? But how does that happen?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the merging occurs when during their movement, their paths would cause them to be adjacent? Hmm, this is too vague.\n\nGiven that the problem is from a competition or a problem-solving context, perhaps there is a standard way to interpret this. Maybe we can think of each snake as a point that moves left or right, and when two points end up on the same spot, they merge into a single point, but the problem says \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\", so perhaps they become a length-2 snake. However, in that case, the movement of the merged snake would have to be defined.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps all snakes are considered as points, and when two points collide, they merge into a single point, but the problem mentions \"occupying the arcs of both\", so maybe it's a misstatement, and they just merge into one point.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps \"snake\" here is just a term for a particle, and \"length\" is irrelevant except that initially they are length 1, but when merged, they just become a single particle. The mention of \"length\" might be a red herring, or perhaps it's indicating that when two snakes merge, the new snake has a length equal to the sum of the lengths of the two snakes. Since initially, each snake is length 1, merging two would give length 2, etc. So in that case, the snake's length is the number of original snakes that have merged into it.\n\nIf that is the case, then when two snakes of length 1 merge, they form a snake of length 2. Then, when moving, the entire snake (of length 2) must move as a single entity. So, for example, if a snake of length 2 is on arcs i and i+1, then moving left would take it to arcs i-1 and i, and moving right would take it to arcs i+1 and i+2. So the entire block shifts left or right by one.\n\nIn this case, the movement of a snake of length L would shift its entire block of L consecutive arcs by one position left or right. So the snake's position is determined by the starting arc of its block. For example, a snake of length 2 starting at arc i occupies arcs i and i+1 (mod 45). Moving left would make it start at i-1, so occupying arcs i-1 and i. Moving right would make it start at i+1, occupying arcs i+1 and i+2.\n\nIn this case, the key is that snakes are blocks of arcs, and when they move, the entire block shifts. Merging occurs when two blocks overlap after moving. When two blocks overlap, they merge into a single block covering the union of their arcs. The merged block then has a length equal to the sum of the lengths of the two blocks, and so on.\n\nThis seems like a plausible interpretation, and it's similar to some models of traffic on a ring or particles merging into larger clusters.\n\nGiven that, let's proceed with this interpretation:\n\n- Each snake is a contiguous block of arcs of some length.\n\n- Initially, there are 15 snakes, each of length 1, positioned at arcs 0, 3, 6, ..., 42.\n\n- At each time step:\n\n   1. Each snake independently chooses to move left or right with probability 1/2 each.\n\n   2. When a snake of length L moves left, its block shifts left by 1, so the starting arc decreases by 1 (mod 45), and the block spans L arcs.\n\n   3. Similarly, moving right shifts the starting arc up by 1 (mod 45).\n\n   4. After all snakes have moved, check for overlapping blocks. If two or more blocks overlap (i.e., share at least one common arc), they merge into a single block that covers the union of all overlapping arcs. The merged block's length is the sum of the lengths of the merged snakes. This merging process is repeated until no overlapping blocks remain.\n\n   5. The remaining non-overlapping blocks are the new configuration for the next time step.\n\nWe need to compute the expected number of time steps until only one snake remains.\n\nThis seems like a reasonable interpretation, and it allows for a more structured analysis. The key is that snakes are blocks that can merge when their blocks overlap after moving.\n\nGiven that, let's see if we can model this process.\n\nFirst, note that all snakes start on positions that are every third arc: 0, 3, 6, ..., 42. Since the circumference is 45, these are spaced 3 apart. Since 45 divided by 15 is 3, so each snake is 3 arcs apart from the next.\n\nGiven that they are spaced 3 apart, initially, the distance between any two snakes is 3 arcs. Since each snake is length 1, the distance between the end of one snake and the start of the next is 3 - 1 - 1 = 1 arc? Wait, no. Wait, each snake is a single arc. The next snake is 3 arcs away. So between snake 1 at arc 0 and snake 2 at arc 3, there are arcs 1 and 2 in between. So the distance between them is 3 arcs (from 0 to 3). Since each snake is length 1, the separation between the end of one snake and the start of the next is 3 - 1 = 2 arcs? Wait, maybe I need to think in terms of positions.\n\nLet me label the arcs from 0 to 44 (since 45 arcs). Each snake is at a single arc initially: 0, 3, 6, ..., 42. The distance between consecutive snakes is 3 arcs. Since the circle is closed, the distance from 42 back to 0 is 3 arcs as well (since 45 - 42 = 3, but moving forward from 42, the next is 0, so 3 arcs).\n\nEach snake is a single arc. When they move, each snake can go left or right. The movement of each snake is independent. After moving, if two snakes end up on the same arc, they merge into a single snake occupying that arc? Or a block of two arcs?\n\nWait, under the interpretation where merging results in a block covering the union of the overlapping arcs, if two single-arc snakes move to the same arc, then they merge into a block of length 2 on that arc. Wait, but two single-arc snakes moving to the same arc would result in overlapping at that arc, so the merged snake would occupy that single arc? Or the two original arcs?\n\nWait, if two snakes are on arc A and arc B, and they move to arc C, then they both end up on arc C, so the merged snake would be on arc C? But the problem says \"occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes\". The previous snakes were on arcs A and B, so maybe the merged snake is on arcs A and B? But they moved to C. Hmm, conflicting.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps when two snakes move to the same arc, they merge into a snake that is on that arc, and the length is 2? So the merged snake has length 2, occupying the single arc? That doesn't make sense because a length 2 snake should occupy two arcs. Maybe the problem's wording is just saying that the merged snake now occupies both arcs that the two previous snakes were on, regardless of where they moved? That seems inconsistent with the movement.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps \"touching\" means being adjacent, so when two snakes are on adjacent arcs, they are considered to be touching, and thus merge into a snake of length 2 occupying both arcs. In that case, merging only occurs when two snakes are on adjacent arcs. But then, how does that happen through movement?\n\nSuppose two snakes are on arcs i and i+3 (since they're initially spaced 3 apart). If the snake on i moves right twice, but in one time step it can only move once. Wait, each time step, each snake moves only one arc left or right. So in one time step, a snake can only move one step.\n\nTherefore, to go from arc i to arc i+1, it takes one step right. To get to arc i+2, it would take two steps, but in our case, we only do one step per time.\n\nTherefore, initially, the snakes are 3 apart. So the distance between any two snakes is 3 arcs. Since each snake can only move one arc per second, in order for two snakes to become adjacent (distance 1 apart), they would have to move towards each other.\n\nFor example, take two adjacent snakes (in the initial configuration, they are not adjacent, but let's consider two snakes that are 3 apart). Let's label them Snake A at position 0 and Snake B at position 3. The distance between them is 3 arcs. To become adjacent, Snake A would have to move right towards Snake B, and Snake B would have to move left towards Snake A. Each has a 1/2 chance to move left or right.\n\nThe probability that Snake A moves right and Snake B moves left is (1/2)*(1/2) = 1/4. In that case, Snake A would move to position 1, and Snake B would move to position 2. Now, the distance between them is 2 - 1 = 1 arc? Wait, no: Snake A is at 1, Snake B is at 2. So they are now adjacent! So after this movement, they are on adjacent arcs, so they have \"touched\" and thus should merge?\n\nWait, under the interpretation that \"touching\" means being on adjacent arcs, then yes, they would merge. So in this case, after moving, they are on adjacent arcs, so they merge into a snake of length 2 occupying arcs 1 and 2.\n\nAlternatively, if they don't move towards each other, say both move away, then Snake A moves left to 44 (since it's a circle) and Snake B moves right to 4, then they are further apart.\n\nAlternatively, if Snake A moves right to 1, and Snake B stays (doesn't move), then Snake B remains at 3, so distance is 2. Etc.\n\nTherefore, in this case, if two snakes are initially 3 apart, there is a 1/4 chance per time step that they move towards each other and become adjacent, triggering a merge. If they do merge, then the merged snake is a length-2 snake occupying arcs 1 and 2 (in this example). Then, in the next time step, that merged snake can move left or right as a block.\n\nThis seems like a feasible interpretation. So let me formalize the rules again with this in mind:\n\n- Each snake is a block of arcs of length L (initially 1).\n\n- Movement: At each time step, each snake chooses independently to move left or right with probability 1/2. When moving, the entire block shifts left or right by one arc. For a block of length L starting at position S, moving left takes it to start at S-1 (mod 45), covering arcs S-1, S, ..., S-1 + L -1. Moving right takes it to start at S+1 (mod 45), covering arcs S+1, S+2, ..., S+L.\n\n- After all movements, check for overlapping blocks. If two blocks overlap (i.e., their ranges on the circle overlap), they merge into a single block that covers the union of their ranges. The length of the merged block is the sum of the lengths of the original blocks. This merging is done for all overlapping pairs until no overlaps remain.\n\n- The process repeats until only one snake remains.\n\nWith this interpretation, we can model the system as a collection of blocks on a circle, merging when their blocks overlap after movement, and we need to compute the expected time until only one block remains.\n\nGiven that the initial configuration has 15 blocks (snakes), each of length 1, spaced 3 apart, we can perhaps model the system using some sort of Markov chain or recursive expectation.\n\nHowever, given the complexity of the merging process on a circle with 45 positions, this might be quite involved. Maybe there's some symmetry or simplification we can exploit.\n\nFirst, note that the circle has 45 positions, and the initial configuration is highly symmetric: the snakes are equally spaced every 3 positions. So perhaps we can model the system in terms of the distances between the snakes, or the gaps between the snakes, and analyze how these gaps evolve over time.\n\nIn many such merging processes on a circle, it's useful to consider the gaps between the blocks. In this case, initially, between each pair of adjacent snakes (considering the circle), there are 2 arcs: since the snakes are every 3 arcs, the distance between them is 3 arcs, so the number of arcs between them is 3 - 1 = 2? Wait, let's clarify.\n\nIf two snakes are on arcs i and j, with j > i, the distance between them along the circle in the clockwise direction is (j - i) mod 45. Since they're initially spaced 3 apart, the clockwise distance from a snake to the next is 3, so the number of arcs between them (not including either snake's position) is 3 - 1 = 2? Hmm.\n\nActually, if two snakes are on arcs separated by k arcs, meaning there are k arcs between them, then the distance between their blocks is k arcs. Since each snake is a single arc, the gap between them is the number of unoccupied arcs between them.\n\nIn the initial configuration, each snake is followed by two unoccupied arcs before the next snake. For example, snake at 0, then arcs 1 and 2 are empty, then snake at 3, etc. So the gap between each snake and the next is 2 arcs. Since the total circumference is 45, and there are 15 snakes, each occupying 1 arc, the total number of occupied arcs is 15, so the number of unoccupied arcs is 30, distributed as 2 gaps between each pair of consecutive snakes.\n\nTherefore, the initial configuration has 15 gaps, each of length 2 (arcs). Since it's a circle, the gaps are all identical.\n\nNow, when snakes move, they can either move towards each other or away from each other, potentially reducing or increasing the gaps between them. When two snakes move towards each other, they might merge, which would effectively eliminate that gap and combine the two gaps on either side? Wait, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model the system in terms of the gaps between the snakes. Since all initial gaps are the same, and the system is symmetric, maybe we can consider the expected time until all gaps are eliminated, i.e., until all snakes have merged into one.\n\nIn such gap models, often the key is to look at the expected time until all gaps are closed. However, in this case, the merging process is not just about closing gaps but also about the dynamics of the snakes moving and potentially creating new gaps or merging gaps.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think of the problem in terms of the number of snakes remaining. Let me denote E(n) as the expected time to reach 1 snake from n snakes. We need to find E(15). If we can find a recurrence relation for E(n), then we can compute it recursively.\n\nHowever, the challenge is that the expected time E(n) likely depends on the specific configuration of the snakes, not just the number n. Because the configuration affects the probabilities of merges happening. For example, if all snakes are bunched closely together, they might merge faster than if they're spread out. However, in our initial configuration, all snakes are equally spaced, so maybe due to symmetry, the expected time only depends on the number of snakes and not their exact arrangement? Maybe.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps because of the high symmetry in the initial configuration, the expected time can be computed by considering the system as a collection of independent gaps, each undergoing some process, and the total time is the maximum of the times for each gap to be closed? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model the problem as a Markov chain where the state is the number of snakes, and we can find the transition probabilities between states. However, the transitions would depend on the current configuration, so unless we can show that from any state with n snakes, the expected time to reach n-1 is the same regardless of the configuration, which might be possible due to symmetry.\n\nWait, in the initial configuration, all the gaps are identical and the snakes are symmetrically placed. If we can show that from any state with n snakes arranged symmetrically (i.e., equally spaced), the expected time to reach n-1 is the same as from any other symmetric configuration with n snakes, then perhaps we can define E(n) as the expected time from n equally spaced snakes to 1 snake.\n\nThis might be a feasible approach. Let me consider that.\n\nSuppose that at any time, the snakes are equally spaced around the circle. Then, the system is symmetric, so the expected time to reach the next state (with n-1 snakes) is the same for all such symmetric states. Therefore, we can define E(n) as the expected time from a symmetric state with n snakes to the absorbing state of 1 snake.\n\nOur goal is to compute E(15).\n\nNow, to find a recurrence relation for E(n). Let's consider going from n snakes to n-1 snakes. To do this, we need at least one pair of snakes to merge in a time step. The probability of at least one merge happening in a time step depends on the current configuration.\n\nBut in the symmetric configuration with n snakes equally spaced, the distance between adjacent snakes is D = (45 / n) arcs? Wait, no. Wait, the circumference is 45 arcs, and there are n snakes, each occupying 1 arc. The number of arcs between adjacent snakes (the gaps) would be (45 - n)/n? Wait, let's think carefully.\n\nTotal number of arcs: 45.\n\nNumber of occupied arcs by snakes: n.\n\nTherefore, the number of unoccupied arcs is 45 - n.\n\nThese unoccupied arcs are distributed as gaps between the snakes. Since the snakes are equally spaced, each gap between two adjacent snakes has the same number of unoccupied arcs.\n\nTherefore, each gap has length g = floor((45 - n)/n) or something? Wait, actually, if there are n gaps (since it's a circle), each gap must have the same number of unoccupied arcs. Therefore:\n\nLet g be the number of unoccupied arcs between two adjacent snakes. Then:\n\nn * g = 45 - n\n\nTherefore, g = (45 - n)/n\n\nBut since g must be an integer, this requires that (45 - n) is divisible by n. However, in our problem, initially n = 15, so g = (45 - 15)/15 = 30/15 = 2, which is integer. So that works. For other n, it may not be integer, but perhaps in the symmetric configurations we consider, we can have gaps that are as equal as possible.\n\nHowever, if n does not divide 45 - n, then we can't have all gaps equal. Hmm. Therefore, perhaps the symmetric configurations we consider are those where the gaps are as equal as possible, differing by at most 1.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can ignore the integrality issue for now and proceed formally, assuming that the gaps can be fractional, but in reality, they must be integers. This might complicate things.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps instead of focusing on gaps, we can think in terms of the distance between snakes. Let me denote that in the symmetric configuration with n snakes, the clockwise distance between two adjacent snakes is d arcs. Since the total circumference is 45, the distance between adjacent snakes is d = 45 / n arcs. Wait, but since each snake occupies 1 arc, the actual distance between the start of one snake and the start of the next is d arcs. Therefore, the number of unoccupied arcs between them is d - 1, because the next snake starts d arcs away, so between them are d - 1 arcs.\n\nWait, for example, with n=15, d = 45 / 15 = 3. So the distance between starts is 3 arcs, meaning there are 3 - 1 = 2 unoccupied arcs between them, which matches our initial condition.\n\nSo in general, for n snakes equally spaced, the distance between adjacent snakes (in terms of arc starts) is d = 45 / n arcs. Therefore, the number of unoccupied arcs between them is d - 1 = (45/n) - 1.\n\nTherefore, in the symmetric configuration with n snakes, each gap has length g = floor((45/n) - 1) or something? Wait no, actually:\n\nWait, if the distance between starts is d arcs, then the number of arcs between the end of one snake and the start of the next is d - 1. Since each snake occupies 1 arc, the next snake starts d arcs away from the current one. Therefore, between the two snakes, there are d - 1 unoccupied arcs.\n\nTherefore, the gap length (number of unoccupied arcs between two snakes) is g = d - 1 = (45/n) - 1.\n\nBut this is only exact if 45 is divisible by n. For example, when n=15, 45/15=3, so g=2, which is correct.\n\nIf n=10, then d=4.5, which is not integer, so this complicates things. However, perhaps in our problem, since we start with n=15 and merge snakes, the number of snakes will decrease, and we can only consider n that divide 45? Because 45 is the circumference.\n\nWait, 45 factors are 1, 3, 5, 9, 15, 45. So the possible numbers of equally spaced snakes would have to divide 45. Since we start at 15, which divides 45, and each time we merge two snakes, the number reduces by 1, but not necessarily to a divisor. Hmm, so maybe this approach is limited.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps instead of requiring the gaps to be equal, we can model the process in terms of the expected time until all snakes have been merged, considering that each pair of adjacent snakes has some probability of merging each time step, and the process is memoryless in some way.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider that in the symmetric configuration with n snakes, each pair of adjacent snakes has the same probability of merging in a given time step, and the merging of one pair doesn't affect the others due to symmetry. Then, we can approximate the expected time as similar to a birth-death process or a coupon collector problem, but it's not straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, let's consider the expected time until the first merge occurs, starting from n snakes. Then, after the first merge, we have n-1 snakes, and so on, until we reach 1.\n\nHowever, the problem is that after a merge, the configuration is no longer symmetric, so the expected time from n-1 might depend on the specific configuration, which complicates things.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps due to the high symmetry and the fact that all gaps are identical and independent in some sense, we can model the system as having n independent gaps, each undergoing a certain process, and the total time until all gaps are closed is the maximum of the individual gap times? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, let's try to compute the expected time for the first merge, then see if we can find a recurrence.\n\nStarting with n=15 snakes equally spaced, each adjacent pair of snakes is separated by 3 arcs (distance 3, so gap length 2 unoccupied arcs). Let me consider two adjacent snakes, say Snake A at position 0 and Snake B at position 3. The gap between them has 2 unoccupied arcs: arcs 1 and 2.\n\nIn order for these two snakes to merge, they need to move towards each other such that their paths cross or they end up adjacent.\n\nEach snake can move left or right with probability 1/2. Let's analyze the possible movements of Snake A and Snake B.\n\nSnake A is at position 0. Its possible moves:\n\n- Move left: since it's on a circle, moving left from 0 would take it to position 44.\n\n- Move right: to position 1.\n\nSnake B is at position 3. Its possible moves:\n\n- Move left: to position 2.\n\n- Move right: to position 4.\n\nWe want to find the probability that after moving, Snake A and Snake B are on adjacent arcs or overlapping, so that they can merge.\n\nLet me tabulate the possible movements:\n\nCase 1: Snake A moves left to 44, Snake B moves left to 2.\n\nThen Snake A is at 44, Snake B is at 2. The distance between them is (2 - 44) mod 45 = (2 - 44 + 45) = 3. So they are still 3 apart. No merging.\n\nCase 2: Snake A moves left to 44, Snake B moves right to 4.\n\nSnake A at 44, Snake B at 4. Distance: (4 - 44 + 45) = 5. So they are 5 apart. No merging.\n\nCase 3: Snake A moves right to 1, Snake B moves left to 2.\n\nSnake A at 1, Snake B at 2. Now, they are adjacent! So they are on arcs 1 and 2, which are adjacent. Therefore, they have \"touched\" and should merge into a single snake of length 2 occupying arcs 1 and 2.\n\nCase 4: Snake A moves right to 1, Snake B moves right to 4.\n\nSnake A at 1, Snake B at 4. Distance: 4 - 1 = 3. So back to original distance. No merging.\n\nSo out of the four possible movement combinations, only Case 3 results in the two snakes merging. The probability of Case 3 is (1/2)*(1/2) = 1/4.\n\nTherefore, the probability that the specific pair of adjacent snakes (Snake A and Snake B) merge in a given time step is 1/4.\n\nHowever, there are 15 such adjacent pairs in the initial configuration (since it's a circle with 15 snakes). However, these events are not independent, because if one pair merges, it affects the configuration of the neighboring pairs.\n\nBut perhaps, due to the symmetry, we can approximate the expected time until the first merge as follows:\n\nLet T be the expected time until the first merge. Then, at each time step, the probability that a specific pair merges is 1/4, and there are 15 pairs. However, the events for different pairs are not independent, so the probability that at least one pair merges is approximately 15*(1/4) - ... (using inclusion-exclusion), but this might get complicated.\n\nAlternatively, if the probability of at least one merge in a time step is small, we might approximate it as a Poisson process, but I'm not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the expected time until the first merge is approximately the reciprocal of the probability that a specific pair merges in a time step, assuming that the events are roughly independent. But that would be 1/(15/4) = 4/15, which is about 0.266 seconds. But this is probably not accurate because the events are not independent and the probability isn't that low.\n\nAlternatively, we can model the first passage time for the system.\n\nLet me consider that in the initial state with 15 snakes, the probability that a particular adjacent pair merges in a given time step is p = 1/4, as calculated above. The number of such pairs is N = 15. However, the merging of one pair affects the probabilities of the neighboring pairs.\n\nBut perhaps, due to the symmetry, we can think of the system as having 15 independent \"attempters\" each trying to merge with their neighbor, each with probability p=1/4 per time step, and the first time any of them succeeds is the time until the first merge. In this case, the expected time until the first success in a binomial process with N independent trials each with success probability p per trial is 1/p, but only if the trials are independent. However, in reality, the trials are not independent because the merging of one pair affects the configuration of neighboring pairs.\n\nHowever, if the number of snakes is large compared to the number of pairs, or if the probability is low, the dependence might be negligible, and we can approximate it as independent. For our case, with N=15 and p=1/4, the expected time might be approximated by 1/p, but adjusted for dependence.\n\nAlternatively, the exact expectation can be calculated using the formula for the expectation of the minimum of dependent geometric random variables. But this is complicated.\n\nAlternatively, consider that in each time step, the probability that at least one merge occurs is q = 1 - (1 - p)^N, where p is the probability that a specific pair merges, and N is the number of pairs. However, this assumes independence between the pairs, which is not true. But if the dependencies are weak, this might be a decent approximation.\n\nSo with p = 1/4 and N = 15,\n\nq ≈ 1 - (1 - 1/4)^15 ≈ 1 - (3/4)^15 ≈ 1 - 0.0439 ≈ 0.9561\n\nTherefore, the expected time until the first merge would be approximately 1/q ≈ 1 / 0.9561 ≈ 1.046 seconds.\n\nBut this is just an approximation. However, even if we accept this approximation, then after the first merge, we would have 14 snakes, but now the configuration is no longer symmetric. The merged snake is now a length-2 snake occupying two adjacent arcs, and the rest are still single snakes, but the spacing around the merged area is disrupted.\n\nThis complicates things because the next state after the first merge is asymmetric, so the expected time from there would not be the same as the expected time from a symmetric configuration with 14 snakes.\n\nTherefore, this approach might not be feasible.\n\nPerhaps we need to think of the problem differently. Let's consider that each snake is a point particle moving left or right, and when two particles meet, they annihilate into a single particle (ignoring the length aspect for a moment). In that case, the problem reduces to the classic \"meeting problem\" on a circle, where particles move randomly and annihilate upon collision. The expected time until all particles have annihilated into one is a known problem? Maybe not exactly, but similar.\n\nHowever, in our case, when two snakes merge, they form a block of length 2, which then behaves differently. So it's not just a simple annihilation; the merged snake can move as a block, which might have different dynamics.\n\nAlternatively, if we think in terms of the number of snakes decreasing by one each time a merge occurs, then the total expected time would be the sum over k from 1 to 14 of the expected time between the (k-1)-snake state and the k-slice state.\n\nBut to compute this, we need the expected time between each state, which requires knowing the transition probabilities between states, which depends on the configuration.\n\nGiven the complexity, perhaps there is a clever way to model this as a Markov chain with states representing the number of snakes, and compute the expected time using some symmetry or by setting up equations.\n\nLet me try to define E(n) as the expected time to reach 1 snake from n snakes in a symmetric configuration. We need to find E(15).\n\nTo find E(n), we can write a recurrence relation. The key is to find the expected time to go from n to n-1, then E(n) = 1 + E(n-1). Wait, but that would only be true if the expected time to go from n to n-1 is 1, which is not the case.\n\nActually, the expected time E(n) should satisfy:\n\nE(n) = 1 + [probability that no merge occurs] * E(n) + [probability that at least one merge occurs] * E(n-1)\n\nWait, that is, with probability q (the probability that at least one merge occurs in a time step), we reduce the number of snakes by at least 1 (actually, by some amount, possibly more if multiple merges happen), and with probability 1 - q, we stay at n snakes. Therefore:\n\nE(n) = 1 + (1 - q_n) * E(n) + q_n * E(n-1)\n\nWhere q_n is the probability that at least one merge occurs in a time step when there are n snakes.\n\nSolving for E(n):\n\nE(n) - (1 - q_n) E(n) = 1 + q_n E(n-1)\n\nE(n) * q_n = 1 + q_n E(n-1)\n\nE(n) = [1 + q_n E(n-1)] / q_n\n\n= 1/q_n + E(n-1)\n\nTherefore, we have the recurrence:\n\nE(n) = E(n-1) + 1/q_n\n\nWhere q_n is the probability that at least one merge occurs in a time step with n snakes in symmetric configuration.\n\nThis is a key insight! If we can compute q_n for each n, then we can compute E(n) recursively.\n\nHowever, this assumes that when a merge occurs, it reduces the number of snakes by exactly 1, which may not be the case. For example, if two pairs of snakes merge simultaneously, then the number of snakes could decrease by 2 or more. However, in the symmetric configuration with n snakes, the probability of multiple merges happening in the same time step might be low, especially for larger n, but we need to account for it in q_n.\n\nAlternatively, if we assume that in the symmetric configuration, the probability of more than one merge happening in a time step is negligible, then we can approximate q_n as the probability of at least one merge, and the expected decrease is approximately 1, so the above recurrence holds. This might be a reasonable approximation, especially for larger n, but for small n it might not be accurate.\n\nAlternatively, if we can compute q_n exactly, including the probability of multiple merges, then the recurrence would need to account for the expected number of merges, which complicates things.\n\nBut let's proceed with the approximation that q_n is the probability of at least one merge, and that merges are independent enough that the expected number of merges is roughly q_n * n / something, but perhaps for the sake of progress, let's see if we can compute q_n for small n and see if a pattern emerges.\n\nFirst, we need to compute q_n for the symmetric configuration with n snakes.\n\nLet's start with n = 15, the initial state.\n\nAs we computed earlier, for n = 15, each adjacent pair of snakes has a probability p = 1/4 of merging in a time step, and there are N = 15 such pairs. Assuming independence (which is not exact but an approximation), the probability that at least one merge occurs is:\n\nq_15 ≈ 1 - (1 - p)^N = 1 - (3/4)^15 ≈ 1 - 0.0439 ≈ 0.9561\n\nSimilarly, for n = 14, after one merge has occurred, the configuration is no longer symmetric, so we cannot directly compute q_14 in this way. Therefore, this approach only works if we can assume that after a merge, the system returns to a symmetric configuration with n-1 snakes, which is not true because merging disrupts the symmetry.\n\nHmm, so this approach is flawed because the state after a merge is asymmetric, so E(n) cannot be expressed solely in terms of E(n-1).\n\nThis suggests that we need a different approach.\n\nPerhaps we can model the entire system as a Markov chain where the state is the number of snakes and the gaps between them, but this would be too complex given the large state space.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a clever observation or transformation that can simplify the problem.\n\nLet me think differently. Since all snakes start equally spaced and the circle is symmetric, perhaps the expected time until all snakes have been merged can be related to the expected time for each individual snake to \"meet\" all others, but I'm not sure.\n\nAlternatively, consider that each snake is performing a random walk on the circle, and two snakes will merge when their paths cross. The expected time until all snakes have merged might be related to the meeting times of all pairs.\n\nHowever, in our case, the merging is not just when two snakes meet, but also depends on their movement directions. For two snakes to merge, they need to move towards each other such that their paths bring them adjacent or onto the same arc.\n\nAlternatively, since each snake moves every second with 50% chance left or right, their movement is a symmetric random walk on the circle. The meeting time of two such walkers on a circle is a known problem.\n\nIn the classic problem of two random walkers on a circle, the expected time until they meet can be computed. Perhaps we can use that.\n\nSuppose we have two snakes starting at positions separated by distance d. What is the expected time until they meet?\n\nOn a circle of circumference L, two particles performing independent symmetric random walks, what is the expected time until they meet?\n\nThis is a classic problem. Let me recall.\n\nFor two independent symmetric random walks on a circle of circumference L, starting at distance d apart, the expected time until they meet is L^2 / (2d). Wait, is that right?\n\nWait, actually, for two independent simple symmetric random walks on the integers mod L, the meeting time can be calculated.\n\nLet me consider the circle as the integers mod L, with positions 0, 1, ..., L-1.\n\nLet the two particles be at positions X and Y, initially separated by distance d = |X - Y| mod L.\n\nThe expected time until they meet is known?\n\nI found a reference in my mind that for two independent symmetric random walks on a circle, the expected meeting time starting from distance d is L^2 / (2d). Let me see if that makes sense.\n\nSuppose L = 2, d = 1. Then the expected time would be (2)^2 / (2*1) = 2. Let's simulate:\n\nPositions 0 and 1. Each step, each particle moves left or right with 50% chance.\n\nPossible movements:\n\nParticle A at 0:\n\n- Move to 1 or to L-1 = 1 (since L=2, moving left from 0 goes to 1? Wait, actually on a circle of 2, moving left from 0 would go to 1, and moving right from 0 would also go to 1? Wait no:\n\nWait, on a circle of 2, positions are 0 and 1.\n\nIf you're at position 0:\n\n- Moving left: since it's a circle, left from 0 would be 1 (if we consider left as counter-clockwise).\n\n- Moving right: right from 0 would be 1 as well? Wait, no, that can't be.\n\nWait, actually, on a circle with two positions, moving left from 0 would go to 1, and moving right from 0 would also go to 1? That can't be right because left and right should be opposite directions.\n\nWait, perhaps on a circle, moving left is clockwise and moving right is counter-clockwise, but on a two-node circle, moving left from 0 would go to 1, and moving right from 0 would also go to 1? That would mean both directions lead to the same node, which is odd.\n\nActually, on a circle with two nodes, each node has two neighbors, which are the same node in the opposite direction. So moving left from 0 is to 1, and moving right from 0 is also to 1? That seems like both moves lead to the same place, which is not a standard random walk. So maybe the circle must have at least 3 nodes for this to make sense.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the formula is for a linear track, but on a circle.\n\nAlternatively, let me look for the formula.\n\nUpon recalling, for two independent random walks on a circle of circumference L, starting at distance d apart, the expected time until they meet is L^2 / (2d). Let me see if this holds for a simple case.\n\nTake L = 3, d = 1.\n\nThen expected time would be 9 / (2*1) = 4.5.\n\nLet's simulate manually.\n\nPositions 0 and 1 on a circle of 3 (positions 0,1,2).\n\nThey start at distance 1 apart.\n\nAt each step, each particle moves left or right with 50% chance.\n\nPossible movements:\n\nParticle A (at 0) can go to 2 or 1.\n\nParticle B (at 1) can go to 0 or 2.\n\nWe can tabulate all possibilities:\n\nCase 1: A moves to 2, B moves to 0.\n\nThen new positions: A at 2, B at 0. Distance between them is 1 (since clockwise from 0 to 2 is 2 steps, but the minimal distance is min(2, 1) = 1). So they are still distance 1 apart.\n\nCase 2: A moves to 2, B moves to 2.\n\nThen both at 2. They meet! So this is a meeting.\n\nCase 3: A moves to 1, B moves to 0.\n\nNew positions: A at 1, B at 0. Distance is 1 (clockwise from 0 to 1 is 1, counter-clockwise is 2).\n\nCase 4: A moves to 1, B moves to 2.\n\nNew positions: A at 1, B at 2. Distance is 1 (clockwise from 1 to 2 is 1, counter-clockwise is 2).\n\nSo in the four cases:\n\n- Case 1: distance remains 1.\n\n- Case 2: they meet.\n\n- Case 3: distance remains 1.\n\n- Case 4: distance remains 1.\n\nSo probability of meeting in this step is 1/4 (only Case 2).\n\nProbability of continuing is 3/4.\n\nLet E be the expected time to meet starting from distance 1.\n\nThen,\n\nE = 1 + (3/4) * E + (1/4)*0\n\nSolving:\n\nE - (3/4)E = 1 => (1/4)E = 1 => E = 4.\n\nBut the formula predicted L²/(2d) = 9/(2*1) = 4.5, which does not match the actual result of 4. So the formula is incorrect, or perhaps my assumption about the model is wrong.\n\nHmm, so maybe the formula isn't L²/(2d). Let me think again.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the formula is different. Let me derive it.\n\nConsider two particles on a circle of circumference L, starting at distance d apart. Let’s fix one particle and consider the position of the other relative to it. Due to symmetry, we can fix one particle and let the other perform a random walk on the circle. The meeting occurs when the relative position is 0.\n\nHowever, on a circle, the relative position is a bit tricky because it's modulo L. Let me model the relative position as a point on the circle between 0 and L, where 0 and L are the same.\n\nThe relative position starts at d, and we want the expected time until it returns to 0.\n\nBut since both particles are moving, the relative motion can be considered as the difference of their two random walks.\n\nAlternatively, consider the relative displacement. Let X_t be the position of particle 1 at time t, Y_t of particle 2. Then the relative position is (X_t - Y_t) mod L. We want the expected time until this relative position is 0.\n\nHowever, since both particles move independently, the relative displacement changes as:\n\n(X_{t+1} - Y_{t+1}) mod L = [(X_t ± 1) - (Y_t ± 1)] mod L = [ (X_t - Y_t) ± (±1) ] mod L.\n\nWait, actually, each particle moves left or right independently, so the change in relative displacement depends on their individual moves.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet ΔX be the step of particle 1: ΔX = +1 (right) or -1 (left), each with probability 1/2.\n\nSimilarly, ΔY for particle 2.\n\nThen the change in relative displacement is ΔX - ΔY.\n\nTherefore, the relative displacement at time t+1 is:\n\n(X_t - Y_t + ΔX - ΔY) mod L.\n\nTherefore, the relative displacement performs a random walk on the circle, with steps that are ΔX - ΔY.\n\nThe possible steps for the relative displacement:\n\nΔX and ΔY can each be +1 or -1, so ΔX - ΔY can be:\n\nIf both move right: 1 - 1 = 0\n\nBoth move left: -1 - (-1) = 0\n\nOne moves right and the other left: 1 - (-1) = 2 or -1 - 1 = -2\n\nWait, hold on:\n\nWait, ΔX and ΔY are each ±1.\n\nSo ΔX - ΔY can be:\n\nIf ΔX = +1, ΔY = +1: 0\n\nΔX = +1, ΔY = -1: +2\n\nΔX = -1, ΔY = +1: -2\n\nΔX = -1, ΔY = -1: 0\n\nTherefore, the step size for the relative displacement is either +2, -2, or 0, each with certain probabilities.\n\nSpecifically:\n\n- Probability of step +2: when ΔX = +1 and ΔY = -1: probability (1/2)(1/2) = 1/4.\n\n- Probability of step -2: when ΔX = -1 and ΔY = +1: probability 1/4.\n\n- Probability of step 0: otherwise, which is 1/2 + 1/2 = 1 (since the other two cases give 0).\n\nWait, actually:\n\nTotal probability:\n\nP(step +2) = 1/4,\n\nP(step -2) = 1/4,\n\nP(step 0) = 1 - 1/4 - 1/4 = 1/2.\n\nTherefore, the relative displacement performs a random walk on the circle with step sizes +2, -2, or 0, with probabilities 1/4, 1/4, and 1/2 respectively.\n\nWe start at position d (distance d apart), and we want the expected time until we reach position 0.\n\nThis is a non-trivial Markov chain on the circle. To compute the expected time to absorption at 0 from position d.\n\nLet me denote E(d) as the expected time to reach 0 from distance d.\n\nWe need to solve the system of equations for E(d) for d = 1, 2, ..., floor(L/2).\n\nIn our earlier example with L=3, d=1:\n\nThe possible positions are 0,1,2 (but since it's a circle, distance 2 is equivalent to distance 1 in the other direction). Wait, actually on a circle of circumference L, the distance between two points is the minimum of the clockwise and counter-clockwise distances. So the actual distance is in [0, L/2].\n\nTherefore, for L=3, the possible distances are 0, 1 (since distance 2 is equivalent to distance 1 in the other direction).\n\nTherefore, in the L=3 case, starting at distance 1, the relative displacement can go to:\n\nFrom position 1:\n\nWith probability 1/2, the step is 0, so stays at 1.\n\nWith probability 1/4, step +2: which would take it to position (1 + 2) mod 3 = 0. Wait, but mod 3, 1 + 2 = 3 ≡ 0 mod 3.\n\nWith probability 1/4, step -2: which would take it to (1 - 2) mod 3 = (-1) mod 3 = 2, which is equivalent to distance 1 (since 2 mod 3 is distance 2 clockwise, but the minimal distance is 1 counter-clockwise).\n\nWait a second, so stepping from distance 1 with step -2 would result in a distance of 1 again (because distance 2 is equivalent to distance 1 in the other direction).\n\nSimilarly, stepping +2 from distance 1 reaches 0.\n\nTherefore, the equations are:\n\nE(1) = 1 + (1/2) E(1) + (1/4)(0) + (1/4) E(1)\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nFrom position 1:\n\n- With probability 1/2, stay at 1: contributes (1/2) * E(1)\n\n- With probability 1/4, move to 0: contributes (1/4)*0\n\n- With probability 1/4, move to distance 1 (since stepping -2 from 1 leads to distance 1): contributes (1/4)*E(1)\n\nTherefore:\n\nE(1) = 1 + (1/2) E(1) + (1/4) E(1) + (1/4)*0\n\nSimplify:\n\nE(1) = 1 + ( (1/2) + (1/4) ) E(1) = 1 + (3/4) E(1)\n\nTherefore,\n\nE(1) - (3/4) E(1) = 1 => (1/4) E(1) = 1 => E(1) = 4, which matches our manual calculation.\n\nSo in this case, the formula gives E(d) = 4 when L=3, d=1.\n\nIf we try the earlier formula L²/(2d) = 9/(2*1)=4.5, which is incorrect. So that formula is wrong.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's another formula.\n\nLet me see if I can derive the general solution.\n\nLet me consider the general case of two random walkers on a circle of circumference L, starting at distance d (where d is between 1 and floor(L/2)), and we want E(d), the expected time until they meet.\n\nThe relative displacement performs a random walk on the circle with steps +2, -2, or 0 with probabilities 1/4, 1/4, and 1/2 respectively.\n\nHowever, due to the circle's symmetry, we can \"unfold\" the circle into a line by considering the relative displacement modulo L, but this might not help directly.\n\nAlternatively, since the walk is on a circle, we can consider it as a Markov chain on the states 0, 1, ..., floor(L/2), where state 0 is absorbing, and other states are transient.\n\nLet me denote the states as s = 0, 1, 2, ..., m, where m = floor(L/2). In our case, L=3, m=1.\n\nThe transition probabilities from state s (for s > 0):\n\nFrom state s, the possible steps are:\n\n- With probability 1/2, stay at s (step 0).\n\n- With probability 1/4, move to (s + 2) mod L, but since we're considering minimal distance, if (s + 2) > m, then it wraps around to (s + 2) - L, but since L=3 and m=1, s can only be 1.\n\nWait, in the L=3 case, from state 1:\n\n- Step +2: goes to 3 mod 3 = 0 (absorbing)\n\n- Step -2: goes to -1 mod 3 = 2, which is equivalent to distance 1 (since 2 > 1.5, so minimal distance is 1).\n\nSo in terms of states (minimal distances):\n\nFrom state s (where s ranges from 1 to m):\n\n- Step +2: if s + 2 ≤ L/2, then new state is s + 2; else, it wraps around to (s + 2) - L, but since L is the circumference, actually on the circle, the minimal distance after a step might require adjustment.\n\nWait this is getting too convoluted. Maybe better to model it as a linear chain with states 1 to m, and transitions accordingly.\n\nAlternatively, let's try to find a general solution for E(d).\n\nLet me consider the relative displacement as a Markov chain on the states 0, 1, ..., m, where 0 is absorbing, and m = floor(L/2).\n\nFor each state s (1 ≤ s ≤ m), the transitions are:\n\n- With probability 1/2, stay at s.\n\n- With probability 1/4, move to s + 2 (if s + 2 ≤ m), else to s + 2 - L (but since it's a circle, actually the distance would wrap around, so need to compute minimal distance).\n\nWait, this is tricky because adding 2 might overshoot m, requiring wrapping.\n\nAlternatively, since L is the circumference, a step of +2 in displacement corresponds to moving 2 positions clockwise, but on the minimal distance, if that exceeds L/2, it's equivalent to moving L - 2 positions counter-clockwise, hence the minimal distance would be L - (s + 2) if that's smaller.\n\nBut since we're considering minimal distances, the actual next state after a +2 step from state s is:\n\nnext_state = min(s + 2, L - (s + 2))\n\nBut since s ≤ m = floor(L/2), then s + 2 might exceed m, but L - (s + 2) could be less than s.\n\nWait, let's take L even for simplicity. Suppose L is even, say L = 2m.\n\nThen, for state s (1 ≤ s ≤ m):\n\n- A +2 step would take it to s + 2, but since L = 2m, s + 2 could be up to m + 2, which is beyond m, so the minimal distance would be (s + 2) - L = s + 2 - 2m. But since s ≤ m, s + 2 - 2m would be negative, so actually minimal distance is L - (s + 2) = 2m - s - 2.\n\nWait, for example, if L=4, m=2, s=2:\n\nA +2 step would take displacement to 4, which is 0 mod 4, so distance 0.\n\nAlternatively, for L=4, s=2:\n\nstep +2: displacement becomes 4, which is 0.\n\nstep -2: displacement becomes 0, which is also 0.\n\nWait, in this case, from state 2 (distance 2 on L=4), a step of +2 or -2 both lead to 0.\n\nHmm, so perhaps when s = m (which is L/2 when L is even), then a step of ±2 both lead to 0.\n\nSimilarly, for other states:\n\nIf L is even, L = 2m:\n\nFor state s < m:\n\n- A +2 step leads to s + 2.\n\n- A -2 step leads to s - 2 (if s > 2), but since s ≥1, if s=1, then s-2 = -1, which would correspond to distance L -1 = 2m -1, but minimal distance is min(2m -1, 1) = 1. Wait, this is getting too complicated.\n\nPerhaps it's better to treat the states as positions on the circle from 0 to L-1, but considering the relative displacement as a point on the circle, and setting up the equations for E(s) where s is the current distance (from 0 to L/2).\n\nAlternatively, let me consider the general case where the circumference is L and the initial distance is d. We can model the expected time E(d) to reach 0.\n\nFor each d from 1 to floor(L/2), we have the equation:\n\nE(d) = 1 + (1/2) E(d) + (1/4) E(d + 2 mod L) + (1/4) E(d - 2 mod L)\n\nBut since we're considering minimal distance, the mod L should be adjusted to stay within [0, L/2].\n\nAlternatively, since moving +2 from distance d might go beyond L/2, which would wrap around to L - (d + 2), but the minimal distance would be min(d + 2, L - (d + 2)).\n\nThis is quite involved. Perhaps instead of trying to solve it generally, let's see if there's a pattern from small L.\n\nWe saw for L=3, d=1, E(1)=4.\n\nLet me try L=4, d=1.\n\nCircumference 4, starting distance 1.\n\nStates are 0,1,2 (since floor(4/2)=2).\n\nWe want E(1).\n\nFrom state 1:\n\nPossible steps:\n\n- With probability 1/2, stay at 1.\n\n- With probability 1/4, move +2: distance becomes 3 mod 4, which is minimal distance 1 (since 3 > 2, minimal is 4-3=1).\n\n- With probability 1/4, move -2: distance becomes -1 mod 4, which is 3 mod 4, minimal distance 1.\n\nWait, so moving +2 or -2 from state 1 both lead to distance 1.\n\nWait, that can't be right.\n\nWait, let's be precise.\n\nIf you're at distance 1 on a circle of 4:\n\n- A +2 step in displacement: the relative position moves +2, so from position 1, moving +2 lands at 3. The minimal distance between 3 and 0 is min(3, 1) = 1.\n\n- A -2 step in displacement: moving -2 from position 1 lands at -1, which is equivalent to 3 mod 4, minimal distance 1.\n\nTherefore, both +2 and -2 steps from state 1 lead back to state 1.\n\nTherefore, from state 1:\n\nE(1) = 1 + (1/2) E(1) + (1/4) E(1) + (1/4) E(1) = 1 + E(1)\n\nThis implies E(1) = infinity, which can't be right.\n\nWait, that suggests that from state 1 on L=4, you can never leave state 1? That must be an error in my analysis.\n\nWait, let's think differently. Maybe my model of the relative displacement is flawed.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps when moving on the circle, a step of +2 or -2 can sometimes reach 0.\n\nWait, in L=4, starting at distance 1:\n\nIf the relative displacement is 1, then:\n\n- Moving +2 would take it to 3, minimal distance 1.\n\n- Moving -2 would take it to -1, which is 3 mod 4, minimal distance 1.\n\n- Moving 0 stays at 1.\n\nTherefore, yes, from state 1 on L=4, you can never reach 0? That seems incorrect.\n\nWait, but in reality, on a circle of 4, if two particles start at distance 1 apart, can they ever meet?\n\nLet's simulate manually.\n\nPositions: particle A at 0, particle B at 1.\n\nEach step, each moves left or right with 50% chance.\n\nPossible movements:\n\nA can go to 3 or 1.\n\nB can go to 0 or 2.\n\nCase 1: A moves to 3, B moves to 0.\n\nNew positions: A at 3, B at 0. Distance is 1 (since from 0 to 3 is 3 clockwise, but minimal is 1 counter-clockwise).\n\nCase 2: A moves to 3, B moves to 2.\n\nNew positions: A at 3, B at 2. Distance is 1 (from 2 to 3 is 1).\n\nCase 3: A moves to 1, B moves to 0.\n\nNew positions: A at 1, B at 0. Distance is 1.\n\nCase 4: A moves to 1, B moves to 2.\n\nNew positions: A at 1, B at 2. Distance is 1.\n\nSo indeed, no matter what, they remain at distance 1 forever? That can't be. Wait, but in this setup, their relative displacement never reaches 0. That suggests that on even L=4, starting at distance 1, they can never meet? That seems odd.\n\nWait, but in this specific case, yes, because their relative displacement only changes by even steps (±2 or 0), and starting from an odd distance (1), they can never reach 0 (even). Since all steps change the displacement by even numbers, the parity remains the same. Therefore, if starting at an odd distance on an even circumference, they can never meet.\n\nAh! So the key is that the parity of the distance must match the parity of the step changes.\n\nIn this case, since steps are ±2 or 0, which are even changes, so the relative displacement's parity remains the same. Therefore, if starting at an odd distance on an even circumference, they can never meet.\n\nSimilarly, if starting at an even distance on an even circumference, they can meet.\n\nTherefore, in our problem with L=45 (odd), starting at distance d (which is 3 initially, which is odd), since 45 is odd, then the parity of the distance can change because steps can be ±2 or 0, but adding/subtracting 2 to an odd number keeps it odd, and adding/subtracting 2 to an even number keeps it even. Since L is odd, the minimal distance can be both even and odd, but the parity of the distance modulo 2 is preserved.\n\nWait, 45 is odd, so minimal distance can be from 0 to 22 (since floor(45/2)=22).\n\nIf starting at distance d=3 (odd), then all subsequent distances will remain odd. Since 0 is even, they can never reach 0. That contradicts our initial problem setup where we start with snakes spaced 3 apart on a circle of 45 arcs (which is odd), and we expect them to eventually merge.\n\nThis suggests a critical flaw in my model.\n\nWait a second, in the original problem, the snakes are length 1, so when two snakes move towards each other and end up on the same arc, they merge into a length-2 snake. This merging changes the parity of the distance in some way?\n\nAh! Here's the key: in the original problem, when two snakes merge, they form a longer snake, which can then move as a block, changing the effective distance between snakes.\n\nTherefore, my previous analysis using relative displacement for two point particles is insufficient because the merging introduces new snake blocks that can affect the distances between other snakes.\n\nHence, the problem is more complex than just two particles on a circle; it involves multiple particles that can merge into larger blocks, which then behave differently.\n\nTherefore, going back, perhaps we need to think of the entire system's expected time until all snakes have merged into one, considering the merging dynamics.\n\nGiven the time I've spent and the complexity, perhaps this problem is expecting an answer that can be derived using linearity of expectation or some clever insight, rather than a detailed Markov chain analysis.\n\nLet me think differently. Suppose we consider that each snake has to \"collide\" with every other snake. Since there are 15 snakes, each snake has to merge with 14 others. However, merges can happen in any order.\n\nAlternatively, think of the process as each pair of snakes has a certain probability of merging over time, and the total expected time is the sum over all pairs of the expected time until they merge.\n\nBut this might overcount since merges can happen simultaneously.\n\nAlternatively, using the concept of expected time until all events have occurred, which for independent events would be the maximum of individual times, but they are not independent.\n\nAlternatively, consider that each time a merge occurs, the number of snakes decreases by 1, so to go from 15 to 1, we need 14 merges. If each merge takes on average T seconds, then the total expected time would be 14*T. But this is only true if the merges are independent and the time between merges is constant, which they are not.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the expected time until the first merge is T1, then the expected time until the second merge is T2, etc., and the total is T1 + T2 + ... + T14. But calculating each Ti is non-trivial.\n\nAlternatively, if we can find that the expected time between each merge is constant, then it would be a geometric series, but I don't think so.\n\nWait, in the initial configuration with n=15 snakes, each adjacent pair has probability p=1/4 of merging each time step, and there are 15 such pairs. The expected time until the first merge is similar to the coupon collector problem where you have 15 coupons, each with probability p_i of being collected per trial, but here it's more like the time until at least one of 15 independent processes succeeds.\n\nThe expected time until the first success in a Poisson process with rate λ is 1/λ. Here, each pair has a probability p=1/4 per time step of merging, so the rate for each pair is 1/4 per second. Since there are 15 pairs, the total rate is 15*(1/4) = 15/4 per second. Therefore, the expected time until the first merge is 1/(15/4) = 4/15 seconds.\n\nThen, after the first merge, we have 14 snakes. But now the configuration is asymmetric, so the next expected time would be different.\n\nHowever, if we assume that after each merge, the system returns to a symmetric configuration with n-1 snakes, then we could model it as E(n) = 1 + E(n-1), leading to E(15) = 15. But this is likely incorrect because the time between merges doesn't stay constant.\n\nAlternatively, if after each merge the expected remaining time is scaled by some factor, but without knowing the factor, this is guesswork.\n\nAlternatively, if we model each merge as an independent event with constant rate, then the total expected time would be the sum over k=1 to 14 of 1/p_k, where p_k is the probability of a merge happening at step k. But this requires knowing p_k, which depends on the current configuration.\n\nGiven the problem's symmetry and the fact that it's a competition problem, maybe there's a