es their initial positions, not their length? Hmm, the problem says: \"each of length 1, occupying every third arc.\" Hmm, maybe the wording is a bit ambiguous. Let me read again:\n\n\"Initially, there are 15 snakes, each of length 1, occupying every third arc.\"\n\nHmm, so \"each of length 1\" – that probably means that each snake has length 1, so occupies 1 arc. \"occupying every third arc\" – maybe that is just specifying their initial positions? That is, the initial positions are every third arc? So each snake is at a position which is a multiple of 3, so positions 0, 3, 6, ..., 42. That seems to make sense. So each snake is at one of these positions, and they are all length 1, so each snake just occupies its own arc, and the next snake is three arcs away, etc.\n\nThen, every second, each snake independently moves either one arc left or one arc right, each with probability 1/2. So, each snake has a 50% chance to move left or right. If two snakes ever touch, they merge into a single snake occupying the arcs of both of the previous snakes, and the merged snake moves as one snake.\n\nWait, so \"touch\" – does that mean that if the movement causes two snakes to end up in adjacent arcs? Or does it mean that if they are already in adjacent arcs and then move into each other's space? Hmm. The problem says \"if two snakes ever touch, they merge...\" So maybe \"touch\" means overlapping in an arc? Since the snakes are on arcs, which are discrete positions? Or is the circle continuous?\n\nWait, the problem says \"the circumference of a circle is divided into 45 arcs, each of length 1.\" So it's discrete, right? So the circle is divided into 45 discrete positions, each of length 1, so each position is an arc. So each snake occupies one position (arc), and when they move, they move to an adjacent position, left or right, with probability 1/2 each.\n\nTherefore, if two snakes are on adjacent positions, say snake A is at position k, snake B is at position k+1, then if snake A