ingle snake of length 1 again? But the problem says \"the merged snake moves as one snake\", so maybe after merging, the snake is considered to have length 2? So it occupies two adjacent arcs? Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the problem is similar to a classic \"snake game\" where when two snakes collide, they merge into a longer snake. In such games, when two snakes touch, they combine into a single snake that is the combination of their lengths. In this case, since each snake is length 1, merging would make a snake of length 2. Then, that snake of length 2 can move as a single entity, but how? If it's length 2, occupying two adjacent arcs, then when it moves, it can move as a block: either the entire block shifts left or right by one arc. So, for example, if the snake is on arcs k and k+1, moving left would take it to arcs k-1 and k, and moving right would take it to arcs k+1 and k+2. That seems plausible.\n\nTherefore, perhaps the rule is:\n\n- Each snake (whether single or merged) is a contiguous block of arcs of length equal to its size.\n\n- When a snake moves, the entire block shifts left or right by one arc, with equal probability.\n\nWait, but the problem says \"each snake independently moves either one arc left or one arc right\", so maybe even the merged snake, which is longer, still moves as a whole, choosing left or right with probability 1/2, causing the entire block to shift left or right by one arc.\n\nTherefore, in that case, the merged snake of length 2 would move as a block left or right. Therefore, the movement is not per individual arc, but the entire snake moves as a single unit.\n\nTherefore, the process is:\n\nAt each time step:\n\n1. Each snake (individual or merged) decides to move left or right, each with probability 1/2.\n\n2. All movements happen simultaneously.\n\n3. After moving, if any two snakes are on the same arc, they merge into a single snake that is the combination of their blocks. Since they are on the same arc, their blocks would be adjac