 S \\) and use vector rotations to form the square.\n   - Vectors between consecutive points must be perpendicular and of equal length.\n\n4. **Vector Calculations**:\n   - Vector \\( \\overrightarrow{PR} = (r - p, 25 - (H - 16)) = (r - p, 41 - H) \\)\n   - Vector \\( \\overrightarrow{RQ} = (q - r, (H - 36) - 25) = (q - r, H - 61) \\)\n   - Vector \\( \\overrightarrow{QS} = (s - q, 49 - (H - 36)) = (s - q, 85 - H) \\)\n   - Vector \\( \\overrightarrow{SP} = (p - s, (H - 16) - 49) = (p - s, H - 65) \\)\n\n5. **Rotating Vectors by 90 Degrees**:\n   - For \\( \\overrightarrow{RQ} \\) to be \\( \\overrightarrow{PR} \\) rotated 90 degrees counterclockwise:\n     - \\( q - r = -(41 - H) \\)\n     - \\( H - 61 = r - p \\)\n   - For \\( \\overrightarrow{QS} \\) to be \\( \\overrightarrow{RQ} \\) rotated 90 degrees counterclockwise:\n     - \\( s - q = -(H - 61) \\)\n     - \\( 85 - H = q - r \\)\n   - Solving these equations leads to \\( H = 63 \\).\n\n6. **Verification**:\n   - Substituting \\( H = 63 \\) confirms the coordinates form a square with all sides equal and right angles.\n   - The y-coordinates \\( 47, 25, 27, 49 \\) and corresponding x-coordinates satisfy the square conditions.\n\nThus, the height of the cave is \\(\\boxed{63}\\).","num_tokens":19753,"correct":true,"formatted":true},{"predicted_answer":"63","full_generation":"Okay, so there's this problem about a two-dimensional cave with a parallel floor and ceiling. There are two stalactites hanging from the ceiling, one with a length of 16 and another with a length of 36, both perpendicular to the ceiling. Then there are two stalagmites growing from the ground, perpendicular to the ground, with heights 25 and 49. The tips of these four structures form the vertices of a square in some order, and we need to compute the height of the cave.\n\nHmm, first, let me try to visualize this. Since it's a two-dimensional cave, I guess we can model the cave as a rectangle or something? The floor and ceiling are parallel,