rtices satisfy the relation that the midpoints of the diagonals coincide, and the diagonals are equal in length.\n\nSo, if we have four points A, B, C, D forming a square, then the midpoint of diagonal AC must be the same as the midpoint of diagonal BD, and the length of AC must equal the length of BD, and each diagonal must be sqrt(2) times the side length.\n\nTherefore, perhaps we can use that.\n\nLet me denote the four points as A, B, C, D. Then:\n\nMidpoint of AC: ((x_A + x_C)/2, (y_A + y_C)/2)\n\nMidpoint of BD: ((x_B + x_D)/2, (y_B + y_D)/2)\n\nThese must be equal.\n\nAlso, the distance between A and C must equal the distance between B and D, and also, the distance between A and B must equal the distance between B and C, etc., but perhaps starting with the midpoint condition is better.\n\nAlternatively, since there are four points, perhaps we can pair them up as diagonally opposite vertices and set their midpoints equal.\n\nBut since we don't know which points are diagonally opposite, this might require considering different pairings.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can look for which pairs of points can be diagonally opposite given their y-coordinates.\n\nLooking at the y-coordinates of the four tips:\n\n- The two stalactite tips have y-coordinates: H - 16 and H - 36\n\n- The two stalagmite tips have y-coordinates: 25 and 49\n\nSince H > 49, as established earlier, so H - 36 is less than H - 16, both of which are less than H, but we need to compare H - 16 and H - 36 with the stalagmite y-coordinates 25 and 49.\n\nWait, H - 36 and H - 16 are both above 25 and 49? Not necessarily. For example, if H is 50, then H - 36 = 14 and H - 16 = 34. Then the y-coordinates of the stalactite tips would be 14 and 34, while the stalagmite tips are at 25 and 49. Hmm, so in that case, 14 is below 25, and 34 is between 25 and 49, while 49 is above 34. So in that case, the y-coordinates are spread out. Alternatively, if H is 60, then H - 36 = 24, H - 16 = 44, so the stalactite tips would be at y=24 and y=44, and the stalagmite tips at y=25 and y=49. Then 24 is just below 25, 44 is between 25 and 49, and 49 is above 44. So in that case, the y-coordinates are still spread out. Alternatively, if H is 100, then H - 36 = 64, H -16 = 84, so the stalactite tips are at y=64 and y=84, while the stalagmite tips are at y=25 and y=49. Then the lower two points (in y-coordinate) are the two stalagmite tips at 25 and 49, and the upper two points are the stalactite tips at 64 and 84. Hmm.\n\nSo in any case, the four y-coordinates are: two from the stalactites (H - 16 and H - 36) and two from the stalagmites (25 and 49). Depending on H, the relative positions of these y-coordinates can vary. Since H must be greater than 49, as we saw, so H - 36 is greater than 13 (since H > 49 ⇒ H - 36 > 13). But 25 is greater than 13, so depending on H, H - 36 could be less than 25 or greater than 25.\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nIf H - 36 < 25, then H < 61.\n\nIf H - 36 = 25, then H = 61.\n\nIf H - 36 > 25, then H > 61.\n\nSimilarly, H - 16 compared to 25:\n\nIf H - 16 < 25 ⇒ H < 41, but since H must be greater than 49, this can't happen. So H - 16 is always greater than 49 - 16 = 33? Wait, no, H must be greater than 49, so H - 16 > 49 - 16 = 33. Wait, actually, if H is just over 49, say H = 50, then H - 16 = 34, which is greater than 25. So in fact, H - 16 is always greater than 25, since H > 49 ⇒ H - 16 > 49 - 16 = 33 > 25. Therefore, the two stalactite tips are always above y = 33, while the two stalagmite tips are at y = 25 and y = 49. Therefore, the lowest point among the four tips is the stalagmite tip at y = 25, then the next lowest is either the other stalagmite tip at y = 49 or the lower stalactite tip at y = H - 36. Wait, but H - 36 could be less than 49 or greater than 49?\n\nWait, if H is greater than 49, then H - 36 could be greater than 13 (as before), but specifically, if H is between 49 and 61, then H - 36 is between 13 and 25, so H - 36 < 25. If H is between 61 and 97, then H - 36 is between 25 and 61, so H - 36 could be greater than 25 but less than 49, or greater than 49? Let's see:\n\nIf H - 36 > 49, then H > 85. So:\n\n- If 49 < H ≤ 61: then H - 36 is between 13 and 25, so less than 25, so the y-coordinates from the four tips, in order from lowest to highest, would be: 25 (stalagmite), H - 36, H - 16, 49 (stalagmite). Wait, but 49 is the highest y-coordinate? Wait, no, if H is, say, 60, then H - 36 = 24, H - 16 = 44, and the stalagmites are at 25 and 49, so ordering the y-coordinates: 24 (stalactite), 25 (stalagmite), 44 (stalactite), 49 (stalagmite). So in this case, the four y's are 24, 25, 44, 49.\n\nAlternatively, if H is 80, then H - 36 = 44, H - 16 = 64, so the y-coordinates would be 25, 44, 49, 64. So ordered: 25, 44, 49, 64.\n\nIf H is 85, then H - 36 = 49, so then the y-coordinates would be 25, 49, 49 (since H - 36 = 49), and H - 16 = 69. So then the ordered y's are 25, 49, 49, 69.\n\nIf H is 90, then H - 36 = 54, H - 16 = 74, so ordered y's: 25, 49, 54, 74.\n\nTherefore, depending on the value of H, the y-coordinates of the four tips can be arranged in different orders.\n\nNow, in a square, the four vertices must have their y-coordinates arranged such that the vertical distances between them correspond to the square's geometry.\n\nBut perhaps another approach is needed. Let me think.\n\nSince the four points form a square, then the four points must satisfy that the distance between each pair of adjacent vertices is the same, and the sides are perpendicular.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider that in a square, the coordinates can be represented in terms of vectors, with each consecutive vector being a 90-degree rotation of the prior one.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can use complex numbers to represent the points, but that might be overcomplicating.\n\nAlternatively, since the square has four points, perhaps we can set up equations based on the distances between the points.\n\nLet me denote the four points as A, B, C, D, with coordinates:\n\nA: (x1, H - 16)\n\nB: (x2, H - 36)\n\nC: (a, 25)\n\nD: (b, 49)\n\nWe need to assign these four points to the four vertices of a square, but we don't know which is which. Since there are four points, there are 4! = 24 possible orderings, but many of these will be equivalent under rotation and reflection. However, this seems too many to handle directly.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can note that in a square, the sides are equal and the diagonals are equal. Therefore, the four points must satisfy that for some pairing of the points as vertices, all the edges are equal and the diagonals are equal and sqrt(2) times the edges.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider that for any two points that are supposed to be adjacent in the square, the squared distance between them should equal the squared distance between any other pair of adjacent vertices.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider that the set of all pairwise squared distances between the four points must consist of two distinct values: one for the edges (which are equal) and one for the diagonals (which are equal and larger by a factor of 2). Since in a square, the diagonal squared is 2 times the side squared.\n\nTherefore, if we compute all six pairwise distances between the four points, there should be exactly two distinct values, one of which occurs four times (the sides) and the other occurs twice (the diagonals), but actually in a square, there are four sides and two diagonals. Wait, actually, in a square, there are four edges and two diagonals, so six distances total: four edges (all equal) and two diagonals (also equal). Therefore, among the six distances between the four points, four should be equal (the sides) and two should be equal and larger (the diagonals). So that might be a way to approach it: compute all six distances between the four points and see when they can be partitioned into four equal sides and two equal diagonals.\n\nHowever, since we don't know which points correspond to which edges or diagonals, this might be a bit involved, but perhaps manageable with some algebra.\n\nLet me denote the four points as follows for clarity:\n\nLet me call the points as follows, with their coordinates:\n\nPoint P: (x1, H - 16)\n\nPoint Q: (x2, H - 36)\n\nPoint R: (a, 25)\n\nPoint S: (b, 49)\n\nSo we have four points: P, Q, R, S.\n\nWe need to compute all six pairwise distances between these points and see if they can form a square.\n\nFirst, let's write expressions for the squared distances between each pair.\n\nDistance squared between P and Q:\n\nd(P,Q)^2 = (x1 - x2)^2 + [ (H - 16) - (H - 36) ]^2\n\nSimplify the y-component: (H - 16) - (H - 36) = (-16) - (-36) = 20. So,\n\nd(P,Q)^2 = (x1 - x2)^2 + (20)^2 = (x1 - x2)^2 + 400.\n\nDistance squared between P and R:\n\nd(P,R)^2 = (x1 - a)^2 + [ (H - 16) - 25 ]^2 = (x1 - a)^2 + (H - 41)^2\n\nDistance squared between P and S:\n\nd(P,S)^2 = (x1 - b)^2 + [ (H - 16) - 49 ]^2 = (x1 - b)^2 + (H - 65)^2\n\nDistance squared between Q and R:\n\nd(Q,R)^2 = (x2 - a)^2 + [ (H - 36) - 25 ]^2 = (x2 - a)^2 + (H - 61)^2\n\nDistance squared between Q and S:\n\nd(Q,S)^2 = (x2 - b)^2 + [ (H - 36) - 49 ]^2 = (x2 - b)^2 + (H - 85)^2\n\nDistance squared between R and S:\n\nd(R,S)^2 = (a - b)^2 + (25 - 49)^2 = (a - b)^2 + (-24)^2 = (a - b)^2 + 576\n\nSo we have six squared distances:\n\n1. d(P,Q)^2 = (x1 - x2)^2 + 400\n\n2. d(P,R)^2 = (x1 - a)^2 + (H - 41)^2\n\n3. d(P,S)^2 = (x1 - b)^2 + (H - 65)^2\n\n4. d(Q,R)^2 = (x2 - a)^2 + (H - 61)^2\n\n5. d(Q,S)^2 = (x2 - b)^2 + (H - 85)^2\n\n6. d(R,S)^2 = (a - b)^2 + 576\n\nIn a square, there should be four equal distances (the sides) and two equal distances (the diagonals), with the diagonals being sqrt(2) times the sides. Therefore, the squared distances should have four equal values (the sides squared) and two equal values which are twice the side squared (the diagonals squared).\n\nTherefore, among these six expressions, four of them must be equal to some value,