ong.\n\nHmm, so my initial interpretation must be incorrect. Maybe the stalactites and stalagmites are not vertical? Wait, the problem says they hang perpendicularly from the ceiling and grow perpendicularly from the ground. In a two-dimensional cave with parallel floor and ceiling, \"perpendicularly\" would mean vertical, right? So perhaps the cave is not just a rectangle with vertical sides, but perhaps the stalactites and stalagmites are not aligned vertically? Wait, but perpendicular to the ceiling and the ground, which are horizontal, so perpendicular would be vertical. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"perpendicularly\" here means perpendicular to the direction of the cave walls? Wait, but the problem says \"perpendicularly from the ceiling\" and \"perpendicularly from the ground.\" Hmm. Wait, perhaps the cave has a different orientation? Maybe the ceiling and floor are not horizontal? But it says \"parallel floor and ceiling,\" so they must be parallel, but maybe they are not horizontal? Hmm, but in that case, the term \"height of the cave\" might refer to the distance between the floor and ceiling along the vertical axis, assuming that \"height\" is measured vertically regardless of the orientation. Hmm, this is getting confusing.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the cave is three-dimensional, but we are looking at a two-dimensional cross-section? The problem says \"two-dimensional cave,\" so perhaps not. Wait, maybe in two dimensions, the \"height\" of the cave is the distance between the floor and ceiling, which are two horizontal lines. Then, the stalactites are vertical segments from the ceiling to some points below, but not necessarily all the way to the floor? Wait, but if they hang perpendicularly from the ceiling, then their tips would be directly below the point they're hanging from, right? So if the ceiling is a horizontal line, then a stalactite hanging perpendicularly from a point on the ceiling would go straight down to the floor, so its length would have t