4) / ( √10 / 2 ) ⇒ √10 = 2(A2 + A4)/√10 ⇒ multiply both sides by √10:\n\n10 = 2(A2 + A4) ⇒ A2 + A4 = 5,\n\nSimilarly, from A3 = k (√10 - y),\n\nand y = A1 / k,\n\nthen √10 - y = A3 / k ⇒ √10 - A1 / k = A3 / k ⇒ √10 = (A1 + A3)/k ⇒ same as above,\n\n√10 = (A1 + A3)/k ⇒ √10 = (A1 + A3)/( √10 / 2 ) ⇒ same steps leading to A1 + A3 = 5,\n\nwhich we already knew.\n\nTherefore, this just confirms that the areas must satisfy A1 + A3 = A2 + A4 = 5,\n\nwhich is consistent with the given areas 1, 2, 3, 4 since 1 + 4 = 5 and 2 + 3 = 5.\n\nTherefore, given that, we can express x and y in terms of the areas:\n\nx = A4 / k,\n\ny = A1 / k,\n\nwhere k = √10 / 2,\n\nso x = (A4) / ( √10 / 2 ) = (2 A4)/√10,\n\nand similarly y = (2 A1)/√10,\n\nTherefore, coordinates of P are:\n\nx = (2 A4)/√10,\n\ny = (2 A1)/√10.\n\nTherefore, once we know which area corresponds to A1, A2, A3, A4, we can compute x and y.\n\nHowever, since the product PA · PB · PC · PD only depends on x and y, and through the areas, but since the product ended up being the same regardless of the assignment of the areas to A1, A2, A3, A4 (as long as the pairing is correct), then the product is fixed.\n\nAlternatively, let me compute PA · PB · PC · PD in terms of the areas.\n\nGiven x = (2 A4)/√10,\n\ny = (2 A1)/√10,\n\nthen PA = √(x² + y²) = √( (4 A4²)/10 + (4 A1²)/10 ) = √( (4/10)(A1² + A4²) ) = (2 / √10) √(A1² + A4²),\n\nSimilarly, PB = √( (s - x)^2 + y² ),\n\ns = √10,\n\nso s - x = √10 - (2 A4)/√10 = (10 - 2 A4)/√10,\n\ntherefore,\n\nPB = √( [ (10 - 2 A4)^2 / 10 ] + [ (2 A1)^2 / 10 ] )\n\n= √( [ (10 - 2 A4)^2 + (2 A1)^2 ] / 10 )\n\n= (1 / √10) √( (10 - 2 A4)^2 + (2 A1)^2 ),\n\nSimilarly, PC = √( (s - x)^2 + (s - y)^2 )\n\ns - y = √10 - (2 A1)/√10 = (10 - 2 A1)/√10,\n\nso,\n\nPC = √( [ (10 - 2 A4)^2 / 10 ] + [ (10 - 2 A1)^2 / 10 ] )\n\n= (1 / √10) √( (10 - 2 A4)^2 + (10 - 2 A1)^2 ),\n\nSimilarly, PD = √(x² + (s - y)^2 )\n\n= √( [ (2 A4 / √10)^2 ] + [ (10 - 2 A1)/√10 )^2 ] )\n\n= √( (4 A4² + (10 - 2 A1)^2 ) / 10 )\n\n= (