have two pairs each adding to s is if either:\n\nEither:\n\nPair1: Term1 + Term4 = 10/s = s ⇒ s^2 = 10,\n\nand Pair2: Term2 + Term3 = 10/s = s ⇒ same equation,\n\nOr,\n\nPair1: Term2 + Term4 = 12/s = s ⇒ s^2 = 12,\n\nand Pair2: Term1 + Term3 = 8/s = s ⇒ s^2 = 8,\n\nBut these are inconsistent because s cannot simultaneously satisfy s^2 = 10 and s^2 = 12, etc.\n\nTherefore, the only viable solution is when s^2 = 10, so that both pairs Term1 + Term4 and Term2 + Term3 add to s, since 10/s = s when s^2 =10,\n\nIndeed, if s^2 = 10,\n\nthen 10/s = s,\n\nso Term1 + Term4 = 2/s + 8/s = 10/s = s,\n\nSimilarly, Term2 + Term3 = 4/s + 6/s = 10/s = s,\n\nTherefore, this works.\n\nTherefore, the only possible side length is s = sqrt(10),\n\nand the distances must be assigned such that:\n\nEither:\n\nd_AB and d_CD are Term1 and Term4 (2/s and 8/s) in some order,\n\nand d_BC and d_DA are Term2 and Term3 (4/s and 6/s) in some order,\n\nOR,\n\nd_AB and d_CD are Term2 and Term3,\n\nand d_BC and d_DA are Term1 and Term4,\n\nBut since the pairs must each add to s, and the assignments can be permuted.\n\nTherefore, this aligns with our earlier findings where in each case, the product PA * PB * PC * PD ended up being 8√10.\n\nTherefore, this alternative approach also leads us to s = sqrt(10), and the distances being 2/sqrt(10), 4/sqrt(10), 6/sqrt(10), 8/sqrt(10).\n\nWait a second, but the distances d_AB, d_BC, d_CD, d_DA are:\n\nd_AB = y,\n\nd_BC = s - x,\n\nd_CD = s - y,\n\nd_DA = x,\n\nand these are equal to either 2/s, 4/s, 6/s, 8/s,\n\nBut s = sqrt(10),\n\nso the distances are:\n\n2/sqrt(10), 4/sqrt(10), 6/sqrt(10), 8/sqrt(10).\n\nTherefore, the coordinates x and y can be found from these distances.\n\nFor instance,\n\nd_DA = x = either 2/sqrt(10), 4/sqrt(10), 6/sqrt(10), or 8/sqrt(10),\n\nand d_AB = y = one of the remaining,\n\nand so on,\n\nbut with the constraints that d_AB + d_CD = s,\n\nand d_BC + d_DA = s,\n\nTherefore, as we saw in the coordinate approach, this leads to the four