o C(c,0), which is c,\n\nand CB is from C(c,0) to B(10,0), which is 10 - c,\n\nso AC·CB = c*(10 - c) = 10c - c²,\n\nwhich is indeed equal to - (c² -10c) = - [ (c² -10c +25) -25 ] = - [ (c -5)^2 -25 ] = - (c -5)^2 +25.\n\nFrom Equation 4,\n\n(c -5)^2 = 100 - 2 r₂²,\n\nso,\n\nAC·CB = - (100 - 2 r₂²) + 25 = -100 + 2 r₂² +25 = 2 r₂² -75,\n\nwhich is what we had.\n\nSo unless r₂² > 37.5, this is negative.\n\nBut according to our previous result, r₂² =10,\n\nso 2*10 -75 = -55,\n\nwhich is negative, impossible.\n\nThis suggests that the only solution from the equations is impossible, meaning there must be an error in the formulation.\n\nPerhaps the error is in the assumption that the semicircle S₁ is also above its diameter? Maybe it's below?\n\nIf S₁ is a semicircle below its diameter, then its center would be at (c, k) where k = r₂ - √(r₂² - 1),\n\nand then the distance from O₂ to O₁ would be |r₂ - k| = |√(r₂² - 1)|,\n\nso the condition becomes √(r₂² -1) + 1 ≤ r₂,\n\nwhich is still impossible for r₂ >1.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the semicircles are drawn differently?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the tangent point C is not between A and B but extended beyond?\n\nBut the problem states that S₂'s arc is tangent to AB at C, and AB is the diameter of S₃, so C must be on AB between A and B.\n\nGiven that all paths lead to a contradiction, I might have made a wrong assumption in the beginning.\n\nLet me try to consider specific values.\n\nSuppose that the radii form a geometric progression: S₁ has radius 1, S₂ has radius r, S₃ has radius 10.\n\nIf they are geometrically similar, then r /1 = 10 / r ⇒ r² = 10 ⇒ r = √10,\n\nwhich matches our earlier result.\n\nThen, AC·CB = 2*(10) -75 = -55,\n\nwhich is impossible, so maybe the question is a trick question and the answer is 50? Wait, but how?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made an error in the sign when computing AC·CB.\n\nWait, if c = 5 - √80 ≈ -3.944,\n\nthen AC·CB = c*(10 - c) = (-3.944)*(13.944) ≈ -55,\n\nbut since distances can't be 