are A and B, and S₂'s arc is tangent to AB at C.\" So point C is where the arc of S₂ touches the diameter AB of S₃. So point C is on AB, and the semicircle S₂ is tangent to AB at C, and also S₂ is inscribed in S₃, so its diameter is a chord of S₃. Since S₂ is a semicircle, its diameter must be a chord of S₃ (the larger semicircle), and the arc of S₂ is above that diameter, and tangent to AB at C.\n\nGiven that, perhaps we can model the positions of these semicircles with coordinates.\n\nLet me try to formalize this.\n\nLet me denote:\n\n- Let S₃ be the largest semicircle, with diameter AB of length 20 (since radius 10), lying on the x-axis from (0, 0) to (20, 0). Its center is at (10, 0), and it has radius 10.\n\n- Let S₂ be a semicircle inscribed in S₃. That means:\n\n  1. The diameter of S₂ is a chord of S₃.\n\n  2. The arc of S₂ is tangent to the diameter AB of S₃ at point C.\n\n  Since S₂ is a semicircle, its diameter must be a chord of S₃, and its arc lies above that diameter (since it's a semicircle). The tangency condition at point C means that the arc of S₂ touches AB (the x-axis) at point C, and the tangent to S₂ at point C is the line AB (the x-axis). Therefore, point C is a point of tangency between S₂ and the x-axis.\n\n  Additionally, since S₂ is a semicircle, its center must lie along the perpendicular bisector of its own diameter. Let me denote the diameter of S₂ as CD, where D is the other endpoint? Wait, but actually, let me define variables.\n\nLet me denote the diameter of S₂ as a chord of S₃. Let me call the endpoints of the diameter of S₂ as P and Q. Then the semicircle S₂ has diameter PQ, which is a chord of S₃, and its arc is above PQ, and tangent to AB (the x-axis) at point C.\n\nSince S₂ is a semicircle, the center of S₂, let's call it O₂, must be the midpoint of PQ, and the radius of S₂ is half the length of PQ. Also, since the arc of S₂ is tangent to AB at C, the distance from the center O₂ to the x-axis must be equal to the radius of S₂. 