per semicircle would be the set of points on the circle with y ≥ something. Wait, actually, the upper semicircle of the circle centered at (c, r₂) with radius r₂ would be the set of points where y ≥ r₂? Wait, no, the entire circle has center at (c, r₂) and radius r₂, so the bottommost point of the circle is at (c, r₂ - r₂) = (c, 0), and the topmost point is at (c, r₂ + r₂) = (c, 2r₂). Therefore, the upper semicircle would be from the top point down to the bottom point along the circle? Wait, actually, a semicircle is half of a circle. If you have a circle centered at (c, r₂) with radius r₂, then the full circle would extend from y = 0 up to y = 2r₂. If we take the upper semicircle, that would be the half of the circle where y ≥ r₂? Or is it the half above the diameter?\n\nWait, actually, the standard semicircle is typically defined as the half of the circle divided by a diameter. So if the diameter is the horizontal line through the center, then the upper semicircle would be the top half. But in this case, the diameter of S₂ is DE, which is a chord of S₃, and the semicircle S₂ is drawn on one side of DE. Since it's tangent to the x-axis, which is below the center F, that suggests that the semicircle is drawn below the diameter DE? Wait, but the problem says \"the smaller semicircle's arc is tangent to the diameter of the larger semicircle.\" In this case, the larger semicircle is S₃, whose diameter is AB (the x-axis). So the arc of S₂ must be tangent to AB (the x-axis). Since S₂ is inside S₃, which is the upper semicircle, but S₂ is also a semicircle. If S₂ is tangent to the x-axis, then its arc must be the lower semicircle of its own circle? Wait, that seems contradictory.\n\nHold on, perhaps I need to clarify the orientation. The semicircle S₂ is inscribed in S₃, which is the upper semicircle with diameter AB on the x-axis. So S₂ must be a semicircle inside S₃. If its arc is tangent to AB (the x-axis), then the semicircle S₂ must be the lower semicircle of its own