specific locus, like the circumcircle of some triangle or something else?\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can use the Law of Sines or Cosines in triangle BPC or other triangles.\n\nWait, in triangle BPC, we know angle at P is 120°, and sides PB and PC are unknown, but BC is known, since BC is a side of the equilateral triangle, so BC = 6. So, in triangle BPC, we have side BC = 6, angle at P is 120°, so by the Law of Cosines, we can relate the other sides:\n\nIn triangle BPC,\n\nBC² = PB² + PC² - 2*PB*PC*cos(angle BPC)\n\nSo,\n\n6² = PB² + PC² - 2*PB*PC*cos(120°)\n\nSince cos(120°) = cos(180° - 60°) = -cos(60°) = -0.5\n\nTherefore,\n\n36 = PB² + PC² - 2*PB*PC*(-0.5)\n\nSimplify the last term: -2*PB*PC*(-0.5) = + PB*PC\n\nTherefore,\n\n36 = PB² + PC² + PB*PC\n\nSo that's one equation relating PB and PC.\n\nHmm, okay, so that's a relation between PB and PC.\n\nBut we might need more relations to find coordinates of P or something else.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use coordinate geometry. Let me proceed with coordinates.\n\nLet me set up coordinates as I did before:\n\nA: (0, 3√3)\n\nB: (-3, 0)\n\nC: (3, 0)\n\nCentroid (circumcenter): O_circum = (0, √3)\n\nCircumradius R = 2√3.\n\nNow, let me denote coordinates of point P as (p, q). Since P is inside triangle ABC, so it must satisfy certain inequalities, but perhaps we can just work algebraically.\n\nWe know that angle BPC = 120°, so using coordinates, we can express this condition via vectors or via the Law of Cosines.\n\nAlternatively, using vectors: Let me consider vectors PB and PC. The angle between vectors PB and PC is 120°, so the dot product formula says:\n\nPB · PC = |PB| |PC| cos(theta), where theta is 120°.\n\nSo, vectors PB and PC: vector PB is from P to B: B - P = (-3 - p, 0 - q)\n\nSimilarly, vector PC is from P to C: C - P = (3 - p, 0 - q)\n\nThen, the dot product PB · PC = [(-3 - p)(3 - p) + (0 - q)(0 - q)]\n\nCompute that:\n\nFirst component: (-3 - p)(3 - p) = (-3)(3 - p) - p(3 - p) = -9 + 3p - 3p + p² = -9 + p²\n\nWait, alternatively, just multiply it out:\n\n(-3 - p)(3 - p) = (-3)(3) + (-3)(-p) + (-p)(3) + (-p)(-p) = -9 + 3p - 3p + p² = -9 + p², yes.\n\nSecond component: (0 - q)(0 - q) = (-q)(-q) = q²\n\nTherefore, the dot product PB · PC = (-9 + p²) + q²\n\nThe magnitudes |PB| and |PC|:\n\n|PB| = sqrt[ (-3 - p)^2 + (0 - q)^2 ] = sqrt[ (p + 3)^2 + q^2 ]\n\n|PC| = sqrt[ (3 - p)^2 + (0 - q)^2 ] = sqrt[ (p - 3)^2 + q^2 ]\n\nTherefore, according to the dot product formula:\n\nPB · PC = |PB| |PC| cos(120°)\n\nSo,\n\n(-9 + p² + q²) = |PB| |PC| * (-1/2)\n\nBecause cos(120°) = -1/2.\n\nSo,\n\n-9 + p² + q² = (-1/2) |PB| |PC|\n\nHmm, that seems a bit complicated, but perhaps manageable.\n\nAlternatively, we can also write the Law of Cosines equation we had earlier:\n\nIn triangle BPC,\n\n36 = PB² + PC² + PB*PC\n\nBecause earlier we had 36 = PB² + PC² + PB*PC.\n\nSo, if we can write PB² and PC² in terms of coordinates, perhaps we can combine the equations.\n\nCompute PB² and PC²:\n\nPB² = (p + 3)^2 + q^2\n\nPC² = (p - 3)^2 + q^2\n\nTherefore, PB² + PC² = (p + 3)^2 + q^2 + (p - 3)^2 + q^2\n\nExpand both squares:\n\n= [p² + 6p + 9 + q²] + [p² - 6p + 9 + q²]\n\n= p² + 6p + 9 + q² + p² - 6p + 9 + q²\n\nSimplify:\n\n= 2p² + 2q² + 18\n\nTherefore, PB² + PC² = 2p² + 2q² + 18\n\nThen, the equation from Law of Cosines:\n\n36 = (PB² + PC²) + PB*PC\n\nSo,\n\n36 = (2p² + 2q² + 18) + PB*PC\n\nTherefore,\n\nPB*PC = 36 - (2p² + 2q² + 18) = 36 - 2p² - 2q² - 18 = 18 - 2p² - 2q²\n\nSo, PB*PC = 18 - 2p² - 2q²\n\nHmm, okay. Now, going back to the dot product equation:\n\nWe had:\n\nPB · PC = (-9 + p² + q²) = (-1/2) PB * PC\n\nTherefore,\n\n(-9 + p² + q²) = (-1/2) PB * PC\n\nBut from above, PB * PC = 18 - 2p² - 2q², so substituting that in,\n\nLeft side: (-9 + p² + q²)\n\nRight side: (-1/2)(18 - 2p² - 2q²) = (-1/2)*18 + (-1/2)*(-2p²) + (-1/2)*(-2q²) = -9 + p² + q²\n\nWait a second! So substituting PB * PC = 18 - 2p² - 2q² into the equation:\n\n(-9 + p² + q²) = (-1/2)(18 - 2p² - 2q²)\n\nLet me compute the right side:\n\n(-1/2)*18 = -9\n\n(-1/2)*(-2p²) = + p²\n\n(-1/2)*(-2q²) = + q²\n\nSo altogether, right side is -9 + p² + q², which is exactly the left side. So this gives an identity: (-9 + p² + q²) = (-9 + p² + q²). So, this equation doesn't give us any new information; it's just a tautology. That means that the two conditions (the Law of Cosines and the dot product) are not independent; they are the same equation. Therefore, we need another equation to solve for coordinates of P.\n\nHmm, so perhaps we need another condition. Since point P is inside the triangle, maybe we can use the fact that it lies somewhere else? Or perhaps the fact that the circle with diameter AP passes through X on the circumcircle, and AX = 5? Wait, but we haven't used that information yet. So maybe we need to connect these two conditions?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can parametrize point P in some way.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using complex numbers? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, let's think about the circle with diameter AP. Since we know that X lies on both the circumcircle of ABC and the circle with diameter AP, and AX = 5, perhaps we can use power of a point or some intersecting chords theorem?\n\nAlternatively, since X is on both circles, the two circles intersect at A and X, so the line AX is the radical axis of the two circles. The radical axis is perpendicular to the line joining the centers of the two circles. Maybe that can help?\n\nAlternatively, since we know AX = 5, and in triangle AX P, which is right-angled at X, so AX = 5, XP = ?, AP = sqrt(AX² + XP²). Also, AP is the diameter of the other circle, so the circle with diameter AP has center at midpoint of AP and radius AP/2.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can write equations for both circles and solve for X?\n\nLet me try that approach.\n\nFirst, let me denote coordinates for point P as (p, q). Then, the circle with diameter AP has endpoints at A(0, 3√3) and P(p, q). The center of this circle, O', is the midpoint of AP: O' = ( (0 + p)/2, (3√3 + q)/2 ) = (p/2, (3√3 + q)/2 )\n\nThe radius of this circle is half of AP, so radius r = (1/2)*sqrt( (p - 0)^2 + (q - 3√3)^2 ) = (1/2)*sqrt(p² + (q - 3√3)^2 )\n\nThe equation of the circle with diameter AP is then:\n\n( x - p/2 )² + ( y - (3√3 + q)/2 )² = [ (1/2)*sqrt(p² + (q - 3√3)^2 ) ]²\n\nSimplify the right-hand side: [ (1/2)*sqrt(...) ]² = (1/4)(p² + (q - 3√3)^2 )\n\nTherefore, the equation is:\n\n( x - p/2 )² + ( y - (3√3 + q)/2 )² = (1/4)(p² + (q - 3√3)^2 )\n\nNow, the circumcircle of triangle ABC has center at (0, √3) and radius 2√3, so its equation is:\n\nx² + ( y - √3 )² = (2√3)^2 = 12\n\nWe need to find the other intersection point X of the two circles (other than A). Since A is (0, 3√3), which is on both circles, so we can solve the two equations simultaneously to find the coordinates of X, then set the distance AX = 5, and then perhaps set up equations to solve for p and q?\n\nThis seems very involved, but let me try.\n\nFirst, let me denote the two circle equations:\n\n1. Circle with diameter AP:\n\n( x - p/2 )² + ( y - (3√3 + q)/2 )² = (1/4)(p² + (q - 3√3)^2 )\n\n2. Circumcircle of ABC:\n\nx² + ( y - √3 )² = 12\n\nWe can attempt to subtract or manipulate these equations to eliminate variables.\n\nAlternatively, let me expand both equations and then subtract them.\n\nFirst, expand equation 1:\n\nLeft-hand side:\n\n( x - p/2 )² + ( y - (3√3 + q)/2 )²\n\n= [x² - p x + (p²)/4] + [ y² - (3√3 + q) y + ( (3√3 + q)^2 ) /4 ]\n\nSo, altogether:\n\nx² - p x + p²/4 + y² - (3√3 + q)y + ( (3√3 + q)^2 ) /4\n\nRight-hand side:\n\n(1/4)(p² + (q - 3√3)^2 )\n\n= (1/4)p² + (1/4)(q² - 6√3 q + 27*3) [Wait, (q - 3√3)^2 = q² - 2*(3√3)*q + (3√3)^2 = q² - 6√3 q + 9*3 = q² - 6√3 q + 27]\n\nTherefore, right-hand side becomes:\n\n(1/4)p² + (1/4)(q² - 6√3 q + 27) = (1/4)p² + (1/4)q² - (3√3 / 2) q + 27/4\n\nTherefore, putting it all together, equation 1 is:\n\nx² - p x + p²/4 + y² - (3√3 + q)y + ( (3√3 + q)^2 ) /4 = (1/4)p² + (1/4)q² - (3√3 / 2) q + 27/4\n\nLet me simplify the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nFirst, note that (3√3 + q)^2 /4 = [ (3√3)^2 + 6√3 q + q² ] /4 = [27 + 6√3 q + q²]/4\n\nTherefore, left-hand side becomes:\n\nx² - p x + p²/4 + y² - (3√3 + q)y + [27 + 6√3 q + q²]/4\n\nLet me combine terms:\n\nx² + y² - p x - (3√3 + q)y + [p²/4 + 27/4 + (6√3 q)/4 + q²/4]\n\nNow, right-hand side is:\n\n(1/4)p² + (1/4)q² - (3√3 / 2) q + 27/4\n\nLet me write both sides with common denominators where needed.\n\nLeft-hand side:\n\nx² + y² - p x - (3√3 + q)y + [ (p² + 27 + 6√3 q + q²)/4 ]\n\nRight-hand side:\n\n[ p² + q² - 6√3 q + 27 ] /4\n\nWait, let me compute right-hand side:\n\n(1/4)p² + (1/4)q² - (3√3 / 2) q + 27/4\n\n= (1/4)p² + (1/4)q² + [ - (3√3 / 2) q + 27/4 ]\n\nTo combine terms over 4:\n\n= [ p² + q² - 6√3 q + 27 ] /4\n\nYes, because:\n\n(1/4)p² + (1/4)q² = (p² + q²)/4,\n\nand then - (3√3 / 2) q = -6√3 q /4,\n\nand 27/4 remains.\n\nTherefore, right-hand side is (p² + q² - 6√3 q + 27)/4.\n\nTherefore, equation 1 becomes:\n\nLeft-hand side: x² + y² - p x - (3√3 + q)y + [ (p² + q² + 6√3 q + 27)/4 ] = [ (p² + q² - 6√3 q + 27)/4 ]\n\nWait, hold on, let me re-express the left-hand side again:\n\nWait, in the left-hand side, the term in brackets was [p²/4 + 27/4 + (6√3 q)/4 + q²/4], which is equal to [p² + q² + 6√3 q + 27]/4, yes.\n\nTherefore, left-hand side is:\n\nx² + y² - p x - (3√3 + q)y + [p² + q² + 6√3 q + 27]/4\n\nSet equal to right-hand side:\n\n[ p² + q² - 6√3 q + 27 ] /4\n\nTherefore, moving all terms to the left-hand side:\n\nx² + y² - p x - (3√3 + q)y + [p² + q² + 6√3 q + 27]/4 - [ p² + q² - 6√3 q + 27 ] /4 = 0\n\nSimplify the terms in the brackets:\n\nThe terms with [p² + q² + 6√3 q + 27]/4 minus [ p² + q² - 6√3 q + 27 ] /4 equals:\n\n[ (p² + q² + 6√3 q + 27) - (p² + q² - 6√3 q + 27) ] /4\n\n= [ (0) + 12√3 q + 0 ] /4 = (12√3 q)/4 = 3√3 q\n\nTherefore, the entire equation becomes:\n\nx² + y² - p x - (3√3 + q)y + 3√3 q = 0\n\nSo, equation 1 simplifies to:\n\nx² + y² - p x - (3√3 + q)y + 3√3 q = 0\n\nNow, let's write the equation of the circumcircle (equation 2):\n\nx² + ( y - √3 )² = 12\n\nExpand this equation:\n\nx² + y² - 2√3 y + (√3)^2 = 12\n\nWhich is:\n\nx² + y² - 2√3 y + 3 = 12\n\nTherefore,\n\nx² + y² - 2√3 y = 9\n\nSo, equation 2 is:\n\nx² + y² - 2√3 y = 9\n\nNow, we have equation 1 simplified as:\n\nx² + y² - p x - (3√3 + q)y + 3√3 q = 0\n\nAnd equation 2 is:\n\nx² + y² - 2√3 y = 9\n\nLet me subtract equation 2 from equation 1 to eliminate x² + y²:\n\n[ (x² + y² - p x - (3√3 + q)y + 3√3 q ) ] - [ x² + y² - 2√3 y ] = 0 - 9\n\nSimplify left-hand side:\n\n- p x - (3√3 + q)y + 3√3 q - (-2√3 y) = - p x - (3√3 + q)y + 3√3 q + 2√3 y\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n- p x + [ - (3√3 + q)y + 2√3 y ] + 3√3 q\n\n= - p x + [ -3√3 y - q y + 2√3 y ] + 3√3 q\n\n= - p x + [ (-√3 y - q y) ] + 3√3 q\n\nFactor y terms:\n\n= - p x - y (√3 + q) + 3√3 q\n\nSo, the entire left-hand side simplifies to:\n\n- p x - y (√3 + q) + 3√3 q = -9\n\nTherefore, moving all terms to the left:\n\n- p x - y (√3 + q) + 3√3 q + 9 = 0\n\nSo,\n\n- p x - y (√3 + q) + 3√3 q + 9 = 0\n\nLet me write this as:\n\np x + y (√3 + q) = 3√3 q + 9\n\nHmm, so this is the equation obtained by subtracting equation 2 from equation 1. This is the equation of the radical axis of the two circles, which is the line along which their intersection points lie (points A and X). Since we already know point A is (0, 3√3) is on both circles, so this line passes through A and X.\n\nTherefore, the radical axis line is given by p x + y (√3 + q) = 3√3 q + 9\n\nSince point X lies on this line and also on the circumcircle, so we can parametrize point X as lying on both the circumcircle and this line.\n\nBut this seems still complicated because we have variables p and q (coordinates of P) in the equation. Perhaps we need another approach.\n\nAlternatively, since we know that AX = 5, and in the right triangle AX P, with right angle at X, so AX = 5, XP = ?, AP = sqrt(AX² + XP²). Also, since X is on the circumcircle of ABC, which has center at (0, √3) and radius 2√3, so the distance from X to the center (0, √3) must be 2√3.\n\nAdditionally, since X is on the circle with diameter AP, then the distance from X to A and to P must satisfy the condition that angle AX P is 90°, as we said before.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can consider inversion or some other transformation, but that might be overkill.\n\nWait, maybe instead of coordinates, use geometric properties?\n\nLet me think again. Since angle BPC is 120°, and ABC is equilateral. Perhaps point P is the Fermat-Toricelli point of triangle ABC? In an equilateral triangle, the Fermat-Toricelli point coincides with the centroid, but the centroid has angles of 120° between the lines to the vertices? Wait, no, in an equilateral triangle, the centroid is also the Fermat-Toricelli point, but the angles at the Fermat-Toricelli point between the lines to the vertices are all 120°, but in this case, the problem specifies angle BPC = 120°, so perhaps P is the Fermat-Toricelli point? Wait, but in an equilateral triangle, the Fermat-Toricelli point is the centroid, but the centroid has angles of 120°? Wait, no, actually in an equilateral triangle, the centroid, circumcenter, etc., are all the same point, and the angles from the centroid to the vertices are 120°? Wait, actually, no.\n\nWait, in an equilateral triangle, if you take the centroid, which is also the circumcenter, then the angles between the lines from the centroid to the vertices are actually 120°, because the triangle is symmetric. Let me confirm.\n\nIn an equilateral triangle, the centroid divides the medians in a 2:1 ratio. The angle between two medians from the centroid to two vertices... Hmm, actually, in an equilateral triangle, the medians are also the altitudes and angle bisectors, and they intersect at 60° angles? Wait, no, actually, the angle between two medians in an equilateral triangle would be 60°, because the triangle is equiangular. Wait, perhaps not.\n\nWait, let me think of coordinates again. Let's take our coordinate system where A is (0, 3√3), B is (-3, 0), C is (3, 0). The centroid is at (0, √3). Let me compute the angle at the centroid between points B and C.\n\nVectors from centroid to B and centroid to C:\n\nVector CB_centroid = B - centroid = (-3 - 0, 0 - √3) = (-3, -√3)\n\nVector CC_centroid = C - centroid = (3 - 0, 0 - √3) = (3, -√3)\n\nWait, actually, angle at centroid between points B and C would be the angle between vectors from centroid to B and centroid to C.\n\nSo vectors:\n\nFrom centroid O_circum (0, √3) to B (-3, 0): vector is (-3, -√3)\n\nFrom centroid O_circum to C (3, 0): vector is (3, -√3)\n\nCompute the angle between these two vectors.\n\nThe dot product is (-3)(3) + (-√3)(-√3) = -9 + (3) = -6\n\nThe magnitudes of both vectors are equal since the triangle is symmetric.\n\n|vector OB| = sqrt( (-3)^2 + (-√3)^2 ) = sqrt(9 + 3) = sqrt(12) = 2√3\n\nSimilarly, |vector OC| = sqrt(3^2 + (-√3)^2) = sqrt(9 + 3) = sqrt(12) = 2√3\n\nTherefore, the cosine of the angle θ between them is:\n\ndot product / ( |OB| |OC| ) = (-6) / ( (2√3)(2√3) ) = (-6)/(12) = -0.5\n\nTherefore, θ = arccos(-0.5) = 120°, so yes, the angle at the centroid between points B and C is 120°. Similarly, the angles at the centroid between any two vertices are 120°, since the triangle is equilateral. Therefore, the centroid is indeed the Fermat-Toricelli point of the equilateral triangle, as all angles from the point to the vertices are 120°, but in this case, the problem states that angle BPC = 120°, so perhaps point P is the centroid? But in that case, if P is the centroid, then we can compute AP, then find X accordingly?\n\nWait, but if P is the centroid, then AP would be the distance from A to centroid. Let me compute that.\n\nIn coordinates, A is (0, 3√3), centroid is (0, √3), so distance AP is sqrt( (0 - 0)^2 + (3√3 - √3)^2 ) = sqrt( (2√3)^2 ) = 2√3. Therefore, AP = 2√3. Then, if we consider the circle with diameter AP, which would have center at midpoint of AP: (0, (3√3 + √3)/2 ) = (0, (4√3)/2 ) = (0, 2√3). The radius is half of AP, so √3.\n\nSo the circle with diameter AP is centered at (0, 2√3) with radius √3.\n\nNow, let's see where this circle intersects the circumcircle of ABC again. The circumcircle of ABC is centered at (0, √3) with radius 2√3.\n\nSo, the two circles:\n\n1. Circle 1: center (0, 2√3), radius √3\n\n2. Circle 2: center (0, √3), radius 2√3\n\nWe can find their intersection points.\n\nSince both circles are centered along the y-axis, their intersection points will be symmetric with respect to the y-axis, so either on the y-axis or symmetric across y-axis. But since point A is (0, 3√3), which is on both circles?\n\nWait, let's check if point A is on both circles.\n\nFor Circle 1: distance from center (0, 2√3) to A (0, 3√3) is |3√3 - 2√3| = √3, which is the radius, so yes, A is on Circle 1.\n\nFor Circle 2: distance from center (0, √3) to A (0, 3√3) is |3√3 - √3| = 2√3, which is the radius, so yes, A is on both circles.\n\nThe other intersection point X should be diametrically opposite to A on one of the circles? Wait, but let's compute it.\n\nLet me write equations for both circles:\n\nCircle 1: (x - 0)^2 + (y - 2√3)^2 = (√3)^2 => x² + (y - 2√3)^2 = 3\n\nCircle 2: x² + (y - √3)^2 = (2√3)^2 => x² + (y - √3)^2 = 12\n\nSubtract equation of Circle 1 from equation of Circle 2:\n\n[ x² + (y - √3)^2 ] - [ x² + (y - 2√3)^2 ] = 12 - 3\n\nSimplify left side:\n\n(y - √3)^2 - (y - 2√3)^2\n\nExpand both squares:\n\n= [ y² - 2√3 y + (√3)^2 ] - [ y² - 4√3 y + (2√3)^2 ]\n\n= [ y² - 2√3 y + 3 ] - [ y² - 4√3 y + 12 ]\n\n= y² - 2√3 y + 3 - y² + 4√3 y - 12\n\n= ( -2√3 y + 4√3 y ) + (3 - 12 )\n\n= 2√3 y - 9\n\nSet equal to 9 (since 12 - 3 = 9):\n\n2√3 y - 9 = 9\n\n2√3 y = 18\n\ny = 18 / (2√3) = 9 / √3 = 3√3\n\nWait, but that's the y-coordinate of point A. So the only solution is y = 3√3, which corresponds to point A. But we were expecting another intersection point X. Hmm, so this suggests that the two circles are tangent at point A? But that can't be, unless they are tangent there.\n\nWait, let me check the distance between centers: Circle 1 center is (0, 2√3), Circle 2 center is (0, √3). The distance between centers is |2√3 - √3| = √3. The sum of the radii is √3 + 2√3 = 3√3, and the difference of radii is 2√3 - √3 = √3. Since the distance between centers is equal to the difference of the radii, so the smaller circle is entirely within the larger one and tangent at one point? Wait, the distance between centers is √3, and the radius of the larger circle is 2√3, so the distance from the center of the larger circle to the center of the smaller circle is √3, which is less than the radius of the larger circle (2√3). The radius of the smaller circle is √3, so the distance between centers plus the radius of the smaller circle is √3 + √3 = 2√3, which is equal to the radius of the larger circle. Therefore, the two circles are tangent externally at one point. Wait, but in this case, since the center of the smaller circle is inside the larger circle (distance between centers √3 < 2√3), then actually, the smaller circle is inside the larger one, and they are tangent at one point? Wait, the distance between centers is √3, and the radius of the larger circle is 2√3, so the smaller circle's center is inside the larger circle. The radius of the smaller circle is √3, so the maximum distance from the larger circle's center to any point on the smaller circle is √3 (distance between centers) + √3 (radius of smaller circle) = 2√3, which is exactly the radius of the larger circle. Therefore, the two circles touch at exactly one point, which is along the line connecting their centers, i.e., along the y-axis. Therefore, the only intersection point is at the top point, which is point A (0, 3√3). Therefore, if P is the centroid, then the circle with diameter AP only meets the circumcircle again at A, so there is no other point X. But in the problem statement, it says \"the circle with diameter AP meets the circumcircle of triangle ABC again at X ≠ A\", so if P is the centroid, there is no such X, which contradicts the problem's condition. Therefore, P is not the centroid. Therefore, P is not the Fermat-Toricelli point in this case, or at least not the centroid.\n\nHmm, so that approach didn't work because assuming P is the centroid led to a contradiction with the problem's stipulation that there is another intersection point X. Therefore, P must be a different point inside the triangle where angle BPC is 120°, and the circle with diameter AP intersects the circumcircle again at X ≠ A, with AX = 5.\n\nHmm, so maybe I need to proceed with coordinates but with more patience.\n\nLet me recap:\n\nWe have coordinates for A, B, C as:\n\nA: (0, 3√3)\n\nB: (-3, 0)\n\nC: (3, 0)\n\nCentroid (circumcenter): O_circum = (0, √3)\n\nCircumradius R = 2√3.\n\nPoint P is (p, q) inside triangle ABC, with angle BPC = 120°, so we have the relation from Law of Cosines:\n\n36 = PB² + PC² + PB*PC\n\nAdditionally, we need to consider the circle with diameter AP, which intersects the circumcircle again at X ≠ A, and AX = 5.\n\nWe also know from the right angle at X in triangle AX P that AX² + XP² = AP².\n\nGiven AX = 5, so XP² = AP² - 25, so XP = sqrt(AP² - 25). So, if we can find AP, we can find XP.\n\nTherefore, the key is to find AP.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can find coordinates for X in terms of P, then use AX = 5 to get an equation.\n\nAlternatively, parametrize point X on the circumcircle such that AX = 5, then see where it lies with respect to the other circle.\n\nLet me think: since X is on the circumcircle of ABC, which has center (0, √3) and radius 2√3, so coordinates of X must satisfy x² + (y - √3)^2 = 12.\n\nAlso, AX = 5, so the distance from A (0, 3√3) to X (x, y) is 5:\n\nsqrt( (x - 0)^2 + (y - 3√3)^2 ) = 5\n\nSo,\n\nx² + (y - 3√3)^2 = 25.\n\nTherefore, we have two equations for point X:\n\n1. x² + (y - √3)^2 = 12\n\n2. x² + (y - 3√3)^2 = 25\n\nSubtract equation 1 from equation 2:\n\n[ x² + (y - 3√3)^2 ] - [ x² + (y - √3)^2 ] = 25 - 12 = 13\n\nExpand the left side:\n\n(y - 3√3)^2 - (y - √3)^2\n\n= [ y² - 6√3 y + (3√3)^2 ] - [ y² - 2√3 y + (√3)^2 ]\n\n= [ y² - 6√3 y + 27 ] - [ y² - 2√3 y + 3 ]\n\n= y² - 6√3 y + 27 - y² + 2√3 y - 3\n\n= (-4√3 y) + 24\n\nTherefore,\n\n-4√3 y + 24 = 13\n\nSo,\n\n-4√3 y = 13 - 24 = -11\n\nThus,\n\ny = (-11)/(-4√3) = 11/(4√3) = (11√3)/12\n\nSo, the y-coordinate of point X is (11√3)/12.\n\nNow, substitute this back into one of the equations to find x.\n\nLet's use equation 1: x² + (y - √3)^2 = 12\n\nFirst compute y - √3:\n\ny - √3 = (11√3)/12 - √3 = (11√3)/12 - (12√3)/12 = (-√3)/12\n\nTherefore,\n\n(y - √3)^2 = ( (-√3)/12 )^2 = (3)/144 = 1/48\n\nTherefore,\n\nx² + 1/48 = 12\n\nThus,\n\nx² = 12 - 1/48 = (576/48 - 1/48) = 575/48\n\nTherefore,\n\nx = ±√(575/48) = ±(√575)/ (4√3) = ±(5√23)/ (4√3) [Wait, let me compute √575. 575 factors: 25*23, so √575 = 5√23. So yes.]\n\nSimplify:\n\nx = ± (5√23)/(4√3) = ± (5√69)/12 [rationalizing the denominator: multiply numerator and denominator by √3:\n\n(5√23 * √3)/(4√3 * √3) = (5√69)/(4*3) = (5√69)/12 ]\n\nWait, √23 * √3 = √(69), yes. So,\n\nx = ± (5√69)/12\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of point X are:\n\nX = ( ± (5√69)/12 , (11√3)/12 )\n\nHmm, okay. So there are two possible points X, symmetric across the y-axis. Since the problem doesn't specify which one, but since the triangle is equilateral and the problem is symmetric, perhaps both would give the same XP distance? Or maybe not, depending on where P is. Hmm.\n\nBut we need more information to determine which one it is. Alternatively, perhaps we can proceed without worrying about the sign, since when we compute XP, the distance will be the same regardless of the sign? Wait, no, because P is a specific point inside the triangle, so depending on which X is chosen (left or right), the distance XP might differ. But since the problem states \"the circle with diameter AP meets the circumcircle again at X ≠ A\", so there are two intersection points, A and X, so X is uniquely determined once P is fixed. But since we don't know P yet, perhaps both possibilities need to be considered? Hmm, this is getting complicated.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can proceed by denoting X as (x, y) with y = 11√3 /12 and x = ±5√69 /12, and then express the condition that X lies on the circle with diameter AP, which gives an equation involving p and q (coordinates of P). Then, along with the earlier equation from angle BPC = 120°, we can solve for p and q, then compute AP and hence XP.\n\nThis is going to be algebraically intensive, but let's try.\n\nFirst, let me note that coordinates of X are ( ±5√69 /12 , 11√3 /12 ). Let me denote X as (x_X, y_X), where:\n\nx_X = ± (5√69)/12\n\ny_X = (11√3)/12\n\nWe can write the equation that X lies on the circle with diameter AP. The circle with diameter AP has the equation:\n\n(x - p/2)^2 + (y - (3√3 + q)/2)^2 = ( (1/2) * sqrt(p² + (q - 3√3)^2 ) )²\n\nBut we already simplified this earlier to:\n\nx² + y² - p x - (3√3 + q)y + 3√3 q = 0\n\nSo, substituting the coordinates of X into this equation:\n\nx_X² + y_X² - p x_X - (3√3 + q)y_X + 3√3 q = 0\n\nLet me compute each term step by step.\n\nFirst, compute x_X² and y_X²:\n\nx_X² = [ (5√69)/12 ]² = (25 * 69) / (144) = (1725)/144\n\nSimilarly, since the other X is with the negative x-coordinate, x_X² will be the same.\n\ny_X² = [ (11√3)/12 ]² = (121 * 3)/144 = 363/144\n\nTherefore, x_X² + y_X² = (1725 + 363)/144 = 2088/144\n\nSimplify:\n\n2088 ÷ 12 = 174, 144 ÷12 =12, so 174/12 = 14.5? Wait, 2088 ÷ 12 = 174, so 2088/144 = 174/12 = 29/2? Wait, 174 divided by 12: 12*14 = 168, so 174 - 168 = 6, so 14 and 6/12 = 14.5, which is 29/2. Wait, but 2088 divided by 144:\n\nWait, 144 * 14 = 2016, so 2088 - 2016 = 72, so 2088/144 = 14 + 72/144 = 14 + 0.5 = 14.5 = 29/2. Yes, so x_X² + y_X² = 29/2.\n\nWait, 29/2? Let me confirm:\n\nWait, 25*69: 25*70 = 1750, minus 25 = 1725. 1725 + 363 = 2088. 2088 divided by 144:\n\nDivide numerator and denominator by 12: 2088 ÷12 = 174, 144 ÷12 =12. So 174/12 = 14.5 = 29/2. Yes, that's correct.\n\nSo x_X² + y_X² = 29/2.\n\nNext term: -p x_X\n\nWe have x_X = ±5√69 /12, so this term is -p*(±5√69 /12 )\n\nSimilarly, next term: -(3√3 + q) y_X\n\nCompute y_X = 11√3 /12, so:\n\n-(3√3 + q)*(11√3 /12 )\n\nLet me compute this:\n\nFirst, distribute:\n\n= - [ 3√3 * 11√3 /12 + q * 11√3 /12 ]\n\n= - [ (33 * 3) /12 + (11√3 q)/12 ]\n\nWait, because √3 * √3 = 3, so 3√3 * 11√3 = 3*11*(√3)^2 = 33*3 = 99.\n\nWait, let me do it step by step:\n\nFirst term inside the brackets: 3√3 * (11√3)/12 = 3*11*(√3)^2 /12 = 33*3 /12 = 99/12\n\nSecond term: q * (11√3)/12 = (11√3 q)/12\n\nTherefore, the entire term is:\n\n- [ 99/12 + (11√3 q)/12 ] = -99/12 - (11√3 q)/12\n\nThird term: +3√3 q\n\nTherefore, putting all terms together:\n\nx_X² + y_X² - p x_X - (3√3 + q)y_X + 3√3 q =\n\n(29/2) - p x_X - 99/12 - (11√3 q)/12 + 3√3 q\n\nNow, combine like terms:\n\nFirst, constants: 29/2 - 99/12\n\nConvert to twelfths:\n\n29/2 = 174/12\n\n174/12 - 99/12 = 75/12 = 25/4\n\nNext, terms with q:\n\n- (11√3 q)/12 + 3√3 q\n\nConvert 3√3 q to twelfths:\n\n3√3 q = (36√3 q)/12\n\nTherefore,\n\n[ -11√3 q + 36√3 q ] /12 = (25√3 q)/12\n\nThen, the term with p x_X: - p x_X\n\nBut x_X is ±5√69 /12, so:\n\n- p*(±5√69 /12 )\n\nTherefore, the entire equation becomes:\n\n25/4 + (25√3 q)/12 - p*(±5√69 /12 ) = 0\n\nMultiply both sides by 12 to eliminate denominators:\n\n12*(25/4) + 12*(25√3 q)/12 - 12*(±5√69 p)/12 = 0\n\nSimplify each term:\n\n12*(25/4) = 3*25 = 75\n\n12*(25√3 q)/12 = 25√3 q\n\n12*(±5√69 p)/12 = ±5√69 p\n\nTherefore, the equation becomes:\n\n75 + 25√3 q ∓ 5√69 p = 0\n\nSo,\n\n±5√69 p = 75 + 25√3 q\n\nDivide both sides by 5:\n\n±√69 p = 15 + 5√3 q\n\nThus,\n\np = [15 + 5√3 q] / (±√69 )\n\nHmm, okay, so we have an expression for p in terms of q, with a ± sign depending on the choice of X's x-coordinate (positive or negative). Since the problem doesn't specify, we might need to consider both cases, but perhaps due to symmetry, both will lead to the same XP length? Or perhaps only one will be valid given that P is inside the triangle.\n\nLet me note that point P is inside the triangle ABC, so its coordinates (p, q) must satisfy certain conditions. Specifically, since ABC has vertices at (0, 3√3), (-3, 0), and (3, 0), the point P must lie within the boundaries defined by the triangle.\n\nGiven that the triangle's base is from (-3, 0) to (3, 0), and the apex at (0, 3√3), the y-coordinate q of point P must be between 0 and 3√3, and the x-coordinate p must be between -3 and 3, but also such that the point is inside the triangle. More precisely, for a point inside the triangle, the coordinates must satisfy the inequalities defining the triangle.\n\nAlternatively, since we have expressions involving p and q, and we also have the earlier equation from the Law of Cosines in triangle BPC:\n\n36 = PB² + PC² + PB*PC\n\nWhich in coordinates became:\n\nPB² + PC² + PB*PC = 36\n\nWhere PB² = (p + 3)^2 + q^2\n\nPC² = (p - 3)^2 + q^2\n\nAnd PB*PC we had earlier as 18 - 2p² - 2q²\n\nWait, let me recall:\n\nEarlier, we had:\n\nPB² + PC² = 2p² + 2q² + 18\n\nAnd from the Law of Cosines:\n\n36 = (PB² + PC²) + PB*PC\n\nTherefore,\n\n36 = (2p² + 2q² + 18) + PB*PC\n\nSo,\n\nPB*PC = 18 - 2p² - 2q²\n\nTherefore, substituting into the equation:\n\nPB² + PC² + PB*PC = (2p² + 2q² + 18) + (18 - 2p² - 2q²) = 36, which checks out, so that equation is just an identity and gives no new information. Therefore, we need another equation to relate p and q.\n\nThe other equation we have is from the condition that X lies on the circle with diameter AP, leading to the equation:\n\n±√69 p = 15 + 5√3 q\n\nSo, depending on the sign of x_X (positive or negative), we have two possibilities:\n\nCase 1: x_X positive, so the equation is:\n\n√69 p = 15 + 5√3 q → p = (15 + 5√3 q)/√69\n\nCase 2: x_X negative, so the equation is:\n\n-√69 p = 15 + 5√3 q → p = - (15 + 5√3 q)/√69\n\nTherefore, we have two cases. Let me consider Case 1 first, then Case 2.\n\nBut also, we need to ensure that point P is inside the triangle. Let's see.\n\nFirst, let me rationalize the expressions for p in terms of q.\n\nIn Case 1:\n\np = (15 + 5√3 q)/√69\n\nWe can write this as:\n\np = [15/√69] + [5√3 / √69] q\n\nSimilarly, in Case 2:\n\np = - [15/√69] - [5√3 / √69] q\n\nHmm, perhaps we can write these as:\n\nCase 1: p = (15 + 5√3 q)/√69\n\nCase 2: p = - (15 + 5√3 q)/√69\n\nAlternatively, to make it simpler, let me denote k = 5/√69, then:\n\nCase 1: p = 3k + k√3 q (since 15/√69 = 3*(5)/√69 = 3k, and 5√3 /√69 = k√3 )\n\nWait, actually, 5/√69 multiplied by √3 is 5√3 /√69, so:\n\nCase 1: p = (15/√69) + (5√3 /√69) q = (15 + 5√3 q)/√69\n\nSimilarly, Case 2: p = - (15 + 5√3 q)/√69\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to leave it as is for now.\n\nNow, we need another equation relating p and q. The only other equation we have is the Law of Cosines in triangle BPC, but we saw that it reduces to an identity. Therefore, we must have missed another condition?\n\nWait a second, perhaps the fact that point P is inside the triangle imposes some constraints on p and q, but that might not give an equation. Alternatively, maybe we need to use the expression for AP in terms of p and q?\n\nWait, recall that in triangle AX P, which is right-angled at X, we have AX = 5, so AP^2 = AX^2 + XP^2. But we need another relation involving AP. Alternatively, perhaps we can express AP in terms of p and q, and then relate it via coordinates?\n\nAP is the distance from A(0, 3√3) to P(p, q):\n\nAP² = p² + (q - 3√3)^2\n\nSimilarly, XP is the distance from X(x_X, y_X) to P(p, q):\n\nXP² = (x_X - p)^2 + (y_X - q)^2\n\nBut from the right triangle AX P, we have:\n\nAX² + XP² = AP² → 25 + XP² = AP² → XP² = AP² - 25\n\nBut also, XP² is as above:\n\nXP² = (x_X - p)^2 + (y_X - q)^2\n\nTherefore,\n\n(x_X - p)^2 + (y_X - q)^2 = AP² - 25\n\nBut AP² = p² + (q - 3√3)^2, so:\n\n(x_X - p)^2 + (y_X - q)^2 = p² + (q - 3√3)^2 - 25\n\nExpand the left side:\n\nx_X² - 2 x_X p + p² + y_X² - 2 y_X q + q² = p² + q² - 6√3 q + 27 - 25\n\nSimplify left side:\n\nx_X² + y_X² - 2 x_X p - 2 y_X q + p² + q²\n\nRight side:\n\np² + q² - 6√3 q + 2\n\nSubtract p² + q² from both sides:\n\nx_X² + y_X² - 2 x_X p - 2 y_X q = -6√3 q + 2\n\nNow, recall from earlier that x_X² + y_X² = 29/2, as computed before.\n\nSubstituting:\n\n29/2 - 2 x_X p - 2 y_X q = -6√3 q + 2\n\nBring all terms to the left:\n\n29/2 - 2 x_X p - 2 y_X q + 6√3 q - 2 = 0\n\nSimplify constants: 29/2 - 2 = 29/2 - 4/2 = 25/2\n\nTerms with q: -2 y_X q + 6√3 q\n\nTerms with p: -2 x_X p\n\nSo,\n\n25/2 - 2 x_X p + q(-2 y_X + 6√3) = 0\n\nNow, substitute the known values of x_X and y_X:\n\nFirst, compute -2 y_X + 6√3:\n\ny_X = 11√3 /12, so:\n\n-2*(11√3 /12) + 6√3 = (-22√3)/12 + 6√3 = (-11√3)/6 + 6√3 = (-11√3)/6 + 36√3/6 = (25√3)/6\n\nSimilarly, compute -2 x_X p:\n\nBut x_X is either ±5√69 /12, so:\n\n-2 x_X p = -2*(±5√69 /12)*p = ∓ (5√69 /6)*p\n\nTherefore, substituting into the equation:\n\n25/2 ∓ (5√69 /6)*p + q*(25√3 /6 ) = 0\n\nMultiply both sides by 6 to eliminate denominators:\n\n25/2 *6 ∓ 5√69 p + 25√3 q = 0\n\nCompute 25/2 *6 = 75\n\nThus:\n\n75 ∓ 5√69 p + 25√3 q = 0\n\nDivide both sides by 5:\n\n15 ∓ √69 p + 5√3 q = 0\n\nTherefore,\n\n∓ √69 p = -15 -5√3 q\n\nMultiply both sides by -1:\n\n±√69 p = 15 +5√3 q\n\nWait a second! This is exactly the same equation we derived earlier from the condition that X lies on the circle with diameter AP. So this doesn't give us a new equation—it’s the same as before. Therefore, this approach is not yielding a new equation, meaning we still need another relation to solve for p and q.\n\nHmm, so we have two equations:\n\n1. From the condition that X lies on the circle with diameter AP: ±√69 p = 15 +5√3 q (depending on the sign of x_X)\n\n2. From the Law of Cosines in triangle BPC, which reduced to an identity, so no new info.\n\nWait, but we also have the coordinates of P must satisfy the triangle inequalities or be inside the triangle, but that's more of a constraint than an equation.\n\nWait, perhaps we need to use the fact that point P is inside the triangle ABC? So its coordinates must satisfy certain inequalities. For instance, in barycentric coordinates or in terms of the edges.\n\nAlternatively, since we have an expression for p in terms of q (from equation 1), we can substitute that into some other condition.\n\nLet me consider Case 1 first: where x_X is positive, so we have:\n\n√69 p = 15 +5√3 q → p = (15 +5√3 q)/√69\n\nThen, we can substitute this into the expression for AP², which is needed for the right triangle AX P, but I'm not sure. Alternatively, perhaps we can use the fact that point P lies inside the triangle to set constraints on p and q?\n\nAlternatively, since we have p expressed in terms of q, perhaps we can find another relation involving q by using the position of P inside the triangle?\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a property or theorem I'm missing that could relate these elements more directly?\n\nWait, let me think differently. Since we know coordinates of X, and we know coordinates of A and P must satisfy the circle condition, perhaps we can parametrize P along the line defined by the equation ±√69 p = 15 +5√3 q and then find where it intersects some other constraint?\n\nAlternatively, since we have only one equation relating p and q, and another implicit constraint from the triangle BPC, but that only gave an identity, perhaps we need to consider that in the Law of Cosines equation, although it reduced to an identity, perhaps combined with the expression for PB*PC from earlier?\n\nWait, recall that PB*PC = 18 - 2p² - 2q²\n\nAlso, from the Law of Cosines in triangle BPC:\n\nPB² + PC² + PB*PC = 36\n\nBut PB² + PC² = 2p² + 2q² + 18, so substituting:\n\n(2p² + 2q² + 18) + (18 - 2p² - 2q²) = 36 → 36 = 36, which is just confirming.\n\nHmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use coordinates of P and the fact that it's inside the triangle to set up inequalities, but that might not be algebraically helpful.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider that in the expression for p in terms of q (Case 1):\n\np = (15 +5√3 q)/√69\n\nLet me rationalize this expression or simplify it:\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator by √69:\n\np = [15√69 + 5√3 * √69 q ] / 69\n\nSimplify √3 * √69 = √(3*69) = √207 = √(9*23) = 3√23\n\nTherefore,\n\np = [15√69 + 5*3√23 q ] / 69 = [15√69 + 15√23 q ] / 69 = 15(√69 + √23 q)/69 = (5/23)(√69 + √23 q )\n\nHmm, not sure if that helps.\n\nAlternatively, let me denote variables:\n\nLet me let’s suppose we are in Case 1, where x_X is positive. Then p = (15 +5√3 q)/√69.\n\nWe can plug this expression for p into the expression for AP², which is p² + (q - 3√3)^2.\n\nBut we also know from the right triangle AX P that AP² = AX² + XP² = 25 + XP², but XP is what we need to find. Hmm, not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps express XP in terms of p and q?\n\nWait, XP is the distance from X to P, so:\n\nXP² = (x_X - p)^2 + (y_X - q)^2\n\nWe can write this as:\n\nXP² = x_X² - 2 x_X p + p² + y_X² - 2 y_X q + q²\n\nBut we know from earlier that x_X² + y_X² = 29/2, so:\n\nXP² = 29/2 - 2 x_X p - 2 y_X q + p² + q²\n\nBut also, AP² = p² + (q - 3√3)^2 = p² + q² - 6√3 q + 27\n\nThus, from the right triangle:\n\nAP² = 25 + XP² → p² + q² - 6√3 q + 27 = 25 + [29/2 - 2 x_X p - 2 y_X q + p² + q²]\n\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\n25 + 29/2 - 2 x_X p - 2 y_X q + p² + q²\n\n= (25 + 14.5) + (-2 x_X p - 2 y_X q) + p² + q²\n\n= 39.5 - 2 x_X p - 2 y_X q + p² + q²\n\nSet equal to left-hand side:\n\np² + q² - 6√3 q + 27 = 39.5 - 2 x_X p - 2 y_X q + p² + q²\n\nSubtract p² + q² from both sides:\n\n-6√3 q + 27 = 39.5 - 2 x_X p - 2 y_X q\n\nRearrange terms:\n\n-6√3 q + 2 y_X q + 2 x_X p = 39.5 - 27\n\nCompute constants: 39.5 - 27 = 12.5 = 25/2\n\nThus,\n\nq(-6√3 + 2 y_X) + 2 x_X p = 25/2\n\nNow, substitute y_X = 11√3 /12 and x_X = 5√69 /12 (Case 1):\n\nCompute coefficients:\n\nFirst term coefficient for q:\n\n-6√3 + 2*(11√3 /12) = -6√3 + (22√3)/12 = -6√3 + (11√3)/6\n\nConvert to sixths:\n\n-6√3 = -36√3/6, so:\n\n-36√3/6 + 11√3/6 = (-25√3)/6\n\nSecond term: 2 x_X p = 2*(5√69 /12)*p = (5√69 /6)*p\n\nTherefore, equation becomes:\n\n(-25√3 /6) q + (5√69 /6) p = 25/2\n\nMultiply both sides by 6 to eliminate denominators:\n\n-25√3 q + 5√69 p = 75\n\nNow, recall from Case 1 that we have:\n\n√69 p = 15 +5√3 q → p = (15 +5√3 q)/√69\n\nSubstitute this into the above equation:\n\n-25√3 q + 5√69 * [ (15 +5√3 q)/√69 ] = 75\n\nSimplify the second term:\n\n5√69 / √69 = 5, so:\n\n5√69 * [ (15 +5√3 q)/√69 ] = 5*(15 +5√3 q) = 75 + 25√3 q\n\nTherefore, the equation becomes:\n\n-25√3 q + 75 +25√3 q = 75\n\nSimplify:\n\n(-25√3 q +25√3 q ) +75 = 75 → 0 +75 =75 → 75=75\n\nAgain, this reduces to an identity. So, this approach also does not yield a new equation. Hmm.\n\nThis suggests that the system of equations is underdetermined, which likely means I need to consider another condition or perhaps there's a geometric insight I'm missing.\n\nWait a minute, perhaps the key is that point P must lie on the circumcircle of triangle BPC? No, angle BPC is 120°, but unless it's a specific circle.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using trigonometric Ceva's theorem? Since P is inside triangle ABC with certain angle conditions.\n\nWait, Ceva's theorem relates to concurrent lines, but here we have an angle condition. Maybe trigonometric Ceva?\n\nTrigonometric Ceva's theorem states that for concurrent lines from vertices to opposite sides, the product of the sines of the angles is equal. But I'm not sure if that applies here.\n\nAlternatively, since angle BPC = 120°, and ABC is equilateral, perhaps there's a way to relate the distances PB, PC, and other sides.\n\nWait, in triangle BPC, we know BC = 6, angle at P is 120°, so using the Law of Sines:\n\nIn triangle BPC,\n\nBC / sin(angle BPC) = BP / sin(angle BCP) = PC / sin(angle CBP)\n\nWe know BC = 6, angle BPC = 120°, so:\n\n6 / sin(120°) = BP / sin(angle BCP) = PC / sin(angle CBP)\n\nSin(120°) = √3 / 2, so:\n\n6 / (√3/2) = 12 / √3 = 4√3 = BP / sin(angle BCP) = PC / sin(angle CBP)\n\nTherefore,\n\nBP = 4√3 sin(angle BCP)\n\nPC = 4√3 sin(angle CBP)\n\nAlso, in triangle ABC, angles at B and C are 60°, so angles at B and C related to point P?\n\nWait, angle CBP is part of angle ABC, which is 60°, so angle CBP + angle PBA = 60°, but I don't know angle PBA.\n\nSimilarly, angle BCP is part of angle BCA = 60°, so angle BCP + angle PCA = 60°.\n\nHmm, this might not be straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, let me denote angle CBP = α and angle BCP = β.\n\nThen, from above, we have:\n\nBP = 4√3 sin β\n\nPC = 4√3 sin α\n\nAlso, in triangle BPC, the angles at B and C are α and β respectively, so angle at P is 120°, so α + β + 120° = 180°, so α + β = 60°.\n\nTherefore, β = 60° - α.\n\nTherefore, PC = 4√3 sin α\n\nBP = 4√3 sin(60° - α)\n\nAlso, in triangle ABC, at vertex B, the angles are:\n\nangle ABC = 60°, which is split into angle CBP = α and angle ABP = 60° - α.\n\nSimilarly, at vertex C, angle BCA = 60°, split into angle BCP = β = 60° - α and angle ACP = 60° - β = 60° - (60° - α) = α.\n\nTherefore, angle at A related to point P? Maybe using the Law of Sines in other triangles.\n\nAlternatively, consider triangle APB and APC?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps using coordinates again but with the expressions we have.\n\nRecall that in Case 1, we have p expressed in terms of q:\n\np = (15 +5√3 q)/√69\n\nAnd we also have the expression for AP² = p² + (q - 3√3)^2\n\nBut we also know from the right triangle AX P that AP² = 25 + XP², but XP is what we need. However, if we can express XP in terms of p and q, and then relate it back?\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to consider that since we have p in terms of q, we can pick a variable substitution and attempt to find q such that point P lies inside the triangle.\n\nGiven that in Case 1, p = (15 +5√3 q)/√69\n\nWe need to ensure that point P(p, q) is inside triangle ABC.\n\nThe triangle ABC has vertices at (0, 3√3), (-3, 0), and (3, 0). To be inside the triangle, the coordinates (p, q) must satisfy:\n\n1. Below the line AB: The line from A(0, 3√3) to B(-3, 0). The equation of line AB can be found as follows.\n\nSlope of AB: (0 - 3√3)/(-3 - 0) = (-3√3)/(-3) = √3\n\nEquation: y - 3√3 = √3(x - 0) → y = √3 x + 3√3\n\nSimilarly, the line AC has slope (0 - 3√3)/(3 - 0) = -√3, equation: y - 3√3 = -√3(x - 0) → y = -√3 x + 3√3\n\nThe line BC is the base from (-3, 0) to (3, 0), which is y = 0.\n\nTherefore, to be inside the triangle, the point P(p, q) must satisfy:\n\n1. q ≥ 0 (above the base BC)\n\n2. Below the lines AB and AC:\n\nFor the left side (below AB):\n\nq ≤ √3 p + 3√3 (since AB is y = √3 x + 3√3, and since we are to the right of B, but actually, since P is inside, depending on p's sign).\n\nWait, actually, the inequalities depend on the region.\n\nActually, for a point inside the triangle ABC, it must satisfy:\n\n- For x between -3 and 3,\n\n- And below both lines AB and AC.\n\nSpecifically:\n\nIf p is between -3 and 0 (left half of the triangle), then it must be below AB: q ≤ √3 p + 3√3\n\nAnd above the line from B to C (which is y=0), so q ≥ 0.\n\nIf p is between 0 and 3 (right half), then it must be below AC: q ≤ -√3 p + 3√3, and q ≥ 0.\n\nAdditionally, the sum of the distances from the sides or something, but maybe the inequalities above are sufficient.\n\nTherefore, in Case 1, with p expressed in terms of q, we can set up inequalities to bound q.\n\nLet me first consider Case 1: p = (15 +5√3 q)/√69\n\nWe can attempt to find the range of q such that P(p, q) is inside the triangle.\n\nFirst, since q must be between 0 and 3√3 (the height of the triangle).\n\nAlso, p must be between -3 and 3.\n\nLet me first find when p = (15 +5√3 q)/√69 is within [-3, 3].\n\nSo:\n\n-3 ≤ (15 +5√3 q)/√69 ≤ 3\n\nMultiply all parts by √69:\n\n-3√69 ≤ 15 +5√3 q ≤ 3√69\n\nSubtract 15:\n\n-3√69 -15 ≤ 5√3 q ≤ 3√69 -15\n\nDivide by 5√3:\n\n[ -3√69 -15 ] / (5√3) ≤ q ≤ [ 3√69 -15 ] / (5√3 )\n\nThis gives the possible range for q in Case 1.\n\nBut this is quite messy. Maybe we can compute approximate numerical values to get an idea.\n\nFirst, compute √69 ≈ 8.306\n\n√3 ≈ 1.732\n\nCompute left side lower bound:\n\n-3√69 -15 ≈ -3*8.306 -15 ≈ -24.918 -15 ≈ -39.918\n\nDivide by 5√3 ≈ 5*1.732 ≈ 8.66\n\nSo lower bound ≈ -39.918 /8.66 ≈ -4.606\n\nUpper bound:\n\n3√69 -15 ≈ 3*8.306 -15 ≈ 24.918 -15 ≈ 9.918\n\nDivide by 5√3 ≈8.66:\n\n≈9.918 /8.66 ≈1.142\n\nTherefore, q must be between approximately -4.606 and 1.142, but since q must also be ≥0 and ≤3√3 ≈5.196, so the valid q range in this case is 0 ≤ q ≤1.142.\n\nSimilarly, in this case, p = (15 +5√3 q)/√69.\n\nLet me compute p when q=0:\n\np =15 /√69 ≈15 /8.306 ≈1.805\n\nWhen q=1.142 (approx upper limit):\n\nCompute q ≈1.142,\n\np ≈ [15 +5*1.732*1.142]/8.306\n\nCompute 5*1.732 ≈8.66,\n\n8.66 *1.142 ≈9.918,\n\nso numerator ≈15 +9.918 ≈24.918,\n\ndivided by 8.306 ≈24.918/8.306≈3.0\n\nSo at q≈1.142, p≈3.0, which is the vertex C. But since P must be inside the triangle, p must be less than 3, so q must be less than approximately 1.142.\n\nTherefore, in Case 1, q ranges from 0 to about 1.142, and p from about 1.805 down to 3.0 (but not reaching 3.0). Wait, actually when q increases, p increases?\n\nWait, when q increases, the term 5√3 q increases, so numerator increases, so p increases. So when q=0, p≈1.805; when q approaches 1.142, p approaches 3. So in this case, the point P moves from near (1.8, 0) up to near (3, ~1.14), staying in the right half of the triangle (since p is positive).\n\nNow, in this case, we might need to see if there's a specific value of q that also satisfies another condition? But since all our equations reduce to identities, perhaps any such P along this line would satisfy the given conditions except for the AX=5 constraint, but we already used AX=5 to derive the equation for p in terms of q. Therefore, perhaps any point along this line would satisfy AX=5? But that can't be, because X is fixed once we chose the positive x-coordinate. Wait no, actually, X was determined based on AX=5, so X is fixed. Therefore, for each P along that line, the circle with diameter AP would pass through X only for specific P?\n\nWait a minute, actually, given that X is fixed (since AX=5 and it's on the circumcircle), then the circle with diameter AP must pass through this specific X. So for each P, the circle with diameter AP either passes through X or not. Therefore, the locus of points P such that X lies on the circle with diameter AP is the line we derived: ±√69 p =15 +5√3 q. Since X is fixed, this is the set of P's such that X is on the circle with diameter AP. Therefore, the problem's conditions fix P along that line, but also P must satisfy the angle BPC=120° condition. However, the angle condition only gave us an identity, so it doesn't constrain P further. Therefore, there must be something wrong in my assumptions.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps both conditions (angle BPC=120° and X on the circle) together only allow for one specific P, which is determined by the intersection of the two lines?\n\nWait, but the angle BPC=120° condition only gave an identity when combined with the other equations, so perhaps it's automatically satisfied for any P on that line?\n\nWait, that can’t be. Probably made a mistake in derivation.\n\nWait, let me recap:\n\nWe used the Law of Cosines in triangle BPC to get an equation which turned out to be an identity when combined with other equations. This suggests that any point P lying on the radical axis (the line we derived) would satisfy the angle condition? That seems unlikely.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the angle BPC=120° is automatically satisfied for any P on that line? That seems improbable.\n\nWait, perhaps in this configuration, with X fixed and P lying on that particular line, the angle BPC is always 120°? That would mean the problem's conditions are over-constrained but consistent, leading to a unique P.\n\nAlternatively, maybe due to the specific length AX=5, there's only one such P that lies on the radical axis and also satisfies the angle condition, which reduces to a single point.\n\nBut how do we find it?\n\nSince in Case 1, we have p expressed in terms of q as p=(15 +5√3 q)/√69, and we need another condition to solve for q. But all other conditions seem to reduce to identities. Therefore, perhaps the only way this works is if the point P is such that the two conditions (angle BPC=120° and X lying on the circle) are both satisfied only at specific points, which can be found by ensuring that point P lies on the radical axis and also lies on the locus of points P such that angle BPC=120°.\n\nThe locus of points P such that angle BPC=120° is a circular arc. In triangle BPC, the locus of points P such that angle BPC=120° is a circular arc with BC as a chord. Specifically, the locus is the arc of the circumcircle of triangle BPC where the angle subtended by BC is 120°. Since in our case, angle at P is 120°, this locus is indeed a circular arc.\n\nThe center of this circle can be found using the fact that the central angle over arc BC would be 2*120°=240°, since the inscribed angle is half the central angle.\n\nTherefore, the center O' of this circle lies such that angle BOC = 240°, where O' is the center.\n\nAlternatively, constructing the circumcircle of triangle BPC with angle at P being 120°, but since BC is fixed, the locus is a circular arc.\n\nAlternatively, the set of points P such that angle BPC = 120° is the intersection of two circular arcs: one above BC and one below, but since P is inside the triangle ABC, it must lie on the arc above BC.\n\nThe equation of this locus can be derived, and then the intersection of this locus with the line we found earlier (from the circle condition) will give the coordinates of P.\n\nThis seems a feasible approach, albeit calculation-heavy.\n\nLet me try to derive the equation of the locus of points P such that angle BPC = 120°.\n\nGiven points B(-3, 0) and C(3, 0), we want all points P(x, y) such that angle BPC = 120°.\n\nUsing the Law of Cosines in triangle BPC:\n\nBC² = BP² + CP² - 2 BP * CP cos(120°)\n\nAs we did earlier, which simplifies to:\n\n36 = BP² + CP² + BP * CP\n\nWhich is the equation we had before.\n\nBut expressing this in coordinates:\n\nLet P = (p, q)\n\nThen,\n\nBP² = (p + 3)^2 + q^2\n\nCP² = (p - 3)^2 + q^2\n\nBP * CP = sqrt[(p+3)^2 + q^2] * sqrt[(p-3)^2 + q^2]\n\nTherefore, the equation is:\n\n36 = [ (p+3)^2 + q^2 ] + [ (p-3)^2 + q^2 ] + sqrt[ ((p+3)^2 + q^2)((p-3)^2 + q^2) ]\n\nThis equation is quite complicated due to the square root term. To eliminate the square root, perhaps we can square both sides after isolating the square root term, but this might get very messy.\n\nAlternatively, let me denote S = BP² + CP² and P = BP * CP, so the equation is 36 = S + P.\n\nWe already know that S = 2p² + 2q² + 18 (from earlier computation), so:\n\n36 = (2p² + 2q² + 18) + P ⇒ P = 18 - 2p² - 2q²\n\nTherefore, BP * CP = 18 - 2p² - 2q²\n\nBut BP * CP is also equal to sqrt[ ((p+3)^2 + q^2)((p-3)^2 + q^2) ]\n\nLet me compute the product inside the square root:\n\n[(p+3)^2 + q^2][(p-3)^2 + q^2]\n\nLet me expand this:\n\nLet me set A = (p+3)^2 + q^2 and B = (p-3)^2 + q^2\n\nThen, A * B = [ (p² +6p +9) + q² ] [ (p² -6p +9) + q² ]\n\nLet me set u = p² + q² +9, v = 6p\n\nThen A = u + v, B = u - v\n\nTherefore, A*B = u² - v² = (p² + q² +9)^2 - (6p)^2\n\nTherefore,\n\nsqrt(A * B) = sqrt[ (p² + q² +9)^2 - 36p² ]\n\nTherefore, the equation becomes:\n\nBP * CP = sqrt[ (p² + q² +9)^2 - 36p² ] = 18 - 2p² - 2q²\n\nLet me square both sides to eliminate the square root:\n\n(p² + q² +9)^2 - 36p² = (18 - 2p² - 2q²)^2\n\nExpand both sides.\n\nLeft side:\n\nLet me denote M = p² + q² +9, so left side is M² - 36p²\n\nRight side: (18 - 2(p² + q²))² = [18 - 2M + 18]^2? Wait, no:\n\nWait, M = p² + q² +9 ⇒ p² + q² = M -9\n\nTherefore, 18 - 2(p² + q²) = 18 - 2(M -9) = 18 -2M +18 = 36 - 2M\n\nTherefore, the right side is (36 - 2M)^2 = [2*(18 - M)]^2 = 4*(18 - M)^2\n\nTherefore, equation becomes:\n\nM² - 36p² = 4*(18 - M)^2\n\nExpand both sides:\n\nLeft side: M² - 36p²\n\nRight side: 4*(324 - 36M + M²) = 1296 - 144M + 4M²\n\nBring all terms to the left:\n\nM² - 36p² - 1296 + 144M -4M² = 0\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n-3M² +144M -36p² -1296 =0\n\nMultiply both sides by -1:\n\n3M² -144M +36p² +1296 =0\n\nDivide both sides by 3:\n\nM² -48M +12p² +432 =0\n\nRecall that M = p² + q² +9, so substitute back:\n\n(p² + q² +9)^2 -48(p² + q² +9) +12p² +432 =0\n\nExpand the first term:\n\nLet me denote N = p² + q², then M = N +9\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n(N +9)^2 -48(N +9) +12p² +432 =0\n\nExpand (N +9)^2 = N² + 18N +81\n\nSo:\n\nN² +18N +81 -48N -432 +12p² +432 =0\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\nN² + (18N -48N) + (81 -432 +432) +12p² =0\n\nWhich is:\n\nN² -30N +81 +12p² =0\n\nBut N = p² + q², so substitute back:\n\n(p² + q²)^2 -30(p² + q²) +81 +12p² =0\n\nExpand (p² + q²)^2:\n\np^4 + 2p² q² + q^4 -30p² -30q² +81 +12p² =0\n\nCombine like terms:\n\np^4 + 2p² q² + q^4 -18p² -30q² +81 =0\n\nThis is a quartic equation, which seems very complicated. This approach might not be the best.\n\nPerhaps there's a smarter way.\n\nWait, but recall that in Case 1, we have p expressed in terms of q: p = (15 +5√3 q)/√69.\n\nLet me substitute this expression into the quartic equation above to solve for q.\n\nLet me denote p = (15 +5√3 q)/√69\n\nLet me compute p²:\n\np² = [ (15 +5√3 q)^2 ] /69\n\nSimilarly, q² is just q².\n\nLet me substitute into the equation:\n\np^4 + 2p² q² + q^4 -18p² -30q² +81 =0\n\nThis will be extremely algebraically intensive, but let me try step by step.\n\nFirst, compute each term:\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet’s let’s set k = 5/√69 for simplicity.\n\nThen p = (15 +5√3 q)/√69 = 15/√69 + (5√3 /√69) q = k*3 + k√3 q, since 15/√69 = 3*(5/√69) = 3k, and 5√3 /√69 = k√3.\n\nBut maybe keeping it as p = (15 +5√3 q)/√69 is better.\n\nAlternatively, let me compute each term:\n\nTerm1: p^4\n\nTerm2: 2p² q²\n\nTerm3: q^4\n\nTerm4: -18p²\n\nTerm5: -30q²\n\nTerm6: +81\n\nLet me compute each term step by step.\n\nFirst, compute p = (15 +5√3 q)/√69\n\nLet me denote numerator as N =15 +5√3 q\n\nThus p = N / √69\n\nTherefore,\n\np² = N² /69\n\np^4 = N^4 / (69)^2\n\nSimilarly,\n\nTerm1: p^4 = (N^4)/ (69^2)\n\nTerm2: 2p² q² = 2*(N² /69)*q²\n\nTerm3: q^4\n\nTerm4: -18p² = -18*(N² /69)\n\nTerm5: -30q²\n\nTerm6: +81\n\nTherefore, the equation becomes:\n\n(N^4)/ (69^2) + 2*(N² /69)*q² + q^4 -18*(N² /69) -30q² +81 =0\n\nMultiply every term by 69^2 to eliminate denominators:\n\nN^4 + 2*69*N² q² + 69^2 q^4 -18*69*N² -30*69^2 q² +81*69^2 =0\n\nThis is extremely complex. Perhaps this indicates that this problem requires a different approach, perhaps using geometric properties instead of coordinates.\n\nLet me think differently.\n\nSince in the right triangle AX P, we have AX =5, and we need to find XP. If we can find AP, then XP = sqrt(AP² -25).\n\nAP is the diameter of the circle passing through X, so the center of that circle is the midpoint of AP, and the radius is AP/2.\n\nAdditionally, since X is on the circumcircle of ABC, which has center at O_circum = (0, √3) and radius 2√3.\n\nThe points A, P, X are related such that X lies on both circles: the circumcircle of ABC and the circle with diameter AP.\n\nThe radical axis of these two circles is the line AX, which we already used to find the coordinates of X.\n\nAlternatively, power of point P with respect to the circumcircle of ABC.\n\nThe power of point P with respect to the circumcircle of ABC is equal to PA * PX, since P lies on the circle with diameter AP (so PX is the other intersection?), wait, no, the power of a point P with respect to a circle is defined as PA * PB where PA and PB are the lengths of intersections from P to the circle along a line. But here, since P is inside the circle (since it's inside the triangle and the circumradius is 2√3 ≈3.464, while AP is at least ... well, AP can vary), the power of P with respect to the circumcircle is equal to OP² - R², where O is the center.\n\nWait, the power of point P with respect to the circumcircle of ABC is:\n\nPower = |PO_circum|² - R²\n\nWhere O_circum is (0, √3), and R = 2√3.\n\nCompute this:\n\nPO_circum² = (p - 0)^2 + (q - √3)^2 = p² + (q - √3)^2\n\nTherefore,\n\nPower = [p² + (q - √3)^2] - (2√3)^2 = p² + q² - 2√3 q + 3 - 12 = p² + q² - 2√3 q -9\n\nOn the other hand, the power of point P with respect to the circumcircle is also equal to PA * PX, since line PA intersects the circle at A and X, so PA * PX = power.\n\nWait, but P is inside the circle, so the power is negative, and PA * PX = - (power), but actually, the formula is:\n\nIf a line through P intersects the circle at two points S and T, then PS * PT = power of P.\n\nBut in this case, line AX passes through P? Wait, no, P is not on line AX unless it's colinear, which it's not necessarily.\n\nWait, actually, the circle with diameter AP passes through X and A, so the points A and X are on that circle, and P is the other end of the diameter from A. Therefore, the line AX is the radical axis of the two circles (the circumcircle and the circle with diameter AP), but actually, radical axis is the line containing their intersection points, which is line AX.\n\nTherefore, the power of point P with respect to the circumcircle of ABC can also be expressed as PA * PX', where X' is the other intersection point along line PA with the circumcircle. Wait, but PA is a diameter of the other circle, so the other intersection would be the point diametrically opposite to A on that circle, but I'm getting confused.\n\nAlternatively, since P lies on the circle with diameter AP, then PA is the diameter, so the circle with diameter AP has center at midpoint of AP and radius AP/2. The power of P with respect to its own circle is zero, but with respect to the circumcircle, it's different.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the key is that since X lies on both circles, then for point X:\n\nXA * XP = power of X with respect to the circle with diameter AP? Wait, no, since X is on that circle, its power is zero.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use inversion. But this might be too complex.\n\nAlternatively, let's think about the right triangle AX P: AX=5, angle at X is 90°, so coordinates of X must satisfy that.\n\nWe already found coordinates of X as ( ±5√69/12, 11√3/12 ). Let me pick one, say the positive x-coordinate one, so X is (5√69/12, 11√3/12). Now, since AP is the diameter of the circle passing through X, then the center of that circle is the midpoint of AP: let’s denote midpoint as M = (p/2, (3√3 + q)/2 ). The vector from M to X should be perpendicular to the vector from M to A, because in the circle with diameter AP, the radius to X is perpendicular to the tangent at X, but actually, since AX is a chord, but since X is on the circle with diameter AP, then angle AX P is 90°, so the vectors XA and XP are perpendicular.\n\nWait, maybe using vectors could help here.\n\nLet me consider vectors:\n\nVector XA = A - X = (0 - x_X, 3√3 - y_X)\n\nVector XP = P - X = (p - x_X, q - y_X)\n\nTheir dot product should be zero because angle at X is 90°, so:\n\nXA • XP = 0\n\nCompute this:\n\n( -x_X )(p - x_X) + (3√3 - y_X)(q - y_X) = 0\n\nExpand:\n\n- x_X p + x_X² + (3√3 - y_X) q - (3√3 - y_X)y_X = 0\n\nRearrange terms:\n\n- x_X p + x_X² + (3√3 - y_X) q - 3√3 y_X + y_X² = 0\n\nGroup terms with p and q:\n\n- x_X p + (3√3 - y_X) q + (x_X² + y_X² - 3√3 y_X ) = 0\n\nWe can plug in the known values of x_X and y_X:\n\nx_X = 5√69 /12, y_X = 11√3 /12\n\nFirst compute x_X² + y_X²:\n\nAs before, x_X² + y_X² = 29/2\n\nThen compute 3√3 y_X:\n\n3√3 * (11√3 /12 ) = 3*11*(√3)^2 /12 = 33*3 /12 = 99/12 = 33/4\n\nTherefore, the constant term:\n\nx_X² + y_X² - 3√3 y_X = 29/2 - 33/4 = (58/4 - 33/4) = 25/4\n\nNow, compute the coefficients for p and q:\n\nCoefficient of p: -x_X = -5√69 /12\n\nCoefficient of q: (3√3 - y_X) = 3√3 - 11√3/12 = (36√3/12 - 11√3/12) = 25√3/12\n\nTherefore, the equation becomes:\n\n(-5√69 /12) p + (25√3 /12) q + 25/4 = 0\n\nMultiply both sides by 12 to eliminate denominators:\n\n-5√69 p + 25√3 q + 75 = 0\n\nDivide both sides by 5:\n\n-√69 p + 5√3 q + 15 = 0\n\nRearranged:\n\n-√69 p + 5√3 q = -15\n\nOr,\n\n√69 p = 5√3 q + 15\n\nWhich is the same as our earlier equation from Case 1: √69 p = 15 + 5√3 q\n\nSo this doesn't provide new information—it's the same equation we had before. Therefore, this approach also doesn’t yield a new equation.\n\nThis suggests that the system of equations is underdetermined, which is impossible because the problem gives specific numerical values (AX=5) and asks for a numerical answer. Therefore, I must have made a wrong assumption somewhere, perhaps in the coordinate setup or in an earlier step.\n\nWait a second, perhaps I made a mistake in assuming both conditions (angle BPC=120° and AX=5) are compatible for any P along that line, but in reality, due to the specific AX=5 constraint, there is only one such P that also satisfies the angle condition. However, since all our equations reduced to identities when combined, it implies that any P along that line would satisfy both conditions? That can't be. There must be something wrong here.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the only solution is when the circle with diameter AP is tangent to the circumcircle at X, making AX the tangent? But the problem states that it meets again at X ≠ A, so it's a secant, not tangent.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the point P is such that AP is perpendicular to AX? Since in the right triangle AX P, AP is the hypotenuse, and AX and XP are legs. But AP is the hypotenuse, so it's not necessarily perpendicular to AX.\n\nAlternatively, since we know coordinates of X, and we know coordinates of A, we can compute vector AX and then find P such that angle at X is 90°, which gives a condition on P.\n\nWait, let's try this.\n\nVector AX is from A to X: X - A = (x_X - 0, y_X - 3√3) = (x_X, y_X - 3√3)\n\nVector XP is from X to P: P - X = (p - x_X, q - y_X)\n\nThe angle at X is 90°, so vectors XA and XP must be perpendicular. Wait, no, the angle at X is between AX and PX, so vectors XA and XP should be perpendicular.\n\nVector XA = A - X = (-x_X, 3√3 - y_X)\n\nVector XP = P - X = (p - x_X, q - y_X)\n\nTheir dot product is zero:\n\n(-x_X)(p - x_X) + (3√3 - y_X)(q - y_X) = 0\n\nWhich is exactly the same equation we used earlier, leading to the same result. So no new information.\n\nHmm, this is perplexing. Given that all equations lead back to the same relation between p and q, perhaps the only way to resolve this is to recognize that AP can be computed via the right triangle AX P: AP² = AX² + XP², and also AP is the distance from A to P, so AP² = p² + (q - 3√3)^2.\n\nAdditionally, since X is on the circumcircle of ABC, and AX =5, we can compute AP using coordinates of X and A:\n\nAP is the distance from A(0, 3√3) to P(p, q), but we need another relation.\n\nWait, but perhaps use the fact that X lies on both circles to form an equation involving AP.\n\nThe circle with diameter AP has the property that for any point Q on it, AQ ⊥ PQ. In particular, for point X, we have AX ⊥ XP, which is already used.\n\nAlternatively, the power of point A with respect to the circle with diameter AP is zero, since A is on the circle. Not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, the power of point X with respect to the circumcircle of ABC is zero, since X is on it.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps use coordinates of X and the expressions for p and q to compute AP.\n\nGiven that in Case 1, we have p = (15 +5√3 q)/√69, and we can choose to express q in terms of p or vice versa, but without another equation, it's stuck.\n\nWait, but perhaps we can compute AP in terms of q:\n\nAP² = p² + (q - 3√3)^2\n\nSubstitute p from Case 1:\n\nAP² = [ (15 +5√3 q)^2 /69 ] + (q - 3√3)^2\n\nExpand the first term:\n\n(225 + 150√3 q + 75 q²)/69\n\nThe second term:\n\n(q² - 6√3 q + 27*3) = q² -6√3 q + 27*3 = q² -6√3 q + 81\n\nWait, (q - 3√3)^2 = q² - 2*(3√3)*q + (3√3)^2 = q² -6√3 q + 27*3 = q² -6√3 q + 81\n\nTherefore, AP² = [225 + 150√3 q +75 q²]/69 + q² -6√3 q +81\n\nCombine terms:\n\nLet me write all terms over 69 denominator:\n\n= [225 + 150√3 q +75 q² + 69(q² -6√3 q +81) ] /69\n\nExpand the numerator:\n\n225 +150√3 q +75 q² +69 q² -414√3 q + 69*81\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nq² terms: 75 q² +69 q² = 144 q²\n\nq terms: 150√3 q -414√3 q = -264√3 q\n\nconstants: 225 + 69*81\n\nCompute constants:\n\n69*81: 70*81 = 5670, minus 1*81 = 5670 -81 = 5589\n\nSo constants total: 225 +5589 = 5814\n\nThus numerator:\n\n144 q² -264√3 q +5814\n\nTherefore,\n\nAP² = (144 q² -264√3 q +5814)/69\n\nSimplify numerator and denominator:\n\nDivide numerator and denominator by 3:\n\nNumerator: 48 q² -88√3 q + 1938\n\nDenominator: 23\n\nSo,\n\nAP² = (48 q² -88√3 q +1938)/23\n\nNow, in the right triangle AX P, we have AP² = AX² + XP² =25 + XP².\n\nBut we need XP in terms of q as well.\n\nCompute XP² = (p -x_X)^2 + (q - y_X)^2\n\nAgain, using p = (15 +5√3 q)/√69, x_X=5√69/12, y_X=11√3/12\n\nLet me compute each term:\n\nFirst term: (p -x_X)^2\n\n= [ (15 +5√3 q)/√69 -5√69/12 ]^2\n\nLet me factor out 1/√69:\n\n= [ (15 +5√3 q - (5√69/12)*√69 ) / √69 ]^2\n\nWait, perhaps better to compute numerically:\n\nFirst, let me compute the expression inside the square:\n\nLet me denote term1 = (15 +5√3 q)/√69 -5√69/12\n\nLet me write both terms with a common denominator. Let me compute:\n\nLet me write term1 as:\n\n= [ (15 +5√3 q) /√69 -5√69 /12 ]\n\nTo combine these terms, let's get a common denominator of 12√69:\n\n= [ 12(15 +5√3 q) -5√69 * √69 ] / (12√69 )\n\nWait, compute numerator:\n\nFirst term: 12*(15 +5√3 q) = 180 +60√3 q\n\nSecond term: -5√69 * √69 = -5*(69) = -345\n\nTherefore numerator: 180 +60√3 q -345 = -165 +60√3 q\n\nThus,\n\nterm1 = ( -165 +60√3 q ) / (12√69 )\n\nTherefore,\n\n(p -x_X)^2 = [ (-165 +60√3 q ) / (12√69) ]^2\n\nSimilarly, compute the second term in XP²: (q - y_X)^2\n\n= ( q - 11√3/12 )^2\n\nThus, XP² = [ (-165 +60√3 q )^2 / (12√69)^2 ] + ( q -11√3/12 )^2\n\nThis expression is extremely complicated. I think this approach is not feasible without computational assistance.\n\nGiven that this is a competition-style problem, there must be a elegant solution using geometric properties rather than brute-force coordinate algebra.\n\nLet me try to think differently.\n\nGiven that in triangle ABC, which is equilateral with side 6, point P is inside such that angle BPC = 120°, and the circle with diameter AP meets the circumcircle again at X with AX =5. Need to find XP.\n\nRecall that in an equilateral triangle, the circumradius is R = 2√3, as we found earlier.\n\nThe point X is on the circumcircle, so AX is a chord of the circumcircle with length 5.\n\nThe length of chord AX can be related to the central angle subtended by AX.\n\nThe length of a chord is given by 2R sin(θ/2), where θ is the central angle.\n\nHere, AX =5, R = 2√3, so:\n\n5 = 2*(2√3)*sin(θ/2)\n\nThus,\n\nsin(θ/2) = 5/(4√3) ≈5/(6.928) ≈0.721\n\nTherefore,\n\nθ/2 = arcsin(5/(4√3)) ≈ arcsin(0.721) ≈46.1 degrees,\n\nso θ ≈92.2 degrees.\n\nTherefore, the central angle for chord AX is approximately 92.2 degrees.\n\nAlternatively, exact value:\n\nLet me compute sin(θ/2) = 5/(4√3)\n\nThus,\n\nθ/2 = arcsin(5/(4√3))\n\nBut perhaps we can find the coordinates of X more cleverly.\n\nAlternatively, since AX =5 and A is a vertex of the equilateral triangle, perhaps triangle AOX has some properties, where O is the circumcenter.\n\nThe distance from A to O (the center) is the circumradius, which is 2√3. So AO = 2√3, AX =5, and OX = 2√3 (since X is on the circumcircle).\n\nTherefore, in triangle AOX, we have sides AO = 2√3, OX = 2√3, and AX =5.\n\nThis is an isoceles triangle with two sides 2√3 and base 5.\n\nWe can compute the angle at O:\n\nUsing the Law of Cosines:\n\nAX² = AO² + OX² - 2*AO*OX*cos(angle AOX)\n\n25 = (2√3)^2 + (2√3)^2 - 2*(2√3)*(2√3)*cos(angle AOX)\n\nCompute:\n\n25 = 12 +12 - 2*(12)*cos(angle AOX)\n\n25 =24 -24 cos(angle AOX)\n\nThus,\n\n25 -24 = -24 cos(angle AOX)\n\n1 = -24 cos(angle AOX)\n\ncos(angle AOX) = -1/24\n\nTherefore, angle AOX = arccos(-1/24) ≈ 92.2 degrees, which matches our earlier approximate calculation.\n\nThus, the central angle for AX is arccos(-1/24).\n\nNow, perhaps we can use some properties related to this angle.\n\nAlternatively, since AP is the diameter of the circle passing through X, then the angle AX P is 90°, so P lies on the circle with diameter AX? Wait no, the circle has diameter AP, so angle AX P is 90°, meaning that P lies on the circle with diameter AX? Wait, no: the circle with diameter AP has the property that any point on it forms a right angle with A and P. So X is on that circle, so angle AX P is 90°, but P is not necessarily on the circle with diameter AX.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's a reflection or some symmetry.\n\nAlternatively, since angle AX P =90°, and AX=5, then if we can find coordinates of P such that it lies on the circle with diameter AP and forms a right angle at X with AX=5, maybe using some geometric mean.\n\nAlternatively, since in triangle AX P, we have AP² = AX² + XP², so AP² - XP² =25.\n\nAlso, since P is inside the triangle ABC, and X is on the circumcircle, perhaps there is a relation between AP and other sides.\n\nAlternatively, use coordinates but with a different coordinate system.\n\nLet me try placing point A at the origin to simplify calculations.\n\nLet me reset the coordinate system:\n\nLet me place point A at (0,0), and since ABC is equilateral with side length 6, let me place point B at (6, 0). Then point C will be at (3, 3√3), since the height of the equilateral triangle is (√3/2)*6 = 3√3.\n\nThus:\n\nA: (0,0)\n\nB: (6,0)\n\nC: (3, 3√3)\n\nThe centroid (and circumcenter) is at the average of the coordinates:\n\nO_circum: ( (0+6+3)/3, (0 +0 +3√3)/3 ) = (9/3, 3√3/3 ) = (3, √3)\n\nThe circumradius R is the distance from O_circum to any vertex, say to A(0,0):\n\nR = sqrt( (3-0)^2 + (√3 -0)^2 ) = sqrt(9 +3) = sqrt(12) = 2√3, which matches.\n\nNow, let me redefine point P as (p, q) inside the triangle.\n\nGiven that angle BPC =120°, and the circle with diameter AP intersects the circumcircle again at X with AX=5.\n\nNow, let's recompute coordinates with this new system.\n\nFirst, coordinates:\n\nA: (0,0)\n\nB: (6,0)\n\nC: (3, 3√3)\n\nO_circum: (3, √3)\n\nCircumradius: 2√3.\n\nPoint X is on the circumcircle and AX=5. Let me find coordinates of X.\n\nThe circumcircle equation is:\n\n(x - 3)^2 + (y - √3)^2 = (2√3)^2 = 12\n\nAX=5, so distance from A(0,0) to X(x,y) is 5:\n\nx² + y² =25\n\nSolve these two equations:\n\n1. (x -3)^2 + (y - √3)^2 =12\n\n2. x² + y² =25\n\nExpand equation 1:\n\nx² -6x +9 + y² - 2√3 y +3 =12\n\nCombine terms:\n\nx² + y² -6x -2√3 y +12 =12\n\nBut x² + y² =25 from equation 2, so substitute:\n\n25 -6x -2√3 y +12 =12\n\nSimplify:\n\n25 -6x -2√3 y +12 -12=0 → 25 -6x -2√3 y =0 → 6x +2√3 y =25\n\nDivide both sides by 2:\n\n3x + √3 y =25/2 → equation of the line where X lies.\n\nThus, coordinates of X satisfy both x² + y² =25 and 3x + √3 y =25/2.\n\nLet me solve these equations.\n\nFrom the linear equation: √3 y =25/2 -3x → y= (25/(2√3)) - (3/√3)x = (25√3)/6 - √3 x\n\nSubstitute into x² + y² =25:\n\nx² + [ (25√3/6 - √3 x ) ]^2 =25\n\nLet me compute the y-term squared:\n\nLet me factor out √3:\n\ny = √3 (25/6 - x )\n\nThus,\n\ny² = 3*(25/6 -x )²\n\nTherefore,\n\nx² + 3*(25/6 -x )² =25\n\nExpand the squared term:\n\n(25/6 -x)^2 =x² - (50/6)x + (625/36)\n\nThus,\n\nx² +3[ x² - (50/6)x +625/36 ] =25\n\nMultiply through:\n\nx² +3x² - (150/6)x + 1875/36 =25\n\nSimplify:\n\n4x² -25x + 1875/36 =25\n\nMultiply all terms by 36 to eliminate denominators:\n\n4x²*36 -25x*36 +1875 =25*36\n\nCompute each term:\n\n4x²*36 =144x²\n\n-25x*36 = -900x\n\n1875 remains\n\n25*36 =900\n\nThus,\n\n144x² -900x +1875 =900\n\nBring 900 to left:\n\n144x² -900x +975 =0\n\nDivide equation by 3:\n\n48x² -300x +325 =0\n\nUse quadratic formula:\n\nx = [300 ±√(300² -4*48*325)]/(2*48)\n\nCompute discriminant:\n\n300² =90000\n\n4*48*325 = 192*325\n\nCompute 192*300=57,600 and 192*25=4,800 → total 62,400\n\nThus discriminant=90,000 -62,400=27,600\n\n√27,600=√(100*276)=10√276=10√(4*69)=10*2√69=20√69\n\nThus,\n\nx = [300 ±20√69]/96 = [300 ±20√69]/96 = [75 ±5√69]/24\n\nTherefore, x-coordinates of X are (75 +5√69)/24 and (75 -5√69)/24\n\nCorresponding y-coordinates from y=√3 (25/6 -x )\n\nLet me compute for x1 = (75 +5√69)/24:\n\ny1 =√3 [25/6 - (75 +5√69)/24 ]\n\nConvert 25/6 to 100/24:\n\n=√3 [ (100 -75 -5√69)/24 ]\n\n=√3 [ (25 -5√69)/24 ]\n\n=√3 *5*(5 -√69)/24\n\n= (5√3)(5 -√69)/24\n\nSimilarly, for x2 = (75 -5√69)/24,\n\ny2 =√3 [25/6 - (75 -5√69)/24 ]\n\n=√3 [ (100 -75 +5√69)/24 ]\n\n=√3 [ (25 +5√69)/24 ]\n\n=5√3 (5 +√69)/24\n\nTherefore, coordinates of X are:\n\nX1: ( (75 +5√69)/24 , (5√3)(5 -√69)/24 )\n\nX2: ( (75 -5√69)/24 , (5√3)(5 +√69)/24 )\n\nThese are the two possible points X.\n\nNow, the circle with diameter AP must pass through X (either X1 or X2). Let me consider one of them, say X1, and see if we can find coordinates of P such that angle BPC=120°.\n\nLet me denote coordinates of P as (p, q). The circle with diameter AP has endpoints at A(0,0) and P(p, q), so its equation is:\n\n(x - p/2)^2 + (y - q/2)^2 = ( (sqrt(p² + q²))/2 )^2 = (p² + q²)/4\n\nThis circle passes through X1, so substituting X1's coordinates into the circle equation:\n\n[ ( (75 +5√69)/24 - p/2 )^2 + ( (5√3)(5 -√69)/24 - q/2 )^2 ] = (p² + q²)/4\n\nThis equation, along with the condition that angle BPC=120°, should allow us to solve for p and q.\n\nThe angle BPC=120° condition can be translated into the Law of Cosines in triangle BPC:\n\nBC² = BP² + CP² - 2 BP*CP cos(120°)\n\nWith BC=6, so:\n\n36 = BP² + CP² + BP*CP\n\nExpressed in coordinates:\n\nBP = distance from B(6,0) to P(p, q): sqrt( (p-6)^2 + q² )\n\nCP = distance from C(3, 3√3) to P(p, q): sqrt( (p-3)^2 + (q -3√3)^2 )\n\nThis seems very complicated, but perhaps there is a better way.\n\nAlternatively, since the problem is likely designed to have an integer or nice fractional answer, perhaps XP is an integer or a simple radical.\n\nGiven that AX=5, 