/440 = (624/44)√15 = (156/11)√15,\n\nBut in the symbolic shoelace formula, I have 6239√15⁄440,\n\nThe difference is 1 in the numerator.\n\nThis suggests that in one of the terms in the symbolic calculation, there was an off-by-1 error.\n\nLet me re-express all terms in Sum1 and Sum2 in fractions:\n\nSum1 terms:\n\nx1y2 = (11/2)(33√15/10)= (363√15)/20,\n\nx2y3 = (17/2)(11√15/10)= (187√15)/20,\n\nx3y4 = (17/2)(79√15/110)= (1343√15)/220,\n\nx4y1 = (61/22)(47√15/10)= (2867√15)/220,\n\nSum1 = 363/20 +187/20 +1343/220 +2867/220,\n\nConvert to denominator 220:\n\n363/20 = (363×11)/220 =3993/220,\n\n187/20= (187×11)/220=2057/220,\n\n1343/220 stays,\n\n2867/220 stays,\n\nTotal numerator: 3993+2057+1343+2867,\n\nLet me compute this step by step:\n\n3993 +2057 = 6050,\n\n6050 +1343 =7393,\n\n7393 +2867 =10260,\n\nCorrect.\n\nSum2 terms:\n\ny1x2 = (47√15/10)(17/2)= (799√15)/20,\n\ny2x3 = (33√15/10)(17/2)= (561√15)/20,\n\ny3x4 = (11√15/10)(61/22)= (671√15)/220,\n\ny4x1 = (79√15/110)(11/2)= (869√15)/220,\n\nSum2 =799/20 +561/20 +671/220 +869/220,\n\nConvert to denominator 220:\n\n799/20 = (799×11)/220= 8789/220,\n\n561/20 = (561×11)/220=6171/220,\n\n671/220 stays,\n\n869/220 stays,\n\nTotal numerator:8789+6171+671+869,\n\nCompute:\n\n8789+6171 =14960,\n\n14960+671=15631,\n\n15631+869=16499,\n\nCorrect.\n\nThus, the symbolic result is indeed 6239√15⁄440,\n\nBut the first method after correcting the addition gives 6240√15⁄440,\n\nThus, there must be an off-by-one error in one of the terms.\n\nWait, let's re-express the coordinates of O4:\n\nO4's x-coordinate is 61/22,\n\ny-coordinate is 79√15/110,\n\nCompute x4y1:\n\nx4=61/22,\n\ny1=47√15/10,\n\nThus,\n\nx4y1 = (61/22)(47√15/10)= (61×47√15)/(22×10)= (2867√15)/220,\n\nCorrect.\n\ny4x1:\n\ny4=79√15/110,\n\nx1=11/2,\n\nThus,\n\ny4x1= (79√15/110)(11/2)= (79×11√15)/(110×2)= (869√15)/220,\n\nCorrect.\n\nThus, those terms are correct.\n\nWait, what about x3y4:\n\nx3=17/2,\n\ny4=79√15/110,\n\nThus,\n\nx3y4= (17/2)(79√15/110)= (1343√15)/220,\n\nCorrect.\n\ny3x4:\n\ny3=11√15/10,\n\nx4=61/22,\n\nThus,\n\ny3x4= (11√15/10)(61/22)= (671√15)/220,\n\nCorrect.\n\nSimilarly, all terms seem correct.\n\nThus, the only conclusion is that there is an arithmetic mistake in the first method when I corrected the addition error.\n\nWait, in the first method, after correcting the addition mistake, I had:\n\nTotal numerator: -6240√15/220,\n\nBut in reality, it should be -6239√15/220,\n\nTherefore, the correct area is 6239√15⁄440,\n\nBut why in the first method I had:\n\nFirst two terms: -436/20 -374/20 = -810/20 = -40.5,\n\nThird and fourth terms: (672+1998)/220 = 2670/220 = 12.13636,\n\nTotal: -40.5 +12.13636 = -28.36364,\n\nWhich is -6239/220,\n\nBecause 6239/220≈28.31818,\n\nWait, 6239 divided by 220:\n\n220×28=6160,\n\n6239-6160=79,\n\nThus, 6239/220=28+79/220≈28.35818,\n\nTherefore, -28.35818,\n\nBut -40.5 +12.13636= -28.36364,\n\nThe difference between 28.35818 and 28.36364 is due to rounding in the decimal approximations.\n\nThus, the exact calculation gives -6239/220,\n\nTherefore, the area is (6239√15)/440,\n\nBut this contradicts the first method's correction.\n\nWait, in the first method after correction:\n\nFirst two terms: -436/20 -374/20 = -810/20 = -40.5,\n\nThird term:672/220,\n\nFourth term:1998/220,\n\nTotal third and fourth terms: (672+1998)/220=2670/220=12.13636,\n\nThus total sum: -40.5 +12.13636= -28.36364,\n\nWhich is exactly -6239/220,\n\nBecause,\n\n-28.36364 *220 =-6239,\n\nYes,\n\n28.36364×220=28×220 +0.36364×220=6160 +80.0368≈6240.0368,\n\nThus,\n\n-28.36364×220≈-6240.0368,\n\nBut exact value is -6239,\n\nThus, the discrepancy is due to the approximate decimal values used in the first method,\n\nTherefore, the exact area is 6239√15⁄440,\n\nBut this reduces to:\n\nDivide numerator and denominator by GCD(6239,440)=1,\n\nThus, it is irreducible,\n\nBut 6239 divided by 11: 11×567=6237, so remainder 2,\n\nThus, not divisible by 11,\n\nTherefore, the exact area is 6239√15⁄440,\n\nBut this seems messy, and given that in the first method with corrected arithmetic I had 156/11√15,\n\nBut the symbolic computation gives 623","num_tokens":32768,"correct":false,"formatted":true},{"predicted_answer":"9\\sqrt{15}","full_generation":"Okay, so I have this problem about a trapezoid ABCD with AB parallel to CD. The side lengths are given: AB is 11 units long, BC is 8 units, CD is 19 units, and DA is 4 units. The question is asking me to compute the area of the convex quadrilateral formed by the circumcenters of the four triangles: triangle ABC, triangle BCD, triangle CDA, and triangle DAB. \n\nHmm, circumcenters... Alright, first things first, I need to recall what a circumcenter is. The circumcenter of a triangle is the point where the perpendicular bisectors of the triangle's sides intersect, and it is the center of the circle that passes through all three vertices of the triangle. So, for each of the four triangles mentioned—ABC, BCD, CDA, and DAB—I need to find their circumcenters, then figure out the coordinates of these four points (assuming I can assign coordinates to the original trapezoid), and then determine the area of the convex quadrilateral formed by these four circumcenters.\n\nI think the standard approach here is to place the trapezoid on a coordinate plane so that we can assign coordinates to each vertex, then use coordinate geometry to find the circumcenters of each triangle, then once we have the coordinates of those four circumcenters, we can use the shoelace formula or some other method to compute the area of the quadrilateral they form.\n\nSo, let me try to set this up step by step.\n\nFirst, let me sketch the trapezoid ABCD with AB and CD being the two bases, with AB parallel to CD. Since AB is 11 and CD is 19, which is longer, I can imagine CD as the lower base and AB as the upper base, or vice versa. It might not matter, but I need to decide on a coordinate system.\n\nLet me decide to place the trapezoid so that the side CD is on the x-axis, from point D to point C, and then point A is somewhere above D, and point B is somewhere above C, such that AB is parallel to CD. Wait, but actually, in a trapezoid with AB || CD, the sides AD and BC are the legs (unless it's an isosceles trapezoid, which we don't know here). Since the side lengths are given as AD = 4 and BC = 8, which are not equal, so it's not isosceles. So, the legs are of different lengths.\n\nTo set up coordinates, I can let point D be at the origin (0, 0). Then, since CD is 19 units long and it's the base, I can place point C at (19, 0). Now, since AB is parallel to CD and AB is 11 units long, I need to place points A and B somewhere above D and C, respectively, such that the distance between A and B is 11, and the distance from A to D is 4, and the distance from B to C is 8.\n\nLet me denote the coordinates:\n\nLet’s let D = (0, 0).\n\nC = (19, 0).\n\nLet me denote the coordinates of A as (x, h), and coordinates of B as (x + 11, h), since AB is parallel to CD and has length 11, so the horizontal distance between A and B should be 11, and they should both be at the same height h above the base CD. Wait, but actually, if AB is parallel to CD, which is horizontal in my coordinate system, then AB must also be horizontal. Therefore, the y-coordinate for both A and B should be the same, let's say h, so that AB is a horizontal segment of length 11. So, if point A is at (p, h) and point B is at (p + 11, h). Then, the legs are AD and BC.\n\nAD is from D(0,0) to A(p, h), so the length of AD is sqrt[(p - 0)^2 + (h - 0)^2] = sqrt(p^2 + h^2) = 4.\n\nSimilarly, BC is from B(p + 11, h) to C(19, 0), so the length of BC is sqrt[(19 - (p + 11))^2 + (0 - h)^2] = sqrt[(8 - p)^2 + h^2] = 8.\n\nTherefore, we have two equations:\n\n1) sqrt(p^2 + h^2) = 4,\n\n2) sqrt[(8 - p)^2 + h^2] = 8.\n\nLet me square both equations to eliminate the square roots.\n\nFrom equation 1:\n\np^2 + h^2 = 16.      (Equation 1 squared)\n\nFrom equation 2:\n\n(8 - p)^2 + h^2 = 64.   (Equation 2 squared)\n\nNow, subtract equation 1 from equation 2:\n\n[(8 - p)^2 + h^2] - [p^2 + h^2] = 64 - 16,\n\nExpand (8 - p)^2: 64 - 16p + p^2,\n\nSo,\n\n[64 - 16p + p^2 + h^2] - [p^2 + h^2] = 48,\n\nSimplify:\n\n64 - 16p + p^2 + h^2 - p^2 - h^2 = 48,\n\nSimplify further:\n\n64 - 16p = 48,\n\nSubtract 48 from both sides:\n\n16 - 16p = 0,\n\nWait, 64 - 48 = 16, so:\n\n16 - 16p = 0,\n\nThen,\n\n16 = 16p,\n\nSo,\n\np = 1.\n\nOkay, so p is 1. Then, substitute p = 1 into equation 1 to find h.\n\nFrom equation 1:\n\n(1)^2 + h^2 = 16,\n\n1 + h^2 = 16,\n\nh^2 = 15,\n\nTherefore,\n\nh = sqrt(15) or h = -sqrt(15). Since we are dealing with a convex quadrilateral above the base CD, we can take h as positive, so h = sqrt(15).\n\nSo, coordinates:\n\nD = (0, 0),\n\nC = (19, 0),\n\nA = (p, h) = (1, sqrt(15)),\n\nB = (p + 11, h) = (1 + 11, sqrt(15)) = (12, sqrt(15)).\n\nLet me verify the length BC:\n\nPoint B is at (12, sqrt(15)), point C is at (19, 0).\n\nDistance BC: sqrt[(19 - 12)^2 + (0 - sqrt(15))^2] = sqrt[7^2 + (sqrt(15))^2] = sqrt[49 + 15] = sqrt[64] = 8. That's correct.\n\nDistance AD: from D(0,0) to A(1, sqrt(15)): sqrt[(1)^2 + (sqrt(15))^2] = sqrt[1 + 15] = sqrt[16] = 4. Correct.\n\nAB is from A(1, sqrt(15)) to B(12, sqrt(15)): difference in x-coordinate is 11, y-coordinate same, so distance is 11. Correct.\n\nCD is from C(19,0) to D(0,0): distance is 19. Correct.\n\nSo, coordinates are set. Now, we have coordinates for all four vertices of the trapezoid ABCD:\n\nA(1, sqrt(15)),\n\nB(12, sqrt(15)),\n\nC(19, 0),\n\nD(0, 0).\n\nNow, the problem wants the circumcenters of the four triangles: ABC, BCD, CDA, and DAB.\n\nLet me list them again:\n\n1. Triangle ABC,\n\n2. Triangle BCD,\n\n3. Triangle CDA,\n\n4. Triangle DAB.\n\nSo, need to find the circumcenter for each of these four triangles, then form a quadrilateral from those four points, and compute its area.\n\nAlright, so let me recall that the circumcenter of a triangle is the intersection point of the perpendicular bisectors of the sides of the triangle. Since I have coordinates for all the vertices, I can compute the perpendicular bisectors for each triangle, find their intersection points (the circumcenters), and then get the coordinates of these four circumcenters.\n\nAlternatively, since coordinates are known, maybe there is a formula for the circumcenter given coordinates of three vertices?\n\nYes, there is. For a triangle with vertices at (x_A, y_A), (x_B, y_B), (x_C, y_C), the circumcenter can be found by solving the perpendicular bisector equations, or by using the formula derived from the perpendicular bisector equations.\n\nAlternatively, using the formula:\n\nThe circumcenter (x, y) satisfies the equation:\n\n(x - x_A)^2 + (y - y_A)^2 = (x - x_B)^2 + (y - y_B)^2,\n\nand\n\n(x - x_A)^2 + (y - y_A)^2 = (x - x_C)^2 + (y - y_C)^2.\n\nWhich can be solved as a system of two equations in x and y.\n\nAlternatively, there's a formula using determinants, but perhaps it's easier to just set up the equations for each triangle.\n\nAlternatively, use the formula for circumcenter coordinates:\n\nGiven three points (x_A, y_A), (x_B, y_B), (x_C, y_C), the circumcenter (x, y) can be found by solving:\n\n(x - x_A)^2 + (y - y_A)^2 = (x - x_B)^2 + (y - y_B)^2,\n\nand\n\n(x - x_B)^2 + (y - y_B)^2 = (x - x_C)^2 + (y - y_C)^2.\n\nExpanding these equations and solving for x and y.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps a vector approach or using midpoints and slopes.\n\nHmm. Let me try with coordinates.\n\nLet me start with triangle ABC first.\n\nTriangle ABC has vertices:\n\nA(1, sqrt(15)), B(12, sqrt(15)), C(19, 0).\n\nSo, let me denote these as:\n\nA: (x_A, y_A) = (1, sqrt(15)),\n\nB: (x_B, y_B) = (12, sqrt(15)),\n\nC: (x_C, y_C) = (19, 0).\n\nFirst, let me find the perpendicular bisector of AB and the perpendicular bisector of AC, then find their intersection point, which will be the circumcenter.\n\nAlternatively, let's pick two sides, compute their midpoints and slopes, then write the equations of the perpendicular bisectors.\n\nLet me first compute the midpoint and slope of AB.\n\nMidpoint of AB:\n\nx_mid_AB = (1 + 12)/2 = 13/2 = 6.5,\n\ny_mid_AB = (sqrt(15) + sqrt(15))/2 = sqrt(15).\n\nSlope of AB:\n\nSince AB is horizontal (both points have the same y-coordinate, sqrt(15)), the slope is (sqrt(15) - sqrt(15))/(12 - 1) = 0. So, the slope of AB is 0, which means it's a horizontal line. Therefore, the perpendicular bisector of AB will be a vertical line passing through the midpoint. Since the slope is 0, perpendicular slope is undefined, so vertical line. Therefore, the perpendicular bisector of AB is x = 6.5.\n\nOkay, that's straightforward.\n\nNow, let's do the same for another side, say BC.\n\nCompute midpoint of BC:\n\nPoint B(12, sqrt(15)), point C(19, 0).\n\nMidpoint of BC:\n\nx_mid_BC = (12 + 19)/2 = 31/2 = 15.5,\n\ny_mid_BC = (sqrt(15) + 0)/2 = sqrt(15)/2 ≈ (sqrt(15))/2, but we can keep it exact.\n\nSlope of BC:\n\nSlope BC = (0 - sqrt(15)) / (19 - 12) = (-sqrt(15))/7.\n\nTherefore, the slope of BC is -sqrt(15)/7.\n\nTherefore, the perpendicular bisector of BC will have a slope that is the negative reciprocal, so slope = 7/sqrt(15).\n\nTherefore, the equation of the perpendicular bisector of BC is:\n\n(y - y_mid_BC) = slope_perp * (x - x_mid_BC)\n\nSo,\n\ny - (sqrt(15)/2) = (7/sqrt(15)) (x - 15.5)\n\nNow, since we already have the perpendicular bisector of AB is x = 6.5, we can plug x = 6.5 into the above equation to find the y-coordinate of the circumcenter.\n\nSo, substituting x = 6.5 into the equation for the perpendicular bisector of BC:\n\ny - (sqrt(15)/2) = (7/sqrt(15)) * (6.5 - 15.5)\n\nCompute 6.5 - 15.5 = -9,\n\nSo,\n\ny - (sqrt(15)/2) = (7/sqrt(15)) * (-9) = -63 / sqrt(15)\n\nTherefore,\n\ny = (sqrt(15)/2) - (63 / sqrt(15))\n\nTo combine these terms, let me write both terms with denominator sqrt(15):\n\nFirst term: (sqrt(15)/2) = (sqrt(15) * sqrt(15)) / (2 * sqrt(15)) ) = (15) / (2 sqrt(15)), but wait, that might complicate. Alternatively, let me rationalize the second term:\n\n-63 / sqrt(15) = -63 sqrt(15) / 15 = - (63/15) sqrt(15) = - (21/5) sqrt(15)\n\nTherefore,\n\ny = (sqrt(15)/2) - (21/5) sqrt(15)\n\nFactor out sqrt(15):\n\ny = sqrt(15) [ 1/2 - 21/5 ]\n\nCompute 1/2 - 21/5:\n\nConvert to common denominator, which is 10:\n\n1/2 = 5/10,\n\n21/5 = 42/10,\n\nSo,\n\n5/10 - 42/10 = (-37)/10,\n\nThus,\n\ny = sqrt(15) * (-37/10) = - (37/10) sqrt(15)\n\nTherefore, the circumcenter of triangle ABC is at (6.5, - (37/10) sqrt(15))\n\nWait, that seems odd. Let me check my calculations again, because the circumcenter being below the base CD (since y-coordinate is negative) might be possible, but let me verify.\n\nFirst, let's check the perpendicular bisector equations again.\n\nFirst, midpoint of AB is indeed (6.5, sqrt(15)), correct.\n\nPerpendicular bisector of AB is vertical line x = 6.5, that's correct since AB is horizontal.\n\nMidpoint of BC: between (12, sqrt(15)) and (19, 0):\n\nx: (12 + 19)/2 = 31/2 = 15.5, correct.\n\ny: (sqrt(15) + 0)/2 = sqrt(15)/2, correct.\n\nSlope of BC: (0 - sqrt(15))/(19 - 12) = (-sqrt(15))/7, correct.\n\nTherefore, the perpendicular slope is 7/sqrt(15), correct.\n\nEquation of perpendicular bisector of BC:\n\ny - sqrt(15)/2 = (7/sqrt(15))(x - 15.5)\n\nThen, substituting x = 6.5 into that equation:\n\ny = sqrt(15)/2 + (7/sqrt(15))(6.5 - 15.5)\n\nCompute 6.5 - 15.5: that's -9, correct.\n\nSo,\n\ny = sqrt(15)/2 + (7/sqrt(15)) * (-9)\n\nWhich is sqrt(15)/2 - 63/sqrt(15)\n\nTo combine these terms, let's write sqrt(15)/2 as (sqrt(15) * sqrt(15))/(2 sqrt(15)) )? Wait, that would be 15/(2 sqrt(15)), but maybe better to rationalize the denominator for the second term.\n\nAlternatively, let me compute sqrt(15)/2 - 63/sqrt(15):\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator of the second term by sqrt(15):\n\n63/sqrt(15) = (63 sqrt(15))/15 = (21 sqrt(15))/5\n\nTherefore,\n\ny = sqrt(15)/2 - (21 sqrt(15))/5\n\nFactor out sqrt(15):\n\nsqrt(15) [1/2 - 21/5] = sqrt(15) [ (5 - 42)/10 ] = sqrt(15) [ (-37)/10 ] = - (37/10) sqrt(15)\n\nSo that's correct. So, the y-coordinate is negative. That is, the circumcenter of triangle ABC is at (6.5, -37/10 sqrt(15)). Since the original trapezoid has points A, B above the x-axis (since their y-coordinate is sqrt(15)) and C, D on the x-axis, so triangle ABC has points A and B above the x-axis and C on the x-axis. The circumcenter being below the x-axis is possible because the triangle might be such that the circumcenter lies outside the triangle. Let me confirm with another approach.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in assuming which perpendicular bisector to take? Wait, the perpendicular bisector of AB is vertical at x = 6.5, and the perpendicular bisector of BC is the line we found. Since the triangle ABC has vertices at (1, sqrt(15)), (12, sqrt(15)), and (19, 0). Let me see if this circumcenter is indeed the intersection.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I should compute the perpendicular bisector of AC instead?\n\nAlternatively, let me compute the perpendicular bisector of AC and see if that gives the same point.\n\nLet me try that.\n\nFirst, compute midpoint of AC.\n\nPoint A(1, sqrt(15)), point C(19, 0).\n\nMidpoint of AC:\n\nx_mid_AC = (1 + 19)/2 = 20/2 = 10,\n\ny_mid_AC = (sqrt(15) + 0)/2 = sqrt(15)/2 ≈ 0.5 sqrt(15).\n\nSlope of AC:\n\nSlope_AC = (0 - sqrt(15)) / (19 - 1) = (-sqrt(15))/18.\n\nTherefore, the perpendicular bisector of AC will have slope reciprocal and opposite: 18/sqrt(15).\n\nTherefore, equation of perpendicular bisector of AC is:\n\ny - y_mid_AC = slope_perp * (x - x_mid_AC)\n\nSo,\n\ny - (sqrt(15)/2) = (18 / sqrt(15)) (x - 10)\n\nNow, let's find the intersection of this perpendicular bisector with the perpendicular bisector of AB, which is x = 6.5.\n\nSubstituting x = 6.5 into the equation above:\n\ny - (sqrt(15)/2) = (18 / sqrt(15)) (6.5 - 10)\n\nCompute 6.5 - 10 = -3.5 = -7/2,\n\nThus,\n\ny = sqrt(15)/2 + (18 / sqrt(15)) * (-7/2)\n\nCompute the second term:\n\n(18 / sqrt(15)) * (-7/2) = (-126) / (2 sqrt(15)) = (-63)/sqrt(15)\n\nAgain, same as before, so then:\n\ny = sqrt(15)/2 - 63/sqrt(15)\n\nWhich is exactly the same expression as before, leading to y = - (37/10) sqrt(15). So that checks out. So, the circumcenter of triangle ABC is indeed at (6.5, -37/10 sqrt(15)). Alright, so that seems correct.\n\nHmm, okay. Let me note that coordinate as O1: (13/2, - (37/10) sqrt(15)). Since 6.5 is 13/2.\n\nAlright, moving on to the next triangle: triangle BCD.\n\nTriangle BCD has vertices B(12, sqrt(15)), C(19, 0), and D(0, 0).\n\nSo, let's find the circumcenter of triangle BCD.\n\nAgain, let's denote the points:\n\nB: (12, sqrt(15)),\n\nC: (19, 0),\n\nD: (0, 0).\n\nLet me find the perpendicular bisectors of two sides and find their intersection.\n\nLet me choose sides BC and BD, perhaps? Or BC and CD? Let me see.\n\nFirst, let's compute the perpendicular bisector of BC and the perpendicular bisector of CD, since CD is from (0,0) to (19,0), which is along the x-axis, so its perpendicular bisector might be easier.\n\nFirst, let's do perpendicular bisector of CD.\n\nCD is from D(0,0) to C(19, 0). The midpoint of CD is (19/2, 0). The slope of CD is (0 - 0)/(19 - 0) = 0; it's horizontal. Therefore, the perpendicular bisector is vertical, going through the midpoint (19/2, 0). Wait, no: the perpendicular to a horizontal line is vertical, so yes, perpendicular bisector is vertical line through midpoint. So, equation is x = 19/2 = 9.5.\n\nWait, but let me confirm. Since CD is horizontal, its perpendicular bisector is vertical, passing through midpoint (9.5, 0). So, equation x = 9.5.\n\nAlternatively, perpendicular bisector of BC:\n\nFirst, let's compute midpoint of BC again. Wait, we did that earlier for triangle ABC. Midpoint of BC is (15.5, sqrt(15)/2). The slope of BC was (-sqrt(15))/7, so the perpendicular slope is 7/sqrt(15), so equation is:\n\ny - sqrt(15)/2 = (7/sqrt(15))(x - 15.5)\n\nTherefore, the perpendicular bisector of BC is the same line as before, which we can use to find intersection with x = 9.5?\n\nWait, but if I take perpendicular bisector of CD (x = 9.5) and perpendicular bisector of BC (the line we had above), then their intersection will be the circumcenter of triangle BCD.\n\nSo, let me proceed.\n\nPerpendicular bisector of CD is x = 9.5.\n\nPerpendicular bisector of BC is:\n\ny - sqrt(15)/2 = (7/sqrt(15))(x - 15.5)\n\nSubstituting x = 9.5 into this equation:\n\ny - sqrt(15)/2 = (7/sqrt(15))(9.5 - 15.5) = (7/sqrt(15))(-6) = -42/sqrt(15)\n\nTherefore,\n\ny = sqrt(15)/2 - 42/sqrt(15)\n\nAgain, let me rationalize the second term:\n\n-42/sqrt(15) = -42 sqrt(15)/15 = -14 sqrt(15)/5\n\nSo,\n\ny = sqrt(15)/2 - (14 sqrt(15))/5\n\nFactor out sqrt(15):\n\nsqrt(15) [1/2 - 14/5] = sqrt(15) [ (5 - 28)/10 ] = sqrt(15) [ -23/10 ] = - (23/10) sqrt(15)\n\nTherefore, the circumcenter of triangle BCD is at (9.5, -23/10 sqrt(15)), or in fractions, (19/2, - (23/10) sqrt(15)).\n\nAlright, moving on to triangle CDA.\n\nTriangle CDA has vertices C(19, 0), D(0, 0), and A(1, sqrt(15)).\n\nSo, points:\n\nC: (19, 0),\n\nD: (0, 0),\n\nA: (1, sqrt(15)).\n\nLet me find its circumcenter.\n\nAgain, let me compute the perpendicular bisectors of two sides.\n\nFirst, let's take side CD again, which is from (0,0) to (19,0). Its perpendicular bisector is the same as before, x = 9.5. Wait, but CD is part of triangle CDA, so the perpendicular bisector of CD is still x = 9.5, but let's see if we can do another side for verification.\n\nAlternatively, let's take side DA and side DC.\n\nWait, let's pick sides CD and CA.\n\nFirst, perpendicular bisector of CD is x = 9.5, as above.\n\nNow, let's compute the perpendicular bisector of DA.\n\nSide DA is from D(0,0) to A(1, sqrt(15)).\n\nMidpoint of DA:\n\nx_mid_DA = (0 + 1)/2 = 0.5,\n\ny_mid_DA = (0 + sqrt(15))/2 = sqrt(15)/2.\n\nSlope of DA:\n\nSlope_DA = (sqrt(15) - 0)/(1 - 0) = sqrt(15)/1 = sqrt(15).\n\nTherefore, the perpendicular bisector of DA has slope = -1/sqrt(15).\n\nEquation of perpendicular bisector of DA:\n\ny - sqrt(15)/2 = (-1/sqrt(15))(x - 0.5)\n\nSo,\n\ny = (-1/sqrt(15))x + 0.5/sqrt(15) + sqrt(15)/2\n\nLet me combine the constants:\n\nFirst term: (-1/sqrt(15))x,\n\nSecond term: 0.5/sqrt(15),\n\nThird term: sqrt(15)/2.\n\nTo combine the constants, let me express sqrt(15)/2 as (sqrt(15) * sqrt(15))/(2 sqrt(15)) )? Wait, perhaps better to get a common denominator.\n\nAlternatively, let me rationalize 0.5/sqrt(15):\n\n0.5/sqrt(15) = (1/2)/sqrt(15) = sqrt(15)/(2*15) = sqrt(15)/30.\n\nWait, actually, no:\n\nWait, 0.5 / sqrt(15) = (1/2) / sqrt(15) = multiply numerator and denominator by sqrt(15):\n\n(1/2) * sqrt(15) / (sqrt(15)*sqrt(15)) = (sqrt(15)/2) / 15 = sqrt(15)/(30).\n\nTherefore,\n\nsqrt(15)/2 = (sqrt(15)/2)*(15/15) = (15 sqrt(15))/30? Wait, no, that's not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps just leave it as is for now.\n\nSo, equation is:\n\ny = (-1/sqrt(15))x + [sqrt(15)/30 + sqrt(15)/2]\n\nWait, let me see:\n\nWait, actually, let me re-express all terms with denominator sqrt(15):\n\nWait, perhaps instead, just keep the terms as they are and plug in x = 9.5 into the equation of the perpendicular bisector of DA to find y.\n\nWait, since the perpendicular bisector of CD is x = 9.5, so the intersection point (the circumcenter) must lie on x = 9.5, so we can plug x = 9.5 into the equation for the perpendicular bisector of DA to find y.\n\nSo, substituting x = 9.5 into the equation:\n\ny = (-1/sqrt(15))*(9.5) + 0.5/sqrt(15) + sqrt(15)/2\n\nCompute each term:\n\nFirst term: (-9.5)/sqrt(15)\n\nSecond term: 0.5/sqrt(15)\n\nThird term: sqrt(15)/2\n\nCombine first and second terms:\n\n(-9.5 + 0.5)/sqrt(15) = (-9)/sqrt(15)\n\nTherefore,\n\ny = -9/sqrt(15) + sqrt(15)/2\n\nAgain, let me rationalize -9/sqrt(15):\n\n-9/sqrt(15) = -9 sqrt(15)/15 = - (3 sqrt(15))/5\n\nAnd sqrt(15)/2 remains as is.\n\nTherefore,\n\ny = - (3 sqrt(15)/5) + (sqrt(15)/2) = sqrt(15) [ -3/5 + 1/2 ] = sqrt(15) [ (-6/10 + 5/10) ] = sqrt(15) [ (-1/10) ] = - sqrt(15)/10\n\nTherefore, the circumcenter of triangle CDA is at (9.5, - sqrt(15)/10) or (19/2, - (sqrt(15)/10)).\n\nWait, that's different from the previous two circumcenters, which were below the x-axis, but this one is only slightly below? Wait, actually, sqrt(15)/10 is positive, so negative of that is negative, so still below the x-axis, but not as far. Let me confirm my calculations.\n\nFirst, equation of perpendicular bisector of DA:\n\nMidpoint of DA is (0.5, sqrt(15)/2).\n\nSlope of DA is sqrt(15), so perpendicular slope is -1/sqrt(15). Correct.\n\nEquation: y - sqrt(15)/2 = (-1/sqrt(15))(x - 0.5). Correct.\n\nSo, when x = 9.5,\n\ny = [ -1/sqrt(15)*(9.5 - 0.5) ] + sqrt(15)/2\n\nWait, hold on: Wait, let me re-express the equation step by step.\n\nThe equation is:\n\ny = (-1/sqrt(15))(x - 0.5) + sqrt(15)/2.\n\nWait, that might be a better way to write it. Let me see:\n\nStarting from point-slope form:\n\ny - y1 = m(x - x1),\n\nso,\n\ny - sqrt(15)/2 = (-1/sqrt(15))(x - 0.5),\n\ntherefore,\n\ny = (-1/sqrt(15))(x - 0.5) + sqrt(15)/2\n\nYes, that's better.\n\nTherefore, substituting x = 9.5,\n\ny = (-1/sqrt(15))(9.5 - 0.5) + sqrt(15)/2\n\nCompute 9.5 - 0.5 = 9,\n\nSo,\n\ny = (-9)/sqrt(15) + sqrt(15)/2\n\nWhich is the same as before.\n\nThen,\n\n-9/sqrt(15) + sqrt(15)/2 = (-9/sqrt(15)) + (sqrt(15)/2)\n\nConvert to common denominator? Let's see:\n\nLet me write both terms with denominator 2 sqrt(15):\n\nFirst term: (-9)/sqrt(15) = (-18)/(2 sqrt(15))\n\nSecond term: sqrt(15)/2 = (sqrt(15) * sqrt(15))/(2 sqrt(15)) ) = (15)/(2 sqrt(15))\n\nWait, that might not be helpful, but let me see:\n\nAlternatively,\n\nLet me factor out 1/sqrt(15):\n\ny = [ -9 + (sqrt(15))^2 / 2 ] / sqrt(15) ?\n\nWait, no, perhaps just compute numerically?\n\nWait, sqrt(15) is approximately 3.87298, but maybe I can keep it symbolic.\n\nWait, sqrt(15)/2 is equal to (sqrt(15)/2), and -9/sqrt(15) is equal to -9/sqrt(15).\n\nLet me write them as:\n\nsqrt(15)/2 - 9/sqrt(15) = [ (sqrt(15))^2 / (2 sqrt(15)) ) ] - [ 9 / sqrt(15) ]\n\nWait, that is:\n\nsqrt(15)/2 = (15)/(2 sqrt(15)), so:\n\nsqrt(15)/2 - 9/sqrt(15) = (15)/(2 sqrt(15)) - 9/sqrt(15) = [15 - 18]/(2 sqrt(15)) = (-3)/(2 sqrt(15)) = - (3)/(2 sqrt(15))\n\nWait, hold on, let me see:\n\nWait, if I have:\n\nsqrt(15)/2 - 9/sqrt(15),\n\nLet me write both terms over a common denominator of 2 sqrt(15):\n\nFirst term: sqrt(15)/2 = (sqrt(15) * sqrt(15)) / (2 sqrt(15)) ) = 15 / (2 sqrt(15))\n\nSecond term: -9/sqrt(15) = -9 * 2 / (2 sqrt(15)) ) = -18 / (2 sqrt(15))\n\nTherefore,\n\nTotal: [15 - 18]/(2 sqrt(15)) = (-3)/(2 sqrt(15)) = - (3)/(2 sqrt(15))\n\nThen, rationalizing the denominator:\n\n- (3)/(2 sqrt(15)) = - (3 sqrt(15)) / (2 * 15) ) = - (sqrt(15)/10 )\n\nAh! There we go. So,\n\ny = - (sqrt(15)/10 )\n\nTherefore, that's correct. So the y-coordinate is -sqrt(15)/10. So the circumcenter of triangle CDA is at (9.5, -sqrt(15)/10). Got it. So that's (19/2, - sqrt(15)/10). Alright.\n\nOkay, moving on to the fourth triangle: triangle DAB.\n\nTriangle DAB has vertices D(0, 0), A(1, sqrt(15)), and B(12, sqrt(15)).\n\nSo, points:\n\nD: (0, 0),\n\nA: (1, sqrt(15)),\n\nB: (12, sqrt(15)).\n\nLet me find its circumcenter.\n\nAgain, let's compute the perpendicular bisectors of two sides.\n\nFirst, perhaps side DA and side DB?\n\nAlternatively, let's pick sides AB and AD.\n\nFirst, side AB is from A(1, sqrt(15)) to B(12, sqrt(15)), which is the same as in triangle ABC. The midpoint of AB is (6.5, sqrt(15)), and since AB is horizontal, the perpendicular bisector is vertical line x = 6.5, same as before.\n\nAlternatively, let's do the perpendicular bisector of AB and the perpendicular bisector of AD.\n\nLet me try that.\n\nFirst, perpendicular bisector of AB is x = 6.5, as before.\n\nNow, let's compute the perpendicular bisector of AD.\n\nSide AD is from D(0,0) to A(1, sqrt(15)).\n\nMidpoint of AD:\n\nx_mid_AD = (0 + 1)/2 = 0.5,\n\ny_mid_AD = (0 + sqrt(15))/2 = sqrt(15)/2.\n\nSlope of AD:\n\nSlope_AD = (sqrt(15) - 0)/(1 - 0) = sqrt(15)/1 = sqrt(15).\n\nTherefore, the perpendicular bisector of AD will have slope = -1/sqrt(15).\n\nEquation of perpendicular bisector of AD:\n\nUsing point-slope form, through midpoint (0.5, sqrt(15)/2):\n\ny - sqrt(15)/2 = (-1/sqrt(15))(x - 0.5)\n\nThis is the same equation as the perpendicular bisector of DA for triangle CDA. Wait, indeed, since DA is the same as AD. So, this is the same line as before for triangle CDA.\n\nSo, the perpendicular bisector of AD is the same as the perpendicular bisector of DA, which we already used for triangle CDA.\n\nTherefore, the perpendicular bisector of AD is the line:\n\ny = (-1/sqrt(15))x + 0.5/sqrt(15) + sqrt(15)/2\n\nWait, let me re-express that equation again, perhaps in a simplified form.\n\nWait, let me do it step by step again:\n\nEquation: y - sqrt(15)/2 = (-1/sqrt(15))(x - 0.5)\n\nSo,\n\ny = (-1/sqrt(15))x + (0.5)/sqrt(15) + sqrt(15)/2\n\nCombine the constants:\n\nFirst term: (-1/sqrt(15))x,\n\nSecond term: 0.5/sqrt(15),\n\nThird term: sqrt(15)/2.\n\nLet me combine the constants:\n\n0.5/sqrt(15) + sqrt(15)/2.\n\nLet me write both terms over a common denominator, which would be 2 sqrt(15):\n\nFirst term: 0.5/sqrt(15) = (1)/ (2 sqrt(15)),\n\nSecond term: sqrt(15)/2 = (sqrt(15) * sqrt(15)) / (2 sqrt(15)) ) = 15 / (2 sqrt(15)),\n\nWait, actually, that's not necessary. Let me compute numerically:\n\nAlternatively,\n\nLet me factor out 1/sqrt(15):\n\n0.5/sqrt(15) + sqrt(15)/2 = [0.5 + (sqrt(15))^2 / 2 ] / sqrt(15) ?\n\nWait, perhaps better to compute numerically:\n\nsqrt(15)/2 is approximately 3.87298/2 ≈ 1.93649,\n\n0.5/sqrt(15) ≈ 0.5 / 3.87298 ≈ 0.129,\n\nSo total ≈ 1.93649 + 0.129 ≈ 2.065, but this might not help.\n\nAlternatively, let's just keep it as is for now.\n\nSo, the equation is:\n\ny = (-1/sqrt(15))x + [0.5/sqrt(15) + sqrt(15)/2]\n\nNow, the perpendicular bisector of AB is x = 6.5. So, substituting x = 6.5 into this equation to find y-coordinate of the circumcenter.\n\nSo,\n\ny = (-1/sqrt(15))*(6.5) + [0.5/sqrt(15) + sqrt(15)/2]\n\nCompute each term:\n\nFirst term: -6.5 / sqrt(15)\n\nSecond term: 0.5 / sqrt(15)\n\nThird term: sqrt(15)/2\n\nCombine first and second terms:\n\n(-6.5 + 0.5)/sqrt(15) = (-6)/sqrt(15)\n\nTherefore,\n\ny = -6/sqrt(15) + sqrt(15)/2\n\nAgain, let me rationalize -6/sqrt(15):\n\n-6/sqrt(15) = -6 sqrt(15)/15 = - (2 sqrt(15))/5\n\nAnd sqrt(15)/2 remains as is.\n\nTherefore,\n\ny = - (2 sqrt(15)/5) + (sqrt(15)/2) = sqrt(15) [ -2/5 + 1/2 ] = sqrt(15) [ (-4/10 + 5/10) ] = sqrt(15) (1/10) = sqrt(15)/10\n\nSo, the y-coordinate is sqrt(15)/10.\n\nTherefore, the circumcenter of triangle DAB is at (6.5, sqrt(15)/10), which is (13/2, sqrt(15)/10).\n\nAlright, so now we have the four circumcenters:\n\n1. Triangle ABC: O1 = (13/2, - (37/10) sqrt(15)) ≈ (6.5, -5.916 sqrt(15))\n\nWait, hold on, actually, let me note the exact coordinates:\n\nO1: (13/2, - (37/10) sqrt(15))\n\nO2: Triangle BCD: (19/2, - (23/10) sqrt(15)) ≈ (9.5, -2.3 sqrt(15))\n\nO3: Triangle CDA: (19/2, - (1/10) sqrt(15)) ≈ (9.5, -0.316 sqrt(15))\n\nO4: Triangle DAB: (13/2, (1/10) sqrt(15)) ≈ (6.5, 0.316 sqrt(15))\n\nSo, these four points form the quadrilateral. Let me list them again with exact coordinates:\n\nO1: (13/2, - (37/10)√15 )\n\nO2: (19/2, - (23/10)√15 )\n\nO3: (19/2, - (1/10)√15 )\n\nO4: (13/2, (1/10)√15 )\n\nHmm, so let me write them as fractions:\n\nO1: (6.5, -3.7√15 )\n\nO2: (9.5, -2.3√15 )\n\nO3: (9.5, -0.1√15 )\n\nO4: (6.5, 0.1√15 )\n\nWait, actually, in fractions:\n\n- (37/10) is -3.7,\n\n- (23/10) is -2.3,\n\n- (1/10) is -0.1,\n\nand + (1/10) is +0.1.\n\nSo, coordinates:\n\nO1: (13/2, -37√15/10 )\n\nO2: (19/2, -23√15/10 )\n\nO3: (19/2, -√15/10 )\n\nO4: (13/2, √15/10 )\n\nNow, to compute the area of the quadrilateral formed by these four points, we can use the shoelace formula. However, since these points might not be in order, we need to make sure they are listed in order (either clockwise or counterclockwise) around the quadrilateral.\n\nFirst, let me try to visualize the positions of these four points.\n\nLooking at their coordinates:\n\n- O1 is at (6.5, -37√15/10). Since √15 ≈ 3.872, so 37√15/10 ≈ 37*3.872/10 ≈ 143.984/10 ≈ 14.3984. So the y-coordinate is about -14.4, so this point is far below the x-axis.\n\n- O2 is at (9.5, -23√15/10). Similarly, 23√15/10 ≈ 23*3.872/10 ≈ 89.056/10 ≈ 8.9056, so y ≈ -8.906.\n\n- O3 is at (9.5, -√15/10) ≈ (9.5, -0.3872).\n\n- O4 is at (6.5, √15/10) ≈ (6.5, 0.3872).\n\nSo, plotting these points mentally:\n\n- O4 is above the x-axis, at (6.5, ~0.387),\n\n- O3 is just below the x-axis at (9.5, ~-0.387),\n\n- O2 is further down at (9.5, ~-8.906),\n\n- O1 is the lowest at (6.5, ~-14.4).\n\nSo, connecting these points in order... Hmm, to form a convex quadrilateral, the order should be such that the points are connected in sequence without crossing.\n\nLooking at their approximate coordinates:\n\nStarting from O4 (6.5, ~0.387), then moving to O3 (9.5, ~-0.387), then to O2 (9.5, ~-8.906), then to O1 (6.5, ~-14.4), and back to O4? Wait, but that might not be convex? Wait, let's see.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the order is O1, O2, O3, O4? Let's see.\n\nWait, perhaps we need to figure out the correct cyclic order. Since it's a convex quadrilateral, the points should be arranged such that each interior angle is less than 180 degrees, and the vertices are ordered either clockwise or counterclockwise around the perimeter.\n\nLet me try to think of their positions:\n\nO4 is the topmost point, at (6.5, ~0.387).\n\nO3 is to the right and slightly below O4, at (9.5, ~-0.387).\n\nO2 is further to the right but much lower at (9.5, ~-8.906).\n\nO1 is to the left of O2 but much lower at (6.5, ~-14.4).\n\nSo, if we imagine connecting them in the order O4 -> O3 -> O2 -> O1 -> O4, this would form a quadrilateral that goes from the top middle, down to the right to O3, then further down to O2, then left and down to O1, then back up to O4? Hmm, not sure if that's convex.\n\nAlternatively, maybe O1 is the bottom-left, O2 is bottom-right, O3 is middle-right, O4 is middle-left? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, let me list all four points with their approximate coordinates:\n\nO1: (6.5, -14.4)\n\nO2: (9.5, -8.906)\n\nO3: (9.5, -0.387)\n\nO4: (6.5, 0.387)\n\nSo, in the coordinate system, if we plot these:\n\n- O1 is the leftmost point? No, x-coordinate 6.5 for O1 and O4, but O2 and O3 are at x=9.5, which is to the right.\n\nWait, actually, the x-coordinates:\n\nO1 and O4 are at x=6.5,\n\nO2 and O3 are at x=9.5.\n\nSo, the quadrilateral has two points on the vertical line x=6.5 (O1 and O4) and two points on the vertical line x=9.5 (O2 and O3). So, the quadrilateral is symmetric? Wait, let me see.\n\nLooking at the coordinates:\n\nO1: (6.5, -14.4)\n\nO4: (6.5, +0.387)\n\nO2: (9.5, -8.906)\n\nO3: (9.5, -0.387)\n\nSo, if you connect O4 to O3 to O2 to O1 and back to O4, that might form a quadrilateral. Let me see:\n\nFrom O4 (6.5, +0.387) to O3 (9.5, -0.387): that's a diagonal down to the right.\n\nThen from O3 (9.5, -0.387) to O2 (9.5, -8.906): straight down along the vertical line x=9.5.\n\nThen from O2 (9.5, -8.906) to O1 (6.5, -14.4): diagonal up and left.\n\nThen from O1 back to O4: diagonal up and right.\n\nHmm, this seems like a trapezoid? Or maybe a kite? Not sure. Alternatively, perhaps it's a convex quadrilateral, but to apply shoelace, we need the points in order.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the correct order is O4, O3, O2, O1, back to O4? Let me see:\n\nPlotting the approximate points:\n\n- Start at O4 (6.5, ~0.387): top center-left,\n\n- Then go to O3 (9.5, ~-0.387): right and down a bit,\n\n- Then to O2 (9.5, ~-8.906): straight down,\n\n- Then to O1 (6.5, ~-14.4): left and down,\n\n- Then back to O4: up and right.\n\nThis seems to form a convex quadrilateral, as all the turns are in the same direction (probably clockwise). Let me check with the coordinates.\n\nAlternatively, let's order the points such that when connected in order, the polygon does not intersect itself.\n\nAlternatively, since two points are on x=6.5 and two on x=9.5, perhaps the quadrilateral is symmetric with respect to the vertical line x = (6.5 + 9.5)/2 = 8?\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nThe x-coordinates are 6.5 and 9.5. The midpoint between 6.5 and 9.5 is (6.5 + 9.5)/2 = 16/2 = 8. So, the vertical line x=8 is the axis of symmetry?\n\nLooking at the y-coordinates:\n\nFor x=6.5: O1 is at y ≈ -14.4 and O4 at y ≈ +0.387,\n\nFor x=9.5: O2 at y ≈ -8.906 and O3 at y ≈ -0.387.\n\nHmm, so not symmetric in y, but maybe symmetric in some other way?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the quadrilateral is symmetric across the horizontal line through the midpoint between O1 and O4?\n\nThe midpoint between O1 (6.5, -14.4) and O4 (6.5, 0.387) is (6.5, (-14.4 + 0.387)/2 ) ≈ (6.5, -6.7565). Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the quadrilateral is a trapezoid? Let's see if any sides are parallel.\n\nCompute the slopes between the points in the order O4-O3-O2-O1-O4.\n\nFirst, O4 to O3: from (6.5, ~0.387) to (9.5, ~-0.387). The slope is (-0.387 - 0.387)/(9.5 - 6.5) = (-0.774)/3 ≈ -0.258.\n\nO3 to O2: from (9.5, -0.387) to (9.5, -8.906). This is vertical line, undefined slope.\n\nO2 to O1: from (9.5, -8.906) to (6.5, -14.4). Slope: (-14.4 - (-8.906))/(6.5 - 9.5) = (-5.494)/(-3) ≈ 1.831.\n\nO1 to O4: from (6.5, -14.4) to (6.5, 0.387). This is a vertical line, undefined slope.\n\nHmm, so two sides are vertical (O3-O2 and O1-O4), and the other two sides have slopes ~-0.258 and ~1.831, which are not parallel. So it's not a trapezoid. So it's a general convex quadrilateral.\n\nAlternatively, if we order the points differently, maybe we can get a better shape? Let me see.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the correct order is O1, O2, O3, O4? Let's see:\n\nO1 (6.5, -14.4) to O2 (9.5, -8.906): slope is (-8.906 +14.4)/(9.5 -6.5)= (5.494)/3 ≈ 1.831,\n\nO2 to O3 (9.5, -0.387): vertical line down? Wait, no, from (9.5, -8.906) to (9.5, -0.387): vertical line upwards,\n\nO3 to O4 (6.5, 0.387): slope (0.387 - (-0.387))/(6.5 -9.5)= (0.774)/(-3)≈ -0.258,\n\nO4 to O1: from (6.5,0.387) to (6.5, -14.4): vertical line down.\n\nHmm, so in this ordering, we have two vertical sides (O2-O3 and O4-O1), and the other sides have slopes ~1.831 and ~-0.258, so still not a trapezoid, but perhaps a convex quadrilateral.\n\nAlternatively, maybe O4, O1, O2, O3? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to just assign coordinates and use the shoelace formula with the points in order as we have them, as long as they are ordered either clockwise or counter-clockwise without crossing.\n\nWait, perhaps the correct order is O4, O3, O2, O1, back to O4. Let me try that.\n\nLet me list the coordinates in that order:\n\nO4: (13/2, √15/10) = (6.5, approx 0.387)\n\nO3: (19/2, -√15/10) = (9.5, approx -0.387)\n\nO2: (19/2, -23√15/10) = (9.5, approx -8.906)\n\nO1: (13/2, -37√15/10) = (6.5, approx -14.4)\n\nThen back to O4.\n\nLet me write them as ordered pairs (x, y):\n\n1. O4: (13/2, √15/10)\n\n2. O3: (19/2, -√15/10)\n\n3. O2: (19/2, -23√15/10)\n\n4. O1: (13/2, -37√15/10)\n\nThen back to O4.\n\nLet me confirm that this ordering is indeed a convex quadrilateral and that the points are ordered correctly (either clockwise or counter-clockwise).\n\nStarting at O4 (top center-left), moving to O3 (right and down a bit), then down along the same x-coordinate to O2 (farther down), then left along the same y-coordinate to O1 (far left and down), then back up to O4? Hmm, actually, from O1 back to O4 is a vertical line upwards? Wait, O1 is at (6.5, -37√15/10) and O4 is at (6.5, √15/10). So yes, the line from O1 to O4 is vertical.\n\nBut in terms of the polygon, this path should not intersect itself. Let me see:\n\n- From O4 to O3: moving right and down,\n\n- O3 to O2: moving straight down,\n\n- O2 to O1: moving left and slightly up (since y goes from -8.906 to -14.4, wait no: O2 is at y ≈ -8.906 and O1 is at y ≈ -14.4, so actually moving down further and left),\n\nWait, hold on: O2 is at (9.5, -23√15/10) ≈ (9.5, -8.906),\n\nO1 is at (6.5, -37√15/10) ≈ (6.5, -14.4),\n\nSo moving from O2 (9.5, -8.906) to O1 (6.5, -14.4): this is moving left and down,\n\nThen from O1 back to O4 (6.5, ~0.387): moving up along the same x-coordinate.\n\nHmm, this seems to form a convex quadrilateral as all the interior angles are less than 180 degrees? Maybe.\n\nAlternatively, to be safe, let's apply the shoelace formula with this ordering and see if we get a positive area (which we should), and then perhaps compute the area.\n\nAlternatively, let me try another order, perhaps O1, O2, O3, O4.\n\nO1: (6.5, -14.4)\n\nO2: (9.5, -8.906)\n\nO3: (9.5, -0.387)\n\nO4: (6.5, 0.387)\n\nBack to O1.\n\nLet me see if this ordering is clockwise or counter-clockwise.\n\nStarting at O1 (lower left), going to O2 (lower right), then up to O3 (middle right), then to O4 (middle left), then back to O1. This seems like a clockwise ordering.\n\nLet me try this order:\n\nOrder: O1, O2, O3, O4, back to O1.\n\nLet me list the coordinates in this order:\n\n1. O1: (13/2, -37√15/10 )\n\n2. O2: (19/2, -23√15/10 )\n\n3. O3: (19/2, -√15/10 )\n\n4. O4: (13/2, √15/10 )\n\nBack to O1.\n\nThis should be a clockwise ordering. Let me apply the shoelace formula.\n\nThe shoelace formula says that the area is 1/2 |sum_{i=1 to n} (x_i y_{i+1} - x_{i+1} y_i)|,\n\nwhere the vertices are ordered sequentially around the polygon, either clockwise or counter-clockwise.\n\nSo, let me assign the coordinates as follows, using fractions to keep exactness:\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet’s use variables for clarity.\n\nLet me let:\n\nLet’s denote:\n\nLet’s let’s set:\n\nLet’s denote:\n\nLet’s let’s define:\n\nLet’s let’s define the four points as:\n\nP1: O1 = (13/2, -37√15/10 )\n\nP2: O2 = (19/2, -23√15/10 )\n\nP3: O3 = (19/2, -√15/10 )\n\nP4: O4 = (13/2, √15/10 )\n\nAnd back to P1.\n\nSo, writing them in order P1, P2, P3, P4, P1.\n\nShoelace formula:\n\nCompute S = 1/2 | (x1y2 - x2y1) + (x2y3 - x3y2) + (x3y4 - x4y3) + (x4y1 - x1y4) |.\n\nLet me compute each term step by step.\n\nFirst, compute x1, y1 through x4, y4:\n\nP1: x1 = 13/2, y1 = -37√15/10\n\nP2: x2 = 19/2, y2 = -23√15/10\n\nP3: x3 = 19/2, y3 = -√15/10\n\nP4: x4 = 13/2, y4 = √15/10\n\nNow compute each term:\n\nTerm1: x1 y2 - x2 y1\n\n= (13/2)(-23√15/10) - (19/2)(-37√15/10)\n\nCompute each part:\n\nFirst part: (13/2)(-23√15/10) = (13 * -23 √15) / (2*10) = (-299 √15)/20\n\nSecond part: -(19/2)(-37√15/10) = + (19 * 37 √15) / (2*10) = (703 √15)/20\n\nTherefore, Term1 = (-299√15 + 703√15)/20 = (404√15)/20\n\nSimplify: 404/20 = 101/5, so Term1 = (101/5)√15\n\nTerm2: x2 y3 - x3 y2\n\n= (19/2)(-√15/10) - (19/2)(-23√15/10)\n\nCompute each part:\n\nFirst part: (19/2)(-√15/10) = (-19√15)/20\n\nSecond part: - (19/2)(-23√15/10) = + (19 * 23 √15)/(2*10) = (437√15)/20\n\nTherefore, Term2 = (-19√15 + 437√15)/20 = (418√15)/20\n\nSimplify: 418/20 = 209/10, so Term2 = (209/10)√15\n\nTerm3: x3 y4 - x4 y3\n\n= (19/2)(√15/10) - (13/2)(-√15/10)\n\nCompute each part:\n\nFirst part: (19/2)(√15/10) = (19√15)/20\n\nSecond part: - (13/2)(-√15/10) = + (13√15)/20\n\nTherefore, Term3 = (19√15 + 13√15)/20 = (32√15)/20 = (8√15)/5\n\nTerm4: x4 y1 - x1 y4\n\n= (13/2)(-37√15/10) - (13/2)(√15/10)\n\nCompute each part:\n\nFirst part: (13/2)(-37√15/10) = (-481√15)/20\n\nSecond part: - (13/2)(√15/10) = - (13√15)/20\n\nTherefore, Term4 = (-481√15 - 13√15)/20 = (-494√15)/20 = (-247√15)/10\n\nNow, sum all four terms:\n\nTerm1 + Term2 + Term3 + Term4\n\n= (101/5)√15 + (209/10)√15 + (8/5)√15 + (-247/10)√15\n\nFirst, let me convert all terms to tenths to combine:\n\n101/5 = 202/10,\n\n209/10 stays as is,\n\n8/5 = 16/10,\n\n-247/10 stays as is.\n\nSo,\n\n= (202/10 + 209/10 + 16/10 - 247/10) √15\n\nCompute the coefficients:\n\n202 + 209 = 411,\n\n411 + 16 = 427,\n\n427 - 247 = 180,\n\nThus,\n\n= (180/10) √15 = 18√15.\n\nTherefore, the sum S before taking absolute value and multiplying by 1/2 is 18√15.\n\nHence, the area is (1/2)*|18√15| = 9√15.\n\nWait, that seems clean. So the area is 9√15?\n\nHmm, let me verify the calculations step by step because that seems surprisingly simple,