- 1540√15 q + 750 q²]\n\nSecond term: 3q² * 361\n\nThird term: [1078 - 70√15 q]/19 * 361 = [1078 - 70√15 q] * (361 / 19) = [1078 - 70√15 q] * 19\n\nBecause 361 /19 = 19.\n\nFourth term: -49 * 361\n\nTherefore, the equation becomes:\n\n[11858 - 1540√15 q + 750 q²] + 3q² * 361 + [1078 - 70√15 q] * 19 - 49 * 361 = 0\n\nNow let me compute each part step by step.\n\nFirst part: [11858 - 1540√15 q + 750 q²]\n\nSecond part: 3q² * 361 = 1083 q²\n\nThird part: [1078 - 70√15 q] * 19\n\nLet me compute that:\n\nFirst, 1078 * 19:\n\n1078 * 20 = 21,560; subtract 1078 to get 1078 * 19 = 21,560 - 1,078 = 20,482.\n\nThen, -70√15 q * 19 = -1,330√15 q.\n\nTherefore, third part is 20,482 - 1,330√15 q.\n\nFourth part: -49 * 361.\n\nCompute 50*361 = 18,050, so 49*361 = 18,050 - 361 = 17,689. Therefore, -49*361 = -17,689.\n\nNow, let me combine all these together:\n\nFirst part: 11858 - 1540√15 q + 750 q²\n\nSecond part: +1083 q²\n\nThird part: +20,482 - 1,330√15 q\n\nFourth part: -17,689\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nStart with the constant terms:\n\n11,858 + 20,482 - 17,689.\n\nCompute 11,858 + 20,482 = 32,340\n\nThen 32,340 - 17,689 = 14,651.\n\nNext, terms with √15 q:\n\n-1540√15 q - 1,330√15 q = (-1540 - 1330)√15 q = -2870√15 q\n\nTerms with q²:\n\n750 q² + 1083 q² = (750 + 1083) q² = 1833 q²\n\nTherefore, the entire equation becomes:\n\n1833 q² - 2870√15 q + 14,651 = 0\n\nHmm, so we have a quadratic equation in terms of q:\n\n1833 q² - 2870√15 q + 14651 = 0\n\nThis is a quadratic equation of the form A q² + B q + C = 0, where:\n\nA = 1833,\n\nB = -2870√15,\n\nC = 14,651.\n\nThis seems very complicated. Let me see if I can compute the discriminant and solve for q?\n\nThe discriminant D = B² - 4AC.\n\nFirst, compute B²:\n\nB² = ( -2870√15 )² = (2870)^2 * (√15)^2 = (2870)^2 * 15.\n\nCompute 2870 squared:\n\n2870^2: Let me compute 287^2 * 10^2, since 2870 = 287 * 10.\n\n287^2: Let me compute 287*287.\n\nFirst, compute 280^2 = 78,400.\n\nThen, (280 + 7)^2 = 280^2 + 2*280*7 + 7^2 = 78,400 + 3,920 + 49 = 78,400 + 3,920 = 82,320; 82,320 + 49 = 82,369.\n\nTherefore, 287^2 = 82,369, so 2870^2 = 82,369 * 100 = 8,236,900.\n\nTherefore, B² = 8,236,900 * 15 = let me compute that:\n\n8,236,900 * 10 = 82,369,000\n\n8,236,900 * 5 = 41,184,500\n\nAdding together: 82,369,000 + 41,184,500 = 123,553,500.\n\nSo B² = 123,553,500.\n\nNow compute 4AC:\n\n4 * A * C = 4 * 1833 * 14,651.\n\nFirst compute 4 * 1833 = 7,332.\n\nThen, 7,332 * 14,651.\n\nThis is going to be a huge number. Maybe we can see if this quadratic factors nicely? Or perhaps there is a miscalculation earlier?\n\nWait a second, this is getting really messy. Maybe I made an error in setting up the equations? Let me check my steps again.\n\nStarting from the angle condition, I used the dot product formula, which should be okay.\n\nThen, when I substituted CP = 2 BP, that should be okay since BP/PC = 1/2 ⇒ CP = 2 BP.\n\nThen, when I set up the equation with the cosines, and then multiplied both sides by 48, that should be okay.\n\nThen expanding everything, perhaps I messed up an arithmetic step?\n\nLet me double-check the expansion of the equation after multiplying by 48:\n\nOriginal equation after multiplying both sides by 48:\n\nLeft side: 48*( [ (3p/2 + 3√15 q / 2 ) / (6 BP) ] ) = 48*( [ (3p + 3√15 q)/2 ) / (6 BP) ] ) = 48*( (3p + 3√15 q) / (12 BP) ) = 48/12 * (3p + 3√15 q)/BP = 4*(3p + 3√15 q)/BP\n\nWait, hold on! Wait, here's a possible error.\n\nWait, let me go back to this step:\n\nOriginal equation before multiplying by 48:\n\nLeft side: [ (3p/2 + 3√15 q / 2 ) / (6 BP) ]\n\nRight side: [ ( (-11/2)(p - 7) + 3√15 q / 2 ) / (16 BP) ]\n\nThen, when I multiplied both sides by 48, I think I made a mistake here.\n\nWait, let me redo this step carefully.\n\nLeft side: [ (3p/2 + 3√15 q / 2 ) / (6 BP) ]\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator:\n\nThe numerator is (3p/2 + 3√15 q / 2 ) = (3/2)(p + √15 q)\n\nDenominator is 6 BP.\n\nSo the entire left side is [ (3/2)(p + √15 q) ] / (6 BP) = (3/2)/(6 BP) * (p + √15 q