*41.\n\n14651: Let me check divisibility by small primes. 14651 ÷ 7: 7*2093 = 14,651? 7*2000=14,000, 7*93=651, so yes, 7*(2000+93)=7*2093=14,651. So 14651 = 7*2093. Then 2093: check divisibility by 7: 7*299=2093? 7*300=2100, so 2100 -7=2093, so yes, 7*299. So 2093=7*299. Then 299: 13*23=299? 13*20=260, 13*23=299. Yes, because 13*20=260, 13*3=39, so 260+39=299. So 299=13*23. Thus, 14651=7*7*13*23.\n\nSo prime factors:\n\n1833 = 3 *13 *47\n\n2870 = 2*5*7*41\n\n14651 =7^2 *13*23\n\nHmm, so common factors? The only common prime factor between coefficients is 13? Let's see:\n\n1833 has 13,\n\n2870 has no 13,\n\n14651 has 13.\n\nSo, 13 is a common factor in first and third terms, but not the second term. So can't factor out 13 from all terms.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps this quadratic is meant to be solved numerically? But since this is a competition problem, likely the answer is a nice fraction, so perhaps there is a smarter approach that I’m missing?\n\nLet me think again.\n\nAlternative approach: Use Ceva’s Theorem?\n\nCeva's Theorem states that for concurrent lines from vertices to opposite sides, but in this case, point P is inside triangle ABC, but the lines might not be cevians to the sides. Wait, unless we can consider the cevians BP and CP, but they aren't necessarily meeting the opposite sides unless P is on a side, which it isn't.\n\nAlternatively, trigonometric Ceva's Theorem?\n\nYes! The trigonometric Ceva's Theorem states that if three cevians are concurrent, then:\n\n[ (sin angle ABP / sin angle CBP) * (sin angle BCP / sin angle ACP) * (sin angle CAP / sin angle BAP) ) ] = 1\n\nBut in our case, we have angle ABP = angle ACP = θ, perhaps we can set up ratios?\n\nWait, let me recall the trigonometric Ceva formula:\n\nFor concurrent cevians from A, B, C to the opposite sides, the following holds:\n\n( sin(∠ABP)/sin(∠CBP) ) * ( sin(∠BCP)/sin(∠ACP) ) * ( sin(∠CAP)/sin(∠BAP) ) = 1\n\nBut in our problem, we know that angle ABP = angle ACP = θ.\n\nLet me denote some variables.\n\nLet me let angle ABP = θ, so angle ACP = θ.\n\nLet me denote angle CBP as φ, so angle CBP = φ.\n\nSimilarly, let me denote angle BCP as ψ, so angle BCP = ψ.\n\nThen, angle ACP = θ, so angle BCP = ψ, and angle ACP is part of angle BCA? Wait, angle at C: angle BCA is split into angle BCP and angle ACP? Wait, no.\n\nWait, point P is inside the triangle, so at vertex C, the angles around point C: the angles at C related to the cevians. Wait, angle ACP is the angle between AC and CP, so that would be part of angle ACB.\n\nWait, angle at vertex C is angle ACB. If we have a cevian from C to P, then angle ACB is split into angle ACP and angle BCP. Therefore, angle ACP + angle BCP = angle ACB.\n\nSimilarly, at vertex B, angle ABC is split into angle ABP and angle CBP (assuming the cevian is BP). So angle ABP + angle CBP = angle ABC.\n\nSimilarly, at vertex A, if there is a cevian from A to P, then angle at A is split into two angles, but we don't have information about that.\n\nGiven that, in trigonometric Ceva, the formula is:\n\n[ (sin(angle ABP)/sin(angle CBP)) * (sin(angle BCP)/sin(angle ACP)) * (sin(angle CAP)/sin(angle BAP)) ] = 1\n\nWe know angle ABP = angle ACP = θ.\n\nLet me write down the known angles:\n\nangle ABP = θ,\n\nangle CBP = let's call it φ,\n\nangle BCP = let's call it ψ,\n\nangle ACP = θ,\n\nangle CAP = let's call it α,\n\nangle BAP = let's call it β.\n\nWe know that angle ABP + angle CBP = angle ABC ⇒ θ + φ = angle ABC,\n\nangle BCP + angle ACP = angle BCA ⇒ ψ + θ = angle BCA,\n\nand at vertex A, angle BAP + angle CAP = angle BAC ⇒ β + α = angle BAC.\n\nThe trigonometric Ceva condition becomes:\n\n[ sinθ / sinφ ) * ( sinψ / sinθ ) * ( sinα / sinβ ) ] = 1\n\nSimplify:\n\n[ (sinθ / sinφ) * ( sinψ / sinθ ) ] * ( sinα / sinβ ) = [ ( sinψ / sinφ ) ] * ( sinα / sinβ ) = 1\n\nTherefore,\n\n( sinψ / sinφ ) * ( sinα / sinβ ) = 1 ⇒ ( sinψ / sinφ ) = ( sinβ / sinα )\n\nHmm, not sure if that helps yet.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps if I can relate the ratios of the sides?\n\nWe also know that BP / PC = 1 / 2, so BP : PC = 1 : 2.\n\nIn Ceva's Theorem, there is also a ratio form involving the divisions on the sides, but since P is not necessarily on the sides, maybe the ratio form of Ceva's Theorem isn't directly applicable.\n\nWait, the ratio form of Ceva's Theorem states that for concurrent cevians from A, B, C to the opposite sides, meeting the sides at points D, E, F respectively, then (BD/DC)*(CE/EA)*(AF/FB) = 1.\n\nBut in our case, point P is inside the triangle, but we don't know where the cevians meet the opposite sides. Since we only know the ratio BP/PC, but PC is not along a side.\n\nHmm.\n\nAlternatively, use mass point geometry? But mass point usually requires ratios along the sides.\n\nWait, but we have BP / PC = 1/2, but BP and PC are not along the same line. So mass point might not be straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, use coordinates but try to find the area ratio without finding P's coordinates?\n\nThe area of triangle BPC over area of triangle ABC. Since we have coordinates for A, B, C, if we can find coordinates of P, then we can compute the areas via determinants.\n\nAlternatively, since we have two equations in p and q, and they are messy, perhaps we can assign variables or make substitutions to simplify?\n\nAlternatively, let me consider that the problem gives AB=6, AC=8, BC=7, so maybe compute the area of ABC first, to have a reference?\n\nYes, let me compute the area of triangle ABC using Heron's formula, since we know all three sides.\n\nGiven triangle ABC with sides a=BC=7, b=AC=8, c=AB=6.\n\nWait, actually, standard notation is usually a=BC, b=AC, c=AB, but let me confirm:\n\nUsually, in triangle ABC, side a is opposite vertex A, so side BC is a, side AC is b, side AB is c.\n\nSo with that:\n\na = BC = 7,\n\nb = AC = 8,\n\nc = AB = 6.\n\nThen semi-perimeter s = (a + b + c)/2 = (7 + 8 + 6)/2 = 21/2 = 10.5\n\nArea = sqrt( s(s - a)(s - b)(s - c) ) = sqrt( 10.5*(10.5 - 7)*(10.5 - 8)*(10.5 - 6) )\n\nCompute each term:\n\ns - a = 10.5 - 7 = 3.5\n\ns - b = 10.5 - 8 = 2.5\n\ns - c = 10.5 - 6 = 4.5\n\nTherefore, area = sqrt( 10.5 * 3.5 * 2.5 * 4.5 )\n\nHmm, let me compute this:\n\nFirst, convert to fractions to make it easier:\n\n10.5 = 21/2,\n\n3.5 = 7/2,\n\n2.5 = 5/2,\n\n4.5 = 9/2.\n\nTherefore,\n\nArea = sqrt( (21/2)*(7/2)*(5/2)*(9/2) ) = sqrt( (21*7*5*9) / (2^4) )\n\nCompute numerator: 21*7 = 147; 147*5 = 735; 735*9 = 6,615\n\nDenominator: 16\n\nTherefore, Area = sqrt( 6615 / 16 ) = (sqrt(6615))/4.\n\nHmm, sqrt(6615). Let me see if 6615 can be simplified.\n\nFactor 6615:\n\nDivide by 5: 6615 ÷ 5 = 1323.\n\n1323 ÷ 3 = 441.\n\n441 is 21 squared, so 441 = 21².\n\nTherefore, 6615 = 5 * 3 * 441 = 5 * 3 * 21².\n\nTherefore sqrt(6615) = sqrt(5 * 3 * 21²) = 21 * sqrt(15).\n\nTherefore, Area = (21 * sqrt(15)) / 4.\n\nSo area of triangle ABC is (21√15)/4.\n\nTherefore, [ABC] = (21√15)/4.\n\nSo if we can find [BPC], then the ratio [BPC]/[ABC] would be [BPC] divided by (21√15)/4, so [BPC]/[ABC] = (4 [BPC])/(21√15). So if we can find [BPC], which is the area of triangle BPC.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps express [BPC] in terms of coordinates?\n\nIf I can find coordinates (p, q) of point P, then the area of triangle BPC can be computed via determinant formula.\n\nCoordinates:\n\nB: (0, 0)\n\nC: (7, 0)\n\nP: (p, q)\n\nArea of triangle BPC is (1/2)*base*height. Since BC is along the x-axis from (0,0) to (7,0), the base BC has length 7, and the height would be the y-coordinate of point P, since the height from P to BC is just the vertical distance from P to the x-axis, which is q.\n\nWait, is that correct?\n\nWait, the area of triangle BPC can be calculated as (1/2)* | (B x C + C x P + P x B) |, but more straightforwardly, since BC is on the x-axis, the area is indeed (1/2)* base BC * height, where height is the y-coordinate of P.\n\nBecause the base is BC which is along the x-axis, so the height is just the vertical distance from P to the base BC, which is indeed q.\n\nTherefore, [BPC] = (1/2)*7*q = (7/2)*q.\n\nSimilarly, the area of triangle ABC we already calculated as (21√15)/4.\n\nTherefore, the ratio [BPC]/[ABC] = (7/2 q) / (21√15 /4 ) = (7/2 q) * (4 / (21√15)) = (28 q)/(42√15) = (2 q)/(3√15).\n\nTherefore, if I can find the value of q, then I can compute this ratio.\n\nTherefore, perhaps instead of solving for both p and q, I can just solve for q?\n\nLooking back at the equations, perhaps we can find q without finding p?\n\nWe have two equations:\n\n1) 19p + 5√15 q = 77,\n\n2) 2p² + 3q² + 14p - 49 = 0.\n\nFrom equation (1), we can express p in terms of q: p = (77 - 5√15 q)/19,\n\nthen substitute into equation (2). But that leads to a quadratic in q which is complicated, but maybe we can denote q as a variable and solve?\n\nAlternatively, let me let’s denote k = q, then express everything in terms of k.\n\nLet me set k = q,\n\nthen p = (77 - 5√15 k)/19,\n\nthen plug into equation (2):\n\n2p² + 3k² +14p -49 = 0,\n\nso substituting p:\n\n2*[ (77 -5√15 k)^2 / (19)^2 ] + 3k² + 14*(77 -5√15 k)/19 -49 = 0,\n\nthis is the same equation as before, leading to the quadratic in k (which is q):\n\n1833 q² - 2870√15 q + 14651 = 0.\n\nHmm. Alternatively, maybe approximate the value numerically?\n\nGiven that this is a competition problem, perhaps the quadratic factors nicely if we consider the terms with sqrt(15). Maybe the solution for q is a multiple of sqrt(15)?\n\nSuppose that q = m√15, where m is a rational number. Let me try this substitution.\n\nLet me let q = m√15, where m is a rational number.\n\nThen, plug into equation (1):\n\n19p + 5√15*(m√15) = 77 ⇒ 19p + 5*15*m = 77 ⇒ 19p + 75m = 77 ⇒ p = (77 -75m)/19.\n\nThen plug into equation (2):\n\n2p² + 3q² +14p -49 = 0,\n\nwith p = (77 -75m)/19 and q = m√15,\n\nso compute each term:\n\nFirst term: 2p² = 2*( (77 -75m)/19 )²\n\nSecond term: 3q² = 3*(m√15)^2 = 3*(15 m²) = 45 m²\n\nThird term:14p =14*(77 -75m)/19\n\nFourth term: -49\n\nSo putting together:\n\n2*( (77 -75m)^2 ) / (19)^2 + 45 m² + 14*(77 -75m)/19 -49 =0\n\nMultiply all terms by 19²=361 to eliminate denominators:\n\n2*(77 -75m)^2 + 45 m² * 361 + 14*(77 -75m)*19 -49*361 =0\n\nLet me compute each term step by step.\n\nFirst term: 2*(77 -75m)^2\n\nLet me expand (77 -75m)^2:\n\n=77² - 2*77*75m + (75m)^2\n\n=5929 - 11550m + 5625 m²\n\nMultiply by 2: 2*5929 - 2*11550m + 2*5625 m² = 11,858 - 23,100m + 11,250 m²\n\nSecond term: 45 m² * 361 = 45*361 m². Compute 45*361:\n\n45*300 = 13,500; 45*61 = 2,745; so total 13,500 + 2,745 = 16,245. So 16,245 m².\n\nThird term:14*(77 -75m)*19\n\nFirst compute 14*19 = 266,\n\nthen 266*(77 -75m) = 266*77 - 266*75 m\n\nCompute 266*77:\n\n266*70 = 18,620; 266*7 = 1,862; so total 18,620 + 1,862 = 20,482\n\n266*75 = (200*75) + (66*75) = 15,000 + 4,950 = 19,950\n\nSo third term: 20,482 - 19,950 m\n\nFourth term: -49*361 = let's compute 50*361 = 18,050, so 49*361 = 18,050 - 361 = 17,689, so -49*361 = -17,689\n\nNow, combine all terms:\n\nFirst term: 11,858 - 23,100m + 11,250 m²\n\nSecond term: +16,245 m²\n\nThird term: +20,482 - 19,950 m\n\nFourth term: -17,689\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nStart with the m² terms:\n\n11,250 m² + 16,245 m² = 27,495 m²\n\nNext, the m terms:\n\n-23,100m -19,950m = -43,050m\n\nConstant terms:\n\n11,858 + 20,482 -17,689 = (11,858 + 20,482) = 32,340; 32,340 - 17,689 = 14,651\n\nTherefore, the entire equation becomes:\n\n27,495 m² - 43,050 m + 14,651 = 0\n\nHmm, so now we have a quadratic in m:\n\n27,495 m² - 43,050 m + 14,651 = 0\n\nHmm, perhaps this factors?\n\nLet me see if all coefficients are divisible by some common factor.\n\n27,495: ends with 5, so divisible by 5.\n\n27,495 ÷5 = 5,499\n\n43,050 ÷5 = 8,610\n\n14,651 ÷5: 14,651 divided by 5 is 2,930.2, which is not integer. So not divisible by 5.\n\nCheck divisibility by 3:\n\nSum of digits of 27,495: 2+7+4+9+5 = 27, which is divisible by 3.\n\n27,495 ÷3: 27,495 ÷3 = 9,165\n\n43,050 ÷3: 43,050 ÷3 = 14,350\n\n14,651 ÷3: 1+4+6+5+1=17, not divisible by 3.\n\nSo only first two terms divisible by 3, so no.\n\nCheck divisibility by 7:\n\n27,495 ÷7: 7*3935 = 27,445, so 27,495 -27,445=50, so not divisible by 7.\n\n43,050 ÷7: 43,050 ÷7 = 6,150, since 7*6,000=42,000; 7*150=1,050; so 42,000 +1,050 =43,050. So yes, divisible by 7.\n\n14,651 ÷7: 7*2093=14,651 (since earlier we had 14,651=7*2093). So yes, divisible by 7.\n\nTherefore, factor out 7 from all terms? Wait, but 27,495 is not divisible by 7.\n\nWait, 27,495 ÷7: 7*3,935 = 27,445, so 27,495-27,445=50. 50/7 is not integer. So no.\n\nAlternatively, factor out GCD of coefficients? Since GCD(27495,43050,14651). Let me compute GCD step by step.\n\nCompute GCD(27,495, 43,050):\n\nFirst, GCD(27495, 43050).\n\nUse Euclidean algorithm:\n\nGCD(43050, 27495)\n\n43050 ÷ 27495 = 1 with remainder 43050 -27495 = 15555\n\nGCD(27495, 15555)\n\n27495 ÷15555 = 1 with remainder 27495 -15555 = 11940\n\nGCD(15555,11940)\n\n15555 ÷11940 =1 with remainder 15555 -11940 = 3615\n\nGCD(11940,3615)\n\n11940 ÷3615=3 with remainder 11940 -3*3615=11940 -10845=1095\n\nGCD(3615,1095)\n\n3615 ÷1095=3 with remainder 3615 -3*1095=3615-3285=330\n\nGCD(1095,330)\n\n1095 ÷330=3 with remainder 1095 -3*330=1095-990=105\n\nGCD(330,105)\n\n330 ÷105=3 with remainder 330-315=15\n\nGCD(105,15)=15.\n\nSo GCD of 27,495 and 43,050 is 15.\n\nNow check GCD(15,14,651):\n\n14,651 ÷15: 15*977=14,655, which is 4 more than 14,651, so remainder -4, so GCD(15,4)=1.\n\nTherefore GCD of all three coefficients is 1.\n\nSo the quadratic equation 27,495 m² -43,050 m +14,651 =0 cannot be simplified by factoring out a common integer. Hmm.\n\nTherefore, perhaps we can apply quadratic formula to solve for m.\n\nQuadratic equation: a m² + b m + c =0,\n\nwhere a=27,495,\n\nb= -43,050,\n\nc=14,651.\n\nThen,\n\nm = [43,050 ± sqrt( (-43,050)^2 -4*27,495*14,651 ) ] / (2*27,495)\n\nThis is going to be very large numbers. Maybe we can compute discriminant D:\n\nD = b² -4ac = (43,050)^2 -4*27,495*14,651\n\nFirst compute (43,050)^2:\n\n43,050 squared. Let me compute this as (43,000 + 50)^2 = 43,000² + 2*43,000*50 + 50²\n\n= (43,000)^2 + 4,300,000 + 2,500\n\nCompute 43,000 squared:\n\n43^2 * 1000^2 = 1,849 * 1,000,000 = 1,849,000,000\n\nWait, 43,000 squared is (43)^2*(1000)^2 = 1,849 * 1,000,000 = 1,849,000,000.\n\nThen 2*43,000*50 = 2*43,000*50 = 43,000*100 = 4,300,000.\n\nAdding all terms:\n\n1,849,000,000 + 4,300,000 + 2,500 = 1,853,302,500.\n\nSo D = 1,853,302,500 - 4*27,495*14,651.\n\nCompute the second term: 4*27,495*14,651.\n\nFirst compute 4*27,495 = 109,980.\n\nThen, 109,980 *14,651.\n\nThis is going to be a huge number. Maybe approximate?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there is a mistake here? Because this is getting too calculation-heavy for a competition problem, which makes me think I must have taken a wrong approach.\n\nWait a second, perhaps I made a wrong assumption when I set q = m√15? Maybe that was incorrect?\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a property or theorem that I'm missing here?\n\nLet me think differently.\n\nGiven that angle ABP = angle ACP = θ, and BP : PC = 1 : 2.\n\nPerhaps use the Law of Sines in triangles ABP and ACP?\n\nLet me consider triangles ABP and ACP.\n\nIn triangle ABP:\n\nWe have angle at B is θ, side AB = 6, side BP = x (let me denote BP = x, so PC = 2x, since BP : PC = 1:2).\n\nSimilarly, in triangle ACP:\n\nAngle at C is θ, side AC = 8, side PC = 2x.\n\nWait, but triangles ABP and ACP share the angle θ, but they are not necessarily similar because other angles and sides may differ.\n\nAlternatively, in triangle ABP:\n\nBy the Law of Sines,\n\nAB / sin(angle APB) = BP / sin(angle BAP) = AP / sin(θ)\n\nSimilarly, in triangle ACP:\n\nAC / sin(angle APC) = PC / sin(angle CAP) = AP / sin(θ)\n\nHmm, but angles at P: angle APB and angle APC are supplementary if points are colinear, but not necessarily here.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps relate the two equations from the Law of Sines.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nIn triangle ABP:\n\nAB / sin(angle APB) = BP / sin(angle BAP) = AP / sin(θ)\n\nIn triangle ACP:\n\nAC / sin(angle APC) = PC / sin(angle CAP) = AP / sin(θ)\n\nNotice that AP / sin(θ) is common in both.\n\nLet me denote AP = k.\n\nThen from triangle ABP:\n\nAB / sin(angle APB) = BP / sin(angle BAP) = k / sinθ\n\nFrom triangle ACP:\n\nAC / sin(angle APC) = PC / sin(angle CAP) = k / sinθ\n\nTherefore,\n\nFrom ABP: AB / sin(angle APB) = k / sinθ ⇒ sin(angle APB) = (AB sinθ)/k = (6 sinθ)/k\n\nFrom ACP: AC / sin(angle APC) = k / sinθ ⇒ sin(angle APC) = (AC sinθ)/k = (8 sinθ)/k\n\nNow, note that angles at P: angle APB and angle APC are adjacent angles if points A, P, and some other point are colinear, but actually, in the full triangle, the sum of angles around point P should be 360°, but specifically, in the quadrilateral or the two triangles.\n\nAlternatively, considering that angle APB + angle APC + other angles around P = 360°, but this might not be straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, note that angles APB and APC are supplementary if points A, P, and the line BC are arranged such that AP is a straight line, but not necessarily.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider that angles APB and APC are related through the straight line?\n\nWait, point P is inside triangle ABC, so angles at P: the sum of angles around P should be 360°, so angle APB + angle BPC + angle CPA + angle CAP = 360°, but this might not be helpful.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider the Law of Sines in triangles BPC?\n\nWait, in triangle BPC, we can apply the Law of Sines as well.\n\nIn triangle BPC:\n\nBP / sin(angle BCP) = PC / sin(angle PBC) = BC / sin(angle BPC)\n\nGiven that BP / PC = 1/2, so BP = x, PC = 2x,\n\nso BP / PC = 1/2 ⇒ x / 2x = 1/2, which holds.\n\nTherefore,\n\nFrom Law of Sines in triangle BPC:\n\nBP / sin(angle BCP) = PC / sin(angle PBC)\n\nSo,\n\nx / sin(angle BCP) = 2x / sin(angle PBC)\n\nCancel x:\n\n1 / sin(angle BCP) = 2 / sin(angle PBC)\n\nTherefore,\n\nsin(angle PBC) = 2 sin(angle BCP)\n\nHmm, angle PBC is the angle at B in triangle BPC, which is angle PBC, which is part of angle ABC.\n\nSimilarly, angle BCP is the angle at C in triangle BPC, which is part of angle BCA.\n\nEarlier, we denoted angle CBP = φ, which is the same as angle PBC = φ.\n\nSimilarly, angle BCP = ψ.\n\nSo, from above, sinφ = 2 sinψ.\n\nAdditionally, from the trigonometric Ceva condition earlier, we had:\n\n(sinψ / sinφ) * ( sinα / sinβ ) =1,\n\nwhere α = angle CAP, β = angle BAP,\n\nand angle ABP = θ = angle ACP,\n\nand angle CBP = φ,\n\nangle BCP = ψ,\n\nand angle ABC = θ + φ,\n\nangle BCA = ψ + θ,\n\nangle BAC = β + α.\n\nHmm, this seems a bit involved, but perhaps we can combine these relations.\n\nWe also have from the Law of Sines in triangles ABP and ACP:\n\nFrom triangle ABP:\n\nAB / sin(angle APB) = BP / sin(angle BAP) = AP / sinθ\n\nFrom triangle ACP:\n\nAC / sin(angle APC) = PC / sin(angle CAP) = AP / sinθ\n\nTherefore, equate the expressions for AP / sinθ from both:\n\nFrom ABP: AP / sinθ = AB / sin(angle APB) ⇒ AP = (AB sinθ) / sin(angle APB)\n\nFrom ACP: AP / sinθ = AC / sin(angle APC) ⇒ AP = (AC sinθ) / sin(angle APC)\n\nTherefore,\n\n(AB sinθ)/sin(angle APB) = (AC sinθ)/sin(angle APC)\n\nCancel sinθ:\n\nAB / sin(angle APB) = AC / sin(angle APC)\n\nTherefore,\n\nAB / AC = sin(angle APB) / sin(angle APC)\n\nGiven that AB = 6, AC = 8,\n\nso 6/8 = 3/4 = sin(angle APB)/sin(angle APC)\n\nThus,\n\nsin(angle APB) = (3/4) sin(angle APC)\n\nHmm, interesting.\n\nAdditionally, in triangle APB and APC, the angles at P: angle APB and angle APC are related through the geometry of the figure.\n\nNote that angle APB + angle APC + angle at P between them = 360°? Wait, actually, points A, P, and the other points. Let me think of the full angle around point P.\n\nAt point P, the sum of angles around P is 360°, so:\n\nangle APB + angle BPC + angle CPA + angle CAP = 360°? Wait, no, actually, the angles around P are the angles between the incoming edges.\n\nSpecifically, the angles at P are:\n\n- angle APB (between PA and PB),\n\n- angle BPC (between PB and PC),\n\n- angle CP A (between PC and