 is the angle at point P between points A and C. Wait no: angle APC is the angle at point A between points P and C? Wait, standard notation: angle APC is the angle at point P between points A and C? No, angle APC is the angle at point A between points P and C.\n\nWait, confusion here. Let me recall:\n\nIn angle notation, angle ABC is the angle at point B between points A and C.\n\nTherefore, angle APB is the angle at point P between points A and B,\n\nangle BPC is the angle at point P between points B and C,\n\nangle CPA is the angle at point P between points C and A,\n\nand angle APC is the angle at point A between points P and C.\n\nWait, so angle APC is at point A, not at point P.\n\nSo my earlier mistake: angle APC is at point A, so that complicates things.\n\nTherefore, going back to the Law of Sines in triangles ABP and ACP.\n\nIn triangle ABP:\n\nangle at A: angle BAP = β,\n\nangle at B: angle ABP = θ,\n\nangle at P: angle APB = 180° - θ - β.\n\nSimilarly, in triangle ACP:\n\nangle at A: angle CAP = α,\n\nangle at C: angle ACP = θ,\n\nangle at P: angle APC = 180° - θ - α.\n\nTherefore, from Law of Sines in triangle ABP:\n\nAB / sin(angle APB) = BP / sin(β) = AP / sinθ\n\nSimilarly, in triangle ACP:\n\nAC / sin(angle APC) = PC / sin(α) = AP / sinθ\n\nTherefore,\n\nFrom triangle ABP:\n\nAB / sin(180 - θ - β) = BP / sinβ\n\nBut sin(180 - x) = sinx, so sin(angle APB) = sin(θ + β)\n\nWait, angle APB = 180 - θ - β,\n\nso sin(angle APB) = sin(θ + β).\n\nWait, sin(180 - x) = sinx, so sin(angle APB) = sin(θ + β).\n\nWait, let me compute:\n\nangle APB = 180° - angle ABP - angle BAP = 180° - θ - β,\n\nso sin(angle APB) = sin(θ + β).\n\nSimilarly, in triangle ACP:\n\nangle APC = 180° - angle ACP - angle CAP = 180° - θ - α,\n\nso sin(angle APC) = sin(θ + α).\n\nTherefore, from the Law of Sines in triangles ABP and ACP,\n\nAB / sin(θ + β) = BP / sinβ,\n\nAC / sin(θ + α) = PC / sinα,\n\nand also, from the common AP / sinθ in both,\n\nAP = (AB sinθ)/sin(angle APB) = (AB sinθ)/sin(θ + β),\n\nand AP = (AC sinθ)/sin(angle APC) = (AC sinθ)/sin(θ + α),\n\ntherefore,\n\n(AB sinθ)/sin(θ + β) = (AC sinθ)/sin(θ + α),\n\nCancel sinθ,\n\nAB / sin(θ + β) = AC / sin(θ + α),\n\nso,\n\nAB / AC = sin(θ + β) / sin(θ + α),\n\nwhich is,\n\n6/8 = 3/4 = sin(θ + β)/sin(θ + α).\n\nThus,\n\nsin(θ + β) = (3/4) sin(θ + α).\n\nAdditionally, from the trigonometric Ceva condition earlier,\n\n(sinψ / sinφ) * ( sinα / sinβ ) = 1,\n\nwhere ψ = angle BCP,\n\nφ = angle CBP,\n\nand angle ABC = θ + φ,\n\nangle BCA = θ + ψ,\n\nangle BAC = α + β.\n\nAlso, from triangle BPC, we had from the Law of Sines:\n\nsinφ = 2 sinψ,\n\nfrom BP / sinψ = PC / sinφ ⇒ 1/sinψ = 2/sinφ ⇒ sinφ = 2 sinψ.\n\nSo, we have a system of equations:\n\n1. sin(θ + β) = (3/4) sin(θ + α),\n\n2. (sinψ / sinφ) * ( sinα / sinβ ) = 1,\n\n3. sinφ = 2 sinψ,\n\n4. angle ABC = θ + φ,\n\n5. angle BCA = θ + ψ,\n\n6. angle BAC = α + β,\n\n7. angle sum at A: α + β + ...? Wait, in triangle ABC, angles sum to 180°,\n\nso angle BAC + angle ABC + angle BCA = 180°,\n\ni.e., (α + β) + (θ + φ) + (θ + ψ) = 180°,\n\nso,\n\nα + β + 2θ + φ + ψ = 180°,\n\nHmm, so that's another equation.\n\nThis is getting very involved with many variables: α, β, θ, φ, ψ.\n\nPerhaps assign variables to some angles and try to express others in terms?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps assume some variable substitutions.\n\nLet me see if I can express α and β in terms of θ, φ, ψ?\n\nAlternatively, since we have multiple relations, perhaps express everything in terms of ψ and φ, using equation 3: sinφ = 2 sinψ.\n\nLet me try that.\n\nLet me let ψ be a variable, then φ is such that sinφ = 2 sinψ,\n\nbut we also have angle relations.\n\nFrom equation 2: (sinψ / sinφ) * ( sinα / sinβ ) = 1,\n\nbut sinφ = 2 sinψ,\n\nso substituting,\n\n(sinψ / (2 sinψ)) * ( sinα / sinβ ) = 1 ⇒ (1/2) * ( sinα / sinβ ) = 1 ⇒ sinα / sinβ = 2 ⇒ sinα = 2 sinβ.\n\nSo equation 2 simplifies to sinα = 2 sinβ.\n\nEquation 1: sin(θ + β) = (3/4) sin(θ + α).\n\nAlso, from the angle sum equation (equation 7):\n\nα + β + 2θ + φ + ψ = 180°,\n\nbut φ and ψ are related via sinφ = 2 sinψ,\n\nand angles φ and ψ are parts of angles at B and C:\n\nangle ABC = θ + φ,\n\nangle BCA = θ + ψ,\n\nso φ = angle ABC - θ,\n\nψ = angle BCA - θ,\n\nThus, φ + ψ = angle ABC + angle BCA - 2θ,\n\nBut in triangle ABC, angle BAC + angle ABC + angle BCA = 180°,\n\nso angle BAC = 180° - angle ABC - angle BCA,\n\ntherefore,\n\nφ + ψ = (angle ABC + angle BCA) - 2θ = (180° - angle BAC) - 2θ,\n\nHmm, not sure if helpful.\n\nAlternatively, express φ and ψ in terms of angles of the triangle.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet angle ABC = β + θ,\n\nangle BCA = ψ + θ,\n\nangle BAC = α + β,\n\nand their sum is 180°,\n\nso,\n\n(β + θ) + (ψ + θ) + (α + β) = 180°,\n\nsimplify:\n\nα + β + ψ + θ + β + θ = 180°,\n\nWait, that's:\n\nα + 2β + ψ + 2θ = 180°,\n\nWait, let me check:\n\nangle ABC = θ + φ,\n\nWait, earlier I defined angle ABC = θ + φ,\n\nand angle BCA = θ + ψ,\n\nand angle BAC = α + β,\n\nso substituting into angle sum:\n\n(θ + φ) + (θ + ψ) + (α + β) = 180°,\n\nwhich is,\n\n2θ + φ + ψ + α + β = 180°,\n\nso that's the same as equation 7.\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n2θ + φ + ψ + α + β = 180°,\n\nand from equation 2 and 3:\n\nsinα = 2 sinβ,\n\nsinφ = 2 sinψ,\n\nand from equation 1:\n\nsin(θ + β) = (3/4) sin(θ + α).\n\nThis is still complex, but maybe we can find relations between α and β, and between φ and ψ.\n\nLet me consider that sinα = 2 sinβ,\n\nso perhaps express α in terms of β,\n\nlet me let β be a variable, then α = arcsin(2 sinβ),\n\nbut that might not be helpful.\n\nAlternatively, use sine formula for sum angles.\n\nLet me consider equation 1:\n\nsin(θ + β) = (3/4) sin(θ + α).\n\nBut since α = arcsin(2 sinβ), perhaps substitute that in,\n\nbut this might not be straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider that α and β are angles in triangle BAC, so they are related through the triangle's angles.\n\nAlternatively, let me suppose that β is some angle, then α = arcsin(2 sinβ),\n\nbut