ximate numerically:\n\nAlternatively, perhaps notice that this is impractical without a calculator, and since this is a competition problem, there must be an elegant solution.\n\nWait a second, going back to the area ratio.\n\nWe found that [BPC] = (7/2) q,\n\nand [ABC] = (21√15)/4,\n\nso the ratio is (7/2 q) / (21√15 /4) = (7/2 q) * (4)/(21√15) = (28 q)/(42√15) = (2 q)/(3√15).\n\nTherefore, if we can find q, then compute this ratio.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the quadratic equation can be divided by something?\n\nLooking back at the exact quadratic equation:\n\n1833 q² - 2870√15 q + 14651 = 0.\n\nLet me see if the coefficients have a common factor with √15.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the solution for q is of the form k√15,\n\nLet me suppose that q = k√15,\n\nthen plug into equation:\n\n1833*(k√15)^2 - 2870√15*(k√15) + 14651 = 0,\n\nCompute each term:\n\nFirst term: 1833*(15 k²) = 27,495 k²,\n\nSecond term: -2870√15 * k√15 = -2870*15 k = -43,050 k,\n\nThird term: 14,651,\n\nThus equation becomes:\n\n27,495 k² -43,050 k +14,651 =0,\n\nwhich is the same equation as when I set q = m√15 earlier (with m = k). So this is consistent.\n\nTherefore, solving for k:\n\nk = [43,050 ± sqrt( (43,050)^2 -4*27,495*14,651 )]/(2*27,495).\n\nBut this is the same as before, leading to the same discriminant.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the discriminant is a perfect square?\n\nLet me compute D = (43,050)^2 -4*27,495*14,651.\n\nFirst compute (43,050)^2 = 1,853,302,500,\n\nthen compute 4*27,495*14,651:\n\nFirst compute 27,495 *14,651,\n\nLet me compute 27,495 *14,651:\n\nThis is equal to 27,495*(14,000 + 651) = 27,495*14,000 + 27,495*651.\n\nCompute 27,495*14,000:\n\n27,495 *10,000 = 274,950,000,\n\n27,495 *4,000 = 109,980,000,\n\nso total 274,950,000 + 109,980,000 = 384,930,000.\n\nThen compute 27,495 *651:\n\nFirst compute 27,495 *600 = 16,497,000,\n\n27,495 *50 = 1,374,750,\n\n27,495 *1 = 27,495,\n\nAdding these: 16,497,000 + 1,374,750 = 17,871,750,\n\nthen +27,495 = 17,899,245.\n\nTherefore,