25≈6,873.25,\n\n-2870√15 * q ≈-2870*3.87298*1.93649.\n\nFirst compute 2870*3.87298≈2870*3.873≈ let's compute:\n\n2870*3 =8,610,\n\n2870*0.873≈2870*0.8=2,296; 2870*0.073≈209.51,\n\nso total≈2,296+209.51≈2,505.51,\n\nso total≈8,610+2,505.51≈11,115.51,\n\nthen multiply by q≈1.93649,\n\n11,115.51*1.93649≈approx 11,115*1.936≈21,500 (since 11,115*2=22,230; subtract 11,115*0.064≈712, so ≈22,230 -712≈21,518).\n\nThus the second term ≈-21,518.\n\nThird term is +14,651.\n\nSo total ≈6,873.25 -21,518 +14,651 ≈(6,873.25 +14,651) -21,518 ≈21,524.25 -21,518≈6.25.\n\nSo it's about 6.25, not zero. So not a solution.\n\nHmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe k = 0.5 is close but not exact.\n\nAlternatively, let me try k=0.49,\n\nthen q=0.49*√15≈0.49*3.872≈1.897.\n\nThen compute the quadratic:\n\n1833*(1.897)^2 -2870√15*(1.897) +14651.\n\nFirst term: 1833*(3.6)≈1833*3 +1833*0.6≈5,499 +1,099.8≈6,598.8,\n\nSecond term: -2870*3.872*1.897≈-2870*3.872*1.897,\n\nFirst compute 3.872*1.897≈7.36,\n\nthen 2870*7.36≈2870*7 +2870*0.36≈20,090 +1,033.2≈21,123.2,\n\nso second term≈-21,123.2,\n\nThird term:+14,651,\n\nTotal≈6,598.8 -21,123.2 +14,651≈(6,598.8 +14,651) -21,123.2≈21,249.8 -21,123.2≈126.6.\n\nHmm, still positive.\n\nTry k=0.45,\n\nq=0.45*3.872≈1.7444,\n\nFirst term:1833*(1.7444)^2≈1833*(3.04)≈1833*3 +1833*0.04≈5,499 +73.32≈5,572.32,\n\nSecond term:-2870*3.872*1.7444,\n\nCompute 3.872*1.7444≈6.75,\n\nthen 2870*6.75≈2870*6 +2870*0.75≈17,220 +2,152.5≈19,372.5,\n\nso second term≈-19,372.5,\n\nThird term:+14,651,\n\nTotal≈5,572.32 -19,372.5 +14,651≈(5,572.32 +14,651) -19,372.5≈20,223.32 -19,372.5≈850.82.\n\nStill positive.\n\nHmm, so when k=0.5, the value is about +6.25,\n\nk=0.49 gives +126,\n\nWait, no, actually when k decreased from 0.5 to 0.49, the value increased?\n\nWait, perhaps my calculations were rough.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I need to do better approximations.\n\nAlternatively, accept that this is too calculation-heavy and that the problem might have an elegant solution.\n\nWait, another approach: use mass point with the given ratio BP:PC=1:2, but considering the angle condition.\n\nWait, mass point typically uses ratios along sides, but here BP:PC is a ratio of lengths from B and C to P, not along the same line.\n\nAlternatively, use barycentric coordinates.\n\nIn barycentric coordinates with respect to triangle ABC, any point P can be expressed as (u, v, w) where u + v + w = 1, and u, v, w >0.\n\nThe coordinates correspond to masses at the vertices.\n\nBut I might need to recall the exact relations.\n\nAlternatively, in barycentric coordinates, the distances can be related to the coordinates, but it might be complex.\n\nAlternatively, since we have ratios involving angles, perhaps use the Law of Sines in triangles involving P.\n\nLet me consider triangles ABP and ACP again.\n\nWe have angle ABP = angle ACP = θ,\n\nand BP/PC =1/2.\n\nLet me denote BP = x, so PC=2x.\n\nLet me denote AP = y.\n\nIn triangle ABP:\n\nBy the Law of Sines,\n\nAB / sin(angle APB) = BP / sin(angle BAP) = AP / sinθ\n\nSimilarly, in triangle ACP:\n\nAC / sin(angle APC) = PC / sin(angle CAP) = AP / sinθ\n\nLet me write ratios:\n\nFrom triangle ABP:\n\nAB / sin(angle APB) = BP / sin(angle BAP)\n\n=> 6 / sin(angle APB) = x / sin(angle BAP)\n\nFrom triangle ACP:\n\nAC / sin(angle APC) = PC / sin(angle CAP)\n\n=> 8 / sin(angle APC) = 2x / sin(angle CAP)\n\nAlso, from earlier:\n\nangle APB + angle APC + angle BPC + ...? Wait, not sure.\n\nBut we also know from the Law of Sines in triangle BPC:\n\nBP / sin(angle BCP) = PC / sin(angle PBC)\n\n=> x / sin(angle BCP) = 2x / sin(angle PBC)\n\n=> sin(angle PBC) = 2 sin(angle BCP)\n\nLet me denote angle PBC = φ,\n\nangle BCP = ψ,\n\nso sinφ = 2 sinψ,\n\nand angle PBC = φ,\n\nangle BCP = ψ.\n\nAlso, angle ABC = angle ABP + angle PBC = θ + φ,\n\nangle BCA = angle ACP + angle BCP = θ + ψ,\n\nangle BAC = angle BAP + angle CAP = let's say α + β,\n\nwith α = angle BAP,\n\nβ = angle CAP.\n\nNow, in triangle ABC,\n\nangle BAC + angle ABC + angle BCA = 180°,\n\nso,\n\n(α + β) + (θ + φ) + (θ + ψ) = 180°,\n\n=> α + β + 2θ + φ + ψ = 180°.\n\nAdditionally, from the Law of Sines in triangles ABP and ACP,\n\nFrom triangle ABP:\n\n6 / sin(angle APB) = x / sinβ,\n\nand angle APB = 180° - θ - β,\n\nso sin(angle APB) = sin(θ + β),\n\nso,\n\n6 / sin(θ + β) = x / sinβ,\n\n=> 6 sinβ = x sin(θ + β) ... (1)\n\nFrom triangle ACP:\n\n8 / sin(angle APC) = 2x / sinβ,\n\nWait, no, in triangle ACP,\n\nthe angle at A is β,\n\nangle at C is θ,\n\nangle at P is angle APC = 180° - θ - β,\n\nso,\n\n8 / sin(angle APC) = 2x / sinβ,\n\n=> 8 / sin(θ + β) = 2x / sinβ,\n\n=> 8 sinβ = 2x sin(θ + β),\n\n=> 4 sinβ = x sin(θ + β) ... (2)\n\nNow, from equations (1) and (2):\n\nFrom (1): 6 sinβ = x sin(θ + β),\n\nFrom (2): 4 sinβ = x sin(θ + β),\n\nWait, this implies 6 sinβ = 4 sinβ ⇒ 6=4, which is impossible.\n\nWait, this is a contradiction. That suggests an error in my setup.\n\nWait, let me check:\n\nIn triangle ACP,\n\nLaw of Sines: AC / sin(angle APC) = PC / sin(angle CAP),\n\nso,\n\nAC / sin(angle APC) = PC / sin(angle CAP),\n\nthus,\n\n8 / sin(angle APC) = 2x / sinβ,\n\nso,\n\n8 sinβ = 2x sin(angle APC),\n\nso,\n\n4 sinβ = x sin(angle APC),\n\nand angle APC = 180° - angle ACP - angle CAP = 180° - θ - β,\n\nso,\n\nsin(angle APC) = sin(θ + β),\n\nso equation (2) becomes:\n\n4 sinβ = x sin(θ + β),\n\nwhile equation (1) from triangle ABP:\n\n6 sinβ = x sin(θ + β),\n\nThus, 6 sinβ = x sin(θ + β) and 4 sinβ = x sin(θ + β),\n\nwhich implies 6 sinβ = 4 sinβ ⇒ 2 sinβ =0 ⇒ sinβ=0,\n\nwhich is impossible as β is an angle in a triangle.\n\nThis contradiction suggests an error in my assumptions or derivations.\n\nHmm, this is concerning. Where did I go wrong?\n\nLet me retrace:\n\nIn triangle ABP,\n\nLaw of Sines: AB / sin(angle APB) = BP / sin(angle BAP),\n\nso,\n\n6 / sin(angle APB) = x / sinβ,\n\nso,\n\nsin(angle APB) = 6 sinβ / x,\n\nSimilarly, angle APB = 180 - angle ABP - angle BAP = 180 - θ - β,\n\nso,\n\nsin(angle APB) = sin(θ + β),\n\nso,\n\nsin(θ + β) = 6 sinβ / x ... (1)\n\nIn triangle ACP,\n\nLaw of Sines: AC / sin(angle APC) = PC / sin(angle CAP),\n\nso,\n\n8 / sin(angle APC) = 2x / sinβ,\n\nso,\n\nsin(angle APC) = 8 sinβ / (2x) = 4 sinβ / x,\n\nangle APC = 180 - angle ACP - angle CAP = 180 - θ - β,\n\nso,\n\nsin(angle APC) = sin(θ + β),\n\nso,\n\nsin(θ + β) = 4 sinβ / x ... (2)\n\nThus, equations (1) and (2):\n\nFrom (1): sin(θ + β) = (6/x) sinβ,\n\nFrom (2): sin(θ + β) = (4/x) sinβ,\n\nTherefore, (6/x) sinβ = (4/x) sinβ ⇒ 6 =4,\n\nwhich is a contradiction.\n\nThis suggests that my assumption that angle APC = 180 - θ - β is incorrect?\n\nWait, angle APC is at point P, not at point A.\n\nWait, angle APC is the angle at point P between points A and C, so it's different from the angle at point A.\n\nAh! Here's the mistake.\n\nAngle APC is the angle at point P between points A and C, so it is not equal to 180° - θ - β.\n\nMy earlier mistake was assuming angle APC = 180° - angle ACP - angle CAP, but that's the angle at point A, not at point P.\n\nThis is a critical error.\n\nLet me correct this.\n\nIn triangle APC, the angles are at points A, P, and C.\n\nAngle at A: angle CAP = β,\n\nAngle at C: angle ACP = θ,\n\nTherefore, angle at P: angle APC = 180° - β - θ.\n\nThus, angle APC = 180° - β - θ,\n\nso sin(angle APC) = sin(β + θ),\n\nsince sin(180 - x) = sinx.\n\nTherefore, in the Law of Sines for triangle ACP,\n\nAC / sin(angle APC) = PC / sin(angle CAP),\n\nso,\n\n8 / sin(β + θ) = 2x / sinβ,\n\nthus,\n\n8 sinβ = 2x sin(β + θ),\n\n=> 4 sinβ = x sin(β + θ) ... (2 corrected)\n\nSimilarly, in triangle ABP,\n\nangle at P: angle APB = 180° - angle ABP - angle BAP = 180° - θ - β,\n\nso sin(angle APB) = sin(θ + β),\n\nLaw of Sines:\n\nAB / sin(angle APB) = BP / sin(angle BAP),\n\n=> 6 / sin(θ + β) = x / sinβ,\n\n=> 6 sinβ = x sin(θ + β) ... (1 corrected)\n\nNow, equations (1) and (2):\n\n(1): 6 sinβ = x sin(θ + β),\n\n(2): 4 sinβ = x sin(β + θ),\n\nThus, both right-hand sides involve x sin(θ + β),\n\nso setting equal:\n\n6 sinβ = 4 sinβ ⇒ 2 sinβ =0 ⇒ sinβ =0,\n\nwhich again implies β=0 or π, which is impossible.\n\nWait, so this still leads to a contradiction?\n\nHmm, this suggests that there is inconsistency in the problem's conditions? Or perhaps my angle definitions are still off?\n\nWait, angle APB is at point P between A and B,\n\nangle APC is at point P between A and C,\n\nso angle APB and angle APC are two different angles at point P.\n\nThe sum of all angles at point P should be 360°,\n\nso angle APB + angle BPC + angle CPA = 360°,\n\nbut angle CPA here is the angle at P between C and A,\n\nwhich is the same as angle APC?\n\nWait, terminology confusion again.\n\nAngle at point P between C and A is angle CPA if it's at point C, but at point P it's angle APC or angle CAP?\n\nWait, angle at point P between points C and A is angle APC,\n\nwhere the vertex is P, so it's angle APC with the vertex at P.\n\nWait, standard notation: angle APC is the angle at point P between points A and C.\n\nYes, so angle APC is at point P,\n\nso in triangle APC, angle at P is angle APC = 180° - angle CAP - angle ACP = 180° - β - θ,\n\nso that part is correct.\n\nSimilarly, in triangle ABP,\n\nangle at P is angle APB = 180° - angle ABP - angle BAP = 180° - θ - β,\n\nso that is also correct.\n\nThus, the two equations are:\n\n6 sinβ = x sin(θ + β),\n\nand,\n\n4 sinβ = x sin(θ + β),\n\nwhich implies 6 sinβ =4 sinβ ⇒ 2 sinβ =0 ⇒ sinβ =0,\n\nwhich is impossible.\n\nThis suggests that the given conditions are contradictory?\n\nBut the problem states that such a point P exists.\n\nTherefore, I must have made a wrong assumption in setting up the equations.\n\nPerhaps the angles ABP and ACP are not both equal to θ but have some other relation?\n\nWait, the problem states angle ABP = angle ACP.\n\nAngle ABP is at point B between A and P,\n\nangle ACP is at point C between A and P,\n\nso they are angles at different vertices.\n\nTherefore, my previous approach to set them both equal to θ is okay,\n\nbut the resulting equations lead to a contradiction,\n\nsuggesting an error in my application of the Law of Sines.\n\nWait, in triangle ABP,\n\nLaw of Sines:\n\nAB / sin(angle APB) = BP / sin(angle BAP) = AP / sin(angle ABP),\n\nso,\n\nAB / sin(angle APB) = BP / sinβ,\n\nso,\n\n6 / sin(angle APB) = x / sinβ ⇒ sin(angle APB) = 6 sinβ / x,\n\nand angle APB = 180° - angle ABP - angle BAP = 180° - θ - β,\n\nso,\n\nsin(angle APB) = sin(θ + β),\n\nso,\n\nsin(θ + β) = 6 sinβ / x,\n\nSimilarly, in triangle ACP,\n\nAC / sin(angle APC) = PC / sin(angle CAP),\n\nso,\n\n8 / sin(angle APC) = 2x / sinβ ⇒ sin(angle APC) = 8 sinβ / (2x) = 4 sinβ /x,\n\nangle APC = 180° - angle ACP - angle CAP = 180° - θ - β,\n\nso,\n\nsin(angle APC) = sin(θ + β),\n\nso,\n\nsin(θ + β) = 4 sinβ /x,\n\nThus,\n\nfrom the two equations:\n\nsin(θ + β) = (6/x) sinβ,\n\nand,\n\nsin(θ + β) = (4/x) sinβ,\n\nTherefore,\n\n(6/x) sinβ = (4/x) sinβ ⇒ 6=4,\n\nwhich is a contradiction.\n\nThis suggests that with the given conditions (angle ABP = angle ACP and BP/PC=1/2),\n\nsuch a point P cannot exist,\n\nwhich contradicts the problem statement.\n\nHmm, this is very confusing.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the error is in assuming that angle ABP = angle ACP,\n\nbut in the problem it says angle ABP = angle ACP,\n\nwhich are angles at different vertices,\n\nso perhaps I need to relate them through another relation?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the Law of Sines in different triangles?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem requires using areas and the given ratio BP/PC=1/2,\n\ncombined with the angle condition,\n\nto find the desired area ratio without finding coordinates?\n\nLet me think.\n\nLet me denote the area of triangle BPC as [BPC] = x,\n\nand the area of triangle ABC as [ABC] = y,\n\nand we need to find x/y.\n\nGiven that BP/PC =1/2,\n\nand angle ABP = angle ACP,\n\nmaybe use the formula for the area in terms of sides and angles.\n\nThe area of triangle BPC is (1/2)*BP*PC*sin(angle BPC),\n\nbut angle BPC is unknown