de both sides by \\( CP \\):\n\n\\( \\frac{1}{2} \\sin (\\angle BAC - \\alpha) = \\sin \\alpha \\)\n\nMultiply both sides by 2:\n\n\\( \\sin (\\angle BAC - \\alpha) = 2 \\sin \\alpha \\)\n\nHmm, interesting equation. Let me denote \\( \\angle BAC = \\phi \\), so we have:\n\n\\( \\sin (\\phi - \\alpha) = 2 \\sin \\alpha \\)\n\nLet me expand the left-hand side using sine subtraction formula:\n\n\\( \\sin \\phi \\cos \\alpha - \\cos \\phi \\sin \\alpha = 2 \\sin \\alpha \\)\n\nBring all terms to one side:\n\n\\( \\sin \\phi \\cos \\alpha - \\cos \\phi \\sin \\alpha - 2 \\sin \\alpha = 0 \\)\n\nFactor out \\( \\sin \\alpha \\):\n\n\\( \\sin \\phi \\cos \\alpha - \\sin \\alpha ( \\cos \\phi + 2 ) = 0 \\)\n\nHmm. Let me rearrange terms:\n\n\\( \\sin \\phi \\cos \\alpha = \\sin \\alpha ( \\cos \\phi + 2 ) \\)\n\nDivide both sides by \\( \\cos \\alpha \\):\n\n\\( \\sin \\phi = \\sin \\alpha \\left( \\frac{ \\cos \\phi + 2 }{ \\cos \\alpha } \\right) \\)\n\nHmm, not sure if this is helpful. Maybe express in terms of tangent?\n\nLet me divide both sides by \\( \\cos \\phi \\):\n\n\\( \\tan \\phi = \\tan \\alpha \\left( \\frac{ \\cos \\phi + 2 }{ \\cos \\alpha } \\right) \\cdot \\frac{ \\cos \\phi }{ \\cos \\phi } \\)\n\nWait, perhaps not. Alternatively, let me set \\( t = \\alpha \\), so the equation is:\n\n\\( \\sin (\\phi - t) = 2 \\sin t \\)\n\nLet me write \\( \\phi \\) as angle \\( BAC \\), which we can compute from the given triangle sides.\n\nSince we have triangle \\( ABC \\) with sides \\( AB = 6 \\), \\( AC = 8 \\), \\( BC = 7 \\), we can compute angle \\( BAC \\) using the Law of Cosines.\n\nYes! Let me compute angle \\( BAC \\).\n\nLaw of Cosines on triangle \\( ABC \\):\n\n\\( BC^2 = AB^2 + AC^2 - 2 \\cdot AB \\cdot AC \\cdot \\cos \\angle BAC \\)\n\nPlugging in the values:\n\n\\( 7^2 = 6^2 + 8^2 - 2 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 8 \\cdot \\cos \\angle BAC \\)\n\nCalculating:\n\n\\( 49 = 36 + 64 - 96 \\cos \\angle BAC \\)\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\( 49 = 100 - 96 \\cos \\angle BAC \\)\n\nSubtract 100 from both sides:\n\n\\( -51 = -96 \\cos \\angle BAC \\implies \\cos \\angle BAC = \\frac{51}{96} = \\frac{17}{32} \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( \\angle BAC = \\arccos \\left( \\frac{17}{32} \\right) \\). Let me compute its sine as well, since we might need it later.\n\n\\( \\sin \\angle BAC = \\sqrt{1 - \\left( \\frac{17}{32} \\right)^2 } = \\sqrt{ \\frac{1024 - 289}{1024} } = \\sqrt{ \\frac{735}{1024} } = \\frac{ \\sqrt{735} }{32 } = \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15} }{32 } \\)\n\nWait, since \\( \\sqrt{735} = \\sqrt{49 \\times 15} = 7 \\sqrt{15} \\), yes.\n\nTherefore, \\( \\sin \\phi = \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15} }{32 } \\), where \\( \\phi = \\angle BAC \\).\n\nSo going back to the equation:\n\n\\( \\sin (\\phi - \\alpha) = 2 \\sin \\alpha \\)\n\nLet me write \\( \\phi - \\alpha = \\beta \\), so that \\( \\alpha = \\phi - \\beta \\), but not sure if that helps.\n\nAlternatively, let me expand \\( \\sin(\\phi - \\alpha) \\):\n\n\\( \\sin \\phi \\cos \\alpha - \\cos \\phi \\sin \\alpha = 2 \\sin \\alpha \\)\n\nLet me rearrange terms:\n\n\\( \\sin \\phi \\cos \\alpha = \\cos \\phi \\sin \\alpha + 2 \\sin \\alpha \\)\n\nFactor \\( \\sin \\alpha \\) on the right:\n\n\\( \\sin \\phi \\cos \\alpha = \\sin \\alpha ( \\cos \\phi + 2 ) \\)\n\nDivide both sides by \\( \\cos \\alpha \\):\n\n\\( \\sin \\phi = \\sin \\alpha \\left( \\frac{ \\cos \\phi + 2 }{ \\cos \\alpha } \\right) \\)\n\nHmm, let me denote \\( t = \\alpha \\), so:\n\n\\( \\sin \\phi = \\sin t \\cdot \\frac{ \\cos \\phi + 2 }{ \\cos t } \\)\n\nMultiply both sides by \\( \\cos t \\):\n\n\\( \\sin \\phi \\cos t = \\sin t ( \\cos \\phi + 2 ) \\)\n\nBring all terms to left-hand side:\n\n\\( \\sin \\phi \\cos t - \\sin t \\cos \\phi - 2 \\sin t = 0 \\)\n\nNotice that \\( \\sin \\phi \\cos t - \\sin t \\cos \\phi = \\sin( \\phi - t ) \\), so:\n\n\\( \\sin( \\phi - t ) - 2 \\sin t = 0 \\)\n\nBut \\( \\phi - t = \\phi - \\alpha \\), which was our original equation, so this seems circular.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider writing everything in terms of \\( \\tan \\alpha \\).\n\nLet me set \\( \\tan \\alpha = m \\), so \\( \\sin \\alpha = \\frac{m}{\\sqrt{1 + m^2}} \\), \\( \\cos \\alpha = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + m^2}} \\).\n\nThen, substitute into the equation:\n\n\\( \\sin \\phi \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + m^2}} - \\cos \\phi \\cdot \\frac{m}{\\sqrt{1 + m^2}} = 2 \\cdot \\frac{m}{\\sqrt{1 + m^2}} \\)\n\nMultiply both sides by \\( \\sqrt{1 + m^2} \\):\n\n\\( \\sin \\phi - \\cos \\phi \\cdot m = 2 m \\)\n\nRearrange terms:\n\n\\( \\sin \\phi = m ( 2 + \\cos \\phi ) \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( m = \\frac{ \\sin \\phi }{ 2 + \\cos \\phi } \\)\n\nSo,\n\n\\( \\tan \\alpha = \\frac{ \\sin \\phi }{ 2 + \\cos \\phi } \\)\n\nNow, substituting the known values of \\( \\sin \\phi = \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{32} \\) and \\( \\cos \\phi = \\frac{17}{32} \\):\n\n\\( \\tan \\alpha = \\frac{ \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{32} }{ 2 + \\frac{17}{32} } = \\frac{ \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{32} }{ \\frac{64 + 17}{32} } = \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{81} \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( \\tan \\alpha = \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{81} \\)\n\nThus, \\( \\alpha = \\arctan \\left( \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{81} \\right) \\)\n\nHmm, this gives us the value of \\( \\alpha \\), which is \\( \\angle BAP \\).\n\nNow, perhaps we can use this to find the ratio of areas?\n\nAlternatively, since we have expressions involving \\( \\alpha \\), perhaps we can find the ratio \\( \\frac{BP}{PC} \\) in terms of \\( \\alpha \\), but we already know \\( BP/PC = 1/2 \\), so maybe this is consistent?\n\nWait, actually, we derived this under the assumption that \\( BP/PC = 1/2 \\), so this is consistent, but I'm not sure how this helps us find coordinates or areas.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use the Law of Sines in triangles \\( ABP \\) and \\( ACP \\).\n\nIn triangle \\( ABP \\):\n\n\\( \\frac{AB}{\\sin \\angle APB} = \\frac{BP}{\\sin \\alpha} = \\frac{AP}{\\sin \\theta} \\)\n\nIn triangle \\( ACP \\):\n\n\\( \\frac{AC}{\\sin \\angle APC} = \\frac{CP}{\\sin (\\angle BAC - \\alpha)} = \\frac{AP}{\\sin \\theta} \\)\n\nHmm, so equate the expressions for \\( AP \\):\n\nFrom triangle \\( ABP \\): \\( AP = \\frac{BP \\sin \\theta}{\\sin \\alpha} \\)\n\nFrom triangle \\( ACP \\): \\( AP = \\frac{CP \\sin \\theta}{\\sin (\\angle BAC - \\alpha)} \\)\n\nSetting them equal gives the previous equation, which we already used.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps express \\( \\sin \\theta \\) from both equations and set them equal?\n\nFrom triangle \\( ABP \\):\n\n\\( \\sin \\theta = \\frac{AP \\sin \\alpha}{BP} \\)\n\nFrom triangle \\( ACP \\):\n\n\\( \\sin \\theta = \\frac{AP \\sin (\\angle BAC - \\alpha)}{CP} \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( \\frac{AP \\sin \\alpha}{BP} = \\frac{AP \\sin (\\angle BAC - \\alpha)}{CP} \\implies \\frac{ \\sin \\alpha }{ BP } = \\frac{ \\sin (\\angle BAC - \\alpha) }{ CP } \\)\n\nCross-multiplying:\n\n\\( CP \\sin \\alpha = BP \\sin (\\angle BAC - \\alpha) \\)\n\nBut we know that \\( BP / CP = 1 / 2 \\implies BP = CP / 2 \\), so substitute:\n\n\\( CP \\sin \\alpha = (CP / 2 ) \\sin (\\angle BAC - \\alpha ) \\implies 2 \\sin \\alpha = \\sin (\\angle BAC - \\alpha ) \\)\n\nWhich is exactly the equation we had earlier, so again, we're going in circles.\n\nHmm. Perhaps we need to find the coordinates of \\( P \\) numerically?\n\nGiven that this problem is likely designed to have an exact answer, perhaps there's a smarter way.\n\nWait, let me recall that in barycentric coordinates, the areas can be related to the weights.\n\nAlternatively, since we have the coordinates of all the vertices, and if we can find the coordinates of \\( P \\), then we can compute the area of \\( BPC \\) via the shoelace formula.\n\nGiven that this might be the only way, albeit tedious, perhaps I can proceed with solving the two equations numerically.\n\nLet me recall the two equations:\n\nFirst equation (Apollonius circle):\n\n\\( 3x^2 + 3y^2 - \\frac{79}{2}x + \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} y + 80 = 0 \\) --- (1)\n\nSecond equation (angle equality):\n\nAfter cross-multiplying and simplifying, we had:\n\n\\( (x - 6) \\cdot 8 \\cdot \\sqrt{ \\left( x - \\frac{17}{4} \\right)^2 + \\left( y - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} \\right)^2 } = \\left( \\frac{17}{4}x + \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} y - 64 \\right) \\cdot \\sqrt{(x - 6)^2 + y^2} \\)\n\nLet me denote this as equation (2).\n\nThis equation is quite complex, but perhaps I can square both sides to eliminate the square roots. Although this will lead to a quartic equation, perhaps terms will cancel out.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet me let \\( A = x - 6 \\)\n\nLet me let \\( B = \\sqrt{ \\left( x - \\frac{17}{4} \\right)^2 + \\left( y - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} \\right)^2 } \\)\n\nLet me let \\( C = \\sqrt{(x - 6)^2 + y^2} \\)\n\nThen equation (2) is:\n\n\\( 8 A B = \\left( \\frac{17}{4}x + \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} y - 64 \\right) C \\)\n\nLet me write equation (2) as:\n\n\\( 8 A B - \\left( \\frac{17}{4}x + \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} y - 64 \\right) C = 0 \\)\n\nNow, squaring both sides:\n\n\\( (8 A B - D C)^2 = 0 \\), where \\( D = \\frac{17}{4}x + \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} y - 64 \\)\n\nExpanding the left side:\n\n\\( 64 A^2 B^2 - 16 A B D C + D^2 C^2 = 0 \\)\n\nBut this is still very complicated.\n\nAlternatively, note that \\( A = x - 6 \\), and \\( C = \\sqrt{A^2 + y^2} \\), so \\( C^2 = A^2 + y^2 \\).\n\nAlso, \\( B^2 = \\left( x - \\frac{17}{4} \\right)^2 + \\left( y - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} \\right)^2 \\)\n\nLet me compute \\( B^2 \\):\n\n\\( B^2 = \\left( x - \\frac{17}{4} \\right)^2 + \\left( y - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} \\right)^2 \\)\n\nExpand this:\n\n\\( x^2 - \\frac{17}{2}x + \\frac{289}{16} + y^2 - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} y + \\frac{735}{16} \\)\n\nCombine terms:\n\n\\( x^2 + y^2 - \\frac{17}{2}x - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} y + \\frac{289 + 735}{16} \\)\n\nWhich simplifies to:\n\n\\( x^2 + y^2 - \\frac{17}{2}x - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} y + \\frac{1024}{16} \\)\n\nWhich is:\n\n\\( x^2 + y^2 - \\frac{17}{2}x - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} y + 64 \\)\n\nHmm, interestingly, this is exactly the right-hand side of the first equation (equation (1)) multiplied by something?\n\nWait, equation (1) was:\n\n\\( 3x^2 + 3y^2 - \\frac{79}{2}x + \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} y + 80 = 0 \\)\n\nHmm, not exactly the same.\n\nWait, but note that \\( B^2 = x^2 + y^2 - \\frac{17}{2}x - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} y + 64 \\)\n\nTherefore, if I denote \\( Q = x^2 + y^2 \\), then \\( B^2 = Q - \\frac{17}{2}x - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} y + 64 \\)\n\nAlso, \\( C^2 = A^2 + y^2 = (x - 6)^2 + y^2 = x^2 - 12x + 36 + y^2 = Q - 12x + 36 \\)\n\nSo, \\( C^2 = Q - 12x + 36 \\)\n\nLet me also note that equation (1) can be written in terms of Q:\n\nEquation (1): \\( 3Q - \\frac{79}{2}x + \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} y + 80 = 0 \\)\n\nSo,\n\n\\( 3Q = \\frac{79}{2}x - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} y - 80 \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( Q = \\frac{79}{6}x - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{6} y - \\frac{80}{3} \\)\n\nHmm, perhaps substituting this into expressions for B^2 and C^2?\n\nAlternatively, this might not be the best path.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can assume that the ratio \\( [BPC]/[ABC] = k \\), and express areas in terms of coordinates?\n\nLet me recall that the area of triangle \\( BPC \\) can be calculated via the determinant formula:\n\n\\( [BPC] = \\frac{1}{2} | (B_x(P_y - C_y) + P_x(C_y - B_y) + C_x(B_y - P_y) ) | \\)\n\nBut since \\( B = (6, 0) \\), \\( C = \\left( \\frac{17}{4}, \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} \\right) \\), and \\( P = (x, y) \\), this becomes:\n\n\\( [BPC] = \\frac{1}{2} | 6(y - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4}) + x(\\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} - 0) + \\frac{17}{4}(0 - y) | \\)\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\nFirst term: \\( 6y - 6 \\cdot \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} = 6y - \\frac{42 \\sqrt{15}}{4} = 6y - \\frac{21 \\sqrt{15}}{2} \\)\n\nSecond term: \\( x \\cdot \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} \\)\n\nThird term: \\( \\frac{17}{4} \\cdot (-y) = -\\frac{17}{4} y \\)\n\nTherefore, combining all terms inside the absolute value:\n\n\\( 6y - \\frac{21 \\sqrt{15}}{2} + \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} x - \\frac{17}{4} y \\)\n\nCombine like terms for \\( y \\):\n\n\\( \\left(6y - \\frac{17}{4}y \\right) + \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} x - \\frac{21 \\sqrt{15}}{2} \\)\n\nConvert 6y to \\( \\frac{24}{4} y \\):\n\n\\( \\left( \\frac{24}{4} y - \\frac{17}{4} y \\right) + \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} x - \\frac{21 \\sqrt{15}}{2} \\)\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\( \\frac{7}{4} y + \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} x - \\frac{21 \\sqrt{15}}{2} \\)\n\nFactor out \\( \\frac{7}{4} \\):\n\n\\( \\frac{7}{4} \\left( y + \\sqrt{15} x - 6 \\sqrt{15} \\right) \\)\n\nTherefore, the area \\( [BPC] \\) is:\n\n\\( \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\left| \\frac{7}{4} \\left( y + \\sqrt{15} x - 6 \\sqrt{15} \\right) \\right| = \\frac{7}{8} \\left| y + \\sqrt{15} x - 6 \\sqrt{15} \\right| \\)\n\nSince \\( P \\) is inside the triangle, the expression inside the absolute value should be positive? Let me check:\n\nAssuming \\( P \\) is inside the triangle, so coordinates \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) are such that the expression \\( y + \\sqrt{15} x - 6 \\sqrt{15} \\) is positive? Not sure, but we can keep the absolute value for now.\n\nThus,\n\n\\( [BPC] = \\frac{7}{8} \\left| y + \\sqrt{15} x - 6 \\sqrt{15} \\right| \\)\n\nAnd the area of \\( ABC \\) is \\( \\frac{21 \\sqrt{15}}{4} \\), so the ratio is:\n\n\\( \\frac{[BPC]}{[ABC]} = \\frac{ \\frac{7}{8} | y + \\sqrt{15} x - 6 \\sqrt{15} | }{ \\frac{21 \\sqrt{15}}{4} } = \\frac{7}{8} \\cdot \\frac{4}{21 \\sqrt{15}} \\cdot | y + \\sqrt{15} x - 6 \\sqrt{15} | \\)\n\nSimplify constants:\n\n\\( \\frac{7 \\cdot 4}{8 \\cdot 21 \\sqrt{15}} = \\frac{28}{168 \\sqrt{15}} = \\frac{1}{6 \\sqrt{15}} \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( \\frac{[BPC]}{[ABC]} = \\frac{1}{6 \\sqrt{15}} \\cdot | y + \\sqrt{15} x - 6 \\sqrt{15} | \\)\n\nHmm, so if I can compute \\( y + \\sqrt{15} x - 6 \\sqrt{15} \\), then I can find the ratio.\n\nLet me denote \\( S = y + \\sqrt{15} x - 6 \\sqrt{15} \\), so the ratio is \\( \\frac{|S|}{6 \\sqrt{15}} \\).\n\nTherefore, if I can find \\( S \\), then I can compute the ratio.\n\nNow, let me see if I can express \\( S \\) in terms of the equations we have.\n\nLet me recall equation (1):\n\n\\( 3x^2 + 3y^2 - \\frac{79}{2}x + \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} y + 80 = 0 \\)\n\nPerhaps I can manipulate this equation to express \\( y \\) in terms of \\( x \\), or vice versa, and substitute into \\( S \\).\n\nAlternatively, let me see if \\( S \\) can be related to equation (1).\n\nLet me compute \\( S = \\sqrt{15} x + y - 6 \\sqrt{15} \\)\n\nLet me rearrange equation (1):\n\nMultiply equation (1) by \\( \\frac{2}{3} \\):\n\n\\( 2x^2 + 2y^2 - \\frac{79}{3}x + \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{3} y + \\frac{160}{3} = 0 \\)\n\nHmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider equation (1) and the expression for \\( S \\).\n\nLet me note that \\( S = \\sqrt{15} x + y - 6 \\sqrt{15} \\). Let me see if I can find \\( \\sqrt{15} x + y \\) from equation (1).\n\nLet me denote \\( T = \\sqrt{15} x + y \\). Then \\( S = T - 6 \\sqrt{15} \\).\n\nSo, if I can find \\( T \\), then \\( S = T - 6 \\sqrt{15} \\).\n\nLet me see if I can express \\( T \\) in terms of equation (1).\n\nLet me try to manipulate equation (1) to involve \\( T \\).\n\nEquation (1):\n\n\\( 3x^2 + 3y^2 - \\frac{79}{2}x + \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} y + 80 = 0 \\)\n\nLet me factor out the 3 from the quadratic terms:\n\n\\( 3(x^2 + y^2) - \\frac{79}{2}x + \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} y + 80 = 0 \\)\n\nLet me recall that \\( x^2 + y^2 = Q \\), and from equation (1), we had \\( 3Q = \\frac{79}{2}x - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} y - 80 \\)\n\nSo, \\( Q = \\frac{79}{6}x - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{6} y - \\frac{80}{3} \\)\n\nBut \\( Q = x^2 + y^2 \\), so not sure if that helps.\n\nAlternatively, express \\( x^2 + y^2 = Q \\), so:\n\n\\( 3Q - \\frac{79}{2}x + \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} y + 80 = 0 \\)\n\nLet me see if I can relate \\( T = \\sqrt{15} x + y \\) to this equation.\n\nLet me consider multiplying \\( T \\) by some factor and adding to equation (1):\n\nSuppose I consider \\( a x + b y \\), but perhaps this is too vague.\n\nAlternatively, let me think of equation (1) as a quadratic in x and y and try to diagonalize or find a linear combination.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can parameterize x and y in terms of S.\n\nGiven that \\( S = \\sqrt{15} x + y - 6 \\sqrt{15} \\), so \\( y = S - \\sqrt{15} x + 6 \\sqrt{15} \\).\n\nLet me substitute this expression for y into equation (1):\n\nSo, substitute \\( y = S - \\sqrt{15} x + 6 \\sqrt{15} \\) into equation (1):\n\n\\( 3x^2 + 3y^2 - \\frac{79}{2}x + \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} y + 80 = 0 \\)\n\nFirst, compute each term:\n\nFirst term: \\( 3x^2 \\)\n\nSecond term: \\( 3y^2 = 3 \\left( S - \\sqrt{15} x + 6 \\sqrt{15} \\right)^2 \\)\n\nThird term: \\( - \\frac{79}{2}x \\)\n\nFourth term: \\( \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} y = \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} \\left( S - \\sqrt{15} x + 6 \\sqrt{15} \\right) \\)\n\nFifth term: \\( + 80 \\)\n\nThis will be very involved, but let me proceed step by step.\n\nFirst, expand the second term:\n\n\\( 3 \\left( S - \\sqrt{15} x + 6 \\sqrt{15} \\right)^2 \\)\n\nLet me denote \\( u = S - \\sqrt{15} x + 6 \\sqrt{15} \\), so \\( y = u \\), but actually, we need to expand the square:\n\n\\( (S - \\sqrt{15} x + 6 \\sqrt{15})^2 = [ (S + 6 \\sqrt{15}) - \\sqrt{15} x ]^2 \\)\n\nLet me denote \\( A = S + 6 \\sqrt{15} \\), \\( B = \\sqrt{15} x \\), so the expression becomes \\( (A - B)^2 = A^2 - 2AB + B^2 \\).\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( (S - \\sqrt{15} x + 6 \\sqrt{15})^2 = (S + 6 \\sqrt{15})^2 - 2 \\sqrt{15} x (S + 6 \\sqrt{15}) + ( \\sqrt{15} x )^2 \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\nSecond term: \\( 3 [ (S + 6 \\sqrt{15})^2 - 2 \\sqrt{15} x (S + 6 \\sqrt{15}) + 15 x^2 ] \\)\n\nSimilarly, the fourth term:\n\n\\( \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} \\left( S - \\sqrt{15} x + 6 \\sqrt{15} \\right) = \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} (S + 6 \\sqrt{15}) - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{15} x \\)\n\nSimplify the last part:\n\n\\( \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{15} x = \\frac{7 \\times 15}{2} x = \\frac{105}{2} x \\)\n\nTherefore, the fourth term becomes:\n\n\\( \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} (S + 6 \\sqrt{15}) - \\frac{105}{2} x \\)\n\nNow, putting all terms into equation (1):\n\nFirst term: \\( 3x^2 \\)\n\nSecond term: \\( 3 [ (S + 6 \\sqrt{15})^2 - 2 \\sqrt{15} x (S + 6 \\sqrt{15}) + 15 x^2 ] \\)\n\nThird term: \\( - \\frac{79}{2}x \\)\n\nFourth term: \\( \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} (S + 6 \\sqrt{15}) - \\frac{105}{2} x \\)\n\nFifth term: \\( + 80 \\)\n\nCombine all terms:\n\nLet me expand the second term:\n\nSecond term expanded:\n\n\\( 3(S + 6 \\sqrt{15})^2 - 6 \\sqrt{15} x (S + 6 \\sqrt{15}) + 45 x^2 \\)\n\nNow, combine all terms:\n\n\\( 3x^2 + 3(S + 6 \\sqrt{15})^2 - 6 \\sqrt{15} x (S + 6 \\sqrt{15}) + 45 x^2 - \\frac{79}{2}x + \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{2} (S + 6 \\sqrt{15}) - \\frac{105}{2}x + 80 = 0 \\)\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nFirst, the \\( x^2 \\) terms:\n\n\\( 3x^2 + 45x^2 = 48x^2 \\)\n\nNext, the \\( x \\) terms:\n\n-6 sqrt(15) x (S + 6 sqrt(15)) - (79/2)x - (105/2)x\n\n= -6 sqrt(15) x (S + 6 sqrt(15)) - ( (79 + 105)/2 ) x\n\n= -6 sqrt(15) x (S + 6 sqrt(15)) - (184/2)x\n\n= -6 sqrt(15) x (S + 6 sqrt(15)) - 92x\n\nThen, the constant terms involving \\( S \\):\n\n3(S + 6 sqrt(15))² + (7 sqrt(15)/2)(S + 6 sqrt(15))\n\nAnd finally, the constant terms without S:\n\n80\n\nSo putting together:\n\n48x² - 6 sqrt(15) x (S + 6 sqrt(15)) - 92x + 3(S + 6 sqrt(15))² + (7 sqrt(15)/2)(S + 6 sqrt(15)) + 80 = 0\n\nThis is still extremely complicated, but perhaps we can collect terms in powers of \\( S \\).\n\nLet me denote \\( K = S + 6 \\sqrt{15} \\), so that \\( S = K - 6 \\sqrt{15} \\). Then:\n\nThe equation becomes:\n\n48x² - 6 sqrt(15) x K - 92x + 3K² + (7 sqrt(15)/2) K + 80 = 0\n\nBut this substitution may not help much.\n\nAlternatively, this approach seems too algebraically intensive. Given the time I've spent and the complexity, perhaps I need to consider that this problem might have a elegant solution using Ceva's theorem with trigonometric form, combined with the given ratio BP:PC.\n\nWait, going back to the trigonometric Ceva equation:\n\nWe had:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin \\theta}{\\sin (\\angle ABC - \\theta)} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin (\\angle BCA - \\theta)}{\\sin \\theta} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin (\\angle BAC - \\gamma)}{\\sin \\gamma} = 1\n\\]\n\nWait, actually, let me recall the exact trigonometric Ceva formula again.\n\nThe trigonometric Ceva's condition for concurrency is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin \\angle ABP}{\\sin \\angle CBP} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin \\angle BCP}{\\sin \\angle ACP} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin \\angle CAP}{\\sin \\angle BAP} = 1\n\\]\n\nGiven that \\( \\angle ABP = \\theta \\), \\( \\angle ACP = \\theta \\), and let me define the other angles:\n\nLet me denote:\n\nAt vertex \\( B \\): \\( \\angle ABP = \\theta \\), so \\( \\angle CBP = \\angle ABC - \\theta \\)\n\nAt vertex \\( C \\): \\( \\angle ACP = \\theta \\), so \\( \\angle BCP = \\angle BCA - \\theta \\)\n\nAt vertex \\( A \\): Let \\( \\angle BAP = \\gamma \\), so \\( \\angle CAP = \\angle BAC - \\gamma \\)\n\nTherefore, plugging into Ceva:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin \\theta}{\\sin (\\angle ABC - \\theta)} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin (\\angle BCA - \\theta)}{\\sin \\theta} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin (\\angle BAC - \\gamma)}{\\sin \\gamma} = 1\n\\]\n\nSimplify the first two fractions:\n\nThe \\( \\sin \\theta \\) cancels:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\sin (\\angle ABC - \\theta)} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin (\\angle BCA - \\theta)}{1} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin (\\angle BAC - \\gamma)}{\\sin \\gamma} = 1\n\\]\n\nSo,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin (\\angle BCA - \\theta)}{\\sin (\\angle ABC - \\theta)} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin (\\angle BAC - \\gamma)}{\\sin \\gamma} = 1\n\\]\n\nNow, from the earlier analysis using Law of Sines in triangles \\( ABP \\) and \\( ACP \\), we found that:\n\n\\( \\sin (\\angle BAC - \\gamma) = 2 \\sin \\gamma \\)\n\nWait, yes! From earlier, we had:\n\n\\( \\sin (\\phi - \\gamma) = 2 \\sin \\gamma \\), where \\( \\phi = \\angle BAC \\), so \\( \\sin (\\angle BAC - \\gamma) = 2 \\sin \\gamma \\)\n\nTherefore, substituting into the Ceva equation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin (\\angle BCA - \\theta)}{\\sin (\\angle ABC - \\theta)} \\cdot \\frac{2 \\sin \\gamma}{\\sin \\gamma} = 1\n\\]\n\nSimplify the \\( \\sin \\gamma \\) terms:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin (\\angle BCA - \\theta)}{\\sin (\\angle ABC - \\theta)} \\cdot 2 = 1\n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin (\\angle BCA - \\theta)}{\\sin (\\angle ABC - \\theta)} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nSo now we have an equation involving \\( \\theta \\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin (\\angle BCA - \\theta)}{\\sin (\\angle ABC - \\theta)} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nNow, if we can compute the angles \\( \\angle ABC \\) and \\( \\angle BCA \\), then we can solve for \\( \\theta \\).\n\nWe already know \\( \\angle BAC = \\phi = \\arccos(17/32) \\), so we can compute \\( \\angle ABC \\) and \\( \\angle BCA \\) using the Law of Sines or Law of Cosines.\n\nLet me compute angles \\( \\angle ABC \\) and \\( \\angle BCA \\).\n\nUsing Law of Sines on triangle \\( ABC \\):\n\n\\( \\frac{AB}{\\sin \\angle BCA} = \\frac{AC}{\\sin \\angle ABC} = \\frac{BC}{\\sin \\angle BAC} \\)\n\nWe know:\n\n\\( AB = 6 \\), \\( AC = 8 \\), \\( BC = 7 \\), and \\( \\sin \\angle BAC = \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{32} \\), as computed earlier.\n\nLet me compute \\( \\sin \\angle BAC = \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{32} \\approx \\frac{7 \\times 3.872}{32} \\approx \\frac{27.104}{32} \\approx 0.847 \\), which seems reasonable.\n\nNow, let me compute \\( \\frac{BC}{\\sin \\angle BAC} = \\frac{7}{\\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{32}} = \\frac{7 \\times 32}{7 \\sqrt{15}} = \\frac{32}{\\sqrt{15}} \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( \\frac{AB}{\\sin \\angle BCA} = \\frac{32}{\\sqrt{15}} \\implies \\sin \\angle BCA = \\frac{AB \\times \\sqrt{15}}{32} = \\frac{6 \\sqrt{15}}{32} = \\frac{3 \\sqrt{15}}{16} \\)\n\nSimilarly,\n\n\\( \\frac{AC}{\\sin \\angle ABC} = \\frac{32}{\\sqrt{15}} \\implies \\sin \\angle ABC = \\frac{AC \\times \\sqrt{15}}{32} = \\frac{8 \\sqrt{15}}{32} = \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4} \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( \\sin \\angle ABC = \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4} \\)\n\n\\( \\sin \\angle BCA = \\frac{3 \\sqrt{15}}{16} \\)\n\nLet me compute the angles \\( \\angle ABC \\) and \\( \\angle BCA \\):\n\nFirst, \\( \\angle ABC \\):\n\n\\( \\sin \\angle ABC = \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4} \\approx \\frac{3.872}{4} \\approx 0.968 \\), so \\( \\angle ABC \\approx \\arcsin(0.968) \\approx 75.5^\\circ \\). Let me compute it exactly.\n\nWait, but maybe we can keep it symbolic.\n\nSimilarly, \\( \\sin \\angle BCA = \\frac{3 \\sqrt{15}}{16} \\approx \\frac{3 \\times 3.872}{16} \\approx \\frac{11.616}{16} \\approx 0.726 \\), so \\( \\angle BCA \\approx \\arcsin(0.726) \\approx 46.6^\\circ \\).\n\nBut perhaps we can find exact expressions or relate them using angle addition formulas.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet \\( \\angle ABC = \\beta \\), \\( \\angle BCA = \\gamma \\), \\( \\angle BAC = \\alpha \\), with \\( \\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = 180^\\circ \\).\n\nWe have:\n\n\\( \\sin \\beta = \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4} \\)\n\n\\( \\sin \\gamma = \\frac{3 \\sqrt{15}}{16} \\)\n\nWe need to compute \\( \\frac{\\sin (\\gamma - \\theta)}{\\sin (\\beta - \\theta)} = \\frac{1}{2} \\)\n\nLet me set \\( \\theta \\) as the variable to solve for.\n\nLet me denote \\( \\delta = \\theta \\), so the equation is:\n\n\\( \\frac{\\sin (\\gamma - \\delta)}{\\sin (\\beta - \\delta)} = \\frac{1}{2} \\)\n\nCross-multiplying:\n\n\\( 2 \\sin (\\gamma - \\delta) = \\sin (\\beta - \\delta) \\)\n\nUsing sine subtraction formulas:\n\nExpand both sides:\n\nLeft side: \\( 2 [ \\sin \\gamma \\cos \\delta - \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\delta ] \\)\n\nRight side: \\( \\sin \\beta \\cos \\delta - \\cos \\beta \\sin \\delta \\)\n\nThus,\n\n\\( 2 \\sin \\gamma \\cos \\delta - 2 \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\delta = \\sin \\beta \\cos \\delta - \\cos \\beta \\sin \\delta \\)\n\nBring all terms to left side:\n\n\\( 2 \\sin \\gamma \\cos \\delta - 2 \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\delta - \\sin \\beta \\cos \\delta + \\cos \\beta \\sin \\delta = 0 \\)\n\nFactor terms with \\( \\cos \\delta \\) and \\( \\sin \\delta \\):\n\n\\( [2 \\sin \\gamma - \\sin \\beta] \\cos \\delta + [ -2 \\cos \\gamma + \\cos \\beta ] \\sin \\delta = 0 \\)\n\nLet me denote coefficients:\n\nLet \\( A = 2 \\sin \\gamma - \\sin \\beta \\)\n\nLet \\( B = -2 \\cos \\gamma + \\cos \\beta \\)\n\nThen,\n\n\\( A \\cos \\delta + B \\sin \\delta = 0 \\)\n\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\n\\( \\tan \\delta = - \\frac{A}{B} \\)\n\nProvided that \\( B \\neq 0 \\).\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( \\tan \\delta = \\frac{ - (2 \\sin \\gamma - \\sin \\beta) }{ -2 \\cos \\gamma + \\cos \\beta } = \\frac{ 2 \\sin \\gamma - \\sin \\beta }{ 2 \\cos \\gamma - \\cos \\beta } \\)\n\nNow, substitute the known values of \\( \\sin \\beta \\), \\( \\sin \\gamma \\), \\( \\cos \\beta \\), \\( \\cos \\gamma \\):\n\nFirst, compute \\( \\sin \\beta = \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4} \\), \\( \\cos \\beta = \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^2 \\beta} = \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{15}{16}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}} = \\frac{1}{4} \\). Wait, is that correct?\n\nWait, \\( \\sin \\beta = \\sqrt{15}/4 \\approx 0.968 \\), so \\( \\cos \\beta = \\sqrt{1 - (15/16)} = \\sqrt{1/16} = 1/4 \\). Yes, since \\( (\\sqrt{15}/4)^2 + (1/4)^2 = (15 + 1)/16 = 16/16 = 1 \\). So yes, \\( \\cos \\beta = 1/4 \\).\n\nSimilarly, \\( \\sin \\gamma = 3 \\sqrt{15}/16 \\), so \\( \\cos \\gamma = \\sqrt{1 - (9 \\times 15)/256} = \\sqrt{1 - 135/256} = \\sqrt{(256 - 135)/256} = \\sqrt{121/256} = 11/16 \\).\n\nTherefore,\n\nCompute numerator:\n\n\\( 2 \\sin \\gamma - \\sin \\beta = 2 \\times \\frac{3 \\sqrt{15}}{16} - \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4} = \\frac{6 \\sqrt{15}}{16} - \\frac{4 \\sqrt{15}}{16} = \\frac{2 \\sqrt{15}}{16} = \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8} \\)\n\nDenominator:\n\n\\( 2 \\cos \\gamma - \\cos \\beta = 2 \\times \\frac{11}{16} - \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{22}{16} - \\frac{4}{16} = \\frac{18}{16} = \\frac{9}{8} \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( \\tan \\delta = \\frac{ \\sqrt{15}/8 }{ 9/8 } = \\frac{ \\sqrt{15} }{ 9 } \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( \\delta = \\arctan \\left( \\frac{ \\sqrt{15} }{ 9 } \\right) \\)\n\nHmm, okay. So \\( \\theta = \\delta = \\arctan \\left( \\frac{ \\sqrt{15} }{ 9 } \\right) \\)\n\nNow, with \\( \\theta \\) known in terms of arctangent, perhaps we can find \\( \\sin \\theta \\) and \\( \\cos \\theta \\) for further calculations?\n\nLet me compute \\( \\sin \\theta \\) and \\( \\cos \\theta \\):\n\nLet \\( \\tan \\theta = \\frac{ \\sqrt{15} }{ 9 } \\), so we can imagine a right triangle with opposite side \\( \\sqrt{15} \\), adjacent side 9, hypotenuse \\( \\sqrt{ (\\sqrt{15})^2 + 9^2 } = \\sqrt{15 + 81} = \\sqrt{96} = 4 \\sqrt{6} \\).\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( \\sin \\theta = \\frac{ \\sqrt{15} }{ 4 \\sqrt{6} } = \\frac{ \\sqrt{15} }{ 4 \\sqrt{6} } = \\frac{ \\sqrt{90} }{ 24 } = \\frac{ 3 \\sqrt{10} }{ 24 } = \\frac{ \\sqrt{10} }{ 8 } \\)\n\nWait, let me do that step-by-step:\n\nFirst, \\( \\sin \\theta = \\frac{ \\text{opposite} }{ \\text{hypotenuse} } = \\frac{ \\sqrt{15} }{ 4 \\sqrt{6} } \\)\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\( \\frac{ \\sqrt{15} }{ 4 \\sqrt{6} } = \\frac{ \\sqrt{15} }{ 4 \\sqrt{6} } \\times \\frac{ \\sqrt{6} }{ \\sqrt{6} } = \\frac{ \\sqrt{90} }{ 4 \\times 6 } = \\frac{ 3 \\sqrt{10} }{ 24 } = \\frac{ \\sqrt{10} }{ 8 } \\)\n\nYes, so \\( \\sin \\theta = \\frac{ \\sqrt{10} }{ 8 } \\)\n\nSimilarly, \\( \\cos \\theta = \\frac{9}{4 \\sqrt{6}} = \\frac{9}{4 \\sqrt{6}} \\times \\frac{ \\sqrt{6} }{ \\sqrt{6} } = \\frac{ 9 \\sqrt{6} }{ 24 } = \\frac{ 3 \\sqrt{6} }{ 8 } \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( \\cos \\theta = \\frac{ 3 \\sqrt{6} }{ 8 } \\)\n\nAlright, so now we have \\( \\sin \\theta \\) and \\( \\cos \\theta \\).\n\nNow, recall that in triangle \\( ABP \\), we had:\n\n\\( \\frac{AP}{\\sin \\theta} = \\frac{BP}{\\sin \\gamma} \\), where \\( \\gamma = \\angle BAP \\)\n\nWait, let me recall:\n\nIn triangle \\( ABP \\):\n\n\\( \\frac{AP}{\\sin \\angle ABP} = \\frac{BP}{\\sin \\angle BAP} \\implies \\frac{AP}{\\sin \\theta} = \\frac{BP}{\\sin \\gamma} \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( AP = \\frac{BP \\sin \\theta }{ \\sin \\gamma } \\)\n\nSimilarly, in triangle \\( ACP \\):\n\n\\( \\frac{AP}{\\sin \\theta} = \\frac{CP}{\\sin ( \\angle BAC - \\gamma ) } \\implies AP = \\frac{ CP \\sin \\theta }{ \\sin ( \\phi - \\gamma ) } \\)\n\nSetting them equal:\n\n\\( \\frac{ BP \\sin \\theta }{ \\sin \\gamma } = \\frac{ CP \\sin \\theta }{ \\sin ( \\phi - \\gamma ) } \\implies \\frac{ BP }{ \\sin \\gamma } = \\frac{ CP }{ \\sin ( \\phi - \\gamma ) } \\implies BP \\cdot \\sin ( \\phi - \\gamma ) = CP \\cdot \\sin \\gamma \\)\n\nWhich is the same equation we had earlier, leading to \\( \\sin ( \\phi - \\gamma ) = 2 \\sin \\gamma \\), which gave us the relation for \\( \\gamma \\).\n\nBut since we already used this to find \\( \\theta \\), perhaps we can now find \\( \\gamma \\).\n\nFrom \\( \\sin ( \\phi - \\gamma ) = 2 \\sin \\gamma \\), and we know \\( \\phi = \\angle BAC \\), which we have its sine and cosine.\n\nLet me denote \\( \\gamma = \\alpha \\), so the equation is:\n\n\\( \\sin ( \\phi - \\alpha ) = 2 \\sin \\alpha \\)\n\nWe can solve for \\( \\alpha \\).\n\nLet me expand the left side using sine subtraction formula:\n\n\\( \\sin \\phi \\cos \\alpha - \\cos \\phi \\sin \\alpha = 2 \\sin \\alpha \\)\n\nRearranged:\n\n\\( \\sin \\phi \\cos \\alpha = \\cos \\phi \\sin \\alpha + 2 \\sin \\alpha \\)\n\nFactor \\( \\sin \\alpha \\) on the right:\n\n\\( \\sin \\phi \\cos \\alpha = \\sin \\alpha ( \\cos \\phi + 2 ) \\)\n\nDivide both sides by \\( \\cos \\alpha \\):\n\n\\( \\sin \\phi = \\sin \\alpha ( \\cos \\phi + 2 ) / \\cos \\alpha \\)\n\nWhich is the same as:\n\n\\( \\sin \\phi = \\tan \\alpha ( \\cos \\phi + 2 ) \\)\n\nWhich led us to \\( \\tan \\alpha = \\sin \\phi / ( \\cos \\phi + 2 ) \\)\n\nPlugging in the known values:\n\n\\( \\sin \\phi = \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{32} \\)\n\n\\( \\cos \\phi = \\frac{17}{32} \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( \\tan \\alpha = \\frac{ \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{32} }{ \\frac{17}{32} + 2 } = \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{32} \\div \\frac{17 + 64}{32} = \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{81} \\)\n\nWhich matches our earlier result.\n\nThus, \\( \\alpha = \\arctan \\left( \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{81} \\right) \\)\n\nThis angle is quite small, but without knowing its exact value, perhaps we can find the ratio 