gle ABP = \\angle ACP \\).\n\nHmm, mass point usually deals with ratios along sides, but here we have an angle condition. Perhaps combining mass point with trigonometric relations?\n\nAlternatively, let me consider the areas.\n\nThe ratio \\( [BPC]/[ABC] \\) is what we need. Let me denote this ratio as \\( k \\).\n\nThe area of \\( ABC \\) is known: \\( \\frac{21 \\sqrt{15}}{4} \\).\n\nIf I can express \\( [BPC] \\) in terms of \\( BP \\) and angles, perhaps using the formula \\( [BPC] = \\frac{1}{2} BP \\times PC \\times \\sin \\theta \\), where \\( \\theta \\) is the angle between \\( BP \\) and \\( CP \\), but wait, actually, in triangle \\( BPC \\), the angle between \\( BP \\) and \\( CP \\) is \\( \\angle BPC \\), not \\( \\theta \\).\n\nAlternatively, if I can find \\( \\sin \\angle BPC \\), then:\n\n\\( [BPC] = \\frac{1}{2} \\times BP \\times PC \\times \\sin \\angle BPC \\)\n\nWe know \\( BP : PC = 1 : 2 \\), let me denote \\( BP = x \\), \\( PC = 2x \\), so:\n\n\\( [BPC] = \\frac{1}{2} \\times x \\times 2x \\times \\sin \\angle BPC = x^2 \\sin \\angle BPC \\)\n\nTherefore, the ratio \\( [BPC]/[ABC] = \\frac{x^2 \\sin \\angle BPC}{ \\frac{21 \\sqrt{15}}{4} } \\)\n\nThus, if I can find \\( x \\) and \\( \\sin \\angle BPC \\), I can compute this ratio.\n\nFrom earlier, in triangle \\( BPC \\), using the Law of Cosines:\n\n\\( \\cos \\angle BPC = \\frac{5x^2 - 49}{4x^2} \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( \\sin \\angle BPC = \\sqrt{1 - \\left( \\frac{5x^2 - 49}{4x^2} \\right)^2 } \\)\n\nThus,\n\n\\( [BPC] = x^2 \\sqrt{1 - \\left( \\frac{5x^2 - 49}{4x^2} \\right)^2 } \\)\n\nThis expression is quite complex, but perhaps substituting the value of \\( x \\) from another equation.\n\nAlternatively, recall from the Law of Sines in triangle \\( ABC \\), we can relate the sides and angles.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps use coordinates again but with the found values of \\( \\theta \\) and \\( \\alpha \\).\n\nRecall that in barycentric coordinates, the coordinates of \\( P \\) can be