e of \\( BP \\) is \\( \\tan(180^\\circ - \\theta) = -\\tan \\theta \\).\n\nWait, since the angle between \\( BA \\) (which is along the negative x-axis from \\( B \\)) and \\( BP \\) is \\( \\theta \\), so the direction of \\( BP \\) is \\( \\theta \\) above the negative x-axis, which would correspond to an angle of \\( 180^\\circ - \\theta \\) from the positive x-axis.\n\nTherefore, the slope of \\( BP \\) is \\( \\tan(180^\\circ - \\theta) = -\\tan \\theta \\).\n\nTherefore, the slope \\( m_{BP} = \\frac{y - 0}{x - 6} = \\frac{y}{x - 6} = -\\tan \\theta \\).\n\nThus,\n\n\\( \\frac{y}{x - 6} = -\\tan \\theta = - \\frac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta} \\)\n\nWe have \\( \\sin \\theta = \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{8} \\), \\( \\cos \\theta = \\frac{3 \\sqrt{6}}{8} \\), so:\n\n\\( \\frac{y}{x - 6} = - \\frac{ \\sqrt{10}/8 }{ 3 \\sqrt{6}/8 } = - \\frac{ \\sqrt{10} }{ 3 \\sqrt{6} } = - \\frac{ \\sqrt{15} }{ 9 } \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( y = - \\frac{ \\sqrt{15} }{ 9 } (x - 6) \\)\n\nThis gives a linear equation relating \\( x \\) and \\( y \\).\n\nSimilarly, we can derive another equation from the angle \\( \\angle ACP = \\theta \\).\n\nAt point \\( C \\), the angle \\( \\angle ACP = \\theta \\), which is the angle between vectors \\( \\vec{CA} \\) and \\( \\vec{CP} \\).\n\nVector \\( \\vec{CA} \\) is from \\( C \\) to \\( A \\): \\( (-\\frac{17}{4}, -\\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} ) \\)\n\nVector \\( \\vec{CP} \\) is from \\( C \\) to \\( P \\): \\( (x - \\frac{17}{4}, y - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} ) \\)\n\nThe angle between these two vectors is \\( \\theta \\).\n\nThe cosine of this angle is given by the dot product divided by the product of magnitudes:\n\n\\( \\cos \\theta = \\frac{ \\vec{CA} \\cdot \\vec{CP} }{ |\\vec{CA}| |\\vec{CP}| } \\)\n\nWe already computed \\( \\vec{CA} \\cdot \\vec{CP} \\) earlier in the angle equality derivation, which was:\n\n\\( -\\frac{17}{4}x - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} y + 64 \\)\n\nThe magnitude \\( |\\vec{CA}| = 8 \\), as computed before.\n\nThe magnitude \\( |\\vec{CP}| = \\sqrt{ \\left( x - \\frac{17}{4} \\right)^2 + \\left( y - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} \\right)^2 } \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( \\cos \\theta = \\frac{ -\\frac{17}{4}x - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} y + 64 }{ 8 \\times |\\vec{CP}| } \\)\n\nBut we know \\( \\cos \\theta = \\frac{ 3 \\sqrt{6} }{ 8 } \\), so:\n\n\\( \\frac{ -\\frac{17}{4}x - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} y + 64 }{ 8 |\\vec{CP}| } = \\frac{ 3 \\sqrt{6} }{ 8 } \\)\n\nMultiply both sides by \\( 8 |\\vec{CP}| \\):\n\n\\( -\\frac{17}{4}x - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} y + 64 = 3 \\sqrt{6} |\\vec{CP}| \\)\n\nThis seems very complicated, but perhaps we can substitute \\( y \\) from the previous equation \\( y = - \\frac{ \\sqrt{15} }{ 9 } (x - 6) \\) into this equation.\n\nLet me do that.\n\nFirst, substitute \\( y = - \\frac{ \\sqrt{15} }{ 9 } (x - 6) \\) into the left-hand side:\n\nLeft-hand side (LHS):\n\n\\( -\\frac{17}{4}x - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} \\left( - \\frac{ \\sqrt{15} }{ 9 } (x - 6) \\right) + 64 \\)\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\nFirst term: \\( -\\frac{17}{4}x \\)\n\nSecond term: \\( - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15}}{4} \\times \\left( - \\frac{ \\sqrt{15} }{ 9 } (x - 6) \\right) = \\frac{7 \\sqrt{15} \\times \\sqrt{15} }{ 4 \\times 9 } (x - 6) = \\frac{7 \\times 15}{36} (x - 6) = \\frac{105}{36} (x - 6) = \\frac{35}{12} (x - 6) \\)\n\nThird term: +64\n\nTherefore, LHS becomes:\n\n\\( -\\frac{17}{4}x + \\frac{35}{12} (x - 6) + 64 \\)\n\nLet me combine the terms:\n\nFirst, convert all terms to twelfths to combine:\n\n\\( -\\frac{17}{4}x = -\\frac{51}{12}x \\)\n\n\\( \\frac{35}{12}(x - 6) = \\frac{35}{12}x - \\frac{210}{12} \\)\n\n64 = \\( \\frac{768}{12} \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\nLHS = \\( -\\frac{51}{12}x + \\frac{35}{12}x - \\frac{210}{12} + \\frac{768}{12} \\)\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n\\( \\left( -\\frac{51}{12} + \\frac{35}{12} \\right) x + \\left( -\\frac{210}{12} + \\frac{768}{12} \\right) \\)\n\nSimplify coefficients:\n\nFor \\( x \\):\n\n\\( \\frac{ -51 + 35 }{ 12 } x = \\frac{ -16 }{ 12 } x = -\\frac{4}{3}