c{4x + 39}{36} \\right)^2 = \\frac{(4x + 39)^2}{36^2} \\)\n\nTherefore, the second term becomes:\n\n\\( 15 \\times \\frac{(4x + 39)^2}{36^2} = \\frac{15}{1296} (4x + 39)^2 \\)\n\nThus,\n\n\\( |\\vec{CP}|^2 = \\left( x - \\frac{17}{4} \\right)^2 + \\frac{15}{1296} (4x + 39)^2 \\)\n\nThis is quite complex, but let me compute each term separately.\n\nFirst term:\n\n\\( \\left( x - \\frac{17}{4} \\right)^2 = x^2 - \\frac{17}{2}x + \\frac{289}{16} \\)\n\nSecond term:\n\n\\( \\frac{15}{1296} (4x + 39)^2 = \\frac{15}{1296} (16x^2 + 312x + 1521) = \\frac{15}{1296} \\times 16x^2 + \\frac{15 \\times 312}{1296}x + \\frac{15 \\times 1521}{1296} \\)\n\nSimplify each coefficient:\n\nFirst coefficient: \\( \\frac{15 \\times 16}{1296} = \\frac{240}{1296} = \\frac{240 ÷ 24}{1296 ÷ 24} = \\frac{10}{54} = \\frac{5}{27} \\)\n\nSecond coefficient: \\( \\frac{15 \\times 312}{1296} = \\frac{4680}{1296} = \\frac{4680 ÷ 12}{1296 ÷ 12} = \\frac{390}{108} = \\frac{65}{18} \\)\n\nThird coefficient: \\( \\frac{15 \\times 1521}{1296} = \\frac{22815}{1296} \\). Let me see if this simplifies:\n\nDivide numerator and denominator by 3:\n\n\\( \\frac{7605}{432} \\). Divide by 3 again: \\( \\frac{2535}{144} \\). Not sure, but maybe keep as is for now.\n\nTherefore, the second term becomes:\n\n\\( \\frac{5}{27}x^2 + \\frac{65}{18}x + \\frac{22815}{1296} \\)\n\nTherefore, combining both terms:\n\n\\( |\\vec{CP}|^2 = \\left( x^2 - \\frac{17}{2}x + \\frac{289}{16} \\right) + \\left( \\frac{5}{27}x^2 + \\frac{65}{18}x + \\frac{22815}{1296} \\right) \\)\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nFirst, \\( x^2 \\) terms:\n\n\\( 1x^2 + \\frac{5}{27}x^2 = \\frac{27}{27}x^2 + \\frac{5}{27}x^2 = \\frac{32}{27}x^2 \\)\n\nNext, \\( x \\) terms:\n\n\\( - \\frac{17}{2}x + \\frac{65}{18}x = \\left( - \\frac{153}{18} + \\frac","num_tokens":32768,"correct":false,"formatted":false},{"predicted_answer":"\\dfrac{7}{18}","full_generation":"Okay, so I have this geometry problem here. Let me read it again to make sure I understand it.\n\n\"